      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Equilibria, Lyapunov stability and asymptotic stability of an autonomous first-order system",
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a map. Consider the autonomous first-order system\n\n$$z'(t)=F(z(t)),$$\n\nread as the first-order system of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]] with $D=\\mathbb R\\times U$ and right-hand side $(t,z)\\mapsto F(z)$. A **solution** below means a solution of this system on an interval containing $0$; a solution whose interval is $[0,\\infty)$ or contains it is said to be **defined for all $t\\ge0$**.\n\nA solution $\\tilde z:J\\to U$ **extends** $z:I\\to U$ when $I\\subseteq J$ and $\\tilde z|_I=z$; the extension is proper if $I\\subsetneq J$. A solution is **maximal** when it has no proper extension ([[def-maximal-ode-solution]]). All stability predicates below quantify over maximal solutions whose intervals contain $0$; their local restrictions remain solutions. This convention asserts neither existence nor uniqueness of maximal solutions for an arbitrary map $F$.\n\nA point $z_*\\in U$ is an **equilibrium** (also **fixed point** or **zero**) of $F$ when $F(z_*)=0$; then the constant function $z(t)=z_*$ is a solution on every interval containing $0$.\n\nLet $z_*\\in U$ be an equilibrium.\n\n1. $z_*$ is **Lyapunov stable** (or simply **stable**) when for every Euclidean ball $B_\\varepsilon(z_*)$ with $\\overline{B_\\varepsilon(z_*)}\\subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]) there is $\\delta\\in(0,\\varepsilon]$ such that every maximal solution $z$ with $\\|z(0)-z_*\\|_2<\\delta$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies\n\n$$\\|z(t)-z_*\\|_2<\\varepsilon\\qquad(t\\ge0).$$\n\n2. $z_*$ is **asymptotically stable** when it is Lyapunov stable and in addition there is $\\delta_0>0$ such that every maximal solution $z$ with $\\|z(0)-z_*\\|_2<\\delta_0$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies $\\|z(t)-z_*\\|_2\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\n\nAn equilibrium which is not Lyapunov stable is **unstable**. An equilibrium is **globally asymptotically stable** when it is asymptotically stable and every maximal solution of the system is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and converges to it as $t\\to\\infty$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-maximal-ode-solution",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-metric-ball"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, Section 6.5 (Stability of fixed points), printed pages 198-200, licensed author preliminary version",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, 2013), Section 6 (Non-inertial frames)",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-orbital-and-linear-stability",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Invariant sets, orbital stability of an invariant set, and linear stability of an equilibrium",
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a map; retain the convention of [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]] that a solution means a solution of $z'(t)=F(z(t))$, read as a system of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]], and that it is defined for all $t\\ge0$ when its interval contains $[0,\\infty)$. A maximal solution means one with no proper extension, as defined there; the invariance and stability predicates below quantify over maximal solutions through time $0$, not their arbitrary local restrictions. No existence or uniqueness is asserted for an arbitrary $F$.\n\n**Invariant sets.** A set $S\\subseteq U$ is **forward invariant** (for this system) when every maximal solution $z$ with $z(0)\\in S$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies $z(t)\\in S$ for all $t\\ge0$.\n\n**Distance to a set.** For a nonempty $S\\subseteq U$ define\n\n$$\\operatorname{dist}(z,S):=\\inf\\{\\,\\|z-s\\|_2:s\\in S\\,\\}\\qquad(z\\in\\mathbb R^n),$$\n\nthe infimum of a nonempty set of nonnegative reals that is bounded below, computed for the Euclidean norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Define the Euclidean closure of $S$ by\n\n$$\\overline S:=\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\text{for every }\\varepsilon>0\\text{ there is }s\\in S\\text{ with }\\|z-s\\|_2<\\varepsilon\\}.$$\n\nThe map $z\\mapsto\\operatorname{dist}(z,S)$ is nonnegative and vanishes exactly on $\\overline S$: by the defining property of the infimum, $\\operatorname{dist}(z,S)=0$ exactly when every positive-radius Euclidean ball about $z$ meets $S$. When $S=\\{z_*\\}$ is a singleton, $\\operatorname{dist}(z,S)=\\|z-z_*\\|_2$.\n\n**Orbital stability.** Let $S\\subseteq U$ be nonempty and forward invariant. Then $S$ is **orbitally stable** when for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $\\delta>0$ such that every maximal solution $z$ with $\\operatorname{dist}(z(0),S)<\\delta$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies\n\n$$\\operatorname{dist}(z(t),S)<\\varepsilon\\qquad(t\\ge0).$$\n\nIt is **asymptotically orbitally stable** when it is orbitally stable and there is $\\delta_0>0$ such that every maximal solution with $\\operatorname{dist}(z(0),S)<\\delta_0$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies $\\operatorname{dist}(z(t),S)\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\n\n**Linear stability.** Let $z_*\\in U$ be an equilibrium of $F$ and suppose $F$ is totally differentiable at $z_*$ in the sense of [[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], with total derivative $DF(z_*):\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$; write $L:=DF(z_*)$. The **linearized system** at $z_*$ is the autonomous linear system\n\n$$y'(t)=L\\,y(t).$$\n\nThe equilibrium $z_*$ is **linearly stable** when every maximal solution $y$ of the linearized system ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]) is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and is bounded on $[0,\\infty)$, that is, there is a real constant $C\\ge0$ with $\\|y(t)\\|_2\\le C$ for every $t\\ge0$. It is **linearly asymptotically stable** when every maximal solution of the linearized system is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies $\\|y(t)\\|_2\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, Sections 3.2 and 6.5, printed pages 61-67 and 198-200",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, 2015), Section 2.2 (small oscillations and equilibrium)",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-hyperbolic-equilibrium-and-limit-cycles",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Hyperbolic equilibria, hyperbolic fixed points, periodic orbits, limit cycles and homoclinic trajectories",
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a map; a solution means a solution of $z'(t)=F(z(t))$ as in [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]].\n\nFor a real linear map $A:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$, define its **complex-linear extension** $A_{\\mathbb C}:\\mathbb C^n\\to\\mathbb C^n$ by $A_{\\mathbb C}(x+iy):=Ax+iAy$ for $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^n$. In both spectral definitions below, an eigenvalue means an eigenvalue of $A_{\\mathbb C}$ over $\\mathbb C$ ([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]] with field $F=\\mathbb C$); $\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda$ and $|\\lambda|$ are the real part and modulus of [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]].\n\n**Hyperbolic equilibrium.** Let $z_*\\in U$ be an equilibrium of $F$ and suppose $F$ is totally differentiable at $z_*$ ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]]) with total derivative $DF(z_*)$. Then $z_*$ is **hyperbolic** when no eigenvalue of the real matrix of $DF(z_*)$ has real part $0$ ([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]]).\n\n**Hyperbolic fixed point of a map.** Let $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $\\varphi:V\\to V$ be a map and let $p\\in V$ satisfy $\\varphi(p)=p$. Suppose $\\varphi$ is totally differentiable at $p$. Then $p$ is a **hyperbolic fixed point** of $\\varphi$ when no eigenvalue of $D\\varphi(p)$ has modulus $1$.\n\n**Periodic solutions and periodic orbits.** A solution $z:\\mathbb R\\to U$ is **$T$-periodic** for $T>0$ when $z(t+T)=z(t)$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$, and **periodic** when it is $T$-periodic for some $T>0$. It is **nonconstant** when $z(t_1)\\ne z(t_2)$ for some $t_1,t_2$. The **image** of a periodic solution is $\\{z(t):t\\in\\mathbb R\\}$; a subset of $U$ is a **periodic orbit** when it is the image of a nonconstant periodic solution. A number $T>0$ is a **least period** of $z$ when $T$ is a period of $z$ and no $T'\\in(0,T)$ is a period of $z$.\n\n**Limit cycle.** A periodic orbit $S\\subseteq U$ is a **limit cycle** when it is **isolated**: there is an open set $W\\subseteq U$ with $S\\subseteq W$ such that the image of every nonconstant periodic solution lying in $W$ equals $S$; equivalently, no other periodic orbit of the system is contained in $W$. Stability or attraction of $S$ is a separate property and is not part of the definition.\n\n**Homoclinic and heteroclinic trajectories.** A solution $z$ defined on all of $\\mathbb R$ is **homoclinic** to the equilibrium $z_*$ when $z(t)\\to z_*$ as $t\\to+\\infty$ and as $t\\to-\\infty$ and $z$ is not the constant solution $z\\equiv z_*$. It is **heteroclinic** from $z_*$ to $z_{**}$ when $z_*\\ne z_{**}$ are equilibria and $z(t)\\to z_{**}$ as $t\\to+\\infty$ and $z(t)\\to z_*$ as $t\\to-\\infty$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability",
            "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, Section 9.1 (hyperbolic systems), printed pages 253-255, and Section 6.4 (the Poincare map), printed page 198",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, 2015), Section 2.2 and Chapter 4",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-stroboscopic-map",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The stroboscopic (time-$P$) map of a $P$-periodic first-order system",
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $P>0$, and let $F:\\mathbb R\\times U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a map that is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, in the sense of the hypotheses of [[thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution]], and that is **$P$-periodic**:\n\n$$F(t+P,z)=F(t,z)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R,\\ z\\in U).$$\n\nConsider the first-order system $x'(t)=F(t,x(t))$ of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]] on the open domain $\\mathbb R\\times U$. For $z\\in U$ let\n\n$$x_z:J_z\\longrightarrow U$$\n\nbe the unique maximal solution of the initial value problem $x'(t)=F(t,x(t))$, $x(0)=z$; here $J_z$ is the open maximal interval of existence containing $0$, and by [[thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution]] every solution of this initial value problem is a restriction of $x_z$ ([[def-maximal-ode-solution]], [[def-interval]]).\n\nThe **domain of the stroboscopic map** is\n\n$$D_P:=\\{\\,z\\in U:[0,P]\\subseteq J_z\\,\\},$$\n\nthe set of initial states whose solution exists over one full period. The **stroboscopic map** (or **time-$P$ map**) of the system is the function\n\n$$\\theta_P:D_P\\longrightarrow U,\\qquad \\theta_P(z):=x_z(P).$$\n\nThis is a well-defined function: for $z\\in D_P$ the interval $J_z$ contains $P$, so $x_z(P)$ is a point of $U$, and by the uniqueness clause of the maximal-solution theorem the value $x_z(P)$ does not depend on any choice, since $x_z$ is the only maximal solution through $(0,z)$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, Section 6.4 (the Poincare map, Problem 6.14) and Sections 4.6-4.7 on periodic equations, printed pages 198 and 133-141",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, 2013), Section 6 (motion in a rotating frame, periodic forcing examples)",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm-stroboscopic-iterate-identity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Iterates of the stroboscopic map, and equilibria as fixed points",
          "statement": "Retain all notation and hypotheses of [[def-cm-stroboscopic-map]]: $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ is open, $P>0$, and $F:\\mathbb R\\times U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is continuous, locally Lipschitz in the state variable, and $P$-periodic.\n\n1. If $z\\in D_P$ and $\\theta_P(z)\\in D_P$, then $[0,2P]\\subseteq J_z$ and\n\n$$\\theta_P(\\theta_P(z))=x_z(2P).$$\n\n2. If $z_*\\in U$ satisfies $F(t,z_*)=0$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$, then $z_*\\in D_P$ and $\\theta_P(z_*)=z_*$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-stroboscopic-map",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "lem-locally-unique-ode-solutions-agree-and-glue-on-overlaps"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-stroboscopic-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-locally-unique-ode-solutions-agree-and-glue-on-overlaps": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, Section 6.4 and Section 4.6, printed pages 198 and 133-141",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              }
            ]
