        {
          "id": "def-cm-material-region-and-density",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Material regions, densities and their mass",
          "statement": "A **material region** is a nonempty, bounded, open subset $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$. This region predicate is choice-free and does not assume a density or measure.\n\nFor the following **Lebesgue density and mass construction**, assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]): [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]] supplies the measure $\\lambda_3$ on its Lebesgue sigma-algebra ([[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]], [[def-measure]]). Since $B$ is open, it is Lebesgue measurable by the Borel-measurability theorem ([[thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable]]). Put $\\mathcal L_B:=\\{E\\cap B:E\\in\\mathcal L(\\mathbb R^3)\\}$ and $\\lambda_B(C):=\\lambda_3(C)$ for $C\\in\\mathcal L_B$. The trace family $\\mathcal L_B$ is a sigma-algebra on $B$ ([[def-sigma-algebra]]); countable additivity of $\\lambda_B$ follows by applying that of $\\lambda_3$ to disjoint trace-measurable sets, so $(B,\\mathcal L_B,\\lambda_B)$ is a measure space ([[def-measure]]). In the displays below, $\\int_B\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3$ means the Lebesgue integral of $\\rho$ on this measure space ([[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]). A **density** on $B$ is a measurable function\n\n$$\\rho:B\\longrightarrow[0,\\infty)$$\n\nmeasurable as a map from $(B,\\mathcal L_B)$ to $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$ ([[def-measurable-space]], [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]], [[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]) and integrable on $(B,\\mathcal L_B,\\lambda_B)$; the **mass** of the pair $(B,\\rho)$ is\n\n$$\\operatorname{mass}(B,\\rho):=\\int_B\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3\\ \\in[0,\\infty).$$\n\nFor this scalar function, **bounded** means that some $M<\\infty$ satisfies $0\\le\\rho(X)\\le M$ for every $X\\in B$. **Continuous** means that for every $X\\in B$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $\\delta>0$ such that $Y\\in B$ and $\\|X-Y\\|_2<\\delta$ imply $|\\rho(X)-\\rho(Y)|<\\varepsilon$. For $k\\ge1$, **of class $C^k$ on $B$** means that every iterated coordinate partial derivative of order at most $k$ exists on $B$ and is continuous there; no extension to $\\partial B$ is required unless a consuming statement says so. A **measured material region** is a triple $(B,\\rho,m)$ with $m=\\operatorname{mass}(B,\\rho)$ recorded.",
          "deps": [
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
            "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
