        {
          "id": "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The orthogonal group $O(3)$ and the rotation group $SO(3)$",
          "statement": "Let $M_3(\\mathbb R)$ be the set of $3\\times3$ matrices over $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-matrix-space]]), with identity $I_3$ and product as in [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]], transpose $R^{\\mathsf T}$ as in [[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]], and determinant $\\det R$ as in [[def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix]].\n\nThe **orthogonal group** and the **special orthogonal group** are the subsets\n\n$$O(3):=\\{R\\in M_3(\\mathbb R):R^{\\mathsf T}R=I_3\\},\\qquad SO(3):=\\{R\\in M_3(\\mathbb R):R^{\\mathsf T}R=I_3\\ \\text{and}\\ \\det R=1\\}.$$\n\nEquivalently $SO(3)=O(3)\\cap\\{R:\\det R=1\\}$. Elements of $SO(3)$ are called **rotations**. The identification of $M_3(\\mathbb R)$ with the function space $\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ and hence with $\\mathbb R^9$ is the one of [[def-matrix-space]]; when $M_3(\\mathbb R)$ is regarded as a metric or topological space, this is always the Euclidean metric of that identification.",
          "deps": [
            "def-matrix-space",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix",
            "prop-transpose-laws",
            "thm-matrix-multiplication-laws",
