          "dependency_level": 3,
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-rotating-frame-identity",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The rotating-frame acceleration identity for a uniform rotation about an axis",
          "statement": "Let $\\nu>0$ and define $R:\\mathbb R\\to SO(3)$ by the rotation about the third standard axis through angle $\\nu t$:\n\n$$R(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\nu t&-\\sin\\nu t&0\\\\ \\sin\\nu t&\\cos\\nu t&0\\\\ 0&0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).$$\n\nThen $R(t)\\in SO(3)$ for every $t$, and $\\Omega=R^{\\mathsf T}R'$ is\n\n$$\\Omega(t)=\\nu\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&0\\\\1&0&0\\\\0&0&0\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThe angular-velocity vector of [[lem-cm-moving-frame-acceleration-identity]] is $\\boldsymbol\\omega(t)=(0,0,\\nu)$, independent of $t$. For any twice continuously differentiable $y:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$, put $x(t):=R(t)y(t)$ and let $c:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be identically zero. That lemma gives\n\n$$R(t)^{\\mathsf T}x''(t)=y''(t)+2\\,\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times y'(t)+\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times(\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times y(t))\\qquad(x=Ry).$$",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-moving-frame-acceleration-identity",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
