        {
          "id": "ex-cm-mass-metric-on-a-sphere",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The mass metric of a particle constrained to the unit sphere in spherical coordinates",
          "statement": "Let $Q:=S^2=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\|x\\|_2=1\\}$ be the unit sphere ([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]), with the smooth two-dimensional embedded structure supplied by [[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] applied to $F(x)=\\langle x,x\\rangle_2$ at the regular value $1$ (verified below), let $X:Q\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb R^3$ be the inclusion, and let $m>0$ be the mass of one particle, so that the mass matrix of [[def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric]] is $M=mI_3$.\n\nThe mass metric of the inclusion is $G_q(u,v)=m\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ for $u,v\\in T_qS^2$, hence positive definite at every point by [[lem-cm-mass-metric-positivity]]. On $D:=(0,\\pi)\\times(0,2\\pi)$ consider the parametrization\n\n$$\\Psi(\\theta,\\varphi):=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\varphi,\\ \\sin\\theta\\sin\\varphi,\\ \\cos\\theta).$$\n\nIts image is the open subset $U:=S^2\\setminus\\{x\\in S^2:x_2=0,\\ x_1\\ge0\\}$, which excludes the poles and the longitude seam. The map $\\Psi:D\\to U$ is a diffeomorphism; in the manifold-to-coordinate convention the chart is $(U,\\Psi^{-1})$. Its differential is injective on $D$, and in the coordinate basis $(\\partial_\\theta,\\partial_\\varphi)$ the mass metric has the classical spherical form\n\n$$G\\bigl(\\dot\\theta\\partial_\\theta+\\dot\\varphi\\partial_\\varphi,\\ \\dot\\theta\\partial_\\theta+\\dot\\varphi\\partial_\\varphi\\bigr)=m\\bigl(\\dot\\theta^2+\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\dot\\varphi^2\\bigr).$$\n\nEquivalently, the kinetic energy of a tangent vector with coordinate velocity $(\\dot\\theta,\\dot\\varphi)$ is $T=\\tfrac m2(\\dot\\theta^2+\\sin^2\\theta\\,\\dot\\varphi^2)$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric",
            "lem-cm-mass-metric-positivity",
            "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
