        {
          "id": "ex-cm-closed-one-form-radial-primitive",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A closed field on a star-shaped domain with its radial primitive computed",
          "statement": "On $U=\\mathbb R^2$, which is star-shaped about the origin ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]), consider the $C^1$ field $F(x,y):=(2xy,\\,x^2)$ ([[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]]). Its potential\n\n$$\\phi(x,y):=x^2y$$\n\nhas gradient $\\nabla\\phi=(2xy,x^2)=F$, so $F$ is exact with potential $\\phi$. The closed field condition $\\partial_1F_0=\\partial_0F_1$ holds as well, and the radial formula of the published Poincare lemma returns exactly $\\phi$:\n\n$$\\int_0^1\\bigl\\langle F(t(x,y)),(x,y)\\bigr\\rangle_2\\,dt=\\int_0^1 3x^2y\\,t^2\\,dt=x^2y.$$",
          "deps": [
            "thm-poincare-lemma-for-star-shaped-domains",
            "def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn",
