        {
          "id": "ex-cm-stroboscopic-map-of-the-plane-oscillator",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The stroboscopic map of the plane oscillator",
          "statement": "Let $\\omega>0$ and let $F:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$, $F(t,z_1,z_2):=(z_2,-\\omega^2z_1)$, the vector field of the undamped plane oscillator. Since $F$ does not depend on $t$, it is $P$-periodic for every $P>0$; it is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, so [[def-cm-stroboscopic-map]] applies for every $P>0$.\n\nFor $z=(z_1,z_2)\\in\\mathbb R^2$ the curve\n\n$$x_z(t)=\\Bigl(z_1\\cos\\omega t+\\frac{z_2}{\\omega}\\sin\\omega t,\\ -\\omega z_1\\sin\\omega t+z_2\\cos\\omega t\\Bigr)$$\n\nis defined on all of $\\mathbb R$ and solves $x'=F(t,x)$ with $x(0)=z$. Hence $D_P=\\mathbb R^2$ for every $P>0$, and the stroboscopic map is\n\n$$\\theta_P(z)=\\Bigl(z_1\\cos\\omega P+\\frac{z_2}{\\omega}\\sin\\omega P,\\ -\\omega z_1\\sin\\omega P+z_2\\cos\\omega P\\Bigr).$$\n\nIn particular, for $P=2\\pi/\\omega$ the map $\\theta_P$ is the identity of $\\mathbb R^2$, so every initial state is a fixed point; for $P=\\pi/\\omega$ it is $\\theta_P(z)=-z$, whose only fixed point is $0$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-stroboscopic-map",
            "lem-cm-stroboscopic-iterate-identity",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
