          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), let $t_0\\in I$, let $x,y:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be differentiable at $t_0$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]) and let $\\lambda:I\\to\\mathbb R$ be differentiable at $t_0$ ([[def-derivative]]). Write $x'(t_0),y'(t_0)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ for the derivatives, $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ for the Euclidean inner product and $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ for the Euclidean norm ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), and $\\times$ for the cross product ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). Then:\n\n1. $x+y$ and $\\lambda x$ are differentiable at $t_0$, with $(x+y)'(t_0)=x'(t_0)+y'(t_0)$ and $(\\lambda x)'(t_0)=\\lambda'(t_0)x(t_0)+\\lambda(t_0)x'(t_0)$;\n2. $t\\mapsto\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$ is differentiable at $t_0$, with derivative $\\langle x'(t_0),y(t_0)\\rangle+\\langle x(t_0),y'(t_0)\\rangle$;\n3. $t\\mapsto x(t)\\times y(t)$ is differentiable at $t_0$, with derivative $x'(t_0)\\times y(t_0)+x(t_0)\\times y'(t_0)$;\n4. $t\\mapsto\\lVert x(t)\\rVert_2^2$ is differentiable at $t_0$, with derivative $2\\langle x'(t_0),x(t_0)\\rangle$.",
          "strategy": "Clause 1 componentwise from the componentwise differentiability and the real algebra of derivatives; clause 2 expands the finite sum of products; clause 3 repeats componentwise for the cross product; clause 4 applies clause 2 with y=x and symmetry.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
