        {
          "id": "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Centre of mass, the identity $\\sum_i m_i\\rho_i=0$, and the splitting of the kinetic sum $\\sum_i m_i\\lVert x_i'\\rVert_2^2$ into centre-of-mass and relative parts (with the two-body reduced-mass case)",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m2",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-interval",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $N\\in\\mathbb N$, $N\\ge1$, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), let $m_1,\\dots,m_N>0$ and let $x_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be differentiable at every point of $I$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]). All sums over $i$ range over $1,\\dots,N$: for a real family $a_1,\\dots,a_N$, define $\\sum_i a_i:=\\sum_{k<N}b_k$, where $b:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ has $b_k=a_{k+1}$ for $k<N$ and $b_k=0$ for $k\\ge N$, using the finite sums of [[lem-finite-sum-laws]]; vector sums are defined coordinatewise, and sums of curves pointwise. Put $M:=\\sum_i m_i>0$, $X:=M^{-1}\\sum_i m_ix_i$ and $\\rho_i:=x_i-X$. The symbols $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ and $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ denote the Euclidean inner product and norm on $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then:\n\n1. $X$ and every $\\rho_i$ are differentiable on $I$, with $X'=M^{-1}\\sum_im_ix_i'$ and $\\rho_i'=x_i'-X'$, and the identities $\\sum_im_i\\rho_i=0$ and $\\sum_im_i\\rho_i'=0$ hold on $I$;\n2. for every $t\\in I$, $$\\sum_{i}m_i\\lVert x_i'(t)\\rVert_2^2=M\\lVert X'(t)\\rVert_2^2+\\sum_{i}m_i\\lVert\\rho_i'(t)\\rVert_2^2;$$\n3. if $N=2$, then with $r:=x_1-x_2$ and $\\mu:=m_1m_2/M$ one has $\\sum_im_i\\lVert\\rho_i'(t)\\rVert_2^2=\\mu\\lVert r'(t)\\rVert_2^2$ and hence $$\\sum_{i}m_i\\lVert x_i'(t)\\rVert_2^2=M\\lVert X'(t)\\rVert_2^2+\\mu\\lVert r'(t)\\rVert_2^2$$ for every $t\\in I$.",
          "strategy": "Differentiate the defining finite sums; differentiate the zero identity; expand the squared norm by bilinearity with the cross term killed by sum m_i rho_i'=0; specialise rho_1=(m_2/M)r, rho_2=-(m_1/M)r.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §1.3, Eqs. (1.15)-(1.16) and (1.22)-(1.23), printed pp. 6-8: the centre of mass and the kinetic-energy split",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics, §3.4, Eqs. (3.30)-(3.35), printed pp. 40-42: centre of mass and reduced mass for two bodies",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
