          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), let $N\\ge1$, let $m_1,\\dots,m_N>0$, and let $x_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be twice differentiable, i.e. differentiable with derivative $x_i'$ differentiable ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]). Write $p_i:=m_ix_i'$ and suppose there are maps $f_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and, for every ordered pair $i\\ne j$ in $\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$, maps $f_{ij}:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ such that\n\n$$f_{ij}(t)=-f_{ji}(t),\\qquad p_i'(t)=f_i(t)+\\sum_{j\\ne i}f_{ij}(t)\\qquad(t\\in I).$$\n\nWrite $P:=\\sum_ip_i$, $J:=\\sum_ix_i\\times p_i$ (cross product in $\\mathbb R^3$, [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). Then:\n\n1. $P$ is differentiable on $I$ with $P'(t)=\\sum_if_i(t)$ for all $t\\in I$;\n2. $J$ is differentiable on $I$ with $$J'(t)=\\sum_ix_i(t)\\times f_i(t)+\\sum_{i<j}\\bigl(x_i(t)-x_j(t)\\bigr)\\times f_{ij}(t)\\qquad(t\\in I);$$\n3. if in addition, for every $i<j$, $f_{ij}$ is parallel to $x_i-x_j$ — that is, there is a real function $\\lambda_{ij}$ on $I$ with $f_{ij}=\\lambda_{ij}(x_i-x_j)$ — then $J'(t)=\\sum_ix_i(t)\\times f_i(t)$ for all $t\\in I$.",
          "strategy": "Sum the balance equations; differentiate each cross product, kill x_i' x p_i by alternation, and reindex the internal double sum using antisymmetry; parallel pairs have vanishing (x_i-x_j) x f_ij.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
