          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $N\\ge1$, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), let $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ be nonempty and open, and let $U:I\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ be the restriction of a function $\\widehat U:J\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^1$, where $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an open interval containing $I$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Write points of $Q$ as $q=(q_1,\\dots,q_N)$ with $q_i\\in\\mathbb R^3$; for $t\\in I$, $q\\in Q$ and each $i$ let $\\nabla_{x_i}U(t,q)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be the gradient of $u\\mapsto U(t,q_1,\\dots,q_{i-1},u,q_{i+1},\\dots,q_N)$ at $u=q_i$ ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]), and let $\\partial_tU$ and the spatial derivatives on $I\\times Q$ denote the derivatives of $\\widehat U$ restricted there. At an included endpoint of $I$, derivatives of curves are limits relative to $I$ (one-sided there); this preserves closed and half-open time intervals. Let $m_1,\\dots,m_N>0$, let $x:I\\to Q$ be twice differentiable ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]), put $K(t):=\\frac12\\sum_im_i\\lVert x_i'(t)\\rVert_2^2$ (Euclidean norm, [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), let $d_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be maps, and suppose\n\n$$m_ix_i''(t)=-\\nabla_{x_i}U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)+d_i(t)\\qquad(t\\in I,\\ 1\\le i\\le N).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $K$ is differentiable on $I$ with $K'(t)=\\sum_i\\langle m_ix_i''(t),x_i'(t)\\rangle$;\n2. $t\\mapsto U(t,x(t))$ is differentiable on $I$ with $$\\frac{d}{dt}U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)=\\partial_tU\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)+\\sum_i\\bigl\\langle\\nabla_{x_i}U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr),x_i'(t)\\bigr\\rangle;$$\n3. consequently $$\\frac{d}{dt}\\Bigl(K(t)+U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)\\Bigr)=\\partial_tU\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)+\\sum_i\\langle d_i(t),x_i'(t)\\rangle\\qquad(t\\in I);$$ in particular, if $d_i\\equiv0$ for all $i$ then $\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(K+U(t,x(t))\\bigr)=\\partial_tU(t,x(t))$, and if in addition $\\partial_tU\\equiv0$ then $t\\mapsto K(t)+U(t,x(t))$ is constant on $I$.",
          "strategy": "Differentiate the squared norms; apply the chain rule to the composite U(t,x(t)) and read the total derivative on (1,x'(t)) through the Jacobian; add and substitute the force identity; conclude constancy by the zero-derivative criterion.",
