        {
          "id": "thm-cm-variable-mass-momentum-balance",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Variable-mass momentum balance: from $m'=r_{\\rm in}-r_{\\rm out}$ and $(mv)'=F+r_{\\rm in}w_{\\rm in}-r_{\\rm out}w_{\\rm out}$ to $mv'=F+r_{\\rm in}(w_{\\rm in}-v)-r_{\\rm out}(w_{\\rm out}-v)$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e11 (variable-mass packet accounting)",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-derivative",
            "def-interval",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior ([[def-interval]]), let $m:I\\to(0,\\infty)$ and $v:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be differentiable ([[def-derivative]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]), and let $F:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$, $r_{\\mathrm{in}},r_{\\mathrm{out}}:I\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $w_{\\mathrm{in}},w_{\\mathrm{out}}:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be maps. Suppose the two **material balance identities**\n\n$$m'(t)=r_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)-r_{\\mathrm{out}}(t),\\qquad (mv)'(t)=F(t)+r_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)w_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)-r_{\\mathrm{out}}(t)w_{\\mathrm{out}}(t)$$\n\nhold for every $t\\in I$, where $mv$ denotes the pointwise scalar multiple. Then\n\n$$m(t)v'(t)=F(t)+r_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)\\bigl(w_{\\mathrm{in}}(t)-v(t)\\bigr)-r_{\\mathrm{out}}(t)\\bigl(w_{\\mathrm{out}}(t)-v(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in I).$$\n\nIn particular, if $F\\equiv0$, $r_{\\mathrm{in}}\\equiv0$ and $r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\equiv r$ for a constant $r\\ge0$, then $mv'=-r(w_{\\mathrm{out}}-v)$: the acceleration is governed by the momentum carried away per unit time relative to the body, not by $(mv)'=0$.\n\nThe two balance identities are the **adopted content of the material momentum accounting** — the mathematical rendering of \"gains $r_{\\mathrm{in}}\\,dt$ of matter with velocity $w_{\\mathrm{in}}$, loses $r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\,dt$ with velocity $w_{\\mathrm{out}}$, and feels the external force $F$\". They are not derived here from any closed-system law; the deduction below is their product-rule consequence.",
          "strategy": "Product rule coordinatewise for (mv)', substitute the mass-rate identity, and group the flux terms.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §1.2, printed p. 2: the momentum form $\\mathbf F=\\dot{\\mathbf p}$ for a body of varying mass, with the warning that the closed-system statement needs open-system momentum accounting",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Classical Mechanics (University of Texas at Austin), Non-Isolated Systems: the total-momentum balance $d\\mathbf P/dt=\\mathbf F$ with external force, the form that fails to track ejected/absorbed material without the flux terms below",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node12.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
