          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, locally uniformly in time, in the sense of [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]. Let $t_0<b$ be reals, let $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$, and let $x:[t_0,b)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$x'(t)=F\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr),\\qquad x(t_0)=x_0$$\n\non $[t_0,b)$ ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). Suppose there is a compact set $K\\subseteq D$ and a real $\\delta>0$ such that\n\n$$\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)\\in K\\qquad\\text{for every }t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. there is $\\varepsilon>0$ and a solution $\\tilde x:[t_0,b+\\varepsilon)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of the same initial value problem with $\\tilde x(t)=x(t)$ for all $t\\in[t_0,b)$; in particular $\\tilde x(b)=\\lim_{t\\uparrow b}x(t)$ exists and lies in the state projection of $K$;\n2. if moreover $D=\\mathbb R\\times U$ for an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $F$ is autonomous, $F(t,z)=G(z)$ with $G:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]), and if there is a compact $K_0\\subseteq U$ with $x(t)\\in K_0$ for every $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b)$, then the extension conclusion of clause 1 holds as well.",
          "strategy": "Bound F on K, get a Lipschitz estimate from the Volterra equation, produce the one-sided limit x_b by completeness, place (b,x_b) in the closed K, apply Picard-Lindelof at (b,x_b), and glue by uniqueness; clause 2 repeats the argument with K0.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, §2.6 Extensibility of solutions, printed pp. 50-53: gluing, Corollary 2.15 (compact continuation) and Corollary 2.16 (escape of every compact set)",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Julien Arino, MATH 8430 Fundamental Theory of Ordinary Differential Equations (University of Manitoba), §1.3 Continuation of solutions, printed pp. 24-28: Lemma 1.3.1, Theorem 1.3.3 and Theorem 1.3.7",
                "url": "https://julien-arino.github.io/assets/pdf/courses/math8430_lecture_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cor-cm-finite-time-breakdown-requires-escape",
          "kind": "corollary",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A non-extendable solution leaves every compact subset of the domain near its finite endpoint; an autonomous solution trapped in a compact subset of its state domain cannot break down there",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m10",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable in the sense of [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]. Let $t_0<b$ be reals, let $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$, and let $x:[t_0,b)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a solution of the initial value problem $x'=F(t,x)$, $x(t_0)=x_0$ ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). Suppose $x$ admits no extension past $b$: there do not exist $\\varepsilon>0$ and a solution $\\tilde x:[t_0,b+\\varepsilon)\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of the same initial value problem with $\\tilde x(t)=x(t)$ for every $t\\in[t_0,b)$. Then:\n\n1. for every compact $K\\subseteq D$ and every $\\eta>0$ there is $t\\in(b-\\eta,b)\\cap[t_0,b)$ with $(t,x(t))\\notin K$; that is, the graph of $x$ leaves every compact subset of $D$ that could contain it arbitrarily close to $b$;\n2. if $D=\\mathbb R\\times U$ for an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, $F$ is autonomous, $F(t,z)=G(z)$ with $G:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, and there are a compact $K_0\\subseteq U$ and $\\delta>0$ with $x(t)\\in K_0$ for all $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap[t_0,b)$, then the no-extension hypothesis is false: $x$ does extend past $b$.",
          "strategy": "Both clauses are the contrapositive/contradiction form of the compact continuation theorem.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, §2.6, Corollary 2.16, printed p. 53: if the maximal right endpoint is finite the solution eventually leaves every compact set",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Julien Arino, MATH 8430 Fundamental Theory of Ordinary Differential Equations (University of Manitoba), §1.3, Theorem 1.3.3 and Corollary 1.3.4, printed pp. 25-26: failure of continuation forces the solution to approach the boundary of the domain",
                "url": "https://julien-arino.github.io/assets/pdf/courses/math8430_lecture_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
