          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]), used only through the Borel-measurability, Lebesgue-integration and distribution suppliers cited below. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval ([[def-interval]]), let $t_*\\in I$, and let $p:I\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy:\n\n1. $p$ is continuous on $I\\setminus\\{t_*\\}$ and of class $C^1$ on each of the two open intervals $I\\cap(-\\infty,t_*)$ and $I\\cap(t_*,\\infty)$;\n2. the one-sided limits $p(t_*^-):=\\lim_{t\\uparrow t_*}p(t)$, $p(t_*^+):=\\lim_{t\\downarrow t_*}p(t)$ and the one-sided limits of the derivative $p'(t_*^-):=\\lim_{t\\uparrow t_*}p'(t)$, $p'(t_*^+):=\\lim_{t\\downarrow t_*}p'(t)$ all exist in $\\mathbb R$.\n\nFix a value $p(t_*)\\in\\mathbb R$ arbitrarily (for instance $p(t_*):=0$) and define $p'_{\\mathrm{reg}}:I\\to\\mathbb R$ by $p'_{\\mathrm{reg}}(t):=p'(t)$ for $t\\ne t_*$ and $p'_{\\mathrm{reg}}(t_*):=0$. Put $[p]:=p(t_*^+)-p(t_*^-)$ and let $u_p,u_{p'_{\\mathrm{reg}}}\\in\\mathcal D'(I)$ be the associated regular distributions ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[def-locally-integrable-function-on-r-n]]): $$u_p(\\varphi)=\\int_Ip(t)\\,\\varphi(t)\\,d\\lambda(t),\\qquad u_{p'_{\\mathrm{reg}}}(\\varphi)=\\int_Ip'_{\\mathrm{reg}}(t)\\,\\varphi(t)\\,d\\lambda(t).$$\n\nThen $p$ and $p'_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ are locally integrable on $I$, and\n\n$$(u_p)'=u_{p'_{\\mathrm{reg}}}+[p]\\,\\delta_{t_*}$$\n\nin $\\mathcal D'(I)$ ([[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]): that is, for every test function $\\varphi\\in\\mathcal D(I)$,\n\n$$-\\int_Ip(t)\\,\\varphi'(t)\\,d\\lambda(t)=\\int_Ip'_{\\mathrm{reg}}(t)\\,\\varphi(t)\\,d\\lambda(t)+[p]\\,\\varphi(t_*).$$\n\nBoth integrals are over the Lebesgue measure $\\lambda$ on $\\mathbb R$, and the pairing convention of the two sides is complex-linear in $\\varphi$; it therefore suffices that the identity hold for real-valued test functions.",
          "strategy": "Local integrability from finitely many discontinuities plus the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison; two half-interval integrations by parts of the C1 extensions, assembled with a.e. equality and linearity.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Semyon Dyatlov, Lecture notes for 18.155 (MIT, 2022): distributional differentiation and the jump of the Heaviside function (the source whose conventions the library distributions use)",
                "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~dyatlov/18.155/155-notes.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §1.2, printed p. 2: the variable-mass momentum remark and the caution that a finite force at each point does not by itself determine momentum jumps",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-cm-impulse-restitution-and-energy-loss",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Isolated reciprocal impulse with full-vector restitution: momentum jumps $\\pm J$, conservation of total momentum, the restitution solution and the kinetic-energy change $-\\frac{1-e^2}{2}\\mu\\lVert v_2^--v_1^-\\rVert_2^2$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e11",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "lem-cm-piecewise-c1-jump-derivative",
            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-interval",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity",
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-cm-piecewise-c1-jump-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]), inherited from the jump lemma below.\nLet $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), let $t_*\\in I$, let $m_1,m_2>0$, and let $x_1,x_2:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be continuous on $I$ and twice continuously differentiable on each of the two open intervals $I\\cap(-\\infty,t_*)$ and $I\\cap(t_*,\\infty)$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Assume the one-sided limits\n$$v_i^-:=\\lim_{t\\uparrow t_*}x_i'(t),\\qquad v_i^+:=\\lim_{t\\downarrow t_*}x_i'(t)\\qquad(i=1,2)$$\nexist in $\\mathbb R^3$. Define $p_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $p_i(t):=m_ix_i'(t)$ for $t\\ne t_*$ and fix the representative value $p_i(t_*):=0$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]]). Let $F_1,F_2:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be continuous and let $J\\in\\mathbb R^3$. The **isolated reciprocal impulse model** at $t_*$ consists of the two requirements:\n\n1. regular dynamics off the impulse: $m_ix_i''(t)=F_i(t)$ for every $t\\in I\\setminus\\{t_*\\}$ and $i=1,2$;\n2. impulse pairing: for every $\\Phi\\in C_c^\\infty(I;\\mathbb R^3)$ — meaning every map whose three components lie in $C_c^\\infty(I)$ ([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) — $$-\\int_I\\langle p_1(t),\\Phi'(t)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda(t)=\\int_I\\langle F_1(t),\\Phi(t)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda(t)+\\langle J,\\Phi(t_*)\\rangle,$$ $$-\\int_I\\langle p_2(t),\\Phi'(t)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda(t)=\\int_I\\langle F_2(t),\\Phi(t)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda(t)-\\langle J,\\Phi(t_*)\\rangle,$$ with integrals against Lebesgue measure $\\lambda$ and the Euclidean inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), using the fixed representative $p_i(t_*)=0$ above.\n\nUnder this model, with $M:=m_1+m_2$ and $\\mu:=m_1m_2/M$:\n\n**a.** $m_1(v_1^+-v_1^-)=J$ and $m_2(v_2^+-v_2^-)=-J$; consequently the total momentum is continuous across the impulse, $p_1(t_*^+)+p_2(t_*^+)=p_1(t_*^-)+p_2(t_*^-)$.\n\n**b.** Let $e\\in[0,1]$ satisfy the **restitution law** $v_2^+-v_1^+=-e(v_2^--v_1^-)$. Then the post-impulse velocities are uniquely determined: with $V:=\\frac{m_1v_1^-+m_2v_2^-}{M}=\\frac{m_1v_1^++m_2v_2^+}{M}$, $$v_1^+=V+\\frac{m_2}{M}e\\,(v_2^--v_1^-),\\qquad v_2^+=V-\\frac{m_1}{M}e\\,(v_2^--v_1^-).$$\n\n**c.** The kinetic-energy change across the impulse is $$\\Delta K:=\\frac{m_1\\lVert v_1^+\\rVert_2^2+m_2\\lVert v_2^+\\rVert_2^2}{2}-\\frac{m_1\\lVert v_1^-\\rVert_2^2+m_2\\lVert v_2^-\\rVert_2^2}{2}=-\\frac{1-e^2}{2}\\,\\mu\\,\\lVert v_2^--v_1^-\\rVert_2^2\\le0;$$ in particular $\\Delta K=0$ for $e=1$ and $\\Delta K=-\\frac12\\mu\\lVert v_2^--v_1^-\\rVert_2^2$ for $e=0$.",
          "strategy": "Apply the scalar jump lemma to each momentum component and compare with the model pairing to get the jumps; solve the linear system for the post-impulse velocities; use the two-body kinetic splitting with the common centre-of-mass velocity.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §1.2-§1.3, printed pp. 2-8: $\\mathbf F=\\dot{\\mathbf p}$ as the primitive balance, together with the caution that the momentum balance of an open (variable-mass) system is not the closed-system statement",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics, §§1.2-1.3, Eqs. (1.8), (1.30)-(1.38), printed pp. 6-12: isolated-system momentum and pair reciprocity underlying impulse accounting",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
