            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-variable-mass-momentum-balance": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Throughout, $e\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is a unit vector, $\\lVert e\\rVert_2=1$, for the Euclidean norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]. All numbers are pure real numbers. For the two collision computations, $v_i^-$ and $v_i^+$ denote pre- and post-collision vectors. Given positive masses and $e_r\\in[0,1]$, require only the finite algebraic relations\n\n$$m_1v_1^++m_2v_2^+=m_1v_1^-+m_2v_2^-,\\qquad v_2^+-v_1^+=-e_r(v_2^--v_1^-).$$\n\nDefine $J:=m_1(v_1^+-v_1^-)$ and\n\n$$\\Delta K:=\\frac{m_1\\lVert v_1^+\\rVert_2^2+m_2\\lVert v_2^+\\rVert_2^2-m_1\\lVert v_1^-\\rVert_2^2-m_2\\lVert v_2^-\\rVert_2^2}{2}.$$\n\nThe two cases below solve these relations uniquely and compute $J$ and $\\Delta K$ directly. No distributional jump theorem or choice assumption is used in these finite calculations.\n\n**(i) Equal masses, half-elastic impulse.** Take $m_1=m_2=1$, approach velocities $v_1^-=1\\,e$, $v_2^-=-1\\,e$ and restitution coefficient $e_r=\\frac12$. Then the unique post-collision vectors, impulse and kinetic-energy change are\n\n$$v_1^+=-\\tfrac12\\,e,\\qquad v_2^+=\\tfrac12\\,e,\\qquad J=-\\tfrac32\\,e,\\qquad \\Delta K=-\\tfrac34 .$$\n\n**(ii) Elastic impulse with unequal masses.** Take $m_1=1$, $m_2=3$, $v_1^-=2\\,e$, $v_2^-=0$ and $e_r=1$. Then\n\n$$v_1^+=-1\\,e,\\qquad v_2^+=1\\,e,\\qquad J=-3\\,e,\\qquad \\Delta K=0 .$$\n\n**(iii) Rocket check.** On $I=[0,1]$ take $m(t):=2-t$, $v(t):=\\ln\\!\\bigl(\\frac{2}{2-t}\\bigr)e$, $F\\equiv0$, $r_{\\mathrm{in}}\\equiv0$, $r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\equiv1$, $w_{\\mathrm{in}}\\equiv0$, $w_{\\mathrm{out}}(t):=v(t)-u_e\\,e$ with $u_e:=1$. Then the two material balance identities of [[thm-cm-variable-mass-momentum-balance]] hold, the derived law reads $m(t)v'(t)=1\\cdot e$, and $v(1)=\\ln(2)\\,e$. The naive closed-system reading $(mv)'=F\\equiv0$ would instead force $v\\equiv v(0)=0$ on $I$, which does not satisfy the material balance; the two readings differ as soon as $t>0$.",
          "strategy": "Solve finite momentum and restitution equations uniquely and evaluate kinetic differences directly, without the distributional jump theorem or choice assumption; differentiate the rocket functions on all [0,1] and compare the naive candidate with fixed outflow data.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §§1.2-1.3, printed pp. 2-8: momentum balances and the variable-mass caution",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
