            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]), and let $\\Phi:\\Omega\\to U$ be the local flow of $x'=F(x)$ as in [[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]], with maximal solutions $x(\\,\\cdot\\,;a):I_a\\to U$ and flow domain $\\Omega=\\{(t,a):t\\in I_a\\}$. Then:\n\n1. $(0,a)\\in\\Omega$ and $\\Phi(0,a)=a$ for every $a\\in U$;\n2. if $(s,a)\\in\\Omega$ and $(t,\\Phi(s,a))\\in\\Omega$, then $(s+t,a)\\in\\Omega$ and $\\Phi(s+t,a)=\\Phi\\bigl(t,\\Phi(s,a)\\bigr)$;\n3. if $(t,a)\\in\\Omega$, then $(-t,\\Phi(t,a))\\in\\Omega$ and $\\Phi\\bigl(-t,\\Phi(t,a)\\bigr)=a$.",
          "strategy": "Glue translates of the maximal solution through a using uniqueness and maximality of solutions.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 6.1 (properties of the flow phi(t, x); the cocycle identity), printed pages 190-192",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
