        {
          "id": "def-cm-finite-time-tangent-growth-rate-and-lyapunov-exponent",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite-time tangent growth rates and Lyapunov exponents of a C¹ flow",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e12",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system",
            "cor-cm-flow-domain-open-and-flow-is-c1",
            "lem-cm-local-flow-composition-law",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-natural-logarithm",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cm-flow-domain-open-and-flow-is-c1": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-local-flow-composition-law": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-natural-logarithm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be of class $C^1$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and let $\\Phi:\\Omega\\to U$ be the local flow of $x'=F(x)$ ([[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]]); recall that $\\Omega$ is open and $\\Phi$ is $C^1$ on $\\Omega$ ([[cor-cm-flow-domain-open-and-flow-is-c1]]).\n\nLet $x\\in U$ and $t>0$ with $(t,x)\\in\\Omega$, and let $u\\in\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\{0\\}$ be such that $D_x\\Phi_t(x)u\\ne0$, where $D_x\\Phi_t(x)$ is the derivative of the map $y\\mapsto\\Phi(t,y)$ at $x$ ([[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). The **finite-time tangent growth rate** of $(x,u)$ over the time $t$ is\n\n$$r(t,x,u):=\\frac{1}{t}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{\\|D_x\\Phi_t(x)u\\|_2}{\\|u\\|_2}\\right)\\in\\mathbb R,$$\n\nthe Euclidean norms being those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and $\\log$ the natural logarithm ([[def-natural-logarithm]]). The expression is a real number because $\\|u\\|_2>0$ and $\\|D_x\\Phi_t(x)u\\|_2>0$.\n\nFor the infinite-time notion require the forward lifetime $I_x=\\{t:(t,x)\\in\\Omega\\}$ to be unbounded above. Since $I_x$ is an interval containing $0$, this means that $(t,x)\\in\\Omega$ for every $t\\ge0$. Under this additional condition, if the limit\n\n$$\\lambda(x,u):=\\lim_{\\substack{t\\to\\infty\\\\(t,x)\\in\\Omega}} r(t,x,u)$$\n\nexists in $\\mathbb R$, it is called the **Lyapunov exponent of $(x,u)$**. If the forward lifetime is bounded above or the limit does not exist, no Lyapunov exponent is assigned; in particular no value $+\\infty$ or $-\\infty$ is named by this definition.",
          "strategy": "Define the rate and the exponent from the derivative of the time-t map, with the existence of the limit an explicit clause and no spectral or ergodic interpretation attached.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 9.1, formulas (9.3)-(9.6): exponential rates for the linearisation of a flow along a hyperbolic orbit",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "C. Ulcigrai, MATH36206/MATHM6206 Topological and Symbolic Dynamics, Section 2.3 (rates of divergence and sensitivity)",
                "url": "https://m-artigiani.github.io/files/SisDin-Part2.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-bilateral-shift-space-and-cylinders",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The bilateral two-symbol shift space, its metric, its shift map and its cylinder sets",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e12",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-function-space",
            "def-series",
            "thm-geometric-series",
            "def-integer-power",
            "def-abs-value"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-function-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-geometric-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integer-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-abs-value": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let\n\n$$X:=\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb Z}=\\{\\,s\\;:\\;s\\text{ is a function }\\mathbb Z\\to\\{0,1\\}\\,\\}$$\n\nbe the set of bi-infinite binary sequences, with the function-set convention of [[def-function-space]]; for $s\\in X$ write $s_k:=s(k)$ for the value at the integer $k$. Define\n\n$$d(s,t):=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}2^{-k}\\,|s_k-t_k|\\;+\\;\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}2^{-k}\\,|s_{-k}-t_{-k}|\\qquad(s,t\\in X).$$\n\nBoth series converge by comparison with the geometric series $\\sum_{k\\ge0}2^{-k}=2$ ([[def-series]], [[thm-geometric-series]], [[def-integer-power]], [[def-abs-value]]), since $|s_j-t_j|\\in\\{0,1\\}$ for every $j\\in\\mathbb Z$; hence $d(s,t)$ is a well-defined real number. That $d$ is a metric on $X$ is proved in the companion lemma [[lem-cm-bilateral-shift-metric-and-sequential-compactness]] and is not part of this definition.\n\nThe **(bilateral) shift** is the map\n\n$$\\sigma:X\\longrightarrow X,\\qquad \\sigma(s)_k:=s_{k+1}\\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ s\\in X).$$\n\nFor $n\\ge1$ the $n$-fold iterate is written $\\sigma^n$, defined by $\\sigma^1:=\\sigma$ and $\\sigma^{n+1}:=\\sigma\\circ\\sigma^n$; a point $s\\in X$ is **periodic** when $\\sigma^n(s)=s$ for some $n\\ge1$.\n\nA **cylinder** is a set of the form\n\n$$[w]_{p,q}:=\\{\\,s\\in X\\;:\\;s_k=w_k\\text{ for }p\\le k\\le q\\,\\},$$\n\nwhere $p\\le q$ are integers and $w=(w_p,\\dots,w_q)\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\{p,\\dots,q\\}}$ is a finite binary word; the integers $p,q$ and the word $w$ are part of the data. When $p=q$ this is the set of sequences with prescribed value at the single coordinate $p$.",
          "strategy": "Define the symbol space, its metric expression, the shift and the cylinders; the metric axioms are proved in the companion lemma and are not part of the definition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "C. Ulcigrai, MATH36206/MATHM6206 Topological and Symbolic Dynamics (University of Bristol/Manchester), Section 2.6: the full bi-sided shift space Sigma_N, and Section 2.7: the metric d_rho and cylinders C_{-m,n}(a_{-m},...,a_n)",
                "url": "https://m-artigiani.github.io/files/SisDin-Part2.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-cm-bilateral-shift-metric-and-sequential-compactness"
          ]
        },
