        {
          "id": "def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Topological mixing, dense periodic points, sensitive dependence, and the stated chaos list",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e12",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-metric-space",
            "def-metric-topology",
            "def-dense-top",
            "def-metric-ball",
            "def-metric-continuity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-metric-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-dense-top": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-continuity": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) with its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]]) and let $f:X\\to X$ be a map. For $n\\ge1$ write $f^n$ for the $n$-fold iterate, $f^1:=f$ and $f^{n+1}:=f\\circ f^n$.\n\n1. $f$ is **topologically mixing** when for all nonempty open sets $U,V\\subseteq X$ there is an integer $N$ such that\n\n$$f^n(U)\\cap V\\ne\\emptyset\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge N .$$\n\n2. A point $x\\in X$ is a **periodic point** of $f$ when $f^n(x)=x$ for some $n\\ge1$. The set of periodic points of $f$ is written $\\operatorname{Per}(f)$. The periodic points are **dense** in $X$ when $\\operatorname{Per}(f)$ is dense in $X$, that is ([[def-dense-top]]) when every nonempty open subset of $X$ meets $\\operatorname{Per}(f)$.\n\n3. $f$ has **sensitive dependence on initial conditions** when there is a real number $\\delta>0$ such that for every $x\\in X$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$ there are $y\\in X$ and $n\\ge1$ with\n\n$$d(x,y)<\\varepsilon\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad d\\bigl(f^n(x),f^n(y)\\bigr)\\ge\\delta .$$\n\nSuch a number $\\delta$ is called a **sensitivity constant** for $f$.\n\nFinally, say that $f$ is **chaotic in the stated sense** when $f$ is continuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]), topologically mixing, has dense periodic points, and has sensitive dependence on initial conditions.",
          "strategy": "Record the three-property chaos package as a definition of the chosen list, with the uniform quantifiers explicit; no entropy or measure-theoretic mixing is asserted.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "C. Ulcigrai, MATH36206/MATHM6206 Topological and Symbolic Dynamics, Definitions 2.2.2-2.2.4 and 2.3.4 (topological transitivity, minimality, mixing and sensitive dependence)",
                "url": "https://m-artigiani.github.io/files/SisDin-Part2.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "E. Glasner and B. Weiss, Sensitive dependence on initial conditions (Nonlinearity 6, 1993, 1067-1075; updated author version), Section 0: the three properties (transitivity, dense periodic points, sensitivity) used in one standard definition of a chaotic system",
                "url": "https://www.math.tau.ac.il/~glasner/papers/sensi.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
