        {
          "id": "rem-cm-recurrence-scope-for-mechanical-systems",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Scope of Poincaré recurrence for mechanical systems: finite invariant volume and complete return maps",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e12",
          "design_state": "completed-scope-remark; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems",
            "cor-topological-poincare-recurrence-on-second-countable-spaces",
            "cor-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-volume-hamiltonian-invariant-regions",
            "def-measure-preserving-transformation-and-system",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system",
            "thm-cm-bilateral-shift-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-topological-poincare-recurrence-on-second-countable-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-volume-hamiltonian-invariant-regions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-measure-preserving-transformation-and-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-bilateral-shift-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "The recurrence content attached to this page is supplied by published items and is subject to their stated hypotheses.\n\n1. In a measure-preserving system $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu,T)$ with $\\mu(X)<\\infty$, for every $E\\in\\mathcal A$ almost every point of $E$ returns to $E$ under $T^n$ for infinitely many positive integers $n$ ([[thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems]], [[def-measure-preserving-transformation-and-system]]). If $X$ is second countable with a countable basis in $\\mathcal A$, then outside one null set every neighbourhood of every point is revisited infinitely often, and in the metrizable case there is a strictly increasing sequence $n_j$ with $T^{n_j}x\\to x$ ([[cor-topological-poincare-recurrence-on-second-countable-spaces]]). For a Hamiltonian flow, a measurable invariant region of finite symplectic volume on which a nonzero time map and all of its iterates are defined satisfies the same recurrence conclusion for that time map ([[cor-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-volume-hamiltonian-invariant-regions]]).\n\n2. The following qualifications are part of the statement and are not removable.\n\n- **Finiteness of measure is a hypothesis.** The conclusion fails in general for infinite measure: for $T(x)=x+1$ on $\\mathbb R$ with Lebesgue measure, the set $E=(0,1/2)$ has positive measure and no point of $E$ returns to $E$ at any positive integer time, so the analogue of clause 1 is false without $\\mu(X)<\\infty$. For a concrete mechanical contrast take the free-particle Hamiltonian $H(q,p)=p^2/(2m)$ on $X=\\mathbb R_q\\times\\mathbb R_p$ with $m>0$, phase volume $dq\\,dp$, and complete flow $\\phi_t(q,p)=(q+tp/m,p)$. Its time-one map preserves this infinite volume (each fixed-$p$ slice is a translation in $q$). In $E_{\\mathrm{free}}=(0,1/2)\\times(m,2m)$ every positive integer iterate has $q_n=q+np/m>1$, so no point returns to this positive-volume set. Thus complete, volume-preserving mechanical motion on the full unconfined phase space need not be recurrent; the finite-invariant-volume hypothesis is essential.\n- **The return map must be defined for all iterates.** The time-$\\tau$ map of a Hamiltonian flow is a measure-preserving map only where it is defined: the invariant region must be contained in the domain of $\\phi_\\tau$ and of every iterate $\\phi_{n\\tau}$. For a local flow in the sense of [[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]] this is an additional hypothesis on the region, not a consequence of the flow being locally defined; on a compact energy level of a complete bounded system it must be verified from the model.\n- **Recurrence is an almost-everywhere statement about a measure, not about every trajectory.** The null exceptional set depends on the measurable set being revisited; a specified trajectory, or a finite-time observation, is not claimed to return. The topological corollary supplies a single null set only after a countable basis is fixed and one grants its countable-choice hypotheses.\n- **No identification with the shift example.** The bilateral shift of [[thm-cm-bilateral-shift-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive]] is a topological example on the symbol space; no claim is made here that it arises as the return map of a smooth mechanical system, and no claim about any physical system is made by this remark.",
          "strategy": "Collect the published recurrence statements with their hypotheses and the explicit counterexample to the infinite-measure analogue; assert no identification of the symbolic example with a mechanical system.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "M. Einsiedler and T. Ward, Ergodic Theory with a view towards Number Theory, first four chapters with appendices (LMS-EPSRC Summer School 2010), Chapter 2 (Poincare recurrence, Theorem 2.11 and Exercise 2.2.3, printed pages 21-23)",
                "url": "https://webspace.maths.qmul.ac.uk/f.vivaldi/teaching/ETAD/NotesI.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "A. Cannas da Silva, Lectures on Symplectic Geometry, Lecture 18 (Hamiltonian flow invariance of symplectic volume), p. 105",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20120413034139if_/http://www.math.ist.utl.pt:80/%7Eacannas/Books/symplectic.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
