          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open with $0\\in U$, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be of class $C^1$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with $F(0)=0$. Put $A:=DF(0)$ ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]) and suppose that every complex eigenvalue of $A$ ([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]]) has negative real part.\n\nThen $0$ is a locally exponentially stable equilibrium of $z'=F(z)$ ([[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]]): there are real numbers $\\delta>0$, $\\beta\\ge1$ and $\\gamma>0$ such that every maximal solution $z$ of $z'=F(z)$ with $\\|z(0)\\|_2<\\delta$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies\n\n$$\\|z(t)\\|_2\\le\\beta e^{-\\gamma t}\\|z(0)\\|_2\\qquad(t\\ge0).$$\n\nIn particular $0$ is Lyapunov stable and asymptotically stable in the sense of [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]].",
          "strategy": "Build the positive definite Lyapunov matrix Q from the integral of e^{tA^T}e^{tA}, verify A^TQ+QA=-I by differentiating the integrand, and show that V=z^TQz decreases exponentially on a small compact sublevel set that traps every nearby solution by compact continuation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "F. Ramponi, Notes on Lyapunov's theorem, Sections 2-4: the Lyapunov function V = x^T P x with P = integral of e^{A^T t} Q e^{A t}, and stability via linearisation",
                "url": "https://federico-ramponi.unibs.it/docs/lyapunov.pdf"
