          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open with $0\\in U$, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be of class $C^1$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with $F(0)=0$. Put $A:=DF(0)$ ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]) and suppose that\n\n- no complex eigenvalue of $A$ ([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]]) has real part $0$, and\n- at least one eigenvalue of $A$ has positive real part.\n\nThen the equilibrium $0$ is not Lyapunov stable ([[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]]): there is a real number $\\varepsilon_0>0$ such that for every $\\delta>0$ there exist $z_0\\in U$ with $\\|z_0\\|_2<\\delta$ and a time $t_1>0$ such that the maximal solution $z$ of $z'=F(z)$, $z(0)=z_0$ is defined on $[0,t_1]$ and satisfies\n\n$$\\|z(t_1)\\|_2=\\varepsilon_0 .$$",
          "strategy": "Use the indefinite form built from the unstable and stable Lyapunov matrices; it grows exponentially on a small ball along unstable data, and compact continuation forces arbitrarily small data to leave a fixed neighbourhood in finite time.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "F. Ramponi, Notes on Lyapunov's theorem, Section 4 (instability when the linearisation has an eigenvalue with positive real part) and the counterexamples x' = -x^2, x' = x^2",
                "url": "https://federico-ramponi.unibs.it/docs/lyapunov.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 9.2 (stable and unstable manifolds of a hyperbolic fixed point), printed pages 255-262",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The mechanical first-order field, Lagrangian and energy of a configuration-dependent kinetic metric in a chart",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m7",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group",
            "def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $M:\\Omega\\to M_n(\\mathbb R)$ be of class $C^2$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with $M(q)$ symmetric and positive definite for every $q\\in\\Omega$, that is $v^{\\mathsf T}M(q)v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let $U:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$. The matrix field $M$ is the **kinetic metric field** and $U$ the **potential** of the data in the chart $\\Omega$.\n\nCoordinates and matrix entries are indexed by $0\\le i,j,k<n$, as in [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]. Vectors are read as columns: for $A\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$, $(Av)_i:=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}A_{ij}v_j$, and $v^{\\mathsf T}Av:=\\sum_{i,j=0}^{n-1}v_iA_{ij}v_j$.\n\nDefine, for $(q,v)\\in\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^n$, the **kinetic energy**, **Lagrangian** and **energy**\n\n$$T(q,v):=\\tfrac12v^{\\mathsf T}M(q)v,\\qquad L(q,v):=T(q,v)-U(q),\\qquad E(q,v):=T(q,v)+U(q),$$\n\nand, for $q\\in\\Omega$ and $v\\in\\mathbb R^n$, the matrix\n\n$$D_qM(q)[v]:=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}v_k\\,\\partial_kM(q),\\qquad\\bigl(\\partial_kM(q)\\bigr)_{ij}=\\partial_kM_{ij}(q)\\quad(0\\le i,j,k<n).$$\n\nThe **mechanical vector field** of the data is the map\n\n$$G:\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^n\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad G(q,v)=\\Bigl(v,\\ M(q)^{-1}\\bigl(\\tfrac12\\nabla_q(v^{\\mathsf T}M(q)v)-\\bigl(D_qM(q)[v]\\bigr)v-\\nabla U(q)\\bigr)\\Bigr),$$\n\nwhere $\\nabla_q$ and $\\nabla U$ are Euclidean gradients ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]) and $M(q)^{-1}$ is the inverse of the positive definite matrix $M(q)$ ([[def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group]], [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]). The **mechanical system** in the chart is the first-order system\n\n$$q'(t)=v(t),\\qquad v'(t)=M(q(t))^{-1}\\Bigl(\\tfrac12\\nabla_qf\\bigl(q(t),v(t)\\bigr)-\\bigl(D_qM(q(t))[v(t)]\\bigr)v(t)-\\nabla U(q(t))\\Bigr),\\qquad f(q,v):=v^{\\mathsf T}M(q)v .$$\n\nA point $q_* \\in\\Omega$ is a **mechanical equilibrium** when $\\nabla U(q_*)=0$.",
          "strategy": "Define the chart model, its Lagrangian, energy and first-order field, with the inverse of the positive definite kinetic metric understood as a matrix inverse; record the equilibrium convention and nothing physical.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 8.6, formulas (8.76)-(8.78): the harmonic oscillator with mass matrix M and stiffness W and the decoupled system after the substitution (P,Q)=(VM^{1/2}p, VM^{-1/2}q)",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 2.6 (small oscillations and the normal-mode equations), printed pages 37-45",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
