          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $M\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ be symmetric and positive definite, that is $v^{\\mathsf T}Mv>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^n$; identify $\\mathbb R^n$ with its standard real inner product space ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then:\n\n1. $M$ is invertible and $M^{-1}$ is symmetric and positive definite;\n2. there is a unique symmetric positive definite matrix $M^{1/2}$ with $(M^{1/2})^2=M$;\n3. $M^{-1/2}:=(M^{1/2})^{-1}$ is symmetric and positive definite, satisfies $(M^{-1/2})^2=M^{-1}$, and $M^{-1/2}MM^{-1/2}=I_n$;\n4. for every symmetric matrix $K$ that is non-negative, that is $v^{\\mathsf T}Kv\\ge0$ for all $v$, the matrix $S:=M^{-1/2}KM^{-1/2}$ is symmetric and non-negative, and $S$ is positive definite if and only if $K$ is.",
          "strategy": "Transfer positivity to the associated operators, use the unique non-negative square root, verify the inverse and congruence identities by matrix algebra, and transport definiteness through the congruence.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
