          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $U:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and let $q_0\\in\\Omega$ be a local minimum of $U$: there is $\\rho>0$ with $\\overline B(q_0,\\rho)\\subseteq\\Omega$ and $U(q)\\ge U(q_0)$ for every $q\\in\\overline B(q_0,\\rho)$. Then\n\n$$\\nabla U(q_0)=0,\\qquad h^{\\mathsf T}D^2U(q_0)h\\ge0\\quad\\text{for every }h\\in\\mathbb R^n,$$\n\nwhere $D^2U(q_0)$ is the Hessian matrix, which is symmetric ([[cor-hessian-is-symmetric]], [[def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point]]).",
          "strategy": "Apply Fermat's theorem along the lines t->q_0+th for the vanishing gradient, and the second-order Taylor expansion with the Peano remainder for the semidefiniteness; no strictness is assumed.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
