          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $M:\\Omega\\to M_n(\\mathbb R)$ be a $C^2$ field of symmetric positive definite matrices, let $U:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^2$, and let $q_0\\in\\Omega$ be a mechanical equilibrium, that is $\\nabla U(q_0)=0$ ([[def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart]]). Let $G$ be the mechanical vector field on $\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^n$. Then:\n\n1. $G$ is totally differentiable at $(q_0,0)$ ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]]) and, writing $D^2U(q_0)$ for the Hessian matrix ([[def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point]]),\n\n$$DG(q_0,0)(\\delta q,\\delta v)=\\Bigl(\\delta v,\\ -M(q_0)^{-1}D^2U(q_0)\\,\\delta q\\Bigr)\\qquad(\\delta q,\\delta v\\in\\mathbb R^n);$$\n\n2. consequently the linearised equation at the equilibrium is\n\n$$\\xi''=-M(q_0)^{-1}D^2U(q_0)\\,\\xi,\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad M(q_0)\\xi''+D^2U(q_0)\\,\\xi=0,$$\n\na constant-coefficient system whose mass matrix $M(q_0)$ is symmetric positive definite and whose stiffness matrix $D^2U(q_0)$ is symmetric ([[cor-hessian-is-symmetric]]);\n3. if in addition $q_0$ is a strict local minimum of $U$, then $K:=D^2U(q_0)$ is non-negative, so the hypotheses of [[thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition]] are satisfied by $M(q_0)$ and $K$, and the linearised system is exactly the constant-coefficient quadratic system of that theorem with mass matrix $M(q_0)$ and stiffness $K$; its modes are the normal modes of the linearisation.",
          "strategy": "Differentiate the explicit components of the field at the equilibrium, using that the v-dependent terms are quadratic in v and that the gradient of U vanishes, and identify the Hessian of U as the stiffness; restrict the quadratic-mode theorem to the strict-minimum case.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 8.4, formulas (8.76)-(8.77): linearisation of the potential at a critical point, M q'' + W q = 0 with W the Hessian",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 2.6, equations (2.113)-(2.116): expansion of the equation of motion about an equilibrium",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm-scope-of-energy-stability-and-quadratic-modes",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Scope of the quadratic-mode and energy-stability results: exact quadratic model versus linearisation approximation",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m7",
          "design_state": "completed-scope-remark; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition",
            "thm-cm-energy-stability-of-a-strict-minimum",
            "prop-cm-linearization-of-the-mechanical-system-at-an-equilibrium",
            "def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-energy-stability-of-a-strict-minimum": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-linearization-of-the-mechanical-system-at-an-equilibrium": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "The three results of this group establish different, and differently qualified, statements.\n\n1. [[thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition]] is an exact theorem about the constant-coefficient linear system $Mq''+Kq=0$ with $M$ symmetric positive definite and $K$ symmetric non-negative: it diagonalises the system, gives all solutions and the modal energies. It is not an approximation statement, and it makes no claim about nonlinear systems or about configuration-dependent kinetic metrics.\n\n2. [[prop-cm-linearization-of-the-mechanical-system-at-an-equilibrium]] computes the derivative of the nonlinear mechanical field at an equilibrium. Its linearised equation is the exact first-order Taylor model at that point; replacing the nonlinear system by it is an approximation whose error over a time interval is not estimated by the proposition. A zero eigenvalue $\\lambda_i=0$ of $M(q_0)^{-1/2}D^2U(q_0)M(q_0)^{-1/2}$ corresponds to an affine mode of the linearisation, not to an oscillation, and a negative eigenvalue corresponds to exponential growth of the linearisation.\n\n3. [[thm-cm-energy-stability-of-a-strict-minimum]] is an exact Lyapunov-stability theorem for the nonlinear system at a strict minimum of the potential. It is a statement about the nonlinear field of [[def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart]], uses exact energy conservation, and therefore proves stability but not attraction: the theorem does not assert that solutions converge to the equilibrium, and in a conservative model with no dissipation they do not in general. It also does not assert that the linearisation determines the nonlinear stability in non-hyperbolic cases, and it does not require the Hessian to be positive definite.",
          "strategy": "Separate the exact quadratic model, the linearisation approximation and the nonlinear stability theorem, and state explicitly which conclusions are and are not drawn in each case.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 2.6 (the small-oscillation approximation and its higher-order terms), printed pages 37-45",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 6.7 (stability and non-asymptotic character of conservative equilibria), printed pages 203-205",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
