          "id": "def-cm-holonomic-constraint-and-ideal-reactions",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A regular holonomic constraint, its virtual displacements and the ideal-reaction law",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m6",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-euclidean-submersions-and-immersions",
            "def-regular-critical-points-values-and-level-sets",
            "def-tangent-space-to-a-regular-level-set",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-interval",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-submersions-and-immersions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-regular-critical-points-values-and-level-sets": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tangent-space-to-a-regular-level-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$ and $k\\ge1$, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open and nonempty, let\n$G:O\\to\\mathbb R^k$ be of class $C^2$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and write $G_q(t,q)$ for the Jacobian matrix of\n$q\\mapsto G(t,q)$ ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]), a $k\\times n$ matrix. The constraint\n\n$$G(t,q)=0$$\n\nis **regular** when\n\n$$G_q(t,q)\\ \\text{has rank }k\\ \\text{for every }(t,q)\\in O,$$\n\nequivalently when $G_q$ is surjective at every point ([[def-euclidean-submersions-and-immersions]]).\nUnder this hypothesis each fibre $G(t,\\cdot)^{-1}(0)$ is a regular level set and every point of it\nis a regular point in the sense of [[def-regular-critical-points-values-and-level-sets]].\n\n**Constraint set and admissible data.** Fix a symmetric positive definite matrix\n$M\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$, meaning $M^{\\mathsf T}=M$ and $u^{\\mathsf T}Mu>0$ for every $0\\ne u\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and a continuous map\n$f:O\\times\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$, the **given force**. A map $q:I\\to\\mathbb R^n$ on an open\ninterval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ ([[def-interval]]) is a **constrained curve** when\n\n$$(t,q(t))\\in O,\\qquad G\\bigl(t,q(t)\\bigr)=0\\qquad(t\\in I).$$\n\n**Virtual displacements and permitted velocities.** For $(t,q)\\in O$ the **virtual displacements**\nat $(t,q)$ are the elements of the kernel\n\n$$V(t,q):=\\ker G_q(t,q)=\\bigl\\{\\delta\\in\\mathbb R^n:G_q(t,q)\\delta=0\\bigr\\}\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n,$$\n\nthe tangent space to the level set of $G(t,\\cdot)$ at $q$ in the sense of\n[[def-tangent-space-to-a-regular-level-set]]; a **permitted velocity** at $(t,q)$ is a vector\n$v\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with\n\n$$G_t(t,q)+G_q(t,q)v=0 .$$\n\nFor a $C^1$ constrained curve the differentiated constraint forces\n$G_t(t,q(t))+G_q(t,q(t))q'(t)=0$, so its velocity is automatically permitted; the permitted\ncondition is stated separately because it is the correct initial condition for the first-order\nsystem below.\n\n**Ideal-constraint motions.** A $C^2$ constrained curve $q:I\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is an\n**ideal-constraint motion** of the data $(G,M,f)$ when\n\n1. $q'(t)$ is a permitted velocity at $(t,q(t))$ for every $t\\in I$, and\n2. there is a continuous map $\\lambda:I\\to\\mathbb R^k$ with\n\n$$Mq''(t)=f\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)+G_q\\bigl(t,q(t)\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T}\\lambda(t)\\qquad(t\\in I).$$\n\nThe vector field $R(t):=Mq''(t)-f(t,q(t),q'(t))$ is the **reaction** and\n$R=G_q^{\\mathsf T}\\lambda$ is its **multiplier form**. Since\n$G_q(t,q)^{\\mathsf T}\\lambda\\cdot\\delta=\\lambda\\cdot G_q(t,q)\\delta=0$ for every\n$\\delta\\in V(t,q)$, every ideal-constraint motion satisfies the **d'Alembert condition**\n\n$$\\bigl(Mq''(t)-f(t,q(t),q'(t))\\bigr)\\cdot\\delta=0\\qquad\\text{for every }\\delta\\in V(t,q(t)),$$\n\ni.e. the reaction performs no virtual work; the converse implication, that every curve satisfying\nthe d'Alembert condition is an ideal-constraint motion with a *unique* multiplier, together with\nuniqueness of the resulting acceleration, is the content of\n[[lem-cm-constraint-multipliers-and-accelerations]] and is not assumed here.\n\n**Scope.** The definition is chartwise and time-dependent: the constraint may depend on $t$\n(rheonomic case), its velocity condition is the fixed-time linearisation recorded above, and no\nrelation between the *variational* (vakonomic) treatment of the constraint and the present\nd'Alembert law is asserted. The construction and long-time behaviour of solutions is the subject of\n[[prop-cm-ideal-holonomic-system-in-a-time-dependent-chart]]. For a holonomic constraint written\nin a chart, the multiplier $\\lambda$ is not a measured quantity: only the reaction\n$G_q^{\\mathsf T}\\lambda$ enters the equations of motion.",
          "strategy": "Define the regular constraint, virtual displacements, permitted velocities and the ideal-reaction law with the d'Alembert condition derived as a consequence.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 5.9 (Lagrange multipliers for Holonomic Constraints) and Section 6.8 (Applications to systems involving holonomic constraints)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.3 (constraints, generalised coordinates and Lagrange multipliers for holonomic constraints), printed pages 17-22",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-infinitesimal-point-symmetry",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Infinitesimal point symmetry of a time-dependent Lagrangian and its conserved charge",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m5",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(O,\\mathcal L)$ be a time-dependent Lagrangian chart over $[a,b]\\times U$ in the sense of\n[[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]]. Let $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times V$ be open sets containing respectively the configuration projection of $O$ and its time-configuration projection. Let\n\n$$\\xi:V\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad B:A\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\n\nbe of class $C^1$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); write $D\\xi(q)\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$\nfor the Jacobian matrix of $\\xi$ at $q$ ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]],\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]), and for a $C^1$ function $\\varphi(t,q)$ write\n\n$$D_t\\varphi(t,q,v):=\\varphi_t(t,q)+\\varphi_q(t,q)\\cdot v .$$\n\nThe pair $(\\xi,B)$ is an **infinitesimal point symmetry of $\\mathcal L$** when the identity\n\n$$\\mathcal L_q(t,q,v)\\cdot\\xi(q)+\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v)\\cdot D\\xi(q)v =B_t(t,q)+B_q(t,q)\\cdot v$$\n\nholds for every $(t,q,v)\\in O$. The **charge of the pair** is the function\n\n$$C(t,q,v):=\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v)\\cdot\\xi(q)-B(t,q).$$\n\n**Reading of the identity.** The left-hand side is the derivative of the Lagrangian under the\ninfinitesimal family of point transformations\n\n$$q\\longmapsto q+s\\,\\xi(q),\\qquad t\\longmapsto t$$\n\nwith the velocity transformed accordingly by $v\\mapsto v+s\\,D\\xi(q)v$, and the right-hand side is\nthe total derivative $\\frac{d}{dt}B(t,q(t))$ along a $C^1$ trajectory with $q'(t)=v$; thus the identity says that the infinitesimal variation of\n$\\mathcal L$ is a total time derivative of the chart function $B$. In particular:\n\n- Taking $B=0$ reduces the identity to\n  $\\mathcal L_q(t,q,v)\\cdot\\xi(q)+\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v)\\cdot D\\xi(q)v=0$, the invariance of\n  $\\mathcal L$ under the infinitesimal family.\n- For a constant field $\\xi\\equiv a$ one has $D\\xi=0$, and the identity reads\n  $\\mathcal L_q\\cdot a=B_t+B_q\\cdot v$; when $B$ is independent of $q$ this is the statement that\n  $\\mathcal L$ is unchanged by the translation $q\\mapsto q+sa$ up to a total time derivative.\n\n**Scope.** The definition is an identity imposed on the chart data; it does not assert that a\ngiven Lagrangian possesses any symmetry, nor that a symmetry of the configuration space, a frame\nchange or any kinematic structure automatically induces one. Whether a symmetry produces a\nconserved quantity along the Euler–Lagrange solutions is the theorem\n[[thm-cm-point-symmetry-charge-is-conserved]]; the general reparametrisation version is\n[[thm-cm-point-noether-identity-with-time-reparametrization]], and the scope of the statement for\nphysical models is recorded in [[rem-cm-scope-of-point-symmetry]].",
          "strategy": "Define the symmetry data, the identity and the charge, distinguishing the time-independent case; the conservation theorem is separately stated.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 7.2 (Generalized Momentum) and Section 7.3 (Invariant Transformations and Noether's Theorem)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.4.1 'Noether's Theorem' (continuous symmetries of the Lagrangian), printed pages 24-26",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Legendre map of a time-dependent Lagrangian: regularity and hyperregularity",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m4",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "**Chart data.** Let $n\\ge1$, let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ and $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and\nnonempty, and let\n\n$$\\mathcal L:J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\n\nbe of class $C^2$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Such a map is a **fibre-full\ntime-dependent Lagrangian chart**; it is the special case of the chart of\n[[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]] in which the velocity block is the whole space\n$\\mathbb R^n$ at every $(t,q)\\in J\\times U$. Write $\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v)\\in\\mathbb R^n$ for the\nEuclidean gradient of $\\mathcal L$ in its third slot and $\\mathcal L_{vv}(t,q,v)\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$\nfor the matrix of second partial derivatives in the third slot\n([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]).\n\n**Legendre map.** The **Legendre map** of $\\mathcal L$ is the map\n\n$$\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}:J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n\\longrightarrow J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n, \\qquad \\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}(t,q,v):=\\bigl(t,q,\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v)\\bigr).$$\n\nThe fibre coordinate of the target is called the **momentum** and is written\n$p:=\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v)$. Thus $\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}$ is the identity in the first two slots and\nthe gradient in the velocity slot. Since $\\mathcal L$ is $C^2$, $\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}$ is $C^1$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]).\n\n**Regularity.** Let $(t,q,v)\\in J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n$. The Legendre map is **regular at\n$(t,q,v)$** when the velocity Hessian is nonsingular,\n\n$$\\det\\mathcal L_{vv}(t,q,v)\\ne0 ;$$\n\nit is **regular (on the chart)** when it is regular at every point. At fixed $(t,q)$ this is\nexactly the condition under which the map $v\\mapsto(q,\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v))$ is locally invertible\nnear $v$, a statement made precise and proved in\n[[lem-cm-legendre-map-local-invertibility]].\n\n**Hyperregularity.** The Lagrangian $\\mathcal L$ is **hyperregular (on the chart)** when for every\n$(t,q)\\in J\\times U$ the map\n\n$$v\\longmapsto\\mathcal L_v(t,q,v)$$\n\nis a bijection of $\\mathbb R^n$ onto $\\mathbb R^n$ and the inverse assignment\n\n$$(t,q,p)\\longmapsto v(t,q,p),\\qquad \\mathcal L_v\\bigl(t,q,v(t,q,p)\\bigr)=p,$$\n\nis defined on all of $J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n$ and is of class $C^1$. In that case $v$ is called\nthe **inverse Legendre branch** of $\\mathcal L$, and the **Hamiltonian of the branch** is\n\n$$H:J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad H(t,q,p):=p\\cdot v(t,q,p)-\\mathcal L\\bigl(t,q,v(t,q,p)\\bigr).$$\n\nThe Hamiltonian is $C^1$ on its domain, being a sum of products of $C^1$ maps.\n\nThe definition asserts nothing about solving the equations of motion: the equivalence of the\nEuler–Lagrange equation with Hamilton's equations on an inverse branch, and the differential of\n$H$, are proved in [[prop-cm-hamiltonian-branch-identities]].",
          "strategy": "Define the fibre-full chart, the Legendre map, regularity, hyperregularity, the inverse branch and the Hamiltonian with all regularity hypotheses explicit.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 8.1-8.2 (Hamiltonian mechanics; Legendre Transformation between Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.1.1 'The Legendre Transform' (the transform from L to H and the convexity requirement), printed pages 82-83",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "forward_refs": [
            "ex-cm-regular-lagrangians-need-not-be-hyperregular"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-nonholonomic-velocity-constraint-law",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The velocity constraint $z'=xy'$: virtual displacements, the d'Alembert law and the vakonomic law",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e5",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
