          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(O,\\mathcal L)$ be a time-dependent Lagrangian chart over $[a,b]\\times U$ in the sense of\n[[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]], let $q:[a,b]\\to U$ be a $C^2$ admissible curve and\nlet $\\eta$ be a variation of $q$. Then:\n\n1. there is $\\varepsilon_0>0$ such that for every $\\varepsilon\\in(-\\varepsilon_0,\\varepsilon_0)$\n   the curve $q+\\varepsilon\\eta$ is again a $C^2$ admissible curve with values in $U$;\n2. for every such $\\varepsilon$ the action $S(q+\\varepsilon\\eta)$ is defined, the function\n   $\\varepsilon\\mapsto S(q+\\varepsilon\\eta)$ is differentiable on $(-\\varepsilon_0,\\varepsilon_0)$\n   and\n\n$$\\delta S(q)[\\eta]=\\int_a^b\\Bigl[\\mathcal L_q\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)\\cdot\\eta(t)+\\mathcal L_v\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)\\cdot\\eta'(t)\\Bigr]dt ;$$\n\n3. the Euler–Lagrange expression $E_{\\mathcal L}(q):[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of\n   [[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]] is continuous.",
          "strategy": "Differentiate under the integral on a compact parameter rectangle; integrability and continuity follow from the C^2 hypotheses.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 5.2 (Euler's Differential Equation: the first variation by integration by parts) and Section 5.5 (Functions with Several Independent Variables)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.1 (first variation of the action and integration by parts), printed pages 10-13",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm-legendre-map-local-invertibility",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Nonsingular velocity Hessian is equivalent to invertibility of the Legendre derivative, hence gives a local C^1 inverse",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m4",
          "design_state": "completed-local-lemma; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
