            "cor-square-matrix-invertible-iff-determinant-is-a-unit": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal L:J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ be a fibre-full time-dependent Lagrangian\nchart of class $C^2$ with Legendre map $\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}$\n([[def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart]]). Then:\n\n1. $\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}$ is $C^1$ and at every $(t,q,v)$ its total derivative acts on\n   $(\\tau,\\delta q,\\delta v)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R^n$ by\n\n$$D\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}(t,q,v)(\\tau,\\delta q,\\delta v) =\\Bigl(\\tau,\\ \\delta q,\\ \\mathcal L_{vt}(t,q,v)\\tau+\\mathcal L_{vq}(t,q,v)\\delta q+\\mathcal L_{vv}(t,q,v)\\delta v\\Bigr);$$\n\n2. $D\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}(t,q,v)$ is invertible ([[def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map]]) if and\n   only if $\\det\\mathcal L_{vv}(t,q,v)\\ne0$; consequently $\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}$ is regular at\n   $(t,q,v)$ in the sense of [[def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart]] exactly when its\n   derivative there is invertible;\n3. if $\\mathcal L_{vv}(t_0,q_0,v_0)$ is nonsingular, then $\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}$ is a local\n   $C^1$ diffeomorphism at $(t_0,q_0,v_0)$: there are open neighbourhoods\n   $W\\ni(t_0,q_0,v_0)$ and $W'\\ni\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}(t_0,q_0,v_0)$ such that\n   $\\mathbb F_{\\mathcal L}|_W:W\\to W'$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism\n   ([[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]).",
          "strategy": "Compute the total derivative of the Legendre map and apply the Euclidean inverse function theorem where the velocity Hessian is nonsingular.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 8.2 (Legendre Transformation between Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.1.1 (inversion of the Legendre transform where the maximum exists), printed pages 82-83",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
