            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open and nonempty, and let\n$X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be of class $C^3$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with\n$X_q(t,q)$ invertible for every $(t,q)\\in\\Omega$\n([[def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map]], [[def-linear-map]]). Let $O_x\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R^n$\nbe open and let $L:O_x\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$. Put\n\n$$O:=\\bigl\\{(t,q,v)\\in\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^n:\\bigl(t,X(t,q),X_t(t,q)+X_q(t,q)v\\bigr)\\in O_x\\bigr\\},\\qquad \\widetilde L(t,q,v):=L\\bigl(t,X(t,q),X_t(t,q)+X_q(t,q)v\\bigr).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $O$ is open, $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R^n$, and $\\widetilde L$ is of\n   class $C^2$ on $O$;\n2. with $w:=X_t(t,q)+X_q(t,q)v$ one has\n   $\\widetilde L_v(t,q,v)=X_q(t,q)^{\\mathsf T}L_v(t,X(t,q),w)$;\n3. $\\widetilde L_q(t,q,v)=X_q^{\\mathsf T}L_x+\\bigl(X_{tq}+X_{qq}[v]\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T}L_v$, where\n   $X_{tq}$ is the $t$-derivative of $X_q$ and $X_{qq}[v]$ is the matrix with entries\n   $\\sum_j\\partial_j\\partial_iX_k(t,q)v_j$, all evaluated at $(t,q)$ and $L_x,L_v$ at\n   $(t,X(t,q),w)$;\n4. **(transport)** let $a<b$ be reals and let $q:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a $C^2$ curve with\n   $(t,q(t),q'(t))\\in O$ for all $t\\in[a,b]$, put $x(t):=X(t,q(t))$, and define\n   $P(t):=\\widetilde L_v(t,q(t),q'(t))$ and $p(t):=L_v(t,x(t),x'(t))$, which are $C^1$. Then\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}P(t)-\\widetilde L_q\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr) =X_q\\bigl(t,q(t)\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T}\\Bigl(\\frac{d}{dt}p(t)-L_x\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr)\\Bigr) \\qquad(t\\in[a,b]);$$\n\n   consequently, since $X_q(t,q(t))$ is invertible, the Euler–Lagrange expression of\n   $\\widetilde L$ vanishes along $q$ if and only if the Euler–Lagrange expression of $L$ vanishes\n   along $x$.",
          "strategy": "Chain rule and mixed-partial equality for the chart change; invertibility of X_q for the equivalence; project the ambient equation for the reduced form.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 6.3 (Lagrange Equations from d'Alembert's Principle) and Section 5.8 (Generalized coordinates in Variational Calculus)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.2 'Changing Coordinate Systems' (Lagrange's equations hold in any coordinate system, with the rotating-coordinate example), printed pages 13-16",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "prop-cm-hamiltonian-branch-identities",
          "kind": "proposition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Hamiltonian on an inverse Legendre branch: differential, equivalence with the Euler-Lagrange equation, and the energy identity",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m4",
          "design_state": "completed-local-proposition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
