            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-darboux-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(O,\\mathcal L)$ be a time-dependent Lagrangian chart over $[a,b]\\times U$\n([[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]]), let $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open and contain the time-configuration projection of $O$, and let $F:A\\to\\mathbb R$\nbe of class $C^3$, and define\n\n$$\\widetilde{\\mathcal L}(t,q,v):=\\mathcal L(t,q,v)+F_t(t,q)+F_q(t,q)\\cdot v .$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $\\widetilde{\\mathcal L}$ is $C^2$ on $O$, and for every $C^2$ curve $q:[a,b]\\to U$,\n\n$$E_{\\widetilde{\\mathcal L}}(q)(t)=E_{\\mathcal L}(q)(t)\\qquad(t\\in[a,b]);$$\n\n2. for every such curve, $\\widetilde S(q)=S(q)+F(b,q(b))-F(a,q(a))$, where $S$ and\n   $\\widetilde S$ are the actions of $\\mathcal L$ and $\\widetilde{\\mathcal L}$;\n3. consequently $q$ is stationary for $\\widetilde S$ if and only if $q$ is stationary for $S$;\n4. for every real $c\\ne0$, $E_{c\\mathcal L}(q)=c\\,E_{\\mathcal L}(q)$ and hence the stationary\n   curves of $c\\mathcal L$ and of $\\mathcal L$ coincide; for $c=0$ one has $E_0=0$, so *every*\n   $C^2$ curve is stationary for the zero Lagrangian, and setting $c=0$ does not preserve the\n   stationary set unless every $C^2$ curve was already stationary for $\\mathcal L$.",
          "strategy": "Compute the Euler-Lagrange expression of L+dF/dt and the action difference; the scaling statement is direct from the definition of stationarity.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 9.3 (Lagrangian; gauge invariance and equivalent Lagrangians under addition of a total time derivative)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.5.7 (the equations of motion are invariant under addition of a total derivative to the Lagrangian), printed page 37",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
