        {
          "id": "rem-cm-scope-of-stationarity",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Scope of stationarity: stationary is not minimum, and no second-variation theory is developed here",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m3",
          "design_state": "completed-local-remark; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
            "thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "- Stationarity, as defined in [[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]] and characterised\n  by [[thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange]], is the vanishing of the first variation for all\n  fixed-endpoint variations. It is *not* the statement that $q$ minimizes the action, nor that it\n  is a local minimum. The companion page exhibits an explicit quadratic model in which a stationary\n  curve is not a local minimum: for the Lagrangian $\\mathcal L(q,v)=\\tfrac12(v^2-4q^2)$ on\n  $[0,\\pi]$ and the stationary curve $q_*(t)=\\sin 2t$, the exact identity\n  $S(q_*+\\epsilon\\sin)-S(q_*)=-\\tfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\epsilon^2$ shows that every nearby element of the\n  one-parameter family has strictly smaller action.\n\n- No second variation, and no convexity or coercivity hypothesis, is introduced on this page. The\n  stationarity theorem is an equivalence between zero first variation and the Euler–Lagrange equation. For a local extremum on fixed-endpoint $C^2$ curves, use the norm $\\|q\\|_{C^2}:=\\max_{0\\le j\\le2}\\sup_{t\\in[a,b]}\\|q^{(j)}(t)\\|_2$. Each admissible family $q+s\\eta$ tends to $q$ in this norm; a local extremum therefore gives an extremum at $s=0$ of its differentiable action, whose derivative is zero. Thus the Euler–Lagrange equation is necessary for such extrema, while stationarity alone need not give an extremum.\n\n- The finite-interval chart statements on this page concern $C^2$ data and a fixed interval\n  $[a,b]$. They make no claim about minimizers in a Sobolev or absolutely continuous class, about\n  broken extremals, or about boundary conditions other than fixed endpoints; those belong to the\n  calculus-of-variations development of the mathematical library.",
          "strategy": "Record the exact content and the exclusions of the stationarity notion; no proof obligation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 5.1 (Introduction to the Calculus of Variations: stationary values versus extrema)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.1 (the remark that the principle of least action is a misnomer: the path may be a minimum or a saddle), printed pages 10-13",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm-scope-of-the-legendre-map",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Scope of the Legendre map: regular versus hyperregular, the meaning of the covector datum, and the limits of the branch description",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m4",
          "design_state": "completed-local-remark; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart",
            "lem-cm-legendre-map-local-invertibility",
            "prop-cm-hamiltonian-branch-identities",
            "prop-cm-mechanical-chart-lagrangian-is-hyperregular",
            "thm-cm-hamilton-phase-action-stationarity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-legendre-map-local-invertibility": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-hamiltonian-branch-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-mechanical-chart-lagrangian-is-hyperregular": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-hamilton-phase-action-stationarity": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "- **Regularity is local; hyperregularity is global in the fibre.** The derivative criterion of\n  [[lem-cm-legendre-map-local-invertibility]] produces inverse branches only near points where the\n  velocity Hessian is nonsingular, whereas hyperregularity as defined in\n  [[def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart]] demands a bijection of each velocity fibre\n  onto the full momentum fibre with globally defined inverse. Hyperregularity implies regularity: differentiating the inverse fibre identity gives $D_pv\\,\\mathcal L_{vv}=I_n$, hence a nonsingular Hessian. Regularity does not imply hyperregularity, as the bounded-gradient example shows; and the propositions on this page state explicitly which hypothesis each\n  conclusion uses: the differential and energy identities\n  [[prop-cm-hamiltonian-branch-identities]] use a branch, and the elimination in\n  [[thm-cm-hamilton-phase-action-stationarity]] uses a global branch for the chart.\n\n- **The mechanical chart is the positive example.** For\n  $\\mathcal L(t,q,v)=\\tfrac12v^{\\mathsf T}G(t,q)v+A(t,q)\\cdot v-V(t,q)$ with $G$ symmetric\n  positive definite, [[prop-cm-mechanical-chart-lagrangian-is-hyperregular]] proves\n  hyperregularity and computes the Hamiltonian explicitly. The companion page records the\n  degenerate cases: a regular Lagrangian whose velocity gradient is not onto the momentum fibre is\n  not hyperregular, and the identically zero Lagrangian is not even regular, so it admits no\n  Legendre inverse at all.\n\n- **The covector datum $A$ and the momentum.** In the mechanical chart the momentum is\n  $p=Gv+A$; it is a covector determined by the chart data, not $\\text{mass}\\times\\text{velocity}$\n  unless $G$ is a constant scalar matrix and $A=0$. The datum $A$ is a mathematical field on the\n  chart and is not, in this development, an electromagnetic potential; identifying $A$ with a\n  physical vector potential is a modelling step belonging to the consuming physics items, and\n  requires its own assumptions and units.\n\n- **No completeness or global solvability is asserted.** The branch description of the motion is\n  local in the momentum variable and requires the invertibility hypotheses above. It does not\n  assert that the Euler–Lagrange or Hamilton equations have global solutions, and the finite-time\n  breakdown of a solution is not excluded by anything on this page.",
          "strategy": "Record the local/global distinction with its witnesses and the scope of the covector datum; no proof obligation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 8.2 (Legendre Transformation between Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics) and Section 8.4 (Hamiltonian in Different Coordinate Systems)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.1.1 (the convexity requirement for the Legendre transform and its inverse), printed pages 82-83",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-cm-endpoint-action-differential",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The endpoint action function of a smooth family of extremals has differential $dW=p\\,dq-H\\,dt$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e20",
          "design_state": "completed-local-theorem; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials",
            "thm-additivity-over-subintervals",
            "def-oriented-integral",
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
            "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle",
            "thm-integration-by-parts",
            "thm-ftc-first-part",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability",
            "thm-ck-euclidean-maps-closed-under-algebra-and-composition",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-darboux-integral",
