            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-integration-by-parts": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-darboux-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-abs-value": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $O_H\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open and nonempty,\nlet $H:O_H\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$, let $a<b$, and suppose that for a $C^1$ path\n$\\gamma(t)=(q(t),p(t))$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]],\n[[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]) with $(t,q(t),p(t))\\in O_H$ for all $t\\in[a,b]$\nthe **phase action**\n\n$$A(\\gamma):=\\int_a^b\\bigl[p(t)\\cdot q'(t)-H(t,q(t),p(t))\\bigr]dt$$\n\nis defined, the integrand being continuous ([[def-darboux-integral]],\n[[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then:\n\n1. for every $C^1$ variation pair $(\\delta q,\\delta p)$ with $\\delta q(a)=\\delta q(b)=0$ and with\n   $\\gamma+s(\\delta q,\\delta p)$ in the domain of $A$ for small $|s|$,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{ds}\\Big|_{s=0}A\\bigl(\\gamma+s(\\delta q,\\delta p)\\bigr) =\\int_a^b\\Bigl[\\bigl(q'-H_p\\bigr)\\cdot\\delta p+\\bigl(-p'-H_q\\bigr)\\cdot\\delta q\\Bigr]dt ;$$\n\n2. $\\gamma$ is **stationary** (the displayed derivative vanishes for every such variation) if and\n   only if Hamilton's equations hold on $[a,b]$:\n\n$$q'(t)=H_p\\bigl(t,q(t),p(t)\\bigr),\\qquad p'(t)=-H_q\\bigl(t,q(t),p(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in[a,b]);$$\n\n3. **(elimination)** let in addition $\\mathcal L:J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ be a\n   hyperregular $C^2$ chart with inverse branch $v(t,q,p)$ and Hamiltonian\n   $H(t,q,p)=p\\cdot v(t,q,p)-\\mathcal L(t,q,v(t,q,p))$\n   ([[def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart]]), such that the phase domain of $H$ contains\n   the points below. For every $C^2$ curve $q:[a,b]\\to U$ with $(t,q(t),q'(t))$ in the domain of\n   $\\mathcal L$, put $p(t):=\\mathcal L_v(t,q(t),q'(t))$. Then\n   $A(q,p)=S(q)$, where $S$ is the action of $\\mathcal L$; consequently $q$ is stationary for $S$\n   if and only if $(q,p)$ is stationary for the phase action.",
          "strategy": "Integrate by parts to remove the interior endpoints of p; a bump argument per component identifies the Hamilton equations as exactly the vanishing coefficient conditions.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 9.2-9.4 (Hamilton's Principle of Stationary Action; Lagrangian; Application of Hamilton's Action Principle to Mechanics)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.1.5 'The Principle of Least Action' (the variational principle in phase space), printed page 87",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
