            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-darboux-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n=1$, $[a,b]=[0,\\pi]$, $U=\\mathbb R$, and\n$\\mathcal L(q,v)=\\tfrac12\\bigl(v^2-4q^2\\bigr)$, a $C^\\infty$ (hence $C^2$) Lagrangian on\n$\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R$ ([[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]],\n[[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Let\n\n$$q_*(t):=\\sin 2t,\\qquad \\eta(t):=\\sin t .$$\n\nUse fixed-endpoint admissible $C^2$ curves with $q(0)=q(\\pi)=0$ and the norm $\\|q\\|_{C^2}:=\\max_{0\\le j\\le2}\\sup_{t\\in[0,\\pi]}|q^{(j)}(t)|$. A local minimum means $S(q_*)\\le S(q)$ for all admissible $q$ in some ball about $q_*$ in this norm. Then $q_*$ is a stationary curve of the action\n$S(q)=\\int_0^\\pi\\tfrac12(q'^2-4q^2)dt$, and for the one-parameter family\n$q_*+\\epsilon\\eta$ one has the exact identity\n\n$$S(q_*+\\epsilon\\eta)=S(q_*)-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\epsilon^2\\qquad(\\epsilon\\in\\mathbb R).$$\n\nIn particular $S(q_*+\\epsilon\\eta)<S(q_*)$ for every $\\epsilon\\ne0$: the stationary curve is not a\nlocal minimum of the action. The model has no minimiser at all, since\n$S(\\lambda\\eta)=-\\tfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\lambda^2\\to-\\infty$ as $|\\lambda|\\to\\infty$.",
          "strategy": "Check the Euler-Lagrange equation and expand the quadratic action exactly along a one-parameter family.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 5.1 (Introduction to the Calculus of Variations: stationary values of a functional need not be extrema)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.1 (the remark that the true path may be a minimum or a saddle), printed pages 10-13",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-regular-lagrangians-need-not-be-hyperregular",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Regular versus hyperregular: the bounded-gradient Lagrangian $\\sqrt{1+v^2}$ and the degenerate zero Lagrangian",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m4",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart",
            "lem-cm-legendre-map-local-invertibility",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-abs-value",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-legendre-map-local-invertibility": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-abs-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $n=1$, $J=U=\\mathbb R$, and consider the three fibre-full $C^2$ Lagrangians\n$\\mathcal L_1(v)=\\sqrt{1+v^2}$, $\\mathcal L_2(v)=0$ and $\\mathcal L_3(v)=\\tfrac12v^2$ on\n$J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R$ ([[def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart]]). Then:\n\n1. $\\mathcal L_1$ is regular on the chart but **not** hyperregular: its velocity Hessian is\n   $(1+v^2)^{-3/2}>0$ at every $v$, while the fibre Legendre map\n   $v\\mapsto\\mathcal L_1'(v)=v/\\sqrt{1+v^2}$ has range $(-1,1)$, so it is not surjective onto\n   $\\mathbb R$;\n2. $\\mathcal L_2$ is neither regular nor hyperregular: $\\mathcal L_2'\\equiv0$ and\n   $\\mathcal L_2''\\equiv0$, so the velocity Hessian is singular everywhere and the fibre map is\n   constant, hence not injective;\n3. $\\mathcal L_3$ is both regular and hyperregular: $\\mathcal L_3''\\equiv1$ and the fibre map\n   $v\\mapsto v$ is a $C^\\infty$ bijection of $\\mathbb R$ onto itself.\n\nThus regularity does not imply hyperregularity, and neither condition is automatic.",
          "strategy": "Compute the derivatives and ranges of the three fibre maps and compare with the definitions of regularity and hyperregularity.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 8.2 (Legendre Transformation between Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.1.1 (the convexity requirement on the Legendre transform), printed pages 82-83",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-ideal-pendulum-equation-and-stability",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The ideal planar pendulum: equation of motion, conserved energy, Lyapunov stability and the frequency $\\sqrt{g/l}$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e22",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "cor-cm-reduced-dalembert-and-generalized-forces",
