      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-cm-central-force-trajectory",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Central-force trajectories: the central-force equation, angular momentum, energy and the effective potential",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m9",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument (M9 split into items; new local prerequisites recorded in the batch notes)",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-interval",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\mu>0$ be a real number, let $J_0\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$ be an open interval,\nlet $U:J_0\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be\nan interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), and let\n$r:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be twice differentiable with $|r(t)|\\in J_0$ for every\n$t\\in I$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]). The curve $r$ is a\n**central-force trajectory for** $(\\mu,U)$ when\n\n$$\\mu\\,r''(t)=-\\frac{U'(|r(t)|)}{|r(t)|}\\,r(t)\\qquad(t\\in I),$$\n\nwhere $|x|:=\\lVert x\\rVert_2$ is the Euclidean norm\n([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) and $U'$ is the derivative of $U$ on the\nopen interval $J_0$.\n\n**Attached objects.** The **angular momentum vector** of such a trajectory is\nthe curve $j:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$,\n\n$$j(t):=\\mu\\,r(t)\\times r'(t),$$\n\nwith $\\times$ the cross product of $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]);\nits **energy** is the function $E:I\\to\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$E(t):=\\tfrac12\\mu\\,\\lVert r'(t)\\rVert_2^2+U(|r(t)|);$$\n\nand for every $J\\ge0$ the **effective potential of momentum magnitude** $J$ is\nthe function $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J:J_0\\to\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J(\\rho):=U(\\rho)+\\frac{J^2}{2\\mu\\,\\rho^2}.$$\n\n**Why the objects are defined.** Since $\\rho>0$ throughout $J_0$, the right-hand\nside of the central-force equation is defined, the map\n$\\rho\\mapsto J^2/(2\\mu\\rho^2)$ is $C^2$ on $(0,\\infty)$ (a quotient of $C^2$\nfunctions with nowhere vanishing denominator), and $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J$ is\nagain $C^2$; in particular second derivatives of $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J$ are\navailable. The vector $j(t)$ is defined for every $t\\in I$ because $r$ is\ndifferentiable there.\n\n**Mathematical status.** The data $\\mu$, $U$ and $J$ are real numbers and\nfunctions; the equation and the attached objects are mathematical statements\nabout curves. Nothing here asserts that the equation describes a physical\ninteraction, and the names are conventional labels for the mathematical\nobjects only.",
          "strategy": "definition",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §2.5.4, printed pp. 31-33, Eqs. (2.82)-(2.89): two-body reduction, the conserved $J=\\mu r^2\\dot\\phi$, the radial equation and the effective potential $V_{\\rm eff}(r)=V(r)+J^2/(2\\mu r^2)$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics, §3.4, Eqs. (3.44)-(3.65): motion in a central field, the first integral and the effective potential",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm-planar-polar-lift",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Local polar coordinates for a curve with nonvanishing position in the plane: a differentiable angle function with prescribed derivative",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m9",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument (M9 split into items; new local prerequisites recorded in the batch notes)",
          "dependency_level": 0,
