          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points\n([[def-interval]]), let $t_0\\in I$, and let $\\rho,\\sigma:I\\to\\mathbb R$ be twice\ndifferentiable with\n$(\\rho(t),\\sigma(t))\\ne(0,0)$ for every $t\\in I$. Here twice differentiable\nmeans that $f$ and its derivative $f\\prime:I\\to\\mathbb R$ are differentiable\nat every point of $I$ in the domain-relative sense of [[def-derivative]];\n$f\\prime\\prime=(f\\prime)\\prime$, including one-sided limits at included endpoints. Put\n\n$$r(t):=\\sqrt{\\rho(t)^2+\\sigma(t)^2}>0\\qquad(t\\in I).$$\n\nThen there is a twice differentiable $\\theta:I\\to\\mathbb R$ with\n\n$$\\rho=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad\\sigma=r\\sin\\theta\\qquad\\text{on }I,$$\n\nand every twice differentiable $\\vartheta:I\\to\\mathbb R$ with\n$\\rho=r\\cos\\vartheta$ and $\\sigma=r\\sin\\vartheta$ on $I$ satisfies\n\n$$\\vartheta'(t)=\\frac{\\rho(t)\\sigma'(t)-\\sigma(t)\\rho'(t)}{r(t)^2}\\qquad(t\\in I).$$",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §2.5.4, printed p. 31, Eqs. (2.83)-(2.84): polar coordinates in the orbital plane and the conserved $J=\\mu r^2\\dot\\phi$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), §6.3 Motion in a General Central Force-Field: use of polar coordinates $(r,\\theta)$ in the orbital plane with the angular first integral",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node44.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm-second-order-oscillator-form",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Solutions of $v''+\\omega^2v=0$ are exactly the functions $A\\cos(\\omega(t-t_0))+B\\sin(\\omega(t-t_0))$",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m9",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument (M9 split into items; new local prerequisites recorded in the batch notes)",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-interval",
            "def-derivative",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\omega>0$, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points\n([[def-interval]]), let $t_0\\in I$, and let $v:I\\to\\mathbb R$ be twice\ndifferentiable with\n\n$$v''(t)+\\omega^2v(t)=0\\qquad(t\\in I).$$\n\nHere twice differentiable means that $v$ and $v\\prime:I\\to\\mathbb R$\nare differentiable at every point of $I$ in the domain-relative sense of\n[[def-derivative]]; $v\\prime\\prime=(v\\prime)\\prime$, with one-sided limits at\nincluded endpoints.\n\nThen there are reals $A,B$ with\n\n$$v(t)=A\\cos\\bigl(\\omega(t-t_0)\\bigr)+B\\sin\\bigl(\\omega(t-t_0)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in I).$$\n\nConversely, for all reals $A,B$ the function\n$t\\mapsto A\\cos(\\omega(t-t_0))+B\\sin(\\omega(t-t_0))$ is twice differentiable on\n$\\mathbb R$ and satisfies $v''+\\omega^2v=0$ there.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §2.6, printed pp. 38-44: small oscillations and the oscillatory solution with frequency squared $\\omega^2$",
