            "def-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Call a triple $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of vectors of $\\mathbb R^3$ an **oriented\northonormal triad** when $\\langle e_i,e_j\\rangle=\\delta_{ij}$ for all\n$i,j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $e_1\\times e_2=e_3$. Then:\n\n1. **Existence.** For every $e_3\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $|e_3|=1$ there are\n   $e_1,e_2\\in\\mathbb R^3$ such that $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is an oriented orthonormal\n   triad.\n2. **Dimension bound.** Every orthonormal list\n   $f_1,\\dots,f_m\\in\\mathbb R^3$ (pairwise orthogonal unit vectors) has\n   $m\\le3$; consequently, for every oriented orthonormal triad $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$\n   and every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$,\n   $$x=\\langle x,e_1\\rangle e_1+\\langle x,e_2\\rangle e_2+\\langle x,e_3\\rangle e_3, \\qquad |x|^2=\\langle x,e_1\\rangle^2+\\langle x,e_2\\rangle^2+\\langle x,e_3\\rangle^2 .$$\n\nThroughout, $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ and $|\\cdot|$ are the Euclidean inner\nproduct and norm ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), $\\times$ is the cross\nproduct ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]), and $s_1,s_2,s_3$ is the standard basis\nof $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]).",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §1.1 and §2.5.4: orthonormal vector triads, the right-handed relation $\\mathbf e_1\\times\\mathbf e_2=\\mathbf e_3$ and planar coordinates in the orbital plane",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), §2.4: Cartesian components, orthonormal bases and the scalar-product expansion $\\mathbf a=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf e_1)\\mathbf e_1+(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf e_2)\\mathbf e_2$",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node12.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
