            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-an-inverse": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\mu>0$, $\\kappa>0$, let $U:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ be $U(\\rho):=-\\kappa/\\rho$\n(of class $C^2$), let $I$ be an interval with at least two points, and let\n$r:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a central-force trajectory for $(\\mu,U)$ with constant\nangular momentum $j=Je_3$, $J>0$. Let $(\\rho,\\theta)$ be polar data as in\n[[thm-cm-central-force-polar-reduction]], let $I^\\circ$ be the interior of $I$\n(a nonempty interval with at least two points, or empty if $I$ is a singleton,\nwhich is excluded), and put $W:=\\theta(I^\\circ)$, an interval. Then:\n\n1. $\\theta:I^\\circ\\to W$ is a $C^1$ bijection with inverse\n   $\\sigma:W\\to I^\\circ$ of class $C^1$, and the function\n   $u:=1/(\\rho\\circ\\sigma):W\\to(0,\\infty)$ is of class $C^2$;\n2. on $W$, $u''+u=\\mu\\kappa/J^2$ (derivatives with respect to the variable of\n   $W$);\n3. the auxiliary identity $\\rho'=-\\dfrac{J}{\\mu}\\,u'\\circ\\theta$ holds on\n   $I^\\circ$;\n4. the energy satisfies $E=\\dfrac{J^2}{2\\mu}\\bigl((u')^2+u^2\\bigr)-\\kappa u$ on\n   $W$, where $E$ is the constant energy of the trajectory.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §2.5.4, printed pp. 32-33: the inverse-square radial equation and the orbit equation for $r(\\phi)$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics, §3.4, Eqs. (3.59)-(3.65): the Binet-type equation and the conic solution with eccentricity $e$ for the Coulomb/Kepler potential",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
