        {
          "id": "def-cm-one-dimensional-action-variable",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The one-dimensional action variable of a family of two-turning-point energy levels",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e6",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument (E6 split into items; recorded in the batch notes)",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-interval",
            "def-continuity-real",
            "def-darboux-integral",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "lem-sine-positive-and-cosine-decreasing-on-zero-two"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-darboux-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sine-positive-and-cosine-decreasing-on-zero-two": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $J_0\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, let $U:J_0\\to\\mathbb R$ be\ncontinuous ([[def-continuity-real]]), let $m>0$, let $E_0<E_1$ be reals, and\nlet $a,b:(E_0,E_1)\\to J_0$ be functions with\n\n$$a(E)<b(E),\\qquad U(a(E))=U(b(E))=E,\\qquad U(q)<E\\ (a(E)<q<b(E)) \\qquad(E_0<E<E_1).$$\n\nThe **action variable** of this datum is the function\n$J_{\\mathrm{a}}:(E_0,E_1)\\to\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$J_{\\mathrm{a}}(E):=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{a(E)}^{b(E)}\\sqrt{2m\\bigl(E-U(q)\\bigr)}\\;dq,$$\n\nthe integral being the Riemann integral on the compact interval\n$[a(E),b(E)]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). It is defined because\n$q\\mapsto\\sqrt{2m(E-U(q))}$ is continuous on $[a(E),b(E)]$: the radicand is a\ncontinuous nonnegative function there (it vanishes exactly at the two\nendpoints). Existence and uniqueness of nonnegative roots follow from\n[[thm-of-square-roots]]. For $x,y\\ge0$, if $s=\\sqrt x\\ge t=\\sqrt y$, then\n$(s-t)^2\\le(s-t)(s+t)=x-y$; interchanging $x,y$ gives\n$|\\sqrt x-\\sqrt y|^2\\le|x-y|$. Thus $|x-y|<\\varepsilon^2$ implies\n$|\\sqrt x-\\sqrt y|<\\varepsilon$, proving continuity, including at zero.\nThe continuous integrand is Riemann integrable by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]].\n\n**Reading of the notation.** The interval $[a(E),b(E)]$ is the classically\nallowed segment between the two turning points of the energy $E$, and the\nclosed curve $p=\\pm\\sqrt{2m(E-U(q))}$ encloses the area\n$A(E)=2\\int_{a(E)}^{b(E)}\\sqrt{2m(E-U(q))}\\,dq$ (the upper graph minus the\nlower graph), so $J_{\\mathrm{a}}(E)=A(E)/(2\\pi)$; no physical interpretation of $m$, $U$ or $E$ is a\nmathematical premise here, and no period is claimed by this definition.",
          "strategy": "definition",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §4.5.3, printed pp. 108-111, Eqs. (4.133)-(4.142): the action variable $I=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\oint p\\,dq$ of a one-dimensional system and the identity $\\omega=dE/dI$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics, §10.4: adiabatic invariants and the action variable of a periodic one-dimensional motion",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-cm-kepler-ellipse-geometry",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The subcritical Kepler conic is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=p/(1-e^2)$, $b=a\\sqrt{1-e^2}$, focus at the origin and area $\\pi ab$; its area is also $\\tfrac12\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\varrho^2$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e6",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument (E6 split into items; recorded in the batch notes)",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
