            "thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $p>0$ and\n$0\\le e<1$, and put\n$$a:=\\frac{p}{1-e^2}>0,\\qquad b:=a\\sqrt{1-e^2},\\qquad \\varrho(\\varphi):=\\frac{p}{1+e\\cos\\varphi}\\quad(\\varphi\\in\\mathbb R).$$\n\n1. $\\varrho$ is $C^1$, $2\\pi$-periodic, and\n   $\\dfrac{p}{1+e}\\le\\varrho\\le\\dfrac{p}{1-e}$.\n2. Let $Q(x,y):=(1-e^2)x^2+y^2+2pex-p^2$ and\n   $E_{\\rm ell}:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:Q(x,y)\\le0\\}$. Then\n   $E_{\\rm ell}$ is Lebesgue measurable, and for the closed unit disc\n   $\\overline B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ and $T(u,v):=(au-ae,bv)$ one has\n   $E_{\\rm ell}=T[\\overline B]$; consequently\n   $\\lambda_2(E_{\\rm ell})=\\pi ab=\\pi a^2\\sqrt{1-e^2}$.\n3. For every $\\varphi$, the ray $\\{s(\\cos\\varphi,\\sin\\varphi):s>0\\}$ meets\n   $\\partial E_{\\rm ell}$ in the unique point\n   $\\varrho(\\varphi)(\\cos\\varphi,\\sin\\varphi)$; that point satisfies\n   $Q=0$, and $\\lambda_2(E_{\\rm ell})=\\frac12\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\varrho(\\varphi)^2\\,\n   \\mathrm d\\varphi$.\n\nThus the conic $Q=0$ is the ellipse with centre $(-ae,0)$, semiaxes $a,b$,\nits right focus at the origin, and enclosed area $\\pi ab$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics, §3.4, Eqs. (3.59)-(3.65) and Fig. 8: the elliptic branch $E<0$, $e<1$, with the turning radii and the ellipse geometry",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), §5.5 and §5.6: the elliptic orbit of the Kepler problem, its semiaxes and the focus at the force centre",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node44.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
