            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
            "thm-of-square-roots"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-action-variable-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-one-dimensional-action-variable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-substitution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $k>0$, $U(q)=\\frac12kq^2$ on $J_0=\\mathbb R$, and for $E>0$ set\n$$a(E):=-\\sqrt{\\frac{2E}{k}},\\qquad b(E):=\\sqrt{\\frac{2E}{k}},\\qquad \\omega:=\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}},\\qquad T:=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.$$\nThen the datum $(J_0,U,m,a,b)$ satisfies the hypotheses of\n[[thm-cm-action-variable-derivative]] on every bounded open interval\n$(E_0,E_1)$ with $0<E_0<E_1<\\infty$. These restrictions define the same\naction on overlaps, hence together give a $C^1$ function on $(0,\\infty)$; the\naction variable of [[def-cm-one-dimensional-action-variable]] is\n$$J_{\\mathrm a}(E)=\\frac{E}{\\omega}\\qquad(E>0),$$\nand\n$$J_{\\mathrm a}'(E)=\\frac{1}{\\omega}=\\frac{T}{2\\pi}\\qquad(E>0),$$\nin agreement with the general theorem, whose period quadrature is\n$T(E)=\\sqrt{2m}\\int_{a(E)}^{b(E)}\\mathrm dq/\\sqrt{E-U(q)}=2\\pi/\\omega$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §4.5.1 and §4.5.3, printed pp. 105-111: the harmonic oscillator action variable and the period relation $\\mathrm dE/\\mathrm dI=\\omega$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-kepler-ellipse-data-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "An exact Kepler ellipse check: $e=1/2$, $J=1$, $\\mu=\\kappa=1$ give $a=4/3$, $b=2\\sqrt3/3$, $E=-3/8$ and $T^2=256\\pi^2/27$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e6",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument (E6 split into items; recorded in the batch notes)",
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-kepler-conic-and-eccentricity",
            "thm-cm-kepler-ellipse-geometry",
            "thm-cm-kepler-orbit-period",
            "def-cm-central-force-trajectory",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
            "def-interval",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-kepler-conic-and-eccentricity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-kepler-ellipse-geometry": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-kepler-orbit-period": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-central-force-trajectory": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the ellipse-area and period suppliers;\nthe conic-and-eccentricity theorem does not impose this hypothesis.\n\nTake the attractive Kepler data $\\mu=\\kappa=1$ and angular momentum magnitude\n$J=1$, and consider the negative-energy orbit with eccentricity $e=\\frac12$.\nThen the conic data of [[thm-cm-kepler-conic-and-eccentricity]] and\n[[thm-cm-kepler-ellipse-geometry]] are\n$$p=\\frac{J^2}{\\mu\\kappa}=1,\\qquad a=\\frac{p}{1-e^2}=\\frac43,\\qquad b=a\\sqrt{1-e^2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt3}{3},$$\nthe energy is $E=\\dfrac{\\mu\\kappa^2}{2J^2}(e^2-1)=-\\dfrac38$, and the period of\n[[thm-cm-kepler-orbit-period]] satisfies\n$$T=\\frac{2\\pi\\mu ab}{J}=\\frac{16\\pi\\sqrt3}{9},\\qquad T^2=\\frac{256\\pi^2}{27}=\\frac{4\\pi^2\\mu a^3}{\\kappa}.$$\nAll numbers are exact; the example checks algebraic consistency of the three\ntheorems and the sign criterion $E<0\\iff e<1$, and makes no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics, §3.4, Eqs. (3.59)-(3.65): the eccentricity, semimajor axis and period relations checked numerically here with exact rational data",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
