            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-space-complete": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-local-flow-composition-law": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-smooth-dependence-and-the-variational-equation": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-variational-equation-along-an-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-linear-matrix-odes-have-unique-global-solutions-on-a-given-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $A\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$. Consider the linear autonomous field $F:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$, $F(x)=Ax$, with local flow $\\Phi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^n$ ([[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]]) and exponential series $e^{tA}=\\sum_{m\\ge0}\\frac{t^m}{m!}A^m$ ([[lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator]]). Then\n\n$$\\Omega=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad \\Phi(t,a)=e^{tA}a,$$\n\nthe exponential family satisfies the composition law $e^{(s+t)A}=e^{sA}e^{tA}$, and for every $a$ the derivative of $b\\mapsto\\Phi(t,b)$ at $a$ is $D_a\\Phi(t,a)=e^{tA}$; the variational equation along $t\\mapsto e^{tA}a$,\n\n$$Y'=AY,\\qquad Y(0)=I_n,$$\n\nhas the unique solution $Y(t)=e^{tA}$ on every compact interval ([[def-variational-equation-along-an-ode-solution]], [[lem-linear-matrix-odes-have-unique-global-solutions-on-a-given-interval]]).",
          "strategy": "Verify the explicit exponential solution, its maximality, the flow identities and the variational equation directly from the exponential-series calculus.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 3.2 (the matrix exponential and linear systems), printed pages 62-67, and Section 2.4 (variational equation), printed pages 45-47",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Arino, MATH 8430 Fundamental Theory of Ordinary Differential Equations (University of Manitoba), Section 1.4 (the variational equation for a linear system), printed pages 32-40",
                "url": "https://julien-arino.github.io/assets/pdf/courses/math8430_lecture_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-coupled-oscillator-modes",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Two coupled equal masses: mode frequencies sqrt(k/m) and sqrt((k+2c)/m), and the free zero mode when k=0",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#m7",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition",
            "lem-cm-positive-definite-matrix-square-roots",
            "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-positive-definite-matrix-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $k\\ge0$ and $c\\ge0$, and consider the constant-coefficient system $Mq''+Kq=0$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with\n\n$$M=\\begin{pmatrix}m&0\\\\0&m\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad K=\\begin{pmatrix}k+c&-c\\\\-c&k+c\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThen $K$ is symmetric and non-negative, $S=M^{-1/2}KM^{-1/2}=K/m$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=k/m$ with eigenvector $\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$ and $\\lambda_2=(k+2c)/m$ with eigenvector $\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1)$, and the general solution is the sum of the modal solutions of [[thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition]]:\n\n- if $k>0$ the first mode oscillates with angular frequency $\\omega_1=\\sqrt{k/m}$, while if $k=0$ it is the free affine mode $a_1+b_1t$ along $\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1,1)$;\n- the second mode oscillates with angular frequency $\\omega_2=\\sqrt{(k+2c)/m}$ whenever $k+2c>0$, and if $k=c=0$ it is also a free affine mode $a_2+b_2t$ along $\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1,-1)$.\n\nIn particular a zero eigenvalue is not an oscillator but a free mode.",
          "strategy": "Multiply the explicit matrices, read off the orthonormal eigenvectors, and specialise the quadratic-mode theorem to each sign case of the eigenvalues.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 8.4, formulas (8.77)-(8.81): the spring-chain harmonic example and its modes",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 2.6 (normal modes of coupled oscillators), printed pages 40-45",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-planar-harmonic-oscillator-return-map",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The local first-return map of a scalar harmonic oscillator near (1,0) is the reflection x->-x",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e12",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
            "prop-cm-first-return-map-is-a-local-c1-diffeomorphism",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-metric-ball",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-first-return-map-is-a-local-c1-diffeomorphism": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\omega>0$ and let $F:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$, $F(x,y)=(y,-\\omega^2x)$, be the phase-plane field of the scalar harmonic oscillator $x''+\\omega^2x=0$, with $y=x'$, with local flow $\\Phi$ ([[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]]). Its solutions are\n\n$$\\Phi\\bigl(t,(x,y)\\bigr)=\\Bigl(x\\cos\\omega t+\\frac{y}{\\omega}\\sin\\omega t,\\ -\\omega x\\sin\\omega t+y\\cos\\omega t\\Bigr),$$\n\ndefined for all real $t$. Take the section $\\Sigma=\\{y=0\\}$, the reference point $x_0=(1,0)$ with $h(x,y):=y$, and $t_0:=\\pi/\\omega$, so that $x_1=\\Phi(t_0,x_0)=(-1,0)$. Then $h(x_0)=h(x_1)=0$ and\n\n$$Dh(x_0)F(x_0)=-\\omega^2\\ne0,\\qquad Dh(x_1)F(x_1)=\\omega^2\\ne0,$$\n\nand $\\Phi(s,x_0)$ has second coordinate $-\\omega\\sin(\\omega s)\\ne0$ for $0<s<\\pi/\\omega$, so it avoids the closed section on that interval. Thus the first-return clause of [[prop-cm-first-return-map-is-a-local-c1-diffeomorphism]] applies and produces a local $C^1$ Poincaré map near $x_0$. On the section itself the map and its inverse can be computed exactly: for $(x,0)$ with $x$ near $1$, the return time is $\\tau(x,0)=\\pi/\\omega$ and the return map is\n\n$$P(x,0)=(-x,0),$$\n\nthe reflection in the origin restricted to the section, with inverse $Q(y,0)=(-y,0)$; this is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism between neighbourhoods of $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ in $\\Sigma$.",
          "strategy": "Write the explicit solution of the oscillator, identify the crossings of the section, and realise the local return map computed by the proposition on the section.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 6.4, Problem 6.14: the Poincare map of the vector field f(x,y)=(-y,x) for the section x>0, y=0",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 4.1 (the harmonic oscillator in phase space), printed pages 75-79",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-bilateral-shift-finite-checks",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Explicit mixing, periodic and sensitivity checks in the bilateral shift",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e12",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-bilateral-shift-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive",
            "lem-cm-bilateral-shift-metric-and-sequential-compactness",
            "def-cm-bilateral-shift-space-and-cylinders",
            "def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps",
            "def-metric-ball",
            "def-metric-topology"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-bilateral-shift-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-bilateral-shift-metric-and-sequential-compactness": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-bilateral-shift-space-and-cylinders": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $X=\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb Z}$ with the metric $d$ and the shift $\\sigma$ of [[def-cm-bilateral-shift-space-and-cylinders]], and let cylinders be written $[w]_{p,q}$.\n\n1. **A metric computation.** Let $x\\in X$ be arbitrary and let $y$ agree with $x$ on the coordinates $-2,\\dots,2$ and differ at coordinate $3$ only. Then $y\\in[x]_{-2,2}$ and $d(x,y)=2^{-3}=1/8$, which is at most $2^{1-2}=1/2$, in accordance with $[x]_{F_m}\\subseteq\\overline B(x,2^{1-m})$.\n2. **A periodic point in a prescribed cylinder.** The word $(1,0,1)$ on the coordinates $-1,0,1$ extends to the $3$-periodic sequence $s$ with $s_{-1}=1$, $s_0=0$, $s_1=1$ and $s_{k+3}=s_k$ for all $k$. Then $s\\in[1,0,1]_{-1,1}$ and $\\sigma^3(s)=s$.\n3. **A sensitivity witness.** For every $x\\in X$ and every $k\\ge1$, let $y$ agree with $x$ except at coordinate $k$, where $y_k=1-x_k$. Then $d(x,y)=2^{-k}$ and $d(\\sigma^k(x),\\sigma^k(y))\\ge1$.\n4. **A mixing computation.** For the cylinders $U=[1,0]_{0,1}$ and $V=[0]_{-1,-1}$, one has $\\sigma^n(U)\\cap V\\ne\\emptyset$ for every $n\\ge2$.",
          "strategy": "Evaluate the definitions on explicitly displayed sequences and compute the cylinder intersections for the two blocks.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "C. Ulcigrai, MATH36206/MATHM6206 Topological and Symbolic Dynamics, Sections 2.6-2.7: cylinders, periodic sequences obtained by repeating blocks, and Theorem 2.7.1",
                "url": "https://m-artigiani.github.io/files/SisDin-Part2.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-jordan-block-dichotomy-computed",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Computed dichotomy constants for a Jordan block and for a diagonal sink-source",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e16",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-complexification-of-a-real-vector-space",
            "def-complexification-of-a-real-linear-map",
            "thm-euclidean-space-complete",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative",
            "thm-cm-exponential-dichotomy-for-a-hyperbolic-endomorphism",
            "lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator",
            "lem-linear-matrix-odes-have-unique-global-solutions-on-a-given-interval",
            "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
            "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
            "lem-exponential-dominates-one-plus-x",
            "thm-exponential-limits-and-range",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-complexification-of-a-real-vector-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complexification-of-a-real-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-space-complete": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-exponential-dichotomy-for-a-hyperbolic-endomorphism": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-linear-matrix-odes-have-unique-global-solutions-on-a-given-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-addition-formula": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-exponential-dominates-one-plus-x": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-limits-and-range": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Use zero-based coordinates and the standard vectors $e_0=(1,0)$ and $e_1=(0,1)$ of $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n1. **A non-diagonalisable Jordan block.** For\n\n$$A=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nthe exponential series gives $e^{tA}=e^{-t}\\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$; the only eigenvalue is $-1$, so the dichotomy theorem has $E_s=\\mathbb R^2$, $E_u=\\{0\\}$, and the constants $C=1$, $c=1/2$ are admissible: for every $v\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and $t\\ge0$,\n\n$$\\|e^{tA}v\\|_2\\le e^{-t/2}\\|v\\|_2 .$$\n\n2. **A diagonal sink-source.** For $B=\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,-3/2)$ the vectors $e_0,e_1$ are eigenvectors with eigenvalues $1/2$ and $-3/2$, which have nonzero real part, and the dichotomy theorem gives $E_u=\\mathbb R e_0$, $E_s=\\mathbb R e_1$ with admissible constants $C=1$, $c=1/4$: for $t\\ge0$ one has $\\|e^{tB}e_1\\|_2=e^{-3t/2}\\le e^{-t/4}$ and $\\|e^{tB}e_0\\|_2=e^{t/2}\\ge e^{t/4}$, and for $t\\le0$ one has $\\|e^{tB}e_0\\|_2=e^{t/2}\\le e^{t/4}$.",
          "strategy": "Solve the two linear matrix initial value problems explicitly, identify the spectra and invariant subspaces, and verify the dichotomy estimates with elementary exponential inequalities.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 9.1, Theorem 9.2 and formula (9.4): exponential dichotomy estimates with the polynomial factor for a Jordan block",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "F. Ramponi, Notes on Lyapunov's theorem, Section 3 (the Lyapunov equation solved by the integral of e^{A^T t} e^{A t})",
