      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A phase domain, its canonical one-form and its standard symplectic two-form",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e7",
          "design_state": "completed local argument; carrier and readiness record authored in this batch",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-smooth-differential-k-form",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-metric-topology",
            "thm-local-coordinate-formula-for-the-exterior-derivative",
            "def-tautological-one-form-on-a-cotangent-bundle"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-smooth-differential-k-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-local-coordinate-formula-for-the-exterior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tautological-one-form-on-a-cotangent-bundle": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.1 (Hamilton's equations and canonical coordinates: lambda = sum p_i dq^i, omega = sum dq^i wedge dp_i), printed pages 80-88",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 15.1-15.2 (Hamiltonian mechanics in phase space and the symplectic structure)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$. A **phase domain** is a nonempty open set $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2n}$\n([[def-metric-topology]]). Its points are written $z=(q,p)$ with\n$q=(q^1,\\dots,q^n)\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $p=(p_1,\\dots,p_n)\\in\\mathbb R^n$; the coordinate functions\n$q^i,p_i:U\\to\\mathbb R$ are smooth ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and\n$\\partial_{q^1},\\dots,\\partial_{q^n},\\partial_{p_1},\\dots,\\partial_{p_n}$ is the coordinate frame\n([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). On a phase domain:\n\n1. the **canonical one-form** is the smooth one-form\n   $\\lambda_U:=\\sum_{i=1}^n p_i\\,dq^i\\in\\Omega^1(U)$;\n2. the **standard symplectic two-form** is the smooth two-form\n   $\\omega_U:=\\sum_{i=1}^n dq^i\\wedge dp_i\\in\\Omega^2(U)$\n   ([[def-smooth-differential-k-form]]);\n3. the **standard symplectic matrix** is the block matrix\n   $$J:=\\begin{pmatrix}0_n&I_n\\\\-I_n&0_n\\end{pmatrix}\\in M_{2n}(\\mathbb R),$$\n   whose blocks are $n\\times n$.\n\nThe identity $\\omega_U=-d\\lambda_U$ holds, because the coordinate formula for the exterior\nderivative ([[thm-local-coordinate-formula-for-the-exterior-derivative]]) gives\n$d\\lambda_U=\\sum_i dp_i\\wedge dq^i=-\\sum_i dq^i\\wedge dp_i$. When $U$ is an induced chart domain of a\ncotangent bundle, the published [[def-tautological-one-form-on-a-cotangent-bundle]] identifies\n$\\lambda_U$ and $\\omega_U$ with the tautological one-form and the canonical cotangent two-form in\nthose coordinates; the present definition is the choice-free local coordinate model that the\ncanonical-calculus items of this page use."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite-regularity differential forms, Hamiltonian fields and canonical maps in phase charts",
          "reference": "local Step-2 finite-regularity prerequisite repair",
          "design_state": "owner-accepted local definition",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-canonical-transformation",
            "def-symplectomorphism-local-symplectomorphism-and-symplectic-embedding"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-canonical-transformation": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-symplectomorphism-local-symplectomorphism-and-symplectic-embedding": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "justified_by": [
            "prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics, Sections 4.1 and 4.4: coordinate Hamiltonian fields and the symplectic Jacobian condition",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Let $U,V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2n}$ be phase domains with standard forms and matrix $J$ of [[def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form]]. All finite differentiability classes and diffeomorphisms use [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]].\n\nOn any open set $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, a **$C^k$ coordinate differential form**, $k\\ge0$, is a sum $\\sum_{i_1<\\cdots<i_j}a_{i_1\\cdots i_j}(z)\\,dz^{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dz^{i_j}$ with $C^k$ coefficient functions. Its value at a point is the indicated alternating multilinear form on $\\mathbb R^m$. For $k\\ge1$ define its exterior derivative by the coordinate formula $d(a\\,dz^{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots)=\\sum_l(\\partial_l a)\\,dz^l\\wedge dz^{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots$, extended linearly. For a $C^1$ map $C:U\\to V$, the **pointwise pullback** is\n$$ (C^*\\eta)_z(v_1,\\ldots,v_j):=\\eta_{C(z)}(DC(z)v_1,\\ldots,DC(z)v_j). $$\nIt is a continuous form when $\\eta$ is continuous; no assertion that this pullback is smooth is part of the definition. Wedges and contractions are the pointwise alternating multilinear operations. Thus the finite-regularity identities below can be read without applying a definition restricted to smooth forms or maps.\n\nA **$C^r$ canonical map**, $1\\le r\\le\\infty$, is a $C^r$ map $C:U\\to V$ satisfying $C^*\\omega_V=\\omega_U$ pointwise. A **$C^r$ canonical transformation** is such a map that is a $C^r$ diffeomorphism onto $V$; a **local $C^r$ canonical transformation** is a $C^r$ local diffeomorphism satisfying the same equality. The $C^\\infty$ case agrees with [[def-canonical-transformation]] and [[def-symplectomorphism-local-symplectomorphism-and-symplectic-embedding]]; the finite-$r$ terminology on this page is the definition just given, and does not assert that a $C^1$ map is smooth.\n\nFor $h\\in C^2(U)$ the **coordinate Hamiltonian field** is the $C^1$ vector-valued map $X_h:=J\\nabla h$, viewed as a field by the canonical identification $T_zU=\\mathbb R^{2n}$. Its characterization by $\\iota_{X_h}\\omega_U=dh$ is proved in [[prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart]]. For $f,g\\in C^1(U)$ define the **coordinate Poisson bracket** by $\\{f,g\\}:=\\nabla f^{\\mathsf T}J\\nabla g$; for $C^2$ inputs it is $C^1$ and equals $\\omega_U(X_f,X_g)$ by that proposition. Smooth inputs recover the smooth symplectic conventions. No closure of the $C^2$ class under this bracket is assumed."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm-standard-symplectic-form-is-closed-and-nondegenerate",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The standard two-form of a phase domain is closed and nondegenerate, with contraction $\\iota_v\\omega=\\sum_i a_i\\,dp_i-\\sum_i b_i\\,dq^i$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e7",
          "design_state": "completed local argument; carrier and readiness record authored in this batch",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form",
