            "def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Sections 4.1 (Hamilton's equations from iota_X omega = dH) and 4.3 (Poisson brackets and the coordinate formula), printed pages 80-99",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 15.3 (canonical transformations and the Hamiltonian field in phase-space coordinates)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U$ be a phase domain with standard two-form\n$\\omega_U=\\sum_i dq^i\\wedge dp_i$ ([[def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form]]), and let\n$H:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Write\n$\\nabla H=(H_{q^1},\\dots,H_{q^n},H_{p_1},\\dots,H_{p_n})$ for the Euclidean gradient\n([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]) and $J$ for the standard symplectic matrix. Then:\n\n1. there is exactly one $C^1$ vector field $X_H$ on $U$ with $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega_U=dH$, and it is\n   $$X_H=J\\nabla H=\\bigl(H_{p_1},\\dots,H_{p_n},-H_{q^1},\\dots,-H_{q^n}\\bigr);$$\n2. an integral curve $t\\mapsto(q(t),p(t))$ of $X_H$ satisfies Hamilton's equations\n   $$\\dot q^i=H_{p_i}(q,p),\\qquad \\dot p_i=-H_{q^i}(q,p)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,n);$$\n3. for $f,g\\in C^2(U)$ the Poisson bracket\n   $\\{f,g\\}:=\\omega_U(X_f,X_g)$ (in the finite-regularity convention of [[def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus]]) is\n   $$\\{f,g\\}=\\nabla f^{\\mathsf T}J\\,\\nabla g=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\bigl(f_{q^i}g_{p_i}-f_{p_i}g_{q^i}\\bigr),$$\n   hence $\\{f,g\\}=-\\{g,f\\}$, and $\\{f,g\\}$ is $C^1$ on $U$."
        },
        {
