          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Sections 4.1-4.2 (the linearised flow of Hamilton's equations and Liouville's theorem), printed pages 80-92",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 15.1-15.2 (the canonical equations and the divergence-free Hamiltonian flow)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U$ be a phase domain with standard two-form $\\omega_U$ and matrix $J$, let\n$H\\in C^2(U)$, and let $\\Phi$ be the local flow of the Hamiltonian field\n$X_H=J\\nabla H$ ([[prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart]],\n[[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]]). Let $z_0\\in U$, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be the open\ninterval of times $t$ with $(t,z_0)$ in the flow domain, and for $t\\in I$ put\n\n$$Y(t):=D_z\\Phi_t(z_0)\\in M_{2n}(\\mathbb R).$$\n\nThen $t\\mapsto Y(t)$ is of class $C^1$ on $I$ and, for every $t\\in I$:\n\n1. (variational equation) $Y'(t)=J\\,\\mathrm{Hess}\\,H(\\Phi_t(z_0))\\,Y(t)$ and $Y(0)=I_{2n}$;\n2. (canonical matrix identity) $Y(t)^{\\mathsf T}J\\,Y(t)=J$;\n3. (unit determinant) $\\det Y(t)=1$.\n\nThe same conclusions hold for a **time-dependent Hamiltonian** $\\mathcal H:T\\times U\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^2$, where $T$ is an open real interval. For $t_0\\in T$ and $z_0\\in U$, write $E(t,t_0,z)$ for the maximal solution of $z'=J\\nabla_z\\mathcal H(t,z)$ with $z(t_0)=z$; its domain consists of the triples where this solution exists. On the maximal open time interval $I_{t_0,z_0}$ set $Y(t)=D_zE(t,t_0,z_0)$. Then\n$$Y'=J\\,\\operatorname{Hess}_z\\mathcal H(t,E(t,t_0,z_0))Y,\\qquad Y(t_0)=I_{2n},\\qquad Y^{\\mathsf T}JY=J,\\qquad\\det Y=1.$$\nThe evolution $E$ is $C^1$ on its open domain, and each fixed-endpoint map has inverse $E(t_0,t,\\cdot)$ on its image. Autonomous $H$ is recovered by $\\mathcal H(t,z)=H(z)$ and $E(t,t_0,z)=\\Phi(t-t_0,z)$."
        },
