            "def-smooth-differential-k-form",
            "def-metric-topology",
            "def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-canonical-map-tests-and-hamiltonian-transport": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-smooth-differential-k-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.4.3 'Generating Functions' (the four kinds of generating function and the relations p = dS_1/dq, P = -dS_1/dQ), printed pages 104-105",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 15.3 (canonical transformations in Hamiltonian mechanics and their generating functions)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "**First-kind data.** Let $n\\ge1$ and let $O_q,O_Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty open sets\n([[def-metric-topology]]). Let $S:O_q\\times O_Q\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^3$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) and assume\n\n$$\\det S_{qQ}(q,Q)\\ne0\\qquad\\bigl((q,Q)\\in O_q\\times O_Q\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere $S_{qQ}(q,Q)$ is the $n\\times n$ matrix with entries\n$\\partial^2 S/\\partial q^i\\partial Q^j(q,Q)$ ([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]],\n[[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]). Identify $O_q\\times O_Q$ with the phase-domain coordinates\n$(q,Q)$ of $\\mathbb R^{2n}$ ([[def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form]]) and form the\n$C^2$ map\n\n$$\\Psi:O_q\\times O_Q\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{2n},\\qquad \\Psi(q,Q):=\\bigl(q,\\ S_q(q,Q)\\bigr),$$\n\nwhose derivative is the block matrix\n$D\\Psi(q,Q)=\\begin{pmatrix}I_n&0\\\\ S_{qq}(q,Q)&S_{qQ}(q,Q)\\end{pmatrix}$. A **generating function of\nthe first kind** is such an $S$; the **local map generated by $S$** is, on a neighbourhood of a point\n$(q_*,Q_*)$, the $C^2$ map $C:(q,p)\\mapsto(Q,P)$ defined by\n\n$$p:=S_q(q,Q),\\qquad P:=-S_Q(q,Q),$$\n\nsolved as follows: since $\\det S_{qQ}\\ne0$, the inverse function theorem\n([[thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem]]) makes $\\Psi$ a local $C^2$ diffeomorphism, so\n$p=S_q(q,Q)$ determines a $C^2$ local inverse $Q=Q(q,p)$ on a neighbourhood of\n$\\Psi(q_*,Q_*)$; then $C(q,p):=(Q(q,p),-S_Q(q,Q(q,p)))$.\n\n**Second-kind data.** Let $O_q,O_P\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty open sets and let\n$S:O_q\\times O_P\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^3$ with $\\det S_{qP}(q,P)\\ne0$ everywhere. A\n**generating function of the second kind** is such an $S$; the **local map generated by $S$** is,\nnear a point $(q_*,P_*)$, the $C^2$ map $C:(q,p)\\mapsto(Q,P)$ defined by\n\n$$p:=S_q(q,P),\\qquad Q:=S_P(q,P),$$\n\nwhere again the relation $p=S_q(q,P)$ is solved locally for $P=P(q,p)$ by the inverse function\ntheorem applied to $(q,P)\\mapsto(q,S_q(q,P))$, whose derivative is\n$\\begin{pmatrix}I_n&0\\\\ S_{qq}&S_{qP}\\end{pmatrix}$.\n\n**Recorded identities.** Wherever $S$ is of the first kind, the identity\n$p\\,dq-P\\,dQ=dS$ holds; wherever $S$ is of the second kind, the identity $p\\,dq+Q\\,dP=dS$ holds,\nboth as identities of $C^2$ coordinate one-forms on the $(q,Q)$- resp. $(q,P)$-space with $p,P,Q$ read as the\ndisplayed functions ([[def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus]]). The mixed-derivative hypotheses\n$\\det S_{qQ}\\ne0$ resp. $\\det S_{qP}\\ne0$ are part of the data."
        },
        {
          "id": "prop-cm-generating-functions-yield-canonical-maps",
          "kind": "proposition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The local map generated by a first- or second-kind generating function is a canonical $C^2$ local diffeomorphism",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e7",
          "design_state": "completed local argument; carrier and readiness record authored in this batch",
