          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.4.3 'Generating Functions' (time-dependent generating functions and the Hamiltonian shift K = H + dS/dt), printed pages 104-105",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 15.3 (time-dependent canonical transformations generated by a function of the old and new coordinates)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, let $O_q,O_Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be\nnonempty open sets ([[def-metric-topology]]), and let\n$S:J\\times O_q\\times O_Q\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^3$ with\n$\\det S_{qQ}(t,q,Q)\\ne0$ for every $(t,q,Q)$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). For\neach $t\\in J$ let $S_t:=S(t,\\cdot,\\cdot)$ and let $C_t$ be the first-kind map generated by $S_t$\n([[def-cm-generating-function-of-the-first-and-second-kind]]). Let\n$H:J\\times V\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^2$ on a phase domain $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2n}$ such that $(q,S_q(t,q,Q))\\in V$ for every $(t,q,Q)\\in J\\times O_q\\times O_Q$; thus every Hamiltonian argument in the identity below is in its declared domain. Then:\n\n1. for every $t\\in J$ the map $C_t$ is a canonical $C^2$ local diffeomorphism\n   ([[prop-cm-generating-functions-yield-canonical-maps]]);\n2. on $J\\times O_q\\times O_Q$, with $p:=S_q(t,q,Q)$, $P:=-S_Q(t,q,Q)$ and\n   $K(t,q,Q):=H\\bigl(t,q,S_q(t,q,Q)\\bigr)+S_t(t,q,Q)$, the one-form identity\n   $$p\\,dq-H(t,q,p)\\,dt=P\\,dQ-K\\,dt+dS$$\n   holds identically: on the region where both descriptions are defined, the Poincare-Cartan\n   one-forms of $H$ and of $K=H+S_t$ differ by the exact form $dS$, with all quantities read as\n   functions of $(t,q,Q)$.\n\nThe words canonical and the finite-regularity form operations here use [[def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus]], with the displayed $C^2$ maps; smoothness is not inferred."
        },
