          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Sections 4.4.3 (generating functions) and 4.5 (action-angle variables: the cylinder as the phase space of an angle variable with a shift symmetry), printed pages 104-112",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Ana Cannas da Silva, Lectures on Symplectic Geometry, revised January 2006, Lecture 18 (integrable systems and the affine structure of the flow on tori)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20120413034139if_/http://www.math.ist.utl.pt:80/%7Eacannas/Books/symplectic.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $\\mathbb T:=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be the one-dimensional torus with quotient map\n$q:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb T$ ([[def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral]]), let\n$C:=\\mathbb T\\times\\mathbb R$ be the cylinder, and identify $C$ with the quotient of $\\mathbb R^2$\nby the deck transformation $\\tau(\\theta,I)=(\\theta+1,I)$, with quotient map\n$\\pi:\\mathbb R^2\\to C$ ([[def-quotient-topology]]); assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ where the cited\ntorus definition requires it. Give $\\mathbb T$ the smooth structure generated by the lift charts $(q(J), (q|_J)^{-1})$ for open intervals $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ of length less than one, and give $C$ their product charts with the real coordinate $I$ ([[def-smooth-manifold]]). The quotient map is open because $q^{-1}(q(V))=\\bigcup_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}(V+k)$ for open $V$; thus $q|_J$ is a homeomorphism onto an open set. Chart transitions are locally translations by integers and leave $I$ unchanged, hence are smooth. Hausdorffness and a countable base follow from the torus supplier and the real coordinate, so these charts specify a smooth cylinder and $\\pi$ is a surjective local diffeomorphism. Since $d\\theta\\wedge dI$ and $I\\,d\\theta$ are invariant under $\\tau$,\nthe expressions in each lift chart agree on overlaps (integer translations fix their differentials and $I$), and hence define unique smooth forms $\\omega_C,\\lambda_C$ on $C$ with\n$\\pi^*\\omega_C=d\\theta\\wedge dI$ and $\\pi^*\\lambda_C=I\\,d\\theta$; while $d\\theta$ descends to a\nsmooth one-form $d\\theta_C$ on $C$ with $\\pi^*d\\theta_C=d\\theta$. Let $a\\ne0$ and let\n$\\Phi:C\\to C$ be the **symplectic shift** given in quotient coordinates by\n$\\Phi(\\theta,I)=(\\theta,I+a)$. Then:\n\n1. $\\Phi$ is a $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism, $\\Phi^*\\omega_C=\\omega_C$, and\n   $\\Phi^*\\lambda_C=\\lambda_C+a\\,d\\theta_C$;\n2. $a\\,d\\theta_C$ is closed but not exact: there is no $C^1$ function $W:C\\to\\mathbb R$ with\n   $dW=a\\,d\\theta_C$;\n3. for every point of $C$ there is a neighbourhood on which $\\Phi$ is generated by the second-kind\n   generating function $S(\\theta,P)=\\theta P-a\\theta$, but there is no $C^1$ function\n   $S:(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ whose generated map is $\\Phi$; the local\n   generating functions do not glue to a global one."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-hamilton-jacobi-equation-and-complete-integral",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Hamilton-Jacobi equation and a complete integral of it",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e7",
          "design_state": "completed local argument; carrier and readiness record authored in this batch",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-metric-topology",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.7 'The Hamilton-Jacobi Equation' (the equation W_t + H(q, W_q, t) = 0 and the role of dW/dbeta constants), printed pages 121-126",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 15.4 (Hamilton-Jacobi theory and Jacobi's complete integral)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval\n([[def-interval]]), let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a nonempty open set, and let\n\n$$H:J\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\n\nbe of class $C^2$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); write $p=(p_1,\\dots,p_n)$ for\nthe third variable, so that $(t,q,p)$ range over phase-type data and $H$ is a Hamiltonian in the\nsense of [[prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart]]. Let\n$J'\\subseteq J$, $U'\\subseteq U$ and $A'\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty open sets\n([[def-metric-topology]]) and let $S:J'\\times U'\\times A'\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^3$; write\n$\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_n)$ for the third variable.\n\n$S$ **solves the Hamilton-Jacobi equation associated with $H$** on $J'\\times U'\\times A'$ if\n\n$$S_t(t,q,\\alpha)+H\\bigl(t,\\,q,\\,S_q(t,q,\\alpha)\\bigr)=0\\qquad\\bigl((t,q,\\alpha)\\in J'\\times U'\\times A'\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere $S_t$ is the partial derivative in $t$, $S_q=(S_{q^1},\\dots,S_{q^n})$ is the gradient in $q$\nand $H$ is evaluated at the momentum $p=S_q(t,q,\\alpha)$\n([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]). $S$ is a\n**complete integral** of the Hamilton-Jacobi equation at $(t_0,q_0,\\alpha_0)$ if in addition\n\n$$\\det S_{q\\alpha}(t_0,q_0,\\alpha_0)\\ne0,$$\n\nwhere $S_{q\\alpha}$ is the $n\\times n$ matrix with entries\n$\\partial^2S/\\partial q^i\\partial\\alpha_j$, called the **mixed derivative**.\n\nIf $S$ is a complete integral, the associated **momentum map** is\n$p(t,q,\\alpha):=S_q(t,q,\\alpha)$ and the associated **constants**\n$\\beta(t,q,\\alpha):=S_\\alpha(t,q,\\alpha)$ are the **Jacobi constants** of the complete integral.\nThis is the Hamiltonian case of the general first-order equation $u_t+H(t,q,D_qu)=0$: the $n$\nparameters $\\alpha$ enter essentially through the nonvanishing mixed derivative, and $S_t$ is\ndetermined algebraically by $S_q$ on the solutions."
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-cm-hamilton-jacobi-characteristics-from-a-complete-integral",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A complete integral produces Hamilton characteristics, the Jacobi constants, a local converse and canonical $(\\beta,\\alpha)$ labels",
