          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John M. Lee, Introduction to Smooth Manifolds, second edition, Theorem 17.14 and its proof (the homotopy formula gives the radial primitive of a closed form on a star-shaped domain)",
                "url": "https://dokumen.pub/introduction-to-smooth-manifolds-2nd-ed-9781441999818-9781441999825-1441999817-1441999825.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Nigel Hitchin, Differentiable Manifolds (2014), the Poincare lemma and the explicit radial contraction formula",
                "url": "https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/~joyce/Nairobi2019/Hitchin-DifferentiableManifolds.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be an open set star-shaped with respect to $0$\n([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]), and let $\\tau$ be a smooth closed two-form on $U$\n([[def-smooth-differential-k-form]], [[def-closed-and-exact-differential-forms]]). Write\n$\\tau=\\sum_{i<j}\\tau_{ij}\\,dx^i\\wedge dx^j$ with smooth coefficient functions $\\tau_{ij}$, and extend\nthe coefficients by $\\tau_{ji}:=-\\tau_{ij}$ and $\\tau_{ii}:=0$. Define\n$\\alpha:U\\to(\\mathbb R^n)^*$ by\n\n$$\\alpha_x(v):=\\int_0^1 t\\,\\tau_{tx}(x,v)\\,dt .$$\n\nThen $\\alpha$ is a smooth one-form on $U$, in coordinates\n\n$$\\alpha=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\alpha_i\\,dx^i,\\qquad \\alpha_i(x)=\\int_0^1 t\\sum_{j=1}^n x^j\\,\\tau_{ji}(tx)\\,dt ,$$\n\nand $d\\alpha=\\tau$ on $U$. In particular every smooth closed two-form on a star-shaped\nopen subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ is exact, with the displayed explicit primitive."
        },
