          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Ana Cannas da Silva, Lectures on Symplectic Geometry, revised January 2006, Lecture 18, Definition 18.10, Lemma 18.11 and Theorem 18.12 (Arnold-Liouville), pp. 110-111",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20120413034139if_/http://www.math.ist.utl.pt:80/%7Eacannas/Books/symplectic.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Eckhard Meinrenken, Symplectic Geometry, Chapter 6, sections 6.1-6.3, Theorem 6.21 (action-angle coordinates), pp. 66-75",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250806200149if_/https://www.math.utoronto.ca/mein/teaching/LectureNotes/symplectic.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.5 'Action-Angle Variables' (integrable systems and the construction of action-angle variables), printed pages 105-112",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $(M,\\omega)$\nbe a symplectic manifold of dimension $2n$, let $F=(F_1,\\dots,F_n)$ be a completely integrable\nsystem on $M$ ([[def-completely-integrable-hamiltonian-system]]) and let $N=F^{-1}(b_0)$ be a\nnonempty compact connected regular fibre. Assume that, after restricting to a saturated\nneighbourhood $U$ of $N$ and a ball $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ of regular values with $F(U)=B$, the map\n$\\pi:=F|_U:U\\to B$ is a proper submersion with connected fibres. Then there are an open neighbourhood $B_0\\subseteq B$ of $b_0$, an open action-coordinate ball $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, and, on the shrunk saturated neighbourhood $U_0:=\\pi^{-1}(B_0)$, action-angle coordinates\n$(I,\\theta):U_0\\to A\\times T^n$ in the sense of [[def-action-and-angle-coordinates]] (period-one\nangles) with\n\n$$\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n d\\theta_i\\wedge dI_i ,$$\n\nand every $C^\\infty$ function on $U_0$ that is constant on the fibres of $\\pi$ (in particular each\n$F_i|_{U_0}$) is a function of $I$ alone. The base neighbourhood $B_0$ is in the original integral-value coordinates, whereas $A=I(B_0)$ is in the action coordinates; they need not be the same set or both Euclidean balls.\n\nHere $T^n=\\mathbb R^n/\\mathbb Z^n$ has the quotient topology and the smooth atlas generated by the inverses of the restrictions of $q_n:x\\mapsto[x]$ to open boxes whose side lengths are less than one. The map $q_n$ is open, since the preimage of $q_n(V)$ is $\\bigcup_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}(V+k)$ for open $V$; its restriction to each such box is injective and a homeomorphism onto an open set. On each overlap component the chart transition is translation by a fixed integer vector, hence smooth. The quotient is Hausdorff and second countable as in [[def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral]], so this atlas specifies a smooth manifold ([[def-smooth-manifold]]), and $q_n$ is a local diffeomorphism. The symbols $d\\theta_i$ mean the global one-forms obtained from the differentials of these local lifts; integer translations leave those differentials unchanged. The product $A\\times T^n$ carries the corresponding product charts."
        },
