          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 4.7 (the free particle as the simplest Hamilton-Jacobi example), printed pages 121-126",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 15.4 (Jacobi's complete integral and worked Hamilton-Jacobi examples)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $m>0$ and let $H:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$,\n$H(t,q,p):=p^2/(2m)$, be the free-particle Hamiltonian\n([[prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart]]), and let\n$S:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$, $S(t,q,\\alpha):=q\\alpha-t\\alpha^2/(2m)$. Then:\n\n1. $S$ solves the Hamilton-Jacobi equation $S_t+H(t,q,S_q)=0$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ and is a complete\n   integral at every point, with $S_{q\\alpha}=1$\n   ([[def-cm-hamilton-jacobi-equation-and-complete-integral]]);\n2. the Jacobi constants are $\\beta:=S_\\alpha=q-t\\alpha/m$, so the characteristics are\n   $q(t)=\\beta+t\\alpha/m$ with momentum $p=\\alpha$; these satisfy $q'=p/m$, $p'=0$, and $\\beta$ is\n   constant along them ([[thm-cm-hamilton-jacobi-characteristics-from-a-complete-integral]]);\n3. on $\\mathbb R^3$ at fixed $t$ one has $dq\\wedge dp=d\\beta\\wedge d\\alpha$: the pair $(\\beta,\\alpha)$\n   is canonically conjugate to $(q,p)$."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-linear-shear-generating-function",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The linear shear: the second-kind generating function $S(q,P)=qP+\\lambda q^2/2$ and the matrix test",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e7",
          "design_state": "completed local argument; carrier and readiness record authored in this batch",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-generating-function-of-the-first-and-second-kind",
            "prop-cm-generating-functions-yield-canonical-maps",
            "prop-cm-canonical-map-tests-and-hamiltonian-transport",
