            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Sections 4.4.3 and 4.5 (generating functions and angle variables on the cylinder), printed pages 104-112",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 15.3-15.4 (canonical transformations and their global behaviour)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Take the cylinder $C=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)\\times\\mathbb R$ with the shift\n$\\Phi(\\theta,I)=(\\theta,I+1)$, i.e. $a=1$ in\n[[prop-cm-cylinder-shift-has-no-global-generating-function]], and with the descended forms\n$\\lambda_C,\\omega_C$ of that proposition. Then:\n\n1. $\\Phi^*\\lambda_C=\\lambda_C+d\\theta_C$ and $\\Phi^*\\omega_C=\\omega_C$;\n2. the loop $\\gamma(t)=\\pi(t,0)$, $t\\in[0,1]$, satisfies\n   $\\int_0^1 d\\theta_C(\\gamma'(t))\\,dt=1\\ne0$, confirming that $d\\theta_C$ is not exact on $C$;\n3. on the chart $\\{\\theta\\in(0,1)\\}$ the second-kind generating function\n   $S(\\theta,P)=\\theta P-\\theta$ generates the shift; globally no such $C^1$ function exists."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-flow-derivative-of-the-isotropic-oscillator",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The isotropic oscillator: the explicit flow, $Y^{\\mathsf T}JY=J$ and $\\det Y=1$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e7",
          "design_state": "completed local argument; carrier and readiness record authored in this batch",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "prop-cm-flow-derivative-satisfies-the-canonical-matrix-identity",
            "prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart",
