          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Sections 4.1-4.2 (the harmonic oscillator in phase space and the linearised flow), printed pages 80-92",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 15.1-15.2 (phase-space flows of the harmonic oscillator)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $n=1$, $U=\\mathbb R^2$ with coordinates $(q,p)$, and $H(q,p)=(p^2+q^2)/2$. Then:\n\n1. $X_H=(p,-q)$ and its flow is\n   $\\Phi_t(q,p)=\\bigl(q\\cos t+p\\sin t,\\ -q\\sin t+p\\cos t\\bigr)$, defined for all $t\\in\\mathbb R$;\n2. the derivative $Y(t)=D\\Phi_t(q,p)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos t&\\sin t\\\\-\\sin t&\\cos t\\end{pmatrix}$\n   satisfies $Y'(t)=J\\,\\mathrm{Hess}\\,H\\,Y(t)$, $Y(0)=I_2$,\n   $Y(t)^{\\mathsf T}J\\,Y(t)=J$ and $\\det Y(t)=1$ for all $t$, in accordance with\n   [[prop-cm-flow-derivative-satisfies-the-canonical-matrix-identity]]."
        }
      ],
