        "cm-math-balances"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The damped driven oscillator model: second-order equation, phase-plane system and characteristic data",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-derivative",
            "def-continuity-real",
            "def-interval",
            "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $c\\ge0$ and $k\\in\\mathbb R$ be given real numbers, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior ([[def-interval]]) and let $f:I\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous ([[def-continuity-real]]). The **damped driven oscillator model** with data $(m,c,k,f)$ is the second-order equation\n\n$$m\\,x''(t)+c\\,x'(t)+k\\,x(t)=f(t),\\qquad t\\in I .$$\n\nA **solution** of the model on an interval $J\\subseteq I$ with nonempty interior is a twice differentiable $x:J\\to\\mathbb R$ such that the identity holds for every $t\\in J$, derivatives being ordinary real derivatives ([[def-derivative]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]). The model is called **homogeneous** when $f\\equiv 0$, **undamped** when $c=0$, and **strictly damped** when $c>0$.\n\nThe **phase-plane equation** of the model has right-hand side\n\n$$G_f:I\\times\\mathbb R^2\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^2,\\qquad G_f(t,x,v):=\\Bigl(v,\\ \\frac{f(t)-c\\,v-k\\,x}{m}\\Bigr).$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb R^2$ is the **phase plane**, with coordinates $(x,v)$, where $x$ is the **displacement** and $v$ the **velocity**. For an interval $J\\subseteq I$ with nonempty interior, a differentiable curve $z=(x,v):J\\to\\mathbb R^2$ solves the phase-plane equation when $z'(t)=G_f(t,z(t))$ for every $t\\in J$, using the domain-relative derivative at any included endpoint ([[def-derivative]]). If $x$ is a solution of the model on $J$, then $t\\mapsto(x(t),x'(t))$ solves this equation; conversely, if $t\\mapsto(x(t),v(t))$ solves it, then $v=x'$ and $x$ is a solution of the model on $J$. When $J$ is open, this is the first-order system of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]] on the open domain $J\\times\\mathbb R^2$. For the homogeneous model the right-hand side is written $G_0(x,v)=(v,-(cv+kx)/m)$.\n\nThe **characteristic polynomial** of the model is\n\n$$p(\\lambda):=m\\lambda^2+c\\lambda+k,$$\n\nwith **discriminant** $\\Delta:=c^2-4mk$. When $k>0$ define the **undamped frequency** $\\omega_0:=\\sqrt{k/m}$, the **damping rate** $\\gamma:=c/(2m)$ and, when $4mk>c^2$, the **damped frequency** $\\omega:=\\sqrt{k/m-\\gamma^2}=\\sqrt{4mk-c^2}/(2m)$; the corresponding square roots are the nonnegative real ones.",
          "strategy": "Define the model, its solution notion, the phase-plane reduction and the characteristic data with explicit domains and regularity; no existence or uniqueness claim is smuggled into the definition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
