                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-parameter-dependent-action-angle-branch",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A one-frequency parameter-dependent action-angle branch, its transformed Hamiltonian and its drift generator",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e8",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\varepsilon>0$, let $\\widehat J,\\widehat S\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be open intervals, and let $J\\subseteq\\widehat J$ and $S\\subseteq\\widehat S$ be intervals with nonempty interiors ([[def-interval]]). Let $h:\\widehat J\\times\\widehat S\\to\\mathbb R$ and $k:\\widehat J\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\widehat S\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$ on these open Euclidean domains ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and suppose $k$ is $2\\pi$-periodic in its second variable on $J\\times\\mathbb R\\times S$:\n\n$$k(I,\\theta+2\\pi,s)=k(I,\\theta,s)\\qquad\\text{for all }(I,\\theta,s)\\in J\\times\\mathbb R\\times S .$$\n\nThe **transformed Hamiltonian** of the datum is\n\n$$K(I,\\theta,s):=h(I,s)+\\varepsilon\\,k(I,\\theta,s),$$\n\nits **frequency function** is\n\n$$\\omega(I,s):=\\partial_Ih(I,s),$$\n\nits **angle average** is $\\langle k\\rangle(I,s):=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}k(I,\\theta,s)\\,d\\theta$, and, on the set where $\\omega\\ne0$, its **drift generator** is\n\n$$g(I,\\theta,s):=\\frac{k(I,\\theta,s)-\\langle k\\rangle(I,s)}{\\omega(I,s)} .$$\n\nThe **branch vector field** of the datum is the restriction to $J\\times\\mathbb R\\times S$ of the map $\\widehat\\Phi:\\widehat J\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\widehat S\\to\\mathbb R^3$,\n\n$$\\widehat\\Phi(I,\\theta,s):=\\Bigl(-\\varepsilon\\,\\partial_\\theta k(I,\\theta,s),\\ \\ \\partial_Ih(I,s)+\\varepsilon\\,\\partial_Ik(I,\\theta,s),\\ \\ \\varepsilon\\Bigr),$$\n\nand a **branch solution** on an interval $T\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with nonempty interior is a differentiable $(I,\\theta,s):T\\to J\\times\\mathbb R\\times S$ satisfying $(I,\\theta,s)'=\\widehat\\Phi(I,\\theta,s)$ at every $t\\in T$, with derivatives relative to $T$ at included endpoints. On open time intervals this is the first-order system of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]] on the open domain $\\widehat J\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\widehat S$ ([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). Partial derivatives are those of the ambient Euclidean coordinates.",
          "strategy": "Define the branch datum, its drift generator and its vector field explicitly; the existence of such a chart for a given family is left as a consumer hypothesis.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 4.6, Eqs. (4.149)-(4.158): the action $I=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\oint p\\,dq$ of a one-dimensional family with slow parameter $\\lambda(t)$ and the averaged statement $\\langle\\dot I\\rangle=0$, printed pages 113-115",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 10 Sections 10.2-10.3, Eqs. (10.28)-(10.42): action variable $J=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\oint p\\,dq$, the averaged energy-change identity and the adiabatic-invariance conclusion $J=\\text{const}$, printed pages 5-8 of Chapter 10 (PDF pages 241-244)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
