            "thm-heine-cantor-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, equip $\\mathbb R^n$ with the Euclidean inner product and norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior ([[def-interval]]), let $t_0\\in I$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval with $I-I:=\\{t-s:t,s\\in I\\}\\subseteq U$, let $g:U\\to M_n(\\mathbb R)$ have $C^1$ entries ([[def-matrix-space]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) and let $f:I\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]]). For $B=(b_{ij})\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ and $v\\in\\mathbb R^n$, define $(Bv)_i:=\\sum_{j<n}b_{ij}v_j$. This is a linear map by the coordinate formula; the Euclidean norm is a norm by [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], and the standard coordinate vectors form an ordered basis by [[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]], so the map is bounded by [[cor-linear-maps-with-finite-dimensional-domain-are-bounded]]. Let $\\|B\\|$ denote its induced operator norm ([[def-operator-norm]]). All matrix actions in the integrals use this convention. Since $0\\in I-I\\subseteq U$, the value $g(0)$ is defined. Define\n\n$$F:I\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad F(t):=\\int_{t_0}^t g(t-s)f(s)\\,ds,$$\n\nwith the oriented integral of [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]] and the matrix action just defined; the integrand is continuous in $s$, hence integrable on every compact subinterval. Then:\n\n1. $F$ is differentiable at every interior point $t$ of $I$ ([[def-derivative]]) and\n\n$$F'(t)=g(0)f(t)+\\int_{t_0}^t g'(t-s)f(s)\\,ds .$$\n\n2. If moreover $g'$ is bounded on compact subintervals of $U$ (which holds automatically because $g$ is $C^1$), then $F$ is of class $C^1$ on the interior of $I$.",
          "strategy": "Split the difference quotient into a parameter-derivative term over a fixed interval, handled by differentiation under the integral sign, and a moving-endpoint term, handled by uniform continuity; then prove continuity of the derivative.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (retrieved 365-page online edition), Section 3.1, printed pp. 59-61 (matrix action and exponential), and Section 3.4, Theorem 3.12, printed pp. 83-84 (variation of constants). The convolution derivative is proved locally.",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis I, Section 8.4 (the second fundamental theorem of calculus and differentiation of parameter integrals), online text",
                "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_svthedercont.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The homogeneous damped oscillator: solution classification by the discriminant and uniqueness for initial data",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system",
            "lem-cm-continuous-dependence-on-initial-data",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
            "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "def-derivative",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-continuous-dependence-on-initial-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $c\\ge0$ and $k>0$, and consider the homogeneous damped oscillator model $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=0$ of [[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]] with time domain $I=\\mathbb R$ and forcing $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ identically zero, with characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)=m\\lambda^2+c\\lambda+k$, discriminant $\\Delta=c^2-4mk$, damping rate $\\gamma:=c/(2m)$, and, when applicable, roots\n$$\\lambda_\\pm:=\\frac{-c\\pm\\sqrt\\Delta}{2m}\\ (\\Delta\\ge0),\\qquad \\lambda:=-\\frac{c}{2m},\\qquad \\omega:=\\frac{\\sqrt{4mk-c^2}}{2m}\\ (\\Delta<0).$$\n\nA local solution here may have any interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with nonempty interior, with domain-relative derivatives at included endpoints. It has a unique extension to the global family below. A **maximal solution** means one that has no solution extension to a strictly larger interval; the maximal solutions of this global autonomous equation are precisely the family members on $\\mathbb R$. For a homogeneous model restricted to a time domain $I$, its solutions have $J\\subseteq I$ and are restrictions of these same global members; their extensions solve the all-$\\mathbb R$ equation.\n\n1. **Distinct real roots.** If $\\Delta>0$, then $\\lambda_+>\\lambda_-$ are exactly the real roots of $p$, and a function $x:J\\to\\mathbb R$ on an interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with nonempty interior is a solution of the model if and only if\n$$x(t)=Ae^{\\lambda_+t}+Be^{\\lambda_-t}\\quad(t\\in J)$$\nfor some $A,B\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\n2. **Double real root.** If $\\Delta=0$, then $\\lambda=-c/(2m)$ is the only root of $p$, and $x:J\\to\\mathbb R$ is a solution if and only if\n$$x(t)=(A+Bt)e^{\\lambda t}\\quad(t\\in J)$$\nfor some $A,B\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\n3. **Complex conjugate roots.** If $\\Delta<0$, then $\\omega>0$, the roots of $p$ are $-\\gamma\\pm i\\omega$, and $x:J\\to\\mathbb R$ is a solution if and only if\n$$x(t)=e^{-\\gamma t}\\bigl(A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)\\quad(t\\in J)$$\nfor some $A,B\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\n4. **Initial data.** In each of the three cases and for every $(x_0,v_0)\\in\\mathbb R^2$ there is exactly one solution $x:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ with $x(0)=x_0$ and $x'(0)=v_0$. Its coefficients are\n$$A=\\frac{v_0-\\lambda_-x_0}{\\lambda_+-\\lambda_-},\\quad B=\\frac{\\lambda_+x_0-v_0}{\\lambda_+-\\lambda_-}\\quad(\\Delta>0),\\qquad A=x_0,\\ B=v_0-\\lambda x_0\\quad(\\Delta=0),$$\n$$A=x_0,\\quad B=\\frac{v_0+\\gamma x_0}{\\omega}\\quad(\\Delta<0).$$",
          "strategy": "Verify each family by direct differentiation (using the complex exponential in the oscillatory case), match initial data by explicit linear algebra, and prove exhaustiveness by a connectedness argument using the local uniqueness lemma for the phase-plane field.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.6)-(5.12): characteristic equation $\\lambda^2+2\\delta\\lambda+\\omega_0^2=0$, its roots (5.8) and the free solution (5.9); printed pages 2-3 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 88-89)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 7 Section 7.1.3, Eq. (7.16): underdamped, critically damped and overdamped solution cases of the linearised oscillator, printed page 148",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (retrieved 365-page online edition), Section 3.1, printed pp. 59-61 (matrix exponential). The three scalar families and their exhaustiveness are verified locally from the declared mathematical suppliers.",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
