          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $c\\ge0$, let $k\\le0$, and consider the model $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=0$ of [[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]] with time domain $I=\\mathbb R$ and forcing $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ identically zero. In the three cases below, $x:J\\to\\mathbb R$ runs over solutions on intervals $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with nonempty interior.\n\nA local solution here may have any interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with nonempty interior, with domain-relative derivatives at included endpoints. It has a unique extension to the global family below. A **maximal solution** means one that has no solution extension to a strictly larger interval; the maximal solutions of this global autonomous equation are precisely the family members on $\\mathbb R$. For a homogeneous model restricted to a time domain $I$, its solutions have $J\\subseteq I$ and are restrictions of these same global members; their extensions solve the all-$\\mathbb R$ equation.\n\n1. **Double zero root ($k=0$, $c=0$).** Then on every such interval $J$, $x$ solves the model if and only if $x(t)=A+Bt$ for some $A,B\\in\\mathbb R$; each such local solution is the restriction of a unique global member of this family. For every datum $(x_0,v_0)$ there is a unique global solution on $\\mathbb R$ with $x(0)=x_0$, $x'(0)=v_0$, namely $x(t)=x_0+v_0t$.\n2. **Simple zero root ($k=0$, $c>0$).** Then on every such interval $J$, $x$ solves the model if and only if\n$$x(t)=A+Be^{-ct/m}$$\nfor some $A,B\\in\\mathbb R$; each such local solution is the restriction of a unique global member of this family. For every datum $(x_0,v_0)$ there is a unique global solution on $\\mathbb R$ with $x(0)=x_0$, $x'(0)=v_0$, namely the member with $A=x_0+\\frac{m v_0}{c}$ and $B=-\\frac{m v_0}{c}$. This global solution has limit $A$ as $t\\to+\\infty$; any local solution on an interval unbounded above has the same limit along that interval.\n3. **One positive and one negative root ($k<0$).** Put $$\\lambda_\\pm:=\\frac{-c\\pm\\sqrt{c^2-4mk}}{2m}.$$ Then $\\lambda_-<0<\\lambda_+$, both are real roots of the characteristic polynomial, and on every such interval $J$, $x$ solves the model if and only if\n$$x(t)=Ae^{\\lambda_+t}+Be^{\\lambda_-t}$$\nfor some $A,B\\in\\mathbb R$; each such local solution is the restriction of a global member of this family. In particular the global solution $t\\mapsto e^{\\lambda_+t}$ has data $x(0)=1$, $x'(0)=\\lambda_+$ and satisfies $e^{\\lambda_+t}\\to+\\infty$ as $t\\to+\\infty$: the model has an unbounded solution growing exponentially on $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "strategy": "Derive the k=0 families by integrating the first-order equation for x'; verify the exponential solutions for k<0, exhibit the growing solution, and obtain uniqueness from the local uniqueness lemma.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.6)-(5.9): characteristic equation and the free damped solutions, including the high-damping and free cases, printed pages 2-3 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 88-89)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 7 Section 7.1.3, Eq. (7.16) and the surrounding classification of linearised oscillator solutions, printed page 148",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
