            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-space-complete": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-gronwall-integral-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, equip $\\mathbb R^n$ with the Euclidean inner product and norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], let $A\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ ([[def-matrix-space]]), let $t_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and $z_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let $b:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]). The Euclidean norm is a norm by [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], and the standard coordinate vectors form an ordered basis by [[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]. For $A=(a_{ij})$ and $z\\in\\mathbb R^n$, define $(Az)_i:=\\sum_{j<n}a_{ij}z_j$; this is a linear map by [[def-linear-map]], hence bounded by [[cor-linear-maps-with-finite-dimensional-domain-are-bounded]], and $\\|A\\|$ denotes its induced Euclidean operator norm ([[def-operator-norm]]). Matrix powers and products use [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]; their action agrees with composition of the corresponding linear maps by distributing the finite sums in the displayed coordinate formula. Define the **matrix exponential**\n\n$$E:\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow M_n(\\mathbb R),\\qquad E(t):=\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty\\frac{t^k}{k!}A^k .$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $E$ is well defined and of class $C^1$, with $E(0)=I_n$, $E(u+v)=E(u)E(v)$, $\\frac{d}{du}E(u)=AE(u)=E(u)A$ for all $u,v\\in\\mathbb R$, and $\\|E(u)\\|\\le e^{\\|A\\||u|}$ for the operator norm ([[def-operator-norm]]) subordinate to the Euclidean norm.\n2. The map\n$$z:\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad z(t):=E(t-t_0)z_0+\\int_{t_0}^tE(t-s)b(s)\\,ds,$$\nsatisfies $z(t_0)=z_0$ and $z'(t)=A\\,z(t)+b(t)$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$.\n3. Uniqueness: if $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an interval with nonempty interior containing $t_0$ ([[def-interval]]) and $w:J\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is differentiable with $w'=Aw+b$ on $J$ and $w(t_0)=z_0$, then $w=z|_J$.",
          "strategy": "Combine the exponential-series lemma with the convolution differentiation lemma to verify the explicit solution, and prove uniqueness by Gronwall.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (retrieved 365-page online edition), Section 3.1, Eqs. (3.2)-(3.12), printed pp. 59-61; Section 3.4, Theorem 3.12, Eq. (3.97), printed pp. 83-84.",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.27)-(5.30): the temporal Green's function as the response to a short pulse, $q(t)=\\int f(t')G(t-t')\\,dt'$, printed pages 6-7 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 92-93)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cor-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-decay",
          "kind": "corollary",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Strictly damped homogeneous oscillator with $k>0$: every solution tends to zero",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
