            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $c>0$ and $k>0$, let $x:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be a solution of the homogeneous model $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=0$ with time domain $I=\\mathbb R$ and $f\\equiv0$ ([[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]], [[thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification]]), and write $\\Delta=c^2-4mk$, $\\gamma=c/(2m)$, $\\lambda_\\pm=(-c\\pm\\sqrt\\Delta)/(2m)$ in case $\\Delta>0$ and $\\lambda=-c/(2m)$ in case $\\Delta=0$. Then $x(t)\\to0$ as $t\\to+\\infty$ ([[def-limits-at-infinity]]); more precisely, with the coefficients $A,B$ of the solution in the notation of [[thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification]]:\n\n1. if $\\Delta>0$: $\\lambda_+<0$ and $|x(t)|\\le(|A|+|B|)e^{\\lambda_+t}$ for all $t\\ge0$;\n2. if $\\Delta=0$: $\\lambda<0$ and $|x(t)|\\le(|A|+|B|t)e^{\\lambda t}$ for all $t\\ge0$;\n3. if $\\Delta<0$: $\\gamma>0$ and $|x(t)|\\le(|A|+|B|)e^{-\\gamma t}$ for all $t\\ge0$.",
          "strategy": "Read the three explicit families off the classification theorem, bound them by the sign of the roots and the range of sine and cosine, and take the limits (including the t e^{lambda t} case by an exponential-series lower bound).",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.9)-(5.12): exponential decay of the free damped oscillations with time constant $\\tau=1/\\delta$ and the quality factor, printed pages 2-3 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 88-89)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
