        {
          "id": "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-variation-of-constants",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The forced oscillator with $k\\ge0$: unique global solution and the convolution (Green's function) formula",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases",
            "lem-cm-convolution-differentiation",
            "cor-cm-variation-of-constants-for-linear-systems",
            "def-derivative",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-convolution-differentiation": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cm-variation-of-constants-for-linear-systems": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $c\\ge0$, $k\\ge0$, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior ([[def-interval]]) and let $f:I\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. Fix $t_0\\in I$ and data $(x_0,v_0)\\in\\mathbb R^2$. Let $g:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be the (unique) solution of the associated homogeneous model with time domain $\\mathbb R$ and zero forcing, with $g(0)=0$ and $g'(0)=1/m$.\n\nThen:\n\n1. There is exactly one solution $x:I\\to\\mathbb R$ of the forced model $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=f$ with $x(t_0)=x_0$ and $x'(t_0)=v_0$. It is twice differentiable on $I$, with derivatives at included endpoints taken relative to $I$, and $x''$ is continuous on $I$ in its relative topology.\n2. If $x_h:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ is any homogeneous solution with $x_h(t_0)=x_0$ and $x_h'(t_0)=v_0$ (it exists and is unique by [[thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification]] and [[prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases]]), then\n$$x(t)=x_h(t)+\\int_{t_0}^tg(t-s)f(s)\\,ds\\qquad(t\\in I).$$",
          "strategy": "Construct the solution as homogeneous part plus convolution, verify the equation by two applications of the convolution lemma using g''=-(c g'+k g)/m, and invoke the variation-of-constants uniqueness.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.13)-(5.18) and (5.27)-(5.30): the forced equation, the response function and the temporal Green's function $q(t)=\\int f(t')G(t-t')\\,dt'$, printed pages 3-7 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 89-93)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (retrieved 365-page online edition), Section 3.4, Theorem 3.12 and Eqs. (3.92)-(3.97), printed pp. 83-84 (continuous inhomogeneous linear forcing).",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cor-cm-forced-modal-oscillator-response",
          "kind": "corollary",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Forced quadratic system $Mq''+Kq=\\varphi(t)$: decoupled forced modes and the convolution solution",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-variation-of-constants",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-variation-of-constants": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $M\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ be symmetric positive definite, let $K\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ be symmetric and non-negative, let $S:=M^{-1/2}KM^{-1/2}=O\\Lambda O^{\\mathsf T}$ with $O$ orthogonal and $\\Lambda=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_n)$, $\\lambda_i\\ge0$ ([[thm-cm-quadratic-mode-decomposition]]), let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior ([[def-interval]]), let $\\varphi:I\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and let $t_0\\in I$. Put\n\n$$\\psi(t):=O^{\\mathsf T}M^{-1/2}\\varphi(t),\\qquad \\omega_i:=\\sqrt{\\lambda_i}\\ (\\lambda_i>0),$$\n\nand for a curve $q:I\\to\\mathbb R^n$ whose first and second derivatives exist on $I$ (relative to $I$ at included endpoints) and whose second derivative is continuous on $I$, put $z:=O^{\\mathsf T}M^{1/2}q$. Then:\n\n1. $q$ solves the forced quadratic system $Mq''+Kq=\\varphi$ on $I$ if and only if $z$ solves the decoupled forced system\n$$z_i''+\\lambda_iz_i=\\psi_i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le n)$$\non $I$.\n2. For each $i$, the unique solution $z_i:I\\to\\mathbb R$ of the scalar equation in clause 1 with data $z_i(t_0)=\\xi_i$, $z_i'(t_0)=\\eta_i$ is\n$$z_i(t)=\\xi_i\\cos\\bigl(\\omega_i(t-t_0)\\bigr)+\\frac{\\eta_i}{\\omega_i}\\sin\\bigl(\\omega_i(t-t_0)\\bigr)+\\frac1{\\omega_i}\\int_{t_0}^t\\sin\\bigl(\\omega_i(t-s)\\bigr)\\psi_i(s)\\,ds\\quad(\\lambda_i>0),$$\n$$z_i(t)=\\xi_i+\\eta_i(t-t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^t(t-s)\\psi_i(s)\\,ds\\quad(\\lambda_i=0).$$\n3. Consequently, for every initial datum $(q(t_0),q'(t_0))\\in\\mathbb R^{2n}$ there is exactly one solution $q:I\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of $Mq''+Kq=\\varphi$ with that datum, and it is $q=M^{-1/2}Oz$ for the $z$ of clause 2.",
          "strategy": "Transport the forced system through the modal transformation of the quadratic-mode theorem and apply the scalar forced-oscillator theorem mode by mode.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (retrieved 365-page online edition), Section 8.4, Eqs. (8.77)-(8.80), printed pp. 245-246 (quadratic harmonic model and modal transformation); Section 3.4, Theorem 3.12, printed pp. 83-84 (inhomogeneous linear response). The present mass convention and forced mode formulas are checked locally.",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 2.6 (normal modes and their equations), printed pages 40-45",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
