          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $m>0$, $k>0$, $c\\ge0$, let $f_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\Omega\\ge0$, and consider the forced model $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=f$ with time domain $I=\\mathbb R$ and forcing $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$, $f(t)=f_0\\cos(\\Omega t)$ ([[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]]). Put\n\n$$D:=k-m\\Omega^2+i\\,c\\,\\Omega\\in\\mathbb C,\\qquad \\omega_0:=\\sqrt{k/m}.$$\n\n1. **Non-resonant response.** If $D\\ne0$, then the function\n$$x_p(t):=\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\left(\\frac{f_0e^{i\\Omega t}}{D}\\right)=f_0\\,\\frac{(k-m\\Omega^2)\\cos(\\Omega t)+c\\,\\Omega\\sin(\\Omega t)}{(k-m\\Omega^2)^2+c^2\\Omega^2}$$\nis a solution of the forced model on all of $\\mathbb R$. Its amplitude about zero is $|f_0|/\\sqrt{(k-m\\Omega^2)^2+c^2\\Omega^2}$. For $\\Omega>0$ and $f_0\\ne0$, a **phase-lag angle relative to the drive** means a real $\\phi$ such that $x_p(t)=(f_0/|D|)\\cos(\\Omega t-\\phi)$ for every $t$, with the signed factor $f_0$ retained in both drive and response. Such an angle exists: any $\\phi\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfying\n$$\\cos\\phi=\\frac{k-m\\Omega^2}{|D|},\\qquad\\sin\\phi=\\frac{c\\Omega}{|D|}$$\nhas this property. No temporal phase is assigned when $\\Omega=0$ or $f_0=0$.\n\n2. **Resonance.** If $c=0$ and $\\Omega=\\omega_0$, then $D=0$ and\n$$x_p(t):=\\frac{f_0\\,t\\sin(\\omega_0t)}{2m\\omega_0}$$\nis a solution of the forced model on all of $\\mathbb R$; if $f_0\\ne0$ then $x_p$ is unbounded on $[0,\\infty)$.\n\n3. **Transient decay.** If $c>0$, then $D\\ne0$ for every $\\Omega\\ge0$, and every solution $x:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ of the forced model satisfies\n$$x(t)=x_p(t)+x_h(t)$$\nwhere $x_h$ is a homogeneous solution; consequently $x(t)-x_p(t)\\to0$ as $t\\to+\\infty$ ([[def-limits-at-infinity]]) and $x$ is bounded on $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "strategy": "Verify the complex ansatz and take real parts, verify the resonant solution directly and exhibit its unbounded sequence, and use the linear splitting plus the homogeneous decay corollary for the transient statement.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.14)-(5.22): the response function, the forced amplitude (5.18), the resonance value (5.19) and the resonance discussion, printed pages 3-5 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 89-91)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 7 Section 7.1.3, Eqs. (7.15)-(7.16): transient plus steady state forced motion with resonant behaviour at drive frequency equal to the natural frequency, printed pages 147-148",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum",
          "kind": "proposition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The amplitude of the sinusoidally forced damped oscillator: location and value of its maximum",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response",
            "cor-mean-value-theorem",
            "thm-chain-rule",
