            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, $P>0$, $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ open, and let $f:\\mathbb R\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous, continuously partially differentiable in the second variable and $P$-periodic in time, with average $\\bar f$, oscillatory part $f-\\bar f$ and periodic deviation $g$ as in [[def-cm-averaging-data-and-averaged-field]] ([[def-interval]]). Here $D_xf(t,x):\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$ denotes the total derivative in the state variable, with coordinate columns $\\partial_{x_j}f(t,x)$; it exists and is continuous by [[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]]. The same notation is used for $D_x\\bar f$ and $D_xg$ when those total derivatives exist. Let $x_0\\in O$, $R>0$ with $\\overline B(x_0,R)\\subseteq O$ ([[def-metric-ball]]) and let $M,L\\ge0$ satisfy\n\n$$\\|f(t,x)\\|_2\\le M,\\qquad \\|f(t,x)-f(t,y)\\|_2\\le L\\|x-y\\|_2\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x,y\\in\\overline B(x_0,R)).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $\\|\\bar f(x)\\|_2\\le M$ and $\\|\\bar f(x)-\\bar f(y)\\|_2\\le L\\|x-y\\|_2$ for all $x,y\\in\\overline B(x_0,R)$.\n2. $g$ is $P$-periodic and $\\|g(t,x)\\|_2\\le2MP$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $x\\in\\overline B(x_0,R)$; moreover $\\|g(t,x)-g(t,y)\\|_2\\le2LP\\|x-y\\|_2$ for all $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $x,y\\in\\overline B(x_0,R)$.\n3. $\\partial_tg(t,x)=f(t,x)-\\bar f(x)$ for every $(t,x)$; consequently $\\|\\partial_tg(t,x)\\|_2\\le2M$ for $x\\in\\overline B(x_0,R)$ and $\\|\\partial_tg(t,x)-\\partial_tg(t,y)\\|_2\\le2L\\|x-y\\|_2$ for all $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $x,y\\in\\overline B(x_0,R)$.\n4. $g$ has continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R\\times O$; its partial derivative in the second variable is $D_xg(t,x)=\\int_0^t\\bigl(D_xf(s,x)-D_x\\bar f(x)\\bigr)ds$, and $\\|D_xg(t,x)\\|\\le2LP$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $x\\in\\overline B(x_0,R)$, with $\\|\\cdot\\|$ the operator norm on $M_n(\\mathbb R)$ ([[def-operator-norm]]). In particular $g$ is totally differentiable at every point of $\\mathbb R\\times O$ ([[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]]).",
          "strategy": "Estimate the integrals with the norm inequality and monotonicity, control the time derivative by the fundamental theorem and periodicity at multiples of P, and obtain the state derivative by differentiation under the integral sign.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.3, Eqs. (5.52)-(5.57): time averaging over one period and the reduced equations, with the first-order-order-of-magnitude bookkeeping, printed pages 12-14 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 99-101)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
