            "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\varepsilon>0$, $A>0$, $\\omega_0>0$, let $\\widehat J,\\widehat S\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be open intervals, and let $J\\subseteq\\widehat J$ and $S\\subseteq\\widehat S$ be intervals with nonempty interiors ([[def-cm-parameter-dependent-action-angle-branch]], [[def-interval]]). Let $h:\\widehat J\\times\\widehat S\\to\\mathbb R$ and $k:\\widehat J\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\widehat S\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^2$, with $k$ $2\\pi$-periodic in $\\theta$ on $J\\times\\mathbb R\\times S$. Let $K\\subseteq J$ and $S_0\\subseteq S$ be compact intervals, let $s_0\\in S_0$, and suppose\n\n$$|\\partial_Ih(I,s)|\\ge\\omega_0\\qquad\\bigl((I,s)\\in K\\times S_0\\bigr).$$\n\nLet $(I,\\theta,s):[0,A/\\varepsilon]\\to J\\times\\mathbb R\\times S$ be a branch solution of the branch vector field of the datum ([[def-cm-parameter-dependent-action-angle-branch]]) with $s(0)=s_0$, and suppose that for every $t\\in[0,A/\\varepsilon]$ one has $I(t)\\in K$ and $s(t)\\in S_0$. Then for every $t\\in[0,A/\\varepsilon]$,\n\n$$|I(t)-I(0)|\\le\\varepsilon\\Bigl[2\\sup_{K\\times[0,2\\pi]\\times S_0}|g|+A\\sup_{K\\times[0,2\\pi]\\times S_0}\\bigl|\\partial_Ig\\,\\partial_\\theta k-\\partial_\\theta g\\,\\partial_Ik-\\partial_sg\\bigr|\\Bigr],$$\n\nwhere $g=(k-\\langle k\\rangle)/\\partial_Ih$ is the drift generator of the datum.",
          "strategy": "Compute the exact chain-rule identity (I+epsilon g)'=epsilon^2(partial_I g partial_theta k - partial_theta g partial_I k - partial_s g), integrate it over the horizon, and bound the two error terms.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 10 Sections 10.2-10.3, Eqs. (10.28)-(10.42): the adiabatic regime, the averaged energy-change identity and the invariance of the action variable to the accuracy of the averaged argument, printed pages 5-8 of Chapter 10 (PDF pages 241-244)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 4.6, Eqs. (4.148)-(4.158): the averaged statement $\\langle\\dot I\\rangle=0$ for a slowly varying parameter, with the author's explicit note that more rigorous proofs exist elsewhere, printed pages 113-115",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 5 Sections 5.2-5.3, Eqs. (5.23)-(5.28): secular versus periodic first-order effects after averaging over one period, printed pages 96-98",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm-approximation-scope-and-limits",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Scope and limits of the oscillator, linearization, averaging and adiabatic items on this page",
          "reference": "batch-7-local-construction",
          "design_state": "scope-remark",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-variation-of-constants",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum",
            "cor-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-decay",
            "cor-cm-forced-modal-oscillator-response",
            "thm-cm-finite-time-linearization-error-bound",
            "thm-cm-averaging-for-fast-periodic-oscillations",
            "thm-cm-adiabatic-invariant-drift-bound"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-variation-of-constants": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-decay": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cm-forced-modal-oscillator-response": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-finite-time-linearization-error-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-averaging-for-fast-periodic-oscillations": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-adiabatic-invariant-drift-bound": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Every item on this page is mathematics: parameters, forcing functions, initial data and error constants are mathematical objects, and none of the statements asserts a physical law, a unit convention or an empirical fact. The following limits are part of the claims and must be carried by any consumer.\n\n- **Linear oscillator results are exact for the stated model only.** [[thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification]] and [[prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases]] classify all solutions of the constant-coefficient model with the stated parameter ranges ($k>0$, resp. $k\\le0$) and are exact equivalences, including the free zero mode when $k=0$ and the exponential growth when $k<0$. [[cor-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-decay]] needs $c>0$ and $k>0$; for $c=0$ no decay is claimed. [[thm-cm-forced-oscillator-variation-of-constants]] gives the exact convolution representation for continuous forcing and $k\\ge0$; the kernel formula is a formula for that model, not a physical superposition principle. [[thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response]] separates the steady response from the homogeneous transient; the resonant clause supplies an exact solution of the undamped resonant model, unbounded when $f_0\\ne0$ and identically zero when $f_0=0$, and [[prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum]] locates the unique maximum of the response amplitude for $c>0$ and $f_0\\ne0$ (for $c=0$ and $f_0\\ne0$ the non-resonant amplitude is unbounded near $\\omega_0$; for $f_0=0$ it is zero wherever its formula is defined). [[cor-cm-forced-modal-oscillator-response]] is exact for constant coefficient matrices $M,K$ and continuous forcing; it asserts nothing about nonlinear systems or configuration-dependent $M$.\n- **Finite-time linearization is not a stability statement.** [[thm-cm-finite-time-linearization-error-bound]] is conditional on the explicit smallness condition $\\delta e^{LT}<r$ and on a quadratic remainder bound on the ball, and concludes only $|z(t)-y(t)|=O(\\delta^2)$ on the finite horizon $[0,T]$. It neither asserts long-time agreement of the nonlinear and linear evolutions nor Lyapunov stability of the origin. For a mechanical system with a configuration-dependent kinetic metric, the remainder hypothesis must be verified for the full first-order field (derivatives of $M$ and $M^{-1}$ included), not for the potential alone.\n- **Averaging compares two different models.** [[thm-cm-averaging-for-fast-periodic-oscillations]] compares the solutions of $x'=\\varepsilon f(t,x)$ and $y'=\\varepsilon\\bar f(y)$ and gives agreement of order $\\varepsilon$ on the horizon $[0,A/\\varepsilon]$ under the trapping condition $\\varepsilon K<\\rho$. The averaged equation is not equivalent to the oscillatory one; a longer horizon requires the trapping and smallness hypotheses to be checked again, and the theorem says nothing about stability or about invariant sets of the exact flow.\n- **Adiabatic bounds need the chart, the frequency gap and the trapping.** [[thm-cm-adiabatic-invariant-drift-bound]] is conditional on a $C^2$ parameter-dependent action-angle datum with $|\\partial_Ih|\\ge\\omega_0>0$ on the stated compact sets and on the branch solution staying in them. The existence of such a family for a given mechanical model, uniform in the parameter, is a separate hypothesis; resonances ($\\omega=0$), separatrix crossings and action values leaving the chart are excluded, and the conclusion is a bound of order $\\varepsilon$ on a finite rescaled-time horizon, not exact conservation of the action.\n\n- **Downstream use.** Physical pages that read these results must adopt their own postulates and interpretations for masses, forces, damping, driving and adiabatic parameter variation, and must re-state the empirical qualifications of any measured input; none of the mathematics above supplies such interpretations.",
          "strategy": "Collect the hypotheses and limits of the preceding items in one place so that consumers carry them; no proof obligation is discharged by the remark.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Sections 5.1 and 5.3 and Chapter 10 Sections 10.2-10.3: the perturbative and averaged character of the oscillator, reduced-equation and adiabatic discussion",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 version), Section 4.6: the author's explicit remark that the intuitive adiabatic argument is approximate and that stricter proofs exist elsewhere",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
