      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-oscillator-cases-computed",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Five worked initial value problems for the homogeneous oscillator across the discriminant, degenerate and repulsive cases",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "scaffolder-constructed-explicit-mathematical-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "In each line, $m=1$ and the initial datum $(x_0,v_0)$ is prescribed for the homogeneous model with time domain $I=\\mathbb R$ and zero forcing, $x''+cx'+kx=0$ ([[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]]). The solution is read off from the classification theorem and the degenerate-case proposition, and its initial values are checked explicitly.\n\n- (i) $k=4$, $c=0$, $(x_0,v_0)=(1,0)$: the solution is $x(t)=\\cos(2t)$.\n- (ii) $k=1$, $c=2$, $(x_0,v_0)=(1,0)$: the solution is $x(t)=(1+t)e^{-t}$.\n- (iii) $k=1$, $c=3$, $(x_0,v_0)=(1,0)$: with $\\lambda_\\pm=(-3\\pm\\sqrt5)/2$ the solution is $x(t)=\\bigl(-\\lambda_-e^{\\lambda_+t}+\\lambda_+e^{\\lambda_-t}\\bigr)/\\sqrt5$.\n- (iv) $k=0$, $c=2$, $(x_0,v_0)=(0,1)$: the solution is $x(t)=\\tfrac12(1-e^{-2t})$.\n- (v) $k=-1$, $c=0$, $(x_0,v_0)=(1,0)$: the solution is $x(t)=\\tfrac12(e^{t}+e^{-t})$, which is unbounded on $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "strategy": "Compute the discriminant and coefficients from the classification theorem and the degenerate-case proposition and check the initial data explicitly.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.2)-(5.9): the free oscillator solution in the undamped, damped and high-damping cases, printed pages 1-3 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 87-89)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 7 Section 7.1.3, Eq. (7.16): underdamped, critically damped and overdamped classification of the linearised oscillator, printed page 148",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-resonant-forcing-and-amplitude-peak",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Resonant forcing at $\\Omega=\\omega_0$ and the two regimes of the amplitude maximum",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "scaffolder-constructed-explicit-mathematical-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Consider the forced model $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=f_0\\cos(\\Omega t)$ on time domain $I=\\mathbb R$ with the values and conclusions below ([[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]], [[thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response]], [[prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum]]).\n\n- (i) Resonance: $m=k=f_0=1$, $c=0$, $\\Omega=\\omega_0=1$. Then $x_p(t)=\\tfrac12t\\sin t$ is a solution of $x''+x=\\cos t$, it satisfies $x_p(0)=0$, $x_p'(0)=0$, and $|x_p|$ is unbounded on $[0,\\infty)$.\n- (ii) Amplitude peak above zero frequency: $m=k=f_0=1$, $c=1$. Then $u_*=1-\\tfrac12=\\tfrac12>0$, the maximum of $A(\\Omega)=1/\\sqrt{(1-\\Omega^2)^2+\\Omega^2}$ is attained at $\\Omega_*=1/\\sqrt2$, and $A(\\Omega_*)=2/\\sqrt3$, while $A(0)=A(1)=1$.\n- (iii) Amplitude peak at zero frequency: $m=k=f_0=1$, $c=2$. Then $u_*=1-2=-1\\le0$, and the maximum of $A(\\Omega)=1/\\sqrt{(1-\\Omega^2)^2+4\\Omega^2}$ on $[0,\\infty)$ is attained at $\\Omega=0$ with value $1$, while $A(1)=1/2$.",
          "strategy": "Substitute the resonant candidate using sine and cosine derivatives and evaluate the amplitude formula in the two regimes.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.18)-(5.22): the resonance peak, the value (5.19) at $\\omega=\\omega_0$ and the FWHM (5.20), printed pages 4-5 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 90-91)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 7 Section 7.1.3, Eq. (7.16): steady state forced motion with resonant behaviour at the drive frequency, printed page 148",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-uniform-force-and-linear-drag",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Constant forcing: free fall and the approach to terminal velocity under linear drag",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e3",
          "design_state": "scaffolder-constructed-explicit-mathematical-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
