            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-mean-value-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-addition-formula": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-integral-elementary-bounds": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $l=g=1$ and consider the planar pendulum field\n\n$$F:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,\\qquad F(\\theta,\\omega)=\\bigl(\\omega,\\ -\\sin\\theta\\bigr),$$\n\nLet $\\mathcal A:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$ be the linear map $\\mathcal A(\\theta,\\omega):=(\\omega,-\\theta)$, and let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\in M_2(\\mathbb R)$ be its standard-coordinate matrix; use the matrix action of $A$ from [[thm-cm-finite-time-linearization-error-bound]]. On the closed ball $\\overline B(0,\\tfrac12)$ one has\n\n$$\\|F(z)-Az\\|_2\\le\\tfrac1{12}\\|z\\|_2^2,\\qquad \\|F(z)\\|_2\\le\\|z\\|_2,$$\n\nso the linearization theorem applies with $r=\\tfrac12$, $C=\\tfrac1{12}$, $L=1$. For the datum $z_0=(\\tfrac1{100},0)$ and $T=1$ one has $\\delta e^{LT}=\\tfrac e{100}<\\tfrac3{100}<\\tfrac1{20}<\\tfrac12$, and the theorem bounds the linearization error by\n\n$$\\|z(t)-y(t)\\|_2\\le\\frac{\\delta^2}{12}\\int_0^1e^{(1-s)}e^{2s}\\,ds<10^{-4}\\qquad(0\\le t\\le1),$$\n\nwhere $y$ solves $y'=Ay$, $y(0)=z_0$; in particular the linear solution stays within $10^{-4}$ of the nonlinear one on $[0,1]$.",
          "strategy": "Bound |sin theta - theta| by the Taylor remainder and |F(z)| by the norm of z, then substitute into the linearization theorem with ||A||=1.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (retrieved 365-page online edition), Section 2.4, Theorem 2.10, printed pp. 45-48 (variational equation), and Section 3.4, Theorem 3.12, printed pp. 83-84 (variation of constants). The numerical pendulum bounds are calculated locally.",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 7 Section 7.1.3: the linearised oscillator equation and the role of the nonlinear term, printed pages 147-148",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-averaging-oscillatory-deviation-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Averaging estimate on $[0,100]$ for the scalar equation $x'=\\varepsilon\\cos t$",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e8",
          "design_state": "scaffolder-constructed-explicit-mathematical-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
