          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $n=1$, $P=2\\pi$, $O=\\mathbb R$, $f(t,x)=\\cos t$ (independent of $x$), $x_0=0$, and the trapping data $R=1$, $\\rho=\\tfrac12$, $A=1$. Then $f$ is continuous, $C^1$ in $x$ with $D_xf=0$, and $2\\pi$-periodic in $t$; on $\\mathbb R\\times\\overline B(0,1)$ one may take $M=1$ and $L=0$. The average and deviation of [[def-cm-averaging-data-and-averaged-field]] are\n\n$$\\bar f=0,\\qquad g(t,x)=\\sin t\\quad(0\\le t\\le2\\pi,\\ \\text{extended }2\\pi\\text{-periodically}).$$\n\nThe averaged system is $y'=0$, so $y(t)\\equiv0$ solves it and stays in $\\overline B(0,\\tfrac12)$. With $B=2MP=4\\pi$, $B_1=2LP=0$ and $K=e^{LA}[B+(LB+B_1M)A]=4\\pi$, the smallness hypothesis reads $\\varepsilon\\cdot4\\pi<\\tfrac12$; for $\\varepsilon=\\tfrac1{100}$ it holds because $\\varepsilon K=\\tfrac{\\pi}{25}<\\tfrac12$. The averaging theorem then gives\n\n$$|x(t)-y(t)|=|x(t)|\\le\\frac{\\pi}{25}\\qquad(0\\le t\\le100).$$\n\nThe exact solution is $x(t)=\\tfrac1{100}\\sin t$, whose modulus is at most $\\tfrac1{100}$; the theorem's bound is valid but not sharp here.",
          "strategy": "Evaluate the averaging data and constants, verify the trapping hypotheses, and compare the theorem's bound with the exact solution.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (complete 252-page volume), Chapter 5 Section 5.3, Eqs. (5.52)-(5.57): first-order averaging of a fast periodic term, printed pages 12-14 of Chapter 5 (PDF pages 99-101)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09 Fall 2014, complete course text), Chapter 5 Sections 5.2-5.3, Eqs. (5.25)-(5.26): a perturbation whose one-period average vanishes produces only a periodic change, printed pages 96-97",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
