          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $U=(0,\\infty)\\times(-2,2)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^2$, $F(x,v)=(v,0)$, so that the first-order system $(x,v)'=F(x,v)$ is the free particle with velocity $v$ ([[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]]). The solution of $z'=F(z)$, $z(0)=(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$z(t)=\\bigl(x(t),v(t)\\bigr)=(1-t,\\ -1),\\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,1).$$\n\nIts maximal interval of existence is $(-\\infty,1)$: at $t=1$ the position reaches the excluded boundary value $x=0$, although $\\|F(x,v)\\|=|v|<2$ is bounded on $U$ and the field extends smoothly to the plane. Hence a finite maximal interval does not require the field to blow up; the domain can force it.",
          "strategy": "Verify the explicit solution and show that no extension past t=1 can meet the domain condition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 2.1 and the blow-up examples of Chapter 2 (maximal domains are determined by the open domain of the field, not only by the size of the field), printed pages 17-24",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
