      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Euclidean spatial calculus: gradient, divergence and material derivative",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e13",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "thm-total-derivative-computes-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "thm-gradient-represents-directional-derivatives-and-steepest-ascent",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "def-euclidean-linear-map",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix",
            "def-matrix-space",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-total-derivative-computes-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-gradient-represents-directional-derivatives-and-steepest-ascent": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "We use one-based component labels: for $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $x_i:=x(i-1)$ for $1\\le i\\le3$, and $e_i$ denotes the imported standard unit vector at index $i-1$. Thus $\\partial_i$ here is the imported partial derivative $\\partial_{i-1}$, and an entry labelled $(i,j)$ of a $3\\times3$ matrix is its imported entry $(i-1,j-1)$. All component formulas below use this relabelling; the underlying maps, matrices, inner product and trace are unchanged.\n\nThroughout, $\\mathbb R^3$ carries its standard ordered orthonormal basis $e_1,e_2,e_3$, its Euclidean inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_2$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).\n\nLet $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, let $a\\in O$ and let $v:O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be of class $C^1$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), so that the total derivative $Dv(a):\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is a linear map ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], [[def-euclidean-linear-map]]) whose matrix in the standard bases is $\\bigl(\\partial_jv_i(a)\\bigr)_{1\\le i,j\\le3}$ ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[thm-total-derivative-computes-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). The **divergence** of $v$ at $a$ is\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}v(a):=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(Dv(a)\\bigr)=\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\partial_iv_i(a),$$\n\nthe trace of the total derivative ([[def-trace-of-a-square-matrix]]), read in the standard basis of $M_3(\\mathbb R)$ ([[def-matrix-space]]).\n\nFor a $C^1$ scalar field $f:O\\to\\mathbb R$ and $a\\in O$, the **gradient** $\\nabla f(a)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is the vector of partial derivatives of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]; it is the unique vector with $Df(a)h=\\langle\\nabla f(a),h\\rangle_2$ for every $h\\in\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[thm-gradient-represents-directional-derivatives-and-steepest-ascent]]). We write $\\langle\\nabla f(a),v(a)\\rangle_2=:\\nabla f(a)\\cdot v(a)$.\n\nLet $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval and let $u:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $f:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^1$. For $(t,x)\\in I\\times O$ write $D_x f(t,x):\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ for the total derivative of $y\\mapsto f(t,y)$ at fixed $t$, and $\\nabla_xf(t,x)$ for its gradient. The derivative $Df(t,x)$ of the full map on $I\\times O$ acts on $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$; it is distinct from $D_x f$. Define the partial time derivative $\\partial_tf(t,x):=\\frac{d}{ds}\\big|_{s=t}f(s,x)$ and the **material derivative** of $f$ along $u$\n\n$$D_tf(t,x):=\\partial_tf(t,x)+D_xf(t,x)\\bigl(u(t,x)\\bigr)=\\partial_tf(t,x)+\\nabla_x f(t,x)\\cdot u(t,x).$$\n\nSpatial derivatives $Du$, $Dw$, $\\nabla f$ and $\\operatorname{div}u$ of time-dependent fields always mean $D_xu$, $D_xw$, $\\nabla_x f$ and spatial divergence at fixed time; a stationary field $u(x)$ is included by setting $u(t,x)=u(x)$. The same definitions apply on any open spacetime domain, by restriction to a product neighborhood of each point.\n\nFor a $C^1$ vector field $w:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ the material derivative $D_tw$ is defined componentwise, and $\\operatorname{div}w(t,x)$ means the divergence of $x\\mapsto w(t,x)$ at $x$ at each fixed $t$.",
          "strategy": "Definition: objects, regularity, conventions and domain hypotheses are fixed; no proof obligation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Sections 1.3–1.6 (Eulerian and Lagrangian descriptions of physical quantities, spatial and time derivatives, motion of a body) and Section 3.3 (velocity gradient, stretching and spin tensors)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 8.3 (the continuum velocity field, the material derivative and the continuity equation)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ],
            "precheck": "pass"
          }
        },
