            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\rho_0>0$ and Lam\\'e parameters $\\lambda,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$ with $\\mu>0$ and $\\lambda+\\frac{2\\mu}{3}>0$, let $a,k\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and define the **plane wave**\n\n$$\\xi:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad \\xi(t,x):=a\\cos\\bigl(\\langle k,x\\rangle_2-\\omega t\\bigr),$$\n\nwith the Euclidean inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_2$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). For each fixed $t$, the spatial displacement map is $\\xi_t(x):=\\xi(t,x)$. Define the strain and stress fields using the spatial derivative at fixed time:\n\n$$e(t,x):=\\frac12\\bigl(D_x\\xi(t,x)+(D_x\\xi(t,x))^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr),\\qquad \\sigma(t,x):=\\lambda(\\operatorname{tr}e(t,x))I_3+2\\mu e(t,x).$$\n\nThese are the pointwise constitutive formulas of the isotropic linear elastic model ([[def-cm-linear-elastic-constitutive-law]]); in particular $D_x\\xi$ is the total derivative of $x\\mapsto\\xi_t(x)$, not the derivative of the full spacetime map.\n\nThen the componentwise identity\n\n$$\\rho_0\\,\\partial_t^2\\xi_i=\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\partial_j\\sigma_{ij}\\qquad(i=1,2,3)$$\n\nholds on all of $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$ if and only if\n\n$$\\rho_0\\omega^2a=\\mu|k|_2^2\\,a+(\\lambda+\\mu)\\,\\langle k,a\\rangle_2\\,k . \\tag{D}$$\n\nWhen (D) holds and $a,k$ are nonzero, define the phase speed by $c_{\\rm ph}:=|\\omega|/|k|_2$. If $a=\\alpha k$ for some $\\alpha\\ne0$ (**longitudinal** polarization), then $\\rho_0\\omega^2=(\\lambda+2\\mu)|k|_2^2$ and $c_{\\rm ph}^2=(\\lambda+2\\mu)/\\rho_0$. If $\\langle k,a\\rangle_2=0$ (**transverse** polarization), then $\\rho_0\\omega^2=\\mu|k|_2^2$ and $c_{\\rm ph}^2=\\mu/\\rho_0$. The constitutive positivity hypotheses imply $\\lambda+2\\mu=(\\lambda+2\\mu/3)+4\\mu/3>0$ and $\\mu>0$, so both branch phase speeds are real and positive. (When $k=0$, (D) has no solution with $a\\ne0$ and $\\omega\\ne0$.)",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Section 11.2 (the isotropic linearized elastic field equations)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
