          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-the-integral-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-first-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, and let $u:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a $C^1$ spatial velocity field with the conventions of [[def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus]]. Write $Du(t,x)$ for the total derivative in the spatial variable and $\\operatorname{div}u=\\operatorname{tr}(Du)$ ([[def-trace-of-a-square-matrix]]).\n\n**(a) Incompressible Newtonian fluid.** Model data are a $C^1$ scalar field $p:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R$ (**pressure**) and a real number $\\eta>0$ (**dynamic viscosity**; a dimensionless mathematical parameter until a unit system is fixed). The model consists of the **incompressibility constraint**\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}u(t,x)=0\\qquad\\bigl((t,x)\\in I\\times O\\bigr)$$\n\nand the **Newtonian stress field**\n\n$$\\sigma(t,x):=-p(t,x)I_3+\\eta\\bigl(Du(t,x)+Du(t,x)^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr)\\in M_3(\\mathbb R),$$\n\nwith $Du^{\\mathsf T}$ the transpose and $I_3$ the identity ([[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]], [[def-matrix-space]]). The second term is symmetric, so $\\sigma$ is symmetric for every $(t,x)$.\n\n**(b) Barotropic inviscid fluid.** Model data are a $C^1$ function $p:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ (**pressure law**) and a $C^1$ scalar map $V:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R$. Calling $V$ a **body-force potential** means that its associated vector field is defined by $b(t,x):=-\\nabla_xV(t,x)$; this field is continuous, and in a consuming momentum equation the body-force density is $\\rho b=-\\rho\\nabla_xV$. This datum is separate from the stress law. The **enthalpy** of the pressure law is the $C^1$ function\n\n$$h(\\rho):=\\int_1^\\rho\\frac{p'(s)}{s}\\,ds\\qquad(\\rho>0),$$\n\nwhich is characterised by $h(1)=0$ and $h'(\\rho)=p'(\\rho)/\\rho$ for every $\\rho>0$ ([[def-the-integral-function]], [[thm-ftc-first-part]]). For a $C^1$ positive density field $\\rho:I\\times O\\to(0,\\infty)$ the branch's stress field is\n\n$$\\sigma(t,x):=-p\\bigl(\\rho(t,x)\\bigr)I_3 ,$$\n\nand the branch is **inviscid** (no velocity-gradient term) and **barotropic** (the stress depends on position and time only through the density).",
          "strategy": "Definition: objects, regularity, conventions and domain hypotheses are fixed; no proof obligation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Sections 12.1–12.3 (compressible inviscid fluids; incompressible viscous fluids with the Newtonian example; incompressible inviscid fluids)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Sections 8.4–8.5 (ideal and viscous fluid models)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ],
            "precheck": "pass"
          }
        },
