          "id": "def-cm-cyclic-spring-chain",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The cyclic spring chain, its discrete energy, and the finite-regularity periodic comparison class",
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e14",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-derivative",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-matrix-space",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-metric-ball"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $N\\ge2$ be an integer, let $a>0$ and $c>0$ be real parameters, and let $L:=Na$. Indices are read modulo $N$: $z_{j+N}:=z_j$.\n\n**(a) The chain.** A **state of the chain** is a pair $(q,p)\\in\\mathbb R^N\\times\\mathbb R^N$, where $q=(q_1,\\dots,q_N)$ are the positions and $p=(q_1',\\dots,q_N')$ the velocities of the $N$ nodes. The **cyclic spring-chain system** is the first-order system\n\n$$q_j'=p_j,\\qquad p_j'=\\frac{c^2}{a^2}\\bigl(q_{j+1}-2q_j+q_{j-1}\\bigr)\\qquad(j\\in\\mathbb Z/N),$$\n\nequivalently the second-order system $q_j''=\\frac{c^2}{a^2}(q_{j+1}-2q_j+q_{j-1})$ for a $C^2$ curve $q:I\\to\\mathbb R^N$. Its **discrete energy** is\n\n$$E(q,p):=\\frac12\\sum_{j=1}^N\\Bigl(p_j^2+\\frac{c^2}{a^2}\\bigl(q_{j+1}-q_j\\bigr)^2\\Bigr).$$\n\n**(b) Discrete norms and differences.** For $z,w\\in\\mathbb R^N$ define the **scaled inner product and norm**\n\n$$\\langle z,w\\rangle_a:=a\\sum_{j=1}^N z_jw_j,\\qquad \\|z\\|_a:=\\langle z,z\\rangle_a^{1/2},$$\n\nand for the discrete difference $(D_+z)_j:=(z_{j+1}-z_j)/a$. The **discrete Laplacian** is $(\\Delta_az)_j:=(z_{j+1}-2z_j+z_{j-1})/a^2$.\n\n**(c) The comparison class.** Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval and let $u:I\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be a function that is $C^2$ in the first variable and $C^4$ in the second, is $L$-periodic in $x$ ($u(t,x+L)=u(t,x)$ for all $(t,x)$), and satisfies\n\n$$u_{tt}(t,x)=c^2u_{xx}(t,x)\\qquad\\bigl((t,x)\\in I\\times\\mathbb R\\bigr).$$\n\nFor a compact subinterval $[0,T]\\subseteq I$ put $M_T:=\\sup\\{|\\partial_x^4u(t,x)|:(t,x)\\in[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R\\}$ (finite when continuous derivatives are bounded on the compact time interval, as assumed in the items that use it), and for $a=L/N$ define the **sampled initial data**\n\n$$q_j(0):=u(0,ja),\\qquad q_j'(0):=u_t(0,ja)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots,N).$$",
          "strategy": "Definition: objects, regularity, conventions and domain hypotheses are fixed; no proof obligation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Sections 6.2–6.3 (N coupled oscillators; the uniform 1D particle chain)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014), Section 6.1 (an infinite chain of masses and springs: Lagrangian (6.1), equations of motion (6.3) and the continuum limit)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ],
            "precheck": "pass"
          }
