          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the actual Lebesgue measure foundation used by the bounded Borel weak/entropy class.\n\nWrite $F(u):=\\tfrac12u^2$ and let $\\lambda_2$ be two-dimensional Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R^2$ ([[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]).\n\nEvery space-time expression written as $\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}(\\cdots)\\,dx\\,dt$ below means the single signed Lebesgue integral over $[0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ with respect to $\\lambda_2$; it is not an iterated-integral convention. The initial-data integral is with respect to one-dimensional Lebesgue measure $\\lambda_1$. In (W) and (E), each integrand is Borel measurable, bounded, and supported in the compact support of the test function (or its initial trace). A compact support is contained in a bounded rectangle of finite Lebesgue measure, by [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]]; monotonicity and homogeneity of the nonnegative integral give $\\int |q|\\le\\|q\\|_\\infty\\lambda_d(K)<\\infty$ for such an integrand $q$ supported in $K$ ([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). Thus all displayed signed integrals are defined as Lebesgue integrals of integrable functions ([[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]).\n\nA **test function** is a function $\\varphi:[0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^1$ up to the boundary: it is the restriction of a $C^1$ function on an open neighborhood of this closed half-space, and its support within the half-space is compact. No vanishing or continuation by zero for $t<0$ is required. Thus the trace $\\varphi(0,x)$ may be nonzero. Derivatives at $t=0$ are the continuous one-sided derivatives. Write $C_c^1([0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R)$ for this class; **admissible** means belonging to this class and **nonnegative** means $\\varphi\\ge0$ on the half-space.\n\n**(a) Weak solutions.** Let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be bounded and Borel measurable ([[def-extended-real-valued-measurable-function]]) and let $u:(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be bounded and Borel measurable. Then $u$ is a **weak solution of**\n\n$$u_t+\\bigl(F(u)\\bigr)_x=0 \\qquad\\text{with }u(0,\\cdot)=f \\tag{CL}$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\Bigl[u(t,x)\\varphi_t(t,x)+F\\bigl(u(t,x)\\bigr)\\varphi_x(t,x)\\Bigr]dx\\,dt+\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(x)\\varphi(0,x)\\,dx=0 \\tag{W}$$\n\nfor every test function $\\varphi$ ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]). The integrals exist because $u,f$ are bounded and the test functions have compact support; the identity depends on $u$ and $f$ only through their almost-everywhere classes.\n\n**(b) Entropy pairs.** An **entropy pair** is a pair $(\\eta,q)$ of $C^1$ functions $\\eta,q:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-derivative]]) such that\n\n$$q'(u)=\\eta'(u)\\,F'(u)=u\\,\\eta'(u)\\qquad\\text{for every }u\\in\\mathbb R;$$\n\nthe function $\\eta$ is then called an **entropy** and $q$ its **entropy flux**. The entropy is **convex** when $\\eta((1-a)r+as)\\le(1-a)\\eta(r)+a\\eta(s)$ for all real $r,s$ and $a\\in[0,1]$.\n\n**(c) Entropy solutions.** A weak solution $u$ of (CL) is an **entropy solution** when, for every convex entropy pair $(\\eta,q)$ with $q'=u\\eta'$, the distributional inequality\n\n$$\\bigl(\\eta(u)\\bigr)_t+\\bigl(q(u)\\bigr)_x\\le0$$\n\nholds, by definition meaning that\n\n$$\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\Bigl[\\eta\\bigl(u\\bigr)\\varphi_t+q\\bigl(u\\bigr)\\varphi_x\\Bigr]dx\\,dt+\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\eta\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)\\varphi(0,x)\\,dx\\ \\ge\\ 0 \\tag{E}$$\n\nfor every nonnegative test function $\\varphi$.\n\n**(d) Riemann data.** For reals $u_L,u_R$ the **Riemann initial data** are\n\n$$f(x):=\\begin{cases}u_L,&x<0,\\\\ u_R,&x\\ge0.\\end{cases}$$",
          "strategy": "Definition: objects, regularity, conventions and domain hypotheses are fixed; no proof obligation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. C. Evans, Entropy and Partial Differential Equations (lecture notes), Chapter V (conservation laws: weak solutions, entropy/entropy-flux pairs and the Lax entropy condition)",
