            "thm-additivity-over-subintervals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-trace-of-a-square-matrix": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "The flux integrals below are ordinary Riemann integrals of continuous profiles on $[0,h]$.\n\nLet $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval, let $\\rho>0$, $\\eta>0$ and $h>0$ be constants, and let $O:=\\mathbb R\\times(0,h)\\times\\mathbb R$ with spatial coordinates $(x,y,z)$. Let $v\\in C([0,h])\\cap C^2((0,h))$, with endpoint values given by its continuous extension, and $p_{\\rm sp}\\in C^1(O)$. Define stationary spacetime fields $u:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $p:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R$ by $u(t,x,y,z):=(v(y),0,0)$ and $p(t,x,y,z):=p_{\\rm sp}(x,y,z)$. Here $D_xu$ denotes the spatial total derivative at fixed $t$, $(u\\cdot\\nabla)u:=D_xu\\,u$, and $\\Delta u:=(\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_j^2u_i)_{i=1}^3$ acts componentwise ([[def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus]]). The incompressible Newtonian equations of [[def-cm-fluid-constitutive-models]] with no body force and steady data read\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}u=0,\\qquad \\rho\\,(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\nabla p+\\eta\\,\\Delta u , \\tag{NS}$$\n\nwhere the second equation is the adopted stationary momentum equation with $\\sigma=-pI_3+\\eta(D_xu+(D_xu)^{\\mathsf T})$.\n\nFor this item, “irrotational” means that the spatial velocity gradient $D_xu$ is symmetric.\n\n**(a) Plane Poiseuille flow.** Let $G\\in\\mathbb R$, $p_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and\n\n$$v(y):=\\frac{G\\,y(h-y)}{2\\eta},\\qquad p_{\\rm sp}(x,y,z):=p_0-Gx.$$\n\nThen $u$ is $C^\\infty$ on $I\\times O$, satisfies (NS) there, and $v(0)=v(h)=0$. Its flux per unit width is\n\n$$\\int_0^hv(y)\\,dy=\\frac{Gh^3}{12\\eta}.$$\n\nMoreover, within the ansatz $u=(v(y),0,0)$, $p=p_0-Gx$ with the same constant $G$ and the no-slip conditions $v(0)=v(h)=0$, this is the only solution: any two such profiles differ by a linear function vanishing at both endpoints, hence coincide.\n\n**(b) Plane Couette flow.** Let $V\\in\\mathbb R$, let $v(y):=Vy/h$, and let $p_{\\rm sp}$ be constant. Then the stationary $u,p$ defined above satisfy (NS) on $I\\times O$ and $v(0)=0$, $v(h)=V$. If $V\\ne0$, the velocity gradient $D_xu$ is not symmetric, so the flow is not irrotational by the definition above.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 8.5 (viscous channel flows: Couette flow and the Poiseuille problem, Eqs. (8.56)–(8.62))",
