          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $L>0$. A function $F:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ is **$L$-periodic** when $F(x+L)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb R$, and **odd** when $F(-x)=-F(x)$ for all $x$.\n\n**(a) Sine coefficients.** Let $F:[0,L]\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous ([[def-continuity-real]]); a function on a larger domain is used through its restriction to $[0,L]$, with no periodicity required for the coefficient definition. In particular both $L$-periodic and odd $2L$-periodic functions are admissible. Then $\\int_0^L|F|<\\infty$ ([[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]). For every integer $n\\ge1$ the **$n$-th sine coefficient** of $F$ is\n\n$$b_n(F):=\\frac{2}{L}\\int_0^LF(x)\\sin\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\Bigr)dx \\qquad \\in\\mathbb R ,$$\n\nan ordinary Riemann integral of a continuous function on a compact interval ([[def-oriented-integral]], [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]). The **sine series** of $F$ is the series of functions\n\n$$\\sum_{n\\ge1}b_n(F)\\sin\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\Bigr),$$\n\nwhose $N$-th **partial sum** is $S_N^{\\rm s}F(x):=\\sum_{n\\le N}b_n(F)\\sin(n\\pi x/L)$.\n\n**(b) Odd periodic extension.** Let $F:[0,L]\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous with $F(0)=F(L)=0$. The **odd $2L$-periodic extension** of $F$ is the function on $\\mathbb R$ defined by\n\n$$F^{\\rm odd}(x):=\\begin{cases}F(x),&0\\le x\\le L,\\\\ -F(-x),&-L\\le x\\le 0,\\end{cases}\\qquad F^{\\rm odd}(x+2L):=F^{\\rm odd}(x).$$\n\nEquivalently, $F^{\\rm odd}$ is the unique $2L$-periodic odd function whose restriction to $[0,L]$ is $F$ (the two definitions agree at $0$ and $L$ because both values vanish; the extension is well defined and continuous).\n\n(c) Given a specified wave speed $c>0$, the **angular frequency** of mode $n$ is $\\omega_n:=cn\\pi/L$; its fundamental ($n=1$) value is $c\\pi/L$.",
          "strategy": "Definition: objects, regularity, conventions and domain hypotheses are fixed; no proof obligation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Laugesen, Harmonic Analysis Lecture Notes (arXiv:0903.3845), Chapter 1 (Fourier coefficients of periodic functions) and Theorem 5.4(c) (the orthonormal trigonometric basis of L²(T))",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0903.3845"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 6.5 (standing waves of the fixed-end string: the sinusoidal modes)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ],
            "precheck": "pass"
          }
