          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $h:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be one-periodic, meaning $h(t+1)=h(t)$ for all real $t$, and continuous, meaning that both real functions $\\operatorname{Re}h,\\operatorname{Im}h:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy [[def-continuity-real]] and suppose that\n\n$$\\int_0^1h(t)\\,e_k(-t)\\,dt=0\\qquad\\text{for every }k\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\n\nAll integrals here are componentwise Riemann integrals: for continuous $p=p_1+ip_2$, $\\int_a^b p:=\\int_a^b p_1+i\\int_a^b p_2$, with the real oriented convention of [[def-oriented-integral]]; both integrals exist by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]. Here $e_k(t):=e^{2\\pi ikt}$ ([[def-complex-exponential]]). Then $h=0$ on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
