          "id": "lem-cm-matrix-curve-derivative-rules",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Derivative rules for differentiable matrix-valued curves, and skewness of $R^{\\mathsf T}R'$ for a curve in $SO(3)$",
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points, let $t_0\\in I$ and let $A,B:I\\to M_3(\\mathbb R)$ be **differentiable at $t_0$**, meaning ([[def-matrix-space]]) that every entry function $t\\mapsto A(t)_{ij}$ and $t\\mapsto B(t)_{ij}$ is differentiable at $t_0$ in the sense of [[def-derivative]]; write $A'(t_0)$ for the matrix with entries $A'(t_0)_{ij}:=(t\\mapsto A(t)_{ij})'(t_0)$, and similarly for $B$.\n\n1. $A+B$ and $A^{\\mathsf T}$ are differentiable at $t_0$, with $(A+B)'(t_0)=A'(t_0)+B'(t_0)$ and $(A^{\\mathsf T})'(t_0)=(A'(t_0))^{\\mathsf T}$.\n2. $AB$ is differentiable at $t_0$, with $(AB)'(t_0)=A'(t_0)B(t_0)+A(t_0)B'(t_0)$.\n3. If $A(t)\\in SO(3)$ for every $t\\in I$ ([[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]), then the matrix $A(t_0)^{\\mathsf T}A'(t_0)$ is skew-symmetric, i.e. $(A^{\\mathsf T}A')^{\\mathsf T}=-A^{\\mathsf T}A'$ at $t_0$, and likewise $A'(t_0)A(t_0)^{\\mathsf T}$ is skew-symmetric. In particular the trace of each of these two matrices vanishes.",
          "deps": [
            "def-derivative",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "def-matrix-space",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix",
            "prop-transpose-laws",
