        {
          "id": "lem-cm-skew-matrix-cross-product-correspondence",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The skew-matrix/cross-product correspondence $[\\omega]_\\times z=\\omega\\times z$ and its conjugation law $R[\\omega]_\\times R^{\\mathsf T}=[R\\omega]_\\times$",
          "statement": "Use the standard basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $\\mathbb R^3$ and coordinates $\\omega=\\omega_1e_1+\\omega_2e_2+\\omega_3e_3$ ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]], [[thm-unique-coordinates-with-respect-to-an-ordered-basis]]). Define the map $[\\cdot]_\\times:\\mathbb R^3\\to M_3(\\mathbb R)$ by\n$$[\\omega]_\\times:=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-\\omega_3&\\omega_2\\\\ \\omega_3&0&-\\omega_1\\\\ -\\omega_2&\\omega_1&0\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nThen:\n\n1. $[\\omega]_\\times z=\\omega\\times z$ for all $\\omega,z\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the cross product being that of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]].\n2. $[\\omega]_\\times^{\\mathsf T}=-[\\omega]_\\times$ for every $\\omega$; the map $\\omega\\mapsto[\\omega]_\\times$ is linear and injective, and its image is exactly the set of skew-symmetric matrices of $M_3(\\mathbb R)$. Consequently every skew-symmetric $\\Omega\\in M_3(\\mathbb R)$ equals $[\\omega]_\\times$ for exactly one $\\omega$, namely $\\omega=(\\Omega_{32},\\Omega_{13},\\Omega_{21})$.\n3. For every $R\\in SO(3)$ ([[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]) and every $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $$R[\\omega]_\\times R^{\\mathsf T}=[R\\omega]_\\times.$$",
          "deps": [
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal",
