        {
          "id": "def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Mass distributions on $\\mathbb R^3$: total mass, first moment and inertia matrix",
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^3)$ be the Borel sigma-algebra of $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]) and let $\\nu$ be a measure on the measurable space $(\\mathbb R^3,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^3))$ ([[def-measure]], [[def-measure-space]]) that is **finite**, $\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)<+\\infty$, and **bounded**, meaning that there is a real $R>0$ with\n$$\\nu\\bigl(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\overline B_R(0)\\bigr)=0,$$\nthe closed ball being that of [[def-metric-ball]]. Throughout, integrals are Lebesgue integrals against $\\nu$ in the sense of [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]] and [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]], and for a measurable vector- or matrix-valued function the integral is defined **componentwise**: $\\int F\\,d\\nu:=\\bigl(\\int F_1\\,d\\nu,\\int F_2\\,d\\nu,\\int F_3\\,d\\nu\\bigr)$ and $\\bigl(\\int M\\,d\\nu\\bigr)_{jk}:=\\int M_{jk}\\,d\\nu$, whenever every displayed scalar integral is finite.\n\nA **mass distribution on $\\mathbb R^3$** is a bounded finite Borel measure $\\nu$ with $m:=\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)\\in(0,+\\infty)$; the number $m$ is its **total mass**. Writing points of $\\mathbb R^3$ as $\\rho$ and using the Euclidean inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ and norm $\\lVert\\rho\\rVert_2=\\sqrt{\\langle\\rho,\\rho\\rangle}$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], define:\n\n1. the **first moment** $\\mu(\\nu):=\\int\\rho\\,d\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^3$, read componentwise as $\\mu(\\nu)_j=\\int\\rho_j\\,d\\nu$;\n2. the **inertia matrix** $I(\\nu)\\in M_3(\\mathbb R)$ ([[def-matrix-space]]) by its entries $I(\\nu)_{jk}:=\\int\\bigl(\\lVert\\rho\\rVert_2^2\\,\\delta_{jk}-\\rho_j\\rho_k\\bigr)\\,d\\nu$, where $\\delta_{jk}=1$ if $j=k$ and $\\delta_{jk}=0$ otherwise; equivalently $I(\\nu)=\\int\\bigl(\\lVert\\rho\\rVert_2^2 I_3-\\rho\\rho^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr)\\,d\\nu$ with $I_3$ the identity matrix and $\\rho\\rho^{\\mathsf T}$ the matrix product of [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]].\n\nThe mass distribution is **centred** when $\\mu(\\nu)=0$. Under the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), each of the integrals above is a finite real number: on $\\overline B_R(0)$ one has $|\\rho_j|\\le R$ and $|\\rho_j\\rho_k|\\le R^2$, and $\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)<+\\infty$; the precise statement and proof are [[lem-cm-mass-moments-are-finite]], which depends on this definition only for its notation. The assumption is used there exactly once, to obtain the Borel measurability of the polynomial integrands from the published continuity criterion; every later item that invokes this finiteness carries the assumption explicitly.",
          "deps": [
            "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
            "def-measure",
            "def-measure-space",
            "def-metric-ball",
            "def-metric-topology",
            "lem-metrics-on-rn",
