        {
          "id": "lem-cm-mass-moments-are-finite",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finiteness of the moments of a bounded finite Borel measure on $\\mathbb R^3$",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\nu$ be a measure on $(\\mathbb R^3,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^3))$ ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]) that is finite and bounded: $\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)<+\\infty$ and $\\nu(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\overline B_R(0))=0$ for some $R>0$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). Then:\n\n1. for all $j,k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ the functions $\\rho\\mapsto\\rho_j$ and $\\rho\\mapsto\\rho_j\\rho_k$ are Borel measurable and $\\nu$-integrable, with $\\int\\lvert\\rho_j\\rvert\\,d\\nu\\le R\\,\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)$ and $\\int\\lvert\\rho_j\\rho_k\\rvert\\,d\\nu\\le R^2\\,\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)$;\n2. consequently the total mass $m=\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)$, the first moment $\\mu(\\nu)$ and every entry of the inertia matrix $I(\\nu)$ of the definition are well-defined finite real numbers, and $\\lvert\\mu(\\nu)_j\\rvert\\le R\\,\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)$ while $\\lvert I(\\nu)_{jk}\\rvert\\le 2R^2\\,\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix",
            "def-countable-choice",
