        {
          "id": "def-cm-rigid-placement-and-body-angular-velocity",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Rigid placements, body and spatial angular velocity, and material velocities",
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), let $\\nu$ be a mass distribution on $\\mathbb R^3$ with total mass $m$ ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]), let $c:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be differentiable and let $R:I\\to SO(3)$ be differentiable ([[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]), differentiability meaning entrywise differentiability of $R$ in the sense of [[def-derivative]] with derivative $R'$; write $c'$ for the derivative of $c$.\n\nThe **rigid placement with reference shape $\\nu$, translation $c$ and rotation $R$** is the map\n$$x:I\\times\\mathbb R^3\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad x(t,\\rho):=c(t)+R(t)\\rho,$$\nso that the material point labelled by $\\rho$ (a point of the reference shape) is carried to $x(t,\\rho)$ at time $t$; the map $x(t,\\cdot)$ is the **placement at time $t$**, and the pair $(c(t),R(t))$ is its **configuration**. The **material velocity** of the label $\\rho$ at time $t$ is the derivative\n$$\\partial_tx(t,\\rho):=c'(t)+R'(t)\\rho,$$\nwhich exists by differentiability of $c$ and of the entries of $R$.\n\n**Body and spatial angular velocity.** By [[lem-cm-matrix-curve-derivative-rules]] the matrix $R(t)^{\\mathsf T}R'(t)$ is skew-symmetric at every $t\\in I$. By [[lem-cm-skew-matrix-cross-product-correspondence]] there is therefore a unique vector $\\omega(t)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with\n$$R(t)^{\\mathsf T}R'(t)=[\\omega(t)]_\\times,$$\nand the map $\\omega:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is the **body angular velocity** of the placement; the **spatial angular velocity** is $\\omega_{\\mathrm{s}}(t):=R(t)\\omega(t)$. Explicitly $\\omega(t)=\\bigl((R^{\\mathsf T}R')_{32},(R^{\\mathsf T}R')_{13},(R^{\\mathsf T}R')_{21}\\bigr)(t)$, a finite sum of products of entries of $R$ and $R'$; consequently, if $R$ is twice differentiable, then $\\omega$ is differentiable and its derivative is the **body angular acceleration**.\n\nEquivalently, by the conjugation clause of [[lem-cm-skew-matrix-cross-product-correspondence]], $R'(t)R(t)^{\\mathsf T}=[\\omega_{\\mathrm{s}}(t)]_\\times$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix",
            "lem-cm-matrix-curve-derivative-rules",
            "lem-cm-skew-matrix-cross-product-correspondence",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "def-derivative",
