        {
          "id": "lem-cm-inertia-matrix-properties",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Symmetry, the quadratic identity $\\omega^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega=\\int\\lVert\\omega\\times\\rho\\rVert_2^2\\,d\\nu$, and the positive-definiteness criterion for the inertia matrix",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), used only in [L1], through [[lem-cm-mass-moments-are-finite]], to make the entries of $I$ finite real numbers. Let $\\nu$ be a mass distribution on $\\mathbb R^3$ with total mass $m=\\nu(\\mathbb R^3)$ and inertia matrix $I=I(\\nu)$ ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]). Write $\\omega^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega:=\\sum_{j,k=1}^{3}\\omega_jI_{jk}\\omega_k$ for the quadratic form of $I$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ for the Euclidean norm ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then:\n\n1. $I$ is symmetric: $I_{jk}=I_{kj}$ for all $j,k$.\n2. For every $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $$\\omega^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega=\\int\\lVert\\omega\\times\\rho\\rVert_2^2\\,d\\nu(\\rho)\\ \\ge 0,$$ the cross product being that of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]].\n3. The symmetric bilinear form $(u,v)\\mapsto u^{\\mathsf T}Iv$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ is therefore positive semidefinite ([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]]); for each $\\omega\\ne0$, writing $\\mathbb R\\omega=\\{t\\omega:t\\in\\mathbb R\\}$, one has $\\omega^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega=0$ if and only if $\\nu(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\mathbb R\\omega)=0$. Thus it is positive definite if and only if $\\nu(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\mathbb R\\omega)>0$ for every $\\omega\\ne0$, that is, no line through the origin carries the entire mass ([[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]]).\n4. If the form is positive definite, then $I$ is invertible.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix",
            "lem-cm-mass-moments-are-finite",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
