        {
          "id": "thm-cm-rigid-kinetic-energy-and-angular-momentum",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Kinetic energy and angular momentum of a rigid placement: $K=\\tfrac12m\\lVert c'\\rVert_2^2+\\tfrac12\\omega^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega$ and $L_c=RI\\omega$",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), used only through the bound of [[lem-cm-mass-moments-are-finite]] that makes the two integrands of clause 1 $\\nu$-integrable. Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points, let $\\nu$ be a **centred** mass distribution on $\\mathbb R^3$ with total mass $m$ and inertia matrix $I$ ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]), let $c:J\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $R:J\\to SO(3)$ be differentiable, and let $x(t,\\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\\rho$ be the rigid placement with body angular velocity $\\omega$ ([[def-cm-rigid-placement-and-body-angular-velocity]]). For $t\\in J$ define\n$$K(t):=\\frac12\\int\\lVert\\partial_tx(t,\\rho)\\rVert_2^2\\,d\\nu(\\rho),\\qquad L_c(t):=\\int\\bigl(x(t,\\rho)-c(t)\\bigr)\\times\\bigl(\\partial_tx(t,\\rho)-c'(t)\\bigr)\\,d\\nu(\\rho),$$\nthe second integral read componentwise ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]). Then for every $t\\in J$:\n\n1. $\\partial_tx(t,\\rho)=c'(t)+R(t)\\bigl(\\omega(t)\\times\\rho\\bigr)$ for every $\\rho\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and $R'(t)=R(t)[\\omega(t)]_\\times=[R(t)\\omega(t)]_\\times R(t)$; moreover the two integrands displayed above are $\\nu$-integrable, so $K(t)$ and $L_c(t)$ are a well-defined finite real number and vector;\n2. $K(t)=\\tfrac12m\\lVert c'(t)\\rVert_2^2+\\tfrac12\\omega(t)^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega(t)$;\n3. $L_c(t)=R(t)I\\omega(t)$.",
          "deps": [
