        {
          "id": "thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Euler's equations $I\\omega'+\\omega\\times I\\omega=R^{\\mathsf T}\\tau$ under an adopted torque balance, and the torque-free invariants and principal-axis equations",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), used through the inertia suppliers to ensure finiteness of the integrals. Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two points ([[def-interval]]), let $\\nu$ be a centred mass distribution on $\\mathbb R^3$ that is not supported on any line through the origin, with inertia matrix $I$ ([[def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix]]), let $c:J\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be differentiable and let $R:J\\to SO(3)$ be twice differentiable, and let $x(t,\\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\\rho$ be the rigid placement with body angular velocity $\\omega$ ([[def-cm-rigid-placement-and-body-angular-velocity]]). Write $l:=I\\omega:J\\to\\mathbb R^3$; by [[cor-cm-principal-axes-of-the-inertia-matrix]] the inertia form is positive definite and $I$ is invertible.\n\nAdopt the **balance hypothesis** (B): there is a map $\\tau:J\\to\\mathbb R^3$ such that the curve $t\\mapsto R(t)l(t)$ is differentiable and\n$$(Rl)'(t)=\\tau(t)\\qquad(t\\in J).$$\nThen:\n\n1. $l$ is differentiable and **Euler's equations with torque** hold: $$I\\omega'+\\omega\\times I\\omega=R^{\\mathsf T}\\tau\\qquad\\text{on }J;$$\n2. if $\\tau=0$ on $J$, then $l$ satisfies the **torque-free Euler equations** $l'=l\\times(I^{-1}l)$, and the functions $t\\mapsto\\langle l(t),l(t)\\rangle$ and $t\\mapsto\\tfrac12\\langle l(t),I^{-1}l(t)\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\omega(t)^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega(t)$ are constant on $J$;\n3. if $\\tau=0$ on $J$ and $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is a positively oriented orthonormal eigenbasis of $I$ with $Ie_j=I_je_j$ and $l=\\sum_jl_je_j$, then $\\langle l,l\\rangle=\\sum_jl_j^2$ and $\\tfrac12\\langle l,I^{-1}l\\rangle=\\sum_jl_j^2/(2I_j)$ are constant on $J$, and the torque-free equations read $$l_1'=\\Bigl(\\frac1{I_3}-\\frac1{I_2}\\Bigr)l_2l_3,\\qquad l_2'=\\Bigl(\\frac1{I_1}-\\frac1{I_3}\\Bigr)l_1l_3,\\qquad l_3'=\\Bigl(\\frac1{I_2}-\\frac1{I_1}\\Bigr)l_1l_2.$$",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-cm-rigid-placement-and-body-angular-velocity",
            "def-cm-mass-distribution-and-inertia-matrix",
            "lem-cm-inertia-matrix-properties",
