        {
          "id": "thm-cm-stability-of-extreme-principal-axes",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Lyapunov stability of spin about the largest and smallest principal axes of the torque-free top",
          "statement": "Let $I_1<I_2<I_3$ be positive reals and let $I:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be the linear map with $Ie_j=I_je_j$ on the standard orthonormal basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $\\mathbb R^3$. Consider the autonomous first-order system\n$$l'(t)=F(l(t)),\\qquad F(l):=l\\times(I^{-1}l),$$\non $U:=\\mathbb R^3$, read as in [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]] with the cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] and $I^{-1}$ the inverse of $I$ ([[cor-cm-principal-axes-of-the-inertia-matrix]]); this is the torque-free Euler system of [[thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants]] in angular-momentum variables. Let $L\\ne0$. Then:\n\n$Le_1$ and $Le_3$ are equilibria of $F$ and each is Lyapunov stable in the sense of [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]]: for $j=1$ and $j=3$ and for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $\\delta\\in(0,\\varepsilon]$ such that every maximal solution $l$ with $\\lVert l(0)-Le_j\\rVert_2<\\delta$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ and satisfies $\\lVert l(t)-Le_j\\rVert_2<\\varepsilon$ for every $t\\ge0$.",
