        {
          "id": "thm-cm-linearized-torque-free-euler-equations",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Linearization of the torque-free Euler system at a principal-axis spin, and the oscillatory/exponential dichotomy",
          "statement": "Let $I_1,I_2,I_3>0$ be pairwise distinct, let $I:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be the linear map with $Ie_j=I_je_j$ on the standard orthonormal basis, and let $I^{-1}$ denote its inverse, given by $I^{-1}e_j=I_j^{-1}e_j$. Define the formal torque-free Euler vector field\n$$F(\\omega):=-I^{-1}\\bigl(\\omega\\times I\\omega\\bigr),\\qquad \\omega\\in\\mathbb R^3.$$\nWhen $I$ is an actual rigid-body inertia map written in a principal basis, this is the angular-velocity vector field obtained from [[thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants]]. Here the positive diagonal entries are otherwise arbitrary; no realization by a mass distribution is assumed. Let $\\Omega\\ne0$. Then:\n\n1. $F(\\Omega e_3)=0$, and for every $\\delta\\in\\mathbb R^3$ $$F(\\Omega e_3+\\delta)=A\\delta+R(\\delta),\\qquad (A\\delta)_1=\\frac{\\Omega(I_2-I_3)}{I_1}\\delta_2,\\quad (A\\delta)_2=\\frac{\\Omega(I_3-I_1)}{I_2}\\delta_1,\\quad (A\\delta)_3=0,$$ where $\\lVert R(\\delta)\\rVert_2\\le C\\lVert\\delta\\rVert_2^2$ for the explicit constant $C:=\\lVert I^{-1}\\rVert_F\\lVert I\\rVert_F$ and $\\lVert M\\rVert_F:=\\bigl(\\sum_{j,k}M_{jk}^2\\bigr)^{1/2}$; consequently $A$ is the total derivative of $F$ at $\\Omega e_3$ ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]]).\n2. Every solution of the linear system $\\delta'=A\\delta$ on $\\mathbb R$ has $\\delta_3$ constant, and with $$\\lambda:=\\frac{\\Omega^2(I_2-I_3)(I_3-I_1)}{I_1I_2}\\ne0$$ the pair $(\\delta_1,\\delta_2)$ is determined by its initial value as follows:\n   - if $\\lambda<0$, then with $\\kappa:=\\sqrt{-\\lambda}>0$, $$\\delta_1(t)=\\delta_1(0)\\cos(\\kappa t)+\\frac{\\Omega(I_2-I_3)}{I_1\\kappa}\\delta_2(0)\\sin(\\kappa t),\\qquad \\delta_2(t)=\\delta_2(0)\\cos(\\kappa t)+\\frac{\\Omega(I_3-I_1)}{I_2\\kappa}\\delta_1(0)\\sin(\\kappa t),$$ and every solution is bounded on $[0,\\infty)$;\n   - if $\\lambda>0$, then the same two formulas hold with $\\cosh$ and $\\sinh$ in place of $\\cos$ and $\\sin$ and $\\kappa:=\\sqrt\\lambda$, the system has unbounded solutions (for instance $\\delta_2(0)=0$, $\\delta_1(0)\\ne0$), and if $\\delta_1(0)+\\frac{\\Omega(I_2-I_3)}{I_1\\kappa}\\delta_2(0)\\ne0$ then $\\lVert\\delta(t)\\rVert_2\\to+\\infty$ as $t\\to+\\infty$.\n3. Hence the linearization at the spin $\\Omega e_3$ is oscillatory exactly when $(I_2-I_3)(I_3-I_1)<0$, i.e. when the third principal moment is a largest or a smallest moment, and it has exponentially growing solutions exactly when $\\lambda>0$, i.e. when $I_3$ lies strictly between $I_1$ and $I_2$; in the latter case the zero solution of the linear system is not stable.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability",
            "thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants",
