      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-cm-roundoff-accumulation-in-one-step-schemes",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Additive per-step errors in a Lipschitzian one-step scheme accumulate geometrically: $\\eta((1+Lh)^n-1)/(Lh)$",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-metric-space",
            "def-lipschitz-holder-contraction",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
            "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
            "thm-ftc-second-part"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-metric-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lipschitz-holder-contraction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "E. Hairer, Geometric Numerical Integration (TU Munchen 2010), Lecture 3 'Backward error analysis', Section 3: accumulation of per-step perturbations with a Lipschitz constant, the summation argument for long-time energy estimates, printed pages 6-7",
                "url": "https://www.unige.ch/~hairer/poly_geoint/week3.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "A. Quillen, PHY411 Lecture notes Part 7 'Integrators' (University of Rochester), Section 1.1: accumulation of per-step errors versus local truncation errors for one-step methods, printed pages 2-5",
                "url": "https://astro.pas.rochester.edu/~aquillen/phy411/lecture7.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e15",
          "design_state": "Fully authored draft; outside artifact completion cycle 1; independent native review remains pending",
          "statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), let $V:X\\to X$ map $X$ into itself, and let $L\\ge0$ and $h>0$. Assume\n\n$$d\\bigl(V(x),V(y)\\bigr)\\le(1+Lh)\\,d(x,y)\\qquad\\text{for all }x,y\\in X,$$\n\nwhich is a Lipschitz hypothesis on $V$ with constant $1+Lh$ ([[def-lipschitz-holder-contraction]]). Fix $z_0\\in X$, put $z_{j+1}:=V(z_j)$ for $j\\in\\mathbb N$, and let $x_0:=z_0$ together with points $x_{j+1}\\in X$ satisfying\n\n$$d\\bigl(x_{j+1},V(x_j)\\bigr)\\le\\eta\\qquad(j\\in\\mathbb N)$$\n\nfor a number $\\eta\\ge0$ (\"computational error per step\"). Then for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$:\n\n1. if $L>0$, $\\displaystyle d(x_n,z_n)\\le\\eta\\,\\frac{(1+Lh)^n-1}{Lh}$, and if $L=0$, $d(x_n,z_n)\\le n\\eta$;\n2. if moreover $F:X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfies the Lipschitz bound $|F(x)-F(y)|\\le\\Lambda\\,d(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in X$ and some $\\Lambda<\\infty$, then\n $$|F(x_n)-F(z_n)|\\le\\Lambda\\eta\\,\\frac{(1+Lh)^n-1}{Lh}\\quad(L>0),\\qquad |F(x_n)-F(z_n)|\\le\\Lambda n\\eta\\quad(L=0);$$\n3. if $nh\\le T$ for some $T>0$ and $L>0$, then $(1+Lh)^n\\le e^{LT}$ and therefore\n $$d(x_n,z_n)\\le\\eta\\,\\frac{e^{LT}-1}{Lh}\\le \\eta\\,e^{LT}\\frac{T}{h}.$$",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-complex-phase-domains-and-holomorphic-maps",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Complex phase domains, holomorphic maps on $\\mathbb C^m$, and the complexified canonical form and bracket",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form",
            "def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-euclidean-linear-map",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-metric-ball",
            "def-metric-topology",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Lebl, Tasty Bits of Several Complex Variables, Chapter 1, Section 1.1 (holomorphic functions in several variables) and Section 1.2 (polydiscs and power series), printed pages 13-27",
                "url": "https://www.jirka.org/scv/scv.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e15",
          "design_state": "Fully authored draft; outside artifact completion cycle 1; independent native review remains pending",
          "statement": "**Holomorphic maps.** Let $m,p\\ge1$ and let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb C^m$ be open. Write each coordinate $z_k=x_k+iy_k$ and identify $D$ with the corresponding open subset of $\\mathbb R^{2m}$ by $(z_1,\\dots,z_m)\\mapsto(x_1,y_1,\\dots,x_m,y_m)$. A map $F:D\\to\\mathbb C^p$ is **complex differentiable at** $a\\in D$ when there is a map $L:\\mathbb C^m\\to\\mathbb C^p$ that is $\\mathbb C$-linear — $L(u+v)=L(u)+L(v)$ and $L(\\lambda u)=\\lambda L(u)$ for $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C$ ([[def-linear-map]], [[def-euclidean-linear-map]], [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]) — with\n\n$$F(a+v)=F(a)+L(v)+r(v),\\qquad \\frac{\\lVert r(v)\\rVert}{\\lVert v\\rVert}\\longrightarrow0\\ \\ (v\\to0),$$\n\nthe norm being the Euclidean norm of $\\mathbb R^{2m}$ and $\\mathbb R^{2p}$ ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]]). Such an $L$ is unique by the argument of [[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]], applied componentwise; it is denoted $DF(a)$. The map $F$ is **holomorphic on** $D$ when it is complex differentiable at every point of $D$; for $m=p=1$ this is the notion of [[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]]. For holomorphic $F$ and $k\\le m$ put $\\partial_{z_k}F(a):=DF(a)(e_k)$, the $k$-th coordinate derivative; for $p=1$ this is the partial derivative of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] in the complex coordinate direction.\n\n**Real analytic functions.** Let $N\\ge1$ and let $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^N$ be open. A function $\\varphi:V\\to\\mathbb R$ is **real analytic at** $x\\in V$ when there are an open set $W\\subseteq\\mathbb C^N$ with $x\\in W$ and a holomorphic $F:W\\to\\mathbb C$ with $F(\\xi)=\\varphi(\\xi)$ for every $\\xi\\in W\\cap V$; $\\varphi$ is **real analytic on** $V$ when it is real analytic at every point of $V$. Thus real analyticity is defined here exactly as *admitting a local holomorphic extension*.\n\n**Polydiscs.** For $a\\in\\mathbb C^m$ and a polyradius $\\rho=(\\rho_1,\\dots,\\rho_m)$ with $0<\\rho_k\\le\\infty$ put\n\n$$\\Delta_\\rho(a):=\\{\\,z\\in\\mathbb C^m:\\ |z_k-a_k|<\\rho_k\\ \\text{for all }k\\,\\},$$\n\nthe open **polydisc**, which is open and convex ([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-metric-topology]]). If every $\\rho_k$ is finite, its **distinguished boundary** is\n\n$$\\Gamma_\\rho(a):=\\{\\,z\\in\\mathbb C^m:\\ |z_k-a_k|=\\rho_k\\ \\text{for all }k\\,\\}.$$\n\nWhen any radius is infinite, no distinguished boundary is defined.\n\n**Complex phase domains.** Let $d\\ge1$. A **complex phase domain** is a nonempty open $D\\subseteq\\mathbb C^{2d}$ whose points are written $Z=(z,w)$ with $z=(z^1,\\dots,z^d)$, $w=(w_1,\\dots,w_d)\\in\\mathbb C^d$. In these coordinates, $\\partial_{z^i}$ and $\\partial_{w_i}$ denote the coordinate derivatives defined above for the corresponding entries of $Z$. On $D$ define the **complexified canonical two-form** and the **complexified Poisson bracket** of holomorphic functions $f,g:D\\to\\mathbb C$ by\n\n$$\\omega_{\\mathbb C}:=\\sum_{i=1}^d dz^i\\wedge dw_i,\\qquad \\{f,g\\}:=\\sum_{i=1}^d\\bigl(\\partial_{z^i}f\\,\\partial_{w_i}g-\\partial_{w_i}f\\,\\partial_{z^i}g\\bigr),$$\n\nand for a holomorphic $f:D\\to\\mathbb C$ its **complex Hamiltonian field** $X_f:=J\\nabla f$, where the same entries of $J=\\begin{pmatrix}0_d&I_d\\\\-I_d&0_d\\end{pmatrix}$ from [[def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form]] are regarded as a matrix in $M_{2d}(\\mathbb C)$, and $\\nabla f=(\\partial_{z^1}f,\\dots,\\partial_{z^d}f,\\partial_{w_1}f,\\dots,\\partial_{w_d}f)$ is the gradient column of coordinate derivatives. If $D_{\\mathbb R}:=D\\cap\\mathbb R^{2d}$ is nonempty, it is open in $\\mathbb R^{2d}$, and the restrictions of $\\omega_{\\mathbb C}$ and $J$ to $D_{\\mathbb R}$ are exactly the standard two-form and matrix of [[def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form]].\n\n**Distance domains.** For a nonempty $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2d}$ and $r>0$ put\n\n$$D(r):=\\{\\,\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C^{2d}:\\ \\operatorname{dist}(\\zeta,\\overline U)<r\\,\\},\\qquad \\operatorname{dist}(\\zeta,\\overline U):=\\inf\\{\\lVert\\zeta-x\\rVert:x\\in\\overline U\\},$$\n\nwhere $\\overline U$ is the closure of $U$ in $\\mathbb R^{2d}$ and the norm is the Euclidean norm of $\\mathbb C^{2d}\\cong\\mathbb R^{4d}$. Each $D(r)$ is open, contains $U$, and $D(r)\\subseteq D(r')$ for $r\\le r'$; if $U$ is star-shaped with respect to $0$ ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]), then every $D(r)$ is star-shaped with respect to $0$ as well.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm-splitting-hamiltonian-and-verlet-map",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The splitting Hamiltonian $H=A+B$, the drift and kick maps, and the velocity-Verlet map",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "E. Hairer, Geometric Numerical Integration (TU Munchen 2010), Lecture 2 'Symplectic integrators', Sections 1 and 5: the Stormer-Verlet scheme and its interpretation as the Strang splitting of the kinetic and potential flows, printed pages 1-3 and 12-13",
                "url": "https://www.unige.ch/~hairer/poly_geoint/week2.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "A. Quillen, PHY411 Lecture notes Part 7 'Integrators' (University of Rochester), Sections 2.3 and 2.5: drift and kick evolution operators and the Stormer-Verlet method, printed pages 18-20 and 23-24",
                "url": "https://astro.pas.rochester.edu/~aquillen/phy411/lecture7.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e15",
          "design_state": "Fully authored draft; outside artifact completion cycle 1; independent native review remains pending",
          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2d}$ be a nonempty open phase domain with coordinates $z=(q,p)$, $q=(q^1,\\dots,q^d)$ and $p=(p_1,\\dots,p_d)$, standard two-form $\\omega_U=\\sum_i dq^i\\wedge dp_i$ and standard symplectic matrix $J$ ([[def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form]]).\n\nFix $M\\in M_d(\\mathbb R)$ and $M^{-1}\\in M_d(\\mathbb R)$ with $MM^{-1}=M^{-1}M=I_d$. Assume $M$ is **symmetric and positive definite**, meaning $M^{\\mathsf T}=M$ ([[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]]) and $v^{\\mathsf T}Mv>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$; here $v^{\\mathsf T}Mv$ is the matrix product of [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]. Transposing the two inverse identities and using uniqueness of a two-sided inverse gives $(M^{-1})^{\\mathsf T}=(M^{\\mathsf T})^{-1}=M^{-1}$. Fix an open $\\Pi\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ with $U\\subseteq\\Pi\\times\\mathbb R^d$ and a function $U_{\\mathrm{pot}}:\\Pi\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^3$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), whose gradient $\\nabla U_{\\mathrm{pot}}$ and Hessian matrix $H_{U_{\\mathrm{pot}}}$ are those of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]] and [[def-hessian-and-euclidean-critical-point]]; write $\\nabla U_{\\mathrm{pot}}(q)$ as a column vector and abbreviate $U_{\\mathrm{pot}}(q)$ by $V(q)$ on $U$.\n\nFor any $C^1$ scalar function $G:U\\to\\mathbb R$, use the coordinate convention $X_G:=J\\nabla G=(\\partial_{p_i}G,-\\partial_{q^i}G)_{i=1}^d$ for its **Hamiltonian field**. With $\\omega_U=\\sum_i dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, contraction is $\\iota_{X_G}\\omega_U:=\\sum_i((X_G)_{q^i}\\,dp_i-(X_G)_{p_i}\\,dq^i)=\\sum_i(\\partial_{p_i}G\\,dp_i+\\partial_{q^i}G\\,dq^i)=dG$.\n\nOn $U$ define the **kinetic part** and **potential part**\n\n$$A(q,p):=\\tfrac12\\,p^{\\mathsf T}M^{-1}p,\\qquad B(q,p):=V(q),\\qquad H:=A+B,$$\n\nand the ambient-valued drift and kick formulas\n\n$$\\widehat D_h(q,p):=(q+h\\,M^{-1}p,\\;p),\\qquad \\widehat K_h(q,p):=(q,\\;p-h\\,\\nabla V(q)) \\qquad ((q,p)\\in U).$$\n\nUse these as partial self-maps of $U$ by setting\n$$\\mathcal D_h:=\\{(q,p)\\in U:\\widehat D_h(q,p)\\in U\\},\\qquad D_h:=\\widehat D_h|_{\\mathcal D_h}:\\mathcal D_h\\to U,$$\n$$\\mathcal K_h:=\\{(q,p)\\in U:\\widehat K_h(q,p)\\in U\\},\\qquad K_h:=\\widehat K_h|_{\\mathcal K_h}:\\mathcal K_h\\to U.$$\nThe **velocity-Verlet map** of the splitting $H=A+B$ at step $h\\in\\mathbb R$ is the partial composite\n\n$$V_h:=K_{h/2}\\circ D_h\\circ K_{h/2}:\\mathcal V_h\\to U,$$\nwhere $\\mathcal V_h:=\\{z\\in\\mathcal K_{h/2}:K_{h/2}(z)\\in\\mathcal D_h\\text{ and }D_h(K_{h/2}(z))\\in\\mathcal K_{h/2}\\}$. Thus every intermediate input and the final output belongs to $U$.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "forward_refs": [
            "ex-cm-verlet-map-of-the-harmonic-oscillator"
          ]
