      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-cm-second-variation-of-the-action",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The fixed-endpoint second variation of a time-dependent action at a curve",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
            "lem-cm-first-variation-formula"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-first-variation-formula": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-cm-second-variation-formula"
          ],
          "reference": "batch-12-local-construction; the fixed-endpoint family of D. Cline, Variational Principles, Sections 5.1-5.2, materialised as a definition and recorded in the batch notes",
          "design_state": "completed-local-definition",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $a<b$ be reals, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and nonempty, and let\n$(O,\\mathcal L)$ be a time-dependent Lagrangian chart over $[a,b]\\times U$ in the sense of\n[[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]]: $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R^n$\nis open with\n\n$$[a,b]\\times U\\times\\mathbb R^n\\subseteq O,$$\n\nand $\\mathcal L:O\\to\\mathbb R$ is of class $C^2$. Recall from that definition that a $C^2$ curve\n$q:[a,b]\\to U$ is the restriction to $[a,b]$ of a $C^2$ map on an open interval, that a\n**variation** of $q$ is such a map $\\eta:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R^n$ with\n\n$$\\eta(a)=\\eta(b)=0,$$\n\nand that the **action** of a $C^2$ curve is\n\n$$S(q):=\\int_a^b\\mathcal L\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)\\,dt .$$\n\n**The fixed-endpoint family.** Let $q:[a,b]\\to U$ be a $C^2$ curve and let $\\eta$ be a variation\nof $q$. For $\\varepsilon\\in\\mathbb R$ put\n\n$$q_\\varepsilon(t):=q(t)+\\varepsilon\\eta(t)\\qquad(t\\in[a,b]).$$\n\nBy clause 1 of [[lem-cm-first-variation-formula]] there is $\\varepsilon_0>0$ such that\n$q_\\varepsilon$ is a $C^2$ admissible curve with values in $U$ for every\n$\\varepsilon\\in(-\\varepsilon_0,\\varepsilon_0)$, so that the action $S(q_\\varepsilon)$ is defined\nfor those $\\varepsilon$.\n\n**The second variation.** The **second variation of $S$ at $q$ in the direction $\\eta$** is the\nsecond derivative at $\\varepsilon=0$ of the function $\\varepsilon\\mapsto S(q+\\varepsilon\\eta)$:\n\n$$\\delta^2S(q)[\\eta,\\eta]:=\\frac{d^2}{d\\varepsilon^2}\\Big|_{\\varepsilon=0}S(q+\\varepsilon\\eta).$$\n\nThis is a well-defined real number under the present hypotheses: the map\n$\\varepsilon\\mapsto S(q+\\varepsilon\\eta)$ is twice differentiable at $0$, with the explicit\nintegral formula, by [[lem-cm-second-variation-formula]], which depends on this definition only\nfor the notation $\\delta^2S(q)[\\eta,\\eta]$. The value is a single real number determined by the\npair $(q,\\eta)$ and does not depend on the choice of the $C^2$ extensions of $q$ and $\\eta$, since\nthe values of $q_\\varepsilon$ and $q_\\varepsilon'$ on $[a,b]$ are those of any such extension and\nthe derivative on $[a,b]$ is extension-independent\n([[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]]).\n\n**Scope.** The definition introduces the one-parameter family and its second derivative at\n$\\varepsilon=0$, nothing else. It asserts nothing about minimising or maximising $S$, nothing\nabout the sign of $\\delta^2S$, and no physical interpretation of $\\mathcal L$; stationarity of a\ncurve is the separate first-order condition recorded in\n[[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]], and no equivalence with the vanishing of an\nEuler-Lagrange expression is used at this point.",
          "strategy": "Define the family and the second derivative with the chart and extension conventions of def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action; state the well-definedness and its exact source without proving it here.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 5.1-5.2: the parametric family $q+\\epsilon\\eta$ of a path with fixed endpoints and stationarity as the vanishing of the first-order term, pages 143-147 of the PDF pagination; the second derivative of the family is not computed there",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Section 2.1: first variation of the action with fixed endpoints, and the remark that the true path may be a minimum or a saddle point, printed page 12",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm-second-variation-formula",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The second variation of a time-dependent action: twice differentiability of the fixed-endpoint family and the Hessian integral formula",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-second-variation-of-the-action",
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
            "lem-cm-first-variation-formula",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle",
