      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-chaos-contraction-and-coded-fixed-sequence-checks",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Explicit constants at $a=100$: the contraction factor, the fixed points $r_\\pm$ and the constant-code solutions",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-bilateral-symbol-and-bounded-sequence-space",
            "lem-coded-quadratic-recurrence-is-a-contraction",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "def-abs-value",
            "lem-of-abs-value",
            "def-integer-power"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "reference": "mechanical-horseshoe.md#1 (numerical evaluation of the constants at a=100 and the constant codes)",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $a=100$ in [[lem-coded-quadratic-recurrence-is-a-contraction]], so that\n\n$$R=\\sqrt{100}+3=13,\\qquad L=\\sqrt{a-2R}=\\sqrt{74},\\qquad \\sqrt{a+2R}=\\sqrt{126}.$$\n\nThen $126<169=13^2$, so $\\sqrt{126}<R=13$; $74>1$, so $L>1$; and the contraction constant of $T_s$ on $B_{13}$ is\n\n$$\\frac1L=\\frac1{\\sqrt{74}}<1 .$$\n\nThe constant codes $\\bar u$ ($u=\\pm1$) are solved by the constant sequences $x_n=r_u$ with\n\n$$11<r_+=1+\\sqrt{101}<12,\\qquad -10<r_-=1-\\sqrt{101}<-9,$$\n\nthe two displayed bounds hold because $100<101<121$; and both satisfy the exact identity $r_u^2=100+2r_u$. Every coded sequence has $|x_n|\\ge\\sqrt{74}$, and its sign is the corresponding symbol.",
          "strategy": "Exact symbolic evaluation of every constant with rational-arithmetic checks and the contraction uniqueness.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140 (author's preliminary online edition, complete PDF), Section 13.1, quadratic horseshoe maps and their invariant values",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "P. Cvitanović, R. Artuso, R. Mainieri, G. Tanner, G. Vattay, Chaos: Classical and Quantum (ChaosBook.org, edition 17.6.8, complete PDF), Chapter 15 Section 15.2, the fixed points of the Hénon horseshoe",
                "url": "https://chaosbook.org/chapters/ChaosBook.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
