        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-chaos-slope-transform-fixed-point-checks",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "The constant-code slope transform at $a=100$: the invariant slope of $h\\mapsto1/(2r_+-h)$ and the cone constants",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-stable-unstable-cones-and-line-slopes",
            "lem-slope-graph-transforms-converge",
            "lem-coded-quadratic-recurrence-is-a-contraction",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "def-abs-value",
            "lem-of-abs-value"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "reference": "mechanical-horseshoe.md#2 (worked evaluation of the constant-code slope and the cone constants)",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $a=100$, so $L=\\sqrt{74}$, $k=1/2$, and let $r_+=1+\\sqrt{101}$ be the constant-code value of the all-$+1$ code. The fixed slope of the forward transform $\\Phi_y(h)=1/(2y-h)$ at $y=r_+$ is the small root of $h=1/(2r_+-h)$, i.e. of\n\n$$h^2-2r_+h+1=0:\\qquad h=r_+-\\sqrt{r_+^2-1}=\\frac1{r_++\\sqrt{r_+^2-1}},$$\n\nand one has $0<h<1/2$. Moreover $\\lambda=2L-k=2\\sqrt{74}-\\tfrac12>1$ and $\\Phi_{r_+}$ maps $[-1/2,1/2]$ into $[-\\lambda^{-1},\\lambda^{-1}]\\subseteq[-1/2,1/2]$ with Lipschitz constant $\\lambda^{-2}<1/2$.",
          "strategy": "Exact algebra with sqrt(101) and sqrt(74) plus rational comparisons, checked against the defining inequalities of the slope lemma.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Devaney and Z. Nitecki, Shift automorphisms in the Hénon mapping, Communications in Mathematical Physics 67 (1979) 137-146, invariant sectors with a uniform expansion factor",
                "url": "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-67/issue-2/Shift-automorphisms-in-the-H%C3%A9non-mapping/cmp/1103905161.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140 (author's preliminary online edition, complete PDF), Section 13.2, expanding and contracting directions near a hyperbolic fixed point",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "dependency_roles": {
