          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-chaos-finite-word-homoclinic-code-checks",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "The single-flip code at $a=100$: a nonperiodic coded orbit converging to the fixed point at both ends",
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "deps": [
            "thm-henon-coded-invariant-set-is-conjugate-to-shift",
            "lem-finite-word-deviations-give-homoclinic-orbits",
            "lem-symbol-locality-gives-coding-continuity",
            "lem-coded-quadratic-recurrence-is-a-contraction",
            "def-bilateral-symbol-and-bounded-sequence-space",
            "def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "def-abs-value"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "reference": "mechanical-horseshoe.md#1 (finite-word codes and their homoclinic limits)",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $a=100$ and let $s\\in X_\\pm$ be the code with\n\n$$s_0=-1,\\qquad s_n=+1\\ (n\\ne0),$$\n\nthe single-flip code. Then $s$ is constant outside a finite set (indeed outside $\\{0\\}$), is not the constant code $\\bar{+1}$, and is not periodic: $S_\\pm^m s\\ne s$ for every $m\\ge1$, because the unique coordinate at which $S_\\pm^ms$ equals $-1$ is $-m\\ne0$ whereas $s$ equals $-1$ only at $0$. Consequently the coded point $z:=h(s)$ satisfies $z\\ne(r_+,r_+)$ and $F^m(z)\\ne z$ for every $m\\ge1$, while\n\n$$F^n(z)\\longrightarrow(r_+,r_+)\\quad(n\\to+\\infty\\ \\text{and}\\ n\\to-\\infty).$$",
          "strategy": "Locality of the coding, the conjugacy's injectivity, and the convergence of constant tails to the fixed point.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140 (author's preliminary online edition, complete PDF), Section 13.2, homoclinic orbits and their codes",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Devaney and Z. Nitecki, Shift automorphisms in the Hénon mapping, Communications in Mathematical Physics 67 (1979) 137-146, coding of invariant orbits by bi-infinite sequences",
                "url": "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-67/issue-2/Shift-automorphisms-in-the-H%C3%A9non-mapping/cmp/1103905161.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "domain": "mathematics",
