        {
          "id": "ex-cm-chaos-pulse-and-rotation-endpoint-checks",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "The $\\delta=0$ endpoint computation: the free rotation $(q,p)\\mapsto(p,-q)$, the kick $(q,p)\\mapsto(q,p+q^2-a)$ and $K\\circ R_0=F$",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "def-smooth-periodic-oscillator-pulse-model",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period",
            "def-interval",
            "cor-pi-is-the-first-positive-sine-zero",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "thm-ftc-first-part"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "reference": "mechanical-horseshoe.md#5 (the explicit delta=0 endpoint computation)",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "In the model of [[def-smooth-periodic-oscillator-pulse-model]]:\n\n1. the free oscillator $q'=p$, $p'=-q$ on the interval $[0,\\pi/2]$ carries the initial data $(q_0,p_0)$ to\n\n$$(q,p)\\Bigl(\\tfrac\\pi2\\Bigr)=\\bigl(q_0\\cos\\tfrac\\pi2+p_0\\sin\\tfrac\\pi2,\\;-q_0\\sin\\tfrac\\pi2+p_0\\cos\\tfrac\\pi2\\bigr)=(p_0,-q_0)=R_0(q_0,p_0);$$\n\n2. the $\\delta=0$ rescaled equation $q_s=0$, $p_s=w(s)(q^2-a)$ has the exact solution $q(s)=q_0$, $p(s)=p_0+(q_0^2-a)\\int_0^sw$, so its endpoint map is\n\n$$K(q_0,p_0)=\\bigl(q_0,\\;p_0+q_0^2-a\\bigr)$$\n\nbecause $\\int_0^1w=1$; and\n\n$$(K\\circ R_0)(q_0,p_0)=K(p_0,-q_0)=\\bigl(p_0,\\,-q_0+p_0^2-a\\bigr)=F(q_0,p_0).$$\n\nMoreover $\\beta_\\delta\\ge0$ with $\\int_0^P\\beta_\\delta=1$ and the pulse is supported in $(\\pi/2,P)$.",
          "strategy": "Solve the two explicit linear ODEs by the sine/cosine addition formulas and evaluate at the endpoint times; no numerical integration is involved.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
