          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, $h>0$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, and let $v:O_h(\\Omega)\\to\\mathbb C^n$ be holomorphic with $|v|_{O_h(\\Omega)}\\le B$ where\n$$0\\le B\\le\\frac{h}{16n}.$$\nThen:\n\n1. **Existence and uniqueness.** For every $\\nu_+\\in O_{h/4}(\\Omega)$ there is exactly one $\\nu\\in O_{h/2}(\\Omega)$ with\n$$\\nu+v(\\nu)=\\nu_+.$$\n   The assignment $\\phi(\\nu_+):=\\nu$ defines a map $\\phi:O_{h/4}(\\Omega)\\to O_{h/2}(\\Omega)$ with $|\\phi(\\nu_+)-\\nu_+|_\\infty\\le B$ for every $\\nu_+$.\n2. **Holomorphy.** $\\phi$ is holomorphic on $O_{h/4}(\\Omega)$.\n3. **Derivative bounds.** With $D\\phi$ the complex Jacobian and the row-sum norm, for every $\\nu_+\\in O_{h/4}(\\Omega)$\n$$\\|D\\phi(\\nu_+)-I_n\\|\\le\\frac{16n}{7}\\,\\frac{B}{h},\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad h\\,\\|D\\phi(\\nu_+)-I_n\\|\\le\\frac{16n}{7}B.$$",
          "strategy": "direct; solve the frequency equation by the contraction principle on a complex ball of radius $2B$, prove holomorphy from the uniformly convergent iteration, and differentiate the defining identity to bound the Jacobian.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jürgen Pöschel, A Lecture on the Classical KAM Theorem, arXiv:0908.2234, §3 (the parameter retraction $\\nu=\\nu_++v(\\nu)$ solved by a contraction with $|Dv|\\le2nB/h\\le1/8$, giving $|\\phi-\\mathrm{id}|\\le B$ and $h|D\\phi-\\mathrm{id}|\\le(16n/7)B$), PDF pp. 16-19.",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0908.2234"
              },
              {
                "title": "Rafael de la Llave, A Tutorial on KAM Theory (AMS Proc. Sympos. Pure Math. 69, revised version), §3.4 (estimates for the linearized equation) and §6.1-§6.2 (solution of the frequency-shift equation by the contraction principle on a complex parameter ball), printed pp. 36-40 and 92-106.",
                "url": "https://www.johndcook.com/KAM.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-kam-parameter-sequences-satisfy-all-step-hypotheses",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The KAM parameter sequence: every step satisfies the smallness, truncation, parameter and contraction hypotheses",
          "reference": "analytic-kam.md#e28",
          "design_state": "completed-local-argument",
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "deps": [
            "def-holomorphic-kam-domain-and-diophantine-labels",
            "lem-kam-generator-flow-and-remainder-bound",
            "lem-frequency-reparametrization-by-contraction",
            "lem-cauchy-fourier-and-lattice-shell-bounds",
            "thm-geometric-series",
            "def-interval",
            "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-holomorphic-kam-domain-and-diophantine-labels": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-kam-generator-flow-and-remainder-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-frequency-reparametrization-by-contraction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cauchy-fourier-and-lattice-shell-bounds": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-geometric-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge2$, $\\tau>n-1$, $\\mu:=\\tau+3n+2$, $\\alpha>0$, $s_0>0$, and let $C\\ge1$ bound the constants in [[lem-kam-generator-flow-and-remainder-bound]] and [[lem-frequency-reparametrization-by-contraction]]. Let $c=c(n,\\tau)\\in(0,1]$ be the one-step smallness constant, and fix $C_*\\ge3C2^\\mu$. Put\n$$q:=4^{-(\\mu+1)},\\qquad D:=\\max(16nC4^\\mu,1/c).$$\nFix $0<\\sigma_0\\le\\min(1,s_0/32)$, $0<r_0\\le1$, $K_0\\ge1$, and $h_0,E_0>0$ satisfying:\n\n1. $E_0\\le\\min(c^2,c,\\sigma_0^2,1/64,q^2/C_*^2)$;\n2. with $A:=C_*^2E_0$ and $L:=|\\log A|+n\\log(1/\\sigma_0)+2\\log C_*+n\\log2+1$, one has $K_0\\sigma_0\\ge\\max(1,2L)$;\n3. $D\\alpha E_0\\sigma_0^\\mu\\le h_0\\le\\min(1,\\alpha/(2K_0^{\\tau+1}))$.\n\nDefine for all integers $j\\ge0$\n$$\\sigma_j:=\\sigma_02^{-j},\\qquad K_j:=K_04^j,\\qquad h_j:=h_04^{-\\mu j},\\qquad s_j:=s_0-8\\sum_{i<j}\\sigma_i,$$\n$$E_{j+1}:=C_*E_j^{3/2},\\qquad\\eta_j:=\\sqrt{E_j},\\qquad r_{j+1}:=\\eta_jr_j,\\qquad\\varepsilon_j:=\\alpha E_jr_j\\sigma_j^\\mu,\\qquad B_j:=C\\varepsilon_j/r_j.$$\nThen every $j\\ge0$ satisfies:\n\n(a) $0<r_j\\le1$, $0<\\sigma_j\\le\\min(1,s_j/8)$, $K_j\\sigma_j\\ge1$, $0<h_j\\le1$, $s_j\\ge s_0/2$, $s_j-8\\sigma_j\\ge s_0/2$, and $0<\\eta_j\\le\\min(1/8,\\sigma_j)$;\n(b) $E_j\\le c\\eta_j$, $E_{j+1}\\le qE_j$, and $E_j\\le q^jE_0\\le4^{-j}E_0$;\n(c) $\\varepsilon_j\\le c\\alpha\\eta_jr_j\\sigma_j^\\mu$ and $\\varepsilon_j\\le ch_jr_j$;\n(d) $h_j\\le\\alpha/(2K_j^{\\tau+1})$ and $h_{j+1}\\le h_j/4$;\n(e) $\\sigma_j^{-n}e^{-K_j\\sigma_j/2}\\le E_j$;\n(f) $B_j=C\\alpha E_j\\sigma_j^\\mu\\le h_{j+1}/(16n)$, so the frequency contraction applies with $B=B_j$; moreover\n$$\\frac{E_j}{h_{j+1}}\\le\\frac{4^\\mu E_0}{h_0}4^{-j},\\qquad \\frac{B_j}{h_{j+1}}\\le\\frac{C4^\\mu\\alpha E_0\\sigma_0^\\mu}{h_0}2^{-(\\mu+2)j};$$\n(g) all the following sums converge:\n$$\\sum_{j\\ge0}(r_j+\\sigma_j)E_j\\le4(r_0+\\sigma_0)E_0,\\qquad \\sum_{j\\ge0}E_j\\le2E_0,\\qquad \\sum_{j\\ge0}2^jE_j\\le2E_0,\\qquad \\sum_{j\\ge0}\\frac{\\varepsilon_j}{r_jh_j}\\le\\frac43\\frac{\\alpha E_0\\sigma_0^\\mu}{h_0}.$$\nThe recurrence has precisely the perturbation budget\n$$\\varepsilon_{j+1}=C_*2^{-\\mu}E_j\\varepsilon_j\\ge3CE_j\\varepsilon_j.$$\nConsequently the one-step remainder estimate $|P_j^+|\\le C[E_j+\\eta_j^2+\\sigma_j^{-n}e^{-K_j\\sigma_j/2}]\\varepsilon_j$ on the retained action and angle domain closes with $|P_{j+1}|\\le\\varepsilon_{j+1}$ after frequency retraction. For fixed $n,\\tau,c,C,C_*,\\sigma_0$ the initial conditions are simultaneously satisfiable for all sufficiently small $E_0$ and every fixed $\\alpha>0$; for $0<\\alpha\\le1$ the allowed threshold for $E_0$ can be chosen independently of $\\alpha$.",
          "strategy": "direct; verify each displayed hypothesis of the one-step and contraction lemmas by induction and elementary estimates.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jürgen Pöschel, A Lecture on the Classical KAM Theorem, arXiv:0908.2234, §4-§5 (iteration parameters, Fourier tails and simultaneous initial choices; the conservative exponents below are adapted to the local one-step estimates), PDF pp. 20-27.",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0908.2234"
              },
              {
                "title": "Rafael de la Llave, A Tutorial on KAM Theory (AMS Proc. Sympos. Pure Math. 69, revised version), §6.1-§6.2 (choice of the geometric sequences of strips and truncations, and the quadratic-convergence bookkeeping), printed pp. 92-106.",
                "url": "https://www.johndcook.com/KAM.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
