            "lem-kam-affine-taylor-fourier-truncation",
            "lem-kam-generator-flow-and-remainder-bound",
            "lem-frequency-reparametrization-by-contraction",
            "lem-kam-composed-map-and-derivative-convergence",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system",
            "thm-weierstrass-convergence-holomorphic-functions",
            "def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire",
            "rem-complex-euclidean-space-dictionary"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-holomorphic-kam-domain-and-diophantine-labels": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-truncated-homological-equation-with-conservative-loss": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-kam-affine-taylor-fourier-truncation": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-kam-generator-flow-and-remainder-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-frequency-reparametrization-by-contraction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-kam-composed-map-and-derivative-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-weierstrass-convergence-holomorphic-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire": "mathematical-premise",
            "rem-complex-euclidean-space-dictionary": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "Assume the data, constants, sequences, one-step construction and notation of\n[[lem-kam-composed-map-and-derivative-convergence]], and assume that the initial\nperturbation is **conjugation-symmetric**:\n$$P_0(\\bar I,\\bar\\vartheta,\\bar\\nu)=\\overline{P_0(I,\\vartheta,\\nu)}\\qquad\\text{for every }(I,\\vartheta,\\nu)\\in D_{r_0,s_0,h_0}(\\Omega_{\\alpha,\\tau}).$$\nFor any map $g:Z\\to\\mathbb C^\\ell$, where $Z\\subseteq\\mathbb C^m$ is\ninvariant under componentwise conjugation and $m,\\ell\\ge1$, put\n$g^c(z):=\\overline{g(\\bar z)}$, and call $g$ conjugation-symmetric when\n$g^c=g$. This operation needs no openness or holomorphy of $Z$ or $g$;\nfor holomorphic maps on open domains it also preserves holomorphy.\nIt applies to scalar functions by taking $\\ell=1$, to parameter maps on\n$\\Lambda:=\\Omega_{\\alpha,\\tau}\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, and to phase maps\non $\\mathcal L:=\\{0\\}\\times S_{s_0/4}\\times\\Lambda$. Then:\n\n1. **One-step objects.** For every $j\\ge0$ the functions $P_j$, $Q_j$, $R_j$,\n   $\\widehat R_j$, $F_j$ and $v_j=\\partial_I\\widehat R_j(0,\\cdot)$ are\n   conjugation-symmetric on their domains, and\n   $\\phi_j(\\bar\\nu)=\\overline{\\phi_j(\\nu)}$; in particular $\\phi_j$ maps real\n   points to real points.\n2. **The maps.** The extended steps $T_j(z,\\nu)=(\\widetilde\\Phi_j(z,\\phi_j(\\nu)),\\phi_j(\\nu))$ and their compositions are conjugation-symmetric. In particular $\\Psi_j^c=\\Psi_j$ and $\\chi_j^c=\\chi_j$ for every $j$.\n3. **The limit.** $\\Psi_\\infty^c=\\Psi_\\infty$ on $\\mathcal L$ and $\\chi_\\infty^c=\\chi_\\infty$ on $\\Lambda$; in particular for real\n   $\\vartheta$ and real $\\nu$ the point $\\Psi_\\infty(0,\\vartheta,\\nu)$ is real,\n   that is $b(\\vartheta,\\nu)\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $w(\\vartheta,\\nu)\\in\\mathbb R^n$.",
          "strategy": "direct; induction on the stage, using that the one-step operations (Taylor truncation, Fourier cutoff, solving the homological equation, flowing, and the frequency retraction) commute with conjugation, and passing to the limit.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jürgen Pöschel, A Lecture on the Classical KAM Theorem, arXiv:0908.2234, §1 and §4-§5 (the real-analytic perturbation is complexified to a holomorphic function of the action-angle-parameter variables; the constructed transformations are limits of real-analytic maps), PDF pp. 5-6 and 20-27.",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0908.2234"
              },
              {
                "title": "Rafael de la Llave, A Tutorial on KAM Theory (AMS Proc. Sympos. Pure Math. 69, revised version), §3.1 (real analytic functions and their complexifications) and §6.1-§6.3 (reality of the limiting embedding when the data are real), printed pp. 24-31 and 92-110.",
                "url": "https://www.johndcook.com/KAM.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
