          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "**Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice** $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), as the Fourier-basis and measure-theoretic suppliers below do. Work with either the scalar spaces $H^s(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R)$ or the vector spaces $H^s=H^s(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ and the weight family $w_k$ of [[def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection]]. In the scalar case replace $\\mathbb R^3$ by $\\mathbb R$ and vector norms by absolute values throughout. Let $\\sigma>3/2$ and $u\\in H^\\sigma$.\n\n1. **Continuous representative.** The coefficient series converges absolutely, $\\sum_k|\\widehat u(k)|_2\\le W_\\sigma\\|u\\|_{H^\\sigma}$ with $W_\\sigma$ as in [[lem-cm-lattice-shell-count-and-weight-sums]], and $S_M:=\\sum_{|k|_\\infty\\le M}\\widehat u(k)e_k$ converges uniformly on $\\mathbb T^3$ to a continuous field $\\widetilde u\\in C(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ with the same Fourier coefficients, $\\widehat{\\widetilde u}(k)=\\widehat u(k)$ for all $k$, which satisfies $\\widetilde u=u$ almost everywhere and\n$$\\|\\widetilde u\\|_{L^\\infty}=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb T^3}|\\widetilde u(x)|_2\\;\\le\\;W_\\sigma\\|u\\|_{H^\\sigma}.$$\n2. **Uniqueness of the continuous representative.** In the scalar case replace $\\mathbb R^3$ by $\\mathbb R$ in the following formulas. If $f,g\\in C(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ satisfy $f=g$ almost everywhere, then $f=g$ everywhere. Consequently the field $\\widetilde u$ is the only continuous field equal to $u$ almost everywhere, and if $u$ has a continuous representative $w$ then $\\widetilde u=w$; in particular for $u\\in C(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)$, $\\widetilde u=u$.\n3. **Higher order.** If $j\\ge0$ is an integer and $\\sigma>j+3/2$, then the canonical representative $\\widetilde u\\in C^j$, and its classical derivatives are the corresponding uniform series\n$$\\partial^\\alpha\\widetilde u=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^3}(2\\pi i k)^\\alpha\\widehat u(k)\\,e_k\\qquad(|\\alpha|\\le j),$$\nthe series being absolutely and uniformly convergent, and\n$$\\|u\\|_{C^j}:=\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le j}\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb T^3}|\\partial^\\alpha\\widetilde u(x)|_2\\;\\le\\;C_{\\sigma,j}\\|u\\|_{H^\\sigma},\\qquad C_{\\sigma,j}:=\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le j}W_{\\sigma-|\\alpha|}.$$\n4. **Derivative bound for differentiable fields.** If $\\sigma>3/2$, $u\\in H^{\\sigma+1}$ and $\\partial_ju$ is continuous for some $j$, then $\\partial_ju\\in H^\\sigma$ has continuous representative $\\partial_ju$ itself and\n$$\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb T^3}|\\partial_ju(x)|_2\\;\\le\\;W_\\sigma\\|\\partial_ju\\|_{H^\\sigma}\\;\\le\\;W_\\sigma\\|u\\|_{H^{\\sigma+1}}.$$",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "E. Wiedemann (ed. J. Skipper), Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations (Universität Ulm, WS 2018/19), Section 2.1 (Fourier characterisation of Sobolev spaces on T^3; the absolutely convergent coefficient series and the embedding H^s(T^3)⊂C(T^3) for s>3/2) and Section 4.3 (Agmon's inequality via low/high frequency splitting)",
                "url": "https://www.uni-ulm.de/fileadmin/website_uni_ulm/mawi.inst.020/skipper/NSEEmilFinished.pdf"
