            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "**Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice** $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $T>0$, let $u:[0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a field such that $u\\in C([0,T],H^m)$ for every real $m\\ge0$, $t\\mapsto u(t)$ is differentiable into $H^m$ with $\\partial_tu\\in C([0,T],H^m)$ for every $m$, $u(t)$ is divergence-free for every $t$, and $u$ solves the projected equation $\\partial_tu=\\nu\\Delta u-P((u\\cdot\\nabla)u)$ for some real $\\nu\\ge0$ (all in the sense of the spaces of [[def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection]]).\n\nDefine the real scalar field $p$ on $[0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^3$ by its Fourier coefficients\n$$\\widehat p(t,0):=0,\\qquad \\widehat p(t,k):=-\\frac{1}{|k|_2^2}\\sum_{i,j=1}^{3}k_ik_j\\,\\widehat{u_iu_j}(t,k)\\quad(k\\ne0),$$\nwhere $\\widehat{u_iu_j}(t,k)$ is the $k$-th Fourier coefficient of the product field $u_i(t,\\cdot)u_j(t,\\cdot)$. Then:\n\n1. $p\\in C([0,T],H^m)$ for every $m\\ge0$, $t\\mapsto p(t)$ is differentiable into every $H^m$, $p\\in C^\\infty([0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^3)$, and $\\int_{\\mathbb T^3}p(t,x)\\,dx=0$ for every $t$.\n2. The following identities hold on the strip:\n$$\\nabla p=-(I-P)\\bigl((u\\cdot\\nabla)u\\bigr),\\qquad \\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=\\nu\\Delta u-\\nabla p,\\qquad \\Delta p=-\\sum_{i,j=1}^{3}\\partial_i\\partial_j(u_iu_j).$$\n3. **Uniqueness.** If $q\\in C^\\infty([0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^3)$ has $\\int_{\\mathbb T^3}q(t,\\cdot)\\,dx=0$ and $\\nabla q=\\nabla p$ for every $t$, then $q=p$; equivalently, $p$ is the unique smooth mean-zero pressure making $u$ solve the primitive-variable system.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
