      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-leray-projection-explicit-computations",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Explicit Leray projection computations on $\\mathbb T^3$: gradients, a mixed field, and a divergence-free mode",
          "reference": "batch-15 B page; constructed from the A-page statements",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus",
            "lem-fourier-lattice-sobolev-embedding",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-fourier-lattice-sobolev-embedding": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Use the character convention $e_k(x)=e^{2\\pi ik\\cdot x}$ and the Leray projection $P$ of [[def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection]]; thus for $k\\ne0$ the $k$-th coefficient of $(I-P)v$ is $\\bigl(k\\cdot\\widehat v(k)/|k|_2^2\\bigr)k$.\n\n**(a) A gradient field.** Let $\\varphi(x):=\\cos(2\\pi x_1)+\\sin\\bigl(2\\pi(x_2+x_3)\\bigr)$ and $v:=\\nabla\\varphi$. Then $Pv=0$ and the decomposed part is the whole gradient: $v=Pv+\\nabla\\varphi$ with $Pv=0$.\n\n**(b) A mixed field.** Let $u(x):=\\bigl(\\sin(2\\pi x_2),\\,0,\\,\\cos(2\\pi x_3)\\bigr)$. Then\n$$Pu=\\bigl(\\sin(2\\pi x_2),\\,0,\\,0\\bigr),\\qquad u-Pu=\\bigl(0,\\,0,\\,\\cos(2\\pi x_3)\\bigr)=\\nabla f,\\quad f(x)=\\tfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\sin(2\\pi x_3),$$\nand one has the orthogonality and Pythagorean identities $\\langle Pu,u-Pu\\rangle=0$, $\\|u\\|_{L^2}^2=\\|Pu\\|_{L^2}^2+\\|u-Pu\\|_{L^2}^2=1$.\n\n**(c) A divergence-free mode.** Let $k\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $A\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $A\\cdot k=0$, and $u(x):=A\\sin(2\\pi k\\cdot x)$. Then $Pu=u$, $\\operatorname{div}u=0$, and $\\langle u,Pv\\rangle=\\langle u,v\\rangle$ for every $v$ in $H^0$, i.e. $u$ is fixed by $P$ and orthogonal to every gradient field.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "A. J. Majda and A. L. Bertozzi, Vorticity and Incompressible Flow (CUP 2002), Proposition 1.18 and formula (1.98) (the Hodge decomposition of the torus and the multiplier k⊗k/|k|^2)",
                "url": "http://inis.jinr.ru/sl/P_Physics/PC_Classical%20physics/PCfm_Fluid%20mechanics/Majda,%20Bertozzi.%20Vorticity%20and%20incompressible%20flow%20(CUP,%202002)(558s).pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "E. Wiedemann (ed. J. Skipper), Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations (Universität Ulm, WS 2018/19), Section 2.2 (Definition 2.7 of the Leray projection and Lemma 2.8)",
                "url": "https://www.uni-ulm.de/fileadmin/website_uni_ulm/mawi.inst.020/skipper/NSEEmilFinished.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm-plane-wave-and-shear-exact-solutions",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Exact transverse plane waves, the sheared single mode, and the Taylor-Green vortex in the periodic models",
          "reference": "batch-15 B page; constructed from the A-page statements",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection",
            "lem-fourier-lattice-sobolev-embedding",
            "lem-fluid-galerkin-coefficient-ivp",
            "lem-periodic-pressure-reconstruction",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-fourier-lattice-sobolev-embedding": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-fluid-galerkin-coefficient-ivp": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-periodic-pressure-reconstruction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Work on $\\mathbb T^3$ with $dx$ the normalized measure and the operators of [[def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection]]: $\\partial_ju$ has symbol $2\\pi ik_j$, $\\Delta$ has symbol $-4\\pi^2|k|_2^2$, and $P$ kills gradient fields.\n\n**(a) Transverse plane waves, inviscid and viscous.** Let $k\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $A\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $A\\cdot k=0$, and put $u_0(x):=A\\sin(2\\pi k\\cdot x)$. Then $\\operatorname{div}u_0=0$, $(u_0\\cdot\\nabla)u_0=0$, $\\Delta u_0=-4\\pi^2|k|_2^2u_0$, and for every $\\nu\\ge0$ the field\n$$u(t,x):=e^{-4\\pi^2\\nu|k|_2^2t}A\\sin(2\\pi k\\cdot x)$$\nis a smooth global solution of $\\partial_tu+P((u\\cdot\\nabla)u)=\\nu\\Delta u$ with $P((u\\cdot\\nabla)u)=0$, and a solution of the primitive system with $p=0$. For $\\nu=0$ the field $u_0$ is stationary; the Galerkin coefficient of the mode $k$ satisfies the scalar equation $c'(t)=-4\\pi^2\\nu|k|_2^2c(t)$.\n\n**(b) The sheared single mode.** For $k=e_2$, $A=e_1$ part (a) gives $u(t,x)=e^{-4\\pi^2\\nu t}\\bigl(\\sin(2\\pi x_2),0,0\\bigr)$: the viscous decay of a shear flow, whose $P$-image is itself because the field is divergence-free and $P$ is the orthogonal projection of $H^0$ onto $H^0_\\sigma$ ([[def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection]]).\n\n**(c) The Taylor-Green vortex.** Write $\\theta_j:=2\\pi x_j$ and\n$$u^0(x):=\\bigl(\\sin\\theta_1\\cos\\theta_2,\\,-\\cos\\theta_1\\sin\\theta_2,\\,0\\bigr),\\qquad p^0(x):=\\tfrac14\\bigl(\\cos(2\\theta_1)+\\cos(2\\theta_2)\\bigr).$$\nThen $\\operatorname{div}u^0=0$, $(u^0\\cdot\\nabla)u^0=-\\nabla p^0$, and $\\Delta u^0=-8\\pi^2u^0$; hence for every $\\nu\\ge0$ the pair\n$$u(t,x):=e^{-8\\pi^2\\nu t}u^0(x),\\qquad p(t,x):=e^{-16\\pi^2\\nu t}p^0(x)$$\nsolves the primitive system $\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\nabla p+\\nu\\Delta u$ on $[0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb T^3$, and $u$ solves the projected equation with $P((u\\cdot\\nabla)u)=0$ because $(u^0\\cdot\\nabla)u^0=-\\nabla p^0$ is a gradient field. In particular these solutions are global in time, so the finite time $T$ of [[thm-local-smooth-periodic-euler-viscous-solution]] is not asserted to be sharp.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "A. J. Majda and A. L. Bertozzi, Vorticity and Incompressible Flow (CUP 2002), Subsection 1.5 (exact solutions with convection, vortex stretching and diffusion, including shear flows and the Taylor-Green-type steady states of Subsection 2.2)",
                "url": "http://inis.jinr.ru/sl/P_Physics/PC_Classical%20physics/PCfm_Fluid%20mechanics/Majda,%20Bertozzi.%20Vorticity%20and%20incompressible%20flow%20(CUP,%202002)(558s).pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "E. Wiedemann (ed. J. Skipper), Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations (Universität Ulm, WS 2018/19), Section 1.2.1 (energy balance and elementary exact solutions) and Section 3.1 (Galerkin reduction on single modes)",
                "url": "https://www.uni-ulm.de/fileadmin/website_uni_ulm/mawi.inst.020/skipper/NSEEmilFinished.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm-absolute-convergence-fails-in-h-zero",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "FALSE: every $L^2$ field on $\\mathbb T^3$ has absolutely convergent Fourier coefficients. An explicit $H^0$ family with $\\sum_k|\\widehat u(k)|=\\infty$",
          "reference": "batch-15 B page; constructed from the A-page statements",
          "design_state": "completed-local-example; recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection",
            "lem-fourier-lattice-sobolev-embedding",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-nonnegative-extended-series",
            "thm-integral-test-for-series",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-fourier-lattice-sobolev-embedding": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-nonnegative-extended-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-integral-test-for-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "**False statement.** Every field $u\\in H^0(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)=L^2(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ has absolutely convergent Fourier coefficients:\n$$\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^3}|\\widehat u(k)|_2<+\\infty .$$\n(By [[lem-fourier-lattice-sobolev-embedding]] the corresponding statement for $H^\\sigma$ with $\\sigma>3/2$ is true; the false statement drops the hypothesis, and that is exactly what fails.)",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "E. Wiedemann (ed. J. Skipper), Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations (Universität Ulm, WS 2018/19), Section 2.1 (the Fourier characterisation of H^s and the threshold s>3/2 for absolute convergence of the coefficient series)",
                "url": "https://www.uni-ulm.de/fileadmin/website_uni_ulm/mawi.inst.020/skipper/NSEEmilFinished.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Laugesen, Harmonic Analysis Lecture Notes (arXiv:0903.3845), Chapters 1-2 (decay of Fourier coefficients versus summability; the failure of absolute convergence for merely L^2 data)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0903.3845"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-non-relativistic-classical-mechanics-track.md",
