        {
          "id": "lem-cm-body-angular-velocity-of-a-rotation-path",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The body angular velocity of a $C^1$ rotation path: $\\Omega=R^{\\mathsf T}R'$ is skew, $\\Omega z=\\omega\\times z$ determines a unique continuous $\\omega$, and $R'=R\\Omega$",
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nondegenerate open interval, let $SO(3)$ be as in [[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]], and let $R:I\\to SO(3)$ be of class $C^1$, that is, all nine entries of $R$ have continuous first derivatives; write $R'(t)$ for the entrywise derivative and put\n\n$$\\Omega(t):=R(t)^{\\mathsf T}R'(t)\\in M_3(\\mathbb R)$$\n\nfor the product of the transpose of $R(t)$ with $R'(t)$ ([[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]], [[def-matrix-space]]). Then:\n\n1. $\\Omega(t)$ is skew-symmetric for every $t\\in I$, that is $\\Omega(t)^{\\mathsf T}=-\\Omega(t)$.\n2. There is exactly one map $\\omega:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with\n\n$$R(t)^{\\mathsf T}R'(t)\\,z=\\omega(t)\\times z\\qquad(z\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ t\\in I),$$\n\nnamely $\\omega(t)=\\bigl(-\\Omega_{23}(t),\\ \\Omega_{13}(t),\\ -\\Omega_{12}(t)\\bigr)$, and this $\\omega$ is continuous. It is called the **body angular velocity** of $R$.\n3. For every $t\\in I$, writing $\\Omega_{\\omega(t)}$ for the matrix $z\\mapsto\\omega(t)\\times z$, one has $R'(t)=R(t)\\,\\Omega_{\\omega(t)}$.",
          "deps": [
            "lem-cm-quaternion-product-rule-and-conjugation-formula",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-matrix-multiplication-laws",
            "prop-transpose-laws",
            "def-matrix-space"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
