        {
          "id": "thm-cm-quaternion-lift-exists-and-is-unique",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Existence, uniqueness and sign ambiguity of the quaternion lift of a $C^1$ rotation path",
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nondegenerate open interval, let $R:I\\to SO(3)$ be of class $C^1$ ([[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]) with body angular velocity $\\omega_R$ in the sense of [[lem-cm-body-angular-velocity-of-a-rotation-path]], let $t_0\\in I$, and let $q_0\\in S^3$ satisfy $\\rho(q_0)=R(t_0)$, with $\\rho$ and $S^3$ as in [[def-cm-quaternion-rotation-map-and-cover]]. Then:\n\n1. There is exactly one $C^1$ quaternion lift $q:I\\to S^3$ of $R$ with $q(t_0)=q_0$, namely the unique solution on $I$ of the initial value problem\n\n$$q'(t)=\\tfrac12\\,q(t)\\,\\varphi\\bigl(\\omega_R(t)\\bigr),\\qquad q(t_0)=q_0 .$$\n\n2. Every $C^1$ lift $\\tilde q:I\\to S^3$ of $R$ satisfies $\\tilde q=q$ or $\\tilde q=-q$; in particular $-q$ is the lift with initial value $-q_0$, and $\\rho\\circ q=\\rho\\circ(-q)=R$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-quaternion-rotation-map-and-cover",
            "lem-cm-quaternion-product-rule-and-conjugation-formula",
            "lem-cm-body-angular-velocity-of-a-rotation-path",
