        {
          "id": "rem-cm-quaternion-lift-scope-and-sign",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Scope of the quaternion lift: the global sign ambiguity, closed loops, and the absence of a physical spin claim",
          "statement": "The following qualifications are part of what the preceding items do and do not claim.\n\n1. **Two lifts and one rotation.** Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nondegenerate open interval and let $R:I\\to SO(3)$ be of class $C^1$. By [[thm-cm-quaternion-lift-exists-and-is-unique]], $R$ has exactly two $C^1$ quaternion lifts, namely $q$ and $-q$ for any one lift $q$, both solve $q'=\\tfrac12q\\,\\varphi(\\omega_R(t))$ for the same body angular velocity $\\omega_R$, and $\\rho(q)=\\rho(-q)=R$. By the scaling recorded in [[def-cm-quaternion-rotation-map-and-cover]], $\\rho(\\hat\\lambda q)=\\rho(q)$ for every nonzero real $\\lambda$, so a rotation is represented by the antipodal pair $\\pm q$ of unit quaternions and by no other data of the quaternion itself.\n\n2. **No globally continuous sign choice.** The sign of a lift is not determined by $R$, and the two signs cannot always be glued continuously along closed motion. The companion examples page exhibits a loop $R:[0,1]\\to SO(3)$, continuous on $[0,1]$ and $C^1$ on $(0,1)$, with $R(0)=R(1)=I_3$, whose explicit lift from $1$ ends at $-1$. By uniqueness on the interior and continuity at the endpoints, this loop admits no closed lift through $1$ with that regularity; the explicit counterexample is linked in the Remarks below. In this sense the sign ambiguity is intrinsic and not an artifact of the formula $\\rho(q)(x)=qxq^{-1}$.\n\n3. **No physical claim.** Every statement on this page concerns curves into $S^3$ and into the matrix group $SO(3)$ and the differential equation $q'=\\tfrac12q\\,\\varphi(\\omega(t))$. No quantum-mechanical spin, relativistic, or half-integer-angular-momentum interpretation is asserted, no measurement is reported, and no empirical precision is claimed anywhere.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-quaternion-rotation-map-and-cover",
            "thm-cm-quaternion-lift-exists-and-is-unique"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-quaternion-rotation-map-and-cover": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-quaternion-lift-exists-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Sola, Quaternion kinematics for the error-state Kalman filter (arXiv:1711.02508), Section 2.4.6 (the double cover of SO(3): a full 2pi turn of the rotation corresponds to a half turn of the quaternion vector, so q and -q encode the same rotation), printed page 24",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.02508"
              },
              {
                "title": "P. Etingof, MIT 18.745 Lie Groups and Lie Algebras I (Fall 2020), Exercise 3.9(ii) (SU(2) -> SO(3) is a universal covering; pi_1(SO(3)) = Z/2; the Dirac belt trick), printed page 26",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-745-lie-groups-and-lie-algebras-i-fall-2020/mit18_745_f20_lec_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "forward_refs": [
            "cex-cm-no-closed-lift-along-a-full-turn"
          ]
        }
      ],
