      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "post-cm01-spacetime",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted Newtonian spacetime structure: absolute time, Euclidean spatial slices and their smooth structure",
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the non-relativistic classical mechanics framework the following statements about spacetime.\n\n**(i) Spacetime data.** Spacetime is the mathematical structure\n$(V,\\tau,g,A,\\text{fixed orientation of }K)$ of [[def-cm-affine-time-space]]: a\nfour-dimensional real vector space $V$; a surjective linear map\n$\\tau:V\\to\\mathbb R$, the **absolute-time covector**; the kernel\n$K=\\ker\\tau$, a three-dimensional real vector space, the **spatial displacement\nspace**; a **spatial metric** $g$, a symmetric positive-definite bilinear form\n$g:K\\times K\\to\\mathbb R$; a fixed **orientation** of $K$, that is, a chosen one\nof the two classes of ordered bases of $K$ under the equivalence generated by\npositive-determinant changes of basis; and an affine space $A$ modelled on $V$\n(a nonempty set with a free and transitive $V$-action, with displacements as in\n[[def-cm-affine-time-space]]). The elements of $A$ are the **events** of the\nmodel. The structure is the arena of the model; no equation of motion, force\nlaw or matter content is asserted by this clause.\n\n**(ii) Interpretation of the data.** For a displacement $w\\in V$, the number\n$\\tau(w)$ is the **elapsed time** carried by $w$, read in seconds; for a\ndisplacement $k\\in K$ inside a simultaneous slice, the number $g(k,k)$ is the\n**squared spatial length** of $k$, read in square metres; the square root\n$\\sqrt{g(k,k)}$ is the length of $k$. The dimensions and units are those fixed\nby [[def-cm01-units]], so that $[\\tau(w)]=\\tau$ and $[g(k,k)]=\\ell^{2}$. The\norientation of $K$ is the convention relative to which spatial bases are called\npositively oriented and against which orientation-preserving rotations are\ndefined.\n\n**(iii) Absolute time, simultaneity and distance.** Two events $p,q\\in A$ are\n**simultaneous** when their displacement lies in $K$, that is, when\n$\\tau(q-p)=0$. Simultaneity is an equivalence relation on $A$: it is reflexive\nbecause $\\tau(0)=0$, symmetric because $\\tau(p-q)=-\\tau(q-p)$ and transitive\nbecause $\\tau$ is additive. Its classes are the **simultaneous slices**\n$A_{t}=\\{p\\in A:\\tau(p-o)=t\\}$ of any adapted chart; the class does not depend\non the choice of adapted chart, and the elapsed time between two events is the\nchart-independent number $\\tau(q-p)$. Two events of a common slice carry the\n**slice distance** $d_{g}(p,q):=\\sqrt{g(p-q,p-q)}$, which is defined because\n$p-q\\in K$; distances are not defined across distinct slices by this structure.\n\n**(iv) Smooth structure.** $A$ is read with the affine smooth structure\ndetermined by its affine coordinates: an adapted chart of\n[[def-cm-affine-time-space]] is a bijection $A\\to\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$\nwhose inverse is the map $(t,x)\\mapsto o+tu+\\sum_{i}x_{i}e_{i}$, and curves are\ndeclared $C^{k}$ when their coordinate functions in one (equivalently, every)\nadapted chart are $C^{k}$. The equivalence of the two readings is supplied by\nthe affine transition law between adapted charts proved in\n[[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]]; this postulate adopts\nthe resulting smooth structure as the one in which regularity of worldlines and\nof force maps is stated.\n\n**(v) What the structure excludes.** The data contain no inner product on $V$,\nno Lorentzian metric, no light cone, no universal speed bound, no null cones\nand no causal classification of displacements other than the split into\n$\\tau$-values; they contain no distinguished origin, no distinguished vector\n$u$ with $\\tau(u)=1$ and no distinguished basis of $K$. Consequently the\nstructure by itself distinguishes no inertial frame: the class of frames and\nthe transformations between them are fixed separately, by the frame postulate\n[[post-cm01-frames]]. No statement about the speed of any signal, about\nelectromagnetic propagation or about gravitation is made here.\n\n**(vi) Status.** These clauses are adopted assumptions of the model, not\ntheorems and not empirical findings. They are not derived from the uniqueness\ntheory of differential equations, from any symmetry principle or from\nexperiment; the Newtonian tradition and Galilean relativity motivate them. The\nmodel makes no claim that the physical world is exactly a four-dimensional\naffine space with these data, and no numerical measurement is recorded.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it adopts the affine event space with absolute time, the Euclidean spatial displacement space and its orientation, absolute simultaneity and the affine smooth structure, and records what the structure excludes; it selects no force law, no equation of motion and no solution.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the Newtonian spacetime model of the framework: a four-dimensional affine event space with a surjective absolute-time covector, a three-dimensional Euclidean spatial displacement space with its metric and orientation, absolute simultaneity and the affine smooth structure. Excludes relativistic kinematics, Lorentzian structure, light cones, quantum and general-relativistic regimes, and any claim that a real system is exactly such an affine space. Adoption is a model assumption, not a derived or empirical result.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.1 'Kinematics: Basic notions', chapter page 2 (PDF page 6): absolute (Newtonian) space and time with the Euclidean metric of Eq. (1.2), and time assumed to run similarly in all reference frames",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2, printed page 4 (PDF page 10): inertial frames and Newton's first law, with the absolute-time convention of Galilean relativity in Section 1.2.1, printed page 5 (PDF page 11)",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): a particle's motion governed by F = dp/dt in an inertial frame, with the Galilean transformations of that frame class",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 3
        },
        {
          "id": "post-cm01-frames",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Inertial frames, stationary observers and the adopted Galilean transformations between frames",
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the framework the following statements about frames, in the spacetime structure of [[post-cm01-spacetime]].\n\n**(i) Rotations.** A **rotation** is a matrix $R\\in SO(3)$, where\n\n$$SO(3)=\\left\\{R\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}:R^{\\mathsf T}R=I_{3},\\ \\det R=1\\right\\}$$\n\nin the sense of [[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]. A rotation acts on spatial\ncolumn vectors by matrix multiplication; its entries are dimensionless under\nthe radian convention of [[def-cm01-units]]. Rotations are part of the\nformulation of spatial changes of basis; the definition of $SO(3)$ itself\nasserts nothing about physical space.\n\n**(ii) Inertial frames.** An **inertial frame** is the global adapted affine\nchart of the spacetime data determined by\n\n$$o\\in A,\\qquad u\\in V\\ \\text{with}\\ \\tau(u)=1,\\qquad (e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})\\ \\text{an ordered,}\\ g\\text{-orthonormal, positively oriented basis of }K,$$\n\nwith adapted coordinates $(t,x)$ as in [[def-cm-affine-time-space]]: the time\ncoordinate is $t(p)=\\tau(p-o)$ and the spatial coordinates are the coefficients\nof $p-o-tu\\in K$ in the basis $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$. Existence and uniqueness\nof the coordinates, and the affine structure of the slices, are the mathematical\ncontent of [[lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique]] and\n[[def-cm-affine-time-space]]; no physical claim beyond the reading of clause\n(iii) is attached to them here.\n\nAn inertial frame is a **coordinate chart**: the triple $(o,u,(e_{i}))$. It is\ndefinitively not an observer congruence and not a time-varying basis field. In\nthe model the basis $(e_{i})$ is fixed once and for all; a family of bases\ndepending on the time coordinate is a different structure, whose kinematics is\ndefined separately where it is used, and is not an inertial frame. The physical\nreading of the frame is that $t$ is the model's time coordinate in seconds and\n$x$ the spatial position in metres, by the conventions of\n[[def-cm01-units]].\n\n**(iii) Stationary observers.** For $x_{0}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ the curve\n$\\alpha_{x_{0}}:\\mathbb R\\to A$, $\\alpha_{x_{0}}(t):=o+tu+\\sum_{j=1}^{3}(x_{0})_{j}e_{j}$,\nis the **observer at rest in the frame at position $x_{0}$**; the family\n$\\{\\alpha_{x_{0}}\\}_{x_{0}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}}$ is the **stationary observer\ncongruence** of the frame. Each curve has $t(\\alpha_{x_{0}}(s))=s$, is\n$C^{\\infty}$ in adapted coordinates, and in the frame's own spatial\ncoordinates has constant coordinate triple $x_{0}$. Here an **observer\nworldline** means precisely a $C^2$ curve $\\alpha:I\\to A$ on a nondegenerate\nopen real interval with $t(\\alpha(s))=s$, used to describe an observer; the\nstationary curves just defined have $I=\\mathbb R$. This kinematic definition\nassigns neither a particle label nor a mass or a dynamical law to an observer.\nA particle worldline will separately be assigned to a particle label in the\nparticle postulate. The congruence is a derived object, determined by the frame and the spacetime data, not an additional primitive. Distinct frames can have the same family of parametrized curves: if $o' = o+\\sum_jc_je_j$ while $u'=u$ and $e_j'=e_j$, then $\\alpha'_{x_0}(t)=\\alpha_{x_0+c}(t)$ for all $t$. Thus shifting only the spatial origin relabels this family. Changing $u$ changes the curve family, since every curve in it has the affine derivative $u$.\n\n**(iv) Allowed changes between frames.** The model adopts the class of\ntransformations between inertial frames\n\n$$t'=t+b,\\qquad x'=Rx+vt+a,$$\n\nwhere $b\\in\\mathbb R$ is a **time shift** ($[b]=\\tau$), $a\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ a\n**spatial translation** ($[a_{i}]=\\ell$), $v\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ a **boost\nvelocity** ($[v_{i}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$) and $R\\in SO(3)$ a **rotation**\n($[R_{ij}]=1$). Every pair of inertial frames is related by exactly one such\ntransformation: that two adapted charts of the same data differ by a\ntransformation of this form, with $R\\in SO(3)$ because both bases are\npositively oriented, is the mathematical transformation law proved in\n[[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]]. Uniqueness follows by comparing the coordinate maps on all $(t,x)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$: evaluating the time map fixes $b$; evaluating the spatial map at $(0,0)$ fixes $a$, at $(1,0)$ fixes $v$, and at $(0,e_j)$ for the three standard coordinate vectors fixes each column of $R$. The kinematic\nconsequences of the law — the transformation of coordinate velocity and\nacceleration, and the frame-independence of zero coordinate acceleration — are\nproved as a statement about the model in\n[[pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics]].\n\n**(v) Free motion is not asserted here.** The framework's dynamics enters\nthrough the force and equation-of-motion postulates of the pages that follow;\nthose pages adopt, as the content of Newton's first law, that there exist\ninertial frames in the sense of clause (ii) in which a particle with zero\napplied interaction has zero coordinate acceleration. This postulate\nintroduces the frames and their changes but asserts no dynamics: it does not\nstate that any particular motion has zero acceleration, and it does not\nprivilege any one inertial frame — each of them is a legitimate arena for the\nformulation of the model's equations.\n\n**(vi) Kinematic symmetry is not conservation.** The class (iv) is a class of\ncoordinate changes. A quantity is **conserved along a motion** when its value as a function of the frame time is constant on the interval of that motion. No such constancy is asserted here, nor is it asserted that every\nmechanical system's description is invariant in form; a force law must also\ntransform appropriately under (iv) to be a law of the model in every frame, and\nan external apparatus or background field can single out frames for the\ndescription of a concrete experiment. Conservation statements, when they hold,\nare theorems of later pages with their own hypotheses, not consequences of\nthis postulate.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it fixes the inertial frames as the adapted charts, derives the stationary congruence, adopts the Galilean transformation class between frames with its dimensions, and separates the kinematic-symmetry content from any dynamical conservation claim.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-spacetime",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
