            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the inertial frames of the Newtonian model as the adapted affine charts of its spacetime, of the stationary observer congruence of a frame, and of the class of Galilean transformations relating two inertial frames, with the rotation group SO(3), time shifts, spatial translations and boosts. The item excludes non-inertial and time-dependent bases as frames, asserts no force law or conservation law, and attaches no measured value to any frame datum.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2.1 'Galilean Relativity', printed page 5 (PDF page 11): translations x' = x + a, rotations x' = R x with R^T R = 1 and boosts x' = x + v t map inertial frames to inertial frames and generate the Galilean group",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.2, chapter page 3 (PDF page 7): there exists at least one inertial frame in which a free particle moves with constant velocity, and frames moving without rotation or acceleration relative to it are also inertial",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): an inertial frame is a frame in which a free particle travels in a straight line, and three rotations, three translations, three boosts and one time translation relate inertial frames",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 4
        },
        {
          "id": "post-cm01-worldlines",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Particle labels, worldlines, the frame-time parameter condition and fixed inertial masses",
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the framework the following statements about particles and their motion, in the spacetime of [[post-cm01-spacetime]] and the frames of [[post-cm01-frames]].\n\n**(i) Particle labels and worldlines.** A model of the framework has **particle\nlabels** $i=1,\\dots,N$ for some $N\\ge1$. To each label and each *inertial\nframe* of the model — an adapted chart $(o,u,(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3}))$ with\ncoordinates $(t,x)$ in the sense of [[post-cm01-frames]] — there is assigned a\n**worldline** $\\gamma_{i}:I\\to A$ described in that frame, where\n$I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a nondegenerate open interval — that is, an interval\nof the form $(a,b)$ with $-\\infty\\le a<b\\le+\\infty$ and at least two points —\nthe curve $\\gamma_{i}$ is of class $C^{2}$ in the adapted coordinates of the frame,\nand the **parameter condition**\n\n$$\\tau\\bigl(\\gamma_{i}(t)-o\\bigr)=t\\qquad(t\\in I)$$\n\nholds: the curve parameter is the elapsed time from the origin of the frame, so\nin the frame's coordinates $\\gamma_{i}(t)=(t,x_{i}(t))$ with\n$x_{i}:I\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ of class $C^{2}$. The parameter condition replaces\nthe causal conditions of relativistic kinematics: no timelike condition on the\nvelocity and no upper bound on its Euclidean norm is imposed, and the\nparameter is not free up to reparametrization.\n\n**(ii) Description in another frame.** The same motion described in a second\ninertial frame is the curve obtained by the time reparametrization of the two\nframes: if $(t',x')$ are the coordinates of the second frame and\n$t'=t+b$ is the time component of the transformation between the two frames\n([[post-cm01-frames]], [[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]]),\nthen the description of the motion in the second frame is the curve\n$\\gamma_{i,b}(t'):=\\gamma_{i}(t'-b)$, defined on $I+b$, and it again satisfies\nthe parameter condition $\\tau(\\gamma_{i,b}(t')-o')=t'$ with respect to the\nsecond frame's origin $o'$. This is a consistency requirement of the model's\nframe language; the accompanying spatial transformation of the coordinate\ncurve is the affine law of [[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]],\nand its kinematic consequences are proved in [[pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics]].\n\n**(iii) Regularity is frame-independent.** The class of the worldline is\nindependent of the frame used to describe it: by the transformation law of\n[[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]], the coordinates of the\nsame event in two inertial frames differ by\n$t'=t+b$, $x'=Rx+vt+a$ with constant $b,a,v$ and $R\\in SO(3)$, so a coordinate\ncurve is twice continuously differentiable in one inertial frame exactly when\nits reparametrized description is twice continuously differentiable in the\nother. The assumptions of this postulate are therefore read in any one inertial\nframe without loss.\n\n**(iv) Velocity and acceleration.** In a fixed inertial frame the quantities\n$w_{i}:=dx_{i}/dt$ and $a_{i}:=d^{2}x_{i}/dt^{2}$ are the **coordinate\nvelocity** and **coordinate acceleration** of the label, of dimensions\n$\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ and $\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ by [[def-cm01-units]]; they are defined\nprecisely, and their transformation under a change of inertial frame is\nrecorded, in [[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]].\n\n**(v) Fixed inertial mass.** Each particle label $i$ carries a **mass**\n$m_{i}>0$, a primitive parameter of the model with dimension $\\mu$\n([[def-cm01-units]]). The mass is fixed: it is the same positive number at all\ntimes and in all inertial frames of this model; the index $i$ identifies a label within that model and asserts no cross-model identification or equality of mass parameters. It\nis not a function of the time coordinate, of the position or of the velocity,\nand no variable-mass model is a model of this clause. The mass enters the\ndynamics only through its later appearance in the equation of motion; this\npostulate attaches no dynamics to it. Distinct labels may carry distinct\nmasses, and the masses are model data, not measured values.\n\n**(vi) Point-particle idealization.** The model retains of each label only its\nposition and its fixed mass, together with the label index: size, shape, spin,\ndeformation, temperature and internal degrees of freedom are omitted, and the\nquantity $x_{i}(t)$ is the single point representing the label at time $t$. An\napplication of the model must state when this idealization is adequate for the\nsystem at hand; for a body whose rotation, deformation or internal structure\nmatters for the question asked, the point-particle model is not adequate and a\nricher model must be specified. The framework makes no claim that every\nphysical body is a point particle.\n\n**(vii) Calibration caveat.** An apparatus calibration may *estimate* a mass\nparameter of a concrete body under a stated measurement model, with its\nassumptions, sampling and uncertainty; such an estimate neither defines the\nmass parameter of a label nor verifies the equation of motion in which it\noccurs, and it is not deduced from this postulate. No measured value of any\nmass is asserted by this item.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it adopts the particle labels, the twice continuously differentiable worldlines parametrized by frame time, their description in other frames, the fixed positive inertial masses and the point-particle idealization, with the calibration caveat delimiting measurement.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-spacetime",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm01-spacetime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the particle idealization of the framework: labeled particles whose motion is a twice continuously differentiable worldline parametrized by the elapsed time of an inertial frame, each label carrying a fixed positive inertial mass. Excludes extended bodies, internal degrees of freedom, variable mass, relativistic worldline conditions and any measured value of a mass. The clause on calibration concerns the interface with measurement, not a verification of the dynamical law.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Sections 1.1-1.2, printed pages 2-4 (PDF pages 8-10): a particle is an object of insignificant size, a reference frame is a choice of origin with axes, and the trajectory x(t) has componentwise velocity and acceleration",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.1 point (vi), chapter page 3 (PDF page 7): a point particle has negligible size and shape, and its position at an instant is a single point; Section 1.2, chapter page 3: each particle is characterized by a scalar constant, its mass",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): a particle of mass m with position r, momentum p = m rdot, and the caution that the particle idealization depends on the use",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm01-units",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Dimensions and SI unit conventions of the non-relativistic classical mechanics framework",
          "statement": "**(a) Dimensions.** Write $\\ell$, $\\tau$, $\\mu$ for the three independent *dimension symbols* of length, duration and mass. A **dimension** is an element of the free abelian group generated by $\\ell,\\tau,\\mu$, that is, a triple $(a,b,c)\\in\\mathbb Z^3$ written $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}$; **products and inverses** of dimensions are componentwise addition and negation, so that $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\cdot\\ell^{a'}\\tau^{b'}\\mu^{c'}=\\ell^{a+a'}\\tau^{b+b'}\\mu^{c+c'}$ and $(\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c})^{-1}=\\ell^{-a}\\tau^{-b}\\mu^{-c}$. The **dimensionless** dimension is $\\ell^{0}\\tau^{0}\\mu^{0}$, abbreviated $1$.\n\n**(b) The dimension map.** The conventions assign to the quantities of the framework a **dimension** $[X]$, subject to the rules\n\n$$[XY]=[X][Y],\\qquad [X/Y]=[X][Y]^{-1},\\qquad \\left[\\frac{dX}{du}\\right]=[X][u]^{-1},$$\n\nand to the requirement that a sum $X+Y$ is meaningful only when $[X]=[Y]$, in which case $[X+Y]=[X]$. The third rule applies to a differentiable dependence of a quantity $X$ on a quantity $u$ and covers iterated differentiation, so that $[d^{2}X/du^{2}]=[X][u]^{-2}$. The assignment is part of the conventions: the rules above are consistency requirements on it, not physical laws and not theorems.\n\n**(c) Reference units.** The **metre** is the reference unit of length, the **second** the reference unit of duration and the **kilogram** the reference unit of mass. A quantity of dimension $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}$ is expressed in the unit $\\mathrm m^{a}\\,\\mathrm s^{b}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{c}$: its value is a real multiple of that unit, and the conventions fix how the numerical value of a quantity is read (a coordinate value alone is a real number, and the unit declaration attaches its dimension). The coordinates of an adapted chart of [[def-cm-affine-time-space]] are read with the time coordinate $t$ in seconds, $[t]=\\tau$, and the spatial coordinate components $x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}$ in metres, $[x_{i}]=\\ell$; the slice distance $d_{g}$ of [[def-cm-affine-time-space]] between simultaneous events is read in metres.\n\n**(d) Typed quantity conventions.** The following constructions specify the quantities whose dimensions are recorded here, without adopting their dynamics. Fix a positive integer $N$, a nondegenerate open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with time parameter $t$ in seconds, position curves $x_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of class $C^2$ with components in metres, and constant parameters $m_i>0$ in kilograms, for $1\\le i\\le N$. Derivatives and integrals have the meanings of [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]. These are conditional numerical constructions in a fixed spatial coordinate system; whether they represent a particular system is a separate model assumption.\n\n1. **Velocity and acceleration** are the maps $w_i=x_i':I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $a_i=x_i'':I\\to\\mathbb R^3$, of dimensions $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ and $\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ componentwise.\n2. The **mass operator** is the linear map $\\mathsf M:(\\mathbb R^3)^N\\to(\\mathbb R^3)^N$, $\\mathsf M(z_1,\\ldots,z_N)=(m_1z_1,\\ldots,m_Nz_N)$, with dimension $\\mu$; the **momentum** of label $i$ is $p_i=m_iw_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$, with component dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1}$.\n3. A **force input** is a specified map $F:I\\times O\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$, where $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ is open, its arguments are time, positions and velocities, and each output component has dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$. No regularity or equation equating $F$ with $\\mathsf M x''$ is adopted here. A later model must state both if needed.\n4. For a specified scalar **potential energy** map $U:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R$ of dimension $\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-2}$ and a tuple $x(t)\\in O$, the **kinetic energy** is $T(t)=\\tfrac12\\sum_{i=1}^N m_i\\sum_{j=1}^3(w_i(t))_j^2$, and the **mechanical energy** is $E(t)=T(t)+U(t,x(t))$. Both are real-valued and have dimension $\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-2}$; no conservation or applicability to every interaction is asserted.\n5. Given a scalar map $L:I\\times O\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R$ of the energy dimension and $t_0<t_1$ in $I$, assume $t\\mapsto L(t,x(t),w(t))$ is integrable on $[t_0,t_1]$. Its **action on this curve segment** is the real number $S[x;t_0,t_1]=\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}L(t,x(t),w(t))\\,dt$, with dimension $\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-1}$ (the integration convention multiplies the integrand's dimension by duration). No variational principle is imposed.\n6. Relative to the chosen spatial origin, define the **angular momentum** $J_i:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $J_i(t)=x_i(t)\\times p_i(t)$, where for $r,s\\in\\mathbb R^3$ the operation is explicitly $r\\times s=(r_2s_3-r_3s_2,r_3s_1-r_1s_3,r_1s_2-r_2s_1)$. Its components have dimension $\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-1}$. These quantities depend on the chosen coordinates; dimensions alone assert no transformation or conservation law.\n7. A **rotation matrix** here means a real $3\\times3$ matrix $R$ satisfying $R^{\\mathsf T}R=I_3$ and $\\det R=1$; its entries are dimensionless. A radian angle is an arc length divided by its positive radius, hence dimensionless. A generalized coordinate means a component of a specified local coordinate chart on a configuration space; each such component has its own declared dimension, and only quantities of equal dimension are added.\n\n**(e) Conventions only.** No numerical value of any constant or parameter of a model is fixed by this item: in particular no mass, length or duration of any concrete system is asserted, and no measured value is recorded. Two numerical values that are equal but carry different dimensions are not thereby the same quantity. Where a treatment or a later item suppresses a dimensionful constant by a natural-unit convention, that convention is read as shorthand for the corresponding SI-dimensioned statement with the constant restored, and it changes no dimension fixed here.",
          "strategy": "A conventions item: it defines the dimension group, the dimension map with its product, quotient and derivative rules, the SI reference units, the dimensions of the page’s quantities and the dimensionless radian convention, and asserts no measured value.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conventions for reading the quantities of the framework with dimensions and SI reference units: the dimension symbols and the dimension map, the metre, second and kilogram as reference units, and the dimensions of coordinates, velocity, acceleration, mass and the standard derived quantities. The item fixes dimensions and unit conventions only; it asserts no measured value, adopts no law of nature and makes no claim about any physical system.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 1 Sections 1.1-1.2, chapter pages 1-3 (PDF pages 5-8): the Euclidean metric, velocity and acceleration (Eqs. 1.3-1.4), and the mass of a particle as a scalar constant",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): mass m, momentum p = m v and force as 3-vectors",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Sections 1.1-1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): componentwise differentiation of a vector-valued position and the definitions v = dx/dt, a = d^2x/dt^2",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm01-configuration",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Configuration domain of $N$ labeled particles: open admissible sets, regular holonomic constraints and the mass operator",
          "statement": "Let $N\\ge1$ particle labels with fixed masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$ be given as\nin [[post-cm01-worldlines]], and fix one inertial frame of the model with\nspatial coordinates $x_{i}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ for each label $i$. Identify the\nspace of all position tuples with $\\mathbb R^{3N}=(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$ and\nwrite its points as $x=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N})$; each component $x_{i}$ has\ndimension $\\ell$ ([[def-cm01-units]]).\n\n**(a) Unconstrained branch.** The **unconstrained configuration domain** is an\nopen set $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$, chosen as part of the model. A tuple\n$x\\in Q$ is an **admissible configuration**: an assignment of one position to\neach label at one time. A **singular-force exclusion** is the removal of the\n**collision set**\n\n$$\\Delta:=\\Bigl\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}:\\ \\text{there are }i\\ne j\\text{ with }x_{i}=x_{j}\\Bigr\\}$$\n\nfrom the domain, which is done when the model's force law is not defined at\ncoincident positions: $\\Delta$ is closed (it is the union of the finitely many\nclosed sets $\\{x_{i}=x_{j}\\}$, each the zero set of the continuous map\n$x\\mapsto x_{i}-x_{j}$), so $Q:=\\mathbb R^{3N}\\setminus\\Delta$ is open and is a\nlegitimate configuration domain. Whether the exclusion is part of the model is\na modelling decision recorded with the force law; the framework itself only\nrequires $Q$ to be open and stated.\n\n**(b) Holonomic constraint branch.** Let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ be an open\n**ambient set**, let $k\\in\\mathbb N$ with $1\\le k\\le3N-1$, and let\n\n$$G=(G_{1},\\dots,G_{k}):O\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{k}$$\n\nbe a smooth map, each component carrying a declared dimension and unit fixed\nby the model (a component can always be rescaled by a nonzero constant without\nchanging its zero set, and the rescaling is part of the declaration). Assume:\n\n1. **Nonempty level set:** $G^{-1}(0)\\ne\\varnothing$;\n2. **Rank condition:** the differential $DG(x):\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{k}$\n   is surjective, equivalently has rank $k$, at every $x\\in G^{-1}(0)$.\n\nThen $0$ is a regular value of $G$ in the sense of\n[[def-regular-and-critical-points-and-values]], and the **constrained\nconfiguration space** is $Q:=G^{-1}(0)$. By\n[[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] — applied to the smooth\nmap $G$ from the open subset $O$ of $\\mathbb R^{3N}$ to $\\mathbb R^{k}$, whose\nregular value $0$ has nonempty fibre — $Q$ is an embedded submanifold of\n$\\mathbb R^{3N}$ of dimension $3N-k$ ([[def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart]]).\nThe **degrees of freedom** of this branch of the model is the number\n$$\\dim Q=3N-k.$$ In the sense of [[def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric]], $Q$ together with the inclusion $X:Q\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb R^{3N}$ is then a particle configuration, and the mass metric of that definition is available with the masses $m_{i}$; the configuration is constrained to $Q$ for all times in this branch.\n\n**(c) Time-dependent holonomic constraints.** If the constraint map depends on\ntime,\n\n$$G:\\mathbb R\\times O\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{k},\\qquad (t,x)\\longmapsto G(t,x),$$\n\nwith $G$ smooth and $D_{x}G(t_{0},x)$ of rank $k$ at every $x$ of the nonempty\nset $G(t_{0},\\cdot)^{-1}(0)$, then for each fixed $t_{0}\\in\\mathbb R$ the\n**instantaneous configuration space** $Q_{t_{0}}:=G(t_{0},\\cdot)^{-1}(0)$ is an\nembedded submanifold of dimension $3N-k$ by the same theorem applied to\n$G(t_{0},\\cdot)$. The family $\\{Q_{t_{0}}\\}$ varies with $t_{0}$; a curve\n$t\\mapsto q(t)$ with $G(t,q(t))=0$ is not thereby a curve in one fixed\nmanifold, and the kinematic treatment of such constraints (velocities, the\nreaction forces that maintain them and the distinction between rheonomic and\nscleronomic constraints) belongs to the framework's constraint development and\nis not part of this definition.\n\n**(d) Mass operator.** In the ambient coordinates define the **mass operator**\n$\\mathsf M:\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$ by block multiplication,\n\n$$\\mathsf M(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N}):=(m_{1}x_{1},\\dots,m_{N}x_{N});$$\n\nits matrix in the standard basis is block diagonal with blocks $m_{i}I_{3}$,\nthe **mass matrix** of [[def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric]].\nIts dimension is $\\mu$: it multiplies positions of dimension $\\ell$ by masses\nof dimension $\\mu$. On the constrained branch the mass metric restricts the\nEuclidean inner product to $Q$ as in that item.\n\n**(e) Velocity constraints are a different object.** A **velocity-map\nconstraint** on the open ambient set $O$ is a pair of smooth maps\n$$A:\\mathbb R\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^{k'\\times3N},\\qquad b:\\mathbb R\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^{k'},\\qquad 1\\le k'\\le3N,$$\nwith $A(t,x)$ of constant rank $k'$ everywhere in the declared domain,\nimposing $A(t,x(t))\\dot x(t)+b(t,x(t))=0$ on a $C^1$ curve $x:J\\to O$, where $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a nondegenerate open interval, the parameter $t$ is frame time, and $\\dot x(t):=x'(t)$ is the componentwise derivative in the convention of [[def-cm01-units]]. Equivalently $A(t,x)$ is a linear map\n$\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{k'}$. Each row's output unit is declared, and\nits corresponding component of $b$ has that same unit. The allowed velocities\nat $(t,x)$ form the affine set $\\{v:A(t,x)v+b(t,x)=0\\}$; its differences form\n$\\ker A(t,x)$.\n\nThe law is **locally holonomic-equivalent at $(t_0,x_0)$** if on some open\nneighbourhood $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times O$ of that point there are a $C^2$\nmap $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^{k'}$ with $D_xg$ of rank $k'$ and a $C^2$ matrix map\n$C:V\\to\\mathbb R^{k'\\times k'}$ with $\\det C\\ne0$ such that\n$$A=C\\,D_xg,\\qquad b=C\\,\\partial_tg\\qquad\\text{on }V.$$\nThus the velocity law is locally the derivative of the regular positional\nconstraints $g(t,x)=\\text{constant}$. It is **locally nonholonomic at the\nspecified point** when no such neighbourhood and pair $(g,C)$ exists; a\nnonholonomic model in this local sense declares a point (or a region of\npoints) with this failure. For a homogeneous time-independent law this\nexcludes local position submanifolds whose tangent spaces equal $\\ker A$;\nfor an affine time-dependent law the criterion also includes the drift $b$.\nAbsence of a single global pair alone does not establish local\nnonintegrability. The rank of $A$ counts instantaneous velocity restrictions,\nnot the dimension of a positional configuration manifold; accessibility\nrequires its own definition. Rank changes require a separate model.",
          "strategy": "A definition: it fixes the admissible configuration domains, the collision exclusion, the regular holonomic constraint branch with its degree-of-freedom count stated through the established regular-level theorem, the instantaneous configuration spaces of time-dependent constraints and the mass operator; the velocity-constraint distinction is recorded but not developed.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric",
            "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold",
            "def-regular-and-critical-points-and-values",
            "def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-regular-and-critical-points-and-values": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Definition of the configuration stage of the framework: for $N$ labeled particles of the model, the admissible position tuples in one inertial frame as an open set or as the regular zero set of holonomic constraint functions, the collision exclusion of a singular force model, the degree-of-freedom count, the mass operator assembled from the fixed masses, and constant-rank affine velocity constraints with an explicit local nonintegrability criterion. The item defines vocabulary only; it adopts no force law, no equation of motion and no constitutive assumption, and it asserts no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Section 1.1 'Configuration Spaces', extracted chapter pages 1-2: the configuration of a system of point particles, the configuration space, its dimension as the number of degrees of freedom, and the worked example that two particles at fixed distance have a five-dimensional configuration space",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.1, printed pages 10-13 (PDF pages 16-19): constraints and generalized coordinates, and Section 3.1, printed pages 46-48: configuration space of a mechanical system",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. M. Lee, Introduction to Smooth Manifolds (2003 first edition), Level Sets, printed pages 104-105: the regular level set theorem (used here through its established statement)",
                "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~hrm/palestine/lee-smooth-manifolds.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Coordinate velocity and acceleration of a worldline in an inertial frame, and the spatial velocity and acceleration vectors",
          "statement": "Let an inertial frame $(o,u,(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3}))$ of the model with adapted\ncoordinates $(t,x)$ be fixed, and let $\\gamma:I\\to A$ be the worldline of a\nparticle label described in this frame as in [[post-cm01-worldlines]]; thus\n$\\gamma$ is of class $C^{2}$, parametrized so that $t(\\gamma(s))=s$ for all\n$s\\in I$, and\n\n$$\\gamma(s)=o+su+\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\xi_{j}(s)\\,e_{j}$$\n\nfor the unique coordinate functions $\\xi_{j}:I\\to\\mathbb R$ of the point\n$\\gamma(s)$ in the adapted chart ([[def-cm-affine-time-space]],\n[[lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique]]).\n\n**(a) The coordinate curve.** The **coordinate curve** of $\\gamma$ in the\nframe is\n\n$$x_{\\gamma}:=\\bigl(\\xi_{1},\\xi_{2},\\xi_{3}\\bigr):I\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{3}.$$\n\nIt is of class $C^{2}$ by the regularity clause of [[post-cm01-worldlines]],\nand it determines $\\gamma$ by the display above, since the parameter condition\nfixes the time coordinate. It has dimension $[\\,\\xi_{j}\\,]=\\ell$\n([[def-cm01-units]]).\n\n**(b) Coordinate velocity and acceleration.** The **derivative** of a\nvector-valued map is taken componentwise: for the map $x_{\\gamma}$ above, its\nderivative at $s$ is\n\n$$x_{\\gamma}'(s):=\\bigl(\\xi_{1}'(s),\\xi_{2}'(s),\\xi_{3}'(s)\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere each $\\xi_{j}'(s)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\bigl(\\xi_{j}(s+h)-\\xi_{j}(s)\\bigr)/h$ is\nthe ordinary derivative of a real function. The **coordinate velocity** of the\nlabel in the frame is\n\n$$w(s):=x_{\\gamma}'(s)\\in\\mathbb R^{3},$$\n\nwith components $w_{j}=\\xi_{j}'$, and the **coordinate acceleration** is the\ncoordinate velocity of the velocity,\n\n$$a(s):=w'(s)=x_{\\gamma}''(s)\\in\\mathbb R^{3}.$$\n\nTheir dimensions are $[w_{j}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ and $[a_{j}]=\\ell\\tau^{-2}$, read\nin metres per second and metres per second squared. The triple $w(s)$ is the\ntriple of numbers read in the frame's axes: it is a coordinate description,\nnot an element of spacetime.\n\n**(c) Spatial velocity and acceleration vectors.** For the affine curve\n$\\gamma:I\\to A$, subtraction of events uses the affine displacement in $V$.\nDefine its derivative by the limit in the finite-dimensional vector space\n$V$,\n$$\\gamma'(s):=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\gamma(s+h)-\\gamma(s)}{h}=u+\\sum_j\\xi_j'(s)e_j,$$\nand define $\\gamma''(s)$ as the derivative of the $V$-valued map\n$\\gamma'$. This is the canonical identification of each affine tangent\nspace with $V$; the $C^2$ coordinate regularity guarantees both limits.\nThe frame's time direction\n$u$ and spatial basis identify two elements of the spatial displacement space\n$K$ attached to the motion:\n\n$$\\underline{v}(s):=\\gamma'(s)-u=\\sum_{j=1}^{3}w_{j}(s)\\,e_{j}\\in K,\\qquad \\underline{a}(s):=\\gamma''(s)=\\sum_{j=1}^{3}a_{j}(s)\\,e_{j}\\in K.$$\n\nHere $\\gamma'(s)-u$ indeed lies in $K=\\ker\\tau$, because $\\tau(\\gamma'(s))=\\frac{d}{ds}\\tau(\\gamma(s)-o)=1$ by linearity of $\\tau$ and the parameter condition. The vectors $\\underline{v}(s)$ and $\\underline{a}(s)$ are the **spatial velocity vector** and **spatial acceleration vector** of the label in the frame; the velocity depends on the frame time direction $u$, whereas the acceleration is an intrinsic element of $K$ unchanged by an inertial frame change (time shifts have derivative $1$). Their coordinates in the frame's basis $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$ are the triples $w(s)$ and $a(s)$.\n\n**(d) Speed.** The **speed** of the label in the frame at time $s$ is the\nEuclidean norm $\\|w(s)\\|_{2}=\\sqrt{\\sum_{j}w_{j}(s)^{2}}$, of dimension\n$\\ell\\tau^{-1}$; equivalently $\\|\\underline{v}(s)\\|_{2}=\\sqrt{g(\\underline{v}(s),\\underline{v}(s))}$,\nthe norm induced by the spatial metric, since the basis is $g$-orthonormal.\n\n**(e) Transformation under a change of inertial frame (recorded).** Let a\nsecond inertial frame be given, with coordinates $(t',x')$ and transformation\nparameters $(b,a,v,R)$ as in [[post-cm01-frames]], and let\n$\\eta(t'):=x'(\\gamma(t'-b))\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ be the description of the motion\nin the second frame. Then\n\n$$\\eta'(t')=R\\,w(t'-b)+v,\\qquad \\eta''(t')=R\\,a(t'-b),$$\n\nthat is, in the differentiated variables and abbreviated notation\n$w'=Rw+v$ and $a'=Ra$. This law is clause 2 of the mathematical transformation\nlaw of [[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]], recorded here\nin the model's notation; the definition itself asserts neither law. The\nmodel-level statement of the law, including the frame-independence of the\nproperty $a\\equiv0$, is proved as a theorem later on this page.",
          "strategy": "A definition of notation: the coordinate curve of a worldline in a frame, its componentwise velocity and acceleration, the associated spatial vectors in the frame’s displacement space, the speed, and the recorded (not reproved) transformation law of the mathematical supplier.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
