        {
          "id": "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Galilean transformation of worldlines: velocity and acceleration laws, frame-independence of zero coordinate acceleration, and the group of frame changes",
          "statement": "Adopt the spacetime assumptions of [[post-cm01-spacetime]], the frame\nassumptions of [[post-cm01-frames]] and the worldline assumptions of\n[[post-cm01-worldlines]]. Let\n\n$$F=\\bigl(o,u,(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})\\bigr),\\qquad F'=\\bigl(o',u',(e_{1}',e_{2}',e_{3}')\\bigr)$$\n\nbe two inertial frames of the model with adapted coordinates $(t,x)$ and\n$(t',x')$, and let $\\gamma:I\\to A$ be a worldline of a particle label described\nin $F$, with coordinate curve $x:I\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ and coordinate velocity and\nacceleration $w=x'$ and $a=x''$ in the sense of\n[[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]]. Then:\n\n1. There are $b\\in\\mathbb R$, $a_{0},v\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ and $R\\in SO(3)$\n   ([[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]) such that for every event $p\\in A$\n\n$$t'(p)=t(p)+b,\\qquad x'(p)=R\\,x(p)+v\\,t(p)+a_{0}.$$\n\n   The parameters have dimensions $[b]=\\tau$, $[a_{0,j}]=\\ell$ and\n   $[v_{j}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$, and $R$ is dimensionless\n   ([[def-cm01-units]]); they are the ones that occur in the adopted class of\n   frame changes of [[post-cm01-frames]].\n\n2. Let $\\eta:I+b\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ be the coordinate curve of the same label in\n   $F'$, defined by $\\eta(t'):=x'(\\gamma(t'-b))$ for $t'\\in I+b$. Then $\\eta$\n   is of class $C^{2}$ and\n\n$$\\eta'(t')=R\\,w(t'-b)+v,\\qquad \\eta''(t')=R\\,a(t'-b)\\qquad(t'\\in I+b).$$\n\n3. The coordinate acceleration vanishes identically in one frame exactly when\n   it does in the other:\n\n$$a\\equiv0\\ \\text{on}\\ I\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad \\eta''\\equiv0\\ \\text{on}\\ I+b.$$\n\n   Equivalently, the property \"the coordinate curve is an affine function of\n   the frame time\" is an inertial-frame-independent property of the motion.\n\n4. Write $G(b,a_{0},v,R)$ for the transformation\n   $(t,x)\\mapsto(t+b,\\,Rx+v\\,t+a_{0})$ of the coordinate space. Then\n\n$$G(b_{2},a_{2},v_{2},R_{2})\\circ G(b_{1},a_{1},v_{1},R_{1})=G\\bigl(b_{1}+b_{2},\\ R_{2}a_{1}+v_{2}b_{1}+a_{2},\\ R_{2}v_{1}+v_{2},\\ R_{2}R_{1}\\bigr)$$\n\nand\n\n$$G(b,a_{0},v,R)^{-1}=G\\bigl(-b,\\ -R^{\\mathsf T}(a_{0}-vb),\\ -R^{\\mathsf T}v,\\ R^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr),$$\n\n   with all parameters again in $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times SO(3)$. Consequently the Galilean transformations form a group of transformations of the coordinate space, and the relation on inertial frames \"the two charts differ by a Galilean transformation\" is an equivalence relation.",
          "strategy": "Direct deduction from the adopted spacetime, frame and worldline assumptions together with the mathematical transformation law between adapted charts: the frame change and its induced velocity and acceleration laws, the equivalence of vanishing coordinate acceleration in the two frames, and the group structure of the Galilean transformations by explicit computation.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-spacetime",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm01-spacetime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The kinematic theorem of the framework: the transformation law between two inertial frames of the Newtonian model, the induced laws for the coordinate velocity and acceleration of a worldline, the frame-independence of the property of zero coordinate acceleration, and the group structure of the Galilean transformations. The result is a conditional deduction from the adopted spacetime, frame and worldline assumptions and the cited mathematical transformation law; it asserts no force law, no conservation law and no empirical claim, and it does not by itself identify free motion with zero coordinate acceleration.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2.1, printed page 5 (PDF page 11): all translations, rotations and boosts map inertial frames to inertial frames, and d^2x/dt^2 = 0 is preserved by each of them",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): the ten transformations relating inertial frames, comprising rotations, translations, boosts and time translation",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.1 point (v) and Section 1.2, chapter pages 2-3 (PDF pages 6-7): transfer of the radius vector, velocity and acceleration between frames moving by translation without rotation, and inertial frames as those moving without rotation or acceleration",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 7
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm01-model-limit",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Status audit and model limits of the framework page: primitive data, adopted assumptions, derived objects and what is not claimed",
          "statement": "This remark records the status of the notions of the framework page. It is\nbookkeeping: it asserts no law and no empirical claim, and every mathematical\nstatement it repeats is proved in the item cited.\n\n**Primitive data of a model.** The spacetime data\n$(V,\\tau,g,A,\\text{orientation of }K)$ of [[post-cm01-spacetime]]: the affine\nevent space, the surjective absolute-time covector, the Euclidean spatial\nmetric on the three-dimensional kernel and the fixed spatial orientation.\nThese are the primitive geometric data; no equation constrains them. The\nparticle labels, their worldlines and their positive masses\n$m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}$ are the primitive material data of\n[[post-cm01-worldlines]]. The class of inertial frames is fixed by\n[[post-cm01-frames]] as the adapted charts of the spacetime data, with the\nadopted Galilean transformations between them.\n\n**Adopted assumptions on this page.** That the primitive data are the\nspacetime of a physical model, with absolute simultaneity, Euclidean distance\nwithin slices and no Lorentzian structure, is the postulate\n[[post-cm01-spacetime]]. That the model's frames are its adapted charts, that\nthe changes between them are the Galilean transformations, and that a frame's\nstationary congruence is derived from it, is the postulate\n[[post-cm01-frames]]. That particles are labels with twice continuously\ndifferentiable worldlines parametrized by frame time and fixed positive\nmasses is the postulate [[post-cm01-worldlines]]. The unit and dimension\nconventions of [[def-cm01-units]] are part of the formulation and assert no\nmeasured value.\n\n**Constructed or proved on this page.** The adapted coordinates and the affine\nstructure of the simultaneous slices, with existence and uniqueness of\ncoordinates, are mathematics: [[def-cm-affine-time-space]],\n[[lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique]]. The transformation law\nbetween adapted charts and its kinematic consequences are the mathematical\nlemma [[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]]; the rotation\ngroup and its group properties are [[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]] and\n[[lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group]]. The coordinate velocity and acceleration and\ntheir frame dependence are the definition\n[[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]]. The configuration domain, its collision\nexclusion, its constrained branch with the dimension count and the mass\noperator are the definition [[def-cm01-configuration]], whose dimension\nstatement rests on the established regular-level theorem and whose mass data\nare those of [[def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric]]. The\nframe change laws $w'=Rw+v$, $a'=Ra$, the frame-independence of vanishing\ncoordinate acceleration and the group structure of the Galilean\ntransformations are the physics theorem\n[[pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics]], a conditional deduction from the postulates\nand the cited mathematics.\n\n**Explicitly not claimed on this page.** No force law and no equation of\nmotion: nothing here determines the acceleration of any particle, and the\nidentification of the acceleration term with a force is made on the pages\nthat introduce dynamics. No conservation law and no specified potential or energy for a concrete system: the conditional typed quantity conventions of [[def-cm01-units]] select none.\nNo gravitational interaction; gravitation is an optional force model adopted\nlater with its own coupling parameters. No electromagnetic coupling and no\nelectromagnetic field: coupling the framework to classical electromagnetism\nwould require that framework's fields, sources and coupling assumptions and is\nnot a premise or a consequence here. No relativity: the model contains no\nLorentzian metric, no light cone and no invariant speed; the Galilean\ntransformations of [[post-cm01-frames]] are not derived as a limit of any\nother theory and no Lorentz transformation appears. No quantum correspondence:\nreplacing a classical coordinate or velocity by an operator is not a\nderivation, and no such replacement is made or promised. No thermodynamic or\nstatistical-mechanical statement: equilibrium, entropy and coarse graining are\nnot consequences of the kinematic framework. No measured value of any mass,\nlength, duration or constant, and no observation.",
          "strategy": "A status audit and scope remark: it classifies the page’s objects as primitive, adopted, constructed or proved, lists the dynamics, coupling, relativistic, quantum, thermodynamic and empirical claims deliberately not made, and states the regime and conditional status of the pair’s conclusions.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-spacetime",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm01-configuration",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group",
            "def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm01-spacetime": "physical-assumption",
            "post-cm01-frames": "physical-assumption",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "physical-assumption",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-configuration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics": "physical-result",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-constrained-configuration-and-mass-metric": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A status audit and scope remark for the framework-and-geometry page: which objects are primitive model data, which statements are adopted postulates, which are constructed or proved, and which physical claims are deliberately not made here. Bookkeeping only; it asserts no law, no measurement and no claim about nature beyond the items it cites.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.1 introductory remarks and Section 1.2, chapter pages 1 and 3 (PDF pages 5 and 7): the notions of kinematics are model-level conventions, and Newton's laws are experimental in nature and cannot be derived from purely theoretical arguments",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.4 'Looking Forwards: The Validity of Newtonian Mechanics', printed page 9 (PDF page 15): the domain limitations of Newtonian mechanics",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 8
        }
      ],
