      "order": 113
    },
    {
      "id": "cm01-framework-quantities-and-geometry-examples",
      "title": "Framework, quantities and geometry — Examples",
      "kind": "B",
      "category": "non-relativistic-classical-mechanics",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "cm01-framework-quantities-and-geometry",
      "requires": [
        "cm01-framework-quantities-and-geometry",
        "sine-cosine-and-the-definition-of-pi",
        "cm-math-geometry"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-cm01-galilean-boost-of-a-free-motion",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A worked Galilean boost: the same uniform motion in two inertial frames",
          "statement": "Take the concrete model data\n\n$$V=\\mathbb R^{4},\\qquad \\tau(x^{0},x^{1},x^{2},x^{3})=x^{0},\\qquad K=\\{0\\}\\times\\mathbb R^{3},$$\n\nwith $g$ the standard Euclidean inner product $g\\bigl((0,u),(0,w)\\bigr)=u\\cdot w$ on $K$, the standard orientation of $K$, and $A=\\mathbb R^{4}$ with its standard affine structure over $V$; the coordinates are read with $t$ in seconds and $(x^{1},x^{2},x^{3})$ in metres. Adopt the spacetime, frame and worldline postulates\n([[post-cm01-spacetime]], [[post-cm01-frames]], [[post-cm01-worldlines]]).\n\n**First frame.** $F=(o,u,(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3}))$ with $o=0$,\n$u=(1,0,0,0)$, $e_{1}=(0,1,0,0)$, $e_{2}=(0,0,1,0)$, $e_{3}=(0,0,0,1)$.\nThe adapted coordinates are the standard ones, written\n$(t,x)=(t,x^{1},x^{2},x^{3})$.\n\n**Transformation data.** Choose\n\n$$b=2\\,\\mathrm s,\\qquad a=(0,\\,3\\,\\mathrm m,\\,0),\\qquad v=\\Bigl(\\frac12,\\,0,\\,0\\Bigr)\\ \\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1},\\qquad R=I_{3}.$$\n\n**Second frame constructed from the data.** Put\n$e_{j}'=e_{j}$ for $j=1,2,3$ (the rotation is trivial),\n$u'=u-\\sum_{j}v_{j}e_{j}'=(1,-\\frac12,0,0)$ and\n$o'=o-bu-\\sum_{j}\\bigl(R^{\\mathsf T}(a-vb)\\bigr)_{j}e_{j}=(-2,\\,1,\\,-3,\\,0)$;\nwrite $(t',x')$ for the adapted coordinates of\n$F'=(o',u',(e_{1}',e_{2}',e_{3}'))$.\n\n**The motion.** Let $\\gamma:\\mathbb R\\to A$ be the worldline whose\n$F$-coordinates are\n\n$$\\gamma(s)=\\bigl(s,\\ 1+\\tfrac14 s,\\ 2,\\ 0\\bigr),$$\n\nthat is, the coordinate curve $x(s)=\\bigl(1+\\tfrac14 s,\\ 2,\\ 0\\bigr)$ with\nconstant coordinate velocity\n$w=(\\tfrac14,0,0)\\ \\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$ and zero coordinate\nacceleration.",
          "strategy": "Direct finite computation: construct the second adapted chart from the transformation parameters, check adaptedness and the coordinate relation, exhibit one explicit uniform motion, and verify the velocity and acceleration transformation laws and the frame-independence of zero acceleration on the data, including a spatial-vector cross-check.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-spacetime",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm01-spacetime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics": "physical-result",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A finite worked verification, on explicit numerical data, of the transformation law between two inertial frames of the model and of the velocity and acceleration laws for one uniform motion: checking adaptedness of a constructed frame, the coordinate relation, the parameter condition, and the transformation of coordinate and spatial velocity. It is a computation inside an explicitly specified model and asserts no force law, no empirical claim and no physical law beyond the cited items.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2.1, printed page 5 (PDF page 11): the boost x' = x + v t with constant v, and the preservation of d^2x/dt^2 = 0",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): the inertial-frame definition r = r_0 + v t and the boost among the transformations relating inertial frames",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.1 point (v), chapter page 3 (PDF page 7): the transfer of radius vector, velocity and acceleration between frames moving by translation without rotation",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 8
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm01-rotating-basis-of-a-free-motion",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A rotating spatial basis is not an inertial frame: nonzero coordinate acceleration of a uniform motion",
          "statement": "Take the concrete model data of [[post-cm01-spacetime]]:\n$V=\\mathbb R^{4}$ with coordinates $(x^{0},x^{1},x^{2},x^{3})$,\n$\\tau=x^{0}$, $K=\\{0\\}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$ with the standard inner product\n$g$, $A=\\mathbb R^{4}$ with its standard affine structure, the standard\norientation; the first inertial frame $F$ is the standard one,\n$o=0$, $u=(1,0,0,0)$, $e_{j}$ the standard basis, with coordinates\n$(t,x)=(t,x^{1},x^{2},x^{3})$, times in seconds and lengths in metres.\n\nLet $\\omega>0$ and define a **time-dependent orthonormal basis field** of $K$\nby\n\n$$\\tilde e_{1}(s)=\\cos(\\omega s)\\,e_{1}+\\sin(\\omega s)\\,e_{2},\\qquad \\tilde e_{2}(s)=-\\sin(\\omega s)\\,e_{1}+\\cos(\\omega s)\\,e_{2},\\qquad \\tilde e_{3}(s)=e_{3},$$\n\nand the associated rotation matrix\n\n$$R(s)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\omega s)&-\\sin(\\omega s)&0\\\\ \\sin(\\omega s)&\\cos(\\omega s)&0\\\\ 0&0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\text{so that}\\qquad (e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})\\,R(s)=(\\tilde e_{1}(s),\\tilde e_{2}(s),\\tilde e_{3}(s)).$$\n\nFor a worldline that is at rest in $F$ at the position $(1\\,\\mathrm m,0,0)$,\nwith $F$-coordinate curve $x(s)=(1,0,0)$, define the\n**rotating-basis coordinate curve** $y(s)$ by the expansion\n$x(s)=R(s)\\,y(s)$, that is, $y(s):=R(s)^{\\mathsf T}x(s)$; the time coordinate\nis unchanged because the basis field rotates in $K$ only.",
          "strategy": "Direct computation with the trigonometric data: pointwise orthonormality of the rotating basis field, explicit differentiation of the rotating-basis coordinate curve of a motion at rest in the inertial frame, verification of the moving-frame acceleration identity of the mathematical supplier, and the deduction that the rotating basis is not an inertial frame.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics",
            "lem-cm-moving-frame-acceleration-identity",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics": "physical-result",
            "lem-cm-moving-frame-acceleration-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked kinematic example of the framework: a uniformly rotating spatial basis field in a fixed inertial frame, and the coordinate acceleration of a worldline that is at rest in the inertial frame. The example verifies the rotating-frame acceleration identity of its mathematical supplier and shows that the rotating basis is not an inertial frame of the model. It asserts no force law, makes no claim about fictitious forces or measured effects, and contains no relativity content.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 6.1.1 'Velocity and Acceleration in a Rotating Frame', printed pages 94-95 (PDF pages 100-101): Eq. (6.4), (d^2r/dt^2)_S = (d^2r/dt^2)_S' + 2 omega x (dr/dt)_S' + omega-dot x r + omega x (omega x r), and the observation that a free particle does not travel in a straight line in the rotating frame",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 3.1.1, printed pages 47-49 (PDF pages 53-55): moving orthonormal bases, the angular velocity vector and the general acceleration identity",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 8
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm01-coordinate-velocity-is-frame-dependent",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Coordinate velocity is not an inertial-frame-independent quantity",
          "statement": "In the model of [[post-cm01-spacetime]], [[post-cm01-frames]] and\n[[post-cm01-worldlines]], the coordinate velocity of a worldline is\nindependent of the inertial frame in which it is computed: if $x$ and $\\xi$\nare the coordinate curves of the same worldline in two inertial frames with\ntime coordinates $t$ and $t'$, then\n\n$$\\xi'(t')=x'(t)\\qquad\\text{at corresponding times }t'=t+b.$$",
          "strategy": "Direct refutation: compute the coordinate velocity of one explicit worldline in the two inertial frames of the worked boost example and exhibit the inequality of the two velocity triples, against the general claim of frame-independence.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm01-spacetime",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics",
            "ex-cm01-galilean-boost-of-a-free-motion"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm01-spacetime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics": "physical-result",
            "ex-cm01-galilean-boost-of-a-free-motion": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A refutation, on explicit data of the model, of the claim that the coordinate velocity of a worldline is independent of the inertial frame used to describe it: two inertial frames of the worked boost example give distinct coordinate velocities for one worldline. The item asserts no force law and no empirical claim; the correct frame-change law it reports is the velocity law of the cited physics theorem.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2.1, printed page 5 (PDF page 11): boosts x' = x + v t change the coordinate description while the condition d^2x/dt^2 = 0 is preserved",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.1 point (v), chapter page 3 (PDF page 7): the definitions of the vectors r, v and a depend on the chosen reference frame and must be related between frames",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
