            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The local existence, uniqueness and maximal-extension statement for the adopted Newtonian force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] on an open configuration domain: every initial datum with a configuration in the domain and an arbitrary coordinate velocity admits a unique motion through it, locally in time, and there is a unique maximal motion of which every motion with that datum is a restriction. The result is a conditional deduction from the adopted force postulate and the cited mathematical ODE theorems; it gives no global existence, no bound on the interval, no empirical statement and no claim that any physical system realizes the model.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 7.1.1, printed page 143 (PDF page 146): the first-order form of the equations of motion and the local existence and uniqueness theorem stated for continuously differentiable right-hand sides, with deterministic evolution from it",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 2.2, Theorem 2.2 (printed pages 36-38), and Section 2.6, Theorem 2.13 (printed page 51): local existence and uniqueness and the unique maximal solution",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): the second-order Newtonian equation of motion in an inertial frame",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 10
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm02-continuation",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Maximal Newtonian motions and the compact-continuation criterion: finite-time breakdown requires escape from every compact subset of the time-state domain",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with time window\n$I_{0}$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$, masses\n$m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$ and force map $F$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the\nstate variable, locally uniformly in time; let $D=I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb\nR^{3})^{N}$, $\\mathcal F(t,x,v)=\\bigl(v,\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)\\bigr)$,\n$U:=Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$, and let motions, maximal motions and the state\ncurve $z=(x,\\dot x)$ be as in [[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]. Fix an initial datum\n$(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$, write\n$z_{0}=(x_{0},v_{0})$, and let $x_{\\max}:J_{\\max}\\to Q$ be the maximal motion\nwith this datum, with state curve $z=(x_{\\max},\\dot x_{\\max}):J_{\\max}\\to U$\nand $J_{\\max}=(a,b)$ its maximal interval, $-\\infty\\le a<b\\le+\\infty$.\n\n1. **Existence and uniqueness of the maximal motion.** $J_{\\max}$ is an open\n   interval containing $t_{0}$ and contained in $I_{0}$, and every motion with\n   the datum is a restriction of $x_{\\max}$.\n2. **Breakdown criterion.** If $b<+\\infty$, then for every compact\n   $K\\subseteq D$ and every $\\eta>0$ with $(b-\\eta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}\\neq\\varnothing$\n   there is $t\\in(b-\\eta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ with $(t,z(t))\\notin K$. Equivalently:\n   if there are a compact $K\\subseteq D$ and $\\delta>0$ with\n   $(t,z(t))\\in K$ for all $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$, then\n   $x_{\\max}$ extends to a motion of the model on a strictly larger interval\n   than $J_{\\max}$ — so $J_{\\max}$ cannot have right endpoint $b$.\n3. **Autonomous case.** Suppose $F$ does not depend on its time argument:\n   $F(t,x,v)=F_{0}(x,v)$ for a map $F_{0}:U\\to(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$ satisfying\n   the standing regularity of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iii).\n   Then the associated field is autonomous,\n   $\\mathcal F(t,x,v)=(v,\\mathsf M^{-1}F_{0}(x,v))$, the equations do not use\n   the time coordinate, and **the model is read on the extended time window\n   $\\mathbb R$** (the standing hypotheses are unchanged by the extension).\n   For this clause, take the same autonomous force law as a model on the\n   extended time window $I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, and let $J_{\\max}=(a,b)$ be the\n   maximal interval for that extended-time model. If $b<+\\infty$ and there\n   are a compact $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ and $\\delta>0$ with $z(t)\\in K_{0}$ for\n   all $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$, then the state curve extends past\n   $b$ as a solution of the same model, contradicting maximality; thus no\n   finite maximal endpoint has such a terminal compact trap. In particular,\n   if one compact $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ contains $z(t)$ for every\n   $t\\in J_{\\max}\\cap[t_{0},+\\infty)$, then $b=+\\infty$ and the motion is\n   defined for all forward times $t\\ge t_{0}$.\n4. **Reading.** Suppose the time window is all of $\\mathbb R$ (the\n   time-dependent case included) and $b<+\\infty$. Then for every compact\n   $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ and every $\\delta>0$ there is $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ with\n   $z(t)\\notin K_{0}$: near a finite breakdown the state leaves every compact\n   subset of the open state domain $U=Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$. Consequently\n   either the state is unbounded on every terminal interval, meaning that for\n   every $M,\\delta>0$ some $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ has\n   $\\lVert z(t)\\rVert_{2}>M$, or $Q\\ne\\mathbb R^{3N}$ and the positions\n   approach the excluded configurations in the following sense: for every\n   $\\varepsilon,\\delta>0$ there are $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ and\n   $y\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}\\setminus Q$ with\n   $\\lVert x_{\\max}(t)-y\\rVert_{2}<\\varepsilon$. If $Q=\\mathbb R^{3N}$, the\n   unbounded-state alternative necessarily holds.",
          "strategy": "A conditional deduction from the compact-continuation and escape theorems applied to the associated first-order system: a maximal motion with a finite endpoint leaves every compact subset of the open state domain, the autonomous case extends past a trapped endpoint, and the reading clause records the unbounded-state or excluded-configuration alternatives.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp",
            "def-cm01-configuration",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution",
            "thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes",
            "cor-cm-finite-time-breakdown-requires-escape",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "physical-assumption",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp": "physical-result",
            "def-cm01-configuration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cm-finite-time-breakdown-requires-escape": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The continuation and breakdown criterion for the adopted Newtonian force model: the maximal motion of an initial datum is unique; at a finite right endpoint its time-state graph leaves every compact subset of the open time-state domain. On the extended time window R, the state leaves every compact subset of the open state domain; equivalently, it is unbounded on every terminal interval or, if it is bounded on some terminal interval and Q is proper, positions approach the excluded configurations in the explicitly stated sense. A finite right boundary of a finite time window is a separate possible endpoint. An autonomous force whose state is trapped in a compact subset of the state domain extends past the putative endpoint. The result is a conditional deduction from the adopted force postulate and the cited mathematical continuation theorems; it asserts no global existence for particular force laws, no empirical statement and no claim that any physical system realizes the model.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 2.6 'Extensibility of solutions', Corollary 2.15 (compact continuation) and Corollary 2.16 (escape of every compact set), printed pages 50-53",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Julien Arino, MATH 8430 Fundamental Theory of Ordinary Differential Equations (University of Manitoba), Section 1.3 'Continuation of solutions', Lemma 1.3.1, Theorem 1.3.3 and Corollary 1.3.4, printed pages 24-28: a maximal solution whose right endpoint is finite approaches the boundary of the domain",
                "url": "https://julien-arino.github.io/assets/pdf/courses/math8430_lecture_notes.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 7.1.1, printed page 143 (PDF page 146): deterministic evolution from the local existence and uniqueness theorem, with no global-existence claim",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 11
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm02-uniform-field",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The uniform-force model: explicit global motion of a label under a constant declared field, with uniqueness, dimensions and frame covariance",
          "statement": "Work in the force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with $N$ labels,\ntime window $I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R^{3N}$, fixed\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, and fix a vector\n$\\mathbf g\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ with dimension $[\\mathbf g]=\\ell\\tau^{-2}$,\nread in metres per second squared ([[def-cm01-units]]); $\\mathbf g$ is a\n**declared parameter** of the model, not a measured value. The **uniform-force\nmodel** is the force map\n\n$$F_{i}(t,x,v):=m_{i}\\,\\mathbf g\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,N),$$\n\nconstant in the state, so that every label has the same coordinate\nacceleration $\\mathbf g$ independently of its mass, position and velocity\n([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]). Verify the following:\n\n1. $F$ satisfies the standing continuity and state-local-Lipschitz hypotheses\n   of clause (iii) of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]], with state-Lipschitz\n   constant $0$ on every cylinder.\n2. For the initial datum $x(0)=x_{0}$, $\\dot x(0)=v_{0}$ the explicit curve,\n   defined for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n   $$x_i(t)=x_{0,i}+v_{0,i}t+\\tfrac12\\mathbf g\\,t^{2}\\quad(i=1,\\dots,N),\\qquad x(t):=(x_1(t),\\dots,x_N(t)).$$\n\n   is a motion of the model on the whole time window $\\mathbb R$ and is the\n   unique maximal motion with this datum; its restriction to $[0,\\infty)$ is\n   therefore also a motion.\n3. The dimension bookkeeping of the terms is consistent: the parameter has\n   dimension length per time squared, the force components have dimension\n   $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$, and the quadratic term has dimension length.\n4. Under a change of inertial frame the model is covariant: in the second\n   frame the same interaction is again a uniform-force model with parameter\n   $\\mathbf g'=R\\mathbf g$, $R\\in SO(3)$.",
          "strategy": "A worked verification: check the constant force map against the standing hypotheses with Lipschitz constant 0, verify the explicit quadratic motion and its initial data by differentiation, conclude uniqueness and global maximality from the initial-value theorem, and compute the dimension and frame-covariance statements.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp",
            "pthm-cm02-continuation",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "def-cm01-units",
