        {
          "id": "cex-cm02-nonunique-flow-for-a-continuous-force",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Continuity of the force map alone does not give unique motions: two distinct Newtonian motions through one datum for $F=\\lambda\\sqrt{|x_1|/\\ell_*}\\,e_1$",
          "statement": "Consider the framework's equation of motion with one label of unit mass\n($m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, [[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)), time window\n$I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R^{3}$, and a force map\n$F:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ with\nforce components of dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ ([[def-cm01-units]]) that is\n**merely continuous** — that is, the standing hypotheses of clause (iii) of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with the state-local-Lipschitz requirement\ndropped. Then for every initial datum $(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})$ there is at most\none motion of the model through that datum, on every interval containing\n$t_{0}$.",
          "strategy": "A direct refutation: exhibit the continuous unit-mass force map F(x)=m sqrt(|x_1|) e_1 and two distinct C^2 motions through the rest datum (zero and a quartic-profile motion), verify the equation by differentiation, and record that the map fails exactly the state-local-Lipschitz clause, so the framework uniqueness theorem is unaffected.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable",
            "def-derivative",
            "def-continuity-real",
            "def-abs-value",
            "def-interval",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions",
            "thm-composition-of-continuous-functions",
            "thm-continuous-inverse",
            "lem-derivative-of-a-power",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp": "physical-result",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-abs-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-composition-of-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-inverse": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-derivative-of-a-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A refutation, inside the framework's equation of motion, of the claim that a merely continuous force map suffices for uniqueness through an initial datum: the unit-mass model in three dimensions with force $F(x)=\\lambda\\sqrt{|x_{1}|/\\ell_{*}}e_{1}$, where $[\\lambda]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ and $[\\ell_*]=\\ell$, is continuous but not state-locally-Lipschitz at the origin and exhibits two distinct $C^{2}$ motions through the rest datum. The item refutes only the weakened variant of the force postulate in which continuity replaces the local-Lipschitz clause; it asserts no motion of any physical body, no measurement and no empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 2.2, printed pages 36-38, and Section 2.6, printed page 51: the role of the Lipschitz hypothesis in the Picard iteration and standard examples of nonunique solutions of continuous right-hand sides",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 7.1.1, printed page 143 (PDF page 146): the existence and uniqueness statement assumes continuous first derivatives of the right-hand side, not mere continuity",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): the force map as model input whose regularity is part of the formulation",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 11
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm02-singular-collision-finite-time",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "An external-source singularity reached in finite time: the inverse-square external model has a motion whose maximal interval is bounded above",
          "statement": "Let $m>0$ and $k>0$ have dimensions $[m]=\\mu$ and\n$[k]=\\mu\\ell^{3}\\tau^{-2}$, respectively ([[def-cm01-units]]). Consider the\nforce model of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] for one label with time window\n$I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, external-source-excluded configuration domain\n$Q=\\mathbb R^{3}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ ([[def-cm01-configuration]] clause (a)), mass\n$m$, and the external potential\n\n$$U(x)=-\\frac{k}{\\lvert x\\rvert},\\qquad U:Q\\to\\mathbb R,$$\n\nof the potential branch of [[post-cm02-interactions]] clause (iii), so that the\nforce map is $F(x)=-\\nabla U(x)=-k\\,x/\\lvert x\\rvert^{3}$ and the equation of\nmotion is\n\n$$m\\,\\ddot x(t)=-\\frac{k\\,x(t)}{\\lvert x(t)\\rvert^{3}}\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x(t)\\neq0).$$\n\nFor this single-label model the distinct-label collision set $\\Delta$ of\n[[def-cm01-configuration]] clause (a) is empty; the excluded point is the\nprescribed external-source singularity, not a collision of model labels.\n\nThen every motion of this model is defined for all real times; equivalently,\nthe maximal interval of every motion is all of $\\mathbb R$.",
          "strategy": "A direct refutation: construct the explicit radial motion r(t)=r_0(1-t/T)^{2/3} for the inverse-square external potential, verify the equation and the stated initial data by differentiation, and show no extension past the finite collision time T exists because a motion would have to pass through the excluded center; the escape alternative of the continuation theorem is exhibited.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "post-cm02-interactions",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp",
            "pthm-cm02-continuation",
            "ex-cm02-force-model-regularity-checks",
