    {
      "id": "cm03-momentum-work-and-energy-balances",
      "title": "Momentum, work and energy balances",
      "category": "non-relativistic-classical-mechanics",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "cm03-momentum-work-and-energy-balances-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "cm02-forces-initial-data-and-newtonian-flow",
        "line-integrals-and-the-gradient-theorem",
        "cm-math-rigid",
        "cm01-framework-quantities-and-geometry",
        "cm-math-balances",
        "cm-math-geometry"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Total mass, total momentum, centre of mass, angular momentum, torque, kinetic energy, power and work of a particle model in an inertial frame",
          "statement": "Fix a model of the force branch [[post-cm02-force-superposition]]: $N\\ge1$\nparticle labels with fixed masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$\n([[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)), a time window $I_{0}\\subseteq\\mathbb\nR$, an open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ with its collision\nset removed when the model's force law is singular there\n([[def-cm01-configuration]]), and a force map\n$$F:I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}\\longrightarrow(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$$\nwith the standing continuity and state-local-Lipschitz hypotheses of clause\n(iii) of that postulate. Fix one inertial frame of the model with adapted\ncoordinates $(t,x)$ ([[post-cm01-frames]]), and let $J\\subseteq I_{0}$ be an\ninterval with nonempty interior ([[def-interval]]). All sums below range over\n$i=1,\\dots,N$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]]), and $u\\times v$, $\\langle u,v\\rangle$\nand $\\lVert u\\rVert_{2}$ are the cross product, the Euclidean inner product and\nthe Euclidean norm on $\\mathbb R^{3}$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]],\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).\n\n**(a) Curve families and motions.** A **curve family** of the model is a map\n$x:J\\to Q$, $x=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N})$, of class $C^{2}$ on $J$\n([[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]]); its **coordinate velocities** are\n$w_{i}:=x_{i}'$ on $J$, written $w=(w_{1},\\dots,w_{N}):J\\to(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$.\nA curve family is a **motion** of the model when\n$$m_{i}\\ddot x_{i}(t)=F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),w(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,N,\\ t\\in J),$$\nthe notion of clause (c) of [[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]. Every quantity of\nthis definition is defined for an arbitrary curve family; the balance theorems\nof this page state which of their properties use the equations of motion.\nAlong a curve family the force map is evaluated at $(t,x(t),w(t))\\in\nI_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$; when the model invokes the\nexternal-plus-pair presentation of clause (i) of [[post-cm02-interactions]],\nthe maps $F_{i}^{\\mathrm{ext}}$ and $F_{ij}$ are evaluated at their declared\narguments on the motion's state graph, where the presentation is an identity\nfor $F_{i}$.\n\n**(b) Total mass, total momentum, centre of mass, relative positions.** Define\n$$M:=\\sum_{i}m_{i}>0,\\qquad p_{i}:=m_{i}w_{i},\\qquad P(t):=\\sum_{i}p_{i}(t),\\qquad X(t):=M^{-1}\\sum_{i}m_{i}x_{i}(t),\\qquad \\rho_{i}:=x_{i}-X.$$\nHere $p_{i}$ is the **momentum** of label $i$, $P$ the **total momentum**, $X$\nthe **centre of mass** and $\\rho_{i}$ the **relative position** of label $i$.\nThe dimensions are $[M]=\\mu$, $[p_{i}]=[P]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ (read in\n$\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$) and $[X]=[\\rho_{i}]=\\ell$\n([[def-cm01-units]]). Since $x_{i}$ is $C^{2}$, each $p_{i}$ is $C^{1}$ and\neach of $P$, $X$, $\\rho_{i}$ is differentiable, as a finite sum of\ndifferentiable curves ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]); write\n$$V:=X'$$\nfor the **centre-of-mass velocity**. The algebraic identities\n$\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\rho_{i}=0$, $P=MV$ and the splitting of the kinetic sum\n$\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\lVert w_{i}\\rVert_{2}^{2}$ are proved in the kinetic-splitting\ntheorem of this page; they are not part of this definition.\n\n**(c) Angular momentum, torque, internal torque sum.** Let a **reference\ncurve** $o:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ of class $C^{1}$ be given: a point about which\nmoments are taken, allowed to move (a constant map $o\\equiv o_{0}$ is the\nfixed-origin case). Define the **angular momentum about $o$** and the **total\ntorque about $o$** by\n$$J_{o}(t):=\\sum_{i}\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-o(t)\\bigr)\\times p_{i}(t),\\qquad T_{o}(t):=\\sum_{i}\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-o(t)\\bigr)\\times F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),w(t)\\bigr),$$\nwith dimensions $[J_{o}]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$ (read in\n$\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m^{2}\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$) and\n$[T_{o}]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ (read in $\\mathrm N\\,\\mathrm m$). When the\nmodel invokes the external-plus-pair presentation of clause (i) of\n[[post-cm02-interactions]], define in addition the **external torque** and the\n**internal torque sum**\n$$T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t):=\\sum_{i}\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-o(t)\\bigr)\\times F_{i}^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\bigl(t,x_{i}(t),w_{i}(t)\\bigr),\\qquad I(t):=\\sum_{i<j}\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-x_{j}(t)\\bigr)\\times F_{ij}\\bigl(t,x_{i}(t),x_{j}(t),w_{i}(t),w_{j}(t)\\bigr).$$\nThe sum $I$ does not involve $o$; it is defined whenever the pair\npresentation is declared. The identity $T_{o}=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}+I$ uses the\nantisymmetry $F_{ji}=-F_{ij}$ of clause (ii)1 of that postulate to reindex the\nordered pairs and is proved as step 1.1 of the angular-momentum balance of\nthis page.\n\n**(d) Kinetic energy, power, work.** Define the **kinetic energy** and the\n**power of the total force** along the curve family by\n$$K(t):=\\frac12\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\lVert w_{i}(t)\\rVert_{2}^{2},\\qquad \\mathcal P(t):=\\sum_{i}\\bigl\\langle F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),w(t)\\bigr),\\,w_{i}(t)\\bigr\\rangle,$$\nof dimensions $[K]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ (read in joules) and\n$[\\mathcal P]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-3}$ (read in watts), and for\n$t_{0}<t_{1}$ in $J$ the **work of the total force** over $[t_{0},t_{1}]$ by\n$$W([t_{0},t_{1}]):=\\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\\mathcal P(t)\\,dt,$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ (read in joules); $\\mathcal P$ is\ncontinuous on $J$ because $F$, $x$ and $w$ are continuous, so the Riemann\nintegral exists ([[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]], see the page\n`the-riemann-integral`). The **potential energy** is not defined by this item:\nit belongs to the potential branch of the model and is defined in the\npotential-and-mechanical-energy item of this page.\n\n**(e) Frame and reference-point dependence.** The quantities $p_{i}$, $P$,\n$X$, $\\rho_{i}$, $V$, $K$, $\\mathcal P$ and $W$ are read in the one inertial\nframe fixed above, and $J_{o}$, $T_{o}$, $T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}$, $I$ are read\nabout the reference curve $o$ in that frame. They are not invariant under a\nchange of frame or reference point: their transformation laws under a change\nof inertial frame are stated and proved in the frame-transformation item of\nthis page, and every conservation statement involving them names the frame\nand the reference point in which it is made.\n\n**(f) Status.** This item is a definition: it introduces symbols, their\ndomains, regularity, dimensions and frame dependence from the model's data.\nIt adopts no force law beyond the force branch already fixed, asserts no\nbalance of any of these quantities and records no measured value.",
          "strategy": "A definition: it fixes the total mass, total momentum, centre of mass, relative positions, angular momentum about a reference curve, total/external/internal torques, kinetic energy, power and work of a force model in one inertial frame, with domains, regularity, dimensions and frame dependence; it adopts no balance and no measured value.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "post-cm02-interactions",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-configuration",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-interval",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm02-interactions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-configuration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Vocabulary and dimension conventions for the mechanical quantities of a force model in one inertial frame: the total mass, total momentum, centre of mass, relative positions and centre-of-mass velocity; the angular momentum, total torque, external torque and internal torque sum about a reference curve; the kinetic energy, the power of the total force and the work over a time interval. The item defines these symbols from the model's particles and force map and fixes their frames; it asserts no balance, no conservation law and no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.3, printed pages 5-9 (PDF pages 11-15): the total mass M and centre of mass R = sum_i m_i r_i / M, the total momentum P = sum_i p_i, the total angular momentum L = sum_i L_i, the total kinetic energy T = (1/2) sum_i m_i rdot_i^2, and the definitions F^ext and tau^ext = sum_i r_i x F_i^ext",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.3, chapter pages 6-10 (PDF pages 10-14): the definitions T = m v^2 / 2 (Eq. 1.19), the angular momentum L = r x p of a particle (Eq. 1.31), the total angular momentum of a system (Eq. 1.36), and the torque tau = r x F (Eq. 1.34)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 10
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Total momentum equals total mass times centre-of-mass velocity, and the kinetic energy splits into centre-of-mass and relative parts (two-body reduced mass)",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model, the inertial frame and the notation of\n[[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]]: masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, a\ncurve family $x:J\\to Q$ of class $C^{2}$ on an interval $J$ with nonempty\ninterior, coordinate velocities $w_{i}=x_{i}'$, and the quantities\n$$M=\\sum_{i}m_{i},\\qquad P=\\sum_{i}m_{i}w_{i},\\qquad X=M^{-1}\\sum_{i}m_{i}x_{i},\\qquad \\rho_{i}=x_{i}-X,\\qquad V=X',\\qquad K=\\frac12\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\lVert w_{i}\\rVert_{2}^{2}.$$\nThen:\n\n1. $P=MV$ on $J$, that is $X'=M^{-1}P$, and the identities\n   $$\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\rho_{i}=0,\\qquad \\sum_{i}m_{i}\\rho_{i}'=0$$\n   hold on $J$;\n2. for every $t\\in J$,\n   $$K(t)=\\frac12M\\lVert V(t)\\rVert_{2}^{2}+\\frac12\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\lVert\\rho_{i}'(t)\\rVert_{2}^{2};$$\n3. if $N=2$, then with $r:=x_{1}-x_{2}$ and $\\mu:=m_{1}m_{2}/M$ one has, for\n   every $t\\in J$,\n   $$K(t)=\\frac12M\\lVert V(t)\\rVert_{2}^{2}+\\frac12\\mu\\lVert r'(t)\\rVert_{2}^{2}.$$\n\nThe statement is an identity for the stated curves and masses in the fixed\nframe; it uses no equation of motion, no property of the force map and no\nconservation claim. The values are frame-dependent; the transformation laws\nare the frame-transformation item of this page.",
          "strategy": "A conditional identity: apply the mathematical centre-of-mass and kinetic-splitting theorem to the model's curves and masses, translate its notation into the model's momentum and kinetic energy, and read off the two-body reduced-mass form; no equation of motion and no force structure is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-interval",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The algebraic identities of a curve family of a force model in one inertial frame: the total momentum is the total mass times the centre-of-mass velocity, the mass-weighted relative positions sum to zero together with their derivatives, and the total kinetic energy equals the centre-of-mass kinetic energy plus the mass-weighted relative kinetic energy, with the two-body reduced-mass form. The result is a conditional identity for the model's masses and curves and asserts no conservation law, no force structure and no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.3, Eqs. (1.15) and (1.22)-(1.23), printed pages 6-8 (PDF pages 12-14): the centre of mass R = sum_i m_i r_i / M and the kinetic-energy decomposition T = (1/2) M Rdot^2 + (1/2) sum_i m_i rtilde_i^2",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 3.4, Eqs. (3.30)-(3.35), printed pages 40-42: the centre-of-mass and reduced-mass construction for two bodies",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 11
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm03-momentum-balance",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Total momentum balance and centre-of-mass equation for a model with antisymmetric pair forces",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model and one inertial frame of\n[[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]], and suppose in addition that the\nmodel invokes the external-plus-pair presentation of clause (i) of\n[[post-cm02-interactions]],\n$$F_{i}(t,z)=F_{i}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t,x_{i},v_{i})+\\sum_{j\\ne i}F_{ij}(t,x_{i},x_{j},v_{i},v_{j}),$$\nwith the antisymmetry clause (ii)1 of that postulate,\n$F_{ij}(t,x_{i},x_{j},v_{i},v_{j})=-F_{ji}(t,x_{j},x_{i},v_{j},v_{i})$ on the\ncommon domain. Let $x:J\\to Q$ be a motion of the model on an interval $J$ with\nnonempty interior, with quantities $M$, $P$, $X$, $V$ as in the definition,\nand write\n$$\\sigma(t):=\\sum_{i}F_{i}^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\bigl(t,x_{i}(t),w_{i}(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in J)$$\nfor the **total external force along the motion**. Then:\n\n1. $P$ is of class $C^{1}$ on $J$ and\n   $$\\dot P(t)=\\sigma(t)\\qquad(t\\in J):$$\n   the total momentum changes at the rate of the total external force, the\n   internal pair forces cancelling in the sum;\n2. if $\\sigma\\equiv0$ on $J$, then $P$ is constant on $J$ and $V=M^{-1}P$ is\n   constant on $J$;\n3. $X$ is of class $C^{2}$ on $J$ and\n   $$M\\,X''(t)=\\sigma(t)\\qquad(t\\in J):$$\n   the centre of mass obeys the equation of a single particle of mass $M$\n   under the total external force.\n\nThe statement assumes the pair presentation and its antisymmetry; for a model\nwhose force map is not presented through such pairs it asserts nothing, and\nthe pair forces of the presentation need not be central (centrality is used\nonly in the angular-momentum balance of this page). No empirical content is\nclaimed.",
          "strategy": "A conditional deduction: evaluate the model's external-plus-pair presentation with antisymmetric pair forces along a motion, apply the mathematical momentum-balance identity, and derive the conservation criterion and the centre-of-mass equation from the definition's identities.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
            "pthm-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "post-cm02-interactions",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "thm-cm-momentum-and-angular-momentum-balance",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-interval",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting": "physical-result",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "physical-assumption",
            "post-cm02-interactions": "physical-assumption",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-cm-momentum-and-angular-momentum-balance": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The momentum balance of a model that declares the external-plus-pair force presentation with antisymmetric pair forces: along every motion the total momentum changes at the rate of the total external force, it is constant on an interval exactly when the total external force vanishes there, and the centre of mass obeys the single-particle equation with the total mass and the total external force. The result is a conditional deduction from the adopted force and interaction posts; it asserts no conservation for models without the antisymmetry presentation and no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.3, Eqs. (1.12)-(1.16), printed pages 5-6 (PDF pages 11-12): F_i = sum_j F_ij + F_i^ext with F_ij = -F_ji, the cancellation of internal forces, Pdot = F^ext and M Rddot = F^ext",
