          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The potential branch of the force model: a scalar potential representing the force map through the Euclidean gradient, with or without declared residual forces; the potential and mechanical energy along a curve family; the non-uniqueness of the potential under addition of a function of time; the distinction between closed and exact time- and velocity-independent force fields, with local potentials on star-shaped subdomains and the failure of global exactness on a punctured domain; and equipotential level sets. The item defines the branch and records cited mathematical facts; it asserts no conservation law, no measured value and no global potential for an arbitrary closed field.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.3, Eqs. (1.22)-(1.27) and footnote 15, chapter pages 7-8 (PDF pages 11-12): the potential (conservative) force F = -nabla U, the work of potential forces as U(A) - U(B), mechanical energy E = T + U, and the additive constant in U",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.3.2, Eqs. (1.24)-(1.25) and the conservative-force conditions, printed page 8 (PDF page 14): conservative external and internal forces from potentials and the total potential energy V = sum_i V_i + sum_{i<j} V_ij",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis II, Section 9.3 Example 9.3.7 (path independence and the vortex field) and J.-B. Campesato, Poincare Lemma, sections 1-2: the closed field on the punctured plane with nonzero circulation is not exact, while closed fields on star-shaped domains are exact with the displayed radial potential",
                "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_pathind.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 11
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm03-energy-balance",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Mechanical energy balance for a potential presentation: $E'=\\partial_tU$ plus the residual power, conservation for a static potential with no residual forces, and the integrated work form",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model, one inertial frame, and the potential presentation of\n[[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause (a): a $C^{2}$ potential\n$$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ and residual forces $d_{i}$ of the standing\nregularity, with\n$$F_{i}(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x)+d_{i}(t,x,v)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,N)$$\non the state domain. Let $x:J\\to Q$ be a **motion** of the model on an\ninterval $J$ with nonempty interior, write $\\mathfrak d_{i}(t):=d_{i}(t,x(t),w(t))$\nfor the evaluated residual forces and\n$$E(t):=K(t)+U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)$$\nfor the mechanical energy of the definition (clause (b)). Then:\n\n1. $E$ is differentiable on $J$ and\n   $$E'(t)=\\partial_{t}U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)+\\sum_{i}\\bigl\\langle\\mathfrak d_{i}(t),\\,w_{i}(t)\\bigr\\rangle\\qquad(t\\in J):$$\n   the mechanical energy changes at the rate of the explicit time dependence\n   of the potential plus the power of the residual forces;\n2. if $\\partial_{t}U\\equiv0$ on $I_{0}\\times Q$ and each evaluated residual\n   force $\\mathfrak d_{i}$ vanishes identically along this motion, then $E$ is\n   constant on $J$. If, more strongly, every residual-force map $d_i$ vanishes\n   on its entire state domain, the model is a global potential model with the\n   declared representative $U$;\n3. for all $t_{0}<t_{1}$ in $J$,\n   $$K(t_{1})-K(t_{0})=U\\bigl(t_{0},x(t_{0})\\bigr)-U\\bigl(t_{1},x(t_{1})\\bigr)+\\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\\Bigl[\\partial_{t}U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)+\\sum_{i}\\bigl\\langle\\mathfrak d_{i}(t),w_{i}(t)\\bigr\\rangle\\Bigr]dt,$$\n   equivalently $W([t_{0},t_{1}])=U(t_{0},x(t_{0}))-U(t_{1},x(t_{1}))+\\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}\\bigl[\\partial_{t}U+\\sum_{i}\\langle\\mathfrak d_{i},w_{i}\\rangle\\bigr]dt$:\n   the work of the total force is the decrease of the potential energy plus\n   the work of the explicit time dependence and of the residual forces.\n\nThe representative $U$ is the one declared by the model; replacing $U$ by\n$U+c(t)$ changes $E$ by $c(t)$ and $E'$ by $c'(t)$, as recorded in\n[[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause (c). The statement is read\nin the fixed frame; no empirical content is claimed.",
          "strategy": "A conditional deduction: instantiate the mathematical work-energy balance for the potential presentation to obtain the mechanical-energy balance, apply the supplier's conservation clause for a static representative without residual forces, and integrate the power identity for the work form.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
            "pthm-cm03-work-energy",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
