        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm03-centrality-is-not-necessary-for-angular-momentum-conservation",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Non-central antisymmetric pair forces with vanishing internal torque sum: the centrality clause is not necessary for angular-momentum conservation",
          "statement": "In the force branch of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with the\nexternal-plus-pair presentation and the antisymmetry clause (ii)1 of\n[[post-cm02-interactions]], **if the internal torque sum\n$\\sum_{i<j}(x_{i}-x_{j})\\times F_{ij}$ vanishes identically, then every pair\nforce $F_{ij}$ is central** (parallel to $x_{i}-x_{j}$).\n\nEquivalently: centrality of the pair forces is necessary for the\ninternal-torque conclusion used by\n[[pthm-cm03-angular-momentum-balance]].",
          "strategy": "A direct refutation: define a three-label model with constant antisymmetric non-central pair forces, verify it against the force branch, exhibit a motion, and prove the internal torque sum vanishes identically by a telescoping cancellation while the individual pair torques are nonzero, so angular momentum is conserved without centrality.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
            "pthm-cm03-angular-momentum-balance",
            "pthm-cm03-momentum-balance",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "post-cm02-interactions",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws",
            "def-interval",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm03-angular-momentum-balance": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm03-momentum-balance": "physical-result",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm02-interactions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A counterexample inside the force branch: a three-label model with constant antisymmetric pair forces has non-central pair forces and yet its internal torque sum vanishes identically, so its total angular momentum about a fixed origin is constant along every motion even though the centrality hypothesis of the balance theorem fails. The item refutes only the claim that centrality is necessary for the internal-torque conclusion; it asserts no measured value and no conservation for general non-central models.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.3.1, Eq. (1.19) and the surrounding discussion, printed pages 6-7 (PDF pages 12-13): the internal-torque sum as a sum over pairs, whose vanishing is discussed through the parallelism of each pair, with the bookkeeping by pairs of forces",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.3, Eq. (1.37) and the pair cancellation argument, chapter pages 9-10 (PDF pages 13-14): the double sum of internal torques grouped into pairs and reduced by the central-force assumption",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm03-closed-force-field-without-a-global-potential",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A closed model force field on an annular domain without a global potential energy: force-generating potentials exist only locally",
          "statement": "In the force branch of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]], use the notion of a\npotential presentation from [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]\nclause (a), but do not assume that such a presentation exists. Consider the\nassertion that **if the model's force map is time- and velocity-independent,\nso it has the form $F(t,x,v)=\\varphi(x)$ for a $C^{1}$ field\n$\\varphi:Q\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$, and that field is closed in the sense of clause\n(d) of [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]], namely\n$\\partial_{j}\\varphi_{k}=\\partial_{k}\\varphi_{j}$ for all coordinate indices\n$j,k\\in\\{0,\\dots,3N-1\\}$ on $Q$, then the model is a global potential model; in\nparticular there is a global potential energy $U$ on the domain with\n$F=-\\nabla U$, the mechanical energy $K+U$ exists for every motion, and it is\nconserved.**",
          "strategy": "A direct refutation: define a single-label model on a declared annular domain with a scaled vortex force field, verify the standing Lipschitz hypothesis by an explicit two-case estimate, compute closedness by the quotient rule and the nonzero circulation from the definition of the line integral, use the gradient theorem to exclude a global potential, and recover local potentials and local energy conservation on star-shaped subdomains.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
