            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the damped oscillator as a restricted one-dimensional model: the harmonic restoring force with spring constant k>0 combined, through additive composition of force contributions, with a linear drag force -c x-dot of declared drag coefficient c>=0 (kilograms per second). The drag term is phenomenological: it represents the net effect of a medium that is not modelled, is adopted for a stated regime (viscous, velocity-proportional resistance at small speeds), neglects the random component of the environment's force, and is a different model from quadratic drag or Coulomb friction outside that regime. The item asserts no value of c, no derivation of the drag law, no fluctuation theory and no measured result.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.5)-(5.6b) with footnote 4, chapter page 2 (PDF page 88): the linear drag force F = -eta qdot and the damped equation m qddot + eta qdot + kappa q = 0, with the drag treated as a phenomenological model whose random component is neglected subject to an energy-scale condition",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2.4, Example 2 (the damped simple harmonic oscillator), printed page 5 (PDF page 11): the friction term F(x,xdot) = -k x - gamma xdot, with energy not conserved and the system losing energy until it comes to rest",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 1.4, printed pages 20-21 (PDF pages 23-24): fluid friction at low velocity F = -b v as the distinct low-speed friction model, alongside the separate static, sliding and rolling friction models",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm04-damped-motion",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Classification of the motions of the damped oscillator by the discriminant, and nonincreasing energy $E'=-c\\,x'^{2}$",
          "statement": "Adopt the damped oscillator model of [[post-cm04-linear-damping-model]] with\n$m>0$, $k>0$, $c\\ge0$, force law $F(x,v)=-kx-cv$ and mechanical energy\n$E(x,v)=\\tfrac12mv^{2}+\\tfrac12kx^{2}$. Write\n$$\\Delta:=c^{2}-4mk,\\qquad \\gamma:=\\frac{c}{2m},\\qquad \\lambda_{\\pm}:=\\frac{-c\\pm\\sqrt{\\Delta}}{2m}\\ (\\Delta\\ge0),\\qquad \\omega:=\\frac{\\sqrt{4mk-c^{2}}}{2m}\\ (\\Delta<0),$$\nand note that $\\Delta=-4mk<0$ and $\\omega=\\sqrt{k/m}$ when $c=0$. Then:\n\n1. **Classification and uniqueness.** Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an\n   interval with nonempty interior. A curve $x:J\\to\\mathbb R$ is a motion of\n   the model if and only if it is one of the following:\n   - $\\Delta>0$: $x(t)=Ae^{\\lambda_{+}t}+Be^{\\lambda_{-}t}$ for reals $A,B$;\n   - $\\Delta=0$: $x(t)=(A+Bt)e^{-ct/(2m)}$ for reals $A,B$;\n   - $\\Delta<0$: $x(t)=e^{-\\gamma t}\\bigl(A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega\n     t)\\bigr)$ for reals $A,B$.\n   For every datum $(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})$ there is exactly one maximal motion,\n   it is defined on all of $\\mathbb R$, and every motion with that datum is\n   its restriction; when its family is written in $s=t-t_0$ instead of $t$, the coefficients\n   are those supplied by clause 4 of\n   [[thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification]] for the shifted\n   curve $s\\mapsto x(t_{0}+s)$.\n   In particular, for $c=0$ the motions are exactly those of the harmonic\n   model of [[post-cm04-harmonic-model]] with the same $m$ and $k$.\n2. **Energy dissipation.** For every motion $x:J\\to\\mathbb R$ the energy\n   $e(t):=E(x(t),x'(t))$ is differentiable at every interior point of $J$\n   with\n   $$e'(t)=-c\\,x'(t)^{2}\\le0,$$\n   and for $t_{1}<t_{2}$ in $J$ one has\n   $e(t_{2})-e(t_{1})=-c\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x'(s)^{2}\\,ds\\le0$: the energy is\n   nonincreasing on $J$. If $c>0$, then the following are equivalent: (i)\n   $e$ is constant on $J$; (ii) $x'(t)=0$ at every interior point of $J$;\n   (iii) $x$ is constant on $J$; (iv) $x\\equiv0$ on $J$, the rest motion of\n   the model. Moreover, at every interior point with $x'(t)\\neq0$ one has\n   $e'(t)<0$, so the energy strictly decreases there.\n3. **Rest points.** The rest points of the model are exactly the pairs\n   $(x,0)$ with $kx=0$, that is $(0,0)$, and the constant curve $x\\equiv0$ is\n   its maximal motion with datum $(t_{0},0,0)$.\n\nThe statement is read for the declared data; it asserts no value of $m,k,c$\nand no empirical decay law.",
          "strategy": "A case deduction: transport the mathematical solution classification by the discriminant to the model, then differentiate and integrate the energy along a motion to obtain the dissipation identity, monotonicity and the characterisation of the constant-energy motions as the rest motion.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm04-linear-damping-model",
            "post-cm04-harmonic-model",
