          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the driven oscillator as a restricted one-dimensional model: the damped oscillator with parameters m>0, k>0, c>=0 together with an external driving force f(t)=f_0 cos(Omega t) of declared amplitude f_0 and angular frequency Omega>0, prescribed as a function of time and independent of the motion. The drive is an idealization of an apparatus or background that maintains a fixed sinusoidal force; it is adopted for a stated regime, its amplitude and frequency are model parameters, and no measured value, no back-reaction model, no non-sinusoidal waveform and no claim that a given apparatus realizes the model is asserted.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.13a)-(5.19), chapter pages 3-4 (PDF pages 89-90): the forced oscillator m qddot + eta qdot + kappa q = F(t) with a sinusoidal component, the response function, the forced amplitude and the resonance discussion",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 7, Section 7.1.3, Eq. (7.15), printed pages 147-148 (PDF pages 150-151): the driven damped linear oscillator with a harmonic driving term and the transient plus steady-state decomposition",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm04-resonance-and-boundedness",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The driven oscillator: steady response and amplitude profile, bounded damped and off-resonant motions, and unbounded undamped resonance",
          "statement": "Adopt the sinusoidally driven oscillator model of\n[[post-cm04-sinusoidal-drive-model]] with data $m>0$, $k>0$, $c\\ge0$,\n$f_{0}\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\Omega>0$, force law\n$F(t,x,v)=f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)-kx-cv$ and equation\n$m\\,x''+cx'+kx=f_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)$. Put\n$$\\omega_{0}:=\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}},\\qquad D:=k-m\\Omega^{2}+i\\,c\\,\\Omega\\in\\mathbb C,$$\nand define the **steady response**\n$$x_{p}(t):=\\operatorname{Re}\\Bigl(\\frac{f_{0}e^{i\\Omega t}}{D}\\Bigr) =\\frac{f_{0}\\bigl[(k-m\\Omega^{2})\\cos(\\Omega t)+c\\,\\Omega\\sin(\\Omega t)\\bigr]} {(k-m\\Omega^{2})^{2}+c^{2}\\Omega^{2}}\\qquad(D\\neq0),$$\nwith **amplitude** $A(\\Omega):=\\lvert f_{0}\\rvert/\\sqrt{(k-m\\Omega^{2})^{2}+c^{2}\\Omega^{2}}$,\nand in the exceptional case $c=0$, $\\Omega=\\omega_{0}$ define\n$x_{p}(t):=f_{0}t\\sin(\\omega_{0}t)/(2m\\omega_{0})$. All all-time boundedness, convergence and unboundedness claims below refer to maximal motions on $\\mathbb R$; local motions are their restrictions as in clause 2. Then:\n\n1. **Steady response.** If $D\\neq0$ — that is $c>0$, or $c=0$ and\n   $\\Omega\\neq\\omega_{0}$ — then $x_{p}$ is a motion of the model on\n   $\\mathbb R$, of period $2\\pi/\\Omega$, with amplitude $A(\\Omega)$ in the\n   sense $\\lvert x_{p}(t)\\rvert\\le A(\\Omega)$ for all $t$ and equality at\n   suitable times.\n2. **Reduction to the homogeneous model.** Every datum has exactly one\n   maximal motion, it is defined on all of $\\mathbb R$, and every motion\n   $x$ of the model on an interval with nonempty interior can be written\n   $x=x_{p}+y$, where $x_{p}$ is the particular motion of clause 1, or the\n   resonant motion in the exceptional case, and $y$ is a motion of the damped\n   homogeneous model with the same $m$, $c$, $k$ (and is a harmonic motion\n   of [[post-cm04-harmonic-model]] when $c=0$).\n3. **Strictly damped case $c>0$.** For every $\\Omega>0$ the following hold:\n  every motion is bounded on $[0,\\infty)$; and\n   $x(t)-x_{p}(t)\\to0$ and $x'(t)-x_{p}'(t)\\to0$ as $t\\to+\\infty$. Moreover\n   $x_{p}$ is the unique motion with datum\n   $(t_{0},x_{p}(t_{0}),x_{p}'(t_{0}))$ for any $t_{0}\\in\\mathbb R$. The\n   amplitude function $\\Omega\\mapsto A(\\Omega)$ has the following shape when $f_0\\ne0$: if\n   $c^{2}<2mk$ it is strictly increasing on $(0,\\Omega_{*})$ and strictly\n   decreasing on $(\\Omega_{*},\\infty)$, so has its unique maximum there, with\n   $$\\Omega_{*}=\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}-\\frac{c^{2}}{2m^{2}}},\\qquad A(\\Omega_{*})=\\frac{2m\\lvert f_{0}\\rvert}{c\\sqrt{4mk-c^{2}}};$$\n   if $c^{2}\\ge2mk$ it is strictly decreasing on $(0,\\infty)$ and does not\n   attain a maximum on the declared positive-frequency domain. Its continuous\n   extension to $\\Omega=0$ has its maximum there. For $f_0=0$ the amplitude is\n   identically zero on the domain, so every frequency has the same value.\n4. **Undamped case away from resonance.** For $c=0$ and\n   $\\Omega\\neq\\omega_{0}$ every motion is bounded on $\\mathbb R$.\n5. **Undamped resonance.** For $c=0$, $\\Omega=\\omega_{0}$ and $f_{0}\\neq0$\n   the motion $x_{p}(t)=f_{0}t\\sin(\\omega_{0}t)/(2m\\omega_{0})$ is unbounded\n   on $[0,\\infty)$, and every motion of the model is unbounded on\n   $[0,\\infty)$, although the drive $t\\mapsto f_{0}\\cos(\\omega_{0}t)$ is\n   bounded.\n6. **Off the singular cases.** For $c>0$ the denominator never vanishes and\n   every $\\Omega>0$ is admissible; for $c=0$ the exceptional frequency is\n   exactly $\\Omega=\\omega_{0}$. Consequently the boundedness assertions of\n   clauses 3 and 4 hold for $c>0$, and for $c=0$ away from $\\Omega=\\omega_{0}$,\n   and also at undamped resonance when $f_0=0$; for a nonzero drive clause 5 gives unboundedness at undamped resonance.\n\nThe statement is read for the declared model data; no value of any parameter\nand no measured response is asserted.",
          "strategy": "A case analysis: verify the complex steady response and its real form, reduce every motion to the particular motion plus a homogeneous motion by linearity, use the strictly damped decay for the boundedness and convergence clauses, use the harmonic boundedness for the undamped off-resonant case, exhibit the unbounded resonant motion at zero damping, and read the amplitude maximum from the amplitude proposition.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm04-sinusoidal-drive-model",
            "post-cm04-linear-damping-model",
            "post-cm04-harmonic-model",
            "def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system",
