            "post-cm04-harmonic-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-variation-of-constants": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-forced-oscillator-sinusoidal-response": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-amplitude-maximum": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-cm04-damped-motion": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm04-damped-decay-and-stability": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm04-harmonic-motion": "physical-result",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-limits-at-infinity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The response of the driven oscillator model with m>0, k>0, c>=0, drive amplitude f_0 and angular frequency Omega>0: the explicit steady sinusoidal response and its amplitude |f_0|/sqrt((k-m Omega^2)^2+c^2 Omega^2) whenever the denominator does not vanish; the reduction of every motion to the steady response plus a homogeneous motion; boundedness of every motion in the strictly damped case, with exponential convergence of the transient to the steady response; boundedness for c=0 away from Omega=sqrt(k/m); unbounded motions at c=0 and Omega=sqrt(k/m) with nonzero amplitude, so boundedness fails exactly in the off-singular-case sense; and the positive-frequency amplitude peak when f_0 is nonzero and c^2<2mk. For c^2>=2mk and f_0!=0 the amplitude strictly decreases on Omega>0 and has no maximum on that domain; for f_0=0 it is constant. The result is a conditional deduction from the declared drive and damping models; it asserts no measured resonance curve, no value of any parameter and no behaviour of an apparatus.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 5.1, Eqs. (5.13a)-(5.22), chapter pages 3-5 (PDF pages 89-91): the forced-oscillator response function, the forced amplitude (5.18), the resonance value (5.19), the quality factor ratio and the width of the resonance peak",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 7, Section 7.1.3, Eqs. (7.15)-(7.16), printed pages 147-148 (PDF pages 150-151): the driven damped linear oscillator and its resonant behaviour when the driving frequency equals the natural frequency",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 19
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm04-linear-model-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Scope and limits of the one-dimensional conservative and dissipative models: declared class, approximation status, empirical parameters and open extensions",
          "statement": "- **The one-dimensional class is declared, not derived.** The models of this\n  page are restricted model classes of\n  [[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]]: a scalar coordinate,\n  a mass and a scalar force law with the standing regularity. Their relation\n  to motions of the three-dimensional force branch along a straight line, or\n  to effective one-dimensional reductions of constrained systems such as the\n  pendulum, is a modelling identification that a consumer item states and\n  uses explicitly; this page contains no three-dimensional reduction\n  theorem, and the pendulum example of the companion page declares its\n  effective model equation with its source support.\n\n- **Declared assumptions versus deductions.** The harmonic, damped and driven\n  models are adopted postulates\n  ([[post-cm04-harmonic-model]], [[post-cm04-linear-damping-model]],\n  [[post-cm04-sinusoidal-drive-model]]); every physics theorem on the page is a\n  conditional deduction from those adopted models: existence and uniqueness, energy\n  conservation and level-set containment\n  ([[pthm-cm04-conservative-motion-and-energy]]), Lyapunov stability at a\n  strict minimum ([[pthm-cm04-equilibrium-stability-at-a-strict-minimum]]),\n  the sinusoid classification, energy and phase ellipses\n  ([[pthm-cm04-harmonic-motion]]), the damping classification and the\n  dissipation identity ([[pthm-cm04-damped-motion]]), decay and asymptotic\n  stability ([[pthm-cm04-damped-decay-and-stability]]), and the response,\n  boundedness and resonance statements\n  ([[pthm-cm04-resonance-and-boundedness]]). No deduction here establishes\n  that a real system obeys a model.\n\n- **The harmonic model is a local quadratic approximation.** In the\n  displacement coordinate $q=x-x_{*}$ centered at a strict minimum with\n  $U''(x_{*})>0$, the quadratic Taylor term is\n  $\\tfrac12U''(x_{*})q^{2}$ after dropping the additive constant\n  $U(x_{*})$. It gives the linearized equation about that minimum; the\n  approximation is exact only for the declared harmonic model. For the\n  pendulum, the difference\n  between the nonlinear and the linearized motion is bounded only in the\n  finite-time sense of\n  [[prop-cm-pendulum-small-angle-linearization-bound]], with the explicit\n  small-amplitude and time hypotheses of that statement; no uniform-in-time\n  approximation is asserted anywhere on this page.\n\n- **The drag is phenomenological and fluctuation-free.** The linear drag\n  model is adopted for a stated viscous regime; its coefficient depends on\n  body and medium; the random component of the environment's force is\n  neglected. Likharev's Section 5.1, footnote 4 (Chapter 5 p. 2; PDF p. 88),\n  limits that omission to oscillator energy much larger than the fluctuation\n  energy scale. In the source's thermal-equilibrium setting, write\n  $T\\ge0$ for absolute temperature in kelvins, $k_B>0$ for the Boltzmann\n  constant in joules per kelvin, $\\hbar>0$ for the reduced Planck constant\n  in joule seconds, and $\\omega_0=\\sqrt{k/m}$ in inverse seconds. The\n  reported scale is $E_{\\rm fluc}:=\\max\\{k_BT,\\hbar\\omega_0/2\\}$, compared\n  with $E_{\\rm osc}:=mv^2/2+kx^2/2$ above the declared potential minimum.\n  The condition is $E_{\\rm osc}\\gg E_{\\rm fluc}$: the ratio\n  $E_{\\rm fluc}/E_{\\rm osc}$ must be small where this approximation is used.\n  The source gives no universal numerical tolerance or error estimate.\n  These thermal and quantum quantities report a source's applicability\n  caveat; they are not premises of the page's deterministic proofs or\n  constants derived within this mechanical framework. Since the adopted\n  strictly damped equation predicts $E_{\\rm osc}\\to0$, its exact long-time\n  decay theorem does not assert indefinite physical applicability when\n  $E_{\\rm fluc}>0$. No fluctuation–dissipation relation, derivation of $c$\n  or validity outside the linear regime is claimed; regimes of quadratic\n  drag or of Coulomb friction are different models outside this page.\n\n- **The drive is prescribed and sinusoidal.** The driving-force contribution\n  is a function of time alone with an idealized sinusoidal waveform; the total\n  force also includes the declared spring and drag terms. No\n  back-reaction of the motion on the drive, no non-sinusoidal waveform and\n  no apparatus characterization is part of the model. The boundedness and\n  resonance clauses of [[pthm-cm04-resonance-and-boundedness]] are\n  statements about this model's motions.\n\n- **Measured parameters are empirical premises.** The values of $m$, $k$,\n  $c$, $f_{0}$ and $\\Omega$ are model data; when a value is obtained from a\n  calibration or an apparatus specification, its uncertainty, the\n  calibration method and the operating conditions are premises of any\n  comparison with observation, and agreement of a model prediction with\n  data is statistical evidence about the combined model and apparatus\n  assumptions, never a proof of the model. This page asserts no measured\n  value and no empirical agreement.\n\n- **Stability conclusions and their boundaries.** The conservative model has\n  a Lyapunov stable rest point at a strict minimum; the strictly damped\n  model has a globally asymptotically stable rest point; the strict-minimum stability conclusion also covers degenerate strict minima, while no conclusion is drawn at other degenerate critical points, at maxima, or for the undamped driven model. In particular the linearized repulsive frequency of a maximum is\n  not used to claim nonlinear instability of the model.\n\n- **Open extensions.** Nonlinear oscillators with large amplitude (the exact\n  pendulum and its secular drift), parametric resonance, nonlinear damping\n  and self-oscillation, dissipative models with fluctuations, and numerical\n  integration studies are outside this page's scope and belong to the later\n  pages of the track; the recorded sources' treatments of those topics are\n  not prerequisites of the claims proved here.",
          "strategy": "A scope remark: it separates declared model assumptions from conditional deductions and approximation or empirical interfaces on the page, records the finite-time character of the small-angle bound, the phenomenological and fluctuation-free character of the drag, the prescribed-drive idealisation, the status of measured parameters as empirical premises, the boundaries of the stability conclusions and the open extensions.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria",
            "def-cm04-phase-plane-and-energy-level-sets",
            "post-cm04-harmonic-model",
            "post-cm04-linear-damping-model",
            "post-cm04-sinusoidal-drive-model",
            "pthm-cm04-conservative-motion-and-energy",
            "pthm-cm04-equilibrium-stability-at-a-strict-minimum",
