          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked check of the adopted ideal pendulum angle-chart equation m l^2 theta-ddot = -m g l sin(theta): its scalar potential and strict minimum, Lyapunov stability from the mathematical chart theorem, the linearized angular equation with frequency sqrt(g/l), the finite-time small-angle error bound at g=l=1, and the failure of the quadratic energy of the linearized angular equation to be conserved along exact motions. The item asserts no measured value, no period formula for finite amplitudes and no derivation of the effective equation from the constrained system.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.2, Eq. (1.18), chapter page 6 (PDF page 10): the point pendulum equation m R theta-ddot = -m g sin(theta); Section 3.1, Eqs. (3.1)-(3.6), chapter pages 1-2 (PDF pages 33-34): the effectively one-dimensional system with effective mass and effective potential; Section 3.2, Eqs. (3.10)-(3.18), chapter pages 2-4 (PDF pages 34-36): the quadratic expansion about a fixed point, the effective spring constant and the frequency (kappa_ef/m_ef)^{1/2}",
