        {
          "id": "pthm-cm05-relative-reduction",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Two-body reduction of a static central pair-potential model: the relative coordinate is a central-force trajectory for the reduced mass, the centre of mass moves uniformly, and the reconstruction is a bijection",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with $N=2$\nlabels, masses $m_1,m_2>0$ ([[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)), a time\nwindow $I_0$, and the collision-excluded configuration domain\n$$Q:=\\{\\,x=(x_1,x_2)\\in(\\mathbb R^3)^2:\\lvert x_1-x_2\\rvert\\in J_0\\,\\}$$\nfor an open interval $J_0\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$\n([[def-cm01-configuration]]). Suppose the model presents its force map\nthrough the time-independent potential\n$$U(x_1,x_2):=u(\\lvert x_1-x_2\\rvert),$$\nwhere $u:J_0\\to\\mathbb R$ is of class $C^2$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), so that\n$F_i(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_i}U(x)$ on the state domain ([[post-cm02-interactions]],\nclauses (iii) and (iv), [[lem-cm05-radial-potential-gradient]]). Let\n$x:J\\to Q$ be a **motion** of the model on an interval $J$ with nonempty\ninterior ([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]) and write\n$$M:=m_1+m_2,\\quad \\mu:=\\frac{m_1m_2}{M},\\quad X:=\\frac{m_1x_1+m_2x_2}{M},\\quad V:=X',\\quad P:=m_1x_1'+m_2x_2',\\quad r:=x_1-x_2,$$\nwith $\\lvert\\cdot\\rvert=\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ the Euclidean norm\n([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) and dimensions as in [[def-cm01-units]]\n($[M]=[\\mu]=\\mu$, $[X]=[r]=\\ell$, $[P]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1}$). Then:\n\n1. **(Pair forces.)** On $J$ the evaluated forces are\n   $$F_1\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr)=-\\frac{u'(\\lvert r\\rvert)}{\\lvert r\\rvert}\\,r,\\qquad F_2\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr)=+\\frac{u'(\\lvert r\\rvert)}{\\lvert r\\rvert}\\,r;$$\n   thus the model invokes the external-plus-pair presentation of\n   [[post-cm02-interactions]] clause (i) with\n   $F_i^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\equiv0$ and the single pair force\n   $F_{12}=-u'(\\lvert r\\rvert)r/\\lvert r\\rvert=-F_{21}$, which is central\n   and antisymmetric in the sense of clause (ii).\n2. **(Centre of mass.)** $P$ is constant on $J$ and $X$ is affine: for all\n   $t,t_0\\in J$, $X(t)=X(t_0)+(t-t_0)V$ with the constant\n   $V=X'=M^{-1}P$.\n3. **(Relative motion.)** $r$ is of class $C^2$ on $J$, satisfies\n   $$\\mu\\,r''(t)=-\\frac{u'(\\lvert r(t)\\rvert)}{\\lvert r(t)\\rvert}\\,r(t)\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\n   and is therefore a **central-force trajectory for $(\\mu,u)$** in the\n   sense of [[def-cm-central-force-trajectory]]; its angular momentum vector\n   $j=\\mu\\,r\\times r'$ and energy $E=\\tfrac12\\mu\\lVert r'\\rVert_2^2+\n   u(\\lvert r\\rvert)$ are the attached objects of that definition.\n4. **(Reconstruction and bijection.)** The identities\n   $x_1=X+\\frac{m_2}{M}r$ and $x_2=X-\\frac{m_1}{M}r$ hold on $J$.\n   Conversely, if $X^{\\circ}:J\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is affine with\n   $(X^{\\circ})''=0$ and $r^{\\circ}:J\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is a $C^2$ curve with\n   $\\lvert r^{\\circ}(t)\\rvert\\in J_0$ and\n   $\\mu(r^{\\circ})''=-u'(\\lvert r^{\\circ}\\rvert)r^{\\circ}/\\lvert r^{\\circ}\\rvert$\n   on $J$, then\n   $$x_1^{\\circ}:=X^{\\circ}+\\frac{m_2}{M}r^{\\circ},\\qquad x_2^{\\circ}:=X^{\\circ}-\\frac{m_1}{M}r^{\\circ}$$\n   define a motion $x^{\\circ}:J\\to Q$ of the model whose centre of mass is\n   $X^{\\circ}$ and whose relative coordinate is $r^{\\circ}$. Consequently\n   the assignment $x\\mapsto(X,r)$ is a bijection from the motions of the\n   model on $J$ onto the pairs (affine curve $X^{\\circ}$ with\n   $(X^{\\circ})''=0$\\!, central-force trajectory $r^{\\circ}$ for $(\\mu,u)$\n   with values in $J_0$) on $J$.",
          "strategy": "A conditional deduction: evaluate the pair potential's gradients to obtain the central antisymmetric pair forces, apply the momentum balance with vanishing external force, divide the two equations of motion by the masses to get the reduced central-force equation, and reconstruct the motion from the centre-of-mass and relative data.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "post-cm02-interactions",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "post-cm01-worldlines",
            "def-cm01-configuration",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
            "pthm-cm03-momentum-balance",
            "def-cm-central-force-trajectory",
            "lem-cm05-radial-potential-gradient",
            "lem-cm-derivative-of-pairings-of-curves",
            "cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-interval",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "physical-assumption",
            "post-cm02-interactions": "physical-assumption",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-worldlines": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-configuration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm03-momentum-balance": "physical-result",
            "def-cm-central-force-trajectory": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm05-radial-potential-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-derivative-of-pairings-of-curves": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The conditional reduction of a two-label model whose force map is presented by a static central pair potential: along every motion the evaluated pair forces are central and antisymmetric, the total momentum and the centre-of-mass velocity are constant, the relative coordinate satisfies the one-body central-force equation with the reduced mass, and the motion is reconstructed from the centre-of-mass motion and the relative trajectory. The result is a deduction inside the adopted force and interaction branches; it uses no measured value, assumes no external forces, excludes collisions, and asserts no conservation law beyond the vanishing of the total external force that the presentation supplies.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), Section 2.5.4, printed pp. 31-33 (PDF pp. 37-39), Eqs. (2.82)-(2.84): the Lagrangian split into centre-of-mass and separation pieces with reduced mass $\\mu=m_1m_2/(m_1+m_2)$, and the conserved $J=\\mu r^2\\dot\\phi$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 3.4, Eqs. (3.30)-(3.37), chapter pages 7-9 (PDF pages 40-42): the change to the relative vector and centre of mass, the reduced-mass reduction to a single particle in the central potential, and the conservation of the total momentum",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Iain Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 4.5, printed page 79 (PDF page 82), Eq. (4.148): the Kepler problem of two masses in a central potential with the centre of mass at the origin and the reduced mass",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "post-cm05-central-model",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted central-force model class: static central pair potentials, the reduced one-label problem, the fixed-centre idealization, reading conventions and exclusions",
          "statement": "Adopt, as the **central-force model** for the two-body part of this page, the\nfollowing restricted model class inside the force and interaction branches of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] and [[post-cm02-interactions]].\n\n**(i) The two-label central model.** A model of class (i) is a force model of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with $N=2$ labels, fixed masses\n$m_1,m_2>0$ ([[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)), a time window $I_0$, the\ncollision-excluded configuration domain\n$$Q:=\\{\\,x=(x_1,x_2)\\in(\\mathbb R^3)^2:\\lvert x_1-x_2\\rvert\\in J_0\\,\\}$$\nfor an open interval $J_0\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$\n([[def-cm01-configuration]]), and the force map presented through the\ntime-independent **static central pair potential**\n$$U(x_1,x_2):=u(\\lvert x_1-x_2\\rvert),\\qquad u:J_0\\to\\mathbb R\\ \\text{of class }C^2,$$\nso that $F_i(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_i}U(x)$ on the state domain (clauses (iii)\nand (iv) of [[post-cm02-interactions]]; $U$ is of class $C^2$ on the open\n$Q$ by [[lem-cm05-radial-potential-gradient]]). In the external-plus-pair\npresentation of clause (i) of that postulate the model has\n$F_i^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\equiv0$: no external forces act on the labels of a model\nof class (i).\n\n**(ii) The reduced one-label problem.** For a model of class (i) put\n$\\mu:=m_1m_2/(m_1+m_2)>0$ of dimension $\\mu$ ([[def-cm01-units]]). The\n**reduced problem** of the model is the one-label problem of mass $\\mu$ whose\nunknown is the relative coordinate $r=x_1-x_2$ and whose equation is\n$$\\mu\\,r''=-\\frac{u'(\\lvert r\\rvert)}{\\lvert r\\rvert}\\,r;$$\nby [[pthm-cm05-relative-reduction]] the relative coordinate of every motion\nof a model of class (i) solves this equation, and every pair of a uniform\ncentre-of-mass motion and a solution of the reduced equation on a common\ninterval reconstructs a motion of the model. Adopt the reduced problem as the\nobject that the central-force statements of this page describe, and the\ncentral-force-trajectory vocabulary of\n[[def-cm-central-force-trajectory]] (attached angular momentum\n$j=\\mu\\,r\\times r'$ and energy $E=\\tfrac12\\mu\\lVert r'\\rVert_2^2+u(\\lvert\nr\\rvert)$) as the vocabulary in which those statements are phrased.\n\n**(iii) The one-label fixed-centre idealization.** A **one-label central\nmodel** is a force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with $N=1$,\nmass $\\mu>0$, time window $I_0$, a fixed **centre** $c\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the\nconfiguration domain $Q_c=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\lvert x-c\\rvert\\in J_0\\}$, and\nthe external force map\n$$F(t,x,v)=-\\frac{u'(\\lvert x-c\\rvert)}{\\lvert x-c\\rvert}\\,(x-c),$$\npresented through the potential $u(\\lvert x-c\\rvert)$ with the same function\n$u\\in C^2(J_0)$. This model is the **fixed-centre idealization** of the\nreduced problem: it drops the centre-of-mass degree of freedom and holds the\nforce centre fixed. It is a separate adopted model class, not a consequence\nof class (i), and every use of it must state that the idealization is\nintended.\n\n**(iv) Reading conventions.** In the statements of this page: the **force\ncentre** of the reduced problem is the origin of the relative coordinate\n$r$; an **orbit** is the trajectory $t\\mapsto r(t)$ of the reduced problem\n(equivalently, by [[pthm-cm05-relative-reduction]], the trajectory of either\nlabel relative to the centre of mass, up to the mass ratio); \"circular\" and\n\"elliptic\" name properties of that trajectory; and the words \"planet\",\n\"satellite\", \"central body\" and \"test particle\" name labels of a model of\nclass (i) or (iii). These words carry no empirical claim about nature.\n\n**(v) Exclusions and status.** The class excludes: velocity-dependent and\nexplicitly time-dependent central forces; non-central and non-potential\nforces; constraints and reaction forces; colliding configurations\n$x_1=x_2$ (equivalently $r=0$); external forces on the labels; and models\nwith more than two labels. Finite-size, rotational, deformational, dissipative\nand relativistic effects are outside the class. The masses, the interval\n$J_0$ and the function $u$ (including any parameters it carries) are model\ndata, not measured values ([[def-cm01-units]]); quantitative empirical use\nrequires separately stated measured inputs with their uncertainties, and no\nstatement of this page asserts that any physical system is exactly a model\nof this class.",
          "strategy": "An adopted model class: the two-label static central pair-potential models without external forces, the reduced one-label problem, the fixed-centre idealization, the reading conventions for force centre and orbit, and the exclusions and status of the model data.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "post-cm02-interactions",
