          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The one-dimensional reduction of the non-radial central problem in the adopted model class for angular-momentum magnitude J>0: the radial coordinate takes values in the declared open radial interval J_0 and obeys the effective-potential equation with the centrifugal term, its energy is the constant first integral of that one-dimensional problem, and conversely every C^2 radial solution valued in J_0 together with the angular quadrature reconstructs a planar central-force trajectory with the prescribed angular momentum, hence a motion of the two-label model together with a uniform centre-of-mass motion. The result is a conditional deduction; it asserts no observation, no measured value, and no global existence for either side.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), Section 2.5.4, printed pp. 32-33 (PDF pp. 38-39), Eqs. (2.85)-(2.89): the radial Lagrange equation, the effective potential $V_{\\rm eff}(r)=V(r)+J^2/(2\\mu r^2)$ with its angular-momentum barrier, the conserved energy and the reduction to quadratures",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 3.4, Eqs. (3.41)-(3.48), chapter pages 10-11 (PDF pages 43-44): the energy as a one-dimensional radial problem in the effective potential $U_{\\rm ef}(r)=U(r)+L_z^2/(2mr^2)$, with the radial and angular quadratures",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Iain Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 4.5, printed pp. 79-80 (PDF pp. 82-83), Eqs. (4.149)-(4.154): the effective potential, the constant energy of the reduced problem and the radial and orbital quadratures for the relative coordinate",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm05-circular-orbit",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Circular relative orbits of the central model: the critical-point criterion $u'(r_0)=J^2/(\\mu r_0^3)$, uniform rotation of both labels about the centre of mass, and the linearised radial frequency",
          "statement": "Adopt the central-force model of [[post-cm05-central-model]] clause (i) with\nmasses $m_1,m_2>0$, $M=m_1+m_2$, reduced mass $\\mu=m_1m_2/M$, and static\ncentral pair potential $u\\in C^2(J_0)$, and time window $I_0$. Fix $J>0$, an oriented orthonormal\ntriad $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$, and a radius $r_0\\in J_0$, and put\n$$U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J(\\rho):=u(\\rho)+\\frac{J^{2}}{2\\mu\\rho^{2}},\\qquad \\omega:=\\frac{J}{\\mu r_0^{2}}.$$\nThen:\n\n1. **(Criterion.)** The following are equivalent:\n   $$\\bigl(U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J\\bigr)'(r_0)=0;\\qquad u'(r_0)=\\frac{J^{2}}{\\mu r_0^{3}};\\qquad \\mu\\,r_0\\,\\omega^{2}=u'(r_0).$$\n2. **(Existence and the two labels.)** If the condition of clause 1 holds,\n   then for every $\\theta_0\\in\\mathbb R$ the curve\n   $$r^{\\circ}(t):=r_0\\bigl(\\cos(\\theta_0+\\omega t)\\,e_1 +\\sin(\\theta_0+\\omega t)\\,e_2\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R)$$\n   is a central-force trajectory for $(\\mu,u)$ defined on all of $\\mathbb R$,\n   with $\\lvert r^{\\circ}\\rvert\\equiv r_0$, angular momentum\n   $j=\\mu\\,r^{\\circ}\\times(r^{\\circ})'=Je_3$ and constant energy\n   $E=U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J(r_0)$; and for every interval $I\\subseteq I_0$ with nonempty interior and\n   affine centre-of-mass curve $X^{\\circ}:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with\n   $(X^{\\circ})''=0$, the restrictions to $I$ reconstruct a motion of the\n   two-label model ([[pthm-cm05-relative-reduction]] clause 4) has both\n   labels moving uniformly on circles about the centre of mass:\n   $$\\lvert x_1(t)-X^{\\circ}(t)\\rvert=\\frac{m_2}{M}r_0,\\qquad \\lvert x_2(t)-X^{\\circ}(t)\\rvert=\\frac{m_1}{M}r_0,$$\n   with angular velocity $\\omega$ about $X^{\\circ}$.\n3. **(Necessity.)** Conversely, if a motion of the model has constant\n   separation $\\lvert r\\rvert\\equiv r_0$ on a time interval $I$ with nonempty\n   interior and nonzero constant angular momentum $j=Je_3$, then\n   $\\bigl(U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J\\bigr)'(r_0)=0$ and its polar angular coordinate\n   satisfies $\\theta'\\equiv\\omega$; the relative motion is the uniform\n   rotation of clause 2 up to the initial value of $\\theta$, and both labels\n   move uniformly on the circles of clause 2 about the affine centre of mass.\n4. **(Linearised radial frequency.)** If\n   $\\bigl(U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J\\bigr)'(r_0)=0$ and\n   $\\bigl(U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J\\bigr)''(r_0)>0$, then\n   $\\omega_{\\mathrm{lin}}:=\\sqrt{\\bigl(U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J\\bigr)''(r_0)/\\mu}>0$,\n   and a twice differentiable $\\delta:I\\to\\mathbb R$ on an interval $I$ with\n   at least two points solves the linearised radial equation\n   $$\\mu\\,\\delta''=-\\bigl(U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J\\bigr)''(r_0)\\,\\delta \\qquad\\text{on }I$$\n   if and only if $\\delta(t)=A\\cos\\bigl(\\omega_{\\mathrm{lin}}(t-t_0)\\bigr)\n   +B\\sin\\bigl(\\omega_{\\mathrm{lin}}(t-t_0)\\bigr)$ for some $A,B\\in\\mathbb R$\n   and $t_0\\in I$. This is a statement about the quadratic radial\n   approximation at $r_0$; it does not assert nonlinear stability of the\n   circular orbit.",
          "strategy": "A conditional deduction: compute the derivative of the effective potential to obtain the equivalent critical-point and force-balance criteria, instantiate the mathematical circular-orbit theorem for existence, model reading and necessity, and read off the linearised radial frequency.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm05-central-model",
            "pthm-cm05-relative-reduction",
            "thm-cm-circular-orbit-and-radial-frequency",
            "def-cm-central-force-trajectory",
            "lem-cm-oriented-orthonormal-triads",
            "lem-cm-second-order-oscillator-form",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-interval",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm05-central-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm05-relative-reduction": "physical-result",
            "thm-cm-circular-orbit-and-radial-frequency": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-central-force-trajectory": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-oriented-orthonormal-triads": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-second-order-oscillator-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Circular relative motion in the adopted central model: the critical-point condition of the effective potential, stated equivalently as the balance $\\mu r_0\\omega^2=u'(r_0)$ between the central force and the circular motion; the explicit uniform rotation of the relative coordinate and of both labels about the centre of mass; the necessity of the condition for a constant separation; and the frequency of the linearised radial equation at a critical point satisfying the nondegeneracy condition $(U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_J)''(r_0)>0$. The linearised clause is a statement about the quadratic approximation only; no nonlinear stability, no existence of circular orbits at arbitrary radii and no empirical claim is made.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 3.4, Eqs. (3.51)-(3.54), chapter page 12 (PDF page 45): the circular orbit at the minimum of the effective potential with $\\mathrm dU_{\\rm ef}/\\mathrm dr=0$, the identification with the radial force balance, and the angular velocity $\\omega=v/r_0$",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), Section 2.5.4, printed pp. 31-33 (PDF pp. 37-39) and Figure 15: circular orbits as the minimum of the effective potential $V_{\\rm eff}(r)=V(r)+J^2/(2\\mu r^2)$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Iain Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 4.5, printed pp. 79-81 (PDF pp. 82-84), Eqs. (4.151)-(4.156): the effective potential and the circular orbit $\\varepsilon=0$ of the gravity-like potential with the minimum of $V_{\\rm eff}$",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
