          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Definition of the moving spatial charts of the Newtonian model: a translating and rotating coordinate description attached to a fixed inertial frame, its reference curve and rotation, the coordinate curves, velocities and accelerations read in it, the angular velocity of the chart in a stated convention, and the four inertial terms. In general the moving chart is not an inertial frame; when its rotation is constant and its reference curve is affine, its restriction is an adapted chart of another inertial frame. A chart is not an observer congruence. The item asserts no force law, no equation of motion and no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Sections 6.1-6.2, printed pages 93-95 (PDF pages 99-101): rotating frames, the angular velocity of the rotating axes, Eq. (6.4) for the acceleration transfer, and the Coriolis, centrifugal and Euler terms of Eq. (6.5)",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 4.6, chapter pages 21-23 (PDF pages 76-78): the general frame-transfer formulas (4.86)-(4.90), the angular velocity of the moving frame, and the four inertial-force terms of (4.92)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 3.1.1, printed pages 47-49 (PDF pages 53-55): moving orthonormal bases, the antisymmetric matrix R^T Rdot and the angular velocity vector in the body convention",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 2.2, printed pages 33-35 (PDF pages 36-38), Eqs. (2.19)-(2.24): fixed and rotating spatial bases, angular velocity, and the rotating-coordinate acceleration terms; the affine spacetime chart and translating origin used here are specified by the local definitions and mathematical suppliers",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 7
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm06-inertial-forces",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The equation of motion in a moving chart: inertial terms, inertial forces, and when it restricts to an inertial chart",
          "statement": "Adopt the force branch of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]]: $N\\ge1$ labels\nwith masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, time window $I_{0}$, open configuration\ndomain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ and force map $F$ with the standing\nregularity; fix one inertial frame $F$ with adapted coordinates $(t,x)$\n([[post-cm01-frames]], [[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]],\n[[def-cm01-units]]). Let a moving spatial chart of $F$ be given by data\n$(I,c,R)$ with $I\\subseteq I_{0}$ ([[def-cm06-moving-frame]]), with angular\nvelocity $\\omega$ and inertial-terms field\n$$\\tau(t,y,\\dot y):=-\\Bigl(2\\,\\omega(t)\\times\\dot y+\\omega'(t)\\times y+\\omega(t)\\times\\bigl(\\omega(t)\\times y\\bigr)+R(t)^{\\mathsf T}c''(t)\\Bigr).$$\n\nLet $x:J\\to Q$ be a **motion** of the model on an interval $J\\subseteq I$ with\nnonempty interior ([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]] clause (c)):\n$$m_{i}x_{i}''(t)=F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),w(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\nwith $w=x'$ the coordinate velocity of the labels, and for each label define\nthe moving-chart coordinate curve and its derivatives\n$$y_{i}(t):=R(t)^{\\mathsf T}\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-c(t)\\bigr),\\qquad \\dot y_{i}=y_{i}',\\qquad \\ddot y_{i}=y_{i}''.$$\nThen:\n\n1. **moving-chart equation.** For every $i$ and every $t\\in J$,\n   $$m_{i}\\,\\ddot y_{i}(t)=R(t)^{\\mathsf T}F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),w(t)\\bigr)+m_{i}\\,\\tau\\bigl(t,y_{i}(t),\\dot y_{i}(t)\\bigr).$$\n\n2. **inertial forces.** Define the **inertial force** on label $i$ along the\n   motion by $F_{i}^{\\mathrm{in}}(t):=m_{i}\\tau(t,y_{i}(t),\\dot y_{i}(t))$,\n   with the four additive components $-2m_{i}\\omega\\times\\dot y_{i}$,\n   $-m_{i}\\omega'\\times y_{i}$, $-m_{i}\\omega\\times(\\omega\\times y_{i})$ and\n   $-m_{i}R^{\\mathsf T}c''$ (Coriolis, Euler, centrifugal and translational).\n   Then clause 1 reads $m_{i}\\ddot y_{i}=R^{\\mathsf T}F_{i}+F_{i}^{\\mathrm{in}}$.\n   The inertial forces are determined by the chart's motion and the label's\n   mass; they are **not** contributions of the model's force map $F$, and\n   a general moving chart is not an inertial frame of the model; clause 3\n   characterizes the case in which it is the restriction of another inertial\n   chart.\n\n3. **criterion for an inertial chart.** The inertial terms vanish\n   identically, $\\tau(t,y,\\dot y)=0$ for every $(t,y,\\dot y)\\in I\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$,\n   if and only if $R$ is constant on $I$ and $c$ is affine on $I$. In that\n   case the moving chart $(t,y)$ on $I$ is the restriction of the adapted\n   coordinate chart of an inertial frame $F_{*}$ of the model related to $F$ by a Galilean\n   transformation with vanishing time shift, and clause 1 reduces for each\n   label to $m_{i}\\ddot y_{i}=R^{\\mathsf T}F_{i}(t,x(t),w(t))$, which is the\n   transformed equation of motion of clause (vi) of\n   [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] read in $F_{*}$. Conversely, if the\n   moving chart on $I$ is the restriction of the adapted chart of an inertial frame related to $F$ by a\n   Galilean transformation with vanishing time shift, then $R$ is constant\n   and $c$ is affine and $\\tau\\equiv0$.\n\nThe identity of clause 1 is a conditional consequence of the adopted force\nbranch and of the chart's kinematics; clause 3 is its consistency check\nagainst the frame class of [[post-cm01-frames]]. No claim is made that a\nmoving chart is physically equivalent to an inertial frame with an additional\nforce field, and no measured value is asserted.",
          "strategy": "A conditional deduction: apply the mathematical rotating-frame acceleration identity to each label of a motion of the force model and read off the inertial terms and their mass multiples; characterize the vanishing of the inertial terms by a short computation as exactly the charts with constant rotation and affine reference curve, and identify those charts as the adapted charts of inertial frames, recovering the force-covariance law.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm06-moving-frame",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
