            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked model check of the rheonomic ideal-constraint branch: one label of mass m constrained without friction to a straight wire through the origin that rotates uniformly in a horizontal plane, under the declared uniform downward field; the verification exhibits the unique maximal constrained motion through consistent data, computes the multiplier and the reaction, verifies the reaction-power identity and the mechanical-energy balance, and shows on explicit model data that an ideal rheonomic constraint performs positive work. The example is a model computation with declared idealizations (frictionless bead, massless rigid wire, prescribed uniform rotation, uniform field with the wire supporting the weight); every number is a model value, not a measurement.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.5.1 'Bead on a Rotating Hoop', Eqs. (2.61)-(2.62), printed pages 26-27 (PDF pages 32-33): a time-dependent holonomic constraint realized by a bead threaded on a uniformly rotating hoop, with the explicit embedding and kinetic energy",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 2.1 and Fig. 2.1, chapter page 1: the bead on a rotating ring as a model with one degree of freedom, and Section 4.6, chapter pages 21-23: the same model described from the non-inertial frame with the inertial forces",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Sections 1.4-1.5, printed pages 19-25 (PDF pages 22-28): time-dependent constraints can do actual work despite zero virtual work of the ideal reaction",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm06-pendulum-tension-and-reaction-power",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The planar pendulum as an ideal holonomic model: the multiplier, the tension and the vanishing reaction power of a scleronomic constraint",
          "statement": "Declare a one-label model of the force branch of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with $N=1$, mass $m>0$, time window\n$I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R^{3}$ (coordinates\n$(x,y,z)$, dimensions of [[def-cm01-units]]) and force map\n$$F:I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{3},\\qquad F(t,\\mathbf x,\\mathbf v):=(0,\\,-mg,\\,0),$$\nwhere $[m]=\\mu$ and the constant $g>0$ has dimension $\\ell\\tau^{-2}$.\nLet $l>0$ have dimension $\\ell$, and use a dimensionless angle $\\theta$ in\nradians. Constrain the label,\nwithout friction, to the circle of radius $l>0$ about the origin in the plane\n$z=0$ --- the ideal pendulum realized as a bead threaded on a smooth circular\nwire in a vertical plane --- by the regular holonomic-constraint datum\n$$O:=\\{(t,x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^{4}:(x,y)\\ne(0,0)\\},\\qquad k:=2,\\qquad G(t,x,y,z):=\\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}-l^{2},\\ z\\bigr)$$\nof [[def-cm06-holonomic-constraint-model]], and adopt the ideal-reaction law\nof [[post-cm06-ideal-reaction-law]]. The two constraint components have\ndimensions $\\ell^{2}$ and $\\ell$. The locus is\n$\\Sigma=\\{(t,l\\sin\\theta,-l\\cos\\theta,0):t,\\theta\\in\\mathbb R\\}$; write\n$$X(\\theta):=\\bigl(l\\sin\\theta,\\,-l\\cos\\theta,\\,0\\bigr),\\qquad X_{\\theta}(\\theta):=\\partial_{\\theta}X(\\theta)=\\bigl(l\\cos\\theta,\\,l\\sin\\theta,\\,0\\bigr),\\qquad u(\\theta):=\\bigl(\\sin\\theta,\\,-\\cos\\theta,\\,0\\bigr),\\qquad X(\\theta)=l\\,u(\\theta).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. the datum is regular and scleronomic, the tangent vector $X_{\\theta}(\\theta)$\n   is permitted for every $\\theta$, and the virtual displacements at\n   $X(\\theta)$ are the line $\\mathbb R\\,X_{\\theta}(\\theta)$;\n2. along any constrained motion the multiplier is\n   $$\\lambda(\\theta,\\theta')=\\Bigl(-\\frac{m}{2l}\\bigl(g\\cos\\theta+l\\,\\theta'^{2}\\bigr),\\,0\\Bigr)\\in\\mathbb R^{2}$$\n   evaluated at the motion's data, the second component --- tied to the plane\n   constraint $z=0$ --- vanishing along the motion, and the reaction is\n   $$R=-T\\,u(\\theta),\\qquad T=m\\bigl(g\\cos\\theta+l\\,\\theta'^{2}\\bigr),$$\n   a force of magnitude $|T|$ along the line of the link, toward the pivot\n   when $T>0$ (the **tension** of the model); the projection of the constrained\n   equation on the tangent gives $ml^{2}\\theta''+mgl\\sin\\theta=0$, and the\n   projections on $u$ and on the normal $\\mathbf e_{3}$ of the constraining\n   plane give the displayed tension and the vanishing of the second multiplier\n   component;\n3. conversely, for every $C^{2}$ function $\\theta:J\\to\\mathbb R$ with\n   $ml^{2}\\theta''+mgl\\sin\\theta=0$ on an interval $J$ with nonempty interior,\n   the curve $x(t):=X(\\theta(t))$ is a constrained motion of the model with\n   multiplier and reaction as in clause 2;\n4. the reaction power vanishes identically, $R\\cdot x'\\equiv0$, in agreement\n   with the scleronomic case of the rheonomic-power theorem, and the\n   mechanical energy $E=\\tfrac12ml^{2}\\theta'^{2}-mgl\\cos\\theta$ is constant\n   along the motion; expressed through the energy,\n   $T=3mg\\cos\\theta+2E/l$, and through the initial data $(\\theta_{0},\\theta_{0}')$,\n   $T=mg(3\\cos\\theta-2\\cos\\theta_{0})+ml\\,\\theta_{0}'^{2}$;\n5. for a motion with initial data at $t_{0}=0$ given by\n   $m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, $l=1\\,\\mathrm m$,\n   $g=10\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}$, $\\theta_{0}=\\pi/3$, $\\theta_{0}'=0$, the\n   initial energy is $E=-5\\ \\mathrm J$ and the tension is\n   $T=30\\cos\\theta-10\\ \\mathrm N$, with $T=20\\ \\mathrm N$ at $\\theta=0$ and\n   $T=5\\ \\mathrm N$ at $\\theta=\\pi/3$, positive on the reachable arc\n   $|\\theta|\\le\\pi/3$.\n\nAll values are model values under the declared idealizations; the model is the\nideal pendulum realized by a smooth rigid wire (equivalently a massless link,\nor a taut string while it stays taut), and the empirical qualifications are\nrecorded in the remarks.",
          "strategy": "A worked model check: verify the regular scleronomic circle datum, compute the multiplier and the reaction read as the tension, project the constrained equation to obtain the pendulum equation and the radial consistency condition, verify the converses on parametrized curves, check the identically vanishing reaction power and the conserved energy, and evaluate the tension formula on explicit model data.",
          "deps": [
            "def-oriented-integral",
            "thm-additivity-over-subintervals",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "thm-ftc-first-part",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle",
            "def-cm06-holonomic-constraint-model",
