          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked model check of the holonomic ideal-constraint branch: a mass point constrained without friction to a circle of radius l in a vertical plane about a fixed pivot under a declared uniform downward field, realized as a bead on a smooth circular wire, with the explicit two-component multiplier (the component tied to the constraining plane vanishing along the motion), the reaction read as the tension along the link, the standard pendulum equation obtained by projecting the constrained equation, the identically vanishing reaction power of the scleronomic constraint, and the conservation of the mechanical energy on explicit model data. The example is a model computation with the declared idealizations (smooth massless wire, equivalently a rigid link or a taut string while it stays taut; frictionless pivot; uniform field); every number is a model value, not a measurement, and the empirical status of a string that can go slack (or of a link that can buckle) is recorded as a limitation of the model.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.3.1, Eqs. (2.27)-(2.28), printed pages 17-18 (PDF pages 23-24): the simple pendulum with the constraint x^2+y^2=l^2, the equation theta-ddot=-(g/l) sin theta and the tension T=ml theta-dot^2+mg cos theta",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 6.5 (constrained systems: the plane pendulum) and Section 5.9: the multiplier solution of the pendulum and the constraint force",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Sections 2.1-2.2, chapter pages 1-4: Lagrange equations with a constraint force for a particle on a ring, and the pendulum as a one-degree-of-freedom constrained system",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Sections 1.4-1.5, printed pages 19-25 (PDF pages 22-28): ideal constraint reactions as covectors annihilating the fixed-time admissible virtual displacements",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm06-rotating-chart-inertial-terms",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "All four inertial terms checked on explicit data, and the exact return to an inertial chart",
          "statement": "Declare a one-label model of the force branch of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with $N=1$, mass $m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, time\nwindow $I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R^{3}$ and the\n**zero force map**\n$$F:I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{3},\\qquad F(t,\\mathbf x,\\mathbf v)\\equiv0$$\n(admissible: constant, hence of the standing regularity), in a fixed inertial frame $F$ with adapted coordinates $(t,x)$\n([[post-cm01-frames]], [[def-cm01-units]]). For real parameters\n$\\omega_{0},\\beta,\\gamma$, vectors $a_{0},v_{c}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ and\n[[def-cm06-moving-frame]] data, take $\\theta$ to be a dimensionless angle in\nradians, with $[\\omega_{0}]=\\tau^{-1}$, $[\\beta]=\\tau^{-2}$,\n$[\\gamma]=\\ell\\tau^{-2}$, $[a_{0,j}]=\\ell$ and\n$[v_{c,j}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ for each spatial component $j$:\n$$\\theta(t):=\\omega_{0}t+\\tfrac12\\beta t^{2},\\qquad R(t):=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta(t)&-\\sin\\theta(t)&0\\\\ \\sin\\theta(t)&\\cos\\theta(t)&0\\\\ 0&0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad c(t):=a_{0}+v_{c}t+\\tfrac12\\gamma t^{2}e_{1},$$\nconsider the motion $x(t):=x_{0}+v\\,t$ of the zero-force model with\n$x_{0}=(1,0,0)\\,\\mathrm m$, $v=(0,1,0)\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, its\nmoving-chart coordinate curve $y(t)=R(t)^{\\mathsf T}(x(t)-c(t))$, and the\ninertial-terms field\n$\\tau(t,y,\\dot y)=-\\bigl(2\\omega(t)\\times\\dot y+\\omega'(t)\\times y+\\omega(t)\\times(\\omega(t)\\times y)+R(t)^{\\mathsf T}c''(t)\\bigr)$.\n\nThen, for the parameter values $\\omega_{0}=2\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$,\n$\\beta=1\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}$, $\\gamma=3\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}$ and\n$a_{0}=v_{c}=0$:\n\n1. $R(t)\\in SO(3)$ is $C^{\\infty}$ and $\\Omega=R^{\\mathsf T}R'$ has the\n   angular velocity $\\omega(t)=\\theta'(t)e_{3}=(2+t)e_{3}$ and\n   $\\omega'(t)=e_{3}$, while $c''(t)=(3,0,0)$;\n2. $y(0)=e_{1}$, $\\dot y(0)=-e_{2}$, and the moving-chart coordinate\n   acceleration is $\\ddot y(0)=-3e_{1}-e_{2}$ (in units\n   $\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}$); the inertial-chart acceleration is\n   $\\ddot x(t)=0$ for every $t$;\n3. the four inertial terms at $t=0$ are $-2\\omega\\times\\dot y=-4e_{1}$,\n   $-\\omega'\\times y=-e_{2}$, $-\\omega\\times(\\omega\\times y)=+4e_{1}$ and\n   $-R^{\\mathsf T}c''=-3e_{1}$, and their sum is $\\tau(0,y(0),\\dot y(0))=-3e_{1}-e_{2}=\\ddot y(0)$;\n   clause 1 of [[pthm-cm06-inertial-forces]] therefore holds at $t=0$ for\n   these data, with $m\\ddot y=R^{\\mathsf T}F+m\\tau=m\\tau$;\n4. because $\\omega\\not\\equiv0$, clause 3 of that theorem identifies the chart\n   as **not** inertial; and with the alternative parameters\n   $\\omega_{0}=\\beta=\\gamma=0$, $a_{0}=(1,0,0)$,\n   $v_{c}=(0,1,0)$, the chart has constant rotation $R\\equiv I_{3}$ and affine\n   reference curve, hence vanishing inertial terms, and the same motion has\n   $\\ddot y\\equiv0$ in the chart, which is the inertial-frame description\n   with $y(t)=x(t)-a_{0}-v_{c}t$.\n\nAll values are model values in the declared coordinates; the example reports\nno measurement and asserts nothing about any apparatus.",
          "strategy": "A finite check: compute the chart quantities, evaluate all four inertial terms at one time on explicit data and compare with the chart acceleration of a uniform motion of the zero-force model, identify the chart as non-inertial through the criterion, and check an affine chart with constant rotation against the exact return to an inertial frame.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm06-moving-frame",
            "pthm-cm06-inertial-forces",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "def-cm01-units",
