            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The first variation of the action of a force model with a declared potential presentation, computed in the inertial chart: for every C^2 admissible curve and fixed-endpoint variation the variation exists and equals the integral of minus the potential gradient paired with the variation plus the mass-operator pairing of velocity with the variation's derivative, and the Euler-Lagrange expression of the mechanical Lagrangian is minus the sum of the mass-operator acceleration and the potential gradient. The result is a conditional deduction from the adopted force branch, the declared potential presentation and the cited mathematical first-variation formula; it asserts no equation of motion, which is the subject of the following items.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.1, printed pages 10-13 (PDF pages 16-19): the first variation of the action by integration by parts and the resulting Euler-Lagrange coefficient",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 5.2 (Euler's differential equation) and Section 5.8 (generalized coordinates in variational calculus)",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 1.2 and Chapter 2: the equation of motion in Cartesian data and the Lagrangian formulation with the kinetic-minus-potential density",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The stationary-action criterion and the potential-part Euler-Lagrange equation",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and the mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\ntime window $I_{0}$, mass operator $\\mathsf M$ and $C^{2}$ potential\n$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]).\nLet $a<b$ with $[a,b]\\subseteq I_{0}$, and let $x:[a,b]\\to Q$ be a $C^{2}$\nadmissible curve ([[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]]). Then the\nfollowing three conditions are equivalent:\n\n1. $x$ is stationary for the action $S$ of $L$;\n2. $E_{L}(x)(t)=0$ for every $t\\in[a,b]$;\n3. $\\mathsf M x''(t)=-\\nabla_{x}U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)$ for every $t\\in[a,b]$.\n\nCondition 3 is the model's equation of motion of the potential branch\n([[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iv)) written along the curve for the\npresentation $(U,d)$ with the potential part alone: the residual forces $d$ do\nnot appear, and the statement is therefore a statement about the potential\npart of the force map. No existence of curves satisfying these conditions is\nasserted.",
          "strategy": "Direct: combine the mathematical stationarity characterisation with the model-terms Euler-Lagrange identity and read the result through the equation of motion.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "pthm-cm07-first-variation-and-euler-lagrange-expression",
            "thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange",
            "lem-cm-first-variation-formula",
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
            "def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm01-configuration",
            "def-cm01-velocity-acceleration",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
