          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The Euler-Lagrange equation of the mechanical Lagrangian of a declared potential presentation: for a C^2 curve of the model on a fixed interval, stationarity of the action, the vanishing of the Euler-Lagrange expression and the pointwise equation mass-operator acceleration equals minus the potential gradient are equivalent. The equivalence identifies the variational critical-point condition with the potential-part equation on the interval, and with the full model equation when d is identically zero; it is conditional on the adopted force branch and the declared presentation, and it asserts no solution existence, no minimisation and no empirical content.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.1, printed pages 10-13 (PDF pages 16-19): stationarity of the action is equivalent to Lagrange's equations",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 5.2-5.3 and 6.2: Euler's differential equation and the Newtonian plausibility argument for the standard Lagrangian",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 2.1, chapter pages 1-2: Lagrange equations of motion for a system with kinetic minus potential energy",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm07-newton-equivalence",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Stationary curves of the mechanical action are exactly the restrictions of the model's motions (global potential branch)",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\ntime window $I_{0}$, mass operator $\\mathsf M$ and $C^{2}$ potential\n$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]).\nAssume the presentation has **no residual forces**, $d\\equiv0$, so that the\nforce map itself is $F_{i}(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x)$. Then:\n\n1. **motions are stationary.** If $x:J\\to Q$ is a motion of the model on an\n   interval $J$ with nonempty interior ([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]) and\n   $a<b$ with $[a,b]\\subseteq J\\cap I_{0}$, then the restriction $x|_{[a,b]}$\n   is stationary for the action $S$ on $[a,b]$.\n\n2. **stationary curves are restrictions of motions.** If $a<b$ with\n   $[a,b]\\subseteq I_{0}$ and $x:[a,b]\\to Q$ is a $C^{2}$ admissible curve that\n   is stationary for $S$, then $x$ is the restriction to $[a,b]$ of the\n   maximal motion of the model through the initial datum\n   $\\bigl(a,x(a),x'(a)\\bigr)$ (whose existence and uniqueness are\n   [[pthm-cm02-ivp]] clause 4); in particular the maximal interval of that\n   motion contains $[a,b]$.\n\n3. **identification.** Consequently the $C^{2}$ curves $x:[a,b]\\to Q$ that are\n   stationary for the action are exactly the restrictions to $[a,b]$ of the\n   motions of the model whose maximal interval contains $[a,b]$, and the model\n   equation satisfied is $\\mathsf M x''=-\\nabla_{x}U$ on that interval.\n\nThe statement is conditional on the adopted force branch and on the declared\npotential presentation with $d\\equiv0$; it asserts no existence of stationary\ncurves for arbitrary endpoint data, no minimisation and no empirical value.",
          "strategy": "Direct: read stationarity as the model equation, then compare a stationary curve with the maximal motion through its datum using uniqueness and the compact continuation criterion.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations",
            "pthm-cm07-first-variation-and-euler-lagrange-expression",
            "thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange",
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
