          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The stationary-action principle for the global potential branch of the force model: every motion of the model restricts to a stationary curve of the mechanical action on each closed subinterval, and conversely every C^2 stationary curve on a closed interval is the restriction of the unique maximal motion of the model through its initial datum, so that the stationary curves are exactly the restrictions of motions. The equivalence is conditional on the adopted force branch, on the declared potential presentation with no residual forces, and on the cited existence, uniqueness and continuation theorems; it asserts no global existence, no minimisation property and no empirical statement.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.1, printed pages 10-13 (PDF pages 16-19): Lagrange's equations as the stationarity condition of the action, equivalent to Newton's equations for a particle in a potential",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 6.2: the Newtonian plausibility argument identifying the stationary curves of the action T - U with the Newtonian trajectories",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 1.2 and Chapter 2: uniqueness of Newtonian evolution from initial data and the equivalence of the Lagrangian and Newtonian descriptions",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm07-coordinate-covariance-of-the-equations",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Time-dependent coordinate maps transform the potential-part equation into the substituted Euler-Lagrange equation",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\ntime window $I_{0}$, mass operator $\\mathsf M$ and $C^{2}$ potential\n$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]).\nLet $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$ be open and nonempty and let\n$X:\\Omega\\to Q$ be of class $C^{3}$ with $X_{q}(t,q)$ invertible for every\n$(t,q)\\in\\Omega$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]],\n[[def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map]]). Put\n\n$$\\widetilde O:=\\bigl\\{(t,q,v)\\in\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}:\\ \\bigl(t,X(t,q),X_{t}(t,q)+X_{q}(t,q)v\\bigr)\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}\\bigr\\},$$\n\n$$\\widetilde L(t,q,v):=L\\bigl(t,X(t,q),\\,X_{t}(t,q)+X_{q}(t,q)v\\bigr).$$\n\nLet $a<b$ and let $q:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R^{3N}$ be a $C^{2}$ curve with\n$(t,q(t),q'(t))\\in\\widetilde O$ for all $t\\in[a,b]$; put $x(t):=X(t,q(t))$.\nThen:\n\n1. $\\widetilde O$ is open, $\\widetilde L$ is of class $C^{2}$ on it, and\n\n$$E_{\\widetilde L}(q)(t)=X_{q}\\bigl(t,q(t)\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T}E_{L}(x)(t)\\qquad(t\\in[a,b]);$$\n\n   consequently $E_{\\widetilde L}(q)=0$ on $[a,b]$ if and only if\n   $E_{L}(x)=0$ on $[a,b]$, and (by [[pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations]])\n   $q$ satisfies the substituted Euler-Lagrange equation if and only if the\n   Cartesian description $x$ satisfies the model's potential-part equation\n   $\\mathsf M x''(t)=-\\nabla_{x}U(t,x(t))$ on $[a,b]$.\n\n2. If in addition there is an open set $W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ with\n   $q([a,b])\\subseteq W$ and $[a,b]\\times W\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}\\subseteq\\widetilde O$,\n   then $(\\widetilde O,\\widetilde L)$ is a time-dependent Lagrangian chart over\n   $[a,b]\\times W$ and: $q$ is stationary for the substituted action\n   $\\widetilde S(q)=\\int_{a}^{b}\\widetilde L(t,q,q')\\,dt$ if and only if $x$ is\n   stationary for $S$.\n\nThe statement is conditional on the adopted force branch, on the declared\npotential presentation and on the smooth invertibility of $X$; the residual\nforces $d$ do not enter, so clause 1 concerns the potential part of the force\nmap.",
          "strategy": "Direct: instantiate the mathematical covariance proposition at the mechanical Lagrangian and read the result through the model-terms identity and the chart dictionary.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "prop-cm-coordinate-covariance-of-the-euler-lagrange-expression",
            "pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations",
