            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Covariance of the model's equation of motion under a time-dependent coordinate map of the configuration domain: along a curve described by a C^3 map X with everywhere invertible configuration Jacobian, the Euler-Lagrange expression of the substituted Lagrangian is the transpose-Jacobian multiple of the inertial-chart expression, so the model's potential-part equation holds along the Cartesian description if and only if the substituted Euler-Lagrange equation holds along the coordinate description; when the chart condition holds, this is equivalent to stationarity of the substituted action. The statement is conditional on the adopted force branch, the potential presentation and the smooth invertibility of the coordinate map; it asserts nothing about constraints with fewer coordinates, which are treated separately, and no empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.2 'Changing Coordinate Systems', printed pages 13-16 (PDF pages 19-22): Lagrange's equations take the same form in any coordinate system, with the rotating-coordinate example",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 6.3-6.4 and 5.8: Lagrange equations from d'Alembert's principle in generalized coordinates and generalized coordinates in variational calculus",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 2.1, chapter pages 1-2: transformation of the Lagrangian description to arbitrary generalized coordinates",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm07-boundary-terms-and-gauge",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Total-derivative Lagrangians, the potential representative and scaling: invariant stationary curves with endpoint terms",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\ntime window $I_{0}$, mass operator $\\mathsf M$ and $C^{2}$ potential\n$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]).\nLet $a<b$ with $[a,b]\\subseteq I_{0}$, let\n$F:I_0\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^{3}$ with dimension\n$[F]=[S]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$, and define\n\n$$L'(t,x,v):=L(t,x,v)+F_{t}(t,x)+F_{x}(t,x)\\cdot v .$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $L'$ is $C^{2}$ on $I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$, has the same\n   dimension as $L$, and for every $C^{2}$ admissible curve $x:[a,b]\\to Q$ one\n   has $E_{L'}(x)(t)=E_{L}(x)(t)$ for all $t\\in[a,b]$ and\n\n$$S'(x)=S(x)+F\\bigl(b,x(b)\\bigr)-F\\bigl(a,x(a)\\bigr),$$\n\n   where $S'$ is the action of $L'$; consequently $x$ is stationary for $S'$\n   if and only if $x$ is stationary for $S$, and, by\n   [[pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations]], both are equivalent to the model's\n   potential-part equation $\\mathsf M x''=-\\nabla_{x}U$ along $x$.\n\n2. **Potential representative.** Let $c:I_{0}\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$\n   with dimension $[c]=[U]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ and antiderivative $C$\n   (i.e. $C'=c$). Then $\\widetilde U:=U+c$ remains of class $C^2$ and is the\n   representative of [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause (c),\n   with Lagrangian $\\widetilde L=K-\\widetilde U$. Then\n   $\\widetilde L=L+M$ where $M(t,x,v)=F_{t}+F_{x}\\cdot v$ for\n   $F(t,x):=-C(t)$, so clause 1 applies: the actions of $L$ and\n   $\\widetilde L$ differ by $C(a)-C(b)$, independently of the curve, and the\n   stationary curves and the model's equation are unchanged.\n\n3. **Scaling.** For every dimensionless real $\\lambda\\ne0$ one has\n   $E_{\\lambda L}(x)=\\lambda E_{L}(x)$ pointwise, so the stationary curves of\n   the Lagrangian $\\lambda L$ coincide with those of $L$; the value $\\lambda=0$\n   is excluded because the zero Lagrangian has every curve stationary.\n\nThe statement is conditional on the adopted force branch and presentation; it\nasserts no minimisation, no conservation law and no empirical content, and it\ndoes not remove the need to declare which representative and normalisation are\nused by an energy statement.",
          "strategy": "Direct: apply the mathematical gauge proposition to the mechanical chart and evaluate the potential-representative substitution and the scaling.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "prop-cm-lagrangian-boundary-terms-and-scaling",
            "pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations",
            "def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action",
