          "physical_scope": "The dictionary from the model's Cartesian description to generalized coordinates: a C^3 configuration map that is an immersion; when the coordinate count is 3N, an everywhere-invertible Jacobian gives local coordinate charts, while a global chart onto the image additionally requires injectivity; for a regular holonomic-constraint locus the parametrization is a surjective immersion and may cover a configuration more than once. The item also defines the substituted Lagrangian and kinetic metric, generalized forces as Jacobian-transpose multiples, and the declared Lagrange equations with residual generalized forces. It defines objects and a model class only; it asserts no equivalence with the constrained equation of motion, which is the reduction theorem of this page, and no empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.3 'Constraints and Generalised Coordinates', printed pages 17-22 (PDF pages 23-28): generalised coordinates, the kinetic energy as a quadratic form and generalised forces",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 6.3-6.4: Lagrange equations from d'Alembert's principle with generalized forces",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Sections 2.1-2.2, chapter pages 1-4: generalized coordinates constrained to define a configuration, and Lagrange equations with constraint forces",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm07-immersion-euler-lagrange-transport",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Transport of the Euler-Lagrange expression under a C^3 map: E of the substituted Lagrangian is the Jacobian-transpose multiple of E of the ambient Lagrangian",
          "statement": "Let $n,m\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n}$ and\n$Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{m}$ be open and nonempty, and let\n$X:\\Omega\\to Q$ be of class $C^{3}$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an\nopen interval and let $L:J\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R^{m}\\to\\mathbb R$\nbe of class $C^{2}$, with gradients $L_{x}$ and $L_{w}$ in the second and third\nslots ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]],\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). Define\n\n$$\\widetilde L:J\\times\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^{n}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad \\widetilde L(t,q,v):=L\\bigl(t,X(q),DX(q)v\\bigr).$$\n\nThen $\\widetilde L$ is of class $C^{2}$, and for every interval $J'\\subseteq J$\nwith nonempty interior and every $C^{2}$ curve $q:J'\\to\\Omega$\n([[def-interval]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]) with\n$x:=X\\circ q$ one has, writing $\\widetilde L_{q}$ and $\\widetilde L_{v}$ for the\ngradients of $\\widetilde L$ in the second and third slots,\n\n$$\\widetilde L_{q}\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)-\\frac{d}{dt}\\Bigl(\\widetilde L_{v}\\bigl(t,q(t),q'(t)\\bigr)\\Bigr)=DX\\bigl(q(t)\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T}\\Bigl(L_{x}\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr)-\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(L_{w}(t,x(t),x'(t))\\bigr)\\Bigr)$$\n\nfor every $t\\in J'$. In words: the Euler-Lagrange expression of the\nsubstituted Lagrangian is the transpose-Jacobian multiple of the\nEuler-Lagrange expression of the ambient Lagrangian. No rank or invertibility\ncondition on $DX$ is assumed; the identity holds for every $C^{3}$ map $X$,\nsquare or not.",
          "strategy": "Direct: differentiate the substituted Lagrangian in both slots by the chain rule and cancel the parametrization-correction terms.",
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
