          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The elimination of ideal scleronomic holonomic constraints by parametrization: for a global C^3 surjective immersion onto the constrained configurations of a scleronomic regular holonomic datum, a C^2 coordinate curve describes an ideal constrained motion of the force model exactly when it solves the declared Lagrange equations of the substituted Lagrangian with the residual generalized force; multiple coordinate representatives of one configuration are allowed. The reaction is then uniquely determined, annihilates the virtual displacements and admits the unique continuous multiplier. The statement is conditional on the adopted force branch, the potential presentation, the constraint datum with its rank hypothesis and the ideal-reaction law; it asserts no solution existence, no uniqueness of the coordinate curve for given data and no empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Sections 2.3.1-2.3.2, printed pages 17-22 (PDF pages 23-28): generalized coordinates eliminate the constraint forces for holonomic constraints, with the pendulum and bead examples",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 6.3 and 6.8: Lagrange equations from d'Alembert's principle and applications with holonomic constraints",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Sections 1.4-1.5, printed pages 19-25 (PDF pages 22-28): ideal constraint reactions annihilate admissible virtual displacements, so constraint forces are absent from the reduced generalized equations",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm07-scope-and-singular-warnings",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Scope of the stationary-action formulation: no minimisation, regular mechanical Lagrangians, no general velocity-constraint equivalence and empirical status",
          "statement": "- **Stationarity is not minimisation.** The defining condition of\n  [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]] is the vanishing of the first\n  variation for all fixed-endpoint variations, and\n  [[pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations]] identifies it with the model's\n  equation. It is not claimed that a stationary curve minimises the action, and\n  no second-variation or convexity hypothesis is imposed on this page:\n  [[rem-cm-scope-of-stationarity]] records the explicit quadratic model in\n  which stationary curves are not local minima, and the verification page of\n  this pair checks the analogous phenomenon for an oscillator of the model\n  class.\n\n- **The mechanical branch is regular.** For the mechanical Lagrangians of this\n  page the velocity Hessian in a chart is the kinetic matrix\n  $G(q)=DX(q)^{\\mathsf T}\\mathsf M\\,DX(q)$ (or $\\mathsf M$ in Cartesian data),\n  which is symmetric positive definite because $DX(q)$ is injective and\n  $\\mathsf M$ is; [[prop-cm-mechanical-chart-lagrangian-is-hyperregular]]\n  proves that such a chart Lagrangian is hyperregular with the explicit\n  Legendre inverse, so no failure of invertibility of the Legendre map\n  [[def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart]] occurs on this branch and\n  the Euler-Lagrange equations determine the coordinate accelerations. A\n  singular velocity Hessian — for which the Legendre map is not locally\n  invertible and the second-order equations need not determine accelerations —\n  is outside the hypotheses of every theorem on this page; the admissible\n  coordinate maps of the generalized-coordinate definition of this page\n  exclude it by injectivity of $DX$ and positive definiteness of $\\mathsf M$.\n\n- **Velocity constraints are not covered by the action principle.** The\n  reduction theorem of this page applies to ideal *holonomic* constraints. For\n  a genuine velocity constraint the unrestricted minimisation of the action of\n  the substituted Lagrangian, or of an augmented action with multipliers, is a\n  different law from the d'Alembert equation of the preceding page:\n  [[def-cm-nonholonomic-velocity-constraint-law]] defines both prescriptions\n  for the constraint $z'=xy'$ and\n  [[cex-cm-dalembert-and-vakonomic-evolutions-differ]] proves that their\n  evolutions differ from the same initial data. Therefore the model's\n  nonholonomic branch is not obtained by \"imposing a constraint inside the\n  action\", and the examples page quantifies the discrepancy. Any identification\n  of the two prescriptions requires its own justification and is not asserted\n  here.\n\n- **Empirical status.** The equivalence of the stationary-action condition with\n  the model's equation of motion is a conditional theorem of the adopted force\n  branch, the declared potential presentation and the cited mathematics: it\n  does not establish that nature extremises or stationarises an action, and it\n  adds no empirical content beyond the force model and its potential\n  presentation. When the presentation has residual forces the equations acquire\n  the generalized forces of the generalized-coordinate definition of this page\n  and the pure action reading is lost; comparing predictions with observations\n  tests the force law and the apparatus, not the variational reformulation.\n  [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause (c) supplies the further\n  reminder that the potential and the Lagrangian are defined only up to the\n  declared normalization, so no numerical value of the action is a model\n  prediction without fixing that convention.\n\n- **Not asserted.** No global existence of stationary curves, no theory of\n  minimisers in function spaces, no conjugate-point or Morse-theoretic\n  statements, no free- or variable-endpoint problems, and no field-theoretic\n  or infinite-dimensional actions are part of this page; the boundary-value\n  non-uniqueness of the oscillator, for instance, is recorded in the\n  verification page of the mathematics track, not here.",
          "strategy": "Scope audit: record the non-minimisation warning, the regularity of the mechanical branch, the nonholonomic exclusion and the empirical status, with the cited suppliers.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "def-cm07-generalized-coordinates-and-forces",
            "pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations",
            "def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy",
            "rem-cm-scope-of-stationarity",
            "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart",
            "prop-cm-mechanical-chart-lagrangian-is-hyperregular",
            "def-cm-nonholonomic-velocity-constraint-law",
            "cex-cm-dalembert-and-vakonomic-evolutions-differ"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm07-generalized-coordinates-and-forces": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations": "physical-result",
            "def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "rem-cm-scope-of-stationarity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-legendre-map-in-a-time-dependent-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-cm-mechanical-chart-lagrangian-is-hyperregular": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-nonholonomic-velocity-constraint-law": "mathematical-premise",
            "cex-cm-dalembert-and-vakonomic-evolutions-differ": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Scope audit of the stationary-action formulation of the force model: stationarity is not minimisation; the mechanical Lagrangians of this framework are regular and the chart Legendre map is hyperregular, so the second-order equations determine accelerations, while degenerate Lagrangians are excluded; unrestricted-action and d'Alembert dynamics are not generally equivalent for velocity constraints, as the cited counterexample shows for its specified model and data; and the stationary-action equivalence is a conditional theorem of the model, not empirical evidence. The remark asserts no new physical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.1, printed pages 10-13 (PDF pages 16-19): 'principle of least action' is a misnomer and the Lagrangian encodes the equations of motion",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 5.1 and 6.2: stationary versus extremal values and the mechanical Lagrangian T - U",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Section 1.10: nonholonomic constraints and the failure of stationary augmented-action to reproduce d'Alembert's equations",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15
        }
      ],
