          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked check of the stationary-action equivalence on the free one-label model: zero force map, zero potential, Cartesian Lagrangian of a free particle; on every closed time interval the stationary curves are exactly the affine uniform motions, for each pair of endpoint configurations there is exactly one, and the action difference to the stationary curve is a nonnegative quadratic functional of the variation, so the stationary curve is a minimiser within the fixed-endpoint class. The mass, position, time and velocity carry dimensions mass, length, time and length per time, respectively. All quantities are model values under the declared idealization of a particle subject to no force.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.1, printed pages 10-13 (PDF pages 16-19): the free particle action and the straight-line stationary path",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 1.1, chapter pages 1-2: free motion as uniform straight motion of a particle with fixed mass",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 16
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm07-oscillator-stationary-saddle-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The harmonic oscillator on a long interval: the stationary curve is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum",
          "statement": "Fix a duration $T>0$ and declare a one-label model of the force branch of\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with $N=1$, mass $m>0$, time window\n$I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R^{3}$, a fixed unit vector\n$e\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ with dimensionless components, and force map\n$F(t,x,v)=-k(e\\cdot x)e$ with constant $k>0$. This is the global potential\nmodel with $U(x)=\\tfrac12k(e\\cdot x)^{2}$ and no residual forces\n([[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]), with mechanical Lagrangian\n$$L(t,x,v)=\\tfrac12m\\lvert v\\rvert_{2}^{2}-\\tfrac12k(e\\cdot x)^{2}$$\n([[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]]). Under\n[[def-cm01-units]], $[m]=\\mu$, $[e]=1$, $[x]=\\ell$, $[t]=[T]=\\tau$,\n$[k]=\\mu\\tau^{-2}$, $[\\omega]=\\tau^{-1}$ and $[\\ell_{0}]=\\ell$, where\n$\\omega:=\\sqrt{k/m}>0$. Fix a length scale $\\ell_{0}>0$ and let\n$\\eta_{n}(t):=\\ell_{0}\\sin(n\\pi t/T)e$; the scalar $\\varepsilon$ below is\ndimensionless. Consider curves on $[0,T]$.\n\nThen:\n\n1. the zero curve $x_{*}\\equiv0$ is stationary for the action $S$;\n2. for every integer $n\\ge1$ the map $\\eta_{n}$ is a variation of $x_{*}$,\n   and for every dimensionless real $\\varepsilon$ one has the exact\n   identity\n   $$S(\\varepsilon\\eta_{n})=\\frac{mT\\ell_{0}^{2}}{4}\\,\\varepsilon^{2}\\Bigl(\\Bigl(\\frac{n\\pi}{T}\\Bigr)^{2}-\\omega^{2}\\Bigr);$$\n3. if $T>\\pi/\\omega$, then $S(\\varepsilon\\eta_{1})<0$ and\n   $S(\\varepsilon\\eta_{n})>0$ for every integer $n>\\omega T/\\pi$ and every\n   $\\varepsilon\\ne0$; since $\\varepsilon\\eta_{n}\\to0$ in the dimensionless\n   $C^{2}$ metric defined below as $\\varepsilon\\to0$, the stationary curve\n   $x_{*}$ is neither a local minimiser nor a local maximiser in that topology;\n4. if $0<T<\\pi/\\omega$, then $S(\\varepsilon\\eta_{n})>0$ for every $n\\ge1$ and\n   every $\\varepsilon\\ne0$: every variation in this family increases the\n   action, and no claim of global minimality is made.",
          "strategy": "Direct: evaluate the action of the scaled sine variations in closed form and read off the two signs.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "pthm-cm07-euler-lagrange-equations",
            "def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
