          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked gauge check on the free one-label model in three-dimensional configuration space: adding the total time derivative of F(t,x)=alpha*t*x_1 to the free Lagrangian changes the action by endpoint values and leaves the Euler-Lagrange expression equal to the original one, hence leaves stationary curves and the model equation unchanged. The position is a vector of length dimension, time has time dimension, alpha has force dimension, and F has action dimension. The potential normalization U + gamma*t is the special case F = -gamma*t^2/2, with gamma of energy-per-time dimension. The values are exact model values of a declared computation.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 9.3: equivalent Lagrangians differing by a total time derivative, and the arbitrary constant in the potential",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.5.7, printed page 37 (PDF page 43): addition of a total derivative to the Lagrangian leaves the equations of motion unchanged",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 16
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm07-nonholonomic-unrestricted-action-fails",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The unrestricted action principle fails for the normalized nonholonomic relation $z_s = x y_s$: the d'Alembert motion is not vakonomic",
          "statement": "Adopt the constraint model of [[def-cm-nonholonomic-velocity-constraint-law]]\nin dimensionless normalized variables: let $s=t/T_{0}$ be dimensionless time\nand $(x,y,z)=\\mathbf r/L_{0}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ be dimensionless coordinates,\nwhere $T_{0}>0$, $L_{0}>0$ and $m_{0}>0$ are reference quantities with\n$[T_{0}]=\\tau$, $[L_{0}]=\\ell$ and $[m_{0}]=\\mu$. Primes below mean differentiation with respect to $s$. The\nnormalized unit-mass equations have zero applied force and velocity constraint\n$z_{s}=xy_{s}$; in physical variables this is\n$\\dot r_{z}=(r_{x}/L_{0})\\dot r_{y}$, which is dimensionally consistent.\nThe physical kinetic action is $m_{0}L_{0}^{2}/T_{0}$ times the normalized\naction; scaling the augmented multiplier by $m_{0}L_{0}/T_{0}$ makes its\naugmented action the same positive multiple of the normalized one, preserving\nstationarity. Physical reactions scale as $m_{0}L_{0}/T_{0}^{2}$ times\nnormalized reactions, and physical virtual displacements scale as $L_{0}$, so\nthe zero-virtual-work condition is preserved. In these normalized variables\nthe d'Alembert prescription is\n$x_{ss}=0$, $y_{ss}=-\\lambda x$, $z_{ss}=\\lambda$ with\n$\\lambda=x_{s}y_{s}/(1+x^{2})$ along motions, and the vakonomic prescription\nis $x_{ss}=-\\eta y_{s}$, $y_{ss}=\\eta_{s}x+\\eta x_{s}$,\n$z_{ss}=-\\eta_{s}$ for the normalized multiplier $\\eta$. Consider the\nnormalized initial data\n$$(x,y,z)(0)=(0,0,0),\\qquad(x_{s},y_{s},z_{s})(0)=(1,1,0).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. the curve\n   $$x(s):=s,\\qquad y(s):=\\int_{0}^{s}\\frac{du}{\\sqrt{1+u^{2}}},\\qquad z(s):=\\int_{0}^{s}u\\,y_{s}(u)\\,du=\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}-1$$\n   is a d'Alembert motion with these data on $\\mathbb R$, and it satisfies\n   $y_{s}(s)=(1+s^{2})^{-1/2}>0$ and\n   $y_{ss}(s)=-s\\,(1+s^{2})^{-3/2}$ for every $s\\in\\mathbb R$, so\n   $y_{ss}(s)\\ne0$ whenever $s\\ne0$;\n2. $y_{ss}(1/2)=-4/(5\\sqrt{5})\\ne0$;\n3. consequently this d'Alembert motion is not a vakonomic motion of the same\n   constraint with the same data: on any nondegenerate interval where a\n   vakonomic pair for this curve is stationary, $x_{ss}=0$ and $y_{s}>0$\n   force $\\eta\\equiv0$, and then the vakonomic equation forces\n   $y_{ss}\\equiv0$. Every nondegenerate interval contains some $s\\ne0$, which\n   contradicts clause 1;\n4. hence, for this normalized model and initial data, the vakonomic\n   stationary-action prescription does not reproduce the d'Alembert motion.\n   This conclusion concerns the augmented-action prescription defined by the\n   vakonomic law above; it makes no claim about a separately specified\n   minimization problem or other variational prescriptions.",
          "strategy": "Direct: substitute the displayed curve into the d'Alembert equations, evaluate the curvature terms, and derive the contradiction with the vakonomic system.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-nonholonomic-velocity-constraint-law",
            "cex-cm-dalembert-and-vakonomic-evolutions-differ",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm01-configuration",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
            "def-real-power",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "thm-ftc-first-part",
            "thm-additivity-over-subintervals",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "def-oriented-integral",
