          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The symmetry vocabulary of the force model with a declared potential presentation in one inertial frame: symmetry data as triples (tau, xi, B) satisfying the model's Noether identity for the mechanical Lagrangian K - U, strict data and their exact relation to the chart-level infinitesimal point symmetries, the charge M v . xi - (K+U) tau - B together with its dimension and its behaviour under rescaling of the family parameter, and the status of the notion. The item defines only; it asserts no conservation law, no existence of any symmetry of a given model, and no empirical content.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.4.1 'Noether's Theorem', printed pages 24-26 (PDF pages 30-32): a one-parameter family of maps q_i(t) -> Q_i(s,t) is a continuous symmetry when the s-derivative of the Lagrangian vanishes, and the conserved quantity is the momentum paired with the generator",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 2.4, chapter pages 10-11 (PDF pages 28-29): cyclic coordinates and the generalized momentum as an integral of motion, the Noether theorem named as the general statement",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Section 1.8.5 'Noether's Theorem': parametric coordinate transformations, invariance of the Lagrangian and the Noether integral as the product of the momentum with the generator",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 1.3 'Symmetry and Conservation Laws', printed pages 12-15 (PDF pages 15-18): cyclic coordinates, conserved canonical momenta and the examples of centre-of-mass momentum and rotational angular momentum",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm08-point-noether",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The point Noether theorem of the model: conservation of the charge of a strict symmetry, and the residual-force rate",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\ntime window $I_{0}$, mass operator $\\mathsf M$ and $U$ of\n[[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]], and let $(\\tau,\\xi,B)$ be a\nsymmetry datum of the model's mechanical Lagrangian with $\\tau\\equiv0$\n([[def-cm08-symmetry-of-the-mechanical-lagrangian]] clause (a)); write\n\n$$C(t,x,v):=\\bigl(\\mathsf M v\\bigr)\\cdot\\xi(t,x)-B(t,x)\\qquad\\bigl((t,x,v)\\in O\\bigr)$$\n\nfor its charge. Then:\n\n1. **(conservation in the global potential model).** If $d\\equiv0$ and $\\xi$\n   is independent of $t$, then for every motion $x:J\\to Q$ of the model on an\n   interval $J$ with nonempty interior ([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]), the\n   function $t\\mapsto C\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr)$ is constant on $J$.\n\n2. **(residual-force rate).** For an arbitrary presentation $(U,d)$, along\n   every motion $x:J\\to Q$ the function $t\\mapsto C(t,x(t),x'(t))$ is of class\n   $C^{1}$ on $J$ and\n\n$$C'(t)=\\bigl\\langle d\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr),\\,\\xi\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr)\\bigr\\rangle\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\n\n   where the pairing is the Euclidean inner product of $\\mathbb R^{3N}$;\n   consequently $C$ is constant on $J$ if and only if this pairing vanishes\n   for every $t\\in J$.\n\nClause 1 is the conditional conservation law of the model's global potential\nbranch under a symmetry identity of its Lagrangian; clause 2 quantifies the\nexact rate at which a residual force destroys it. Neither clause asserts that\nany model possesses a strict symmetry datum, and neither carries empirical\ncontent.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm08-symmetry-of-the-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "def-cm-infinitesimal-point-symmetry",
            "thm-cm-point-symmetry-charge-is-conserved",
