          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Rotation symmetry of the force model with a declared potential presentation in one inertial frame: with a dimensionless generator vector and a rotation parameter measured in radians, the equivalence between the differential invariance condition of the potential along the rotation family with axis a and finite invariance on admissible rotation arcs, with the exact component compatibility criterion for all admissible endpoints, the resulting strict symmetry datum, conservation of the component a . J of the total angular momentum about the inertial origin along every motion of the global potential branch, its sharpness in that branch under a nonzero rotational derivative of U, and the vector form when the potential is invariant under all rotations about the origin. The theorem is conditional on the adopted force branch, the declared potential and the absence of residual forces in the conservation clauses; it asserts no rotation symmetry of any particular force law and carries no empirical content.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.4.1 'Example: Isotropy of Space', printed page 25 (PDF page 31): rotational invariance of a closed system and conservation of the component of total angular momentum in the direction of the rotation axis, with the sign convention of the cross product r_i x p_i",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 1.3, printed pages 13-14 (PDF pages 16-17): a system invariant under rotations about a symmetry axis has a conserved angular momentum, with p_phi = n . sum_i r_i x m_i v_i and the central-force note",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Section 1.8.5: for the central-potential Lagrangian the Noether integral of the rotation family is the angular momentum (with the source's inverse-family sign convention)",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm08-boost-boundary",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Galilean boost charge of the model: exact condition, uniform centre-of-mass motion, the induced motions and the boundary of the conservation law",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with\nmasses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$,\ntime window $I_{0}$, mass operator $\\mathsf M$ and $U$ of\n[[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]]. For this boost family take the\nparameter $s$ to have units of velocity and take $a\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ to be\ndimensionless; then $st a$ is a displacement, $\\xi^{a}=t\\mathbf a$ with\n$\\mathbf a:=(a,\\dots,a)\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}$ has units of time, and $B^{a}$ and\n$K_{a}$ have units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m}$, equal to\naction per unit velocity. Fix a nonzero vector\n$a\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ and consider the maps\n\n$$\\xi^{a}(t,x):=t\\mathbf a,\\qquad B^{a}(t,x):=a\\cdot\\sum_{i}m_{i}x_{i},$$\n\nso that $(\\tau,\\xi,B)\\equiv(0,\\xi^{a},B^{a})$ is a datum of\n[[def-cm08-symmetry-of-the-mechanical-lagrangian]]. Write\n$P(t):=\\sum_{i}m_{i}x_{i}'(t)$ for the total momentum and $X(t)$ for the\ncentre of mass of [[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]], where the scalar\ntotal mass is $M:=\\sum_i m_i$ and $M X(t)=\\sum_i m_i x_i(t)$; thus\nalong a motion $B^{a}(t,x(t))=M\\,a\\cdot X(t)$. Define the **boost charge**\n\n$$K_{a}(t):=a\\cdot\\bigl(t\\,P(t)-M X(t)\\bigr)=t\\,\\bigl(a\\cdot P(t)\\bigr)-\\sum_{i}m_{i}\\,a\\cdot x_{i}(t).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. **(the identity).** The datum $(0,\\xi^{a},B^{a})$ satisfies the model's\n   Noether identity on $O$ if and only if the translation-invariance condition\n   $\\sum_{i}a\\cdot\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x)=0$ for all $(t,x)\\in I_{0}\\times Q$\n   of clause 1 of [[pthm-cm08-translation]] holds.\n2. **(conservation).** If in addition $d\\equiv0$, then for every motion\n   $x:J\\to Q$ of the model on an interval $J$ with nonempty interior\n   ([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]), the function $t\\mapsto K_{a}(t)$ is constant\n   on $J$.\n3. **(uniform centre-of-mass motion).** Under the hypotheses of clause 2, the\n   function $t\\mapsto M\\,a\\cdot X(t)$ is affine on $J$ with slope\n   $a\\cdot P$.\n4. **(boosted motions).** Under the hypotheses of clause 2, let $x:J\\to Q$\n   be a motion and $s\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy $x(t)+st\\mathbf a\\in Q$ for every\n   $t\\in J$. Then $\\widetilde x(t):=x(t)+st\\mathbf a$ is a motion if and only if\n   $\\nabla_xU(t,\\widetilde x(t))=\\nabla_xU(t,x(t))$ for every $t\\in J$.\n   This equality, and hence the boosted-motion conclusion, follows if\n   each segment between $x(t)$ and $\\widetilde x(t)$ is contained in $Q$,\n   or if the all-admissible-endpoint finite invariance criterion of\n   [[pthm-cm08-translation]] clause 1 holds.\n\n5. **(the general rate and the boundary).** For an arbitrary presentation\n   $(U,d)$ and every motion $x:J\\to Q$,\n\n$$K_{a}'(t)=t\\,a\\cdot F_{\\mathrm{tot}}(t),\\qquad F_{\\mathrm{tot}}(t):=\\sum_{i}F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr);$$\n\n   consequently, when $d\\equiv0$, the charge $K_{a}$ is constant on $J$ if and\n   only if $t\\mapsto t\\sum_{i}a\\cdot\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x(t))$ vanishes\n   identically on $J$, and if the translation-invariance condition fails at\n   some state, then there are a time $t_{0}\\ne0$ and a state $x_{0}$ with\n   $g:=\\sum_{i}a\\cdot\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t_{0},x_{0})\\ne0$ such that the maximal\n   motion through $(t_{0},x_{0},0)$ satisfies $K_{a}'(t_{0})=-t_{0}\\,g\\ne0$\n   and $K_{a}$ is not constant on its interval.\n\nThe charge exists as a conserved quantity exactly under the same potential\ninvariance that conserves the momentum component $a\\cdot P$; the conservation\nclauses are conditional deductions of the global potential branch, and no\nempirical content is asserted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm08-symmetry-of-the-mechanical-lagrangian",
            "pthm-cm08-general-noether",
            "pthm-cm08-translation",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
