            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A mechanical system in a chart at a strict local minimum of its potential: the assigned small-oscillation quadratic model with mass metric M(q_0) and stiffness the Hessian D^2 U(q_0) (symmetric and non-negative), the identification of that model with the derivative linearization of the mechanical field, the complete normal-mode classification of the linearized motions with frequencies sqrt(lambda_i) and no exponential directions, and the Lyapunov stability of the nonlinear equilibrium by exact energy conservation. The stability conclusion is a statement about the declared chart model; it asserts no asymptotic attraction, no measured frequency, no particular physical system and no empirical claim, and the accuracy of the linear model as an approximation of the nonlinear motion is a separate finite-time statement on this page.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.6, Eqs. (2.113)-(2.129), printed pages 38-41 (PDF pages 44-47): expansion about an equilibrium, the quadratic matrices and the stability reading of the normal modes",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 3, Sections 3.1-3.2, printed pages 52-59 (PDF pages 55-62): the quadratic natural Lagrangian about a minimum and the generalized symmetric eigenproblem",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140, online edition), Section 6.7, Theorem 6.16: a fixed point of Newton's equation is stable if the potential has a local minimum there",
                "url": "https://schecter.math.ncsu.edu/ma_732_sp13/teschl_ode.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 16
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm09-linearization-error-bound",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Finite-time error control for the small-oscillation approximation: the quadratic remainder of the mechanical field and the variation-of-constants bound",
          "statement": "Fix positive coordinate units $s_i$, a time unit $t_*$ and an energy unit $e_*$ as in [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]]. Throughout this theorem $q_i=Q_i/s_i$, $t=t_{\\mathrm{phys}}/t_*$ and $v_i=(t_*/s_i)\\dot Q_i$ are dimensionless numerical chart and phase coordinates. $M$ and $U$ are the corresponding normalized metric and potential specified in that definition. In particular $(\\delta q,\\delta v)$ is a vector in one Euclidean numerical space; $r_0,\\delta,C,L,A,T$ below refer to that space and numerical time. All constants are calculated after this normalization. If the numerical error bound is $b(t)$, its physical position and velocity components are bounded respectively by $s_i b(t)$ and $(s_i/t_*)b(t)$ at physical time $t_*t$. Fixed positive linear coordinate/time changes preserve the stated regularity and mechanical equation, so no original chart model is excluded.\n\nLet $n\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n}$ be open, let\n$M:\\Omega\\to M_{n}(\\mathbb R)$ be a field of class $C^{3}$ with $M(q)$\nsymmetric positive definite for every $q\\in\\Omega$, and let\n$U:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^{3}$ with $\\nabla U(q_{0})=0$ at a point\n$q_{0}\\in\\Omega$. Let $G$ be the mechanical vector field of\n[[def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart]] on\n$\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^{n}$, let $r_{0}>0$ satisfy\n$\\overline B(q_{0},r_{0})\\subseteq\\Omega$ and define\n$F:\\{\\zeta=(\\delta q,\\delta v):q_{0}+\\delta q\\in\\Omega\\}\\to\\mathbb R^{2n}$ by\n$F(\\delta q,\\delta v):=G(q_{0}+\\delta q,\\delta v)$. Put\n$A:=DF(0)=DG(q_{0},0)$. Then:\n\n1. $F$ is of class $C^{2}$, $F(0)=0$, $F$ is locally Lipschitz in the state\nvariable, and\n$$DG(q_{0},0)(\\delta q,\\delta v)=\\bigl(\\delta v,\\,-M(q_{0})^{-1}D^{2}U(q_{0})\\,\\delta q\\bigr);$$\nmoreover there are constants $C,L\\ge0$ with\n$$\\lVert F(\\zeta)-A\\zeta\\rVert_{2}\\le C\\lVert\\zeta\\rVert_{2}^{2},\\qquad \\lVert F(\\zeta)\\rVert_{2}\\le L\\lVert\\zeta\\rVert_{2}\\qquad(\\lVert\\zeta\\rVert_{2}\\le r_{0});$$\none may take $L=\\sup_{\\lVert\\eta\\rVert_{2}\\le r_{0}}\\lVert DF(\\eta)\\rVert$\n([[def-operator-norm]]) and $C=\\Lambda/2$ for any Lipschitz constant\n$\\Lambda$ of $DF$ on the closed ball\n$\\overline B(0,r_{0})\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2n}$ ([[def-metric-ball]]).\n2. For every datum $z_{0}=(q_{0}+\\delta q_{0},v_{0})$ with\n$\\delta:=\\lVert(\\delta q_{0},v_{0})\\rVert_{2}>0$ and every $T>0$ with\n$\\delta e^{LT}<r_{0}$, the maximal solution $z=(q,v)$ of $z'=G(z)$ with\n$z(0)=z_{0}$ is defined on $[0,T]$ ([[def-maximal-ode-solution]]) and\nsatisfies, for every $t\\in[0,T]$,\n$$\\lVert z(t)-y(t)\\rVert_{2}\\le C\\delta^{2}\\,t\\,e^{(\\lVert A\\rVert+2L)t},$$\nwhere $y(t)=(q_0+\\xi(t),\\eta(t))$ and $\\widetilde y=(\\xi,\\eta):\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^{2n}$ solves $\\widetilde y'=A\\widetilde y$, $\\widetilde y(0)=(\\delta q_0,v_0)$. Thus $y$ is the linear approximation translated back to the original chart.\n3. Writing $\\widetilde y=(\\xi,\\eta)$ for the displacement linear solution of clause 2, the first\ncomponent $\\xi$ is exactly the unique motion of the small-oscillation model\nat $q_{0}$ of clause (d) of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]] with datum\n$(\\delta q_{0},v_{0})$: $\\xi$ is $C^{2}$ on $\\mathbb R$ with\n$M(q_{0})\\xi''+D^{2}U(q_{0})\\xi=0$, $\\xi(0)=\\delta q_{0}$ and\n$\\xi'(0)=v_{0}$.\n\nNo all-time statement, no statement for data violating the smallness\ncondition $\\delta e^{LT}<r_{0}$, and no value of any measured quantity is\nasserted.",
          "strategy": "Direct: prove the quadratic-remainder bounds for the translated mechanical field by compactness and the fundamental theorem along rays, then apply the finite-time linearization theorem and identify the linear solution with the small-oscillation motion.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model",
