          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked instance of the coupled quadratic model: two equal masses m on a line joined by a coupling spring c with outer springs k to fixed points, the explicit normal modes, frequencies sqrt(k/m) and sqrt((k+2c)/m), the single-mode initial data, the conserved mode energies, and the zero-stiffness case k=0 in which the pair's translation is an affine zero mode and only the relative motion oscillates. The masses and spring constants are model parameters with declared units; no measured value, no realized apparatus and no empirical claim is present.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 6, Section 6.2, Eqs. (6.16)-(6.30), chapter pages 3-8 (PDF pages 127-132): two coupled oscillators, the eigenproblem and the in-phase and anti-phase normal modes",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.6, printed pages 38-45 (PDF pages 44-51): normal modes of coupled systems and the two-mode structure",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 18
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm09-triatomic-translation-zero-mode",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A linear triatomic chain: the translation zero mode, the antisymmetric stretch and the symmetric stretch, with the corrected frequency comparison",
          "statement": "The algebra below uses the fixed-unit numerical convention of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]], with coordinate units metres, time unit $1\\,\\mathrm s$ and energy unit $1\\,\\mathrm J$. Physical model parameters are given their stated units; in matrix products, initial-vector identities and coefficient formulas their numerical coefficients in these fixed units are used. Modal amplitudes have common unit $a_*=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{1/2}(1\\,\\mathrm s)$; the $j$-th component of a mass-normalized modal vector has unit $s_j/a_*$. Modal oscillatory and hyperbolic coefficients have unit $a_*$, and zero-mode slopes have unit $a_*/(1\\,\\mathrm s)$. Physical coordinates and energies are reconstructed with the coordinate units and $1\\,\\mathrm J$, respectively. In particular a numerical single-mode datum $q(0)=u_i$ means a modal amplitude of one in this common amplitude unit.\n\nGlobal-time, growth and boundedness claims below refer to the unique maximal extension of the autonomous free equation on $\\mathbb R$; local motions remain its restrictions. Values at $0$ in coefficient formulas are evaluated on that extension when the original interval omits $0$.\n\nConsider three particle labels constrained to a line, with coordinates\n$x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}$ in metres and masses $m,m_c,m>0$ read in kilograms\n([[def-cm01-units]]); the outer labels have mass $m$ and the middle label\nmass $m_c$. Adjacent labels interact through identical springs of stiffness\n$k>0$ in kilograms per second squared, and the potential is approximated by\nits quadratic part $U=\\tfrac{k}{2}\\bigl((x_{1}-x_{2})^{2}+(x_{2}-x_{3})^{2}\\bigr)$\nabout the equilibrium. The model is the coupled quadratic model of\n[[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]] with $n=3$, $q=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3})$,\n\n$$M=\\operatorname{diag}(m,m_c,m),\\qquad K=k\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1&0\\\\-1&2&-1\\\\0&-1&1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $M$ is symmetric positive definite and $K$ is symmetric with\n$\\ker K=\\operatorname{span}\\{(1,1,1)\\}$;\n2. the modal data are as follows, with $M$-orthonormal modal vectors:\n   (a) $\\lambda_{1}=0$ with $u_{1}=(1,1,1)/\\sqrt{2m+m_c}$: the **translation\n   mode**, in which the three labels move together;\n   (b) $\\lambda_{2}=k/m$ with $u_{2}=(1,0,-1)/\\sqrt{2m}$: the\n   **antisymmetric stretch**, in which the outer labels move oppositely and\n   the middle label is at rest;\n   (c) $\\lambda_{3}=\\frac{k}{m}\\bigl(1+\\frac{2m}{m_c}\\bigr)$ with\n   $u_{3}=(1,-2m/m_c,1)/\\sqrt{2m(1+2m/m_c)}$: the **symmetric stretch**, in which\n   the outer labels move together and the middle label moves oppositely;\n3. every motion is\n$$q(t)=(a+bt)u_{1}+\\bigl(A\\cos(\\omega_{2}t)+B\\sin(\\omega_{2}t)\\bigr)u_{2}+\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega_{3}t)+D\\sin(\\omega_{3}t)\\bigr)u_{3}$$\nwith $\\omega_{2}=\\sqrt{k/m}$ and $\\omega_{3}=\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}(1+\\frac{2m}{m_c})}$,\nand the pure-zero-mode motions are exactly the affine curves\n$q(t)=(a+bt)u_{1}$; for $b\\ne0$ they are unbounded on $[0,\\infty)$;\n4. $\\omega_{3}>\\omega_{2}$ for all $m,m_c>0$: in this model the symmetric\nstretch is the stiffer vibration.",
          "strategy": "Direct: verify the three eigenpairs of the linear triatomic chain by explicit matrix multiplication, read the motion statement from the free-mode theorem and compare the frequencies.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy",
            "prop-cm-two-mass-spring-modes",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy": "physical-result",
            "prop-cm-two-mass-spring-modes": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
