        {
          "id": "ex-cm09-double-pendulum-linear-modes",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The equal-arm double pendulum in the linear approximation: the slow same-side mode, the fast opposite-side mode and the bounded modal motions",
          "statement": "The algebra below uses the fixed-unit numerical convention of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]], with coordinate units radians, time unit $1\\,\\mathrm s$ and energy unit $1\\,\\mathrm J$. Physical model parameters are given their stated units; in matrix products, initial-vector identities and coefficient formulas their numerical coefficients in these fixed units are used. Modal amplitudes have common unit $a_*=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{1/2}(1\\,\\mathrm s)$; the $j$-th component of a mass-normalized modal vector has unit $s_j/a_*$. Modal oscillatory and hyperbolic coefficients have unit $a_*$, and zero-mode slopes have unit $a_*/(1\\,\\mathrm s)$. Physical coordinates and energies are reconstructed with the coordinate units and $1\\,\\mathrm J$, respectively. In particular a numerical single-mode datum $q(0)=u_i$ means a modal amplitude of one in this common amplitude unit.\n\nGlobal-time, growth and boundedness claims below refer to the unique maximal extension of the autonomous free equation on $\\mathbb R$; local motions remain its restrictions. Values at $0$ in coefficient formulas are evaluated on that extension when the original interval omits $0$.\n\nLet a planar double pendulum consist of two equal particle labels of mass\n$m>0$ connected by two equal massless rods of length $l>0$, pivoted at a\nfixed point; let $\\theta_{1},\\theta_{2}$ be the angles of the rods from the\ndownward vertical, read in radians (dimensionless under the angle convention\nof [[def-cm01-units]]), and let $g>0$ be the uniform gravitational\nacceleration of the model. In the linear approximation about the downward\nequilibrium of the cited treatment, the small-oscillation data are the\ncoupled quadratic model of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]] with $n=2$,\n\n$$M=ml^{2}\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad K=mgl\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad J\\equiv0 .$$\n\nWrite $\\gamma_{s}:=2-\\sqrt2$, $\\gamma_{f}:=2+\\sqrt2$. Then:\n\n1. $M$ is symmetric positive definite (with $M^{1/2}$ its unique positive\ndefinite square root) and $K$ is symmetric positive definite, so the model\nis oscillatory with both modal stiffnesses positive;\n2. the modal data are\n$$\\lambda_{s}=\\frac{g}{l}\\gamma_{s}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad w_{s}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\\\sqrt2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\lambda_{f}=\\frac{g}{l}\\gamma_{f}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad w_{f}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-\\sqrt2\\end{pmatrix},$$\nthat is $Kw_{s}=\\lambda_{s}Mw_{s}$ and $Kw_{f}=\\lambda_{f}Mw_{f}$; the\n$M$-orthonormal modal vectors are\n$u_{s}=w_{s}/\\sqrt{2ml^{2}\\gamma_{f}}$ and\n$u_{f}=w_{f}/\\sqrt{2ml^{2}\\gamma_{s}}$;\n3. the mode frequencies are\n$$\\omega_{s}=\\sqrt{\\frac{g}{l}\\bigl(2-\\sqrt2\\bigr)}<\\omega_{f}=\\sqrt{\\frac{g}{l}\\bigl(2+\\sqrt2\\bigr)},$$\nand every motion is bounded on $\\mathbb R$ and has the form\n$$q(t)=\\bigl(A\\cos(\\omega_{s}t)+B\\sin(\\omega_{s}t)\\bigr)u_{s}+\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega_{f}t)+D\\sin(\\omega_{f}t)\\bigr)u_{f};$$\n4. in the slow mode $u_{s}$ the two angles have the same sign\n($\\theta_{2}=\\sqrt2\\,\\theta_{1}$) and in the fast mode $u_{f}$ they have\nopposite signs ($\\theta_{2}=-\\sqrt2\\,\\theta_{1}$); the datum\n$q(0)=u_{s}$, $q'(0)=0$ excites exactly the slow mode and the datum\n$q(0)=u_{f}$, $q'(0)=0$ exactly the fast mode.",
          "strategy": "Direct: check positive definiteness, verify the two eigenpairs of the equal-arm double pendulum data by explicit multiplication, and read the bounded modal motions and the frequency comparison from the free-mode theorem.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy": "physical-result",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked instance of the coupled quadratic model: the two-angle small-oscillation data M = m l^2 [[2,1],[1,1]], K = m g l diag(2,1) attributed to the linearisation of an equal-mass equal-length planar double pendulum, with its two modal data (the slower same-side mode of frequency sqrt((g/l)(2-sqrt2)) and the faster opposite-side mode of frequency sqrt((g/l)(2+sqrt2))), the general bounded modal motion and the pure-mode data. The example verifies the quadratic model on the declared matrices; it does not derive the pendulum Lagrangians, does not control the error of the linear approximation at finite amplitude and asserts no measured period.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.6.1, Eqs. (2.130)-(2.132), printed pages 40-42 (PDF pages 46-48): the equal-arm double pendulum Lagrangian truncated to second order, the linearised equations and the two normal modes",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 3, Sections 3.1-3.2, printed pages 52-59 (PDF pages 55-62): the quadratic natural Lagrangian about a minimum and the decoupled normal modes",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 16
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm09-degenerate-oscillator-subspace",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A doubly degenerate quadratic model: every direction is a mode, the modal basis is not intrinsic, and coupling splits the double frequency",
          "statement": "The algebra below uses the fixed-unit numerical convention of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]], with coordinate units metres, time unit $1\\,\\mathrm s$ and energy unit $1\\,\\mathrm J$. Physical model parameters are given their stated units; in matrix products, initial-vector identities and coefficient formulas their numerical coefficients in these fixed units are used. Modal amplitudes have common unit $a_*=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{1/2}(1\\,\\mathrm s)$; the $j$-th component of a mass-normalized modal vector has unit $s_j/a_*$. Modal oscillatory and hyperbolic coefficients have unit $a_*$, and zero-mode slopes have unit $a_*/(1\\,\\mathrm s)$. Physical coordinates and energies are reconstructed with the coordinate units and $1\\,\\mathrm J$, respectively. In particular a numerical single-mode datum $q(0)=u_i$ means a modal amplitude of one in this common amplitude unit.\n\nGlobal-time, growth and boundedness claims below refer to the unique maximal extension of the autonomous free equation on $\\mathbb R$; local motions remain its restrictions. Values at $0$ in coefficient formulas are evaluated on that extension when the original interval omits $0$.\n\nConsider two uncoupled oscillators of equal mass and stiffness, modelled by\nthe coupled quadratic model of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]] with\n$n=2$,\n\n$$M=mI_{2},\\qquad K=kI_{2},\\qquad J\\equiv0,\\qquad m>0,\\ k>0 .$$\n\nThe configuration-vector coefficients $a,b$ in clause 2 are not modal coefficients: their physical components have the coordinate unit $1\\,\\mathrm m$; converting them to modal amplitudes multiplies by $M^{1/2}$ in the numerical convention.\n\nThen:\n\n1. $M$ and $K$ are symmetric positive definite, the matrix\n$M^{-1/2}KM^{-1/2}=(k/m)I_{2}$ has the **double** eigenvalue\n$\\lambda=k/m$, the modal eigenspace is $E_{\\lambda}=\\mathbb R^{2}$ and\n$N_{\\lambda}:=\\mathbb R^{2}$; every orthonormal basis\n$\\{e_{1},e_{2}\\}$ of $\\mathbb R^{2}$ is an orthonormal eigenbasis of that\nmatrix, so the decoupling data are not unique;\n2. every motion is\n$$q(t)=\\cos(\\omega t)\\,a+\\sin(\\omega t)\\,b,\\qquad a,b\\in\\mathbb R^{2},\\qquad \\omega=\\sqrt{k/m},$$\nand every nonzero vector $a\\in\\mathbb R^{2}$ spans a pure mode: the datum\n$q(0)=a$, $q'(0)=0$ produces the single-frequency oscillation\n$q(t)=\\cos(\\omega t)a$;\n3. the frequency $\\omega$ and the subspace $N_{\\lambda}=\\mathbb R^{2}$ are\nintrinsic, while individual modal vectors are not: there is no\nbasis-independent meaning to \"the first mode\" and no separation of the\nmotions into two distinguished one-parameter families;\n4. **splitting by coupling.** The two-mass model of\n[[ex-cm09-two-mass-spring-modes]] with coupling $c>0$ has stiffness\n$K_{c}=\\begin{pmatrix}k+c&-c\\\\-c&k+c\\end{pmatrix}$, which equals $kI_{2}$ at\n$c=0$ and coincides with the data of this example when $c=0$; for $c>0$ its\nmodal stiffnesses are the **distinct** numbers $k/m$ and $(k+2c)/m$, so an\nexplicit change of the model data separates the double eigenvalue into the\nunchanged in-phase frequency and the raised relative frequency.",
          "strategy": "Direct: identify the scalar multiple of the identity, read the double eigenvalue and the flexibility of the modal basis, and compare the data with the coupled two-mass instance to exhibit the splitting.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy",
            "ex-cm09-two-mass-spring-modes",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy": "physical-result",
            "ex-cm09-two-mass-spring-modes": "operational-prerequisite",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked instance of degeneracy in the coupled quadratic model: two identical uncoupled oscillators, M = m I, K = k I, with the double modal stiffness k/m, the full two-dimensional eigenspace, the four-parameter family of motions, the non-intrinsic status of every modal basis and the splitting of the double frequency into k/m and (k+2c)/m when a coupling c>0 of the two-mass model is switched on. The parameters are model data; the example asserts no measured frequency and no general stability-of-degeneracy theorem.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 3, Sections 3.1-3.2, printed pages 52-59 (PDF pages 55-62): the generalized symmetric eigenproblem and the warning that degenerate eigenspaces need careful treatment",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 6, Section 6.2, chapter pages 3-6 (PDF pages 127-130): normal modes of coupled oscillators and the merging of mode frequencies when the coupling vanishes",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 19
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm09-unstable-quadratic-mode",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The inverted pendulum about its upward equilibrium: the negative modal stiffness, the exponential quadratic mode and the nonlinear instability",
          "statement": "The algebra below uses the fixed-unit numerical convention of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]], with coordinate units radians, time unit $1\\,\\mathrm s$ and energy unit $1\\,\\mathrm J$. Physical model parameters are given their stated units; in matrix products, initial-vector identities and coefficient formulas their numerical coefficients in these fixed units are used. Modal amplitudes have common unit $a_*=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{1/2}(1\\,\\mathrm s)$; the $j$-th component of a mass-normalized modal vector has unit $s_j/a_*$. Modal oscillatory and hyperbolic coefficients have unit $a_*$, and zero-mode slopes have unit $a_*/(1\\,\\mathrm s)$. Physical coordinates and energies are reconstructed with the coordinate units and $1\\,\\mathrm J$, respectively. In particular a numerical single-mode datum $q(0)=u_i$ means a modal amplitude of one in this common amplitude unit.\n\nGlobal-time, growth and boundedness claims below refer to the unique maximal extension of the autonomous free equation on $\\mathbb R$; local motions remain its restrictions. Values at $0$ in coefficient formulas are evaluated on that extension when the original interval omits $0$.\n\nIn the formulas below, $\\cosh u=(e^{u}+e^{-u})/2$ and\n$\\sinh u=(e^{u}-e^{-u})/2$, as in the hyperbolic family of\n[[pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy]].\n\nLet a planar pendulum consist of one particle label of mass $m>0$ attached\nto a fixed pivot by a massless rod of length $l>0$, and let $\\theta$ be its\nangle from the **upward** vertical, read in radians (dimensionless under\n[[def-cm01-units]]); let $g>0$ be the uniform gravitational acceleration of\nthe model. In the chart with kinetic metric field $M(\\theta)=ml^{2}$ and\npotential $U(\\theta)=mgl\\cos\\theta$, the mechanical system of\n[[def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart]] has energy\n$E=\\tfrac12ml^{2}\\theta'^{2}+mgl\\cos\\theta$ and equation\n$ml^{2}\\theta''=mgl\\sin\\theta$, that is $\\theta''=(g/l)\\sin\\theta$. Then:\n\n1. $\\theta=0$ is a mechanical equilibrium and a strict local **maximum** of\n$U$ on the chart, with $K:=D^{2}U(0)=-mgl<0$; the small-oscillation model\nof clause (d) of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]] is the one-dimensional\nquadratic model $ml^{2}\\xi''-mgl\\,\\xi=0$, whose modal stiffness is\n$\\lambda=-g/l<0$;\n2. with $\\kappa:=\\sqrt{g/l}>0$, physical frequency unit $\\mathrm s^{-1}$, every motion of that quadratic model on an\ninterval with nonempty interior is\n$$\\xi(t)=a\\cosh(\\kappa t)+d\\sinh(\\kappa t),\\qquad a,d\\in\\mathbb R,$$\nHere $a,d$ are coefficients of the angle coordinate, read in radians; $b$ below denotes its initial angular velocity in radians per second. The datum $\\xi(0)=0$, $\\xi'(0)=b\\ne0$ gives\n$\\xi(t)=(b/\\kappa)\\sinh(\\kappa t)$, unbounded on $[0,\\infty)$;\n3. the rest point of the quadratic model is not Lyapunov stable\n([[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]]): with\n$\\varepsilon_{0}=1$ and every $\\delta>0$, a datum of size\n$<\\delta$ leads to a motion reaching norm $1$;\n4. the **nonlinear** upward equilibrium $(\\theta,\\theta')=(0,0)$ of the\npendulum chart system is not Lyapunov stable either: the field\n$F(\\theta,v)=(v,(g/l)\\sin\\theta)$ is $C^{1}$ with $F(0,0)=0$ and\n$DF(0,0)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\ g/l&0\\end{pmatrix}$, whose eigenvalues\n$\\pm\\sqrt{g/l}$ are real, nonzero and include the positive value\n$\\sqrt{g/l}$, so the hyperbolic-saddle theorem applies.",
          "strategy": "Direct: read the negative stiffness of the inverted pendulum from its chart potential, solve the repulsive scalar equation, and apply the free-mode instability clause and the hyperbolic-saddle theorem.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy",
            "rem-cm09-zero-modes-degeneracy-and-scope",
            "pthm-cm09-linearization-at-a-strict-minimum",
            "def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart",
            "prop-cm-linearization-of-the-mechanical-system-at-an-equilibrium",
            "thm-cm-instability-of-a-hyperbolic-saddle",
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous",
            "thm-intermediate-value",
            "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "thm-cm-homogeneous-oscillator-solution-classification",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "prop-cm-oscillator-degenerate-and-repulsive-cases"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
