            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked instance of an unstable quadratic direction: the planar inverted pendulum of mass m and length l about its upward equilibrium, with chart data M = m l^2 and U(theta) = m g l cos(theta) measured from the upward vertical, negative Hessian -m g l, the exact exponential motions of the small-oscillation model and the exponential escaping family of the quadratic model, together with the nonlinear Lyapunov instability of the upward equilibrium obtained from the hyperbolic-saddle theorem. The example is a conditional statement about the declared mathematical model; g, m and l are model parameters and no measured period or escape time is asserted.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.6, Eqs. (2.116)-(2.124), printed pages 38-41 (PDF pages 44-47): the two behaviours f'(x_0)<0 and f'(x_0)>0 about an equilibrium, the exponential family and the linear instability for a positive derivative",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 3, Section 3.2, Eqs. (3.8)-(3.23), chapter pages 2-8 (PDF pages 34-40): Taylor expansion at a fixed point, the inverted-pendulum linearization and the caution that a nondegenerate critical point does not by itself settle nonlinear stability",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 18
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm09-forced-resonant-mode-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A drive along the in-phase pattern of the two-mass chain: resonance exactly at that mode's frequency and a bounded steady response off resonance",
          "statement": "The algebra below uses the fixed-unit numerical convention of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]], with coordinate units metres, time unit $1\\,\\mathrm s$ and energy unit $1\\,\\mathrm J$. Physical model parameters are given their stated units; in matrix products, initial-vector identities and coefficient formulas their numerical coefficients in these fixed units are used. Modal amplitudes have common unit $a_*=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{1/2}(1\\,\\mathrm s)$; the $j$-th component of a mass-normalized modal vector has unit $s_j/a_*$. Modal oscillatory and hyperbolic coefficients have unit $a_*$, and zero-mode slopes have unit $a_*/(1\\,\\mathrm s)$. Physical coordinates and energies are reconstructed with the coordinate units and $1\\,\\mathrm J$, respectively. In particular a numerical single-mode datum $q(0)=u_i$ means a modal amplitude of one in this common amplitude unit.\n\nAdopt the two-mass chain of [[ex-cm09-two-mass-spring-modes]]: mass metric\n$M=mI_{2}$, stiffness $K=\\begin{pmatrix}k+c&-c\\\\-c&k+c\\end{pmatrix}$ with\n$m>0$, $k>0$, $c>0$, and modal vectors $u_{\\pm}=(1,\\pm1)/\\sqrt{2m}$ with\nmodal stiffnesses $\\lambda_{+}=k/m$, $\\lambda_{-}=(k+2c)/m$ and frequencies\n$\\omega_{+}=\\sqrt{k/m}$, $\\omega_{-}=\\sqrt{(k+2c)/m}$. Choose a real drive coefficient $F\\ne0$. In the physical equation $J=Fu_+\\cos(\\Omega t)$ its unit is $\\mathrm{kg}^{3/2}\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}$, since $u_+$ has unit $\\mathrm{kg}^{-1/2}$ and $J$ is a force. Thus $F/m$ is a modal force amplitude with unit $a_*/\\mathrm s^2$, and $|F|/(2c)$ below is a modal coordinate amplitude with unit $a_*$; multiplying by $u_+$ gives each particle's displacement amplitude. Choose a frequency $\\Omega\\ge0$ and prescribe\n$$J(t)=F\\cos(\\Omega t)\\,u_{+}.$$\nFor $\\Omega>0$ this is the sinusoidal drive of\n[[post-cm04-sinusoidal-drive-model]]; for $\\Omega=0$ it is the constant\nforce allowed by clause (b) of [[def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model]]. In\neither case it is prescribed external data, so the model is the forced\ncoupled quadratic model $Mq''+Kq=J$. The all-time claims below concern\nmaximal motions on $\\mathbb R$; local solutions are their restrictions. Then:\n\n1. the modal force components of $J(t)=J_{0}\\cos(\\Omega t)$ with\n$J_{0}=Fu_{+}$ are $\\psi_{0}=O^{\\mathsf T}M^{-1/2}J_{0}=(F/m,\\,0)$: the\ndrive excites the in-phase modal coordinate alone, with modal amplitude\n$F/m$, and leaves the relative coordinate unforced;\n2. if $\\Omega=\\omega_{+}=\\sqrt{k/m}$, then\n$$q^{\\mathrm{p}}(t)=\\frac{F}{2m\\omega_{+}}\\,t\\sin(\\omega_{+}t)\\,u_{+}$$\nis a motion of the model, of in-phase pattern, with linearly growing\namplitude $|F|t/(2m\\omega_{+})$, and **every** motion of the model is\nunbounded on $[0,\\infty)$;\n3. if $\\Omega^{2}\\ne k/m$ then every motion of the model is bounded on\n$[0,\\infty)$; in particular at $\\Omega=\\omega_{-}=\\sqrt{(k+2c)/m}$ — the\nfrequency of the *unforced* relative mode — the drive is non-resonant and\nthe steady modal response has amplitude\n$$\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert/m}{\\lvert k/m-(k+2c)/m\\rvert}=\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{2c};$$\n4. the resonance condition is exactly that the drive frequency equal the\nfrequency of a mode whose modal force component does not vanish: here the\nin-phase mode at $\\omega_{+}$, not the relative mode at $\\omega_{-}$.",
          "strategy": "Direct: compute the modal force of the in-phase drive, instantiate the resonant and non-resonant cases of the forced modal theorem, and evaluate the steady amplitude at the relative-mode frequency.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model",
            "pthm-cm09-forced-modal-response",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy",
            "ex-cm09-two-mass-spring-modes",
            "post-cm04-sinusoidal-drive-model",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm09-coupled-quadratic-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm09-forced-modal-response": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm09-free-modes-and-degeneracy": "physical-result",
            "ex-cm09-two-mass-spring-modes": "operational-prerequisite",
            "post-cm04-sinusoidal-drive-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked instance of the forced coupled quadratic model: the two equal-mass chain of the companion example with a prescribed generalized force J(t)=F cos(Omega t) u_+ along the in-phase pattern (a sinusoidal drive for Omega>0 and a constant-force specialization for Omega=0), the modal force components (F/m,0), the unbounded resonance at Omega = sqrt(k/m) with the explicit linearly growing modal motion, the bounded steady response of amplitude |F|/(2c) at Omega = sqrt((k+2c)/m), and the boundedness of every motion for Omega^2 not equal to k/m. The force is prescribed external data; no back-reaction, measured response or empirical value is asserted.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 6, Section 6.2, chapter pages 3-8 (PDF pages 127-132): coupled oscillators and the response of the normal modes to an external periodic force",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 7, Section 7.1.3, printed pages 147-148 (PDF pages 150-151): the driven linear oscillator, resonance and the limits of linear reasoning",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 19
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm09-weak-damping-averaging-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "An explicit weakly damped oscillator: the averaging constants and the energy-decay bound over five thousand seconds (about 796 undamped periods)",
          "statement": "Adopt the damped oscillator model of [[post-cm04-linear-damping-model]] with\ndata\n\n$$m=1\\ \\text{kg},\\qquad k=1\\ \\text{kg/s}^{2},\\qquad \\varepsilon=10^{-4},\\qquad \\gamma=1\\ \\text{kg/s},$$\n\nso that $c=\\varepsilon\\gamma=10^{-4}$ kg/s and $\\omega_{0}=\\sqrt{k/m}=1$\nrad/s; the angles and frequencies are read under the convention of\n[[def-cm01-units]]. Fix amplitude unit $a_*=1\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1/2}$ and time unit $1\\,\\mathrm s$. Take $\\zeta_0=a_*$, $R=a_*/2$, $\\rho=a_*/4$ and $A=(1\\,\\mathrm s)/2$. Below these symbols, $t$ and the constants in coefficient calculations mean their numerical values in these fixed units. In particular $M$ is in $a_*/\\mathrm s$, $L$ in $\\mathrm s^{-1}$, $B,K$ in $a_*$ and $B_1$ is dimensionless. Every energy exponential below is multiplied by $E_0=1\\,\\mathrm J$ when read as a physical quantity, and the horizon $[0,5000]$ means $[0,5000\\,\\mathrm s]$. For $x>0$, write $\\ln x:=\\log x$, where $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse of the real exponential ([[def-natural-logarithm]]). Then:\n\n1. the averaging data of [[pthm-cm09-averaging-for-weak-damping]] specialize\nto $P=2\\pi$, $F(t,\\zeta)=-ie^{it}(\\cos t\\operatorname{Im}\\zeta-\\sin t\\operatorname{Re}\\zeta)$\nand $\\bar F(\\zeta)=-\\zeta/2$, with averaged solution\n$\\bar\\zeta(t)=e^{-\\varepsilon t/2}=e^{-t/(2\\cdot10^{4})}$;\n2. the constants of the averaging estimate are\n$$M=\\frac{3\\sqrt2}{2},\\qquad L=\\sqrt2,\\qquad B=6\\sqrt2\\,\\pi,\\qquad B_{1}=4\\sqrt2\\,\\pi,\\qquad K=2\\pi\\bigl(3\\sqrt2+6\\bigr)e^{\\sqrt2/2},$$\nso that\n$$K\\approx1.30522\\times10^{2},\\qquad \\varepsilon K\\approx1.30522\\times10^{-2}<0.25=\\rho;$$\n3. the trapping hypothesis holds:\n$\\lvert\\zeta_{0}\\rvert(1-e^{-\\gamma A/(2m)})=1-e^{-1/4}\\approx0.22120< R-\\rho=0.25$,\nand also $1/4=\\gamma A/(2m)\\le\\ln(4/3)\\approx0.28768$;\n4. consequently the motion with datum $x(0)=\\sqrt2$ m, $x'(0)=0$ — which\nhas $\\zeta(0)=1$ and mechanical energy $E_{0}=m\\omega_{0}\\lvert\\zeta_{0}\\rvert^{2}=1$ J —\nsatisfies\n$$\\bigl\\lvert E(t)-e^{-t/10^{4}}\\bigr\\rvert\\le m\\omega_{0}\\varepsilon K\\bigl(2\\lvert\\zeta_{0}\\rvert+\\varepsilon K\\bigr)\\approx2.62748\\times10^{-2}\\ \\text{J}\\qquad(0\\le t\\le5000),$$\nthe interval $[0,5000]$ being $[0,A/\\varepsilon]$ and containing\n$5000/(2\\pi)\\approx795.8$ oscillation periods of the undamped frequency;\n5. at the end of the horizon the averaged energy is\n$e^{-1/2}\\approx0.60653$ J, so the estimate bounds the deviation of the\nexact energy from the averaged exponential decay by about $4.3\\%$ of its\nvalue there.",
          "strategy": "Direct: specialize the averaging theorem data, evaluate the exact constants, check the smallness and trapping hypotheses and state the energy-decay bound with its decimal values.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-cm09-averaging-for-weak-damping",
            "def-cm-averaging-data-and-averaged-field",
            "post-cm04-linear-damping-model",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three",
