          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the rigid-material model branch of the framework: a bounded finite Borel measure (the reference mass distribution) of positive total mass with reference coordinates centred at its first moment, a nondegenerate time window, and motions given by rigid placements $x(t,\\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\\rho$ with $C^{2}$ translation and rotation curves into $SO(3)$. The idealization adopted is kinematic rigidity: all material mutual distances are constant in time. The branch excludes deformation, internal degrees of freedom, variable mass, impacts, and every force, torque, equation of motion, balance or conservation law for the configuration path; it asserts no measured mass, length, duration or orientation and no claim that any concrete body is rigid. It is a declared model branch distinct from the finitely many particle labels of the particle branch.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge Part II lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 3.1, printed pages 46-49 (PDF pages 52-55): the rigid body as material points with fixed pairwise distances, the displacement as translation plus rotation, the body-fixed orthonormal basis, and the count of six degrees of freedom",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 4 Section 4.1, chapter pages 1-3 (PDF pages 55-57): rigidity as the absence of mutual-distance changes, the moving orthonormal basis and the six degrees of freedom of a rigid body",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed. (MIT Press, 2014), Section 2.1, Eqs. (2.8)-(2.10), and Section 2.2, Eqs. (2.14)-(2.30): the rigid body as a system whose configuration is a placement of a mass distribution, the orientation path and the centre-of-mass split",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 2 Sections 2.1-2.2, printed pages 29-33 (PDF pages 32-36): $SO(3)$ and the configuration of a nondegenerate oriented body as a translation together with a rotation",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm10-rigid-body-configuration",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Configuration, configuration space and orientation of a rigid-material model in an inertial frame",
          "statement": "Adopt the model data, the time window $I_0$, the inertial frame and the\nnotation of [[post-cm10-rigid-material]]: the reference mass distribution\n$\\nu$ with total mass $m$ and centred reference coordinates, and a motion\n$x(t,\\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\\rho$ with configuration path $(c,R)$ of class $C^2$.\n\n**(a) Configuration and configuration space.** The **configuration** of the\nmodel at time $t$ in the frame is the pair $(c(t),R(t))$; the pair\n$(c,R)$ is a **configuration path**. The **configuration space** of the model\nis the set\n$$\\mathcal C:=\\mathbb R^3\\times SO(3),$$\nread through the identification of $M_3(\\mathbb R)$ with $\\mathbb R^{9}$\n([[def-matrix-space]]) so that $\\mathcal C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{12}$. The\nconfiguration determines the placement $x=c+R\\,\\cdot$ at that time, and the\nreference placement is $(c,R)=(0,I_3)$. Under the identification of\n$M_3(\\mathbb R)$ with $\\mathbb R^{9}$, the group $SO(3)$ is a compact\nthree-dimensional embedded submanifold of $M_3(\\mathbb R)$ and a subgroup of\nthe general linear group ([[lem-cm-so3-is-a-compact-three-dimensional-embedded-submanifold]]\nclauses 1 and 3, [[lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group]]); consequently\n$\\mathcal C=\\mathbb R^3\\times SO(3)$ is a six-dimensional smooth manifold\nwith the product structure. Its dimension $3+3=6$ is read as three\ntranslation coordinates of $c$ and three orientation degrees of freedom of\n$R$; the model of [[post-cm10-rigid-material]] has no other configuration\ndegrees of freedom, since deformation and internal variables are excluded by\nthat postulate.\n\n**(b) Centre of mass, orientation, body frame.** Because\n$\\int\\rho\\,d\\nu=0$, the configuration's translation is the position of the\ncentre of mass of the model,\n$\\int x(t,\\rho)\\,d\\nu(\\rho)=mc(t)$. The rotation $R(t)$ is the **orientation**\nof the model at time $t$: the linear map that carries reference coordinates\ninto the frame's spatial coordinates, so that a material point whose\nreference coordinate is $\\rho$ is found at the spatial position $x(t,\\rho)$.\nThe **body-fixed frame** at time $t$ is the ordered orthonormal basis\n$(R(t)e_1,R(t)e_2,R(t)e_3)$ of the spatial space, obtained by applying the\norientation to the standard basis; the basis is $g$-orthonormal because\n$R(t)\\in O(3)$ preserves the Euclidean inner product\n([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]). The\nmaterial point of reference coordinate $\\rho$ has **spatial position**\n$x(t,\\rho)$ and **reference coordinates** $\\rho$.\n\n**(c) Physical labels and faithfulness.** The **physical label set** is\n$S:=\\operatorname{supp}\\nu$, defined here as the complement of the union of\nall open $\\nu$-null subsets of $\\mathbb R^3$. Thus $\\rho\\in S$ exactly when\nevery open neighbourhood of $\\rho$ has positive mass. This is a closed set\ncarrying all mass: the rational open boxes form a countable basis\n([[thm-rational-points-and-boxes-in-rn]], $n=3$), so its complement is the\nunion of the countably many null basis boxes. Disjointifying this union and\nusing countable additivity ([[def-measure]]) gives $\\nu(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus S)=0$.\nIn particular $S\\neq\\varnothing$ because $m>0$.\n\nIf two configurations satisfy $c+R\\rho=\\bar c+\\bar R\\rho$ for every\n$\\rho\\in S$, integration against the centred measure gives $mc=m\\bar c$,\nhence $c=\\bar c$. Their exact orientation ambiguity is therefore\n$$\\bar R^{-1}R\\in\\operatorname{Stab}_{SO(3)}(S):=\\{H\\in SO(3):H\\rho=\\rho\\text{ for every }\\rho\\in S\\},$$\nthe pointwise stabilizer of [[lem-cm-rigid-labeling-stabilizer]]. Conversely,\n$c=\\bar c$ and this stabilizer condition give equality of the placements on\n$S$ by multiplication. A **nondegenerate physical labeling** is one whose\n$S$ contains two linearly independent vectors; its stabilizer is $\\{I_3\\}$\nby that lemma, so the physical placement determines $(c,R)$ uniquely.\nThe same injectivity conclusion holds for any auxiliary label set containing\nthe origin and two independent vectors, by evaluation at the origin followed\nby the lemma, as in [[post-cm10-rigid-material]] clause (iii).\n\nA **degenerate physical labeling** has no two independent vectors. If\n$S$ contains a nonzero vector $u$, then $S\\subseteq\\mathbb Ru$, and its\nstabilizer is exactly $\\{H\\in SO(3):Hu=u\\}$, the rotations fixing the line\n$\\mathbb Ru$ pointwise; the orientation is determined only up to right\ncomposition with this subgroup. If $S$ contains no nonzero vector, then\n$S=\\{0\\}$ and its stabilizer is all of $SO(3)$. More generally a singleton\nsupport $\\{q\\}$ must have $q=0$, since centring gives $mq=0$.\nThus a centred point mass has no distinguished orientation axis. These are\nall the degenerate cases: after choosing any nonzero $u\\in S$, failure of\nindependence forces every vector in $S$ to be a multiple of $u$.\nConfiguration pairs remain model data in every case, including orientation\ndata invisible in the placed physical labels.\n\n**(d) Description in another inertial frame.** Let a second inertial frame be\nrelated to the first by the transformation $t'=t+b$, $x'=Qx+ut+a$ of\n[[post-cm01-frames]] clause (iv), with $b\\in\\mathbb R$, $a,u\\in\\mathbb R^3$\nand $Q\\in SO(3)$\n([[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]] clause 1); the letters\n$Q$ and $u$ are the rotation and the boost velocity written $R$ and $v$ in\n[[post-cm01-frames]], renamed to avoid clashes with the orientation path\n$R(t)$ and the material velocity field of clause (e). The\n**description of the same motion in the second frame** assigns to the same\nmaterial label $\\rho$ the events of the motion in the new coordinates,\n$$\\tilde x(t',\\rho):=Q\\,x(t'-b,\\rho)+u\\,(t'-b)+a .$$\nSubstituting $x(t,\\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\\rho$ and using distributivity of the matrix\nproduct over sums expresses the description as a motion of the same model in\nthe second frame, with configuration path\n$$\\tilde c(t')=Qc(t'-b)+u\\,(t'-b)+a,\\qquad \\tilde R(t')=Q\\,R(t'-b),$$\nwhere $\\tilde R(t')\\in SO(3)$ for every $t'$ because $SO(3)$ is closed under\nmatrix multiplication ([[lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group]]); the pair\n$(\\tilde c,\\tilde R)$ is again of class $C^2$. This description is a\nconvention forced by the frame change,\nnot a dynamical statement; the transformation laws of the velocity field, the\nangular velocity and the inertia data are proved in the frame-covariance\ntheorem of this page.\n\n**(e) Material velocities.** For a motion with configuration path $(c,R)$, the\n**material velocity** of the label $\\rho$ at time $t$ is the derivative\n$$\\partial_tx(t,\\rho):=c'(t)+R'(t)\\rho,$$\nwhich exists by differentiability of $c$ and of the entries of $R$\n([[def-cm-rigid-placement-and-body-angular-velocity]]). The **velocity field\nof the motion** at time $t$ is the map\n$p\\mapsto v(t,p)$ assigning to a spatial point the velocity of the material\npoint currently placed there, defined on the image of the placement. Neither\nthe configuration nor the placement by itself asserts a dynamics: the pair\n$(c,R)$ is a description of the position and orientation of the body, and the\nmaterial velocity is its kinematic derivative.",
          "strategy": "A definition: it fixes the configuration pair (c,R) of the rigid-material model, the configuration space R^3 x SO(3) with its manifold structure and dimension six, the centre-of-mass and orientation readings, the body-fixed frame, the faithfulness of the parametrization for nondegenerate labelings and the description of the same motion in a second inertial frame.",
