          "physical_scope": "Transformation laws of the rigid-material model branch of [[post-cm10-rigid-material]] between two inertial frames related by $t'=t+b$, $x'=Qx+ut+a$: the configuration path and material velocity field of the same motion in the new frame, invariance of the body angular velocity and rotation of the spatial angular velocity and of the spatial inertia tensor, and the transformation of the total momentum, kinetic-energy splitting and centre-of-mass angular momentum. The statements are conditional kinematic and inertial identities for the model; no dynamics and no measured value is asserted.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 3.1.2, printed pages 49-50 (PDF pages 55-56): the parallel discussion of body and space angular-velocity descriptions and the convention-dependence of the extracted vector",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 4 Sections 4.1-4.2, Eqs. (4.1)-(4.22), chapter pages 1-6 (PDF pages 55-60): moving-basis kinematics and the inertia data of the moving frame",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed. (MIT Press, 2014), Section 2.2: the body-frame description of the same rigid motion and the transformation between body and space quantities",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 10
