            "pthm-cm10-frame-covariance",
            "pthm-cm03-angular-momentum-balance"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm10-rigid-material": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm10-rigid-body-configuration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm10-angular-velocity": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm10-inertia-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-cm10-kinetic-splitting": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm10-frame-covariance": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm03-angular-momentum-balance": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the external-torque branch of the rigid-material model branch of [[post-cm10-rigid-material]]: a declared continuous external-torque datum $\\tau:I_0\\to\\mathbb R^3$ (dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$, newton metres), read as the total external torque about the centre of mass, and the adopted balance $L_c'=\\tau$ of the angular momentum about the centre of mass. The postulate asserts no force law producing $\\tau$, no balance for the centre-of-mass motion, no conservation law, no uniformity of rotation and no measured torque or observation; the countable-choice assumption is carried only for the finiteness of the defining integrals of the model's angular momentum. The branch excludes deformable bodies, impacts and variable mass; friction, contact and drive torques must be declared as part of $\\tau$ with their own models.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge Part II lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 3.3, printed pages 53-55 (PDF pages 59-61): the angular-momentum rate equals the applied torque, and Euler's equations in the body frame",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 4 Section 4.4, Eqs. (4.63)-(4.66), chapter page 15 (PDF page 69): the angular-momentum balance of a rigid body and Euler's equations with torque components",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed. (MIT Press, 2014), Section 2.9, Eqs. (2.80)-(2.83): the evolution of the angular momentum of a rigid body and Euler's equations under an applied torque",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "An adopted model branch: formulation, scope and exclusions are stated; no derivation is claimed.",
          "dependency_level": 12
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm11-euler-equations",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Euler's equations with torque for the rigid-material model, the rotational power identity, the torque-free reduction and frame covariance",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]), used only through the integrals of\n[[pthm-cm10-kinetic-splitting]]; the theorems below neither use nor imply any\nother choice principle. Adopt a model of the rigid-material branch\n[[post-cm10-rigid-material]] with the external-torque branch\n[[post-cm11-torque-balance]]: a reference mass distribution $\\nu$ with total\nmass $m$ and centred reference coordinates, a time window $I_0$\n([[def-interval]]), an inertial frame, a motion $x(t,\\rho)=c(t)+R(t)\\rho$ with\nconfiguration path $(c,R)$ of class $C^{2}$\n([[def-cm10-rigid-body-configuration]]), body angular velocity $\\omega$\n([[def-cm10-angular-velocity]]) and body inertia matrix $I$\n([[def-cm10-inertia-tensor]]), and a continuous external torque datum\n$\\tau:I_0\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ with respect to which the adopted balance of\n[[post-cm11-torque-balance]] clause (iii) holds along the motion. Then:\n\n1. **Euler's equations with torque.** Writing $l:=I\\omega$, one has\n   $l'=R^{\\mathsf T}\\tau-\\omega\\times I\\omega$ and\n   $$I\\omega'(t)+\\omega(t)\\times I\\omega(t)=R(t)^{\\mathsf T}\\tau(t)\\qquad(t\\in I_0).$$\n2. **Principal-axis components.** If $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$ is a positively oriented orthonormal\n   eigenbasis of $I$ with $Ie_{j}=I_{j}e_{j}$ ([[pthm-cm10-principal-axes]]\n   clause (a)) and $\\omega=\\sum_{j}\\omega_{j}e_{j}$,\n   $R^{\\mathsf T}\\tau=\\sum_{j}\\tau_{j}e_{j}$, then the equations are\n   componentwise\n   $$I_{1}\\omega_{1}'=(I_{2}-I_{3})\\omega_{2}\\omega_{3}+\\tau_{1},\\qquad I_{2}\\omega_{2}'=(I_{3}-I_{1})\\omega_{3}\\omega_{1}+\\tau_{2},\\qquad I_{3}\\omega_{3}'=(I_{1}-I_{2})\\omega_{1}\\omega_{2}+\\tau_{3}.$$\n3. **Rotational power identity.** The rotational kinetic energy\n   $K_{\\mathrm{rot}}(t):=\\tfrac12\\omega(t)^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega(t)$ is\n   differentiable on $I_0$ with\n   $$\\frac{d}{dt}K_{\\mathrm{rot}}(t)=\\omega(t)\\cdot R(t)^{\\mathsf T}\\tau(t)\\qquad(t\\in I_0);$$\n   in particular, if $\\tau\\equiv0$, then $K_{\\mathrm{rot}}$ is constant on\n   $I_0$, and $K_{\\mathrm{rot}}$ coincides with the rotational term\n   $\\tfrac12\\omega^{\\mathsf T}I\\omega$ of the model's kinetic energy in\n   clause 3 of [[pthm-cm10-kinetic-splitting]].\n4. **Torque-free reduction and nondegeneracy.** Suppose $\\tau\\equiv0$ and the\n   model is nondegenerate in the sense of [[def-cm10-inertia-tensor]] clause\n   (a), so that $I$ is invertible with $I^{-1}l=\\omega$. Then, writing\n   $\\omega$ as a function of $l$,\n   $$l'=l\\times(I^{-1}l),\\qquad \\omega'=F(\\omega):=-I^{-1}\\bigl(\\omega\\times I\\omega\\bigr);$$\n   the second form exhibits the torque-free body angular velocity as a\n   solution of the autonomous field $F$ on $\\mathbb R^{3}$.\n5. **Frame covariance.** Let a second inertial frame be related to the first\n   by $t'=t+b$, $x'=Qx+ut+a$ with $b\\in\\mathbb R$, $a,u\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$,\n   $Q\\in SO(3)$ ([[post-cm01-frames]] clause (iv)), and let\n   $\\tilde x(t',\\rho):=Qx(t'-b,\\rho)+u(t'-b)+a$, $\\tilde\\tau(t'):=Q\\tau(t'-b)$\n   be the transformed motion and datum of [[post-cm11-torque-balance]] clause\n   (vii). Then the balance holds in the second frame, and\n   $$I\\tilde\\omega'(t')+\\tilde\\omega(t')\\times I\\tilde\\omega(t')=\\tilde R(t')^{\\mathsf T}\\tilde\\tau(t')\\qquad(t'\\in I_0+b),$$\n   where $\\tilde\\omega(t')=\\omega(t'-b)$ and\n   $\\tilde R(t')=QR(t'-b)$; the body-frame torque is unchanged:\n   $\\tilde R(t')^{\\mathsf T}\\tilde\\tau(t')=R(t'-b)^{\\mathsf T}\\tau(t'-b)$.\n\nThe statement is conditional on the adopted model and torque balance; it\nasserts no force law for $\\tau$, no conservation of $L_c$ and no measured\nvalue.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm11-torque-balance",
            "post-cm10-rigid-material",
            "def-cm10-rigid-body-configuration",
            "def-cm10-angular-velocity",
            "def-cm10-inertia-tensor",
            "def-countable-choice",
