          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 19,
          "physical_scope": "The Hamiltonian field datum of a declared potential presentation: for a $C^{2}$ phase function the unique $C^{1}$ field contracted against the presentation's two-form to the differential, together with its coordinate expression in the model's Cartesian and generalized-coordinate charts and its specialization to the branch Hamiltonian, including the residual-force source that is generally not a Hamiltonian field. The item defines model fields and their chart expressions; it asserts no existence of integral curves, no equation of motion and no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Sections 4.1.2 and 4.4, printed pages 83-84 and 100 (PDF pages 89-90 and 106): Hamilton's equations $\\dot q=\\partial H/\\partial p$, $\\dot p=-\\partial H/\\partial q$ read as the flow of the field $J\\nabla H$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 15.1-15.2: Hamilton's equations in phase space and the association of a dynamical vector field with the Hamiltonian",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 10.1, chapter pages 2-3 (PDF pages 238-239): the differential of $H$ compared term by term yielding $\\dot q_j=\\partial H/\\partial p_j$ and $\\dot p_j=-\\partial H/\\partial q_j$",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm13-hamiltonian-field-integrates-the-model-equations",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Integral curves of the presentation's Hamiltonian field are exactly the momentum-phase curves of the model's motions, in every chart; and the forced field is Hamiltonian exactly when the residual force has a velocity-independent potential",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]] with the\\ndata, charts and Hamiltonian field of [[def-cm13-two-form]],\\n[[def-cm13-hamiltonian-field]]: masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$ and open\\nconfiguration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ with mass operator\\n$\\mathsf M$ ([[def-cm01-configuration]]), time window $I_{0}$, $C^{2}$\\npotential $U$ and residual forces $d$ of the standing regularity\\n([[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]],\\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]], [[def-cm01-units]]), the Cartesian\\nHamiltonian $H(t,x,p)=\\tfrac12p\\cdot\\mathsf M^{-1}p+U(t,x)$ and, for a\\ngeneralized-coordinate map $X:\\Omega\\to Q$ with kinetic metric\\n$G=DX^{\\mathsf T}\\mathsf M\\,DX$, the chart Hamiltonian\\n$\\widetilde H(t,q,P)=\\tfrac12P\\cdot G(q)^{-1}P+\\widetilde U(t,q)$\\n([[def-cm12-hamiltonian]], [[pthm-cm12-mechanical-hyperregularity]],\\n[[def-cm07-generalized-coordinates-and-forces]],\\n[[def-cm12-cotangent-momentum]]). Let $J\\subseteq I_{0}$ be a nonempty open\\ninterval. For clause 4, a time-dependent phase function is a map\\n$f:I_{0}\\times W\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^{2}$; write $f_{t}(z):=f(t,z)$\\nand use [[def-cm13-hamiltonian-field]] to define the associated family of\\nHamiltonian fields $t\\mapsto X_{f_t}$. The forced field is called Hamiltonian\\non this full chart when it equals $X_{f_t}$ for every $t\\in I_{0}$. Then:\\n\\n1. **(potential branch, Cartesian chart)** Assume $d\\equiv0$ on the state\\n   domain. For a $C^{2}$ curve $x:J\\to Q$ with $p(t):=\\mathsf Mx'(t)$ the\\n   following are equivalent: (a) $x$ is a motion of the model\\n   ([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]); (b) $\\dot x(t)=H_{p}(t,x(t),p(t))$ and\\n   $\\dot p(t)=-H_{x}(t,x(t),p(t))$ for every $t\\in J$; (c) the curve\\n   $t\\mapsto(x(t),p(t))$ is an integral curve of the time-dependent field\\n   $t\\mapsto X_{H_{t}}$, i.e. satisfies\\n   $\\dot z(t)=X_{H_{t}}(z(t))$ with $z=(x,p)$\\n   ([[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]).\\n2. **(generalized-coordinate chart)** Let $X:\\Omega\\to Q$ be a\\n   generalized-coordinate map and let $q:J\\to\\Omega$ be a $C^{2}$ curve with\\n   $x:=X\\circ q$ and $P(t):=G(q(t))q'(t)$. Then the declared Lagrange\\n   equations with residual generalized force,\\n   $\\Lambda(\\widetilde L)(q)(t)=Q^{\\mathrm{res}}(t,q(t),q'(t))$, are\\n   equivalent to\\n   $q'(t)=\\widetilde H_{P}(t,q(t),P(t))$ and\\n   $P'(t)=-\\widetilde H_{q}(t,q(t),P(t))+Q^{\\mathrm{res}}(t,q(t),q'(t))$;\\n   in particular, when $d\\equiv0$, they are equivalent to\\n   $(q,P)$ being an integral curve of $X_{\\widetilde H_{t}}$.\\n3. **(residual forces, Cartesian chart)** For a $C^{2}$ curve $x:J\\to Q$\\n   with $p=\\mathsf Mx'$, the model's forced equation\\n   $\\mathsf Mx''=-\\nabla_{x}U(t,x)+d(t,x,x')$ holds on $J$ if and only if\\n   $\\dot x=H_{p}$ and $\\dot p=-H_{x}+d(t,x,\\mathsf M^{-1}p)$ on $J$.\\n4. **(when is the forced field Hamiltonian?)** The forced field\\n   $(x,p)\\mapsto\\bigl(\\mathsf M^{-1}p,-\\nabla_{x}U(t,x)+d(t,x,\\mathsf M^{-1}p)\\bigr)$\\n   is Hamiltonian in the preceding time-dependent sense if and only if there\\n   is a $C^{2}$ function $V:I_{0}\\times Q\\to\\mathbb R$ with\\n   $d(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x}V(t,x)$ for every $(t,x,v)$ of the state domain; in\\n   that case the phase function is $H+V$ up to an independently chosen\\n   additive $C^2$ function of $t$ on each connected component of $Q$, and the model's forced motions are the motions of the potential\\n   presentation $(U+V,0)$.\\n\\nThe statement is conditional on the adopted force branch, the declared\\npresentation and the declared chart; it asserts no existence or uniqueness of\\nsolutions, no completeness, no conservation law and no empirical content.",
          "strategy": "direct",
