          "dependency_level": 20,
          "physical_scope": "The identification of the model's Hamiltonian evolution with the integral curves of the presentation's Hamiltonian field: on the potential branch the model's motions, the solutions of Hamilton's equations for the branch Hamiltonian and the integral curves of $X_H$ coincide, and in a declared generalized-coordinate chart the declared Lagrange equations coincide with the corresponding Hamilton system; with residual forces the momentum equation carries the force as a source, and the forced field is Hamiltonian exactly when that source derives from a velocity-independent potential. The statement is a conditional deduction from the adopted force branch, the declared presentation and the cited mathematics; it makes no existence, uniqueness, completeness or empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Sections 4.1.2 and 4.1.3, printed pages 83-85 (PDF pages 89-91): the derivation of Hamilton's equations and the worked particle-in-a-potential case in which they are Newton's equation",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Sections 8.2-8.3 and 15.1: the conversion of Lagrange's equations into Hamiltonian form and the phase-space form of the equations of motion",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Section 10.1, chapter pages 2-3 (PDF pages 238-239): $\\dot q_j=\\partial H/\\partial p_j$ and $\\dot p_j=-\\partial H/\\partial q_j$ with the recovery of the Lagrange equation",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm13-poisson",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Poisson bracket of phase functions of a potential presentation: $\\{f,g\\}=\\omega(X_f,X_g)$ and the coordinate formula in each canonical chart",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with the\nmomentum charts and two-form datum of [[def-cm13-two-form]], the canonical\ncoordinates of [[def-cm13-canonical-form]] and the Hamiltonian fields of\n[[def-cm13-hamiltonian-field]]. Dimensions are read by [[def-cm01-units]];\ndifferentiability classes are those of\n[[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]].\n\n**(a) Phase functions.** Let $W_{0}$ be a nonempty open subset of a phase\ndomain of the presentation. A **phase function of the presentation on\n$W_{0}$ of class $C^{k}$** is a map $f:W_{0}\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^{k}$\n($k\\ge1$); its physical reading is a model quantity computed from the\npositions and momenta of the labels, and its dimension is declared as part of\nthe datum. A **time-dependent phase function** on an interval $J\\subseteq\nI_{0}$ is a map $f:J\\times W_{0}\\to\\mathbb R$ of the declared class, written\n$f_{t}:=f(t,\\cdot)$.\n\n**(b) The bracket.** For $C^1$ phase functions $f,g:W_0\\to\\mathbb R$, define their **Poisson bracket for the presentation** by the pointwise coordinate formula $\\{f,g\\}=\\nabla f^{\\mathsf T}J\\nabla g$ in a canonical chart, in the finite-regularity convention of [[def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus]]. This is a continuous function. The definition is independent of the canonical chart: for a canonical $C^1$ coordinate change with derivative $M$, the matrix identity implies $MJM^{\\mathsf T}=J$, and the equivalence of the two matrix identities is proved in [[prop-cm-canonical-map-tests-and-hamiltonian-transport]], and the chain rule transforms the two gradients so their pairing is unchanged.\n\nWhen both functions are $C^2$, their Hamiltonian fields of [[def-cm13-hamiltonian-field]] are $C^1$, and the bracket has the intrinsic formula\n$$\\{f,g\\}=\\omega(X_f,X_g)=df(X_g)=X_g(f)=-X_f(g).$$\nIn this case it is $C^1$, with the same sign convention as the smooth root bracket and [[prop-cm-hamiltonian-field-and-poisson-bracket-in-a-phase-chart]]. The extension to $C^1$ inputs makes evolution identities for $C^1$ observables and nested brackets of $C^2$ functions meaningful; it does not assert closure of the $C^2$ class under the bracket. Its dimension is $[f][g](\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-1})^{-1}$, the product of the entries' dimensions divided by the action dimension of the two-form.\n\n**(c) Coordinate formula.** In the Cartesian canonical coordinates $(x,p)$\nof the presentation, and more generally in any canonical chart\n$(\\theta^{1},\\dots,\\theta^{2m})$ of [[def-cm13-canonical-form]],\n\n$$\\{f,g\\}=\\sum_{a=1}^{3N}\\Bigl(f_{x^{a}}g_{p_{a}}-f_{p_{a}}g_{x^{a}}\\Bigr)=\\nabla f^{\\mathsf T}J\\,\\nabla g,\\qquad \\{f,g\\}=\\sum_{a=1}^{m}\\Bigl(f_{\\theta^{a}}g_{\\theta^{m+a}}-f_{\\theta^{m+a}}g_{\\theta^{a}}\\Bigr),$$\n\nwith derivatives those of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]] and\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. The coordinate formula is\nrecorded here and its identities (bilinearity, skew-symmetry, the Leibniz\nrule, the Jacobi identity and the fundamental brackets) are proved in the\nbracket-identities theorem of this page.\n\n**(d) Status.** The item defines a bilinear operation on the declared phase\ndomain by the pointwise coordinate formula, and for $C^2$ entries by $\\{f,g\\}=\\omega(X_f,X_g)$, hence\nindependently of the canonical chart used to evaluate the coordinate\nformula. It asserts no equation of motion, no conservation law, no algebraic\nidentity beyond well-definedness and no measured value; in particular the\nbracket with the Hamiltonian and the notion of first integral are the subject\nof the evolution theorem of this page.",
          "strategy": null,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm13-two-form",
            "pthm-cm13-two-form-is-symplectic",
            "def-cm13-canonical-form",
            "def-cm13-hamiltonian-field",
