          "dependency_level": 20,
          "physical_scope": "The pointwise bracket is defined for C1 inputs, with the C2 intrinsic-field expression and C1 output regularity retained. The Poisson bracket datum of a declared potential presentation: phase functions read off the labels' positions and momenta, the bracket $\\{f,g\\}=\\omega(X_f,X_g)$ defined by the presentation's two-form, and its coordinate formula in the model's Cartesian and generalized-coordinate canonical charts. The item defines a model bilinear operation and fixes its dimension; it asserts no algebraic identity, no equation of motion, no conservation law and no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 4.3, printed pages 93-95 (PDF pages 99-101): the Poisson bracket definition, the properties displayed for it, the fundamental brackets $\\{q_i,q_j\\}=0$, $\\{p_i,p_j\\}=0$, $\\{q_i,p_j\\}=\\delta_{ij}$, and the evolution formula $df/dt=\\{f,H\\}+\\partial f/\\partial t$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 15.2: the Poisson bracket representation of Hamiltonian mechanics",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Sections 5.2.2 and 5.3: the Poisson bracket in the $\\tilde J$ representation and the fundamental Poisson brackets of the phase-space coordinates",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm13-bracket-identities",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The bracket of a potential presentation is bilinear, skew, a derivation in each slot, satisfies the Jacobi identity, and reproduces the fundamental brackets and the Hamiltonian field",
          "statement": "Adopt the force model with potential presentation $(U,d)$ and mechanical\nLagrangian $L=K-U$ of [[def-cm07-action-and-mechanical-lagrangian]], with the\nmomentum charts and two-form datum of [[def-cm13-two-form]], the canonical\ncoordinates of [[def-cm13-canonical-form]], the Hamiltonian fields of\n[[def-cm13-hamiltonian-field]] and the bracket of [[def-cm13-poisson]] on a\nnonempty open subset $W_{0}$ of a phase domain of the presentation. Then:\n\n1. **(bilinearity and skew-symmetry)** The bracket is real-bilinear:\n   $\\{\\alpha f+\\beta g,h\\}=\\alpha\\{f,h\\}+\\beta\\{g,h\\}$ and\n   $\\{f,\\alpha g+\\beta h\\}=\\alpha\\{f,g\\}+\\beta\\{f,h\\}$ for real $\\alpha,\\beta$\n   and $C^{2}$ phase functions; it is skew-symmetric,\n   $\\{f,g\\}=-\\{g,f\\}$, and in particular $\\{f,f\\}=0$.\n2. **(Leibniz rule)** For $C^{2}$ functions $f,g,h$,\n\n$$\\{f,gh\\}=\\{f,g\\}h+g\\{f,h\\},\\qquad \\{fg,h\\}=f\\{g,h\\}+g\\{f,h\\}.$$\n\n3. **(Jacobi identity)** For $C^{2}$ functions $f,g,h$,\n\n$$\\{f,\\{g,h\\}\\}+\\{g,\\{h,f\\}\\}+\\{h,\\{f,g\\}\\}=0.$$\n\n4. **(relation to the fields)** For $C^{2}$ functions $f,g$ one has\n   $\\{f,g\\}=X_{g}(f)=-X_{f}(g)=d\\!f(X_{g})=-d\\!g(X_{f})$, and the bracket is\n   $C^{1}$; in the Cartesian canonical coordinates\n   $\\{f,g\\}=\\sum_{a}(f_{x^{a}}g_{p_{a}}-f_{p_{a}}g_{x^{a}})=\\nabla f^{\\mathsf T}J\\,\\nabla g$,\n   and in any canonical chart $(\\theta^{1},\\dots,\\theta^{2m})$ the analogous\n   formula of [[def-cm13-poisson]] clause (c) holds.\n5. **(fundamental brackets)** In the Cartesian canonical coordinates the\n   coordinate functions satisfy $\\{x^{a},x^{b}\\}=0$, $\\{p_{a},p_{b}\\}=0$ and\n   $\\{x^{a},p_{b}\\}=\\delta^{a}_{b}$; more generally, in any canonical chart\n   $(\\theta^{1},\\dots,\\theta^{2m})$ one has\n   $\\{\\theta^{i},\\theta^{j}\\}=J^{ij}$, the entries of the standard symplectic\n   matrix.\n\nFor nested brackets use the pointwise $C^1$-slot coordinate extension of [[def-cm-finite-regularity-canonical-calculus]], which agrees with the presentation bracket on $C^2$ inputs. Thus the Jacobi identity above is meaningful for $C^2$ functions despite their brackets being only $C^1$. The $C^2$ class is not in general closed under the bracket and is not asserted to be a Poisson algebra. The smooth $C^\\infty$ phase functions, equipped with this bracket and pointwise product, form a Poisson algebra.\nThe statement is conditional on the adopted force branch, the declared\npresentation and the cited mathematics; it asserts no equation of motion, no\nconservation law and no measured value.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm13-poisson",
            "def-cm13-hamiltonian-field",
            "def-cm13-two-form",
            "def-cm13-canonical-form",
