          "dependency_level": 21,
          "physical_scope": "The algebraic identities of the Poisson bracket of a declared potential presentation: bilinearity, skew-symmetry, the Leibniz rule in each slot, the Jacobi identity for $C^{2}$ phase functions through the pointwise C1-slot extension, the smooth Poisson algebra, the identity $\\{f,g\\}=X_g(f)$ relating the bracket to the presentation's Hamiltonian fields, and the fundamental brackets of the model's canonical coordinates. The statement is a conditional deduction from the adopted force branch, the declared presentation and the cited mathematics; it asserts no equation of motion, no conservation law and no measured value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 4.3, printed pages 93-95 (PDF pages 99-101): the properties of the Poisson bracket — antisymmetry, linearity, the Leibniz rule, the Jacobi identity — and the fundamental brackets $\\{q_i,q_j\\}=0$, $\\{p_i,p_j\\}=0$, $\\{q_i,p_j\\}=\\delta_{ij}$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
