            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 22,
          "physical_scope": "Transformation theory for equal-dimensional local canonical transformations of the declared potential presentation: a $C^{2}$ local diffeomorphism preserving the two-form conjugates the Hamiltonian field of a phase function to the field of the composed function, preserves the model's Poisson bracket under composition, and carries solutions of one Hamiltonian system to solutions of the other, also for time-dependent Hamiltonians. No such ambient field-transport result is asserted for a lower-dimensional symplectic immersion. The statement is a conditional deduction from the adopted force branch, the declared presentation and the cited mathematics; it asserts no empirical content or global existence.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 4.4, printed pages 100-102 (PDF pages 106-108): the theorem that the Poisson bracket is invariant under canonical transformations, and the equivalence of the canonical condition with the preservation of the bracket structure",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Sections 5.2-5.3: canonical transformations as maps between Hamiltonian systems preserving the dynamics, and the invariance of the Poisson bracket",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Cline, Variational Principles in Classical Mechanics (2nd edition, University of Rochester River Campus Libraries, open textbook, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), Section 15.3: canonical transformations and the transformation of the Hamiltonian under a generating-function change of variables",
                "url": "https://downloads.libretexts.org/api/v1/download/phys-9557/pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-cm13-poincare-cartan-annihilation-characterization",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A curve solves Hamilton's equations if and only if its velocity annihilates $d(p\\cdot dq-H\\,dt)$, with the three coordinate components computed",
          "statement": "Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, let\n$W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2n}$ be open and nonempty with coordinates\n$z=(q,p)=(q^{1},\\dots,q^{n},p_{1},\\dots,p_{n})$, and let\n$H:J\\times W\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^{1}$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]],\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). On $J\\times W$ let\n$\\alpha:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_i\\,dq^i-H\\,dt$, a coordinate one-form with $C^1$\ncoefficients. Define its coordinate exterior derivative by\n$d\\alpha:=\\sum_{a<b}(\\partial_a\\alpha_b-\\partial_b\\alpha_a)\\,dy^a\\wedge dy^b$\nin the ordered coordinates $y=(t,q^1,\\dots,q^n,p_1,\\dots,p_n)$; this is a\ncontinuous coordinate two-form, using the same alternating multilinear\nconvention as [[def-smooth-differential-k-form]] but making no smooth-form\nclaim. In expanded form, set\n\n$$\\sigma:=d\\alpha=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}dp_i\\wedge dq^i+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}H_{q^i}\\,dt\\wedge dq^i+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}H_{p_i}\\,dt\\wedge dp_i.$$\n\nEvery coefficient of $\\sigma$ is continuous because $H$ is $C^1$. Let\n$J'\\subseteq J$ be a nonempty open interval and let $z:J'\\to W$ be a $C^{1}$\ncurve. Then the following are equivalent:\n\n1. $z$ satisfies Hamilton's equations on $J'$:\n   $\\dot q^{i}(t)=H_{p_{i}}\\bigl(t,z(t)\\bigr)$ and\n   $\\dot p_{i}(t)=-H_{q^{i}}\\bigl(t,z(t)\\bigr)$ for $i=1,\\dots,n$ and every\n   $t\\in J'$;\n2. for every $t\\in J'$ the covector\n   $\\iota_{(1,\\dot z(t))}\\sigma$ vanishes, that is\n   $\\sigma_{(t,z(t))}\\bigl((1,\\dot z(t)),w\\bigr)=0$ for every\n   $w\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{2n}$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-smooth-differential-k-form",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-interval",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution"
          ],
