            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 24,
          "physical_scope": "A worked projection and generating-function computation for the declared potential presentation: a genuine chart $X$ of the configuration domain induces the momentum chart transition from generalized momentum $\\pi$ to Cartesian momentum $P$, $(q,\\pi)\\mapsto(X(q),DX(q)^{-\\mathsf T}\\pi)$, generated in the second kind by the action-dimensioned function $S(q,P)=P\\cdot X(q)$; the transition has unit determinant and fails the first-kind projection hypothesis identically. The degenerate case is compared with the momentum shift, whose second-kind generating function is global. All quantities are model values of the declared idealization; no measurement is reported.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Sections 4.4 and 4.4.3, printed pages 100-105 (PDF pages 106-111): the extension of a configuration-coordinate change to a canonical phase-space map, $P_i=(\\Theta^{-1})^{j}{}_{i}\\,p_j$, and the generating functions of the various kinds",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Sections 5.1 and 5.4.1: point transformations as canonical transformations extending configuration-coordinate changes, and the projection conditions for generating functions",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm13-determinant-tests-for-linear-canonical-maps",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Determinant tests on explicit linear maps: $\\det A=1$ is equivalent to canonicity in dimension two, and strictly weaker in dimension four (the volume-preserving shear)",
          "statement": "Let $J_{2}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and, in the ordered\\ncoordinates $(q^{1},p_{1},q^{2},p_{2})$ of $\\mathbb R^{4}$, let\\n$J_{4}=\\operatorname{diag}(J_{2},J_{2})$ be the standard symplectic matrix of\\nthe phase domain $\\mathbb R^{4}$\\n([[def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form]]); recall that a\\nlinear map $A$ is canonical exactly when $A^{\\mathsf T}JA=J$\\n([[prop-cm-canonical-map-tests-and-hamiltonian-transport]]). Then:\\n\\n1. **(dimension two)** For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}$ one has\\n\\n$$A^{\\mathsf T}J_{2}A=(ad-bc)\\,J_{2}=(\\det A)\\,J_{2},$$\\n\\n   so an invertible $A$ is canonical if and only if $\\det A=1$: in one degree\\n   of freedom canonicity and oriented area preservation coincide. For instance\\n   $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$ has $\\det A=1$ and is canonical.\\n2. **(scaling)** For $\\lambda\\neq0$ the block-diagonal map\\n   $C(q,p)=(\\lambda q,\\lambda^{-1}p)$ on the phase domain\\n   $\\mathbb R^{2n}$ is canonical: its derivative\\n   $\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda I_{n},\\lambda^{-1}I_{n})$ satisfies\\n   $DC^{\\mathsf T}J\\,DC=J$ and $\\det DC=1$; it even preserves the canonical\\n   one-form, $C^{*}\\lambda=\\lambda$, because\\n   $(\\lambda^{-1}p)\\cdot d(\\lambda q)=p\\cdot dq$.\\n3. **(volume preservation is weaker in dimension four)** The linear shear\\n\\n$$C(q^{1},q^{2},p_{1},p_{2})=(q^{1},\\,q^{2}+q^{1},\\,p_{1},\\,p_{2})$$\\n\\n   is written in grouped coordinates $(q^1,q^2,p_1,p_2)$, whose\\n   symplectic matrix is $J_{\\mathrm{grp}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I_2\\\\-I_2&0\\end{pmatrix}$.\\n   It has $\\det DC=1$, so it preserves volume, but\\n   $DC^{\\mathsf T}J_{\\mathrm{grp}}\\,DC=J_{\\mathrm{grp}}+e_{1}e_{4}^{\\mathsf T}-e_{4}e_{1}^{\\mathsf T}\\neq J_{\\mathrm{grp}}$:\\n   the $(1,4)$-entry is $1$ instead of $0$. Equivalently the fundamental\\n   bracket $\\{Q^{2},P_{1}\\}=1$ does not vanish, while canonicity would force\\n   $\\{Q^{2},P_{1}\\}=\\delta^{2}_{1}=0$. Hence $|\\det DC|=1$ does **not**\\n   imply canonicity in dimension $4$.\\n4. **(positive control)** If $A=\\operatorname{diag}(A_{1},A_{2})$ in the\\n   ordered coordinates $(q^{1},p_{1},q^{2},p_{2})$ with each $2\\times2$ block\\n   satisfying $A_{i}^{\\mathsf T}J_{2}A_{i}=J_{2}$, then\\n   $A^{\\mathsf T}J_{4}A=J_{4}$: canonicity is preserved blockwise.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-phase-domain-and-standard-symplectic-two-form",
            "lem-cm-standard-symplectic-form-is-closed-and-nondegenerate",
            "prop-cm-canonical-map-tests-and-hamiltonian-transport",
