          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 27,
          "physical_scope": "A worked instance of the Hamilton-Jacobi and action-angle items inside the declared scalar harmonic model, identified with the generalized-coordinate presentation of the explicit straight-line immersion into a three-dimensional force model: for one label of mass $m>0$ and spring constant $k>0$, the explicit family $S$ is a complete integral of the model's Hamilton-Jacobi equation wherever $\\sin(\\omega t)\\neq0$; its characteristic curves are the harmonic motions with $\\beta=S_\\alpha$ constant; and the explicit chart $(\\theta,I)$ is canonical with $H=I/T$, $\\dot I=0$, $\\dot\\theta=1/T$ and $I=\\oint p\\,dx$, in the period-one convention, with the radian rescaling recovering the sources' chart. The example asserts no measured value, no claim that a real oscillator realizes the model, and no global extension of $S$ across the conjugate times $\\omega t\\in\\pi\\mathbb Z$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Sections 4.5.1 and 4.7, printed pages 105-107 and 121-126 (PDF pages 111-113 and 127-132): the oscillator chart $q=\\sqrt{2I/(m\\omega)}\\sin\\theta$, $p=\\sqrt{2Im\\omega}\\cos\\theta$ with $H=\\omega I$, and the Hamilton-Jacobi construction with $p=W_q$",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. J. Sussman and J. Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, second edition (MIT Press, 2014/2015), Chapter 6, Sections 6.1 and 6.1.1: the Hamilton-Jacobi strategy of Chapter 6 and the oscillator branch; the source states no caustic condition",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Sections 5.1 and 10.2, chapter pages 1-2 and 5-7 (PDF pages 87-88 and 241-243): the harmonic solution with period $2\\pi/\\omega$ and the action variable $J=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\oint p\\,dq$",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm14-kepler-radial-action-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The gravitational two-body model on an elliptic orbit: two simple turning points of the effective potential and $J_{\\mathrm a}'(E)=T/(2\\pi)=\\sqrt{\\mu/\\kappa}\\,a^{3/2}$",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the conic, ellipse and period suppliers.\n\nAdopt the two-label gravitational model of [[post-cm05-gravity]] clause (i)\ninside the central-force model of [[post-cm05-central-model]]: inertial\nmasses $m_{1},m_{2}>0$, $M=m_{1}+m_{2}$, reduced mass\n$\\mu=m_{1}m_{2}/M$, gravitational couplings $m_{1}^{g},m_{2}^{g}>0$,\n$\\kappa=G_{\\mathrm N}m_{1}^{g}m_{2}^{g}>0$ and the pair potential of\n[[pthm-cm05-relative-reduction]]. Let $x:I\\to Q$ be a motion whose\nrelative coordinate $r$ has constant angular momentum $j=Je_{3}$ with\n$J>0$ and constant energy $E<0$. Let $\\bar r:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^{3}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ be the unique global central-force extension of $r$ supplied by [[thm-cm-kepler-orbit-period]] clause 1 and [[pthm-cm05-kepler-period]] clause 1. In all subsequent period and quadrature statements, write $r:=\\bar r$, $\\rho:=|\\bar r|$ and let $\\theta:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be its increasing polar-angle lift. Its restriction to the initial interval is the original relative motion; no global physical time window is assumed. Let $0\\le e<1$, $\\theta_{0}$,\n\n$$p=\\frac{J^{2}}{\\mu\\kappa},\\qquad a=\\frac{p}{1-e^{2}},\\qquad b=a\\sqrt{1-e^{2}},\\qquad T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\kappa}}\\,a^{3/2}$$\nbe its conic data ([[thm-cm-kepler-conic-and-eccentricity]],\n[[pthm-cm05-kepler-period]]). Write\n\n$$I_{0}:=\\left(-\\frac{\\mu\\kappa^{2}}{2J^{2}},\\,0\\right),\\qquad r_{*}:=\\frac{J^{2}}{\\mu\\kappa}=p,\\qquad U^{\\mathrm{eff}}(\\rho):=-\\frac{\\kappa}{\\rho}+\\frac{J^{2}}{2\\mu\\rho^{2}}\\quad(\\rho>0)$$\nfor the effective potential of [[pthm-cm05-effective-potential]] (its\neffective potential $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}_{J}$). The example verifies:\n\n1. **(two simple turning points)** $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}$ is of class\n   $C^{\\infty}$ on $(0,\\infty)$, has the unique critical point $r_{*}$\n   with $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}(r_{*})=-\\mu\\kappa^{2}/(2J^{2})$, is strictly\n   decreasing on $(0,r_{*})$ and strictly increasing on $(r_{*},\\infty)$;\n   hence for every $E\\in I_{0}$ there are exactly two solutions\n   $0<r_{-}(E)<r_{*}<r_{+}(E)$ of $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}(\\rho)=E$, with\n   $(U^{\\mathrm{eff}})'(r_{-}(E))<0<(U^{\\mathrm{eff}})'(r_{+}(E))$ and\n   $U^{\\mathrm{eff}}<E$ between them, and the datum\n   $(J_{0},U^{\\mathrm{eff}},\\mu)$ with $J_{0}=(0,\\infty)$ satisfies the\n   hypotheses of [[thm-cm-action-variable-derivative]] on the energy\n   window $I_{0}$.\n2. **(action derivative)** consequently the action variable\n   $J_{\\mathrm a}$ of [[def-cm-one-dimensional-action-variable]] is of\n   class $C^{1}$ on $I_{0}$ with\n$$J_{\\mathrm a}'(E)=\\frac{T_{\\mathrm{rad}}(E)}{2\\pi},\\qquad T_{\\mathrm{rad}}(E)=\\sqrt{2\\mu}\\int_{r_{-}(E)}^{r_{+}(E)}\\frac{\\mathrm d\\rho}{\\sqrt{E-U^{\\mathrm{eff}}(\\rho)}}\\in(0,\\infty).$$\n\n3. **(quadrature equals the orbital period)** for a noncircular orbit $0<e<1$ (equivalently $E\\in I_0$), the radial coordinate of\n   this global extension solves the one-dimensional problem of clause 1 of\n   [[pthm-cm05-effective-potential]] with the same energy, and its least\n   positive period is the orbital period $T$ of the conic data; the time\n   quadrature of that problem is $T_{\\mathrm{rad}}(E)=T$, so that\n$$J_{\\mathrm a}'(E)=\\frac{T}{2\\pi}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\kappa}}\\,a^{3/2},\\qquad T^{2}=\\frac{4\\pi^{2}\\mu a^{3}}{\\kappa}.$$\n\n4. **(sharpness of the hypotheses)** for $e=0$ the two turning points\n   coalesce at the critical point $r_{*}=a$ and the simple-turning-point\n   hypothesis of the action-variable theorem fails; the value $J=0$ is\n   outside this example (no two-sided bound radial motion), so both\n   hypotheses of the construction are genuinely used.\n\nThe couplings $\\kappa$ and the masses are declared model parameters; no\nmeasured value of $G_{\\mathrm N}$ and no empirical premise enters.",
          "strategy": "direct",
