          "dependency_level": 27,
          "physical_scope": "Assuming countable choice for the orbit suppliers, A worked instance inside the two-label gravitational model of the framework's force branch: on the unique global central-force extension of an elliptic relative orbit ($j=Je_3$, $J>0$, $E<0$, eccentricity $0\\le e<1$), the Kepler effective potential has exactly two simple turning points on the energy window between its minimum and $0$; the one-dimensional action variable of the radial problem is $C^1$ there with derivative $T/(2\\pi)$; and for a noncircular orbit the radial quadrature is the orbital period, so $J_{\\mathrm a}'(E)=T/(2\\pi)=\\sqrt{\\mu/\\kappa}\\,a^{3/2}$ with $T^2=4\\pi^2\\mu a^3/\\kappa$. The example uses only the model's declared couplings $\\kappa=G_{\\mathrm N}m_1^{g}m_2^{g}$ and masses; no measured value of $G_{\\mathrm N}$ and no empirical premise is used, and no real orbit is claimed to obey the model.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Sections 4.5.3 and 4.5.4, printed pages 108-113 (PDF pages 114-119): the one-dimensional frequency identity and the Kepler action variables of the bound orbits",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part CM: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Sections 3.4 and 10.2, chapter pages 7-15 and 5-8 (PDF pages 39-47 and 241-244): the effective potential of the central motion with its minimum, and the action variable of a periodic motion",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Chapter 4, Sections 4.4-4.6, printed pages 74-91 (PDF pages 77-94): the Hamilton-Jacobi equation, the Kepler problem and action-angle variables in the course's local treatment",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
