          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Assuming countable choice for cross-section measures, the separate long-range Coulomb scattering theorem, as a model prediction: the repulsive $\\kappa/r$ potential has a unique simple outer turning point and every relative motion of the stated data extends to all of time without collision, with asymptotic velocities of speed $\\sqrt{2E/\\mu}$ and asymptotic impact distance $b=J/\\sqrt{2\\mu E}$; the deflection map is the $C^1$ decreasing bijection $2\\arctan(\\kappa/(2Eb))$ with a single branch, the differential cross-section is the Rutherford density $(\\kappa/4E)^2\\csc^4(\\theta/2)$, every angular band has the finite truncated cross-section $\\pi(b(\\theta_1)^2-b(\\theta_2)^2)$, and the ideal total cross-section diverges at forward angles. The parameter $\\kappa$ is a model datum of the mechanical model and is not identified with any electromagnetic quantity; the result reports no measurement, no screening law and no capture, radiation, relativistic or quantum modification.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), §6.4, Eqs. (358)-(367): the inverse-square scattering integral, $\\tan(\\theta/2)=k/(2Eb)$, the Rutherford cross-section and the small-angle divergence of the total cross-section",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node51.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), §3.5, Eqs. (3.68)-(3.74): $\\theta=\\pi-2\\varphi_0$ with the Coulomb half-angle, the Rutherford formula and the forward-angle divergence, with the caveats on inelastic and quantum scattering",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 18
        },
        {
          "id": "post-cm15-impulse",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted impact and open-mass extensions of the force branch: the isolated reciprocal impulse model, the collinear restitution contact law and the open-body material accounting",
          "statement": "Adopt, as this page's **impact and open-mass extensions** of the force\nbranch of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] and [[post-cm02-interactions]],\nthe following three model extensions. Each is an adopted model, not a\nconsequence of the smooth-force postulates; each applies only where a\nstatement explicitly invokes it.\n\n**(i) The isolated reciprocal impulse model.** A two-label force model with\nmasses $m_1,m_2>0$ ([[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)), time window $I_0$,\nconfiguration domain $Q$ ([[def-cm01-configuration]]) and continuous\nexternal-plus-pair force maps $F_1,F_2$ is **run in the isolated reciprocal\nimpulse model at $t_*\\in I_0$** when there is an interval\n$I\\subseteq I_0$ with $t_*\\in\\operatorname{int}I$ and a position curve\n$x=(x_1,x_2):I\\to Q$ such that the following conditions hold. This curve is\ncalled an **impulse motion**; only its restrictions away from $t_*$ are\nmotions of the associated first-order system of [[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]],\nand no differentiability at $t_*$ is required:\n\n1. $x_1,x_2$ are continuous on $I$ and twice continuously differentiable on\n   each of $I\\cap(-\\infty,t_*)$ and $I\\cap(t_*,\\infty)$\n   ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]],\n   [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and\n   $m_ix_i''(t)=F_i(t,x(t),x'(t))$ for every $t\\in I\\setminus\\{t_*\\}$;\n2. the one-sided limits\n   $v_i^{\\pm}:=\\lim_{t\\to t_*\\pm}x_i'(t)$ exist in $\\mathbb R^3$\n   ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]]);\n3. there is a vector $J\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the **impulse**, with\n   $$m_1\\bigl(v_1^+-v_1^-\\bigr)=J,\\qquad m_2\\bigl(v_2^+-v_2^-\\bigr)=-J .$$\n\nThe two equations of clause 3 are the **reciprocal finite-jump form** of the\nisolated impulse: the two labels receive equal and opposite momentum changes.\nThe regular force maps remain continuous on their state domain; their\nevaluations along the curve can have different one-sided limits because the\nvelocity can jump. The dimension of the impulse\nis $[J]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ ([[def-cm01-units]]).\n\nFor the distributional rendering on $\\operatorname{int}I$, assume\n$\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Put\n$p_i(t)=m_ix_i'(t)$ and $f_i(t)=F_i(t,x(t),x'(t))$ off $t_*$, assigning both\nthe value $0$ at $t_*$. Their one-sided limits are finite: those of $p_i$\nare $m_iv_i^\\pm$, and continuity of the state-domain force maps gives\n$f_i(t_*^\\pm)=F_i(t_*,x(t_*),v^\\pm)$, with\n$v^\\pm=(v_1^\\pm,v_2^\\pm)$. Also $p_i'=f_i$ off the impulse. Thus each\ncomponent meets the piecewise-$C^1$ and one-sided derivative-limit\nhypotheses of [[lem-cm-piecewise-c1-jump-derivative]]. Its identity,\nsummed componentwise, gives for every real vector test function\n$\\Phi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\operatorname{int}I;\\mathbb R^3)$ (each component is\nsmooth and compactly supported, [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]])\n$$-\\int_{\\operatorname{int}I}\\langle p_i,\\Phi'\\rangle\\,d\\lambda=\\int_{\\operatorname{int}I}\\langle f_i,\\Phi\\rangle\\,d\\lambda+s_i\\langle J,\\Phi(t_*)\\rangle,\\qquad s_1=1,\\quad s_2=-1,$$\nwhere $\\lambda$ is Lebesgue measure and the integral convention is that of\n[[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]. This is the pairing form of the\nadopted jumps. Unlike [[thm-cm-impulse-restitution-and-energy-loss]], whose\nhypotheses require continuous time forces and assume the pairings to derive\nthe jumps, this rendering allows distinct one-sided regular-force values.\n\n**(ii) The collinear restitution contact law.** A model run in the isolated\nreciprocal impulse model at $t_*$ is **collinear** when there is a unit vector\n$n\\in\\mathbb R^3$ (the **contact direction**) with $v_i^{\\pm}\\parallel n$ for\n$i=1,2$ and $\\langle v_2^--v_1^-,n\\rangle<0$ (the labels approach along $n$).\nFor a collinear contact, adopt a **coefficient of restitution** $e\\in[0,1]$ —\na dimensionless model datum of the pair and the contact — and the **restitution\nlaw**\n$$v_2^+-v_1^+=-e\\,\\bigl(v_2^--v_1^-\\bigr).$$\nFor $e=1$ the contact is called **elastic**, for $e=0$ **perfectly inelastic**\nand otherwise **semi-elastic**; the law is a constitutive assumption, not a\nconsequence of the impulse equations of clause (i), and $e$ is not derived\nfrom the masses or the approach speed. The law is stated only for a single\ncollinear contact; simultaneous contacts, repeated contacts and tangential\n(friction) impulses are outside the model.\n\n**(iii) The open-body material accounting.** Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a\nnonempty open interval. Adopt for a body whose mass\n$m:I\\to(0,\\infty)$ and velocity $v:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ are differentiable, and\nwhose external force and exchanged matter are described by maps\n$F:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$, $r_{\\mathrm{in}},r_{\\mathrm{out}}:I\\to[0,\\infty)$ and\n$w_{\\mathrm{in}},w_{\\mathrm{out}}:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ (mass rates and *inertial*\nvelocities of the matter entering and leaving, measured in the same inertial\nframe as $v$), the **material balance identities**\n$$m'=r_{\\mathrm{in}}-r_{\\mathrm{out}},\\qquad (mv)'=F+r_{\\mathrm{in}}\\,w_{\\mathrm{in}}-r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\,w_{\\mathrm{out}} .$$\nThey are the mathematical rendering of \"gains $r_{\\mathrm{in}}\\,\\mathrm dt$ of\nmatter with velocity $w_{\\mathrm{in}}$, loses $r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\,\\mathrm dt$\nwith velocity $w_{\\mathrm{out}}$, and feels the external force $F$\", and they\nare adopted rather than derived from a closed-system law. Their product-rule\nconsequence $mv'=F+r_{\\mathrm{in}}(w_{\\mathrm{in}}-v)-r_{\\mathrm{out}}(w_{\\mathrm{out}}-v)$\nis proved in [[thm-cm-variable-mass-momentum-balance]]; in particular the\nclosed-system law $(mv)'=F$ is recovered only when\n$r_{\\mathrm{in}}w_{\\mathrm{in}}=r_{\\mathrm{out}}w_{\\mathrm{out}}$.\nThe quantities have types and SI dimensions\n$[m]=\\mu$, $[v]=[w_{\\mathrm{in}}]=[w_{\\mathrm{out}}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$,\n$[F]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ and\n$[r_{\\mathrm{in}}]=[r_{\\mathrm{out}}]=\\mu\\tau^{-1}$; they are model\ndata, not measured values.",
          "strategy": "An adopted model extension: the isolated reciprocal impulse with its finite momentum jump, the collinear restitution contact law with coefficient in [0,1], and the open-body material accounting with entering and leaving mass rates and inertial velocities, together with their status and exclusions.",
