          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Assuming countable choice for the scattering measures and vocabulary, A numerical check of the repulsive-Coulomb model prediction on declared model data in SI units: reduced mass $2\\times10^{-26}\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, energy $10^{-18}\\,\\mathrm{J}$, angular momentum $4\\times10^{-34}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$ and coupling $\\kappa=4\\times10^{-30}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m}$ give $v_\\infty=10^4\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $b=2\\times10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{m}$, $\\alpha=1$, scattering angle $\\pi/2$ and density $4\\times10^{-24}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}$; the angular band values $\\pi(b(\\theta_1)^2-b(\\theta_2)^2)$ and the small-angle growth $\\approx(16\\pi\\times10^{-24}\\,\\mathrm{m^2})/\\theta_1^2$ are checked. All numbers are declared model data fed into the model formulas; the example asserts no measurement, no uncertainty budget and no comparison with any experiment.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), §6.4, Eqs. (358)-(367): the inverse-square scattering integral, the Rutherford angle relation and the divergence of the total cross-section",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node51.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), §3.5, Eqs. (3.69)-(3.74): the Coulomb scattering angle, the Rutherford formula and the forward-angle divergence",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 19
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm15-deflection-need-not-decrease",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Smooth finite-range free scattering refutes global strict decrease; as a separate contact comparison, an attractive refracting shell with index $3/2$ has $\\theta(b)=2(\\arcsin(b/a)-\\arcsin(2b/(3a)))$ strictly increasing, with a jump down to $0$ at grazing incidence",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the cited scattering measures and vocabulary.\n\n**False statement.** *Let a conservative central scattering model with a\nfinite-range interaction, positive energy and nonzero angular momentum have\na unique scattering angle for every impact parameter. Then the scattering\nangle is a strictly decreasing function of the impact parameter.*\n\nThe smooth finite-range witness below refutes this claim within the exact\ncentral-scattering class of [[def-cm15-asymptotic-data]]: the zero potential\nhas deflection $0$ at every positive impact parameter. More generally,\nbeyond the range of any finite-range potential the scattering trajectories\nare free, so a globally defined deflection map has a constant tail. The\nseparate adopted refracting-shell comparison retains its increasing branch;\nthat contact rule is outside the smooth $C^2$ class and is not used as the\nwitness for the stated claim. [[pthm-cm15-central-scattering]] clause 5 makes\nno global monotonicity assertion, and [[def-cm15-cross-section]] uses\nspecified deflection branches instead of inverting a global decreasing map.",
          "strategy": "Refute global strict decrease with the exact smooth zero-potential free trajectories; separately compute the adopted refracting-shell branch by chord geometry and prove its derivative is positive using a local inverse-sine difference-quotient argument.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm15-impulse",
            "def-cm15-asymptotic-data",
            "def-cm15-cross-section",
            "pthm-cm15-central-scattering",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "post-cm01-frames",
            "def-interval",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine",
            "def-derivative",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
            "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative",
            "thm-intermediate-value",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "cor-mean-value-theorem",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm15-impulse": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm15-asymptotic-data": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm15-cross-section": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm15-central-scattering": "physical-result",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm01-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-intermediate-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-mean-value-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Assuming countable choice for the scattering measures and vocabulary, the smooth finite-range model has zero deflection for every impact parameter beyond its range, so any globally defined deflection map is constant on this tail and cannot be strictly decreasing. The zero potential is a genuine smooth witness with unique deflection for every positive impact parameter. As a separate example-specific contact model, the adopted attractive refracting shell of index 3/2 has a strictly increasing branch on $0<b<a$ and a jump to zero at grazing incidence $b=a$; the endpoint extension at $b=0$ is not a branch value, and the contact model is outside the smooth central-scattering class and the restricted restitution postulate. All computations concern declared model data and report no observations.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), §3.5, the discussion after Eq. (3.69): the assertion that the scattering angle decreases as the particle energy or the impact parameter is increased, the unqualified form of which is refuted here",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), §6.4, Eqs. (352)-(357): the impenetrable-sphere example, where the deflection is monotone for the reflection contact rule; the contrast with the refracting rule below shows that monotonicity is a property of the interaction rule, not of central scattering as such",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node51.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 18
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm15-impact-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A declared-data impact check: $m_1=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, $m_2=6\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, $v_1^-=3\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $v_2^-=-1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, with elastic, semi-elastic and sticking outcomes",
          "statement": "**Declared model data.** Adopt a collinear impact of two labels of the\ncontact model of [[post-cm15-impulse]] clauses (i)-(ii) along the contact\ndirection $n$, with masses and incoming velocities\n$$m_1=2\\ \\mathrm{kg},\\qquad m_2=6\\ \\mathrm{kg},\\qquad v_1^-=3\\,n\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}},\\qquad v_2^-=-1\\,n\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}} .$$\nThus $M=8\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, $\\mu=\\frac{2\\cdot6}{8}=\\frac32\\ \\mathrm{kg}$,\n$V=\\frac{2\\cdot3+6\\cdot(-1)}{8}=0$, and the relative velocity is\n$d^-:=v_2^--v_1^-=-4\\,n\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\n**Assertions to check.** For $e=1$ (elastic): $v_1^+=-3n$,\n$v_2^+=+1n$, $\\Delta K=0$. For $e=\\frac12$: $v_1^+=-\\frac32n$,\n$v_2^+=+\\frac12n$, impulse $J=-9n\\ \\mathrm{N\\,s}$,\n$\\Delta K=-9\\ \\mathrm{J}$. For $e=0$ (sticking): $v_1^+=v_2^+=0$,\n$\\Delta K=-12\\ \\mathrm{J}$. In every case the total momentum $P=0$ is\ncontinuous across the impact, as [[pthm-cm15-impact-balance]] clause 1\nrequires, and the energy changes are those of its clause 4.",
          "strategy": "A declared-data impact check: elastic, semi-elastic and sticking outcomes for one data set, verifying momentum continuity, the impulse, the velocities and the energy changes.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm15-impulse",
            "pthm-cm15-impact-balance",
            "thm-cm-impulse-restitution-and-energy-loss",
            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting",
            "def-cm01-units",
