            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A declared-data check of the adopted collinear impact model: masses $2$ and $6\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ with one-dimensional velocities $3$ and $-1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ along the contact direction, for coefficients of restitution $e=1,\\frac12,0$; the example verifies total momentum continuity, the impulse $J=-9\\,\\mathrm{N\\,s}$ at $e=\\frac12$, the post-impact velocities, and the kinetic-energy changes $0,-9,-12\\,\\mathrm{J}$, all in SI units. Every number is declared model data substituted into the model formulas; the example reports no measurement and no empirical comparison.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jacob Moore and Contributors, Mechanics Map (Pennsylvania State University, 2nd ed.), Engineering LibreTexts, §§11.3-11.5: the impulse-momentum principle, momentum conservation and the coefficient-of-restitution treatment of one-dimensional particle collisions",
                "url": "https://eng.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Mechanical_Engineering/Mechanics_Map_(Moore_2nd_Edition)/11%3A_Impulse_and_Momentum_in_Particles/11.05%3A_Particle_Collisions_in_One_Dimension"
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), 'Non-Isolated Systems', Eqs. (38)-(44): the momentum and external-force accounting that the impulse model extends to an isolated contact",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node12.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm15-restitution-above-one-increases-energy",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A coefficient of restitution above $1$ manufactures kinetic energy: $e=2$ on $m_1=m_2=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ with velocities $\\pm1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ raises the kinetic energy by $3\\,\\mathrm{J}$ while the configuration is unchanged",
          "statement": "**False statement.** If the collinear reciprocal-impulse equations of\n[[post-cm15-impulse]] clause (i) are retained but its clause (ii) is\n**hypothetically extended** to allow every real restitution coefficient $e$,\nthen momentum conservation and $v_2^+-v_1^+=-e(v_2^--v_1^-)$ guarantee\nconservation of total mechanical energy across the impact in a static\ncentral-potential model.\n\nThe adopted postulate restricts $e$ to $[0,1]$, as do the restitution and\nenergy clauses of [[pthm-cm15-impact-balance]] and\n[[thm-cm-impulse-restitution-and-energy-loss]]. Those clauses are not\napplied at $e=2$ here. The extension is a separate hypothetical law, and the\npost-impact velocities and energy difference below are derived locally from\nits equations. Only the elastic case $e=1$ guarantees kinetic-energy\nconservation for nonzero relative velocity within the adopted range.",
          "strategy": "A refutation by witness: with e=2 the contact law raises the kinetic energy by 3 J while the configuration is unchanged, so e>1 is incompatible with the conservative energy account.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm15-impulse",
            "pthm-cm15-impact-balance",
