          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A hypothetical extension of the adopted impact model, replacing only its restitution range by arbitrary real e; a refutation of the claim that every real coefficient of restitution is admissible in the adopted isolated-impact model: with the collinear restitution law and $e=2$ on declared data, the kinetic energy rises by $3\\,\\mathrm{J}$ across an impact while the configuration is instantaneously unchanged, so the total mechanical energy of a conservative two-label model is not conserved; values $e>1$ therefore signal an omitted internal energy reservoir and are excluded from the conservative contact model of this page, which restricts $e$ to $[0,1]$. The witness uses declared model data and asserts no measurement.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jacob Moore and Contributors, Mechanics Map (Pennsylvania State University, 2nd ed.), Engineering LibreTexts, §11.4: the coefficient of restitution between 0 and 1, with the remark that values above 1 would describe a superelastic collision in which additional stored energy is released",
                "url": "https://eng.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Mechanical_Engineering/Mechanics_Map_(Moore_2nd_Edition)/11%3A_Impulse_and_Momentum_in_Particles/11.04%3A_Surface_Collisions_and_the_Coefficient_of_Restitution"
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Newtonian Dynamics (University of Texas at Austin), §6.5, Eqs. (368)-(396): the elastic two-body collision with kinetic energy preserved, the conservative case against which the superelastic witness is contrasted",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newtonhtml/node52.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm15-momentum-alone-does-not-fix-the-impact",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Reciprocal impulses do not determine a collinear impact: the same incoming data admit the sticking outcome $v^+=0$ and the elastic outcome $(v_1^+,v_2^+)=(-3,1)\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ with different impulses and energies",
          "statement": "**False statement.** *In the collinear isolated-impact model of\n[[post-cm15-impulse]] clause (i), the reciprocal impulse equations\n$m_1(v_1^+-v_1^-)=J$ and $m_2(v_2^+-v_2^-)=-J$, with the impulse $J$\ndetermined by momentum balance of the two labels, fix the post-impact\nvelocities for given incoming data; no additional contact law is needed.*\n\n[[post-cm15-impulse]] clause (ii) adopts the restitution law as an\n*independent* contact assumption, and [[pthm-cm15-impact-balance]] clause 5\nrecords that the impulse equations leave the scalar impulse magnitude free.\nThe witness exhibits two admissible outcomes for one data set.",
          "strategy": "A refutation by witness: two admissible outcomes with different impulses satisfy the same reciprocal impulse equations, so an independent contact law is required.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm15-impulse",
            "pthm-cm15-impact-balance",
            "thm-cm-impulse-restitution-and-energy-loss",
            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-interval",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm15-impulse": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-cm15-impact-balance": "physical-result",
            "thm-cm-impulse-restitution-and-energy-loss": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-center-of-mass-and-kinetic-splitting": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A refutation of the claim that the reciprocal impulse equations alone determine a collinear impact: with masses $2$ and $6\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ and incoming velocities $3$ and $-1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, both the sticking outcome and the elastic outcome satisfy the equal-and-opposite momentum jumps with impulses $-6\\,\\mathrm{N\\,s}$ and $-12\\,\\mathrm{N\\,s}$ respectively and total momentum $0$, while velocities and kinetic-energy changes differ. Hence the contact law fixing the impulse (or the coefficient of restitution) is an independent model datum. The witness uses declared model data and asserts no measurement, no determination of any real coefficient and no observation.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jacob Moore and Contributors, Mechanics Map (Pennsylvania State University, 2nd ed.), Engineering LibreTexts, §11.5 'Particle Collisions in One Dimension': conservation of momentum as the first equation and an independent second relation (energy or restitution) as the second, the structure the witness exploits",
                "url": "https://eng.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Mechanical_Engineering/Mechanics_Map_(Moore_2nd_Edition)/11%3A_Impulse_and_Momentum_in_Particles/11.05%3A_Particle_Collisions_in_One_Dimension"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Peraire and S. Widnall, 16.07 Dynamics (MIT OpenCourseWare, Fall 2008/2009), Lecture L14: the discrete mass-loss examples in which the final velocity depends on the adopted ejection rule, the finite version of the underdetermination recorded here",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-07-dynamics-fall-2009/98d19cb2b9983c14c6907052499e7a98_MIT16_07F09_Lec14.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm15-rocket-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A declared rocket check: initial mass $3\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, loss rate $1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,s^{-1}}$ and relative exhaust speed $2\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ give $v=u_e\\ln(m_0/m)n$ and show that $(mv)'=F$ fails",
          "statement": "**Declared model data.** Adopt the open-body material accounting of\n[[post-cm15-impulse]] clause (iii) with, on the open interval\n$I=(-\\frac12\\ \\mathrm s,\\frac52\\ \\mathrm s)$ containing the initial time $0$,\nset $m_0:=3\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, $r_0:=1\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,s^{-1}}$, and\n$$m(t):=m_0-r_0t,\\qquad F\\equiv0,\\qquad r_{\\mathrm{in}}\\equiv0,\\qquad r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\equiv r_0,\\qquad w_{\\mathrm{out}}(t):=v(t)-u_e\\,n,$$\nwith a fixed unit vector $n$, $u_e:=2\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, and initial\nvelocity $v(0)=0$. The ejecta therefore leave with the body's velocity\nreduced by the constant exhaust speed $u_e$ along $n$. Set\n$q:=t/(1\\ \\mathrm s)$, the dimensionless numerical time, so\n$m(t)=(3-q)\\ \\mathrm{kg}$. Primes denote differentiation with respect to\nphysical frame time $t$, read in seconds.\n\n**Assertions to check.** The balance identities hold with\n$$v(t)=u_e\\ln\\frac{m_0}{m(t)}\\,n=2\\ln\\frac{3}{3-q}\\ n\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\\qquad(t\\in I),$$\nthe thrust is $r_{\\mathrm{out}}u_e=2\\ \\mathrm{N}$ along $n$, and the naive\nclosed-system law $(mv)'=F$ fails: it would force $v\\equiv0$ on $I$.",
          "strategy": "A declared-data check of the open-body law: linear mass loss with constant relative exhaust speed gives the Tsiolkovsky velocity and shows that the closed-system law fails.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm15-impulse",
            "pthm-cm15-impact-balance",
            "thm-cm-variable-mass-momentum-balance",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-interval",
