            "cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A declared-data check of the open-body material accounting: a body of initial mass $m_0=3\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ losing mass at $r_0=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,s^{-1}}$ has $m(t)=m_0-r_0t$. With no external force and ejecta of relative speed $u_e=2\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ opposite a fixed unit direction $n$, its velocity from rest is $v(t)=u_e\\ln(m_0/m(t))n$. It satisfies the material balance identities and violates the naive closed-system law $(mv)'=F$; the example verifies the identities, the thrust $2\\,\\mathrm{N}$ and the failure of the naive law on declared model data. It reports no measurement, no engine data and no empirical comparison.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Peraire and S. Widnall, 16.07 Dynamics (MIT OpenCourseWare, Fall 2008/2009), Lecture L14 'Variable Mass Systems: The Rocket Equation', Eqs. (11)-(15): $m\\,\\mathrm d\\mathbf v/\\mathrm dt=\\mathbf F+(\\mathrm dm/\\mathrm dt)\\mathbf v_0$ with $\\mathbf v_0$ the transferred matter's velocity relative to the body, and the Tsiolkovsky integral",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-07-dynamics-fall-2009/98d19cb2b9983c14c6907052499e7a98_MIT16_07F09_Lec14.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §1.2, printed p. 2: the caution that the closed-system form of the momentum balance does not describe an open variable-mass body",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm15-control-volume-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A declared smooth open flow: the uniform scaling $\\rho=\\rho_0e^{-3\\alpha t}$, $u=\\alpha x$ satisfies the control-volume identities on fixed and material regions, and elbow port data illustrate the inlet/outlet decomposition",
          "statement": "**(i) Declared uniform scaling.** Fix constants $\\rho_0>0$,\n$\\alpha\\ne0$ and $p_0$, with $[\\rho_0]=\\mu\\ell^{-3}$,\n$[\\alpha]=\\tau^{-1}$ and $[p_0]=\\mu\\ell^{-1}\\tau^{-2}$. On an open\ninterval $I\\ni0$ use uniform scaling, meaning expansion for $\\alpha>0$\nand contraction for $\\alpha<0$, and set\n$$\\rho(t)=\\rho_0e^{-3\\alpha t},\\qquad u(t,x)=\\alpha x,\\qquad \\sigma(t,x)=-p(t,x)\\,\\mathrm{Id},\\qquad p(t,x)=p_0-\\frac{\\rho(t)\\alpha^2}{2}\\lvert x\\rvert^2,\\qquad b\\equiv0,$$\nand fix radii $R_0>0$ and $R>0$ with units $\\ell$. Let the material region\nbe a ball $B_{R_B}$ with radius $R_B>\\max(R,R_0)$.\nDefine the material motion $\\chi(t,X)=e^{\\alpha t}X$ on a neighbourhood of\n$\\overline{B_{R_B}}$. More precisely, choose an open ball $O=B_{R_O}$ with\n$R_O>R_B$ and define $\\chi:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by this formula.\nIts restriction to $B_{R_0}$ has current radius\n$R(t)=R_0e^{\\alpha t}$. Define the fixed-ball control motion by\n$\\psi_R(t,X)=X$ for $X\\in O$, with control region $B_R$, and the\nmaterial-ball control motion by $\\psi_{\\mathrm{mat}}(t,X)=e^{\\alpha t}X$\nfor $X\\in O$, with control region $B_{R_0}$. Each map is a $C^2$ motion on\nthe open reference domain $O$ and has positive spatial Jacobian. Take the\nfields on a common open neighbourhood of\nthe closed sweep of $\\chi$ and the closed sweeps of these controls. The fields\nsatisfy the adopted balances of\n[[pthm-cm15-control-volume-balance]] (the density is spatially uniform, the\nvelocity is $\\alpha x$, and the pressure is quadratic).\n\n**(ii) Declared elbow control-volume data.** Take a material region $B$ with\nmotion $\\chi$ and associated spatial velocity field $u$ as in\n[[def-cm-continuum-motion]], and a material subregion $B'\\subseteq B$ with\n$C^1$ boundary. On an open reference neighbourhood\n$O\\supset\\overline{B'}$, define the fixed control motion\n$\\psi:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $\\psi(t,X)=X$; its control region is\n$C_t=C:=B'$ and its boundary velocity is $w=0$. Adopt the material balances\nof [[pthm-cm15-control-volume-balance]] for this model and the local mass and\nmomentum equations on an open field neighbourhood of the closed control\nregion, as allowed by that theorem's local-equation alternative.\n\nAssume $\\rho=1000\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$ is constant and the fields are\ntime-independent on that neighbourhood. The bounded $C^1$ boundary of $C$\nis partitioned into finitely many $C^1$ boundary pieces, up to\nsurface-measure-zero edges; designate an inlet patch $\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{in}}$,\nan outlet patch $\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{out}}$, and let the remaining pieces form\nan impermeable remainder $\\Sigma_0$. The outward unit normals on the ports are $-\\hat x$ and $+\\hat y$.\nOn the ports take $u=q_{\\mathrm{in}}\\hat x$ and\n$u=q_{\\mathrm{out}}\\hat y$, respectively, where each $q$ is a\nnonnegative, nonzero $C^1$ profile supported compactly inside its port,\nwith units $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$; take $u\\cdot\\nu=0$ on the remainder.\nThese traces vanish near the port edges and are compatible with a\n$C^1$ field and continuous boundary normal. For either port define\n$$Q:=\\int_{\\Sigma}q\\,\\mathrm dS>0,\\qquad v_{\\mathrm{eff}}:=\\frac{\\int_{\\Sigma}q^2\\,\\mathrm dS}{Q},\\qquad A_{\\mathrm{eff}}:=\\frac{Q^2}{\\int_{\\Sigma}q^2\\,\\mathrm dS},$$\nso $Q=A_{\\mathrm{eff}}v_{\\mathrm{eff}}$, the mass rate is $\\rho Q$,\nand the flux-averaged velocity is $v_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ in the port direction.\nDeclare $A_{\\mathrm{eff,in}}=10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$,\n$A_{\\mathrm{eff,out}}=5\\times10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$ and\n$v_{\\mathrm{eff,in}}=4\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$; the outlet effective\nspeed is unknown. The effective areas are defined by these integrals,\nnot the geometric patch areas. Assume the time-independent velocity\nfield is $C^1$ on the common neighbourhood. Set the body-force field to $b=0$ and assume\nthe stress field is $C^1$ on the same neighbourhood. These are conditional\nmodel premises for a balance calculation; no existence claim for a\nboundary-value solution or decomposition of the boundary stress is made.\nThe calculation determines only the net external resultant required by the\nsteady balance.",
          "strategy": "Two declared checks: the exact uniform-expansion field satisfies the control-volume identities on fixed and material balls, and the ideal elbow illustrates the inlet/outlet decomposition with the dynamic force.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-cm15-control-volume-balance",
            "post-cm15-impulse",
            "thm-cm-control-volume-balance",
            "thm-cm-variable-mass-momentum-balance",
            "def-cm-continuum-motion",
            "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-interval",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-cm15-control-volume-balance": "physical-result",
            "post-cm15-impulse": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-cm-control-volume-balance": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-variable-mass-momentum-balance": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-continuum-motion": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Two declared checks of the finite-regularity open-flow bookkeeping: (i) the exact uniform-scaling model $\\rho(t)=\\rho_0e^{-3\\alpha t}$, $u(t,x)=\\alpha x$, $p=p_0-\\rho\\alpha^2\\lvert x\\rvert^2/2$, with the control maps specified and the units of $\\alpha,\\rho_0,p_0$ declared; (ii) a conditional steady-elbow calculation with declared inlet/outlet data, where the balance gives mass flow $0.4\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,s^{-1}}$ and requires a net external resultant on the fluid of magnitude about $3.578\\,\\mathrm N$. No pressure-free momentum-flux force decomposition is inferred, since no boundary-stress data are given. Both are computations on declared model data; the example reports no measurement and does not solve a boundary-value problem.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), §3.7 and §4.4: the material transport and balance equations specialised here to a declared velocity and density field",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "W. Barsukow, Introduction to Conservation Laws: Theory and Numerics (lecture notes, TUM, Winter term 2021/22), §§1.1-1.3: the fixed-volume and Lagrangian descriptions of the same balance, used for the fixed-ball and material-ball checks",
                "url": "https://user.math.uzh.ch/barsukow/exercises2122/conservationlaws.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Jacob Moore and Contributors, Mechanics Map (Pennsylvania State University, 2nd ed.), Engineering LibreTexts, §11.7 'Steady-Flow Devices': the mass-flow relation $\\dot m=\\rho vA$ and the steady-flow momentum relation used in the elbow illustration",
                "url": "https://eng.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Mechanical_Engineering/Mechanics_Map_(Moore_2nd_Edition)/11%3A_Impulse_and_Momentum_in_Particles/11.07%3A_Steady-Flow_Devices"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        }
      ],
