        {
          "id": "def-cm16-chaos-list-for-model-maps",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Chaos in the stated sense for maps of the model: topological mixing, dense periodic points and sensitive dependence, as one precise list among several",
          "statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let\n$f:X\\to X$ be a map. Say that $f$ is **chaotic in the stated sense** when all\nfour of the following hold; this is the definition of\n[[def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps]], quoted here\nto fix the exact content used on this page:\n\n1. $f$ is **continuous** with respect to the metric topology\n   ([[def-metric-continuity]]);\n2. $f$ is **topologically mixing**: for all nonempty open sets $U,V\\subseteq X$\n   there is an integer $N$ with $f^n(U)\\cap V\\ne\\emptyset$ for every $n\\ge N$;\n3. the periodic points of $f$ are **dense** in $X$: every nonempty open subset\n   of $X$ meets $\\{x\\in X:f^n(x)=x\\text{ for some }n\\ge1\\}$\n   ([[def-dense-top]]);\n4. $f$ has **sensitive dependence on initial conditions**: there is\n   $\\delta>0$ (a sensitivity constant) such that for every $x\\in X$ and every\n   $\\varepsilon>0$ there are $y\\in X$ and $n\\ge1$ with $d(x,y)<\\varepsilon$\n   and $d(f^n(x),f^n(y))\\ge\\delta$.\n\nThe predicate is applied on this page to the following model maps: (a) the\nstroboscopic map $\\Psi$ of a periodic model, restricted to a subset\n$X\\subseteq\\Sigma$ with $\\Psi(X)\\subseteq X$; (b) the local return map $P$ of\na transversal section restricted to an invariant subset of its domain\n([[def-cm16-stroboscopic-and-poincare-maps]] clauses (i) and (iii)); and (c)\nfor an autonomous model with local flow $\\Phi$\n([[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]]), its time-one map\n$f:=\\Phi_1|_X:X\\to X$ when every point of $X$ has a forward solution through\ntime $1$ and $\\Phi_1(X)\\subseteq X$. Here time $1$ is the unit value of the\nmodel's stated time parameter. In every case $d$ is the Euclidean\ndistance of the declared state chart restricted to $X$. The properties\nare relative to that set and that metric, so changing the invariant set may\nchange whether the list holds. An invertible linear change of coordinates is\na bi-Lipschitz conjugacy for Euclidean state norms: it preserves continuity,\ntopological mixing and density of periodic points, and preserves sensitivity\nwith a possibly rescaled positive sensitivity constant. Thus a fixed\ncoordinate rescaling cannot change whether the map satisfies the four clauses.\n\n**Conventions and scope.** (a) This is one precise list among several\ninequivalent usages of the word \"chaos\" in the literature; the page asserts\nnone of the other usages, and no item of the page claims that a model is\n\"chaotic\" without exhibiting the four properties for a stated map, set and\nmetric. (b) This item makes no pairwise-independence claim about the four\nproperties. The examples show that dense periodic points alone do not imply\nmixing and that positive growth at an equilibrium alone does not give the\nassociated time-one map a chaotic invariant set in this sense. (c) The list is purely topological: it asserts no invariant\nmeasure, no positivity of an entropy, no fractal dimension, no statistical\nstability and no property of any physical system. (d) A numerical trajectory,\na histogram or a plotted section is not evidence that the four properties\nhold; they are established by proof for a stated model.\n\n",
          "strategy": "A vocabulary item fixing one precise chaos list for the model maps (continuity, mixing, dense periodic points, sensitivity) with the independence, topological and non-numerical caveats.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm16-stroboscopic-and-poincare-maps",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system",
            "def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps",
            "def-metric-space",
            "def-metric-continuity",
            "def-dense-top"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm16-stroboscopic-and-poincare-maps": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-dense-top": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The chaos vocabulary of the page, applied to stroboscopic maps of periodic models, Poincare return maps, and time-one maps of autonomous models when restricted to invariant domains: a continuous map of a metric space is chaotic in the stated sense when it is topologically mixing, has dense periodic points and has sensitive dependence on initial conditions with a positive constant, with all four clauses stated exactly. The item fixes one precise list among several used in the literature, requires an invariant set and a metric relative to which the properties are stated, and records that an invertible linear coordinate rescaling preserves the list. It asserts no measure-theoretic property, no entropy or dimension and no empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Sussman and Jack Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed., §4.3 'Homoclinic Tangle': qualitative tangle and divergence discussion, not the four-clause definition adopted here",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip/chapter004.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Sussman and Jack Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed., Chapter 3 opening epigraph: numerical experiments do not prove theorems",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip/chapter003.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Iain Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309, Fall 2014), §7.5 pp. 182-188: nonlinear discrete maps as qualitative motivation; the exact mixing, periodic-point and sensitivity predicates are supplied by the local mathematical definition",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), Chapter 9 'Chaos': qualitative chaos discussion retained here only as motivation for the precise list",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 12
