        {
          "id": "pthm-cm16-hyperbolic-linearization",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Hyperbolic linearisation of an equilibrium of the model: exponential dichotomy of the linearised flow, local exponential asymptotic stability in the stable case, and instability in the saddle case",
          "statement": "Let an autonomous stability model be fixed whose force map is of class $C^1$\nin the state, with state domain $S_0\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{6N}$, associated\n$C^1$ field $G$ and local flow\n$\\Phi:\\Omega\\to S_0$ ([[def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets]] clauses\n(i)-(ii)); all differentiability is that of\n[[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. Let $z_*\\in S_0$ be an\nequilibrium state, $G(z_*)=0$, and let\n$A:=DG(z_*)$ ([[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]]). All state\nand tangent norms below are Euclidean norms of the dimensionless SI\nnumerical coordinates $z=(X/(1\\ \\mathrm m),V/(1\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}))$\nfixed by [[def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets]] clause (iv), and\n$t=t_{\\rm phys}/t_{\\rm ref}$ with $t_{\\rm ref}=1\\ \\mathrm s$.\nThus $G$ and $A$ are the numerical field and its derivative in that chart;\nno norm adds physical positions to physical velocities. Then:\n\n1. **(Exponential dichotomy.)** If no complex eigenvalue of $A$\n   ([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]]) has real part\n   $0$ --- that is, if $z_*$ is hyperbolic in the sense of\n   [[def-cm16-stability-and-hyperbolicity-concepts]] clause (iv) --- then\n   there are $A$-invariant subspaces $E_s,E_u\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{6N}$ with\n   $\\mathbb R^{6N}=E_s\\oplus E_u$ and real constants $C\\ge1$, $c>0$ such\n   that, with the Euclidean norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and the\n   exponential series $e^{tA}$:\n   $$\\lVert e^{tA}v\\rVert_2\\le Ce^{-ct}\\lVert v\\rVert_2\\ (v\\in E_s,\\ t\\ge0), \\qquad \\lVert e^{tA}v\\rVert_2\\ge C^{-1}e^{ct}\\lVert v\\rVert_2\\ (v\\in E_u,\\ t\\ge0),$$\n   $$\\lVert e^{tA}v\\rVert_2\\le Ce^{ct}\\lVert v\\rVert_2\\ (v\\in E_u,\\ t\\le0),$$\n   and $E_s=\\{v:e^{tA}v\\to0\\ (t\\to+\\infty)\\}$,\n   $E_u=\\{v:e^{tA}v\\to0\\ (t\\to-\\infty)\\}$. The spaces $E_s,E_u$ are the\n   **stable and unstable subspaces** of the linearised flow.\n2. **(Stable case.)** If every complex eigenvalue of $A$ has negative real\n   part, then $z_*$ is locally exponentially asymptotically stable: there are\n   $\\delta>0$, $\\beta\\ge1$, $\\gamma>0$ such that every maximal solution $z$\n   of the model with $\\lVert z(0)-z_*\\rVert_2<\\delta$ is defined for all\n   $t\\ge0$ and satisfies\n   $$\\lVert z(t)-z_*\\rVert_2\\le\\beta e^{-\\gamma t}\\lVert z(0)-z_*\\rVert_2\\qquad(t\\ge0).$$\n   In particular $z_*$ is Lyapunov stable and asymptotically stable\n   ([[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]]).\n3. **(Saddle case.)** If no complex eigenvalue of $A$ has real part $0$ and\n   at least one has positive real part, then $z_*$ is unstable: there is\n   $\\varepsilon_0>0$ such that for every $\\delta>0$ there are a state $z_0$\n   with $\\lVert z_0-z_*\\rVert_2<\\delta$ and a time $t_1>0$ such that the\n   maximal solution through $z_0$ is defined on $[0,t_1]$ and\n   $\\lVert z(t_1)-z_*\\rVert_2=\\varepsilon_0$.\n4. **(Rates, charts and scope.)** The constants $C,c,\\beta,\\gamma$ and the\n   coordinate descriptions of the subspaces depend on the chosen chart and\n   norm; the physical rates $c/t_{\\rm ref},\\gamma/t_{\\rm ref}$ have dimension\n   $\\tau^{-1}$. Under a smooth\n   change of chart the derivative matrices at the equilibrium are similar, so\n   their eigenvalue spectrum is invariant, while the Euclidean estimates may\n   change as recorded in [[def-cm16-stability-and-hyperbolicity-concepts]]\n   clause (vi)(b). No conclusion is asserted when some eigenvalue has zero real part\n   (the centre case), and no statement is made about the size of the stable\n   set, the basin of attraction, uniformity in a family of models or any\n   empirical realization.\n\n",
          "strategy": "Direct transfer of the exponential dichotomy, Lyapunov stability and instability suppliers to an equilibrium of the model field, with the chart and centre-case qualifications.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets",
            "def-cm16-stability-and-hyperbolicity-concepts",
            "thm-cm-exponential-dichotomy-for-a-hyperbolic-endomorphism",
            "thm-cm-exponential-stability-by-a-lyapunov-quadratic-form",
            "thm-cm-instability-of-a-hyperbolic-saddle",
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability",
            "def-variational-equation-along-an-ode-solution",
            "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
            "def-operator-norm",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-cm01-units"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm16-stability-and-hyperbolicity-concepts": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-cm-exponential-dichotomy-for-a-hyperbolic-endomorphism": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-exponential-stability-by-a-lyapunov-quadratic-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-instability-of-a-hyperbolic-saddle": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-variational-equation-along-an-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The consequences of the hyperbolic linearisation of an equilibrium state of an autonomous model whose force map is of class C1 in the state: invariant stable and unstable subspaces of the linearised flow with exponential estimates and exponential separation from the equilibrium; local exponential asymptotic stability of the equilibrium when the whole spectrum lies in the open left half-plane; and instability when no eigenvalue lies on the imaginary axis and one lies in the open right half-plane. The rates and constants belong to the linearised flow in the declared state chart; no centre-case, global, parameter-uniform or empirical statement is made.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Iain Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309, Fall 2014), §7.3 pp. 164-172 with the corrected epsilon-delta definitions: linearisation at an equilibrium, stability in the stable case and instability from a positive real eigenvalue",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), §§3.2, 5.6: the source's linearised-stability discussion with its omitted hypotheses, replaced here by the complete supplier statements",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Sussman and Jack Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed., §4.3: stable and unstable sets and the warning that the exponential dichotomy must be proved rather than read off a picture",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 12
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm16-two-rotor-kam",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The two-rotor model: a positive-measure family of invariant Lagrangian tori with Diophantine frequencies for sufficiently small coupling, with the explicit units and the stated scope",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), inherited from the analytic KAM suppliers. Adopt the **two-rotor model**: two ideal rotors with fixed axes, equal moments\nof inertia $\\mathcal I>0$, angular coordinates $\\theta_1,\\theta_2$ in\n$\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ (radians, dimensionless), conjugate angular momenta\n$\\Pi_1,\\Pi_2$, the physical Hamiltonian\n$$H_{\\mathrm{phys}}(\\theta,\\Pi)=\\frac{\\Pi_1^2}{2\\mathcal I}+\\frac{\\Pi_2^2}{2\\mathcal I} +E_0\\varepsilon\\cos\\theta_1\\cos\\theta_2$$\non the phase domain $(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^2\\times\\mathbb R^2$, with the\ncanonical two-form $d\\theta_1\\wedge d\\Pi_1+d\\theta_2\\wedge d\\Pi_2$ and\nHamilton equations $\\theta'=\\partial_\\Pi H_{\\mathrm{phys}}$,\n$\\Pi'=-\\partial_\\theta H_{\\mathrm{phys}}$; here $E_0>0$ is an energy scale\nand $\\varepsilon\\ge0$ a dimensionless coupling. All quantities are model data;\n$[H_{\\mathrm{phys}}]=\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-2}$, $[\\Pi_i]=\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-1}$,\n$[\\mathcal I]=\\mu\\ell^2$ ([[def-cm01-units]]). Put\n$$J_0:=\\sqrt{\\mathcal I E_0},\\qquad t_0:=\\frac{J_0}{E_0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathcal I}{E_0}},\\qquad \\omega_0:=\\frac1{t_0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{E_0}{\\mathcal I}},$$\nand read the **dimensionless model** through $I_i:=\\Pi_i/J_0$ and the\ndimensionless time $s:=t/t_0$,\n$$H(I,\\vartheta)=\\tfrac12\\bigl(I_1^2+I_2^2\\bigr)+\\varepsilon\\cos\\vartheta_1\\cos\\vartheta_2.$$\nLet $C^*:=\\{\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\lvert\\nu\\rvert_\\infty\\le\\tfrac1{16}\\}$ be the\ndeclared frequency cube and, for $\\tau>1$ and $\\alpha>0$, let\n$$\\Omega_{\\alpha,\\tau}:=\\Bigl\\{\\nu\\in C^*:\\lvert k\\cdot\\nu\\rvert\\ge\\alpha\\lvert k\\rvert_1^{-\\tau} \\ \\text{for all }k\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\setminus\\{0\\}\\Bigr\\}$$\nbe its $(\\alpha,\\tau)$-Diophantine subset\n([[def-holomorphic-kam-domain-and-diophantine-labels]]). Then for every\n$\\tau>1$ there is a constant $c>0$, depending only on the fixed\ndimensionless analytic data (the action cube, the strip width $s_0$, the\nexponent $\\tau$, $M_2=2$, and the normalized perturbation profile\n$g(\\vartheta)=\\cos\\vartheta_1\\cos\\vartheta_2$ with strip bound\n$\\lvert g\\rvert\\le\\cosh^2(s_0)$), and uniform in $\\alpha$ and the amplitude\n$\\varepsilon$, such that for every $0<\\alpha<c$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$ with\n$\\varepsilon\\le c\\,\\alpha^2$:\n\n1. **(Positive measure of frequencies.)** The Diophantine set has positive\n   Lebesgue measure, with\n   $$\\lvert\\Omega_{\\alpha,\\tau}\\rvert\\ge\\lvert C^*\\rvert-C_{2,\\tau}\\, (\\operatorname{diam}C^*)\\,\\alpha=\\frac1{64}-\\frac{C_{2,\\tau}}{8}\\,\\alpha,$$\n   where $C_{2,\\tau}$ is the lattice constant of\n   [[lem-diophantine-resonance-slab-measure]].\n2. **(Invariant Lagrangian tori.)** For every $\\nu\\in\\Omega_{\\alpha,\\tau}$\n   there is a $C^1$ embedding\n   $\\widehat\\Psi_\\nu:\\mathbb T^2\\to(\\mathbb R^2)\\times\\mathbb T^2$ (a\n   $C^1$ injective immersion that is a homeomorphism onto its image), and it\n   is Lagrangian\n   ($\\widehat\\Psi_\\nu^*\\omega=0$ for the two-form $\\omega$ of the\n   dimensionless model), and satisfies\n   $$X_H\\bigl(\\widehat\\Psi_\\nu(\\vartheta)\\bigr)=\\sum_{j=1}^2\\nu_j\\, \\partial_{\\vartheta_j}\\widehat\\Psi_\\nu(\\vartheta);$$\n   consequently a torus parameter phase follows $\\varphi(s)=\\varphi_0+\\nu s$,\n   and its dimensionless phase-space trajectory is $\\widehat\\Psi_\\nu(\\varphi_0+\\nu s)$.\n   At physical time $t$ it is the scaled image of\n   $\\widehat\\Psi_\\nu(\\varphi_0+\\omega_0\\nu t)$: the physical angle is the\n   angular component of this embedding, not generally the linear parameter phase.\n   Thus the torus carries quasi-periodic motion with fundamental angular\n   frequencies $\\omega_0\\nu_1,\\omega_0\\nu_2$. The\n   parametrisation is close to the unperturbed one and Lipschitz in $\\nu$ with\n   the bounds of [[thm-positive-measure-analytic-kam-family]] clause 3.\n3. **(Scope.)** Nothing is asserted about the complement of\n   $\\Omega_{\\alpha,\\tau}$ (resonant or non-Diophantine labels), about tori\n   surviving outside the smallness threshold, about all initial data, about\n   diffusive or chaotic behaviour of the complement, or about any laboratory\n   rotor: the smallness condition $\\varepsilon\\le c\\alpha^2$ is a model-data\n   condition, the constant $c$ is not computed numerically here, and no\n   measured value of $\\mathcal I$, $E_0$ or $\\varepsilon$ is used or implied.",
          "strategy": "Verification of the analytic KAM hypotheses for the declared two-rotor potential and unit restoration, concluding positive-measure Diophantine tori and quasi-periodic motions with angular frequencies omega0 nu.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "thm-positive-measure-analytic-kam-family",
            "lem-kam-composed-map-and-derivative-convergence",
            "def-holomorphic-kam-domain-and-diophantine-labels",
            "lem-diophantine-resonance-slab-measure",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-positive-measure-analytic-kam-family": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-kam-composed-map-and-derivative-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-holomorphic-kam-domain-and-diophantine-labels": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-diophantine-resonance-slab-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
