          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The conditional chaos theorem for one declared smooth mechanical model: a single label on full Cartesian configuration space R^3 with fixed positive mass, a pulsed polynomial force in one coordinate and inverted harmonic forces in the other two. The axis with zero transverse coordinates and velocities is invariant, and its pulse dynamics is the one-coordinate pulsed oscillator of the mathematical supplier. For every pulse width below a threshold depending only on the dimensionless amplitude a>=100 and the bump, the physical stroboscopic map has a compact invariant set on that axis conjugate to the two-sign shift and chaotic in the stated sense; its two fixed points are hyperbolic for the full six-dimensional map, and its nonconstant finite-deviation codes give transverse homoclinic points in the full phase space. The coded states satisfy |Q|<=(sqrt(a)+3) l0 and |Pi|<=m l0 w0 (sqrt(a)+3). The transverse force terms are adopted model assumptions, not an observed drive or apparatus; no measurements, empirical chaos claim or claim about other driven or nearly integrable models are made.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "P. Cvitanovic, R. Artuso, R. Mainieri, G. Tanner, G. Vattay, Chaos: Classical and Quantum (ChaosBook.org, edition 17.6.8), Chapter 14 Section 14.6 and Chapter 15 Sections 15.1-15.3: symbolic dynamics, the Henon horseshoe and homoclinic tangles as concrete constructions rather than pictures",
                "url": "https://chaosbook.org/chapters/ChaosBook.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Devaney and Z. Nitecki, Shift automorphisms in the Henon mapping, Communications in Mathematical Physics 67 (1979) 137-146: the shift structure of a quadratic horseshoe and the stable/unstable sectors",
                "url": "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-67/issue-2/Shift-automorphisms-in-the-H%C3%A9non-mapping/cmp/1103905161.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Sussman and Jack Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed., §4.3: the tangle narrative and the explicit warning that a pictured crossing does not prove chaos",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm16-recurrence-for-finite-volume-models",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Poincare recurrence for the model's Hamiltonian flow on a bounded invariant set of finite phase volume: almost every state of any Borel subset returns to it infinitely often",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]), inherited from the phase-volume preservation\ntheorem [[pthm-cm13-liouville]] (its measure-preservation clause uses the\nchange-of-variables theorem under that assumption). Adopt the force model with\npotential presentation and the Hamiltonian data of [[pthm-cm13-liouville]]:\ntime-independent potential $U:Q\\to\\mathbb R$ with residual forces\n$d\\equiv0$, Cartesian phase domain $W=Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$\n([[def-cm13-two-form]]), Hamiltonian\n$$H:I_0\\times W\\to\\mathbb R,\\qquad H(t,x,p)=\\tfrac12p\\cdot\\mathsf M^{-1}p+U(x),$$\nphase-volume form $\\Omega=\\omega^{\\wedge3N}/(3N)!$. Let $A\\subseteq W$ be a\nbounded Borel set ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]) whose closure is contained in\nthe open phase domain $W$. Let $\\Phi_t$ denote the potential-branch flow on\nits maximal time-slice domain, and suppose it is defined at every point of $A$\nfor all real times and maps $A$ onto itself:\n$$\\Phi_t(A)=A\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R),$$\nso that $A$ is invariant in the sense of\n[[def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets]] clause (iv). Then\n$\\lambda_{6N}(A)<\\infty$, and the flow preserves $\\lambda_{6N}$ on every\nBorel subset of $A$, by the finite-cover and time-subdivision argument in the\nproof below. For every Borel set $E\\subseteq A$ almost every\n$x\\in E$ with respect to the Lebesgue measure $\\lambda_{6N}$ of the chart\n([[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]) has\n$$\\Phi_n(x)\\in E\\qquad\\text{for infinitely many }n\\ge1.$$\nThe time unit is the frame time $[t]=\\tau$ ([[def-cm01-units]]), so\n\"infinitely many $n$\" means infinitely many returns at unit time steps.",
          "strategy": "Composition of the phase-volume preservation theorem with the finite-measure recurrence theorem: a bounded invariant Borel set of finite phase volume has almost-everywhere infinite returns at unit time steps, under countable choice.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets",
            "pthm-cm13-liouville",
            "pthm-cm13-flow-preservation",
            "thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems",
            "def-cm13-two-form",
            "thm-cm-smooth-dependence-and-the-variational-equation",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
            "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "cor-cm-flow-domain-open-and-flow-is-c1"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm13-liouville": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm13-flow-preservation": "physical-result",
            "thm-poincare-recurrence-for-finite-measure-preserving-systems": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm13-two-form": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-cm-smooth-dependence-and-the-variational-equation": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-borel-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Poincare recurrence for the Hamiltonian flow of a declared autonomous potential presentation: if a bounded Borel set of phase space with compact closure in the phase domain is invariant under a complete flow on that set, then the flow preserves phase volume on its Borel subsets by a finite local-cover and time-subdivision argument, and almost every state of each such subset returns to it infinitely often at integer multiples of the time unit. The statement assumes the Axiom of Countable Choice inherited from the local phase-volume theorem and asserts nothing about return times, which individual states return, infinite phase volume or incomplete flows outside the invariant set, and nothing empirical.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §4.2.3 pp. 92-93: recurrence and the warning that positivity of energy alone does not make the accessible volume finite",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Sussman and Jack Wisdom, Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed., Chapter 4: integrable and return-map discussions with the finite-phase-space qualifications",
                "url": "https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/9579/sicm_edition_2.zip"
              },
              {
                "title": "Iain Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309, Fall 2014), Chapter 7: dynamical-systems scope warnings on recurrence, invariant measures and numerical portraits",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 23
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm16-scope-of-stability-and-chaos",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Scope of the stability, perturbation and chaos statements: conditional model deductions, the limits of finite-time and numerical evidence, and the empirical qualifications",
          "statement": "This remark records what the page's statements do and do not establish. It adds\nno new claim of its own.\n\n**1. Model deductions only.** The force-model results of the page are\nconditional deductions about the framework class of\n[[def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets]]: a force model with stated\nmasses, configuration domain, regularity and — where relevant — a\ntime-shift-invariant periodic restriction. The two-rotor KAM result instead\nadopts a reduced angle-action Hamiltonian on a torus phase domain. The pulsed\nhorseshoe result is a full one-label force model on $\\mathbb R^3$; its axial\npulse dynamics is embedded by explicitly adopted transverse inverted-oscillator\nforces. The conclusions about equilibria, tori, horseshoes and recurrence are\nproperties of these declared models. Continuous-time periodic-orbit,\nlimit-cycle and homoclinic vocabulary is used for autonomous models; periodic\nmotions of a driven model are treated by its stroboscopic map. The energy and\nvariational theorems, the\npendulum check and the stable-without-attraction harmonic counterexample use\ndeclared mechanical chart data: an open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, a $C^2$\npositive-definite kinetic metric $M$, and a $C^2$ potential $U$. The growth and\nisotropic-oscillator checks are declared force models of the framework.\nNone asserts that nature realizes the\nmodel, and none uses a measured value: couplings, energy scales, pulse\nparameters and the thresholds $c$ and $\\delta_0$ are model data; the page has\nno measurement, calibration, sampling or uncertainty budget.\n\n**2. Stability is not attraction, and one notion is not another.**\n[[pthm-cm16-energy-stability-and-instability]] establishes Lyapunov stability\nof a strict minimum, linear instability from a negative Hessian eigenvalue,\nnonlinear instability from a strictly concave potential, and the absence of\nasymptotic stability under exact energy conservation. A zero Hessian eigenvalue alone gives no stability conclusion; strict\nminima remain covered even when their Hessians are degenerate. For a mixed\nsaddle (a negative eigenvalue together with oscillatory directions), only the\nlinear-instability statement is made. The harmonic-oscillator counterexample shows\nthat the page's stability vocabulary cannot be collapsed.\n\n**3. Linearisation has separate local and finite-time conclusions.**\n[[pthm-cm16-variational-equation-and-linearization]] gives finite-time flow\nderivative, Lipschitz and linear-approximation estimates under its declared\nball and remainder hypotheses. By contrast,\n[[pthm-cm16-hyperbolic-linearization]] gives all-time exponential estimates for\nthe linearized flow and, under the hyperbolic spectral hypotheses, local\nexponential asymptotic stability or nonlinear instability; it gives no centre\ncase or global conclusion. A finite-time growth rate is attached to a state,\ntangent vector and interval ([[def-cm16-finite-time-growth-rates]]); positive\ngrowth is not a definition of chaos. At an equilibrium, smooth chart changes\nconjugate derivative matrices and preserve their spectrum, while norms and\nfinite-time growth bounds can depend on the chart.\n\n**4. Chaos is a proved property of a stated map and set.**\n[[pthm-cm16-separatrix-splitting-and-horseshoe]] proves the four properties of\n[[def-cm16-chaos-list-for-model-maps]] for the full model's stroboscopic map on\nan explicitly constructed compact invariant set contained in its invariant\naxis, by transferring the supplied shift conjugacy. Its full six-dimensional\nfixed points are hyperbolic because the two transverse inverted-oscillator\nmodes have multipliers off the unit circle; the coded homoclinic intersections\nare transverse in the full phase space by the product construction. This is\nexistence for the declared pulsed model under its small-pulse condition, not a\nclaim about all driven oscillators, every separatrix, Hénon-Heiles energies or\nany numerical portrait. Quantified trajectories and plotted sections are\ncomputations under chosen parameters, not experiments or proofs. The page has\nno experiment item and reports no observed data.\n\n**5. KAM persistence is a positive-measure conditional statement.**\n[[pthm-cm16-two-rotor-kam]] gives, for the adopted two-rotor potential and\nsufficiently small coupling, a positive-measure set of Diophantine frequencies\nwhose tori persist, with quasi-periodic motions on them and explicit unit\nrestoration. It says nothing about the complement of that set, about resonant\ngaps, about all initial data, about chaotic diffusion, or about a laboratory\nrotor. The threshold $\\varepsilon\\le c\\alpha^2$ is a model-data condition; the\nconstant $c$ is not computed numerically and no empirical smallness is\nasserted.\n\n**6. Recurrence needs finite volume and invariance.**\n[[pthm-cm16-recurrence-for-finite-volume-models]] assumes a bounded\nflow-invariant Borel set with compact closure in the potential phase domain\nand a flow complete on that set. Its proof extends the suppliers' local\nphase-volume preservation by a finite cover and time subdivision. The\nconclusion is almost-everywhere with respect to phase volume and gives no\nreturn time, rate or individual-state statement. Free and unconfined models\nfail the finite-volume hypothesis, and the theorem says nothing about them or\nthermodynamic irreversibility.\n\n**7. Choice conventions.** The horseshoe theorem assumes the Axioms of\nCountable and Dependent Choice, inherited from its supplier, and the\nrecurrence theorem assumes Countable Choice, inherited from\n[[pthm-cm13-liouville]]. The KAM theorem also assumes Countable Choice, inherited from its analytic and measure-theoretic suppliers. The other statements of the page are choice-free:\ntheir suppliers use finite-dimensional linear algebra, explicit\nconstructions and finite estimates. No item reaches a recorded or unproved\nset-theoretic item.\n\n**8. Relation to the sources.** The four complete works consulted for this\npage (Tong, Likharev, SICM and Stewart) supply the model formulations,\nworked examples and caveats; their informal stability and chaos discussions\nare replaced by the exact supplier statements, and their numerical portraits\nare treated as illustrations, never as evidence. The empirical status of any\nmodel on this page would require a separately specified apparatus, sampling\nmodel and statistical analysis, which this page does not provide.",
          "strategy": "A scope remark separating model deductions from empirical claims, listing the hypotheses and gaps of every page statement and the choice conventions.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets",
            "def-cm16-stability-and-hyperbolicity-concepts",
            "def-cm16-finite-time-growth-rates",
