        {
          "id": "ex-cm16-pendulum-equilibrium-checks",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The ideal pendulum on declared data: strict minimum and Lyapunov stability at the downward equilibrium, negative Hessian and instability at the upward equilibrium, the exact linearised eigenvalues and oscillation frequency, and failure of attraction",
          "statement": "**Declared model data.** Take one label on the real line as the angle chart of\nthe ideal planar pendulum: the chart domain is $\\Omega:=\\mathbb R$, the\nconfiguration coordinate is the angle $q$ (radians, identified modulo $2\\pi$\nat the end), the kinetic metric is the constant matrix\n$M:=m\\ell^2$ with $m:=1\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, $\\ell:=1\\ \\mathrm m$, and the potential\nis\n$$U(q):=mg\\ell\\,(1-\\cos q),\\qquad g:=9.81\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad mg\\ell=9.81\\ \\mathrm J .$$\nThis is the mechanical chart datum of\n[[def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart]] with\n$\\Omega=\\mathbb R$, $M$ constant and $U$ of class $C^\\infty$. Its equations\nare $m\\ell^2\\ddot q=-mg\\ell\\sin q$, equivalently\n$\\ddot q=-(g/\\ell)\\sin q$.\n\n**Assertions to check.** (i) $q=0$ and $q=\\pi$ are mechanical equilibria;\n(ii) at $q=0$ the potential is a strict local minimum and\n[[pthm-cm16-energy-stability-and-instability]] clause 1 makes $(0,0)$\nLyapunov stable, while the linearised eigenvalues are\n$\\pm i\\sqrt{g/\\ell}=\\pm i\\,3\\sqrt{109}/10\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ (so the angular\noscillation frequency is $3\\sqrt{109}/10\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$) and the\nequilibrium is not asymptotically stable; (iii) at $q=\\pi$ the Hessian is\n$-mg\\ell<0$ and clause 3 makes $(\\pi,0)$ unstable, with linearised eigenvalues\n$\\pm 3\\sqrt{109}/10\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$.",
          "strategy": "Declared-data check of the energy-stability theorem on the ideal pendulum: strict minimum and stability with imaginary frequencies; negative Hessian and instability with real rates; failure of attraction.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm16-stability-and-hyperbolicity-concepts",
            "pthm-cm16-energy-stability-and-instability",
            "def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
            "def-cm01-units"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm16-stability-and-hyperbolicity-concepts": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm16-energy-stability-and-instability": "physical-result",
            "def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A declared-data check of the page's energy-stability theorem on the ideal planar pendulum: with mass 1 kg, length 1 m and g=9.81 m s^-2 in the angle chart, the downward equilibrium is a strict minimum, hence Lyapunov stable but not attracting, with purely imaginary linearised eigenvalues and a positive angular oscillation frequency; the upward equilibrium has a strictly negative Hessian, hence is unstable, with real linearised eigenvalues. All data are declared model data; no measurement and no empirical comparison is made.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Mechanics (title page last edited September 1, 2026), §3.2 and §3.3: pendulum equilibrium types and the phase-plane discussion, with the linearised-stability caveats",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Classical Dynamics (University of Cambridge), §2.1 pp. 10-13: the pendulum Lagrangian and its equilibria",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Iain Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309, Fall 2014), §7.3 pp. 164-172: the corrected stability definitions and the small-oscillation frequency about a stable equilibrium",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm16-linearization-and-growth-checks",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Two declared linear models worked through: the harmonic flow has zero finite-time growth in every direction, while the repulsive linear model has exact rates $\\pm1$ and the positive Lyapunov exponent $1$ at its equilibrium",
          "statement": "**Declared model data and normalization.** Take one label with\n$Q:=\\mathbb R^3$ and declare physical reference data\n$m_0:=1\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, $\\ell_0:=1\\ \\mathrm m$, $t_0:=1\\ \\mathrm s$.\nFor $\\sigma_1=-1$, $\\sigma_2=1$ define physical force maps\n$F_{i,\\rm phys}:\\mathbb R\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by\n$F_{i,\\rm phys}(T,\\mathbf X,\\mathbf V)=\\sigma_i(m_0/t_0^2)\\mathbf X$,\nwith $T$ in seconds, $\\mathbf X$ in metres, $\\mathbf V$ in metres per second,\nand outputs in newtons. These are smooth globally state-Lipschitz force\nmaps of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with physical mass $m_0$.\nPut $t=T/t_0$, $\\mathbf q=\\mathbf X/\\ell_0$,\n$\\mathbf v=t_0\\mathbf V/\\ell_0$ and $E_0=m_0\\ell_0^2/t_0^2$. Then\n$m_0\\ddot{\\mathbf X}=F_{i,\\rm phys}$ becomes\n$\\mathbf q''=\\sigma_i\\mathbf q$, with $\\mathbf q'=\\mathbf v$, since\n$\\ddot{\\mathbf X}=(\\ell_0/t_0^2)\\mathbf q''$. The normalized force maps are\n$F_i(t,\\mathbf q,\\mathbf v)=F_{i,\\rm phys}(t_0t,\\ell_0\\mathbf q,(\\ell_0/t_0)\\mathbf v)/(m_0\\ell_0/t_0^2)=\\sigma_i\\mathbf q$,\nwith normalized mass $m=1$. The physical potentials are\n$U_{i,\\rm phys}(\\mathbf X)=-\\sigma_i m_0\\lVert\\mathbf X\\rVert_2^2/(2t_0^2)$;\nnormalizing their energies by $E_0$ gives, in the constant-metric chart $\\Omega:=\\mathbb R^3$ with $M:=I_3$, they have\nthe potentials $U_1(\\mathbf q):=\\tfrac12\\lVert\\mathbf q\\rVert_2^2$ and\n$U_2(\\mathbf q):=-\\tfrac12\\lVert\\mathbf q\\rVert_2^2$ of\n[[def-cm-mechanical-vector-field-and-energy-in-a-chart]]. The associated fields\nand complete motion maps are listed below; $\\mathbf e_1,\\mathbf e_2,\\mathbf\ne_3$ denote the coordinate basis, explicitly\n$\\mathbf e_j(k)=1$ for $k=j-1$ and $0$ otherwise, for $k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$ and\n$j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$. All state and tangent norms below apply to the\n dimensionless numerical coordinates $(\\mathbf q,\\mathbf v)$.\n\n1. The **harmonic model** has field\n   $G_1(\\mathbf q,\\mathbf v)=(\\mathbf v,-\\mathbf q)$ and motion map\n   $$\\Phi_t^1(\\mathbf q_0,\\mathbf v_0)=\\bigl(\\mathbf q_0\\cos t+\\mathbf v_0\\sin t,\\,-\\mathbf q_0\\sin t+\\mathbf v_0\\cos t\\bigr).$$\n2. The **repulsive linear model** has field\n   $G_2(\\mathbf q,\\mathbf v)=(\\mathbf v,\\mathbf q)$ and motion map\n   $$\\Phi_t^2(\\mathbf q_0,\\mathbf v_0)=\\bigl(\\tfrac12(\\mathbf q_0+\\mathbf v_0)e^{t}+\\tfrac12(\\mathbf q_0-\\mathbf v_0)e^{-t},\\,\\tfrac12(\\mathbf q_0+\\mathbf v_0)e^{t}-\\tfrac12(\\mathbf q_0-\\mathbf v_0)e^{-t}\\bigr).$$\n\nFor a state $a$ and a nonzero tangent vector $u$ with\n$D_a\\Phi_t(a)u\\ne0$, the **finite-time tangent growth rate** is\n$t^{-1}\\log(\\lVert D_a\\Phi_t(a)u\\rVert_2/\\lVert u\\rVert_2)$; when the\nforward lifetime is unbounded and its finite limit as $t\\to\\infty$ exists,\nthat limit is the **Lyapunov exponent** ([[def-cm16-finite-time-growth-rates]]\nclauses (i)-(ii)). Both displayed flows are complete, so this lifetime\ncondition holds for each state.\n\n**Assertions to check.** (i) Both displayed maps are the local flows of their\nfields; (ii) for the harmonic model\n$D_{a}\\Phi_t^1(a)$ is the orthogonal block rotation\n$$\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos t\\,I_3&\\sin t\\,I_3\\\\-\\sin t\\,I_3&\\cos t\\,I_3\\end{pmatrix},$$\nso every finite-time tangent growth rate vanishes and every existing\nLyapunov exponent is $0$; (iii) for the repulsive model the rates along the directions\n$(\\mathbf e_1,-\\mathbf e_1)$ and $(\\mathbf e_1,\\mathbf e_1)$ are $-1$ and\n$+1$, so the Lyapunov exponent of the equilibrium along the unstable\neigenvector is $1$.\n\n",
          "strategy": "Declared-data check of the variational equation and the growth-rate definition on two linear models: zero rates for the harmonic flow, exact rates plus and minus one for the repulsive flow.",
          "deps": [
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "def-cm16-flow-equilibria-and-invariant-sets",
            "def-cm16-finite-time-growth-rates",
            "pthm-cm16-variational-equation-and-linearization",
