            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional on the adopted mass-balance postulate, the smooth kinematic model and the Axiom of Countable Choice used by the transport machinery: conservation of the model mass of every material subregion is equivalent to the partial differential equation partial_t rho + div(rho u) = 0 on the current regions; equivalently the material derivative satisfies D_t rho = -rho div u, the product rho times the Jacobian is constant along each label worldline, the relative volume rate is div u, and the incompressibility condition div u = 0 is equivalent to the density being constant along each worldline in this branch. The theorem is a conditional model deduction and asserts no empirical value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Section 4.2 (conservation of mass, local forms and the volumetric interpretation)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "W. Barsukow, Introduction to Conservation Laws: Theory and Numerics (lecture notes, TUM, Winter term 2021/22), Section 1.1 (integral and differential forms of the continuity equation)",
                "url": "https://user.math.uzh.ch/barsukow/exercises2122/conservationlaws.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 8.3 (continuity equation and incompressibility of a continuum)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $(B,O,I)$ be body data\n([[def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units]]), let $\\chi$ be a motion of the model\nwith spatial velocity $u$ ([[post-cm17-continuum-kinematic-model]],\n[[def-cm-continuum-motion]]), let $J_0\\subseteq I$ be an open interval with\n$\\overline{J_0}\\subseteq I$, let $\\Sigma$ be the swept region over\n$\\overline{J_0}$, and let $\\rho:U\\to(0,\\infty)$ be a density field of class\n$C^1$ on an open set $U$ containing $\\Sigma$\n([[def-cm17-density-and-mass]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]).\nAssume the mass-balance postulate (CM) of [[post-cm17-mass-balance]] for every\nmaterial subregion and every $t\\in J_0$. Then:\n\n**(i) Local form.** For every $(t,x)\\in J_0\\times\\chi_t(B)$,\n\n$$\\partial_t\\rho(t,x)+\\operatorname{div}\\bigl(\\rho u\\bigr)(t,x)=0 . \\tag{C}$$\n\nConversely, if (C) holds on an open neighbourhood of $\\Sigma$, then (CM)\nholds for every material subregion and every $t\\in J_0$.\n\n**(ii) Material form.** On $J_0\\times\\chi_t(B)$ the material derivative of the\ndensity satisfies\n\n$$D_t\\rho=-\\rho\\operatorname{div}u ,$$\n\nand writing $J(t,X)=\\det D_X\\chi_t(X)$ along the motion, the product\n$\\rho\\bigl(t,\\chi(t,X)\\bigr)J(t,X)$ is constant in $t$ for every label\n$X\\in B$.\n\n**(iii) Volume rate.** For every $X\\in B$ and $t\\in J_0$ one has\n$\\partial_tJ(t,X)=J(t,X)\\operatorname{div}u(t,\\chi(t,X))$: the relative rate of\nchange of an infinitesimal material volume is $\\operatorname{div}u$, and the\nidentity of (ii) is the statement that density times volume is conserved\nalong a label. In particular $\\operatorname{div}u=0$ on the swept region if\nand only if $\\rho$ is constant along every label worldline ($D_t\\rho=0$).\n\nThe statement is a conditional deduction from the adopted mass balance and\nthe kinematic model; it asserts no equation of motion, no constitutive\nrelation and no empirical value.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-cm17-traction-and-stress",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Traction fields and stress matrices of the continuum model, with units and sign conventions",
          "reference": "batch-33 A page; design research/plan-non-relativistic-classical-mechanics-track.md L919",
          "design_state": "authored; step-1 readiness recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 10,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units",
            "post-cm17-continuum-kinematic-model",
            "def-cm-traction-and-stress",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm-continuum-motion"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm17-continuum-kinematic-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-traction-and-stress": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-continuum-motion": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The model's contact-force quantity: a traction field assigning to each point and unit normal the force per unit area exerted across a surface, its dimensions, and the stress matrix representing it when one exists; the sign convention identifying compressive normal traction with negative scalar stress, the component reading, and the distinction from body-force densities. Existence of a representing stress matrix, its continuity and its symmetry are not asserted here; they are conditional conclusions of the balance items of this page. No measured force is asserted.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Section 4.3 (force, Cauchy's hypothesis for contact forces, traction and the stress tensor)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 7.2 (the stress tensor and the force on a surface element)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Fix body data $(B,O,I)$ and a motion $\\chi$ of the model with current regions\n$\\Omega_t=\\chi_t(B)$ and $\\mathcal U=\\{(s,x):s\\in I,\\ x\\in\\chi_s(O)\\}$.\nLet $U\\subseteq\\mathcal U$ be open and contain the swept closure\n$\\{(s,x):s\\in\\overline{J_0},\\ x\\in\\chi_s(\\overline B)\\}$, where $J_0$\nis an open interval with compact closure in $I$\n([[post-cm17-continuum-kinematic-model]], [[def-cm-continuum-motion]]).\nUse on $U$ the restriction of the positive $C^1$ density slot\n$\\rho:\\mathcal U\\to(0,\\infty)$ of [[def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units]],\nand denote that restriction again by $\\rho$. Let\n$S^2=\\{n\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\|n\\|_2=1\\}$ be the unit sphere.\n\nA **traction field of the model** is a map\n\n$$t:U\\times S^2\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^3,$$\n\nread with the convention of [[def-cm-traction-and-stress]]: $t(s,x,n)$ is the\nforce per unit area exerted **by the material on the side towards which $n$\npoints upon the material on the other side**, at the point $x$ of a surface\nwhose unit normal is $n$, at time $s$. Its dimension is that of force per\narea, $[t]=\\mu\\ell^{-1}\\tau^{-2}$, read in $\\mathrm{N\\,m^{-2}}$\n([[def-cm01-units]]).\n\nA $C^1$ field of matrices $\\sigma:U\\to M_3(\\mathbb R)$ **represents** the\ntraction field when\n\n$$t(s,x,n)=\\sigma(s,x)\\,n\\qquad\\text{for every }(s,x,n)\\in U\\times S^2 ,$$\n\nthe product being matrix-vector multiplication. The existence of such a\n$\\sigma$ for a given traction field is **not asserted here**: it is a\nconditional conclusion of the balance items of this page. When it exists,\n$\\sigma(s,x)$ is the **stress matrix** of the model at that event, and its\nentries read $\\sigma_{ij}=\\bigl(t(s,x,e_j)\\bigr)_i$: the $i$-th component of\nthe traction across the surface with normal $e_j$.\n\nThe isotropic matrix $-\\lambda I_3$ with $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ represents the\n**normal** traction $t(n)=-\\lambda n$: under the convention above, positive\n$\\lambda$ is a compressive normal traction and $\\lambda$ is called the\n**pressure** in this sign convention. Within this isotropic family,\n$\\lambda=0$ gives the zero stress matrix, a pressure-free unstressed state.\nThe **body force per unit mass** is a separate object: a continuous\nfield $b:U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with the dimension of acceleration,\n$[b]=\\ell\\tau^{-2}$. The **body-force density per unit volume** is\n$\\rho b:U\\to\\mathbb R^3$, of dimension $\\mu\\ell^{-2}\\tau^{-2}$. A traction field need not satisfy\n$t(-n)=-t(n)$ and its representing matrix, when one exists, need not be\nsymmetric; both properties are separate conditional conclusions.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable"
        },
