            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional on the small-strain isotropic linear elastic branch: a plane displacement field a cos(<k,x> - omega t) satisfies the model's displacement equation with zero body force on all of space-time if and only if its dispersion relation holds; only solutions of that equation have the stated longitudinal or transverse phase speeds, which are real and positive under the branch's positivity hypotheses. The theorem asserts no completeness of plane waves, no measured modulus and no empirical value.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 7.7 (plane longitudinal and transverse elastic waves and their speeds)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Section 11.2 (the isotropic linearized elastic field equations)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Adopt the small-displacement isotropic linear elastic branch of\n[[post-cm17-linear-elastic-constitutive]] with reference density $\\rho_0>0$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell^{-3}$ and Lamé parameters $\\lambda,\\mu_s$ of dimension\n$\\mu\\ell^{-1}\\tau^{-2}$, satisfying $\\mu_s>0$ and\n$\\lambda+\\tfrac{2\\mu_s}{3}>0$ ([[def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units]]). Let\n(E) be the displacement equation of\n[[pthm-cm17-linear-elastic-equation-of-motion]] with body force\n$b\\equiv0$. Let $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ denote position of dimension $\\ell$ and\n$t\\in\\mathbb R$ time of dimension $\\tau$. Let $k\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$\nbe a wave vector of dimension $\\ell^{-1}$, $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$ a polarization\namplitude of dimension $\\ell$, and $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$ an\nangular frequency of dimension $\\tau^{-1}$. The phase is dimensionless.\nDefine the **plane displacement field**\n\n$$\\xi(t,x):=a\\cos\\bigl(\\langle k,x\\rangle_2-\\omega t\\bigr),$$\n\nwith the Euclidean inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_2$\n([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then $\\xi$ satisfies (E) on all of\n$\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$ if and only if\n\n$$\\rho_0\\omega^2a=\\mu_s|k|_2^2\\,a+(\\lambda+\\mu_s)\\,\\langle k,a\\rangle_2\\,k. \\tag{D}$$\n\nMoreover, **if this plane wave satisfies (E)** (equivalently, if (D) holds),\nthen for $a\\ne0$: if $a$ is parallel to $k$ (**longitudinal**), then\n$\\rho_0\\omega^2=(\\lambda+2\\mu_s)|k|_2^2$ and its phase speed is\n$|\\omega|/|k|_2=c_L$, where\n$c_L:=\\sqrt{(\\lambda+2\\mu_s)/\\rho_0}>0$; if $a$ is orthogonal to $k$\n(**transverse**), then $\\rho_0\\omega^2=\\mu_s|k|_2^2$ and its phase speed is\n$|\\omega|/|k|_2=c_T$, where $c_T:=\\sqrt{\\mu_s/\\rho_0}>0$. Positivity follows\nfrom the branch hypotheses, and $[c_L]=[c_T]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$\n([[def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units]], [[def-cm01-units]]). No assertion is\nmade that every solution of (E) is a superposition of plane waves, and no\nmeasured modulus or speed is claimed.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
