          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional on the adopted incompressible Newtonian (or inviscid Euler) fluid branch and its periodic-cell idealization, and on the Axiom of Countable Choice used by the Fourier machinery: for smooth divergence-free periodic initial velocity data of finite H^s norm (integer s at least 4) there is a local time T depending only on that norm, and a smooth periodic velocity and a unique smooth mean-zero pressure satisfying the model's field equations exactly; the velocity is unique on the interval among smooth divergence-free solutions, depends continuously on initial data in H^{s-1}, and no all-time bound or global regularity conclusion is claimed. The theorem concerns the Eulerian field equations and does not construct the material label map; no measured viscosity or pressure is asserted.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Sections 12.2-12.3 (the incompressible viscous fluid equations and the inviscid limit)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Sections 8.4-8.5 (ideal and viscous incompressible fluid dynamics, with the Navier-Stokes equation and its exact channel solutions)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Adopt the constant-density incompressible Newtonian or inviscid branch and periodic-cell convention of [[post-cm17-fluid-constitutive]] clauses (a), (a0), (c), with $\\rho>0$, $\\eta\\ge0$, and no body force. Fix the model scales $L_*,U_*>0$, $T_*=L_*/U_*$ and put $\\nu_*:=\\eta/(\\rho U_*L_*)$. Let $s\\ge4$ be an integer and $U_0$ a smooth divergence-free field on the dimensionless unit torus, with $Y_0:=\\|U_0\\|_{H^s}<\\infty$. Set\n$$T:=\\begin{cases}(2C_sY_0)^{-1},&Y_0>0,\\\\1,&Y_0=0,\\end{cases}$$\nwhere $C_s$ is the constant used by [[thm-local-smooth-periodic-euler-viscous-solution]]. Then:\n\n**(i) Reduction and physical-time conversion.** Under $\\tau=t/T_*$, $y=x/L_*$ and the scaling of the postulate, the physical continuity and momentum equations with $\\sigma=-P_{\\mathrm m}I_3+\\eta(D_xu_{\\mathrm{phys}}+D_xu_{\\mathrm{phys}}^{\\mathsf T})$ are equivalent to\n$$\\partial_\\tau U+(U\\cdot\\nabla_y)U=-\\nabla_y\\Pi+\\nu_*\\Delta_yU,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}_yU=0.$$\nFor smooth fields with zero-mean pressure, these are equivalent to\n$$\\partial_\\tau U+P((U\\cdot\\nabla_y)U)=\\nu_*\\Delta_yU \\tag{PE}$$\nand the pressure reconstruction below. A solution on $[0,T]$ is a physical solution on $[0,T_*T]$.\n\n**(ii) Existence and pressure.** There is $U\\in C^\\infty([0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $U(0)=U_0$, $\\operatorname{div}_yU=0$ and (PE), and $\\sup_{[0,T]}\\|U\\|_{H^m}\\le\\|U_0\\|_{H^m}e^{4C_mY_0T}$ for every $m\\ge s$. There is a unique smooth dimensionless zero-mean pressure $\\Pi$ giving the primitive equations in (i). The physical fields are\n$$u_{\\mathrm{phys}}(t,x)=U_*U(t/T_*,x/L_*),\\qquad P_{\\mathrm m}(t,x)=\\rho U_*^2\\Pi(t/T_*,x/L_*).$$\nThey satisfy the adopted field equations for both $\\eta>0$ and the separately adopted $\\eta=0$ Euler branch.\n\n**(iii) Uniqueness and dependence on data.** Fix $B>0$. For all smooth divergence-free data $U_0,V_0$ with both $H^s$ norms at most $B$, the corresponding solutions exist on the common dimensionless interval $[0,T_B]$, $T_B=(2C_sB)^{-1}$, with $U(0)=U_0$ and $V(0)=V_0$. If $U_0=V_0$ then $U=V$. On this bounded data set the solution map is continuous from the $L^2$ topology into $C([0,T_B],H^{s-1})$, as in [[thm-fluid-uniqueness-and-continuous-dependence]]. The common physical interval is $[0,T_*T_B]$ for the same fixed scales and material parameters.\n\n**(iv) Scope.** The Sobolev norm and $T,T_B$ are dimensionless; physical time also depends on the chosen scales. The theorem concerns smooth Eulerian fields on the adopted periodic cell. It constructs no material label map, no nonperiodic or rough-data solution and no global-in-time bound; no measured parameter is used.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm17-chain-limit-of-the-string-model",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The continuum string model as the proved limit of the mass-spring chain, with explicit convergence and dispersion error",
          "reference": "batch-33 A page; design research/plan-non-relativistic-classical-mechanics-track.md L919",
          "design_state": "authored; step-1 readiness recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-cyclic-spring-chain",
            "thm-cm-spring-chain-continuum-limit",
            "thm-cm-spring-chain-dispersion-relation",
            "lem-cm-second-difference-remainder-bound",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units",
            "def-cm01-units",
            "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-cyclic-spring-chain": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-spring-chain-continuum-limit": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-spring-chain-dispersion-relation": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-second-difference-remainder-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional on the adopted mass-spring chain with the stated mass and stiffness scaling: for every smooth periodic solution of the one-dimensional wave equation with speed c = square root of tension over linear density, the chain with node mass density-times-spacing and spring constant tension-over-spacing reproduces the sampled continuum motion with explicit uniform O(a^{3/2}) nodal and interpolated displacement error, with an additional O(a^2) chord-interpolation term, and the chain's exact dispersion relation reduces to the continuum one with a second-order error in the spacing for fixed wave number. The theorem justifies the string model as an approximation of the discrete mechanical model in this regime; it asserts no claim that the string model is exact for any material, and no measured constant.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 6.1 (the chain of masses and springs, its Lagrangian and the continuum limit)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Sections 6.2-6.5 (coupled oscillators, the discrete chain and its continuum/standing-wave limits)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $L>0$, let $\\rho_\\ell>0$ be a linear mass density and let $\\tau_s>0$ be a\ntension scale, both in the units of [[def-cm01-units]], and put\n$c:=\\sqrt{\\tau_s/\\rho_\\ell}$, of dimension $[c]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$\n([[def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units]]). For an integer $N\\ge2$ put\n$a:=L/N$ and consider the cyclic spring chain of\n[[def-cm-cyclic-spring-chain]] with parameter $c$ and spacing $a$,\nequivalently the chain of $N$ nodes of mass $m=\\rho_\\ell a$ and spring\nconstant $k_s=\\tau_s/a$; the two descriptions agree because\n$c^2/a^2=k_s/m$. Let $[0,T_{\\max}]$ be a compact time interval and let\n$u:[0,T_{\\max}]\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^2$ in $t$ and $C^4$ in $x$,\n$L$-periodic in $x$, and satisfy\n\n$$u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}\\qquad\\text{on }[0,T_{\\max}]\\times\\mathbb R ,$$\n\nwith $M:=\\sup|\\partial_x^4u|<+\\infty$ and $M_2:=\\sup|\\partial_x^2u|<+\\infty$.\nLet $q$ be the chain solution with the sampled initial data\n$q_j(0)=u(0,ja)$, $q_j'(0)=u_t(0,ja)$, put $e_j(t):=q_j(t)-u(t,ja)$ and\n$C:=c^2\\sqrt L\\,M/12$. In the physical reading, $u$ is the transverse\ndisplacement of the one-dimensional string model: $[L]=[a]=[x]=\\ell$,\n$[t]=[T_{\\max}]=\\tau$, $[u]=[q_j]=[e_j]=\\ell$,\n$[\\rho_\\ell]=\\mu\\ell^{-1}$, $[\\tau_s]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$, and\n$[c]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$; consequently $[M]=\\ell^{-3}$ and\n$[M_2]=\\ell^{-1}$. In clause (iii), $A$ is a displacement amplitude,\n$[A]=\\ell$, $[\\kappa]=\\ell^{-1}$ and $[\\omega]=\\tau^{-1}$. Then:\n\n**(i)** $q$ exists and is unique on $[0,T_{\\max}]$, and for every\n$t\\in[0,T_{\\max}]$\n\n$$\\|e(t)\\|_a\\le\\frac{Ca^2t^2}{2},\\qquad\\|e'(t)\\|_a\\le Ca^2t,\\qquad \\max_j|e_j(t)|\\le\\frac{Ca^{3/2}t^2}{2},$$\n\nin the discrete norm of [[def-cm-cyclic-spring-chain]]; consequently the\nnodal displacements converge to the sampled continuum motion uniformly on\n$[0,T_{\\max}]$ as $a\\to0$ with $L,T_{\\max}$ fixed.\n\n**(ii)** With $I_aq$ the piecewise-linear interpolation of the chain data on\nthe circle of circumference $L$,\n\n$$\\sup_{x\\in[0,L]}|I_aq(t,x)-u(t,x)|\\le\\max_j|e_j(t)|+\\frac{M_2a^2}{8}\\qquad(0\\le t\\le T_{\\max}),$$\n\nso the interpolated chain displacement converges uniformly to the continuum\nsolution as $a\\to0$.\n\n**(iii)** For $A\\ne0$ and cyclic wave numbers $\\kappa Na\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, the travelling ansatz $q_j(t)=A\\cos(\\kappa ja-\\omega t)$ solves the\nchain equation if and only if $\\omega^2=(4c^2/a^2)\\sin^2(\\kappa a/2)$, and for these solutions with\n$|\\kappa a|\\le1$ one has $|\\omega^2-c^2\\kappa^2|\\le c^2\\kappa^4a^2/12$: the\ncontinuum dispersion relation is the long-wave limit with an explicit\nsecond-order error in the spacing for fixed wave number.\n\nThe result justifies the continuum string model with speed $c$ as the proved\nlimit of the discrete mechanical chain in this regime; it asserts no\nexactness of the string model for any material and no measured constant.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-cm17-string-wave-solutions",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The string model: d'Alembert solutions, fixed-end modes and uniqueness for the adopted one-dimensional continuum",
          "reference": "batch-33 A page; design research/plan-non-relativistic-classical-mechanics-track.md L919",
          "design_state": "authored; step-1 readiness recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 10,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-cm17-chain-limit-of-the-string-model",
            "thm-cm-dalembert-formula-and-string-solutions",
            "thm-cm-smooth-string-fourier-series-solution",
            "def-cm-sine-coefficients-of-periodic-functions",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units",
            "def-cm01-units"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-cm17-chain-limit-of-the-string-model": "physical-result",
            "thm-cm-dalembert-formula-and-string-solutions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-smooth-string-fourier-series-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-sine-coefficients-of-periodic-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional on the adopted one-dimensional string model with tension and linear density (wave speed c = square root of tension over linear density, justified by the chain limit) and on the small transverse displacement idealization: the d'Alembert formula gives the unique C^2 solution of the Cauchy problem on the line for C^2 initial displacement and C^1 initial velocity, and for smooth odd periodic data the fixed-end sine-series solution exists, is unique among C^2 solutions and carries an explicit tail bound. The theorem asserts no measured tension or density, no nonlinear or bending corrections and no validity of the string model for any particular material.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 6.5 (standing waves of a fixed-end string and the wave equation)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 6.1 (continuum limit of the chain and the wave equation)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Adopt the one-dimensional string model: a uniform string of length $L>0$\n(fixed ends) or of infinite extent, with linear mass density $\\rho_\\ell>0$\nand tension $\\tau_s>0$, whose small transverse displacement\n$u:I\\times J\\to\\mathbb R$ (where $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a nonempty time\ninterval and $J=\\mathbb R$ or $J=[0,L]$) satisfies\n\n$$u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx},\\qquad c:=\\sqrt{\\tau_s/\\rho_\\ell},$$\n\nwhere $[L]=[x]=\\ell$, $[t]=\\tau$, $[\\rho_\\ell]=\\mu\\ell^{-1}$,\n$[\\tau_s]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$, and $[u]=\\ell$. The initial displacement data\n$f$ and $F$ have dimension $\\ell$, while the initial velocity data $g$ and $G$\nhave dimension $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$. Thus $[c]=\\sqrt{[\\tau_s]/[\\rho_\\ell]}=\n\\ell\\tau^{-1}$, and $[\\omega_n]=\\tau^{-1}$. These are the dimensions of\n[[def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units]], [[def-cm01-units]]. The identification\nof $c$ with the chain limit is that of\n[[pthm-cm17-chain-limit-of-the-string-model]]. Then:\n\n**(i) Cauchy problem on the line.** For $f\\in C^2(\\mathbb R)$ and\n$g\\in C^1(\\mathbb R)$ the function\n\n$$u(t,x):=\\frac{f(x-ct)+f(x+ct)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2c}\\int_{x-ct}^{x+ct}g(s)\\,ds$$\n\nis of class $C^2$ on $\\mathbb R^2$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), satisfies $u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}$\nwith $u(0,\\cdot)=f$ and $u_t(0,\\cdot)=g$, and is the unique $C^2$ field on\n$\\mathbb R^2$ with those data.\n\n**(ii) Fixed ends.** Let $F$ be odd and $2L$-periodic of class $C^5$ and let\n$G$ be odd and $2L$-periodic of class $C^4$\n([[def-cm-sine-coefficients-of-periodic-functions]]) with finite\n$M_5=\\sup|F^{(5)}|$, $M_4=\\sup|G^{(4)}|$. Then the sine series $u$ of the\nsmooth string theorem exists with absolutely and uniformly convergent\ntermwise derivatives up to second order, satisfies $u_{tt}=c^2u_{xx}$,\n$u(t,0)=u(t,L)=0$, $u(0,\\cdot)=F|_{[0,L]}$, $u_t(0,\\cdot)=G|_{[0,L]}$, is the\nunique $C^2$ solution on $[0,T]\\times[0,L]$ of that boundary-value problem,\nand for every $N\\ge1$ satisfies the tail bound\n$|u-S_Nu|\\le C(N+1)^{-4}$ with $C=2\\bigl(2L^5M_5/\\pi^5+2L^5M_4/(c\\pi^5)\\bigr)$\non $[-T,T]\\times[0,L]$, where $S_Nu$ is the $N$-th partial sum of the series.\n\n**(iii) Reading and scope.** The initial functions $f,g$ or $F,G$ are the\nmodel's initial transverse displacement and velocity; the solutions are exact\nfor the model and are not approximations within it. The model is the linear\nsmall-transverse-displacement idealization of the string: bending stiffness,\nlongitudinal motion, external forcing and large-amplitude geometric\nnonlinearity are excluded, and no measured tension, density or displacement\nis asserted.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm17-boundary-regularity-and-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Boundary data, regularity limits and open scopes of the continuum page, and the model-versus-nature reading",
          "reference": "batch-33 A page; design research/plan-non-relativistic-classical-mechanics-track.md L919",
          "design_state": "authored; step-1 readiness recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 16,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-cm17-incompressible-periodic-local-evolution",
            "pthm-cm17-linear-elastic-equation-of-motion",
            "pthm-cm17-string-wave-solutions",
            "pthm-cm17-momentum-equation",
            "post-cm17-fluid-constitutive",
            "post-cm17-linear-elastic-constitutive",
            "post-cm17-momentum-balance",
            "def-cm-scalar-conservation-law-weak-and-entropy-solution",
            "rem-local-versus-known-open-global-fluid-scope",
            "post-cm17-continuum-kinematic-model"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-cm17-continuum-kinematic-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-cm17-incompressible-periodic-local-evolution": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm17-linear-elastic-equation-of-motion": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm17-string-wave-solutions": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm17-momentum-equation": "physical-result",
            "post-cm17-fluid-constitutive": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm17-linear-elastic-constitutive": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-cm17-momentum-balance": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm-scalar-conservation-law-weak-and-entropy-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "rem-local-versus-known-open-global-fluid-scope": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Scope audit of the continuum page: the ideal boundary data used by its boundary-value and evolution results (periodic cell, fixed ends, whole line, and the no-slip condition of the channel example), the actual pointwise applicability limits of the smoothness and positive-Jacobian hypotheses of the finite-motion transport and balance branch, its separately adopted exclusion of material interfaces and its theorem-specific boundary regularity, the separately adopted reference-coordinate linear elastic balance without a finite-motion requirement, the validity regimes of the constitutive branches and the separate one-dimensional string model, the open specialist scopes deliberately not claimed (general compressible entropy systems, rough walls and boundary layers, nonlinear constitutive PDEs, turbulence closure, all-time three-dimensional regularity), and the model-versus-nature status of every result on the page. The remark asserts no new physical claim, no measurement and no theorem.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Sections 12.1-12.3 (fluid boundary conditions and the limits of the inviscid and viscous models)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Sections 7.1, 7.3 and 8.5 (idealizations of the continuum description, elastic moduli and viscous channel flows with their conditions)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "- **Boundary data are idealizations, not derived.** Boundary-value and\n  evolution results use explicit ideal domains and boundary data: the periodic cell\n  of [[post-cm17-fluid-constitutive]] clause (c) (fields on $\\mathbb T^3$,\n  with the mean of the pressure fixed), fixed ends for the string\n  ([[pthm-cm17-string-wave-solutions]]), the whole line for the d'Alembert\n  problem, and the no-slip conditions $v(0)=v(h)=0$ used by the channel-flow\n  example of the companion page. These conditions are **adopted**; they are\n  not derived from the balance principles and are not claims about real\n  boundaries. Rough walls, wall slip at small scales, boundary layers,\n  free surfaces, contact lines and moving contact sets require boundary\n  models outside this page and are not covered by any theorem here.\n\n- **Regularity limits and separate model exclusions.** The finite-motion\n  transport and balance theorems assume a $C^2$ motion with positive Jacobian\n  and $C^1$ fields, and the local fluid theorem assumes smooth data. These\n  classical identities apply only where their stated hypotheses hold; they\n  do not apply pointwise across a jump discontinuity of a required $C^1$\n  field or where the motion loses its required regularity, injectivity or\n  positive Jacobian. A **material interface**, a surface separating material\n  portions, need not produce such a discontinuity: the motion and fields can\n  extend smoothly across it. [[post-cm17-continuum-kinematic-model]] clause\n  (iv) separately excludes material interfaces from its adopted physical\n  scope; this is a model restriction, not a consequence of smoothness.\n  Boundary corners likewise need not violate field regularity: the body data\n  require a bounded open region, while each boundary integral has its own\n  domain hypotheses. In particular, [[post-cm17-momentum-balance]] permits\n  finite piecewise $C^1$ presentations with face integrals and surface-null\n  edges; a corner alone does not require a weak formulation. For fields with\n  jumps, these classical results supply no balance across the discontinuity.\n  [[def-cm-scalar-conservation-law-weak-and-entropy-solution]] separately\n  defines a scalar weak balance tested against compactly supported $C^1$\n  functions and its entropy inequalities; the companion page exhibits shock\n  and rarefaction solutions in that specified formulation. No general\n  multidimensional shock theory, entropy existence theorem or interface\n  kinematics is claimed by this page.\n\n- **Constitutive validity is restricted and stated.** The linear elastic\n  branch is a small-strain first-order idealization with two constant moduli\n  ([[post-cm17-linear-elastic-constitutive]]); the Newtonian branch assumes a\n  constant viscosity and an incompressible, isothermal description, and the separately adopted constant-density incompressible Euler branch sets the viscous stress to zero, while the\n  inviscid barotropic branch assumes a pressure law and a conservative body\n  force ([[post-cm17-fluid-constitutive]]). In every branch the parameters\n  ($\\lambda,\\mu_s$; $\\eta$; the pressure law) are model data: a numerical\n  comparison uses measured values as qualified empirical premises with their\n  conditions and uncertainty, and no such value is asserted on this page.\n  Plastic, viscoelastic, anisotropic, temperature-coupled, non-Newtonian and\n  turbulent-closure responses are outside the page.\n\n- **Open specialist scopes.** The following are deliberately **not** claimed,\n  as general results or as special cases of the theorems above: general\n  compressible entropy systems in several space dimensions; rough-wall\n  traces and boundary-layer asymptotics; nonlinear constitutive partial\n  differential equations beyond the stated branches;\n  turbulence universality or closure; and all-time three-dimensional\n  regularity of the viscous model. The local periodic theorem is local, and\n  [[rem-local-versus-known-open-global-fluid-scope]] records the dated 2026 CMI announcement about the Euclidean problem and the absence of an audited transfer to this unforced periodic model; this page inherits the local-proof boundary without asserting a blanket present-day open status or a global conclusion.\n\n- **Model deductions versus nature.** All results of this page are conditional\n  deductions from each result's explicitly adopted model assumptions and\n  the cited mathematics, subject to its stated hypotheses. The\n  finite-motion momentum deduction uses the kinematic and mass-balance\n  hypotheses of [[pthm-cm17-momentum-equation]] and the momentum-balance\n  postulate [[post-cm17-momentum-balance]]; a constitutive application also\n  adopts its specified branch. The linear elastic deduction\n  [[pthm-cm17-linear-elastic-equation-of-motion]] instead uses\n  [[post-cm17-linear-elastic-constitutive]]: a $C^2$ reference displacement\n  $\\xi:I\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^3$, constant reference density $\\rho_0>0$,\n  the isotropic stress $\\sigma=\\lambda(\\operatorname{tr}e)I_3+2\\mu_s e$\n  with $e=\\tfrac12(D_X\\xi+D_X\\xi^{\\mathsf T})$ and its stated moduli,\n  and the adopted reference balance\n  $\\rho_0\\partial_t^2\\xi=\\rho_0b_{\\mathrm{ref}}+\\operatorname{div}_X\\sigma$.\n  It requires neither a finite motion $X+\\xi$ nor a positive Jacobian,\n  and constant reference density does not assert constant current density.\n  The string results separately adopt the one-dimensional model\n  of [[pthm-cm17-string-wave-solutions]]: uniform linear density\n  $\\rho_\\ell>0$, tension $\\tau_s>0$, and small transverse displacement\n  satisfying $u_{tt}=(\\tau_s/\\rho_\\ell)u_{xx}$ with the specified line or\n  fixed-end data. Its model equation is adopted, not deduced from the\n  three-dimensional constitutive postulates. They do not\n  establish that the model describes any material, and no measurement is used\n  or asserted. Comparing a prediction with an observation tests the model,\n  the boundary data and the apparatus together; agreement is evidence under\n  stated conditions, not a proof of the model, and disagreement may expose a\n  constitutive law, a boundary condition, or an analysis assumption rather\n  than a single principle.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-non-relativistic-classical-mechanics-track.md",
      "order": 145
    },
