            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-mean-value-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-first-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-cm17-chain-limit-of-the-string-model": "physical-result",
            "thm-cm-spring-chain-continuum-limit": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cm-spring-chain-dispersion-relation": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-cyclic-spring-chain": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked comparison in SI-normalized numerical variables of the cyclic mass-spring chain and continuum string on the standing wave sin(x)cos(t), using a physical circumference of 2π m, linear density 1 kg m^{-1}, tension 1 N, and speed 1 m s^{-1}. It computes the exact chain solution and frequency, nodal error, the theorem's nodal and full interpolation bounds at N=100 (both O(a^{3/2}) for fixed positive time, with interpolation remainder Aa^2/8), the sharper O(a^2) nodal and interpolation errors for these standing-wave data, and the dispersion-relation error a^2/12. The normalized results are model calculations, not measurements.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "I. Stewart, Advanced Classical Mechanics (MIT 8.09/8.309 Fall 2014 course text), Section 6.1 (discrete mass-spring chain and continuum elastic-rod derivation; the standing-wave numerical comparison is verified locally)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/d9bac33f6c60b304dc0398e99b327102_MIT8_09F14_full.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Sections 6.2-6.5 (the discrete chain, its dispersion and its continuum limit)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Take the periodic string model of [[pthm-cm17-chain-limit-of-the-string-model]]\nwith physical circumference $L_*=2\\pi\\,\\mathrm m$, linear mass density\n$\\rho_{\\ell,*}=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-1}}$ and tension\n$\\tau_{s,*}=1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}}$. Then\n$c_*:=\\sqrt{\\tau_{s,*}/\\rho_{\\ell,*}}=1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.\nUse reference units $\\ell_*=1\\,\\mathrm m$, $\\tau_*=1\\,\\mathrm s$ and\ndisplacement scale $A_*=1\\,\\mathrm m$. In the equations below, $x,t,L,c$\ndenote numerical values in these units, so $L=2\\pi$, $c=1$, and the\ndimensionless displacement $u=u_{\\mathrm{phys}}/A_*$ has the exact\ntravelling-standing solution with physical wave number $1\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$\nand angular frequency $1\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$\n\n$$u(t,x):=A\\sin x\\cos t\\qquad(A>0),$$\n\nwhere $A$ is dimensionless. Physical displacements and their error bounds are\nobtained by multiplying the displayed values by $A_*$. The normalized density\nand tension values are $\\rho_\\ell=\\rho_{\\ell,*}/(1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-1}})=1$\nand $\\tau_s=\\tau_{s,*}/(1\\,\\mathrm N)=1$; these are numerical values of\ndifferent dimensioned quantities. The normalized field is $2\\pi$-periodic and\nsolves $u_{tt}=u_{xx}$. For $N\\ge2$ put\n$a=2\\pi/N$ and consider the cyclic spring chain of\n[[def-cm-cyclic-spring-chain]] with parameter $c=1$ and spacing $a$, i.e.\nthe normalized node mass is $m=\\rho_\\ell a$ in units of $1\\,\\mathrm{kg}$ and\nthe normalized spring constant is $k_s=\\tau_s/a$ in units of\n$1\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m^{-1}}$. Then:\n\n1. $M=\\sup|\\partial_x^4u|=A$, $M_2=\\sup|\\partial_x^2u|=A$ and the theorem's\n   constant is $C=\\sqrt{2\\pi}A/12\\approx0.20889A$;\n2. the exact chain solution with the sampled data is\n   $q_j(t)=A\\sin(ja)\\cos(\\omega_at)$ with\n   $\\omega_a=2\\sin(a/2)/a$ (positive for $0<a<\\pi$), the exact nodal error is\n   $e_j(t)=A\\sin(ja)[\\cos(\\omega_at)-\\cos t]$, and the theorem's bounds read\n   $\\|e(t)\\|_a\\le Ca^2t^2/2$,\n   $\\max_j|e_j(t)|\\le \\tfrac12Ca^{3/2}t^2$ and\n   $\\sup_x|I_aq-u|\\le\\max_j|e_j|+Aa^2/8$;\n3. at $N=100$ (so $a=2\\pi/100\\approx0.062832$) and $t=1$ the evaluated bounds\n   are $\\|e(1)\\|_a\\le0.0004124A$,\n   $\\max_j|e_j(1)|\\le0.001645A$ and\n   $\\sup|I_aq-u|\\le0.002139A$, while the exact error satisfies\n   $|e_j(1)|\\le A|\\omega_a-1|\\approx0.00016449A$, so the theoretical bound is\n   verified and visibly conservative;\n4. the exact dispersion relation is\n   $\\omega_a^2=(4/a^2)\\sin^2(a/2)$, and the theorem's bound\n   $|\\omega_a^2-1|\\le a^2/12$ gives $\\le0.00032899$ at $N=100$, while the\n   exact value satisfies $|\\omega_a^2-1|\\approx0.00032894$.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm17-riemann-shock-and-rarefaction-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A Riemann shock and a rarefaction of the scalar model: explicit weak solutions, entropy coefficients and initial trace",
          "reference": "batch-33 B page; constructed from the A-page statements",
          "design_state": "authored; step-1 readiness recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "deps": [
