          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "cor-mean-value-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-cm17-fluid-constitutive": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-cm17-momentum-equation": "physical-result",
            "pthm-cm17-continuity-equation": "physical-result",
            "thm-cm-plane-couette-and-poiseuille-flows": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-fluid-constitutive-models": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm01-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked steady plane channel problem of the adopted incompressible Newtonian branch: the Poiseuille profile with pressure gradient 6, height 2 and viscosity 3 is verified against the model's momentum equation with zero body force, its flux per unit width 4/3 and centre speed 1 are computed, the Couette profile is verified with its nonzero curl, and the no-slip wall conditions are recorded as adopted ideal boundary data. The example reports no measurement.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Section 8.5, Eqs. (8.53)-(8.57) (Newtonian equation, plane Couette flow and pressure-driven pipe-flow background; the plane Poiseuille profile is checked locally)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Section 12.2.1, Eqs. (12.57)-(12.60) (Newtonian stress and incompressible Navier-Stokes equation; the plane channel profiles are checked locally)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Adopt the incompressible Newtonian branch of [[post-cm17-fluid-constitutive]]\nwith constant density $\\rho>0$, dynamic viscosity $\\eta=3\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}$ and channel height $h=2\\,\\mathrm m$, whose SI coordinate domain is $O=\\mathbb R\\times(0,2)\\times\\mathbb R$ with\ncoordinates $(x,y,z)$, in the steady description and with no body force, and explicitly adopt the steady incompressible model equation\n$\\rho(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\nabla p+\\eta\\Delta u$ together with\n$\\operatorname{div}u=0$ ([[def-cm-fluid-constitutive-models]]). Here displayed coordinate profiles and numbers are SI numerical values: $v$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, the flux per width has units $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$, $x,y,z$ are in metres and $p$ is in pascals. Deriving the adopted differential equation from integral balances would additionally require the mass and force premises of [[pthm-cm17-momentum-equation]]; those are not inferred from a constitutive law alone. For a $C^1$ spatial field $w=(w_1,w_2,w_3)$, define $\\operatorname{curl}w:=(\\partial_yw_3-\\partial_zw_2,\\partial_zw_1-\\partial_xw_3,\\partial_xw_2-\\partial_yw_1)$; **irrotational** means this vector vanishes, equivalently the spatial derivative matrix is symmetric. Then:\n\n1. **(Poiseuille.)** With pressure gradient $G=6\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,m^{-1}}$, i.e.\n   $p(x)=p_0-6x$, the profile\n   $u=(v(y),0,0)$, $v(y)=y(2-y)$ (the value $Gy(h-y)/(2\\eta)$ of the general\n   formula with $h=2,\\eta=3$) satisfies $\\operatorname{div}u=0$ and\n   $\\eta v''(y)=-6=-G$, hence the steady momentum equation with no body\n   force; it satisfies the no-slip data $v(0)=v(2)=0$; its flux per unit\n   width is $\\int_0^2v(y)\\,dy=4/3$ and its centre speed is $v(1)=1$.\n2. **(Couette.)** With constant pressure and wall speed $V=2\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, the profile\n   $v(y)=Vy/2=y$ satisfies the same equations with zero body force and the\n   boundary data $v(0)=0$, $v(2)=2$; its velocity gradient\n   $D u=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\\\0&0&0\\\\0&0&0\\end{pmatrix}$ is not symmetric, so\n   the flow is not irrotational: $\\operatorname{curl}u=(0,0,-V/h)=(0,0,-1)$.\n3. **(Uniqueness in the ansatz.)** Within the ansatz\n   $u=(v(y),0,0)$, $p=p_0-Gx$, the difference of two profiles with the same\n   $G$ and the same no-slip data satisfies $w''=0$ with $w(0)=w(2)=0$, hence\n   $w\\equiv0$: the Poiseuille profile is the unique steady solution in this\n   class with those data.\n4. **(Reading.)** The wall conditions are **adopted ideal boundary data**:\n   the model is used with the no-slip assumption $v=0$ at the fixed walls,\n   which is the idealization recorded in [[post-cm17-fluid-constitutive]];\n   the profiles are exact solutions of the model and are not approximations\n   within it. Dimensions: $[G]=\\mu\\ell^{-2}\\tau^{-2}$, so\n   $[Gh^3/\\eta]=\\ell^2\\tau^{-1}$ (flux per unit width) and\n   $[Gh^2/\\eta]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ (speed), consistently with\n   [[def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units]], [[def-cm01-units]].",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm17-periodic-shear-exact-solution-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Periodic shear fields: an exact steady Euler solution, failure for the viscous branch, and the classification of steady periodic shears",
          "reference": "batch-33 B page; constructed from the A-page statements",
          "design_state": "authored; step-1 readiness recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 16,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-cm17-incompressible-periodic-local-evolution",
            "post-cm17-fluid-constitutive",
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection",
