            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-cm17-incompressible-periodic-local-evolution": "physical-result",
            "post-cm17-fluid-constitutive": "physical-assumption",
            "def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-fluid-constitutive-models": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm17-continuum-body-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Worked checks on the periodic cell of the adopted fluid branch: the sinusoidal shear is verified to be an exact steady solution of the inviscid (Euler) branch with zero pressure and to fail the local existence theorem's projected equation for the viscous branch; among classical steady periodic shears with a C^3 velocity profile and C^2 pressure, the viscous branch with no body force admits only constant profiles. The example uses the periodic-cell idealization and reports no measurement.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Section 12.3 (incompressible inviscid fluid and simple exact solutions)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Classical Mechanics (September 1, 2026 complete volume), Sections 8.4-8.5 (ideal fluid solutions and viscous shear flows)",
                "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/CM%20merged.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the cited periodic Sobolev and local-evolution suppliers.\n\nUse the dimensionless variables of [[post-cm17-fluid-constitutive]] clause (c): $t,x,u$ in this example denote the scaled time, unit-torus position and velocity, $\\nu=\\eta/(\\rho U_*L_*)$, and $p=P_{\\mathrm m}/(\\rho U_*^2)$. The physical fields are restored by $u_{\\mathrm{phys}}=U_*u(t/T_*,x/L_*)$, $P_{\\mathrm m}=\\rho U_*^2p(t/T_*,x/L_*)$. Work on the periodic cell $\\mathbb T^3=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^3$ of\n[[post-cm17-fluid-constitutive]] clause (c), with constant density $\\rho>0$,\nno body force and the stress of the branch. The variables and field values here\nare dimensionless. Let $A>0$ be a dimensionless velocity amplitude and\n$\\varphi\\in\\mathbb R$ a dimensionless phase,\nand\n\n$$u(t,x):=\\bigl(A\\sin(2\\pi x_2+\\varphi),0,0\\bigr).$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. $\\operatorname{div}u=0$ and $(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=0$, so with $p\\equiv0$ the\n   field is an exact steady solution of the **Euler** branch ($\\nu=0$) of the\n   model on the periodic cell: $\\partial_tu+P((u\\cdot\\nabla)u)=\\nu\\Delta u$\n   holds as $0=0$, and the primitive equation\n   $\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\nabla p+\\nu\\Delta u$ holds with $p\\equiv0$;\n2. the field satisfies the hypotheses of\n   [[pthm-cm17-incompressible-periodic-local-evolution]]: it is smooth,\n   divergence-free, periodic and has finite $H^s$ norm ($s\\ge4$), and the\n   displayed global solution is consistent with the local\n   existence-uniqueness statement;\n3. for the **viscous** branch ($\\nu>0$) the field is **not** a solution:\n   $\\Delta u=-(2\\pi)^2u\\ne0$ and $P\\Delta u=\\Delta u$ because $u$ is\n   divergence-free, so the projected equation would require\n   $-\\nu\\cdot(2\\pi)^2u=0$, which fails for $A>0$;\n4. among classical periodic shears $u=(v(x_2),0,0)$ with\n   $v\\in C^3(\\mathbb T)$ and periodic pressure $p\\in C^2(\\mathbb T^3)$,\n   every steady solution of the viscous branch with no body force and constant\n   density is **constant**: the dimensionless equations force\n   $\\nabla p=\\nu(v''(x_2),0,0)$, and periodicity of $p$ in $x_1$ together with\n   periodicity of $v$ in $x_2$ forces $v''\\equiv0$ and then\n   $v\\equiv\\mathrm{const}$.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-cm17-traction-symmetry-without-stress-matrix",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "FALSE: the reversed-normal convention alone makes a continuous traction field a stress matrix. The field t(n)=|n_1|n refutes it",
          "reference": "batch-33 B page; constructed from the A-page statements",
          "design_state": "authored; step-1 readiness recorded in the batch notes",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-traction-and-stress",
            "def-matrix-space",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-continuity-real",
            "thm-algebra-of-limits",
            "lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-traction-and-stress": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-limits": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Abeyaratne, Continuum Mechanics, Volume II of Lecture Notes on the Mechanics of Solids (MIT, 1988, revised 2020), Section 4.3 (Cauchy's hypothesis and the need for force balance in the tetrahedron argument)",
                "url": "https://web.mit.edu/abeyaratne/Volumes/RCA_Vol_II.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 12.005, Applications of Continuum Mechanics, Lecture 3 (Cauchy tetrahedron and Cauchy's formula t = sigma n)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-005-applications-of-continuum-mechanics-to-earth-atmospheric-and-planetary-sciences-spring-2006/5005ac6aec16d031bffbd361c792ca70_lec3.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "The false statement is: *every continuous traction field\n$t:S^2\\to\\mathbb R^3$ satisfying the reversed-normal convention\n$t(-n)=-t(n)$ for every $n\\in S^2$ is represented by a matrix\n$\\sigma\\in M_3(\\mathbb R)$ with $t(n)=\\sigma n$ for every $n$.*\n\nThe field constructed below is continuous and satisfies the convention, and\ninherits Cauchy's hypothesis (it depends on the surface only through the unit\nnormal), but no matrix represents it. The refutation shows that the balance\nhypothesis of the Cauchy-tetrahedron theorem is essential: symmetry of the\ntraction in the normal alone is not enough.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-non-relativistic-classical-mechanics-track.md",
      "order": 146
    },
    {
      "id": "cm18-measurement-uncertainty-and-later-bridges",
      "title": "Measurement, uncertainty and later bridges",
      "category": "non-relativistic-classical-mechanics",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "cm18-measurement-uncertainty-and-later-bridges-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "cm-math-evidence",
        "cm-math-numerical",
        "cm03-momentum-work-and-energy-balances",
        "cm01-framework-quantities-and-geometry",
        "cm02-forces-initial-data-and-newtonian-flow",
