          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e17",
          "design_state": "Fully authored draft; complete local authoring for batch 34 of run physics-cm-qm-gr-79-2026-10-11; independent native review remains pending",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "physical_scope": "Repeated-observation protocols with a scalar readout for an operational observable model of the force branch: a repetition count, one error random element per repetition on a common probability space, the resulting observations, and the adopted IID and second-moment hypotheses on the repeated preparation. The item records what the sampling hypotheses assume about repetitions and what follows for the sample mean by the cited mathematical supplier; it does not assert that any physical sequence of repetitions satisfies the hypotheses and reports no observed value."
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm18-model-test",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Model testing for the force branch: what a test specifies, what rejection and retention establish, and what is not claimed",
          "statement": "## The ingredients a test must specify\n\nA test of a null set is a rejection region $R$ inside a measurable data space\n$S$ for a specified family $(\\mathbb P_{\\theta})_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}$ of\nprobability measures and a specified null set $\\Theta_{0}\\subseteq\\Theta$;\nits power function is $\\beta(\\theta)=\\mathbb P_{\\theta}(R)$ and its size is\n$\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_{0}}\\beta(\\theta)$\n([[def-cm-statistical-test-and-size]]). The size is computed from the specified\ndata space, family, null set and rejection region; no alternative set and no\nprior distribution enter it. A test at level $\\alpha$ rejects a true null\nwith probability at most $\\alpha$ **under the specified family**.\n\nFor the force branch of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]], a family and a\nrejection region are obtained only after specifying: one inertial frame; an\noperational observable model of [[def-cm18-observable-model]] — parameters,\npreparations, observation times, readout and units — whose prediction map is\ncomputed from the maximal motions supplied by [[pthm-cm02-ivp]]; a\ncalibration datum of [[def-cm18-calibration]] with its reference deviations;\na sampling model of [[def-cm18-sampling-model]] with its adopted IID and\nsecond-moment hypotheses, or an explicitly different dependence model; the\nlaw of the error random element, or an explicit class of laws satisfying declared bounds with the robust size defined as a supremum over that class as well as over $\\Theta_0$; bounds alone do not select a probability family; a null set\n$\\Theta_{0}$ of parameter values; and a rejection region. Until these data\nare fixed, the phrase \"a test of Newton's law\" names no test, and the size\nand power of no test are defined.\n\n## What rejection and retention establish\n\nA rejection is a decision about the specified triple of null family,\napparatus behaviour and sampling assumptions. It is evidence against that\ntriple at the declared size; it is not a proof that a force law, a spacetime,\nor classical mechanics is false, and the realized discrepancy does not by\nitself identify which ingredient failed. A discrepancy may expose the force\nlaw, a wrong or incomplete calibration, a changed preparation, correlated\nerrors violating the sampling hypothesis, or an analysis error; separating\nthese requires further stated models and further data. Conversely, retaining\nthe null is not proof of the null: a test with low power fails to reject\nalternatives it cannot distinguish, and nonrejection alone neither proves nor estimates an effect size. Under a specified family the nonrejection event has parameter-dependent probability $1-\\beta(\\theta)$ and can therefore inform comparisons of effect values; quantification requires that family and an explicit inferential construction.\n\nThe indices of this page attach no probability to a parameter value. The\nlevel of a confidence set is a pre-data infimum of coverage probabilities\n([[def-cm-confidence-set-and-coverage]]); a likelihood value is the density\nof the observed datum at a parameter value, not the probability of the value\n([[def-cm-likelihood-function]]); and a size is a supremum over the null set\nof probabilities of a region. In particular a $p$-value computed from a\nspecified family is not the probability that the tested hypothesis is true,\nas [[rem-cm-inference-and-uncertainty-scope]] records for this mathematical\ninterface. The cited interpretive warnings of the sources state the same\nproperties for statistical tests in general.\n\n## What agreement does not establish\n\nAgreement of a prediction with observations is evidence for the combined\nadopted assumptions — force model, calibration, preparation, sampling model\nand analysis — at the stated uncertainty; it is not a deductive proof of the\nmodel and it does not test \"classical physics\" as a whole, only the specified\nfamily. A finite sample can fail to exhibit a feature that the declared\nsampling model predicts with positive probability per trial; such a sample is\nnot by itself a refutation of the model, and the elementary probability of\nthat outcome under the sampling model is part of the specification, not an\nempirical result.\n\nNo datum, no rejection, no retention, no $p$-value, no effect size and no\nmeasured uncertainty is asserted anywhere in this item.",
          "strategy": "Scope and status remark; no proof supplied.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-statistical-test-and-size",
            "def-cm-confidence-set-and-coverage",
            "def-cm-likelihood-function",
            "def-cm18-observable-model",
            "def-cm18-calibration",
            "def-cm18-sampling-model",
            "rem-cm-inference-and-uncertainty-scope",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-statistical-test-and-size": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-confidence-set-and-coverage": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-likelihood-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm18-observable-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm18-calibration": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cm18-sampling-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "rem-cm-inference-and-uncertainty-scope": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-cm02-ivp": "physical-result"
          },
          "dependency_level": 13,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. Greenland, S. J. Senn, K. J. Rothman, J. B. Carlin, C. Poole, S. N. Goodman and D. G. Altman, Statistical tests, P values, confidence intervals, and power: a guide to misinterpretations, European Journal of Epidemiology 31 (2016), 337-350, sections on uncertainty and statistical significance and on the interpretation of P values",
                "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4877414/"
              },
              {
                "title": "NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods, section 7.1.3, What are statistical tests?, on the components of a statistical test including the null hypothesis, the test statistic and the critical region",
                "url": "https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section1/prc13.htm"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. Wasserman, All of Statistics: A Concise Course in Statistical Inference (Springer, 2004), Chapter 10 (hypothesis testing and p-values) and the warnings in Sections 10.2 and 11.1; full text copy at the cited URL",
                "url": "https://egrcc.github.io/docs/math/all-of-statistics.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "reference": "expansion-proofs.md#e17",
          "design_state": "Fully authored draft; complete local authoring for batch 34 of run physics-cm-qm-gr-79-2026-10-11; independent native review remains pending",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "physical_scope": "A scope remark on the logic of testing a force model against data: the ingredients a test must specify, the meaning of a rejection or a retention under a specified model family and sampling model, the distinction between evidence and proof, and the reasons a discrepancy does not by itself identify a failing ingredient. The item reports no measurement, asserts no p-value, no effect size and no empirical conclusion, and makes no claim that any model or law has been tested or confirmed."
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm18-numerical-error",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Numerical error in a prediction pipeline: certified velocity-Verlet bounds, additive per-step error, the analytic long-time branch, and the separation from model error",
          "statement": "## Model error and numerical error are different\n\nThe prediction map of an operational observable model\n([[def-cm18-observable-model]]) is defined from the exact maximal motions of\nthe force model. A numerical pipeline replaces those motions by a computed\nsequence, and at least five distinct sources of discrepancy must be kept\napart. A **model-to-system discrepancy** can be defined only after an\napplication supplies a reconstructed physical trajectory $\\chi:J\\to Q$ in\nthe same frame and a metric $d$ on $Q$: for a model motion $x:J\\to Q$, one\npossible pointwise discrepancy is $t\\mapsto d(x(t),\\chi(t))$. This\nframework supplies neither $\\chi$ nor $d$ and proves no bound on that\ndiscrepancy. The **discretization error** is the difference between a\ncomputed sequence and the exact flow of the same model; the\n**implementation error** is the per-step residual of the arithmetic\nactually performed; the **observation error** is $\\varepsilon$ of the\nprotocol; and the **sampling error** is the fluctuation of an estimator such\nas $\\overline Y$ around its model mean in [[def-cm18-sampling-model]], under\nits adopted dependence and moment hypotheses. Only the discretization and\nimplementation errors admit the certified statements below, and those\nstatements are always about the model.\n\n## Finite-time discretization error of velocity Verlet\n\nFix a splitting $H=A+B$ of a mechanical Hamiltonian and the velocity-Verlet\nmap $V_{h}=K_{h/2}\\circ D_{h}\\circ K_{h/2}$ of\n[[def-cm-splitting-hamiltonian-and-verlet-map]], and let $\\Phi_{t}$ be the\nexact flow of the system ([[def-cm-local-flow-of-an-autonomous-system]]).\nUnder the hypotheses of [[thm-cm-verlet-finite-time-error-is-quadratic]] — a\nbounded convex tube with the local truncation constants $h_{1},C_{1}$ of\n[[lem-cm-verlet-local-truncation-error-is-cubic]], a compact set $K_{1}$\ninside the tube, a step $0<\\lvert h\\rvert\\le h_{1}$ and $n\\lvert h\\rvert\\le\nT$, with the computed iterates $z_{j}=V_{h}^{j}(z_{0})$ and the exact flow\npoints $y_{j}=\\Phi_{jh}(z_{0})$ both retained in $K_{1}$ — the finite-time\nbound\n$$\\lVert z_{n}-y_{n}\\rVert\\le C_{1}h^{2}\\frac{e^{LT}-1}{L}\\quad(L>0), \\qquad \\lVert z_{n}-y_{n}\\rVert\\le C_{1}Th^{2}\\quad(L=0)$$\nholds with $L$ the sup-norm bound on the derivative of the Hamiltonian\nfield over the tube. The hypothesis that the iterates remain in the compact\nset is part of the statement; no first-exit claim is made here.\n\n## Additive per-step error\n\nThe implementation error is not analysed as floating-point arithmetic.\nInstead, fix a metric state set $(X,d)$, for example a subset of phase space with the Euclidean distance, on which every Verlet stage is defined and $V_h:X\\to X$. Require a positive step $h>0$, a separately established map bound $d(V_h(x),V_h(y))\\le(1+L_{\\mathrm{map}}h)d(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in X$, $L_{\\mathrm{map}}\\ge0$, and computed points $x_j\\in X$ with $x_0=z_0$ and $d(x_{j+1},V_h(x_j))\\le\\eta$. The exact scheme iterates are $z_{j+1}=V_h(z_j)$. [[lem-cm-roundoff-accumulation-in-one-step-schemes]] then gives\n$$d(x_n,z_n)\\le\\eta\\frac{(1+L_{\\mathrm{map}}h)^n-1}{L_{\\mathrm{map}}h}\\le\\eta e^{L_{\\mathrm{map}}T}\\frac{T}{h}\\qquad(nh\\le T,\\ L_{\\mathrm{map}}>0),$$\nwith $d(x_n,z_n)\\le n\\eta\\le\\eta T/h$ if $L_{\\mathrm{map}}=0$. For a readout $F:X\\to\\mathbb R$ with $|F(x)-F(y)|\\le\\Lambda d(x,y)$, multiply these bounds by $\\Lambda$. The constant $L_{\\mathrm{map}}$ is a bound for the numerical map, not the exact-field derivative constant $L$ of the preceding section; that flow bound does not establish the map hypothesis.\nA Lipschitz readout of the protocol therefore converts a trajectory bound\ninto a bound on the difference of the computed and exact prediction maps by multiplication with its Lipschitz constant, after combining discretization and implementation bounds by the triangle inequality on a common admissible set; a multi-time readout requires a bound in the declared product-state metric. The number $\\eta$ is a\n**hypothesis on the per-step residual**, not a measurement of an arithmetic\nunit, and the lemma says nothing about the distribution of rounding.\n\n## The analytic long-time branch\n\nFor analytic splitting data on a complex neighbourhood, with the domain\nradius, step-size restriction $0<h\\le h_{0}$ and compact retention of the\niterates through the stated time window, there are constants $C,c>0$ with\n$$|H_{h}(z_{n})-H_{h}(z_{0})|\\le Cnh\\,e^{-c/h},\\qquad |H(z_{n})-H(z_{0})|\\le Ch^{2}\\quad\\text{when }nh\\le e^{c/(2h)},$$\nwhere $H_{h}$ is the finite optimally truncated modified Hamiltonian of\n[[thm-cm-analytic-verlet-energy-on-exponential-times]]. Adding the\nimplementation error, [[lem-cm-separate-roundoff-energy-accumulation]] gives\n$$|H(x_{n})-H(x_{0})|\\le 2C_{E}h^{2}+Cnh\\,e^{-c/h}+C_{G}n\\eta$$\nfor computed points with per-step residual at most $\\eta$. Both statements\nkeep every hypothesis of the cited items, including the small-step bound,\nthe holomorphy domain and the retention of iterates in the compact set; the\nfirst is a discrete-time energy estimate, not an estimate of the original\ntrajectory on exponentially long times.\n\n## What is and is not certified\n\nThe bounds above compare the computed sequence with the exact motion of the\nsame model. They are silent about the difference between the model and a\nphysical system, and they do not certify any apparatus. Exact\nsymplecticity of the Verlet map is a statement about exact arithmetic\n([[rem-cm-scope-of-the-certified-verlet-estimates]]); the additive residual\nhypothesis is the only interface through which the arithmetic enters, and\nrounded symplecticity is not claimed. Consequently a numerically computed\ntrajectory, fit or error budget is a computation on the model under the\nstated hypotheses, not an experiment, not a measurement, and not evidence\nfor the model. The constants $C_{1},L,L_{\\mathrm{map}},h_{1},C,C_{E},C_{G},c$ are data of\nthe cited statements; no numerical value of any of them for a physical\nsystem is asserted here.",
          "strategy": "Scope and status remark; no proof supplied.",
