          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "physical_scope": "A scope remark on the numerical phase of a prediction pipeline for an operational observable model of the force branch: the certified finite-time discretization and additive per-step error bounds for the velocity-Verlet method, the analytic long-time energy estimates under their holomorphy, step-size and compact-retention hypotheses, and the strict separation of these numerical bounds from any separately defined model-to-system discrepancy and the observation and sampling errors. The item asserts no numerical result for a physical system and no all-time accuracy of any method."
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-cm18-galilean-regime",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Galilean regime of the framework: frame-relative protocols, the absence of an intrinsic slow-motion parameter, and the empirical status of a validity claim",
          "statement": "## The adopted kinematic regime\n\nThe spacetime of the framework is the affine event space with its surjective\nabsolute-time covector, its three-dimensional Euclidean spatial displacement\nspace and its fixed orientation ([[post-cm01-spacetime]]); it contains no\ninner product on the full displacement space, no light cones, no null\ndisplacements and no universal speed bound. The frames are the adapted\naffine charts of these data, and the allowed changes between them are the\nGalilean transformations $t'=t+b$, $x'=Rx+vt+a$ with $R\\in SO(3)$\n([[post-cm01-frames]]); the resulting kinematic laws, the\nframe-independence of vanishing coordinate acceleration and the group\nstructure of the changes are the content of\n[[pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics]]. The force model transforms by its own\ndeclared covariance rule ([[post-cm02-force-superposition]]), and the\nexistence and uniqueness statements for maximal motions are stated in the\nchosen frame ([[pthm-cm02-ivp]]).\n\n## Protocols are frame-relative\n\nAn operational observable model ([[def-cm18-observable-model]]) is specified\nin one inertial frame: its observation times are times of that frame, its\nreadout is a function of the coordinate states, and its error random element\ncarries the units of the readout coordinates. Changing the frame shifts the\ncoordinate times, transforms positions affinely and rotates and boosts\ncoordinate velocities, so the same physical protocol has a different\nprediction map in a different frame. A measurement model must therefore name\nits frame, and a claim that two frames \"agree\" is a claim about the declared\ntransformation law of the model, not a separate empirical discovery.\n\n## No intrinsic slow-motion parameter\n\nThe dimension group of the framework is generated by length, duration and\nmass ([[def-cm01-units]] clause (a)); the framework contains no quantity of\ndimension $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ that is a constant of nature. Consequently the\nframework cannot by itself form a dimensionless parameter that measures how\nclose a system is to some other kinematic regime, and it states no numerical\ncutoff: every regime statement such as \"the motion is adequately described\nby this model at the required accuracy\" is an **external, empirically\nqualified premise** of a use of the model, not a theorem of it. Quantifying\nthe error of such a statement would require a distinct model carrying a\nspeed scale together with a limit or approximation theorem relating the two\nmodels; no such theorem is asserted, used or presupposed here.\n\n## What is not claimed\n\nThe framework distinguishes no inertial frame ([[rem-cm01-model-limit]]);\n\"at rest\" is a frame-relative description, not an absolute state. No\nmeasured speed, no numerical validity boundary, no comparison with any other\nkinematic framework, and no claim that a physical system is exactly\nGalilean is made. The Galilean transformations are exact symmetries of the\nadopted model by [[pthm-cm01-galilean-kinematics]]; whether a physical\nsituation is adequately represented by the model is an empirical question\nthat this item leaves open.",
