          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "physical_scope": "A worked, explicitly hypothetical parameter-estimation calculation for a separately declared constrained pendulum model: a data-reduction map uses the adopted small-angle period relation 4 pi^2 l / T^2 to estimate a gravitational parameter from declared numerical length and period inputs. This is not a calibration of an apparatus and does not instantiate the force-branch observable model, which this framework defines separately. The item computes the first and second derivatives of the data-reduction map, the exactly propagated covariance of its linearization, and a bounded nonlinearity remainder on a declared box; it asserts no measured length, period, parameter value or uncertainty."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-cm18-uniform-motion-sampling-check",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Repeated observation of a uniform motion: prediction map, adopted IID sampling hypotheses and the resulting sample-mean bound on declared data",
          "statement": "**The declared model.** In the force branch [[post-cm02-force-superposition]]\ntake $N=1$ label of mass $m>0$, time window $I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration\ndomain $Q=\\mathbb R^{3}$ and the **zero force map** $F(t,x,v):=0$. It is\ncontinuous and state-Lipschitz with constant $0$, so it satisfies the\nstanding hypotheses of the branch. With $e_{1}$ the first standard basis vector, let $\\Theta=Z=\\mathbb R$,\nwith both labels read in velocity units $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$\n([[def-cm01-units]]). Use the constant family $F_\\theta=0$ for every\n$\\theta\\in\\Theta$ and the preparation map\n$\\zeta:Z\\to\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3$,\n$\\zeta(z):=(0,0,z e_1)$ at time $t_0=0$.\nThen $x_{\\theta,z}(t)=(zt,0,0)$ is the global motion for every pair.\nFix an observation time $t_1>0$ and the position readout\n$R(\\theta,z,(x,w)):=x\\cdot e_1$, so the full prediction map is\n$\\Phi(\\theta,z)=z t_1$. For $v\\in\\mathbb R$ choose the diagonal pair\n$(\\theta,z)=(v,v)$ and abbreviate $x_v:=x_{v,v}$ and\n$\\Phi(v):=\\Phi(v,v)=x_v(t_1)\\cdot e_1$.\nThis explicit choice permits a one-label formula without identifying the\nparameter argument with the preparation argument of\n[[def-cm18-observable-model]]. The readout has dimension $\\ell$ and is read\nin metres; the force family does not depend on its parameter label.\n\n**The motion.** The curve $x_{v}(t):=(vt,0,0)$, $t\\in\\mathbb R$, is a\nmotion of the declared model with datum $\\zeta(v)$: its coordinate velocity\nis $w_{v}(t)=(v,0,0)$ ([[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]]), its coordinate\nacceleration vanishes, and $m\\ddot x_{v}=0=F$ identically\n([[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]). Hence the prediction map is\n$\\Phi(v)=v\\,t_{1}$, of dimension $\\ell$ with $[t_{1}]=\\tau$.\n\n**The repeated protocol.** Fix $n\\ge1$ and adopt the IID sampling hypothesis\nand the second-moment hypothesis of [[def-cm18-sampling-model]] for the\nerrors $\\varepsilon_{1},\\dots,\\varepsilon_{n}$ of $n$ repetitions of the\nprotocol at the fixed pair $(\\theta,z)=(v,v)$, on a common\nprobability space. Suppose additionally that $E[\\varepsilon_{1}]=0$ and\n$\\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon_{1})=\\sigma^{2}\\in[0,+\\infty)$; these two\nstatements are extra model hypotheses, not part of either definition. Then\nthe observations are $Y_{k}=v t_{1}+\\varepsilon_{k}$ and\n$$\\mu:=E[Y_{1}]=v\\,t_{1},\\qquad \\sigma^{2}=\\operatorname{Var}(Y_{1}),\\qquad \\overline Y=\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^{n}Y_{k}.$$\nConsequently, by [[lem-cm-finite-iid-sample-mean-bound]],\n$$E\\bigl\\lvert\\overline Y-v t_{1}\\bigr\\rvert^{2}=\\frac{\\sigma^{2}}{n}, \\qquad \\mathbb P\\bigl(\\bigl\\lvert\\overline Y-v t_{1}\\bigr\\rvert\\ge a\\bigr) \\le\\frac{\\sigma^{2}}{n\\,a^{2}}\\quad(a>0).$$\n\n**A declared illustration.** With the declared model data\n$v:=2\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$, $t_{1}:=3\\,\\mathrm s$, $\\sigma^{2}:=4\\,\\mathrm m^{2}$\nand $n:=16$, the nominal prediction is $v t_{1}=6\\,\\mathrm m$ and the bound\nreads $\\mathbb P(\\lvert\\overline Y-6\\,\\mathrm m\\rvert\\ge1\\,\\mathrm m)\\le\\frac{4}{16\\cdot1}=\\frac14$.\nThese numbers are hypothetical illustration of the formula; they are not\nmeasured values, and no apparatus is asserted to realize the protocol.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm18-observable-model",
            "def-cm18-sampling-model",
            "def-cm02-newtonian-flow",
            "post-cm02-force-superposition",
