        {
          "id": "cex-cm18-finite-sample-may-miss-the-signal",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A finite sample can miss a predicted outcome with positive probability: an elementary product-model counterexample",
          "statement": "Let a repeated-observation protocol of [[def-cm18-sampling-model]] satisfy\nits IID sampling hypothesis and its second-moment hypothesis, and let $A$ be\nan event of a single observation with $0<\\mathbb P(A)=p<1$. Then **every\nfinite sample $(Y_{1},\\dots,Y_{n})$ of the protocol contains at least one\noutcome in $A$** for every $n\\ge1$; equivalently, if a finite sample\ncontains no outcome in $A$, the declared sampling model is refuted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
