            "thm-kato-rellich": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-relative-boundedness-with-respect-to-an-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-self-adjoint-operator-generates-a-strongly-continuous-unitary-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), let $d\\ge1$ and work on the complex Hilbert space $H=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$, whose completeness follows from [[thm-riesz-fischer-completeness-of-l-p]] and whose Hilbert-space structure is defined in [[def-hilbert-space]].\n\n(a) For a real measurable finite-valued function $a:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ define $M_af:=af$ on $D(M_a):=\\{f\\in H: af\\in H\\}$. Then $M_a$ is a densely defined self-adjoint operator on $H$ ([[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]], [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]).\n\n(b) Let $c>0$, $b\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $\\mathcal F_2$ be the unitary Plancherel transform with the convention $\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi ix\\cdot\\xi}dx$ on $L^1$ ([[def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn]], [[thm-plancherel]]). Define\n\n$$H_0:=\\mathcal F_2^*\\,M_{c|\\xi|^2+b}\\,\\mathcal F_2,\\qquad D(H_0):=\\{f\\in H:|\\xi|^2\\mathcal F_2f\\in H\\}.$$\n\nThen $H_0$ is self-adjoint, $D(H_0)$ coincides with the Sobolev space $W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ as a set (with equivalent norms, [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], [[thm-fourier-characterisation-of-integer-order-hilbert-sobolev-spaces]]), $H_0\\ge b$ in the quadratic-form sense, and for every Schwartz function $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, $H_0f=-(c/4\\pi^2)\\Delta f+bf$.\n\n(c) If $V:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ is measurable with $\\|V\\|_\\infty<\\infty$ and $H:=H_0+M_V$ with domain $D(H_0)$, then $H$ is self-adjoint on $D(H_0)$, is bounded below, and the assignment $t\\mapsto e^{itH}$ is a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group whose generator is $iH$: the limit $\\lim_{t\\to0}\\frac1t(e^{itH}\\psi-\\psi)$ exists exactly for $\\psi\\in D(H)$ and equals $iH\\psi$ ([[lem-self-adjoint-operator-generates-a-strongly-continuous-unitary-group]]).\n\n(d) *Scope*: the argument uses only boundedness and measurability of $V$; it proves nothing for $V\\propto-1/|x|$, $V\\propto|x|^2$ or the delta interaction, does not identify $H_0$ with any measured free-particle Hamiltonian, and asserts no physical interpretation.",
          "strategy": "Prove the multiplier self-adjointness by testing the adjoint identity on truncated dense sets, conjugate by the unitary Plancherel transform, identify the domain with W^{2,2} via the Fourier Sobolev characterisation, and apply Kato-Rellich with relative bound zero for the bounded perturbation; conclude the unitary group from the self-adjoint generator lemma.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Thm 6.4 p. 135 (Kato-Rellich), Thm 7.8 p. 168 (free Hamiltonian), Thm 5.1-5.2 pp. 123-125 (Stone)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
