          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-non-negative-and-positive-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-spectral-resolution-and-polynomial-spectral-projections": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-functional-calculus-for-a-normal-endomorphism": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-trace-of-ab-equals-trace-of-ba": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-trace-of-an-endomorphism": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-hilbert-adjoint-properties": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-isometry-and-orthogonal-or-unitary-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-orthogonal-projection": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $H$ be a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space of dimension $d\\ge1$ ([[def-hilbert-space]]), with adjoints as in [[def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces]] and the finite trace as in [[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]]. Recall that an endomorphism $F$ is an **effect** when $F\\ge0$ and $I-F\\ge0$, i.e. $0\\le F\\le I$ in the quadratic-form order ([[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]]).\n\n(a) *Definitions.* A finite POVM is a finite family $(F_a)_{a=1}^p$, $p\\ge1$, of effects with $\\sum_aF_a=I$. An **instrument** here is a finite family $(M_{a,r})_{1\\le a\\le p,\\,1\\le r\\le q_a}$ of endomorphisms of $H$, where each $q_a$ is a non-negative integer; its outcome operators and update maps are $F_a:=\\sum_rM_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}$ and $I_a:\\operatorname{End}(H)\\to\\operatorname{End}(H)$, $I_a(R):=\\sum_rM_{a,r}RM_{a,r}^*$, with empty sums equal to zero. It is **normalized** when $\\sum_{a,r}M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}=I$. For a normalized instrument the $F_a$ are its **outcome effects**, as proved in (b); without normalization they need not be effects.\n\n(b) If $(F_a)$ is a finite POVM and $\\rho$ a density operator ([[lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability]]) then $p_a:=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho F_a)\\ge0$ and $\\sum_ap_a=1$. If $(M_{a,r})$ is a normalized instrument then the $F_a$ form a finite POVM, $\\operatorname{tr}I_a(\\rho)=p_a$, each $I_a(\\rho)$ is non-negative, and whenever $p_a>0$ the normalized update $I_a(\\rho)/p_a$ is a density operator.\n\n(c) *Ideal Lüders case.* For an orthogonal projection $P$ ([[def-orthogonal-projection]]) put $M_0=P$, $M_1=I-P$. Then $\\sum_jM_j^*M_j=P+(I-P)=I$, the effects are $F_0=P$, $F_1=I-P$, and $I_j(\\rho)=P\\rho P$ or $(I-P)\\rho(I-P)$.\n\n(d) *Non-determination.* Effects alone do not determine the update: there are two normalized instruments on $\\mathbb C^2$ with the same outcome effects $F_0=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$, $F_1=\\operatorname{diag}(0,1)$ whose updates differ on the same input.",
          "strategy": "Diagonalize ρ in a finite orthonormal eigenbasis, sum the nonnegative quadratic forms (F_a u_n,u_n) to get normalization, and use finite trace cyclicity for the update traces; exhibit two finite instruments with equal effects and different updates for the non-determination clause.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 2 'Spin One-half, Bras, Kets, and Operators' and Chapter 4 'Dirac's Bra and Ket Notation'",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/4de6d044fa9d7e5b8998c5f8ca984a42_MIT8_05F13_Chap_04.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, §1.4 p. 15 and §6.3 pp. 121-128",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "aliases": [
            "nrqm-composites-finite-effects-and-instruments"
          ]
        },
        {
