          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), let $a,b\\ge1$, and let $\\lambda_n$ denote Lebesgue measure on $\\mathcal L(\\mathbb R^n)$ as defined in [[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]] and completed from Borel measure as in [[cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure]]. The product measure $\\lambda_a\\times\\lambda_b$ exists by [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]], and under $\\mathbb R^{a+b}=\\mathbb R^a\\times\\mathbb R^b$, $\\lambda_{a+b}$ is its completion ([[thm-euclidean-lebesgue-measure-is-the-completion-of-the-product-of-lebesgue-measures]]). Write $H_a=L^2(\\mathbb R^a,\\lambda_a)$ and $H_b=L^2(\\mathbb R^b,\\lambda_b)$, with $L^2$ understood as the almost-everywhere quotient of square-integrable measurable functions ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]). Then there is a unique linear isometry\n\n$$\\Theta:H_a\\widehat\\otimes H_b\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^{a+b})$$\n\nwith $\\Theta(f\\otimes g)(x,y)=f(x)g(y)$ for all $f\\in H_a$, $g\\in H_b$ and a.e. $(x,y)$, and $\\Theta$ is onto: it is a surjective linear isometry, hence unitary. Vector-valued or spin factors follow by finite component sums. No identification of non-Euclidean product measures is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Check the inner-product identity on finite sums by Fubini for the L¹ products ff' and gg', extend isometrically, and prove density of the product span by a box-integral argument: sections with vanishing box integrals vanish a.e. by finite-measure uniqueness on the rational-box pi-system.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
