            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "def-improper-integral-at-infinity",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-euclidean-gaussian-fourier-transform-with-two-pi-normalization": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-logarithms-principal-logarithm-and-complex-powers": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-principal-logarithm-is-holomorphic-on-the-slit-plane": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-complex-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-identity-theorem-holomorphic-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-improper-integral-at-infinity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). For $z\\in\\mathbb C$ with $\\operatorname{Re}z>0$ and $n\\ge1$ let\n\n$$G_n(z):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-z|x|^2}\\,dx,$$\n\nwhere $dx$ is product Lebesgue measure and the complex integral is computed from its real and imaginary parts. Equivalently, the absolutely convergent improper multiple integral used here is the cube-truncation limit $\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{[-R,R]^n}e^{-z|x|^2}\\,dx$ of these finite Lebesgue integrals. For $n=1$ it also equals the sum of the two separately convergent one-sided improper Riemann integrals at $0$, computed componentwise ([[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]]); this agreement is proved below. Then:\n\n(a) $G_n(z)$ converges absolutely for every $z$ with $\\operatorname{Re}z>0$, and $G_1$ is holomorphic on the right half-plane $\\Omega:=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:\\operatorname{Re}z>0\\}$ with $G_1'(z)=-\\int_{\\mathbb R}x^2e^{-zx^2}dx$ ([[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]], [[def-complex-domain]]);\n\n(b) with $\\operatorname{Log}$ the principal logarithm and the branch $z^{-1/2}:=\\exp\\bigl(-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Log}z\\bigr)$ on the slit plane ([[def-complex-logarithms-principal-logarithm-and-complex-powers]], [[cor-principal-logarithm-is-holomorphic-on-the-slit-plane]]),\n\n$$G_1(z)=\\sqrt{\\pi}\\,z^{-1/2},\\qquad G_1(z)^2=\\frac{\\pi}{z}\\qquad(z\\in\\Omega),$$\n\nthe value on the positive real axis being $G_1(t)=\\sqrt{\\pi/t}$ for $t>0$;\n\n(c) for every $n\\ge1$ and $z\\in\\Omega$, $G_n(z)=G_1(z)^n=(\\pi/z)^{n/2}$, the power being the branch $(\\pi/z)^{n/2}=\\exp\\bigl(\\tfrac n2(\\log\\pi-\\operatorname{Log}z)\\bigr)$.\n\nIn particular the factor $(1+i\\tau)^{-1/2}$ is defined by this branch for every real $\\tau$, since $\\operatorname{Re}(1+i\\tau)=1>0$. No asymptotic or distributional interpretation is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Reduce the positive-parameter identity to the published Gaussian transform at frequency zero, prove holomorphy of G by dominated convergence of the difference quotients, identify the branch on (0,∞) and extend by the identity theorem on the half-plane domain, then factor the d-dimensional integral by Tonelli.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, §7.2-7.3 pp. 167-172 (Gaussian kernels for the free evolution)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 1 'Wave Mechanics' (Gaussian integrals and free packets)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/61bc31b8d8bf0680c322733910a71aa0_MIT8_05F13_Chap_01.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "aliases": [
            "nrqm-composites-complex-gaussian-continuation"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-composites-momentum-fourier-jacobian",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Momentum Fourier Jacobian: the unitary momentum representation",
          "reference": "inventory.json#nrqm-composites-momentum-fourier-jacobian; proof module M27 (scaffold/mathematical-prerequisites.md)",
          "design_state": "reserved-local-prerequisite; complete-conditional-argument",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "thm-plancherel",
            "def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn",
            "thm-dominated-convergence",
            "thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure",
            "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
            "lem-smooth-compactly-supported-functions-are-dense-in-ltwo-of-an-open-set",
            "thm-extension-of-a-bounded-map-from-a-dense-subspace",
            "thm-riesz-fischer-completeness-of-l-p",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions",
            "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-plancherel": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-smooth-compactly-supported-functions-are-dense-in-ltwo-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extension-of-a-bounded-map-from-a-dense-subspace": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riesz-fischer-completeness-of-l-p": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), let $d\\ge1$ and $\\hbar>0$. Write $\\mathcal F_2$ for the unitary Plancherel transform extending $\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f(x)e^{-2\\pi ix\\cdot\\xi}dx$ ([[def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn]], [[thm-plancherel]]); the $L^2$ spaces are Hilbert spaces by [[thm-riesz-fischer-completeness-of-l-p]] and [[def-hilbert-space]]. For $\\psi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, denote by $\\widehat\\psi$ the continuous representative of $\\mathcal F_2\\psi$ given by that absolutely convergent integral.\n\n(a) For $\\psi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ the momentum wavefunction\n\n$$\\psi_p(p):=(2\\pi\\hbar)^{-d/2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}e^{-ip\\cdot x/\\hbar}\\psi(x)\\,dx$$\n\nis well defined, bounded and continuous, and $\\psi_p(p)=(2\\pi\\hbar)^{-d/2}\\widehat\\psi\\bigl(p/(2\\pi\\hbar)\\bigr)$ for every $p$; here $\\widehat\\psi$ is the stated continuous representative, not a point evaluation of an $L^2$ equivalence class.\n\n(b) The dilation operator $D_\\hbar g(p):=(2\\pi\\hbar)^{-d/2}g\\bigl(p/(2\\pi\\hbar)\\bigr)$ is a surjective linear isometry of $L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ onto itself, with inverse $D_\\hbar^{-1}g(\\xi)=(2\\pi\\hbar)^{d/2}g(2\\pi\\hbar\\,\\xi)$.\n\n(c) The formula $U_0\\psi:=\\psi_p$ defines a linear isometry $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^d)$ (for the Lebesgue spaces $dx$ and $dp$) that extends uniquely to a surjective linear isometry $U:L^2(\\mathbb R^d,dx)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^d,dp)$; on Schwartz functions $U=D_\\hbar\\mathcal F_2$, and for every $\\psi\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^d)$,\n\n$$\\|U\\psi\\|_{L^2(dp)}=\\|\\psi\\|_{L^2(dx)} .$$\n\nThe normalization statement is: the substitution $\\xi=p/(2\\pi\\hbar)$ carries $d\\xi=(2\\pi\\hbar)^{-d}dp$, and the factor $(2\\pi\\hbar)^{-d/2}$ in $U_0$ is exactly what makes $U$ an isometry with respect to $dp$. No physical identification of $U\\psi$ with a measured momentum distribution is made.",
          "strategy": "Rewrite the momentum integral as the L¹ Fourier transform at ξ=p/(2πℏ), use the dilation/change-of-variable law |det A| of the Fourier transform for the normalization factor, conclude isometry on Schwartz functions, and extend by the bounded-extension theorem.",
          "provenance": {
