          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-nrqm-composites-nuclear-bounded-cyclicity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-trace-class-is-a-two-sided-banach-operator-ideal": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-trace-of-a-positive-operator-is-the-sum-of-its-eigenvalues": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a nonzero complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let $(X,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable space. In this item, a positive trace-class operator means a self-adjoint trace-class operator $T$ with $\\langle Tx,x\\rangle\\ge0$ for every $x\\in H$; a bounded effect means a self-adjoint $F\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ with $0\\le\\langle Fx,x\\rangle\\le\\|x\\|^2$ for every $x\\in H$.\n\nBy an **abstract instrument** on $(X,\\Sigma)$ we mean a map $J:\\Sigma\\to\\mathcal B(\\mathcal S_1(H))$ into bounded linear maps of the trace-class operators such that (i) each $J_B$ is completely positive (every finite amplification $I_{\\mathbb C^n}\\otimes J_B$ preserves positivity on positive trace-class operators); (ii) for pairwise disjoint $B_n$ with union $B$, $J_B(\\rho)=\\sum_nJ_{B_n}(\\rho)$ in trace norm for every trace-class $\\rho$; (iii) $J_X$ is trace preserving.\n\nThen:\n\n(a) for every $B\\in\\Sigma$ there is a unique effect $F_B\\in\\mathcal B(H)$, $0\\le F_B\\le I$ ([[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]]), with $\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(J_B(T)\\bigr)=\\operatorname{tr}(TF_B)$ for every trace-class $T$;\n\n(b) $F_X=I$, and $B\\mapsto F_B$ is weakly countably additive: for pairwise disjoint $B_n$ with union $B$ and all $x,y\\in H$, $\\langle F_Bx,y\\rangle=\\sum_n\\langle F_{B_n}x,y\\rangle$;\n\n(c) for every $B$ and every bounded $A\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ there is a unique bounded operator $J_B^*(A)$ with $\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(J_B(T)A\\bigr)=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(TJ_B^*(A)\\bigr)$ for every trace-class $T$; the map $A\\mapsto J_B^*(A)$ is linear and bounded with $J_B^*(A)\\ge0$ for $A\\ge0$ and $J_X^*(I)=I$;\n\n(d) for a density operator $\\rho$ and $B\\in\\Sigma$ the number $p(B)=\\operatorname{tr}(J_B(\\rho))=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho F_B)$ lies in $[0,1]$, and $p$ is countably additive. Whether an experimental procedure realizes such an instrument is a separate model assumption; the conditional update $J_B(\\rho)/p(B)$ is defined only when $p(B)>0$.\n\nNo universal representation theorem for arbitrary instruments is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Use the trace-class pairing functional T↦tr J_B(T), evaluate it on rank-one maps, obtain a bounded sesquilinear form, apply Riesz representation to get F_B with 0≤F_B≤I, and transfer the identities by linearity and trace-norm continuity.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
