            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-complex-lp-completeness-density-and-inner-product": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $(D,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a $\\sigma$-finite measure space, let $L^2(D,\\mu)$ denote the quotient of square-integrable measurable functions modulo almost-everywhere equality ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]), let $K$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) with unit vectors $e_1,e_2$, let $a,b\\in L^2(D,\\mu)$ be unit vectors, and let $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C$ with $|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1$. Choose measurable representatives of $a,b$; all displayed pointwise expressions are understood almost everywhere, and the integrals below are independent of the representatives. Put\n\n$$\\Psi:=\\alpha\\,a\\otimes e_1+\\beta\\,b\\otimes e_2\\in L^2(D)\\widehat\\otimes K .$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) the reduced positive trace-class weight $\\rho:=\\operatorname{Tr}_KT_{\\Psi,\\Psi}$ on $L^2(D)$ is\n\n$$\\rho=|\\alpha|^2T_{a,a}+|\\beta|^2T_{b,b}+\\alpha\\overline\\beta\\,\\langle e_1,e_2\\rangle\\,T_{a,b}+\\overline\\alpha\\beta\\,\\langle e_2,e_1\\rangle\\,T_{b,a};$$\n\n(b) for every measurable $B\\subseteq D$ with finite measure, writing $P_B$ for the multiplication operator by $\\mathbf 1_B$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho P_B)=\\int_B\\Bigl(|\\alpha|^2|a|^2+|\\beta|^2|b|^2+2\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(\\alpha\\overline\\beta\\langle e_1,e_2\\rangle\\,a(x)\\overline{b(x)}\\bigr)\\Bigr)d\\mu ;$$\n\n(c) the marker overlap contracts the cross term: $|\\langle e_1,e_2\\rangle|\\le1$, and if $e_1\\perp e_2$ the cross term in (b) vanishes, leaving the incoherent weight $|\\alpha|^2|a|^2+|\\beta|^2|b|^2$.\n\nThe weight satisfies $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=\\|\\Psi\\|^2=1+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha\\overline\\beta\\langle a,b\\rangle\\langle e_1,e_2\\rangle)$; it is a density operator precisely when $\\|\\Psi\\|=1$. The stated assumptions do not force this normalization. These are identities for the specified vector and its unnormalized weight; they assert no measured detector law and cover no selective conditioning.",
          "strategy": "Write the rank-one reduced state via the partial-trace pairing identity against T_{f,g}, expand the four products, and read off the bin weights as the diagonal pairing of ρ with the multiplication indicator.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, §6.3 pp. 121-128 (reduced states and marker-induced loss of coherence)",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
