            "def-borel-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a nonzero complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let $E,F$ be **regular** projection-valued measures on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-projection-valued-measure]], [[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]) satisfying the strong commutation relation\n\n$$E(B)F(C)=F(C)E(B)\\qquad\\text{for all Borel }B,C\\subseteq\\mathbb R .$$\n\nThen there is a unique regular projection-valued measure $G$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R^2$ with\n\n$$G(B\\times C)=E(B)F(C)\\qquad\\text{for every Borel rectangle }B\\times C,$$\n\nwhere rectangle means a measurable rectangle in the sense of [[def-measurable-rectangle]]. Its marginals are $G(B\\times\\mathbb R)=E(B)$ and $G(\\mathbb R\\times C)=F(C)$.\n\nFor the scalar construction, let $\\mathcal A_0$ be the algebra of finite unions of Borel rectangles, and define $P_0(\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^n B_i\\times C_i):=\\sum_{i=1}^nE(B_i)F(C_i)$ on disjoint rectangular decompositions. For every $\\psi\\in H$, $\\mu_\\psi(A):=\\langle P_0(A)\\psi,\\psi\\rangle$ is a finite premeasure on $\\mathcal A_0$ and has a unique extension $G_\\psi=\\langle G(\\cdot)\\psi,\\psi\\rangle$ to the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra. For $x,y\\in H$, define the polarized measure $\\nu_{x,y}:=\\frac14\\sum_{k=0}^3 i^kG_{x+i^ky}$. Then every Borel $S\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ has a unique bounded operator $G(S)$ satisfying $\\langle G(S)x,y\\rangle=\\nu_{x,y}(S)$ for all $x,y\\in H$, and $0\\le G(S)\\le I$.",
          "strategy": "Build the finitely additive projection P on the algebra of finite disjoint rectangle unions, prove the scalar set functions are premeasures by compact inner approximation, extend by Carathéodory, polarize, represent each bounded sesquilinear form by Riesz to obtain G(S) with 0≤G(S)≤I, and verify the product identities by uniqueness on rectangles.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Thm 3.1-3.2 pp. 90-92 (projection-valued measures and Borel calculus)",
