          "reference": "inventory.json#nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-block-decomposition; proof module M18 (scaffold/mathematical-prerequisites.md)",
          "design_state": "reserved-local-prerequisite; complete-conditional-argument",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $V$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner product space, with inner product linear in the first\nargument ([[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-dimension]]), and let $J_x,J_y,J_z\\in\\operatorname{End}(V)$ be\nself-adjoint operators satisfying ([[def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism]])\n\n$$[J_x,J_y]=iJ_z,\\qquad [J_y,J_z]=iJ_x,\\qquad [J_z,J_x]=iJ_y .$$\n\nPut $J_+:=J_x+iJ_y$, $J_-:=J_x-iJ_y$ and $J^2:=J_x^2+J_y^2+J_z^2$. Then:\n\n(a) $J_+^*=J_-$, $J_-^*=J_+$, $[J_z,J_\\pm]=\\pm J_\\pm$, $[J_+,J_-]=2J_z$,\n$J^2=J_-J_++J_z^2+J_z=J_+J_-+J_z^2-J_z$, and $J^2$ is self-adjoint, commutes with\n$J_x,J_y,J_z,J_\\pm$, and satisfies $\\langle J^2v,v\\rangle=\\|J_xv\\|^2+\\|J_yv\\|^2+\\|J_zv\\|^2\\ge0$ for every $v\\in V$;\n\n(b) there are an integer $r\\ge1$, numbers $j_1,\\dots,j_r\\in\\frac12\\mathbb N=\\{0,\\frac12,1,\\dots\\}$ with\n$\\sum_{\\nu=1}^r(2j_\\nu+1)=\\dim V$, and an orthonormal basis $(v_{\\nu,k})$, $\\nu\\in\\{1,\\dots,r\\}$,\n$k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots,2j_\\nu\\}$, of $V$ such that\n\n$$J_zv_{\\nu,k}=(j_\\nu-k)v_{\\nu,k},\\quad J_+v_{\\nu,k}=\\sqrt{k(2j_\\nu-k+1)}\\,v_{\\nu,k-1},\\quad J_-v_{\\nu,k}=\\sqrt{(k+1)(2j_\\nu-k)}\\,v_{\\nu,k+1},$$\n\n$$J^2v_{\\nu,k}=j_\\nu(j_\\nu+1)v_{\\nu,k},\\qquad J_xv_{\\nu,k}=\\tfrac12\\bigl(J_++J_-\\bigr)v_{\\nu,k},\\qquad J_yv_{\\nu,k}=\\tfrac1{2i}\\bigl(J_+-J_-\\bigr)v_{\\nu,k},$$\n\nwith $v_{\\nu,-1}:=0$ and $v_{\\nu,2j_\\nu+1}:=0$; each summand $V_\\nu:=\\operatorname{span}\\{v_{\\nu,0},\\dots,v_{\\nu,2j_\\nu}\\}$\nis $J_x,J_y,J_z$-invariant, the decomposition $V=\\bigoplus_{\\nu=1}^rV_\\nu$ is orthogonal, and the map\n$v_{\\nu,k}\\mapsto e_{j_\\nu-k}$ is a unitary equivalence of $(J_x,J_y,J_z)$ on $V_\\nu$ with the spin-$j_\\nu$ matrices\nof [[prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices]];\n\n(c) let $\\lambda$ be the largest eigenvalue of $J_z$ (it exists and is real), and let $u\\ne0$ satisfy\n$J_zu=\\lambda u$. Then $\\lambda\\ge0$, $J_+u=0$, and with $w_k:=J_-^ku$ one has $w_k\\ne0$ for\n$0\\le k\\le2\\lambda$, $w_k=0$ for $k=2\\lambda+1$, $2\\lambda\\in\\mathbb N$, and\n$\\|w_k\\|^2=k!(2\\lambda)(2\\lambda-1)\\cdots(2\\lambda-k+1)\\|u\\|^2$ for $0\\le k\\le2\\lambda+1$; the vectors\n$w_k/\\|w_k\\|$, $0\\le k\\le2\\lambda$, form the block of (b) with $j=\\lambda$; consequently $\\lambda\\in\\frac12\\mathbb N$\nand the largest eigenvalue of $J_z$ on each block is one of the numbers $j_1,\\dots,j_r$;\n\n(d) for every $M\\in\\frac12\\mathbb N$ the number of blocks with $j_\\nu\\in M+\\mathbb Z$ and $j_\\nu\\ge M$ equals\n$\\dim\\ker(J_z-MI)$, and the number of blocks with $j_\\nu=M$ equals\n$\\dim\\ker(J_z-MI)-\\dim\\ker(J_z-(M+1)I)$; in particular the multiset $\\{j_1,\\dots,j_r\\}$ is determined by the\npair $(V,J_z)$ together with the relations, and the spectrum of $J_z$ is the multiset union of the numbers\n$j_\\nu-k$, $0\\le k\\le2j_\\nu$ (a block with parameter $j$ contributes its weights only to the lattice coset\n$j+\\mathbb Z$).\n\nNo physical interpretation of the operators as angular momentum, spin or observables is asserted; the theorem\nis a classification of finite-dimensional triples satisfying the displayed commutation relations.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument via the raising-operator chain argument; see the numbered steps in the item body."
        },
