          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 10.4 pp. 229-235 (lowest Coulomb eigenvalue, simplicity and ground-state formula; angular normalization is proved locally)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics (Cappella Archive), Section 8.1 pp. 186-193 (radial wavefunctions and radial probability densities of hydrogen, and the expectation values for powers of r)",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $\\gamma>0$, let\n$C=-\\Delta-\\gamma/|\\cdot|$ on $D(C)=H^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$ be as above,\nand put $a_0:=2/\\gamma>0$. Define\n$$\\psi_{100}(x):=\\Bigl(\\frac{\\gamma^3}{8\\pi}\\Bigr)^{1/2}e^{-r/a_0}\\quad(x=r\\Omega,\\ r=|x|).$$\nThen:\n\n(a) $\\psi_{100}\\in D(C)$, $\\|\\psi_{100}\\|_{L^2}=1$, and $C\\psi_{100}=-\\frac{\\gamma^2}{4}\\psi_{100}$;\n\n(b) for every Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$, writing $dx$ for Lebesgue measure,\n$$\\int_B|\\psi_{100}(x)|^2dx=\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{\\gamma^3}{8\\pi}r^2e^{-2r/a_0}\\Bigl(\\int_{S^2}\\mathbf1_B(r\\Omega)d\\sigma(\\Omega)\\Bigr)dr,$$\nso the radial probability density $p(r):=\\frac{\\gamma^3}{2}r^2e^{-2r/a_0}=4r^2a_0^{-3}e^{-2r/a_0}$ is a probability density on $(0,\\infty)$: $p\\ge0$ and $\\int_0^\\infty p(r)dr=1$;\n\n(c) the mean of $p$ is $\\int_0^\\infty r\\,p(r)dr=3a_0/2$, and the unique maximum (mode) of $p$ is at $r=a_0$;\n\n(d) the spatial density $|\\psi_{100}(x)|^2=\\frac{\\gamma^3}{8\\pi}e^{-2r/a_0}$ is strictly decreasing in $r$ and maximal at $x=0$. For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is a remainder $R_\\varepsilon$ such that\n$$\n\\int_{|x|<\\varepsilon}|\\psi_{100}(x)|^2\\,dx=\\frac16\\Bigl(\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{a_0}\\Bigr)^3+R_\\varepsilon, \\qquad |R_\\varepsilon|\\le\\frac18\\Bigl(\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{a_0}\\Bigr)^4.\n$$\nThus this probability is positive and tends to $0$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$, and the spatial probability measure has no point atoms, including at the density maximum $x=0$;\n\n(e) *parametrisation and dimensions.* If $\\hbar,m,\\kappa>0$ and $\\gamma:=2m\\kappa/\\hbar^2$, then $a_0=\\hbar^2/(m\\kappa)$ and the operator\n$$\nC_{\\hbar,m,\\kappa}:=-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\Delta-\\frac{\\kappa}{|\\cdot|},\\qquad D(C_{\\hbar,m,\\kappa})=H^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C),\n$$\nhas eigenvalues $-\\frac{m\\kappa^2}{2\\hbar^2n^2}=\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\Bigl(-\\frac{\\gamma^2}{4n^2}\\Bigr)$ ($n\\ge1$); under the dimensional conventions $[\\hbar]=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $[m]=\\mathrm{kg}$, $[\\kappa]=\\mathrm{J\\,m}$, the constants $a_0^2$ and $m\\kappa^2/(2\\hbar^2)$ have the dimensions of $\\mathrm m^2$ and $\\mathrm J$ respectively.\n\nClauses (a)–(d) are mathematical statements about the normalised ground vector\nand its distribution; clause (e) is a dimensional-consistency statement about\nthe parametrisation and is not a physical claim, measurement or identification\nof any quantity with nature.",
