          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 10.1 pp. 221-222 (the one-particle reduction -Laplacian - gamma/|x| with the nucleus fixed at the origin and sigma_ess = [0,infinity))",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics (Cappella Archive), Section 8.1 pp. 182-185 (centre-of-mass motion and the reduced mass in the two-body Coulomb model)",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $m_1,m_2,\\mu,M,\\kappa,\\hbar$\nbe as in [[thm-nrqm-coulomb-two-body-reduced-mass]], let $C_\\mu$ and $T_R$ be\nthe relative and centre-of-mass kinetic operators there, let\n$u_1\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3_r)$ be any nonzero relative ground vector with\n$C_\\mu u_1=E_1u_1$, where $E_1=-\\frac{\\mu\\kappa^2}{2\\hbar^2}$, and let\n$\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3_R;\\mathbb C)$ be a nonzero centre-of-mass\ntest function. Consider the product vector\n$$\\Psi:=\\varphi\\otimes u_1\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3_R)\\widehat\\otimes L^2(\\mathbb R^3_r),$$\nviewed as an element of $L^2(\\mathbb R^6)$ through the identification of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-euclidean-l2-product-unitary]]. This example verifies\nthat:\n\n(a) $(T_R\\otimes I+I\\otimes C_\\mu)\\Psi=E_1\\Psi+(T_R\\varphi)\\otimes u_1$, and $T_R\\varphi\\ne0$, so $\\Psi$ is not an eigenvector of the two-body operator;\n\n(b) $\\langle(T_R\\otimes I+I\\otimes C_\\mu)\\Psi,\\Psi\\rangle=\\|u_1\\|^2(E_1\\|\\varphi\\|^2+\\langle T_R\\varphi,\\varphi\\rangle)>E_1\\|\\Psi\\|^2$: the product vector has energy strictly above the bottom $E_1$ of the two-body spectrum, and the bottom is not attained. When $\\|u_1\\|=1$, the identity reduces to $E_1\\|\\varphi\\|^2+\\langle T_R\\varphi,\\varphi\\rangle$;\n\n(c) the relative bound state $u_1$ is an eigenvector of the relative operator $C_\\mu$, but the two-body operator has no eigenvalues at all ([[thm-nrqm-coulomb-two-body-reduced-mass]] (d)); the relative bound energy is a threshold of the continuous spectrum of the total operator, so \"relative bound state\" and \"bound state of the two-body problem\" are distinct notions.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "inventory.json#nrqm-coulomb-example-relative-versus-total-bound-state; design reservation",
          "design_state": "leaf example reservation; complete-conditional-argument"
        }
