            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-banach-valued-continuous-curves": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-linearity-of-the-bochner-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-bochner-integral-norm-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bochner-integrability-criterion": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "aliases": [
            "nrqm-controlled-dynamics-norm-dyson-series-and-uniqueness"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Chapter 5 pp. 123-131 (quantum dynamics, Stone's theorem) and Chapter 12 pp. 245-256 (scattering theory, Cook's Lemma 12.3)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Quantum Mechanics (University of Texas at Austin, author-hosted course text), Time-Dependent Perturbation Theory pp. 175-182 (perturbation expansion and the two-state system)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/qmech/qmech.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be an arbitrary complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]), $I=[a,b]\\subset\\mathbb R$ a nonempty compact interval, $\\hbar>0$, and $\\mathcal H:I\\to\\mathcal B(H)$ a norm-continuous family of bounded self-adjoint operators. Put $A(t)=-i\\mathcal H(t)/\\hbar$ and $K=\\sup_{t\\in I}\\|A(t)\\|<\\infty$. For every $s,t\\in I$ define $U_0(t,s)=I_H$ and, recursively, the oriented Bochner integrals\n$$U_{n+1}(t,s)=\\int_s^t A(r)U_n(r,s)\\,dr,$$\nwhere $\\int_s^t=-\\int_t^s$ when $t<s$. Equivalently, for $s\\le t$ these are the **latest-time-left** ordered integrals\n$$U_n(t,s)=\\Bigl(\\frac{-i}{\\hbar}\\Bigr)^n\\int_{s<t_1<\\cdots<t_n<t}\\mathcal H(t_n)\\cdots\\mathcal H(t_1)\\,dt_1\\cdots dt_n,$$\nwith the simplex integral meaning the nested integral with $t_n$ outermost and $t_1$ innermost. For arbitrary $s,t$ its unambiguous nested form is $\\int_s^t dr_1\\int_s^{r_1}dr_2\\cdots\\int_s^{r_{n-1}}dr_n\\,A(r_1)\\cdots A(r_n)$. Then:\n\n(a) $\\|U_n(t,s)\\|\\le(K|t-s|)^n/n!$ for all $n\\ge0$ and all $s,t\\in I$.\n\n(b) $U(t,s)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}U_n(t,s)$ converges in operator norm uniformly on $I\\times I$, is jointly norm-continuous, satisfies $U(s,s)=I_H$, and obeys $\\|U(t,s)-I_H\\|\\le e^{K|t-s|}-1$. In particular all original forward-time convergence claims hold.\n\n(c) For every $s,t\\in I$,\n$$U(t,s)=I_H-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\int_s^t\\mathcal H(r)U(r,s)\\,dr.$$\nFor fixed $s$ this is the unique continuous $\\mathcal B(H)$-valued solution on $I$. More generally, the solution with initial value $C\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ at $s$ is $U(t,s)C$. For $a<b$, the operator-norm derivatives are\n$$\\partial_tU(t,s)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\mathcal H(t)U(t,s),\\qquad \\partial_sU(t,s)=+\\frac{i}{\\hbar}U(t,s)\\mathcal H(s),$$\nwith the corresponding one-sided derivatives at the endpoints. Therefore, for every $\\psi\\in H$, $U(t,s)\\psi$ is strongly continuously differentiable in either variable and satisfies the same equations applied to $\\psi$, with the common bounded-generator domain equal to all of $H$.\n\n(d) Every $U(t,s)$ is unitary, $U(t,s)^*=U(s,t)=U(t,s)^{-1}$, and\n$$U(t,s)U(s,r)=U(t,r)\\qquad(r,s,t\\in I).$$\nThese conclusions include both orientations of the time interval and intervals containing positive and negative times. If $a=b$, all terms with $n\\ge1$ vanish and $U(a,a)=I_H$; the derivative assertions then have no nontrivial interval on which to apply.\n\nNo separability hypothesis on $H$, invariant domain for an unbounded operator, differentiability of $\\mathcal H$, spectral theorem or physical interpretation is required.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "reserved claim of the qm-math-controlled-dynamics design reservation; complete-conditional-argument",
          "design_state": "reserved-closure; complete-conditional-argument"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-dyson-truncation-bound",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Dyson truncation bound",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "lem-linearity-of-the-bochner-integral",
