          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "T. Kato, Perturbation Theory for Linear Operators, Chapter II.5 (splitting of a degenerate eigenvalue; Feshbach reduction)",
                "url": "https://sci.mu.edu.iq/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/FUNCTIONAL-ANALYSIS-freebookcenter.net_.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 6.6 pp. 153-159 (analytic perturbation and resolvent identities)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be an arbitrary complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let $H_0$ be self-adjoint on its exact dense domain $D(H_0)$ ([[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]). Use $\\sigma(H_0)$ for the resolvent spectrum ([[def-resolvent-and-spectrum-of-a-closed-unbounded-operator]]). Let $E\\in\\sigma(H_0)$ be isolated, with $\\dim\\ker(H_0-E)=r\\ge1$ and $\\operatorname{dist}(E,\\sigma(H_0)\\setminus\\{E\\})\\ge g>0$. Write $P=E_{H_0}(\\{E\\})$ for its orthogonal spectral projection and $Q=I-P$, using the CC arbitrary-H calculus of [[lem-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-cc-arbitrary-hilbert-spectral-calculus]]. Let $V=V^*\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ with $v=\\|V\\|>0$, and let $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy $|\\lambda|v<g/4$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-operator-norm]]). Put $H_\\lambda=H_0+\\lambda V$ on $D(H_0)$ and assume it is self-adjoint there. Then:\n\n(a) For every real $z$ with $|z-E|+|\\lambda|v\\le g/2$, the operator $T_\\lambda(z)=Q(H_\\lambda-z)Q$ **on $QH$**, with domain $QD(H_0)=D(H_0)\\cap QH$, has a bounded two-sided inverse $B(z):QH\\to QD(H_0)$ with $\\|B(z)\\|\\le4/g$. This includes $z=E$ and the case $QH=\\{0\\}$, with its unique zero-space inverse.\n\n(b) If such $z$ is an eigenvalue of $H_\\lambda$ with eigenvector $\\psi\\ne0$, then $\\varphi=P\\psi\\ne0$, $\\chi=Q\\psi=-\\lambda B(z)QVP\\varphi$, and\n\n$$[EP+\\lambda PVP-\\lambda^2PVQB(z)QVP]\\varphi=z\\varphi.$$\n\n(c) Every such eigenvalue satisfies\n\n$$\\operatorname{dist}(z,E+\\lambda\\operatorname{spec}_{PH}(PVP))\\le\\frac{4\\lambda^2v^2}{g},$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{spec}_{PH}(PVP)$ is the spectrum of the Hermitian operator $(PVP)|_{PH}$ on the finite-dimensional space $PH$. The statement retains all finite multiplicities $r$ and does not identify any eigenvector of $PVP$ with an exact eigenvector of $V$.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "reserved claim of the qm-math-controlled-dynamics design reservation; complete-conditional-argument",
          "design_state": "reserved-closure; complete-conditional-argument"
