            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "aliases": [
            "nrqm-controlled-dynamics-simple-level-reduced-resolvent-coefficients"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 6.6 pp. 153-159 (analytic perturbation series for a simple eigenvalue)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Quantum Mechanics (University of Texas at Austin, author-hosted course text), Non-Degenerate Perturbation Theory pp. 155-156 (first- and second-order energy shifts)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/qmech/qmech.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and adopt the\nhypotheses of [[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-contour-perturbation-projection]]:\n$H_0$ self-adjoint with isolated simple eigenvalue $E$, gap $g>0$, normalized\neigenvector $\\psi_0$ with $P_0\\psi_0=\\psi_0$, $V=V^*\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ with\n$v=\\|V\\|$, and $q=2|\\lambda|v/g<1/2$. Write\n$Q=I-P_0$, $H_Q=H_0|_{D(H_0)\\cap QH}$ on the reducing space $QH$, and define the reduced inverse\n$$S=\\iota_Q(H_Q-E)^{-1}Q:H\\longrightarrow D(H_0)\\cap QH,$$\nwhere $\\iota_Q:QH\\hookrightarrow H$ is the inclusion (and the zero complementary space gives the zero map). Then $S$ is bounded with $\\|S\\|\\le1/g$; no inverse of $H_0-E$ on all of $H$ is asserted. Let $P_\\lambda$, $E_\\lambda$ be the perturbed projection and\nits unique eigenvalue inside the contour, as in\n[[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-contour-perturbation-projection]]. Define the\ngauge-normalized eigenvector\n$$\\psi(\\lambda):=\\frac{P_\\lambda\\psi_0}{\\langle P_\\lambda\\psi_0,\\psi_0\\rangle}.$$\nThen $\\psi(\\lambda)$ is analytic in $\\lambda$ with $\\|\\psi(\\lambda)\\|\\le3$ for\n$q<1/2$, $\\langle\\psi(\\lambda),\\psi_0\\rangle=1$, and $(H_0+\\lambda V)\\psi(\\lambda)=E_\\lambda\\psi(\\lambda)$.\nMoreover the first and second coefficients are\n$$E'(0)=\\langle V\\psi_0,\\psi_0\\rangle,\\qquad \\psi'(0)=-SV\\psi_0,\\qquad E''(0)=-2\\langle VSV\\psi_0,\\psi_0\\rangle,$$\nwhere all pairings are finite and the second derivative involves the whole\ncomplementary spectral subspace, not only bound states. Equivalently\n$E_\\lambda=E+\\lambda\\langle V\\psi_0,\\psi_0\\rangle-\\lambda^2\\langle VSV\\psi_0,\\psi_0\\rangle+O(\\lambda^3)$\nfor $|\\lambda|$ in the disk of the remainder estimate; the $O(\\lambda^3)$ is made\nexplicit in [[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-analytic-perturbation-remainder]].",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "reserved claim of the qm-math-controlled-dynamics design reservation; complete-conditional-argument",
          "design_state": "reserved-closure; complete-conditional-argument"
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-strong-bounded-interaction-picture",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Strong bounded interaction picture",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-common-domain-nonautonomous-evolution",
            "lem-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-banach-valued-continuous-curves",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-bounded-linear-operator",
            "def-operator-norm",
            "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint",
            "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator",
            "lem-bochner-integral-norm-inequality",
            "thm-bochner-integrability-criterion",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-common-domain-nonautonomous-evolution": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-banach-valued-continuous-curves": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-bochner-integral-norm-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bochner-integrability-criterion": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "aliases": [
            "nrqm-controlled-dynamics-strong-bounded-interaction-picture"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 5.1 pp. 123-126 (Stone's theorem and the time evolution) and Section 7.3 pp. 169-171 (time evolution in the free case)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be any complex Hilbert space, let $H_0=H_0^*$ have its\nexact dense domain $D(H_0)$, let $\\hbar>0$, let $I=[a,b]$, and let\n$V:I\\to\\mathcal B(H)$ be strongly continuous with each $V(t)$ self-adjoint\nand $v=\\sup_{t\\in I}\\|V(t)\\|<\\infty$. Put $K=v/\\hbar$ and\n\n$$E(t)=e^{-itH_0/\\hbar},\\qquad V_I(t)=E(-t)V(t)E(t),\\qquad B(t)=-iV_I(t)/\\hbar.$$\n\nThe autonomous group is the CC arbitrary-H construction in [F1]. Then\n$V_I$ is strongly continuous, self-adjoint, and\n$\\|V_I(t)\\|=\\|V(t)\\|\\le v$.\n\nFor $s\\le t$, define bounded operators by **vector strong integrals**:\n\n$$D_n(t,s)\\psi=(-i/\\hbar)^n\\int_{s<t_1<\\cdots<t_n<t}V_I(t_n)\\cdots V_I(t_1)\\psi\\,dt_1\\cdots dt_n,\\qquad D_0(t,s)=I.$$\n\nFor every fixed vector, these are integrals of continuous $H$-valued\nfunctions on a compact simplex (equivalently iterated integrals). They\ndefine a bounded operator by their action on vectors. This is the meaning\nof a strong operator integral; no norm-Bochner integrability into the\nBanach space $\\mathcal B(H)$ is asserted. The series\n$U_I(t,s)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}D_n(t,s)$ converges in operator norm uniformly on\nthe time triangle, with\n\n$$\\|D_n(t,s)\\|\\le\\frac{(K(t-s))^n}{n!},\\qquad \\left\\|U_I(t,s)-\\sum_{n=0}^ND_n(t,s)\\right\\|\\le e^{K(t-s)}\\frac{(K(t-s))^{N+1}}{(N+1)!}.$$\n\nIt extends to all $s,t\\in I$ as a strongly continuous unitary evolution,\nwith genuine inverse $U_I(s,t)=U_I(t,s)^{-1}=U_I(t,s)^*$ and full cocycle.\nFor every $\\psi\\in H$ its first-endpoint map is $C^1$ in $H$, with\n\n$$U_I(t,s)\\psi=\\psi+\\int_s^tB(r)U_I(r,s)\\psi\\,dr,\\qquad \\partial_tU_I(t,s)\\psi=B(t)U_I(t,s)\\psi.$$\n\nThe second endpoint satisfies\n$\\partial_sU_I(t,s)\\psi=-U_I(t,s)B(s)\\psi$. These derivatives are strong\nderivatives; operator-norm differentiability is not claimed.\n\nThe physical propagator\n$U(t,s)=E(t)U_I(t,s)E(-s)$ is unitary, jointly strongly continuous, has\nthe full cocycle and inverse, and satisfies the vectorwise mild identity\n\n$$U(t,s)\\psi=E(t-s)\\psi-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\int_s^tE(t-r)V(r)U(r,s)\\psi\\,dr.$$\n\nFor $\\psi,\\varphi\\in D(H_0)$ the scalar curve is continuously\ndifferentiable, with\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\langle U(t,s)\\psi,\\varphi\\rangle=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\left[\\langle U(t,s)\\psi,H_0\\varphi\\rangle+\\langle V(t)U(t,s)\\psi,\\varphi\\rangle\\right].$$\n\nThe proof actually permits arbitrary initial $\\psi\\in H$ in this weak\nequation. The original statement on $D(H_0)$ is therefore preserved.\nNo invariance of $D(H_0)$ under the physical $U$, and no strong full\nSchrödinger derivative for its domain vectors, is deduced from these\nmere strong-continuity hypotheses. In particular, differentiating\n$E(t)U_I(t,s)E(-s)\\psi$ by an unbounded product rule would require\ndomain membership which has not been established.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "reserved claim of the qm-math-controlled-dynamics design reservation; complete-conditional-argument",
          "design_state": "reserved-closure; complete-conditional-argument"
