          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-finite-gapped-adiabatic-estimate": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-norm-dyson-series-and-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-finite-spin-matrices": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "aliases": [
            "nrqm-controlled-dynamics-example-gap-closing-failure"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Quantum Mechanics (University of Texas at Austin, author-hosted course text), Two-State System pp. 153-155 (level crossing and breakdown of adiabatic tracking)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/qmech/qmech.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "T. Kato, Perturbation Theory for Linear Operators, Chapter II.4 (the uniform-gap hypothesis is essential in the adiabatic theorem)",
                "url": "https://sci.mu.edu.iq/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/FUNCTIONAL-ANALYSIS-freebookcenter.net_.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $\\sigma_z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$ on $\\mathbb C^2$, with orthonormal basis $e_\\uparrow=(1,0)$, $e_\\downarrow=(0,1)$. Let $H(s)=s\\sigma_z$ for $s\\in[-1,1]$, and let $U_T$ solve $iU_T'=TH(s)U_T$, $U_T(-1)=I$, for $T>0$. For $s\\ne0$ define $P_+(s)$ to be the spectral projection onto the **ordered upper eigenvalue** $E_+(s)=|s|$.\n\n(a) The eigenvalues are $s$ and $-s$, so their separation $2|s|$ closes at $0$. The uniform positive-gap hypothesis of [[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-finite-gapped-adiabatic-estimate]] fails.\n\n(b) The ordered upper projection is $P_+(s)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,1)$ for $s<0$ and $P_+(s)=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$ for $s>0$. Its distinct one-sided limits preclude a continuous extension through $0$. At the crossing $H(0)=0$, the spectral projection for its only eigenvalue is $I$, not a distinguished rank-one projection. Assigning any rank-one projection at the crossing does not repair the unequal one-sided limits.\n\n(c) The exact propagator is $U_T(s)=\\exp[-iT\\sigma_z(s^2-1)/2]$, so $U_T(1)=I$ and\n\n$$\\|(I-P_+(1))U_T(1)P_+(-1)\\|=1\\qquad(T>0).$$\n\nThus upper-energy tracking fails maximally at the endpoints. This is the ordered branch for which the leakage assertion is made.\n\n(d) There is nevertheless a continuous constant rank-one branch $P_\\downarrow=\\operatorname{diag}(0,1)$ whose vectors obey $H(s)e_\\downarrow=-s e_\\downarrow$ for every $s$. It starts at energy $+1$ at $s=-1$, ends at energy $-1$ at $s=1$, and satisfies $(I-P_\\downarrow)U_T(s)P_\\downarrow=0$ for every $s,T$. At $s=0$ this is a subprojection of the degenerate eigenspace; it is not the rank-two full spectral projection $E_{H(0)}(\\{0\\})$. The original claim that no continuous rank-one eigenbranch can pass through this crossing was false.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "reserved claim of the qm-math-controlled-dynamics design reservation; complete-conditional-argument",
          "design_state": "reserved-closure; complete-conditional-argument"
        },
        {
