          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Quantum Mechanics (University of Texas at Austin, author-hosted course text), Spin Magnetic Resonance pp. 178-180 (rotating-wave approximation and its long-time limitations)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/qmech/qmech.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Chapter 5 pp. 123-131 (exponential of a bounded self-adjoint generator)",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Work on $\\mathbb C^2$ with $\\hbar=1$. Keep the original commuting example $A=B=\\sigma_z$, $\\omega=1$, so $H(t)=(1+2\\cos t)\\sigma_z$ and $H_{\\rm avg}=\\sigma_z$. The exact and averaged propagators from time $0$ are\n\n$$U(t)=e^{-i(t+2\\sin t)\\sigma_z},\\qquad U_{\\rm avg}(t)=e^{-it\\sigma_z}.$$\n\nTheir norm difference is $2|\\sin(\\sin t)|$. At $t_n=\\pi/2+2\\pi n$ it equals $2|\\sin1|>0$ for every $n\\ge0$. This proves persistent nondecay of the original commuting error. It alone does not disprove uniform-time smallness as the ratio $\\|B\\|/|\\omega|$ becomes small: in the commuting extension $A=\\sigma_z$, $B=\\beta\\sigma_z$, $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\omega>0$, the exact error satisfies\n\n$$\\|U(t)-U_{\\rm avg}(t)\\|=2\\left|\\sin\\left(\\frac\\beta\\omega\\sin\\omega t\\right)\\right|\\le\\frac{2|\\beta|}{\\omega}\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).$$\n\nTo prove the stronger intended general limitation, fix $\\omega>0$ and define $\\sigma_+=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_-=\\sigma_+^*$, $\\sigma_x=\\sigma_++\\sigma_-$. For every integer $n\\ge1$, let\n\n$$\\varepsilon_n=\\omega\\sqrt{\\frac1{2n}+\\frac1{4n^2}},\\qquad A_n=0,\\qquad B_n=\\varepsilon_n\\sigma_+,$$\n\n$$H_n(t)=e^{i\\omega t}B_n+e^{-i\\omega t}B_n^*,\\qquad H_{{\\rm avg},n}=0.$$\n\nThese are smooth bounded self-adjoint Hamiltonians of precisely the form of [[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-rotating-wave-finite-time-error]]. They are noncommuting in time: for suitable $t,u$, $[H_n(t),H_n(u)]=2i\\varepsilon_n^2\\sin(\\omega(t-u))\\sigma_z\\ne0$. Their oscillatory coefficient norm satisfies $\\|B_n\\|/\\omega=\\varepsilon_n/\\omega\\to0$. Nevertheless at the growing times $\\tau_n=2\\pi n/\\omega$ their exact propagator obeys $U_n(\\tau_n)=-I$, while $U_{{\\rm avg},n}(\\tau_n)=I$. Thus\n\n$$\\|U_n(\\tau_n)-U_{{\\rm avg},n}(\\tau_n)\\|=2\\qquad(n\\ge1).$$\n\nThis is the maximum possible norm distance between unitaries. In particular no general bound $\\sup_{t\\ge0}\\|U(t)-U_{\\rm avg}(t)\\|\\le f(\\|B\\|/\\omega)$ with $f(r)\\to0$ as $r\\to0$ can hold for this whole class, even with $A=0$, fixed positive $\\omega$ and dimension two. This does not assert that the finite-time theorem's linear growth rate is sharp; it proves the absence of general uniform-time smallness without additional hypotheses. All assertions concern explicit mathematical models, not a laboratory error or measurement.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "reserved claim of the qm-math-controlled-dynamics design reservation; complete-conditional-argument",
          "design_state": "reserved-closure; complete-conditional-argument"
        }
