          },
          "aliases": [
            "nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fermion-and-dilute-grand-canonical-formulas"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "I. Burmistrov and A. Lunkin, Lecture Notes on Statistical Mechanics (HSE, 2023), Sections 1.3-1.4 pp. 24-35 (Fermi-Dirac and Bose-Einstein distributions, canonical and grand canonical partition functions)",
                "url": "http://home.itp.ac.ru/~burmi/HSE/Lectures-StatPhys-temp.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "F. H. L. Essler, Advanced Quantum Theory (Oxford lecture notes, 2017/18), Sections 4.1-4.2 pp. 25-27 (ideal Bose gas, occupation numbers, chemical potential)",
                "url": "http://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/courses/C6/C6_2017/C6Notes/lecturenotes2017AQT.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$\\beta>0$ and let $(\\varepsilon_j)_{j\\ge1}\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$ and\n$\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$ be real data; write $q_j:=e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_j-\\mu)}$.\n\n(a) *Single fermionic orbital.* On $\\mathfrak h:=\\mathbb C^2$ with orthonormal\nbasis $(u_0,u_1)$ define the bounded self-adjoint operator $N_-$ by\n$N_-u_0=0$, $N_-u_1=u_1$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). Then the diagonal\noperator $F:=\\sum_{k=0}^1e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)k}\\,T_{u_k,u_k}$ is\npositive, self-adjoint, finite rank, and\n$$\\operatorname{tr}F=1+e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)},\\qquad \\frac{\\sum_{k=0}^1k\\,e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)k}}{\\operatorname{tr}F}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)}+1}$$\n([[def-trace-class-operator]], [[def-trace-of-a-trace-class-operator]],\n[[thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent]]).\n\n(b) *Countable grand-canonical factor products.* Define\n$\\mathcal N:=\\{N\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots\\}:N\\text{ finite}\\}$ and set\n$$Z_{\\mathrm B}:=\\sup_{N\\in\\mathcal N}\\ \\prod_{j\\in N}\\frac1{1-q_j}\\in(0,\\infty],\\qquad Z_{\\mathrm F}:=\\sup_{N\\in\\mathcal N}\\ \\prod_{j\\in N}(1+q_j)\\in[1,\\infty],$$\nwith the convention $Z_{\\mathrm B}:=+\\infty$ if some $j$ has\n$\\varepsilon_j-\\mu\\le0$. Then:\n$$Z_{\\mathrm B}<\\infty\\iff\\sum_j e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j}<\\infty \\ \\text{ and }\\ \\mu<\\inf_j\\varepsilon_j,\\qquad Z_{\\mathrm F}<\\infty\\iff\\sum_je^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j}<\\infty,$$\nand in these finite cases $Z_{\\mathrm B}=\\prod_{j\\ge1}(1-q_j)^{-1}$ and\n$Z_{\\mathrm F}=\\prod_{j\\ge1}(1+q_j)$.\n\n(c) *Mode occupancies and dilute remainders.* For a single mode with\n$0<q<1$ (boson) and $q>0$ (fermion),\n$$\\frac{\\sum_{n\\ge0}n\\,q^n}{\\sum_{n\\ge0}q^n}=\\frac q{1-q}=q+\\frac{q^2}{1-q},\\qquad \\frac{\\sum_{n=0}^1n\\,q^n}{\\sum_{n=0}^1q^n}=\\frac q{1+q}=q-\\frac{q^2}{1+q};$$\nconsequently the deviations from $q$ are bounded by $q^2/(1-q)$ and\n$q^2/(1+q)$ respectively, and both are at most $2q^2$ when $q\\le1/2$.\n\nAll statements are mathematical statements about diagonal weights and products;\nno identification of $\\beta,\\mu,\\varepsilon_j$ with physical temperatures,\nchemical potentials or energies, and no thermal or thermodynamic conclusion,\nis asserted.",
          "strategy": "Complete conditional argument authored on this page; see the item body for the numbered steps.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "reference": "reserved claim of the qm-math-fock-and-equilibrium design reservation; complete-conditional-argument",
          "design_state": "reserved-closure; complete-conditional-argument"
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-finite-gibbs-free-energy-minimum",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite Gibbs free-energy minimum",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
