      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Newtonian spacetime arena of the framework",
          "statement": "**The arena.** The non-relativistic quantum framework of this category uses as its spacetime the affine time space of [[def-cm-affine-time-space]]: a quadruple $(V,\\tau,g,A)$ consisting of a four-dimensional real vector space $V$, a surjective linear map $\\tau:V\\to\\mathbb R$ (the **absolute-time covector**), the three-dimensional kernel $K=\\ker\\tau$ (the **spatial displacement space**), a symmetric positive definite bilinear form $g:K\\times K\\to\\mathbb R$ (the **spatial metric**), and an affine space $A$ modelled on $V$ whose elements are called **events**. When the framework speaks of \"a particle at position $x$ at time $t$\", of \"the time interval between two events\" or of \"the distance between two simultaneous events\", these are read in this structure.\n\n**Absolute time and slices.** The absolute time of a displacement $w\\in V$ is $\\tau(w)$; two events $p,q\\in A$ are **simultaneous** when $\\tau(q-p)=0$, and simultaneity is an equivalence relation whose classes are the **simultaneous slices**. For a fixed adapted chart and its coordinates $(t,x)$ of [[lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique]], the slices are the level sets of $t$ and the number $t(p)$ is the **time coordinate** of the event $p$. The pair of events of one slice carries the slice distance $d_{g}(p,q)=\\sqrt{g(p-q,p-q)}$; no distance is defined between events of different slices by this structure.\n\n**Smooth structure.** $A$ is read with the affine smooth structure determined by adapted coordinates: for a nonempty open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a curve $\\gamma:I\\to A$ is of class $C^{k}$ when its coordinate representation $s\\mapsto(t(\\gamma(s)),x(\\gamma(s)))$ in one adapted chart is of class $C^{k}$; the transition law between adapted charts of [[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]] makes this reading independent of the chart. Regularity of curves, of potential functions and of apparatus descriptions of the framework is stated with respect to this smooth structure.\n\n**What the arena does not contain.** The data contain no inner product on $V$, no Lorentzian metric, no light cone, no causal classification, no universal speed and no curvature; they contain no distinguished event, no distinguished time vector and no distinguished spatial basis. In particular the arena alone does not select a frame: the inertial frames of the model and the transformations between them are fixed by the separate definition of the frames on this page and the accompanying postulate.\n\n**Status.** These clauses define the spacetime objects of the model and their elementary readings. They are not a physical postulate that nature is such an arena and not a mathematical theorem; the corresponding adoption is made by the kinematics postulate of this page. No dynamics, force law, potential or matter model is fixed here, and no empirical measurement is recorded. Fix the spatial orientation required by positively oriented adapted charts.",
          "strategy": "Definition of the Newtonian arena used by the framework: the affine time space with its Euclidean slices, absolute time and smooth structure, and the list of what the structure excludes.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-affine-time-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "physical_scope": "The spacetime arena used by the non-relativistic quantum framework: the affine time space with a surjective absolute-time covector, Euclidean simultaneous slices and their affine smooth structure. The definition fixes the objects and their readings in the model; it asserts no dynamics, no equation of motion and no claim that nature is exactly such an arena, and it excludes Lorentzian structure, light cones and any spacetime curvature.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2.1, printed pages 5-7 (PDF pages 11-13): inertial frames, Galilean relativity and absolute time t' = t + t_0",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 2.1, printed pages 55-58 (PDF pages 67-70): a particle in R^3 described by a wave function, with the probability interpretation and observable operators",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Galilean transformations of the coordinate space",
          "statement": "**Coordinate space.** Let $C:=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$, whose elements are written $(t,x)$ with $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $x=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3})\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$. The Euclidean inner product and norm on the second factor are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]; addition and scalar multiplication on $C$ are componentwise, and $I_{3}$ is the identity matrix of [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]].\n\n**The transformations.** For a quadruple $\\theta=(b,a,v,R)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times SO(3)$ ([[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]) define the map $g_{\\theta}:C\\to C$ by\n\n$$g_{(b,a,v,R)}(t,x):=\\bigl(t+b,\\ Rx+v\\,t+a\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere $Rx$ and $vt$ are the usual matrix–vector products with $R=(R_{ij})$ and with the column vectors $x,v,a$. The four components of $\\theta$ are called the **time shift** $b$, the **spatial translation** $a$, the **boost velocity** $v$ and the **rotation** $R$, and $\\theta$ is called the **parameter quadruple** of $g_{\\theta}$. Write\n\n$$G:=\\bigl\\{g_{(b,a,v,R)}:(b,a,v,R)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times SO(3)\\bigr\\}$$\n\nfor the set of all these maps and $\\Theta:=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\times SO(3)$ for the parameter set. The elements of $G$ are the **Galilean transformations of the coordinate space**, and the assignment $\\theta\\mapsto g_{\\theta}$ is the **parameter map**.\n\n**Elementary readings.** For $R=I_{3}$ and $v=0$ the map $g_{(b,a,0,I_{3})}$ is the pure time-and-space translation $(t,x)\\mapsto(t+b,x+a)$; for $b=0$, $a=0$ and $R=I_{3}$ it is the pure boost $(t,x)\\mapsto(t,x+vt)$; and for $b=0$, $a=0$, $v=0$ it is the rotation $(t,x)\\mapsto(t,Rx)$ acting only on the spatial factor. The time coordinate of $g_{\\theta}(t,x)$ depends only on $t$ and $b$, in accordance with the absolute-time convention of the model that consumes this definition.\n\n**What is and is not asserted.** This definition introduces the maps and names their parameters. It asserts no property of the parameter map (such as injectivity), no closure of $G$ under composition or inversion, no continuity, smoothness or differentiability of the maps, and no physical interpretation: whether these maps describe changes between inertial frames of a physical model is a separate, explicitly adopted assumption of the physics items that consume this definition. The group structure of $G$ is proved in the group lemma of this page, and the units in which the parameters are read are fixed by the model's unit conventions, not here.",
          "strategy": "Definition of the Galilean transformation class of the coordinate space: the maps, their parameters and the elementary cases, with no group or smoothness claim asserted here.",
          "deps": [
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2.1, printed pages 5-7 (PDF pages 11-13): the Galilean group generated by translations, rotations and boosts, and the absolute-time shift t' = t + t_0",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. M. Lee, Introduction to Smooth Manifolds (2003), Example 8.35, printed page 196: the special orthogonal group SO(3) of rotations",
                "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~hrm/palestine/lee-smooth-manifolds.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations-form-a-group",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Galilean transformations form a group of bijections of the coordinate space",
          "statement": "In the notation of [[def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations]], with $C=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$, $G$ the set of Galilean transformations and $\\theta=(b,a,v,R)$ the parameters of $g_{\\theta}$:\n\n1. The parameter map $\\theta\\mapsto g_{\\theta}$ is injective: if $g_{(b,a,v,R)}=g_{(b',a',v',R')}$ then $b=b'$, $a=a'$, $v=v'$ and $R=R'$.\n2. Each $g_{(b,a,v,R)}$ is a bijection of $C$, with inverse\n$$g_{(b,a,v,R)}^{-1}=g_{(-b,\\,-R^{\\mathsf T}(a-vb),\\,-R^{\\mathsf T}v,\\,R^{\\mathsf T})}.$$\n3. Composition of Galilean transformations is Galilean, with\n$$g_{(b_{2},a_{2},v_{2},R_{2})}\\circ g_{(b_{1},a_{1},v_{1},R_{1})}=g_{(b_{1}+b_{2},\\,R_{2}a_{1}+v_{2}b_{1}+a_{2},\\,R_{2}v_{1}+v_{2},\\,R_{2}R_{1})},$$\nand $g_{(0,0,0,I_{3})}$ is the identity map of $C$.\n4. Consequently $G$ is a subgroup of the group of all bijections of $C$ under composition.",
          "strategy": "Direct computation: injectivity of the parameter map, the composition and inverse formulas of the transformation class, and the subgroup conclusion.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-so3-is-a-matrix-group",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group": "mathematical-premise",
