        },
        {
          "id": "def-nrqm-framework-inertial-frames",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Inertial frames of the framework and the class of frame changes",
          "statement": "**Inertial frames.** Fix the arena $(V,\\tau,g,A)$ of [[def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime]]. An **inertial frame** of the framework is an adapted chart\n\n$$F=\\bigl(o,u,(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})\\bigr)$$\n\nof that arena in the sense of [[def-cm-affine-time-space]]: an event $o\\in A$ (the **spatial and time origin** of the frame), a vector $u\\in V$ with $\\tau(u)=1$ (the frame's **time vector**), and an ordered $g$-orthonormal positively oriented basis $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$ of $K$ (the frame's **spatial triad**). The associated **frame coordinates** are the functions $(t,x):A\\to\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$ of [[lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique]], defined by $p=o+t(p)u+\\sum_{i}x_{i}(p)e_{i}$.\n\n**What kind of object a frame is.** A frame is a coordinate chart of $A$: a bijection of $A$ with $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$ carrying the affine structure, and in particular a global chart, not a local one. It is deliberately not an observer congruence (no family of curves indexed by spatial position, no four-velocity field) and not a time-varying orthonormal basis field: the triad $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$ is a fixed basis of $K$ and the time vector $u$ is fixed once the frame is chosen. A time-dependent family of bases or a congruence of observers is a different geometric object, and statements about them are not statements about frames unless a separate construction relates them.\n\n**Allowed changes of frame.** Let $F$ and $F'$ be two inertial frames with coordinates $(t,x)$ and $(t',x')$. The **change of frame** from $F$ to $F'$ is the bijection of $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}$ that sends the $F$-coordinates of an event to its $F'$-coordinates. By clause 1 of [[lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations]] there are $b\\in\\mathbb R$, $a,v\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ and $R\\in SO(3)$ ([[def-cm-special-orthogonal-group]]) with\n\n$$t'=t+b,\\qquad x'=Rx+vt+a;$$\n\nthat is, the change of frame is the Galilean transformation $g_{(b,a,v,R)}$ of [[def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations]] applied to the coordinates. The class of transformations between inertial frames is therefore the set $G$ of the Galilean transformations, which by [[lem-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations-form-a-group]] is a group of bijections of the coordinate space: the composite of two frame changes is again a frame change, and the inverse of a frame change is a frame change. The parameters are the time shift $b$, the spatial translation $a$, the boost velocity $v$ and the rotation $R$ of the two frames.\n\n**Conventions.** The spatial triad of every frame is positively oriented for the fixed orientation of $K$ used in the definition of adapted charts, so all frame changes have $R\\in SO(3)$ rather than $R\\in O(3)$ with negative determinant. The reading of the coordinates in seconds and metres, and the physical meaning of the parameters as measured quantities, are fixed by the unit conventions recorded on this page; no measured value of any parameter is asserted here.\n\n**Status.** This definition fixes the frames and the class of frame changes of the model. It asserts no property of forces or of the motion of any body, selects no distinguished frame, and makes no empirical claim. The adoption of these frames as the frames of the physical model, together with the reading of apparatus motion as classical, is part of the kinematics postulate of this page.",
          "strategy": "Definition of the inertial frames as adapted charts with a time origin, spatial origin and positively oriented orthonormal triad, and of the Galilean class of frame changes, with the chart-versus-congruence distinction made explicit.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime",
            "def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations",
            "lem-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations-form-a-group",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations",
            "def-cm-affine-time-space",
            "lem-cm-adapted-coordinates-exist-and-are-unique",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime": "formulation-prerequisite",
