          },
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "physical_scope": "The frames of the non-relativistic quantum framework: inertial frames are adapted coordinate charts of the Newtonian arena, determined by a time origin, a spatial origin and a positively oriented orthonormal spatial triad, and the allowed changes between them are the Galilean transformations. The definition fixes these objects; it makes no claim about forces, about the motion of apparatus and no empirical claim, and it deliberately excludes observer congruences and time-varying basis fields.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2 and 1.2.1, printed pages 4-7 (PDF pages 10-13): inertial frames defined by the failure of Newton's first law, and the Galilean transformations t' = t + t_0, x' = Rx - vt + a between them",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 1.2, printed pages 2-5 (PDF pages 8-11): the class of transformations between inertial frames generated by rotations, translations, boosts and time shifts",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-nrqm-framework-classical-apparatus-trajectories",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Classical apparatus and preparation trajectories of the framework",
          "statement": "**Classical trajectories.** Fix the arena of [[def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime]] and an inertial frame $F$ of [[def-nrqm-framework-inertial-frames]] with coordinates $(t,x)$. A **classical trajectory** (also called a **worldline** of the model) is a pair $(I,\\gamma)$ consisting of a nondegenerate open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ and a map $\\gamma:I\\to A$ such that:\n\n1. **Parameter condition.** The parameter of the curve is the frame time: $t(\\gamma(s))=s$ for every $s\\in I$. In the coordinates of the frame this reads $\\gamma(s)=(s,x(s))$ with $x:I\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$.\n2. **Regularity.** The coordinate map $x:I\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ is of class $C^{2}$, so that the coordinate velocity $w(s):=x'(s)$ and the coordinate acceleration $a(s):=x''(s)$ exist as continuous curves; derivatives are those of [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]] and the chain rule used to differentiate compositions is [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]].\n\n**Apparatus and preparation constituents.** The macroscopic parts of a laboratory arrangement - sources, slits and collimators, shutters, field-generating coils and magnets, screens and detector housings - are described in the model by classical trajectories: to each such constituent and each inertial frame there is assigned a trajectory in the above sense, with the same event of the arena carrying its position at the frame time. A **preparation device** is such a constituent together with the classical trajectory assigned to it.\n\n**Quantum particles.** A trajectory in the above sense is not assigned to a quantum particle of the model: the state of a particle between preparations and detections is given by a state in the sense of the state postulate of this page, and the model asserts no definite trajectory of a particle between measurements. A detected particle event is a localized detection record; its distribution is computed with the model's measurement assumptions, not by reading off a worldline.\n\n**Status.** This definition fixes the mathematical object used for apparatus descriptions and its regularity, and records the two restrictions above: apparatus motion is described classically, and no worldline is assigned to an unmeasured quantum particle. It adopts no force law determining the apparatus trajectories, no interaction between apparatus and particle other than those specified on the pages that state a measurement model, and no empirical claim. The identification of a particular laboratory device with a trajectory of the model is a physical assumption with uncertainty, in the sense of the preparation and apparatus postulate of this page.",
          "strategy": "Definition of the classical trajectories of apparatus and preparation constituents, the parameter condition and regularity, and the exclusion of a definite worldline for an unmeasured quantum particle.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime",
            "def-nrqm-framework-inertial-frames",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-framework-inertial-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "physical_scope": "The classical trajectories used for apparatus constituents and preparation devices of the framework: twice continuously differentiable curves in the Newtonian arena whose parameter is the frame time. The definition records that these objects describe macroscopic apparatus, that a quantum particle is not assigned a definite worldline between measurements, and that no force law for the apparatus is adopted here; it makes no empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2, printed pages 4-5 (PDF pages 10-11): the trajectory of a particle as a curve of positions as a function of time, and inertial frames",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Quantum Mechanics (author-hosted lecture notes), Section 1.1, printed pages 3-5 (PDF pages 6-8): the wave-function description and the definite tracks of detected particles",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/quantum-mechanics/qm.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-framework-ccr-on-the-schwartz-core",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Canonical commutation relations of position and momentum on the Schwartz core",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Fix $d\\ge1$ and $\\hbar>0$, let $Q_{j},P_{k}$ be the position and momentum operators on $L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{d};\\mathbb C)$ of [[def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators]], and let\n\n$$\\mathcal S:=\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^{d};\\mathbb C)$$\n\nbe the Schwartz space of [[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Then:\n\nIn this operator statement, $\\mathcal S$ denotes the image in $L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ of the actual Schwartz functions under the quotient map by almost-everywhere equality ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]); the smooth representatives are used in the pointwise derivative calculations.\n\n(a) $\\mathcal S\\subseteq D(Q_{j})\\cap D(P_{k})$ for all $j,k$, and $Q_{j}f\\in\\mathcal S$ and $P_{k}f\\in\\mathcal S$ for every $f\\in\\mathcal S$.\n(b) for every $f\\in\\mathcal S$ the products $Q_{j}P_{k}$ and $P_{k}Q_{j}$ are defined at $f$ and\n$$(Q_{j}P_{k}-P_{k}Q_{j})f=i\\hbar\\,\\delta_{jk}f,\\qquad \\delta_{jk}=\\begin{cases}1,&j=k,\\\\0,&j\\ne k;\\end{cases}$$\nthat is, the canonical commutation relation holds on the common core $\\mathcal S$.\n\nNo statement is made about products of $Q_{j}$ and $P_{k}$ outside $\\mathcal S$: the relation is asserted on the Schwartz core only, not as an identity of unbounded products on all of $L^{2}$.",
          "strategy": "Direct computation: membership of Schwartz classes in the operator domains, the two products evaluated with the weak product rule, and the commutator on the Schwartz core.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions",
            "lem-schwartz-functions-and-all-derivatives-are-integrable",
            "lem-classical-derivatives-are-weak-derivatives",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "lem-schwartz-cutoffs-from-the-standard-smooth-step",
            "def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump",
            "lem-complex-translation-and-approximate-identity-interfaces",
            "thm-dominated-convergence",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
