        {
          "id": "cex-nrqm-framework-zero-mass-has-no-self-adjoint-limit",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The massive free-particle family has no strong zero-mass limit on Schwartz states",
          "statement": "In the one-particle model of [[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]] with potential $V=0$, the free kinetic operators\n$$K_{m}:=-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}\\Delta,\\qquad D(K_{m})=W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C),\\qquad m>0,$$\nhave a massless strong-action limit: there exist a self-adjoint operator $K_{0}\\ge0$ on $L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ with $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)\\subseteq D(K_0)$ such that $K_{m}f\\to K_{0}f$ in $L^{2}$ for every $f$ in the Schwartz class $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ as $m\\downarrow0$, and the formal free-plane-wave frequencies of this massive family have a finite pointwise limit at every fixed nonzero wave vector. Precisely, for $k\\in\\mathbb R^{3}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ with $[k]=\\ell^{-1}$, define $\\omega_m(k):=\\hbar|k|^{2}/(2m)$ by the plane-wave solution $e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega_m(k)t)}$ of the free equation; the additional claim is that $\\lim_{m\\downarrow0}\\omega_m(k)$ is finite, where $[\\omega_m]=\\tau^{-1}$ under [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]].",
          "strategy": "Direct refutation: compute the kinetic values, the energy expectation and the free frequencies on explicit smooth data and exhibit the divergence of each as the mass tends to zero.",
          "deps": [
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition",
            "post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability",
            "post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics",
            "post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units",
            "def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators",
            "lem-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-momentum-operators-self-adjoint",
            "thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators",
            "def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial",
            "thm-plancherel",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-momentum-operators-self-adjoint": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-plancherel": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "physical_scope": "A refutation, in the spin-zero massive free-particle model, of the specific limit asserted below: convergence K_m f to K_0 f in L^2 for every Schwartz vector to a self-adjoint K_0, together with a finite free-frequency limit at each fixed nonzero wave number. One explicit Schwartz vector has divergent kinetic-operator norm; for every fixed normalized operator-domain state with nonzero momentum second moment the kinetic expectation diverges as m tends to zero. For each m>0, this class has finite nonzero model root-mean-square speed v_rms,m=m^{-1}(sum_j ||P_j psi||^2)^{1/2}; that speed is not held fixed as m varies. The formal free frequency at each fixed nonzero wave number also diverges. The item excludes that specified limit, not other mathematical notions of convergence or independently specified massless quantum models, and asserts no empirical claim. The radiation extension is a separate adopted model.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Quantum Mechanics (author-hosted lecture set), Section 1.2, printed pages 9-14 (PDF pages 12-17): the free Hamiltonian, plane-wave solutions and the dispersion of the Schroedinger equation",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/quantum-mechanics/qm.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Chapter 7, Theorem 7.8 and Section 7.3, printed pages 168-171 (PDF pages 180-183): the free Schroedinger operator is self-adjoint with spectrum [0,infinity) and its time evolution is the free propagator",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-nrqm-framework-quadratic-shear-is-not-galilean",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A smooth time-dependent translation chart need not be an inertial frame",
          "statement": "Fix the spacetime arena of [[def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime]] together with an inertial frame $F$ of [[def-nrqm-framework-inertial-frames]] with coordinates $(t,x)$. Every smooth chart of the arena of the form\n$$G_{w}:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3}\\to\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3},\\qquad G_{w}(t,x)=\\bigl(t,\\,x+w(t)\\bigr),$$\nwith $w:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ of class $C^{\\infty}$ and $[w_i]=\\ell$ for every component, is an inertial frame of the model; that is, the map $G_{w}$ belongs to the Galilean transformation class $G$ of [[def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations]] for every such $w$.",
          "strategy": "Direct refutation: exhibit a smooth time-dependent translation chart of the arena, show that it is not a Galilean transformation, and compute the coordinate acceleration it assigns to a curve of zero coordinate acceleration.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations",
            "def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime",
            "def-nrqm-framework-inertial-frames",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-framework-inertial-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-cm-adapted-charts-and-galilean-transformations": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cm-special-orthogonal-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "physical_scope": "A refutation, on explicit data of the model, of the claim that every chart of the Newtonian arena obtained by a smooth time-dependent spatial translation is an inertial frame of the framework. It exhibits a smooth chart that is a bijection of the coordinate space but not a Galilean transformation, and shows that a parametrized curve of zero coordinate acceleration in the standard chart has nonzero coordinate acceleration in that chart. The item refutes the stated frame-class claim; it adopts no force law and makes no empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Dynamics and Relativity (Cambridge lecture notes, Lent 2013), Section 1.2.1, printed pages 5-7 (PDF pages 11-13): the Galilean transformations between inertial frames and the preservation of zero coordinate acceleration",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Classical Dynamics (Cambridge lecture notes, January 2015 printing), Section 2.2.1, printed pages 14-15 (PDF pages 20-21): the rotating coordinate system example and the fictitious forces of a non-inertial frame",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/classical-dynamics/clas.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-nrqm-framework-mean-position-can-fail-to-exist",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The mean position of a normalized state need not exist",
          "statement": "Specialize P1 to a one-dimensional system with state space $H=L^{2}(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ and specialize P2 to the position observable $Q\\psi=x\\psi$, with outcome coordinate measured in metres. Its spectral projection for a Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is multiplication by $\\mathbf 1_B$, so a pure preparation $[\\psi]$ has position law $\\mu_{\\psi}^{Q}(B)=\\int_B|\\psi(x)|^{2}\\,dx$. Define its mean position to be the Lebesgue expectation $\\mathbb E_{\\mu_{\\psi}^{Q}}[X]=\\int_{\\mathbb R}x\\,d\\mu_{\\psi}^{Q}(x)$ when it is a finite real number; this is so exactly when $x\\in L^{1}(\\mu_{\\psi}^{Q})$, equivalently $\\int_{\\mathbb R}|x|\\,|\\psi(x)|^{2}\\,dx<\\infty$. The refuted claim is that every normalized $\\psi\\in L^{2}(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ has such a finite mean.",
          "strategy": "Direct refutation: exhibit a normalized state and compute its divergent first absolute moment and second moment with the rational p-test on the half line.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units",
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition",
            "post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability",
            "def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators",
            "lem-nrqm-observables-l2-multiplication-spectral-measure",
            "def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions",
            "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions",
            "def-improper-integral-at-infinity",
            "thm-improper-p-test-rational",
