    {
      "id": "qm-state-geometry",
      "title": "Qm State Geometry",
      "category": "non-relativistic-quantum-mechanics",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "qm-state-geometry-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "qm-framework-and-quantities",
        "qm-math-composites"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "State vectors, unit vectors, rays, normalization and phases of a Hilbert space",
          "statement": "**The Hilbert space and its norm.** Throughout this item $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ is a nonzero complex Hilbert space, with pairing $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the second ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]), and with the induced norm $\\|\\psi\\|:=\\sqrt{\\langle\\psi,\\psi\\rangle}$ ([[def-inner-product-norm]], [[cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm]]). A **vector** is an element of $H$, and a **unit vector** is a vector $\\psi$ with $\\|\\psi\\|=1$. The **unit sphere** of $H$ is\n$$S(H):=\\{\\psi\\in H:\\|\\psi\\|=1\\},$$\nand the set of **nonzero vectors** is $H^{\\times}:=H\\setminus\\{0\\}$. The hypothesis $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ says exactly that $H^{\\times}$ is nonempty; then $S(H)$ is nonempty as well, since for every nonzero $\\psi$ the vector $\\psi/\\|\\psi\\|$ has norm one, because $\\|\\psi\\|>0$ and $\\|\\psi/\\|\\psi\\|\\,\\|=|\\|\\psi\\|^{-1}|\\,\\|\\psi\\|=1$.\n\n**Rays.** For $\\psi\\in H^{\\times}$ the **ray** of $\\psi$ is\n$$[\\psi]:=\\{z\\psi:z\\in\\mathbb C,\\ z\\ne0\\}\\subseteq H^{\\times}.$$\nA **ray** of $H$ is a subset of the form $[\\psi]$ for some nonzero $\\psi$, and an element of a ray is called a **representative** of it. The set of rays\n$$R(H):=\\{[\\psi]:\\psi\\in H^{\\times}\\}$$\nis the **ray space** of $H$. Two nonzero vectors $\\psi,\\varphi$ are **ray-equivalent** when they lie in a common ray, that is, when $\\varphi=z\\psi$ for some nonzero complex number $z$. Ray equivalence is an equivalence relation on $H^{\\times}$: it is reflexive with $z=1$, symmetric with $z^{-1}$, and transitive by multiplying scalars. Its classes are exactly the rays, so $R(H)$ is the quotient set of $H^{\\times}$ by this relation, and every nonzero vector lies in exactly one ray.\n\n**Normalization.** The **normalization map** is\n$$\\nu:H^{\\times}\\longrightarrow S(H),\\qquad \\nu(\\psi):=\\frac{\\psi}{\\|\\psi\\|},$$\nwhere the right-hand side is the scalar multiple $\\|\\psi\\|^{-1}\\psi$; the displayed definition is meaningful because $\\|\\psi\\|>0$ for $\\psi\\ne0$. The vector $\\nu(\\psi)$ is the **normalization** of $\\psi$.\n\n**Phases.** The **phase group** is\n$$U(1):=\\{\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C:|\\zeta|=1\\}$$\n([[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]), and a **phase factor** is an element of $U(1)$. Two vectors $\\psi,\\varphi$ are **phase-equivalent** when $\\varphi=\\zeta\\psi$ for some phase factor $\\zeta$. The **phase action** of $U(1)$ on the unit sphere is the map\n$$U(1)\\times S(H)\\longrightarrow S(H),\\qquad (\\zeta,\\psi)\\longmapsto\\zeta\\psi,$$\nwhich is well defined because $\\|\\zeta\\psi\\|=|\\zeta|\\,\\|\\psi\\|=\\|\\psi\\|$ for $\\zeta\\in U(1)$ and $\\psi\\in S(H)$. Ray equivalence allows multiplication by an arbitrary nonzero complex scalar, while phase equivalence allows multiplication by a scalar of modulus one only; within a given ray the two notions coincide exactly on representatives of equal norm, and the precise statements are proved in the normalization lemma of this page.\n\n**Scope.** This definition fixes the mathematical objects of the state space of a Hilbert space and their elementary vocabulary: nonzero vectors, unit vectors, rays, the ray space, the normalization map and the phase action. It introduces no topology or measure on $R(H)$, no dynamics and no physical interpretation, and it uses no choice principle. The identification of rays with preparations and of the unit sphere with their normalized representatives is the model assumption stated in the framework's state postulate, not part of this mathematical definition; the orbit structure of the phase action is analysed in the normalization lemma of this page.",
          "strategy": "Definition of the state-space objects of a Hilbert space: nonzero vectors, unit sphere, rays, ray space, normalization map and phase action, with their readings and scope.",
          "deps": [
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
            "def-inner-product-norm",
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
            "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inner-product-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
