          "dependency_level": 0,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 'Foundations I: States and Ensembles' (updated July 2015), Section 2.1, printed pages 3-4: the Hilbert-space axioms, the ray of a state, the normalized representative and the physical insignificance of the overall phase",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 1.3.4, printed page 12: normalization of a state and the modulus-one phase convention",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 2.1, printed pages 55-56 (PDF pages 66-67): the possible states of a quantum system are the elements of norm one of a complex separable Hilbert space",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-normalization-and-phase-classes",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Normalization, ray equivalence and the phase orbits of the unit sphere",
          "statement": "Let $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be a complex Hilbert space and use the notation of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays]]: the unit sphere $S(H)$, the nonzero vectors $H^{\\times}$, the ray $[\\psi]$, the ray space $R(H)$, the normalization map $\\nu$, the phase group $U(1)$ and the phase action. Then:\n\n(a) For every $\\psi\\in H^{\\times}$ one has $\\nu(\\psi)\\in[\\psi]\\cap S(H)$ and $\\nu(\\nu(\\psi))=\\nu(\\psi)$; and for every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ with $z\\ne0$,\n$$\\nu(z\\psi)=\\frac{z}{|z|}\\,\\nu(\\psi).$$\nMoreover $\\nu$ maps $H^{\\times}$ onto $S(H)$.\n\n(b) For $\\psi,\\varphi\\in H^{\\times}$: $[\\psi]=[\\varphi]$ if and only if $\\nu(\\varphi)=\\zeta\\nu(\\psi)$ for some phase factor $\\zeta\\in U(1)$; the phase factor is then unique.\n\n(c) For every $\\psi\\in S(H)$ the intersection of the ray of $\\psi$ with the unit sphere is exactly the phase orbit of $\\psi$,\n$$[\\psi]\\cap S(H)=\\{\\zeta\\psi:\\zeta\\in U(1)\\}=U(1)\\psi .$$\nConsequently the fibres of the map $S(H)\\to R(H)$, $\\psi\\mapsto[\\psi]$, are exactly the phase orbits, and this map induces a bijection from the set of phase orbits on $S(H)$ onto $R(H)$.\n\n(d) For $\\psi,\\varphi\\in S(H)$: $[\\psi]=[\\varphi]$ if and only if $\\varphi=\\zeta\\psi$ for a unique $\\zeta\\in U(1)$; in that case $\\zeta=\\langle\\varphi,\\psi\\rangle$ and $|\\langle\\varphi,\\psi\\rangle|=1$.",
          "strategy": "Direct computation: normalization of a nonzero vector, its behaviour under rescaling, the unit representatives of a ray as one phase orbit, and the bijection between rays and phase orbits.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays",
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 (updated July 2015), Section 2.1, printed page 4: a ray is an equivalence class of vectors differing by a nonzero complex scalar, with a unit-norm representative, and |psi> and e^{i alpha}|psi> describe the same state",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 1.3.4, printed page 12: a state is normalized by dividing by its norm, and overall phase factors have no physical effect",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Rays, unit vectors and rank-one Hilbert projections",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice, which is the choice hypothesis of the rank-one calculus [[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]. Let $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be a complex Hilbert space and let\n$$T_{u,v}x=\\langle x,v\\rangle u\\qquad(x\\in H)$$\nbe the rank-one operator calculus of that item. Use the notation of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays]] for the unit sphere $S(H)$, the nonzero vectors $H^{\\times}$, the ray $[\\psi]$, the ray space $R(H)$ and the normalization $\\nu$. Then:\n\n(a) For every $\\psi\\in H^{\\times}$ the operator $T_{\\psi,\\psi}$ is a nonzero self-adjoint positive trace-class operator with\n$$\\|T_{\\psi,\\psi}\\|_1=\\operatorname{tr}(T_{\\psi,\\psi})=\\|\\psi\\|^{2},\\qquad T_{\\psi,\\psi}=\\|\\psi\\|^{2}\\,T_{\\nu(\\psi),\\nu(\\psi)},\\qquad \\langle T_{\\psi,\\psi}x,x\\rangle=|\\langle x,\\psi\\rangle|^{2}\\quad(x\\in H).$$\n\n(b) For every $u\\in S(H)$ the operator $T_{u,u}$ is the Hilbert orthogonal projection onto the one-dimensional linear subspace $\\mathbb Cu$; in particular $T_{u,u}\\ne0$, $T_{u,u}^{2}=T_{u,u}$ and $T_{u,u}^{*}=T_{u,u}$. Conversely, if $M$ is a one-dimensional linear subspace of $H$ and $u\\in S(H)$ satisfies $M=\\mathbb Cu$, then the Hilbert orthogonal projection $P_M$ onto $M$ equals $T_{u,u}$. Thus the operators $T_{u,u}$ with $u\\in S(H)$ are exactly the Hilbert orthogonal projections of $H$ with one-dimensional range.\n\n(c) For nonzero $\\psi,\\varphi\\in H^{\\times}$:\n$$T_{\\psi,\\psi}=T_{\\varphi,\\varphi}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\|\\psi\\|=\\|\\varphi\\|\\ \\text{and}\\ [\\psi]=[\\varphi].$$\nIn particular, for $u,w\\in S(H)$ one has $T_{u,u}=T_{w,w}$ if and only if $w=\\zeta u$ for some $\\zeta\\in U(1)$.\n\n(d) The map\n$$j:R(H)\\longrightarrow \\mathcal S_1(H),\\qquad j([\\psi]):=T_{\\nu(\\psi),\\nu(\\psi)},$$\nis well defined and injective, and its image is exactly $\\{T_{u,u}:u\\in S(H)\\}$.",
          "strategy": "Direct computation with the rank-one operator calculus: trace, positivity and scaling of T_{psi,psi}, the identification of T_{u,u} with the one-dimensional Hilbert projection, the ray equality criterion, and the well-defined injective ray-to-state map.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-normalization-and-phase-classes",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus",
            "def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator",
            "def-hilbert-orthogonal-projection",
            "cor-finite-dimensional-subspaces-are-closed",
            "def-normed-subspace",
            "def-linear-combination-and-span",
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-normalization-and-phase-classes": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator": "mathematical-premise",
