        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-extreme-points-of-the-state-space",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The extreme points of the state space are exactly the pure states",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be a complex Hilbert space and let $D(H)$ be its state space ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]]), regarded as a subset of the real vector space $X$ of self-adjoint trace-class operators, closed under finite real convex combinations by [[lem-nrqm-state-geometry-density-set-convex-and-complete]]. Call a state $\\rho\\in D(H)$ an **extreme point** of $D(H)$ when\n$$\\rho=(1-t)\\sigma+t\\tau,\\qquad \\sigma,\\tau\\in D(H),\\quad 0<t<1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sigma=\\tau=\\rho,$$\nholds for all real $t$ with $0<t<1$ and all $\\sigma,\\tau\\in D(H)$. Then $\\rho$ is an extreme point of $D(H)$ if and only if $\\rho$ is a pure state.",
          "strategy": "Direct convexity argument: a pure state pins the quadratic forms of any convex decomposition to one and forces both components to equal it, while a mixed state is split by an explicit small perturbation of its two leading spectral terms.",
