        {
          "id": "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The state space of the framework: preparations, pure rays and density operators",
          "statement": "**(a) State space.** Adopt the state postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]] for a declared system of the declared massive-matter sector, with its assigned nonzero separable complex Hilbert space $H$. The **state space of the system in the model** is the set\n$$D(H)=\\{\\rho:\\rho\\ \\text{is a density operator on}\\ H\\}$$\nof [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]]: the self-adjoint positive trace-class operators on $H$ of trace one. A **state** of the system is an element of $D(H)$; this is the geometry vocabulary for the general preparations of clause (iii) of the state postulate. The model's adopted assignment of a state to each preparation is not produced by this definition; it is the content of that postulate.\n\n**(b) Pure and mixed states.** A state is **pure** when it is one of the operators $T_{u,u}$ with $u\\in S(H)$, and **mixed** when it is not pure, as in [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]]. By [[lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection]] the map\n$$j:R(H)\\to D(H),\\qquad j([\\psi])=T_{\\nu(\\psi),\\nu(\\psi)},$$\nis a bijection of the ray space onto the set of pure states; and by clause (ii) of the state postulate the pure preparations are the unit rays of $H$, so the model's pure preparations are exactly its pure states.\n\n**(c) State assigned to a state vector.** For a nonzero vector $\\psi\\in H^{\\times}$ the **state assigned to $\\psi$** is the normalized rank-one operator\n$$\\widehat\\rho(\\psi)=\\frac{T_{\\psi,\\psi}}{\\|\\psi\\|^{2}}=T_{\\nu(\\psi),\\nu(\\psi)}\\in D(H),$$\nwhich by [[lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection]] depends only on the ray $[\\psi]$; clause (ii) of the state postulate assigns the same operator to the pure preparation $[\\psi]$. The zero vector is excluded here because $\\|\\psi\\|^{-1}$ is defined only for $\\psi\\ne0$; no state is assigned to it, in agreement with the state postulate.\n\n**(d) Mixtures and purity.** A **statistical mixture** of preparations with weights is the trace-norm convergent sum of clause (iii) of the state postulate; its value is the corresponding convex combination of states. The **purity** of a state $\\rho$ is $\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho^{2})$, and by [[lem-nrqm-state-geometry-purity-criterion]] a state is pure exactly when its purity equals $1$.\n\n**(e) Status.** Clauses (a)-(d) record the readings of the state postulate in the geometry vocabulary of this page and fix the terminology used by the later pages of the category. The cited mathematical suppliers of the ray-to-state correspondence and of the purity criterion assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), which is carried as a hypothesis of these readings. They adopt no assumption beyond the state postulate and that hypothesis, assert no dynamics, no probability law and no measurement model, and make no empirical claim: in particular, whether a given laboratory preparation is pure or mixed, and whether a given apparatus realizes a prescribed mixture, are physical identifications with uncertainty, governed by the preparation and apparatus assumptions of the framework, not consequences of this definition. Superselection restrictions and identical-particle symmetry sectors, where imposed, are separate assumptions by clause (v) of the state postulate and are not part of the state space fixed here.",
          "strategy": "Definition of the framework state-space vocabulary under the adopted state postulate: states as density operators, pure states as rays, the state assigned to a state vector, mixtures and purity, with the scope of the readings recorded.",
          "deps": [
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-purity-criterion",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-purity-criterion": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "physical_scope": "The state-space vocabulary of the non-relativistic quantum framework in the declared massive-matter sector: given the state postulate's assignment of a nonzero separable complex Hilbert space and its identification of pure preparations with unit rays and general preparations with density operators, this item fixes the readings 'state space', 'state', 'pure state', 'mixed state', 'state assigned to a state vector' and 'purity', and the correspondence between pure preparations and pure states. It adds no physical assumption beyond the state postulate, asserts no dynamics, probability law, measurement model or empirical result, and excludes superselection restrictions, identical-particle sectors and all field or radiation content.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 (updated July 2015), Sections 2.1 and 2.3, printed pages 3-4 and 16-18: states as rays, the density operator of an ensemble, and the convex structure of the set of states",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Sections 1.3.4 and 6.3, printed pages 12 and 121-126: normalized states, pure states and the density operator of an impure state",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-preparation-phase-invariance",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Pure preparations are well defined by their rays, and phase factors do not change their states",
          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate P1 of the framework ([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]]) and use the state-space vocabulary of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), which is the choice hypothesis of the rank-one suppliers cited below. Let $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be the Hilbert space assigned to a declared system, let $\\psi,\\varphi\\in H^{\\times}$ be nonzero state vectors, and let $[\\psi],[\\varphi]$ be their rays. Then:\n\n(a) **Representative independence.** If $\\varphi=z\\psi$ for some nonzero $z\\in\\mathbb C$, then the normalized rank-one operators agree,\n$$\\frac{T_{\\varphi,\\varphi}}{\\|\\varphi\\|^{2}}=\\frac{T_{\\psi,\\psi}}{\\|\\psi\\|^{2}}=T_{\\nu(\\psi),\\nu(\\psi)},$$\nand this operator is a pure state. In particular the density operator associated with a pure preparation depends only on the ray $[\\psi]$ and not on the representative used to name it.\n\n(b) **Phase invariance and injectivity.** If $u,w\\in S(H)$ are unit representatives of the same ray, then $w=\\zeta u$ for a unique $\\zeta\\in U(1)$ and $T_{w,w}=T_{u,u}$; conversely, if two pure preparations $[\\psi]\\ne[\\varphi]$ are distinct, then their assigned pure states are distinct. Hence the assignment $[\\psi]\\mapsto T_{\\nu(\\psi),\\nu(\\psi)}$ is a bijection from the set of pure preparations onto the set of pure states.\n\n(c) **Exclusion of the zero vector.** The zero vector is not a pure preparation of the model: it has no unit representative, the normalization map is undefined at it, the operator $T_{0,0}=0$ has trace $0$ and is not a density operator, and the state postulate assigns pure preparations only to unit rays of vectors of norm one. Consequently no pure preparation and no state is assigned to $0$.",
          "strategy": "Conditional deduction from the state postulate and the mathematical state-space lemmas: representative and phase independence of the pure-state assignment, its injectivity on rays, and the exclusion of the zero vector.",
          "deps": [
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-normalization-and-phase-classes",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus",
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-normalization-and-phase-classes": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "physical_scope": "A model-internal well-definedness statement about the state assignment of the non-relativistic quantum framework: with the state postulate adopted, the pure preparation of a nonzero state vector is independent of the chosen representative of its ray, phase-equivalent unit representatives yield the same pure state, distinct rays yield distinct pure states, and the zero vector is assigned no preparation or state. The item makes no empirical claim, asserts no dynamics or probability law, and does not assert that any particular laboratory procedure prepares a ray.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 (updated July 2015), Section 2.1, printed page 4: rays are equivalence classes of vectors differing by a nonzero complex scalar, normalized representatives give the same state, and |psi> and e^{i alpha}|psi> describe the same state",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 1.3.4, printed page 12: normalization of a state and the global phase convention",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-nrqm-state-geometry-mixtures-and-extremal-preparations",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Statistical mixtures, spectral decompositions and the extremal role of pure preparations",
          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate P1 of the framework ([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]]) and use the state-space vocabulary of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), which is the choice hypothesis of the spectral and mixture suppliers cited below. Let $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be the Hilbert space assigned to a declared system. Then:\n\n(a) **Mixtures.** Let $(\\rho_n)$ be a finite or countable family of preparations with real weights $w_n\\ge0$ satisfying $\\sum_nw_n=1$. Then the statistical mixture $\\sum_nw_n\\rho_n$ of clause (iii) of P1 converges in trace norm to a preparation, namely to the density operator $T=\\sum_nw_n\\rho_n$ of [[lem-nrqm-state-geometry-density-set-convex-and-complete]]. The mixture is affine in its data: a term of zero weight may be added or removed without changing $T$, and splitting one weight between two copies of the same preparation, $w\\rho=w_1\\rho+w_2\\rho$ with $w_1+w_2=w$, leaves $T$ unchanged. If the index set is partitioned into consecutive finite blocks with total weights $u_k>0$ and block mixtures $\\sigma_k$, then $\\sum_ku_k\\sigma_k=\\sum_nw_n\\rho_n$ in trace norm, so mixtures may be regrouped.\n\n(b) **Spectral decomposition.** Every preparation $\\rho$ is a countable statistical mixture of pairwise orthogonal pure preparations: there exist a finite or countable orthonormal family $(u_n)$ of unit vectors and positive weights $\\lambda_n$ with $\\sum_n\\lambda_n=1$ such that\n$$\\rho=\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\,T_{u_n,u_n},$$\nthe sum converging in trace norm, and the weights $\\lambda_n$ are exactly the positive eigenvalues of $\\rho$ counted with multiplicity.\n\n(c) **Extremality and nontrivial decompositions.** A preparation is pure if and only if its density operator is an extreme point of the state space $D(H)$ in the sense of the condition displayed in [[lem-nrqm-state-geometry-extreme-points-of-the-state-space]]; consequently every mixed preparation $\\rho$ admits a representation as a statistical mixture of two preparations in a nontrivial way: if $\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2>0$ are two positive eigenvalues of $\\rho$ with orthonormal eigenvectors $u_1$ and $u_2$, then with $\\varepsilon:=\\lambda_2/2$ the operators\n$$\\sigma_\\pm:=\\rho\\pm\\varepsilon\\bigl(T_{u_1,u_1}-T_{u_2,u_2}\\bigr)$$\nare preparations with $\\rho=\\tfrac12\\sigma_++\\tfrac12\\sigma_-$ and $\\sigma_+\\ne\\sigma_-$.",
          "strategy": "Conditional deduction from the state postulate: well-defined affine statistical mixtures with block grouping, the spectral decomposition of every preparation into orthogonal pure preparations, and the equivalence of purity with extremality together with the explicit splitting of mixed preparations.",
          "deps": [
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays",
