          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "physical_scope": "A scope audit of the state-geometry pair: it separates the mathematical layer (rays, normalization, rank-one projections, convexity, purity and extremality of the state space) from the readings of the state postulate adopted on the framework page, records that this page adopts no new physical assumption and no empirical claim, and lists the deliberate deferrals: observable consequences of phase, the probability law, measurement models, superselection and identical-particle sectors, and the photon interpretation of two-level polarization vectors. The remark proves nothing and asserts no observation.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 (updated July 2015), Sections 2.1 and 2.5.5, printed pages 3-4 and 34-35: rays versus normalized vectors, and the ambiguity of the ensemble interpretation",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 6.3, printed pages 121-126, especially page 122, equations (6.61)-(6.64): uncertainty about which state the system is in is represented by a probability mixture, distinct from the coherent superposition with amplitudes equal to the square roots of those probabilities",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 2.1, printed pages 55-58 (PDF pages 66-69): the axiomatic status of the state space and the separability assumption",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
