          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "physical_scope": "A worked computation in the model's two-level finite-spin state space: for the spin-one-half assignment of the framework, the density operators on the two-dimensional internal space are exactly the operators (I + r dot sigma)/2 with |r| <= 1, and the pure states are exactly the boundary points |r| = 1. Rank means the number of vectors in a finite basis of the operator range; each boundary state has one-dimensional range and is the state of the spin-up ray, the ray of a unit eigenvector of r dot sigma with eigenvalue +1. The Bloch ball is a parametrization of the model's states; the item asserts no measurement outcome probability, no experimental result and no interpretation of r beyond the displayed formula.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 (updated July 2015), Section 2.3.2, printed pages 21-23: the general qubit density matrix rho = (I + P dot sigma)/2, det rho = (1 - P^2)/4, the Bloch ball and its boundary as the pure states",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 2 'Spin One-Half, Bras, Kets, and Operators', Sections 1-3, printed pages 1-16: the Pauli matrices, spin states and the density operator of a spin-one-half system",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/a56c316e4ec548d8bd0a554fac9a74e8_MIT8_05F13_Chap_02.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 7.4.2, printed pages 161-164: the spin-one-half operators and their matrices",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-state-geometry-fixed-mode-two-level-states",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Pure and mixed states of a fixed two-level internal space",
          "statement": "Let $H$ be a two-dimensional complex Hilbert space with an orthonormal basis written $(h,v)$, and use the state space $D(H)$ of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]. Put\n$$u:=\\frac{h+v}{\\sqrt2}\\in S(H),\\qquad \\rho_+:=\\frac12T_{h,h}+\\frac12T_{v,v},$$\nHere $u$ is the unit vector of the equal-amplitude superposition, with ray $[u]$, and $\\rho_+$ is the density operator of the equal statistical mixture of the two basis rays. For an operator $A:H\\to H$, write $\\operatorname{ran}A:=\\{Ax:x\\in H\\}$ and define its **rank** by $\\operatorname{rank}A:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}(\\operatorname{ran}A)$, using [[def-dimension]]. Then:\n\n(i) The state assigned to the ray $[u]$ is the pure state $T_{u,u}$, whose matrix relative to the basis $(h,v)$ is\n$$T_{u,u}=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\ 1&1\\end{pmatrix};$$\nits purity is $1$ and its rank is one.\n\n(ii) The mixture is the state $\\rho_+=\\frac12I$, whose matrix relative to $(h,v)$ is diagonal with entries $\\frac12$; its purity is $\\frac12$, it is mixed, and it is not a pure state.\n\n(iii) The two states are distinct: $T_{u,u}\\ne\\frac12I$, and they differ in rank and in purity.\n\nThe basis labels $h$ and $v$ are abstract here. The identification of such a two-level space with the polarization states of a fixed propagation mode, and every statement about photon number, energy, momentum or detection, is deferred to the radiation assumptions adopted on the radiation pages of the category; no such identification or statement is made in this item.",
          "strategy": "Worked computation of the pure state of a diagonal vector and of the equal statistical mixture in an abstract two-level space with a polarization basis, with the photon reading deferred.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-purity-criterion",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-density-set-convex-and-complete",
            "def-inner-product-space",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-dimension",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity": "mathematical-premise",
