            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "physical_scope": "A worked computation in a fixed two-dimensional internal space carrying an orthonormal pair of 'polarization' labels: the state of the diagonal vector is the pure state with matrix (1/2)[[1,1],[1,1]], and the equal statistical mixture of the two basis labels is the mixed state (1/2)I. The two-level space is treated abstractly; the identification of its vectors with photon polarization states, and any statement about photon number, energy or momentum, is deferred to the radiation assumptions of the category and is not made in this item. The item asserts no measurement probability and no empirical result.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 1.3.6, printed pages 14-15: two orthonormal basis states of a fixed photon polarization mode, the superposition with equal amplitudes, and the transmission probabilities for a rotated analyser",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 (updated July 2015), Section 2.2.2, printed pages 14-16: photon polarization states as a two-dimensional internal space with orthonormal basis {|x>,|y>}",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-state-geometry-coherent-versus-mixed-preparation",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A coherent two-level preparation and the equal statistical mixture are different states",
          "statement": "Let $H$ be a two-dimensional complex Hilbert space with orthonormal basis $(h,v)$, and use the state space $D(H)$ and the preparation semantics of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]. Consider the two preparations\n$$\\text{superposition:}\\quad w=\\frac{h+iv}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad\\qquad \\text{equal mixture:}\\quad \\rho_+=\\frac12T_{h,h}+\\frac12T_{v,v},$$\nwhere $w$ is a unit vector and $\\rho_+$ is the statistical mixture of the two basis rays with weights $\\frac12,\\frac12$. The assigned state matrices, in the given order, are:\n$$T_{w,w}=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&-i\\\\ i&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\rho_+=\\frac12 I$$\nFor an operator $A:H\\to H$, write $\\operatorname{ran}A:=\\{Ax:x\\in H\\}$ and define its **rank** by $\\operatorname{rank}A:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}(\\operatorname{ran}A)$, using [[def-dimension]]. Relative to the ordered basis $(h,v)$, call exactly the ray $[w]$ of this displayed superposition the **coherent preparation** in this example. Its density matrix has off-diagonal entries $(T_{w,w})_{12}=-i/2$ and $(T_{w,w})_{21}=i/2$; these are the **coherence entries** in this basis. The mixture has zero off-diagonal entries. These terms refer only to the displayed state data and assert no outcome-statistical or apparatus property.\n\nThe two assigned operators are distinct states of the model: $T_{w,w}$ is pure with purity $1$ and rank one, while $\\rho_+$ is mixed with purity $\\frac12$ and rank two. In particular the relative phase of the superposition is retained in the assigned state, and the superposition and the mixture are not the same preparation.",
          "strategy": "Worked comparison of a relative-phase superposition with the equal mixture: the two assigned matrices differ in rank and purity, so the preparations are distinct states.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-purity-criterion",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-density-set-convex-and-complete",
            "pthm-nrqm-state-geometry-mixtures-and-extremal-preparations",
            "def-inner-product-space",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-dimension",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-purity-criterion": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-ray-to-rank-one-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-density-set-convex-and-complete": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-nrqm-state-geometry-mixtures-and-extremal-preparations": "physical-result",
            "def-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-dimension": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "physical_scope": "A worked comparison, inside the framework's state space, of two preparations of a two-level system: the pure preparation of the relative-phase superposition (h + i v)/sqrt2 and the equal statistical mixture of the h and v rays. The two assigned states are computed explicitly and shown to be distinct, with different rank and purity: the superposition is pure and the mixture is mixed. Here rank means the number of vectors in a finite basis of the operator's range. In this item, coherent preparation denotes only the pure ray of (h + i v)/sqrt2, whose density matrix in the ordered basis (h,v) has off-diagonal entries (-i/2, i/2); the equal mixture has zero off-diagonal entries. These are descriptions of assigned states only. The item asserts no outcome probability, no measurement model, no apparatus realization and no empirical result; whether any measurement distinguishes the states belongs to the framework's probability law and is not decided here.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Preskill, Lecture Notes for Ph219/CS219: Quantum Information, Chapter 2 (updated July 2015), Sections 2.2.1 and 2.3, printed pages 8-12 and 16-19: superpositions with a relative phase versus incoherent mixtures, and their different density matrices",
                "url": "https://preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap2_15.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Sections 1.3.5 and 6.3, printed pages 12-13 and 121-126: the role of relative phase in superpositions and the distinction between a superposition and a probability mixture embodied in the density operator",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-state-geometry-nonunique-preparation-ensembles",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "One two-level state from two different preparation ensembles",
          "statement": "Let $H$ be a two-dimensional complex Hilbert space with orthonormal basis $(e_1,e_2)$ and use the state space and preparation semantics of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]. Let $\\rho$ be the diagonal state\n$$\\rho=\\begin{pmatrix}3/4&0\\\\ 0&1/4\\end{pmatrix}$$\nrelative to $(e_1,e_2)$, and put\n$$\\psi=\\Bigl(\\cos\\frac\\pi6,\\ \\sin\\frac\\pi6\\Bigr)=\\Bigl(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\frac12\\Bigr),\\qquad \\varphi=\\Bigl(\\cos\\frac\\pi6,\\ -\\sin\\frac\\pi6\\Bigr)=\\Bigl(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},-\\frac12\\Bigr).$$\nThen:\n\n(i) $\\rho=\\frac34T_{e_1,e_1}+\\frac14T_{e_2,e_2}$: the orthonormal ensemble with weights $\\frac34,\\frac14$.\n\n(ii) $\\rho=\\frac12T_{\\psi,\\psi}+\\frac12T_{\\varphi,\\varphi}$: the uniform ensemble of the two pure preparations $[\\psi]$ and $[\\varphi]$, which are not orthogonal, since\n$$\\langle\\psi,\\varphi\\rangle=\\cos^{2}\\frac\\pi6-\\sin^{2}\\frac\\pi6=\\frac34-\\frac14=\\frac12\\ne0 .$$\n\n(iii) The two ensembles differ in their members and in their weights, yet they determine the same preparation, whose purity is $\\frac34^{2}+\\frac14^{2}=\\frac58<1$. Hence the assigned state does not determine the ensemble, and two distinct statistical preparations may determine the same state.",
          "strategy": "Worked computation exhibiting one mixed two-level state as an orthonormal ensemble with unequal weights and as the uniform mixture of two non-orthogonal pure states.",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space",
            "def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity",
            "lem-nrqm-state-geometry-purity-criterion",
