        {
          "id": "rem-nrqm-state-geometry-examples-scope-limits",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "What the worked checks of the companion page establish",
          "statement": "This remark audits the companion page. It records what each item computes, and what none of them claims. It proves nothing and reports no observation.\n\n**Elementary normalization and phase.** [[ex-nrqm-state-geometry-normalization-and-phase-check]] computes, on the explicit two-dimensional data $\\psi=(1,i)$ and $\\varphi=(1+i)\\psi$, the norms, the normalized representatives, the phase relating them and the normalized rank-one operators; it verifies the normalization and phase class lemma of the main page and exhibits the representative independence of the pure state on a worked vector pair.\n\n**Finite spin and the two-level state space.** [[ex-nrqm-state-geometry-finite-spin-bloch-ball]] proves that the states of the model's two-level finite-spin system are exactly the operators $\\frac12(I+r\\cdot\\sigma)$ with $|r|\\le1$, that the pure states are the boundary points $|r|=1$, and that each boundary point is the rank-one state of the corresponding spin-up ray. [[ex-nrqm-state-geometry-fixed-mode-two-level-states]] repeats the corresponding elementary computation for an abstract two-level internal space carrying a pair of polarization labels, showing the pure state of the diagonal vector and the equal mixture of the basis rays. Neither item asserts the identification of the abstract two-level space with photon polarization; that reading belongs to the radiation assumptions of the category, and no statement about photon number, energy or momentum is made here.\n\n**Superposition versus mixture, and non-unique ensembles.** [[ex-nrqm-state-geometry-coherent-versus-mixed-preparation]] shows that the relative-phase superposition $(h+iv)/\\sqrt2$ and the equal statistical mixture of the rays $[h]$ and $[v]$ are assigned different states, with different rank and purity, so the two preparations are not the same. [[ex-nrqm-state-geometry-nonunique-preparation-ensembles]] shows the converse direction of freedom: one and the same mixed state is determined by two genuinely different ensembles, an orthonormal pair with unequal weights and a uniform pair of non-orthogonal states. Both computations stop at the level of the assigned states; which outcome probabilities distinguish states, and with which apparatus, is the subject of the framework's probability law and measurement assumptions.\n\n**Zero-vector exclusion.** [[cex-nrqm-state-geometry-zero-vector-is-not-a-preparation]] refutes the informal claim that every vector determines a pure preparation with the zero vector as an empty preparation: the zero vector has no unit representative, the normalization map is undefined at it, and the zero operator has trace zero and is not a state. The refutation concerns the model's definitions and assignment; it is not an experiment and it makes no claim about nature.\n\n**Empirical neutrality.** Every item of this page is a finite computation or the refutation just described. None of them uses measured data, none adopts a law of nature, none predicts the outcome of any measurement, and none compares a model prediction with an observation. Agreement or disagreement with data would test the combined framework and apparatus assumptions, and is not established or claimed here.",
          "strategy": "Scope audit of the companion page: what each finite check verifies and the empirical neutrality of all of them.",
          "deps": [
            "ex-nrqm-state-geometry-normalization-and-phase-check",
            "ex-nrqm-state-geometry-finite-spin-bloch-ball",
            "ex-nrqm-state-geometry-fixed-mode-two-level-states",
