          "physical_scope": "The observable and outcome-law vocabulary of the non-relativistic quantum framework: the reading of the observable and probability postulate P2 as assigned sharp self-adjoint quantities with a declared unit, their spectral projection-valued measures, the outcome law of a preparation, the spectral-support reading of outcome sets, the conditional readings of mean and variance, and the affinity of the law under statistical mixing. This item fixes terminology and readings only: it adopts no assumption beyond P1 and P2, asserts no detector or apparatus realization, makes no joint-measurement claim, records no empirical data, and excludes the finite unsharp-effect extension of P2(iv), which belongs to the measurement-and-instruments page."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-nrqm-observables-born-outcome-law-properties",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Born outcome law: probability measure, mixtures, atoms and spectral support",
          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]] (P1) and the observable and probability postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]] (P2), and assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), the choice hypotheses of the spectral suppliers. Let $H$ be the state space assigned to a declared system, let $(A,u_A)$ be an assigned sharp observable of the framework with spectral projection-valued measure $E_A$ and spectrum $\\sigma(A)$, and let $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}$ be the outcome law of [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]]. Then for every preparation $\\rho$ ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]) and every Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$:\n\n(a) **Probability measure.** $0\\le\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)\\le1$ and $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(\\mathbb R)=1$; for pairwise disjoint Borel sets $B_n$ with union $B$ one has $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)=\\sum_n\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B_n)$; and for the pure preparation of a unit vector $\\psi$ the law is $\\mu^{A}_{\\psi}(B)=\\|E_A(B)\\psi\\|^{2}$. The declared outcome law of the model is therefore a probability measure on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$, not a formal weight attached to eigenvectors.\n\n(b) **Mixtures.** If $\\rho=\\sum_nw_n\\rho_n$ is a statistical mixture of preparations with weights $w_n\\ge0$, $\\sum_nw_n=1$, then $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)=\\sum_nw_n\\mu^{A}_{\\rho_n}(B)$ for every Borel $B$, the series converging absolutely: the outcome law of a mixture is the weighted average of the outcome laws, with classical weights.\n\n(c) **Exact values.** For $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ the probability of the exact value $\\lambda$ is $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(\\{\\lambda\\})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho E_A(\\{\\lambda\\}))$; it is zero for every preparation whenever $\\lambda$ is not an eigenvalue of $A$, and when $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue it equals the trace weight of the eigenspace $\\ker(A-\\lambda)$, with $\\mu^{A}_{T_{u,u}}(\\{\\lambda\\})=1$ for every unit eigenvector $u$.\n\n(d) **Spectral support.** $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)=\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B\\cap\\sigma(A))$ and $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(\\sigma(A))=1$: outcomes outside the spectrum of the observable have probability zero.\n\n(e) **No density and no discreteness are asserted.** The postulate asserts that $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}$ is a probability measure on the Borel sets and does not assert that it is a density with respect to Lebesgue measure or a finite or countable sum of point masses; the operational object named there is the probability of a declared Borel set, such as a finite-resolution bin, not the value of a density at a point ([[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]] clause (iii)). Which structure a particular pair $(A,\\rho)$ exhibits is a property of that pair and is not fixed by the adopted law.\n\n(f) **What is not claimed.** No mean, variance or higher moment is asserted by this item; their existence is conditional and is treated separately. No claim is made about the joint statistics of two or more observables, about the state after the outcome, about the physical mechanism producing an outcome, or about any concrete apparatus. The statement is conditional on P1 and P2 and on the cited mathematics.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "def-countable-choice",
