          "physical_scope": "Conditional statements, within the framework's observable and probability postulate, about the moments of an assigned sharp observable in a preparation. For a pure preparation represented by a unit vector ψ, the second moment is finite exactly when ψ lies in D(A), the mean is finite exactly when ψ lies in D(|A|^{1/2}), and for ψ in D(A) the mean is the quadratic form and the variance is the squared norm of the centred vector. For a density-operator expansion ρ=ΣₙwₙT_{uₙ,uₙ}, the mean is finite exactly when Σₙwₙ∫|λ|dE_{A,uₙ}<∞, and the second moment is finite exactly when every positive-weight uₙ lies in D(A) and Σ_{n:wₙ>0}wₙ||Auₙ||²<∞. A preparation need not have finite moments automatically. The item asserts no apparatus error, no disturbance and no empirical result."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-nrqm-observables-position-and-momentum-outcome-laws",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Position and momentum outcome laws and the Born density of the massive particle model",
          "statement": "Adopt the observable and probability postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]] (P2) and the kinematics and massive-particle model postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]] (P5) for a spinless particle of mass $m>0$ and action constant $\\hbar>0$, with state space $H=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ and position and momentum operators $Q_{j},P_{j}$ as in [[def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators]], and assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $\\mathcal F_{2}$ be the unitary Plancherel transform with the convention $\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi ix\\cdot\\xi}dx$ on $L^{1}$ ([[def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn]], [[thm-plancherel]]), and for a unit vector $\\psi\\in H$ let $\\mu^{Q_{j}}_{\\psi}$ and $\\mu^{P_{j}}_{\\psi}$ be the outcome laws of the assigned observables $Q_{j}$ and $P_{j}$ in the reading of [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]]. Then, using the numerical SI coordinates of [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]: $x_j$ and the marginal coordinate $t$ are read in metres, $\\xi_j$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, $p$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, $m$ in kilograms and $\\hbar$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. The assigned pairs are $(Q_j,\\mathrm m)$ and $(P_j,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}})$; the factor $2\\pi\\hbar\\xi_j$ has momentum units and $x\\cdot\\xi$ is dimensionless. Normalized position wave functions have units $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$, their Fourier transforms $\\mathrm m^{3/2}$, and the marginal densities below have the inverse units of their respective outcome coordinates.\n\n(a) **Position observable.** $Q_{j}$ is self-adjoint with domain $D(Q_{j})=\\{\\psi\\in H:x_{j}\\psi\\in H\\}$, its spectral PVM is given by $E_{Q_{j}}(B)\\psi=\\mathbf 1_{x_{j}^{-1}(B)}\\psi$, and\n$$\\mu^{Q_{j}}_{\\psi}(B)=\\int_{x_{j}^{-1}(B)}|\\psi(x)|^{2}\\,dx\\qquad(B\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\ \\text{Borel}).$$\nThe law is absolutely continuous with respect to one-dimensional Lebesgue measure. Its density is the $L^{1}(\\mathbb R)$ equivalence class $f_{j}$ given for almost every $t$ by\n$$f_{j}(t):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^{2}}|\\psi(x_{\\perp},t)|^{2}\\,dx_{\\perp};$$\nthis class is independent of the chosen representative of $\\psi$ and is the $j$-th marginal density of $|\\psi|^{2}$. The law is atomless: $\\mu^{Q_{j}}_{\\psi}(\\{\\lambda\\})=0$ for every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$. Moreover $\\sigma(Q_{j})=\\mathbb R$.\n\n(b) **Joint position law.** There is a unique regular projection-valued measure $G$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R^{3}$ with\n$$G(B_{1}\\times B_{2}\\times B_{3})=E_{Q_{1}}(B_{1})E_{Q_{2}}(B_{2})E_{Q_{3}}(B_{3})$$\nfor all Borel $B_{1},B_{2},B_{3}\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, and its scalar law in the preparation $\\psi$ is the measure of the density $|\\psi|^{2}$:\n$$\\mu^{G}_{\\psi}(B)=\\|G(B)\\psi\\|^{2}=\\int_{B}|\\psi(x)|^{2}\\,dx\\qquad(B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3}\\ \\text{Borel}).$$\nFor a density-operator preparation $\\rho=\\sum_nw_nT_{u_n,u_n}$, its joint scalar law is\n$$\\mu^{G}_{\\rho}(B):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho G(B))=\\sum_nw_n\\int_B|u_n(x)|^{2}\\,dx=\\int_Bf_{\\rho}(x)\\,dx,\\qquad f_{\\rho}(x):=\\sum_nw_n|u_n(x)|^{2},$$\nwhere $f_{\\rho}\\in L^{1}(\\mathbb R^{3})$ is a nonnegative density class of total integral $1$, by [F8] and Tonelli. Thus the model's joint position law in a spatial region $B$ has density $|\\psi|^{2}$ for a pure preparation and $f_{\\rho}$ for a density-operator preparation.\n\n(c) **Momentum observable.** $P_{j}$ is self-adjoint with domain $D(P_{j})=\\{\\psi\\in H:\\xi_{j}\\mathcal F_{2}\\psi\\in L^{2}\\}$ and Fourier representation $\\mathcal F_{2}(P_{j}\\psi)(\\xi)=2\\pi\\hbar\\,\\xi_{j}\\,\\mathcal F_{2}\\psi(\\xi)$; its spectral PVM is $E_{P_{j}}(B)=\\mathcal F_{2}^{-1}M_{\\mathbf 1_{m^{-1}(B)}}\\mathcal F_{2}$ with $m(\\xi)=2\\pi\\hbar\\xi_{j}$, and\n$$\\mu^{P_{j}}_{\\psi}(B)=\\int_{m^{-1}(B)}|\\mathcal F_{2}\\psi(\\xi)|^{2}\\,d\\xi,\\qquad m^{-1}(B)=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^{3}:2\\pi\\hbar\\xi_{j}\\in B\\}.$$\nThe law is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure in the momentum variable $p$. Its density is the $L^{1}(\\mathbb R)$ equivalence class $g_{j}$ given for almost every $p$ by\n$$g_{j}(p)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\hbar}\\int_{\\mathbb R^{2}}\\bigl|\\mathcal F_{2}\\psi\\bigl(\\xi_{\\perp},\\tfrac{p}{2\\pi\\hbar}\\bigr)\\bigr|^{2}d\\xi_{\\perp}$$\nwhere the class is independent of the chosen representative of $\\mathcal F_{2}\\psi$. The law is atomless; moreover $\\sigma(P_{j})=\\mathbb R$.\n\n(d) **Exact values.** For every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ one has $\\mu^{Q_{j}}_{\\psi}(\\{\\lambda\\})=0$ and $\\mu^{P_{j}}_{\\psi}(\\{\\lambda\\})=0$ for every preparation, although both observables have spectrum $\\mathbb R$. This says that each coordinate law is atomless; it does not remove $\\mathbb R$ from the law's Borel outcome space. Finite-resolution readings are the Borel bin probabilities of (a)-(c).\n\n(e) **Scope.** The statement is conditional on P2, on the massive spinless model of P5 and on the cited mathematics. The componentwise extension to the spin-$s$ model is not proved here; no joint statistics of a position and a momentum observable, no dynamics and no apparatus or empirical claim are made. The identification of the operators $Q_{j},P_{j}$ with concrete laboratory position and momentum measurements is a tested identification under P7 and is not asserted by this item.",
          "strategy": "direct",
