          "physical_scope": "Conditional consequences of the adopted observable and probability postulate for the spinless massive-particle model of the kinematics postulate: the position operators are self-adjoint with maximal domains and pure-preparation coordinate laws given by the marginals of |ψ|²; their unique joint position PVM has density |ψ|² for pure preparations and mixture density Σₙwₙ|uₙ|² for a density-operator preparation ρ=ΣₙwₙT_{uₙ,uₙ}; the momentum operators are self-adjoint with Fourier multipliers; |F₂ψ(ξ)|² is the frequency density, and the momentum-coordinate density is its marginal scaled by (2πℏ)⁻¹; exact values of each position and momentum coordinate have probability zero for every preparation although the spectra are all of R. The item is spinless, asserts no joint position-momentum statistics, no dynamics and no empirical result."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-nrqm-observables-states-separated-by-born-statistics",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Distinct preparations are separated by the statistics of a bounded sharp observable",
          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]] (P1) and the observable and probability postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]] (P2), and assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $H$ be the state space assigned to a declared system and let $\\rho\\ne\\sigma$ be two preparations of the system in the sense of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]] clauses (a)-(c). Then there is a bounded sharp observable of the framework, namely an orthogonal projection $P$ of rank one, such that the outcome laws of [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] differ at the outcome set $\\{1\\}$:\n$$\\mu^{P}_{\\rho}(\\{1\\})=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho P)\\ne\\operatorname{tr}(\\sigma P)=\\mu^{P}_{\\sigma}(\\{1\\}).$$\nMoreover for every rank-one projection $P$ one has $|\\mu^{P}_{\\rho}(\\{1\\})-\\mu^{P}_{\\sigma}(\\{1\\})|\\le\\|\\rho-\\sigma\\|_{1}$; consequently, if $\\mu^{P}_{\\rho}=\\mu^{P}_{\\sigma}$ for every bounded sharp observable $P$ (or merely for every rank-one projection), then $\\rho=\\sigma$.\n\nThe statement is conditional on P1 and P2 and asserts no procedure that determines a preparation from finitely many measurements, no apparatus realization, no error rate and no empirical result.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
