                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A conditional separation statement for the framework's preparations: two distinct preparations of a declared system assign different probabilities to some outcome of some bounded sharp observable of the framework, namely to the outcome 1 of a rank-one orthogonal projection; the difference is bounded by the trace-norm distance of the two states. The item asserts no finite-measurement procedure that identifies a preparation, no apparatus realization and no empirical result; it concerns the model's state space under the state and observable postulates."
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-nrqm-observables-scope-and-empirical-status",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "What the observable and Born-probability page adopts, proves and does not claim",
          "statement": "**Adopted, not restated.** The physical content of this page adopts the state and composition postulate P1 ([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]]), assigning unit rays and density operators as preparations, and the observable and probability postulate P2 ([[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]]), read in the vocabulary of [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]]: assigning to a declared system a self-adjoint operator with a declared unit, its spectral projection-valued measure, and the outcome law $\\mu^{A}_{\\rho}(B)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho E_A(B))$. The general outcome-law, moment and state-separation statements are conditional on P1 and P2; the choice hypotheses of the spectral theorem are carried explicitly, and the position and momentum statements additionally adopt the massive-particle model postulate P5 ([[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]]), assigning the spinless state space $L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$ and its position and momentum operators, as read through the magnetic-and-detection operator definitions. No additional physical postulate is introduced on this page.\n\n**What is proved.** [[lem-nrqm-observables-spectral-measure-atoms-and-eigenvalues]] identifies the atoms of the spectral measure with the eigenspace projections, so that an exact value has zero probability unless it is an eigenvalue. [[lem-nrqm-observables-spectral-support-and-outcome-law]] identifies the support of the spectral measure with the spectrum, giving the precise reading of the statement that an outcome lies in the spectrum. [[lem-nrqm-observables-outcome-law-affinity-and-trace-norm-continuity]] proves affinity under statistical mixtures and uniform trace-norm continuity. [[lem-nrqm-observables-moments-and-variance]] assembles the mean, second moment and variance formulas with their exact domain criteria, and [[lem-nrqm-observables-states-separated-by-bounded-projections]] proves that distinct states are separated by the law of a rank-one projection. The physics theorems read these results as conditional statements about assigned observables: [[pthm-nrqm-observables-born-outcome-law-properties]] (the law is a probability measure, affine in mixtures, atomic only at eigenvalues, supported in the spectrum and not assumed to have a density), [[pthm-nrqm-observables-preparation-moments]] (moments exist exactly under the stated domain hypotheses), [[pthm-nrqm-observables-position-and-momentum-outcome-laws]] (the joint position density $|\\psi|^{2}$ and the momentum-coordinate density obtained from the frequency density $|\\mathcal F_2\\psi|^{2}$ by taking its marginal and the Jacobian factor $(2\\pi\\hbar)^{-1}$) and [[pthm-nrqm-observables-states-separated-by-born-statistics]] (distinct preparations differ in some bounded observable's statistics; all such exact laws uniquely determine the preparation within the model).\n\n**No complete eigenbasis.** Following the correction recorded in the source audit, the page never asserts that a general self-adjoint operator has a complete discrete system of eigenvectors. The spectral projection-valued measure replaces the eigenbasis; discrete sums are the special case of an atomic law, and continuous parts are handled through measures. The expectation formula $\\langle\\hat O\\rangle=\\langle\\psi,\\hat O\\psi\\rangle$ is proved here only for $\\psi$ in the domain of the operator and through the spectral integral, not through an assumed eigenfunction expansion.\n\n**What is not claimed.** No item of this page asserts an apparatus realization of any observable, a joint measurement of two observables, a state update after an outcome, a density for an arbitrary outcome law, the existence of a mean or variance for an arbitrary preparation, a measured value, determination of a preparation from finitely many measurements, or a comparison with data. The finite unsharp-effect extension of P2(iv) and all instrument content belong to the measurement-and-instruments page, the componentwise spin extension of the position and momentum laws belongs to the kinematics page, and the identification of concrete laboratory procedures with assigned observables is a tested identification under P7, not a consequence of the postulates. Agreement of the conditional predictions with data would test the combined model and apparatus assumptions; it is not established or claimed here.",
          "strategy": "n/a",
          "deps": [
