          "physical_scope": "A finite model computation of position-bin probabilities in the spinless massive-particle model: for a normalized Gaussian packet in the first coordinate and an arbitrary normalized transverse factor, the probability that the first position coordinate lies in a Borel bin is the Gaussian integral over that bin, and the probability of an exact value is zero while every bin of positive length gets positive probability with the stated elementary bounds. The item is conditional on the model's postulates, uses no measured data and asserts no apparatus behaviour."
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-nrqm-observables-plane-wave-is-not-a-preparation",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The plane wave and the point-localized delta are not preparations of the model",
          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the observable and probability postulate P2, and the massive-particle model postulate P5 of the framework ([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]], [[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]], [[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]]), with Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]) for the cited spectral suppliers. Then: **every** bounded smooth function $\\psi:\\mathbb R^{3}\\to\\mathbb C$ that solves the free time-independent Schrödinger equation $-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}\\Delta\\psi=E\\psi$ pointwise determines a pure preparation of the model with position density $|\\psi|^{2}$; in particular the plane wave $\\psi_{k}(x)=e^{ik\\cdot x}$ with $k\\ne0$ is a preparation with constant position density $|\\psi_{k}|^{2}=1$, and the point-localized object $\\delta^{3}(x-X)$ is the preparation of a particle exactly at $X$.\n\n**Point-localized symbol.** Here $\\delta^3(x-X)$ denotes the evaluation functional $\\delta_X:C_c(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)\\to\\mathbb C$, $\\delta_X(\\eta):=\\eta(X)$, where $C_c$ is the space of continuous complex functions with compact support. An $L^2$ representation would be a class $f$ with $\\int f(x)\\eta(x)\\,dx=\\eta(X)$ for every such $\\eta$; these integrals are defined by Cauchy–Schwarz because $\\eta\\in L^2$.",
