              {
                "title": "B. Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 4 \"Dirac's Bra and Ket Notation\", Section 3, printed pages 11-13 (position states have infinite norm and are not allowed states; the position basis is an idealization)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/4de6d044fa9d7e5b8998c5f8ca984a42_MIT8_05F13_Chap_04.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A refutation under P1, P2 and P5, with Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice for the cited spectral suppliers, of the informal claim that a free plane wave, or the point-localized distribution symbol delta(x minus X), is a preparation of the massive-particle model with the idealized position density one: the plane wave is not an element of the state space and the exact-position symbol is not an element of the state space either, while the model's own exact-position statement is that the probability of an exact value is zero and finite bins carry the probability. The item refutes that specific informal claim; it asserts no empirical result and no claim about approximation schemes in which such objects are formally useful."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-observables-position-mean-without-variance",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A preparation with finite mean position and infinite variance",
          "statement": "Work in the spinless massive-particle model of P5 with $H=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ and position operator $Q_{1}$ ([[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]], [[def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators]]). Use the numerical SI spatial coordinates of [[pthm-nrqm-observables-position-and-momentum-outcome-laws]]: $x_1,x_2,x_3,t$ are read in metres and the assigned observable is $(Q_1,\\mathrm m)$. Thus the cutoff $|t|\\le1$ below means a distance of at most one metre; powers of $t$ in the profile apply to its numerical value in that fixed unit. The unnormalized $g_0$ is dimensionless, $Z$ has length units, and $g$, $\\varphi$, $\\psi$ carry units $\\mathrm m^{-1/2}$, $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$ respectively, so the mean is read in metres and the second moment and variance in $\\mathrm m^2$. Define the symmetric profile\n$$g_{0}(t):=\\begin{cases}1,&|t|\\le1,\\\\[2pt] |t|^{-3/2},&|t|>1,\\end{cases}\\qquad Z:=\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g_{0}(t)|^{2}dt,\\qquad g:=Z^{-1/2}g_{0},$$\nso that $g\\in L^{2}(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ has $\\|g\\|_{2}=1$; fix a unit vector $\\varphi\\in L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{2};\\mathbb C)$, choose a finite Borel representative as in the cited outcome-law theorem, and put $\\psi(x):=g(x_{1})\\varphi(x_{2},x_{3})$. Then $\\psi\\in H$ is a unit vector, $[\\psi]$ is a preparation, and for its position law $\\mu^{Q_{1}}_{\\psi}$ with density $|g|^{2}$:\n\n(i) The mean exists and equals $0$:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}|t|\\,|g(t)|^{2}dt<\\infty,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathbb R}t\\,|g(t)|^{2}dt=0,$$\nand $\\psi\\in D(|Q_{1}|^{1/2})$ while $\\psi\\notin D(Q_{1})$.\n\n(ii) The second moment diverges, so the variance of the position law does not exist:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}t^{2}\\,|g(t)|^{2}dt=+\\infty,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\bigl(t-0\\bigr)^{2}d\\mu^{Q_{1}}_{\\psi}(t)=+\\infty.$$\n\nThus a preparation of the model can have a finite mean position without a finite second moment: the moment identities of the framework are conditional on the domains, and a finite mean does not require membership in $D(Q_{1})$.\n\nThe example is a model computation; it asserts no measured value and no apparatus behaviour.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "cor-additivity-of-the-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral",
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
