            "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 11,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 7.4.2, printed pages 161-164 (Stern-Gerlach filters in series, the amplitude cos((phi-theta)/2) and the quarter-intensity example)",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 2 'Spin One-half, Bras, Kets, and Operators', Section 1, printed pages 1-7 (Stern-Gerlach boxes in series and the loss of memory of the first filter)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/a56c316e4ec548d8bd0a554fac9a74e8_MIT8_05F13_Chap_02.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "An exact finite model computation for a declared two-level spin-half system and declared ideal filters: spin-component readouts along successive magnetic axes, their outcome probabilities and conditional states, and the order dependence of the serial statistics. The computation idealises a Stern-Gerlach filter as a declared ideal sharp measurement of a spin component; it does not derive the spatial deflection, the field geometry or the detector response, reports no measurement and asserts no property of silver atoms or of the 1922 experiments."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-measurement-three-polarizer-chain",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Three ideal analysers in series: the quarter-intensity model computation",
          "statement": "Adopt the following finite polarization assignment and measurement rules as additional model assumptions for this example, using the declared-instrument vocabulary of [[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]]. These are stipulated for the finite polarization space, beyond the massive-matter sector of P1; they are not consequences of its massive-particle assignment. Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the mathematical suppliers. Fix a propagation label $k_0\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ relative to fixed Euclidean spatial coordinates, and a real orthonormal transverse pair $v_x,v_y\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $k_0\\cdot v_x=k_0\\cdot v_y=0$. Identify $H=\\mathbb C^2$, with its standard inner product and orthonormal basis $(e_x,e_y)$, unitarily with $\\mathbb Cv_x\\oplus\\mathbb Cv_y$ by $e_x\\mapsto v_x$, $e_y\\mapsto v_y$. Its state space is $D(H)$ and a unit ray $[\\psi]$ represents $T_{\\psi,\\psi}$ for $\\|\\psi\\|=1$ ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]], [[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]). The identification of these unit rays as polarization preparations is the adopted finite assignment, motivated by the source account in Binney and Skinner, Section 1.3.6, printed pages 14-15. It supplies no photon number or radiation dynamics. Angles are dimensionless, read in radians. For a real angle $\\alpha$ put\n$$e_{\\alpha}:=\\cos\\alpha\\,e_{x}+\\sin\\alpha\\,e_{y},\\qquad e_{\\alpha}^{\\perp}:=-\\sin\\alpha\\,e_{x}+\\cos\\alpha\\,e_{y}.$$\nThe **ideal analyser** $A(\\alpha)$ is the declared ideal sharp measurement with the two outcomes transmit and block, whose projections are $T_{e_{\\alpha},e_{\\alpha}}$ and $T_{e_{\\alpha}^{\\perp},e_{\\alpha}^{\\perp}}$ respectively, with outcome and update rules stipulated in [F2] below ([[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]] clause (c), [[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]).\n\nThe example takes the preparation $[e_{x}]$, the chain of analysers $A(0)$ with $\\alpha=0^\\circ$, $A(\\pi/4)$ with $\\alpha=45^\\circ$ and $A(\\pi/2)$ with $\\alpha=90^\\circ$, and computes:\n\n**(i)** The analyser $A(\\alpha)$ transmits the preparation $[e_{\\beta}]$ with probability\n$$\\mathbb P(\\text{transmit})=\\bigl|\\langle e_{\\alpha},e_{\\beta}\\rangle\\bigr|^{2}=\\cos^{2}(\\alpha-\\beta),$$\nand, when $\\cos^2(\\alpha-\\beta)>0$, after transmission the conditional state is the pure state of $e_{\\alpha}$.\n\n**(ii)** Consequently the chain $0^\\circ,45^\\circ,90^\\circ$ transmits with joint probability\n$$\\cos^{2}(0-0)\\cdot\\cos^{2}(\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}-0)\\cdot\\cos^{2}(\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\tfrac{\\pi}{4})=1\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12=\\tfrac14 ,$$\nwhereas the chain $0^\\circ,90^\\circ$ alone transmits with probability $\\cos^{2}(\\pi/2)=0$.\n\n**(iii)** Removing the middle analyser therefore changes the transmission of the chain from $\\tfrac14$ to $0$ within the declared model: the intervening declared measurement changes the state, and the later analyser sees the state selected by the earlier one, not the original preparation.\n\n**(iv)** *Model comparison only.* A declared classical amplitude model for a transverse field in the same plane, in which the analyser transmits the component along $e_{\\alpha}$ and the transmitted intensity is stipulated to be proportional to the squared modulus of that component, gives the same ratio $\\cos^{2}(\\alpha-\\beta)$ for a preparation along $e_{\\beta}$. The agreement of these two model computations does not establish that either describes nature, and it does not by itself establish field quantization ([[rem-nrqm-measurement-examples-scope-limits]]); no classical Maxwell theorem is invoked, and the field model is a stipulated finite-dimensional comparison. No transmission counts, detector efficiencies, backgrounds or sampling statistics are reported.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
            "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-hilbert-space",
