          },
          "physical_scope": "An exact finite model computation for a declared three-level system with a declared two-outcome instrument: a polarization-sensitive absorbing branch that leaves the absorbed level rather than an outgoing polarization state, a non-detection branch with amplitude suppression, and the non-repeatability of both branches. The three-level space and its level labels are a declared finite idealization of a fixed-mode absorbing detector, separately adopting its finite polarization/detector-marker state and instrument assignment, with Countable Choice assumed; no real detector, photon number, efficiency, dark count or measured rate is described, and no claim about photon absorption in nature is made."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-measurement-degenerate-coarse-versus-fine-update",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Coarse and fine declared measurements of a degenerate subspace: the Lueders update is not eigenvector selection",
          "statement": "Declare a three-level system with assigned Hilbert space $H=\\mathbb C^{3}$ and orthonormal basis $(e_{1},e_{2},e_{3})$, and put\n$$K:=\\operatorname{span}(e_{1},e_{2}),\\qquad P:=T_{e_{1},e_{1}}+T_{e_{2},e_{2}},\\qquad \\psi:=\\frac{e_{1}+e_{2}}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad w':=\\frac{e_{1}-e_{2}}{\\sqrt2},$$\nwith $T_{x,y}z=\\langle z,y\\rangle x$ ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]], [[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]). Declare the two ideal sharp measurements\n$$\\mathcal C:=(P,\\,I-P)\\quad\\text{(coarse readout of }K\\text{)},\\qquad \\mathcal G:=(T_{e_{1},e_{1}},\\,T_{e_{2},e_{2}},\\,T_{e_{3},e_{3}})\\quad\\text{(fine readout)},$$\nand consider the pure preparation $\\rho=T_{\\psi,\\psi}$ and the bounded self-adjoint observable, declared dimensionless with spectral unit $1$,\n$$W:=T_{\\psi,\\psi}-T_{w',w'}$$\nwith spectral projections $T_{\\psi,\\psi}$ at $+1$, $T_{w',w'}$ at $-1$ and $I-T_{\\psi,\\psi}-T_{w',w'}$ at $0$ ([[def-nrqm-measurement-framework-declared-measurements-and-instruments]], [[thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms]]). Then:\n\n**(i)** In the coarse measurement $\\mathcal C$ the outcome $K$ has probability $1$, and the conditional state is again $\\rho=T_{\\psi,\\psi}$: the coarse readout leaves the coherent superposition inside the degenerate subspace intact.\n\n**(ii)** In the fine measurement $\\mathcal G$ the outcomes $e_{1}$ and $e_{2}$ have probabilities $\\tfrac12$ each (and $e_{3}$ has probability $0$), with conditional states $T_{e_{1},e_{1}}$ and $T_{e_{2},e_{2}}$: the fine readout selects a basis state.\n\n**(iii)** For the observable $W$ the two declared measurements give different model predictions: after the coarse outcome the value $+1$ has probability $1$; the mixture $\\tfrac12T_{e_{1},e_{1}}+\\tfrac12T_{e_{2},e_{2}}$ (the discarded ensemble of the fine measurement) assigns probability $\\tfrac12$ to $+1$ and $\\tfrac12$ to $-1$. Hence the coarse Lueders update is not equivalent to selecting one eigenvector of the degenerate subspace with probability $\\tfrac12$ and forgetting the other: that replacement destroys the coherence of $\\psi$ and changes the statistics of $W$.\n\n**(iv)** The fine measurement refines the coarse outcome structure: grouping outcomes $e_{1},e_{2}$ gives $K$, and outcome $e_{3}$ gives $K^{\\perp}$, since $P=T_{e_{1},e_{1}}+T_{e_{2},e_{2}}$ and $I-P=T_{e_{3},e_{3}}$. It is nevertheless a different declared measurement with more detailed outcomes. The coarse outcome \"the system is in $K$\" does not itself report whether the fine outcome was $e_{1}$ or $e_{2}$; replacing the coarse Lueders update by the discarded fine ensemble is a different model, with the observable consequence exhibited in (iii). No apparatus is asserted to realize either declared measurement, and no empirical comparison is made.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
            "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-inner-product-space",
