              {
                "title": "B. Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 7 'Two State Systems', Sections 2-3, chapter pages 2-6 (spin precession and the general time-independent two-state Hamiltonian with its precession frequency)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/7c376a3b5b27e5ea586a7e37b85a05d3_MIT8_05F13_Chap_07.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 2.2, printed pages 21-23 (energy eigenstates acquire phases e^{-iEt/hbar}; general states evolve as finite superpositions with time-dependent relative phases)",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A finite, exact model computation in the two-level state space of the framework: the assigned energy observable with two distinct energies in joules, its unitary flow, the exact evolution of a pure preparation, the outcome laws and expectations of three bounded self-adjoint observables, the resulting rotation of the expectation-value triple, and the explicit period of the laws. The energies are model parameters; the item asserts no apparatus realization, no detection model and no empirical value, and it identifies the two-level system with no specific physical species."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-finite-stationary-superposition",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A finite superposition of energy eigenrays: exact phases, laws and return times",
          "statement": "Let $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be the state space of a declared isolated system of the framework ([[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]), let $(H_{\\mathrm{op}},u_H)$ be its assigned energy observable with spectral projection-valued measure $E_H$ and flow $U(t)=\\exp(-itH_{\\mathrm{op}}/\\hbar)$ ([[def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution]]), and let $u_{1},\\dots,u_{N}$ be a finite orthonormal family of eigenvectors of $H_{\\mathrm{op}}$ with real eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1},\\dots,\\lambda_{N}$: $H_{\\mathrm{op}}u_{n}=\\lambda_{n}u_{n}$ for $n=1,\\dots,N$. Fix coefficients $c_{n}\\in\\mathbb C$ and the nonzero vector\n$$\\psi_{0}=\\sum_{n=1}^{N}c_{n}u_{n},\\qquad S:=\\{n:c_{n}\\ne0\\}.$$\nFor a nonzero vector $\\phi$, write $\\mu^{A}_{[\\phi]}$ for the outcome law of the pure preparation $[\\phi]$, computed on the unit representative $\\phi/\\|\\phi\\|$ as in [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] clause (b) and [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-state-vectors-and-rays]].\nThen, with $\\psi(t)=U(t)\\psi_{0}$:\n\n(i) $\\psi(t)=\\sum_{n\\in S}c_{n}e^{-i\\lambda_{n}t/\\hbar}u_{n}$ for every $t$, and $\\|\\psi(t)\\|=\\|\\psi_{0}\\|$; if $S$ has one element, or more generally if all $\\lambda_{n}$ with $n\\in S$ coincide, the ray $[\\psi(t)]$ is constant.\n\n(ii) For every bounded assigned sharp observable $(A,u_{A})$ and every Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, the outcome law in the preparation $[\\psi(t)]$ is\n$$\\mu^{A}_{[\\psi(t)]}(B)=\\frac{1}{\\|\\psi_{0}\\|^{2}}\\sum_{m,n\\in S}c_{n}\\overline{c_{m}}\\,e^{-i(\\lambda_{n}-\\lambda_{m})t/\\hbar}\\,\\langle E_{A}(B)u_{n},u_{m}\\rangle .$$\nThus the probability is a finite trigonometric polynomial in $t$ with frequencies $(\\lambda_{n}-\\lambda_{m})/\\hbar$.\n\n(iii) For $T>0$ the following are equivalent: (a) $[\\psi(t+T)]=[\\psi(t)]$ for every $t$; (b) $e^{-i\\lambda_{n}T/\\hbar}$ is independent of $n\\in S$; (c) $(\\lambda_{m}-\\lambda_{n})T/\\hbar\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ for all $m,n\\in S$.\n\n(iv) Consequently a positive return time exists if and only if there are $\\omega_{0}>0$ and integers $k_{n}$ ($n\\in S$) with $(\\lambda_{n}-\\lambda_{n_{0}})/\\hbar=\\omega_{0}k_{n}$ for all $n\\in S$, where $n_{0}$ is any fixed element of $S$; and then $T=2\\pi/\\omega_{0}$ is a return time.\n\n(v) Two-energy case $N=2$, $\\lambda_{1}\\ne\\lambda_{2}$, $c_{1}c_{2}\\ne0$: the positive return times are exactly $T=2\\pi\\hbar k/|\\lambda_{1}-\\lambda_{2}|$, $k=1,2,3,\\dots$; the least return time is $2\\pi\\hbar/|\\lambda_{1}-\\lambda_{2}|$, which is the period $2\\pi/|\\omega|$ of the precession example with $\\omega=(\\lambda_{2}-\\lambda_{1})/\\hbar$.\n\n(vi) In the stationary case of (i), every outcome law of every assigned sharp observable is independent of $t$: $\\mu^{A}_{[\\psi(t)]}(B)=\\mu^{A}_{[\\psi(0)]}(B)$.\n\nThe computation is a model-internal one: the eigenvalues are parameters of the assigned observable, and no identification with a physical spectrum, apparatus or measured value is asserted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
