            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 11,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Quantum Mechanics (author-hosted lecture set), Section 1.3, printed pages 14-18 (PDF pages 17-21): two-slit cross terms and loss of interference under which-path monitoring; background, not a derivation of this finite phase ensemble",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/quantum-mechanics/qm.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Sections 1.2-1.2.1, printed pages 5-7 (PDF pages 13-15): alternative-route cross terms and coarse spatial averaging; background, not averaging of the preparation phase",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "An exact computation in the declared two-path preparation model of the pair for a declared statistical ensemble of preparations whose relative phase is uniformly distributed over the finitely many n-th roots of unity, in the case of orthogonal path components: the averaged bin law equals the which-path law with the norm weights, and the cross term averages to zero bin by bin. The example is a conditional computation in the declared model; it asserts no decoherence mechanism, no environmental model, no detector response, no counts and no empirical result, and it does not cover the continuous uniform phase distribution."
        },
        {
          "id": "exp-nrqm-interference-bach-electron-double-slit",
          "kind": "experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Reported controlled double-slit electron diffraction with single-electron detection (Bach, Pope, Liou and Batelaan)",
          "statement": "The paper reports, and the retained figures show, slit-selective probability\ndistributions: a single-slit central diffraction order in the P1 and P2\nconfigurations and a double-slit distribution in the P12 configuration, with\nweak double-slit contributions visible in the first diffraction order of the\nnominal single-slit configurations, which the paper attributes to the finite\nseparation between the mask and the double-slit. For the mask-centred build-up\nthe figure captions report accumulated localized-event counts of 2, 7, 209,\n1004 and 6235 in five successive images of the central five orders, the paper\ndescribing the earliest images as appearing random and a pattern becoming\ndiscernible at the largest counts, with a clearly visible pattern in the final\nimage. Each reported detection is a localized blob whose size represents the\nlocalization error of the detection scheme; the accumulated distribution, not\nany single event, carries the reported pattern.",
          "strategy": "n/a",
          "deps": [
            "post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Bach, D. Pope, S.-H. Liou and H. Batelaan, Controlled double-slit electron diffraction, arXiv:1210.6243v1, 22 October 2012 (8 pages); also published in New Journal of Physics 15 (2013) 033018. This item reports the exact preprint version retrieved and read: Sections 1-4 and figures 1-3, printed pages 1-8",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1210.6243v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A faithful report of the primary preprint R. Bach, D. Pope, S.-H. Liou and H. Batelaan, 'Controlled double-slit electron diffraction', arXiv:1210.6243v1 (22 October 2012): the reported apparatus, procedure, slit-selective distributions and single-electron event build-up, with the report's disclosed and undisclosed limitations. The item reports the source's observations and its qualified interpretation; it does not reanalyze the data, does not establish trial independence or single-electron occupancy, records no uncertainty budget beyond what the source supplies, and draws no conclusion about any physical theory.",
          "empirical_result": {
            "observation": "The report describes a 600 eV electron beam transmitted through a nanofabricated double-slit (two 50 nm slits, 280 nm center-to-center separation) and detected on a microchannel-plate/phosphor screen imaged by a CCD camera. Sweeping a movable mask downstream of the slit produces the slit-selective distributions the paper labels P1 (one slit), P12 (both slits) and P2 (the other slit); the paper reports weak double-slit contributions in the first diffraction order of the nominal single-slit configurations, attributed to the finite mask-to-slit separation. With the mask centred and the source intensity reduced to a detection rate of about 1 Hz, localized events were accumulated for about 2 hours; the figure captions report 2, 7, 209, 1004 and 6235 accumulated localized events in five successive central-five-order images, in which a diffraction pattern becomes discernible at the largest counts.",
            "uncertainty": "The report supplies no numerical uncertainty budget: no calibrated detector efficiency or background map, no numerical localization standard deviation (the blob size is said to represent the localization error, without a value), no arrival-process law or test of mutual independence of detections, no event-selection error rate, no statistical significance, confidence interval or fitted competing-model comparison, and no uncertainty on beam energy, slit dimensions or mask position. A source inconsistency in the mask height (5 micrometre by 10 micrometre in the body versus 5 micrometre by 20 micrometre in the figure 1 caption) is unresolved. The statements that at most one electron is present and that electron-electron interactions are eliminated rest on an average-separation estimate (2.3e6 m) rather than on a bound on two-electron occupancy. Approximate rate and duration values are not converted into an exact trial count, efficiency or calibrated rate.",
            "conditions": "Reported apparatus and settings: thermionic tungsten filament with electrostatic lenses producing a 600 eV beam; a 2 micrometre by 10 micrometre collimation aperture 16.5 cm from the source; the double-slit 30.5 cm beyond the collimation aperture, milled by a 30 keV Ga+ focused-ion-beam system into a 100 nm silicon-nitride membrane coated with about 2 nm of gold, with slits 50 nm wide, 280 nm centre-to-centre, 4 micrometre tall with a 150 nm support midway; a movable mask 5 micrometre wide (height given inconsistently as 10 or 20 micrometre) placed 230 micrometre downstream and driven by a piezoelectric actuator; electrostatic quadrupole magnification; a two-dimensional microchannel plate and phosphor screen imaged by a CCD camera, with each localized event recorded by a blob-detection scheme; mask-centred event accumulation at about 1 Hz over about 2 hours. These are reported specifications and settings, not independently recalibrated measurements.",
            "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1210.6243"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-nrqm-interference-examples-scope-limits",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "What the worked items and the reported experiment of the examples page establish",
          "statement": "**The two computations.** [[ex-nrqm-interference-ideal-flat-two-path-screen-distribution]]\ncomputes, in the declared two-path model with flat product-form amplitudes, the\nexact coherent and which-path probabilities of the overlap and outer regions of\nthe declared detection coordinate: at relative phase $\\pi$ the coherent law\nassigns probability zero to the whole overlap region while the balanced\nwhich-path law assigns it positive probability, and a slab bin\n$B_1\\times\\mathbb R^2$ has positive coherent probability at that phase\nexactly when $|B_1\\cap(I_1\\triangle I_2)|>0$, where $I_1,I_2$ are the\ndeclared intervals and $I_1\\triangle I_2$ is their symmetric difference\n(the two outer regions). Mere nonempty intersection does not suffice.\n[[ex-nrqm-interference-uniform-phase-averaging]]\nshows that for orthogonal path components a declared ensemble whose relative\nphase is uniformly distributed over the finitely many $n$-th roots of unity has\nthe which-path law with the norm weights as its averaged law. Both are\nconditional computations in the declared model under all the stated supplier\nhypotheses, including Countable Choice and Choice, and the pair's detection laws\n([[pthm-nrqm-interference-coherent-and-mixture-detection-laws]]); neither is a\ndynamics, an environmental decoherence model, a detector response or a\nstatement about an apparatus.\n\n**The reported experiment.** [[exp-nrqm-interference-bach-electron-double-slit]]\nreports the primary preprint of Bach, Pope, Liou and Batelaan: a 600 eV\nelectron beam through a nanofabricated double-slit with slit-selective\ndistributions and a single-electron event build-up over 2, 7, 209, 1004 and\n6235 accumulated localized events. The report supplies no calibrated\nefficiency or background, no numerical localization error, no uncertainty for\nits apparatus parameters, no occupancy bound and no statistical comparison\nwith a specified competing model; the mask-height inconsistency between its\nbody and its figure caption is recorded as unresolved. The item therefore\nsupports a qualitative operational account only, and its agreement with any\nmodel is evidence about the combined model and apparatus assumptions, not a\nproof of the framework, not a demonstration that every event is independent,\nand not a demonstration that only one electron is in the apparatus at a time.\n\n**What the page does not address.** No photon experiment is reported here; a\nsingle-photon polarization or counting experiment belongs to the photon and\nradiation page, and the classical wave comparison for light is a separate\ndeclared model. No experiment testing the which-path or marker variants of the\narrangement is reported. No claim is made that the finite sample of the\nreported experiment would distinguish the coherent from the which-path\nrecorded law; [[texp-nrqm-interference-finite-sample-fringe-failure]] shows\nthat both can fail or pass a finite-sample criterion and that coincident\nrecorded laws are indistinguishable by any criterion.\n\n**Evidence status.** The page combines exact conditional computations with one\nfaithfully reported experiment. It contains no simulation presented as data,\nno invented count, precision or significance, and no conclusion about nature;\nthe empirical qualifications of the report are carried in its Observations and\nUncertainty sections and are not strengthened here.",
          "strategy": "n/a",
          "deps": [
            "ex-nrqm-interference-ideal-flat-two-path-screen-distribution",
            "ex-nrqm-interference-uniform-phase-averaging",
            "exp-nrqm-interference-bach-electron-double-slit",
