          "dependency_level": 10,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Quantum Mechanics (author-hosted lecture set), Section 3.4.1, printed pages 84-85 (PDF pages 87-88): the variance of an observable in a state defined as the uncertainty, the eigenstate case and the position-momentum example",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/quantum-mechanics/qm.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 5 'Uncertainty Principle and Compatible Observables', Section 1, printed pages 1-3: the uncertainty defined as the norm of the centered vector and the identity (Delta A)^2 = <A^2> - <A>^2",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/005979fa741c3ea2e0430456b70caf93_MIT8_05F13_Chap_05.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, Section 3.1, printed pages 88-95 (PDF pages 100-107): expectation values and moments of a self-adjoint operator in a state",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The reading of preparation variance and preparation spread for an assigned sharp observable of the framework: the variance and standard deviation of the outcome law of a preparation, their units, their existence hypotheses through the moment theorem, the centered-vector form for pure preparations of domain vectors, and the resulting definition of the product of two spreads. The item fixes terminology and readings of P1 and P2 only; it asserts no apparatus resolution, no measurement error, no disturbance, no joint measurement, no dynamics and no empirical result, and it does not claim that any preparation has finite moments automatically."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-uncertainty-bounded-borel-calculus-of-commuting-operators",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Bounded Borel calculus of strongly commuting self-adjoint operators",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the Axiom of Countable Choice\n([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a nonzero\ncomplex Hilbert space and let $A,B$ be self-adjoint operators on $H$ with\nspectral projection-valued measures $E_{A},E_{B}$ that strongly commute in the\nsense of [[def-nrqm-uncertainty-strong-spectral-commutation]] clause (b):\n$$E_{A}(\\Lambda)E_{B}(\\Sigma)=E_{B}(\\Sigma)E_{A}(\\Lambda)\\qquad\\text{for all Borel }\\Lambda,\\Sigma\\subseteq\\mathbb R .$$\nFor a bounded Borel function $h$ write $h(A):=\\int h\\,dE_{A}$ and\n$h(B):=\\int h\\,dE_{B}$ for its operators under the bounded Borel functional\ncalculus ([[thm-bounded-borel-pvm-integral]]). Then:\n\n(a) for all bounded Borel $f,g:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$,\n$$f(A)g(B)=g(B)f(A);$$\n\n(b) for every bounded Borel $f$ one has $f(B)D(A)\\subseteq D(A)$, and\n$$A\\,f(B)x=f(B)Ax\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in D(A);$$\nthe same statement holds with the roles of $A$ and $B$ exchanged, and it is\nalso true with $f(B)$ replaced by any bounded linear combination or norm limit\nof bounded Borel functions of $B$;\n\n(c) for every bounded Borel $f$ the bounded operator $f(B)$ commutes with all\nspectral projections of $A$: $f(B)E_{A}(\\Lambda)=E_{A}(\\Lambda)f(B)$ for every\nBorel $\\Lambda\\subseteq\\mathbb R$. Consequently, for real-valued bounded Borel\n$f$ the pair $(f(A),B)$ strongly commutes: the map\n$F(\\Lambda):=E_{A}(f^{-1}(\\Lambda))$ is a projection-valued measure on the\nBorel sets of $\\mathbb R$ representing the bounded self-adjoint operator\n$f(A)$, and $F(\\Lambda)E_{B}(\\Sigma)=E_{B}(\\Sigma)F(\\Lambda)$ for all Borel\n$\\Lambda,\\Sigma$; hence also $(f(A),g(B))$ strongly commutes for real-valued\nbounded Borel $f,g$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
