          "dependency_level": 9,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, §3.1 printed pages 37-41 (PDF pages 45-49): the quadratic potential as the equilibrium approximation, the Hamiltonian (3.1), the length scale (3.15) and the stationary states",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 6 'Quantum Dynamics', §1 printed pages 1-9: the quantum harmonic oscillator Hamiltonian, its length scale and its spectrum",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/79a8091ef4f18d8e3ff76a097e8db33c_MIT8_05F13_Chap_06.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "G. Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 1st edition 2009, §8.3 printed pages 178-179 (PDF pages 190-191): the oscillator as an exactly solvable self-adjoint operator and the polynomial-Gaussian eigenbasis",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The one-dimensional and isotropic three-dimensional quadratic-potential (harmonic) oscillator model of a spinless massive particle of the non-relativistic framework: state space L^2(R,dx) respectively L^2(R^3,dx), position and momentum operators with declared units m and kg m s^-1, the assigned energy observable H_osc with unit J and a potential-energy convention in which V(0) is subtracted, and the assigned dimensionless excitation-number observable N_psi with declared unit 1. The one-dimensional energy operator is the self-adjoint realization of the formal differential expression -hbar^2/(2m) d^2/dx^2 + (m omega^2/2) x^2 fixed by the oscillator scaling theorem for the declared parameters m>0, omega>0 and hbar>0. The model is an adopted idealization of the quadratic part of a C^2 scalar potential-energy function V:R->R near a non-degenerate local minimum, after subtracting V(0); it asserts no measured values, no derivation of m or omega from any microscopic theory, no damping, driving or time dependence, no coupling to electromagnetic fields or radiation, no spin-dependent force, no anharmonic or relativistic correction and no boundary or many-body effect."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-nrqm-oscillator-energy-spectrum-and-completeness",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The energy observable of the oscillator model: spectrum, eigenbasis and outcome law",
          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the observable and probability postulate P2, the autonomous-dynamics postulate P4 and the kinematics and massive-particle model postulate P5, read through the oscillator model of [[def-nrqm-oscillator-framework-quadratic-potential-model]] with parameters $m>0$, $\\omega>0$ and action constant $\\hbar>0$, and assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $\\ell=\\sqrt{\\hbar/(m\\omega)}$, let $(g_n)$, $D$, $A$, $(\\psi_n)$ and $H_1$ be the Hermite family, the unitary, the dimensionless diagonal operator, the scaled eigenbasis and the energy operator of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-oscillator-scaling-and-exact-realization]] clauses (a) and (b), so that $\\psi_n=Dg_n$ and $(H_1,\\mathrm J)$ is the model's one-dimensional assigned energy observable of the model definition clause (c). Put\n$$N_\\psi:=DND^{-1},\\qquad a_\\psi:=DaD^{-1},\\qquad a^\\dagger_\\psi:=Da^\\dagger D^{-1},$$\nwhere $N$ is the number operator of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-closed-ladder-domains]] and $N_\\psi$ is the assigned excitation-number observable with unit $1$ of the model definition clause (c); these are the number and ladder operators transported from that lemma, with domains $D(N_\\psi)=\\{Dx:x\\in D(N)\\}$, $D(a_\\psi)=\\{Dx:x\\in D(a)\\}$ and $D(a^\\dagger_\\psi)=\\{Dx:x\\in D(a^\\dagger)\\}$. Write $E_n:=\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)$ and, for the three-dimensional realization of the model definition clause (d), let $(H_3,\\mathrm J)$ and $(\\psi_N)_{N\\in\\mathbb N^3}$ be its assigned energy observable and its product eigenbasis. Then:\n\n(a) **Spectrum, eigenbasis and simplicity.** $D(H_1)=\\{f\\in\\mathcal H_1:\\sum_nn^{2}|\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle|^{2}<\\infty\\}$, the family $(\\psi_n)$ is a complete orthonormal family of $\\mathcal H_1$ and its algebraic span is a core for $H_1$, $H_1\\psi_n=E_n\\psi_n$, and the spectrum and eigenspaces are\n$$\\sigma(H_1)=\\{E_n:n\\in\\mathbb N\\},\\qquad \\ker(H_1-E_nI)=\\mathbb C\\psi_n\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb N);$$\nthe ground energy is $E_0=\\hbar\\omega/2$ and the gap between consecutive levels is $E_{n+1}-E_n=\\hbar\\omega$;\n\n(b) **Excitation reading.** $H_1=\\hbar\\omega\\bigl(N_\\psi+\\tfrac12I\\bigr)$ with common domain $D(H_1)=D(N_\\psi)$; $N_\\psi$ is self-adjoint with spectrum $\\mathbb N$ and eigenbasis $(\\psi_n)$; $a_\\psi$ and $a^\\dagger_\\psi$ are closed with $a_\\psi^{*}=a^\\dagger_\\psi$, and on the eigenbasis\n$$a_\\psi\\psi_n=\\sqrt n\\,\\psi_{n-1}\\ (n\\ge1),\\qquad a_\\psi\\psi_0=0,\\qquad a^\\dagger_\\psi\\psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}\\,\\psi_{n+1},\\qquad N_\\psi\\psi_n=n\\psi_n;$$\nthe eigenspace of $N_\\psi$ at each $n\\in\\mathbb N$ is exactly $\\mathbb C\\psi_n$, so its nonzero eigenvectors are precisely the nonzero scalar multiples of the $\\psi_n$; the outcome law of $N_\\psi$ in the pure preparation of $\\psi_n$ is the point mass $\\delta_n$;\n\n(c) **Energy outcome law.** For every unit $f\\in\\mathcal H_1$ the outcome law of the assigned energy observable $(H_1,\\mathrm J)$ in the pure preparation of $f$ is purely atomic with\n$$\\mu^{H_1}_f\\bigl(\\{E_n\\}\\bigr)=|\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle|^{2}\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb N),\\qquad \\mu^{H_1}_f(B)=\\sum_{n:\\,E_n\\in B}|\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle|^{2}$$\nfor every Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, and $\\mu^{H_1}_f(\\sigma(H_1))=1$; in particular the law has no mass outside $\\{E_n:n\\in\\mathbb N\\}$, and $E_n$ is an atom of the law of $f$ exactly when $\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle\\ne0$;\n\n(d) **Three-dimensional isotropic realization.** The energy observable $(H_3,\\mathrm J)$ of the model definition clause (d) has spectrum $\\sigma(H_3)=\\{\\hbar\\omega(M+\\tfrac32):M\\in\\mathbb N\\}$; for every $M\\in\\mathbb N$ the eigenspace $\\ker(H_3-\\hbar\\omega(M+\\frac32)I)$ has the orthonormal basis $\\{\\psi_N:N\\in\\mathbb N^{3},\\ n_1+n_2+n_3=M\\}$ and dimension $(M+1)(M+2)/2$; the ground energy is $\\tfrac32\\hbar\\omega$;\n\n(e) **Status.** Clauses (a)-(d) are conditional consequences of P1, P2, P4, P5, the adopted oscillator model and the cited mathematics. They concern the eigenvalue list, the eigenbasis, the excitation operators and the atomic outcome law of the model's assigned energy observable; they assert no measured spectral line, no numerical value of $m$, $\\omega$ or $\\hbar$, no apparatus realization, no selection rule and no transition rate. The identification of a laboratory resonance or spectral line with an eigenvalue difference of this model is a tested identification under the preparation and apparatus postulate P7 and is not a consequence of this theorem.\n\n",
