            "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-random-element-and-real-random-variable": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "N. Brunner, D. Cavalcanti, S. Pironio, V. Scarani and S. Wehner, 'Bell nonlocality', Reviews of Modern Physics 86 (2014) 419, Section I.A, PDF page 3: the local decomposition p(ab|xy) = integral of q(lambda) p(a|x,lambda) p(b|y,lambda), its factorability reading and the assumption that the settings are chosen independently of lambda",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1303.2849"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 6.1.4, printed pages 113-115 (PDF pages 121-123): response functions of the two stations on a common hidden-variable space and the expectation values they define",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "prop-nrqm-bell-local-representations-are-no-signalling",
          "kind": "proposition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Local-response representability implies setting-independent marginals",
          "statement": "Let $\\mu$ be a binary correlation model on finite setting sets $I,J$ with\noutcome labels $\\Sigma=\\{-1,+1\\}$\n([[def-nrqm-bell-binary-correlation-models]]), and suppose that $\\mu$ admits a\nlocal-response representation with response laws $(\\alpha_{i})$, $(\\beta_{j})$\nand probability space $(\\Lambda,\\Sigma_{\\Lambda},\\mathbb P)$\n([[def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation]]). Then:\n\n(a) for every $i\\in I$ the marginal law of the first station satisfies\n$$\\mu^{A}_{ij}=\\mu^{A}_{ij'}\\qquad\\text{for all }j,j'\\in J,$$\nand for every $j\\in J$ the marginal law of the second station satisfies\n$\\mu^{B}_{ij}=\\mu^{B}_{i'j}$ for all $i,i'\\in I$; that is, $\\mu$ is\nno-signalling;\n\n(b) consequently, if a binary correlation model is not no-signalling, then it\nadmits no local-response representation.\n\nNo physical assumption and no choice principle is used; the proposition is a\nstatement about the tables and their representation.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-bell-binary-correlation-models",
            "def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation",
            "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
