          "dependency_level": 2,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "N. Brunner, D. Cavalcanti, S. Pironio, V. Scarani and S. Wehner, 'Bell nonlocality', Reviews of Modern Physics 86 (2014) 419, Section I.A, PDF page 3: a marginal p(a|x) = sum over b of p(ab|xy) is independent of the distant setting y, and this is identified with the no-signalling property of the local decomposition",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1303.2849"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-nrqm-bell-local-representations-satisfy-chsh",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The CHSH bound for every binary correlation model admitting a local-response representation",
          "statement": "Let $\\mu$ be a binary correlation model on the setting sets $I=J=\\{0,1\\}$\nwith outcome labels $\\Sigma=\\{-1,+1\\}$, with correlations $E_{ij}(\\mu)$ and\nCHSH functional $S(\\mu)$ as in\n[[def-nrqm-bell-binary-correlation-models]] clauses (d), and suppose that\n$\\mu$ admits a local-response representation with response laws\n$(\\alpha_{i})_{i\\in I}$, $(\\beta_{j})_{j\\in J}$ on a probability space\n$(\\Lambda,\\Sigma_{\\Lambda},\\mathbb P)$\n([[def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation]]). Then\n$$\\bigl|S(\\mu)\\bigr|\\le 2 .$$\nThe statement is a theorem about the tables and their representation; it\nasserts no measurement, no dynamics, no spatial or causal structure, and it\ncontains no physics. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-bell-binary-correlation-models",
            "def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation",
