          "physical_scope": "A conditional finite-sampling analysis for a declared spin correlation arrangement: under a declared independent and identically distributed sampling model with n runs per setting pair, the sample CHSH functional is unbiased, has variance at most 4/n and satisfies the stated Chebyshev concentration inequality; threshold events on either side of 2 have explicitly bounded model probabilities, and every acceptance criterion that rejects a positive-probability outcome sequence fails with positive probability. The item asserts no measured count, no p-value, no power, no error rate, no sample size and no empirical conclusion; the independence and identical distribution of trials is the declared statistical assumption of clause (ii) of P7, not a consequence of the framework."
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-nrqm-bell-selection-loss-and-fair-sampling",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Selection and loss: the precise hypothesis under which the CHSH bound transfers to recorded pairs",
          "statement": "## Remarks\n\n- **Recorded tables are conditional tables.** The arrangement's laws\n  $\\nu^{\\rho}_{ij}$ of\n  [[def-nrqm-bell-declared-spin-arrangement]] clause (d) describe a declared\n  pair of local sign measurements for every setting pair on the declared\n  preparation. A comparison with recorded data ordinarily uses only the runs\n  in which both stations register an event; the laws of the recorded runs are\n  therefore **conditional laws** of a larger model, and they are not the\n  arrangement's tables unless a detection and selection model connecting the\n  two is declared. This is part of the preparation and apparatus discipline of\n  P7 clause (i) ([[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]).\n\n- **A sufficient condition for the transfer.** If the full tables admit a\n  local-response representation and the recorded runs are exactly those\n  specifications lying in a measurable selection event $D$ of the\n  specification space with $\\mathbb P(D)>0$ — membership is determined by the\n  specification alone, using the same event for every setting pair — then the conditional\n  tables again admit a local-response representation, with the same response\n  laws over the conditional measure, and hence satisfy the CHSH bound\n  ([[lem-nrqm-bell-setting-independent-selection-closure]]). This is the\n  mathematical content of a **setting-independent detection** or\n  **fair-sampling** hypothesis for the declared model class. It does not\n  require statistical independence of $D$ from the outcomes: reweighting the\n  specifications can change the tables.\n\n- **The condition is a hypothesis, not a theorem about detectors.** The\n  selection-closure lemma is a conditional statement about the declared model.\n  It does not assert that any laboratory detector, coincidence circuit or\n  post-selection rule realizes a selection event of the required form;\n  identifying a concrete apparatus with such a model is a tested\n  identification with uncertainty under P7 and carries its calibration and\n  control-experiment qualifications. Efficiency, dead time, misalignment,\n  background and drift each require their own declared model.\n\n- **No transfer without a selection model.** If the recorded sample is\n  selected by a rule that depends on the settings or on the observed outcomes,\n  the selection-closure lemma does not apply, and no claim is made here that\n  the CHSH bound constrains the recorded tables. Correspondingly, a deviation\n  computed from recorded pairs is a statement about those recorded tables and\n  their own declared model; it becomes a statement about the full arrangement\n  only through an explicitly justified detection and selection model; the\n  condition above is sufficient, not necessary. Finite-sample fluctuations\n  are a further, separate matter. The result\n  [[texp-nrqm-bell-finite-sample-chsh]] assumes $n$ trials per setting pair,\n  all $4n$ outcome pairs mutually independent, with each trial law equal to\n  the arrangement's table $\\nu^{\\rho}_{ij}$. It applies to recorded pairs\n  only when their declared sampling model satisfies those hypotheses. A\n  postselected law different from $\\nu^{\\rho}_{ij}$, random recorded trial\n  counts or correlations induced by selection require a separate analysis;\n  the cited result does not supply one. The singlet contrast itself is the\n  conditional model computation of\n  [[texp-nrqm-bell-singlet-arrangement-contrast]].\n\n- **Settings choice.** In a specified experiment model, let $Q_{ij}$ be the\n  actual conditional probability law of the specification given that setting\n  pair $(i,j)$ is chosen whenever that conditional law is defined. The\n  common-law condition required by a local-response representation is that\n  these actual conditional laws are all one common law,\n  $Q_{00}=Q_{01}=Q_{10}=Q_{11}$ ([[def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation]]\n  clause (c)); if a setting pair is never chosen, its law must still be\n  specified by the comparison model before the four-table representation is\n  applied. The possibility that a setting procedure could depend on the\n  specification does not establish that the actual conditional laws differ;\n  an analysis using a local-response representation must establish their\n  equality from its model and the relevant evidence. This page neither\n  assumes nor reports such a setting procedure or its conditional laws.\n",
          "strategy": "n/a",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-bell-declared-spin-arrangement",
            "def-nrqm-bell-local-response-representation",
            "lem-nrqm-bell-setting-independent-selection-closure",
