          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 11,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 10.1.2 printed pages 229-230 (PDF pages 237-238): for a pair of spin-half particles, the spin-triplet states have antisymmetric spatial wavefunctions and the spin-singlet state a symmetric one",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Skinner, Principles of Quantum Mechanics (Cambridge Part II, Michaelmas 2021), Chapter 7 'Identical Particles', pp. 4-10: exchange symmetry, spin, and the symmetric and antisymmetric decomposition of a two-particle state; Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/PQM/chap7.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240801085112id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/PQM/chap7.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The conditional model computation for two declared spin-half fermions with a finite orbital factor: the decomposition of the antisymmetric sector into spin-singlet and spin-triplet parts, the opposite orbital symmetry in each part, and the explicit components of an antisymmetrised product of two declared one-particle states. The computation is conditional on the adopted antisymmetric sector of P6 and on the declared finite orbital factor; it asserts no spin-statistics theorem, no interaction, no dynamics, no measurement and no empirical claim."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-nrqm-identical-particles-exchange-versus-coulomb-terms",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Exchange versus direct Coulomb terms: the pair-energy split of the two-orbital spin-half model",
          "statement": "Adopt the state and composition postulate P1 and the identical-particle and spin\npostulate P6 in the readings of\n[[def-nrqm-identical-particles-framework-species-sectors-and-preparations]],\nand assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Declare the following\n**two-orbital spin-half model**:\n\n(a) the species is a spin-half fermion with an arbitrary model-assigned mass\nparameter $M_{\\mathrm{sp}}>0\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, unused in this computation and\nwith no empirical value asserted; its orbital factor is\n$K:=\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\\{u,v\\}$ where\n$u,v\\in W^{1,2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)\\cap L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$\nare real-valued, normalized and orthogonal in $L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3})$\n([[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]],\n[[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]), with $uv\\ne0$ in $L^{1}$; the internal factor is $V=\\mathbb C^{2}$ with basis\n$(e_{+},e_{-})$; the one-particle space is $K\\widehat\\otimes V$ and $n=2$\nparticles are declared in the antisymmetric sector\n$\\mathfrak H_2^-=\\operatorname{ran}P_-^{(K\\otimes V)}$\n([[texp-nrqm-identical-particles-two-fermion-spatial-spin-symmetry]]);\n\n(b) write $E:=K\\widehat\\otimes K$ and let $J:E\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^6)$ be the restriction of the Euclidean product unitary $\\Theta_6$ ([[lem-nrqm-composites-euclidean-l2-product-unitary]]), so $J(a\\otimes b)(x,y)=a(x)b(y)$. Here $L^2(\\mathbb R^r)$ consists of almost-everywhere classes $[f]$ of complex Lebesgue-measurable functions with $\\int_{\\mathbb R^r}|f|^2\\,dx<\\infty$ ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]). In SI units, let $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be spatial coordinates measured in metres and define $W:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ by $W(z)=\\kappa/|z|$ for $z\\ne0$ and $W(0)=0$. The value at zero is an arbitrary representative on a measure-zero set. Here $\\kappa>0$ is an adopted parameter of unit $\\mathrm{J\\,m}$, with no relation to a charge assumed, so $W$ has unit $\\mathrm J$. Define the multiplication operator\n$$M_W:D(M_W)\\subseteq L^2(\\mathbb R^6)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^6),\\qquad D(M_W):=\\{[g]\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^6):[(x,y)\\mapsto W(x-y)g(x,y)]\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^6)\\},\\qquad M_W[g]:=[(x,y)\\mapsto W(x-y)g(x,y)]$$\nwhere $g$ is any representative. This is independent of the representative because $W$ is finite and measurable everywhere. The declared pair interaction is the compression of this multiplier to $E$: for $z,z'\\in E$, set\n$$q(z,z'):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^6}W(x-y)(Jz)(x,y)\\overline{(Jz')(x,y)}\\,dx\\,dy.$$\nThe normalized position-space orbitals have units $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$, and\n$D$, $X$ and the pair-interaction expectations below have units $\\mathrm J$.\nAll $Jz$ lie in $D(M_W)$, as verified below.\nFor the orthonormal basis $(b_1,b_2,b_3,b_4):=(u\\otimes u,u\\otimes v,v\\otimes u,v\\otimes v)$ of $E$, define\n$$Az:=\\sum_{j=1}^4q(z,b_j)b_j.$$\nThus $\\langle Az,z'\\rangle=q(z,z')$; $A$ is a bounded self-adjoint pair-energy operator on $E$, has units $\\mathrm J$, and has real matrix entries in this basis. It commutes with spatial exchange. The model's sector interaction is the restriction to $\\mathfrak H_2^-$ of $\\Theta^{-1}(A\\otimes I_{V\\otimes V})\\Theta$, with $\\Theta$ the finite-dimensional regrouping unitary of the symmetry-decomposition theorem. This restriction is well defined because the conjugated operator commutes with total exchange. On a factored preparation with unit $\\chi$ its expectation is $q(\\varphi,\\varphi)=\\langle M_WJ\\varphi,J\\varphi\\rangle$;\n\n(c) the declared pair preparations are the images under the inverse regrouping\nmap of the symmetry-decomposition theorem\n([[thm-nrqm-identical-particles-two-factor-symmetry-decomposition]]) of the\nfactored vectors\n$$\\Psi_{T}:=\\Theta^{-1}\\bigl(\\varphi_-\\otimes(e_+\\otimes e_+)\\bigr),\\qquad \\Psi_{S}:=\\Theta^{-1}\\bigl(\\varphi_+\\otimes\\sigma_-\\bigr),\\qquad \\varphi_\\pm:=\\frac{u\\otimes v\\pm v\\otimes u}{\\sqrt2},\\quad \\sigma_-:=\\frac{e_+\\otimes e_--e_-\\otimes e_+}{\\sqrt2},$$\nwhich are unit vectors of $\\mathfrak H_2^-$ by the cited thought experiment.\n$\\Psi_{T}$ is the parallel-spin (spin-triplet) preparation and $\\Psi_{S}$ the\nspin-singlet preparation of the declared two-orbital model.\n\nThe example computes the declared pair-interaction expectations\n$q(\\varphi_\\pm,\\varphi_\\pm)$ of the two preparations and shows\nthat the exchange integral $X$ separates them strictly.",
          "strategy": "direct",
