        {
          "id": "lem-nrqm-central-exponential-moments",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Moments of the exponential law on the half-line",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), the choice\nhypothesis used by the change-of-variables lemma cited below. For every real\n$\\lambda>0$ and every integer $k\\ge0$ the function $r\\mapsto r^{k}e^{-\\lambda\nr}$ is Lebesgue integrable on $(0,\\infty)$ and\n\n$$\\int_0^\\infty r^{k}e^{-\\lambda r}\\,dr=\\frac{k!}{\\lambda^{k+1}}.$$\n\nThe assertion is an identity for the Lebesgue integral of a nonnegative Borel\nfunction; no probabilistic interpretation and no normalised density are\nasserted. In particular no statement is made about any other exponent, about\ncomplex $\\lambda$, or about negative powers.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-real-gamma-function-by-the-euler-integral",
            "cor-real-gamma-positive-integer-values",
