        {
          "id": "def-nrqm-central-framework-central-potential-model",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The declared central-potential model: state space, assigned energy operator, spherical reading and bound-state vocabulary",
          "statement": "**(a) Framework data.** Adopt the state and composition postulate P1, the\nobservable and probability postulate P2, the autonomous-dynamics postulate P4,\nthe kinematics and massive-particle model postulate P5, the identical-particle\nand spin postulate P6, and the preparation and apparatus discipline P7\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-nonrelativistic-kinematics-and-massive-particle]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-identical-particles-and-spin]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]]). Fix a mass parameter\n$m>0$ and a spin label $s=0$, so that the framework's one-particle model\n[[def-nrqm-kinematics-framework-massive-spin-s-model]] reads its state space as\n$H_{0}=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ with the pairing linear in the first\nargument, its componentwise position family $Q_{1},Q_{2},Q_{3}$, its\ncomponentwise momentum family $P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}$ and the action constant\n$\\hbar>0$; the spin-zero internal factor carries the trivial representation of\nP6. Units are those of [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]: position is\nread in metres, momentum in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, energy and potential\nenergy in joules, and $\\hbar$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Assume the Axiom of\nCountable Choice and the Axiom of Choice as the choice hypotheses of the cited\nmathematical suppliers ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]).\n\n**(b) The declared central-potential model.** As an additional model\nassumption of the pair, adopt the **central-potential model**. Its declared\nstate space is $H_{0}=L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ of clause (a), with the\nposition and momentum families $Q_{j}$, $P_{k}$ of that clause\n([[def-nrqm-kinematics-framework-massive-spin-s-model]]). A **declared central\npotential** of the model is a function $V:\\mathbb R^{3}\\to\\mathbb R$ of the\nform $V(x):=v(|x|)$ for $x\\ne0$, where $v:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is real,\nbounded and measurable and $\\|V\\|_{\\infty}:=\\|v\\|_{\\infty}=\\sup_{r>0}|v(r)|$ is\nfinite; the assignment $V(0):=0$ is a declared convention that does not affect\nthe $L^{2}$ theory. The potential energy is read in joules. The model declares\nthe **Born position law** of the framework for a unit class $\\psi$,\n$$\\mu^{G}_{\\psi}(B):=\\int_{B}|\\psi(x)|^{2}\\,dx\\qquad(B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3}\\ \\text{Borel}),$$\nthe spin-zero reading of clause (b) of\n[[pthm-nrqm-kinematics-spin-s-outcome-laws]]. The pair asserts no derivation\nof a central force law from $H_{0}$ or from P5: the restriction to a declared\ncentral potential is an assumption of the model, and an assignment of a\nconcrete material system to the model is an identification in the sense of\nclause (iii) of P7 and carries the corresponding uncertainty.\n\n**(c) The assigned energy operator.** Let $\\mathcal F_{2}$ be the unitary\nPlancherel transform of\n[[thm-nrqm-composites-real-multipliers-and-free-bounded-potential-operators]]\nclause (b), $c:=2\\pi^{2}\\hbar^{2}/m$, and let\n$$H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}:=\\mathcal F_{2}^{*}M_{c|\\xi|^{2}}\\mathcal F_{2},\\qquad D(H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}):=\\bigl\\{f\\in H_{0}:|\\xi|^{2}\\mathcal F_{2}f\\in H_{0}\\bigr\\}$$\nbe the free operator of that clause with $d=3$ and $b=0$. With $M_{V}$ the\nbounded multiplication operator of clause (a) of the same theorem, the\n**assigned energy operator** of the model with central potential $V$ is\n$$H_{V}:=H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}+M_{V},\\qquad D(H_{V}):=D(H_{0}^{\\mathrm{free}}).$$\nThe declared energy unit is the joule. By clause (c) of the cited theorem\n$H_{V}$ is self-adjoint on $D(H_{V})$, bounded below by $-\\|V\\|_{\\infty}$; by\n[[lem-nrqm-central-free-operator-differential-identification]] the domain is\nthe Sobolev space $W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ and\n$$H_{V}f=-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}\\Delta_{w}f+Vf\\qquad(f\\in D(H_{V})),$$\n$\\Delta_{w}=D_{1}^{2}+D_{2}^{2}+D_{3}^{2}$ the weak Laplacian of that lemma. If\n$\\psi\\in D(H_{V})\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and $E\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy\n$H_{V}\\psi=E\\psi$, the time-independent equation\n$-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}\\Delta_{w}\\psi+V\\psi=E\\psi$ is an equality in\n$L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)$ and hence in distributions; it is a partial\ndifferential equation in the three spatial variables. If $V\\in C^{2}(\\Omega)$\non an open set $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3}$, then the class $\\psi$ has a\nrepresentative in $C^{2,\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ for every\n$0<\\alpha<1/2$, and the equation holds pointwise there. To justify this,\nwrite $f:=(2m/\\hbar^{2})(E-V)\\psi$, so $-\\Delta\\psi=f$ in distributions.\nOn every relatively compact open subset of $\\Omega$, the weak product rule\n(obtained by local smooth mollification of the $C^{2}$ factor and passage to\nthe $L^{2}$ limit) gives, for every multi-index $\\beta$ with $|\\beta|\\le2$,\n$$D^{\\beta}f=\\frac{2m}{\\hbar^{2}}\\sum_{\\eta\\le\\beta}\\binom{\\beta}{\\eta}D^{\\eta}(E-V)D^{\\beta-\\eta}\\psi\\in L^{2};$$\nthe factors $D^{\\eta}(E-V)$ are bounded on compact subsets and\n$\\psi\\in W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^{3})$, so $f\\in H^{2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$.\nIntegration by parts against compactly supported smooth test functions makes\n$\\psi\\in H^{1}(\\Omega)$ a local weak solution in the sense of\n[[def-local-weak-solution-for-a-divergence-form-operator]] for\n$L=-D_i(\\delta_{ij}D_j)=-\\Delta$, whose divergence coefficients are\n$a^{ij}=\\delta_{ij}$ and $b^{i}=c=0$. They satisfy the uniform ellipticity\ncondition of [[def-uniformly-elliptic-divergence-form-operator]] with\n$\\theta=1$ and have the required local coefficient derivatives of every\norder. The interior regularity theorem\n[[thm-interior-h-k-plus-two-elliptic-regularity]] with $k=2$ therefore gives\n$\\psi\\in H^{4}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$. Every ball compactly contained in\n$\\Omega$ is a bounded smooth domain and hence a $W^{4,2}$ extension domain by\n[[thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains]]. The higher-order Sobolev\nembedding [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]] with $n=3$, $k=4$, $p=2$\nthen gives a representative with continuous derivatives through order two and\nlocally Hölder-continuous second derivatives of every exponent\n$0<\\alpha<1/2$; this is the meaning of\n$C^{2,\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ here. These representatives agree on\noverlaps because they are continuous and represent the same almost-everywhere\nclass. Both sides of the equation are then continuous and agree almost\neverywhere, so they agree pointwise. The model adopts\n$(H_{V},\\mathrm J)$ as its declared energy observable; its self-adjointness is\nthe cited theorem's, and no further spectral property is presupposed here.\n\n**(d) The declared spherical reading.** Let $U$ be the unitary of\n[[thm-nrqm-coulomb-angular-radial-unitary-reduction]] from\n$H_{0}$ onto $K=\\bigoplus_{(l,\\bar m)}L^{2}((0,\\infty),dr;\\mathbb C)$, written\n$U\\psi=(u_{l\\bar m})_{l\\ge0,-l\\le\\bar m\\le l}$, and let $(Y_{l\\bar m})$ be the complete\northonormal family of that theorem; $l,\\bar m$ are integers, while $m>0$\ncontinues to denote the mass. For a fixed $(l,\\bar m)$, let\n$\\iota_{l\\bar m}:L^{2}((0,\\infty),dr;\\mathbb C)\\to K$ insert its argument\nin that coordinate and zero in every other coordinate, and define the\nisometric **spherical lift** $J_{l\\bar m}:=U^{-1}\\iota_{l\\bar m}$, so that\n$$J_{l\\bar m}u(x)=\\frac{u(r)}{r}Y_{l\\bar m}(\\Omega)\\qquad(x=r\\Omega,\\ r>0).$$\nThe **spherical sector** $(l,\\bar m)$ is the range of this lift. Its\n**admissible reduced domain** is fixed by the assigned energy domain:\n$$\\mathcal D_{l\\bar m}:=\\{u\\in L^{2}((0,\\infty),dr;\\mathbb C):J_{l\\bar m}u\\in W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C)\\}.$$\nThus every weak Cartesian derivative through order two of the lift must\nbelong to $L^{2}$ on all of $\\mathbb R^{3}$, including neighbourhoods of the\norigin. This condition and the global square integrability specify\nadmissibility at both endpoints; no independent half-line boundary parameter\nis part of the model. In particular $C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty);\\mathbb C)\n\\subseteq\\mathcal D_{l\\bar m}$ by clause (d) of the cited theorem. The\n**effective potential** is\n$$v^{\\mathrm{eff}}_{l}(r):=v(r)+\\frac{\\hbar^{2}l(l+1)}{2mr^{2}}\\qquad(r>0),$$\nwhose second summand is called the **angular-momentum barrier**; both\nsummands, and $v^{\\mathrm{eff}}_{l}$, are read in joules. For $E\\in\\mathbb R$,\nthe model's **declared radial equation** in the sector $(l,\\bar m)$ is\n$$u\\in\\mathcal D_{l\\bar m},\\qquad H_VJ_{l\\bar m}u=EJ_{l\\bar m}u\\quad\\text{in }L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{3};\\mathbb C).$$\nA **radial eigenpair** additionally requires $u\\ne0$. The associated\none-dimensional differential expression is\n$$u\\longmapsto-\\frac{\\hbar^{2}}{2m}u''+\\Bigl(v(r)+\\frac{\\hbar^{2}l(l+1)}{2mr^{2}}\\Bigr)u,\\qquad u:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb C,$$\nwhere $u''$ means the distributional second derivative on $(0,\\infty)$\n([[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]]). The expression\nalone, without the specified lifted domain, does not define a radial\neigenvalue problem. On $C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty);\\mathbb C)$, its equation\nwith right-hand side $Eu$ is equivalent almost everywhere to the declared\nradial equation above by the spherical-reduction theorem of this page;\npointwise equivalence additionally requires continuity of $v$. No full-domain\ndifferential reduction or endpoint classification is presupposed here.\nThe angular-momentum quantum numbers $l$ and $\\bar m$\nappearing in the reading are labels of the orthonormal family $(Y_{l\\bar m})$; the\nidentification of $l$ with a total angular momentum of a material system is a\nseparate assignment of the framework's angular-momentum items and is not made\nhere.\n\n**(e) Stationary and bound-state vocabulary.** A **stationary vector** of the\nmodel with potential $V$ and **energy** $E\\in\\mathbb R$ is a nonzero class\n$\\psi\\in D(H_{V})$ with $H_{V}\\psi=E\\psi$; the pair $(\\psi,E)$ is an\n**eigenpair** of the model. A **bound state** of the model is a stationary\nvector with energy $E<0$ whose representative is normalised, $\\|\\psi\\|=1$; its\neigenvalue is its **binding energy**, read in joules, relative to the\nchosen **energy reference** $0$. Here $E<0$ is a vocabulary convention;\nno assertion that $0$ is a spectral continuum threshold follows for an\narbitrary bounded central potential. A continuum threshold requires a\nseparate spectral determination for the particular operator. In particular,\nadding a constant potential shifts the free operator and its spectrum by\nthat constant without changing its eigenvectors. The model reads the energy\noutcome law of the pure preparation of a unit eigenvector with eigenvalue $E$\nas the point mass $\\delta_{E}$, the reading of clause (c) of\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]], and the\nposition density of that preparation as $|\\psi|^{2}$ by\n[[pthm-nrqm-kinematics-spin-s-outcome-laws]] clause (b), with $s=0$\nand the state, position and choice hypotheses of (a). These are\ndeclarations of the model under P2; they construct no sample, detector or\nmeasured value, and they assert no stability, decay or formation claim.\n\n**(f) Status and scope.** Clauses (a)-(e) fix the data of the pair: a declared\nstate space, position and momentum assignments, an assigned energy operator\nfor a real bounded measurable central potential, a declared spherical reading\nwith its effective potential and radial equation, and a stationary-state and\nbound-state vocabulary. They adopt P1, P2, P4, P5, P6, P7, the choice hypotheses of the cited\nmathematics, and the additional central-potential model assumption and\nspherical reading of clauses (b) and (d); in particular the central form of the\npotential, the model's use of the full three-dimensional space as its state\nspace and the identification of the spherical sectors with angular-momentum\nlabels are declarations of the pair. The model excludes from its scope, until\ntheir own items introduce them: the Coulomb potential $\\propto-1/r$ and other\nunbounded or singular potentials; spin and magnetic couplings; relativistic\ncorrections; finite nuclear size; the radiation extension, spontaneous\nemission and radiation reaction; external electromagnetic fields; and any\nmany-body or shielding modification of a central potential. No numerical value\nof $m$, $\\hbar$ or of any potential is fixed here, and no empirical result is\nasserted.",
          "strategy": "n/a",
