          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 12,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 9.3 printed pages 213-214 (PDF pages 221-222): the first-order amplitude a_k(t) (9.47) and the transition probability of equation (9.48) for a harmonic perturbation V(t) = V0 exp(i omega t), with the resonant sin^2 growth",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "First-order transition amplitudes and their error in the bounded-control driven model: the exact amplitude in an orthonormal family of H0 eigenvectors, its first-order term with the explicit Dyson remainder bound, the closed evaluation for a sinusoidal control split into co-rotating and counter-rotating parts with their bounds, the linear growth at resonance, and the resulting bound on the outcome probability. The theorem is conditional on a bounded self-adjoint control and an orthonormal eigenfamily of H0; it asserts no long-time limit, no Fermi golden rule, no continuum density of states, no transition rate, no decay and no empirical result.",
          "empirical_premises": []
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-nrqm-driven-dynamics-common-domain-propagator",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The common-domain driven model: strong propagator with domain invariance, and the driven oscillator as its declared instance",
          "statement": "Adopt the framework data and the declared driven model of [[def-nrqm-driven-dynamics-framework-driven-model]] in the **common-domain class** of clause (b)(iii): $\\mathfrak H$ is the model's state space ([[def-hilbert-space]]), $Y\\subseteq\\mathfrak H$ is a dense linear subspace that is itself a Hilbert space continuously embedded in $\\mathfrak H$, $D(H(t))=Y$ for every $t\\in I=[a,b]$, every $H(t)$ is self-adjoint as an operator on $\\mathfrak H$ ([[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]), and $t\\mapsto H(t)$ is of class $C^{1}(I;\\mathcal B(Y,\\mathfrak H))$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-operator-norm]]); put $L:=\\sup_{t\\in I}\\|H'(t)\\|_{\\mathcal B(Y,\\mathfrak H)}<\\infty$ and $A(t):=H(t)/\\hbar$. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Then:\n\n(a) *Well-posedness of the declared model.* There is a family $U(t,s)$, $a\\le s\\le t\\le b$ on $I$, of unitary operators on $\\mathfrak H$ with $U(s,s)=I$, the cocycle $U(t,r)=U(t,s)U(s,r)$, joint strong continuity in $(t,s)$, and:\n$$U(t,s)Y=Y,\\qquad \\|U(t,s)y\\|_Y\\le C_{I}\\|y\\|_Y\\quad(y\\in Y), \\qquad i\\hbar\\,\\partial_{t}U(t,s)y=H(t)U(t,s)y\\quad(y\\in Y),$$\nwhere the derivative is in $\\mathfrak H$ and $C_{I}<\\infty$ is independent of the endpoints and depends on the actual graph equivalence of $Y$ with $D(H(t)/\\hbar)$ as well as $I$ and $L/\\hbar$; this family is unique among bounded families with the same strong equation on $Y$, $U(s,s)=I$ and locally bounded strong orbits. The model's clause (c)(iii) declaration therefore has exactly one solution.\n\n(b) *Reverse and adjoint equations.* With $U(s,t):=U(t,s)^{-1}$ one has $U(t,s)^{*}=U(s,t)$ and $i\\hbar\\partial_{s}U(t,s)y=-U(t,s)H(s)y$ for $y\\in Y$; equivalently $i\\hbar\\partial_{s}U(s,t)y=H(s)U(s,t)y$. The domain maps $(t,s)\\mapsto U(t,s)|_{Y}$ are jointly strongly continuous in $\\mathcal B(Y)$.\n\n(c) *Declared instance: the driven oscillator.* Let $m,\\omega,\\hbar>0$ and let $(H_{\\mathrm{osc}},Q)$ be the energy and position observables of the one-dimensional oscillator model of [[def-nrqm-oscillator-framework-quadratic-potential-model]] clauses (b), (c) on $L^{2}(\\mathbb R,dx)$, with $Y=D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ carrying the graph norm, and let $f\\in C^{1}(I;\\mathbb R)$. Declare, as a further model assumption of the pair, the **driven oscillator family**\n$$H(t):=H_{\\mathrm{osc}}-f(t)Q\\qquad(t\\in I),$$\nread as the system's energy at time $t$, with $f(t)Q$ in joules for $f(t)$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-1}}$. Then for every $t$ the operator $H(t)$ is self-adjoint on the single domain $Y$, the family belongs to $C^{1}(I;\\mathcal B(Y,L^{2}))$ with derivative $-f'(t)Q$, and the hypotheses of clause (a) are satisfied with $\\mathfrak H=L^{2}(\\mathbb R,dx)$; consequently the driven oscillator has a unique strong propagator preserving $D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ and satisfying $i\\hbar\\partial_{t}U(t,s)y=H(t)U(t,s)y$ for $y\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$.\n\n(d) *Scope.* The theorem is conditional on the common-domain regularity class and on the cited oscillator construction; it asserts no existence statement outside that class, no explicit solution, no long-time or adiabatic statement, no measurement and no empirical comparison.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-nrqm-driven-dynamics-framework-driven-model",
            "post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition",
            "post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability",
            "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units",
            "thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-common-domain-nonautonomous-evolution",
