          "physical_scope": "The declared fixed propagation-mode polarisation model of the framework: a nonzero wavevector label, a declared real orthonormal pair spanning the real transverse plane with an optional common phase, the two-dimensional complex polarisation space with its unit rays as idealised one-photon polarisation preparations, and the two-outcome polarisation analyser family with the Born outcome law of P2 and the Lueders reading of P3 where used. The model is an adopted idealisation of a transverse mode with a definite propagation label; it is not a spatial wave function, has no position or momentum observable, is not the massless limit of the massive model, and asserts no localisation, no detector response, no loss, no efficiency and no empirical result.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, Section 1.3.6 printed pages 14-15 (PDF pages 22-23): the transmitting and blocking states of a photon, the amplitudes cos(theta) and sin(theta), the transmitted probability cos^2(theta), and the circular-polarisation alternative basis of the same two-dimensional space",
                "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II (Fall 2013), Chapter 6 'Quantum Dynamics', Section 5.3 printed pages 48-50: the single electromagnetic field mode and its quantum oscillator description",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/79a8091ef4f18d8e3ff76a097e8db33c_MIT8_05F13_Chap_06.pdf"
              }
