          "dependency_level": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "eta-orthogonality and orthogonal complements in a Lorentz vector space",
          "statement": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$\n([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]). Two vectors $u,v\\in V$ are\n**$\\eta$-orthogonal**, written $u\\perp v$, when $\\eta(u,v)=0$. For a subset\n$S\\subseteq V$ the **orthogonal complement** of $S$ is\n\n$$S^{\\perp}:=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,s)=0\\text{ for every }s\\in S\\},$$\n\nand for a vector $u$ one writes $u^{\\perp}:=\\{u\\}^{\\perp}$.\n\n**Elementary properties.** For every subset $S\\subseteq V$:\n\n- $S^{\\perp}$ is a linear subspace of $V$: if $v,w\\in S^{\\perp}$ and\n  $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, then for every $s\\in S$ bilinearity gives\n  $\\eta(v+\\lambda w,s)=\\eta(v,s)+\\lambda\\eta(w,s)=0$, so $v+\\lambda w\\in S^{\\perp}$.\n  In particular the zero vector lies in $S^{\\perp}$ and $S^{\\perp}$ is never\n  empty.\n- $S\\subseteq (S^{\\perp})^{\\perp}$: if $s\\in S$, then $\\eta(v,s)=0$ for every\n  $v\\in S^{\\perp}$ by the definition of $S^{\\perp}$, and symmetry of $\\eta$\n  gives $\\eta(s,v)=0$ for every $v\\in S^{\\perp}$, that is,\n  $s\\in(S^{\\perp})^{\\perp}$.\n- $S^{\\perp}=(\\operatorname{span}S)^{\\perp}$: the inclusion\n  $S\\subseteq\\operatorname{span}S$ gives\n  $(\\operatorname{span}S)^{\\perp}\\subseteq S^{\\perp}$; conversely, if\n  $v\\in S^{\\perp}$ and $u=\\sum_i\\lambda_i s_i$ is a finite linear combination\n  with $s_i\\in S$, then linearity in the second argument gives\n  $\\eta(v,u)=\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\eta(v,s_i)=0$, so $v\\in(\\operatorname{span}S)^{\\perp}$.\n\nIn particular, for every $u\\in V$ one has $u\\in u^{\\perp}$ exactly when\n$\\eta(u,u)=0$, that is, exactly when $u$ is null or $u=0$\n([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]).\n\n**A nonzero vector can be orthogonal to itself.** Take a basis\n$e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3$ of $V$ in which the matrix of $\\eta$ is diagonal with\ndiagonal entries $-1,1,1,1$; such a basis exists by clause 2 of the definition\nof a Lorentz form. Then $v:=e_0+e_1$ is nonzero while\n$\\eta(v,v)=\\eta(e_0,e_0)+2\\eta(e_0,e_1)+\\eta(e_1,e_1)=-1+0+1=0$, so\n$v\\in v^{\\perp}$ with $v\\neq 0$. This is exactly what fails for a\npositive-definite inner product and is why orthogonal complements in a Lorentz\nvector space need not be positive definite.",
          "strategy": "Bilinearity gives that S^perp is closed under addition and scalar multiplication (each condition eta(.,s) = 0 is linear), and the containments and the equality S^perp = (span S)^perp follow by linearity in the second argument. The final sentence is witnessed by the classification: any nonzero null vector v satisfies eta(v,v) = 0, so v lies in v^perp.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "K. Conrad, Bilinear Forms (orthogonality, radicals, inertia)",
                "url": "https://kconrad.math.uconn.edu/blurbs/linmultialg/bilinearform.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), sections 2.1-2.2 pp. 25-28 (PDF pp. 26-29); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Time orientation, future and past timelike/null/causal vectors, and the closed cones",
          "statement": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$\n([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]), with causal terminology as in\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], and let\n\n$$T:=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$$\n\nbe its **timelike set**. Components and closures refer to the topology obtained\nby using any ordered basis to identify $V$ with Euclidean $\\mathbb R^4$;\n[[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]], step 5.1, proves that the\nresulting open sets are independent of the basis. By that lemma the\nset $T$ has exactly two connected components, and each of them is nonempty,\nopen, convex and path-connected. A **time orientation** of $(V,\\eta)$ is a\nchoice of one of these two components. Fixing such a choice, write $C^+$ for the\nchosen component and\n\n$$C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$$\n\nfor the other component; thus $T=C^+\\sqcup C^-$ is a disjoint union and\n$C^+$, $C^-$ are exactly the two components supplied by the lemma. Relative to\nthis choice a vector $v\\in V$ is\n\n- **future timelike** when $v\\in C^+$;\n- **past timelike** when $v\\in C^-$;\n- **future null** when $v\\neq 0$, $\\eta(v,v)=0$ and $v\\in\\operatorname{cl}(C^+)$;\n- **past null** when $v\\neq 0$, $\\eta(v,v)=0$ and $v\\in\\operatorname{cl}(C^-)$;\n- **future causal** when it is future timelike or future null;\n- **past causal** when it is past timelike or past null.\n\nThe sets $\\operatorname{cl}(C^+)$ and $\\operatorname{cl}(C^-)$ are the\n**future closed cone** and the **past closed cone** of the time-oriented space\n$(V,\\eta,C^+)$. By [[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]] each of them\nis a convex cone, their intersection is $\\{0\\}$, and a vector is causal exactly\nwhen it is future causal or past causal, in which case exactly one of these two\nalternatives holds: no nonzero vector is both future causal and past causal.\nThe timelike vectors are exactly the points of $T=C^+\\cup C^-$, and\n$0\\notin T$ because $\\eta(0,0)=0$; since a null vector is nonzero with\n$\\eta(v,v)=0$, no vector is both timelike and null, so the classes above are\nmutually exclusive where the definitions say they are: no vector is both future\nand past causal, and no timelike vector is null.",
          "strategy": "Well-definedness rests on the components lemma: T has exactly two components, so 'a choice of one of them' names a definite pair {C^+,C^-}. The claimed properties of the closures (convexity, the intersection {0}, and exclusivity of the future and past causal sets) are proved in the components lemma via the orthonormal coordinate description {v^0 >= |v_vec|} and {v^0 <= -|v_vec|} of the two closed cones.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), sections 2.1-2.2 pp. 25-28 (PDF pp. 26-29); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Ch. 1 pp. 2-7 (PDF pp. 9-14)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, rev. 2020-12-10, sections 1.3-1.4 pp. 23-31 and Ch. 2 pp. 43-51 (signature (+---) converted to (-+++))",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The timelike set has exactly two convex components, described in orthonormal coordinates",
          "statement": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and let\n$(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an $\\eta$-orthonormal ordered basis. Write\n$v=v^0e_0+v^1e_1+v^2e_2+v^3e_3$ for the coordinates of $v$\n([[thm-unique-coordinates-with-respect-to-an-ordered-basis]]) and\n\n$$\\lvert v_{\\mathrm{vec}}\\rvert:=\\sqrt{(v^1)^2+(v^2)^2+(v^3)^2}.$$\n\nThen $\\eta(v,v)=-(v^0)^2+\\lvert v_{\\mathrm{vec}}\\rvert^2$, and the timelike set\n$T:=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$ satisfies\n\n$$T=T_+\\cup T_-,\\qquad T_+:=\\{v:v^0>\\lvert v_{\\mathrm{vec}}\\rvert\\},\\qquad T_-:=\\{v:v^0<-\\lvert v_{\\mathrm{vec}}\\rvert\\}.$$\n\nThe sets $T_+$ and $T_-$ are nonempty, disjoint, open in $V$ (for the metric\ntopology of the distance $d(u,w):=\\lVert c(u)-c(w)\\rVert_2$ induced by the\ncoordinates $c(v):=(v^0,v^1,v^2,v^3)$), convex and path-connected, and they are\nexactly the two connected components of $T$. In every $\\eta$-orthonormal\nordered basis the two displayed coordinate sets coincide with the two\ncomponents of $T$ in some order, and they may be interchanged relative to a\npreviously fixed naming of the components. Moreover\n\n$$\\operatorname{cl}(T_+)=\\{v:v^0\\ge\\lvert v_{\\mathrm{vec}}\\rvert\\},\\qquad \\operatorname{cl}(T_-)=\\{v:v^0\\le-\\lvert v_{\\mathrm{vec}}\\rvert\\},\\qquad \\operatorname{cl}(T_+)\\cap\\operatorname{cl}(T_-)=\\{0\\}.$$",
          "strategy": "In the orthonormal basis, eta(v,v) = -(v^0)^2 + sum_i (v^i)^2, so eta(v,v) < 0 iff |v^0| > |v_vec|, which is the displayed union; the two sets are disjoint and nonempty (e_0 lies in the first, -e_0 in the second). Convexity of T_+: for u,w in T_+ and t in [0,1], (tu+(1-t)w)^0 = t u^0 + (1-t) w^0 > t|u_vec| + (1-t)|w_vec| >= |t u_vec + (1-t) w_vec| by the triangle inequality; T_- is handled by applying this to -v. Convex sets are path-connected, and T_+ and T_- are open (they are the preimages of (0,inf) under the continuous maps v |-> v^0 - |v_vec| and v |-> -v^0 - |v_vec|). Since T is the disjoint union of two nonempty relatively open connected sets, it is disconnected and these are its two components: any connected subset of T lies in one of them, because a connected subset meeting both would give a separation, while a path in T cannot change the sign of the continuous function v^0 (which is nowhere zero on T) by the intermediate value theorem. The closures are computed by continuity of v^0 -/+ |v_vec|; their intersection consists of vectors with v^0 >= |v_vec| and v^0 <= -|v_vec|, hence of 0 alone. Finally, any two orthonormal bases are related by a linear isomorphism, and the components of T are intrinsic to eta, so the coordinate description agrees with the components up to the naming of T_+ and T_-.",
          "deps": [
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
            "cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm",
            "ex-convex-subsets-of-rn-are-path-connected",
