        {
          "id": "cex-sr-causal-vectors-are-not-closed-under-addition",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The causal vectors of a Lorentz vector space are not closed under addition",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'the set of causal vectors of a Lorentz vector space is a linear subspace, in particular closed under addition'. Counterexample: in an inertial frame with eta = diag(-1,1,1,1), let u = (1,0,0,0) and w = (-1,9/10,0,0). Then eta(u,u) = -1 < 0 and eta(w,w) = -1 + 81/100 = -19/100 < 0, so both u and w are timelike (u future, w past); but u + w = (0,9/10,0,0) has eta(u+w,u+w) = 81/100 > 0 and is spacelike. Moreover -u is causal but not future causal, so the future causal set is not closed under negation either. Closure of the full causal set under addition therefore fails; the future causal cone is closed under addition of future causal vectors, and that distinct statement belongs to the causality page.",
          "strategy": "Evaluate the diagonal form on the displayed vectors: the two timelike verifications and the spacelike value of the sum are finite arithmetic. The sentence about the future cone records that no claim is made here about sums of future causal vectors; that closure is proved later.",
