        {
          "id": "cex-sr-lorentz-form-is-not-an-inner-product",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Lorentz form is not an inner product",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'the Lorentz form eta of a Lorentz vector space is an inner product, i.e. a positive-definite symmetric bilinear form'. Counterexample: let (e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) be an eta-orthonormal basis, whose existence is the orthonormal-basis lemma. The vector e_0 is nonzero but eta(e_0,e_0) = -1 < 0, so eta is not positive definite and hence is not an inner product; consequently eta does not induce a norm, and the positive-definite inner-product theory (Cauchy-Schwarz, norms, orthogonal projection) cannot be applied to eta without additional argument.",
          "strategy": "A single evaluation refutes positive definiteness: e_0 is nonzero and eta(e_0,e_0) = -1 by the definition of an eta-orthonormal basis. The definition of an inner product requires positive-definiteness, so the refutation is immediate.",
          "deps": [
