        {
          "id": "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The passive boost is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map with inverse B(-beta)",
          "statement": "Let $B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ be the passive boost of [[def-lorentz-boost]] in an\ninertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$. For every\n$\\boldsymbol\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $|\\boldsymbol\\beta|<1$ the following\nhold.\n\n(i) $B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map:\n$\\eta(B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)v,B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)w)=\\eta(v,w)$ for all $v,w\\in V$,\n$\\det B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)=+1$, and $B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)(C^+)=C^+$.\n\n(ii) $B(\\mathbf 0)$ is the identity, and for every $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ the image\nof the frame vector $e_0$ is\n$B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)e_0=\\gamma\\,(e_0-\\beta_1e_1-\\beta_2e_2-\\beta_3e_3)$, so\nthe map $\\boldsymbol\\beta\\mapsto B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ is injective and\n$B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ fixes $0_V$; moreover every vector $y$ with\ncoordinates $(0,y_{\\mathrm{vec}})$ and\n$\\boldsymbol\\beta\\cdot y_{\\mathrm{vec}}=0$ satisfies\n$B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)y=y$.\n\n(iii) $B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta)B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\n=B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta)=\\operatorname{id}_V$; in particular\n$B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ is invertible with\n$B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)^{-1}=B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta)$, and $B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ and\n$B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ are inverse bijections of $V$.\n\n(iv) If $u$ is a future timelike unit vector with coordinates\n$(u^0,u_{\\mathrm{vec}})$, so that $\\eta(u,u)=-1$, $u^0>0$ and\n$|u_{\\mathrm{vec}}|<u^0$, then the boost with parameter\n$\\boldsymbol\\beta:=u_{\\mathrm{vec}}/u^0$ satisfies\n$B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)u=e_0$.\n\n(v) If a vector $x$ has $x^0>|x_{\\mathrm{vec}}|$, then the first coordinate of\n$B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)x$ is $\\gamma\\,(x^0-\\boldsymbol\\beta\\cdot x_{\\mathrm{vec}})>0$;\nif $x^0<-|x_{\\mathrm{vec}}|$ then that first coordinate is negative. In\nparticular $B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ maps the future open cone\n$\\{x:x^0>|x_{\\mathrm{vec}}|\\}$ into itself.",
          "strategy": "Proof plan, for beta not equal to 0 (the case beta = 0 is the identity, for which (i)-(v) are immediate). Put b := |beta| and e := beta/b, so (e_0,e) is an eta-orthonormal pair spanning a timelike 2-plane P, and V = P direct-sum P^perp with P^perp := {y : y^0 = 0 and beta . y = 0}, on which eta is positive definite. By construction B(beta) fixes P^perp pointwise (this is (ii)'s last clause) and on P its matrix in the basis (e_0,e) is [[gamma,-gamma b],[-gamma b,gamma]]. (i) For v = (v^0, v_parallel e + v_perp) and w likewise, eta(Bv,Bw) expands into the P-block computed by matrix multiplication, using gamma^2(1 - b^2) = 1 and gamma^2 b^2 + 1 = gamma^2, plus the P^perp-block and the vanishing of the cross terms (P perpendicular to P^perp); this gives eta(Bv,Bw) = eta(v,w). The determinant is the 2x2 determinant gamma^2(1 - b^2) = 1 times the identity on P^perp, hence +1. For the future cone: beta . x <= b|x_vec| < b x^0 whenever x^0 > |x_vec| >= 0, so x^0 - beta . x > x^0(1 - b) > 0; then B(beta)(C^+) is contained in C^+ (it is contained in the future open cone and connected), and since B(beta) is a Lorentz map with inverse B(-beta) (proved next) that also maps C^+ into C^+, one gets B(beta)(C^+) = C^+. The inequality beta . x <= b|x_vec| uses the Cauchy-Schwarz/triangle inequality for the Euclidean form, and (i) then follows from lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure. (ii) Evaluating the defining formulas at the coordinates of e_0 gives (gamma, -gamma beta_1, -gamma beta_2, -gamma beta_3); injectivity follows because the spatial coordinates of B(beta)e_0 determine beta and the time coordinate determines gamma. (iii) On P the two matrices multiply to gamma^2(1 - b^2) times the identity, using gamma^2(1 - b^2) = 1; on P^perp both are the identity. Hence B(-beta)B(beta) = B(beta)B(-beta) = identity. (iv) Since u is future timelike unit, |u_vec|^2 = (u^0)^2 - 1 < (u^0)^2, so |beta| = |u_vec|/u^0 < 1 and B(beta) is defined; substituting x = u into the formulas in the (e_0,e)-frame with e = beta/|beta| gives spatial part zero and first coordinate gamma(u^0 - beta . u) = gamma(u^0 - |u_vec|^2/u^0) = gamma((u^0)^2 - |u_vec|^2)/u^0 = gamma/u^0 = 1, using gamma = (1 - |beta|^2)^(-1/2) and the unit condition. (v) is the inequality computed in (i). Every step is a finite computation from the definition; the cited predecessor lemma supplies the conclusion that B(beta) is Lorentz, proper and orthochronous.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-boost",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure",
