        {
          "id": "lem-collinear-boost-composition-and-velocity-addition",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Collinear boost composition and the relativistic velocity-addition law",
          "statement": "Let $S=\\operatorname{span}(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be the spatial three-space of a frame\n$(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with its Euclidean structure $\\delta$\n([[def-spatial-rotation-of-an-inertial-frame]]), and let $e\\in S$ be a unit\nvector, $\\delta(e,e)=1$. Let $\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\in(-1,1)$ be real parameters and\nlet $B(\\beta_1e)$ and $B(\\beta_2e)$ be the corresponding passive boosts\n([[def-lorentz-boost]]). Then\n\n$$B(\\beta_1e)\\,B(\\beta_2e)=B(\\beta e),\\qquad \\beta:=\\frac{\\beta_1+\\beta_2}{1+\\beta_1\\beta_2},$$\n\nand $|\\beta|<1$. In particular the two collinear boosts commute,\n$B(\\beta_1e)B(\\beta_2e)=B(\\beta_2e)B(\\beta_1e)$, and\n\n$$B(\\beta_1e)\\,B(-\\beta_2e)=B\\!\\left(\\frac{\\beta_1-\\beta_2}{1-\\beta_1\\beta_2}\\,e\\right).$$\n\nThe composed parameter $\\beta$ is the displayed rational expression, which\ngenerally differs from $\\beta_1+\\beta_2$; this algebraic law is conventionally\ncalled velocity addition. In particular,\nno composition of two boosts in a common direction leaves the open interval\n$(-1,1)$: the composed parameter always satisfies $|\\beta|<1$.",
          "strategy": "Compute the product of the two matrices on the common (e_0,e)-plane: both boosts act as the identity on the common perpendicular P^perp and fix no other direction, so the product is again the identity on P^perp and the 2x2 matrix product [[gamma_1,-gamma_1 beta_1],[-gamma_1 beta_1,gamma_1]] [[gamma_2,-gamma_2 beta_2],[-gamma_2 beta_2,gamma_2]] equals gamma_1 gamma_2 (1 + beta_1 beta_2) [[1, -(beta_1+beta_2)/(1+beta_1 beta_2)], [-(beta_1+beta_2)/(1+beta_1 beta_2), 1]], which is the boost matrix with parameter beta given above and gamma = gamma_1 gamma_2 (1 + beta_1 beta_2). The algebra proving the display uses gamma_i^2 (1 - beta_i^2) = 1 and the rational power laws. To see |beta| < 1: the denominator 1 + beta_1 beta_2 > 0 because |beta_1 beta_2| < 1, and |beta_1 + beta_2| < 1 + beta_1 beta_2 is equivalent to (beta_1 + beta_2)^2 < (1 + beta_1 beta_2)^2, i.e. to (1 - beta_1^2)(1 - beta_2^2) > 0, which holds since |beta_i| < 1. The minus case is the same multiplication with beta_2 replaced by -beta_2 and 1 - beta_1 beta_2 > 0. Since the identity on P^perp and the matrices on P together define the whole map, the computation is complete; no continuity or limiting argument is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-boost",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse",
            "def-spatial-rotation-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-rational-power",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-real-order",
            "lem-of-abs-value",
            "lem-of-square-monotone"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook), Section 1.4 'The Lorentz transformation' pp. 30-32 including Examples 11-14, Sections 2.3-2.4 'Additional postulates'/'Other axiomatizations' pp. 46-49, and Section 8.3 'Boosts and rotations' pp. 174-176; signature (+---) converted to (-+++) with x^0 = ct restored",
