        {
          "id": "lem-boost-rotation-factorization",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Unique boost-rotation factorization of proper orthochronous Lorentz maps",
          "statement": "Let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial frame\n([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), let\n$S:=\\operatorname{span}(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be its spatial three-space with\nEuclidean form $\\delta$ ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]),\nand let $L:V\\to V$ be a proper orthochronous Lorentz map\n([[def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group]]). Write $u:=Le_0$, with coordinates\n$(u^0,u_{\\mathrm{vec}})$ in the frame, so that $\\eta(u,u)=-1$ and $u^0>0$.\nThen:\n\n(i) $|u_{\\mathrm{vec}}|<u^0$ and the parameter\n$\\boldsymbol\\beta:=u_{\\mathrm{vec}}/u^0\\in\\mathbb R^3$ satisfies\n$|\\boldsymbol\\beta|<1$;\n\n(ii) the linear map $\\widehat R:=B(\\boldsymbol\\beta)L$ satisfies\n$\\widehat Re_0=e_0$ and $\\widehat R(S)=S$, and its restriction $R:S\\to S$ preserves $\\delta$ and has determinant $+1$ ([[def-spatial-rotation-of-an-inertial-frame]]);\nhence $\\widehat R$ is the extension $\\operatorname{diag}(1,R)$ of the unique\nmap $R:=\\widehat R|_S$; this extension is a spatial rotation of the frame and\n$L=B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta)\\widehat R$ is a product of a passive boost and a\nspatial rotation;\n\n(iii) conversely, for every $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ with $|\\boldsymbol\\beta|<1$ and\nevery linear map $R:S\\to S$ preserving $\\delta$ with $\\det R=1$, the extension\n$\\widehat R(v^0e_0+w):=v^0e_0+Rw$ ($w\\in S$), denoted\n$\\operatorname{diag}(1,R)$ in the frame basis, is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map and\nthe product $B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta)\\widehat R$ is a proper orthochronous Lorentz\nmap;\n\n(iv) the factorization $L=B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta)\\widehat R$ of (ii) is unique: if\n$B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta_1)\\widehat R_1=B(-\\boldsymbol\\beta_2)\\widehat R_2$ with\n$|\\boldsymbol\\beta_i|<1$ and spatial rotations $\\widehat R_i$, then\n$\\boldsymbol\\beta_1=\\boldsymbol\\beta_2$ and $R_1=R_2$;\n\n(v) $L$ is a passive boost, that is $L=B(\\boldsymbol\\beta')$ for some\n$|\\boldsymbol\\beta'|<1$, if and only if the rotation $R$ in (ii) is the\nidentity, and then $\\boldsymbol\\beta'=-\\boldsymbol\\beta$ with\n$\\boldsymbol\\beta$ the parameter of (i).",
          "strategy": "Proof plan. (i) From eta(u,u) = -1 one has |u_vec|^2 = (u^0)^2 - 1, and u^0 > 0 because L is orthochronous, so |u_vec| < u^0. (ii) By lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse(iv), B(beta)u = e_0 with beta := u_vec/u^0; since L e_0 = u this gives Rhat e_0 = B(beta) L e_0 = e_0. Rhat is a composition of Lorentz maps, hence Lorentz (lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure), and it preserves S = e_0^perp because for w in S, eta(e_0,Rhat w) = eta(Rhat e_0, Rhat w) = eta(e_0,w) = 0. Hence Rhat maps S into S and, being invertible, onto S; on S it preserves delta because delta is the restriction of eta. For the determinant: Rhat has block matrix diag(1, Rhat|_S) in the frame; det Rhat = det(Rhat|_S) and det Rhat = det B(beta) det L = 1, so det(Rhat|_S) = 1, and Rhat|_S is a spatial rotation. The representation Rhat = diag(1,R) and the equation L = B(beta)^{-1}Rhat = B(-beta)Rhat are then immediate. (iii) A spatial rotation Rhat fixes e_0 and preserves delta on S; expanding eta(Rhat v, Rhat w) in components (v^0,w^0 in the time direction and delta on S) gives eta(Rhat v,Rhat w) = eta(v,w), so Rhat is a Lorentz map; det Rhat = det R = 1, so it is proper, and it is orthochronous because it fixes the future timelike vector e_0 (a Lorentz map maps the cone containing e_0 to itself, by lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure(iv)); products of proper orthochronous Lorentz maps are proper orthochronous by the same lemma. (iv) Apply both sides of B(-beta_1)Rhat_1 = B(-beta_2)Rhat_2 to e_0: B(-beta_i)e_0 = gamma_i(e_0 + beta_i^sharp), and the coordinate columns gamma_1(e_0 + beta_1) = gamma_2(e_0 + beta_2) force gamma_1 = gamma_2 and beta_1 = beta_2 (compare the time and spatial coordinates); then Rhat_1 = Rhat_2 by invertibility of the common boost, hence R_1 = R_2. (v) If L = B(beta'), then u = L e_0 = gamma'(e_0 - beta'), so beta = u_vec/u^0 = -beta' and B(beta) = B(-beta'), giving Rhat = B(beta)B(beta') = identity and R = identity; conversely if R = identity then L = B(-beta). No step uses an unstated classification of Lorentz maps; the proof is the finite computation above plus the cited predecessor items.",
          "deps": [
