        {
          "id": "lem-lorentz-lie-algebra-structure",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Lie algebra of the Lorentz group: eta-antisymmetric endomorphisms and the generators M_ab",
          "statement": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a $4$-dimensional Lorentz vector space and let\n$(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an ordered $\\eta$-orthonormal basis with\n$\\eta_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. For a linear map $K:V\\to V$\n([[def-linear-map]]) with matrix $M$ in this basis, the following are\nequivalent:\n\n(a) $\\eta(Kv,w)+\\eta(v,Kw)=0$ for all $v,w\\in V$;\n(b) $M^{T}\\eta_0+\\eta_0M=0$;\n(c) $M$ has the block form\n$M=\\begin{pmatrix}0&u^{T}\\\\ u&\\Omega\\end{pmatrix}$ with\n$u\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\Omega^{T}=-\\Omega$.\n\nThe set\n$\\mathfrak g:=\\{K:\\eta(Kv,w)+\\eta(v,Kw)=0\\text{ for all }v,w\\}$ is a\n$6$-dimensional linear subspace of the space of linear maps $V\\to V$. Define\nthe maps $M_{ab}$ for all $0\\le a,b\\le3$ by\n$(M_{ab})^{\\mu}{}_{\\nu}=\\delta^{\\mu}_{a}\\eta_{b\\nu}\n-\\delta^{\\mu}_{b}\\eta_{a\\nu}$. Thus $M_{aa}=0$ and $M_{ba}=-M_{ab}$; the six maps with $a<b$ form a basis of\n$\\mathfrak g$ and satisfy\n$[M_{ab},M_{cd}]=\\eta_{bc}M_{ad}-\\eta_{ac}M_{bd}-\\eta_{bd}M_{ac}\n+\\eta_{ad}M_{bc}$ for all $0\\le a,b,c,d\\le3$, where\n$[K_1,K_2]:=K_1K_2-K_2K_1$ is the commutator. Here a real Lie algebra means a real vector space with a bilinear antisymmetric bracket satisfying $[K_1,[K_2,K_3]]+[K_2,[K_3,K_1]]+[K_3,[K_1,K_2]]=0$. The commutator makes $\\mathfrak g$ such an algebra.\nIf $L:I\\to O(V,\\eta)$, with $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ an open interval containing $0$, is a\n$\\mathcal C^1$ curve in $O(V,\\eta)$ with $L(0)=\\operatorname{id}_V$, then its\nderivative $K:=L'(0)$ belongs to $\\mathfrak g$, and $L(s)=\\operatorname{id}_V+sK+o(s)$ as $s\\to0$. Here $\\mathcal C^1$ means that each matrix entry is continuously differentiable on $I$, $K$ has entries $(L^a{}_b)\\prime(0)$, and $o(s)$ denotes a matrix remainder each of whose entries divided by $|s|$ tends to $0$.",
          "strategy": "Proof plan. (a) <=> (b): writing v,w as coordinate columns, eta(Kv,w) + eta(v,Kw) = v^T M^T eta_0 w + v^T eta_0 M w = v^T(M^T eta_0 + eta_0 M)w, and by nondegeneracy of the standard pairing this bilinear form vanishes for all v,w exactly when the matrix is zero. (b) <=> (c): write M = [[k, u^T],[w, Omega]] with a real k, u,w in R^3 and Omega 3x3; then eta_0 M = [[-k, -u^T],[w, Omega]], so M^T eta_0 + eta_0 M = 0 reads k = 0, w = u and Omega^T = -Omega. Dimension and basis: the space of skew 3x3 matrices has dimension 3 and u ranges over R^3, so g is 6-dimensional; the maps M_0i (i = 1,2,3) correspond to u = e_i, Omega = 0, and the maps M_ij (1 <= i < j <= 3) correspond to u = 0, Omega = e_i e_j^T - e_j e_i^T, so the six maps are linearly independent and span g. The commutator identity is a finite matrix computation: substitute (M_ab)^mu_nu = delta^mu_a eta_{b nu} - delta^mu_b eta_{a nu}, expand the product M_ab M_cd into the four rank-one products delta^mu_a eta_{b nu}-terms, subtract the expression with (a,b) and (c,d) interchanged, and pair matching terms to obtain the four displayed generator terms; the computation is valid for all index positions including repeated indices, and for the diagonal generators one checks separately that the formula gives the correct sign. Finally, if s |-> L(s) is C^1 in O(V,eta) with L(0) = identity, differentiate eta(L(s)v,L(s)w) = eta(v,w) at s = 0: by bilinearity and the product rule this gives eta(L'(0)v,w) + eta(v,L'(0)w) = 0, i.e. K in g; the expansion L(s) = identity + sK + o(s) is the definition of the derivative at 0 in the finite-dimensional space of linear maps.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-vector-space-of-linear-maps",
            "def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix",
            "def-derivative",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Chapter 1 'Special relativity and flat spacetime' pp. 3-7 (PDF pp. 10-14), equations (1.3)-(1.21)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Mathematical Tripos Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), Section 2.2 'Lorentzian signature' pp. 26-28 (PDF pp. 27-29); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_roles": {
