        {
          "id": "lem-lorentz-exponential-map-and-rapidity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Lorentz exponential: exp(sK) is proper orthochronous, and boosts are exponentials of generators",
          "statement": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a $4$-dimensional Lorentz vector space with a chosen time\norientation $C^+$ ([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]],\n[[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]), fix an inertial frame\n$(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with coordinates $x^a$\n([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), and give $V$ the Euclidean norm\n$\\lVert v\\rVert:=\\lVert E^{-1}(v)\\rVert_2$, where $E(u)=\\sum_a u^ae_a$ is the coordinate isomorphism. Let\n$\\mathfrak g$ be the six-dimensional space of $\\eta$-antisymmetric\nendomorphisms of [[lem-lorentz-lie-algebra-structure]]. Then:\n\n(i) For every $K\\in\\mathfrak g$ and every $s\\in\\mathbb R$ the series\n$\\exp(sK):=\\sum_{n\\ge0}(sK)^n/n!$ converges absolutely in the operator norm on\nthe space of linear maps $V\\to V$; the map $s\\mapsto\\exp(sK)$ is of class\n$\\mathcal C^\\infty$ with $\\frac{d}{ds}\\exp(sK)=K\\exp(sK)=\\exp(sK)K$,\n$\\exp(0)=\\operatorname{id}_V$, and\n$\\exp((s+t)K)=\\exp(sK)\\exp(tK)$ for all $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\n(ii) For every $K\\in\\mathfrak g$ and every $s\\in\\mathbb R$ the map\n$\\exp(sK)$ is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map; equivalently\n$s\\mapsto\\exp(sK)$ is a $\\mathcal C^\\infty$ curve in $SO^+(V,\\eta)$ with\n$\\exp(0)=\\operatorname{id}_V$. In particular every $K\\in\\mathfrak g$ arises as\nthe derivative at $0$ of a curve of proper orthochronous Lorentz maps. No\nclaim is made that every element of $SO^+(V,\\eta)$ is the exponential of an\nelement of $\\mathfrak g$.\n\n(iii) Let $e\\in S$ be a unit spatial vector of the frame, $\\delta(e,e)=1$, and\nwrite $e=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\hat e_ie_i$; set\n$N_e:=-\\sum_{i=1}^3\\hat e_iM_{0i}\\in\\mathfrak g$. Then $N_e$ vanishes on the\n$\\eta$-orthogonal complement of $\\operatorname{span}(e_0,e)$ and acts on that\nplane by the matrix\n$\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\ -1&0\\end{pmatrix}$; and\n$\\exp(rN_e)=B(\\tanh(r)e)$ for every $r\\in\\mathbb R$. In particular every\npassive boost is the exponential of a generator in $\\mathfrak g$, and\n$\\exp(rN_e)\\exp(sN_e)=\\exp((r+s)N_e)$ for all $r,s\\in\\mathbb R$.",
          "strategy": "Proof plan. Make V a Banach space using any norm (cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach); every linear map V -> V is bounded (cor-linear-maps-with-finite-dimensional-domain-are-bounded), so the exponential series converges absolutely with the estimates of lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator, which supplies (i) verbatim (absolute convergence, C^infinity, derivative, and the group law). (ii) If K in g, then exp(sK) satisfies d/ds[exp(sK)^T eta exp(sK)] = exp(sK)^T (K^T eta + eta K) exp(sK) = 0 by (i) and K^T eta + eta K = 0 (in the frame matrix form of lem-lorentz-lie-algebra-structure); since the expression equals eta at s = 0, exp(sK) is a Lorentz map for every s. It is proper: det exp(sK) is continuous in s, takes only the values +-1 because exp(sK) is Lorentz, and equals +1 at s = 0; a continuous function on the interval R with values in {+1,-1} starting at +1 is identically +1. It is orthochronous: for a fixed v in C^+ the curve s |-> exp(sK)v is continuous, its values are timelike by the Lorentz property, and a continuous path in the timelike set that starts in C^+ remains in C^+ because the timelike set has exactly two connected components (lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components); taking v = e_0 or any v gives exp(sK)(C^+) contained in C^+, hence equal to C^+ by lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure(iv). The assertion that K arises as a derivative is immediate from (i). The explicit disclaimer about surjectivity of the exponential is part of the statement and must be retained. (iii) For N_e one computes (N_e)^2 = projection onto span(e_0,e), N_e e_0 = -e, N_e e = -e_0, and N_e = 0 on the perpendicular complement; hence the exponential series splits into cosh(r) times the identity on the plane plus sinh(r) N_e, which is exactly the matrix [[cosh r,-sinh r],[-sinh r,cosh r]] on the plane and the identity on the complement. By lem-rapidity-parametrization-of-collinear-boosts this equals B(tanh(r) e); the last identity is exp((r+s)N_e) = exp(rN_e)exp(sN_e), which follows from the series or from the addition law of the exponential of a single bounded operator.",
          "deps": [
