        {
          "id": "thm-sr-affine-isometry-classification",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "C^2 eta-preserving maps of affine Minkowski space are affine Lorentz maps",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski space with its inertial smooth\nstructure ([[lem-affine-minkowski-charts-form-a-smooth-atlas]]), and let\n$F:A\\to A$ be a map whose coordinate expression in some (equivalently, every)\ninertial chart is of class $C^2$ and which satisfies\n\n$$\\eta(dF_pv,dF_pw)=\\eta(v,w)\\qquad\\text{for all }p\\in A\\text{ and all }v,w\\in V,$$\n\nwhere $dF_p:T_pA\\to T_{F(p)}A$ is the total derivative and the tangent spaces\nare identified with $V$ by the canonical isomorphism of\n[[lem-tangent-space-of-affine-minkowski-space-is-v]]. Then $F$ is an affine\nLorentz map: there exist $p_0\\in A$ and a linear Lorentz map $L$ with\n$F(p)=F(p_0)+L(p-p_0)$ for all $p\\in A$\n([[def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group]]). Conversely every affine Lorentz map is\na $C^\\infty$ map of $A$ satisfying the same preservation identity.\nConsequently the $C^2$ isometries of the affine Minkowski model are exactly its\naffine Lorentz maps, and these form a group under composition.",
          "strategy": "Proof plan, following the differentiation argument. Fix an inertial chart x: A -> R^4 with coordinate functions x^a and write Phi := x o F o x^{-1} for the coordinate expression, a C^2 map from R^4 into R^4. The preservation identity in coordinates is eta_0(partial_j Phi(u), partial_k Phi(u)) = eta_0(e_j,e_k) = eta_{jk} for all u in R^4, a constant right-hand side. Write A_j := partial_j Phi and C_{ijk} := eta_0(partial_i A_j, A_k). Differentiating the identity in the i-th coordinate and using bilinearity of eta_0 gives C_{ijk} + C_{ikj} = 0; Clairaut's theorem (thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials, available since Phi is C^2) gives partial_i A_j = partial_j A_i, i.e. C_{ijk} = C_{jik}. Iterating the two relations shows C_{ijk} = C_{jik} = -C_{jki} = -C_{kji} = C_{kij} = C_{ikj} = -C_{ijk} for distinct i,j,k, hence C_{ijk} = 0; the cases with a repeated index follow from the same two relations (for instance C_{iik} + C_{iki} = 0, C_{iki} = -C_{iik} and C_{kii} = C_{iki} give C_{iik} = 0). Since the vectors A_k(u) form a basis of R^4 (they are the images of the standard basis under the invertible map dPhi_u, invertible because it preserves the nondegenerate form eta_0) and eta_0 is nondegenerate, eta_0(partial_i A_j, A_k) = 0 for all k implies partial_i A_j = 0 for all i,j; thus all second partial derivatives of Phi vanish on R^4. By cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant applied on the segments u + t h (t in [0,1]), each first partial derivative partial_j Phi is constant on R^4; integrating once more along segments gives Phi(u) = L u + b for a fixed 4x4 matrix L and b in R^4. Evaluating the preservation identity at any u gives L^T eta_0 L = eta_0, so the linear map L determined by the same symbol is a Lorentz map by def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group(b), and F(p) = F(p_0) + L(p - p_0) in affine coordinates. The converse is immediate: an affine Lorentz map has constant derivative, equal to a Lorentz map on each tangent space, so the preservation identity holds; the group statement is lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure(v). No differentiability beyond C^2 is used and no global topology beyond the affine structure of A enters.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
