        {
          "id": "lem-inertial-chart-transitions-are-affine-lorentz",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Inertial charts are related by proper orthochronous affine Lorentz maps, and every such map is a chart transition",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski space and let\n$(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$, $(o';e'_0,e'_1,e'_2,e'_3)$ be two inertial frames with\ncharts $x$, $x'$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). Let $M$ be the\nchange-of-basis matrix defined by $e'_a=\\sum_bM^b_ae_b$. Then:\n\n(i) $M$ is the matrix, in the ordered basis $(e_0,\\dots,e_3)$, of a linear\nLorentz map $L:V\\to V$, that is $L(e_a)=e'_a$ for all $a$; $M$ is proper and\northochronous: $M^{T}\\eta_0M=\\eta_0$, $\\det M=+1$, and $M$ maps the future cone\nonto the future cone. The same holds for $M^{-1}$, the matrix of $L^{-1}$.\n\n(ii) The chart transition\n$x'\\circ x^{-1}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R^4$ is the affine map\n$u\\mapsto M^{-1}(u-x(o'))$, whose linear part $M^{-1}$ is a proper\northochronous Lorentz matrix; consequently it preserves the quadratic form\n$q(u):=\\eta_0(u,u)=-(u^0)^2+|u_{\\mathrm{vec}}|^2$ of coordinate differences,\nand it maps future causal coordinate vectors to future causal coordinate\nvectors.\n\n(iii) Conversely, for every proper orthochronous linear Lorentz map\n$L:V\\to V$ and every point $c\\in A$, the tuple\n$(c;Le_0,Le_1,Le_2,Le_3)$ is an inertial frame, and for its chart $x_L$ one has\nPut $M_L:=E^{-1}\\circ L\\circ E:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R^4$, where $E(u)=\\sum_a u^ae_a$. Then $x_L(p)=M_L^{-1}(x(p)-x(c))$ for all $p\\in A$. Hence the transitions from the\nchart $x$ are exactly the affine maps $u\\mapsto M_L^{-1}(u-x(c))$ with $L$\nranging over the proper orthochronous linear Lorentz maps and $c$ over $A$,\nand the linear parts of transitions are exactly the proper orthochronous\nLorentz matrices and their inverses.",
          "strategy": "Proof plan. (i) Since both ordered bases are eta-orthonormal and positively oriented with future first vector, the linear map L with L(e_a) = e'_a satisfies eta(L e_a, L e_b) = eta(e'_a,e'_b) = eta_ab, so L is a Lorentz map by def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group(a) and its matrix is M; det M = +1 because both bases represent the same orientation, and L(C^+) = C^+ because L e_0 = e'_0 lies in C^+ and a Lorentz map permutes the two components (lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure(iv)); alternatively both facts follow from the component structure. The inverse is a Lorentz map of the same type by lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure(ii). (ii) The formula x'(p) = M^{-1}(x(p) - x(o')) is exactly the transition identity recorded in def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart; it is affine with linear part M^{-1}, which is proper orthochronous by (i). Preservation of q and of the future cone is then the coordinate form of lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure(iii),(iv) applied to L^{-1} (in coordinates, eta_0(M^{-1}u, M^{-1}u) = eta_0(u,u)). (iii) If L is a proper orthochronous linear Lorentz map, then the images L e_a again form an eta-orthonormal basis (eta(L e_a, L e_b) = eta_ab), with future first vector L e_0 (orthochrony) and positive orientation (det L = +1), so (c; L e_0, L e_1, L e_2, L e_3) is an inertial frame. Its linear isomorphism E_L is E o L, hence its chart is x_L(p) = E_L^{-1}(p - c) = L^{-1}(E^{-1}(p - c)) = L^{-1}(x(p) - x(c)). The final statement combines (ii) and (iii); no classification theorem beyond the definitions is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "def-change-of-basis-matrix",
            "def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space",
            "def-determinant-of-a-linear-operator",
            "lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Mathematical Tripos Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), Section 2.2 'Lorentzian signature' pp. 26-28 (PDF pp. 27-29); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Chapter 1 'Special relativity and flat spacetime' pp. 3-7 (PDF pp. 10-14), equations (1.3)-(1.21)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group": "mathematical-premise",
