          "dependency_level": 9
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-sr-noncollinear-boosts-do-not-commute",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Counterexample: noncollinear boosts do not commute",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'for all beta_1, beta_2 in R^3 with |beta_i| < 1 the passive boosts satisfy B(beta_1)B(beta_2) = B(beta_2)B(beta_1)'. Counterexample: take beta_1 = (3/5,0,0) and beta_2 = (0,3/5,0), with gamma = 5/4. Then B(beta_1)B(beta_2) = [[25/16, -3/4, -15/16, 0],[-15/16, 5/4, 9/16, 0],[-3/4, 0, 5/4, 0],[0,0,0,1]], B(beta_2)B(beta_1) = [[25/16, -15/16, -3/4, 0],[-3/4, 5/4, 0, 0],[-15/16, 9/16, 5/4, 0],[0,0,0,1]]. The two matrices differ, for instance in the (0,1) entry (-3/4 versus -15/16), the (0,2) entry (-15/16 versus -3/4) and the (2,1) entry (0 versus 9/16). Hence the composition of boosts is order dependent; the counterexample does not claim that the two compositions fail to be Lorentz maps (both are proper orthochronous Lorentz maps, as products of such), only that the group is not abelian and that the boost composition law is a collinear law.",
          "strategy": "The two products are computed directly from the 4x4 boost matrices with beta = 3/5 (the same matrices used in ex-sr-one-plus-one-boost-matrix, extended by identities off their planes), and a single differing entry refutes the asserted commutation. The source's Section 8.3.2 reports the same phenomenon numerically for boosts along x and y; here the exact rational matrices are displayed and the (2,1) entry 9/16 versus 0 witnesses the difference. The counterexample is consistent with lem-collinear-boost-composition-and-velocity-addition, which asserts commutation only for boosts in a common direction, and with lem-boost-rotation-factorization, which explains the discrepancy as a spatial rotation.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-boost",
            "lem-collinear-boost-composition-and-velocity-addition",
            "lem-boost-rotation-factorization",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-lorentz-boost": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-collinear-boost-composition-and-velocity-addition": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-boost-rotation-factorization": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "counterexample"
          },
          "dependency_level": 9
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-sr-discrete-lorentz-maps-are-not-proper-orthochronous",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Counterexample: improper and non-orthochronous Lorentz maps, and det = 1 without orthochrony",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'every Lorentz map is proper and orthochronous' and, separately, 'every Lorentz map with det L = 1 is orthochronous'. Counterexample: in an inertial frame with eta_0 = diag(-1,1,1,1) define the parity map Pi and the time-reversal map T by their matrices Pi = diag(1,-1,-1,-1) and T = diag(-1,1,1,1). Both satisfy M^T eta_0 M = eta_0 because both are diagonal with entries +-1, so both are Lorentz maps; det Pi = -1 and det T = -1, so neither is proper; Pi fixes the time coordinate and hence maps the future cone onto itself, so Pi is orthochronous but improper, while T maps every future timelike vector to a past timelike vector, so T is non-orthochronous. The product Pi T = -I_4 has det = +1 but maps the future cone onto the past cone, so det = 1 does not imply orthochrony. Consequently O(V,eta) strictly contains SO^+(V,eta), the four sign classes of Lorentz maps are genuinely distinct, and the convention of def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group(e), which reserves 'Lorentz transformation' for the proper orthochronous maps, is not automatic.",
          "strategy": "Every claim is a finite matrix evaluation: the metric condition for diagonal matrices, the determinants, and the action on the time coordinate. The action on the cone is decided by the sign of the transformed time coordinate of a future timelike vector, using the coordinate description of the future cone in lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components. The product Pi T = -I_4 witnesses the second refuted statement. This example is the reason thm-sr-proper-orthochronous-lorentz-algebra(iv) states explicitly that det = 1 alone does not force orthochrony.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "thm-determinant-of-a-triangular-matrix",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-determinant-of-a-triangular-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
