        {
          "id": "lem-sr-weak-piecewise-causal-integrability",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Improper integrability and endpoint increments of weak piecewise future causal curves",
          "statement": "Let $x : [a,b] \\to A$, $a < b$, be a piecewise $C^{1}$ curve in the sense of\n[[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]], with a finite partition\n$a = s_0 < \\cdots < s_m = b$, $C^{1}$ on each open piece and finite one-sided\ndifference-quotient derivatives at both ends of each piece. Assume its tangents\nare nonzero future causal at every open-piece point and at each one-sided\nendpoint.\n\nIn any inertial chart ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]) write\n$y = (y^{0},z)$, $z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3}$, for the coordinate path. On a piece\n$(\\alpha,\\beta)$, choose $r \\in (\\alpha,\\beta)$; for a function $g$ continuous\nthere, write\n\n$$\\int_{(\\alpha,\\beta)} g \\;:=\\; \\lim_{u \\downarrow \\alpha} \\int_u^{r} g \\;+\\; \\lim_{v \\uparrow \\beta} \\int_r^{v} g$$\n\nwhen both limits are finite. Then:\n\n**(a)** $y$ is continuous on $[\\alpha,\\beta]$, $y^{0'} \\ge |z'| \\ge 0$ on\n$(\\alpha,\\beta)$, and $y^{0'} > 0$ there.\n\n**(b)** Every coordinate derivative is absolutely improperly Riemann integrable\n(its absolute value has finite improper integral), and its signed improper\nintegral equals the corresponding endpoint increment. The continuous functions\n$|z'|$ and $f = \\sqrt{(y^{0'})^{2} - |z'|^{2}} = \\sqrt{-\\eta(x',x')}$ have\nfinite nonnegative improper integrals, with\n$\\int_{(\\alpha,\\beta)} f \\le \\int_{(\\alpha,\\beta)} y^{0'} = y^{0}(\\beta) - y^{0}(\\alpha)$.\n\n**(c)** The improper values are independent of $r$, additive under subdivision,\nand the finite piece sums are independent of the admissible partition.\n\n**(d)** $D = x(b)-x(a)$ is nonzero future causal: $D^{0} > 0$ and\n$|D_{\\mathrm{spatial}}| \\le D^{0}$ in every inertial chart. If some interior or\none-sided endpoint tangent is future timelike, then $D$ is future timelike.\nCoordinate time is strictly increasing on every nondegenerate subinterval. The\nintegrals need not be ordinary closed-piece Riemann integrals. No\nderivative-limit hypothesis, Lebesgue-integration premise, clock identification,\nor choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Complete local adaptation of the flat future-cone integral argument, with endpoint integrability supplied rather than assumed. (a) Finite quotient derivative v at an endpoint gives x(t+h)-x(t)=h(v+o(1)), hence endpoint continuity; C1 gives interior continuity. The future cone coordinate criterion gives y0'>=|z'| and y0'>0 on each open piece. The square root is continuous on nonnegative reals: positive arguments use thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives with exponent 1/2; at zero, 0<=t<epsilon^2 implies 0<=sqrt(t)<epsilon. Thus the spatial norm and causal density are continuous even at a null tangent or a zero spatial component. Since y0'>=|z'|>=0, their squared values lie between 0 and (y0')^2, giving 0<=f<=y0' by the nonnegative square-root order. (b) On every alpha<u<v<beta, the derivative is continuous on [u,v], so thm-continuous-implies-integrable and thm-ftc-second-part give integral_u^v yj'=yj(v)-yj(u). Fix r inside. For y0' the left and right limits are y0(r)-y0(alpha) and y0(beta)-y0(r), finite by endpoint continuity and nonnegative. For any continuous 0<=g<=y0', the left truncation integrals are increasing as u decreases, bounded above by y0(r)-y0(alpha); completeness supplies their supremum S. Given epsilon>0 choose u* with integral_u*^r g>S-epsilon; every alpha<u<u* then has integral_u^r g between S-epsilon and S, proving the limit S. The right side is identical. Apply this to |yj'|, |z'| and f; all are continuous inside and bounded above by y0'. Signed coordinate integrals converge by the truncated FTC and endpoint continuity; thus each derivative is absolutely improperly integrable and has the exact endpoint increment. (c) Ordinary integral additivity on truncations and finite limits show independence of r and additivity under each interior cut. Any two admissible finite partitions have a common refinement, so all finite sums agree. (d) On truncations, the vector-integral norm inequality gives |z(v)-z(u)|<=integral_u^v |z'|<=integral_u^v y0'. Pass to endpoints and sum with the Euclidean triangle inequality to obtain |D_spatial|<=D0. Positive y0' on a compact interior interval gives a positive time increment, and nonnegativity on the rest gives D0>0; apply the same argument to any nondegenerate restriction for strict time monotonicity. At an interior timelike tangent, y0'-|z'| is continuous and positive in a compact interior neighborhood, so its integral is positive and the inequality becomes strict. At a left endpoint with timelike one-sided quotient v, (y(alpha+h)-y(alpha))/h tends to v, so for some h>0 that quotient is future timelike by openness of the cone. This subdisplacement is future timelike, and the remaining subdisplacement is future causal by the already proved non-strict inequality; adding their coordinates yields a future timelike sum. At a right endpoint use (y(beta)-y(beta-h))/h similarly. At a corner use the corresponding adjacent piece. This proves the endpoint-only timelike clause without continuity of the derivative at the corner. No closed-piece derivative integrability or countable choice is assumed.",
          "deps": [
