        {
          "id": "lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The proper-time function is a strictly increasing C^1 parametrization with C^1 inverse",
          "statement": "Let $c > 0$ and let $x : I \\to A$ be a $C^{1}$ future timelike worldline\n([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]) with proper-time function\n$\\tau(s) = c^{-1}\\int_{s_0}^{s}\\sqrt{-\\eta(x'(u),x'(u))}\\,du$ for a base point\n$s_0 \\in I$ ([[def-sr-proper-time]]). Then:\n\n**(i)** $\\tau$ is differentiable at every $s \\in I$, with one-sided derivatives\nat the endpoints of $I$, and\n\n$$\\tau'(s) = c^{-1}\\sqrt{-\\eta\\bigl(x'(s),x'(s)\\bigr)} > 0$$\n\nfor every $s \\in I$; since $\\tau'$ is this composition of continuous maps,\n$\\tau$ is $C^{1}$ on $I$.\n\n**(ii)** $\\tau$ is strictly increasing on $I$, hence injective, and its image\n$J := \\tau[I]$ is an order-convex subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ with at least two\nelements, so $\\tau : I \\to J$ is a bijection.\n\n**(iii)** The inverse $\\rho : J \\to I$ of $\\tau$ is continuous and strictly\nincreasing, and it is differentiable at every point $\\tau(s)$ of $J$ with\n\n$$\\rho'\\bigl(\\tau(s)\\bigr) = \\frac{1}{\\tau'(s)} = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{-\\eta(x'(s),x'(s))}} > 0 ;$$\n\nhence $\\rho$ is $C^{1}$ on $J$.\n\n**(iv)** The reparametrized curve $y := x \\circ \\rho : J \\to A$ is a $C^{1}$\nfuture timelike worldline whose tangent at the proper-time parameter $\\tau(s)$ is\n\n$$(x \\circ \\rho)'\\bigl(\\tau(s)\\bigr) = \\frac{x'(s)}{\\tau'(s)} = \\frac{c\\,x'(s)}{\\sqrt{-\\eta(x'(s),x'(s))}} ;$$\n\nin particular $y$ is parametrized by its own proper time up to the additive\nconstant of the base point.",
          "strategy": "(i) thm-ftc-first-part applied to the continuous integrand u |-> c^{-1} sqrt(-eta(x'(u),x'(u))) on a compact neighbourhood of s (contained in I using def-interval) gives tau'(s) equal to that value, with the one-sided reading at endpoints; positivity is the future timelike hypothesis. (ii) For a < b in I, monotonicity of the integral (thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral) gives tau(b) - tau(a) >= 0, and equality would force the continuous nonnegative integrand to vanish on [a,b] (thm-nonnegative-continuous-with-zero-integral-vanishes), contradicting its strict positivity; so tau is strictly increasing and injective, and its image is order-convex and nondegenerate by cor-continuous-image-of-an-interval-is-an-interval (tau is continuous). (iii) A continuous injective map on an interval has a continuous strictly monotone inverse (thm-continuous-inverse), and the reciprocal derivative formula is thm-derivative-of-an-inverse, applicable since tau'(s) > 0; tau' continuous gives rho C^1. (iv) Differentiating componentwise in an inertial chart and applying the one-variable chain rule to each coordinate (def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral, thm-chain-rule) gives (x o rho)' = (x' o rho) rho' at interior points, with one-sided readings at endpoints; rho' > 0 preserves future timelikeness. The base point shifts rho's argument and leaves derivatives unchanged. No choice principle is used.",
