        {
          "id": "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Norms of four-velocity and four-acceleration, and the observer rest-space decomposition",
          "statement": "Let $c > 0$ and let $(A,V,\\eta,C^{+})$ be an oriented time-oriented affine\nMinkowski space ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]).\n\n**(a) Norm of the four-velocity.** Let $x : I \\to A$ be a $C^{1}$ future\ntimelike worldline with four-velocity $U$\n([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]). Then\n$\\eta(U(s),U(s)) = -c^{2}$ for every $s \\in I$; equivalently the dimensionless\nfour-velocity $u = U/c$ satisfies $\\eta(u,u) = -1$, and $U(s)$ is future\ntimelike. Thus the dimensionless four-velocity lies on the future unit\nhyperboloid $\\{v \\in C^{+}:\\eta(v,v)=-1\\}$.\n\n**(b) Orthogonality of the four-acceleration.** If $x$ is $C^{2}$ at $s$, then\n$\\eta(U(s),A(s)) = 0$: the four-acceleration has no component along the\nfour-velocity; if in addition $A(s) \\ne 0$, then $A(s)$ is spacelike.\n\n**(c) Rest space and projection.** Let $n \\in V$ be future unit timelike,\n$\\eta(n,n) = -1$, and put $n^{\\perp} := \\{\\,v \\in V : \\eta(n,v) = 0\\,\\}$\n([[def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]). Then $n^{\\perp}$ is a\n3-dimensional linear subspace of $V$, the restriction of $\\eta$ to $n^{\\perp}$\nis positive definite, and the map $h : V \\to V$, $h(v) := v + \\eta(n,v)\\,n$, is\nlinear, idempotent with $h \\circ h = h$, has image $n^{\\perp}$ and kernel\n$\\operatorname{span}(n)$, satisfies $h(n) = 0$ and $\\eta(n,h(v)) = 0$ for all\n$v \\in V$, and every $v \\in V$ has the unique decomposition\n$v = -\\eta(n,v)\\,n + h(v)$. Moreover $|w| := \\sqrt{\\eta(w,w)}$ defines a norm\non $n^{\\perp}$.\n\n**(d) Relative velocity and Lorentz factor.** Let $n$ and $u$ be future unit\ntimelike vectors, put $\\gamma := -\\eta(n,u)$ and $w := c\\,h(u)/\\gamma$. Then\n$\\gamma \\ge 1$, $w$ lies in $n^{\\perp}$,\n$u = \\gamma\\,(n + w/c)$, $|w|^{2} = c^{2}(1-\\gamma^{-2}) < c^{2}$,\n$\\gamma = (1-|w|^{2}/c^{2})^{-1/2}$, and $\\gamma = 1$ if and only if $u = n$.\nThe vector $w$ is the velocity of a worldline with four-velocity $cu$ relative\nto the observer $n$, and $\\gamma$ is its Lorentz factor.\n\n**(e) Coordinate form.** Let $(o;e_0,\\dots,e_3)$ be an inertial frame and let\n$y$ be a $C^{1}$ future timelike worldline whose inertial coordinates are\n$t \\mapsto (ct,r(t))$, with coordinate velocity $w(t) := r'(t)$ in\n$\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ and, when $y$ is $C^{2}$, coordinate acceleration\n$a(t) := w'(t)$. Then $|w(t)| < c$, the four-velocity is\n$U = \\gamma_w\\,(c,w)$ with $\\gamma_w := (1-|w|^{2}/c^{2})^{-1/2}$, and the\nfour-acceleration is $A = \\gamma_w\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\gamma_w(c,w)\\bigr)$, so\nthat\n\n$$A^{0} = \\gamma_w^{4}\\,\\frac{w \\cdot a}{c}, \\qquad A_{\\mathrm{vec}} = \\gamma_w^{2}\\,a + \\gamma_w^{4}\\,\\frac{(w \\cdot a)\\,w}{c^{2}} ;$$\n\nconsequently\n$\\eta(A,A) = \\gamma_w^{4}|a|^{2} + \\gamma_w^{6}(w \\cdot a)^{2}/c^{2} \\ge 0$,\nwith $\\eta(A,A) = 0$ if and only if $a = 0$, and $\\eta(U,A) = 0$. For the\nobserver $n = e_0$ of the chart, clauses (c) and (d) specialize to\n$\\gamma = \\gamma_w$ and $w_{\\mathrm{rel}} = (0,w)$ in the frame coordinates, identified with $w$ via the spatial basis.\n\n**(f) Scope.** The statement is a theorem about the affine Minkowski model with\na fixed positive constant $c$; it asserts no clock reading and identifies no\nphysical four-velocity. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Proof in dependency order; the hard part is (c), which is pure algebra plus one coordinate computation, and the remaining clauses follow from it. (c) Fix an eta-orthonormal basis with e_0 future timelike (lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis; use an inertial frame of def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart). Write n = n^0 e_0 + n_s and v = v^0 e_0 + v_s with n_s,v_s in S = span(e_1,e_2,e_3), where eta restricts to the positive definite delta with norm |.| (def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame). From eta(n,n) = -(n^0)^2 + |n_s|^2 = -1 one gets (n^0)^2 = 1 + |n_s|^2 > 0. For v with eta(n,v) = -n^0 v^0 + delta(n_s,v_s) = 0 one has v^0 = delta(n_s,v_s)/n^0, hence eta(v,v) = -(v^0)^2 + |v_s|^2 = |v_s|^2 - delta(n_s,v_s)^2/(n^0)^2 >= |v_s|^2 (1 - |n_s|^2/(n^0)^2) = |v_s|^2/(n^0)^2 >= 0 by the finite Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for delta (thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite); equality forces |v_s| = 0 and then v^0 = 0, so v = 0 and the restriction is positive definite. The map v_s |-> v^0 e_0 + v_s with v^0 = delta(n_s,v_s)/n^0 is a linear bijection S -> n^perp, so n^perp is 3-dimensional. The properties of h are immediate from bilinearity of eta (def-lorentz-form-and-inertia, def-linear-map): eta(n,h(v)) = eta(n,v) + eta(n,v)eta(n,n) = 0, so h(v) lies in n^perp; h(n) = n + eta(n,n)n = 0; h(h(v)) = h(v) + eta(n,h(v))n = h(v); and v = -eta(n,v)n + h(v) is unique because the two summands lie in span(n) and n^perp, which meet only in 0. (a) By def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration, U = x'/tau' with tau' = c^{-1} sqrt(-eta(x',x')), so eta(U,U) = eta(x',x')/(tau')^2 = eta(x',x') c^2/(-eta(x',x')) = -c^2; timelikeness and the future direction of U follow from those of x' and positivity of 1/tau'. (b) eta(U,U) is the constant -c^2 along a C^2 worldline; differentiating componentwise in an eta-orthonormal basis with thm-algebra-of-derivatives gives 0 = (d/ds) eta(U,U) = 2 eta(U,U'), hence eta(U,U') = 0, and dividing by tau' > 0 gives eta(U,A) = 0. If A(s) is nonzero, then A(s) lies in (U(s)/c)^perp, a positive definite space by (c), so eta(A,A) > 0: A(s) is spacelike. (d) By (c), h(u) lies in n^perp and u = gamma n + h(u) with gamma = -eta(n,u). First, gamma > 0: in the inertial frame of (c), future timelikeness of n and u gives n^0 > |n_s| and u^0 > |u_s| (lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components), so eta(n,u) = -n^0 u^0 + delta(n_s,u_s) <= -n^0 u^0 + |n_s||u_s| < 0. Second, eta(u,u) = gamma^2 eta(n,n) + eta(h(u),h(u)) = -gamma^2 + |h(u)|^2 = -1, so |h(u)|^2 = gamma^2 - 1 >= 0, giving gamma >= 1; substituting w = c h(u)/gamma gives |w|^2 = c^2 (gamma^2-1)/gamma^2 = c^2(1 - gamma^{-2}) < c^2, and solving for gamma gives gamma = (1 - |w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2} and gamma = 1 iff h(u) = 0 iff u = n. (e) In the chart the coordinate path is t |-> (c t, r(t)), so x' = (c,w) and -eta(x',x') = c^2 - |w|^2 > 0 by future timelikeness, giving |w| < c and tau' = c^{-1} sqrt(c^2-|w|^2) = (1 - |w|^2/c^2)^{1/2} = 1/gamma_w; hence U = x'/tau' = gamma_w (c,w). For C^2 paths, d gamma_w/dt = gamma_w^3 (w.a)/c^2 by thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives (applied to the power -1/2) with the chain rule, and collecting terms in A = gamma_w dU/dt gives the displayed components; expanding eta(A,A) in the diagonal form and using gamma_w^2(1-|w|^2/c^2) = 1 gives eta(A,A) = gamma_w^4 |a|^2 + gamma_w^6 (w.a)^2/c^2 >= 0 with equality iff |a| = 0, and eta(U,A) = 0 agrees with (b). (f) The specialization n = e_0 is the evaluation h(u) = u - u^0 e_0 = (0, w/c gamma_w) and gamma = u^0 = gamma_w, so w_rel has coordinates (0,w), identified with w via the spatial basis. No choice principle is used; all arguments are finite-dimensional or one-variable calculus. The dimensionless vector U/c, rather than U, lies on the unit hyperboloid. On the arbitrary observer complement, expand the nonnegative quadratic eta(w-tz,w-tz) at t=eta(w,z)/eta(z,z) for z nonzero; this gives Cauchy–Schwarz and the norm triangle inequality, with zero case immediate.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
