        {
          "id": "post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted free trajectories of massive particles and the clock hypothesis",
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of this framework, extending the ideal-clock\nreading rule of [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] to the general worldline\ncalculus of this page:\n\n**(i) Free trajectories.** A massive test particle that is subject to no\ninteraction (a **free particle**) has as its worldline a straight future\ntimelike line of the affine model ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]): there are\nan event $p \\in A$ and a future timelike vector $v \\in V$ with\n$x(s) = p + (s-s_0)v$ for $s\\in I$, where $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a nondegenerate interval and $s_0\\in I$ ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]).\nInteractions, forces and collisions are not part of this assumption: a particle\nthat is not free may have any future timelike worldline admitted by its own\ndynamical model, and no force law is adopted here. The kinematical consequences\nof this shape for the four-velocity are derived, not assumed, in\n[[pthm-sr-four-velocity-norm-and-orthogonality]].\n\n**(ii) Clock hypothesis.** An ideal clock carried along a future timelike\nworldline of the model, including an accelerated one and a piecewise $C^{1}$ one\nwith finitely many kinks, reads between any two parameter values the proper-time\ndifference of [[def-sr-proper-time]] for $C^{1}$ histories and of\n[[def-sr-piecewise-proper-time]] for weak piecewise $C^{1}$ histories, with no\nadditional term depending on acceleration, jerk or any higher derivative. A\nkink is an idealization of a finite short acceleration phase whose contribution\nis included through the integral on the smooth pieces, and the reading does not\ndepend on an increasing regular change of parameter admitted by the curve\ndefinition — its derivatives on smooth pieces and all finite one-sided endpoint\nderivatives are positive, as in\n[[lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance]] and\n[[lem-sr-piecewise-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance]]. Zero-tangent\ncompositions are not worldlines of this model.\n\n**(iii) Scope and qualification.** The assumptions concern ideal point\nparticles, ideal clocks and flat classical spacetime. They are adopted, not\nproved; they are not consequences of the affine geometry, and no experiment is\nclaimed here to establish them. They inherit the idealizations of\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]: real clocks have finite resolution,\ncalibration and apparatus limitations, and real particles are not exactly free.\nThe coordinate and unit conventions are those of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], and inertial charts are those\nof [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]].",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it fixes the physical interpretation of straight future timelike lines as the histories of free massive test particles and makes the clock-reading rule applicable to the general worldline class of this page. Obligations: (i) straight future timelike lines are declared objects of the affine model, and each is a future timelike worldline with constant tangent (def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline); (ii) the reading rule is an identification of a physical clock reading with the mathematical proper-time difference, so def-sr-proper-time and its reparametrization invariance are the mathematical suppliers carrying the content, while the identification itself is adopted; (iii) the free-trajectory clause is deliberately silent about interactions and force laws, so it cannot be used to infer conservation laws, mass-shell relations or collision kinematics, which require the separate momentum postulate of the later energy-momentum page; (iv) the clock hypothesis is the assertion that ideal clocks have no acceleration-dependent term, so it must not be presented as following from the geometry. No proof is manufactured; the item is an adopted premise with an exact scope. The weak piecewise mathematical referent is supplied by def-sr-piecewise-proper-time and its integrability lemma, and regular increasing weak-piecewise parameter independence by lem-sr-piecewise-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance. This repairs the formulation of the already adopted clock assumption; it does not derive clock behavior or extend its empirical scope.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "def-sr-piecewise-proper-time",
            "lem-sr-piecewise-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance",
            "lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance",
            "lem-sr-weak-piecewise-causal-integrability"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Chapter 1 pp. 26-27 (PDF pp. 33-34; worldline, proper time (1.97), four-velocity (1.98)-(1.99)) and Chapter 3 pp. 68-71 (PDF pp. 75-78)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Mathematical Tripos Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), Section 2.2 pp. 27-28 and Section 2.3 pp. 28-30 (PDF pp. 28-31); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook), Sections 3.5 and 3.7 pp. 60-68; signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Free massive test particles and ideal clocks in flat classical special-relativistic spacetime: straight future timelike histories for free particles, and proper-time readings for arbitrary future timelike histories with no acceleration-dependent correction. Excludes interactions and force laws, gravitational and quantum effects, real clock imperfections and every empirical measurement claim; the assumptions are adopted, not derived, and their idealizations travel to all consumers.",
          "dependency_level": 11,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "formulation-prerequisite",
