          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-four-velocity-norm-and-orthogonality",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Four-velocity norm and four-acceleration orthogonality for the model's clocks",
          "statement": "Adopt the postulates of the model: [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]] and\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] of the spacetime-primitives page, together\nwith [[post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis]] of this page, and let\n$c$ and the SI coordinates be those of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let $x : I \\to A$ be the\nhistory of an ideal clock of the model, a $C^{1}$ future timelike worldline\n($C^{2}$ where acceleration is used), with proper time $\\tau$, four-velocity\n$U = dx/d\\tau$ and, when $x$ is $C^{2}$, four-acceleration $A = dU/d\\tau$\n([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]). Then:\n\n**(i)** the reading of the ideal clock between parameter values $s_1 < s_2$\nequals $\\tau(s_2)-\\tau(s_1)$;\n\n**(ii)** $\\eta(U,U) = -c^{2}$ everywhere, so $U$ is future timelike with velocity units, and the dimensionless\nvector $U/c$ is future unit timelike;\n\n**(iii)** if $x$ is $C^{2}$ at $s$, then $\\eta(U(s),A(s)) = 0$ and $A(s)$ is\nzero or spacelike, with the unit of acceleration;\n\n**(iv)** if the ideal clock is carried by a massive test particle subject to\nno interaction, and $x$ is also that particle's worldline (a **free clock**\nin this theorem), then its four-velocity is constant along the worldline and\n$A = 0$ identically;\n\n**(v)** for an ideal observer at the same event, described by a future unit\ntimelike vector $n = U_{o}/c$ with $\\eta(n,n) = -1$, the clock's speed relative\nto the observer is $|w| = c\\sqrt{1-\\gamma^{-2}}$ with\n$\\gamma = -\\eta(n,U/c) \\ge 1$, so it is strictly less than $c$, and\n$\\gamma = 1$ exactly when the clock's four-velocity equals the observer's.\n\n**Scope and qualification.** This is a conditional statement about the adopted\nmodel; it asserts no measurement outcome, contains no statistical inference, and\ninherits the idealizations of the postulates (ideal point particles, ideal\nclocks, flat spacetime, no gravitational or quantum effects).",
          "strategy": "Conditional deduction from the adopted premises; the mathematical content is thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition. (i) is the clock hypothesis of post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis together with the proper-time definition, restated for the page's worldline calculus. (ii) and (iii) are clauses (a) and (b) of thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition, with U identified with the clock's four-velocity by the clock hypothesis; the identification supplies the physical meaning of U, the identities are mathematical. (iv) The clock is carried by a free massive test particle whose worldline is the same x. Applying the free-trajectory clause to that carrier, there are p and future timelike v with x(s) = p + (s - s_0)v; then x' = v is constant, tau' = c^{-1} sqrt(-eta(v,v)) is constant and positive, so U = x'/tau' is the constant vector c v/sqrt(-eta(v,v)), and A = U'/tau' = 0. (v) is clause (d) of the mathematical theorem, with the observer vector n allowed to be any future unit timelike vector; post-sr-inertial-covariance licenses the interpretation of observers and clocks as model objects and def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions fixes c and the units of the speed. The proof must not upgrade the conditional statement to a claim about nature and must carry the idealizations to the conclusion.",
          "deps": [
            "post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook), Sections 3.5 and 3.7 pp. 60-68; signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Mathematical Tripos Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), Section 2.2 pp. 27-28 and Section 2.3 pp. 28-30 (PDF pp. 28-31); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
