      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-sr-clock-at-rest-in-a-chart",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Worked example: the clock at rest in an inertial chart",
          "statement": "Worked computation. In an inertial chart of the model with $\\eta_0 =\n\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ let $c>0$ and $(a_1,a_2,a_3)\\in\\mathbb R^3$. For $t\\in\\mathbb R$ consider the coordinate worldline\n$x(t) = (ct, a_1, a_2, a_3)$ of a clock at rest at the spatial position\n$(a_1,a_2,a_3)$, parametrized by the coordinate time $t$. Then\n$x'(t) = (c,0,0,0)$, so $-\\eta(x',x') = c^{2}$ and the proper-time function is\n$\\tau(t)-\\tau(t_0) = t-t_0$; the four-velocity is $U = x'/\\tau' = (c,0,0,0)$,\nwith $\\eta(U,U) = -c^{2}$, and the four-acceleration vanishes, $A = 0$.\nRelative to the chart's observer $e_0$ the dimensionless four-velocity is\n$u = U/c = (1,0,0,0) = e_0$, so $\\gamma = -\\eta(e_0,u) = 1$ and the relative\nvelocity is $w = c\\,h(u)/\\gamma = 0$: the clock is at rest in the chart.",
          "strategy": "Direct evaluation: the diagonal form applied to (c,0,0,0) gives -c^2, so the proper-time integrand is the constant c/c = 1 and tau is t - t_0; U = x'/tau' = (c,0,0,0) and U' = 0, so A = 0. The relative-velocity clauses are clauses (c) and (d) of thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition evaluated at these vectors. Every step is a finite computation from the definitions.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
