        {
          "id": "ex-sr-proper-time-reparametrization",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Worked example: invariance of elapsed proper time under a cubic reparametrization",
          "statement": "Worked computation. In an inertial chart with eta_0 = diag(-1,1,1,1) let d := (5,3,0,0), a future timelike displacement with -eta(d,d) = 25 - 9 = 16 and sqrt(-eta(d,d)) = 4. (a) The worldline x(s) := s d on the parameter interval [1,8] has x'(s) = d, integrand sqrt(-eta(d,d)) = 4, and elapsed proper time tau(8) - tau(1) = (1/c) integral_1^8 4 ds = 28/c. (b) The reparametrization phi(u) := u^3 is a strictly increasing C^1 surjection of [1,2] onto [1,8]; its derivative 3u^2 is strictly positive throughout [1,2], so the reparametrized curve y(u) := x(u^3) = u^3 d has y'(u) = 3u^2 d, hence sqrt(-eta(y',y')) = 3u^2 sqrt(-eta(d,d)) = 12u^2, and elapsed proper time (1/c) integral_1^2 12u^2 du = (4/c)(8 - 1) = 28/c. The two elapsed times agree, as lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance requires, and the proper-time function of x is tau(s) = 4s/c up to an additive constant, with inverse rho(tau) = c(tau - tau_0)/4, where tau_0 is the chosen additive constant. (c) Boundary check: instead take the same straight curve x(s)=s d on [-1,1] and phi(u)=u^3 on [-1,1]. This is a strictly increasing C^1 surjection, but y'(0)=0, so y is not a worldline of def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline. The broad length-integral identity nevertheless holds: integral_{-1}^{1} 12u^2 du=8=integral_{-1}^{1} 4 ds. No proper-time function of y is invoked outside its defining worldline domain.",
          "strategy": "Two independent exact computations of the same elapsed time: in (a) the integrand is the constant 4; in (b) the chain rule gives y' = 3u^2 d and the integrand 12u^2, whose integral over [1,2] is 28. The equality is the concrete instance of the invariance lemma, and the inverse function is read off the linear proper-time function. All arithmetic is rational. On [1,2], 3u^2>=3>0 verifies the strengthened worldline consequence. For the boundary interval [-1,1], differentiate u^3 d to obtain the zero tangent at zero, and integrate 12u^2 to get 8; this checks precisely the distinction between the broad substitution identity and the regular-worldline consequence.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "dependency_level": 10,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-proper-time": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance": "mathematical-premise"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-sr-four-velocity-and-acceleration-of-a-circular-worldline",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Worked example: four-velocity and four-acceleration of uniform circular motion",
          "statement": "Worked computation. In an inertial chart with $\\eta_0 =\n\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ let $c>0$, $R > 0$ and let $\\omega$ be real with\n$R|\\omega| < c$. Here $\\sin,\\cos:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ are the everywhere-convergent real power-series functions of\n[[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]; their derivatives are $\\cos$ and $-\\sin$\n([[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]]), and $\\sin^2+\\cos^2=1$\n([[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]]). For $t\\in\\mathbb R$, consider the coordinate worldline\n$x(t) = \\bigl(ct, R\\cos(\\omega t), R\\sin(\\omega t), 0\\bigr)$, parametrized by\ncoordinate time: uniform circular motion in the first two spatial coordinates.\nThen $w(t) = \\bigl(-R\\omega\\sin(\\omega t), R\\omega\\cos(\\omega t), 0\\bigr)$ has\n$|w| = R|\\omega|$, so $-\\eta(x',x') = c^{2}-R^{2}\\omega^{2}$ and the\nproper-time factor is\n$\\tau' = c^{-1}\\sqrt{c^{2}-R^{2}\\omega^{2}} = 1/\\gamma$ with\n$\\gamma := (1-R^{2}\\omega^{2}/c^{2})^{-1/2}$; the four-velocity is\n$U = \\gamma\\,(c,w)$. Since $|w|$ is constant, $\\gamma$ is constant; the\ncoordinate acceleration is\n$a(t) = \\bigl(-R\\omega^{2}\\cos(\\omega t), -R\\omega^{2}\\sin(\\omega t), 0\\bigr)$,\nwith $|a| = R\\omega^{2}$ and $w \\cdot a = 0$. By clause (e) of\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] the four-acceleration is\n$A = (0,\\gamma^{2}a)$, so $A^{0} = 0$, $\\eta(U,A) = 0$ and\n$\\eta(A,A) = \\gamma^{4}R^{2}\\omega^{4} \\ge 0$. Its magnitude is\n$\\gamma^{2}R\\omega^{2}$; for $R > 0$ it is spacelike exactly when\n$\\omega \\ne 0$, while $\\omega = 0$ is the static limit with $\\gamma = 1$,\n$U = (c,0,0,0)$ and $A = 0$. Numerical specialization: if $R\\omega = 3c/5$,\nthen $\\gamma = 5/4$ and the magnitude of the four-acceleration is\n$\\gamma^{2}R\\omega^{2} = (25/16)R\\omega^{2} = (15c\\omega)/16$.",
          "strategy": "Finite computation: differentiate the coordinate path, evaluate the diagonal quadratic form to get tau', read gamma, and apply clause (e) of the theorem with w.a = 0 to obtain A = gamma^2 a; the numerical values are exact rational multiples of c and omega. The acceleration is centripetal and orthogonal to the velocity, so the four-acceleration is orthogonal to the four-velocity by the theorem. Since R>0 and gamma>0, gamma^4 R^2 omega^4 is zero exactly when omega=0 and positive otherwise. At zero frequency the path is (ct,R,0,0) and both the coordinate and four-acceleration vanish. The specialization R omega=3c/5 has omega>0 and preserves the displayed exact value and acceleration units.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
