        {
          "id": "cex-sr-null-curves-have-no-proper-time-parametrization",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Counterexample: a future null curve has no proper-time parametrization",
          "statement": "**False claim:** every regular $C^{1}$ curve of the model admits a proper-time\nparametrization, with strictly increasing proper-time function and normalized\ntimelike four-velocity.\n\nThe claim is refuted by a straight future null line. It is a regular future\ncurve whose tangent is null at every point, so the proper-time integrand\nvanishes identically, its formal length integral is constant and cannot\nserve as a parameter, and no unit timelike tangent can be attached to the\ncurve. The existence of this curve is exactly why\n[[def-sr-proper-time]] carries the future timelike hypothesis and why null\nrays need their own parametrization.",
          "strategy": "For k=(1,1,0,0), eta(k,k)=0 and x:R->A, x(s)=p+sk. Formal length L(s)=c^-1 integral_0^s 0=0 cannot parametrize it; def-sr-proper-time assigns no function to this null curve. Bilinearity keeps every scalar multiple null, excluding a normalized timelike tangent. Coordinates x_A(p)+(s,s,0,0) give geometric speed c, without a physical light premise.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
