      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Charts, tetrads, observer congruences and adapted charts in affine Minkowski space",
          "statement": "Work in the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]) with its inertial atlas ([[lem-affine-minkowski-charts-form-a-smooth-atlas]]) and the canonical identification of tangent spaces with $V$ ([[lem-tangent-space-of-affine-minkowski-space-is-v]]). The objects defined below are, in general, different objects, and the distinctions between them are part of the definition.\n\n**(a) Chart and inertial chart.** A **chart** of the model is a diffeomorphism $u:U\\to u(U)$ from a nonempty open subset $U\\subseteq A$ onto an open subset of $\\mathbb R^4$ ([[def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds]], [[def-smooth-manifold]]), written with components $(u^0,u^1,u^2,u^3)$. An **inertial chart** is the global chart $x(p)=E^{-1}(p-o)$ of an inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). A general chart need not be inertial: if $x$ is an inertial chart and $N:x(U)\\to\\mathbb R^4$ is a diffeomorphism onto its image which is not the restriction of an affine map, then $u:=N\\circ x$ is a chart that is not inertial, because if $u$ were the inertial chart of a frame then $x\\circ u^{-1}=N^{-1}$ would be affine by [[lem-inertial-chart-transitions-are-affine-lorentz]] and hence $N=(N^{-1})^{-1}$ would be affine as the inverse of an affine bijection, a contradiction; the map $N(y)=(y^0,y^1+(y^0)^2,y^2,y^3)$ on $\\mathbb R^4$ is an explicit example.\n\n**(b) Tetrad at an event and tetrad field.** A **tetrad at $p\\in A$** is an ordered basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $V$ with $\\eta(e_a,e_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$ and $e_0$ future, $e_0\\in C^+$ ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]): this is the basis of an inertial frame without an origin and without the orientation condition. A **tetrad field** on a nonempty open subset $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ is a tuple $(E_0,E_1,E_2,E_3)$ of smooth vector fields on $\\Omega$ ([[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]], type $(1,0)$) whose values at every $p\\in\\Omega$ form a tetrad at $p$. A tetrad field is **observer-adapted** to a prescribed congruence $n$ when $E_0=n$. Along a prescribed $C^1$ future timelike worldline $x:I\\to A$, observer adaptation means $E_0(x(s))=x'(s)/\\sqrt{-\\eta(x'(s),x'(s))}$ whenever $x(s)\\in\\Omega$; this is its normalized future unit tangent ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]).\n\n**(c) Observer congruence.** An **observer congruence** on a nonempty open subset $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ is a smooth vector field $n:\\Omega\\to V$ with $\\eta(n,n)=-1$ and $n(p)\\in C^+$ for every $p\\in\\Omega$. Its **observers** are its maximal integral curves ([[def-integral-curve-of-a-vector-field]], [[thm-unique-maximal-integral-curve-through-each-point]]). Each observer is a future timelike worldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]): its tangent at a parameter value $s$ is $n(x(s))$, which is future timelike, and it is regular because $\\eta(n,n)=-1$ makes $n$ nowhere zero. **Completeness** of the integral curves, a global product structure of $\\Omega$, and global synchronization are not asserted by this definition.\n\n**(d) Adapted chart.** For a congruence $n$ on $\\Omega$, an **$n$-adapted chart** is a chart $(u^0,u^1,u^2,u^3):U\\to\\mathbb R^4$ with $U$ a nonempty open subset of $\\Omega$ whose spatial coordinates $u^1,u^2,u^3$ are constant along every integral curve of $n$ contained in $U$. No global adapted chart is asserted, and $u^0$ is not required to be affine.\n\n**(e) Distinctions.** A chart need not be inertial, as in (a); a chart need not be $n$-adapted for any congruence. A tetrad field is not in general the coordinate frame of a chart: if $(E_0,E_1,E_2,E_3)$ is the coordinate frame of a chart $(v^0,v^1,v^2,v^3)$, that is, $E_a=\\partial/\\partial v^a$, then $[E_a,E_b]=0$ for all $a,b$ ([[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]], [[prop-coordinate-formula-for-the-lie-bracket]]), so a tetrad field whose members do not commute pairwise is not the coordinate frame of a chart; no converse is claimed here. A congruence need not be the flow of a coordinate, and its pointwise rest spaces need not assemble into hypersurfaces; that integrability question is decided later on this page. In an inertial chart $x$ of $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$, the coordinate vector fields $\\partial/\\partial x^a=e_a$ form a constant tetrad field, with values equal to the frame's ordered basis under $T_pA=V$. Its time vector field $n=\\partial/\\partial x^0=e_0$ alone defines the congruence of stationary observers, whose integral curves are $s\\mapsto x^{-1}(s+s_0,r^1,r^2,r^3)$ for fixed $s_0,r^1,r^2,r^3$. The chart, the ordered tetrad and the single vector field $n$ remain distinct objects.",
          "strategy": "Define charts as open-domain diffeomorphisms, tetrads as pointwise Lorentz-orthonormal bases with a future time leg, unit congruences and adapted charts separately. Check the polynomial non-affine chart by its explicit inverse, normalize the along-worldline tangent and use maximal integral-curve existence. Coordinate frame commutation is only a necessary condition.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "lem-affine-minkowski-charts-form-a-smooth-atlas",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
