        {
          "id": "def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The flat covariant derivative on affine Minkowski space and fields along curves",
          "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be the affine Minkowski space with tangent spaces canonically identified with V, and let Omega be a nonempty open subset of A. (a) Vector fields. For smooth vector fields X,Y on Omega (def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space) define the covariant derivative of Y in the direction X at p by (nabla_X Y)(p) := (d/dt)|_{t=0} Y(p + t X(p)); in the inertial chart x of any inertial frame this is (nabla_X Y)^a = X^b partial_b Y^a. The value does not depend on the inertial chart: for another inertial chart x' = Lambda x + b one has partial'_a = (Lambda^{-1})^b_a partial_b and Y'^a = Lambda^a_b Y^b (thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation), and Lambda is constant, so (Lambda^{-1})^a_c X'^b partial'_b Y'^c = X^b partial_b Y^a. nabla_X Y is again a smooth vector field. (b) Covector and general tensor fields. For a smooth covector field alpha set (nabla_X alpha)(Y) := X(alpha(Y)) - alpha(nabla_X Y); for a type (r,s) tensor field T define nabla_X T by the derivation rule on the evaluation of T on smooth vector and covector fields, subtracting the covariant derivative of each vector argument and also subtracting the covariant derivative of each covector argument; Precisely, for $T:(V^*)^r\\times V^s\\to\\mathbb R$, $(\\nabla_XT)(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\ldots,Y_s)=X[T(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\ldots,Y_s)]-\\sum_iT(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\nabla_X\\alpha_i,\\ldots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\ldots,Y_s)-\\sum_jT(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_r;Y_1,\\ldots,\\nabla_XY_j,\\ldots,Y_s)$. In inertial coordinates every component of nabla_X T is X^b partial_b of that component, with no connection coefficients. (c) Properties. nabla is R-bilinear, C^infinity(Omega)-linear in X, satisfies nabla_X(fY) = X(f)Y + f nabla_X Y and the analogous Leibniz rules for tensor fields, is metric compatible (nabla eta = 0, because partial_a eta_{bc} = 0 in every inertial chart), is torsion-free (nabla_X Y - nabla_Y X = [X,Y], def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields and prop-coordinate-formula-for-the-lie-bracket) and has zero curvature (nabla_X nabla_Y Z - nabla_Y nabla_X Z - nabla_{[X,Y]} Z = 0), the last by equality of mixed partial derivatives of the components (thm-young-mixed-partial-theorem). (d) Fields along a curve. If gamma: I -> A is a C^1 curve and W: I -> V is a C^1 field along it (def-vector-field-and-section-along-a-smooth-curve), the covariant derivative of W along gamma is the ordinary V-valued derivative DW/ds := W' (def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral), because the canonical identification T_pA = V is independent of p; consequently parallel transport along any curve is the identity and a field is parallel exactly when it is constant. The derivative (d) is the one used for the acceleration of congruences, for Fermi-Walker transport and for the kinematic decomposition below; the model needs no Christoffel symbols or parallel-transport ODE in this formulation. (e) First-order C1 version. For C1 vector fields X,Y and C1 covector/tensor fields on Omega, the same directional/evaluation definitions are well-defined and have continuous coefficients: in an inertial chart $(\\nabla_XY)^a=X^b\\partial_bY^a$ and each tensor component is differentiated in the same way. The component recovery and subtraction of all argument derivatives remain valid. C1 scalar multiplication satisfies the same first-order Leibniz rules, metric compatibility remains $\\nabla\\eta=0$, and the first-order torsion identity uses the continuous coordinate bracket $[X,Y]^a=X^b\\partial_bY^a-Y^b\\partial_bX^a$. No C1 closure of this bracket or second covariant derivative of arbitrary C1 data is asserted. For C2 vector fields the second derivatives needed for the zero-curvature identity in(c) exist, and that identity follows by equality of the continuous mixed second partials. All original smooth-field properties and the C1 along-curve derivative in(d) are retained.",
          "strategy": "Definition with well-definedness obligations. (i) The directional derivative in (a) is the derivative of the V-valued composite t |-> Y(p+tX(p)); its coordinate expression is the chain rule, and chart independence is the corrected identity X'^b partial'_b Y'^a=Lambda^a_c X^b partial_b Y^c, equivalently inverse-Lambda recovery of the unprimed components, using that inertial transitions are affine (their second derivatives vanish, so no inhomogeneous term appears) and their linear parts are constant. (ii) The extension in(b) subtracts the derivative of every argument, whether vector or covector. In an inertial chart use the constant coordinate basis and constant dual basis; their covariant derivatives vanish, so evaluating the rule on those bases leaves only X acting on each tensor component. For variable arguments the ordinary product rule cancels all their derivatives with the displayed subtractions. This proves the stated coordinate formula and the evaluation derivation rule; the coordinate expression has no Gamma terms because eta is constant and transition matrices are constant in inertial charts. (iii) The properties in (c) are coordinate computations: torsion-freeness is X^b partial_b Y^a - Y^b partial_b X^a = [X,Y]^a, zero curvature is the interchange of mixed partials, and metric compatibility is the constancy of the component matrix eta_{ab}. (iv) In (d), the canonical isomorphism T_pA = V is independent of p, so the covariant derivative along a curve coincides with the ordinary derivative and parallel transport is trivial; this is exactly what makes Fermi-Walker transport below an ordinary linear ODE. No choice principle is used. For(e), a C1 map Y has continuous differential, and differentiating $Y(p+tX(p))$ at zero gives $dY_p[X(p)]$; the continuous product of dY and X yields the claimed C0 field. The coordinate tensor rule is identical on constant bases, with the ordinary C1 product rule for variable arguments. Only first derivatives occur in the C1 Leibniz/metric/torsion assertions. When X,Y,Z are C2, the fields $\\nabla_YZ$ and $\\nabla_XZ$ are C1, so the two iterated derivatives and the bracket term in the curvature identity are defined; expansion cancels the first-order bracket terms and the remaining mixed second partials commute. This supplies the actual first-order C1 interface used by dust/fluid and the C1 Bell generator with its explicitly given C2 flow family, without treating C1 fields as smooth or promising a joint C2 flow for every C1 field.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "lem-tangent-space-of-affine-minkowski-space-is-v",
            "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields",
            "prop-coordinate-formula-for-the-lie-bracket",
            "thm-young-mixed-partial-theorem",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-vector-field-and-section-along-a-smooth-curve",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-derivative",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Mathematical Tripos Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision): Sections 2.2-2.3 pp. 27-30 (Minkowski metric, causal classification, timelike proper time, observer measurements in an inertial chart, radar coordinates); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997): Chapter 1 pp. 8-16 (inertial frames built from rigid rods and synchronized clocks, simultaneity, time dilation, length contraction) and pp. 26-27 (worldline, proper time (1.97)-(1.99), four-velocity); Chapter 3 pp. 68-71 (proper-time functional)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
