        {
          "id": "def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Simultaneity slices and rest distance relative to a future unit timelike vector",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski space ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]) and let $n\\in V$ be future unit timelike, $\\eta(n,n)=-1$ ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]).\n\n**(a) Rest space.** The **rest space** of $n$ is the hyperplane $n^\\perp=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(n,v)=0\\}$; by [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] clause (c) it is a three-dimensional linear subspace on which $\\eta$ restricts to a positive-definite symmetric form, and the orthogonal projection onto it is $h(v)=v+\\eta(n,v)n$ ([[def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]).\n\n**(b) Simultaneity slice.** For $p\\in A$ the **simultaneity slice of $p$ relative to $n$** is the affine hyperplane $H(p,n):=p+n^\\perp=\\{q\\in A:\\eta(n,q-p)=0\\}$; it contains $p$ and is three-dimensional, not a single point.\n\n**(c) Simultaneity relation.** Define $q\\sim_nq'$ if and only if $\\eta(n,q'-q)=0$, that is, if and only if $q'-q\\in n^\\perp$. Then $\\sim_n$ is an equivalence relation on $A$ whose equivalence classes are exactly the slices $H(p,n)$; reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity are consequences of $n^\\perp$ being a linear subspace and are not physical assumptions.\n\n**(d) Rest distance.** For $p,q$ with $q\\in H(p,n)$ define $d_n(p,q):=\\sqrt{\\eta(q-p,q-p)}$. This is a real number $\\ge0$, symmetric in $p,q$, vanishes exactly when $p=q$, and satisfies the triangle inequality on each slice, because the restriction of $\\eta$ to $n^\\perp$ is a positive-definite inner product.\n\n**(e) Dependence on $n$ and scope.** The relation $\\sim_n$ and the slices $H(p,n)$ depend on the chosen future unit timelike vector $n$: an event $q$ with $\\eta(n,q-p)=0$ generally has $\\eta(n',q-p)\\ne0$ for another future unit $n'$ (an explicit witness is given on this page). Simultaneity here is defined relative to a prescribed observer vector and is not an absolute relation. For a congruence $n(\\cdot)$ one may form the pointwise slices $H\\bigl(p,n(p)\\bigr)$; the separate integrability question concerns the tangent distribution $p\\mapsto n(p)^\\perp$, not the assertion that these affine hyperplanes themselves are its integral hypersurfaces. Integral hypersurfaces can be curved; no global foliation is asserted.",
          "strategy": "The exact observer-decomposition supplier gives the positive-definite rest space and norm. Affine displacement addition proves the equivalence relation and its slice classes. For varying n(p), Frobenius concerns integral hypersurfaces tangent to the rest distribution rather than the affine pointwise slices themselves.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets"
          ],
          "provenance": {
