        {
          "id": "def-sr-operational-radar-protocol",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The operational radar protocol of an ideal observer",
          "statement": "Adopt the ideal-clock and ideal-light assumptions of [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] and the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let $x:I\\to A$ be a future timelike worldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]) with proper-time function $\\tau$ ([[def-sr-proper-time]]), and let $s_-<s_+$ be parameter values in $I$. Suppose an event $q$ and the events $e_-:=x(s_-)$, $e_+:=x(s_+)$ satisfy: $q-e_-$ is future null and $e_+-q$ is future null ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]], [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]). Under the adopted ideal-light assumption an ideal ray is emitted at $e_-$, reaches $q$, and an ideal ray emitted at $q$ reaches $e_+$; the event $q$ is idealized as an instantaneous reflection that changes nothing else. The **radar protocol** of the observer assigns to $q$\n\n(i) the **radar time** $\\tau_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q):=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\tau(s_-)+\\tau(s_+)\\bigr)$, the ideal clock's reading midway between emission and reception, and\n\n(ii) the **radar distance** $\\rho_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q):=\\tfrac{c}{2}\\bigl(\\tau(s_+)-\\tau(s_-)\\bigr)$, half the round-trip light time multiplied by $c$.\n\nTwo events measured by the same observer are **radar-simultaneous** when their radar times agree. The assignment does not change under increasing $C^1$ reparametrizations with everywhere positive derivative and the same clock origin of the observer's worldline, because the proper-time function does not ([[lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance]]).\n\n**Conventions and scope.** (a) The protocol is a definition of an operational assignment conditional on the existence of the emission, reflection and reception events, and it assumes an available ideal reflector and ideal apparatus. (b) The split of the round trip into a \"time\" and a \"distance\" uses the symmetric convention in which half of the round-trip time is assigned to the outgoing and half to the returning segment; the protocol determines the round-trip quantity $\\tau(s_+)-\\tau(s_-)$ operationally, while the derived one-way assignment is a convention and does not measure a one-way light speed independently of a synchronization convention. (c) The conditional assignment is defined for the stated future timelike worldline, including an accelerated one, but no general existence, uniqueness or synchronization theorem for accelerated observers is asserted. Nothing is claimed about dispersive or absorbing media, finite-resolution apparatus, global single-valuedness or detector models, and no measurement outcome, statistical inference or empirical comparison is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Define the conditional symmetric radar assignment from emission/reception proper-time readings under ideal clocks, vacuum light and an instantaneous reflector. Reparametrization uses everywhere-positive derivatives and a common clock origin. No general accelerated synchronization theorem follows, and ring rest-line synchronization differs from one observer radar assignment.",
          "deps": [
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The radar protocol is defined for a future timelike worldline of the affine Minkowski model in the complete vacuum of the adopted ideal-light postulate, with ideal clocks and an available ideal reflector, and only for those events that are connected to the observer's worldline by the stated emission and reception null rays. The derived one-way time and distance assignment is a symmetric half-split convention, not an independent measurement of a one-way light speed; no general existence, uniqueness or synchronization result is asserted for accelerated observers; nothing is asserted about dispersive or absorbing media, finite-resolution apparatus, global single-valuedness, detector models, measurement outcomes, statistical inference or empirical comparison.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages): Sections 1.1-1.3 pp. 12-28 (vector representation of observer motion, operational radar calibration, time dilation, length-contraction road-trip example), Sections 3.5 and 3.7 pp. 60-68 (four-velocity, acceleration, projection operator), Section 3.9.3 pp. 73-74 (local failure of accelerated simultaneity), Sections 7.1-7.3 pp. 147-151 (Rindler coordinates and nonuniform proper acceleration), Section 8.1 pp. 165-169 (rotating synchronization), Section 9.5 pp. 205-211 (congruences, expansion, Born rigidity); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997): Chapter 1 pp. 8-16 (inertial frames built from rigid rods and synchronized clocks, simultaneity, time dilation, length contraction) and pp. 26-27 (worldline, proper time (1.97)-(1.99), four-velocity); Chapter 3 pp. 68-71 (proper-time functional)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
