        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-radar-synchronization-of-inertial-observers",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Radar synchronization of an inertial observer reproduces its inertial chart",
          "statement": "Adopt [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] (ideal clocks read proper time; ideal vacuum light rays are exactly the future null straight lines) and the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial frame with chart $x$, put $n:=e_0$ (future unit, $\\eta(n,n)=-1$) and let $W:=x^{-1}(\\{(\\lambda,0,0,0):\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R\\})$ be the coordinate axis, a straight future timelike worldline whose proper time between the events with coordinates $(\\lambda_-,0,0,0)$ and $(\\lambda_+,0,0,0)$ is $(\\lambda_+-\\lambda_-)/c$ up to an additive constant ([[def-sr-proper-time]], [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). Let $q$ be an event whose spatial coordinates $r:=\\bigl(x^1(q),x^2(q),x^3(q)\\bigr)$ are nonzero, and put $\\lambda_\\pm:=x^0(q)\\pm|r|$ with $|r|$ the Euclidean norm of [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]. Then:\n\n(i) the events $e_-:=x^{-1}(\\lambda_-,0,0,0)$ and $e_+:=x^{-1}(\\lambda_+,0,0,0)$ lie on $W$, and $q-e_-$, $e_+-q$ are future null; hence the radar protocol of [[def-sr-operational-radar-protocol]] applies with emission $e_-$, reflection $q$ and reception $e_+$;\n\n(ii) the radar assignments are $\\tau_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q)=x^0(q)/c$ and $\\rho_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q)=|r|$ up to the additive constant of the observer's proper time: the radar time is the chart time of $q$ and the radar distance is the chart's rest-frame distance;\n\n(iii) consequently, on the region of events with nonzero spatial part, radar simultaneity of this observer coincides with chart simultaneity (equal radar times are equal $x^0$), and the radar distance equals the rest distance in the simultaneity slice $H(m,n)$ of the midpoint event $m:=x^{-1}\\bigl(x^0(q),0,0,0\\bigr)$ ([[def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance]]);\n\n(iv) scope and qualification: this is a conditional statement of the adopted ideal model, showing that the operational round-trip protocol reproduces the inertial chart's time and rest distance, so the operational content of inertial simultaneity is this two-way radar convention; it asserts no one-way light-speed measurement without a synchronization convention, no empirical calibration, no result for accelerated, rotating or dispersive situations, and no deviation from the model. The idealizations of the postulates are carried into the conclusion.",
          "strategy": "For |r|>0 set lambda-plus/minus=x0(q) plus/minus |r|. The midpoint criterion supplies future-null legs. With one fixed clock origin tau(lambda)=lambda/c+constant, substitution gives radar time x0(q)/c+constant and distance |r|, so simultaneity agrees in the stated ideal off-axis scope.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-operational-radar-protocol",
            "lem-sr-radar-midpoint-for-inertial-observer",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The statement is conditional on the adopted ideal-clock and ideal-light postulates and on the SI coordinate conventions of the model; it concerns one inertial observer's straight worldline and those events off its axis whose spatial coordinates are nonzero, and it concludes only that the two-way radar protocol of the observer reproduces the inertial chart's time and rest-frame distance. It asserts no one-way light-speed measurement without a synchronization convention, no empirical calibration, no deviation from the ideal model, and nothing about accelerated, rotating, dispersive or finite-resolution situations. The idealizations of the postulates are carried into the conclusion.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
