        {
          "id": "lem-sr-fermi-walker-transport-existence-and-uniqueness",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Existence, uniqueness and linearity of Fermi-Walker transport",
          "statement": "Let $x:I\\to A$ be a $C^2$ future timelike worldline with four-velocity $U$ and four-acceleration $A$ ([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]], [[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]), let $\\tau_0\\in I$ and $X_0\\in V$. Then there is exactly one $C^1$ field $X:I\\to V$ along $x$ with $D_{\\mathrm{FW}}X/d\\tau=0$ and $X(\\tau_0)=X_0$ ([[def-sr-fermi-walker-transport]]); if $x$ is $C^k$ with $k\\ge2$ then $X$ is $C^{k-1}$. The map $X_0\\mapsto X$, from $V$ to the $C^1$ fields along $x$ satisfying the transport equation, is linear. Consequently, for every tetrad $(E_0(\\tau_0),\\dots,E_3(\\tau_0))$ at $x(\\tau_0)$ with $E_0(\\tau_0)=U(\\tau_0)/c$ there is a unique tuple of Fermi–Walker transported fields with those initial values; whether that tuple remains a tetrad at every time is proved in the following item.",
          "strategy": "In an inertial basis solve X-prime=C(s)X with C=tau-prime c^(-2)(U tensor A-flat-A tensor U-flat). Patch unique fundamental matrices on nondegenerate compact intervals containing the initial parameter; values agree on overlaps, defining a C1 solution without choice. Matrix-column embedding proves vector uniqueness, and X=Phi X0 gives existence/linearity. Differentiate X-prime=CX for C^(k-1) solution regularity. Time leg is U/c by uniqueness; preservation is deferred.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-fermi-walker-transport",
            "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space",
            "def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction",
            "lem-linear-matrix-odes-have-unique-global-solutions-on-a-given-interval",
