        {
          "id": "thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Fermi-Walker transport preserves the metric, and explicit rigid and non-rigid observer congruences",
          "statement": "**(a) Preservation.** Let $x:I\\to A$ be a $C^2$ future timelike worldline and let $X,Y:I\\to V$ be $C^1$ Fermi–Walker transported fields along $x$ ([[def-sr-fermi-walker-transport]]). Then $\\tau\\mapsto\\eta(X(\\tau),Y(\\tau))$ is constant on $I$; in particular Fermi–Walker transport preserves norms and orthogonality, and the unique Fermi–Walker transported family with initial values a tetrad $(E_0(\\tau_0),\\dots,E_3(\\tau_0))$ with $E_0(\\tau_0)=U(\\tau_0)/c$ is an orthonormal tetrad $(E_0(\\tau),\\dots,E_3(\\tau))$ at every $\\tau$ with $E_0(\\tau)=U(\\tau)/c$ (observer-adapted tetrad), by [[lem-sr-fermi-walker-transport-existence-and-uniqueness]].\n\n**(b) Rindler wedge congruence.** In an inertial chart $(x^0,x,y,z)$ consider $\\Phi(\\theta,\\rho,y,z):=(\\rho\\sinh\\theta,\\rho\\cosh\\theta,y,z)$ on $\\mathbb R\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^2$, with $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\rho>0$. Then $\\Phi$ is a smooth bijection onto the right wedge $W:=\\{(x^0,x):x>|x^0|\\}$, with smooth inverse $\\rho=\\sqrt{x^2-(x^0)^2}$, $\\theta=\\tfrac12\\log\\bigl((x+x^0)/(x-x^0)\\bigr)$ ([[thm-hyperbolic-identities-and-derivatives]], [[def-hyperbolic-functions]]); the pullback of $\\eta$ is $g=-\\rho^2d\\theta^2+d\\rho^2+dy^2+dz^2$; the field $n:=\\rho^{-1}\\partial/\\partial\\theta$ is a future unit timelike field on $W$ whose congruence is Born rigid and irrotational, with acceleration $a=\\nabla_nn=\\rho^{-1}\\partial/\\partial\\rho$, hence $\\eta(a,a)=\\rho^{-2}$; its rest slices $\\theta=const$ are open subsets of hyperplanes orthogonal to $n$ with induced metric $d\\rho^2+dy^2+dz^2$ independent of $\\theta$; and the frame $(n,\\partial/\\partial\\rho,\\partial/\\partial y,\\partial/\\partial z)$ is a tetrad field which is not a coordinate frame, since $[n,\\partial/\\partial\\rho]=+(1/\\rho)n$ is nonzero ([[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]]).\n\n**(c) Rigid rotation, ambient nonintegrability and ring holonomy.** Fix real $\\Omega$. In the inertial chart let $U_\\Omega$ be the open timelike region $r^2=(x^1)^2+(x^2)^2<c^2/\\Omega^2$ when $\\Omega\\ne0$, and the whole chart when $\\Omega=0$. On this four-dimensional open set put $K=\\partial_0+(\\Omega/c)(-x^2\\partial_1+x^1\\partial_2)$, $\\gamma(r)=(1-\\Omega^2r^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, and $n=\\gamma(r)K$. This is a smooth future unit timelike Born-rigid congruence. If $\\Omega\\ne0$, its three-dimensional rest distribution in the ambient open set is nowhere integrable: its vorticity is nowhere zero and no ambient rest-simultaneity hypersurface exists locally. If $\\Omega=0$, $n=\\partial_0$ is inertial and irrotational, and the rest hypersurfaces are $x^0=const$. Now fix $R>0$ with $|\\Omega|R<c$ and restrict to the timelike ring cylinder $r=R$, $x^3=const$, whose rotating angle is $\\varphi=\\phi-\\Omega t$ modulo $2\\pi$, with $x^0=ct$. The ring observer $\\phi=\\phi_0+\\Omega t$ has $n=\\gamma(R)\\bigl(1,-(\\Omega R/c)\\sin\\phi,(\\Omega R/c)\\cos\\phi,0\\bigr)$. The induced metric is $-(c^2-\\Omega^2R^2)dt^2+2\\Omega R^2\\,dt\\,d\\varphi+R^2d\\varphi^2$. The ambient rest covector restricted to the cylinder is proportional to $-(c^2-\\Omega^2R^2)dt+\\Omega R^2d\\varphi$, so the one-dimensional rest distribution on that cylinder is locally integrable and satisfies $dt/d\\varphi=\\Omega R^2/(c^2-\\Omega^2R^2)$. After one positive circuit in the rotating label, its lift starting at $t=0$ has signed time offset $\\Delta t=2\\pi\\Omega R^2/(c^2-\\Omega^2R^2)$. For $\\Omega\\ne0$ it returns to the same rotating label at a different time and does not close as a ring rest-simultaneity curve; there is no globally single-valued real rest-synchronization time on the entire cylinder with $C^1$ regularity, $df(\\partial_t)>0$ and differential annihilating these rest lines. This global ring holonomy is distinct from the ambient local Frobenius obstruction. For $\\Omega=0$ the offset is zero and ring rest lines are $t=const$.\n\n**(d) Bell first-order field.** Let $q:J\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $C^2$ with $q(t)=(q_1(t),0,0)$ and $|q_1'(t)|<c$, and consider the $C^1$ future unit field of the translated worldlines $x_\\xi(t)=(ct,q(t)+\\xi)$ parametrized by $\\xi$ in an open subset of $\\mathbb R^3$, with $n=\\gamma\\bigl(1,q_1'(t)/c,0,0\\bigr)$, $\\gamma=(1-q_1'(t)^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, and unit longitudinal rest vector $s=\\gamma\\bigl(q_1'(t)/c,1,0,0\\bigr)$. For this $C^1$ field define the first-order rest projection $h^a{}_b=\\delta^a{}_b+n^an_b$ and projected deformation $\\theta_{ab}=h^c{}_ah^d{}_b\\partial_{(c}n_{d)}$, using the explicit first-order version of [[def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space]]. These coordinate formulas need only $C^1$ $n$, give a continuous tensor, and agree with the smooth congruence definitions when $q$ is smooth; in this first-order sense Born rigidity means $\\theta=0$. Then\n\n$$\\theta(s,s)=s^as^b\\partial_an_b=\\gamma^3q_1'(t)q_1''(t)/c^3,$$\n\nwhich is nonzero exactly when $q_1'(t)q_1''(t)$ is nonzero; hence the congruence is not Born rigid on any neighbourhood of a parameter value with $q_1'(t)q_1''(t)\\ne0$, although for every fixed chart time $t$ the separation between the members labelled $\\xi_1$ and $\\xi_2$ is the constant $|\\xi_1-\\xi_2|$. A breaking or fracture conclusion about a material rod would additionally require an elasticity model and a failure criterion and is not a kinematic statement.",
          "strategy": "(a) For Fermi-Walker transported X,Y, d/dtau eta(X,Y) = eta(X',Y) + eta(X,Y') and substituting X' = c^{-2}(eta(A,X)U - eta(U,X)A) and the analogous expression for Y' gives c^{-2}[eta(A,X)eta(U,Y) - eta(U,X)eta(A,Y) + eta(A,Y)eta(U,X) - eta(U,Y)eta(A,X)] = 0. The initial-tetrad family therefore remains orthonormal, and E_0 = U/c is transported by def-sr-fermi-walker-transport (b); uniqueness is lem-sr-fermi-walker-transport-existence-and-uniqueness. (b) Injectivity of Phi follows from x^2 - (x^0)^2 = rho^2 and the sign of x; the inverse formulas are the standard hyperbolic identities cosh^2 - sinh^2 = 1 and tanh(theta) = x^0/x, and both maps are smooth on their domains because rho > 0 and x - x^0 > 0. Differentiating Phi gives dx^0 = sinh theta d rho + rho cosh theta d theta, dx = cosh theta d rho + rho sinh theta d theta, so -(dx^0)^2 + dx^2 = -rho^2 d theta^2 + d rho^2. The field n is unit future by inspection; a direct computation with the metric gives nabla_{partial_theta} partial_theta = rho partial_rho and hence a = nabla_n n = rho^{-1} partial_rho; the rest slices are the theta-level sets, orthogonal to n because the metric has no theta-rho cross term, with the displayed induced metric independent of theta, so theta_{ab} = 0 and, since the slice is orthogonal to a field whose rest distribution is integrable, omega = 0; the commutator is [rho^{-1}partial_theta, partial_rho] = +(1/rho^2)partial_theta = +(1/rho)n. (c) K is future timelike exactly on the stated U_Omega and is Killing by direct coordinate differentiation. Projecting the symmetric derivative of n=gamma K to its rest space kills the derivative-of-gamma terms (K is parallel to n) and the Killing terms, so theta=0. Put kappa=eta(K,.)=-dx^0+(Omega/c)(x^1 dx^2-x^2 dx^1). Then d kappa=2(Omega/c)dx^1 wedge dx^2 and kappa wedge d kappa=-2(Omega/c)dx^0 wedge dx^1 wedge dx^2, nonzero exactly when Omega!=0. Since n-flat=gamma kappa, n-flat wedge d(n-flat)=gamma^2 kappa wedge d kappa; the stated ambient Frobenius/vorticity result follows, including at the axis by these smooth Cartesian formulas. The Omega=0 limit has the constant unit field. On the ring cylinder, substitute x^0=ct, x^1=Rcos(varphi+Omega t), x^2=Rsin(varphi+Omega t) to obtain the displayed metric and covector. Its one-dimensional kernel is the line field generated by partial_varphi+[Omega R^2/(c^2-Omega^2R^2)]partial_t, whose integral gives the signed holonomy. A globally defined time-oriented rest-synchronization function would have differential a positive multiple of dt-[Omega R^2/(c^2-Omega^2R^2)]dvarphi. Its integral around a fixed-t ring would have nonzero constant sign for Omega!=0, contradicting exactness of that differential. No local nonintegrability is attributed to the cylinder's one-dimensional rest-line field. (d) C2 q makes q1' C1, gamma C1 and n C1. Use the explicitly given first-order projected symmetric derivative of n; no smooth-only congruence or later connecting-field theorem is invoked. Write beta=q_1'/c, beta'=q_1''/c, x^0=ct and n_b=(-gamma,gamma beta,0,0), so partial_0=(1/c)d/dt and partial_1 n_b=0. Differentiation gives partial_0 n_0=-gamma^3 beta beta'/c and partial_0 n_1=gamma^3 beta'/c, using 1+gamma^2 beta^2=gamma^2. Contract with s=(gamma beta,gamma,0,0): s^0[s^0 partial_0 n_0+s^1 partial_0 n_1]=gamma^5 beta beta'(1-beta^2)/c=gamma^3 beta beta'/c=gamma^3 q_1' q_1''/c^3. Projection to the rest space does not change this contraction since s is rest-orthogonal. All transverse expansion components vanish, so it is nonzero exactly under the stated condition, and fixed chart-time separations remain xi_1-xi_2. The material fracture conclusion is excluded. All computations use finite algebra and the declared calculus, with no choice principle.",
          "deps": [
