          "id": "cex-sr-simultaneity-is-observer-dependent",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Counterexample: simultaneity is relative to the observer vector",
          "statement": "**False claim:** the simultaneity relation $\\eta(n,q-p)=0$ between events of the affine model is independent of the future unit timelike vector $n$ ([[def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance]]).\n\n**Counterexample.** In an inertial chart let $p:=x^{-1}(0,0,0,0)$ and $q:=x^{-1}(0,1,0,0)$, so that $d:=q-p$ is spacelike with $\\eta(d,d)=1$, and let $n:=e_0=(1,0,0,0)$ and $n':=\\gamma\\bigl(e_0+\\tfrac35e_1\\bigr)=\\tfrac54(1,\\tfrac35,0,0)$, a boost of speed $3c/5$ along the first axis. Then $\\eta(n',n')=\\tfrac{25}{16}\\bigl(-1+\\tfrac{9}{25}\\bigr)=-1$, so $n'$ is also a future unit timelike vector; $\\eta(n,d)=0$, so $p$ and $q$ are simultaneous relative to $n$ and lie in the slice $H(p,n)$; and $\\eta(n',d)=\\tfrac54\\cdot\\tfrac35=\\tfrac34\\ne0$, so $p$ and $q$ are not simultaneous relative to $n'$ and lie in different slices for $n'$. The two slices $H(p,n)$ and $H(p,n')$ are distinct three-dimensional affine hyperplanes through $p$. Hence simultaneity in the sense of [[def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance]] depends on the observer vector and is not an observer-independent relation.",
          "strategy": "Evaluate d=e1, n=e0 and n-prime=B(-3/5)e0=(5/4,3/4,0,0). Pairings are 0 and 3/4, and e1 lies in n-perp but not n-prime-perp. Equal rest spaces for arbitrary future unit directions would force the projected second direction to vanish and hence the directions to coincide.",
