      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Causal reachability, causal futures and pasts, causal diamonds, and reachability by future causal paths in affine Minkowski space",
          "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space (def-sr-affine-minkowski-model) and write Cbar^+ := cl(C^+) and Cbar^- := cl(C^-) for the closed future and past cones of the time orientation; these are the convex cones with Cbar^+ = C^+ union N^+ union {0}, Cbar^- = C^- union N^- union {0}, where N^+ and N^- are respectively the nonzero future and past null vectors and Cbar^+ intersect Cbar^- = {0} (def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets, lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components). This definition fixes the following objects. (a) Causal and chronological reachability. For events p,q in A set p <=q iff q - p is in Cbar^+ and p <q iff q - p is in C^+; the first is read 'q is causally reachable from p' and the second 'q is strictly (chronologically) reachable from p'. (b) Futures and pasts. J^+(p) := {q : p <= q} = p + Cbar^+, I^+(p) := {q : p < q} = p + C^+, J^-(p) := {q : q <= p} = p + Cbar^- and I^-(p) := {q : q < p} = p + C^-; p lies in J^+(p) intersect J^-(p) and in neither I^+(p) nor I^-(p). (c) Causal diamonds. For events p,q in A with p <= q the causal diamond of the ordered pair (p,q) is the set J^+(p) intersect J^-(q). (d) Reachability by future causal paths. A piecewise C^1 future causal path is a piecewise C^1 curve x: [a,b] -> A (def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline) whose one-sided tangents at every point of [a,b] are future causal (def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors, def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets). For events p,q in A say that q is reachable from p by a future causal path when p = q, or when p is not q and there are real numbers a < b and a piecewise C^1 future causal path x: [a,b] -> A with x(a) = p and x(b) = q; write R^+(p) for the set of events reachable from p in this sense. (e) Conventions and scope. All objects are defined from the affine structure, the form eta and the time orientation alone; no chart, origin, spatial orientation of V or unit convention enters. The statement asserts no property of these objects: the reflexivity of <= is (b), while transitivity of <=, the identification R^+(p) = J^+(p), the antisymmetry 'p <= q and q <= p imply p = q', the monotonicity of inertial coordinate time and the invariance under proper orthochronous affine Lorentz maps are proved later on this page (lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion, thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence, lem-sr-spacelike-pairs-have-frame-dependent-time-order). No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Definition item; the obligations are well-definedness of the new symbols. (i) Cbar^+ and Cbar^- are the closed future and past cones of def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets, where convexity and Cbar^+ intersect Cbar^- = {0} are stated; the equality Cbar^+ = C^+ union N^+ union {0}, and its past counterpart, follows from the description cl(T_+) = {v : v^0 >= |v_s|} of lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components in an eta-orthonormal basis whose first vector lies in C^+, which identifies the remaining boundary points as exactly 0 and the future null vectors. (ii) J^+, I^+ and the past versions are images of p under the translation v |-> p + v of the affine space A (def-affine-space-and-displacement), hence well-defined subsets; p is in J^+(p) because 0 is in Cbar^+, and p is not in I^+(p) because 0 is not timelike. (iii) 'Piecewise C^1 future causal path' restricts the piecewise C^1 curves of def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline by the future causal character of the one-sided tangents; every event q with q - p in Cbar^+ and q not p is reachable from p by the straight path s |-> p + s(q - p), s in [0,1], whose constant tangent q - p is future causal, but this realization is used, not asserted, here. (iv) No property of <=, J^+, I^+ or R^+ is claimed in this item; the later items prove them. No choice principle is used: all objects are subsets of a finite-dimensional affine space or finite data.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-affine-space-and-displacement",
