        {
          "id": "lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The closed future cone in inertial coordinates: description, finite sums, and the coordinate criterion for causal reachability",
          "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space and use the reachability objects J^+, I^+ of def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space. Let (o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) be an inertial frame with chart x (def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart) and let |v_s| := sqrt((v^1)^2 + (v^2)^2 + (v^3)^2) be the Euclidean norm of the spatial part v_s := v^1 e_1 + v^2 e_2 + v^3 e_3 of a vector v = v^0 e_0 + v_s (def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame). Then: (a) Cone description. C^+ = {v : v^0 > |v_s|}, Cbar^+ = {v : v^0 >= |v_s|}, C^- = {v : -v^0 > |v_s|} and Cbar^- = {v : -v^0 >= |v_s|}; moreover every v in Cbar^+ with v not 0 satisfies -eta(v,e_0) = v^0 > 0 and |v_s| <= v^0, and v is future null exactly when |v_s| = v^0 > 0. (b) Finite sums. If v_1,...,v_k are in Cbar^+ then v_1 + ... + v_k is in Cbar^+; if, in addition, at least one v_i is in C^+, then the sum is in C^+; and if v + w = 0 with v,w in Cbar^+, then v = w = 0. (c) Coordinate criterion. For events p,q in A with D^a := x^a(q) - x^a(p) and spatial part D_s := (D^1,D^2,D^3): q is in J^+(p) if and only if D^0 >= |D_s|; q is in I^+(p) if and only if D^0 > |D_s|; q is in J^+(p) with q not p if and only if D^0 >= |D_s| and D^0 > 0; and for q in J^+(p) with q not p one has 0 < D^0 and |D_s| <= D^0. The criteria are stated for the chart of any inertial frame, because every inertial basis is eta-orthonormal with future first vector. (d) Scope. The lemma is a statement about the affine model and the fixed time orientation; it attaches no physical meaning to <=, J^+ or I^+ and uses no choice principle.",
          "strategy": "All three clauses are coordinate algebra in one inertial basis, plus the triangle inequality. (a) In the eta-orthonormal basis (e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) one has eta(v,v) = -(v^0)^2 + |v_s|^2 for every v (lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components), so the timelike set is T_+ union T_- with T_+ = {v : v^0 > |v_s|} and T_- = {v : v^0 < -|v_s|}, and these are the two connected components of the timelike set. Because e_0 is in C^+ and lies in T_+, the component C^+ equals T_+; the same lemma gives cl(T_+) = {v : v^0 >= |v_s|}, whence Cbar^+ = cl(C^+) = {v^0 >= |v_s|}, and the past statements follow by applying this to -v with C^- = -C^+. Since |v_s| <= v^0 = -eta(v,e_0) for v in Cbar^+, and |v_s| = v^0 is the defining equation of the nonzero null vectors of cl(T_+) with positive time component, the equality statement follows. (b) For v,w in Cbar^+ the displayed description gives (v+w)^0 = v^0 + w^0 >= |v_s| + |w_s| >= |v_s + w_s| by the triangle inequality for the Euclidean norm on span(e_1,e_2,e_3) (cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm, def-euclidean-inner-product), so v + w is in Cbar^+; if v is timelike then v^0 > |v_s| makes the first inequality strict and gives v + w in C^+. Induction on k applies the two steps successively (finite sums are defined by induction, so no choice is used). If v + w = 0 with v,w in Cbar^+, then v = -w lies in Cbar^+ intersect Cbar^- = {0} by def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets. (c) q in J^+(p) means q - p in Cbar^+, and the coordinates of q - p are D by affine linearity of the chart x = E^{-1}(p - o); so (a) gives the three criteria, and D = 0 is equivalent to q = p. No choice principle is used; the only analytic input is the triangle inequality for a finite-dimensional Euclidean norm.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages): Section 1.3.2 pp. 22-23 (light-rectangles as causal diamonds with invariant area), Section 2.1 pp. 43-45 (the modest causality postulate P1 and the limits of a general causality principle), Section 4.7 pp. 110-113 (superluminal transmission of information, the tachyonic antitelephone attributed to Tolman, reported tachyon searches); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997): Chapter 1 pp. 5-7 (light cones; timelike, null and spacelike separation; no coordinate-independent future for a spacelike-separated event), Chapter 4 pp. 124-127 (no signals faster than light; information flows only along timelike or null trajectories; future domain of dependence; Misner space with closed timelike curves as a warning about other topologies); PDF page = printed page + 7",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Mathematical Tripos Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision): Sections 2.1-2.3 pp. 25-30 (Minkowski metric (2.6) and inertial frames; timelike, null, spacelike and causal vectors with the light-cone double cone and its two disconnected causal sets; timelike and causal curves; proper time (2.19); curves of extremal proper time, straight lines in inertial frames and the unproved maximization statement); Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
