        },
        {
          "id": "thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Future causal reachability, absence of causal loops, time monotonicity and the straight-worldline proper-time maximum",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space with\nthe reachability objects $\\le$, $<$, $J^+$, $I^+$ and $R^+$ of\n[[def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space]].\n\n**(a) Displacement of a future causal path.** Let $a<b$ be real numbers and let\n$x:[a,b]\\to A$ be a piecewise $C^1$ future causal path with $D:=x(b)-x(a)$. Then\n$D\\in\\overline{C^+}$. If $x$ has a future timelike one-sided tangent at some\npoint of $[a,b]$, then $D\\in C^+$. If $x$ is nonconstant, then $D\\ne 0$.\n\n**(b) Reachability and straight segments.** The relation $\\le$ is reflexive and transitive, and $J^+(p)=R^+(p)$, and for $q\\ne p$\none has $q\\in J^+(p)$ if and only if the straight path\n$s\\mapsto p+s(q-p)$, $s\\in[0,1]$, is a future causal path; moreover\n$$I^+(p)=\\{q:\\text{the straight path from }p\\text{ to }q\\text{ is future timelike}\\} =\\{q:q\\text{ is reachable from }p\\text{ by a piecewise }C^1\\text{ future causal path having a future timelike one-sided tangent at some point}\\},$$\nand $p$ is in neither of these two sets.\n\n**(c) No causal loops.** If $p\\le q$ and $q\\le p$ then $p=q$; equivalently\nthere is no nonconstant piecewise $C^1$ future causal path $x:[a,b]\\to A$ with\n$x(b)=x(a)$.\n\n**(d) Time monotonicity.** For the chart $x$ of every inertial frame and every\npiecewise $C^1$ future causal path $y:[a,b]\\to A$, the composition $x^0\\circ y$\nis nondecreasing, and if $y$ is nonconstant on a subinterval $[a',b']$ of\n$[a,b]$ then $(x^0\\circ y)(b')>(x^0\\circ y)(a')$; in particular every inertial\nframe agrees that a nonconstant future causal path does not go backwards in\ncoordinate time.\n\n**(e) Straight-worldline maximum.** Let $p,q$ be events with $q\\in I^+(p)$, put\n$D:=q-p$ and $T:=\\sqrt{-\\eta(D,D)}>0$. Then for every piecewise $C^1$ future\ntimelike worldline $y:[a,b]\\to A$ with $y(a)=p$ and $y(b)=q$, the elapsed\nproper time\n$$\\Delta\\tau(y)=c^{-1}\\sum_i\\int_{(a_i,b_i)}\\sqrt{-\\eta(y',y')},$$\ndefined by the finite improper-piece sum of\n[[def-sr-piecewise-proper-time]] on the weak $C^1$ pieces\n$a=a_1<b_1=a_2<\\dots<b_m=b$, is at most $T/c$; equality holds if and only if\nthe image of $y$ is the straight segment from $p$ to $q$ and $y$ traverses it\nwith a strictly increasing time coordinate, that is, $y$ is a strictly\nincreasing reparametrization of $s\\mapsto p+sD$.\n\n**(f) Invariance.** For every proper orthochronous affine Lorentz map $F$ of the\nmodel one has $F(J^+(p))=J^+(F(p))$ and $F(I^+(p))=I^+(F(p))$ for every event\n$p$, and $p\\le q$ if and only if $F(p)\\le F(q)$ for all events $p,q$.\n\n**(g) Scope.** The theorem concerns the affine model: it attaches no physical\ninterpretation to $\\le$, $J^+$, $I^+$ and $R^+$, asserts nothing about partial\ndifferential equations, propagation of particular fields or differentiability\nbeyond piecewise $C^1$, and uses no choice principle.",
          "strategy": "Proof in dependency order; (a)-(d) are integral inequalities, (e) is the same inequality after a boost normalization, (f) is the cone invariance of a proper orthochronous Lorentz map. (a) Fix an inertial chart. By lem-sr-weak-piecewise-causal-integrability, all coordinate derivatives are absolutely improperly integrable and their finite sums equal D, with D0>=|D_spatial|. The lemma's strictness clause covers both an interior timelike tangent and a timelike one-sided quotient tangent at a corner, without derivative-limit continuity. A regular future causal path has D0>0; more generally D0=0 implies every nonnegative interior time derivative is zero: any positive value would by continuity have a compact positive neighborhood and force a positive time increment. Then |z'|<=y0'=0 on every open piece, so every coordinate is constant on the pieces and endpoint continuity makes the complete path constant. This repairs the D0=0 inference explicitly; spatial vanishing alone does not prove time vanishing. (b) q in J^+(p) with q not p means D in Cbar^+ with D not 0; the straight path s |-> p + sD has constant tangent D (def-derivative, def-affine-space-and-displacement), which is future causal and nonzero, so it is a future causal path; conversely a future causal straight path has nonzero future causal tangent D, so D is in Cbar^+; this proves R^+(p) = J^+(p) and the straight-segment criterion. For I^+(p): if D is in C^+, the straight path is a future causal path with future timelike tangent, so I^+(p) is contained in the second set; conversely, if a piecewise C^1 future causal path from p to q has a future timelike tangent, then (a) gives D in C^+, so q is in I^+(p); and no closed future causal path can have a timelike tangent because its displacement would have to lie in C^+ intersect {0}. (c) If p <= q and q <= p then q - p lies in Cbar^+ intersect (Cbar^-) = {0} by def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets, so p = q; a nonconstant closed future causal path would give D = 0 with nonconstant path, contradicting (a). (d) Apply (a) to the restriction of y to [a',b'] for arbitrary a' <= b'; the displacement has nonnegative time coordinate by (a) and the second clause of (a) with (b) of lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion gives strict positivity exactly for nonconstant restrictions. (e) Let x be an inertial chart and let v := D/T (in V), a future unit timelike vector with coordinates (v^0,v_s), v^0 > 0 and |v_s| < v^0 (lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion(a)); put beta := v_s/v^0 in R^3, so |beta| = |v_s|/v^0 < 1, and let B(beta) be the passive boost of def-lorentz-boost. Then B(beta)v = e_0: indeed B(beta) is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map (lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse, def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group), so B(beta)v is future unit timelike with time coordinate gamma(v^0 - beta . v_s) = gamma((v^0)^2 - |v_s|^2)/v^0 = gamma/v^0 = 1, since gamma = (1 - |beta|^2)^{-1/2} = v^0 by the unit identity, hence B(beta)v = e_0 and B(beta)D = T e_0. Let x_1 be the chart of the inertial frame whose ordered basis is e'_a := B(beta)^{-1} e_a (a valid eta-orthonormal, future and positively oriented basis because B(beta)^{-1} is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map, def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map, thm-unique-coordinates-with-respect-to-an-ordered-basis): then x_1 = B(beta) o x up to translation and x_1(q) - x_1(p) = T e_0 with coordinates (T,0,0,0). The chart change is an affine map whose linear part is a Lorentz map, so it preserves eta: eta(y',y') = eta(y_1',y_1') for the coordinate paths, and it preserves the future direction of timelike tangents; hence y_1' is future timelike, its time coordinate is positive, and pointwise sqrt((y_1'^0)^2 - |y_1'_s|^2) <= y_1'^0 with equality exactly when y_1'_s = 0 (elementary inequality for 0 <= |b| <= a: sqrt(a^2 - b^2) <= a with equality iff b = 0). By lem-sr-weak-piecewise-causal-integrability and def-sr-piecewise-proper-time, the finite improper-piece sum gives c Delta tau(y)=sum_i integral f_i<=sum_i integral y1'^0=x1^0(q)-x1^0(p)=T. Thus Delta tau(y)<=T/c, with exactly one conversion factor c^{-1}. Each integral is first taken on proper interior truncations and then passed to its finite endpoint limits. For equality put h_i=y1'^0-f_i>=0 on each open piece; it is continuous and dominated by y1'^0, hence improperly integrable. Equality forces every integral h_i to vanish. If h_i were positive at an interior point, continuity would give a compact interval with positive integral, contradicting that vanishing. Hence every spatial derivative vanishes on every open piece. Zero-derivative constancy and endpoint continuity give a constant spatial coordinate throughout; the positive time derivative makes the time coordinate strictly increasing, so y(s)=p+sigma(s)D with sigma continuous, strictly increasing and onto [0,1], C1 on open pieces and with finite positive one-sided quotient derivatives inherited from y. Conversely such a worldline has density T sigma', and its improper integral telescopes by the supplier lemma to T(sigma(b)-sigma(a))=T. This proves the bound and exact equality case for the approved weak class, retaining every straight attainment conclusion. (f) If F(p) = F(p_0) + L(p - p_0) with L proper orthochronous (def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group), then F(p + Cbar^+) = F(p) + L(Cbar^+) = F(p) + Cbar^+ because L preserves the future and past closed cones (thm-sr-proper-orthochronous-lorentz-algebra(iv)), which is exactly F(J^+(p)) = J^+(F(p)) and likewise for C^+ and I^+; the relation form follows. No choice principle is used: the arguments are finite-dimensional coordinate calculus, one explicit boost and ordinary integrals on compact interior truncations and finite improper endpoint limits.",
