        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-causal-signal-order-and-composition",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Signals in the model: receipt in the future cone, relay composition, absence of closed signal loops, and frame independence of the causal signal condition",
          "statement": "Adopt the postulates [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]],\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] and the causal signal restriction\n[[post-sr-causal-signal-propagation]], and use the events, the relations $\\le$\nand $<$ and the sets $J^+$ and $I^+$ of\n[[def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space]] together with the\nmathematical results\n[[lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion]],\n[[lem-sr-spacelike-pairs-have-frame-dependent-time-order]] and\n[[thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence]]. Let $x$ be the chart of\nany inertial frame with $x^0=ct$\n([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]],\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]). Then, as conditional\nstatements about the ideal model and not about any actual apparatus:\n\n**(i) Receipt in the future light cone and absolute temporal order.** If a\nsignal is transmitted from $p$ to $q$, then $q\\in J^+(p)$; if $q\\ne p$, then\n$x^0(q)>x^0(p)$ and $|x_s(q)-x_s(p)|\\le x^0(q)-x^0(p)$ in every inertial chart,\nand every inertial frame's time order places the reception after the emission.\nHence no inertial change of description reports the reception before the\nemission, and the temporal order of the endpoints of a transmission is the same\nin every inertial frame.\n\n**(ii) Relay composition.** If signals are transmitted from $p$ to $q$ and from\n$q$ to $r$, then $r\\in J^+(p)$; if in addition $q\\in I^+(p)$ or\n$r\\in I^+(q)$, then $r\\in I^+(p)$. Thus relaying never leaves the causal future\nof the first emission; a relay with at least one nonzero leg never returns to\nthe emission event.\n\n**(iii) No closed signal loop.** If signals are transmitted from $p$ to $q$ and\nfrom $q$ to $p$, then $p=q$. More generally, a finite chain of transmissions is closed if and\nonly if every leg has zero displacement; no nontrivial chain returns to its\nown emission event.\n\n**(iv) Frame independence of the causal signal condition.** For every proper\northochronous affine Lorentz map $F$ of the model, which is an inertial change\nof description by [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]], one has\n$F(J^+(p))=J^+(F(p))$ and $F(I^+(p))=I^+(F(p))$ for all events $p$, and\n$p\\le q$ if and only if $F(p)\\le F(q)$; hence the necessary causal condition on a signal between two events is the\nsame in every inertial frame, in agreement with\n[[pthm-sr-interval-and-cone-invariance]]. This does not assert existence of a\ntransmission for every causally related pair, or invariance of a separately\nchosen transmission relation without its own covariance assumption.\n\n**(v) Spacelike pairs admit no signal.** If $q-p$ is spacelike, then no signal\nis transmitted from $p$ to $q$, while there are inertial frames whose charts\nassign $x^0(q)-x^0(p)$ either sign; consequently the model's signal restriction\nis exactly the requirement that transmissions occur only between coincident events or distinct events whose temporal order is\nobserver-independent, with the emission the earlier endpoint.\n\n**(vi) Scope and qualification.** Every clause is conditional on the adopted\npostulates and is a statement about the ideal flat model; no actual signal,\ntransmitter, receiver, detector or measurement is described, no statistical or\nempirical claim is made, and no inference from the model's structure to the\nphysical world is drawn. Clause (v) uses the mathematical fact\n[[lem-sr-spacelike-pairs-have-frame-dependent-time-order]] and not an\nexperiment. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Conditional deduction from the adopted premises; the mathematical content is the three named items. (i) is the defining clause of post-sr-causal-signal-propagation combined with the coordinate criterion of lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion(c), which gives x^0(q) - x^0(p) = D^0 > 0 and |D_s| <= D^0 for q not p in J^+(p); the statement for every inertial chart uses that the criterion holds in the chart of every inertial frame and that a proper orthochronous transition preserves the closed cone (thm-sr-proper-orthochronous-lorentz-algebra(iv), lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure). (ii) applies the restriction to the two transmissions and then the finite-sums clause of lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion(b) to r - p = (q - p) + (r - q), with the timelike case following from the strict part of the same clause. (iii) is the antisymmetry of <= proved in thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence(c): q - p and p - q both in Cbar^+ force q - p = 0. (iv) is thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence(f), with post-sr-inertial-covariance identifying proper orthochronous affine Lorentz maps as the inertial changes of description; pthm-sr-interval-and-cone-invariance already records the invariance of the causal classification and of the future/past alternative for the specified model equations. (v) combines the restriction with lem-sr-spacelike-pairs-have-frame-dependent-time-order(b)-(d), which exhibits inertial frames with time differences of either sign and an inertial frame of simultaneity, none of which is compatible with receipt in the causal future. (vi) carries the idealizations of the postulates into the conclusion, states that no empirical or statistical claim is made, and marks clause (v) as a mathematical exclusion rather than an observed one. No choice principle is used.",
