        {
          "id": "cex-sr-null-separated-events-are-not-timelike-joinable",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Counterexample: a causally related pair of events need not be joinable by a future timelike worldline",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'if q lies in J^+(p) then q lies in I^+(p), i.e. every causally related pair of events can be joined by a future timelike worldline.' Counterexample: let p := x^{-1}(0,0,0,0) and q := x^{-1}(3,3,0,0) in the chart x of an inertial frame. Then D := q - p = (3,3,0,0) has eta(D,D) = -9 + 9 = 0 with D^0 = 3 > 0, so D is future null and q lies in J^+(p) but not in I^+(p) by the coordinate criterion; in particular the straight path from p to q is future null, not future timelike. Moreover no future timelike worldline y: [a,b] -> A with y(a) = p and y(b) = q exists: if it did, its displacement q - p would be future timelike by clause (a) of thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence, contradicting eta(D,D) = 0. The pair (p,q) is nonetheless causally related and the null straight segment realizes the relation, so the refuted statement is false.",
          "strategy": "The evaluations eta(D,D) = 0 and D^0 = 3 > 0 are direct; the coordinate criterion of lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion(c) gives q in J^+(p) and q not in I^+(p) because the inequality D^0 >= |D_s| is an equality. The non-existence argument is the final clause of thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence(a): a future timelike worldline has a future timelike tangent everywhere, and the same clause applied to its displacement would make eta(q - p, q - p) negative, contradicting the computed zero. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence",
