        {
          "id": "cor-sr-reverse-triangle-and-future-cone-convexity",
          "kind": "corollary",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The reversed triangle inequality and convexity of the future timelike cone",
          "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space. (a) Sum of two future timelike vectors. If v,w in V are future timelike then v + w is future timelike and sqrt(-eta(v+w,v+w)) >= sqrt(-eta(v,v)) + sqrt(-eta(w,w)), with equality if and only if v and w are positive proportional. (b) Convexity and concavity along segments. If v,w are future timelike then t v + (1-t) w is future timelike for every real t in [0,1], so the future timelike set is convex; moreover sqrt(-eta(t v + (1-t) w, t v + (1-t) w)) >= t sqrt(-eta(v,v)) + (1-t) sqrt(-eta(w,w)) for every t in [0,1], with equality for 0 < t < 1 if and only if v and w are positive proportional. (c) Finite sums. If v_1,...,v_k are future timelike then v_1 + ... + v_k is future timelike and sqrt(-eta(sum_i v_i, sum_i v_i)) >= sum_i sqrt(-eta(v_i,v_i)), with equality if and only if all v_i are positive multiples of one single future timelike vector. The inequalities are those of the model's eta and are independent of any chart; they are the reversed (Lorentzian) forms of the triangle inequality of the spatial Euclidean structure.",
          "strategy": "Proof from lem-sr-reverse-cauchy-schwarz-for-future-timelike-vectors and the coordinate description of the cone. (a) Future direction and timelikeness: in an inertial frame write v = (v^0,v_s), w = (w^0,w_s); the coordinate description of the future cone (lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components) gives v^0 > |v_s| and w^0 > |w_s|, so (v+w)^0 = v^0 + w^0 > |v_s| + |w_s| >= |v_s + w_s| by the triangle inequality for the Euclidean norm (cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm), which is exactly the coordinate description of future timelikeness for v + w. Inequality: sqrt(-eta(v+w,v+w))^2 = -eta(v,v) - 2 eta(v,w) - eta(w,w) = a^2 + b^2 + 2(-eta(v,w)) with a := sqrt(-eta(v,v)), b := sqrt(-eta(w,w)); by the reverse Cauchy-Schwarz inequality -eta(v,w) >= ab, so the square is at least (a+b)^2, and monotonicity of squaring on the nonnegatives (lem-of-square-monotone, thm-of-square-roots) gives the claim. Equality requires -eta(v,w) = ab, which by clause (ii) of the reverse Cauchy-Schwarz lemma is exactly positive proportionality. (b) For 0 <= t <= 1 the vector t v is future timelike when t > 0 (its coordinates are (t v^0, t v_s) with t v^0 > t|v_s| = |t v_s|) and 0 when t = 0; applying (a) to the two future timelike vectors t v and (1-t) w (with the zero vector case handled directly) gives both the membership and the inequality, and the equality statement reduces to the equality case of (a) applied to those two vectors, which are positive proportional exactly when v and w are. (c) Induction on k using (a): thm-induction-principle; the equality case is the conjunction of the equality conditions at each step, which forces all vectors onto one positive ray. All arguments are finite-dimensional and no choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "lem-sr-reverse-cauchy-schwarz-for-future-timelike-vectors",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
