        {
          "id": "lem-sr-rest-frame-of-a-future-timelike-displacement",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Existence of an inertial frame whose time axis is a prescribed future timelike direction",
          "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space, let d in V be a future timelike displacement and put T := sqrt(-eta(d,d)) > 0 and n := d/T. Then: (i) eta(n,n) = -1 and n is future timelike, so n is a future unit timelike vector; (ii) for every event o there is an inertial frame (o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) of the model with e_0 = n; (iii) for the inertial chart x of any such frame one has x(o + lambda d) = (lambda T, 0, 0, 0) for every real lambda; in particular x(o) = 0 and x(o + d) = (T, 0, 0, 0). Consequently, for any two events p,q with q - p = d, the chart of such a frame represents the displacement q - p by the coordinate vector (T,0,0,0): the spatial coordinates of p and q agree and their time coordinates differ by T.",
          "strategy": "Proof. (i) Bilinearity gives eta(n,n) = eta(d,d)/T^2 = -1 (def-lorentz-form-and-inertia, def-rational-power), and n is future timelike because the future timelike set is closed under multiplication by the positive real 1/T (cor-sr-reverse-triangle-and-future-cone-convexity, clause (b)). (ii) Fix any inertial frame with origin o, with basis (f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3) (lem-future-oriented-inertial-frames-exist). Let (n^0, n_s) be the coordinates of n in this frame. Since n is future timelike, the coordinate description of the future cone (lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components) gives n^0 > |n_s| >= 0, and eta(n,n) = -(n^0)^2 + |n_s|^2 = -1 gives (n^0)^2 = 1 + |n_s|^2 > 0, so the boost parameter beta := n_s/n^0 (a vector of R^3) satisfies |beta| = |n_s|/n^0 < 1 (def-lorentz-boost). Let B := B(beta) be the passive boost of the frame. By lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse (iv), B n = f_0; by the same lemma (i) and (iii), B is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map with inverse B^{-1} = B(-beta). Define the ordered basis e_0 := n and e_i := B^{-1} f_i for i = 1,2,3; then it is the image under the Lorentz map B^{-1} of the eta-orthonormal basis (f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3) with B^{-1} f_0 = n = e_0, so it is eta-orthonormal with eta(e_0,e_0) = -1 and eta(e_0,e_i) = 0 (lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure (i),(iii)); its first vector is future, and it is positively oriented because B^{-1} is proper, hence preserves the oriented volume form and the orientation class (lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure (iii)). Therefore (o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) is an inertial frame with first vector n (def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart). (iii) For its chart x(p) = E^{-1}(p - o) with E(u) = sum_a u^a e_a one has E(1,0,0,0) = e_0 = n, hence x(o + lambda d) = E^{-1}(lambda d) = lambda T E^{-1}(n) = lambda T (1,0,0,0), and x(o) = 0. No choice principle is used: the construction uses one finite-dimensional basis and the explicit boost of def-lorentz-boost.",
          "deps": [
