        {
          "id": "lem-sr-endpoint-displacement-of-a-future-timelike-worldline",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The displacement of a future timelike worldline is future timelike",
          "statement": "Let $c>0$ and let $x:[a,b]\\to A$ be a future timelike worldline with $a<b$\n([[def-interval]]) that is piecewise $C^1$ with partition\n$a=s_0<s_1<\\dots<s_m=b$ in the weak sense of\n[[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]], with future timelike interior\ntangents and finite one-sided difference-quotient tangents. Put $p:=x(a)$,\n$q:=x(b)$ and $d:=q-p$. Then $d$ is future timelike. More precisely, writing\n$\\xi^a:=x_A^a\\circ x$ for the coordinate path of the chart $x_A$ of any inertial\nframe, one has\n$$\\xi^0(b)-\\xi^0(a)>0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad|\\xi_{\\mathrm{vec}}(b)-\\xi_{\\mathrm{vec}}(a)|<\\xi^0(b)-\\xi^0(a),$$\nwhere $|\\cdot|$ is the Euclidean spatial norm of\n[[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]. Consequently $p\\ne q$,\nthe number $\\sqrt{-\\eta(d,d)}$ is a positive real number, and in every inertial\nchart the time component of $d$ is positive and dominates the Euclidean norm of\nits spatial part, which is exactly the coordinate description of future\ntimelikeness in that chart. Completeness of the real numbers is inherited\nfrom the improper-integrability supplier; no compactness assumption beyond\nthe given compact interval or choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Apply lem-sr-weak-piecewise-causal-integrability in an arbitrary inertial chart xi=(xi0,xi_vec). Finite one-sided quotient derivatives give continuity at the corners; on every compact interior truncation each derivative is continuous and the ordinary FTC applies. The lemma proves absolute improper integrability and the exact finite telescoping endpoint increments. Since every interior tangent is future timelike, xi0'-|xi_vec'|>0 throughout each open piece. It is continuous and positive on a compact interior neighborhood, giving a positive integral there; the rest contributes nonnegatively. The improper vector norm inequality proved by truncation gives |xi_vec(b)-xi_vec(a)|<=sum_i integral |xi_vec'|<sum_i integral xi0'=xi0(b)-xi0(a). Therefore the displacement is future timelike and nonzero in the arbitrary frame, its Lorentz norm gives a strictly positive square root, and all claimed coordinate inequalities follow. No continuous extension of the derivative, closed-piece Riemann integrability, or (H) is used; the former (H) cases remain included.",
          "deps": [
