          "physical_scope": "Ideal clocks and prescribed histories in the flat classical special-relativity model: the reading of an ideal clock between two fixed endpoint events along a piecewise C^1 future timelike worldline, compared with the reading along the straight inertial worldline between the same events. Conditional on the named postulates (ideal clocks with no acceleration-dependent term, prescribed histories, flat spacetime, fixed c). Excludes real clocks and apparatus, calibration and resolution effects, gravity, quantum phenomena, and any empirical or statistical claim.",
          "dependency_level": 12
        },
        {
          "id": "texp-sr-piecewise-inertial-twin-reunion",
          "kind": "thought-experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The piecewise inertial twin reunion: conditional differential aging",
          "statement": "Thought experiment: the piecewise inertial twin reunion of the adopted model, with its conditional conclusion. Adopt post-sr-inertial-covariance, post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light and post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis, and let c and the SI conventions be those of def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions. Fix an inertial frame (o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) with chart x, a duration T > 0 in seconds and a speed v with 0 < |v| < c; put gamma := (1 - v^2/c^2)^{-1/2}. Define two histories (coordinate time t in seconds, space and time coordinates in metres with x^0 = c t): (stay-at-home) x_stay(t) := x^{-1}(c t, 0, 0, 0) for t in [0, 2T]; (traveler) x_travel(t) := x^{-1}(c t, v t, 0, 0) for t in [0, T] and x_travel(t) := x^{-1}(c t, v(2T - t), 0, 0) for t in [T, 2T]. Then: (i) x_stay is the inertial future timelike worldline from p := x^{-1}(0,0,0,0) to q := x^{-1}(2cT,0,0,0), and x_travel is a piecewise inertial future timelike worldline from p to q whose two pieces have the constant tangents (c, v, 0, 0) and (c, -v, 0, 0), with the corner r := x^{-1}(cT, vT, 0, 0); the image of x_travel contains the three events p, r, q, which are not collinear in the affine model, so the image of x_travel is not contained in the straight segment from p to q. (ii) Conditional on the postulates, an ideal clock carried from p to q along x_stay reads 2T, while an ideal clock carried from p to q along x_travel reads 2T/gamma = 2T sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2); hence at the reunion event q the traveling clock is behind the stay-at-home clock by 2T(1 - 1/gamma) = 2T(1 - sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)) > 0. (iii) The conclusion is a comparison of two clock readings at the common events p and q; it does not depend on a simultaneity convention or on the inertial frame used to describe the histories, because both readings are proper-time integrals of the two histories. (iv) Scope and idealization: the scenario is a conditional model statement; the instantaneous turn at r idealizes a turnaround of finite duration, the clocks are ideal with no acceleration-dependent reading term, spacetime is flat, and no gravitational, quantum, apparatus or statistical effect is modeled; no measurement is reported. The conclusion is conditional on the adopted postulates and does not assert that nature is exactly described by the model.",
          "strategy": "Conditional deduction with the two explicit histories as witnesses. Step (i): differentiate the coordinate formulas; the coordinate derivatives are (c,0,0,0), (c,v,0,0) and (c,-v,0,0) respectively; each has -eta equal to c^2, c^2 - v^2, c^2 - v^2, all positive, and positive time component, so each piece is future timelike (def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors, def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets, def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart); hence x_stay is inertial and x_travel is piecewise inertial in the sense of def-sr-inertial-and-piecewise-inertial-worldline with corner r, and both are worldlines through p and q. Non-collinearity: p, r, q have coordinate vectors (0,0,0,0), (cT,vT,0,0), (2cT,0,0,0); since vT is nonzero, r is not on the affine line through p and q (whose second coordinate is 0), so the three are not collinear and the image of x_travel is not contained in the straight segment (an affine line maps to an affine line under the chart, so collinearity is exactly collinearity of the coordinate vectors). Step (ii): the straight history has displacement q - p with coordinates (2cT,0,0,0) and -eta(q-p,q-p) = 4c^2T^2, so its reading is c^{-1} 2cT = 2T (def-sr-proper-time straight-segment value and the clock hypothesis of post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light); each leg of x_travel has a constant tangent with -eta = c^2(1 - v^2/c^2) = c^2/gamma^2, so its proper time is T/gamma by the same straight-segment value applied to the leg restricted to its closed parameter interval, and the two-leg total is 2T/gamma by the constant-tangent special case (v) and concatenation additivity (vi) of the extended def-sr-piecewise-proper-time. Constant leg derivatives satisfy (H), so these improper values are the same ordinary Riemann values; the clock hypothesis of post-sr-free-trajectories-and-clock-hypothesis (ii) identifies the reading with this total proper time. The comparison 2T/gamma < 2T for v nonzero is the strict case of pthm-sr-same-endpoint-clock-comparison (iii), whose equality case is excluded by the non-collinearity of step (i); it can also be read off directly since 0 < 1 - 1/gamma. Step (iii): both readings are given by proper-time integrals built from eta and the tangents alone, so no chart or simultaneity convention enters; the events p and q are common to the two histories. Step (iv): record the idealizations and the conditionality; do not infer any measured outcome, and do not upgrade the statement to a claim about real clocks.",
