      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-sr-reverse-cauchy-schwarz-and-equality",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Worked reverse Cauchy-Schwarz and reverse triangle inequalities",
          "statement": "Worked computation in an inertial frame with eta = diag(-1,1,1,1). (a) For v := (5,3,0,0) and w := (5,-3,0,0): eta(v,v) = -25 + 9 = -16 and eta(w,w) = -16, so the timelike lengths are sqrt(16) = 4; eta(v,w) = -25 - 9 = -34, so -eta(v,w) = 34 >= 4*4 = 16, and the inequality is strict because v and w are not proportional. (b) For w' := 2v = (10,6,0,0): eta(w',w') = -100 + 36 = -64, sqrt(-eta(w',w')) = 8, eta(v,w') = -50 + 18 = -32 (twice eta(v,v) = -16), and -eta(v,w') = 32 = 4*8, the equality case of the reverse Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for the positive proportional pair (v, 2v). (c) Reverse triangle: v + w = (10,0,0,0) has -eta(v+w,v+w) = 100, so sqrt = 10 >= 4 + 4 = 8, again strict since v and w are not proportional. For u := (5,0,0,0), of timelike length 5, one has -eta(v,u) = 25 >= 4*5 = 20 and v + u = (10,3,0,0) with sqrt(-eta(v+u,v+u)) = sqrt(100-9) = sqrt(91) >= 4 + 5 = 9. (d) The two vectors v and w are both future timelike (5 > 3 and 5 > 3 in the first coordinate), so all inequalities are instances of the future timelike hypotheses.",
          "strategy": "Every line is a finite evaluation of the displayed diagonal form: squares of the coordinate entries, the diagonal metric, and a comparison of exact integers or of the square root of 91 with 9 (via 91 > 81 and monotonicity of the square root). The equality check evaluates -eta(v,2v) = 8^2 - 4^2 + ... directly: -eta(v,2v) = 2*16 = 32 = 4*8. No limiting, continuity or choice argument occurs.",
          "deps": [
