        {
          "id": "cex-sr-reverse-cauchy-schwarz-fails-outside-the-cone-pair",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Counterexample: the reversed Cauchy-Schwarz inequality fails outside same-orientation timelike pairs",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'the reversed Cauchy-Schwarz inequality -eta(v,w) >= sqrt(-eta(v,v)) sqrt(-eta(w,w)) holds for every pair of vectors v,w that are individually timelike, regardless of their time orientations, and the analogous reversed form |eta(v,w)| >= sqrt(eta(v,v)) sqrt(eta(w,w)) holds for every pair of spacelike vectors'. First witness: in an inertial frame with eta = diag(-1,1,1,1) let v := (5,3,0,0), future timelike with -eta(v,v) = 16, and w := (-5,3,0,0), past timelike with -eta(w,w) = 16. Then eta(v,w) = 25 + 9 = 34 > 0, so -eta(v,w) = -34, while sqrt(-eta(v,v)) sqrt(-eta(w,w)) = 16; the asserted inequality -34 >= 16 is false. The sum v + w = (0,6,0,0) is spacelike (eta(v+w,v+w) = 36 > 0), so the failure is exactly the failure of the future cone to contain sums of vectors of opposite orientation. Second witness: v := (0,1,0,0) and w := (0,1,1,0) are spacelike with eta(v,v) = 1, eta(w,w) = 2 and eta(v,w) = 1, so |eta(v,w)| = 1 < sqrt(2) = sqrt(eta(v,v)) sqrt(eta(w,w)): the reversed spacelike form fails for a pair whose span contains no null direction. Hence the same-orientation timelike hypothesis of lem-sr-reverse-cauchy-schwarz-for-future-timelike-vectors cannot be dropped, and the spacelike reversed inequality is not universal.",
          "strategy": "Both witnesses are finite evaluations of the diagonal form: eta(v,w) for the mixed-orientation pair, eta(v+w,v+w), and the spacelike quantities are computed by direct multiplication and addition; the comparisons 34 > 16 and 1 < sqrt(2) are exact (squaring the second). The examples make no claim beyond the failure of the stated universal quantifiers; the positive results are lem-sr-reverse-cauchy-schwarz-for-future-timelike-vectors (same-orientation future timelike pairs) and its past-timelike clause.",
          "deps": [
