      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-sr-metric-duality-on-lorentz-vector-space",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The metric pairing is an isomorphism to the dual, and the raised covector in an orthonormal frame",
          "statement": "Let (V,eta) be a 4-dimensional Lorentz vector space (def-lorentz-form-and-inertia): eta is symmetric and bilinear, nondegenerate in the sense that both radicals are {0} (def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form), and of inertia (3,1,0). Let V* be the algebraic dual (def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional). Then: (a) the map iota: V -> V*, iota(v)(w) := eta(v,w), is linear and injective, since iota(v) = 0 means eta(v,w) = 0 for all w in V, i.e. v lies in the left radical, which is {0}. (b) dim V* = dim V = 4, so iota is a linear bijection (rank-nullity, thm-rank-nullity). (c) Consequently for every covector k in V* there is a unique vector k^sharp in V with eta(k^sharp,w) = k(w) for all w in V; the assignment sharp: V* -> V, k |-> k^sharp, is linear and is the inverse of iota, so (v^flat)^sharp = v for v^flat := iota(v) and (k^sharp)^flat = k for every k; in particular k^sharp = 0 if and only if k = 0. (d) If (e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) is an eta-orthonormal ordered basis of V (lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis) and k_a := k(e_a) are the components of k in it, then k^sharp = -k_0 e_0 + k_1 e_1 + k_2 e_2 + k_3 e_3; that is, in this basis (k^sharp)^0 = -k_0 and (k^sharp)^i = k_i for i = 1,2,3, and eta(k^sharp,k^sharp) = -(k_0)^2 + (k_1)^2 + (k_2)^2 + (k_3)^2. Hence k^sharp is null exactly when (k_0)^2 = (k_1)^2 + (k_2)^2 + (k_3)^2, timelike when the left side is smaller, and spacelike when it is larger (def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors). No orientation, time orientation or affine structure is used, and no choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Proof strategy. (a) Linearity in v is the bilinearity of eta; iota(v) = 0 is the statement that v lies in the left radical, and the defining nondegeneracy clause says the left radical is {0}. (b) The dual basis theorem (thm-dual-family-is-a-basis-in-finite-dimension) exhibits a basis of V* with one element per basis element of V, so dim V* = dim V; an injective linear map between finite-dimensional spaces of equal dimension is bijective, either directly by thm-rank-nullity (nullity 0 forces rank = dim V = dim V*) or by the equivalent characterization of invertibility. (c) Surjectivity supplies existence, injectivity supplies uniqueness, and the two inverse identities are immediate from the definitions. (d) Evaluate eta(-k_0 e_0 + sum_i k_i e_i, e_b) using eta-orthonormality: eta(-k_0 e_0, e_0) = -k_0 eta(e_0,e_0) = -k_0(-1) = k_0 and eta(k_i e_i, e_i) = k_i (no summation), with all cross terms zero; this equals k_b on the basis, hence the covectors agree by linearity, giving the component formula. The quadratic form is the diagonal evaluation diag(-1,1,1,1); the causal trichotomy is the definition of causal character. The statement is pure algebra of the Lorentz form and asserts nothing physical. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional",
            "def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form",
