        {
          "id": "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Affine phase functions and wave covectors on affine Minkowski space",
          "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space (def-sr-affine-minkowski-model), with displacements q - p in V (def-affine-space-and-displacement). (a) A map theta: A -> R is an affine phase function if there are q in A and a covector k in V* (def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional) with theta(p) - theta(q) = k(p - q) for all p in A; the covector k is then unique (the differential d theta of theta, called its wave covector only when k is nonzero), and for each fixed q the map theta |-> (k, theta(q)) is a bijection onto V* x R, with theta(p) = k(p - q) + theta(q). In the inertial chart x of a frame (o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) one has theta(p) = k_a x^a(p) + theta(o) with k_a := k(e_a) the components of k in the frame (thm-unique-coordinates-with-respect-to-an-ordered-basis), and theta(o') = theta(o) + k(o' - o) for two origins. (b) Constant-phase directions: for v in V one has k(v) = 0 if and only if theta is constant on the line p + R v for every p in A, since theta(p + s v) = theta(p) + s k(v); if k is nonzero, the direction subspace ker k := {v in V : k(v) = 0} is a 3-dimensional linear subspace and every nonempty level set Sigma_t := {p in A : theta(p) = t} is the affine hyperplane p_0 + ker k through any of its points p_0; if k = 0 then theta is constant and Sigma_t is empty or all of A. For every C^1 curve gamma: I -> A one has (theta o gamma)'(s) = k(gamma'(s)) (thm-chain-rule, def-derivative). (c) A wave covector on (A,V,eta) is a nonzero element k of V*, and with k^sharp the raised vector of lem-sr-metric-duality-on-lorentz-vector-space k is called null, timelike or spacelike when eta(k^sharp,k^sharp) is zero, negative or positive, and future, past, future null, past null or causal when k^sharp is respectively future causal, past causal, future null, past null or causal in the sense of def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets; a future null wave covector is one that is both future and null. These definitions are well posed because the causal classification of a nonzero vector is by the sign of eta and by membership in the closed cones, and k^sharp = 0 exactly when k = 0 (duality lemma). (d) This item introduces mathematical objects and terminology only: it excludes the zero covector from 'wave covector', it fixes no units and no physical interpretation, and no claim that light or any physical wave is described by such a phase is made here; that identification is adopted in post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Proof strategy. (a) Uniqueness of k: if k and k' both satisfy the defining identity then (k - k')(p - q) = 0 for all p, so k - k' = 0; the bijection with V* x R is the two inverse constructions theta |-> (d theta, theta(q)) and (k, theta_0) |-> [p |-> k(p - q) + theta_0]. The chart formula is the affine chart identity x(p) - x(q) = (x^a(p) - x^a(q)) e_a together with linearity of k. (b) The displayed identity theta(p + s v) = theta(p) + s k(v) is linearity of k in the displacement; the kernel of a nonzero linear functional on a 4-dimensional space is 3-dimensional (thm-rank-nullity) and the level set is a coset of it. The curve derivative is the chain rule for theta o gamma, whose total derivative at p is k for every p (the phase is affine). (c) The definitions transfer verbatim the vector classification of def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors and def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets along the linear bijection sharp of lem-sr-metric-duality-on-lorentz-vector-space; injectivity of sharp makes the zero-covector exclusion and the well-definedness statement true. (d) A definition item carries formulation obligations only, so no proof of a physical claim is manufactured. Scope: pure linear algebra and affine geometry on A; no choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-affine-space-and-displacement",
            "def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional",
            "lem-sr-metric-duality-on-lorentz-vector-space",
