        {
          "id": "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Null wave covectors, frame frequency data and the observer frequency of an ideal plane wave",
          "statement": "Adopt the SI coordinate and unit conventions of def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions: in every inertial chart x^0 = c t with c > 0 in metres per second and spatial coordinates in metres, so c is the model's conversion constant. Let k be a future null wave covector and theta its affine phase (def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector). (a) Frame data as wave data. In the inertial chart of a frame let (varpi,s) be the frame data of lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data, so varpi = -k(e_0) > 0 and |s| = 1. Define the angular frequency omega := c varpi (unit s^-1), the linear frequency nu := omega/(2 pi) (unit s^-1, hertz), the wave vector kappa := (omega/c) s (unit m^-1; |kappa| = omega/c), the wavelength lambda := 2 pi/|kappa| = 2 pi c/omega = c/nu (unit m), and the propagation direction s (dimensionless). In these conventions the phase is theta(p) = -omega t + kappa . x_vec(p) + const with t := x^0(p)/c the coordinate time in seconds, so the components of k in the chart are k_a = (-omega/c, (omega/c)s_1, (omega/c)s_2, (omega/c)s_3). (b) Observer frequency. For an ideal observer whose four-velocity is U_o = c n with n in V future unit timelike (eta(n,n) = -1; def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration, thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition) define the observer angular frequency of the wave as omega_n := -c k(n) = -k(U_o) in s^-1, and the observer linear frequency as omega_n/(2 pi); by lem-sr-phase-rate-along-worldline this is minus the rate of change of the phase with respect to the observer's own proper time, and the sign convention makes it positive. (c) Well-definedness. varpi = -k(e_0) > 0 and |s| = 1 in every inertial frame (frame-data lemma), hence omega > 0 and lambda > 0 are well defined and independent of the choices made in their computation; omega_n > 0 for every future unit timelike n and every future null wave covector (lem-sr-future-causal-pairing-negative), so every ideal observer reads a positive angular frequency; omega_n depends only on k and the observer vector n. (d) This item fixes definitions, units and sign conventions only. It does not assert that any physical light is described by such a phase; that identification is adopted in post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model. It contains no quantum identification of energy with hbar omega, no amplitude or intensity content, no medium or dispersion model, no source or detector model and no measurement or empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "Formulation obligations, not a proof of a physical claim. (a) The definitions are functions of k and the frame; well-definedness of varpi and s is lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data and the SI conversion is def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions, so the physical names attached to the mathematical numbers are a convention fixed here. The relation theta(p) = -omega t + kappa . x_vec(p) + const is the substitution x^0 = c t in k_a x^a. (b) n = U_o/c is future unit timelike by thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition(a); omega_n = -c k(n) = -k(U_o) by linearity; positivity and the proper-time-derivative reading are lem-sr-future-causal-pairing-negative and lem-sr-phase-rate-along-worldline with the sign convention explicitly chosen here. (c) Positivity and uniqueness in each named frame are supplied by the quoted lemmas. (d) The scope clause records that the physical interpretation (light is described by an ideal plane wave, and the number defined here is the frequency an ideal observer reads) is adopted only in post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model; the definition item must not be presented as implying it. No proof of a physical claim is manufactured; no choice principle is used. Angular well-definedness: define the dot product and norm of spatial coordinate triples by their three-entry sums; thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite gives |v dot w| <= |v||w|, so nonzero v,w have positive norms and normalized dot product in [-1,1], the domain of the constructed arccos.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector",
            "lem-sr-metric-duality-on-lorentz-vector-space",
            "lem-sr-future-causal-pairing-negative",
