          "physical_scope": "Ideal wave vocabulary in flat classical special-relativistic spacetime: the angular frequency, linear frequency, wave vector, wavelength and propagation direction attached to a future null wave covector in an inertial frame, and the observer-frequency sign convention omega_n = -c k(n) = -k(U_o) for an ideal observer. Conditional on the model's SI conventions; excludes quantum photon and energy identifications, media and dispersion, amplitude and intensity, sources and detectors, real apparatus, and every measurement or empirical claim.",
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted ideal plane-wave and frequency-reading model",
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of this framework, extending post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light (ideal vacuum light rays are exactly the future null straight lines, with coordinate speed c in every inertial chart) and post-sr-inertial-covariance (the affine Minkowski model, with the model's equations covariant under proper orthochronous affine Lorentz maps): (i) Ideal plane wave. An ideal monochromatic light wave in vacuum is modeled, in any inertial chart, by a dimensionless affine phase theta with future null wave covector k (def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector, lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data): the level sets of theta are the wave fronts, every line p + R k^sharp has constant phase, and the rays of this ideal wave are the future null straight lines p + R_{>=0} k^sharp, consistently with the general ideal-light clause that the ideal vacuum light rays are exactly the future null straight lines. The model is one ideal plane wave: constant wave covector, no medium, no dispersion, no absorption, no modulation or envelope, no boundary or source model, no intensity or polarization model. (ii) Frequency reading. An ideal observer of the model with four-velocity U_o = c n (n future unit timelike) that receives this wave reads the angular frequency omega_n := -c k(n) = -k(U_o) and the linear frequency omega_n/(2 pi) of def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency, i.e. minus the rate of change of the phase with respect to the observer's own proper time with the sign convention that this number is positive (lem-sr-phase-rate-along-worldline, lem-sr-future-causal-pairing-negative). The reading is attached to the observer's worldline at the event of reception. (iii) Frequency comparisons. Comparing the frequency of one wave between two ideal observers or between two inertial descriptions means comparing omega_n at specified reception events, or the frame data (omega,s) of the same phase function in specified inertial charts; this postulate asserts no Doppler or aberration formula, and comparing events requires propagating the phase along the specified observer worldlines. (iv) Scope and exclusions. These are adopted idealizations, not deductions from experiment and not claims about nature. No quantum photon model and no identification of energy with hbar omega is made (excluded by the conventions of def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions); no claim is made that real light, real clocks, real receivers or real sources are exactly described; dispersive, absorbing, nonlinear or modulated media, finite line width, intensity, polarization, source and detector physics, gravitation, quantum effects and accelerated-observer reception are outside the model. Empirical tests of the model belong to the framework's experimental work and are not asserted here.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it fixes the physical interpretation of the mathematical phase functions and of the observer-frequency number, and it fixes the ideal wave and receiver regime. Obligations: (i) the phase functions with future null wave covectors and their level sets are mathematical objects supplied by def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector and lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data; the identification of the level sets with wave fronts and of the future null lines along k^sharp with this wave's rays is adopted, extending the ideal-light clause of post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light; for a single plane wave every future null straight line of constant phase is exactly a line p + R_{>=0} k^sharp, so the adoption introduces no competing ray system. (ii) The reading rule is an identification of a physical clock reading of a periodic process with the mathematical number -k(U_o); the mathematical positivity and the proper-time derivative are lem-sr-phase-rate-along-worldline and lem-sr-future-causal-pairing-negative, while the identification itself is adopted. (iii) The postulate is deliberately silent about Doppler and aberration formulas, so it cannot be used to infer them; the comparison protocol is fixed so that downstream theorems state exactly which frequencies at which events they compare. (iv) The exclusions are part of the adopted content and must travel with every conclusion. No proof is manufactured; no choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector",
            "lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data",
