            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages): Section 3.2 'The stretch factor is the Doppler shift' pp. 55-57, Section 3.3 'Combination of velocities' pp. 57-59, Section 6.4 'Duality' pp. 134-137 (the frequency covector and its dual), Section 6.5.1 'Doppler shift' p. 137, Section 6.5.2 'Aberration' pp. 137-139 and Section 6.6.1 'Phase velocity' pp. 140-141; signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. Murugesh, 'Relativity - Special Theory' (IIST course notes for PH33003/PH43017, last update 2009-02-18, 31 PDF pages): Chapter 6 'Waves' pp. 25-28: phase matching and the wave four-vector (6.1)-(6.5) pp. 25-26, longitudinal Doppler effect (6.1.1) p. 26, transverse Doppler effect (6.1.2) p. 27 and aberration (6.2) p. 28",
                "url": "https://www.iist.ac.in/sites/default/files/2025-06/relativity.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "A. Einstein, 'Zur Elektrodynamik bewegter Koerper', Annalen der Physik 322 (10) 891-921 (1905), Section 7 'Theorie des Dopplerschen Prinzips und der Aberration', journal pp. 910-912: transformed wave phase and frequency nu' = nu (1 - cos(phi) v/V)/sqrt(1-(v/V)^2), the longitudinal special case nu' = nu sqrt((1-v/V)/(1+v/V)) and the aberration law cos(phi') = (cos(phi)-v/V)/(1-(v/V)cos(phi)); proofread transcription of the original at German Wikisource",
                "url": "https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Zur_Elektrodynamik_bewegter_K%C3%B6rper"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Ideal monochromatic vacuum plane waves and ideal observers in the flat classical special-relativity model: the phase model with future null wave covector, the identification of wave fronts and ideal rays, and the phase-per-proper-time frequency reading by an ideal observer. Adopted assumptions, not deductions and not empirical findings; excludes quantum, medium, dispersion, source, detector, intensity, polarization, gravity and real-apparatus effects; empirical tests are reserved to the framework's experimental work.",
          "dependency_level": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Doppler shift and aberration for an ideal plane wave between inertial descriptions",
          "statement": "Adopt post-sr-inertial-covariance, post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light and post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model, and the conventions of def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions. Let x and x' be inertial charts whose transition is x' = B(beta) x + b with beta in R^3, |beta| < 1, b in R^4 and B(beta) the passive boost of def-lorentz-boost; write gamma := (1 - |beta|^2)^(-1/2) and beta_hat := beta/|beta| for beta nonzero, so that the frame x' moves with velocity v = c beta relative to the frame x in the direction beta_hat. Let the ideal wave of the model have phase theta with future null wave covector k and frame-x data (omega,s) in the sense of def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency: omega > 0, s in R^3 with |s| = 1 and k_a = (-omega/c, (omega/c)s) in x. Then, conditionally on the adopted model: (i) Frame data in x'. The data of the same covector k in x' is (omega',s') with omega' = gamma(1 - beta . s) omega > 0 and s' = [s + ((gamma - 1)/|beta|^2)(beta . s) beta - gamma beta]/[gamma(1 - beta . s)] for beta nonzero (and omega' = omega, s' = s for beta = 0), so |s'| = 1 and k'_a = (-omega'/c, (omega'/c)s'_1, (omega'/c)s'_2, (omega'/c)s'_3) in x'. In the axis case beta = b e_1 with b in (-1,1): s'_1 = (s_1 - b)/(1 - b s_1) and (s'_2,s'_3) = (s_2,s_3)/(gamma(1 - b s_1)). (ii) Reading and frequency ratios. The rest observers of x and x' (four-velocities c e_0 and c e'_0) read angular frequencies omega = -c k(e_0) and omega' = -c k(e'_0); the linear frequencies and wavelengths of the two descriptions satisfy nu' = gamma(1 - beta . s) nu and lambda' = lambda/[gamma(1 - beta . s)]. More generally, an ideal observer whose coordinate velocity in x is w (a vector of the frame's spatial three-space with |w| < c) reads omega_o = gamma_w(1 - (w . s)/c) omega, where gamma_w := (1 - |w|^2/c^2)^(-1/2); equivalently omega_o = gamma_w(1 - beta_w . s) omega with beta_w := w/c. (iii) Longitudinal cases. If s = beta_hat then omega' = omega sqrt((1 - |beta|)/(1 + |beta|)) < omega, nu' = nu sqrt((1 - |beta|)/(1 + |beta|)) and lambda' = lambda sqrt((1 + |beta|)/(1 - |beta|)): the observer moving in the direction of propagation (away from a source at rest in x and located behind it) reads a red-shifted frequency and lengthened wavelength. If s = -beta_hat then omega' = omega sqrt((1 + |beta|)/(1 - |beta|)) > omega and lambda' = lambda sqrt((1 - |beta|)/(1 + |beta|)): blue shift for an observer moving opposite to the propagation direction (toward such a source). (iv) Transverse protocols. If beta . s = 0 (propagation direction perpendicular to the relative velocity in the frame x) then omega' = gamma omega; this is greater than omega when beta!=0 and equals omega when beta=0. If beta . s = |beta|^2, for beta!=0 equivalently s' . beta_hat = 0 (propagation direction perpendicular to the relative velocity in the frame x'), then omega' = omega/gamma; this is less than omega when beta!=0 and equals omega when beta=0. Both configurations are called transverse Doppler configurations in the literature and the two factors differ for beta!=0 (both equal one at beta=0); the direction and size of the shift are not determined by the word 'transverse' without specifying the frame in which transversality holds. (v) Aberration. For beta!=0, if theta_x, theta_x' in [0,pi] are the angles between the propagation directions s, s' and the boost axis beta_hat, then cos theta_x' = (cos theta_x - |beta|)/(1 - |beta| cos theta_x); the map u |-> (u - |beta|)/(1 - |beta| u) is a strictly increasing bijection of [-1,1] onto itself, and in particular cos theta_x = 0 gives cos theta_x' = -|beta|. (vi) Scope and protocol. All clauses concern one ideal plane wave and ideal observers, and frequencies are compared through the phase model at specified reception events; the results are conditional on the adopted postulates (ideal plane wave, ideal clocks, inertial covariance, flat classical spacetime) and are not empirical claims about nature, sources or apparatus. They exclude media and dispersion, modulation and signal speeds, intensity and polarization, real line widths, accelerating observers (reserved to the framework's accelerated-observer page), gravitation and quantum effects; no statistical or measurement statement is made. If beta = 0 the transition is the pure translation x'=x+b: the inertial axes and wave-covector frame data agree, with omega'=omega and s'=s, while the affine phase constant is transformed as in the covector/phase law.",
          "strategy": "Proof in dependency order; the mathematical core is lem-sr-null-covector-boost-transformation with the reading lemmas. (i) By def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency the frame data of k in x is (omega,s) with omega = c varpi, varpi = -k(e_0), and k_a = (-omega/c,(omega/c)s). Apply lem-sr-null-covector-boost-transformation(i)-(ii) with (varpi,s): its conclusions give varpi' = gamma(1 - beta . s) varpi > 0 and the displayed s' with |s'| = 1; multiplying by c gives omega' = gamma(1 - beta . s) omega; the axis formulas are clause (iii) of the lemma. The relation beta . s = |beta|^2 <=> s' . beta_hat = 0 is clause (iv) of the lemma, and the identity s' . beta_hat = (beta . s - |beta|^2)/(|beta|(1 - beta . s)) for beta nonzero is its display. (ii) For the rest observer of x, U = c e_0 and omega = -c k(e_0) by the definition; for x' likewise with the frame vector e'_0 (def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart). The wavelength ratio is the reciprocal of the frequency ratio because lambda = c/nu and lambda' = c/nu' by definition. For a general observer write U_o = gamma_w(c,w) by thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition(e); then k(U_o) = c gamma_w k(e_0) + gamma_w k(w), and k(w) = (omega/c) w . s because w lies in the frame's spatial three-space and k_a = (-omega/c,(omega/c)s) (def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame); hence omega_o = -k(U_o) = gamma_w(omega - (omega/c) w . s) = gamma_w(1 - (w . s)/c) omega. (iii) Substitute s = +/- beta_hat into (i): gamma(1 -/+ |beta|) = sqrt((1 -/+ |beta|)/(1 +/- |beta|)) because gamma^2(1 -/+ |beta|)^2 = (1 -/+ |beta|)/(1 +/- |beta|); the wavelength ratios are the reciprocals. The receding/approaching identification uses the fixed protocol: the source is at rest in x and the wave propagates from it, so s = beta_hat means the x'-rest observer moves away from the source and s = -beta_hat means it moves toward it. (iv) beta . s = 0 gives omega' = gamma omega from (i); beta . s = |beta|^2 gives omega' = gamma(1 - |beta|^2) omega = omega/gamma, and the equivalence with s' . beta_hat = 0 is the displayed identity; at beta=0 both read omega'=omega and no direction beta_hat is assigned; for nonzero beta, gamma>1 gives the stated strict inequalities. The two protocols are distinct because a unit s with beta . s = 0 is not a unit s with beta . s = |beta|^2 when |beta| > 0. The existence of both configurations for every |beta| < 1 is exhibited by the examples on the companion page. (v) For beta!=0 set b:=|beta| and use the invariant display s' . beta_hat = (beta . s - b^2)/(b(1 - beta . s)). Substitute beta . s=b cos theta_x and cancel the strictly positive b to obtain cos theta_x'=(cos theta_x-b)/(1-b cos theta_x). No division of a coordinate axis formula by beta is used. The denominator is positive on [-1,1] because 0<b<1, and the derivative of u |-> (u-b)/(1-bu) is (1-b^2)/(1-bu)^2>0; its endpoint values are -1 and1, so it is the stated strictly increasing bijection. At beta=0 frequency/direction data agree and no boost-axis angle is assigned. (vi) The scope clause records the hypotheses and the exclusions; no step uses a source or detector model, and no choice principle is used. The postulate post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model supplies the physical interpretation of theta, k and the reading rule, and the two other postulates supply the model's light and covariance clauses; the deduction is conditional on all of them.",
          "deps": [
