        {
          "id": "ex-sr-doppler-longitudinal-beta-three-fifths",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Worked longitudinal Doppler shift at beta = 3/5",
          "statement": "Worked example in the model of sr-light-and-doppler. Let x and x' be inertial charts with x' = B(beta) x + b, beta = (3/5) e_1, so gamma = 5/4 and the frame x' moves with velocity (3/5)c e_1 relative to x. Let an ideal plane wave have frame-x data (omega,s) with omega > 0 and (a) s = e_1, (b) s = -e_1. Then pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration gives: (a) omega' = gamma(1 - 3/5) omega = (5/4)(2/5) omega = omega/2, nu' = nu/2, lambda' = 2 lambda and s' = e_1; an observer at rest in x' moving in the direction of propagation (away from a source at rest in x and located behind it) reads half the frequency and twice the wavelength. (b) omega' = gamma(1 + 3/5) omega = (5/4)(8/5) omega = 2 omega, nu' = 2 nu, lambda' = lambda/2 and s' = -e_1; an observer at rest in x' moving opposite to the propagation direction (toward such a source) reads twice the frequency and half the wavelength. In both cases |s'| = 1 and omega' > 0, in agreement with the general theorem; the verification uses only the exact rational values gamma = 5/4, 1 - 3/5 = 2/5 and 1 + 3/5 = 8/5. Scope: a conditional computation in the ideal plane-wave model; no measurement is performed and no empirical claim is made.",
          "strategy": "Verification by substitution into the theorem at the stated exact data. (a) The axis formula of pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration(i) gives s'_1 = (1 - 3/5)/(1 - 3/5) = 1, s'_2 = s'_3 = 0, so s' = e_1; the frequency factor is gamma(1 - beta s_1) = (5/4)(1 - 3/5) = (5/4)(2/5) = 1/2; the wavelength and linear frequency ratios are the reciprocal and the same factor by clause (ii): nu' = (1/2)nu and lambda' = 2 lambda. (b) s_1 = -1 gives s'_1 = (-1 - 3/5)/(1 + 3/5) = (-8/5)/(8/5) = -1 and the same formulas give the frequency factor (5/4)(8/5) = 2. The receding/approaching reading is fixed by the protocol clause (iii) of the theorem (the source is at rest in x). The example inherits all exclusions of the theorem: no quantum, medium, detector, intensity or gravity content, and no empirical claim. No choice principle is used.",
