          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Worked transverse Doppler configurations: gamma versus 1/gamma at beta = 3/5",
          "statement": "Worked example in the model of sr-light-and-doppler showing that the transverse Doppler shift depends on the frame in which transversality is required. Let x and x' be inertial charts with x' = B(beta) x + b, beta = (3/5) e_1 and gamma = 5/4, and let an ideal plane wave have frame-x data (omega,s) with omega > 0. (a) If s = e_2, so beta . s = 0 and the propagation direction is perpendicular to the relative velocity in x, then pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration gives omega' = gamma omega = (5/4)omega > omega and s' = (-3/5, 4/5, 0), which has |s'| = 1; the frequency read by the x'-rest observer is blue-shifted by the factor 5/4. (b) If s = (3/5, 4/5, 0), so beta . s = 9/25 = |beta|^2 and equivalently s' . e_1 = 0, the propagation direction is perpendicular to the relative velocity in x', and the theorem gives omega' = gamma(1 - 9/25) omega = (5/4)(16/25) omega = (4/5)omega = omega/gamma < omega and s' = e_2; the frequency read by the x'-rest observer is red-shifted by the factor 4/5. In neither case is omega' = omega for beta nonzero, and the two exact factors differ; a statement of a transverse Doppler shift is therefore incomplete until the frame in which the direction is transverse is specified. Scope: a conditional computation in the ideal plane-wave model; no measurement and no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "Verification by substitution into pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration with exact rational arithmetic. (a) With s_1 = 0, s_2 = 1 the axis formulas give s'_1 = (0 - 3/5)/(1 - 0) = -3/5 and s'_2 = s_2/(gamma(1 - 0)) = 1/(5/4) = 4/5, so s' = (-3/5, 4/5, 0), and the frequency factor is gamma(1 - beta . s) = gamma = 5/4. (b) With s_1 = 3/5, s_2 = 4/5 the formulas give s'_1 = (3/5 - 3/5)/(1 - 9/25) = 0 and s'_2 = (4/5)/((5/4)(16/25)) = (4/5)/(4/5) = 1, so s' = e_2, and the frequency factor is gamma(1 - 9/25) = (5/4)(16/25) = 4/5 = 1/gamma. Both cases satisfy |s'| = 1; the identification of the second condition with transversality in x' is s' . e_1 = 0, which the theorem proves equivalent to beta . s = |beta|^2. The example inherits the theorem's scope and exclusions. No choice principle is used.",
