          "id": "ex-sr-aberration-worked-beta-three-fifths",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Worked aberration and Doppler shift at beta = 3/5 and cos theta = 4/5",
          "statement": "Worked example in the model of sr-light-and-doppler. Let x and x' be inertial charts with x' = B(beta) x + b, beta = (3/5) e_1 and gamma = 5/4, and let an ideal plane wave have frame-x propagation direction s = (4/5, 3/5, 0), so cos theta = 4/5 and sin theta = 3/5 with theta in [0,pi] the angle between s and the boost axis. Then pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration gives s' = (5/13, 12/13, 0), hence cos theta' = 5/13, and the angular frequency ratio omega'/omega = gamma(1 - beta s_1) = (5/4)(1 - 12/25) = (5/4)(13/25) = 13/20, so nu' = (13/20) nu and lambda' = (20/13) lambda. Since arccos(5/13) > arccos(4/5), the apparent direction of the source, which is opposite to the propagation direction, moves from an angle arccos(-4/5) to an angle arccos(-5/13) measured from the boost axis, i.e. toward the direction of motion of the observer's frame: this is the sign of stellar aberration in this configuration. The example records the exact angle transformation and leaves any observational interpretation to the framework's experimental work. Scope: a conditional computation in the ideal plane-wave model; no measurement and no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "Verification by substitution into the theorem with exact rational arithmetic. (a) s'_1 = (4/5 - 3/5)/(1 - (3/5)(4/5)) = (1/5)/(13/25) = 5/13 and s'_2 = (3/5)/((5/4)(13/25)) = (3/5)/(13/20) = 12/13, so s' = (5/13, 12/13, 0) and |s'|^2 = 25/169 + 144/169 = 1. (b) The frequency factor is gamma(1 - beta s_1) = (5/4)(1 - 12/25) = (5/4)(13/25) = 13/20; nu' = (13/20) nu is clause (ii) of the theorem and lambda' = (20/13) lambda is the reciprocal. (c) The angle statement uses cos theta' = s'_1 (axis component) and the strictly increasing map of the theorem's clause (v); the apparent direction is -s' because the wave propagates from the source toward the observer, so its angle to the boost axis is arccos(-s'_1) = arccos(-5/13) and the displacement toward the direction of motion is the standard aberration sign; the example states it as an interpretation of the exact transformation and makes no observational claim. No choice principle is used.",
