        {
          "id": "cex-sr-transverse-doppler-is-not-unshifted",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Counterexample: a transverse wave configuration need not have zero Doppler shift",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'if the propagation direction of an ideal plane wave is perpendicular to the relative velocity of two inertial frames, then the frequency read by the observer at rest in one frame equals the frequency read by the observer at rest in the other, omega' = omega.' Counterexample in the model of sr-light-and-doppler: let x and x' be inertial charts with x' = B(beta) x + b and beta = (3/5) e_1. (a) For the wave with frame-x data s = e_2 one has beta . s = 0, so the propagation direction is perpendicular to the relative velocity in x, and pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration gives omega' = gamma omega = (5/4)omega, which is not omega. (b) For the wave with frame-x data s = (3/5, 4/5, 0) one has s' = e_2, so the propagation direction is perpendicular to the relative velocity in x', and the same theorem gives omega' = (4/5)omega, which is not omega. Hence transversality of the propagation direction to the relative velocity in one of the two frames is compatible with the blue-shifted factor 5/4 and with the red-shifted factor 4/5, and it never gives omega' = omega when beta is nonzero; the refuted statement is false as a universal claim in the ideal model. The counterexample is conditional on the model's ideal plane-wave and ideal-clock assumptions, performs no measurement, and claims no empirical falsification.",
          "strategy": "Refutation by two exact configurations. (a) Substituting s = e_2 into pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration(i) gives the frequency factor gamma(1 - beta . s) = gamma = 5/4, so omega' = (5/4)omega != omega. (b) Substituting s = (3/5,4/5,0) gives beta . s = 9/25 = |beta|^2, hence the frequency factor gamma(1 - |beta|^2) = 4/5, so omega' = (4/5)omega != omega; the theorem's clause (iv) identifies this case with s'.e_1 = 0, i.e. transversality in x'. Since a single configuration with beta nonzero and omega' != omega refutes the universal claim, and both configurations are within the model's hypotheses, the refutation is complete; the two different factors also show that the shift's direction is protocol-dependent. The counterexample inherits the theorem's scope and excludes all measurement, statistical and empirical claims. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
