        {
          "id": "def-sr-proper-time-derivative-and-four-force",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Proper-time derivative of a vector field along a worldline and the four-force notation",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski model, $c>0$, and let $x:I\\to A$ be a $C^1$ future timelike worldline with proper-time function $\\tau:I\\to J$ and inverse $\\rho:J\\to I$ ([[def-sr-proper-time]], [[lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization]]).\n(a) For a $C^1$ map $P:I\\to V$ define its proper-time derivative $\\dot P:I\\to V$ by\n$$\\dot P(s):=\\frac{P'(s)}{\\tau'(s)},$$\nwhere $P'$ is the derivative of $P$ with respect to the worldline parameter, taken componentwise in any affine basis of $V$; the result does not depend on the basis because two affine bases differ by a constant invertible linear map. Equivalently $\\dot P(s)=(P\\circ\\rho)'(\\tau(s))$, so $\\dot P$ is the derivative of the proper-time parametrised field $P\\circ\\rho$. Since $\\tau'(s)>0$ by [[lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization]], $\\dot P$ is well defined and continuous whenever $P$ is $C^1$.\n(b) If $P=mU$ with $m>0$ constant and $U$ the four-velocity of $x$ ([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]), then $F:=\\dot P$ is called the four-force on the particle; in an inertial frame $F$ has the components of the derivative of the four-momentum with respect to proper time.\n(c) This item introduces a derivative and a name only. It asserts no force law, no field equation, no equation of motion and no existence or uniqueness of solutions; the identities satisfied by $F$ for a stipulated four-momentum field are proved in [[lem-sr-four-force-and-work-energy-identities]], and a physical force law requires a further model premise.",
          "strategy": "Definition item. Obligations: (i) the division by $\\tau'$ is legitimate because $\\tau'>0$ ([[lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization]]); (ii) the equivalence $\\dot P(s)=(P\\circ\\rho)'(\\tau(s))$ is the chain rule along the reparametrisation, $\\rho$ being the inverse of the increasing function $\\tau$; (iii) componentwise differentiation in one affine basis and change of basis commute because the transition map is constant and linear, so the definition is basis-free; (iv) no dynamics is imported: $F$ is a derived vector field attached to a given $P$, not a prescribed law. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization",
