          "physical_scope": "Classical massive point particles with positive constant invariant mass in flat Minkowski spacetime, with ideal clocks, ideal light signals and SI conventions. The model identifies E_n, q_n, T_n with observer measurements and adopts four-momentum balance for isolated collisions, including ideal massless legs as defined by the separate massless postulate. The single-particle equation applies only to massive C2 worldlines. Excludes internal structure, spin, radiation reaction, variable-mass dynamics, gravity, continuum matter, quantum and statistical effects, and empirical reports; a force law and interaction mechanism are separate premises.",
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t; Sections 4.3.1 pp. 88-89 (E-p four-vector, m^2 = E^2 - p^2), Section 4.3.4 p. 96 (momentum-velocity relation p = m gamma v) and Section 4.3.2 p. 90 (conservation in collisions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Chapter 1; PDF page = printed page + 7; printed p. 27, equations (1.100)-(1.102) (p^mu = m U^mu; f^mu = dp^mu/d tau) and printed p. 30 (local stress-energy conservation, contextual only; finite collision balance is separately adopted)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Mathematical Tripos Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), c = 1; Internet Archive copy of the original https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf; Section 6.3 pp. 63-64, equations (6.17)-(6.19) (P^a = m u^a, g(P,P) = -m^2, observer energy -g(v,P))",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "title": "Adopted momentum model for an ideal massless particle",
          "domain": "physics",
          "statement": "Adopt as a separate model premise for ideal massless particles, independent of\n[[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]] whose formula $P=mU$ uses $m>0$ and does\nnot define a massless case:\n\n**(i) The object.** An ideal massless particle of the model is described by a\nnonzero future null vector $P\\in V$\n([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]],\n[[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]) whose SI unit is kilogram\nmetre per second ([[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]). Such a $P$\nis not of the form $mU$ for any $m>0$ and any future timelike $U$ with\n$\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$: a positive multiple of a timelike vector is timelike, and\nthe zero vector is neither timelike, null nor spacelike, so the massive\nexpression $mU$ with this normalized $U$ degenerates\nto $0$ at $m=0$ and does not describe a nonzero null momentum. The particle\ntherefore has no four-velocity, no proper-time parametrisation and no rest\nframe. Its scale is fixed by a measured energy in an observer's frame.\n\n**(ii) Observer identification.** Relative to a future unit observer $n$, the\nmodel identifies the measured energy and spatial momentum of the particle with\nthe quantities $E_n(P)=-c\\,\\eta(P,n)$ and $q_n(P)=h(P)$ of\n[[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]. Necessarily\n$E_n(P)=c\\,|q_n(P)|>0$, by\n[[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(a)(i), and no rest mass, rest\nenergy or kinetic energy is assigned to the particle — the definition of\n$T_n$ requires a rest-mass parameter $m$ and no such parameter is introduced\nhere. This clause is a formulation convention identifying mathematical\nquantities with the quantities the model calls measured; it is not a\nmeasurement report and no experiment is claimed.\n\n**(iii) No identification with light or quantum objects.** The ideal massless\nparticle is not identified here with light, with a classical wave or with any\nquantum object: no frequency, wavelength, quantisation, polarisation or\nPlanck constant enters this premise, and the identification of light with\nmassless particles requires the wave model and its own postulates on a\nseparate page.\n\n**(iv) No process.** This premise asserts no interaction of massless\nparticles and no decay or production process; such processes require a\nstipulated dynamics. Empirical support for the assumption is a nonlogical\nrelation of the framework, not a proof, and the idealisations inherited from\nthe flat spacetime model and the ideal-point description travel to every\nconsumer of this premise.",
          "strategy": "Postulate for the massless case, kept separate because $P=mU$ at $m=0$ degenerates to $0$ ([[def-sr-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]: the zero vector is neither timelike, null nor spacelike) and cannot describe a nonzero null momentum. Obligations: (i) the object is a nonzero future null vector, using the causal classification and time orientation; (ii) the identification of $E_n$ and $q_n$ is a formulation convention, with the mathematical identity $E_n=c|q_n|$ supplied separately by [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]] and not adopted; (iii) no identification with light or with quantum concepts is made; (iv) no process is asserted. Empirical support is a nonlogical relation, not a proof.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
