        {
          "id": "ex-sr-equal-mass-head-on-collision-mass",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Two equal masses in head-on motion: system mass exceeds the sum of the masses",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $m>0$ and let two massive particles at a common event have four-momenta $P_1=m\\gamma(c,w)$ and $P_2=m\\gamma(c,-w)$ with $w=(3c/5,0,0)$ and $\\gamma=5/4$. Their total is $Q=P_1+P_2=(5mc/2,0,0,0)$, which is future timelike with invariant mass $M=5m/2$. Since the first basis vector $e_0$ already equals $Q/(Mc)$, the frame is a center-of-momentum frame; moreover $M=5m/2>2m=m_1+m_2$, and the mass excess $(M-2m)c^2=mc^2/2$ equals the total kinetic energy of the two particles relative to $e_0$ ($mc^2/4$ each). Thus the rest mass of the system is not the sum of the rest masses of its parts.",
          "strategy": "Add the four-momenta in the common frame; identify $Q$ with its first component only, so $-c^2\\eta(Q,Q)=c^2(5mc/2)^2$ gives $M=5m/2$ by [[def-sr-total-four-momentum-and-invariant-mass]]; apply [[lem-sr-center-of-momentum-frame]] and [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b) for the kinetic energies. The computation is exact at $\\gamma=5/4$ and asserts no collision.",
          "deps": [
            "ex-sr-four-momentum-at-beta-three-fifths",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities",
            "lem-sr-four-momentum-in-inertial-frame",
