        {
          "id": "ex-sr-perfectly-inelastic-collision-rest-mass",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A conserving two-into-one collision datum and the non-uniqueness of its final state",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "In the setting of [[ex-sr-equal-mass-head-on-collision-mass]], the datum with incoming sides $(P_1,P_2)$ and the single outgoing vector $Q=(5mc/2,0,0,0)=(5m/2)c\\,e_0$ is an on-shell conserving collision datum: $Q$ is massive with mass $5m/2$ and four-velocity $ce_0$, and $P_1+P_2=Q$. The outgoing rest mass $5m/2$ exceeds the incoming sum $2m$ by $m/2$, and the incoming total kinetic energy $mc^2/2=(M-2m)c^2$ has become rest energy. The final state is not unique: the same total $Q$ is also the sum of two outgoing massive legs at rest with masses $m$ and $3m/2$ (four-velocities $ce_0$), so four-momentum conservation fixes only the total, not the number or masses of the outgoing particles.",
          "strategy": "Check that the outgoing leg satisfies the massive-leg clause of [[def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation]] with mass $5m/2$ (its four-velocity is $\\eta$-normalised), verify conservation by adding components, and verify the alternative decomposition. Apply [[lem-sr-collision-necessary-conditions]]\\,(a) for the mass inequality. The datum is a mathematical configuration; no interaction is asserted.",
          "deps": [
