        {
          "id": "cex-sr-massless-momentum-not-mu-at-zero-mass",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "title": "A massless four-momentum is not obtained from the massive formula at m = 0",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: \"The four-momentum of an ideal massless particle is obtained by setting $m=0$ in $P=mU$, where $U$ is a future timelike four-velocity with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$.\" Refutation. For every future timelike $U$ one has $0\\cdot U=0$, and $0$ is not a nonzero future null vector: it is not null (null means $\\eta(P,P)=0$ with $P\\ne0$) and it has zero energy relative to every observer, $E_n(0)=0$. Conversely, a nonzero future null vector such as $P=(E/c)(e_0+e_1)$, $E>0$, in the first two vectors of an inertial frame satisfies $\\eta(P,P)=0$, $E_{e_0}(P)=E>0$, but is not of the form $mU$ with $m>0$ (such a vector is future timelike) or $m=0$ (which gives $0\\ne P$). Hence no value of $m\\ge0$ produces $P$ by the formula $mU$: a massless momentum is a separate model primitive, and the degenerate case $m=0$ of the massive formula carries no information about it.",
          "strategy": "Compute directly: $\\eta(0,0)=0$ with $0$ excluded from the null class by [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]; $\\eta(P,P)=(E/c)^2(\\eta(e_0,e_0)+\\eta(e_1,e_1))=0$ for the displayed $P$; separate the cases $m=0$ and $m>0$ in $P=mU$ and use $\\eta(mU,mU)=-m^2c^2<0$ for $m>0$ and timelike $U$. No physical measurement; the positive energy is the observer quantity of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]].",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities",
