        {
          "id": "cex-sr-kinetic-energy-is-observer-dependent",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "title": "Kinetic energy depends on the observer even though the invariant mass does not",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: \"The kinetic energy $T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ of a massive particle is a property of the particle alone, independent of the observer $n$.\" Refutation. With $m>0$, $w=(3c/5,0,0)$, $U=\\gamma(c,w)$, $\\gamma=5/4$ and $P=mU$ in a frame with $n_1=e_0$, one has $T_{n_1}(P)=mc^2/4\\ne0$ by [[ex-sr-four-momentum-at-beta-three-fifths]]; for the observer comoving with the particle, $n_2:=U/c$, one has $U=cn_2$, so $T_{n_2}(P)=0$ by [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b). The two future unit vectors $n_1\\ne n_2$ are legitimate observers of the same particle and the same vector $P$, so $T_n$ depends on $n$; likewise $E_n$ takes the values $5mc^2/4$ and $mc^2$ while the invariant mass $m$ and the identity $\\eta(P,P)=-m^2c^2$ are observer-independent.",
          "strategy": "Use the two explicit observers from [[ex-sr-four-momentum-at-beta-three-fifths]] and [[ex-sr-four-momentum-boost-to-rest]]: in the particle's rest frame $U=ce'_0$ hence $\\gamma=1$ and $T=0$; in the original frame $T=mc^2/4$. Check that both $n_1,n_2$ are future unit. Exact arithmetic; no measurement claim.",
