      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-sr-stress-energy-tensor-and-force-density",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Stress-energy tensor, force density and local balance on flat spacetime",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski model, $c>0$, and let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be a nonempty open set. (a) A \\emph{stress-energy tensor} on $\\Omega$ of class $C^k$, $k\\ge0$ (with $C^0$ meaning continuous), is a $C^k$ symmetric type-$(2,0)$ tensor field $T$ on $\\Omega$ in the sense of [[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]]: at every $p$, $T(p):V^*\\times V^*\\to\\mathbb R$ is bilinear and symmetric, so its inertial components satisfy $T^{ab}=T^{ba}$ ([[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]]\\,(iii)). (b) Fix the positive numerical constant $c$ and, in each inertial chart, define the component families $\\varepsilon_x:=T^{00}$, $\\pi_x^i:=T^{0i}/c=T^{i0}/c$ and $S_x^{ij}:=T^{ij}$. These are numerical component and scaling conventions; their transformation is inherited from $T$. No physical units or measurement interpretation form part of this mathematical definition. (c) When $k\\ge1$, the \\emph{four-force density} of $T$ is the $C^{k-1}$ field with inertial components $f^b:=\\partial_aT^{ab}$, where $\\partial_a$ are the coordinate partial derivatives of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. By [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]]\\,(iv) the components $\\partial_aT^{ab}$ transform as a vector, so $f$ is a well-defined vector field on $\\Omega$, independent of the inertial chart. For $k\\ge1$, one says that $T$ is \\emph{locally conserved}, or \\emph{balanced}, on $\\Omega$ when $f=0$ on $\\Omega$, and that $T$ is balanced against the four-force density $f$ when $\\partial_aT^{ab}=f^b$. (d) The definition asserts no conservation of any prescribed symmetric field $T$: whether a given $T$ is conserved, whether it arises from an action or a matter model, and whether any of its integrals over a slice converges are separate hypotheses stated elsewhere. No choice principle is used. For a merely continuous tensor ($k=0$), the component conventions and pointwise contractions are defined, while divergence, four-force density and the balance terminology in (c) require the additional $C^1$ hypothesis; no derivative of a merely continuous field is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Definition of symmetric C^k type-(2,0) fields for k>=0, and of their divergence only for k>=1, with purely numerical component conventions. Symmetry is chart-independent by the rank-two tensor transformation law of [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]], clause (iii). For k>=1, under an affine inertial transition xprime=Lambda x+a the constant inverse Jacobian in partialprime_a cancels the first Lambda factor in Tprime^{ab}; hence partialprime_a Tprime^{ab}=Lambda^b_d partial_c T^{cd}, clause (iv). This proves chart-independence of the divergence field. The constants and component families in (b) are numerical definitions, not assertions of measured energy or momentum. No conservation, positivity, matter model, slice convergence or choice principle is asserted. The C0 case is well-defined by the existing tensor-field supplier, which permits k=0; the rank-two component law and symmetry use finite linear evaluation and no derivative of T. The observer decomposition expands delta=h-n tensor n pointwise, so its original continuous-T scope and all contraction/uniqueness conclusions are retained. The C1 and higher cases keep exactly the original divergence and balance claims.",
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
