        {
          "id": "lem-sr-scalar-field-euler-lagrange",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "First variation of the scalar-field action and the Euler-Lagrange equation",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be a nonempty open set with an inertial chart $x$, let $c,K>0$ be numerical constants, let $V:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth and let $\\varphi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be a smooth real scalar field ([[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]]). For every smooth compactly supported $h:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ define the first variation $$\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]:=-\\frac{K}{c}\\int_\\Omega\\Big(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\varphi\\,\\partial_bh+V'(\\varphi)h\\Big)\\,d^4x,$$ a finite Riemann integral by compact support of $h$ ([[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]]\\,(c)). Then (a) $\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]=\\dfrac{K}{c}\\int_\\Omega\\big(\\Box\\varphi-V'(\\varphi)\\big)h\\,d^4x$, where $\\Box\\varphi:=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b\\varphi$; (b) $\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]=0$ for every such $h$ if and only if $\\Box\\varphi=V'(\\varphi)$ on $\\Omega$. The equation in (b) is called the scalar-field equation of this model. The statement is the first variation of the action on compactly supported variations; it asserts nothing about convergence of $S$ on unbounded domains, no boundary term, no dynamics beyond the equivalence in (b) and no empirical fact. No choice principle is used. Precisely, with $L(\\phi):=\\tfrac12\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\partial_b\\phi+V(\\phi)$, define the relative action $S_h(s):=-(K/c)\\int_\\Omega[L(\\varphi+sh)-L(\\varphi)]\\,d^4x$. Its integrand has support in $\\operatorname{supp}h$, so it is defined for every real $s$ even when either background action diverges; $S_h\\prime(0)=\\delta S(\\varphi)[h]$.",
          "strategy": "For a fixed compactly supported h, the density difference L(phi+sh)-L(phi) and its s-derivative are supported in supp(h). On each bounded parameter interval they are continuous with continuous s-derivative, so [[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]], clause (c), differentiates the relative action and gives exactly the stated first variation. Apply clause (b) to h and eta^{ab} partial_a phi, noting the metric components are constant, to obtain delta S=(K/c) integral(Box phi-Vprime(phi))*h. Mixed partial symmetry gives the displayed Box formula. If the residual is zero, stationarity follows. Conversely stationarity against every compactly supported h makes the continuous residual vanish by clause (a), including both signs. Neither a full-domain background action nor a boundary condition is used. This is the compact-support flat specialization of Tong (4.42)-(4.43) and its equation-of-motion calculation, with the relative functional made explicit; no choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
