        {
          "id": "lem-sr-scalar-field-hilbert-stress-energy",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The Hilbert stress-energy tensor of the scalar-field action on flat spacetime",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be any nonempty open set with inertial chart $x$, let $c,K>0$ be numerical constants, and let $V:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ and $\\varphi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth. For each smooth symmetric $(0,2)$ tensor $h$ with compact support, set $g_{ab}(s)=\\eta_{ab}+s h_{ab}$. Choose $s_0>0$ so that these matrices are nondegenerate with negative determinant for $|s|<s_0$; outside $\\operatorname{supp}h$ they equal $\\eta$. Put $D_h(s,x):=[\\tfrac12 g^{ab}(s,x)\\partial_a\\varphi(x)\\partial_b\\varphi(x)+V(\\varphi(x))]\\sqrt{-\\det g(s,x)}$ and define the relative action $$\\mathcal A_h(s):=-\\frac Kc\\int_\\Omega[D_h(s,x)-D_h(0,x)]\\,d^4x.$$ This is the integral of a compactly supported density difference, without assuming either background density has a finite integral. (a) $\\mathcal A_h$ is differentiable at $0$ and $$\\mathcal A_h\\prime(0)=\\frac1{2c}\\int_\\Omega T^{ab}h_{ab}\\,d^4x,\\qquad T^{ab}:=K[\\partial^a\\varphi\\partial^b\\varphi-\\eta^{ab}(\\tfrac12\\partial_d\\varphi\\partial^d\\varphi+V(\\varphi))],$$ with indices raised by $\\eta$. (b) $T$ is smooth and symmetric, and is the unique continuous symmetric $(2,0)$ field for which $\\int_\\Omega T^{ab}h_{ab}\\,d^4x=2c\\mathcal A_h\\prime(0)$ for every such $h$. It is called the Hilbert stress-energy tensor of this scalar-field density. (c) The result holds on unrestricted open $\\Omega$, uses only compactly supported metric variations, and imposes no convergence or boundary condition at infinity. If the full background action happens to exist, the relative action is its variation difference and gives the same derivative. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "On the compact support of h its entries are bounded. The determinant is a polynomial in s with continuous coefficients, and det(eta)=-1; compactness supplies a uniform s0 for which det(g)<0. The adjugate formula then makes the inverse entries smooth in s and x, as is the square root. Outside supp(h), g=eta. Thus D_h(s)-D_h(0) and its parameter derivative have a common compact support; the compact-variation lemma applies to the relative integral.\nDifferentiate g^{ac}g_cb=delta^a_b to obtain delta g^{ab}=-eta^{ac}eta^{bd}h_cd=-h^{ab}. Cofactor expansion gives delta det(g)=det(eta)*eta^{ab}h_ab=-eta^{ab}h_ab, so delta sqrt(-det(g))=+(1/2)eta^{ab}h_ab. Therefore delta D=-(1/2)h^{ab}partial_a phi partial_b phi+(1/2)eta^{ab}h_ab L(phi). Multiplication by -K/c gives exactly +(1/(2c))integral T^{ab}h_ab. Symmetry and smoothness follow from the formula. For uniqueness, take a continuous symmetric difference Q: a test with one diagonal component psi gives integral Q^{aa}psi=0; a test with h_ab=h_ba=psi for a<b gives 2 integral Q^{ab}psi=0. The signed fundamental lemma yields every component Q^{ab}=0. Tong (4.46) uses the inverse-metric variation convention; converting it to the covariant variation delta g_ab=h_ab yields the positive sign here. This convention change, compact density difference, and explicit finite-dimensional derivation preserve the source-derived formula without presuming a convergent background action. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
