        {
          "id": "lem-sr-scalar-field-stress-divergence-identity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Divergence identity for the scalar-field stress-energy tensor",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "In the setting of [[lem-sr-scalar-field-hilbert-stress-energy]], with $\\varphi$ smooth and $T$ the Hilbert stress-energy tensor of the scalar-field action, $$\\partial_aT^{ab}=K\\big(\\Box\\varphi-V'(\\varphi)\\big)\\partial^b\\varphi\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega,$$ where $\\partial^b=\\eta^{bc}\\partial_c$ and $\\Box\\varphi=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b\\varphi$. In particular every solution of the scalar-field equation $\\Box\\varphi=V\\prime(\\varphi)$ has conserved tensor, $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0$. Conversely conservation implies the scalar-field equation at every point where $d\\varphi\\ne0$, and hence throughout an open set on which $d\\varphi$ is nowhere zero. In full generality conservation is equivalent to the pointwise product condition $(\\Box\\varphi-V\\prime(\\varphi))d\\varphi=0$; it need not imply the scalar equation where $d\\varphi=0$. Off shell the balance holds with the four-force density $f^b=K(\\Box\\varphi-V'(\\varphi))\\partial^b\\varphi$. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Expand $T^{ab}=K(\\partial^a\\varphi\\partial^b\\varphi-\\eta^{ab}(\\tfrac12\\partial_c\\varphi\\partial^c\\varphi+V))$: $\\partial_a(\\partial^a\\varphi\\partial^b\\varphi)=(\\Box\\varphi)\\partial^b\\varphi+\\partial^a\\varphi\\,\\partial_a\\partial^b\\varphi$, and $\\partial^b(\\tfrac12\\partial_c\\varphi\\partial^c\\varphi+V)=\\partial^c\\varphi\\,\\partial^b\\partial_c\\varphi+V'(\\varphi)\\partial^b\\varphi$; the two second-derivative terms cancel after relabeling by symmetry of mixed partials ([[thm-young-mixed-partial-theorem]]) and $\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b=\\Box$. Source: the direct divergence check is the flat-space restriction of the scalar computation in G6 of the research argument and of the analogous check (4.51) of Tong. No choice principle is used. Write E=Box(phi)-Vprime(phi). The displayed identity is exact off shell. Since K>0 and the metric is nondegenerate, at a point with dphi nonzero at least one raised component partial^b phi is nonzero; zero divergence then forces E=0 there. At points with dphi=0 no such conclusion follows. For example phi=0 and V(s)=s give T=0, divergence zero, and E=-1 on any open Omega. Therefore the unconditional converse is false and is replaced by the exact product criterion and its noncritical-point converse.",
          "deps": [
