        {
          "id": "lem-sr-dust-conservation-equivalence",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Dust: conservation of rho n tensor n is equivalent to continuity and inertial flow",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be open, let $\\rho:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^1$ with $\\rho>0$, let $n:\\Omega\\to V$ be a $C^1$ field which is future timelike and satisfies $\\eta(n,n)=-1$ everywhere (a future unit timelike field, [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(c)), and set $T^{ab}:=\\rho\\,n^an^b$ (a $C^1$ symmetric $(2,0)$ field). Then, on $\\Omega$, (a) $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0$ if and only if both $\\partial_a(\\rho n^a)=0$ (continuity) and $n^a\\partial_an^b=0$ (inertial flow); (b) if $\\rho\\ge0$ is allowed to vanish, the implication $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0\\Rightarrow n^a\\partial_an^b=0$ is asserted only on the open set $\\{\\rho>0\\}$, and no assertion is made where $\\rho=0$; (c) Under the conservation hypothesis $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0$ in (a), if $J$ is an interval and $x:J\\to\\Omega$ is a $C^2$ curve with $x'(s)=n(x(s))$ for all $s$ (an integral curve of $n$), then $x''(s)=0$: the flow lines of an inertial unit field are affine straight lines in the affine structure of $A$. For the nonnegative-density branch in (b), the same conclusion holds for an integral curve whose image lies in $\\{\\rho>0\\}$, under conservation there. The lemma is the exact mathematical content of the dust equations; it asserts no matter model, no interaction and no empirical fact. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Expand $\\partial_aT^{ab}=(\\partial_a\\rho)n^an^b+\\rho(\\partial_an^a)n^b+\\rho n^a\\partial_an^b$ and rewrite the first two terms as $(\\partial_a(\\rho n^a))n^b+\\rho n^a\\partial_an^b$. Contract with $n_b$ and use $n_bn^b=-1$, $n_b\\partial_an^b=\\tfrac12\\partial_a(n_bn^b)=0$ to obtain $n_b\\partial_aT^{ab}=-\\partial_a(\\rho n^a)$. Hence conservation gives continuity, and then the remaining horizontal part gives $n^a\\partial_an^b=0$; the converse is immediate. For (b), the same contraction gives continuity without dividing by $\\rho$; the remaining term is $\\rho n^a\\partial_an^b=0$, so division is justified exactly where $\\rho>0$. For (c), use the conservation antecedent to obtain inertial flow from (a), then differentiate: $x''(s)=n^a\\partial_an^b(x(s))=0$ by the chain rule, so $x'$ is constant and $x$ is affine. Source: the equivalence is the flat restriction of the dust computation of G6 and of the dust equations read off from (4.53)-(4.55) of Tong; the positivity hypothesis is stated because the converse needs it. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space",
