        {
          "id": "lem-sr-perfect-fluid-conservation-equations",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Perfect fluid: conservation is equivalent to the energy and Euler equations",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ be open, let $\\varepsilon,P:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^1$, let $n:\\Omega\\to V$ be a $C^1$ future unit timelike field ($\\eta(n,n)=-1$) and set $T^{ab}:=(\\varepsilon+P)n^an^b+P\\eta^{ab}$. Write $D\\varepsilon:=n^a\\partial_a\\varepsilon$, $\\vartheta:=\\partial_an^a$, $a^b:=n^a\\partial_an^b$ and $h^{bc}:=\\eta^{bc}+n^bn^c$ (these formulas define the first-order expansion scalar, acceleration and raised rest projection for the stipulated $C^1$ field; they agree with [[def-sr-observer-congruence-and-kinematics]] and [[def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space]] when the field is smooth). Then the identity $$\\partial_aT^{ab}=n^b\\big(D\\varepsilon+(\\varepsilon+P)\\vartheta\\big)+\\big((\\varepsilon+P)a^b+h^{bc}\\partial_cP\\big)$$ holds on $\\Omega$, and consequently $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0$ on $\\Omega$ if and only if $D\\varepsilon+(\\varepsilon+P)\\vartheta=0$ and $(\\varepsilon+P)a^b+h^{bc}\\partial_cP=0$ on $\\Omega$. The two equations are the energy-balance and Euler equations of the model; the lemma asserts no equation of state, no thermodynamic relation and no causality condition. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Compute $\\partial_aT^{ab}=\\partial_a[(\\varepsilon+P)n^a]n^b+(\\varepsilon+P)n^a\\partial_an^b+\\partial^bP$, rewrite $\\partial_a[(\\varepsilon+P)n^a]=D\\varepsilon+DP+(\\varepsilon+P)\\vartheta$ with $DP=n^a\\partial_aP$, and decompose $\\partial^bP=h^{bc}\\partial_cP-n^bDP$ because $\\eta^{bc}=h^{bc}-n^bn^c$; the terms in $DP$ cancel, leaving the displayed identity. The second bracket is horizontal: $n_ba^b=\\tfrac12n^a\\partial_a(\\eta(n,n))=0$ and $n_bh^{bc}=0$. Contracting with $n_b$ gives $-\\big(D\\varepsilon+(\\varepsilon+P)\\vartheta\\big)$ and projecting with $h$ gives the second bracket, so the two equations are equivalent to conservation. Source: the flat restriction of the perfect-fluid computation of G6 and of (4.53)-(4.55) of Tong. No choice principle is used. This argument divides by neither $\\varepsilon+P$ nor $P$: the degenerate case $\\varepsilon+P=0$ is retained. All quantities use at most first coordinate derivatives of the $C^1$ fields.",
