        },
        {
          "id": "thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Hodge reconstruction of an alternating two-tensor and the optional SI field split",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be the oriented Lorentz vector space of the affine Minkowski model with orientation and volume form $\\varepsilon$ of [[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]], and let $F:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb R$ be an alternating bilinear form ([[def-alternating-k-linear-map]], [[def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms]]). Define the Hodge dual by $(*F)_{ab}:=\\tfrac12\\varepsilon_{ab}{}^{cd}F_{cd}$ and set $(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)_{ab}:=\\alpha_a\\beta_b-\\alpha_b\\beta_a$. Use the standard form normalization $F=\\tfrac12\\sum_{a,b}F_{ab}\\theta^a\\wedge\\theta^b$ in the dual coframe $\\theta^a(e_b)=\\delta^a_b$ of an oriented orthonormal frame, and define $(F\\wedge F)(v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3):=2[F(v_0,v_1)F(v_2,v_3)-F(v_0,v_2)F(v_1,v_3)+F(v_0,v_3)F(v_1,v_2)]$, the alternating four-form exterior square. Let $n\\in V$ be future unit, $\\eta(n,n)=-1$, and put $e_a:=F_{ab}n^b$, $b_a:=(*F)_{ab}n^b$. (a) $e(n)=b(n)=0$, and $F=n^\\flat\\wedge e-*(n^\\flat\\wedge b)$ with $n^\\flat:=\\eta(n,\\cdot)$; also $*(*F)=-F$, so the Hodge dual squares to $-1$ on two-forms. (b) In an oriented orthonormal frame with $n=e_0$: $e_i=F_{i0}$, $b_i=-\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}$, $F_{0i}=-e_i$ and $F_{ij}=-\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$; hence the six components of $F$ are exactly the six components of the pair $(e,b)$ and the reconstruction in (a) is equivalent to these component formulae. (c) The quadratic invariants are $\\tfrac12F_{ab}F^{ab}=|b|^2-|e|^2$ and $F_{ab}(*F)^{ab}=-4\\,(e\\cdot b)$, where $|\\cdot|$ and $\\cdot$ are those of the spatial structure of the frame ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]); also $F\\wedge F=2(e\\cdot b)\\varepsilon$. The two scalar expressions $I_1:=\\tfrac12F_{ab}F^{ab}$ and $I_2:=F_{ab}(*F)^{ab}$ form a basis of the homogeneous quadratic polynomials in the six components of $F$ invariant under the proper orthochronous Lorentz group; the square-wedge expression is dependent, $F\\wedge F=-\\tfrac12 I_2\\varepsilon$. $I_2$ is an oriented pseudoscalar and changes sign if the chosen orientation is reversed with the metric and $F$ held fixed. (d) (Optional SI identification.) Let $c>0$. If in an inertial frame one identifies $F^{i0}=E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=-\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ for numerical spatial vectors $E,B\\in\\mathbb R^3$, then $e_i=-E_i/c$ and $b_i=B_i$, the invariants of (c) read $\\tfrac12F_{ab}F^{ab}=|B|^2-|E|^2/c^2$ and $F_{ab}(*F)^{ab}=4\\,(E\\cdot B)/c$, and $F\\wedge F=-2(E\\cdot B)\\varepsilon/c$. If $\\kappa:=(F\\wedge F)(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)=(F\\wedge F)_{0123}$, then $E\\cdot B=-c\\kappa/2$. Under the passive boost of [[def-lorentz-boost]] along $e_1$ with parameter $\\beta\\in(-1,1)$ one has $E'_1=E_1$, $B'_1=B_1$, $E'_\\perp=\\gamma(E+v\\times B)_\\perp$ and $B'_\\perp=\\gamma(B-v\\times E/c^2)_\\perp$ where the cross product is the right-handed product ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]), with $\\gamma=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$ and $v=\\beta c$ along $e_1$. In an SI reading, $c$ has units $\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s$, $E$ has units $\\mathrm V/\\mathrm m$ and $B$ has units tesla, so $E/c$ and all components of $F$ have units tesla; these are conditional unit assignments, not measurements. (e) The theorem is algebra on the oriented Lorentz vector space with the stated index and duality conventions; clause (d) is an explicitly optional identification of the components of $F$ with fields the electromagnetic framework names $E,B$, and the theorem asserts no field equation, no dynamics, no measurement and no transformation beyond the stated component law. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "First obtain an oriented rest frame adapted to $n$: the observer decomposition theorem proves $n^\\perp$ is a three-dimensional positive inner-product space ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (c)); apply finite Gram–Schmidt to a basis of it ([[thm-gram-schmidt-orthonormalisation]]) and reverse one spatial basis vector if the resulting frame orientation is negative. This uses only finite choices. Let $a_i=\\theta^0\\wedge\\theta^i$ and $s_1=\\theta^2\\wedge\\theta^3$, $s_2=\\theta^3\\wedge\\theta^1$, $s_3=\\theta^1\\wedge\\theta^2$. The declared dual and volume conventions give $*a_i=-s_i$, $*s_i=a_i$ by direct component evaluation. Thus $*^2=-1$ on all six basis forms, $F=-e_i a_i-b_i s_i$ and $*F=-b_i a_i+e_i s_i$, proving the annihilation, six-component recovery and $F=n^\\flat\\wedge e-*(n^\\flat\\wedge b)$. Raise the time-spatial indices with the diagonal metric and count both orders of each skew pair to get $\\frac12F_{ab}F^{ab}=|b|^2-|e|^2$ and $F_{ab}(*F)^{ab}=-4e\\cdot b$. The explicitly normalized square wedge has $a_i\\wedge s_i=\\varepsilon$ and all unmatched terms zero, so $F\\wedge F=2e\\cdot b\\,\\varepsilon$; the witness $F_{01}=F_{23}=1$ gives $e_1=b_1=-1$ and square wedge $+2\\varepsilon$. The tensor/volume laws ([[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iii),(v)) show that these expressions are invariant under proper Lorentz changes. For completeness of the quadratic invariant claim, spatial half-turns force the three coefficient blocks of a rotation-invariant quadratic polynomial to be diagonal, and proper axis permutations force scalar diagonals; it has the form $A|e|^2+B|b|^2+C e\\cdot b$. For $e=(0,1,0),b=0$, an axis-one passive boost gives $e'=(0,\\gamma,0),b'=(0,0,\\gamma\\beta)$; invariance gives $A(\\gamma^2-1)+B\\gamma^2\\beta^2=0$, whence $A+B=0$ for any nonzero allowed $\\beta$. Both surviving invariant polynomials are linearly independent by testing $b=0,e\\ne0$ and $e=b\\ne0$. For the optional SI identification, apply the actual passive boost matrix to the two raised indices of $F$ ([[def-lorentz-boost]], [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iii)). All six entries are $E'_1=E_1$, $E'_2=\\gamma(E_2-vB_3)$, $E'_3=\\gamma(E_3+vB_2)$, $B'_1=B_1$, $B'_2=\\gamma(B_2+vE_3/c^2)$, $B'_3=\\gamma(B_3-vE_2/c^2)$, which are exactly the promised vector formulas with the right-handed cross product ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). Substituting $e=-E/c,b=B$ gives the displayed invariants and standard four-form coefficient $\\kappa=-2E\\cdot B/c$, hence $E\\cdot B=-c\\kappa/2$. The zero-boost case gives $\\gamma=1$ directly. All arguments are finite-dimensional, choice-free algebra and assert no EM dynamics. The cached Carroll full-text equations (1.79)–(1.88), printed pp.21–23, were read for wedge normalization and the Lorentzian star-square rule; the S8 local research route is independently recomputed, not accepted by citation. Horizontal covectors e,b are identified with their metric-dual vectors in n-perp. Each frame uses its own time-axis observer in the displayed boosted component lists; a fixed observer transforms with the vector law. For P=a|e|^2+b|b|^2+c e dot b, the coefficients are P=-a I1-c I2/4. Optional E,B are pointwise numerical triples; conditional SI units assign E/c and B tesla.",
          "deps": [
