          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity, University of Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos (Michaelmas Term 2019); c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t; 4.5.1-4.5.5 printed pp. 181-191: scalar-field action (4.42)-(4.43) and equation of motion, Hilbert energy-momentum tensor (4.46), diffeomorphism invariance and conservation (4.50), flat-space Noether form, scalar tensor (4.51), Maxwell tensor (4.52), perfect fluid (4.53) with conservation equations (4.54)-(4.55), and the flat-space conserved-charge box argument of 4.5.5",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Chapter 1 printed pp. 28-30 (energy-momentum tensor, dust T^{mu nu}=rho U^mu U^nu (1.106), perfect fluid (1.107)-(1.110), scalar and Maxwell tensors (1.111)-(1.112), conservation (1.113) with the remark that the proof follows from each matter model's equations of motion) and Chapter 5 printed pp. 138-139 (on-shell conservation from diffeomorphism invariance with compact-support variations, (5.34)-(5.38))",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t; Chapter 9 pp. 179-197: 9.1 pp. 179-182 (current vector, flux, charge conservation), 9.2 pp. 183-191 (stress-energy tensor, conservation and flux, symmetry, dust, rank-2 transformation law, pressure, perfect fluid, worked examples, energy conditions), 9.3 pp. 192-197 (Gauss's theorem, integral conservation laws, the energy-momentum vector, angular momentum); the printed 9.2.8 dominant energy condition p. 191 has the defective |P| > rho and is not used",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Classical continuum matter in flat affine Minkowski spacetime: a real scalar field with potential, dust and a perfect fluid, with the stated evolution equations adopted as model premises. Excludes gravity, electromagnetic coupling, general relativity, thermodynamics and equations of state, viscosity, dissipation and heat conduction, quantum or statistical matter, and every empirical or measurement claim; the models are adopted, not derived from experiment."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-sr-matter-isolation-and-total-stress-energy",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "title": "Adopted total stress-energy and isolation premise for integrated conservation",
          "domain": "physics",
          "statement": "Adopt as the boundary and isolation premise of the continuum framework the following.\n\n**(i) Total tensor.** In every conservation or integration statement of this framework the tensor used is the total stress-energy tensor of the system considered, including every constituent that carries energy-momentum — matter, fields and supporting structures present in the region — assembled by the additivity clause of [[post-sr-matter-models-and-evolution]].\n\n**(ii) Localisation.** Fix an inertial chart with $x^0=ct$. Choose real $s_1<s_2$ and a nondegenerate closed spatial rectangle $\\bar K=\\prod_{i=1}^3[a_i,b_i]$, $a_i<b_i$. The interval $[s_1,s_2]$ parametrizes $x^0$ in metres; the corresponding coordinate-clock interval is $[s_1/c,s_2/c]$ in seconds. Whenever conservation of a slice four-momentum on this slab is asserted, the model assumes that during that interval the total tensor is $C^1$ and symmetric on a neighbourhood of a closed box $[s_1,s_2]\\times\\bar K$ ([[def-sr-stress-energy-tensor-and-force-density]], [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), that its components satisfy the zero side-flux condition $T^{ib}=0$ on the spatial faces of the box for $i=1,2,3$, and that the slice integrals converge; equivalently, the system is isolated inside the chosen region for the chosen interval.\n\n**(iii) Failure of isolation.** If the side flux does not vanish, only the exact finite-flux balance of [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]] is asserted; no conservation of a slice integral is inferred and no limiting procedure at spatial infinity is adopted.\n\n**(iv) Unmodelled matter.** For a matter system not governed by one of the models of [[post-sr-matter-models-and-evolution]], conservation of its stress-energy tensor is not inferred; if used it must be adopted or derived separately.\n\n**(v) Exclusion caution.** Removing a constituent — a support, a field or a container — from the total tensor can make the side-flux condition fail; the premise concerns the total tensor of the system, not an arbitrary partial tensor.\n\nScope and status\n\nThis premise is an idealisation of an isolated bounded system in flat spacetime; it is not a measurement and makes no apparatus, statistical or empirical claim. The localisation clause is a boundary hypothesis on a finite box in a single inertial chart; it neither supplies an equation of motion for the total tensor nor implies the vanishing of the force density $f^b=\\partial_aT^{ab}$, which is a separate hypothesis of the integration statements that use this postulate.",
          "strategy": "Adopted physical premise; no proof obligation. The zero-flux condition is exactly the hypothesis of the mathematical finite-flux theorem [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]]\\,(c), so the premise is stated in the form the deduction consumes. Sources: the isolation/vacuum-side argument is 9.3.4 of Crowell pp. 195-197; the flat conserved-charge box argument is 4.5.5 of Tong, including the warning that one must require vanishing current on the timelike boundary; the container/support caution is the reader-recorded defect discussion of Crowell pp. 101-103 and 233-234. No measurement is reported and no empirical claim is made.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
