        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-integrated-conservation-for-isolated-matter",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "title": "Integrated four-momentum of an isolated system on finite slices",
          "domain": "physics",
          "statement": "Fix $c>0$ and an inertial chart $x$ with $x^0=ct$. Let $\\bar K=\\prod_{i=1}^3[a_i,b_i]$, $a_i<b_i$, be a nondegenerate closed spatial rectangle and let $s_1<s_2$ be real length-coordinate values, and let $T$ be the total stress-energy tensor of a system in the situation of [[post-sr-matter-isolation-and-total-stress-energy]]: $T$ is $C^1$ and symmetric on a neighbourhood of $[s_1,s_2]\\times\\bar K$, satisfies $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0$ there (for the adopted models this is the conclusion of [[pthm-sr-local-stress-energy-conservation]]; for any other system it is an explicit premise), and satisfies the zero side-flux condition $T^{ib}=0$ on the spatial faces for $i=1,2,3$. Put $Q^b(s):=\\int_{\\bar K}T^{0b}(s,x)\\,d^3x$ and $P^b:=Q^b/c$. Then: (a) $Q^b(s)$ is finite for every $s\\in[s_1,s_2]$ and $Q^b(s_1)=Q^b(s_2)$; (b) consequently $P^b$ is independent of $s\\in[s_1,s_2]$; if $P$ is future timelike, its invariant mass $\\sqrt{-\\eta(P,P)}/c$ is independent of $s$ as well; (c) if the side flux does not vanish, the exact balance is the finite-flux identity of [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]], and no conservation is asserted; (d) no transformation law for $P^b$ under a change of inertial chart or slicing is asserted: without an admissible-region flux argument, $P^b$ is a chart-component integral and is not claimed to be a four-vector; no noncompact, asymptotic or gravitational-energy statement is made, and the reading of $P^b$ as the system's four-momentum is the model's interpretation of the integral, not a measurement.",
          "strategy": "Clause (a) applies [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]]\\,(c) to the box $[s_1,s_2]\\times\\bar K$ under the stipulated model hypotheses (conservation and vanishing side flux); the invariance of the invariant mass in (b) is immediate because $P$ is a constant future timelike vector and $\\eta(P,P)$ is then constant, using the invariant-mass formula of [[def-sr-total-four-momentum-and-invariant-mass]]. Clause (c) is the general balance case of the same theorem. Clause (d) records the exact scope: the theorem proves constancy of the chart integrals and nothing about their transformation under a change of chart, which would require the oriented hypersurface flux construction; no noncompact limit is taken. No empirical premise is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
