        {
          "id": "rem-sr-continuum-force-and-conservation-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "title": "Scope of the page: two notions of force, conditional conservation and excluded claims",
          "domain": "physics",
          "statement": "The force vocabulary of this framework has two distinct mathematical carriers: the four-force $F=\\dot P=dP/d\\tau$ on a massive particle worldline ([[def-sr-proper-time-derivative-and-four-force]]; its identities are conditional on [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]]) and the four-force density $f^b=\\partial_aT^{ab}$ of the continuum ([[def-sr-stress-energy-tensor-and-force-density]]). Neither is a force law. The page's conservation statements are conditional deductions: local conservation of the scalar, dust and fluid tensors is proved from the adopted model equations ([[pthm-sr-local-stress-energy-conservation]]), while for any other matter the conservation of $T$ must be adopted separately. Integrated conservation additionally requires the isolation premise ([[post-sr-matter-isolation-and-total-stress-energy]]) and is asserted only for finite boxes and slices in one inertial chart ([[pthm-sr-integrated-conservation-for-isolated-matter]]). The framework contains no gravity, no electromagnetic coupling, no derivation of an equation of state, no energy conditions (the printed dominant-energy-condition inequality of the Crowell source p. 191 is defective and is not used), no angular-momentum theorem and no empirical premise; no measurement, apparatus or uncertainty claim is made anywhere on this page.",
          "strategy": "Scope remark; no proof obligation. It records which page claims are adopted premises, which are conditional deductions, and which promised or source-adjacent topics are deliberately not asserted (energy conditions, angular momentum, gravity, electromagnetism, equations of state, asymptotic charges, four-vector transformation of chart integrals). Sources: the errata and scope findings of the Crowell reader report (printed p. 191 defective DEC; pp. 231, 240 field-stress caveats) and the conservation caveats of Tong 4.5.3 and Carroll p. 30 / pp. 138-139.",
