        {
          "id": "ex-sr-perfect-fluid-rest-and-moving-frame",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Perfect fluid components in its rest frame and in a moving frame",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $T^{ab}=(\\varepsilon+P)n^an^b+P\\eta^{ab}$ be the perfect-fluid tensor of [[lem-sr-perfect-fluid-conservation-equations]], with $n$ future unit. In an inertial frame with $n=e_0$ (fluid at rest) the components are $T^{00}=\\varepsilon$, $T^{11}=T^{22}=T^{33}=P$ and all other components zero. Let $|\\beta|<1$ and $\\gamma=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$. Under the passive boost along $x^1$ with parameter $\\beta$ (so that its primed coordinate velocity is $-\\beta c\\,e_1\\prime$, with speed $|\\beta|c$), the component law gives $$T'^{00}=\\gamma^2(\\varepsilon+P)-P,\\quad T'^{01}=T'^{10}=-\\gamma^2(\\varepsilon+P)\\beta,\\quad T'^{11}=\\gamma^2(\\varepsilon+P)\\beta^2+P,\\quad T'^{22}=T'^{33}=P,$$ all remaining components zero. In particular the adapted-frame numerical contraction is $T^{ab}(e_0\\prime)_a(e_0\\prime)_b=T\\prime{}^{00}=\\gamma^2(\\varepsilon+P)-P$. Its difference from $\\varepsilon$ is $(\\gamma^2-1)(\\varepsilon+P)$, so equality holds if and only if $\\beta=0$ or $\\varepsilon+P=0$. It is at least $\\varepsilon$ whenever $\\varepsilon+P\\ge0$; no positivity of $\\varepsilon+P$ is assumed here. The transverse diagonal components remain $P$. The computation uses the observer decomposition of [[lem-sr-observer-stress-energy-decomposition]] to identify this numerical contraction; it asserts no equation of state and no measurement.",
          "strategy": "Compute $T'^{ab}=\\Lambda^a{}_c\\Lambda^b{}_d[(\\varepsilon+P)n^cn^d+P\\eta^{cd}]$ using $\\eta'=\\eta$ (metric preservation, [[thm-sr-proper-orthochronous-lorentz-algebra]]) and $n'=\\Lambda n$ with $n'^0=\\gamma$, $n'^1=-\\gamma\\beta$; the displayed components follow. The adapted-frame numerical contraction is clause (c) of [[lem-sr-observer-stress-energy-decomposition]] at $n=e_0'$. Source: (4.53) of Tong, (1.107)-(1.110) of Carroll and 9.2.6 of Crowell. No choice principle is used. Subtracting $\\varepsilon$ gives $(\\gamma^2-1)(\\varepsilon+P)$, and $\\gamma^2-1=\\beta^2/(1-\\beta^2)$ vanishes exactly for $\\beta=0$. Thus equality also holds for every boost when $\\varepsilon+P=0$, and the inequality has exactly the stated conditional sign.",
          "deps": [
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-lorentz-boost",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-rational-power",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "lem-sr-observer-stress-energy-decomposition",
            "lem-sr-perfect-fluid-conservation-equations",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
