        {
          "id": "ex-sr-constant-velocity-dust-with-transverse-profile",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A dust solution with constant velocity and transverse density profile",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $|\\beta|<1$, $\\gamma=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$, fix an inertial chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$, let $n:A\\to V$ be the constant field with inertial components $n^0=\\gamma$, $n^1=\\gamma\\beta$, $n^2=n^3=0$ (so $\\eta(n,n)=-1$), and let $\\rho_0:\\mathbb R^2\\to(0,\\infty)$ be $C^1$ and define $\\rho:A\\to(0,\\infty)$ by $\\rho(p)=\\rho_0(x^2(p),x^3(p))$. Then $n^a\\partial_a\\rho=\\gamma(\\partial_0\\rho+\\beta\\partial_1\\rho)=0$ and $n^a\\partial_an^b=0$ because $n$ is constant, so $(\\rho,n)$ satisfies the dust equations and $T=\\rho\\,n\\otimes n$ is conserved by [[lem-sr-dust-conservation-equivalence]]. For any nondegenerate finite spatial box $\\bar K=[c_1,d_1]\\times[c_2,d_2]\\times[c_3,d_3]$ whose slab lies in the chart domain, the finite slice integrals $Q^b(s)=\\int_{\\bar K}T^{0b}(s,x)d^3x$ are constant in $s$, because $T^{0b}$ is time independent. This also follows from the slice-balance identity [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]]\\,(b): $T^{2b}=T^{3b}=0$, and the two $x^1$-face fluxes $T^{1b}=\\rho_0\\gamma\\beta n^b$ agree pointwise and cancel with opposite orientations. The individual $x^1$-face flux need not vanish. A nonzero transverse profile is not compactly supported in all three spatial variables, since it is independent of $x^1$. The nonnegative variant $\\rho_0\\ge0$, including compact support in the transverse $(x^2,x^3)$ plane, gives the same exact conserved solution and finite-box constancy, with the zero-density qualification of [[lem-sr-dust-conservation-equivalence]]\\,(b). The flow lines are the parallel straight lines $x^1=\\beta x^0+\\mathrm{const}$, $x^2,x^3$ constant. The example exhibits a family of exact solutions of the dust equations; it asserts no interaction and no measurement.",
          "strategy": "Verify $\\eta(n,n)=-\\gamma^2+\\gamma^2\\beta^2=-1$. Since $n$ is constant, $\\partial_an^b=0$ and $\\partial_an^a=0$; since $\\rho$ depends only on $x^2,x^3$, $n^a\\partial_a\\rho=0$. Hence both dust equations hold, including for the nonnegative-profile variant, and [[lem-sr-dust-conservation-equivalence]] gives conservation. On any finite spatial box, $T^{0b}=\\gamma\\rho_0 n^b$ is continuous and independent of $x^0$, so its finite integral is constant. In the exact slice identity [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]]\\,(b), the divergence term vanishes; the $x^2,x^3$ terms vanish separately and the $x^1$ terms cancel because the integrands and their transverse integration domains are equal on both faces. Clause (c), which requires each side flux to vanish, is not used. The integral curves have constant tangent $n$, hence the stated parallel affine lines. The entire calculation uses prescribed fields and no matter-model or empirical assertion. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
