        {
          "id": "ex-sr-static-tension-stress-balance",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A static uniaxial stress: tension must be constant along the stressed direction",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "In an inertial chart $x$ of the affine Minkowski model with orthonormal basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval (the range of the coordinate $x^1$) and let $\\rho,P:J\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^1$. Define the symmetric type-$(2,0)$ field $$T^{ab}(x):=\\rho(x^1)\\,e_0^ae_0^b+P(x^1)\\,e_1^ae_1^b$$ on an open set $\\Omega$ whose $x^1$-coordinates fill $J$ (a static uniaxial stress field along the $e_1$ direction, with $P<0$ representing tension). For $\\Omega\\ne\\varnothing$, this is a $C^1$ symmetric stress-energy field in the sense of [[def-sr-stress-energy-tensor-and-force-density]]. If $\\Omega=\\varnothing$, then $J=\\varnothing$ and the displayed formula defines the unique empty mathematical tensor field; the stress-energy and four-force terminology of that definition is not applied to this branch. The coordinate divergence $D^b:=\\partial_aT^{ab}$ vanishes on $\\Omega$ if and only if $P$ is constant on $J$; for nonempty $\\Omega$ this is exactly local stress-energy conservation, while for $\\Omega=J=\\varnothing$ both mathematical conditions are vacuous. The density $\\rho$ remains unrestricted apart from $C^1$ regularity, and negative constant $P$ gives the tension branch only on a nonempty domain.",
          "strategy": "Compute the coordinate divergence directly: $T^{00}=\\rho(x^1)$, $T^{11}=P(x^1)$ and all other components vanish, so $D^0=D^2=D^3=0$ and $D^1=P\\prime(x^1)$. The chart image of open $\\Omega$ is open, and varying only $x^1$ in a small chart ball makes its stipulated interval image $J$ open. Hence all its points are interior; [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]] gives $D=0$ if and only if $P$ is constant on $J$. On nonempty $\\Omega$, the stress-energy supplier identifies this equation with local conservation. On empty $\\Omega=J$, only the unique empty mathematical tensor and vacuous coordinate-divergence/constancy statements are retained, with constant witness $0$. The full derivative of $\\rho e_0\\otimes e_0$ may be nonzero, but does not enter divergence since its first tensor index is $0$. No material model, constitutive law, existence assertion or choice principle is used.",
