        {
          "id": "ex-sr-scalar-field-plane-wave-stress-energy",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Null plane-wave scalar field and its conserved stress-energy tensor",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "Let $k\\neq0$ be a null covector, $\\eta^{ab}k_ak_b=0$, with $k^\\sharp$ future, let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth, $\\theta_0\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $\\varphi(x):=f(k_ax^a+\\theta_0)$ in an inertial chart; take $V\\equiv0$ and $K>0$. Then $\\varphi$ is a smooth scalar field, $\\partial_a\\varphi=f'(k_ax^a+\\theta_0)k_a$ and $\\Box\\varphi=k^ak_af''(k_ax^a+\\theta_0)=0$, so $\\varphi$ solves the massless scalar-field equation of [[lem-sr-scalar-field-euler-lagrange]]. Its Hilbert stress-energy tensor is $$T^{ab}=Kf'(k_ax^a+\\theta_0)^2\\,k^ak^b,$$ symmetric; wherever $f\\prime\\ne0$ it is a positive multiple of the rank-one tensor $k^\\sharp\\otimes k^\\sharp$ with future-null generating vector $k^\\sharp$, and wherever $f\\prime=0$ it is the zero tensor. In either case one has $T^{ab}n_an_b=Kf'^2\\,(k_an^a)^2\\ge0$ for every future unit observer $n$, and conserved, $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0$, by [[lem-sr-scalar-field-stress-divergence-identity]] evaluated on the solution $\\Box\\varphi=0$. In an inertial frame $T^{00}=Kf'^2(k^0)^2\\ge0$. The example is an explicit conditional computation for the massless scalar model; it asserts no wave observation and no measurement.",
          "strategy": "Substitute $\\partial_a\\varphi=f'k_a$ into the Hilbert tensor formula of [[lem-sr-scalar-field-hilbert-stress-energy]]; the potential term drops and $\\eta^{ab}k_ak_b=0$ kills the trace term, leaving $Kf'^2k^ak^b$. The equation of motion holds because $k$ is null, and conservation follows from [[lem-sr-scalar-field-stress-divergence-identity]]. Source: the scalar stress tensor (4.51) of Tong with V=0; the null-covector condition is the framework's own. No choice principle is used. The coefficient $Kf\\prime{}^2$ is strictly positive exactly where $f\\prime\\ne0$ and vanishes exactly where $f\\prime=0$, because $K>0$. Since $k^\\sharp$ is a fixed nonzero future-null vector, this proves the precise rank-one/zero alternative. Squaring $k_an^a$ gives the nonnegative observer contraction stated without any claim that the zero tensor is future null.",
          "deps": [
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space",
