        {
          "id": "cex-sr-divergence-free-stress-energy-with-varying-slice-momentum",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "title": "Divergence-free stress-energy with a nonconserved slice integral",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "In an inertial chart on all of affine Minkowski space define the smooth symmetric field $T^{00}=x^0$, $T^{01}=T^{10}=-x^1$, $T^{11}=x^0$, with every other component zero. Then $\\partial_aT^{ab}=0$ everywhere, since the only possibly nonzero divergence components are $\\partial_0T^{00}+\\partial_1T^{10}=1-1=0$ and $\\partial_0T^{01}+\\partial_1T^{11}=0$. For $L>0$, $c_2<d_2$, $c_3<d_3$, put $A=(d_2-c_2)(d_3-c_3)>0$ and $K=[0,L]\\times[c_2,d_2]\\times[c_3,d_3]$. The full spatial slice integral is $Q^0(s)=\\int_K T^{00}(s,x)\\,d^3x=A Ls$. The axis-one side-flux difference on a slab $[s_1,s_2]\\times K$ is $\\int_{[s_1,s_2]\\times K_1^+}T^{10}\\,d^3x-\\int_{[s_1,s_2]\\times K_1^-}T^{10}\\,d^3x=-A L(s_2-s_1)$; all transverse fluxes vanish. Consequently [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]] gives $Q^0(s_2)-Q^0(s_1)=A L(s_2-s_1)\\ne0$ whenever $s_1<s_2$. For example $A=6,L=2,s_2-s_1=3$ gives $36$. Thus zero local divergence alone does not conserve a finite slice integral: the side-flux/isolation hypothesis of the integrated-conservation theorem cannot be dropped. This prescribed tensor is not claimed to arise from any selected matter model.",
          "strategy": "Compute the divergence components directly, then perform the full three-dimensional slice integration and the three-dimensional side-face integration. Both gain exactly the same transverse area A. The negative side-face difference gives the positive slice change through the finite-box theorem. No noncompact limit, physical model, or choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
