          "generation": {
            "role": "counterexample"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-sr-prescribed-stress-energy-need-not-be-conserved",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "title": "A prescribed covariant stress-energy tensor need not be conserved",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "statement": "In an inertial chart let $\\Omega$ be nonempty, open and connected and let $F:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^1$. The symmetric type-$(2,0)$ field $T^{ab}=F\\eta^{ab}$ is $C^1$, and its continuous force density is $f^b=\\partial_aT^{ab}=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_aF=\\partial^bF$. It is conserved on $\\Omega$ exactly when $F$ is constant; if $F$ is nonconstant then $f$ is not identically zero, although it can vanish at some points. For the smooth explicit witness $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^4$, $F(x)=(x^1)^2$, one has $f=(0,2x^1,0,0)$, which vanishes on the plane $x^1=0$ and is nonzero off it. The tensor expression $F\\eta^{-1}$ is covariant under every affine Lorentz change of inertial chart: for $x'=\\Lambda x+a$ the scalar component is $F'(x')=F(\\Lambda^{-1}(x'-a))$ and $T'^{ab}=F'\\eta^{ab}$. Thus covariance of a prescribed tensor expression does not itself imply conservation; balance is an additional premise. The field is not claimed to be the stress-energy tensor of any of the page's matter models.",
          "strategy": "Differentiate $F\\eta^{ab}$ with the constant inverse metric to obtain the force formula. Nondegeneracy of the metric makes $f=0$ equivalent to all partial derivatives of $F$ vanishing. A $C^1$ scalar is totally differentiable ([[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]]), so the exact connected-open zero-derivative theorem ([[thm-zero-derivative-on-connected-open-euclidean-set-iff-constant]]) gives constancy; the converse follows directly. For $F=(x^1)^2$ compute $f=(0,2x^1,0,0)$, including its stationary plane. Apply the earlier tensor law ([[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iii)) with the scalar pulled back to the same physical point and $\\Lambda\\eta^{-1}\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}=\\eta^{-1}$. This proves covariance without deriving conservation. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-sr-stress-energy-tensor-and-force-density",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation",
            "thm-zero-derivative-on-connected-open-euclidean-set-iff-constant"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
