      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-sr-proper-acceleration",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Proper acceleration of a massive worldline and its agreement with the acceleration of an observer congruence",
          "statement": "Let $c>0$ and let $x:I\\to A$ be a $C^2$ future timelike worldline in the affine Minkowski model ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], [[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]), with generic parameter $s\\in I$. Write $\\tau:I\\to\\tau[I]$ for its proper-time function and $U(s):=x'(s)/\\tau'(s)$, $A(s):=U'(s)/\\tau'(s)$ for its four-velocity and four-acceleration ([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]). These are proper-time derivatives evaluated at the event $x(s)$; $s$ need not itself be proper time. Pointwise $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$, $\\eta(U,A)=0$, and $A(s)$ is spacelike whenever it is nonzero ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (a),(b)).\n\n**(a) Definition.** The **proper acceleration** of $x$ at the parameter value $s$ is the real number\n\n$$a(s) := \\sqrt{\\eta(A(s),A(s))},$$\n\nthe nonnegative square root of [[def-rational-power]]. This is well defined for every $s \\in I$. Indeed, fix an inertial chart of $A$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]); since $x$ is $C^2$, the component functions of $A$ are continuous, and $\\eta(A,A)$ is a sum of products of continuous component functions, hence continuous, by [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]]. If $A(s) = 0$ then $\\eta(A,A) = 0$ at $s$, and if $A(s) \\neq 0$ then $A(s)$ is spacelike and $\\eta(A,A) > 0$ ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]); in both cases the argument of the square root is nonnegative, so $a(s)$ is a well-defined nonnegative real number for every $s \\in I$. With the SI coordinate and unit conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], in which $x^0 = c t$ with $t$ in seconds and the spatial coordinates are in metres while $\\eta$ has the dimensionless matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ in an inertial chart, the components of $A$ carry the unit metre per second squared and $\\eta(A,A)$ carries the unit $(\\text{metre per second squared})^2$, so $a$ carries the unit metre per second squared. The worldline is **uniformly accelerated** when $a$ is constant on $I$.\n\n**(b) Agreement with the acceleration of a congruence.** Let $\\Omega$ be a nonempty open subset of $A$ and let $n : \\Omega \\to V$ be an observer congruence with acceleration field $a_{\\mathrm{cg}} := \\nabla_n n$, where $\\nabla$ is the flat covariant derivative of [[def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space]] ([[def-sr-observer-congruence-and-kinematics]] (b)). If $x$ is a flow line of $n$ and $U = c n$ along $x$, then $A = c^2 a_{\\mathrm{cg}}$ along $x$: the derivative along the worldline of the field $n$ is the covariant derivative $\\nabla_{dx/d\\tau} n = \\nabla_{cn} n = c \\nabla_n n = c\\, a_{\\mathrm{cg}}$, by the defining properties of the flat covariant derivative along curves ([[def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space]] (d)), so $A = d(c n)/d\\tau = c\\, \\nabla_{dx/d\\tau} n = c^2 a_{\\mathrm{cg}}$. Consequently $a = c^2 \\sqrt{\\eta(a_{\\mathrm{cg}},a_{\\mathrm{cg}})}$ along $x$: the proper acceleration is $c^2$ times the magnitude of the congruence acceleration. In particular the proper acceleration of a congruence worldline is determined by the congruence field alone, and such a worldline is uniformly accelerated exactly when $\\eta(a_{\\mathrm{cg}},a_{\\mathrm{cg}})$ is constant along it.\n\n**(c) Reading and scope.** This item defines a quantity of the model and fixes its name, its unit and its physical reading. The model reads $a(s)$ as the magnitude that an ideal accelerometer carried along the worldline would register at $s$; this ideal-instrument identification is adopted with the model in the same sense as the framework's ideal-clock reading of proper time and is not derived here. No accelerometer response law, calibration, frequency dependence, force law or interaction is specified or asserted; the definition asserts nothing about which bodies follow which worldlines; no measurement outcome and no statistical, uncertainty or empirical claim is made. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Definition with its well-definedness and its unit and congruence clauses justified by the cited suppliers. (a) A is continuous because x is C^2 (def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline, def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration (b)); eta(A,A) is the sum of products of continuous component functions in any inertial chart (def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart, thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions); eta(A,A) >= 0 pointwise by thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition (b) together with the classification of nonzero spacelike vectors by def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors. Hence sqrt(eta(A,A)) is well defined for every tau as the nonnegative real whose square is eta(A,A) (def-rational-power). Units are read off directly from def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions, which lists the unit metre per second squared for the four-acceleration and the dimensionless matrix diag(-1,1,1,1) for eta in an inertial chart. (b) If U = c n along x with c a constant, then A = dU/dtau = c d(n o x)/dtau; the derivative of the field n along the worldline is the covariant derivative nabla_{dx/dtau} n = nabla_{cn} n = c nabla_n n = c a_cg by the defining properties of the flat covariant derivative along curves (def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space (d)); hence A = c^2 a_cg and eta(A,A) = c^4 eta(a_cg,a_cg), so a = c^2 sqrt(eta(a_cg,a_cg)) because c > 0. The acceleration a_cg is spacelike or zero by def-sr-observer-congruence-and-kinematics (b), so eta(a_cg,a_cg) >= 0. (c) The scope clause records that the accelerometer reading is an ideal-instrument identification of the model and that no apparatus, force or measurement model is asserted; this mirrors the treatment of clock readings in post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light and of wave frequencies in def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
