            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages): Section 3.5.2 'The acceleration vector' p. 61 (the acceleration four-vector, the identity d gamma/dt = gamma^3 a.v as the acceleration measured by an accelerometer carried along the worldline, and the normalisation of the four-acceleration); signature (+---) converted to (-+++), c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "M. Blau, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (University of Bern, last update October 5, 2026, 1052 PDF pages): Sections 1.2 items 8-9 pp. 34-36 (four-velocity normalisation, four-acceleration and its orthogonality to the four-velocity) and Section 1.3 pp. 36-39 (constant proper acceleration)",
                "url": "http://www.blau.itp.unibe.ch/newlecturesGR.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional kinematic definition inside the flat classical special-relativistic model: the proper acceleration of a C^2 future timelike worldline, its SI unit, and its identification with the magnitude of an ideal accelerometer reading. It presupposes ideal clocks and rods and the model's SI conventions; it asserts no force law, no accelerometer response or calibration model, no gravity, no rotational or quantum effect and no measurement, statistical or empirical content, and it makes no claim about any material body.",
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-sr-hyperbolic-motion-worldline",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The uniformly accelerated worldline: hyperbolic motion, constant four-acceleration magnitude, and identification as a Rindler flow line",
          "statement": "Let $c > 0$ and $\\alpha > 0$, set $R := c^2/\\alpha$, and let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski space with an inertial chart $\\chi = (x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$, $x^0 = c t$ ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). Write $x^a(\\tau):=\\chi^a(x(\\tau))$ for the coordinate components of the curve $x:\\mathbb R\\to A$ defined by $x(\\tau) := \\chi^{-1}(R \\sinh \\theta, R \\cosh \\theta, 0, 0)$, where $\\theta := \\alpha \\tau/c$.\n\n(a) **Regularity, causal character and parametrization.** $x$ is $C^\\infty$, and $x'(\\tau) = c(\\cosh \\theta, \\sinh \\theta, 0, 0)$ is future timelike for every $\\tau$ ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]), so $x$ is a future timelike worldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]). Since $\\eta(x',x') = -c^2$ is constant, the parameter $\\tau$ is a proper-time parameter of the curve: the proper-time function $T$ of [[def-sr-proper-time]] satisfies $T(b)-T(a)=b-a$ for all $a < b$ in $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-sr-proper-time]], [[lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization]]), and the four-velocity is $U := dx/d\\tau = c(\\cosh \\theta, \\sinh \\theta, 0, 0)$ with $\\eta(U,U) = -c^2$ ([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]], [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (a)).\n\n(b) **Constant four-acceleration magnitude.** The four-acceleration $A := dU/d\\tau = \\alpha(\\sinh \\theta, \\cosh \\theta, 0, 0)$ satisfies $\\eta(A,A) = \\alpha^2$ and $\\eta(U,A) = 0$. The chart coordinate velocity is $dx^1/dt = c \\tanh \\theta$, so $|dx^1/dt| < c$ for every $\\tau$, and $x^1 = R \\cosh \\theta > R|\\sinh \\theta| = |x^0|$ for every $\\tau$: the worldline lies in the right wedge $W := \\{x^1 > |x^0|\\}$ and its speed in the chart tends to $c$ only as $\\tau \\to \\pm\\infty$.\n\n(c) **Rindler flow line.** Let $\\Phi(\\theta,\\rho,y,z) := \\chi^{-1}(\\rho \\sinh \\theta, \\rho \\cosh \\theta, y, z)$ on $\\mathbb R \\times (0,\\infty) \\times \\mathbb R^2$ and let $n := \\rho^{-1} \\partial_\\theta$ be the Rindler congruence of [[thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples]] (b), whose pullback metric is $-\\rho^2 d\\theta^2 + d\\rho^2 + dy^2 + dz^2$ and whose congruence acceleration $a_{\\mathrm{cg}} := \\nabla_n n$ is $\\rho^{-1} \\partial_\\rho$, with $\\eta(a_{\\mathrm{cg}},a_{\\mathrm{cg}}) = \\rho^{-2}$ ([[def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space]], [[def-sr-observer-congruence-and-kinematics]]). Then $x$ is exactly the flow line $\\Phi(\\cdot, R, 0, 0)$ reparametrized by $\\theta = \\alpha \\tau/c$, and along $x$ one has $A = c^2 a_{\\mathrm{cg}}$: the four-acceleration of the worldline is $c^2$ times the congruence acceleration, and $\\eta(a_{\\mathrm{cg}},a_{\\mathrm{cg}}) = 1/R^2 = \\alpha^2/c^4$ on the flow line $\\rho = R$.\n\n(d) **Asymptotic behaviour.** Along $x$ one has $x^1 - x^0 = R e^{-\\theta} \\to 0$ with $x^0 \\to +\\infty$ as $\\tau \\to +\\infty$, and $x^1 + x^0 = R e^{\\theta} \\to 0$ with $x^0 \\to -\\infty$ as $\\tau \\to -\\infty$: the worldline approaches each of the null planes $\\{x^1 = x^0\\}$ and $\\{x^1 = -x^0\\}$ asymptotically without meeting either, and the chart coordinate speed tends to $c$ at both ends.\n\n(e) **Scope.** This is an exact kinematic computation in the affine model. It uses no force law, no mass, no clock hypothesis and no empirical input; beyond identifying the displayed parameter with the proper time of the curve, only the flow parametrization of the congruence is used. The curve is not asserted to be a geodesic and no statement is made about the forces that would maintain it. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Exact computation with the hyperbolic identities and their derivatives. (a) Differentiating the two components of x with the chain rule (thm-chain-rule, def-derivative) and thm-hyperbolic-identities-and-derivatives gives x' = c(cosh theta, sinh theta, 0, 0); cosh > 0 makes it future and eta(x',x') = c^2(-cosh^2 + sinh^2) = -c^2 (def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors, def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets). The integrand of def-sr-proper-time is the constant c, so its integral over [a,b] is c(b-a) and tau(b) - tau(a) = b - a; the assertions about the four-velocity then follow from lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization (iv) and thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition (a). (b) Differentiating U gives A = alpha(sinh theta, cosh theta, 0, 0); eta(A,A) = alpha^2(cosh^2 - sinh^2) = alpha^2 and eta(U,A) = c alpha(-cosh sinh + sinh cosh) = 0 (thm-hyperbolic-identities-and-derivatives); the chart velocity is dx^1/dt = (dx^1/dtau)/(dt/dtau) = c sinh/cosh = c tanh, and cosh > |sinh| gives the wedge and the speed bound. (c) Direct comparison: Phi(theta, R, 0, 0) = (R sinh theta, R cosh theta, 0, 0) = x(tau) with theta = alpha tau/c, and the congruence data are those of thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples (b); the identity A = c^2 a_cg is proved directly on the flow line: since dx/dtau = c n there, d(n o x)/dtau = nabla_{dx/dtau} n = c nabla_n n = c a_cg by the defining properties of the flat covariant derivative along curves (def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space (d)), so A = dU/dtau = c d(n o x)/dtau = c^2 a_cg. (d) The identities x^1 -+ x^0 = R(cosh -+ sinh) = R e^{-+theta} and e^{+-theta} -> 0 (thm-exponential-limits-and-range) give the limits; tanh -> +-1 gives the speed statement. (e) Scope only. No choice principle is used: all steps are finite-dimensional algebra and calculus.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
