            "thm-exponential-limits-and-range": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages): Section 3.5.2 'The acceleration vector', Example 4 'Constant proper acceleration' and the following page (hyperbolic motion t = (1/a) sinh(a tau), x = (1/a) cosh(a tau), the identity x = (1/a) sqrt(1 + a^2t^2) and the lightlike asymptote) and Section 7.1 'An example: accelerated coordinates' p. 148 (t = X sinh T, x = X cosh T, proper acceleration 1/X); signature (+---) converted to (-+++), c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "M. Blau, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (University of Bern, last update October 5, 2026, 1052 PDF pages): Section 1.3 pp. 36-38 (the worldline u = (cosh a tau, sinh a tau), xi = (a^{-1} sinh a tau, a^{-1} cosh a tau), the hyperbola eta(xi,xi) = -a^{-2} in the quadrant x^1 > |x^0|, and the proper time and acceleration a = 1/rho_0 of a rho = const worldline) and Section 3.4 pp. 90-93 (Rindler coordinates, their ranges and the right Rindler wedge)",
                "url": "http://www.blau.itp.unibe.ch/newlecturesGR.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-sr-connecting-field-stretching",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The material metric of a congruence: evolution of rest separations and preservation of the internal metric under Born rigidity",
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega$ be a nonempty open subset of affine Minkowski space $A$, $c>0$, and let $n:\\Omega\\to V$ be a $C^1$ future unit timelike field, $\\eta(n,n)=-1$. Define the following first-order quantities in any inertial chart:\n\n$$h^a{}_b:=\\delta^a{}_b+n^an_b,\\qquad h_{ab}:=\\eta_{ab}+n_an_b,\\qquad a^b:=n^c\\partial_cn^b,\\qquad\\theta_{ab}:=h_a{}^ch_b{}^d\\partial_{(c}n_{d)},$$\n\nwhere symmetrization means half the sum, $n_a:=\\eta_{ab}n^b$, and $h(v,w):=h_{ab}v^aw^b$. Here $h$ is $C^1$ and $a,\\theta$ are continuous. The rest projection $h(v):=v+\\eta(n,v)n$ is as in [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (c). For $C^1$ fields and tensors define\n\n$$(L_nh)_{ab}:=n^c\\partial_ch_{ab}+h_{cb}\\partial_an^c+h_{ac}\\partial_bn^c.$$\n\nFor smooth data, define the ordinary Lie derivative by $(\\mathcal L_nh)(X,Y):=n(h(X,Y))-h([n,X],Y)-h(X,[n,Y])$ for smooth vector fields $X,Y$; the brackets have the coordinate formula of [[prop-coordinate-formula-for-the-lie-bracket]]. These first-order coordinate formulas transform tensorially under affine inertial chart changes and agree, on smooth fields, with [[def-sr-observer-congruence-and-kinematics]], [[def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space]] and the ordinary smooth Lie derivative. Say the $C^1$ field is **Born rigid** when $L_nh=0$; the identity $L_nh=2\\theta$ makes this equivalent to $\\theta=0$.\n\n(a) Let $J$ be a nonempty open interval, $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ a nonempty open set, and $\\Phi:J\\times B\\to\\Omega$ an injective $C^2$ immersion with $\\partial_s\\Phi=n(\\Phi)$. Put $e_I:=\\partial_{\\xi^I}\\Phi$, $I=1,2,3$, and $G_{IJ}(s,\\xi):=h(e_I,e_J)$ at $\\Phi(s,\\xi)$. Then $n,e_1,e_2,e_3$ are linearly independent, $G$ is symmetric positive definite at every point, and its entries are $C^1$. Thus for each $s$ it is a $C^1$ positive-definite material metric on $B$. If $n$ and $\\Phi$ are smooth, $G$ is a smooth Riemannian metric, as in the original smooth branch.\n\n(b) For all $I,J$,\n\n$$\\partial_sG_{IJ}=(L_nh)(e_I,e_J)=2\\theta(e_I,e_J),\\qquad\\partial_\\tau G_{IJ}=2c\\theta(e_I,e_J),$$\n\nwhere $\\tau=s/c$ is the proper-time parameter along each flow line, whose four-velocity is $cn$. For a nonzero label direction $e_I$, put $\\ell_I:=\\sqrt{h(e_I,e_I)}>0$ and $u_I:=h(e_I)/\\ell_I$. This $u_I$ is a horizontal unit rest vector, and\n\n$$\\partial_\\tau\\ell_I=c\\theta(u_I,u_I)\\ell_I.$$\n\nThe alternative normalized material vector $e_I/\\ell_I$ need not be horizontal, but has the same $\\theta$ contraction because $\\theta$ is horizontal. Positive/negative expansion gives positive/negative fractional change of this material rest length per proper time.\n\n(c) If $\\theta=0$, then $\\partial_sG_{IJ}=0$ and $G_{IJ}(s,\\xi)$ is constant in $s$ for each fixed label, with no restriction on material-label separation: the entire internal metric is preserved exactly. Conversely, a nonzero contraction $\\theta(e_I,e_J)$ gives a nonzero derivative of that metric component at the point and hence nonconstancy on a neighbourhood. The smooth Born-rigid branch and its original conclusions are included.\n\n(d) This is a local statement about the given family and its material metric. Integrating the material metric at a common flow parameter gives an internal label-space length; it does not identify that length with a finite endpoint distance on a single observer's orthogonal simultaneity hyperplane without an additional argument. No matter model, elasticity, field equation, global rigidity theorem, boundary condition or choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Use only first derivatives of $n$ and two derivatives of $\\Phi$. The coordinate rules are tensorial under constant Lorentz changes of inertial coordinates by [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]]; for smooth data they agree with the established smooth definitions. Differentiating $\\eta(n,n)=-1$ gives $n_b\\partial_an^b=0$, hence $\\eta(n,a)=0$. Expansion of the projected symmetrization gives\n$$2\\theta_{ab}=\\partial_an_b+\\partial_bn_a+n_aa_b+a_an_b.$$\nDifferentiating $h_{ab}=\\eta_{ab}+n_an_b$ in the displayed first-order Lie formula gives exactly this right side, proving $L_nh=2\\theta$. The projectors make $\\theta$ horizontal in both slots.\nFor the material fields, equality of the mixed partials of $C^2$ components of $\\Phi$ ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]) and the total chain rule ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]) give\n$$\\partial_se_I^a=\\partial_{\\xi^I}n^a(\\Phi)=(\\partial_bn^a)(\\Phi)e_I^b.$$\nDifferentiating $G_{IJ}=h_{ab}(\\Phi)e_I^ae_J^b$ by the product rule gives the displayed coordinate formula for $(L_nh)(e_I,e_J)$, and thus $\\partial_sG_{IJ}=2\\theta(e_I,e_J)$. This direct calculation does not invoke a smooth-only Lie-bracket supplier for $C^1$ fields. In the smooth branch, the same mixed-partial identity is the usual commuting material-coordinate calculation $[n,e_I]=0$ ([[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]], [[prop-coordinate-formula-for-the-lie-bracket]]), and recovers the original smooth route.\nThe derivative of $\\Phi$ has four independent columns by immersion, so no nonzero linear combination of the three $e_I$ lies in $\\operatorname{span}(n)$. The rest projection has that one-dimensional kernel and is positive definite on $n^\\perp$ ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]](c)); hence $G$ is positive definite. $h(\\Phi)$ and $e_I$ are $C^1$, so $G$ is $C^1$, and smooth if all data are smooth. Along a flow line $\\partial_s\\Phi=n$ is nonzero future timelike, and the proper-time definition gives $d\\tau/ds=\\sqrt{-\\eta(n,n)}/c=1/c$, so $\\partial_\\tau=c\\partial_s$ ([[def-sr-proper-time]], [[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]). Apply the chain rule to $\\ell_I^2=G_{II}$: $\\partial_\\tau\\ell_I=c\\theta(e_I,e_I)/\\ell_I$. Since $h(e_I)-e_I$ is parallel to $n$, horizontality gives $\\theta(e_I,e_I)=\\ell_I^2\\theta(u_I,u_I)$, proving the fractional-rate formula. If $\\theta=0$, the interval zero-derivative corollary [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]] proves constancy for each fixed label. A nonzero derivative prevents local constancy. All claims, including the smooth special case, are proved with the stated finite regularity and no zero tangent is called timelike.",
