        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Rindler chart: causal horizons of a uniformly accelerated observer and the restrictions of radar time and distance",
          "statement": "Adopt [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]], [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] and [[post-sr-causal-signal-propagation]], and the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]] ($x^0 = c t$). Let $x = (x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ be the chart of an inertial frame ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]) and let $W := \\{x^1 > |x^0|\\}$ be the right wedge. Let $\\Phi$ and $n$ be the Rindler chart and congruence of [[thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples]] (b): $\\Phi(\\theta,\\rho,y,z) = (\\rho \\sinh \\theta, \\rho \\cosh \\theta, y, z)$ on $\\mathbb R \\times (0,\\infty) \\times \\mathbb R^2$ with inverse $\\rho = \\sqrt{(x^1)^2 - (x^0)^2}$, $\\theta = \\frac12 \\log((x^1+x^0)/(x^1-x^0))$, pullback metric $-\\rho^2 d\\theta^2 + d\\rho^2 + dy^2 + dz^2$, and acceleration field $a_{\\mathrm{cg}} = \\nabla_n n = \\rho^{-1} \\partial_\\rho$. Fix $\\rho_0 > 0$ and real $y_0,z_0$, and let $O$ be the congruence worldline $\\rho = \\rho_0$, $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$, parametrized by its proper time $\\tau$ with $\\theta = c \\tau/\\rho_0$; by [[def-sr-proper-acceleration]] its proper acceleration is $c^2/\\rho_0$. Write $U(q) := q^0 + q^1$ and $V(q) := q^0 - q^1$ for events $q$, so that $U = \\rho e^{\\theta}$ and $V = -\\rho e^{-\\theta}$ on $W$, and write $\\le$ and $J^+$, $J^-$ for the causal reachability relations of [[def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space]]. Then, conditionally on the adopted model:\n\n(i) **Chart domain and light rays.** $\\Phi$ is a smooth bijection of $\\mathbb R \\times (0,\\infty) \\times \\mathbb R^2$ onto $W$ with the stated smooth inverse, so the chart domain is exactly $W$ and $\\rho = 0$ is not part of its coordinate domain. The boundary of $W$ in the affine model is $\\{x^1 = |x^0|\\}$, with arbitrary $x^2,x^3$: it is the union of the two null half-planes $\\{x^1 = x^0 \\ge 0\\}$ and $\\{x^1 = -x^0 \\ge 0\\}$. These half-planes are portions of the full null planes appearing as causal horizons in (ii); the full planes are not the wedge boundary. Along the worldline $\\rho = \\rho_0$ one has $V = -\\rho_0 e^{-\\theta} \\to 0^-$ and $U \\to +\\infty$ as $\\theta \\to +\\infty$, while $U = \\rho_0 e^{\\theta} \\to 0^+$ and $V \\to -\\infty$ as $\\theta \\to -\\infty$. The future null straight lines of the model that lie in the plane $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$ and meet $O$ are exactly the lines of constant $V$ (on which $d\\theta = +d\\rho/\\rho$) and the lines of constant $U$ (on which $d\\theta = -d\\rho/\\rho$).\n\n(ii) **Causal horizons.** An event $q$ satisfies $q \\le p$ for some event $p$ of $O$ if and only if $V(q) < 0$; an event $q$ satisfies $p \\le q$ for some event $p$ of $O$ if and only if $U(q) > 0$. Consequently no event with $V(q) \\ge 0$ can ever influence $O$; this set contains the null plane $\\{x^1 = x^0\\}$, the left wedge $\\{x^1 < -|x^0|\\}$ and the future wedge $\\{x^0 > |x^1|\\}$; and no event with $U(q) \\le 0$ can ever be influenced by $O$, a set containing the null plane $\\{x^1 = -x^0\\}$, the left wedge and the past wedge $\\{x^0 < -|x^1|\\}$. The null plane $\\{x^1 = x^0\\}$ is the boundary of the causal past of $O$ (the future horizon: the surface its worldline approaches as $\\theta \\to +\\infty$), and the null plane $\\{x^1 = -x^0\\}$ is the boundary of the causal future of $O$ (the past horizon); every event of the left wedge is causally unrelated to $O$ in both directions. The same criteria with $O$ any other congruence worldline $\\rho = \\rho_1$, $y = y_1$, $z = z_1$ hold verbatim: the constants $\\rho_0$, $y_0$, $z_0$ do not enter.\n\n(iii) **Radar time and distance to a coaxial congruence reflector.** Fix $\\rho_m > 0$ with $\\rho_m \\ne \\rho_0$ and let $R_m$ be the congruence worldline $\\rho = \\rho_m$, $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$ (a second uniformly accelerated observer, coaxial with $O$, with proper acceleration $c^2/\\rho_m$). Let $e_-$ be the event of $O$ at parameter $\\theta_e$ (so $\\tau(e_-) = \\rho_0 \\theta_e/c$ up to the base point of $\\tau$), let $q_m$ be the event of $R_m$ lying on the future null straight line from $e_-$ in the plane $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$, and let $e_+$ be the event of $O$ lying on the future null straight line from $q_m$; the reflection at $q_m$ is idealized as instantaneous and as leaving $R_m$'s motion unchanged, as in [[def-sr-operational-radar-protocol]]. Then $e_-, q_m, e_+$ exist uniquely, the Rindler time of $q_m$ is $\\theta_m = \\theta_e + |\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$, the return event satisfies $\\theta(e_+) = \\theta_e + 2|\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$ and $(\\theta_e + \\theta(e_+))/2 = \\theta_m$, so the round trip lasts $2|\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$ in Rindler time and $(2\\rho_0/c)|\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$ in $O$'s proper time. The radar protocol therefore assigns to $q_m$ the radar time $\\tau_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q_m) = (\\rho_0/c) \\theta_m$ plus the base constant of $\\tau$ and the radar distance $\\rho_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q_m) = \\rho_0 |\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$; within this coaxial family the radar-time level sets are the intersections of the Rindler rest slices $\\theta=\\mathrm{const}$ with $y=y_0$, $z=z_0$, excluding the observer radius $\\rho_0$ ([[def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance]]). The radar distance is not the rest distance: in the slice $\\theta = \\theta_m$ the two worldlines are at rest distance $|\\rho_m - \\rho_0|$, whereas for $\\rho_m = 2\\rho_0$ the radar distance is $\\rho_0 \\log 2 < \\rho_0$, because $\\log$ is strictly increasing and $e > 2$ implies $\\log 2 < \\log e = 1$ ([[cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]). For every distinct positive reflector radius the notions differ: with $r = \\rho_m/\\rho_0$, one has $\\log r < r - 1$ when $r > 1$ and $-\\log r > 1 - r$ when $0 < r < 1$. The two distance formulas agree only at $r = 1$, where both have the continuous degenerate value zero. The coincident case is not a nonzero emission-reflection-reception triple of the protocol, whose distinct-reflector hypothesis remains in force. As $\\rho_m \\to 0^+$ the round-trip proper time and the radar distance grow without bound while the reflector's proper acceleration $c^2/\\rho_m$ diverges; as $\\rho_m \\to +\\infty$ the round-trip proper time and the radar distance also grow without bound while the reflector's proper acceleration tends to $0$.\n\n(iv) **Restrictions of the protocol.** No event with $V \\ge 0$ --- in particular no event of the horizon plane $\\{x^1 = x^0\\}$, of the left wedge or of the future wedge --- can be assigned a radar time or radar distance by this protocol from $O$, because by (ii) it cannot causally precede any reception event on $O$: the required reflection-to-reception leg is impossible. Separately, every event with $U \\le 0$ lacks an emission-to-reflection leg from $O$. Thus any event admitting this radar triple must satisfy $U > 0$ and $V < 0$, equivalently it must belong to $W$; the horizon is at infinite radar distance in the limit $\\rho_m \\to 0^+$. For example $q = x^{-1}(2\\rho_0,0,y_0,z_0)$ has $U(q) = V(q) = 2\\rho_0 > 0$. It is causally after $O$ at $\\theta = 0$, since the displacement is $(2\\rho_0,-\\rho_0,0,0)$, future timelike, and it even lies on a future null ray from $O$ at $\\theta = \\log 2$, with displacement $(5\\rho_0/4,-5\\rho_0/4,0,0)$. Nevertheless it cannot send a causal return to $O$, so it receives no radar assignment from $O$. The protocol therefore does not extend to a global coordinate system of $A$.\n\n(v) **Scope.** All clauses are conditional computations and readings in flat classical spacetime with ideal clocks, ideal light rays and ideal reflectors, for the specified congruence worldlines. They are not empirical claims and contain no measurement, apparatus, source, detector or statistical content; gravitation, media, dispersion, finite resolution, quantum effects and material models of the reflector are excluded. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "Instantiate the causality lemmas of the causal model in the Rindler chart. (i) The chart assertions are those of thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples (b), together with the identities U = rho e^{theta}, V = -rho e^{-theta}: on a line of constant V one has rho = rho_0 e^{theta - theta_0}, so dtheta = drho/rho and ds^2 = -rho^2 dtheta^2 + drho^2 = 0 exactly, and the tangent with increasing theta has positive time component, so the line is future null; on a line of constant U one has rho = rho_0 e^{theta_0 - theta} and dtheta = -drho/rho, and again ds^2 = 0 with a future tangent (def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors, def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets, post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light). The limits along rho = rho_0 use the defining exponentials. The wedge boundary follows from the continuous inequality x1>|x0|: its closure is x1>=|x0|, since a boundary point is approached by increasing x1 by a positive amount. Thus the boundary is x1=|x0|, comprising only the indicated half-planes; at fixed finite theta, rho tending to zero does not parametrize the entire boundary. (ii) Write p(theta)=(rho_0 sinh theta,rho_0 cosh theta,y_0,z_0), Up=rho_0 e^theta, Vp=-rho_0 e^(-theta), and r^2=(q^2-y_0)^2+(q^3-z_0)^2. For a displacement D, the future causal coordinate criterion is equivalent to DeltaU>=0, DeltaV>=0 and DeltaU DeltaV>=r^2: D0=(DeltaU+DeltaV)/2, D1=(DeltaU-DeltaV)/2 and D0^2-D1^2=DeltaU DeltaV. For q<=p, necessity gives Vp-Vq>=0, hence Vq<=Vp<0. Conversely if Vq<0, as theta tends to +infinity the difference Vp-Vq tends to the positive number -Vq and Up-Uq tends to +infinity. Therefore both differences are positive and their product exceeds the fixed r^2 for sufficiently large theta; the coordinate criterion proves q<=p. For p<=q, necessity gives Uq-Up>=0, hence Uq>=Up>0. Conversely if Uq>0, as theta tends to -infinity the difference Uq-Up tends to the positive number Uq and Vq-Vp tends to +infinity; again their product eventually exceeds r^2 and p<=q follows. These complete arguments include arbitrary transverse positions. The full planes V=0 and U=0 are respectively the boundaries of these open causal unions; direct coordinate signs give the left/future/past wedge inclusions. The signal reading is conditional on post-sr-causal-signal-propagation, and the criteria do not depend on rho_0,y_0,z_0. (iii) The null line of constant V through e_- has rho = rho_0 e^{theta-theta_e}, which is increasing in theta; it meets rho = rho_m at theta_m = theta_e + log(rho_m/rho_0) when rho_m > rho_0. The null line of constant U through e_- has rho = rho_0 e^{theta_e-theta}, decreasing in theta; it meets rho = rho_m at theta_m = theta_e + log(rho_0/rho_m) when rho_m < rho_0. In both cases the displacement from e_- to q_m is a positive multiple of (1,1,0,0) or (1,-1,0,0), hence future null, and theta_m = theta_e + |log(rho_m/rho_0)|. From q_m the return line is the other null family: it reaches rho = rho_0 at theta(e_+) = theta_m + |log(rho_m/rho_0)| = theta_e + 2|log(rho_m/rho_0)|, again along a future null displacement, and uniqueness holds because through a point of the plane there passes exactly one future null line of each family and a fixed congruence worldline is met by such a line in exactly one event. The proper time of O between the two events is rho_0 (theta(e_+) - theta_e)/c by def-sr-proper-time and lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization applied to the unit-tangent flow line; def-sr-operational-radar-protocol converts the round trip into radar time (tau(e_-)+tau(e_+))/2 and radar distance c(tau(e_+)-tau(e_-))/2, giving the displayed values. The comparison with the rest distance uses the slice metric drho^2 + dy^2 + dz^2 of thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples (b) and def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance; the numerical case rho_m = 2rho_0 uses log 2 < 1, which follows from e > 2, log e = 1 and strict increase of log (cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three, thm-natural-logarithm-laws). For the full equality classification, put $r=\\rho_m/\\rho_0>0$. By thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral, log is differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$ with derivative $1/u$, and therefore continuous there by cor-differentiable-implies-continuous. If $r>1$, cor-mean-value-theorem on $[1,r]$ gives $\\xi\\in(1,r)$ with $\\log r=(r-1)/\\xi<r-1$. If $0<r<1$, the same theorem on $[r,1]$ gives $\\xi\\in(r,1)$ with $-\\log r=(1-r)/\\xi>1-r$. Multiplying by $\\rho_0>0$ proves radar distance strictly smaller than rest distance for an outer reflector and strictly larger for an inner reflector. Only $r=1$ gives zero in both formulas. Their continuity at $r=1$ follows from continuity of log and absolute value; this is the degenerate formula extension, not an assertion that a zero-length null leg satisfies the distinct-reflector protocol. The limits as rho_m -> 0^+ and rho_m -> +infinity follow from |log| -> +infinity and from c^2/rho_m. (iv) A radar triple requires e_-<=q and q<=e_+ with e_-,e_+ on O, indeed both displacements null in the protocol. By (ii), Vq>=0 makes q<=e_+ impossible, while Uq<=0 makes e_-<=q impossible. This is a return obstruction in the former case and an emission obstruction in the latter; both are needed for the round trip. The displayed q=(2rho_0,0,y_0,z_0) has future timelike displacement (2rho_0,-rho_0,0,0) from O(0), norm -3rho_0^2<0. O(log2) has coordinates (3rho_0/4,5rho_0/4,y_0,z_0), yielding the displayed future null displacement to q. Since Vq=2rho_0>0 no causal return to O exists. The infinite radar distance as rho_m decreases to zero is the logarithmic limit in (iii), not a spacetime singularity. (v) Scope only. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-proper-acceleration",
            "lem-sr-hyperbolic-motion-worldline",
            "thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples",
            "def-sr-observer-congruence-and-kinematics",
