            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional kinematic computation in flat classical special relativity for an ideal rigidly rotating circular ring, an adopted ideal circular guide carrying two counter-propagating guided signals with inertial tangential speed c, and an ideal clock detector on the ring. The guide and detector models are explicit idealizations, not consequences of the special-relativistic postulates; the item excludes real optical fibers, refractive and dispersive media, apparatus, finite resolution, interference and wave optics, gravitation and quantum effects, and it makes no measurement, statistical or empirical claim and no claim about sending signals between different material points of the ring.",
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "texp-sr-bell-rockets-thread",
          "kind": "thought-experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Bell's rockets and thread: constant chart separation and the criterion for longitudinal expansion of the translated congruence",
          "statement": "Adopt [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]] and the SI conventions $x^0=ct$ of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]] in an inertial chart ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). Let $J$ be an open interval containing $0$, let $q:J\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^2$ with $|q'(t)|<c$, set $\\beta=q'/c$ and $\\gamma=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$, let $L>0$, and fix transverse coordinates $(y_0,z_0)$. The rocket and thread-element worldlines are $x_\\xi(t)=x^{-1}(ct,q(t)+\\xi,y_0,z_0)$, with labels in an open interval $I$ containing $[0,L]$; the rockets are labels $0,L$.\n\n(a) Every member is $C^2$ future timelike: its chart-time tangent is $(c,q',0,0)$ with squared norm $-c^2/\\gamma^2<0$. Its common proper-time function is $\\tau(t):=\\int_0^t\\gamma(v)^{-1}dv$, and its proper four-velocity is $U=\\gamma(c,q',0,0)$ with squared norm $-c^2$. All members have equal chart velocity and acceleration, $q',q''$, and their chart separation is the constant $L$.\n\n(b) The four-dimensional extension is essential for applying the material-family lemma. Choose open transverse intervals $Y,Z$ containing $y_0,z_0$, put $\\sigma:=c\\tau$, let $t=t(\\sigma)$ be the inverse on $c\\tau(J)$, and set\n\n$$\\Phi(\\sigma,\\xi,y,z):=x^{-1}(ct(\\sigma),q(t(\\sigma))+\\xi,y,z),\\qquad(\\xi,y,z)\\in B:=I\\times Y\\times Z.$$\n\nThis is an injective $C^2$ immersion onto an open set, with $dt/d\\sigma=\\gamma/c$ and $\\partial_\\sigma\\Phi=n(\\Phi)$, where $n=\\gamma(1,\\beta,0,0)$ is a $C^1$ future unit field. Apply the first-order definitions in [[lem-sr-connecting-field-stretching]], which agree with [[def-sr-observer-congruence-and-kinematics]] when smooth. The longitudinal unit rest vector is $s_\\parallel:=\\gamma(\\beta,1,0,0)$, and\n\n$$\\theta(s_\\parallel,s_\\parallel)=\\gamma^3q'q''/c^3.$$\n\nThis agrees with [[thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples]] (d), is nonzero exactly where $q'q''\\ne0$, and changes sign with that product. When $q$ is smooth the full original smooth-congruence branch is recovered. Restricting the extension to fixed $y_0,z_0$ gives the prescribed thread.\n\n(c) For its material label tangent $e=\\partial_\\xi\\Phi=(0,1,0,0)$, $h(e,e)=\\gamma^2$ and $h(e)/\\gamma=s_\\parallel$. The vector $e/\\gamma$ generally is not $s_\\parallel$, but horizontality implies $\\theta(e/\\gamma,e/\\gamma)=\\theta(s_\\parallel,s_\\parallel)$. The internal longitudinal material metric is $G_{11}=\\gamma^2$, so its length at common flow parameter is\n\n$$\\mathcal L(t):=\\int_0^L\\sqrt{G_{11}}\\,d\\xi=\\gamma(t)L.$$\n\nIt is an internal material metric length, not automatically the finite endpoint distance on one observer's orthogonal simultaneity hyperplane. The local evolution is\n\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}\\log(\\mathcal L/L)=c\\theta(s_\\parallel,s_\\parallel)=\\gamma^3q'q''/c^2,\\qquad\\frac{d\\mathcal L}{d\\tau}=\\gamma^4Lq'q''/c^2.$$\n\nAt a time with $q'=v$, $\\mathcal L=L/\\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}\\ge L$, with equality exactly for $v=0$. Chart separation stays $L$; the material length is constant precisely when $q'{}^2$ is constant, and has nonzero local rate wherever $q'q''\\ne0$.\n\n(d) Under the stipulated material reading that thread elements follow these flow lines, the initial internal length is $\\gamma(0)L$ and its relative factor is $\\gamma(t)/\\gamma(0)$. Relative to that initial state it stretches where $\\gamma(t)>\\gamma(0)$, shrinks where $\\gamma(t)<\\gamma(0)$, and has equal length where the factors agree. In the special initially resting case $q'(0)=0$, the initial length is $L$ and every state with $q'(t)\\ne0$ has stretch factor $\\gamma(t)>1$. No elasticity or failure criterion is supplied, so neither breaking nor a definite tension of an actual thread follows.\n\n(e) The Rindler congruence in [[thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples]] (b) is Born rigid, with constant internal rest separations and unequal proper accelerations $c^2/\\rho$ at distinct radial labels ([[def-sr-proper-acceleration]]). Equal chart-velocity and acceleration profiles in the translated family do not imply Born rigidity where $q'q''\\ne0$. Constant chart separation and constant internal rest metric are distinct kinematic conditions.\n\n(f) This is a conditional flat-spacetime deduction for prescribed worldlines and the stated material reading. It assumes no dynamical or elastic model, force law, material failure criterion, measurement or empirical fact, and asserts nothing about real threads or rockets. No choice principle is used.",
          "strategy": "(a) Differentiate the given worldlines. The chart-time tangent has norm $-c^2+q\\prime{}^2=-c^2/\\gamma^2$ and positive time component, so it is nonzero future timelike. Proper time gives $d\\tau/dt=1/\\gamma$ and $U=dx/d\\tau=\\gamma(c,q\\prime,0,0)$; its norm is $-c^2$ ([[def-sr-proper-time]], [[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]], [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]). The separation and equality of chart derivatives follow directly.\n(b) Since $q$ is $C^2$, $\\gamma$ is $C^1$ and $\\tau\\prime=1/\\gamma$ is positive $C^1$, so $\\tau$ is $C^2$ and strictly increasing. [[lem-sr-proper-time-parametrization]] gives its $C^1$ inverse; its derivative $dt/d\\tau=\\gamma(t(\\tau))$ is $C^1$, hence the inverse is $C^2$. Scaling to $\\sigma=c\\tau$ gives $dt/d\\sigma=\\gamma/c$. Thus $\\Phi$ is $C^2$, its four derivative columns are $n,e_1,e_2,e_3$, and its determinant in the chart is $\\gamma>0$. Its inverse is explicit from $t=x^0/c$, $\\xi=x^1-q(t)$, $y=x^2,z=x^3$, so it is injective with open image and satisfies the precise $J\\times B\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$ family hypothesis of [[lem-sr-connecting-field-stretching]]. The $C^1$ field $n$ depends only on $t=x^0/c$. Using the first-order projected formula for $\\theta$, and the fact that $s_\\parallel$ is horizontal, Writing $\\dot\\beta=d\\beta/dt$, the only nonzero coordinate derivatives are $\\partial_0n_0=-\\gamma^3\\beta\\dot\\beta/c$ and $\\partial_0n_1=\\gamma^3\\dot\\beta/c$. Therefore $s_\\parallel^a s_\\parallel^b\\partial_an_b=\\gamma^3\\beta\\dot\\beta/c$, giving $\\theta(s_\\parallel,s_\\parallel)=s_\\parallel^a s_\\parallel^b\\partial_an_b=\\gamma^3q\\prime q\\prime\\prime/c^3$. This proves the formula with exactly $C^2$ data and agrees with [[thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples]](d); it does not require a smooth-only supplier for the nonsmooth branch.\n(c) Compute $h(e,e)=1+\\gamma^2\\beta^2=\\gamma^2$ and $h(e)=e+\\gamma\\beta n=\\gamma s_\\parallel$. Since $\\theta(n,\\cdot)=0$, the difference of $e/\\gamma$ and $s_\\parallel$ is annihilated by $\\theta$. Applying [[lem-sr-connecting-field-stretching]] to the four-dimensional family, then restricting to fixed transverse labels, gives $G=\\operatorname{diag}(\\gamma^2,1,1)$ and the length/rate identities. Independently $d\\gamma/dt=\\gamma^3q\\prime q\\prime\\prime/c^2$ and $d/d\\tau=\\gamma d/dt$ give $d(\\gamma L)/d\\tau=\\gamma^4Lq\\prime q\\prime\\prime/c^2$. The derivative with respect to geometric proper length is instead $d/d\\sigma=(\\gamma/c)d/dt$. The length $\\gamma L$ is exactly the integral of the pulled-back local rest metric over the fixed-label segment; the fixed-$\\sigma$ segment need not lie in an endpoint observer's orthogonal hyperplane. The equality/constancy statements follow from $\\gamma=(1-q\\prime{}^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$.\n(d) Compare $\\mathcal L(t)$ with $\\mathcal L(0)=\\gamma(0)L$. This gives the factor and the stretch/shrink cases without an initial-rest hypothesis. Specializing $q\\prime(0)=0$ gives the original initially resting stretch conclusion. Material failure and constitutive stress require an additional model and are not deduced.\n(e) Apply the exact smooth Rindler clause of [[thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples]] and [[def-sr-proper-acceleration]]; its rigidity and unequal radial accelerations provide the stated contrast. (f) Retain all physical qualifications and the absence of empirical or material-dynamics conclusions.",
          "deps": [
