        "sr-accelerated-observers"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-sr-rindler-radar-distance-worked",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Rindler radar distances worked: reflectors at twice and at half the observer's radius",
          "statement": "Worked computation in the Rindler chart of [[pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar]]. Let $c > 0$, fix $\\rho_0 > 0$ and take $y_0 = z_0 = 0$; let $O$ be the congruence worldline $\\rho = \\rho_0$ with Rindler time $\\theta = c \\tau/\\rho_0$, and take the emission event $e_-$ at $\\theta_e = 0$, so that $e_- = x^{-1}(0, \\rho_0, 0, 0)$ in the inertial chart $x$.\n\n(a) **Reflector at $\\rho_m = 2\\rho_0$.** The future null line from $e_-$ of constant $V$ has $V = -\\rho_0$, and it meets $\\rho = 2\\rho_0$ at the Rindler time $\\theta_m = \\log 2$; using $\\sinh(\\log 2) = 3/4$ and $\\cosh(\\log 2) = 5/4$ ([[thm-hyperbolic-identities-and-derivatives]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]) the reflection event is $q_m = x^{-1}(3\\rho_0/2, 5\\rho_0/2, 0, 0)$, and the return event is $e_+ = x^{-1}(\\rho_0 \\sinh(2\\log 2), \\rho_0 \\cosh(2\\log 2), 0, 0) = x^{-1}(15\\rho_0/8, 17\\rho_0/8, 0, 0)$ at Rindler time $\\theta(e_+) = 2 \\log 2$. Thus $(\\theta_e + \\theta(e_+))/2 = \\log 2 = \\theta_m$, the radar time assigned to $q_m$ is $(\\rho_0/c) \\log 2$ plus the base constant of $O$'s proper time, and the radar distance is $\\rho_0 \\log 2$. The two worldlines $\\rho = \\rho_0$ and $\\rho = 2\\rho_0$ are at rest distance $\\rho_0$ in the slice $\\theta = \\log 2$; since $e > 2$ gives $\\log 2 < 1$ ([[cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]), the radar distance is strictly smaller than the rest distance.\n\n(b) **Reflector at $\\rho_m = \\rho_0/2$.** The future null line from $e_-$ of constant $U$ has $U = \\rho_0$, and it meets $\\rho = \\rho_0/2$ at the Rindler time $\\theta_m = \\log 2$; using $\\sinh(\\log 2) = 3/4$ and $\\cosh(\\log 2) = 5/4$ the reflection event is $q'_m = x^{-1}(3\\rho_0/8, 5\\rho_0/8, 0, 0)$ and the return event is again $e_+ = x^{-1}(15\\rho_0/8, 17\\rho_0/8, 0, 0)$ at Rindler time $2 \\log 2$. The radar time is again $(\\rho_0/c) \\log 2$ plus the base constant and the radar distance is again $\\rho_0 \\log 2$, while the rest distance in the slice $\\theta = \\log 2$ is now $\\rho_0/2$; since $e < 3 < 4$ gives $\\log 2 > 1/2$ ([[cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]), the radar distance is now strictly larger than the rest distance.\n\n(c) The two cases (a)-(b) give the same radar distance $\\rho_0\\log2$ at distinct rest distances $\\rho_0$ and $\\rho_0/2$; this demonstrates noninjectivity of the assignment as the reflector radius varies. To prove that radar distance is not determined by the rest distance alone, also take $\\rho_m=3\\rho_0/2$. With the same emission at $\\theta_e=0$, the reflection occurs at $\\theta_m=\\log(3/2)$, at $q''_m=x^{-1}(5\\rho_0/8,13\\rho_0/8,0,0)$, and the return occurs at $2\\log(3/2)$, at $e''_+=x^{-1}(65\\rho_0/72,97\\rho_0/72,0,0)$. This reflector has rest distance $\\rho_0/2$, equal to case (b), but radar distance $\\rho_0\\log(3/2)<\\rho_0\\log2$, because $3/2<2$ and $\\log$ is strictly increasing. These equal inputs and unequal outputs prove the stated failure of dependence on rest distance alone. The original two cases both satisfy $\\theta_m=(\\theta_e+\\theta(e_+))/2$ and have round-trip proper time $2\\rho_0\\log2/c$; the third case satisfies the same midpoint identity and has round-trip proper time $2\\rho_0\\log(3/2)/c$.\n\n(d) **Scope.** Conditional model computation with ideal clocks, ideal light rays and ideal reflectors in the flat classical model; no measurement, apparatus, source or statistical claim, and no empirical inference, is made, and the idealizations of [[pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar]] (v) are retained.",
          "strategy": "Substitute the two reflector radii into the exact radar computation of pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar (iii). (a) With theta_e = 0 the constant-V line through e_- carries V = -rho_0; meeting rho = 2rho_0 gives -2rho_0 e^{-theta} = -rho_0, i.e. e^{-theta} = 1/2 and theta_m = log 2; the chart coordinates of q_m are (2rho_0 sinh(log 2), 2rho_0 cosh(log 2), 0, 0) = (3rho_0/2, 5rho_0/2, 0, 0) by the exact values sinh(log 2) = 3/4, cosh(log 2) = 5/4, which follow from the defining exponentials. The return line is the constant-U line through q_m and meets rho = rho_0 at theta(e_+) = 2log 2, giving e_+ = (rho_0 sinh(2log 2), rho_0 cosh(2log 2), 0, 0) = (15rho_0/8, 17rho_0/8, 0, 0) (sinh(2log 2) = 15/8, cosh(2log 2) = 17/8). The radar time and distance follow from the protocol formula of pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar (iii) with the round-trip proper time rho_0(2log 2)/c of O; the rest distance uses the slice metric drho^2 + dy^2 + dz^2 (def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance). The inequality log 2 < 1 is the strict increase of log and 2 < e (cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three, thm-natural-logarithm-laws). (b) With theta_e = 0 the constant-U line through e_- carries U = rho_0; meeting rho = rho_0/2 gives (rho_0/2) e^{theta} = rho_0, i.e. theta_m = log 2, and the reflection event is ((rho_0/2) sinh(log 2), (rho_0/2) cosh(log 2), 0, 0) = (3rho_0/8, 5rho_0/8, 0, 0); the return line is the constant-V line through q'_m and meets rho = rho_0 again at theta = 2log 2, giving the same return event as in (a). The radar quantities are as displayed, and log 2 > 1/2 follows from log4=2log2>log e=1, since 4 > e and log is strictly increasing (cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three, thm-natural-logarithm-laws). (c) Cases (a)-(b) alone prove that distinct reflector radii and rest distances can give the same radar distance; they do not establish failure of dependence on rest distance alone. For the additional radius 3rho_0/2, constant V=-rho_0 gives theta_m=log(3/2). The defining exponentials give sinh(log(3/2))=5/12 and cosh(log(3/2))=13/12; multiplying by 3rho_0/2 gives (5rho_0/8,13rho_0/8). The return has theta=2log(3/2), for which e^theta=9/4 and e^(-theta)=4/9, hence sinh theta=65/72 and cosh theta=97/72; multiplying by rho_0 gives the stated return coordinates. The outgoing displacement from (0,rho_0) is (5rho_0/8,5rho_0/8), future null. The return displacement from q''_m is (5rho_0/18,-5rho_0/18), future null. The third reflector and the radius rho_0/2 reflector both have rest distance rho_0/2, while their radar distances are rho_0 log(3/2) and rho_0 log2, unequal by strict increase of log. This completes the full claim with all earlier examples and midpoint calculations preserved. (d) Scope only. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar",
            "def-sr-operational-radar-protocol",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
