          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Wikipedia, 'Rindler coordinates': section 'Notions of distance' (the null geodesic equation t - t_0 = log(x/x_0) and the radar distance x_0 log(1 + h/x_0) between Rindler observers at x_0 and x_0 + h, with the rider that radar distance need not be symmetric)",
                "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rindler_coordinates"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages): Section 3.5.2 and Section 7.1 pp. 61-62 and 147-148 (hyperbolic motion, accelerated coordinates and their horizons, in the convention x^0 = c t); signature (+---) converted to (-+++), c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Worked numerical instance of the conditional Rindler radar protocol for two coaxial uniformly accelerated ideal observers with ideal clocks, ideal light rays and ideal reflectors in flat classical spacetime. No apparatus, measurement, statistical or empirical claim is made, and all idealizations of pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar are retained.",
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-sr-rindler-horizon-is-not-a-physical-boundary",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Rindler causal horizon is not a boundary or singularity of spacetime",
          "statement": "In the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space with the inertial chart $x = (x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$, the null plane $H := \\{x^1 = x^0\\}$ is a boundary of the model in the following three senses: (i) no future causal curve crosses from $\\{x^1 > x^0\\}$ to $\\{x^1 < x^0\\}$; (ii) $H$ separates all causal relations, so no event of $\\{x^1 > x^0\\}$ is causally related to any event of $\\{x^1 < x^0\\}$; and (iii) the metric degenerates on $H$, so that $H$ is a singular boundary of the spacetime on which the model fails.\n\nHere $H$ is the full causal-past boundary of the accelerated congruence; only its half-plane $\\{x^1 = x^0 \\ge 0\\}$ belongs to the boundary of the right wedge $W = \\{x^1 > |x^0|\\}$.",
          "strategy": "Explicit witnesses checked against the coordinate criterion of lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion. (a) The affine segment y has constant derivative with coordinates (4,1,0,0); eta applied to it is -16+1=-15 < 0 and its time component is positive, so y is a future timelike curve by the definition of causal classification and the future cone; the coordinate function x^0 - x^1 along y is affine with values -1 and 2 at the endpoints, so it vanishes inside [0,1] and the crossing event y(1/3)=x^{-1}(1/3,1/3,0,0) lies on H. This is a direct refutation of the first two clauses of the quoted statement. (b) The displayed displacements (1,0,0,0) and (1,1/2,0,0) are future timelike, with norms -1 and -3/4 respectively by the coordinate criterion ((c) of the cited lemma), and the initial and final events straddle H while the middle event lies on it; r=(0,1,0,0) is explicitly off H. (c) Nondegeneracy and constancy of the metric matrix are read from the inertial chart (def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart, def-sr-affine-minkowski-model); the domain property of Phi is clause (b) of thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples. The crossing event is at y(1/3), not the falsely identified midpoint. The inertial metric is nondegenerate at every event; restricting a coordinate chart to the wedge changes neither it nor the model domain. Distinguish the two half-plane portions of the wedge boundary from the full horizon planes, as in the repaired Rindler theorem. (d) The worldline z is affine with constant future timelike tangent (c,0,0,0), so it is a future timelike worldline defined for all t (def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline), and the sign of x^0 - x^1 = c t changes at t = 0. (e) Scope only. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space",
            "lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion",
            "thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
