              {
                "title": "M. Blau, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (University of Bern, last update October 5, 2026, 1052 PDF pages): Section 1.3 items 2-3 pp. 38-40 (the Rindler metric is ill-defined only at rho = 0 as a coordinate singularity; an inertial observer crosses eta = +infinity at finite proper time and Minkowski space continues beyond the wedge)",
                "url": "http://www.blau.itp.unibe.ch/newlecturesGR.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Counterexample within the flat classical model: the null coordinate boundary of the Rindler chart is shown to be a coordinate-map boundary rather than a boundary or singularity of the spacetime, and the only horizon statement concerning it is the causal restriction of reachability of particular congruence worldlines. No physical interpretation of the witnesses beyond the model's causal reachability is asserted, and no measurement, statistical or empirical claim is made.",
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-sr-rindler-radar-distance-is-not-symmetric",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Rindler radar distance between two coaxial observers is not symmetric under interchange",
          "statement": "Statement refuted: 'For every pair of future timelike worldlines y and y' of the model for which the radar protocol of def-sr-operational-radar-protocol applies with y as observer and y' as reflector, the radar distance assigned by y to the reflection event on y' equals the radar distance assigned by y' to the reflection event on y; that is, the radar distance between two observers is symmetric under interchange of observer and reflector.' Counterexample in the Rindler chart of pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar with y = O the worldline rho = rho_0 and y' = R the worldline rho = 2rho_0 (coaxial, with the same y = z = 0 coordinates), rho_0 > 0, c > 0. (a) Radar distance from O to R. Emit from O at the Rindler time theta_e = 0, i.e. from e_- = x^{-1}(0, rho_0, 0, 0). By pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar (iii) the reflection event on R is q_m = x^{-1}(3rho_0/2, 5rho_0/2, 0, 0) at Rindler time log 2 and the reception event on O is e_+ = x^{-1}(15rho_0/8, 17rho_0/8, 0, 0) at Rindler time 2 log 2; the round trip takes 2rho_0 log 2/c of O's proper time, so the radar distance assigned by O is D_1 := rho_0 log 2. (b) Radar distance from R to O. Emit from R at theta = 0, i.e. from r_- = x^{-1}(0, 2rho_0, 0, 0). The future null line from r_- towards O is the constant-U line with U = 2rho_0; it meets rho = rho_0 at the Rindler time log 2, at the reflection event q'_m = x^{-1}(3rho_0/4, 5rho_0/4, 0, 0), and the return line reaches R at the Rindler time 2 log 2, at the event x^{-1}(15rho_0/4, 17rho_0/4, 0, 0). The round trip takes 4rho_0 log 2/c of R's proper time, so the radar distance assigned by R is D_2 := 2rho_0 log 2. (c) Hence D_2/D_1 = 2 = rho_m/rho_0: the two radar distances between the same pair of worldlines differ, and the assignment is not symmetric. Both assignments are finite and both reflection events lie in the causal future of their respective emission events; the asymmetry is a property of the radar assignment with the two different observers, not a physical anisotropy. (d) Scope. Conditional model computation with ideal clocks, ideal light rays and ideal reflectors; the symmetric half-split convention of def-sr-operational-radar-protocol is retained, and no measurement, statistical or empirical claim is made.",
          "strategy": "Recompute the radar protocol of pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar (iii) with the roles of the two worldlines interchanged. (a) Recompute directly from the theorem and chart: from (0,rho_0), the constant-V=-rho_0 ray meets rho=2rho_0 at theta=log2, giving q_m=(3rho_0/2,5rho_0/2); its constant-U return meets rho=rho_0 at theta=2log2, giving (15rho_0/8,17rho_0/8). Both displacements are future null. This uses no B-page example as a supplier. (b) The future null line from r_- towards smaller rho is the constant-U line U = 2rho_0, i.e. rho = 2rho_0 e^{-theta}; it meets rho = rho_0 at theta = log 2, and the chart coordinates there are (rho_0 sinh(log 2), rho_0 cosh(log 2), 0, 0) = (3rho_0/4, 5rho_0/4, 0, 0) by sinh(log 2) = 3/4 and cosh(log 2) = 5/4 (thm-hyperbolic-identities-and-derivatives, thm-natural-logarithm-laws). The return line is the constant-V line through q'_m and meets rho = 2rho_0 at theta = 2log 2, with coordinates (2rho_0 sinh(2log 2), 2rho_0 cosh(2log 2), 0, 0) = (15rho_0/4, 17rho_0/4, 0, 0). The proper-time conversion factor on the worldline rho = const is rho/c per unit Rindler time by the proper-time formula in pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar (iii), giving the round-trip proper times 2rho_0 log 2/c and 4rho_0 log 2/c respectively and the radar distances D_1 = rho_0 log 2 and D_2 = 2rho_0 log 2. (c) Since log2>log1=0, D1>0 and the quotient D_2/D_1=2 is well defined. The radius-dependent proper-time conversion factor, not any physical anisotropy, causes the two observer assignments to differ. (d) Scope only. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-sr-rindler-horizons-and-radar",
