        {
          "id": "ex-sr-guided-ring-sagnac-worked",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Guided-ring Sagnac delay worked at angular velocity Omega R = 3c/5",
          "statement": "Worked instance of pthm-sr-guided-ring-sagnac-delay. Let R > 0 and Omega = 3c/(5R), so that Omega R = 3c/5, |Omega|R < c and gamma = (1 - 9/25)^{-1/2} = 5/4. Work in the inertial chart with x^0 = c t and let the ring have its plane x^3 = 0 and let the emission event be E = x^{-1}(0, R, 0, 0), where the material point labelled varphi = 0 has phi_0 = 0. (a) Return times. The co-rotating guided signal has phi_+(t) = c t/R and the emitting material point has phi(t) = Omega t = 3c t/(5R); the signal meets that point when c t/R = 3c t/(5R) + 2 pi, i.e. t_1 = 5 pi R/c (the co-rotating relative angular rate is 2c/(5R)); the counter-rotating signal C_-, phi_-(t) = -c t/R, meets it when -c t/R = 3c t/(5R) - 2 pi, i.e. t_2 = 5 pi R/(4c), with signed relative angular rate -8c/(5R) and magnitude 8c/(5R). The reception events are e_1 = x^{-1}(5 pi R, R cos(3 pi), R sin(3 pi), 0) = x^{-1}(5 pi R, -R, 0, 0) and e_2 = x^{-1}(5 pi R/4, R cos(3 pi/4), R sin(3 pi/4), 0) = x^{-1}(5 pi R/4, -R/sqrt(2), R/sqrt(2), 0), where the angle values use the parity, addition and Pythagorean identities for sine and cosine (def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero, thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas, cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity). (b) Differences and the factor-2 holonomy check. The chart-time difference of the two reunions is t_1 - t_2 = 5 pi R/c - 5 pi R/(4c) = 15 pi R/(4c) = 4 pi Omega R^2/(c^2 - Omega^2R^2), and the detector proper-time difference is (t_1-t_2)·(4/5) = 3 pi R/c = 4 pi Omega R^2/(c^2 sqrt(1 - Omega^2R^2/c^2)). The ring rest-line chart-time holonomy of pthm-sr-guided-ring-sagnac-delay (iv) is Delta t_h = 15 pi R/(8c), exactly half of t_1 - t_2, and the corresponding receiver proper-time holonomy is (4/5)·15 pi R/(8c) = 3 pi R/(2c), exactly half of 3 pi R/c; this verifies the factor-2 relation of that clause. The ambient rest distribution of the rotating congruence is nowhere integrable while the fixed-height-cylinder-restricted one-dimensional rest distribution is locally integrable with the nonzero global holonomy 15 pi R/(8c) (thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples (c)). (c) Inertial limit. For Omega = 0 with the same R the two return times are both 2 pi R/c and both differences vanish. (d) Scope. Conditional model computation with the adopted ideal circular guide, ideal signals and the ideal clock detector of pthm-sr-guided-ring-sagnac-delay (ii); no interference phase, apparatus, medium, measurement, statistical or empirical claim is made, and the idealizations of that theorem are retained.",
          "strategy": "Substitute Omega = 3c/(5R) into the exact formulas of pthm-sr-guided-ring-sagnac-delay. (a) The angle of the material point is phi(t) = Omega t = 3c t/(5R); the co-rotating guided signal has angle c t/R, so the relative angular rate is 2c/(5R) and the reunion condition is c t/R = 3c t/(5R) + 2 pi k, with smallest positive solution k = 1, i.e. t_1 = 2 pi R/(c - 3c/5) = 5 pi R/c; the counter-rotating signal has angle -c t/R, so the signed relative angular rate is -8c/(5R) and the condition is -c t/R = 3c t/(5R) - 2 pi k, i.e. t_2 = 2 pi R/(c + 3c/5) = 5 pi R/(4c). The reception events are obtained by substituting these times into the parametrisation of pthm-sr-guided-ring-sagnac-delay (i), with the elementary sine and cosine values cos(3 pi) = -1, sin(3 pi) = 0 and cos(3 pi/4) = -sin(3 pi/4) = -1/sqrt(2) supplied by the parity, addition, quarter-turn, positive-sine and Pythagorean identities: sin(pi/4)=cos(pi/4)>0 by the half-pi shift and positive-sine result, and each equals 1/sqrt(2) by Pythagoras; subsequent shifts give the displayed values. (b) The differences are the substitutions into 4 pi Omega R^2/(c^2 - Omega^2R^2) = 4 pi (3c/(5R))R^2/(c^2(1-9/25)) = 15 pi R/(4c) and into (t_1-t_2)/gamma with 1/gamma = 4/5; the holonomy Delta t_h = 2 pi Omega R^2/(c^2 - Omega^2R^2) evaluates to 2 pi (3c/(5R))R^2/(c^2(16/25)) = 15 pi R/(8c), half of 15 pi R/(4c), and its proper-time counterpart is (4/5)·15 pi R/(8c) = 3 pi R/(2c), half of 3 pi R/c. The nonintegrability statements are those of thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples (c). (c) is the Omega = 0 substitution. (d) Scope only. No choice principle is used.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-sr-guided-ring-sagnac-delay",
