      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Newtonian comparison momentum and kinetic energy in a named inertial frame",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR11 reservation (\"pthm specified p/E correspondence\"); scaffold/mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08",
          "design_state": "definition introducing the comparison functionals the specified p/E correspondence is stated against; no approximation is asserted here",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-interval",
            "def-integer-power",
            "def-linear-map"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integer-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Formulation definition inside the flat special-relativity framework: the Newtonian comparison momentum and kinetic energy are built from a named inertial frame, its spatial Euclidean structure, a positive mass parameter and a coordinate velocity, in the SI conventions of the framework. The definition asserts no approximation, correspondence or error bound, no Newtonian theory, no force or matter law, and no empirical content; the correspondence claim is pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence.",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski model ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), let $c>0$ with the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial frame with inertial chart $x=(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ and spatial Euclidean structure $(S,\\delta,|\\cdot|)$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]], [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]), and let $m>0$.\n\n(a) For $w\\in S$ define the *Newtonian comparison momentum* and the *Newtonian comparison kinetic energy* of the pair $(m,w)$ by $$N_p(m,w):=mw,\\qquad N_K(m,w):=\\tfrac12\\,m\\,|w|^2,$$ where $mw$ is the scalar multiple of $w$ by the real number $m$ ([[def-linear-map]]) and $|w|^2=\\delta(w,w)$ ([[def-integer-power]]). With the SI conventions these carry the units kilogram metre per second and joule respectively.\n\n(b) Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval ([[def-interval]]) and let $y:I\\to A$ have inertial coordinate path $t\\mapsto(ct,r(t))$; writing $r(t)=r^1(t)e_1+r^2(t)e_2+r^3(t)e_3$ with real functions $r^1,r^2,r^3$, at each parameter value $t$ where all three coordinate derivatives exist, define the *coordinate velocity* $w(t)\\in S$ of the path by $$w(t):=(r^1)'(t)\\,e_1+(r^2)'(t)\\,e_2+(r^3)'(t)\\,e_3,$$ the componentwise derivative of [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]. For a path differentiable at every parameter value these are functions on $I$; otherwise they are defined on its differentiability set. The comparison functionals along the path are $t\\mapsto N_p(m,w(t))$ and $t\\mapsto N_K(m,w(t))$.\n\n(c) The functionals $N_p$ and $N_K$ are comparison expressions built from a named inertial frame, the mass parameter $m$ and the path's coordinate velocity. They are not defined by, and do not presuppose, a separately developed Newtonian theory, and this definition asserts no approximation, correspondence, error bound or physical claim; those are the content of [[pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence]].",
          "strategy": "A definition item. Well-definedness: clause (a) applies the scalar multiple of a vector of the real vector space $V$ (restricted to $S\\subseteq V$) and the square of the norm $|\\cdot|$ supplied by the frame's spatial structure, both total on $S$; clause (b) uses the componentwise derivative of a function $I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and the unique expansion of a vector of $S$ in the ordered basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]. No division occurs and no choice is used. The definition deliberately introduces no physical correspondence: it only names the two expressions that [[pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence]] compares with the model's measured quantities, and the units are the ones fixed by [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. The derivative symbols in(b) are used only at points where the coordinate derivatives exist; no velocity is assigned to an arbitrary nondifferentiable path. Physical correspondence consumers retain their stated differentiable future-timelike worldline hypotheses.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Section 4.2 pp. 82-86 (relativistic kinetic energy; Example 5 p. 86, the Newtonian prediction eV = (1/2)mv^2), Section 4.3.3 Example 8 p. 91 (the Newtonian limit v = p/m), Section 4.3.4 pp. 95-96 (p = m gamma v; the momentum of a material particle approaches infinity as its speed approaches c) and problems 11-12 p. 117 (expand K = m(gamma-1) and p = m gamma v in Taylor series; show the first terms are the nonrelativistic expressions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The uniform second-order Taylor remainder of $z\\mapsto(1-z)^{-1/2}$ on $[0,z_0]$",
          "reference": "scaffold/mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08 (\"Taylor with bounded second derivative\"; constants depend on z_0); audit-expansion-report.md M08 branch",
          "design_state": "complete elementary argument from the published Lagrange-remainder supplier with an explicit uniform constant",
          "deps": [
            "cor-taylor-remainder-bound",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
            "thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent",
            "def-rational-power",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "lem-derivative-of-a-power",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "lem-of-inverse-positive",
            "def-abs-value",
            "lem-of-abs-value"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "cor-taylor-remainder-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-derivative-of-a-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-inverse-positive": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-abs-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-abs-value": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Let $z_0\\in[0,1)$ and define $f:(-\\infty,1)\\to\\mathbb R$ by $f(z):=(1-z)^{-1/2}=\\bigl((1-z)^{1/2}\\bigr)^{-1}$, the reciprocal of the nonnegative square root, with rational powers as in [[def-rational-power]]; put $K(z_0):=\\tfrac38(1-z_0)^{-5/2}$.\n\n(a) $f$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty,1)$ with $f'(z)=\\tfrac12(1-z)^{-3/2}$, and twice differentiable there with $f''(z)=\\tfrac34(1-z)^{-5/2}$; in particular $|f''(z)|\\le\\tfrac34(1-z_0)^{-5/2}$ for every $z\\in[0,z_0]$.\n\n(b) For every $z\\in[0,z_0]$ there is a real $R(z)$ with $$f(z)=1+\\tfrac z2+R(z),\\qquad |R(z)|\\le K(z_0)\\,z^2,$$ so $|f(z)-1|\\le\\tfrac z2+K(z_0)z^2$ for $z\\in[0,z_0]$. Consequently, for every $\\eta>0$, $$0\\le z\\le\\min\\!\\left\\{z_0,\\frac{\\eta}{\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0}\\right\\}\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ |f(z)-1|\\le\\eta ;$$ the display is the quantitative form of $f(z)\\to1$ as $z\\to0^+$ used by [[lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts]].\n\nThe lemma is a statement of real analysis about the single function $f$; it attaches no physical meaning to $z$ and makes no claim about a Lorentz factor or a physical velocity.",
          "strategy": "(a) Write $f(z)=g(1-z)^{-1}$ with $g(y):=y^{1/2}$ for $y>0$, or directly apply the power rule: by [[thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent]] the rational powers of [[def-rational-power]] agree on rational exponents with the real powers differentiated in [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], whose second clause gives $(x^{\\alpha})'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}$ on $(0,\\infty)$; the affine map $z\\mapsto 1-z$ has derivative the constant $-1$ (identity and sum rules, [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]]), so the chain rule [[thm-chain-rule]] applied to $z\\mapsto(1-z)^{-1/2}=y^{\\alpha}\\circ(1-z)$ with $\\alpha=-1/2$ gives $f'(z)=-\\tfrac12(1-z)^{-3/2}\\cdot(-1)=\\tfrac12(1-z)^{-3/2}$, and repeating with $\\alpha=-3/2$ gives $f''(z)=\\tfrac34(1-z)^{-5/2}$; the identities $(1-z)^{\\alpha}(1-z)^{-\\alpha}=1$ and $a^{-r}=(a^r)^{-1}$ are [[lem-rational-power-laws]]. For the bound: from $0\\le z\\le z_0$ we have $0<1-z_0\\le1-z$, so positive-base $5/2$-power monotonicity in the base of [[lem-rational-power-monotone]] with $r=5/2$ gives $(1-z_0)^{5/2}\\le(1-z)^{5/2}$ (equality exactly when $z=z_0$, by the strict part otherwise), and [[lem-of-inverse-positive]] gives $(1-z)^{-5/2}\\le(1-z_0)^{-5/2}$; multiplying by $\\tfrac34$ and reading $|f''|=f''$ (the strict positivity of the power, [[lem-rational-power-laws]] clause 1) gives the claim.\n\n(b) Put $n=1$, $a=0$, $x=z\\in[0,z_0]$ in [[cor-taylor-remainder-bound]]: $f$ has derivatives through order $2$ on the closed interval between $0$ and $z$ with endpoint continuity (indeed it is $C^{\\infty}$ on the open neighbourhood $(-\\infty,1)$ of that interval), and $|f''(t)|\\le\\tfrac34(1-z_0)^{-5/2}=:M$ there by (a); hence $|R_{1,0}f(z)|\\le M z^2/2!=K(z_0)z^2$ for $R(z):=R_{1,0}f(z)=f(z)-f(0)-f'(0)z=f(z)-1-\\tfrac z2$. The final display follows from $|f(z)-1|\\le z(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z)\\le z(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0)$ for $z\\le z_0$ and the order arithmetic of $\\mathbb R$ with the absolute-value properties of [[lem-of-abs-value]] ([[def-abs-value]]). The final implication explicitly retains z<=z0; its bound is not asserted beyond the original Taylor interval. At z0=0 it applies only to z=0, while the one-sided convergence statement may use any fixed positive z0<1.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis I: Introduction to Real Analysis, Volume I, Section 5.4 \"Taylor's theorem\" (Taylor's theorem with the Lagrange remainder and its error estimate)",
                "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/realanal.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 18.100B Real Analysis, Spring 2025 full lecture notes (Taylor's theorem, the Lagrange remainder and the differentiation rules)",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/resources/lecture-notes/"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Uniform low-speed expansion of the observer energy, momentum and kinetic energy of a massive four-momentum",
          "reference": "scaffold/mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08 (restricted algebra branch) and sr-supplier-proofs.md#S7 (mass shell)",
          "design_state": "complete transport of the Taylor remainder through the mass-shell identities with uniform constants depending only on the speed bound",
          "deps": [
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-rational-power",
            "def-integer-power",
            "def-abs-value",
            "lem-of-abs-value"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integer-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-abs-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-abs-value": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 14,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski model ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), $c>0$, let $n\\in V$ be future unit with $\\eta(n,n)=-1$, and let $P=mU$ with $m>0$ and $U\\in V$ future timelike, $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $E_n(P)$, $q_n(P)$, $T_n(P)$ and $m(P)$ be the observer energy, spatial momentum, kinetic energy and invariant mass of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]], put $h(v):=v+\\eta(n,v)n$ as in [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]](c), and let $\\gamma:=-\\eta(U/c,n)\\ge1$ and $w:=h(U)/\\gamma=c\\,h(U/c)/\\gamma\\in n^\\perp$ be the Lorentz factor and relative velocity of [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(d); then $u:=U/c$ satisfies $u=\\gamma(n+w/c)$, $|w|^2=c^2(1-\\gamma^{-2})$ and $\\gamma=(1-|w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, and [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b) gives $E_n(P)=mc^2\\gamma$, $q_n(P)=m\\gamma w$, $T_n(P)=mc^2(\\gamma-1)$ and $m(P)=m$.\n\nLet $z_0\\in[0,1)$, suppose $z:=|w|^2/c^2\\le z_0$, put $K(z_0):=\\tfrac38(1-z_0)^{-5/2}$, and let $f,R$ be as in [[lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder]], so that $\\gamma=f(z)$ and $\\gamma=1+\\tfrac z2+R(z)$ with $|R(z)|\\le K(z_0)z^2$. Then:\n\n(a) $E_n(P)=mc^2+\\tfrac12m|w|^2+R_E$ and $T_n(P)=\\tfrac12m|w|^2+R_E$ for the common real number $R_E:=mc^2R(z)$, and $$|R_E|\\le K(z_0)\\,z\\,m|w|^2=K(z_0)\\,\\frac{m|w|^4}{c^2},\\qquad \\Bigl|T_n(P)-\\tfrac12m|w|^2\\Bigr|\\le 2K(z_0)\\,z\\,\\tfrac12m|w|^2 .$$\n\n(b) $q_n(P)=mw+R_q$ with $R_q:=m(\\gamma-1)w$, and $$|R_q|\\le m|w|\\Bigl(\\frac z2+K(z_0)z^2\\Bigr)\\le\\Bigl(\\frac12+K(z_0)z_0\\Bigr)z\\,m|w| ;$$ moreover $|q_n(P)|=mc\\sqrt{\\gamma^2-1}=m\\gamma|w|$ and $m(P)=m$.\n\n(c) Every constant above depends only on $z_0$, so the estimates are uniform over all future unit $n$, all $m>0$ and all $P=mU$ with $|w|^2\\le z_0c^2$; if $z=0$ (equivalently $w=0$, $U=cn$) then $R_E=R_q=0$, $E_n(P)=mc^2$, $q_n(P)=0$ and $T_n(P)=0$. The lemma is a statement about the mathematical model only; it asserts no measured quantity, no approximation of a physical theory and no error estimate for any experiment.",
          "strategy": "First use the linearity of h in thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition(c): h(U)=c h(U/c), so w=h(U)/gamma=c h(u)/gamma for u=U/c. The future-unit u has gamma=−eta(n,u), and the actual supplier(d) then gives u=gamma(n+w/c), |w|²=c²(1−gamma^−2) and gamma=f(z). No extra factor c multiplies h(U)/gamma. Combine [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b), [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(d) and [[lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder]]. Since $z=|w|^2/c^2$, clause (d) of the theorem gives $\\gamma=(1-|w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}=(1-z)^{-1/2}=f(z)$, hence $\\gamma-1=\\tfrac z2+R(z)$ with $|R(z)|\\le K(z_0)z^2$.\n\n(a) Multiply by $mc^2$: $E_n(P)=mc^2\\gamma=mc^2+\\tfrac12m|w|^2+mc^2R(z)$, because $mc^2\\cdot\\tfrac z2=\\tfrac12mc^2z=\\tfrac12m|w|^2$; and $T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ by [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]\\,(b), so the same remainder occurs. The bound $|mc^2R(z)|\\le mc^2K(z_0)z^2=K(z_0)zm|w|^2$ gives the first display, and $2K(z_0)z\\cdot\\tfrac12m|w|^2=K(z_0)zm|w|^2$ gives the second.\n\n(b) $q_n(P)=m\\gamma w=mw+m(\\gamma-1)w$, and $|m(\\gamma-1)w|=m|\\gamma-1|\\,|w|$ by the product rule for absolute values ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-abs-value]]); substituting $\\gamma-1=\\tfrac z2+R(z)$ and $|R(z)|\\le K(z_0)z^2$ gives $|R_q|\\le m|w|(\\tfrac z2+K(z_0)z^2)\\le m|w|z(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0)$ because $z\\le z_0$ and $m|w|,K(z_0)\\ge0$; the norm identity is clause (b) of the batch-8 lemma and $m(P)=m$ is [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]\\,(b). The case $z=0$ is the vanishing of the remainders at the origin, $f(0)=1$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Section 4.2 pp. 82-86 (relativistic kinetic energy; Example 5 p. 86, the Newtonian prediction eV = (1/2)mv^2), Section 4.3.3 Example 8 p. 91 (the Newtonian limit v = p/m), Section 4.3.4 pp. 95-96 (p = m gamma v; the momentum of a material particle approaches infinity as its speed approaches c) and problems 11-12 p. 117 (expand K = m(gamma-1) and p = m gamma v in Taylor series; show the first terms are the nonrelativistic expressions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III lecture notes, 2019), Section 1.2.2 pp. 14-16 (relativistic free-particle action and the Lorentz factor gamma = (1-|xdot|^2/c^2)^(-1/2)) and Section 1.2.3 pp. 16-19, in particular the Taylor expansion (1.22) on printed p. 17 (-mc^2 + (m/2)|xdot|^2 - m Phi(x) + ... obtained by expanding the square root) (author-hosted copy of the official Cambridge PDF)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-sr-galilean-limiting-charts",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Galilean limiting chart maps and bounded chart regions",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR11 reservation (\"explicit Galilean limiting chart maps with bounded domains\")",
          "design_state": "definition of the family of affine chart maps whose bounded-region limit is proved in lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-lorentz-boost",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-rational-power"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-boost": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski model and let $c>0$ be a fixed mathematical speed scale, with the coordinate convention $x^0=ct$, let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial frame with inertial chart $x$ and spatial Euclidean structure $(S,\\delta,|\\cdot|)$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]], [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]), and write the points of $\\mathbb R\\times S$ as $(t,x)$.\n\n(a) *Lorentz boost charts with a variable speed scale.* For $v\\in S$ and a real $C>|v|$ put $\\gamma_C(v):=(1-|v|^2/C^2)^{-1/2}$ ([[def-rational-power]]) and define the affine map $\\Phi_{v,C}:\\mathbb R\\times S\\to\\mathbb R\\times S$ by $$\\Phi_{v,C}(t,x):=\\Bigl(\\gamma_C(v)\\bigl(t-\\tfrac{\\delta(v,x)}{C^2}\\bigr),\\ \\ x+\\tfrac{\\gamma_C(v)-1}{|v|^2}\\delta(v,x)\\,v-\\gamma_C(v)\\,t\\,v\\Bigr)\\quad(v\\ne0),$$ with $\\Phi_{0,C}:=\\mathrm{id}$; when $|v|<c$, at $C=c$ this is the coordinate expression, in the convention $x^0=ct$, of the passive Lorentz boost $B(v/c)$ of [[def-lorentz-boost]]: if an event has coordinates $(ct,x)$ in the unprimed inertial chart of the frame, then $\\Phi_{v,c}(t,x)$ are its coordinates $(x'^0/c,\\,x'^1,x'^2,x'^3)$ in the boosted inertial chart with parameter $v/c$.\n\n(b) *Galilean boost.* For $v\\in S$ define the affine map $G_v:\\mathbb R\\times S\\to\\mathbb R\\times S$ by $G_v(t,x):=(t,\\,x-tv)$.\n\n(c) *Bounded chart region.* For reals $T,R>0$ put $D_{T,R}:=\\{(t,x)\\in\\mathbb R\\times S:|t|\\le T,\\ |x|\\le R\\}$, with $|\\cdot|$ the norm of the named frame.\n\n(d) *Scope of the family.* The parameter $C$ is a variable speed scale inside a family of coordinate expressions; it is not a change of the model's adopted constant $c$, and the members with $C\\ne c$ are auxiliary mathematical maps used only to state the limit of [[lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts]]. Nothing here asserts a physical limit of the framework, and no limiting trajectory, force or interaction is defined.",
          "strategy": "A definition item. Well-definedness: for $v\\ne0$, $C>|v|$ the number $\\gamma_C(v)$ is a rational power of the positive base $1-|v|^2/C^2$ ([[def-rational-power]]), and the displayed formulas assign to each pair $(t,x)$ a pair of elements of $\\mathbb R\\times S$ using the bilinear form $\\delta$ and scalar multiplication in $S$; for $v=0$ the middle term is $0/0$ and the map is declared separately to be the identity (the same convention as [[def-lorentz-boost]]). The identification at $C=c$ is made only when $|v|<c$, so this member belongs to the family; it is a substitution: writing $\\beta:=v/c$ and $x^0:=ct$ in the definition of $B(\\beta)$ of [[def-lorentz-boost]] gives $x'^0=\\gamma(x^0-\\beta\\cdot x)$ and $x'=x+((\\gamma-1)/|\\beta|^2)(\\beta\\cdot x)\\beta-\\gamma\\beta x^0$, that is $x'^0/c=\\gamma_C(v)(t-\\delta(v,x)/C^2)$ and the displayed spatial formula, because $\\beta\\cdot x=\\delta(v,x)/c$, $|\\beta|^2=|v|^2/c^2$ and $\\beta x^0=(v/c)(ct)=vt$. The definition proves no uniform limit; the purely mathematical bounded-region limit is proved later. It assigns no physical interpretation to the auxiliary family, which varies C without changing the fixed model parameter c.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Section 4.2 pp. 82-86 (relativistic kinetic energy; Example 5 p. 86, the Newtonian prediction eV = (1/2)mv^2), Section 4.3.3 Example 8 p. 91 (the Newtonian limit v = p/m), Section 4.3.4 pp. 95-96 (p = m gamma v; the momentum of a material particle approaches infinity as its speed approaches c) and problems 11-12 p. 117 (expand K = m(gamma-1) and p = m gamma v in Taylor series; show the first terms are the nonrelativistic expressions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Uniform Galilean limit of the boost charts on bounded regions, and its failure on unbounded regions",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR11 reservation (\"explicit Galilean limiting chart maps with bounded domains\"); prose-scaffold.md SR11 row (Carroll slow-motion discussion pp. 105-106)",
          "design_state": "complete explicit bound-and-limit argument on the bounded chart region from the pair's own definitions and the uniform Taylor remainder",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-galilean-limiting-charts",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite",
            "lem-of-triangle-inequality",
            "def-abs-value",
            "lem-of-abs-value",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-rational-power",
            "cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "lem-of-inverse-positive",
            "thm-of-square-roots"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-galilean-limiting-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-triangle-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-abs-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-abs-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-inverse-positive": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Let $v\\in S$, $T,R>0$, and let $(\\Phi_{v,C})_{C>|v|}$, $G_v$ and $D_{T,R}$ be as in [[def-sr-galilean-limiting-charts]]. For $C>|v|$ write $\\gamma_C:=(1-|v|^2/C^2)^{-1/2}$ and $z:=|v|^2/C^2$, and for $(t,x)\\in\\mathbb R\\times S$ write $\\Phi_{v,C}(t,x)=(t',x')$.\n\n(a) *Exact differences.* For every $C>|v|$ and all $(t,x)$,\n$$t'-t=(\\gamma_C-1)t-\\gamma_C\\frac{\\delta(v,x)}{C^2},\\qquad x'-(x-tv)=(\\gamma_C-1)(x_\\parallel-tv),$$ where $x_\\parallel:=\\delta(v,x)v/|v|^2$ for $v\\ne0$; for $v=0$ one has $\\Phi_{0,C}=\\mathrm{id}$ and both differences vanish.\n\n(b) *Uniform bounds on bounded regions.* For every $v\\ne0$, every $C>|v|$ and every $(t,x)\\in D_{T,R}$,\n$$|t'-t|\\le(\\gamma_C-1)T+\\gamma_C\\frac{|v|R}{C^2},\\qquad |x'-(x-tv)|\\le(\\gamma_C-1)(R+|v|T).$$\n\n(c) *Quantitative rate and uniform convergence to the Galilean chart map.* Fix $z_0\\in(0,1)$ and set $K_0=\\tfrac38(1-z_0)^{-5/2}$, $A_0=\\tfrac12+K_0z_0$ and $\\Gamma_0=(1-z_0)^{-1/2}$. Whenever $v\\ne0$ and $C\\ge |v|/\\sqrt{z_0}$, the following hold uniformly on $D_{T,R}$: $$|t'-t|\\le\\frac{A_0|v|^2T+\\Gamma_0|v|R}{C^2},\\qquad |x'-(x-tv)|\\le\\frac{A_0|v|^2(R+|v|T)}{C^2}.$$ Thus the rate is $O(C^{-2})$ with explicit constants for fixed $v,T,R,z_0$. For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $C_\\varepsilon>|v|$ such that for every $C\\ge C_\\varepsilon$ and every $(t,x)\\in D_{T,R}$, $$\\max\\{|t'-t|,\\,|x'-(x-tv)|\\}\\le\\varepsilon .$$ For $v\\ne0$, put $H=T+R+|v|T$, choose any $z_1$ with $0<z_1\\le\\min\\{z_0,(2A_0)^{-1},\\varepsilon/(2A_0H)\\}$ (for example the displayed minimum), and set $$C_\\varepsilon=\\max\\{|v|/\\sqrt{z_1},\\sqrt{3|v|R/\\varepsilon}\\}.$$ This is sufficient, and since $z_1<1$ it gives $C_\\varepsilon>|v|$. For $v=0$ both errors vanish identically and any $C_\\varepsilon>0$ suffices. Hence $\\sup_{D_{T,R}}\\max\\{|t'-t|,|x'-(x-tv)|\\}\\to0$ as $C\\to\\infty$ with $v,T,R$ fixed, the convergence is uniform on $D_{T,R}$, and for each fixed $(t,x)$ the image $\\Phi_{v,C}(t,x)$ converges to the Galilean value $G_v(t,x)=(t,x-tv)$.\n\n(d) *Failure on unbounded regions.* For every $v\\ne0$ and every $C>|v|$, $\\sup_{\\mathbb R\\times S}|t'-t|=+\\infty$ and $\\sup_{\\mathbb R\\times S}|x'-(x-tv)|=+\\infty$; so the boundedness of $T,R$ in (c) is essential and the limit is not uniform on the whole chart domain.",
          "strategy": "(a) Subtract the Galilean values from the definition: t'-t=(gamma_C-1)t-gamma_C delta(v,x)/C^2 and x'-(x-tv)=(gamma_C-1)(x_parallel-tv). For v=0 both maps are the identity and both differences vanish.\n\n(b) Scalar absolute-value triangle inequality gives |t'-t|<=(gamma_C-1)|t|+gamma_C|delta(v,x)|/C^2. In a delta-orthonormal basis, thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite gives |delta(v,x)|<=|v||x|. The spatial inner-product norm's homogeneity and triangle inequality are cor-triangle-inequality-for-inner-product-norm: |x_parallel|=|delta(v,x)|/|v|<=|x|, hence |x_parallel-tv|<=|x_parallel|+|-tv|<=|x|+|t||v|. The absolute value of t is essential for negative chart times. Since gamma_C>=1, |t|<=T and |x|<=R, substitution proves both bounded-region displays.\n\n(c) Fix z0 in (0,1). For 0<=z=|v|^2/C^2<=z0, the Taylor supplier's first remainder bound gives 0<=gamma_C-1<=z/2+K0 z^2<=A0 z. Positive-base rational-power monotonicity and reciprocal order give gamma_C<=(1-z0)^(-1/2)=Gamma0. Substitute into (b) to obtain both explicit C^-2 bounds. For epsilon>0, set H=T+R+|v|T>0 and choose 0<z1<=min{z0,1/(2A0),epsilon/(2A0H)}; each entry of the minimum is positive. For C>=|v|/sqrt(z1), z<=z1, so gamma_C-1<=A0 z1<=1/2 and (gamma_C-1)H<=epsilon/2. Thus gamma_C<=3/2. If also C>=sqrt(3|v|R/epsilon), the additional time term gamma_C|v|R/C^2<=epsilon/2, so both error bounds from (b) are <=epsilon on the whole bounded region. Because z1<=z0<1 and |v|>0, the first threshold is strictly larger than |v|; the inadmissible boost boundary C=|v| is excluded. For v=0 handle the identity family separately with any positive threshold. Supremum over the bounded region gives uniform convergence; each fixed point lies in such a region, giving pointwise convergence.\n\n(d) For v nonzero and fixed C>|v|, gamma_C>1. At (t,x)=(0,lambda v/|v|), lambda>0, the spatial error norm is (gamma_C-1)lambda, unbounded as lambda increases. At (t,x)=(0,-mu v), mu>0, the time error is gamma_C mu |v|^2/C^2, unbounded as mu increases. Thus both whole-domain suprema are infinite. This is a coordinate-family limit only; it changes no physical postulate, adopted constant c, trajectory, force or interaction. No omitted expression or qualitative smallness choice replaces a quantitative step.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III lecture notes, 2019), Section 1.2.2 pp. 14-16 (relativistic free-particle action and the Lorentz factor gamma = (1-|xdot|^2/c^2)^(-1/2)) and Section 1.2.3 pp. 16-19, in particular the Taylor expansion (1.22) on printed p. 17 (-mc^2 + (m/2)|xdot|^2 - m Phi(x) + ... obtained by expanding the square root) (author-hosted copy of the official Cambridge PDF)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Specified low-speed momentum-energy correspondence between the relativistic measured quantities and the Newtonian comparison quantities",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR11 reservation (\"pthm specified p/E correspondence\"; closure \"baseline M08; Q4 controlled correspondence\"); scaffold/mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08",
          "design_state": "complete conditional deduction of the uniform correspondence from the adopted momentum/measurement postulate and the pair's uniform expansion lemma; constants depend on the speed bound",
          "deps": [
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
            "def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "lem-sr-four-momentum-in-inertial-frame",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics": "physical-assumption",
            "def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-four-momentum-in-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 16,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Flat special-relativity massive classical particles (constant mass m>0, P=mU) with the measured energy, spatial momentum and kinetic energy identified as E_n, q_n, T_n by post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics, in inertial frames and in the bounded low-speed domain |w|^2 <= z_0 c^2 with z_0<1, in the SI conventions. The error bounds are uniform on that domain and depend on z_0; nothing is asserted uniformly as |w| -> c. Excludes massless particles, variable mass, forces, interactions and interactions mechanisms, accelerated observers, gravity, quantum and statistical descriptions, and every empirical claim, measurement value or precision statement.",
          "statement": "Adopt [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]] (massive-particle momentum, the identification of measured quantities and isolated collision balance) and the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the affine Minkowski model, $c>0$, let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial frame with stationary observer $n:=e_0$, spatial Euclidean structure $(S,\\delta,|\\cdot|)$ and inertial chart $x$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]], [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]), and let a massive particle have constant mass $m>0$ and four-momentum $P=mU$ with $U$ future timelike, $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $y$ be a worldline of the particle whose inertial coordinate path is $t\\mapsto(ct,r(t))$ and whose coordinate velocity $w(t)=r'(t)\\in S$ of [[def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities]]\\,(b) equals a fixed $w$ at the instant considered; then in this frame $P=m\\gamma_w(c,w)$ with $\\gamma_w=(1-|w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$ ([[lem-sr-four-momentum-in-inertial-frame]]\\,(b), [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(e)).\n\nFix $z_0\\in[0,1)$ and suppose $z:=|w|^2/c^2\\le z_0$; put $K(z_0):=\\tfrac38(1-z_0)^{-5/2}$, and let $N_p(m,w)=mw$ and $N_K(m,w)=\\tfrac12m|w|^2$ be the Newtonian comparison quantities of [[def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities]]. With $E_n(P)$, $q_n(P)$ and $T_n(P)$ the measured energy, spatial momentum and kinetic energy identified in clause (ii) of the adopted postulate, $m(P)$ the invariant mass of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]] and $R_E,R_q$ the remainders of [[lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities]]:\n\n(a) [Energy] $E_n(P)=mc^2+N_K(m,w)+R_E$ and $E_n(P)-T_n(P)=mc^2$; $|R_E|\\le K(z_0)\\,z\\,m|w|^2$. So the measured energy of the moving particle exceeds its rest energy by the Newtonian kinetic energy up to the displayed uniform bound of order $m|w|^4/c^2$.\n\n(b) [Kinetic energy] $T_n(P)=N_K(m,w)+R_E$, hence $|T_n(P)-N_K(m,w)|\\le 2K(z_0)\\,z\\,N_K(m,w)$: for $w\\ne0$ the relative deviation from the Newtonian kinetic energy is at most $2K(z_0)z$.\n\n(c) [Momentum] $q_n(P)=N_p(m,w)+R_q$, and $|R_q|\\le\\bigl(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0\\bigr)z\\,|N_p(m,w)|$; for $w\\ne0$ the relative deviation from the Newtonian momentum is at most $(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0)z$.\n\n(d) [Momentum-to-velocity inversion] $w=q_n(P)/(m\\gamma_w)$ exactly, and $|w-q_n(P)/m|=(\\gamma_w-1)|w|\\le\\bigl(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0\\bigr)z\\,|w|$; at $z=0$ one has $w=0$, $q_n(P)=0$, $E_n(P)=mc^2$ and $T_n(P)=0$, and $m(P)=m$ always.\n\n(e) [Scope and uniformity] The constants $K(z_0)$ and $\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0$ depend on $z_0$ alone, so the estimates hold uniformly over all masses $m>0$, all states, instants and inertial frames with $|w|^2\\le z_0c^2$. The statement is a conditional correspondence between the model's adopted measured quantities and the comparison functionals of a named frame: it identifies no framework with another, asserts no convergence of trajectories, forces or interactions and no limit in which the constant $c$ varies, gives no bound uniform in $|w|<c$ (see [[rem-sr-newtonian-bridge-limits-and-failures]]) and makes no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "The deduction is the transport of [[lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities]] through the adopted identification of measured quantities. By clause (ii) of [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]] the measured energy, spatial momentum and kinetic energy of the particle are $E_n(P)$, $q_n(P)$ and $T_n(P)$, and by clause (i) the particle's four-momentum is $P=mU$ with $m>0$ constant; the SI units of the comparison functionals and of $E_n,q_n,T_n$ are those of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. The frame data identify the state's relative velocity with the coordinate velocity: clause (e) of [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] states that for the frame's stationary observer $n=e_0$ the relative velocity of clause (d) is the coordinate velocity $w$, and clause (b) of [[lem-sr-four-momentum-in-inertial-frame]] gives the components $P=m\\gamma_w(c,w)$ with $\\gamma_w=(1-|w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$.\n\nUnder the hypothesis $z=|w|^2/c^2\\le z_0$, the batch lemma gives $E_n(P)=mc^2+\\tfrac12m|w|^2+R_E$, $T_n(P)=\\tfrac12m|w|^2+R_E$ and $q_n(P)=mw+R_q$ with the displayed bounds, and $R_E,R_q$ depend on the state only through $z$, $m$, $|w|$ and the constants $K(z_0)$; clause (a) is exactly this substitution together with the definition $T_n=E_n-mc^2$ and the postulate's identification of $mc^2$ as the rest energy, clause(b) retains the absolute bound also at w=0, and divides by the positive $N_K=\\tfrac12m|w|^2$ only when w is nonzero, clause(c) divides by the positive $|N_p|=m|w|$ only when w is nonzero, and clause (d) uses $q_n=m\\gamma_ww$ so that $w-q_n/m=(1-\\gamma_w)w$, hence $|w-q_n/m|=(\\gamma_w-1)|w|$ with $|\\gamma_w-1|$ bounded as in the Taylor lemma. Clause (e) records that no step used more than $z\\le z_0$, and that no claim beyond the stated class follows: the bounds are conditional, and the failure of any speed-independent constant is exhibited in [[rem-sr-newtonian-bridge-limits-and-failures]] and on the companion example page.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Section 4.2 pp. 82-86 (relativistic kinetic energy; Example 5 p. 86, the Newtonian prediction eV = (1/2)mv^2), Section 4.3.3 Example 8 p. 91 (the Newtonian limit v = p/m), Section 4.3.4 pp. 95-96 (p = m gamma v; the momentum of a material particle approaches infinity as its speed approaches c) and problems 11-12 p. 117 (expand K = m(gamma-1) and p = m gamma v in Taylor series; show the first terms are the nonrelativistic expressions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III lecture notes, 2019), Section 1.2.2 pp. 14-16 (relativistic free-particle action and the Lorentz factor gamma = (1-|xdot|^2/c^2)^(-1/2)) and Section 1.2.3 pp. 16-19, in particular the Taylor expansion (1.22) on printed p. 17 (-mc^2 + (m/2)|xdot|^2 - m Phi(x) + ... obtained by expanding the square root) (author-hosted copy of the official Cambridge PDF)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-sr-newtonian-bridge-limits-and-failures",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Limits of the Newtonian bridge: speed-dependent error constants, the excluded massless case, and bounded chart regions",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR11 reservation (\"rem failure at relativistic/null speeds\"); prose-scaffold.md SR11 row (\"not a universal cross-framework identification\")",
          "design_state": "scope remark recording the three failure modes as consequences of the cited displayed identities",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities",
            "post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy",
            "def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities",
            "lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-rational-power",
            "thm-of-archimedean"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence": "physical-result",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum": "physical-assumption",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-archimedean": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 17,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Scope remark for the flat special-relativity massive-particle model and the same bounded low-speed domain as pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence: it records the failure of any speed-independent error constant as |w| -> c, the exclusion of ideal massless particles, and the Galilean chart limit on bounded regions and its whole-domain failure for nonzero boost velocity (the zero-velocity maps coincide everywhere). It asserts no new physical law, no dynamics, and no empirical statement.",
          "statement": "Scope remark on the limits of the low-speed bridge. Each quantitative assertion is an immediate consequence of the cited displayed identities; none of them is used as a premise elsewhere on this page.\n\n(i) [No speed-independent error constant] Let $m>0$. For every $B\\ge0$ there are a future unit $n\\in V$, a vector $U$ with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ and a state $P=mU$ whose relative velocity $w$ of [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(d) satisfies $|w|<c$ and $\\bigl|T_n(P)-\\tfrac12m|w|^2\\bigr|>B$, with $T_n$ as in [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]. Explicitly, for every integer $\\gamma\\ge2$ there is such a state with Lorentz factor $\\gamma$, namely $|w|=c\\sqrt{1-\\gamma^{-2}}$, for which [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b) gives $T_n(P)=mc^2(\\gamma-1)$ while $\\tfrac12m|w|^2=\\tfrac12mc^2(1-\\gamma^{-2})$, so $$T_n(P)-\\tfrac12m|w|^2=mc^2\\Bigl(\\gamma-\\tfrac32+\\tfrac{1}{2\\gamma^2}\\Bigr)\\to\\infty\\quad(\\gamma\\to\\infty),$$ and the relative deviation from the Newtonian value equals $\\frac{(2\\gamma+1)(\\gamma-1)}{\\gamma+1}\\to\\infty$. Consequently the constants $K(z_0)$ and $\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z_0$ of [[pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence]] necessarily depend on the speed bound $z_0$, no bound of that form is uniform on the whole domain $|w|<c$, and $E_n(P)=mc^2\\gamma$ and $|q_n(P)|=mc\\sqrt{\\gamma^2-1}$ are unbounded as $|w|\\to c$ although $\\tfrac12m|w|^2\\le\\tfrac12mc^2$ there.\n\n(ii) [The massless case is excluded, not a limit] An ideal massless particle of [[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]] carries a nonzero future null four-momentum $P$ and, by [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(a)(i), satisfies $E_n(P)=c|q_n(P)|>0$ exactly; it has no four-velocity, proper time or relative velocity, and no rest mass, rest energy or kinetic energy is assigned to it. The hypotheses $m>0$ and $z\\le z_0<1$ exclude it, and no substitution $w$ with $|w|=c$ is available because $\\gamma_w$ is undefined there. The bridge therefore concerns massive particles only, and it neither derives nor constrains the ideal massless case or light.\n\n(iii) [Bounded chart regions, not a limit of frameworks] For fixed $v\\in S$, the uniform chart statement [[lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts]] holds on each bounded region $D_{T,R}$ as $C\\to\\infty$ (its clause (c)). For $v\\ne0$ it fails on the whole chart domain $\\mathbb R\\times S$ (its clause (d)); for $v=0$, $\\Phi_{0,C}=G_0=\\mathrm{id}$ for every $C>0$, so convergence is uniform on the whole chart domain. The nonzero-velocity failure does not assert failure on every unbounded subset; the correspondence is a conditional statement about the adopted model in a bounded low-speed domain, and it is neither an equivalence between the relativistic and Newtonian frameworks, nor a physical process in which the constant $c$ varies, nor empirical evidence for either framework. The Newtonian comparison quantities [[def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities]] are comparison expressions, not the derived quantities of a separate theory.",
          "strategy": "A scope remark whose clauses are the displayed consequences of earlier items, recorded without proof obligations. (i) For each integer $\\gamma\\ge2$ one has $\\sqrt{1-\\gamma^{-2}}<1$; taking $w$ of that rescaled norm in $n^\\perp$ and $U=c\\gamma(n+w/c)$ gives $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ by [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(d), and [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b) supplies $T_n(P)=mc^2(\\gamma-1)$ and $|w|^2=c^2(1-\\gamma^{-2})$; the displayed difference and its ratio $\\frac{(2\\gamma+1)(\\gamma-1)}{\\gamma+1}$ are elementary algebra in $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-rational-power]] conventions for the exponents). Since $\\gamma$ is arbitrary, no real $B$ bounds the difference uniformly, which is exactly the claim that the constant of the correspondence must depend on $z_0$. (ii) Repeats the defining exclusions of [[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]] and the null identity of the batch-8 lemma; there is no new claim. (iii) Repeats bounded-region convergence in clause (c) of the chart lemma, whole-domain failure for v!=0 in clause (d), and identity of both maps for v=0 in clause (a); it makes no failure claim for every unbounded subset. The correspondence theorem clause (e) supplies the conditional framework scope. Fix n and a unit e in its rest space once, and take w=c sqrt(1−gamma^−2)e. Then −eta(U/c,n)=gamma exactly. For any B>=0, thm-of-archimedean supplies an integer gamma>max(2,B/(mc²)+3/2); the displayed difference is greater than mc²(gamma−3/2)>B. The relative ratio exceeds gamma−1 and is unbounded by the same Archimedean property. No sequence of independently chosen unit vectors or additional choice premise is needed.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Section 4.2 pp. 82-86 (relativistic kinetic energy; Example 5 p. 86, the Newtonian prediction eV = (1/2)mv^2), Section 4.3.3 Example 8 p. 91 (the Newtonian limit v = p/m), Section 4.3.4 pp. 95-96 (p = m gamma v; the momentum of a material particle approaches infinity as its speed approaches c) and problems 11-12 p. 117 (expand K = m(gamma-1) and p = m gamma v in Taylor series; show the first terms are the nonrelativistic expressions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-special-relativity-track.md",
      "order": 229
