            "lem-rational-power-laws"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence": "physical-result",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-expansion-of-observer-quantities": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 17,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Numerical specialisation of pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence for a single massive particle state with |w|=c/5 in a named inertial frame of the flat model, in SI conventions. The displayed decimals are rounded values of exact radical expressions, not measurements; no experimental value, uncertainty or precision claim is made, and no other state or frame is covered.",
          "statement": "Specialise [[pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence]] to one massive particle of mass $m>0$ whose coordinate velocity $w$ in a named inertial frame satisfies $|w|/c=1/5$, so that $z=1/25$ and $z_0=z$. Then $\\gamma_w=(1-1/25)^{-1/2}=5/\\sqrt{24}=5\\sqrt6/12\\approx1.0206207262$ and $K(z_0)=\\tfrac38(25/24)^{5/2}\\approx0.4152916366$ ([[def-rational-power]], [[lem-rational-power-laws]]); all decimals below are rounded and all inequalities are between the exact expressions.\n\n(a) [Rate of the expansion at $z=1/25$] $\\gamma_w-1-\\tfrac z2=5\\sqrt6/12-51/50\\approx0.0006207262$, while the bound of [[lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder]] is $K(z_0)z^2=\\tfrac38(25/24)^{5/2}/625\\approx0.0006644666$, so the remainder bound holds and is sharp to about $7\\%$ here.\n\n(b) [Energy and kinetic energy] $E_n(P)-mc^2=mc^2(5\\sqrt6/12-1)\\approx0.0206207262\\,mc^2$ while $N_K(m,w)=\\tfrac1{50}mc^2=0.02\\,mc^2$; the deviation $\\approx0.0006207262\\,mc^2$ obeys the bound $K(z_0)z\\,m|w|^2=mc^2K(z_0)z^2\\approx0.0006644666\\,mc^2$ (all in units of $mc^2$).\n\n(c) [Momentum] $|q_n(P)|=m\\gamma_w|w|=\\tfrac{\\sqrt6}{12}mc\\approx0.2041241452\\,mc$ while $|N_p(m,w)|=mc/5=0.2\\,mc$; the deviation is $\\approx0.0041241452\\,mc$ against the bound $(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z)z\\,mc/5=(0.5+0.0166116655)\\cdot0.04\\,mc/5\\approx0.0041328933\\,mc$.\n\n(d) [Momentum-to-velocity inversion] $|w-q_n(P)/m|/|w|=\\gamma_w-1=5\\sqrt6/12-1\\approx0.0206207262$, within the bound $(\\tfrac12+K(z_0)z)z\\approx0.0206644666$ of clause (d) of the theorem.\n\nThe state is admissible: $z=1/25<1$, and $w$ of norm $c/5$ is realized in a frame chart by the hypothesis; the case $|w|=c$ is not considered, and the numbers carry no empirical content.",
          "strategy": "(a) Substitution of $z=1/25$ in [[lem-sr-uniform-low-speed-taylor-remainder]]\\,(b), with the exact value $\\gamma_w=(24/25)^{-1/2}=(25/24)^{1/2}=5/\\sqrt{24}=5\\sqrt6/12$ computed through [[lem-rational-power-laws]] ($(a/b)^{1/2}=a^{1/2}b^{-1/2}$); the displayed decimals are rounded to the displayed decimal places. (b) and (c) substitute $|w|=c/5$ and $\\gamma_w=5\\sqrt6/12$ into clauses (a)-(c) of [[pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence]], whose premises (the adopted postulate and the frame data) hold by hypothesis; the comparison quantities are $N_K=mc^2/50$ and $|N_p|=mc/5$ from [[def-sr-newtonian-comparison-quantities]]. (d) Uses $w-q_n(P)/m=(1-\\gamma_w)w$ from [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b) and the bound of the theorem's clause (d). The inequality checks are arithmetic comparisons of the displayed exact expressions; the rounded decimals are computational illustrations only.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Section 4.2 pp. 82-86 (relativistic kinetic energy; Example 5 p. 86, the Newtonian prediction eV = (1/2)mv^2), Section 4.3.3 Example 8 p. 91 (the Newtonian limit v = p/m), Section 4.3.4 pp. 95-96 (p = m gamma v; the momentum of a material particle approaches infinity as its speed approaches c) and problems 11-12 p. 117 (expand K = m(gamma-1) and p = m gamma v in Taylor series; show the first terms are the nonrelativistic expressions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-sr-uniform-galilean-chart-bound-worked",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Worked uniform Galilean chart bound on a bounded region",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR11 reservation (\"explicit Galilean limiting chart maps with bounded domains\")",
          "design_state": "explicit numerical instance of the exact differences, the uniform bounds and the epsilon choice",
          "deps": [
            "lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts",
            "def-sr-galilean-limiting-charts",
            "def-rational-power",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-galilean-limiting-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Take $v=e_1$ (so $|v|=1$), $T=1$, $R=2$ and the point $(t,x)=(1,2e_1)$ in [[def-sr-galilean-limiting-charts]], and let $\\Phi_{v,C}(1,2e_1)=(t',x')$. Then for $C=10$: $\\gamma_C=(99/100)^{-1/2}=10/\\sqrt{99}=10/(3\\sqrt{11})\\approx1.0050378153$, $t'\\approx0.9849370590$, $x'\\approx1.0050378153$, so with the Galilean value $G_v(1,2e_1)=(1,e_1)$,\n$$|t'-t|\\approx0.0150629410\\le(\\gamma_C-1)T+\\gamma_C\\tfrac{|v|R}{C^2}\\approx0.0251385716,$$ $$|x'-(x-tv)|\\approx0.0050378153\\le(\\gamma_C-1)(R+|v|T)\\approx0.0151134458 ;$$ for $C=100$ the same quantities are $\\gamma_C\\approx1.0000500038$, $|t'-t|\\approx0.0001500063\\le0.0002500138$ and $|x'-(x-tv)|\\approx0.0000500038\\le0.0001500113$. The observed deviations shrink by the factors $\\approx100.4$ and $\\approx100.7$ when $C$ grows from $10$ to $100$, consistent with the explicit $O(1/C^2)$ rate of [[lem-sr-uniform-galilean-limit-of-boost-charts]]\\,(c). The ratios of the exact deviations, evaluated before rounding, are approximately 100.4154227043 and 100.7487483984, respectively. Finally, for tolerance $\\varepsilon=0.01$, take $z_0=1/4$. Then $K_0=4\\sqrt3/9$ and $A_0=1/2+\\sqrt3/9<3/4$. Choose exactly $z_1=1/900$. With $H=T+R+|v|T=4$, one has $z_1<z_0$, $A_0z_1<1/1200<1/2$ and $A_0Hz_1<1/300<\\varepsilon/2=1/200$, so every threshold inequality is satisfied. The sufficient threshold is $$C_\\varepsilon=\\max\\{1/\\sqrt{z_1},\\sqrt{3|v|R/\\varepsilon}\\}=\\max\\{30,\\sqrt{600}\\}=30>1=|v|.$$ Thus $C=30$ is sufficient uniformly on $D_{1,2}$. At the specified point $\\gamma_{30}=30/\\sqrt{899}$, $|t'-t|=1-449/(15\\sqrt{899})\\approx0.0016674389$ and $|x'-(x-tv)|=30/\\sqrt{899}-1\\approx0.0005560189$, both below $0.01$. The whole-region bound values at $C=30$ are approximately $0.0027794768$ and $0.0016680568$, also below $0.01$. All displayed decimals are rounded to ten places after the decimal point and all inequalities compare exact values. No physical interpretation of the auxiliary $\\Phi_{v,C}$ family is asserted.",
          "strategy": "With delta(e1,2e1)=2 and |v|=1, put g_C=C/sqrt(C^2-1). The definition gives gamma_C=g_C, t'=(C^2-2)/(C sqrt(C^2-1)) and x'=g_C e1; the Galilean value is (1,e1). At C=10,30,100, t'<1: both sides are positive, and (C^2-2)^2<C^2(C^2-1) is equivalent to 4<3C^2. Thus the exact deviations are d_t(C)=1-(C^2-2)/(C sqrt(C^2-1)) and d_x(C)=C/sqrt(C^2-1)-1. The exact uniform bound values from lemma(b) are B_t(C)=C/sqrt(C^2-1)-1+2/(C sqrt(C^2-1)) and B_x(C)=3(C/sqrt(C^2-1)-1). Evaluate these exact radical expressions at the three scales, then round to ten places after the decimal point. Ratios are evaluated from the exact radical expressions, not already rounded deviations: d_t(10)/d_t(100)=100.4154227043 to ten decimal places and d_x(10)/d_x(100)=100.7487483984 to ten decimal places, hence 100.4 and 100.7 to one decimal place. For the tolerance proof, z0=1/4 gives K0=(3/8)(3/4)^(-5/2)=4sqrt(3)/9 and A0=1/2+sqrt(3)/9<3/4, since sqrt(3)<9/4 follows by squaring the positive quantities: 3<81/16. Therefore z1=1/900 is positive, <=z0, A0 z1<1/1200<1/2 and A0 H z1<1/300<1/200=epsilon/2. This proves z1<=min{z0,1/(2A0),epsilon/(2A0H)} explicitly. The sufficient threshold is |v|/sqrt(z1)=30, while sqrt(3|v|R/epsilon)=sqrt(600)<30 because 600<900; hence C_epsilon=30>|v|. The lemma therefore controls both errors uniformly on the entire bounded region. Exact radical evaluation gives the stated point and bound values at C=30. No ellipsis or empirical claim is used, and the fixed model scale c is not varied.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III lecture notes, 2019), Section 1.2.2 pp. 14-16 (relativistic free-particle action and the Lorentz factor gamma = (1-|xdot|^2/c^2)^(-1/2)) and Section 1.2.3 pp. 16-19, in particular the Taylor expansion (1.22) on printed p. 17 (-mc^2 + (m/2)|xdot|^2 - m Phi(x) + ... obtained by expanding the square root) (author-hosted copy of the official Cambridge PDF)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-sr-uniform-low-speed-remainder-fails-near-light-speed",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Counterexample: no speed-independent uniform low-speed remainder for the kinetic energy",
