          "dependency_level": 14,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "**Statement refuted.** Let $c>0$ and $m>0$. There is a real $B\\ge0$ such that $|T_n(P)-\\tfrac12m|w|^2|\\le B$ for every future unit $n\\in V$, every $P=mU$ with $U$ future timelike and $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$, and the relative velocity $w$ of [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(d), where $T_n$ is the kinetic energy of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]].\n\n**Counterexample.** Fix a future unit $n$ and a unit vector $e\\in n^\\perp$ once; for every integer $N\\ge2$ use that same vector (possible because $n^\\perp$ is a three-dimensional real subspace on which the restriction of $\\eta$ is positive definite, so every nonzero vector of $n^\\perp$ may be divided by its norm, [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(c)) and put $$w_N:=c\\sqrt{1-N^{-2}}\\;e,\\qquad U_N:=cN\\Bigl(n+\\frac{w_N}{c}\\Bigr),\\qquad P_N:=mU_N .$$ Then $|w_N|^2=c^2(1-N^{-2})<c^2$, and $\\eta(U_N,U_N)=c^2N^2(-1+|w_N|^2/c^2)=-c^2$ with $U_N$ future timelike, so $U_N$ has the required four-velocity normalization; $u_N=U_N/c$ is future unit timelike; the relative velocity and Lorentz factor of $U_N$ are $w_N$ and $N$, and [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b) gives $T_n(P_N)=mc^2(N-1)$ together with $\\tfrac12m|w_N|^2=\\tfrac12mc^2(1-N^{-2})$. Hence $$T_n(P_N)-\\tfrac12m|w_N|^2=mc^2\\Bigl(N-\\tfrac32+\\tfrac1{2N^2}\\Bigr),$$ which exceeds any prescribed real $B$ for all sufficiently large $N$; equivalently the relative deviation $\\frac{T_n(P_N)-\\frac12m|w_N|^2}{\\frac12m|w_N|^2}=\\frac{(2N+1)(N-1)}{N+1}$ is unbounded in $N$. Therefore the refuted statement is false for every pair $(c,m)$, and any valid low-speed error bound must depend on the admissible speed domain (the constants $K(z_0)$ of [[pthm-sr-newtonian-p-e-correspondence]] do). The construction is a statement about the stipulated model and contains no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "Assume the refuted statement and derive a contradiction. Fix a future unit $n$ and $e\\in n^\\perp$ with $|e|=1$; for each $N\\ge2$ set $a_N:=c\\sqrt{1-N^{-2}}$, $w_N:=a_Ne$ and $U_N:=cN(n+w_N/c)$. By [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]\\,(c) $\\eta(n,w_N)=0$ and $\\eta(w_N,w_N)=a_N^2$, so $\\eta(U_N,U_N)=c^2N^2(\\eta(n,n)+2\\eta(n,w_N)/c+\\eta(w_N,w_N)/c^2)=c^2N^2(-1+a_N^2/c^2)=-c^2$, and $U_N$ is future timelike; clause (d) of the same theorem then identifies $w_N=h(U_N)/\\gamma=c\\,h(U_N/c)/\\gamma$ with $\\gamma=N$, because $\\gamma=-\\eta(U_N/c,n)=N$. Applying [[lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities]]\\,(b) to $P_N=mU_N$ gives $T_n(P_N)=mc^2(N-1)$, $|w_N|^2=c^2(1-N^{-2})$ and the displayed difference; the relative deviation is the quotient of these expressions, namely $\\frac{(2N+1)(N-1)}{N+1}$ (verified by multiplying out). Since $N-\\tfrac32+\\tfrac1{2N^2}\\to\\infty$ as $N\\to\\infty$ in the ordered field $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-rational-power]], [[lem-of-abs-value]] conventions), for every real $B$ there is $N$ with $mc^2(N-\\tfrac32+\\tfrac1{2N^2})>B$, contradicting the assumed uniform bound. The norms and the causality classification are those of [[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]] and [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]] for the model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]. More explicitly choose a canonical integer N>max(2,B/(mc²)+3/2) using thm-of-archimedean. The displayed absolute difference is greater than mc²(N−3/2)>B, and the relative ratio exceeds N−1. This closes both unboundedness assertions by the exact published real-field supplier rather than an unspecified limit argument.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Section 4.2 pp. 82-86 (relativistic kinetic energy; Example 5 p. 86, the Newtonian prediction eV = (1/2)mv^2), Section 4.3.3 Example 8 p. 91 (the Newtonian limit v = p/m), Section 4.3.4 pp. 95-96 (p = m gamma v; the momentum of a material particle approaches infinity as its speed approaches c) and problems 11-12 p. 117 (expand K = m(gamma-1) and p = m gamma v in Taylor series; show the first terms are the nonrelativistic expressions)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997); signature (-+++) with c = 1. Chapter 1 Section 1.5 printed p. 27 (PDF p. 34): for small v, p^0 = m + (1/2)mv^2 (rest energy plus kinetic energy) and p^1 = mv (Newtonian momentum); Chapter 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 105-106 (PDF pp. 112-113): the Newtonian limit, the slow-motion condition (4.10) and the weak static field",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-special-relativity-track.md",
      "order": 230
    },
    {
      "id": "sr-experimental-interface",
