      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Operational model, data, calibration and uncertainty vocabulary of the experiment interface",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR12 reservation (\"def operational model/data/calibration/uncertainty vocabulary\"); prose-scaffold.md SR12 row (\"All four secondary narratives, exact report destinations above; no primary experiment source\")",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; formulation vocabulary only, no measurement, no sampling law and no statistical inference",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction",
            "def-sr-operational-radar-protocol",
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency",
            "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy",
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
            "post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-proper-time": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-operational-radar-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Formulation vocabulary inside the flat special-relativity framework: it names run descriptions, operational models, data records, calibration declarations and uncertainty bounds built from the framework's own objects (events, worldlines, clocks, observers, ideal waves, radar readings, observer energies and frequencies) and its SI conventions. It asserts no measurement, no apparatus behaviour, no realized experiment, no sampling law, no statistical inference and no value of any physical constant; in particular it does not claim that any laboratory realizes an operational model.",
          "statement": "Adopt the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], the ideal-clock and ideal-light assumptions of [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]], the ideal plane-wave and frequency-reading model of [[post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model]], the operational radar protocol of [[def-sr-operational-radar-protocol]], the massive-particle measurement identification of [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]] clause (ii) and the massless-particle measurement identification of [[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]] clause (ii). This definition introduces the framework's experimental-interface vocabulary; it selects no experiment, records no value and asserts no comparison.\n\n**(a) Run descriptions.** A *run description* is a finite tuple $D=(d_1,\\dots,d_r)$ whose entries are framework objects of the following kinds, each declared with its domain and regularity as specified by the cited item, together with the frame, observer, event or unit convention required for a numerical reading where such a reading is used: a point of the affine Minkowski space $A$ (an event, [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]); a vector of $V$ of declared causal character ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]); a future timelike worldline together with its proper-time function ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]], [[def-sr-proper-time]]); a future unit timelike vector or a smooth observer congruence ([[def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction]]); a future null wave covector with its affine phase ([[def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector]]); a radar time or radar distance assigned by the operational radar protocol ([[def-sr-operational-radar-protocol]]); an observer angular frequency ([[def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency]]); an observer energy, spatial momentum, kinetic energy or invariant mass ([[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]); and a real parameter vector in a declared subset of $\\mathbb R^m$ with declared units. A run description is a description of a model configuration; the tuple asserts nothing about any apparatus or about nature.\n\n**(b) Operational model.** An *operational model* is a tuple $M=(S,\\Theta,\\theta,D,F)$ in which: $S$ is a nonempty set, the *preparation index set*; $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ is a nonempty *parameter set* and $\\theta:S\\to\\Theta$ is a surjection, the *parameter map*; $D$ assigns to each $s\\in S$ a run description $D(s)$ of (a); and for some integer $d\\ge1$ the map $F:\\Theta\\to\\mathbb R^d$ is a *prediction map* for $D$, subject to the declared interpretation $(*)$: for every $s\\in S$, each component of $F(\\theta(s))$ is a number that the framework's definitions, postulates and proved consequences assign to the description $D(s)$, with every intermediate quantity, frame, event, sign and unit declared. A pair $(M,F)$ is called an operational model for the quantity whose values $F$ computes. Condition $(*)$ is a declared interpretation that reviewers check when the model is used; it is not a consequence of the tuple structure, and no clause of this definition asserts that any apparatus realizes $M$ or that $F(\\theta(s))$ is measured.\n\n**(c) Data records.** A *data record* is a tuple $R=(N,z,\\varepsilon,a)$ with an integer $N\\ge1$; $z=(z_1,\\dots,z_N)$ with $z_k\\in\\mathbb R^d$; $\\varepsilon=(\\varepsilon_1,\\dots,\\varepsilon_N)$ with $\\varepsilon_k\\in[0,\\infty)^d$; and $a=(a_1,\\dots,a_N)$ a finite tuple of *condition annotations*, each a finite string identifying the declared run condition of the datum and, when the datum is taken from a report, the bibliographic identification of that report. The record declares: for each $k$, the value of the quantity of (b) under the condition annotated by $a_k$ lies in the closed box $\\prod_{j=1}^d[z_{k,j}-\\varepsilon_{k,j},\\,z_{k,j}+\\varepsilon_{k,j}]$, in the declared units. A data record contains no probability space, random variable, distribution, sampling law, independence assumption, likelihood or coverage statement; it declares nothing about how the values $z_k$ were produced and nothing about the correctness of any calibration.\n\n**(d) Calibration declarations.** Let $Z$ be a nonempty set of declared *instrument-output values*. A *calibration declaration* is a tuple $C=(Z,c,\\delta,h)$ with $c:Z\\to\\mathbb R^d$, $\\delta:Z\\to[0,\\infty)^d$ and $h$ a condition annotation; it declares that for every $z\\in Z$ obtained under the condition annotated by $h$, the value of the declared quantity lies in the closed box $\\prod_j[c_j(z)-\\delta_j(z),\\,c_j(z)+\\delta_j(z)]$ in the declared units. A *calibration record* is a data record of (c) whose declared quantity is the value of the calibration map's output. The declaration asserts no accuracy beyond the bound $\\delta$ and no statistical property of $c$ or $\\delta$.\n\n**(e) Uncertainty vocabulary.** For a data record of (c), the *declared uncertainty* of the $k$-th datum is the vector $\\varepsilon_k$; a *source label* is an element of the finite declared set $\\{\\text{calibration},\\ \\text{systematic},\\ \\text{sampling-variability},\\ \\text{rounding},\\ \\text{other}\\}$ attached to a bound; and a *total declared bound* for a value computed from finitely many recorded values by a declared finite arithmetic expression is any $B\\in[0,\\infty)$ for which the declaration states: whenever each recorded value lies in its declared closed box, the computed value lies in $[\\hat y-B,\\hat y+B]$ for the declared number $\\hat y$ (for instance, for a sum of recorded scalars the sum of the recorded bounds is such a bound). Every uncertainty entry is a declared constraint of the form in (c) and (d); no probability law, coverage probability, confidence level, p-value or posterior is defined by this vocabulary.\n\n**(f) Comparison claims are separate.** A claim that a data record $R$ and an operational model $M$ are consistent, or that $R$ supports, refutes or fails to distinguish $M$, is not part of (a)-(e): it requires its own statement, an explicitly declared comparison rule and adequate primary-report and analysis prerequisites. For the current stored-lithium-ion optical Doppler report, that research prerequisite, labelled M20 in the source design, is supplied by `exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014` and `pthm-sr-specified-model-comparison`. The comparison theorem distinguishes hard-bound records of (c) from separately defined source-reported estimates and standard-uncertainty scales; the latter are not hard bounds or coverage guarantees. It permits comparison with specified alternative prediction hypotheses, which are not thereby operational models of the SR framework and are not asserted to satisfy condition $(*)$ in (b). Other primary-report families remain subject to their own retrieval and analysis obligations. These are nonlogical status pointers to the separate empirical record and comparison claim; no clause of this vocabulary licenses a conclusion about the truth of a postulate or of the framework.\n\n**(g) Well-definedness and scope.** All components of (a)-(e) are finite tuples of sets, maps and real numbers with declared domains and units; the constructions use no choice principle and no measurability, integrability, completeness or differentiability beyond the cited items, and clause (b) evaluates a prediction only at parameter values for which the cited items supply its ingredients. The vocabulary asserts no measurement, no apparatus behaviour, no empirical support or refutation, no value of the constant $c$ or of any other physical parameter, and no statistical inference.",
          "strategy": "A definition item. Well-definedness: (a) enumerates object kinds each supplied by a cited item with its own domain and regularity, and requires the reading conventions to be declared rather than inferred; (b) is a tuple of a set, a subset of $\\mathbb R^m$, two maps and a finite tuple, all of them finite data with declared domains; the map $F$ is evaluated only at the parameter values $\\theta(s)$ for which the cited items define its ingredients; (c) and (d) are finite tuples of real vectors, nonnegative bounds and annotation strings, so each closed box $\\prod_j[z_j-\\varepsilon_j,z_j+\\varepsilon_j]$ is a well-defined subset of $\\mathbb R^d$ because $\\varepsilon_j\\ge0$; (e) defines the declared-bound vocabulary by a stated property rather than by an extremal or optimality claim, and (f) states the separateness of comparison claims. Condition $(*)$ of (b) is deliberately an interpretation obligation: the definition names the tuple and its intended reading, and any use of an operational model must check $(*)$ against the specific framework items (for the Doppler instance of this page, [[pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration]] supplies it). No choice principle is used anywhere: all constructions are finite tuples and pointwise evaluations. The scope clauses exclude measurements, apparatus behaviour, calibration correctness and all statistical notions; the reader should note that an operational model is a mathematical structure of the framework and not a claim that any laboratory realizes it.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages; the author's lightandmatter.com page links this Archive upload); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Sections 1.3 'Measurement' and 1.4 'The Lorentz transformation', pp. 23-31 (operational definition of time and the operationalism passage p. 26), Sections 3.2-3.3 pp. 55-59, Section 5.1 pp. 121-123",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Chapter 1 'Special Relativity and Flat Spacetime' printed pp. 1-5: the operational construction of an inertial coordinate system from freely moving rigid rods and a set of synchronized clocks, the definition of an event, the interval and the metric",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 16
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-sr-primary-report-research-queue",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Primary-report research queue for the special-relativity framework",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR12 reservation (\"rem proposed primary-report research queue; no exp item until actual reports read\") and its 'Bridges and empirical boundary' section; prose-scaffold.md SR12 row",
          "design_state": "Primary-report queue and scope remark; the 2014 lithium-ion Doppler branch and its explicit comparison analysis are supplied, while the other report families remain queued.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Scope declaration of the framework's empirical interface: it records a research queue of primary-report destinations and the qualifications future experiment items must carry. It asserts no measurement, no dataset, no uncertainty value, no comparison result, no deviation, no empirical support for any postulate and no precision of any physical constant.",
          "statement": "Scope-and-queue record for the empirical layer of the flat special-relativity framework, stated in the vocabulary of [[def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary]].\n\n(i) The actual primary Botermann et al.2014 stored-lithium-ion Ives-Stilwell report has now been retrieved and read in full. The proposed experiment records its apparatus,calibration,finite sampling,frequency estimates and1sigma budget; the comparison theorem supplies explicit conditional bound and reported-scale analysis. No raw dataset or new coverage claim is supplied.\n\n(ii) The source design declares four *primary-report search destinations* for future experiment items of this framework. Each is named here as a destination, that is, a family of published reports to retrieve and read in full text, and none of them is evidence for any postulate: (a) historical ether and light-propagation experiments (interferometric or resonance tests of the constancy of the coordinate speed of light between inertial charts, and Doppler-shift tests of moving emitters); (b) unstable-particle lifetime measurements (beam or storage-ring measurements of a particle's lifetime at a measured speed compared with its lifetime at rest); (c) accelerator kinematics of charged particles at high Lorentz factor (measurements of momentum, energy or speed relating a particle's measured quantities to its velocity); (d) clock-transport and clock-comparison experiments (aircraft-, satellite- or laboratory-borne atomic clocks comparing the elapsed times of differently moving or differently situated ideal clocks). The secondary narratives consulted for this page mention instances of these families, among them the historical ether-and-Maxwell context (Crowell pp. 13-15), the 1938 Ives-Stilwell Doppler test (Crowell p. 57), storage-ring muon-lifetime measurements (Crowell pp. 28-29), the 1971 Hafele-Keating aircraft atomic-clock comparison (Crowell pp. 15, 26, 28; Tong pp. 24-25) and a 2010 low-speed atomic-clock measurement (Crowell p. 28); those mentions are contextual motivation only and are not documentation of reported measurements here.\n\n(iii) Before any experiment item can be authored on a destination, the actual primary report must be retrieved and read, and the record must carry the apparatus and preparation, operational definitions of the measured quantities, the calibration model, the sampling and error model, the treatment of systematic errors, the measured values with their reported uncertainties, and the source's limitations. Textbook and review narratives are not substitutes for the primary reports.\n\n(iv) The comparison of model predictions with recorded data is a separate promised physics-theorem obligation which the source design declares to require the primary-report and analysis layer (research label M20); that layer is now supplied for the specified2014 stored-lithium-ion report by the experiment and comparison theorem, while the other primary-report families remain research queues.\n\n(v) Qualifications any future comparison must carry: observations test the combined model and apparatus assumptions; finite-sample agreement does not establish a postulate or a model; a p-value would not be the probability that a theory is false and a confidence level would not be a posterior probability; and a finite bounded record does not by itself determine a unique parameter or adjudicate between distinct models.\n\n(vi) This remark is a queue/status pointer; measured estimates and their qualifications are recorded only in the experiment item. It supplies no independent empirical conclusion or certainty.\n\nThe four-family search queue remains intact: this single optical Doppler primary report supplies one branch of family(a); historical ether/interferometer reports and the lifetime,accelerator and transported-clock families still require actual retrieval and reading. Source-cited RMS/SME analyses remain separate queue obligations.\n\nCurrent nonlogical status pointers: `exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014` records the primary observations; `pthm-sr-specified-model-comparison` gives the separate conditional comparison. These pointers add no logical prerequisite.",
          "strategy": "This is a scope-and-queue remark. Its current status is that the full Botermann et al. (2014) primary report has been retrieved and read, the same-page experiment proposal records its qualified observations, and `pthm-sr-specified-model-comparison` supplies complete finite calibrated-bound and reported-scale comparison rules with a faithful report instantiation. The research label M20 is therefore supplied for this specified optical Doppler report. It is not an unresolved global requirement for this comparison, and it is not certification of the remaining report families. Clause (ii) preserves all four original search destinations and identifies textbook narratives as contextual motivation only. Clause (iii) retains the apparatus, preparation, operational definition, calibration, finite sampling, systematic-error, observation and limitation requirements for future reports. Clause (v) preserves the distinction between conditional model comparison and empirical certainty. The source's raw spectra, fit weights and covariance are not supplied or invented; its standard uncertainties are not hard bounds or coverage guarantees. This remark provides nonlogical status pointers to `exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014` and `pthm-sr-specified-model-comparison`; it does not supply either item's proof or empirical premise. No dependency cycle is introduced, and no original source-harvest or queue history is removed.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages; the author's lightandmatter.com page links this Archive upload); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Chapters 1-3 and 5, including the narrative context on pp. 13-15, the 1938 Ives-Stilwell test mention on p. 57, the storage-ring muon-lifetime account on pp. 28-29, the Hafele-Keating account on pp. 15, 26 and 28, and the 2010 atomic-clock measurement mention on p. 28",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III course notes, 2019), author-hosted copy of the official Cambridge full PDF; Chapter 1 Sections 1.2.1-1.2.2 printed pp. 12-16 (proper time on p. 14), and Section 1.2.5 printed pp. 24-26: Hafele-Keating on p. 25 and Pound-Rebka on p. 26",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014",
          "kind": "experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Reported stored-lithium-ion Ives-Stilwell Doppler experiment at 0.338c (2014)",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Botermann et al., Test of Time Dilation Using Stored Li+ Ions as Clocks at Relativistic Speed, arXiv:1409.7951v1 (2014), full five-page primary report; equations (1)-(4), Figures 1-2, Table I. Published Phys. Rev. Lett. 113, 120405 (2014).",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1409.7951v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Primary report of optical-optical double resonance spectroscopy of stored metastable 7Li+ ions in the ESR interaction section, with nominal speed beta = 0.338, calibrated laser frequencies, active-transition invariance and resonance-model auxiliaries. Describes a measured estimate and source-labelled standard uncertainty, not exact frequencies, a hard true-value bound, guaranteed coverage, raw data, or validation of every SR postulate. Atomic spectroscopy, real line widths, beam dynamics and calibration are empirical apparatus auxiliaries, not deductions from the ideal-plane-wave postulate.",
          "setup": "**Quantities and apparatus notation.** The laboratory speed parameter is $\\beta=v/c$, with $v$ the selected ion-class speed in an approximately inertial laboratory description; its quoted value is nominal. Frequencies $\\nu_1,\\nu_2,\\nu_p,\\nu_a>0$ are scalar estimates in hertz of the two rest transitions and the parallel/antiparallel laser readings, respectively; the ideal-model convention is $\\nu=\\omega/(2\\pi)$ and $\\lambda=c/\\nu$ from [[def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency]], while the real apparatus has finite-width spectra. Here $\\mathrm{MHz}=10^6\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $\\mathrm{nm}=10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm m$, and $\\mu\\mathrm{rad}=10^{-6}$ radians. For a single-peaked nonnegative profile, its full width at half maximum (FWHM) is the separation of the two frequencies (or speeds) at half its peak height, when those crossings exist; the reported $\\delta v/v$ uses the speed width divided by the selected speed. Beam divergence and alignment are angles between ion trajectories and the specified beam axis. A Lambda system here means three source-labelled atomic levels, two lower and one common upper, with the two listed driven transitions; the symbols $3S_1$, $3P_2$ and $F$ distinguish the report's atomic levels, rather than supplying a quantum-state model. Metastable lifetime means the reported characteristic persistence time of the prepared level; no survival distribution is inferred. The Penning source, ESR (Experimental Storage Ring), electron cooling, lasers and optics below are the report's named preparation and control apparatus; their performance is reported empirically, not derived from SR. The energy label $\\mathrm{MeV/u}$ is energy per atomic-mass unit: $1\\,\\mathrm{MeV}=10^6$ electronvolts, with one electronvolt the energy acquired by elementary charge across one volt; it is not used here to deduce the nominal speed.\n\nThe experiment used $^7\\mathrm{Li}^+$ ions from a Penning ion gauge source, accelerated to $58.6\\,\\mathrm{MeV/u}$ and stored in the ESR at nominal $\\beta=0.338$. Electron cooling reduced the reported longitudinal velocity spread to $\\delta v/v\\approx9\\times10^{-6}$ (FWHM) and transverse divergence to less than $100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$. About $10^7$ to $10^8$ ions were stored, with an estimated metastable fraction below $1\\%$. The metastable $3S_1$ lifetime was $15$–$30\\,\\mathrm{s}$ in the ring, compared with $50\\,\\mathrm{s}$ in vacuum. The Lambda system used the transitions $3S_1(F=3/2)\\to3P_2(F=5/2)$ and $3S_1(F=5/2)\\to3P_2(F=5/2)$. Parallel and antiparallel beams near $386\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ and $780\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ addressed its two legs; an additional $780\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ beam repumped ions into the system.",
          "procedure": "**Count and fit objects.** A spectrum in this account is a finite list of frequency settings $f_i\\in\\mathbb R$ with detected pulse counts $n_{i,h}\\in\\mathbb N_0$ in each switching configuration $h\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ and window. Fluorescence denotes the emitted light counted by the photomultipliers; a dark count is a registered pulse without applied excitation. Subtracted or averaged spectra are real-valued lists, not nonnegative integer counts or independent trials. A Lorentzian peak plus linear background means a fitted function $L:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ of the form $L(f)=A/[1+4(f-f_0)^2/w^2]+b_0+b_1(f-f_0)$ with $A>0$, $w>0$; $f_0,w$ have frequency units, and the peak term has half height at $f_0\\pm w/2$. Residuals are the differences of processed values and the fitted values at the recorded settings. A weighted average of fitted centers has form $\\sum_i w_i f_{0,i}/\\sum_i w_i$ for nonnegative weights with positive sum; the report does not give the weights or their construction. Frequency locking means controlling a laser reading relative to a specified reference or offset; second-harmonic generation denotes conversion to twice the optical frequency. Optical-optical double resonance is the selected condition that both listed transitions are resonant for the same ion velocity class; it is an apparatus selection model, not a measured probability law.\n\nThe parallel beam was generated by doubling a $772\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ Ti:Sa laser locked to an iodine marker previously calibrated with a frequency comb against a rubidium clock. The antiparallel diode laser was offset-locked to a rubidium reference, with a $600\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ tuning range. Polarization-maintaining fibers transported the beams. Motorized stages controlled angles and positions to $20\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$ and $50\\,\\mu\\mathrm{m}$; scrapers established laser/ion overlap within $80\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$. Photomultipliers counted fluorescence in four successive $100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{s}$ configurations: both excitation beams, antiparallel only, parallel only, and neither. Dark counts were subtracted, then the separate-beam background was subtracted from the both-beam spectrum. Optical-optical double resonance selected ions for which both Lambda legs were resonant at the same velocity. To compensate metastable depletion, an $81$-point scan was paused after $69$ points for injection and cooling. Figure 2 shows an example averaged over $44$ scans, with $49\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ laboratory linewidth and $35\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ rest-frame linewidth. The authors fitted $40$ spectra with Lorentzians on linear backgrounds and formed a weighted average. Raw count tables, fit weights, a covariance matrix and a complete likelihood are not published in this report.",
          "observations": "Table I gives the frequency estimates, in MHz, $\\nu_1=546455143.0$, $\\nu_2=546474960.7$, $\\nu_a=384225534.98$ and $\\nu_p=777210326.98$. The rest-frame inputs $\\nu_1,\\nu_2$ come from earlier measurements cited as references 21–22; those papers were not retrieved or read here. Equation (4) reports $\\epsilon=\\sqrt{\\nu_a\\nu_p/(\\nu_1\\nu_2)}-1=(1.5\\pm2.3)\\times10^{-9}$ at nominal $\\beta=0.338$. These are estimates with the source-labelled $1\\sigma$ uncertainty budget, not exact values or guaranteed bounds. The rounded Table I centers yield approximately $1.4744849565\\times10^{-9}$, consistent with the reported rounded estimate, without reconstructing unrounded data.",
          "uncertainty": "The Gouy phase is the additional spatial phase of a focused beam relative to a plane-wave phase; for a $C^1$ phase correction $\\psi:J\\to\\mathbb R$ on a proper-time interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, the phase-reading convention assigns a frequency correction $-(2\\pi)^{-1}d\\psi/d\\tau$ under the stipulated phase-reading interpretation. No beam profile $\\psi$ is reconstructed here. The $1\\sigma$ notation below is the source\\'s label for an uncertainty scale; without its full estimation model it specifies neither a guaranteed interval nor a sampling distribution.\n\nTable I labels its uncertainties $1\\sigma$. In MHz, the aggregate entries are $0.43$ for each rest frequency, $1.6$ for the antiparallel frequency and $1.25$ for the parallel frequency. Antiparallel contributions are: weighted-fit statistical uncertainty $0.6$, rubidium reference $0.05$, diode stabilization $0.64$, background linearization $0.5$, Gouy phase $1.0$, angular alignment $0.6$, and ion divergence $0.4$. Parallel contributions are: iodine reference $0.06$, Ti:Sa stabilization $0.12$, Gouy phase $1.0$, alignment $0.6$, and divergence $0.4$. The inferred Gouy correction for each laser was $0.44\\pm1.0\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$, from experimentally validated numerical simulation using measured beam parameters. The authors reported no significant residual asymmetry; simulated background linearization was assigned $0.5\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$. The excess linewidth, about five times the expected width, was attributed mainly to drift-induced fluorescence. The $0.6\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ statistical entry comes from the weighted analysis of forty fitted spectra, not forty independent raw clock readings. These reported statistical and systematic scales are preserved without inventing independence, a Gaussian total sampling law, fit weights, covariance or coverage.",
          "interpretation": "The measured observable tests the combined Doppler/time-dilation, active atomic-transition and vacuum-wave invariance assumptions together with resonance selection, calibration, beam alignment and correction models. The source reports no resolved deviation at its uncertainty level and quotes $|\\alpha|\\le2.0\\times10^{-8}$ for $\\alpha=\\epsilon/\\beta^2$. That quotation is recorded as the source's test-theory sensitivity statement, rather than a newly certified hard bound or confidence interval. RMS and SME parameter translations and future higher-boost prospects remain contextual research topics; they are not prerequisites or claims of the comparison theorem.",
          "empirical_result": {
            "observation": "Reported epsilon = (1.5 +/- 2.3) x 10^-9, source equation (4), with 1-sigma budget in Table I.",
            "uncertainty": "Reported u_epsilon = 2.3 x 10^-9; includes fitting, calibration, background, Gouy-phase, alignment and divergence uncertainty scales; no probability or coverage guaranteed.",
            "conditions": "Stored 7Li+ ESR optical-optical double resonance experiment at nominal beta = 0.338; Table I laser and rest-frequency inputs, same-velocity Lambda resonance, source correction and calibration models and active-invariance auxiliary assumptions.",
            "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1409.7951v1"
          },
          "statement": "## Setup\n\n**Quantities and apparatus notation.** The laboratory speed parameter is $\\beta=v/c$, with $v$ the selected ion-class speed in an approximately inertial laboratory description; its quoted value is nominal. Frequencies $\\nu_1,\\nu_2,\\nu_p,\\nu_a>0$ are scalar estimates in hertz of the two rest transitions and the parallel/antiparallel laser readings, respectively; the ideal-model convention is $\\nu=\\omega/(2\\pi)$ and $\\lambda=c/\\nu$ from [[def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency]], while the real apparatus has finite-width spectra. Here $\\mathrm{MHz}=10^6\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $\\mathrm{nm}=10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm m$, and $\\mu\\mathrm{rad}=10^{-6}$ radians. For a single-peaked nonnegative profile, its full width at half maximum (FWHM) is the separation of the two frequencies (or speeds) at half its peak height, when those crossings exist; the reported $\\delta v/v$ uses the speed width divided by the selected speed. Beam divergence and alignment are angles between ion trajectories and the specified beam axis. A Lambda system here means three source-labelled atomic levels, two lower and one common upper, with the two listed driven transitions; the symbols $3S_1$, $3P_2$ and $F$ distinguish the report's atomic levels, rather than supplying a quantum-state model. Metastable lifetime means the reported characteristic persistence time of the prepared level; no survival distribution is inferred. The Penning source, ESR (Experimental Storage Ring), electron cooling, lasers and optics below are the report's named preparation and control apparatus; their performance is reported empirically, not derived from SR. The energy label $\\mathrm{MeV/u}$ is energy per atomic-mass unit: $1\\,\\mathrm{MeV}=10^6$ electronvolts, with one electronvolt the energy acquired by elementary charge across one volt; it is not used here to deduce the nominal speed.\n\nThe experiment used $^7\\mathrm{Li}^+$ ions from a Penning ion gauge source, accelerated to $58.6\\,\\mathrm{MeV/u}$ and stored in the ESR at nominal $\\beta=0.338$. Electron cooling reduced the reported longitudinal velocity spread to $\\delta v/v\\approx9\\times10^{-6}$ (FWHM) and transverse divergence to less than $100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$. About $10^7$ to $10^8$ ions were stored, with an estimated metastable fraction below $1\\%$. The metastable $3S_1$ lifetime was $15$–$30\\,\\mathrm{s}$ in the ring, compared with $50\\,\\mathrm{s}$ in vacuum. The Lambda system used the transitions $3S_1(F=3/2)\\to3P_2(F=5/2)$ and $3S_1(F=5/2)\\to3P_2(F=5/2)$. Parallel and antiparallel beams near $386\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ and $780\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ addressed its two legs; an additional $780\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ beam repumped ions into the system.\n\n## Procedure\n\n**Count and fit objects.** A spectrum in this account is a finite list of frequency settings $f_i\\in\\mathbb R$ with detected pulse counts $n_{i,h}\\in\\mathbb N_0$ in each switching configuration $h\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ and window. Fluorescence denotes the emitted light counted by the photomultipliers; a dark count is a registered pulse without applied excitation. Subtracted or averaged spectra are real-valued lists, not nonnegative integer counts or independent trials. A Lorentzian peak plus linear background means a fitted function $L:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ of the form $L(f)=A/[1+4(f-f_0)^2/w^2]+b_0+b_1(f-f_0)$ with $A>0$, $w>0$; $f_0,w$ have frequency units, and the peak term has half height at $f_0\\pm w/2$. Residuals are the differences of processed values and the fitted values at the recorded settings. A weighted average of fitted centers has form $\\sum_i w_i f_{0,i}/\\sum_i w_i$ for nonnegative weights with positive sum; the report does not give the weights or their construction. Frequency locking means controlling a laser reading relative to a specified reference or offset; second-harmonic generation denotes conversion to twice the optical frequency. Optical-optical double resonance is the selected condition that both listed transitions are resonant for the same ion velocity class; it is an apparatus selection model, not a measured probability law.\n\nThe parallel beam was generated by doubling a $772\\,\\mathrm{nm}$ Ti:Sa laser locked to an iodine marker previously calibrated with a frequency comb against a rubidium clock. The antiparallel diode laser was offset-locked to a rubidium reference, with a $600\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ tuning range. Polarization-maintaining fibers transported the beams. Motorized stages controlled angles and positions to $20\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$ and $50\\,\\mu\\mathrm{m}$; scrapers established laser/ion overlap within $80\\,\\mu\\mathrm{rad}$. Photomultipliers counted fluorescence in four successive $100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{s}$ configurations: both excitation beams, antiparallel only, parallel only, and neither. Dark counts were subtracted, then the separate-beam background was subtracted from the both-beam spectrum. Optical-optical double resonance selected ions for which both Lambda legs were resonant at the same velocity. To compensate metastable depletion, an $81$-point scan was paused after $69$ points for injection and cooling. Figure 2 shows an example averaged over $44$ scans, with $49\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ laboratory linewidth and $35\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ rest-frame linewidth. The authors fitted $40$ spectra with Lorentzians on linear backgrounds and formed a weighted average. Raw count tables, fit weights, a covariance matrix and a complete likelihood are not published in this report.\n\n## Observations\n\nTable I gives the frequency estimates, in MHz, $\\nu_1=546455143.0$, $\\nu_2=546474960.7$, $\\nu_a=384225534.98$ and $\\nu_p=777210326.98$. The rest-frame inputs $\\nu_1,\\nu_2$ come from earlier measurements cited as references 21–22; those papers were not retrieved or read here. Equation (4) reports $\\epsilon=\\sqrt{\\nu_a\\nu_p/(\\nu_1\\nu_2)}-1=(1.5\\pm2.3)\\times10^{-9}$ at nominal $\\beta=0.338$. These are estimates with the source-labelled $1\\sigma$ uncertainty budget, not exact values or guaranteed bounds. The rounded Table I centers yield approximately $1.4744849565\\times10^{-9}$, consistent with the reported rounded estimate, without reconstructing unrounded data.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nThe Gouy phase is the additional spatial phase of a focused beam relative to a plane-wave phase; for a $C^1$ phase correction $\\psi:J\\to\\mathbb R$ on a proper-time interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, the phase-reading convention assigns a frequency correction $-(2\\pi)^{-1}d\\psi/d\\tau$ under the stipulated phase-reading interpretation. No beam profile $\\psi$ is reconstructed here. The $1\\sigma$ notation below is the source\\'s label for an uncertainty scale; without its full estimation model it specifies neither a guaranteed interval nor a sampling distribution.\n\nTable I labels its uncertainties $1\\sigma$. In MHz, the aggregate entries are $0.43$ for each rest frequency, $1.6$ for the antiparallel frequency and $1.25$ for the parallel frequency. Antiparallel contributions are: weighted-fit statistical uncertainty $0.6$, rubidium reference $0.05$, diode stabilization $0.64$, background linearization $0.5$, Gouy phase $1.0$, angular alignment $0.6$, and ion divergence $0.4$. Parallel contributions are: iodine reference $0.06$, Ti:Sa stabilization $0.12$, Gouy phase $1.0$, alignment $0.6$, and divergence $0.4$. The inferred Gouy correction for each laser was $0.44\\pm1.0\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$, from experimentally validated numerical simulation using measured beam parameters. The authors reported no significant residual asymmetry; simulated background linearization was assigned $0.5\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$. The excess linewidth, about five times the expected width, was attributed mainly to drift-induced fluorescence. The $0.6\\,\\mathrm{MHz}$ statistical entry comes from the weighted analysis of forty fitted spectra, not forty independent raw clock readings. These reported statistical and systematic scales are preserved without inventing independence, a Gaussian total sampling law, fit weights, covariance or coverage.\n\n## Interpretation\n\nThe measured observable tests the combined Doppler/time-dilation, active atomic-transition and vacuum-wave invariance assumptions together with resonance selection, calibration, beam alignment and correction models. The source reports no resolved deviation at its uncertainty level and quotes $|\\alpha|\\le2.0\\times10^{-8}$ for $\\alpha=\\epsilon/\\beta^2$. That quotation is recorded as the source's test-theory sensitivity statement, rather than a newly certified hard bound or confidence interval. RMS and SME parameter translations and future higher-boost prospects remain contextual research topics; they are not prerequisites or claims of the comparison theorem.",
          "strategy": "Source-backed experiment exposition, not a deductive proof. Author the five public sections from the structured setup, procedure, observations, uncertainty and interpretation fields, keeping their numerical estimates, units and source limitations unchanged. Ground apparatus and preparation in primary-report pages 2–3 and Figure 1; ground the four-counter switching, scan schedule and line-fitting summary in pages 3–4 and Figure 2; ground frequency estimates and every listed statistical/systematic scale in Table I on page 4; ground the reported observable and its qualified interpretation in equations (1)–(4) and the limiting discussion on pages 4–5. The actual pinned full paper was retrieved and all five pages read. Preserve the distinction between published summary estimates and unavailable raw data, fit weights and covariance; do not infer an unpublished likelihood, independence or coverage. The earlier rest-frequency/calibration references are borrowed inputs documented by this primary report and are not claimed independently retrieved here. Keep the atomic resonance and active-invariance reading as explicit apparatus/model assumptions rather than deductions from the ideal-wave supplier. Leave proof provenance not-applicable, with no Proof section or proof-audit claim. Native authors and independent source reviewers must perform the class-specific formulation, setup, calibration, sampling, systematics, observation, uncertainty and interpretation review on stable integrated inputs; this proposal is not a fabricated independent source-check certificate.",
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-sr-specified-model-comparison",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Specified-model comparison with calibrated bounds and reported uncertainty scales",
          "reference": "scaffold/special-relativity.md SR12 reservation (\"comparison pthm requires M20\"); closure-ledger.json label M20 (\"no-experiment-authored; primary-report-queue-retained\")",
          "design_state": "Complete conditional general comparison rules and actual primary-report instantiation; proposal awaiting native integration/authoring/review",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary",
            "exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014",
            "pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency",
            "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone",
            "lem-of-sign-rules",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-lorentz-boost",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse",
            "lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data",
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014": "empirical-premise",
            "pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration": "physical-result",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model": "physical-assumption",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-sign-rules": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-boost": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "General finite calibrated/nuisance model comparison and exact positive-frequency bound propagation, with separate source-reported-estimate/standard-uncertainty score analysis. Instantiates the actual 2014 stored 7Li+ optical-optical double resonance report under explicit atomic, optical, resonance and beam-calibration auxiliaries. Hard-bound declarations are conditional; reported 1-sigma scales imply no guaranteed bounds or coverage. No p-values, confidence levels, raw-data reanalysis or unique validation of SR are asserted. The comparator Gamma=1 is explicitly specified and evaluated at the nominal beta; all source empirical conditions and uncertainties are inherited.",
          "statement": "Adopt [[def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary]]. Comparison labels below range over explicitly specified prediction hypotheses, each with its own apparatus and calibration assumptions. When a label is an operational model of the SR framework, its prediction map must satisfy clause (b), condition $(*)$, of that vocabulary using the specified SR suppliers. A competing prediction hypothesis need not be an SR operational model and is not represented as satisfying $(*)$. Reported standard uncertainties are a separate record type, not the vocabulary's declared hard-bound records.\n\n**(a) General finite comparison.** An apparatus/calibration nuisance parameter means an additional declared model input constrained together with the physical parameter; its domain is part of the admissible set below, and no probability law is implicit. Flatten a finite $d$-dimensional record with $N$ entries to $m=Nd$ real coordinates. For each label $\\ell$, let $A_\\ell$ be a declared set of admissible tuples $a=(\\theta,\\eta,b)$, where $\\theta$ is a physical parameter, $\\eta$ an apparatus or calibration nuisance parameter, and $b\\in\\mathbb R^m$ an additive calibration correction. Let $P_\\ell:A_\\ell\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be its corrected prediction, $P_\\ell(a)=F_\\ell(\\theta,\\eta)+b$; every correction and admissibility constraint is explicitly stated. For a declared hard-bound record $(z,e)$ put $B(z,e)=\\prod_{j=1}^m[z_j-e_j,z_j+e_j]$ and $C_\\ell=\\{a\\in A_\\ell:P_\\ell(a)\\in B(z,e)\\}$. Then $C_\\ell\\neq\\varnothing$ if and only if $P_\\ell(A_\\ell)\\cap B(z,e)\\neq\\varnothing$. If the true calibrated vector satisfies the declared bounds and a candidate with its auxiliaries assigns that vector the value $P_\\ell(a)$ for some $a\\in A_\\ell$, then $a\\in C_\\ell$. Thus $C_\\ell=\\varnothing$ excludes that conjunction of candidate, parameter, calibration and hard-bound assumptions, rather than proving another model. If every prediction in $P_\\ell(A_\\ell)$ has coordinate $j$ in $[L_j,U_j]$ disjoint from $[z_j-e_j,z_j+e_j]$, then $C_\\ell=\\varnothing$. This criterion is sufficient; overlap of marginal intervals alone does not establish joint feasibility.\n\n**(b) Exact finite uncertainty propagation.** For positive frequencies $\\nu_a,\\nu_p,\\nu_1,\\nu_2$ define $Q=\\sqrt{\\nu_a\\nu_p/(\\nu_1\\nu_2)}-1$. If separately declared hard bounds place these frequencies in positive closed intervals $[\\nu_{a,-},\\nu_{a,+}]$, $[\\nu_{p,-},\\nu_{p,+}]$, $[\\nu_{1,-},\\nu_{1,+}]$ and $[\\nu_{2,-},\\nu_{2,+}]$, then\n\n$$Q\\in\\left[\\sqrt{\\frac{\\nu_{a,-}\\nu_{p,-}}{\\nu_{1,+}\\nu_{2,+}}}-1,\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{\\nu_{a,+}\\nu_{p,+}}{\\nu_{1,-}\\nu_{2,-}}}-1\\right].$$\n\nBoth endpoints are attained on this rectangular input domain. This is conditional on the hard-bound event; standard-uncertainty entries alone assert neither that event nor probabilistic independence.\n\n**(c) Reported-estimate comparison.** A reported-estimate record $H=(\\widehat z,u,\\alpha,\\mathcal M)$ consists of a finite real estimate vector, strictly positive reported scale entries $u_j$, bibliographic and run annotations $\\alpha$, and a source description $\\mathcal M$ of estimation and uncertainty construction. It asserts no hard bound or coverage. Here $|t|=t$ for $t\\ge0$ and $|t|=-t$ for $t<0$; a nonempty finite maximum is obtained by recursively comparing pairs. For a declared multiplier $k\\ge0$ define\n\n$$D_\\ell(a)=\\max_{1\\le j\\le m}\\frac{|\\widehat z_j-P_{\\ell,j}(a)|}{u_j},\\qquad S_\\ell(k)=\\{a\\in A_\\ell:D_\\ell(a)\\le k\\}.$$\n\nThen $S_\\ell(k)=P_\\ell^{-1}(\\prod_j[\\widehat z_j-ku_j,\\widehat z_j+ku_j])$. This is a deterministic comparison of estimates and reported scales. No probability, $p$-value, confidence level or rejection error rate follows without a separately justified sampling and error model.\n\n**(d) Primary-report instantiation.** Under the qualified conditions of `exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014`, additionally stipulate that resonance identifies each laser frequency in the ions' instantaneous rest frame with its stated rest transition frequency, that those transition frequencies are unchanged under active boosts, and that the ideal vacuum phase and frequency law applies in the interaction section after the report's beam, Gouy-phase and line-shape corrections. These atomic, optical and apparatus premises are not derived by [[pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration]]. At each selected reception event, additionally represent the ion by an inertial rest observer with velocity $c\\beta e_1$, $|\\beta|<1$, in a laboratory inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$. This is a stipulated reception idealization in the interaction section, not an application of the supplier to an accelerating storage-ring worldline. For oppositely propagating laboratory waves, that theorem gives\n\n$$\\nu_1=\\gamma(1-\\beta)\\nu_p,\\qquad \\nu_2=\\gamma(1+\\beta)\\nu_a,\\qquad \\gamma=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}.$$\n\nHence $Q_{\\mathrm{SR}}=0$. To make that interpretation explicit, fix arbitrary positive model transition frequencies $\\nu_1,\\nu_2$ in hertz. For each $\\beta\\in(-1,1)$ take the chart $x_\\beta=B(\\beta e_1)x$, its rest vector $n_\\beta=B(-\\beta e_1)e_0$, and laboratory frequencies $\\nu_p=\\nu_1/[\\gamma(1-\\beta)]$, $\\nu_a=\\nu_2/[\\gamma(1+\\beta)]$. Take covectors with laboratory components $k_p=(2\\pi\\nu_p/c)(-1,1,0,0)$ and $k_a=(2\\pi\\nu_a/c)(-1,-1,0,0)$ and phases $p\\mapsto k_p(p-o)$, $p\\mapsto k_a(p-o)$. Let $D(\\beta)$ contain these waves, $n_\\beta$, reception event $o$ and the declared scalar parameters, with the two frames attached to the readings. Then $S=\\Theta=(-1,1)$, parameter map $\\mathrm{id}$, and prediction $F(\\beta)=Q_{\\mathrm{SR}}=0$ give an operational model for $Q$ under the stipulated resonance identification. The transition frequencies here are model parameters; this construction does not assert exact knowledge of the report's uncertain frequency values. Define the alternative prediction hypothesis by replacing the common factor $\\gamma$ in those resonance equations by $\\Gamma(\\beta)=1$, retaining the same directional factors and apparatus assumptions. It predicts $Q_1(\\beta)=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}-1$. This deliberately alternative hypothesis is not an SR operational model and makes no claim to satisfy $(*)$; it is not identified with all classical physics.\n\nFor the source-reported dimensionless estimate $\\widehat z=1.5\\times10^{-9}$ and scale $u=2.3\\times10^{-9}$, the declared $k=1$ criterion gives $D_{\\mathrm{SR}}=15/23<1$, with descriptive scale band $[-0.8,3.8]\\times10^{-9}$. At the report's nominal $\\beta=0.338$, one has $0.062<Q_1(0.338)<0.063$, hence $D_1>10^7$: that nominal alternative prediction fails this scale criterion. This is not a statistical significance claim. The speed is nominal, no calibrated speed uncertainty is supplied for this evaluation, and no distribution or coverage is inferred. The SR product prediction is independent of $\\beta$; the alternative prediction depends on its declared value. Agreement of SR with the descriptive scale band tests the physical and apparatus conjunction within the report's uncertainty treatment; it neither proves SR nor uniquely identifies its auxiliary assumptions. All conclusions inherit the primary report's conditions, calibration dependence and statistical and systematic limitations.",
          "strategy": "The proof below uses $\\nu_a=a$, $\\nu_p=p$, $\\nu_1=r_1$, $\\nu_2=r_2$ when abbreviating the four frequencies; candidate labels include explicitly alternative prediction hypotheses as well as SR operational models. Only the SR member is required and shown to satisfy vocabulary condition $(*)$. 1.1 By definition C_l is the preimage of B under P_l; existence of a in C_l is precisely existence of a in A_l with P_l(a) in B, equivalent to nonempty intersection of the image with B. Under the declared true-value/bound and candidate assumptions, that specified a belongs to C_l. If C_l is empty the conjunction is impossible, with no claim about which premise fails. [given]\n1.2 If every candidate coordinate j lies in [L_j,U_j] disjoint from its data interval, no prediction lies in the product B. Thus C_l is empty. The criterion is sufficient only: for example the prediction set {(0,1),(1,0)} has both coordinate projections overlapping the box {(0,0)}, yet the intersection is empty. Calibration/nuisance constraints are retained inside A_l and cannot be discarded by replacing a joint feasible set with its marginal projections. [step 1.1]\n2.1 In the positive frequency rectangle, multiplying positive intervals increases numerator bounds and division by positive denominator reverses denominator extrema, by sign rules. The increasing positive square root preserves these inequalities. Hence the stated lower/upper Q bounds hold. The corner a_minus,p_minus,r1_plus,r2_plus attains the lower value, and the opposite corner the upper value. This uses only the stated rectangular hard-bound domain, not a probabilistic interpretation of standard uncertainties. [given]\n3.1 Because u_j>0, abs(zhat_j-P_j)<=k*u_j is exactly zhat_j-k*u_j<=P_j<=zhat_j+k*u_j. A finite maximum is <=k exactly when every coordinate ratio is <=k. Intersecting these constraints proves the asserted scale-consistency preimage identity. It is arithmetic of fixed real estimates and scales, with no random variable or probability measure, so it supplies no statistical rejection/coverage conclusion. [given]\n4.1 Apply the existing SR Doppler result separately to the parallel and antiparallel ideal waves and the instantaneous rest observer beta e1. Linear rather than angular frequencies share the factors because division by2pi cancels. With the explicitly stipulated resonance/active-transition auxiliaries this gives r1=gamma(1-beta)p and r2=gamma(1+beta)a. Multiply, obtaining ap/(r1r2)=1/(gamma^2(1-beta^2))=1; positive square root gives Q_SR=0. Replacing the stipulated common factor by Gamma=1 gives Q_1=1/sqrt(1-beta^2)-1. The SR supplier proves only the ideal transformation, not the atomic or line-width premises. [given]\n4.2 The source estimate/scale pair gives abs(1.5e-9)/2.3e-9=15/23 and endpoints1.5e-9±2.3e-9=(-.8,3.8)e-9. For beta=169/500,1-beta^2=221439/250000. The exact positive-square comparisons (531/500)^2*(221439/250000)<1<(1063/1000)^2*(221439/250000) prove1.062<1/sqrt(1-beta^2)<1.063 by order and rational-power laws. Hence Q_1>.062 and (Q_1-1.5e-9)/(2.3e-9)>10^7, since .062-1.5e-9>.023=10^7*2.3e-9. Thus the respective k=1 classifications follow from3.1. No count model, covariance or exact speed-calibration claim enters this arithmetic. [step 3.1, step 4.1]\n5.1 The quoted uncertainties remain source-reported standard uncertainties. The hard-bound premise of(a)-(b) is a separate condition, and is never instantiated by the1sigma table as certain. The experiment's apparatus, finite sampling, corrections, borrowed rest frequencies and unresolved distribution/correlation details carry through this conditional comparison. It proves the finite comparison rules and their source-record instantiation without a statistical rejection rate, empirical certainty, or truth claim about the framework. No choice principle or unproved statistical theorem is used. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 4.2]\n\nStep5a current-carrier reconciliation: For every beta in(-1,1), construct the inverse-boosted inertial observer, two future null covectors and affine phases at o, with positive laboratory frequencies chosen to satisfy the stipulated resonance identities and F(beta)=0.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages; the author's lightandmatter.com page links this Archive upload); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Sections 1.3 'Measurement' and 1.4 'The Lorentz transformation', pp. 23-31 and Sections 3.2-3.3 pp. 55-59 (the stretch factor as Doppler shift)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III course notes, 2019), author-hosted copy of the official Cambridge full PDF; Chapter 1 Sections 1.2.1-1.2.2 printed pp. 12-16 (proper time on p. 14): proper time along a particle's worldline and the aging comparison",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "B. Botermann et al., Test of Time Dilation Using Stored Li+ Ions as Clocks at Relativistic Speed, arXiv:1409.7951v1 (2014), full five-page primary report; equations (1)-(4), Figures 1-2, Table I. Published Phys. Rev. Lett. 113, 120405 (2014).",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1409.7951v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "empirical_premises": [
            {
              "experiment": "exp-sr-stored-lithium-ion-doppler-time-dilation-2014",
              "conditions": "Stored 7Li+ ESR optical-optical double resonance experiment at nominal beta = 0.338; Table I laser and rest-frequency inputs, same-velocity Lambda resonance, source correction and calibration models and active-invariance auxiliary assumptions.",
              "uncertainty": "Reported u_epsilon = 2.3 x 10^-9; includes fitting, calibration, background, Gouy-phase, alignment and divergence uncertainty scales; no probability or coverage guaranteed.",
              "use": "Reported epsilon estimate and standard scale only, under the stated apparatus and atomic auxiliaries; used for a deterministic descriptive scale score, never as hard truth bounds or a coverage/rejection rate."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
