          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Worked arithmetic instance inside the flat special-relativity framework: it instantiates the operational vocabulary on the framework's own longitudinal Doppler prediction with a declared, explicitly illustrative record. It asserts no apparatus, no measurement, no calibration correctness, no sampling model, no statistical inference and no empirical support or refutation of the Doppler theorem or of any postulate.",
          "statement": "Adopt the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], the vocabulary of [[def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary]] and the ideal plane-wave model of [[post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model]]. Consider the longitudinal configuration of [[pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration]]: inertial charts $x$ and $x'$ whose transition is $x'=B(\\beta e_1)\\,x$ for a real $\\beta\\in(-1,1)$, where $B(\\cdot)$ is the passive boost of [[def-lorentz-boost]], so that the frame $x'$ moves with velocity $c\\beta e_1$ relative to $x$; an ideal emitter history, meaning the $C^\\infty$ rest worldline $h:\\mathbb R\\to A$, $h(\\rho)=o+\\rho e_0$, with length parameter $\\rho$, future unit tangent $e_0$ and proper-time function $\\tau_h(\\rho)=\\rho/c$ based at $0$ (no emission dynamics is stipulated); an ideal plane wave whose frame-$x$ data are $(\\omega,s)$ with angular frequency $\\omega>0$ and $s=e_1$; and the rest observer of $x'$, whose four-velocity is $c f_0$ for the future unit timelike vector $f_0$ constructed below. By clauses (i) and (ii) of that theorem this observer reads $\\omega_o(\\beta)=\\gamma_\\beta(1-\\beta)\\omega$ with $\\gamma_\\beta=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$, so the *Doppler ratio* is\n\n$$F(\\beta):=\\frac{\\omega_o(\\beta)}{\\omega}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\beta}{1+\\beta}},\\qquad \\beta\\in(-1,1),$$\n\nthe rational power being that of [[def-rational-power]].\n\n(a) Let $S:=\\Theta:=(-1,1)$, $\\theta:=\\mathrm{id}$, let $D(\\beta)$ be the run description comprising the inertial charts $x$ and $x'$, the emitter's straight rest curve, the future null wave covector with frame-$x$ data $(\\omega,e_1)$ and its affine phase, its proper-time function $\\tau_h$, and the observer's rest vector $f_0$ with reception event $o$, and let $F$ be as displayed. Then $M:=(S,\\Theta,\\theta,D,F)$ is an operational model for the dimensionless ratio $\\omega_o/\\omega$ in the sense of clause (b) of [[def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary]]: condition $(*)$ holds with the prediction supplied by the cited Doppler theorem, and $d=1$. The map $F$ is strictly decreasing on $(-1,1)$, and for every real $r>0$ there is exactly one $\\beta\\in(-1,1)$ with $F(\\beta)=r$, namely\n\n$$\\beta(r)=\\frac{1-r^2}{1+r^2};$$\n\nconsequently $F$ is a strictly decreasing bijection of $(-1,1)$ onto $(0,\\infty)$.\n\n(b) Let $z\\in(0,1]$ and $\\varepsilon\\in[0,z)$ be real, so that $0<z-\\varepsilon\\le z+\\varepsilon$, and let $R$ be the declared one-point record with $N=1$, $z_1=z$, $\\varepsilon_1=\\varepsilon$ and condition annotation \"longitudinal Doppler ratio of the moving observer in the propagation direction\" in the sense of clause (c) of the vocabulary. Then the set of parameters consistent with $R$ and $M$,\n\n$$C(R,M):=\\{\\beta\\in(-1,1):F(\\beta)\\in[z-\\varepsilon,z+\\varepsilon]\\},$$\n\nequals the closed interval $[\\beta(z+\\varepsilon),\\beta(z-\\varepsilon)]$; it is nonempty, and every element of it satisfies the declared bound of $R$. If $\\varepsilon=0$, it is the singleton $\\{\\beta(z)\\}$. If $\\varepsilon>0$, its endpoints are strictly ordered and its exact positive length is\n\n$$\\beta(z-\\varepsilon)-\\beta(z+\\varepsilon)=\\frac{8z\\varepsilon}{[1+(z-\\varepsilon)^2][1+(z+\\varepsilon)^2]}>0.$$\n\nThus the strict inequality between the record endpoints and the positive-length conclusion apply exactly when the declared uncertainty is positive.\n\n(c) Illustrative instance (declared illustrative values, not a measurement report): $z=9/10$ and $\\varepsilon=1/100$. Then $\\beta(91/100)=1719/18281\\approx0.094032$ and $\\beta(89/100)=2079/17921\\approx0.116009$, and $C(R,M)=[1719/18281,\\ 2079/17921]$, an interval of exact length $7200000/327613801$ in $\\beta$, whose endpoints satisfy $F(1719/18281)=91/100$ and $F(2079/17921)=89/100$.\n\n(d) Scope. Every number in (c) is a declared illustrative value supplied to instantiate the vocabulary's clauses and their arithmetic; the example asserts no apparatus, no calibration correctness, no measurement, no sampling model, no statistical inference and no empirical support or refutation of the Doppler theorem or of any postulate.",
          "strategy": "Direct verification of each clause. (a) Condition $(*)$: the ratio $\\omega_o/\\omega$ for the fixed configuration $(\\beta e_1,\\ s=e_1)$ is read by the rest observer of $x'$ as $\\gamma_\\beta(1-\\beta)$ by clause (ii) of [[pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration]] with $\\beta\\cdot s=\\beta$ and $|\\beta|<1$; since $1-\\beta>0$ and $1+\\beta>0$ for $\\beta\\in(-1,1)$, the identity $\\gamma_\\beta(1-\\beta)=\\sqrt{(1-\\beta)/(1+\\beta)}$ is an elementary consequence of the rational-power laws [[lem-rational-power-laws]] and of the positivity of the square root, with the sign chosen positive. Strict decrease: for $-1<\\beta_1<\\beta_2<1$, the difference of the squared ratios is $2(\\beta_2-\\beta_1)/[(1+\\beta_1)(1+\\beta_2)]>0$, since both denominator factors are positive ([[lem-of-sign-rules]]); composing with the strictly increasing square-root map ([[lem-rational-power-monotone]], claim 2 with $r=1/2$) gives $F(\\beta_1)>F(\\beta_2)$. The inverse: $F(\\beta)=r$ for $r>0$ is equivalent to $(1-\\beta)/(1+\\beta)=r^2$, that is to $\\beta(1+r^2)=1-r^2$, i.e. $\\beta=\\beta(r)$; this $\\beta(r)$ lies in $(-1,1)$ because $|1-r^2|<1+r^2$, and the solution is unique by strict monotonicity. (b) Since $F$ is strictly decreasing with inverse $\\beta(\\cdot)$, $F(\\beta)\\in[z-\\varepsilon,z+\\varepsilon]$ is equivalent to $\\beta\\in[\\beta(z+\\varepsilon),\\beta(z-\\varepsilon)]$, and both endpoints lie in $(-1,1)$ because $z-\\varepsilon>0$ makes $z+\\varepsilon>0$; the declared box of the record is a closed interval in the value variable. At $\\varepsilon=0$ both record endpoints equal z and the inverse produces the singleton $\\{\\beta(z)\\}$. At $\\varepsilon>0$, $z-\\varepsilon<z+\\varepsilon$ and the inverse is strictly decreasing, so the parameter endpoints are strictly ordered. Direct rational subtraction gives the displayed length $8z\\varepsilon/[ (1+(z-\\varepsilon)^2)(1+(z+\\varepsilon)^2)]$, whose denominator is positive and whose numerator is positive exactly for positive uncertainty. (c) Substitute $r=91/100$ and $r=89/100$ into $\\beta(r)$ and reduce: exact rational arithmetic; the length is $\\beta(89/100)-\\beta(91/100)$, computed by common denominators as $7200000/327613801>0$. The decimals are displayed to six places and are not used in any claim. No step uses a probability model, a sampling assumption or a measurement. The complete run configuration exists for every beta in (-1,1): the passive boost is proper orthochronous by lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse and thus yields an inertial boosted frame; choose the fixed covector with components (-omega/c,omega/c,0,0), future null by lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data(e). The two rest worldlines are straight future timelike histories of their respective frames. Their conditional frequency readings are supplied by the plane-wave postulate and Doppler theorem; the operational model introduces no claim that an apparatus realizes this configuration.\n\nStep5a current-carrier reconciliation: Use the emitter h:R->A, h(rho)=o+rho e0, proper time rho/c, explicit affine phase and observer reception o; invoke Doppler(i),(ii).",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages; the author's lightandmatter.com page links this Archive upload); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Sections 1.3 'Measurement' and 1.4 'The Lorentz transformation', pp. 23-31 (operational definition of time, the metric, time dilation) and Sections 3.2-3.3 pp. 55-59 (the stretch factor as Doppler shift)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Chapter 1 'Special Relativity and Flat Spacetime' printed pp. 1-5: the operational construction of an inertial coordinate system, the definition of an event, the interval and the metric",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "cex-sr-bounded-record-does-not-identify-the-parameter",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Counterexample: a bounded one-point record need not determine the parameter of an operational model",
          "reference": "content-model statistical-evidence guidance (\"a finite sample can lack ...\", \"a bounded record does not determine a unique parameter\"); B-page construction corresponding to def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary",
          "design_state": "complete refutation by two exact rational parameter witnesses consistent with one illustrative bounded record; declared illustrative values",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary",
            "pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency",
            "def-lorentz-boost",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-rational-power",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "lem-of-sign-rules",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model",
            "lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data",
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration": "physical-result",
            "def-sr-null-wave-covector-and-observer-frequency": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-monotone": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-sign-rules": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-boost": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model": "physical-assumption",
            "lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conceptual counterexample inside the flat special-relativity framework: it shows that a declared bounded record of the operational vocabulary need not determine a unique parameter of an operational model, using the framework's own Doppler prediction and declared illustrative values. It asserts no measurement, no statistical inference and no empirical conclusion about any model or postulate.",
          "statement": "**Statement refuted.** A declared one-point data record of the vocabulary with positive declared uncertainty determines the parameter of an operational model uniquely: if a record R of clause (c) of [[def-sr-experiment-interface-vocabulary]] is consistent with two parameters $\\beta_1,\\beta_2$ of one operational model M, then $\\beta_1=\\beta_2$.\n\n**Counterexample.** Adopt the SI convention $x^0=ct$ and the ideal plane-wave/frequency-reading model of [[post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model]], conditionally as in [[pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration]]. Fix an inertial chart x and a positive angular frequency $\\omega$. For every $\\beta\\in(-1,1)$ let $x'=B(\\beta e_1)x$ be the passive boosted inertial chart; use an ideal emitter at rest in x, the future null wave covector with frame-x components $(-\\omega/c,\\omega/c,0,0)$ and propagation direction $s=e_1$, and the straight rest observer of x'. Let $S=\\Theta=(-1,1)$, $\\theta=\\mathrm{id}$ and let $D(\\beta)$ be this finite declared run description. The Doppler theorem gives $\\omega_o=\\gamma_\\beta(1-\\beta)\\omega$, where $\\gamma_\\beta=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$, so the dimensionless prediction is $$F(\\beta)=\\frac{\\omega_o}{\\omega}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\beta}{1+\\beta}}.$$ Thus $M=(S,\\Theta,\\theta,D,F)$ satisfies the operational vocabulary's interpretation condition (*), with $d=1$; the interpretation is a conditional model computation, not an apparatus claim. Use the explicitly declared comparison rule that a parameter is consistent with a one-point scalar record exactly when its prediction lies in that record's declared closed interval.\n\nTake the illustrative record $N=1$, $z_1=z=9/10$, $\\varepsilon_1=\\varepsilon=1/100$ and annotation 'longitudinal Doppler ratio of the moving observer in the propagation direction'. These are declared illustrative values, not measurements. Its declared prediction interval is $[89/100,91/100]$. The map F is strictly decreasing on $(-1,1)$ and has inverse $\\beta(r)=(1-r^2)/(1+r^2)$ for every $r>0$. Therefore the full consistency set is $$C(R,M)=\\{\\beta:F(\\beta)\\in[89/100,91/100]\\}=[1719/18281,\\,2079/17921].$$ In particular put $\\beta_1=1719/18281$, $\\beta_2=2079/17921$. Both are in $(-1,1)$ and $F(\\beta_1)=91/100$, $F(\\beta_2)=89/100$, hence both are consistent. Yet $$\\beta_2-\\beta_1=\\frac{2079}{17921}-\\frac{1719}{18281}=\\frac{7200000}{327613801}>0,$$ with $17921\\cdot18281=327613801$. This refutes uniqueness. In fact every parameter in that entire positive-length interval is consistent, including its endpoints. Nothing in this algebraic consistency rule supports the truth of M or of any physical postulate; no probability model, measurement, calibration, statistical inference or empirical conclusion is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Full inline refutation from A-page and published mathematical suppliers, without a B-example dependency. The inertial boosted chart is admissible for every beta in (-1,1) by lem-boost-is-proper-orthochronous-with-inverse and def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart. The fixed covector has varpi=omega/c>0 and spatial unit direction e1, so lem-sr-null-covector-frame-data(e) proves it future null. The rest observer's frequency ratio is gamma_beta(1-beta) by pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration(ii) in the conditional ideal plane-wave model. Since 1-beta and 1+beta are positive, the rational-power laws yield the positive square root F(beta)=sqrt((1-beta)/(1+beta)). Thus the declared finite configuration D and F satisfy the operational model interpretation condition (*); S=Theta=(-1,1) and the identity parameter map are nonempty and surjective.\n\nFor -1<beta_a<beta_b<1, the difference (1-beta_a)/(1+beta_a)-(1-beta_b)/(1+beta_b)=2(beta_b-beta_a)/[(1+beta_a)(1+beta_b)] is positive by the sign rules. The square-root map is strictly increasing on positive reals by lem-rational-power-monotone, so F is strictly decreasing. For r>0, solving F(beta)=r is equivalent to 1-beta=r^2(1+beta), hence beta=(1-r^2)/(1+r^2). Its denominator is positive, and beta+1=2/(1+r^2)>0, 1-beta=2r^2/(1+r^2)>0, so the inverse lies in (-1,1). Direct substitution gives F(beta(r))=r, with positive square-root sign; this also proves onto and uniqueness. Monotonicity and the inverse give the entire preimage of [89/100,91/100] as [beta(91/100),beta(89/100)].\n\nExact rational arithmetic yields beta(91/100)=(10000-8281)/(10000+8281)=1719/18281 and beta(89/100)=(10000-7921)/(10000+7921)=2079/17921. For the first endpoint, (1-beta1)/(1+beta1)=8281/10000=(91/100)^2; for the second it is 7921/10000=(89/100)^2, so the positive square root gives the displayed predictions. Both lie in the declared closed interval. Subtracting parameters gives numerator 2079*18281-1719*17921=38006199-30806199=7200000>0 and positive denominator17921*18281=327613801. Therefore two distinct endpoint witnesses, and indeed the whole stated positive-length interval, satisfy the declared algebraic comparison rule. This contradicts only the uniqueness claim; it supplies no model-truth, probability, sampling, apparatus, calibration or empirical inference. The record is illustrative throughout.\n\nStep5a current-carrier reconciliation: Use k with affine phase p->k(p-o), observer f0 and reception event o; invoke Doppler(i),(ii) for the ratio.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages; the author's lightandmatter.com page links this Archive upload); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas restored to x^0 = c t; Sections 1.3 'Measurement' and 1.4 'The Lorentz transformation', pp. 23-31 (operational definition of time, the metric, time dilation) and Sections 3.2-3.3 pp. 55-59 (the stretch factor as Doppler shift)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/sr_20220102/sr.pdf"
              },
