        {
          "id": "post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted spacetime model and classical domain of the gravitational framework",
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the classical gravitational framework the\nfollowing statements.\n\n**(i) Spacetime model.** The spacetime of a model of the framework is a\ntime-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ as in\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]: a smooth four-manifold $M$ without\nboundary, a Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ on $M$\n([[def-smooth-manifold]]), and a time orientation $N$. Its points represent\nidealized physical events; the vector $N$ fixes which causal directions are\nfuture-directed. At each event $p\\in M$, every nonzero tangent vector $v\\in T_pM$ is\nclassified by the sign of $g_p(v,v)$ as in\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]], and each nonzero causal vector is\nfuture- or past-directed according to the sign of $g_p(v,N_p)$. The zero\nvector is neither timelike, null, spacelike nor causal. This is a\npointwise rule and does not require choosing a curve through $p$. The metric\n$g$ is the gravitational field of the model: it is the object that the field\nlaw of the framework constrains, and the curvature $R^g$ of the uniquely constructed torsion-free\n$g$-compatible connection $\\nabla^g$ of\n[[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]] is the model's representation of\ngravity. That supplier defines $R^g(X,Y)Z=\\nabla^g_X\\nabla^g_YZ-\\nabla^g_Y\\nabla^g_XZ-\\nabla^g_{[X,Y]}Z$\nand proves the connection exists; this clause adds no independent connection\nfield. The rules by which ideal clocks, test bodies and\nlight rays couple to $g$ are adopted separately as physical assumptions of the\nframework.\n\n**(ii) Classical domain.** Geometry and matter are treated as classical\ncontinuum fields: the model describes macroscopic configurations of matter and\na classical metric, with no quantum description of either. The framework makes\nno claim that a real spacetime is exactly such a manifold, that matter is\nexactly a classical continuum, or that the smooth description applies to\narbitrarily small scales or arbitrarily strong fields; those are domain\nlimitations of the model family, not consequences of it.\n\n**(iii) Unity of the model.** A model of the framework is specified by its\ngeometry $(M,g,N)$ together with its matter content and the field law adopted\nfor it; the geometry alone selects no matter configuration and no dynamics\n(the matter assumptions are\nadopted separately, in the companion postulate on matter content). Physical\nparameters enter through\nthe unit conventions of [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]]; no\nnumerical value of a constant is asserted here.\n\n**(iv) Smooth-branch restriction.** Each field of a model is defined and\n$C^\\infty$ at every point of its specified open domain in $M$; the metric is\n$C^\\infty$ on all of $M$ and $M$ has no boundary. A field is smooth here when\nits component functions in the model's smooth coordinate and bundle charts\nare $C^\\infty$ on that domain. Configurations with a field undefined or\nnonsmooth at any point of its stated domain are outside this smooth branch,\neven when the exceptional set has empty interior. Matter concentrated on a\nlower-dimensional set in a way that produces a distributional stress-energy\ntensor, and manifolds with boundary, are also outside this branch and require\nadditional mathematics. A model may instead specify an open domain excluding\nan exceptional locus; its smooth-branch claims then apply only on that domain\nand assert no extension across the excluded locus.\n\n**(v) Status.** These are adopted assumptions. They are not derived from the\nmathematics of manifolds, from any symmetry principle, or from experiment; the\nequivalence principle and covariance arguments motivate but do not prove them.\nTesting the framework means comparing predictions computed from a fully\nspecified model — geometry, matter content and field law — with observations,\nand agreement is evidence about the model, not a proof of these postulates.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it adopts the smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold as the classical arena, with its continuum, smooth-branch, boundaryless and four-dimensional restrictions and its status as adopted rather than deduced; no dynamics, no parameter value and no empirical claim are asserted.",
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
            "def-smooth-manifold",
            "def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "def-gr-metric-selected-connection"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-smooth-manifold": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adoption of the smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of the framework as the spacetime of a classical model, with the framework's classical continuum domain and its smooth-branch and four-dimensional restrictions. Excludes quantum-gravity and quantum-field regimes, weak-regularity or singular regions, distributional sources, boundaries, and dimensions other than four. Adoption is a model assumption, not a derived or empirical result.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), section 6.4 'Postulates of General Relativity', postulate 1 (spacetime is a 4d Lorentzian manifold), PDF p. 68",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Ch. 4 'physics in curved spacetime', printed pp. 97-99 (PDF pp. 104-106)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, Michaelmas 2019), sections 3.1.2-3.1.3 and 4.1 on Lorentzian manifolds and the matter-geometry relation, printed pp. 93-96 and 149-153 (PDF pp. 100-103 and 156-160)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/2023id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted matter-content assumptions: fields, equations, stress-energy and energy conditions",
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the classical gravitational framework, in\naddition to [[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]], the following statements\nabout the matter content of a model.\n\n**(i) Matter content is model input.** A model of the framework specifies its\nnongravitational fields or matter and their explicit equations of motion and\ncouplings. The geometric data $(M,g,N)$ of\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]] and covariance of the model's equations\nunder relabelling of events do not select those fields, equations or couplings.\nHere covariance means that, on overlapping smooth charts, the component\ntransformations specified for the model's fields carry solutions of the\nequations in one chart to solutions of the same equations in the other.\nDifferent matter specifications over the same geometry are different models of\nthe framework, and no dynamical selection principle is asserted.\n\n**(ii) Smooth matter and its stress-energy tensor.** The matter of a model on\nits matter domain is represented by smooth fields on an open domain\n$U\\subseteq M$, and the model's stress-energy tensor is the smooth symmetric\ncovariant $2$-tensor field $T$ on $U$ of\n[[def-gr-stress-energy-tensor-field]], with the unit reading recorded there.\nThe relation of $T$ to the matter fields is part of the model: a model derived\nfrom an action defines $T$ as the Hilbert stress-energy tensor of that action,\nwhile a model that prescribes $T$ directly is a separate source model.\n\n**(iii) Consistency with the field law.** Where a model prescribes $T$\nindependently of matter fields, the prescription is admissible only if it meets\nthe consistency conditions imposed by the gravitational field law adopted for\nthe model, stated where that law is stated. If a model specifies a real-valued\naction functional on its admissible metric and matter configurations, the\naction, its dependence on those fields, its stated symmetries, and the matter\nequations obtained from it are part of the model. Any conservation identity\nused for $T$ when those equations hold must be established from that model's\naction, symmetries, equations, and regularity hypotheses; it is not assumed\nfor an arbitrary action or arbitrary smooth symmetric $T$.\n\n**(iv) Energy conditions are optional additional assumptions.** A model may\nadopt any of the pointwise inequalities of [[def-gr-energy-conditions]] as\nfurther assumptions on its stress-energy tensor. The general framework\npostulates impose none automatically. A particular matter model may imply one\nor more of them; any such implication must be established from that model's\nequations and hypotheses. Each model records which conditions, if any, it\nadopts, and a model that adopts none is not thereby excluded.\n\n**(v) Scope and exclusions.** Idealizations such as unmodeled self-force,\nfinite-size and tidal corrections, dispersion, dissipation, and quantum effects\nare outside the framework unless the model's explicit equations include them;\nmatter concentrated in a distributional or lower-dimensional source, and\nmatter configurations whose fields fail to be smooth on the model domain, are\noutside the stated smooth branch and require additional mathematics. The\nadoption of a matter model is not a claim that it describes nature.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it records that matter fields, their equations and couplings, and the stress-energy tensor are model input; that prescribed stress-energy is admissible only under the consistency conditions of the adopted field law; that energy conditions are optional; and that distributional sources and unmodelled corrections lie outside the smooth branch.",
          "deps": [
            "post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain",
            "def-gr-stress-energy-tensor-field",
            "def-gr-energy-conditions",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-gr-stress-energy-tensor-field": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-gr-energy-conditions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Matter-content assumptions of a classical smooth gravitational model: the nongravitational fields and their explicit equations and couplings are model input; matter is represented by smooth fields whose stress-energy tensor occupies the slot of the stress-energy definition; consistency with the adopted field law and optional energy conditions. Excludes quantum matter, distributional or lower-dimensional sources in the smooth branch, unmodeled self-force, finite-size and tidal corrections, dispersion and dissipation, and any claim that a particular matter model describes nature.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), section 6.4, postulate 3 (energy, momentum and stresses of matter described by a symmetric conserved tensor), PDF p. 68",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
