      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-gr-tensor-bundle-coordinate-construction",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The tensor bundle on a smooth four-manifold from coordinate evaluations",
          "deps": [
            "def-smooth-manifold",
            "def-topological-manifold-without-boundary",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space",
            "thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space",
            "thm-coordinate-differentials-form-the-dual-cotangent-basis",
            "thm-change-of-coordinate-formula-for-tangent-bases",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-ck-euclidean-maps-closed-under-algebra-and-composition",
            "thm-vector-bundle-construction-from-a-smooth-cocycle",
            "def-smooth-section-local-section-and-support"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-smooth-manifold": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-topological-manifold-without-boundary": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-coordinate-differentials-form-the-dual-cotangent-basis": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-change-of-coordinate-formula-for-tangent-bases": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ck-euclidean-maps-closed-under-algebra-and-composition": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-vector-bundle-construction-from-a-smooth-cocycle": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-smooth-section-local-section-and-support": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. M. Lee, Introduction to Smooth Manifolds, 2nd ed., Chapter 12: tensor bundles and smooth tensor fields",
                "url": "https://julianchaidez.net/materials/reu/lee_smooth_manifolds.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $M$ be a smooth\nfour-manifold without boundary and $r,s\\ge0$ integers. Write\n$E=\\coprod_{p\\in M}\\operatorname{Mult}((T_p^*M)^r\\times(T_pM)^s,\\mathbb R)$\nand $\\pi(p,T)=p$. For a smooth chart $(U,x)$, let $C_x(T)$ be the array\nof the evaluations $T(dx^{a_1},\\ldots,dx^{a_r},\\partial_{x^{b_1}},\\ldots,\\partial_{x^{b_s}})$,\nwith all indices in $\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. Then the bijections\n$\\Phi_x:\\pi^{-1}(U)\\to U\\times\\mathbb R^{4^{r+s}}$, $\\Phi_x(p,T)=(p,C_x(T))$,\ndefine a unique smooth vector-bundle structure on $E$.\n\nFor $J_{yx}(p)=D(y\\circ x^{-1})(x(p))$, the transition\n$\\Phi_y\\Phi_x^{-1}$ multiplies each upper component index by $J_{yx}$\nand each lower component index by $J_{yx}^{-1}$, with the intermediate\nindices summed. These maps obey $g_{xx}=I$ and\n$g_{zy}g_{yx}=g_{zx}$. A family $p\\mapsto T_p$ is a smooth section\nexactly when its arrays $C_x(T_p)$ are smooth in the charts of one smooth\natlas, equivalently in every smooth chart. No metric or global frame is required.",
          "strategy": "Construct coordinate evaluation maps, derive correct Jacobian factors, prove identity and composition, apply the supplied countable cocycle construction and prove the smooth section criterion."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-gr-tensor-fields-and-components",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Tensor fields of type (r,s) on a Lorentzian four-manifold and their components",
          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\n([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]) and let $r,s\\ge0$ be integers.\n\n**(a) Tensors at a point.** For $p\\in M$, the tangent space $T_pM$ is the real\nvector space of derivations at $p$\n([[def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space]]) and the cotangent space is\nits algebraic dual, $T_p^*M=(T_pM)^*$\n([[def-cotangent-space-and-cotangent-bundle-as-a-disjoint-union]]). A **type\n$(r,s)$ tensor at $p$** is a multilinear map\n\n$$T_p:(T_p^*M)^r\\times(T_pM)^s\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,$$\n\nin the sense of [[def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space]]:\nthe first $r$ arguments are covectors and the last $s$ are vectors. In\nparticular a type $(1,0)$ tensor is a vector, a type $(0,1)$ tensor is a\ncovector and a type $(0,2)$ tensor is a bilinear form, so the covariant\n$2$-tensors of [[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]] are exactly the\ntype $(0,2)$ tensors of this definition.\n\n**(b) The tensor bundle and smooth fields.** The **type $(r,s)$ tensor bundle**\n$T^r_sM$ is the disjoint union of the fibres $T^r_sM|_p=\\operatorname{Mult}((T_p^*M)^r\\times(T_pM)^s,\\mathbb R)$\nover $p\\in M$ ([[def-type-r-s-tensor-bundle]]); it carries the smooth\nvector-bundle structure defined by the tensor-coordinate change rules\n([[lem-gr-tensor-bundle-coordinate-construction]]). A **smooth\ntensor field of type $(r,s)$** on $M$ is a family $T=(T_p)_{p\\in M}$ assigning\nto each $p$ a type $(r,s)$ tensor at $p$, such that for every smooth chart\n$(U,x)$ of the maximal smooth atlas the **component functions**\n\n$$T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}(q):=T_q\\bigl(dx^{a_1}|_q,\\dots,dx^{a_r}|_q,\\ \\partial_{b_1}|_q,\\dots,\\partial_{b_s}|_q\\bigr),\\qquad q\\in U,$$\n\nare $C^\\infty$ on $U$; here the coordinate derivations\n$\\partial_b|_q=\\partial/\\partial x^b|_q$ form a basis of $T_qM$\n([[thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space]]) and the\ncoordinate differentials $dx^a|_q$ form the dual basis of $T_q^*M$\n([[thm-coordinate-differentials-form-the-dual-cotangent-basis]]). This is\nequivalent to $T$ being a smooth section of $T^r_sM$ in the sense of\n[[def-smooth-tensor-field]]: the component functions are the frame\ncoefficients of the section in the local frame induced by the chart. The\nsmoothness condition is independent of the chosen smooth atlas and the\ncomponents in two overlapping charts are related by the transformation law\nproved in the lemma on this page; the definition is therefore chart\nindependent rather than merely chart-wise.\n\n**(c) Index conventions.** Indices are written $a,b,c,\\dots$ for components in\na chart, with the Einstein summation convention: a repeated index, once upper\nand once lower, is summed over $0,1,2,3$. An upper index is a contravariant\nslot in which a covector is evaluated, a lower index a covariant slot in which\na vector is evaluated. Slots are moved between these positions with the\nmusical isomorphisms $\\flat_p,\\sharp_p$ of\n[[def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms]] to change slot variance.\nFor example, if $T$ is a type $(1,1)$ field, lowering its contravariant slot\ngives the type $(0,2)$ field with components $T_{ab}:=g_{ac}T^c{}_b$; if $S$\nis a type $(0,2)$ field, raising its first covariant slot gives the type\n$(1,1)$ field with components $S^a{}_b:=g^{ac}S_{cb}$. Each operation changes\nthe tensor type and is invertible because $g_p$ is nondegenerate.\n\n**(d) Scope.** The definition is purely local and chart-based; no global\ntriviality of $T^r_sM$, no global frame and no choice of metric-compatible\nfields is assumed or implied. All fields are smooth ($C^\\infty$); no\nlower-regularity or distributional tensor field is defined here. The smooth\nstructure of the tangent bundle, and hence of $T^r_sM$\n([[lem-gr-tensor-bundle-coordinate-construction]]), inherits the\naxiom $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]] recorded with\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]; the component formulation of smoothness\nitself makes no choice.",
          "strategy": "Define type (r,s) tensor fields by chart components over the maximal atlas, with the tensor-bundle formulation recorded and index conventions fixed; chart independence and the component law are proved in the companion lemma, and contraction in the next.",
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
            "def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field",
            "def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms",
            "def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space",
            "def-type-r-s-tensor-bundle",
            "def-smooth-tensor-field",
            "lem-gr-tensor-bundle-coordinate-construction",
            "def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space",
            "def-cotangent-space-and-cotangent-bundle-as-a-disjoint-union",
            "thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space",
            "thm-coordinate-differentials-form-the-dual-cotangent-basis",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-type-r-s-tensor-bundle": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-smooth-tensor-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-gr-tensor-bundle-coordinate-construction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cotangent-space-and-cotangent-bundle-as-a-disjoint-union": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-coordinate-differentials-form-the-dual-cotangent-basis": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Ch. 1 'tensors', 'tensor products' and 'index manipulation' on components, contraction and raising and lowering (printed pp. 14-19; PDF pp. 21-26) and Ch. 2 'vectors as derivatives', 'coordinate bases' and 'the tensor transformation law' (printed pp. 44-48; PDF pp. 51-55)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), sections 1.6-1.8 on abstract index notation, tensors, tensor fields, components and coordinate changes, printed pp. 15-21 (PDF pp. 16-22)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. M. Lee, Introduction to Smooth Manifolds, 2nd ed., Ch. 12 'Tensors and Tensor Fields on Manifolds' (tensor bundles, sections and components), printed pp. 316-324",
                "url": "https://julianchaidez.net/materials/reu/lee_smooth_manifolds.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 3
        },
        {
          "id": "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Observers, rest spaces and the spatial projection at an event",
          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold and let\n$p\\in M$ ([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]).\n\n**(a) Observers.** An **observer at $p$** is a tangent vector\n$n\\in T_pM$ such that $g_p(n,n)=-1$ and $n$ is future-directed in the sense of\n[[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]], that is, $g_p(n,N_p)<0$. Because\n$g_p(n,n)=-1\\neq0$, an observer is timelike and in particular nonzero\n([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]). Observers at $p$ exist:\nthe vector $N_p$ is timelike, and\n$n:=N_p/\\sqrt{-g_p(N_p,N_p)}$ satisfies $g_p(n,n)=-1$ and\n$g_p(n,N_p)=-\\sqrt{-g_p(N_p,N_p)}<0$, where the square root is the\nnonnegative root of [[def-rational-power]]; then $-n$ is a unit timelike\nvector with $g_p(-n,N_p)>0$, so it is not an observer, and the two vectors\n$n,-n$ point in opposite time directions.\n\n**(b) Rest space.** For an observer $n$ at $p$, the **rest space** of $n$ is\nthe $g_p$-orthogonal complement of the line $\\mathbb Rn$,\n\n$$n^{\\perp}=\\{v\\in T_pM: g_p(v,n)=0\\},$$\n\nin the sense of [[def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]. It is\na linear subspace of $T_pM$. The further facts that $\\dim n^{\\perp}=3$ and\nthat the restriction of $g_p$ to $n^{\\perp}$ is positive definite are proved\nin [[lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition]]; they are consequences, not\npart of the definition.\n\n**(c) Spatial projection.** The **spatial projection** of the observer $n$ is\nthe map\n\n$$h_n:T_pM\\longrightarrow T_pM,\\qquad h_n(v):=v+g_p(n,v)\\,n .$$\n\nThe map is linear because $g_p$ is bilinear, its image is contained in\n$n^{\\perp}$, and it fixes $n^{\\perp}$ pointwise; that it is the\n$g_p$-orthogonal projection onto $n^{\\perp}$ with kernel $\\mathbb Rn$ is\nproved in [[lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition]].\n\n**(d) Scope.** An observer at $p$ is a single tangent vector. It is not an\nordered basis or frame of $T_pM$, not a chart, not a field of observers such as\nthe congruences defined later on this page, and not a curve or a worldline: no\nparametrized curve, clock reading, proper time or measurement is attached to\n$n$ by this definition. The physical reading of an observer as the direction\nof an ideal clock's worldline, and the measurement interpretation of the\nobserver-relative quantities introduced later on this page, belong to the\nphysical postulates and unit conventions of the framework and are not asserted\nhere.",
          "strategy": "Define observers as future-directed unit timelike vectors, rest spaces as orthogonal complements and the spatial projection by its formula; existence is by normalizing the time-orientation field, and the structural properties are proved in the companion lemma.",
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
            "def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-rational-power"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
