          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adopted coupling assumptions of the classical smooth gravitational model: ideal clocks measure the proper time of their future timelike worldlines; ideal freely falling massive test bodies follow future timelike geodesics of the metric-selected connection; ideal light rays are future null geodesics in the geometric-optics approximation. The assumptions are stated in the test-field regime of the smooth four-dimensional model and exclude self-force and radiation back-reaction, finite-size and tidal couplings beyond the test-body idealization, non-geometric-optics propagation, dispersion and dissipation, matter with non-negligible gravitational self-field, distributional or lower-dimensional sources, and quantum effects. They are adopted assumptions of the framework, not derived or empirical results; empirical comparison belongs to fully specified models and reported observations.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), section 3.3: the postulate that in GR free particles move on geodesics, timelike for massive particles and null for massless particles, and the remark that proper times along a geodesic differ by a constant, printed p. 38 (PDF p. 39); section 6.4: the test-body idealization and the geodesic postulate, printed pp. 57-58 (PDF pp. 58-59)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Ch. 1 'worldlines', 'proper time' and 'four-velocity' (1.97)-(1.99), printed pp. 25-27 (PDF pp. 32-34); Ch. 4 'the principle of equivalence' and 'physics in curved spacetime', printed pp. 97-106 (PDF pp. 104-113)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, Michaelmas 2019), sections 1.1.1-1.1.2: the point-particle action, its geodesic equation (1.28)-(1.30), the affine parameter and the null case for light, printed pp. 24-30 (PDF pp. 31-37); section 3.3.2: the local-inertial-frame reading and its limitations, printed pp. 121-122 (PDF pp. 128-129)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/2023id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 9
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-gr-free-fall-worldline-is-determined-by-initial-data",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The free-fall worldline of an ideal test body is determined by its initial event and four-velocity",
          "statement": "Let a model of the classical gravitational framework be specified by a\ntime-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ with its\nmetric-selected connection\n([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]],\n[[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]), and assume the\nclock, free-fall and light-ray assumptions of\n[[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]]. Let $p\\in M$ and let\n$U_0\\in T_pM$ be a future timelike unit vector,\n$g_p(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$ ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]).\n\nSuppose $B_1$ and $B_2$ are two ideal freely falling massive test bodies of\nthe model that are released at the event $p$ with physical four-velocity $U_0$:\ntheir worldlines $\\gamma_i:I_i\\to M$ pass through $p$ and their four-vectors\nthere equal $U_0$. Let $\\tau_i$ be their proper times, with origins chosen so\nthat $0\\in I_i$ and $\\gamma_i(0)=p$. Let\n$\\gamma_{p,U_0}:I_{p,U_0}\\to M$ be the unique maximal geodesic through\n$(p,U_0)$. Then each physical worldline is a restriction of that maximal\ngeodesic:\n\n$$I_i\\subseteq I_{p,U_0},\\qquad \\gamma_i(\\tau)=\\gamma_{p,U_0}(\\tau)\\quad(\\tau\\in I_i),\\qquad i=1,2.$$\n\nIn particular, $\\gamma_1$ and $\\gamma_2$ coincide wherever both are defined.\nThe theorem does not assume that either physical worldline has the entire\nmaximal geodesic's domain. The unique geodesic continuation is determined by\nthe release event and the initial four-velocity alone; parameters that\ndistinguish the two bodies, such as their rest masses, do not enter.",
          "strategy": "Derive from the adopted free-fall rule and the unique maximal geodesic through (p,U_0) that two ideal freely falling massive test bodies released at the same event with the same four-velocity coincide, and that their common worldline is independent of rest mass and other body parameters.",
          "deps": [
            "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
            "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
            "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
            "def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection",
            "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map",
            "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time",
            "lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall": "physical-assumption",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
