          "dependency_level": 10
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-gr-normal-coordinates-and-local-inertial-frames-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Scope of normal coordinates and of the local-inertial-frame reading",
          "statement": "This remark separates the mathematics of\n[[thm-gr-point-normal-and-exponential-coordinate-charts]] and\n[[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]] from the physical reading\nusually attached to them, and is bookkeeping: it establishes no mathematical\nresult and no physical law.\n\n- **What the construction gives.** For every event $p$ there is a chart with\n  $g_{ab}(p)=\\eta_{ab}$, $\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}(p)=0$ and\n  $\\partial_ag_{bc}(p)=0$, and in the exponential chart of an orthonormal\n  basis the geodesics through $p$ are the straight coordinate lines through\n  the origin\n  ([[thm-gr-point-normal-and-exponential-coordinate-charts]]). The identities\n  are first order at the single point $p$.\n\n- **What it does not give.** The construction asserts nothing about the second\n  and higher derivatives of the metric, and therefore nothing about the\n  curvature of the model; a chart of this kind is not an isometry and does not\n  make any neighborhood of $p$ identical with a region of Minkowski space.\n  The remark makes no curvature claim here; the second-order behavior is the\n  subject of the curvature material of this track, which is a later page of\n  the same category.\n\n- **No global inertial frame, no congruence.** A chart with the normal-form\n  identities at one event is not a global label of events by inertial\n  coordinates, and the exponential charts at different points are not\n  compatible frames. An \"observer\" in this library is a single future unit\n  timelike vector at a point, and a \"congruence\" is a field of such vectors\n  on an open set ([[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]],\n  [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]); a freely falling\n  observer is one timelike geodesic, not a global reference frame. Charts\n  adapted to a congruence are a different construction from the normal\n  coordinates of a geodesic through a point.\n\n- **The physical reading is adopted, not derived.** The statement that an\n  ideal freely falling test body or observer, using normal coordinates around\n  an event of its own worldline, finds the local form of the metric to be that\n  of Minkowski space at first order — and the associated local\n  inertial-frame reading of the equivalence principle — depends on the\n  adopted clock and free-fall assumptions of\n  [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]]. It is a statement about the model in\n  the test-body idealization, not a derivation from the geometry alone and not\n  an empirical claim that real experiments cannot detect a gravitational\n  field locally. The limitations recorded in the postulate — finite-size and\n  tidal couplings of extended bodies, self-force, and nonlocal measurements —\n  are precisely what the first-order identities cannot exclude.",
          "strategy": "Separate the first-order normal-coordinate mathematics from its physical reading: no second-order or neighborhood-flatness claim, no global inertial frame, no observer congruence, and no empirical equivalence-principle statement.",
          "deps": [
            "thm-gr-point-normal-and-exponential-coordinate-charts",
            "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map",
            "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall",
            "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
            "def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts",
            "def-gr-metric-selected-connection"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-gr-point-normal-and-exponential-coordinate-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall": "physical-assumption",
            "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Scope note on the physical reading of point-normal and exponential coordinate charts: what the construction does and does not assert about local inertial frames, freely falling observers and the equivalence principle in the classical smooth model. Excludes global frames, neighborhood flatness, curvature claims, and any empirical equivalence-principle statement.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity (Cambridge, October 12, 2022 revision), section 3.4: normal coordinates are valid at the point p only and the connection components away from p need not vanish, printed pp. 38-40 (PDF pp. 39-41); section 6.1: the equivalence principle is a motivating principle, printed pp. 57-59 (PDF pp. 58-60)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20230323191108id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Ch. 2 'Riemann normal coordinates', printed pp. 50-51 (PDF pp. 57-58), and Ch. 4 'the principle of equivalence', printed pp. 97-99 (PDF pp. 104-106)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, Michaelmas 2019), section 3.3.2 'The Equivalence Principle' with the limitations of the local statement, printed pp. 121-122 (PDF pp. 128-129)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/2023id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 10
        }
      ],
