          "physical_scope": "Conditional model deduction inside the classical gravitational framework with the field law in its direct-equation presentation: the Schwarzschild exterior datum of mass parameter m_* > 0 with the vacuum stress-energy prescription T = 0 and zero cosmological parameter is a model of the framework satisfying the adopted field law; its ideal static clocks read the proper times of the static congruence, and the parameter enters the model through the Schwarzschild radius, the circular-orbit angular velocity and the leading far-field coefficient. Excludes: any claim that the model or its vacuum content is realized in nature, any empirical comparison (no observation, apparatus, calibration or uncertainty), non-vacuum matter, nonzero Lambda, the interior and horizon region (which belongs to the extension and Kruskal items of the page), distributional sources, and quantum effects. All conclusions are conditional on the adopted postulates.",
          "dependency_level": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-gr-schwarzschild-stationary-redshift",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Restricted redshift prediction of the Schwarzschild vacuum model: the observer-energy ratio between static observers",
          "statement": "Adopt the Schwarzschild exterior vacuum model of\n[[pthm-gr-schwarzschild-vacuum-model]]: the exterior datum $(M,g,N)$ of mass\nparameter $m_*>0$ with $s=2G_Nm_*/c^2$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$\n([[def-gr-schwarzschild-exterior-model]]), its metric-selected connection\n$\\nabla$ ([[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]],\n[[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]), its static\ncongruence $n=f^{-1/2}\\partial_0$\n([[lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates]]), and the light-ray\nrule that ideal light rays are future null geodesics of $\\nabla$\n([[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]] clause (iii)). Let $\\eta:J\\to M$ be\nthe specified ray, a smooth affinely parametrized geodesic of $\\nabla$ on an\ninterval $J$, with tangent $W=\\eta'$, $g(W,W)=0$ and $W$ future-directed\n([[def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection]],\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]). Let\n$\\lambda_E,\\lambda_R\\in J$ with $\\lambda_E<\\lambda_R$ and suppose\n$\\eta(\\lambda_E)$ and $\\eta(\\lambda_R)$ lie on static observer worldlines of\nradii $r_E=r(\\eta(\\lambda_E))$ and $r_R=r(\\eta(\\lambda_R))$ with\n$r_E,r_R\\in(s,\\infty)$. For each parameter value define the **observer energy\nof the ray relative to the static observers** by\n\n$$E(\\lambda):=-c\\,g_{\\eta(\\lambda)}\\bigl(W(\\lambda),n(\\eta(\\lambda))\\bigr),$$\n\nthe value of the observer-energy definition of\n[[def-gr-observer-relative-energy-and-momentum]] clause (a) for the unit\nobserver $n$. Then:\n\n**(a) Constancy of the Killing pairing.** $K=\\partial_0$ is a Killing field of\n$g$ and the function $C(\\lambda):=-g_{\\eta(\\lambda)}(W(\\lambda),K(\\eta(\\lambda)))$\nis constant along $J$;\nmoreover $C>0$.\n\n**(b) The observer-energy ratio.** $E(\\lambda)>0$ along the ray and\n\n$$\\frac{E(\\lambda_R)}{E(\\lambda_E)}=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(r_E)}{f(r_R)}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-s/r_E}{1-s/r_R}} .$$\n\n**(c) Redshift parameter.** If the **redshift parameter** of the pair of events\nis defined by $z:=\\dfrac{E(\\lambda_E)}{E(\\lambda_R)}-1$, equivalently by\n$1+z=E(\\lambda_E)/E(\\lambda_R)$, then\n\n$$1+z=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(r_R)}{f(r_E)}} .$$\n\nThe ratio in (b) and (c) is unchanged if $W$ is replaced by $\\mu W$ with a\npositive constant $\\mu$: a future null ray determines its future tangent only\nup to positive scale,\nand only the ratio of energies is asserted. No observed wavelength, spectral\nline, calibration or measurement enters the statement; the identification of\nthe ratio with a frequency ratio of an ideal ray is part of the framework's\ngeometric-optics reading, and moving observers require additional factors.\n\n**(d) Scope.** The statement is a model prediction for the specified ideal\nray and the specified static observers; it asserts no redshift measurement and\nno property of any real source, and it excludes non-geometric-optics\npropagation, dispersion and any apparatus or analysis model.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
