        {
          "id": "def-gr-flrw-friedmann-system",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Friedmann, acceleration and continuity equations of an FLRW model datum",
          "statement": "**(a) Data.** A **Friedmann-system datum** is a tuple\n\n$$(c,G_N,\\Lambda,I,k,a,\\varepsilon,p)$$\n\nconsisting of positive real constants $c>0$ and $G_N>0$, a real constant\n$\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, a nonempty open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$\n([[def-interval]]), a real number $k$ (the **curvature parameter** of the\ndatum), and smooth functions $a:I\\to(0,\\infty)$, $\\varepsilon:I\\to\\mathbb R$\nand $p:I\\to\\mathbb R$, with values in the open interval of strictly positive\nreals in the case of $a$\n([[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]). We write\n$\\dot a,\\ddot a,\\dot\\varepsilon$ for the derivatives of these functions of one\nvariable and\n\n$$H:=\\frac{\\dot a}{a}:I\\to\\mathbb R$$\n\nfor the **Hubble parameter** of the datum ([[def-derivative]]). No relation\nbetween $k$ and the three signs $\\{-1,0,1\\}$ is imposed by this definition;\nthe spatial model data of [[def-gr-flrw-model-data]] restrict to those signs,\nand the real parameter is used so that the equations can also be examined\nafter a rescaling of the spatial metric.\n\n**(b) The equations.** For a Friedmann-system datum define the following three\nscalar equations on $I$:\n\n**(F) The Friedmann equation.**\n\n$$H(t)^2+\\frac{kc^2}{a(t)^2}=\\frac{8\\pi G_N}{3c^2}\\,\\varepsilon(t)+\\frac{\\Lambda c^2}{3}\\qquad\\text{for all }t\\in I;$$\n\n**(A) The acceleration equation.**\n\n$$\\frac{\\ddot a(t)}{a(t)}=-\\frac{4\\pi G_N}{3c^2}\\bigl(\\varepsilon(t)+3p(t)\\bigr)+\\frac{\\Lambda c^2}{3}\\qquad\\text{for all }t\\in I;$$\n\n**(C) The continuity equation.**\n\n$$\\dot\\varepsilon(t)+3H(t)\\bigl(\\varepsilon(t)+p(t)\\bigr)=0\\qquad\\text{for all }t\\in I .$$\n\nWe also record the **spatial-component equation**\n\n**(S)**\n\n$$-\\frac{2a(t)\\ddot a(t)+\\dot a(t)^2}{c^2}-k+\\Lambda a(t)^2=\\frac{8\\pi G_N}{c^4}\\,p(t)\\,a(t)^2\\qquad\\text{for all }t\\in I .$$\n\nEach of (F), (A), (C), (S) is an equality of real numbers at each point of\n$I$; the equations involve the data of clause (a) alone. Their relation to the\ncurvature components of an FLRW metric, and their logical relations among one\nanother, are the content of the results on this page and are not asserted by\nthis definition. In particular, this definition adopts no equation of state:\na relation $p=P(\\varepsilon)$, when used, is an additional datum.",
          "strategy": null,
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-flrw-model-data",
            "def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds",
            "def-interval",
            "def-derivative"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-gr-flrw-model-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Ch. 8 (8.20)-(8.23) and (8.35)-(8.36): the conservation equation, the equation of state p = w rho and the two Friedmann equations, printed pp. 221-223 (PDF pp. 228-230)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, Michaelmas 2019), section 4.6.2: the continuity equation, the Friedmann equation (4.66) and the acceleration equation (4.67) with the cosmological term, printed pp. 201-203 (PDF pp. 208-210)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/2023id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/gr.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Cosmology (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, 2019 revision), section 1.2.2 and section 1.2.3: the continuity equation (1.39), the Friedmann equation (1.42) with c restored, printed pp. 28-29 (PDF pp. 34-35)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/2023id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/cosmo/cosmo.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 3
        },
        {
          "id": "prop-gr-flrw-equation-equivalences",
          "kind": "proposition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Equivalence of the Friedmann and acceleration systems, and the derivation of acceleration from Friedmann and continuity where the Hubble parameter does not vanish",
          "statement": "Let $(c,G_N,\\Lambda,I,k,a,\\varepsilon,p)$ be a Friedmann-system datum, with the\nequations (F), (A), (C), (S) as in\n[[def-gr-flrw-friedmann-system]] and $H=\\dot a/a$. Then:\n\n**(a)** On the interval $I$, the pair of equations (F) and (S) holds if and\nonly if the pair of equations (F) and (A) holds.\n\n**(b)** If (F) and (C) hold on $I$ and $t_0\\in I$ satisfies\n$H(t_0)\\neq0$, then equation (A) holds at $t_0$. In particular, on any\nsubinterval on which $H$ has no zero, (F) and (C) imply (A).\n\nThe proof of (b) divides by $H(t_0)$; the proposition makes no claim about\npoints with $H(t_0)=0$, and no claim that (F) and (C) alone determine the\nsecond derivative of $a$ there.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-flrw-friedmann-system",
            "def-derivative",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-gr-flrw-friedmann-system": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1 (1997), Ch. 8 (8.33)-(8.36): the 00 and ij equations and the elimination of second derivatives leading to the Friedmann equations, printed pp. 222-223 (PDF pp. 229-230)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, General Relativity (Cambridge Part III Mathematical Tripos, Michaelmas 2019), section 4.6.2: the acceleration equation and the remark that it follows by differentiating the Friedmann equation and using conservation, printed p. 203 (PDF p. 210)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/2023id_/https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/gr.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 4
        },
        {
          "id": "post-gr-flrw-homogeneous-cosmological-model",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted homogeneous and isotropic cosmological model with a comoving perfect fluid",
          "statement": "Adopt as the geometry and matter content of a **homogeneous cosmological\nmodel** of the classical gravitational framework, to be used together with\n[[post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain]],\n[[post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions]] and the field law of\n[[post-gr-einstein-field-law]], the following assumptions.\n\n**(i) Spacetime geometry.** The model's spacetime is an FLRW model datum\n$(I,\\Sigma,\\gamma,k,a)$ with the framework's conversion constant $c>0$, its\nmetric $g=-c^2dt^2+a(t)^2\\gamma$ and its comoving congruence\n$n=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ ([[def-gr-flrw-model-data]],\n[[lem-gr-flrw-metric-and-comoving-data]]). The spatial model\n$(\\Sigma,\\gamma,k)$ is one of the standard complete simply connected models of\n[[def-gr-flrw-spatial-model]] clause (d), with normalized curvature parameter\n$k\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$: the round unit three-sphere for $k=+1$, Euclidean space for\n$k=0$, or hyperbolic three-space for $k=-1$; by\n[[lem-gr-constant-curvature-three-manifold-ricci]] each of these carries\nconstant sectional curvature $k$ and $\\operatorname{Ric}(\\gamma)=2k\\gamma$.\nThe name **homogeneous and isotropic cosmological model** denotes here the\nexplicit standard-spatial-model FLRW ansatz just specified. Its operative\ngeometric assumptions are the chosen global standard model, the displayed\nmetric and the curvature condition; the deductions do not use an additional\nundefined symmetry premise. The identification of the coordinate\n$t$ with the readings of ideal comoving clocks is not part of this clause: it\nis the separate clock rule of [[post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall]], applied\nonly where a physical clock reading is asserted. The\nmathematical content used by the deductions of this page is the FLRW form\ndisplayed above together with the curvature condition; no classification\ntheorem, and no transitivity statement about isometry groups, is proved or\nused here.\n\n**(ii) Matter content.** Exactly one of the following two sectors is adopted.\n\n- **Vacuum sector.** No matter is present: the model's stress-energy tensor is\n  the zero tensor, $\\varepsilon=p=0$.\n- **Comoving perfect-fluid sector.** The model's matter is a perfect-fluid\n  model $(\\varepsilon,p,n_F)$ on the matter domain $U:=M$, the model's whole\n  spacetime, in the sense of [[def-gr-perfect-fluid-matter-model]]: that is,\n  $U=M$ is the nonempty open domain, $\\varepsilon$ and $p$ are the smooth\n  rest energy density and pressure on it, and the fluid's unit timelike\n  four-velocity field $n_F$ is assumed to be the comoving congruence\n  $n=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ of clause (i). The density and pressure are assumed\n  **homogeneous**, that is, functions of cosmic time alone:\n  $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon(t)$ and $p=p(t)$ in the product charts, with\n  values in the units of [[def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions]]. The\n  model additionally adopts an **explicit equation of state**, one of:\n  a barotropic relation $p=P(\\varepsilon)$ with $P$ a smooth real function\n  defined on an open interval containing the range of $\\varepsilon$; or the\n  constant-parameter relation $p=w\\varepsilon$ with a real constant $w$ and\n  the stated domain of positive $\\varepsilon$. The equation of state is an\n  adopted constitutive restriction, not a consequence of the field law.\n  The fluid stress-energy $T_{ab}=(\\varepsilon+p)n_an_b+pg_{ab}$ is the\n  gravitating source in $G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=(8\\pi G_N/c^4)T_{ab}$\n  ([[post-gr-einstein-field-law]]). The scale factor and fluid data must\n  jointly satisfy that law when a solution is claimed; the fluid is not\n  treated as test matter on a separately prescribed background.\n\n**(iii) Status, scope and exclusions.** Clauses (i) and (ii) are adopted\nassumptions of the model. They are not derived from the field law, from\nmathematical symmetry, or from observation; the symmetry of the ansatz\nmotivates but does not prove them, and no claim is made that any actual\ncosmological system is exactly homogeneous, isotropic, comoving or\nbarotropic. The model contains no observed value: the scale factor, the\ndensities, the parameters $k,\\Lambda,w$ and the spacetime interval are model\ndata, and every conclusion conditional on the model inherits the idealizations\nlisted in the physical scope of this item. Anisotropic stresses, heat flow and\ndissipation, spatial inhomogeneities and perturbations, distributional or\nlower-dimensional sources, non-smooth fields and quantum effects lie outside\nthe model and require additional content. Comparisons of the model's\npredictions with reported observations test the combined model — geometry,\nmatter content, field law and the observation and analysis assumptions — and\nagreement is evidence about that combination, not a proof of this postulate.",
          "strategy": null,
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-flrw-model-data",
            "def-gr-flrw-spatial-model",
            "lem-gr-flrw-metric-and-comoving-data",
            "lem-gr-constant-curvature-three-manifold-ricci",
            "def-gr-perfect-fluid-matter-model",
            "def-gr-si-unit-and-coordinate-conventions",
            "post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain",
            "post-gr-field-and-matter-assumptions",
            "post-gr-einstein-field-law",
            "post-gr-ideal-clocks-and-free-fall"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-gr-flrw-model-data": "mathematical-premise",
