          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "H. Friedrich and A. D. Rendall, The Cauchy Problem for the Einstein Equations, Living Reviews in Relativity 3 (2000), article 1 (arXiv:gr-qc/0002074), Section 4.2 (The Einstein-Euler System): the hyperbolic reduction of the Einstein-Euler system, the positivity conditions on specific enthalpy and speed of sound (4.38), the frame variables and their reduced equations, and the pressure-free (dust) subsidiary system of Sections 4.2.4-4.2.5, equations (4.26)-(4.48) and (4.56)-(4.63)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/0002074"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-gr-dust-weyl-reconstruction-from-electric-magnetic",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Reconstruction of a Weyl tensor from its electric and magnetic parts: symmetries, trace-freeness and recovery",
          "statement": "Work in the frame conventions of\n[[def-gr-dust-reduction-variables-and-gauge]] and let $E_{ij},B_{ij}$ be\nsymmetric spatial tracefree fields, that is $E_{ij}U^j=B_{ij}U^j=0$ and $h^{ij}E_{ij}=h^{ij}B_{ij}=0$. Define $C$ by the\nreconstruction formula of\n[[def-gr-dust-electric-magnetic-weyl-variables-and-schouten]] clause (d). Then:\n\n**(a)** $C_{ijkl}$ is antisymmetric in $(i,j)$ and in $(k,l)$, symmetric under\nthe exchange of the pairs $(ij)\\leftrightarrow(kl)$, satisfies the first\nBianchi (cyclic) identity $C_{i[jkl]}=0$, and is trace-free in every pair of\ncontracted indices;\n\n**(b)** the electric and magnetic parts of $C$ with respect to $U$ are\n$C_{0i0j}=E_{ij}$ and $C^*{}_{0i0j}=B_{ij}$;\n\n**(c)** consequently if $E,B$ are in addition trace-free with respect to $h$,\nthen $C$ is a Weyl tensor and the map $(E,B)\\mapsto C$ is a bijection onto the\nWeyl tensors with the given $U$-electric and $U$-magnetic parts.",
          "strategy": "direct",
