        {
          "id": "lem-gr-scalar-einstein-symmetric-hyperbolic-reduction",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Symmetric hyperbolic reduction of the Einstein-scalar system and on-shell conservation",
          "statement": "Fix one scalar model with smooth potential $V$ and fixed stress normalization $K_\\phi$ as in [[def-gr-scalar-field-matter-model]]. On a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold its equations and stress are\n$$\\Box_g\\varphi-V'(\\varphi)=0,\\qquad T_{ab}=K_\\phi\\left[\\partial_a\\varphi\\partial_b\\varphi-g_{ab}\\left(\\tfrac12g^{cd}\\partial_c\\varphi\\partial_d\\varphi+V(\\varphi)\\right)\\right].$$\nFix a chart and a smooth background $\\bar g$ for the reduced Einstein equation of [[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]]. On the open state set where $g^{00}<0$ and $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite, the reduced Einstein equation coupled to this scalar equation has a smooth first-order symmetric hyperbolic reduction with variables\n$$z=(g_{ab},p_{ab}=\\partial_0g_{ab},q_{abi}=\\partial_ig_{ab},\\varphi,P=\\partial_0\\varphi,Q_i=\\partial_i\\varphi).$$\nThe time matrix is block diagonal with metric block $\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ and scalar block $\\operatorname{diag}(1,-g^{00},g^{ij})$. Matter and metric cross-coupling is zeroth order in derivatives of $z$. The partial-derivative metric variables and the background-covariant ones are related by an invertible smooth zeroth-order change of variables; either gives the reduction.\n\nFor complete initial scalar data $(\\varphi_0,\\pi_0)$ in an initially orthogonal chart with $\\partial_0=n$, prescribe $(\\varphi,P,Q_i)=(\\varphi_0,\\pi_0,\\partial_i\\varphi_0)$. The auxiliary constraints propagate, the reconstructed scalar solves its full equation on the reduced metric, and its stress is conserved there before imposing the unreduced Einstein equation. Conversely every smooth physical scalar solution extracts to the same system. No potential positivity or mass sign is required; local arguments do not require a global orientation.",
