        {
          "id": "thm-gr-barotropic-fluid-local-evolution",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Local evolution for a strict barotropic perfect fluid: smooth constrained data admit a development, unique up to a data-fixing isometry",
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and Dependent Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]). Fix $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\kappa>0$ and one smooth EOS $p=p(\\epsilon)$ on an open interval $J$ with $\\epsilon+p>0$ and $0<p'(\\epsilon)<1$ ([[def-gr-perfect-fluid-matter-model]]). Let $\\Sigma$ be a smooth three-manifold with smooth metric $h$, symmetric tensor $K_2$, density $\\epsilon_0:\\Sigma\\to J$ and tangent field $v_0$ with $h(v_0,v_0)<1$. These specify the future unit initial velocity\n$$U_0=W_0(N+v_0),\\qquad W_0=(1-h(v_0,v_0))^{-1/2}$$\nin $\\mathcal V=\\mathbb RN\\oplus T\\Sigma$, with metric $g_0(aN+X,bN+Y)=-ab+h(X,Y)$. Require the constraints of [[thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame]] for\n$$T_0=(\\epsilon_0+p(\\epsilon_0))U_0^{\\flat_{g_0}}\\otimes U_0^{\\flat_{g_0}}+p(\\epsilon_0)g_0.$$\nThen a smooth time-oriented local development has metric $g$, density $\\epsilon$ and future unit fluid velocity $u$ satisfying\n$$G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa\\left[(\\epsilon+p)u_au_b+pg_{ab}\\right],\\qquad D\\epsilon+(\\epsilon+p)\\vartheta=0,\\qquad (\\epsilon+p)a^b+\\Pi^{ba}\\partial_ap=0.$$\nHere $D=\\nabla_u$, $\\vartheta=\\nabla_a u^a$, $a=\\nabla_u u$ and $\\Pi^{ab}=g^{ab}+u^au^b$. Initial geometric data are $(h,K_2)$ and initial matter data are $(\\epsilon_0,U_0)$ under $N\\mapsto n$, $X\\mapsto i_*X$. Arbitrary smooth subluminal tilt is allowed. The initial surface lies in the interior of its intrinsic domain of dependence; its interior Cauchy neighborhood is globally hyperbolic. Any two developments of this same EOS and complete data agree near the entire initial surface by a data-fixing future isometry matching density and velocity. The neighborhood may have position-dependent time width; shocks, vacuum boundaries and vanishing sound speed are outside this strict interior-fluid statement ([[thm-gr-local-geometric-development]]).",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-perfect-fluid-matter-model",
            "def-gr-barotropic-enthalpy-variable",
