        {
          "id": "thm-gr-dust-local-evolution",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Local Einstein-dust evolution: smooth constrained data with prescribed velocity admit a smooth local development, unique up to a data-fixing isometry",
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) and Dependent Choice ([[def-dependent-choice]]). Fix constants $c>0$, $\\kappa>0$ and $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$. Let $\\Sigma$ be a smooth three-manifold with smooth Riemannian metric $h$, smooth symmetric tensor $K$ and a smooth mass-normalized density $\\rho_0\\ge0$. Specify initial velocity as a smooth section $U_0$ of $\\mathcal V=\\mathbb RN\\oplus T\\Sigma$, with the bundle metric $g_0(aN+X,bN+Y)=-ab+h(X,Y)$, satisfying $g_0(U_0,U_0)=-1$ and positive $N$ coefficient. Thus the velocity need not be normal and does not presuppose a spacetime development. Put $\\varepsilon_0=\\rho_0c^2$ and require the constraints of [[thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame]] for $T_0=\\varepsilon_0U_0^{\\flat_{g_0}}\\otimes U_0^{\\flat_{g_0}}$. On $\\{\\rho_0>0\\}$ this is exactly the dust supplier with density parameter $\\varepsilon_0$, rather than $\\rho_0$ ([[def-gr-dust-matter-model]]); zero regions have independently prescribed velocity.\n\nThen there is a smooth local development: a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian metric $g$, density $\\rho\\ge0$ and future unit timelike field $u$ near an embedded copy of $\\Sigma$, solving\n$$G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa\\rho c^2u_au_b,\\qquad \\nabla_uu=0,\\qquad u(\\rho)+\\rho\\nabla\\!\\cdot u=0.$$\nThe induced geometric and matter data are $(h,K,\\rho_0,U_0)$, with $\\mathcal V$ identified by $aN+X\\mapsto an+i_*X$ using the future unit normal $n$. The initial surface lies in the interior of its domain of dependence; restriction to its Cauchy neighborhood gives a local Cauchy development. Every two such developments agree near $\\Sigma$ by a data-fixing time-orientation-preserving isometry matching $\\rho,u$. No division by density is used, so smooth empty regions are admitted. The neighborhood may have position-dependent time width; no uniform noncompact slab or continuation past flow crossings is asserted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
