        {
          "id": "rem-gr-high-regularity-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Scope and status of the Gr14 high-regularity symmetric hyperbolic material",
          "statement": "This page contains the Gr14 high-regularity symmetric hyperbolic proof material:\nthe periodic first-order theory (E0), the harmonic-gauge reduced Einstein\nequation and its subsidiary system (E1), the geometric data and local\ndevelopment theorems (E2), and the selected matter reductions with the complete\ngeodesic-dust residual chain (E3, E3d--E3d.2). Its content assumes Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) for local analytic extraction and manifold patching, and Dependent Choice ([[def-dependent-choice]]) for the causal-curve calculus in the Cauchy-neighborhood argument. Its physical content is conditional:\nthe physical reading is the model deduction of\n[[pthm-gr-high-regularity-local-development]] from the adopted postulates\n[[post-gr-einstein-field-law]] and the matter definitions\n[[def-gr-scalar-field-matter-model]], [[def-gr-perfect-fluid-matter-model]],\n[[def-gr-dust-matter-model]]; the mathematical carriers are\n[[thm-gr-high-regularity-selected-matter-development]] and its prerequisites.\n\n**What is established.** Local-in-time smooth developments of the adopted field\nlaw with the selected matter sectors, from smooth constrained data, with\nuniqueness near the initial surface up to a data-fixing time-orientation-\npreserving isometry, and with the initial surface lying in the interior of its\ndomain of dependence.\n\n**What is not established or assumed away.** No global or all-time existence,\nno uniform existence time on noncompact data, and no maximality or boundary\nbehaviour of the spacetime development (that is the scope of the successor\npage). The matter results do not treat shocks, nonsmooth free boundaries,\nnonsmooth vacuum interfaces, junction conditions, distributional or\nlow-regularity sources, or boundary layers; the theorem works in the\nsmooth/high-regularity branch with $s\\ge5$ and makes no optimal Sobolev\nthreshold claim. These exclusions are distinct from smooth zeros of the dust\ndensity. For dust, the construction occurs before flow crossings and a smooth\nfuture unit velocity is prescribed and remains geodesic even where the smooth\nnonnegative energy coefficient $\\varepsilon$ vanishes. Thus smooth zero sets\nand open empty regions are included, with no division by density. The source\ndensity parameter is $\\varepsilon$, with mass-normalized\n$\\rho_{\\rm mass}=\\varepsilon/c^2$ for every $c>0$; in the reduction notation\n$r=\\varepsilon$ solves $\\partial_sr=-r\\chi$ with the explicit exponential\nsolution of [[thm-gr-dust-local-evolution]]. For the fluid branch the strict\nhypotheses $\\epsilon+p>0$, $0<p'<1$ are essential; the failure at negative\n$p'$ is the companion counterexample on the examples page. No statement is\nmade that any model here describes nature, and no empirical premise enters.\n\n**Conventions and sources.** The reduction uses $\\gamma=-g$ and the explicit Schouten normalization and curvature decomposition of its definition. Vacuum reduction signs therefore differ from the original metric signs by that constant flip. The physical unit convention, including the distinction between the normalized velocity $u$ with $g(u,u)=-1$ and the SI proper-time four-velocity $U_{\\rm SI}=cu$ with $g(U_{\\rm SI},U_{\\rm SI})=-c^2$, and the selected scalar, fluid and dust units are stated in [[pthm-gr-high-regularity-local-development]]. The local dust proof derives the electric/magnetic projections and the Cartan subsidiary system in those conventions; its magnetic double-epsilon term has the sign required by trace preservation. Friedrich and Rendall supplies derivation provenance; the displayed local arguments carry the deductions.",
          "strategy": null,
          "deps": [
            "thm-gr-dust-local-evolution",
            "pthm-gr-high-regularity-local-development",
            "thm-gr-high-regularity-selected-matter-development",
