      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "SI quantities and unit conventions",
          "statement": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m³); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_roles": {},
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "BIPM, The International System of Units, 9th edition, version 4.01, §§2.3.1–2.3.2 and Table 4",
                "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "SI types and units used by the nonrelativistic macroscopic thermodynamic framework; this item does not define a thermometer calibration or an equation of state."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Thermodynamic systems and permitted exchanges",
          "statement": "A thermodynamic system $s$ is a model-selected body or collection of bodies together with a specified outer boundary $\\partial s$. The boundary separates the system from its environment for the purpose of classifying transfers; it need not be a material wall. A compound system has named subsystems inside one outer boundary. Transfers between its subsystems are internal to the compound; only transfers across $\\partial s$ are external.\n\nThe model specifies which transfers across $\\partial s$ are permitted. A system is closed when no matter crosses its outer boundary; energy may cross. A system is open when only the named matter species and other named transfer channels may cross its boundary. A system or compound is isolated when no matter or energy crosses its outer boundary. These terms classify the declared model boundary, not the accuracy with which a real apparatus approximates it.\n\nThis boundary classification alone asserts neither an energy-balance law nor equilibration.",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_roles": {},
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Ling, Moebs, and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §3.1 Thermodynamic Systems",
                "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-1-thermodynamic-systems"
              },
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §II.A",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Boundary and exchange classifications for the nonrelativistic macroscopic thermodynamic framework; no energy-balance law is asserted here."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-galilean-rest-frame",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Galilean inertial charts and the center-of-mass rest frame",
          "statement": "For this nonrelativistic framework, a Newtonian event-coordinate domain is the Cartesian product $I\\times\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-cartesian-product]]), where $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an open time interval as in [[def-interval]], measured in seconds, and $\\mathbb R^3$ has the Euclidean inner product and norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] (the norm properties follow from [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]); lengths are measured in metres as in [[def-td-si-quantities]]. A Galilean inertial chart labels events in such a domain by coordinates $(t,\\mathbf x)$. If a second chart uses the translated time domain $I'=I+\\tau$, the allowed coordinate changes are\n$$t'=t+\\tau,\\qquad \\mathbf x'=R\\mathbf x+\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b t,$$\nwhere $\\tau$ is a constant time shift measured in seconds, $\\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is a constant spatial translation measured in metres, $\\mathbf b\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is a constant boost velocity measured in metres per second, and $R\\in M_{3\\times3}(\\mathbb R)$ is a dimensionless constant matrix ([[def-matrix-space]]) satisfying $R^{\\mathsf T}R=I_3$ using matrix transpose and product ([[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]], [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]), with $I_3$ the identity matrix. In the coordinate formula, $(R\\mathbf x)_i:=\\sum_{k<3}R_{ik}x_k$ for $i<3$. Thus time is absolute in this model and no Lorentzian structure is used. Here “frame” means this coordinate chart, not an observer congruence or a basis/tetrad field.\n\nFor a finite point-mass representation with $N\\in\\mathbb N$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), $N\\ge1$, masses $m_i>0$ in kg at positions $\\mathbf x_i\\in\\mathbb R^3$ in metres for $i<N$, assume $M:=\\sum_{i<N}m_i>0$ ([[def-finite-sum]]) and define the center-of-mass coordinates by $X_{\\mathrm{cm},j}:=M^{-1}\\sum_{i<N}m_i x_{i,j}$ for $j<3$; these three real coordinates form $\\mathbf X_{\\mathrm{cm}}$. The masses are invariant under the allowed chart changes. Other mechanical models must supply their total mass and center-of-mass map; this thermodynamic item does not construct those quantities for a continuum or field model. Every admitted mechanical model must also supply center-of-mass maps and velocities that transform under the displayed Galilean chart changes by $\\mathbf X'_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t')=R\\mathbf X_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t)+\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b t$ and $\\mathbf v'_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t')=R\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t)-\\mathbf b$, where $t'=t+\\tau$; these are kinematic compatibility conditions, not dynamical theorems about arbitrary mechanical models. When a model supplies three coordinate functions $X_{\\mathrm{cm},j}:I\\to\\mathbb R$ for $j<3$ as in [[def-function-space]], assemble its center-of-mass position as $\\mathbf X_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t):=(X_{\\mathrm{cm},0}(t),X_{\\mathrm{cm},1}(t),X_{\\mathrm{cm},2}(t))\\in\\mathbb R^3$. At a time $t\\in I$ where each component limit exists, define the velocity by $v_{\\mathrm{cm},j}(t)=\\lim_{h\\to0}(X_{\\mathrm{cm},j}(t+h)-X_{\\mathrm{cm},j}(t))/h$ through $H_t:=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R:t+h\\in I,\\ h\\ne0\\}$, for each $j<3$, using the scalar limit of [[def-function-limit]]; these three components form $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t)$. A statewise instantaneous center-of-mass rest chart at a specified time $t$ is the Galilean chart obtained by the pure boost $\\mathbf b=\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t)$ with $R=I_3$, $\\mathbf a=0$, and $\\tau=0$. Its center-of-mass velocity vanishes at that event, but need not vanish at other times because the boost is constant. For an interval $J\\subseteq I$ on which $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ exists at every time, a single Galilean chart has zero center-of-mass velocity throughout $J$ if and only if $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is constant on $J$: in such a chart $R\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t)=\\mathbf b$ for every $t\\in J$, so $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}(t)=R^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf b$ is constant, and conversely a constant velocity $\\mathbf v_0$ is made zero by the pure boost $\\mathbf b=\\mathbf v_0$. For a fixed closed body with total mass $M>0$ in kg, its separately tracked translational kinetic energy in a selected inertial chart is $K_{\\mathrm{cm}}=\\tfrac12M\\|\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}\\|_2^2$ in joules; under a pure boost it is $K'_{\\mathrm{cm}}=\\tfrac12M\\|\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}-\\mathbf b\\|_2^2$.\n\nIn this framework $U$ denotes the scalar internal-energy coordinate evaluated at a state in its statewise instantaneous center-of-mass rest chart. Thus an equilibrium preparation's internal energy is evaluated using the boost selected by its center-of-mass velocity at that preparation; a process using one chart with the center of mass at rest throughout its interval must separately satisfy the constant-velocity condition above. Bulk translational kinetic energy and any separately tracked external-field potential energy are excluded from $U$. For a given state, the model treats $U$ as invariant between its center-of-mass rest charts that differ only by spatial translation or orthogonal orientation, and fixes its numerical zero, including any retained or subtracted constituent rest-energy offsets, consistently across the composition changes it admits. This is a nonrelativistic modeling convention, not a derived energy-transformation law.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "def-interval",
            "def-cartesian-product",
