                "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-2-newtons-first-law"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "The Newtonian frame convention and internal-energy split used in this thermodynamic framework, restricted to mechanical models that supply positive mass, center-of-mass data, and Galilean-compatible transformations; no relativistic energy law or dynamical theorem is asserted."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Equilibrium state domains",
          "statement": "Fix a system $s$ as in [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]]. A material constitution $c$ specifies a finite ordered list of named species $(\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_r)$, with $r\\in\\mathbb N$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]) and $r\\ge1$, and the model parameters that identify the material. An equilibrium-preparation label in the set $E_c$ selects a single macrostate that the model designates as an equilibrium state for constitution $c$. The equilibrium designation is primitive model input here; it is not a dynamical equilibration theorem or a global-stability claim. For each admitted preparation, its mechanical model supplies a positive total mass and a defined center-of-mass velocity as in [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], so the allowed boost can select a center-of-mass rest chart in which its internal energy is recorded. A preparation for which these mechanical data are unavailable lies outside this framework. A statistical preparation that instead supplies a normalized probability measure on a declared measurable space is as in [[def-probability-measure]] and belongs to a separate statistical branch; it is an element of $E_c$ only if that model also assigns it a single equilibrium macrostate.\n\nEach $e\\in E_c$ is assigned a unique coordinate tuple $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, where $U$ is the adopted internal-energy coordinate in joules as in [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], $V>0$ is volume in cubic metres, and each $n_j>0$ is an amount in moles; the SI unit assignments are those of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. These are real-valued mole amounts in the bulk continuous chart, not integer particle counts. Define the dimensionless numerical coordinates $\\hat U=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$, $\\hat V=V/(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$, and $\\hat n_j=n_j/(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. The representation map $\\pi_c:E_c\\to\\mathbb R^{r+2}$ is a function as in [[def-function-space]], sends $e$ to $(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)$, and the equilibrium state domain is its image $D_c:=\\pi_c(E_c)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{r+2}$ using inclusion as in [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in D_c$, write $U_c(x):=\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J$, $V_c(x):=\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3}$, and $n_{c,j}(x):=\\hat n_j\\,\\mathrm{mol}$ for its dimensioned coordinates. Preparation labels with the same coordinate tuple represent the same thermodynamic state in this framework. Statistical preparations that specify a probability law belong to a separate branch and are not used in this state-domain definition.\n\nFor fixed $n=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$ in a chart used by the model, write $\\Gamma_{c,n}=\\{(\\hat U,\\hat V):(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)\\in D_c\\}\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$, with inclusion notation as in [[def-subset-and-proper-subset]]. This designated slice is a specified nonempty open convex subset. With the dimensionless Euclidean norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] (a norm by [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]), openness means that every $z\\in\\Gamma_{c,n}$ has some $\\epsilon>0$ for which $\\{w\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\|w-z\\|_2<\\epsilon\\}\\subseteq\\Gamma_{c,n}$. Convexity means that $(1-t)z_0+tz_1\\in\\Gamma_{c,n}$ whenever $z_0,z_1\\in\\Gamma_{c,n}$ and $t\\in[0,1]$, as in [[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]]. Both properties are assumptions about the particular slice, not conclusions about every $D_c$; no coordinate tuple is presumed physically realizable merely because it lies in an ambient Euclidean space. A zero amount for an absent species belongs only to a separately specified boundary domain and is not in this positive-mole chart.\n\nIn a simple-compressible chart, volume $V$ is the only mechanical coordinate in the equilibrium representation; composition changes, fields, polarization, surface terms, and other mechanical coordinates require a separately declared state domain and exchange rules. No pressure or reversible-work formula follows from this definition.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
            "def-td-galilean-rest-frame",
            "def-natural-numbers",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
            "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
            "def-probability-measure",
            "def-function-space",
            "def-subset-and-proper-subset"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
