          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §II.A",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §III.A",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Ling, Moebs, and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §3.1 Thermodynamic Systems",
                "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-1-thermodynamic-systems"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Equilibrium macrostates of nonrelativistic, macroscopic, simple-compressible systems represented by internal energy, volume, and named mole amounts, with admitted preparations recorded in a supplied center-of-mass rest chart; no stability or constitutive law follows from this definition."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-process-and-protocol",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Thermodynamic process records and transfer conventions",
          "statement": "For a system $s$ of constitution $c$, the process model fixes one Galilean inertial chart $g_s$ for kinetic-energy bookkeeping and transfer records and holds that chart fixed throughout each process. A process record is a tuple\n$$P=(I_P,x_P^-,x_P^+,z_P,\\kappa_P,r_P).$$\nFor each finite closed time interval $I_P=[t_0,t_1]\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with $t_0<t_1$ as in [[def-interval]], measured in seconds, its component fields have these types: $x_P^-,x_P^+\\in D_c$ are equilibrium endpoint coordinates from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]; $Z_s$ is the model-declared configuration space of the system, its auxiliaries and devices; $Z_s^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\subseteq Z_s$ is the model-declared set of equilibrium configurations and $\\xi_s:Z_s^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\to D_c$ assigns their state coordinates; $z_P:I_P\\to Z_s$ is a function ([[def-function-space]]); $\\mathcal K_s$ is the model-declared set of control/contact records and $\\kappa_P\\in\\mathcal K_s$; and $\\mathcal R_s$ is the model-declared set of signed transfer records and $r_P\\in\\mathcal R_s$. These fields form a finite Cartesian product ([[def-cartesian-product]]). The admitted records $\\mathcal P_s$ are the model-specified subset of tuples with these types that additionally satisfy $z_P(t_0),z_P(t_1)\\in Z_s^{\\mathrm{eq}}$, $\\xi_s(z_P(t_0))=x_P^-$, and $\\xi_s(z_P(t_1))=x_P^+$. No continuity or differentiability is imposed on $z_P$, and its values at interior times need not represent equilibrium states.\n\nFor an endpoint bookkeeping quantity $F$, write $\\Delta F=F_P^+-F_P^-$. A closed record in the sense of [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]] has no matter crossing the chosen outer boundary. The core closed-record class $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)\\subseteq\\mathcal P_s$ ([[def-subset-and-proper-subset]]) contains only records for which the system has a supplied total mass $M>0$ and defined center-of-mass velocities at both endpoints in the fixed chart $g_s$, so the kinetic energies $K_{\\mathrm{cm}}^\\pm=\\tfrac12M\\|\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}^\\pm\\|_2^2$ are defined as in [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. It further requires $\\Delta K_{\\mathrm{cm}}=0$ and $\\Delta U_{\\mathrm{ext}}=0$, where $U_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ denotes any model-specified external-field potential energy tracked separately from the internal-energy coordinate in $g_s$. Other process classes require an expanded energy bookkeeping rule.\n\nFor each $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$, the protocol supplies maps $Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}:\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)\\to\\mathbb R$ whose values are interpreted in joules as in [[def-td-si-quantities]] and whose energy bookkeeping uses $g_s$. $Q[P]>0$ means energy enters the system through the declared thermal-transfer channel; $W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]>0$ means mechanical work is done on the system. These are functionals of the process record, not state functions of $x_P^-$ and $x_P^+$. Heat is the protocol-assigned thermal mode of energy transfer, not a quantity stored in the equilibrium state. A record is adiabatic exactly when $Q[P]=0$. A closed record may still exchange energy; it is not thereby isolated.\n\nA preparation is a model-specified rule selecting the particular initial equilibrium state $x_P^-$. A statistical extension may instead specify a normalized probability measure on a declared measurable space, as in [[def-probability-measure]]; such a statistical preparation is outside this deterministic process class. A preparation does not assert later equilibration. An admitted record may pass through nonequilibrium configurations even though both endpoints lie in $D_c$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
            "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "def-td-galilean-rest-frame",
            "def-interval",
            "def-cartesian-product",
            "def-function-space",
            "def-probability-measure",
            "def-subset-and-proper-subset"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
