        {
          "id": "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Calibration of an arbitrary family of simple-system entropies",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 13,
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator",
            "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
            "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison",
            "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I$ be a nonempty set of primitive simple-system labels containing a distinguished meter label $m$. Assume every finite separated product is explicitly represented by its finitely many tagged elementary factors; positive scaling and finite product are the only identifications. Suppose the join-closed family satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator]], and fix a strict meter pair $z_0\\prec\\prec z_1$ in $\\Gamma_m$. Assume the Axiom of Choice. Then one can choose, for every $i\\in I$, an additive constant and positive scale for its same-type entropy so that\n\n$$S\\big((t_1i_1,x_1),\\ldots,(t_ni_n,x_n)\\big)=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$$\n\nis additive, extensive, and characterizes every same-composition comparison on every finite separated product. No conclusion is asserted for transitions that change composition.",
          "proof": "**Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F3].\n\n1.1 Apply [F2] to the meter type and choose its entropy $S_m$ so $S_m(z_0)=0$ and $S_m(z_1)=1$. Set the meter reference to $r_m=z_0$, and use [A1] to choose a reference state $r_i$ and one same-type entropy representative for every $i\\ne m$. For each $i\\ne m$, use [F1] to choose calibrator states $a_i,b_i\\in\\Gamma_i$ and $c_i,d_i\\in\\Gamma_m$ such that $a_i\\prec\\prec b_i$, $c_i\\prec\\prec d_i$, and $(a_i,d_i)\\sim(b_i,c_i)$; also choose $r_i\\in\\Gamma_i$. For the meter label set $a_m=c_m=z_0$ and $b_m=d_m=z_1$; the two pairs $(z_0,z_1)$ and $(z_1,z_0)$ are the same two-copy meter compound up to permutation. Write $S_i^0$ for the selected entropy representative, with $S_m^0=S_m$, and put $\\Delta_i=S_i^0(b_i)-S_i^0(a_i)>0$ and $\\mu_i=S_m(d_i)-S_m(c_i)>0$. Define $S_i(x):=\\frac{\\mu_i}{\\Delta_i}\\bigl(S_i^0(x)-S_i^0(r_i)\\bigr)$. Then $S_i(r_i)=0$ and $S_i(b_i)-S_i(a_i)=\\mu_i$, matching the meter entropy increment across the chosen calibrator endpoints. For $i=m$, $\\Delta_m=\\mu_m=1$, so this agrees with the fixed meter normalization. [A1, F1, F2, choose]\n\n2.1 For a finite separated compound $A$ with factors $(t_\\ell i_\\ell,x_\\ell)$, define $S(A):=\\sum_\\ell t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$. The sum is finite and invariant under reordering; concatenating compounds adds the sums, and scaling every amount by $t>0$ multiplies it by $t$. The explicit-factor convention in the Statement therefore makes $S$ well-defined, additive, and extensive. [A1, step 1.1, construct]\n\n3.1 Fix a composition with amounts $M_i$ for finitely many labels and let $A,B$ be two states of that same composition. For a state $x$ of type $i$, put\n$s_i(x):=\\frac{S_i(x)-S_i(a_i)}{\\mu_i}.$\nThe same-type entropy representation and the CH for type $i$ show\n$e_x\\sim_s(1-s_i(x))e_{a_i}+s_i(x)e_{b_i}.$\nIndeed, the signed difference has total coefficient zero and $S_i$-value zero, so its positive and negative parts have equal mass and entropy and therefore compare both ways by [F2]. The calibrator relation gives\n$(e_{b_i}-e_{a_i})\\sim_s(e_{d_i}-e_{c_i})$\nby taking the signed difference of $(a_i,d_i)\\sim(b_i,c_i)$ and canceling common terms with [F3]. Add and scale these identities over the finitely many factors of $A$. With\n$q_i(A):=\\sum_{\\ell:\\,i_\\ell=i}t_\\ell s_i(x_\\ell),$\nthe resulting signed map is\n$A\\sim_s \\sum_i M_i e_{a_i}+\\sum_i q_i(A)(e_{d_i}-e_{c_i}),$\nwhere the sums range over the finite support of the composition. The same holds for $B$. Since they have the same composition, the reference base cancels, and $B-A$ is signed-equivalent to the finite meter map\n$D:=\\sum_i\\bigl(q_i(B)-q_i(A)\\bigr)(e_{d_i}-e_{c_i}).$\nExtend the meter entropy linearly to finite signed maps. Then\n$S_m(D)=\\sum_i\\bigl(q_i(B)-q_i(A)\\bigr)\\mu_i=S(B)-S(A).$\nThe positive and negative parts of $D$ have equal mass. If $S(A)\\le S(B)$, the same-type entropy representation [F2] for finite meter products gives $D^-\\prec D^+$, hence $D$ compares in the forward signed direction. Translation, addition, and ordinary-order compatibility [F3] transfer this to $A\\prec B$. Reversing the entropy inequality gives the reverse comparison. Thus $S$ characterizes every same-composition comparison. Each particular comparison uses only finitely many labels; no infinite sum or uncountable product occurs. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The meter normalization fixes the multiplicative unit. Changing the chosen reference state for type $i$ changes only its additive constant; on a finite compound these constants contribute amount-weighted conserved-type offsets. This proves the stated freedom and completes the construction. [A1, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 2.5 and §IV.B.2, pp. 27–29, 56–59",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Decorated finite-chain costs with catalysts",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 14,
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of tagged system types closed under finite composition $T\\times T'$ and positive scaling $tT$. Scaling is associative and acts componentwise on products: $s(tT)=(st)T$ and $t(T\\times T')=(tT)\\times(tT')$. Each type $T$ has a nonempty state set $X_T$ of finite tagged compounds, every state in $X_T$ is represented by the coefficient-map interpretation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and has the fixed aggregate composition $\\nu(a)=\\nu_T$ of its declared type, using the amount-function interpretation in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and has total coefficient mass $m(T)=w_T\\ge0$, and $\\nu_{T\\times T'}=\\nu_T+\\nu_{T'}$, $\\nu_{tT}=t\\nu_T$, with $m(T\\times T')=m(T)+m(T')$ and $m(tT)=t\\,m(T)$. Matching scalar masses alone do not identify aggregate composition. The entropy $\\sigma_T:X_T\\to\\mathbb R$ is the finite additive extensive entropy from [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]]; in particular $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nInclude the formal empty unit type $1$, with $X_1=\\{*\\}$, $m(1)=0$, $\\sigma_1(*)=0$, $T\\times1=T$, and $t1=1$. A transition edge from type $T$ to type $T'$ is a pair $a\\in X_T$, $b\\in X_{T'}$ such that the underlying equal-mass compounds satisfy $a\\prec b$ under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Thus an edge can exist only when $m(T)=m(T')$. Its cost is the finite real number $\\sigma_{T'}(b)-\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nA decorated finite chain from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ consists of a catalyst type $C\\in\\mathcal G$, an integer $N\\ge1$, types $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, and states $a_k\\in X_{T_k}$, $b_{k+1}\\in X_{T_{k+1}}$ with an edge $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$ for every $0\\le k<N$. Its cost is the finite real sum\n\n$$c=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\bigl(\\sigma_{T_{k+1}}(b_{k+1})-\\sigma_{T_k}(a_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nDefine $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ as the infimum of all such witness costs. Set $F=+\\infty$ if there is no witness and $F=-\\infty$ if the witness costs are unbounded below. The infimum is over real witness costs, so no subtraction of extended-real infima is used. Type permutations, reassociation, reversible identifications, and the identity $T\\times1=T$ are part of the declared type structure. The cost identities and inequalities require a separate proof.",
          "proof": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, Eqs. 6.6, 6.9, and 6.10, pp. 74–75",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Algebraic properties of decorated-chain costs",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 15,
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
            "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "For the cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$, $F$ is positively homogeneous, invariant under adding the same catalyst to both endpoints, and satisfies the triangle inequality whenever the extended-real sum on its right is defined. It is subadditive under composition whenever $F(\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma'_1)+F(\\Gamma_2,\\Gamma'_2)$ is defined. Every pair of actual forward and reverse decorated-chain witnesses has nonnegative total cost. A finite reverse witness therefore bounds all forward costs below; $F=-\\infty$ can occur only when the reverse direction has no witness.",
          "proof": "1.1 A reflexive identity edge from any nonempty state of $\\Gamma$ to itself has cost zero, so $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)\\le0$. For a decorated closed chain, start with the compound containing every edge input $a_0,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$. Apply the edge $a_0\\prec b_1$ with all other edge inputs as spectators, then apply $a_1\\prec b_2$ with the already produced $b_1$ and all still-unused inputs as spectators, and continue through $a_{N-1}\\prec b_N$. Consistency and transitivity give $\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}a_k\\prec\\sum_{k=1}^{N}b_k$. Since $T_N=T_0$ and the intermediate types occur once on each side, these two compounds have the same total type up to the declared reassociation and permutation. Their entropy difference is exactly the chain cost. The same-type entropy representation [F2] makes this difference nonnegative. Hence every closed-chain witness costs at least zero and $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$. [A1, F1, F2, construct]\n\n1.2 Take finite witnesses for $(\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma'_1)$ and $(\\Gamma_2,\\Gamma'_2)$. Run the first chain with a fixed state of $\\Gamma_2$ and its second catalyst as spectators; then run the second chain with the first chain's output state and first catalyst as spectators. Consistency allows both sequences, and each fixed spectator contributes zero entropy difference. The resulting witness from $\\Gamma_1\\times\\Gamma_2$ to $\\Gamma'_1\\times\\Gamma'_2$ has the sum of the two costs. If both infima are finite real numbers, choose witnesses within $\\epsilon/2$ of each infimum and let $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$. If one infimum is $-\\infty$ and the other is finite, use a fixed finite witness for the latter and arbitrarily negative witnesses for the former; the product costs are unbounded below. If both are $-\\infty$, choose both costs arbitrarily negative, so the product infimum is $-\\infty$. If either is $+\\infty$ and the other is not $-\\infty$, the right side is $+\\infty$ and the inequality is automatic. The mixed extended sum $+\\infty+(-\\infty)$ is undefined and is excluded by the Statement. [A1, F1, F2, construct]\n\n1.3 A witness for $F(\\Gamma\\times C_0,\\Gamma'\\times C_0)$ is a witness for $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ with enlarged catalyst $C_0\\times C$, so $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma\\times C_0,\\Gamma'\\times C_0)$. Conversely, add one fixed state of the nonempty catalyst $C_0$ to every edge of an arbitrary witness for $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Its cost is unchanged, proving the reverse inequality. Thus $F(\\Gamma\\times C_0,\\Gamma'\\times C_0)=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. [A1, F1, F2]\n\n2.1 Scaling every state, type, and catalyst in a witness by $t>0$ multiplies its cost by $t$ because $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$. This gives $F(t\\Gamma,t\\Gamma')\\le tF(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$; applying the same argument with scale $1/t$ gives equality, including the extended values. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 Concatenate a witness from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ with one from $\\Gamma'$ to $\\Gamma''$, adding the other chain's fixed catalyst state as spectator to each edge. The result is a witness from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma''$ whose cost is the sum. Taking finite approximations proves the triangle inequality when both costs are finite; an unbounded-below term with a finite witness in the other direction gives the corresponding $-\\infty$ case; if a term is $+\\infty$ and the sum is defined, the inequality is automatic. For an actual forward witness and an actual reverse witness with possibly different catalysts, add the reverse catalyst's fixed state to every forward edge and the forward catalyst's fixed state to every reverse edge. Consistency supplies these padded edges, and additivity makes each edge cost unchanged. The padded chains now have the same catalyst, so concatenate them into a closed chain; step 1.1 gives $c+d\\ge0$. Thus any fixed finite reverse witness of cost $d$ bounds every forward witness below by $-d$. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, construct] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, Eqs. 6.6–6.16, pp. 74–75",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Catalyst-chain cost characterizes cross-type transitions",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 16,
          "deps": [
            "lem-tdmath-reciprocal-naturals-tend-zero",
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
            "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-tdmath-reciprocal-naturals-tend-zero": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the calibrated entropy family of [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]], the cross-type accessibility axioms A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and the decorated cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. For every pair of types $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$ and states $x\\in X_\\Gamma$, $y\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$,\n\n$$x\\prec y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le \\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y).$$\n\nThe infimum defining $F$ need not be attained. If $F=+\\infty$, the right side is false; if $F=-\\infty$, the right side is true.",
          "proof": "1.1 If $x\\prec y$, the one-edge witness with an identity catalyst has cost $\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$. Hence $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$, which is the forward inequality, including the case $F=+\\infty$ where no such transition can exist. [F1, F2]\n\n1.2 Conversely assume the displayed entropy inequality. It is impossible when $F=+\\infty$. For each integer $n\\ge1$, choose a decorated witness of real cost $c_n\\le\\Delta\\sigma+h/n$, where $\\Delta\\sigma=\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$ and $h=\\sigma(z_1)-\\sigma(z_0)>0$ is the entropy gap of a fixed strict meter pair from [F1]. Such witnesses exist from the definition of infimum, also if $F=-\\infty$. Write their types as $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, their edges as $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$, and their endpoints as $a_0=(\\widetilde x,c_0)$, $b_N=(\\widetilde y,d_0)$. [F1, F2, F3, choose]\n\n2.1 Put $\\delta_n=1/n$. In one finite compound type form\n$L_n=(x,b_N,b_1,\\ldots,b_{N-1},\\delta_n z_0),\\qquad R_n=(a_0,y,a_1,\\ldots,a_{N-1},\\delta_n z_1).$\nThese have the same composition: both contain $\\Gamma$, $\\Gamma'$, the catalyst $C$, every intermediate type $T_1,\\ldots,T_{N-1}$, and one scaled meter state, up to reassociation and permutation. Additivity of $\\sigma$ and the definition of chain cost give\n$\\sigma(R_n)-\\sigma(L_n)=\\Delta\\sigma-c_n+\\delta_n h\\ge0.$\nThus the same-composition entropy representation [F1] gives $L_n\\prec R_n$. [A1, F1, F3, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 Starting from $R_n$, use consistency on $a_0\\prec b_1$ with $y,\\delta_nz_1,a_1,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$ as spectators. The result contains $b_1$ in place of $a_0$. Next use $a_1\\prec b_2$ with $y,\\delta_nz_1,b_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$ as spectators, and continue in order. At stage $k$, the current compound contains $a_k$, the earlier outputs $b_1,\\ldots,b_k$, all later inputs $a_{k+1},\\ldots,a_{N-1}$, and the unchanged $y,\\delta_nz_1$. After the last edge, transitivity gives $R_n\\prec R'_n$, where $R'_n=(b_1,\\ldots,b_N,y,\\delta_nz_1)$. Compose this with $L_n\\prec R_n$ from step 2.1. The compounds $L_n$ and $R'_n$ share the finite spectator $b_1+\\cdots+b_N$; cancel it by [F4]. The result is $x+\\delta_nz_0\\prec y+\\delta_nz_1$ for every $n$. [A1, F4, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 Since $\\delta_n\\downarrow0$, stability in [A1] with bases $x,y$ and fixed positive spectators $z_0,z_1$ yields $x\\prec y$. This proves the reverse implication without assuming that any infimum is attained. [A1, step 3.1] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 6.1 and proof, pp. 75–76",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Cost-zero type offsets and their difference space",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 16,
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra"
          ],
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Let $\\mathcal P:=\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G$ be the set of raw type pairs. Define a relation on raw pairs by\n\n$$(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\exists C\\text{ such that }A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C.$$\n\nThe separate well-definedness lemma proves that $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with composition and positive scaling, that $\\equiv$ is an equivalence on $\\mathcal P$, and that the following operations are well-defined on its classes. After that proof, write $V_F:=\\mathcal P/\\equiv$. Define addition by $[(A,B)]+[(D,E)]:=[(A\\times D,B\\times E)]$, negation by $-[(A,B)]:=[(B,A)]$, and positive scaling by $t[(A,B)]:=[(tA,tB)]$. Set zero to $[(1,1)]$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. The same lemma establishes that these operations make $V_F$ a real vector space; no vector-space law is assumed by this Definition.",
          "proof": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, pp. 76–79",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "The cost-zero offset construction is a real vector space",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 17,
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
            "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "For the construction in [[def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space]], $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with product and positive scaling; $V_F$ is a real vector space and its displayed operations are representative-independent.",
          "proof": "1.1 The relation $\\approx_0$ is reflexive because $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$, and symmetric by definition. Suppose $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ and $\\Gamma'\\approx_0\\Gamma''$. The triangle inequality gives both directed costs between $\\Gamma$ and $\\Gamma''$ at most zero. For any $\\epsilon>0$, choose a finite reverse witness of cost $d<\\epsilon$; such witnesses exist because the reverse infimum is zero. Every forward witness of cost $c$ closes with it, so [F2] gives $c+d\\ge0$, hence $c>-\\epsilon$. Letting $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ shows every forward witness has nonnegative cost and its infimum is at least zero; the same argument reverses the direction. Both infima are therefore zero, proving transitivity. The same argument proves product compatibility: subadditivity makes both product costs at most zero, and any finite reverse witness bounds every forward product witness below by a quantity tending to zero. Positive homogeneity gives scaling compatibility directly. Thus $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with product and positive scaling, and the quotient is a commutative monoid. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 Reversible splitting [A1] gives accessible transitions in both directions between $(s+t)\\Gamma$ and $s\\Gamma\\times t\\Gamma$. Extensivity makes each such edge have zero entropy cost. Any reverse witness bounds all forward costs below by [F2]'s closed-cycle inequality, so both infima are zero. Hence $(s+t)\\Gamma\\approx_0 s\\Gamma\\times t\\Gamma$. The empty type $1$ is an identity under composition; its reassociation and permutation identifications have zero cost by the type conventions. [A1, F1, F2]\n\n2.1 The pair relation $\\equiv$ is reflexive using unit catalyst $1$ and symmetric by swapping the two sides. Suppose $(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')$ via catalyst $C$, and $(A',B')\\equiv(A'',B'')$ via catalyst $D$. Their witnesses are\n$A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C,\\qquad\nA'\\times B''\\times D\\approx_0 A''\\times B'\\times D.$\nMultiply the first equivalence by $B''\\times D$, the second by $B\\times C$, and compose. Reassociating and permuting gives\n$A\\times B''\\times(B'\\times C\\times D)\\approx_0 A''\\times B\\times(B'\\times C\\times D),$\nwhich witnesses $(A,B)\\equiv(A'',B'')$. Hence $\\equiv$ is an equivalence relation. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 If $(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')$ via $C$, multiplying its witness by any type $D$ shows $(A\\times D,B\\times D)\\equiv(A'\\times D,B'\\times D)$. Applying this to each summand proves addition is independent of representatives. Compatibility of $\\approx_0$ with positive scaling from step 1.1 similarly proves positive scalar multiplication is independent of representatives. Product commutativity and associativity make addition commutative and associative; swapping $(A,B)$ gives an additive inverse, and $(1,1)$ is an additive identity. [F1, step 1.1, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 For $t>0$, the displayed operation $t(A,B)=(tA,tB)$ satisfies $t(v+w)=tv+tw$ because scaling distributes over type composition, and $t(sv)=(ts)v$ for $s>0$ by associativity of type scaling. Also $1v=v$. The scalar-addition law for positive $s,t$ follows from step 1.2:\n$(s+t)[A,B]=[(s+t)A,(s+t)B]=[sA\\times tA,sB\\times tB]=s[A,B]+t[A,B].$\nThe zero scalar is defined as the additive identity, and $(-t)v$ is defined as $t(-v)$, where $-v$ swaps the pair. [F1, step 1.2, step 3.1]\n\n5.1 The positive scalar laws extend to negative scalars by swapping pairs. For the scalar-addition law with opposite signs, write $s-t$ with $s,t\\ge0$. If $s\\ge t$, positive scalar addition gives $sv=(s-t)v+tv$, so cancellation in the additive group gives $(s-t)v=sv-tv$; if $t>s$, apply the same identity to $t=(t-s)+s$ and negate. Cases with zero are immediate. For scalar composition, write each nonzero scalar as its sign times a positive magnitude; the defined action swaps the pair once for each negative sign and scales by the product of magnitudes, so $(rs)v=r(sv)$, with zero cases immediate. For distributivity over vector addition, positive scalars distribute by step 4.1 and a negative scalar distributes by taking the additive inverse of that identity. Together with the abelian-group laws in step 3.1, these are all real-vector-space axioms. [F1, step 4.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, pp. 76–79",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-tdmath-splitting-cost-quotient",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "No-sinks cost offsets give a weak global entropy",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
            "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space",
            "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined",
            "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference",
            "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $F$ be the decorated cost of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], and assume the **no-sinks condition**\n\n$$F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)<+\\infty$$\n\nfor every type pair. Let $V_F$ be the real difference space of [[def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space]]. Then there is an additive extensive offset $B:\\mathcal G\\to\\mathbb R$ such that\n\n$$-F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)\\le B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma').$$\n\nIf every admitted cross-type transition $x\\prec y$ from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ satisfies the calibrated cost inequality of [[thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference]], then $\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)$ is nondecreasing across every such transition. Only this weak inequality is asserted; no-gap equalities and strict extensions are not concluded.",
          "proof": "**Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F4], and the no-sinks condition.\n\n1.1 If $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty$, no-sinks gives a finite reverse witness of some real cost $c$. Concatenating it with any forward witness makes a closed chain, so [F2] gives forward cost at least $-c$. Thus $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')>-\\infty$ whenever it is finite; all cost values are in $\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$. [A1, F2, given]\n\n2.1 For a pair class $v=[\\Gamma,\\Gamma']\\in V_F$, define $p(v):=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Suppose $(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\equiv(\\Lambda,\\Lambda')$ via $C$, so $P=\\Gamma\\times\\Lambda'\\times C\\approx_0 Q=\\Lambda\\times\\Gamma'\\times C$. Put $D=\\Gamma'\\times\\Lambda'\\times C$. Since $P\\approx_0 Q$, the triangle inequality gives $F(P,D)\\le F(Q,D)$ and $F(Q,D)\\le F(P,D)$; the costs have no $-\\infty$ by step 1.1, so these inequalities imply equality. Catalyst invariance from [F2] gives $F(P,D)=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ and $F(Q,D)=F(\\Lambda,\\Lambda')$. Therefore $p$ is independent of the pair representative. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 The identity $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$ gives $p(0)=0$. Homogeneity of $F$ gives $p(tv)=tp(v)$ for $t>0$, and its product subadditivity gives $p(v+w)\\le p(v)+p(w)$. The no-sinks condition says $p(v)<+\\infty$ exactly when $p(-v)<+\\infty$. By step 1.1, $p$ has no $-\\infty$ value. [F1, F2, given, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 Apply [F3] to $V_F$ and $p$. It gives a real-linear $L:V_F\\to\\mathbb R$ with $-p(-v)\\le L(v)\\le p(v)$ for all $v$. Put $B(\\Gamma)=L([\\Gamma,1])$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. Since $[\\Gamma\\times\\Gamma',1]=[\\Gamma,1]+[\\Gamma',1]$, $B$ is additive. Since $[t\\Gamma,1]=t[\\Gamma,1]$ (the unit type satisfies $t1=1$), $B(t\\Gamma)=tB(\\Gamma)$ for $t>0$. Also $[\\Gamma,\\Gamma']=[\\Gamma,1]-[\\Gamma',1]$, so applying the sandwich to this pair gives the displayed two-sided cost bound. [A1, F1, F3, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 For an admitted transition, [F4] gives $\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)\\ge F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$, while step 3.1 gives $B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Subtracting the latter from the former yields\n$[\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)+B(\\Gamma')]-[\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)]\\ge0.$\nThus the offset entropy is nondecreasing across every admitted transition. [F4, step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, pp. 76–80",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md",
      "order": 270
    },
    {
      "id": "td-accessibility-foundation",
      "title": "Accessibility Foundation",
      "category": "thermodynamics",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "td-accessibility-foundation-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "td-math-convex-contact-and-mixing",
        "td-thermodynamic-framework"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adiabatic accessibility",
          "statement": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. For $x,y\\in D_c$, write $x\\prec_c y$, and say that $y$ is adiabatically accessible from $x$, exactly when the declared process model admits at least one record $P\\in\\mathcal P_s$ from [[def-td-process-and-protocol]] with $x_P^-=x$, $x_P^+=y$, $Q[P]=0$, and the model’s stated auxiliary and work-device endpoint conditions satisfied. The material constitution is the same at both endpoints. The process record must account for the selected outer boundary and every auxiliary included by its protocol.\n\nThe sign convention is $Q>0$ into the system and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}>0$ for work done on it, as fixed in [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. Thus adiabatic means zero protocol-assigned heat, not zero work, zero energy change, isolation, quasistatic evolution, or reversibility. The relation concerns equilibrium endpoints only; intermediate configurations may be nonequilibrium. Existence of a witness record is model input, not a consequence of this definition.\n\nWhen the constitution is clear, write $x\\prec y$. Define $x\\sim y$ by $x\\prec y$ and $y\\prec x$, and $x\\prec\\prec y$ by $x\\prec y$ and not $y\\prec x$. These are respectively accessibility equivalence and strict one-way accessibility; neither is defined by equality of energy alone.\n\nThe order-theoretic relation of Lieb and Yngvason is an abstract model of this operation. Goddard’s Carathéodory formulation uses a distinct local-inaccessibility route; it is cited as context and does not supply the product comparison results used later.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
            "def-td-process-and-protocol",
            "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-process-and-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.A–II.B",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §2",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              },
              {
                "title": "Samuel J. Ling, William Moebs, and Jeff Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §4.1 Reversible and Irreversible Processes",
                "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-1-reversible-and-irreversible-processes"
              },
              {
                "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Model-defined transitions between equilibrium states of a fixed thermodynamic composition when the admitted process has no heat transfer; no claim is made that a proposed transition is physically realizable without a process witness."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Tagged scaled systems and separated products",
          "statement": "Fix a family $I$ of primitive material labels. A tagged type $c\\in I$ retains its identity even if another type has the same coordinate domain. A positive scale $\\lambda>0$ is dimensionless and is represented by the separately tagged bulk copy $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and map $\\delta_\\lambda$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A finite separated compound is a finite tuple of tagged scaled systems, with permutation and reassociation identifications and an empty unit type. Its state is a tuple of constituent equilibrium states; its extensive coordinate tuple is the sum specified by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] whenever the pair is admitted there.\n\nThe formal composition vector of a compound records, for each primitive label $c$, the sum of its positive scale coefficients. Two compounds have the same composition exactly when these vectors agree. The distinct **formal mass** $m(A)$ is the sum of all scale coefficients in $A$, a dimensionless bookkeeping quantity; it is not physical mass in kilograms, mole amount, energy, or volume. Same-composition compounds have equal formal mass. For states $A,B$ of the same compound type, write $A\\prec B$ when the declared adiabatic-accessibility relation of [[def-td-adiabatic-accessibility]] admits that endpoint comparison. This definition does not infer accessibility from coordinate addition.\n\nAn equal-composition comparison is a comparison between states of types with the same formal composition vector. The later reaction-network definition may separately admit transitions between distinct composition types, but the mathematical supplier still requires equal formal mass at both endpoints. A physical application must state how its channel preserves the chosen formal mass and separately record its dimensioned conservation balances. The empty unit has one state and formal mass zero. No identity is imposed between a primitive type and a scaled tag, or between two different decompositions of a compound, beyond the explicitly declared reassociation, permutation, and bulk-composition maps.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.A–II.C",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §2",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "The formal state and comparison bookkeeping for the declared finite family of separated bulk systems; mixed-composition transitions are excluded here and are defined separately."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Accessibility axioms A1–A6",
          "statement": "Adopt the following axioms for $\\prec$ on every declared pair of finite compounds with equal formal mass in [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]]. Initially this covers equal-composition adiabatic accessibility. If a later channel model admits comparisons between distinct composition types, the same axioms apply to those comparisons and all their finite scaled products; no cross-type transition is admitted by this postulate alone. They are assumptions of the operational model, not consequences of the process definition or of energy additivity.\n\n**A1 (reflexivity).** $A\\prec A$ for every state $A$.\n\n**A2 (transitivity).** If $A\\prec B$ and $B\\prec C$, then $A\\prec C$.\n\n**A3 (consistency).** If $A\\prec B$, then $A+C\\prec B+C$ for every finite spectator compound $C$ for which both endpoint compounds are admitted and the resulting formal masses agree.\n\n**A4 (scaling).** If $A\\prec B$, then $\\lambda A\\prec\\lambda B$ for every $\\lambda>0$, using the tagged scaling maps of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]].\n\n**A5 (splitting).** For each state $x$, positive $s<t$, and tagged scaled copies of its material, the state $t x$ is accessible in both directions to the separated pair $(s x,(t-s)x)$. This states reversible splitting of an equilibrium preparation into scaled copies, not additivity of arbitrary state functions.\n\n**A6 (stability).** Let $A,B$ have equal formal mass. Let $Z_0,Z_1$ be fixed positive spectator compounds of equal formal mass, and let $\\epsilon_n>0$ decrease to zero. If $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\prec B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, then $A\\prec B$.\n\nEquality of formal masses in A6 is required so each comparison is defined; equality of composition vectors is not required when a declared model includes cross-type transitions. A6 is a limiting closure assumption on the relation; it does not assert continuity of any physical process or the existence of a limiting apparatus.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §II.C",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, Appendix A-1–A-6",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              },
              {
                "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §3",
                "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Adopted order assumptions for adiabatic accessibility on equal-formal-mass finite compounds in the declared family, including cross-type comparisons only when a channel network later admits them; they do not establish that an actual material family satisfies them."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-thermal-join",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Thermal joins and thermal equivalence",
          "statement": "For the simple-compressible physical branch, a primitive label is a pair $i=(c_i,n^i)$ of constitution and fixed positive mole amounts. Its numerical state domain is exactly $\\Gamma_i=\\Gamma_{c_i,n^i}$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], with embedding $b_i(u,v)=(u,v,\\hat n^i)\\in D_{c_i}$. The unit map sends $(u,v)$ to $(u\\,\\mathrm J,v\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$; thus $u$ is the numerical value of the fixed internal energy, not a newly oriented energy coordinate. The underlying accessibility is the pullback of the admitted physical endpoint relation along $b_i$. Positive bulk scaling uses the declared scaling of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], carries the mole vector to $\\lambda n^i$, and retains the distinct scaled tag. More general work coordinates or formal thermometer examples require their own explicitly declared domain, coordinate unit map and endpoint relation; no identification with $D_c$ is supplied by this definition.\n\nFor two named simple-system types $i,j$ with energy/work coordinates $x=(u_i,v_i)$ and $y=(u_j,v_j)$, a thermal join is the model-declared compound state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ whose energy coordinate is $u_i+u_j$ and whose work coordinates retain the ordered pair $(v_i,v_j)$. Its join domain consists exactly of those tuples $(u,v_i,v_j)$ for which there are $u_i,u_j$ with $(u_i,v_i)$ and $(u_j,v_j)$ in the component domains and $u=u_i+u_j$. This is a tagged join state, distinct from the separated pair $(x,y)$, even when their total coordinates agree. The joined domain retains both work coordinates and has aggregate mole composition $n^i+n^j$ (in a common declared species list); it is not thereby a simple-compressible slice of one $D_c$. The contact model supplies its joined equilibrium endpoint space, endpoint coordinate map and admitted process relation. The contact model declares the pair encoding and join encoding to have the same aggregate composition and extends its accessibility relation to these two encodings; the contact axioms specify which such comparisons are admitted.\n\nWrite $x\\sim_T y$ exactly when the pair $(x,y)$ and its join state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ are accessible in both directions under the declared contact operation. Here $\\sim_T$ is a relation between states of the two named systems; it is not numerical equality of temperature. Nested joins use only the model’s declared reassociation and permutation identifications. The coordinate sum alone supplies no accessibility comparison.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §IV.A",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "The energy-summing contact operation for the declared simple-system family; it does not prescribe a temperature scale or guarantee that contact equilibrates any chosen pair."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-cross-type-reaction-network",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Cross-type mixing and reaction network",
          "statement": "A cross-type mixing and reaction network consists of a declared set $\\mathcal G$ of tagged composition types and a state set $X_\\Gamma$ for each type. Each state set comes with a fixed formal composition $\\nu_\\Gamma$, and its states satisfy $\\nu(x)=\\nu_\\Gamma$ as in [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. These mathematical coefficients and their positive type weights are distinct from dimensioned material amounts. For physical channels the model additionally supplies endpoint quantity maps $U_\\Gamma:X_\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ and $n_\\Gamma:X_\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R^r\\,\\mathrm{mol}$ in a declared finite species list, together with any separately tracked mechanical or external-field endpoint quantities. No identification of two distinct scaled tags is made.\n\nFor each channel $\\alpha$ from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$, the model supplies sets $Z_\\alpha$ of configurations, $\\mathcal K_\\alpha$ of protocol records, $\\mathcal B_\\alpha$ of outer-boundary records, and $\\mathcal R_\\alpha$ of transfer records, and equilibrium subsets and endpoint maps $\\xi^-_\\alpha:Z^-_\\alpha\\to X_\\Gamma$, $\\xi^+_\\alpha:Z^+_\\alpha\\to X_{\\Gamma'}$. A typed record is\n$$P=(I,x^-,x^+,z,\\kappa,b,r),$$\nwhere $I=[t_0,t_1]$, $t_0<t_1$, is a finite time interval in seconds, $x^-\\in X_\\Gamma$, $x^+\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$, $z:I\\to Z_\\alpha$, $z(t_0)\\in Z^-_\\alpha$, $z(t_1)\\in Z^+_\\alpha$, $\\xi^-_\\alpha(z(t_0))=x^-$, $\\xi^+_\\alpha(z(t_1))=x^+$, $\\kappa\\in\\mathcal K_\\alpha$, $b\\in\\mathcal B_\\alpha$, and $r\\in\\mathcal R_\\alpha$. Transfer maps $Q_\\alpha,W_{\\mathrm{on},\\alpha}:\\mathcal R_\\alpha\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ and $N_\\alpha:\\mathcal R_\\alpha\\to\\mathbb R^r\\,\\mathrm{mol}$ record heat, work and material transfer with declared signs; any tracked $\\Delta K_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ and $\\Delta U_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ are also joule-valued record maps. The channel specifies a balance predicate on this typed record set, or equivalently a balance map into a declared product of quantity spaces whose required value is zero, and a model-admitted subset satisfying that predicate. The predicate states the actual energy and material constraints of that channel; no balance law, physical admission or endpoint realizability follows from the tuple alone. For a fixed-constitution core closed channel, its endpoint embeddings, fields and admitted subset are exactly those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], with no material transfer and its stated kinetic/external restrictions. Other channels require their own adopted constraints. Define $(\\Gamma,x)\\mathcal R(\\Gamma',y)$ exactly when an admitted channel record has those endpoints. No channel is inferred from separated composition.\n\nFor any permitted equal-formal-mass compound states $A,B$ of the network, define $A\\prec B$ exactly when at least one admitted channel record has initial state $A$ and final state $B$; for named single types this is the relation $\\mathcal R$ above. This is the exact relation supplied to [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], not a separate abstract order that enlarges or restricts the channel edges. Its extension to finite tagged products and common spectators is governed by the adopted A1–A6 conditions below. Thus every edge in a decorated chain is precisely an admitted channel transition. This identification is a declared model condition.\n\nThe network is **finite-factor closed** when its types admit the declared finite composition and positive scaling operations and each allowed channel respects the model’s stated conservation constraints. In addition, each channel must preserve the dimensionless formal mass $m$ of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]]; this is the equal-coefficient-mass convention required by the mathematical chain-cost supplier, not a statement that formal mass is kilograms or moles. The common relation on all equal-formal-mass compounds satisfies A1–A6 of [[post-td-accessibility-a1-a6]], including consistency with spectators and stability. A decorated finite chain is exactly a finite sequence of such allowed transitions with a common catalyst and the real baseline-entropy edge costs in [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]].\n\nFor $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$, let $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ be the infimum of those finite decorated-chain costs, with $+\\infty$ if no witness exists and $-\\infty$ if costs are unbounded below. The network has **no sinks** when, for every ordered type pair,\n\n$$F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)<+\\infty.$$\n\nNo-sinks is an explicit property required of a network used in the global-offset theorem; it is not proved by this definition, and it is not asserted for every physical mixing or reaction. The definition permits the channel set to be empty; then the condition must still be checked rather than inferred.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-process-and-protocol",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
            "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-process-and-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "physical-assumption",
            "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§VI.A–VI.B",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 15,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "A declared family of allowed transitions between distinct tagged composition types, with finite catalyst chains and an explicit no-sinks condition; this definition does not claim a particular material network satisfies it."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Simple-system geometric axioms",
          "statement": "For each simple-system chart $\\Gamma_i\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_i+1}$, $n_i\\ge1$, let $x=(u,v)$, with one energy coordinate $u$ and $n_i$ work coordinates $v$. The chart is a nonempty open convex domain. The simple-compressible physical case is the fixed-composition slice and embedding of [[def-td-thermal-join]], with $n_i=1$, $u=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $v=V/(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$. A declared multi-work chart or joined chart instead supplies its own equilibrium endpoint domain and diagonal unit map: $U=u(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $W_a=v_a w_a$ for fixed positive work-coordinate units $w_a$. The numerical domain is identified with these physical endpoints by that supplied map, and accessibility is pulled back from the admitted endpoint relation. These identifications preserve the internal-energy meaning and fixed composition; extra work coordinates do not follow from the simple-compressible state definition. Adopt these assumptions for every state $x$ and for every thermal join treated as a simple system:\n\n**A7 (convex-combination accessibility).** For $0<t<1$, the separated scaled pair $(t x,(1-t)y)$ is accessible to the chart state $t x+(1-t)y$, whenever these states are in the declared domains.\n\nFor each $x$, define its forward sector $A_x=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:x\\prec y\\}$. Adopt:\n\n**S1 (strict successor).** Some $y\\in\\Gamma_i$ satisfies $x\\prec\\prec y$.\n\n**S2 (unique finite-slope support).** At $x=(u_x,v_x)$, $A_x$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane through $x$, and it can be written\n$$\nu-u_x+P_i(x)\\cdot(v-v_x)=0\n$$\nwith finite $P_i(x)\\in\\mathbb R^{n_i}$. The map $P_i:\\Gamma_i\\to\\mathbb R^{n_i}$ is locally Lipschitz: every point has a neighborhood on which one finite Lipschitz constant bounds $\\|P_i(x)-P_i(y)\\|/\\|x-y\\|$. A supporting hyperplane means $A_x$ lies in one of its closed half-spaces and meets it at $x$.\n\n**S3 (connected relative boundary).** The boundary $\\partial_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$, taken in the topology relative to $\\Gamma_i$, is arcwise connected.\n\nThese are explicit adopted conditions. The slope $P_i$ is a geometric support parameter here; no equation identifying it with measured pressure is asserted. In particular, S1–S3 and A7 do not assert comparison hypothesis for products.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-thermal-join",
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
            "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-thermal-join": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-adiabatic-accessibility": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§III.A–III.B",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §2",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              },
              {
                "title": "Goddard, The second law of thermodynamics as variation on a theme of Carathéodory, §3",
                "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt27k6m59q/qt27k6m59q.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Adopted convexity, irreversibility, support regularity and boundary-connectivity conditions on the forward sectors of simple thermodynamic state domains and admitted joins."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-td-thermal-join-axioms",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Thermal-contact axioms T1–T4",
          "statement": "Use thermal joins and $\\sim_T$ exactly as defined in [[def-td-thermal-join]]. For every ordered pair of simple systems and every admitted finite join, adopt:\n\n**T1 (joining).** $(x,y)\\prec J_{ij}(x,y)$.\n\n**T2 (reversible splitting).** Every join state has at least one split into component states at its stated total energy and work coordinates that is reversible to the join state. Also, for every state $x$ and every $0<t<1$, the equal-state scaled split $(t x,(1-t)x)$ is reversible to $x$.\n\n**T3 (thermal-equilibrium transitivity).** If $x\\sim_T y$ and $y\\sim_T z$ in the model’s declared contact family, then $x\\sim_T z$. This requires the model to provide the relevant joined types and identifications.\n\n**T4 (transversality).** For each simple-system state $x$, there are $x_0,x_1$ in that system with $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $x_0\\sim_T x_1$.\n\nThe axioms apply to admitted types and their joins only. T4 is an assumption about the contact relation; it is not a consequence of an equation of state or of a thermometer reading. The stronger range condition T5 is stated separately in [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and join coherence C1 is separately stated in [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]].",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-thermal-join",
            "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
            "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-thermal-join": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6": "operational-prerequisite",
            "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §IV.A",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Adopted existence, reversible-splitting, transitivity and transversality assumptions for thermal contact among the declared simple-system family and its finite joins."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-td-universal-thermal-range",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Universal thermal-equilibrium range T5",
          "statement": "Adopt **T5 (universal thermal-equilibrium range)**: for every ordered pair of simple systems $i,j$, each state $x$ of $i$, and every work-coordinate point $v_j$ in the projection of the state domain of $j$, there is some state $y=(u_j,v_j)$ of $j$ with $x\\sim_T y$, using [[def-td-thermal-join]].\n\nThis is a quantified existence assumption for a thermal-equilibrium partner at each permitted work coordinate. It does not define a numerical temperature, assert that all systems possess the same temperature range as a real apparatus, or supply a calibration map and its uncertainty.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-thermal-join",
            "post-td-thermal-join-axioms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-thermal-join": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-thermal-join-axioms": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §IV.A, T5",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "The adopted universal range condition for thermal-equilibrium partners in the declared contact family; it is an operational assumption, not an empirical thermometer calibration."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Equilibrium-join coherence C1",
          "statement": "Adopt **C1 (equilibrium-join coherence)**: whenever $x\\sim_T y$, the joined state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ is thermally equivalent to each constituent, $J_{ij}(x,y)\\sim_T x$ and $J_{ij}(x,y)\\sim_T y$, using the model’s declared nested-join identifications.\n\nC1 is an additional postulate of this completed contact branch. The source’s Theorem 4.8 is stated with T1–T4; the local conditional calibrator route uses the constituent-to-join bridge in addition to the stronger T5 range assumption. Neither fact licenses deriving C1 from $u=u_i+u_j$, from T1–T4, or from transitivity alone. The displayed source supports the contact context, not the claim that its bare printed axioms already contain C1.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-thermal-join",
            "post-td-thermal-join-axioms"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-thermal-join": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-thermal-join-axioms": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§IV.A–IV.B",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "André Thess, The Lieb–Yngvason Machine: A didactical tool for the definition of entropy in classical thermodynamics, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://www.dlr.de/de/tt/downloads/dokumente/institut/institutsleitung/Entropie.pdf/@@download/file"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "An explicit coherence assumption for nested thermal joins of equilibrated pairs; it supplements the energy-summing join rule and is not inferred from that rule."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Conditional contact calibrator and product comparison",
          "statement": "For every declared finite family of simple-system domains closed under the thermal joins of [[def-td-thermal-join]], assume the accessibility axioms A1–A6, the simple-system assumptions A7 and S1–S3, the contact assumptions T1–T4, the universal thermal-equilibrium range T5, and equilibrium-join coherence C1, with the exact formulations in [[post-td-accessibility-a1-a6]], [[post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms]], [[post-td-thermal-join-axioms]], [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]]. Then for any two simple types $i,j$, there are $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ in $i$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ in $j$ such that\n\n$$ (x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0). $$\n\nMoreover, for this fixed pair $i,j$, every finite separated product made only from positive scaled copies of $i$ and $j$ satisfies the comparison hypothesis: any two equal-formal-mass states $A,B$ of that product type satisfy $A\\prec B$ or $B\\prec A$. No comparison conclusion for products involving a third primitive type is contained in this pairwise result. These are conditional model deductions. In particular, the conclusion is not claimed from the printed Theorem 4.8 interface with T1–T4 alone; the local mathematical supplier explicitly requires the additional T5 and C1 branch.",
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
            "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
            "def-td-thermal-join",
            "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
            "post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence",
            "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms",
