          "physical_scope": "Smooth single-phase charts with positive finite entropy derivative in the adopted nonrelativistic macroscopic thermodynamic framework; the quantities are state functions, not empirical thermometer readings.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "post-td-mechanical-conjugate-identification",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Mechanical pressure-volume work coupling",
          "statement": "For a simple-compressible system whose only admitted mechanical equilibrium coordinate is volume, adopt the following piston coupling. The boundary is a planar piston with constant area $A>0$ in $\\mathrm{m^2}$ and outward displacement coordinate $\\ell$ in metres; increasing $\\ell$ increases the enclosed volume by $dV=A\\,d\\ell$. Define the positive mechanical pressure $p_{\\mathrm{mech}}$ as the outward normal force exerted by the system on the piston divided by $A$, so $p_{\\mathrm{mech}}$ is in pascals. When the system is in quasistatic frictionless mechanical equilibrium with this piston, adopt the identification\n\n$$p_{\\mathrm{mech}}=p_c,$$\n\nwhere $p_c$ is the entropic pressure of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. With $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ positive for work done on the system, the same coupling assigns the incremental mechanical work\n\n$$\\delta W_{\\mathrm{on}}=-p_{\\mathrm{mech}}A\\,d\\ell=-p_c\\,dV.$$\n\nThe sign agrees with [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]: compression has $dV<0$ and positive work on the system. This is an adopted, regime-limited physical assumption, not a deduction from the entropy derivative or from the first-law balance [[post-td-first-law-energy]]. It makes no claim for friction, nonquasistatic processes, changing piston area, shear, surface forces, fields, composition transfer, or other generalized coordinates.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
            "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "def-td-process-and-protocol",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
            "post-td-first-law-energy"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
