        {
          "id": "ex-td-retained-volume-thermal-equilibrium",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Thermal equilibrium with retained unequal volumes",
          "statement": "For each of two separated fixed-composition subsystems $i\\in\\{A,B\\}$, let $O_i\\subseteq D_{c_i}$ be an open dimensionless single-phase subset with physical chart $\\Omega_i=\\Phi_{c_i}(O_i)$ as in [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Fix positive amounts $n_i$ with nonempty physical slices $\\Omega_{i,n_i}:=\\{(U_i,V_i):(U_i,V_i,n_i)\\in\\Omega_i\\}$. Fix unequal volumes $V_A=V_0$ and $V_B=2V_0$, where $V_0>0$ is measured in cubic metres, and choose the slices to contain every tuple $(U_i,V_i)$ with $0<U_i<2U_0$ at its respective fixed volume. Let each physical-coordinate entropy representative be $S_i(U_i,V_i)=a\\ln(U_i/U_0)+b\\ln(V_i/V_0)$ on its slice for $U_i>0$, where $U_0>0$ is in joules and $a,b>0$ are in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The isolated compound permits energy exchange but retains the volume partition, and its adopted total entropy is $S_A+S_B$. For total energy $2U_0$, its unique entropy-maximizing energy split is $U_A=U_B=U_0$, so $T_A=T_B=U_0/a$, while $p_A=bT_A/V_0$ and $p_B=bT_B/(2V_0)=p_A/2$. Thermal equilibrium therefore does not imply pressure equality when volume exchange is forbidden.",
          "deps": [
