        {
          "id": "cex-td-integrable-capacity-not-integer-power",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Finite entropy integral does not force a heat-capacity power bound of order one or higher",
          "statement": "For a positive-temperature fixed-$V,n$ chart, write $\\theta=T/T_*$. For a dimensionless real exponent $q\\ge1$, the notation $C_V(T)=O(\\theta^q)$ as $\\theta\\downarrow0$ means that there exist a constant $M>0$ measured in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ and a dimensionless $\\delta\\in(0,1]$ such that $|C_V(T)|\\le M\\theta^q$ whenever $0<\\theta<\\delta$. Finite convergence of the entropy integral alone does not imply such a bound for any real $q\\ge1$, even when the entropy has a finite limit and the local response-chart identities apply. The displayed branch is a formal chart calculation, not a claim that a particular substance realizes the chart.\n\nThe displayed entropy is $S^{\\rm SI}$ in physical SI coordinates. For this constructed local model, choose the positive-amount open neighborhood stated here (or any positive neighborhood of the fixed amount, extending this entropy independently of amount) and let $O_c$ be the numerical preimage of that product patch under $\\Phi_c(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Declare $O_c$ as the model's local state-domain patch and set $S_c=S^{\\rm SI}\\circ\\Phi_c$. The derivatives below are those of $S^{\\rm SI}=S_c\\circ\\Phi_c^{-1}$ on $\\Phi_c(O_c)$, exactly as in [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. This supplies the numerical-to-SI chart identification for the formal witness, with no assertion of a measured material or a global constitutive extension.",
          "deps": [
            "def-td-response-functions",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
            "def-real-power",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
