        {
          "id": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Measure space and integral",
          "reference": "research/physics-td-qft-rpm-62-2026-10-14-batch-11.notes.md#item-inventory",
          "design_state": "Draft definition carrier recorded; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-complete-ordered-field",
            "def-finite-sum"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-finite-sum": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete numbered argument is in items/def-tdmath-measure-integral.md and its proof contract.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Terence Tao, 245A Notes 3: Integration on Abstract Measure Spaces and the Convergence Theorems, full author-hosted lecture text",
                "url": "https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2010/09/25/245a-notes-3-integration-on-abstract-measure-spaces-and-the-convergence-theorems/",
