      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Compact phase-generator mixture",
          "reference": "items/def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy.md#definition",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-borel-probability-law-on-a-polish-space",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-borel-probability-law-on-a-polish-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $K$ be a nonempty compact subset of $\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, with its Borel sigma-algebra, and let $a:K\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. The **convex hull** $\\operatorname{conv}K$ is the set of finite convex combinations of points of $K$. A **phase generator** is a point $y\\in K$ with declared energy $a(y)$. A **mixture** is a Borel probability measure $P$ on $K$. Its barycenter and mean energy are the coordinatewise Lebesgue integrals\n$$\\bar y(P):=\\int_K y\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad \\bar a(P):=\\int_K a(y)\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R.$$\nThese integrals are finite because each coordinate and $a$ are bounded on compact $K$. For prescribed mean $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$, the **mixture energy** is\n$$a_{\\rm mix}(x):=\\inf\\{\\bar a(P):P\\text{ is a Borel probability on }K,\\ \\bar y(P)=x\\}.$$\nThe admissible set is nonempty since every finite convex combination of points of $K$ is the barycenter of a finite atomic probability. The definition does not assert that the infimum is attained; the theorem on this page gives a compact continuous sufficient condition.",
          "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy.md.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete author-hosted lecture text",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1",
                "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter read; §§1.2–1.4, finite canonical and microcanonical ensemble conventions."
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 635-page lecture notes retrieved and read; §§4.2–4.6, pp.192–217, ensembles, microcanonical phase space, quantum density matrices, and traces."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Elastic collision model and weak collision identity",
          "reference": "items/def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel.md#definition",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-measure",
            "def-measure-space"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-measure-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Fix $d\\ge2$. The **elastic collision set** is\n$$Q_{\\rm el}:=\\{(v,w,v',w')\\in\\mathbb R^{4d}:v+w=v'+w',\\ |v|^2+|w|^2=|v'|^2+|w'|^2\\},$$\nwith its relative Borel sigma-algebra. A collision measure is a nonnegative countably additive measure $\\nu$ on this measurable space. It is symmetric if it is invariant under the exchanges $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(w,v)$, $(v',w')\\leftrightarrow(w',v')$, and $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(v',w')$. These invariances are hypotheses on $\\nu$, not consequences of energy and momentum conservation.\n\nFor a positive function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to(0,\\infty)$, write $Q(f)$ for a collision operator when the following **weak collision identity** holds for each test function $\\varphi$ used:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\varphi(v)Q(f)(v)\\,dv=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(f(v')f(w')-f(v)f(w))(\\varphi(v)+\\varphi(w)-\\varphi(v')-\\varphi(w'))\\,d\\nu,$$\nwith absolute convergence. This identity is the adopted mathematical collision model. Molecular chaos or a microscopic derivation of it is not part of the definition.",
          "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel.md.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Kinetic Theory, Part 2",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/kintheory/two.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 39-page lecture-note part retrieved and read; §2.1, pp.14–16, Liouville phase-space volume; §§2.2–2.3, pp.20–35, Boltzmann equation and H-theorem."
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Plasma Physics, Boltzmann H-Theorem",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/plasma/lectures1/node40.html",
                "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML section read; equations (3.123)–(3.144), collision production, detailed balance, and Maxwellian form."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-tdmath-finite-site-shell-and-canonical-law",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite-site canonical law and narrow microcanonical shell",
          "reference": "items/def-tdmath-finite-site-shell-and-canonical-law.md#definition",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-sum",
            "def-natural-logarithm",
            "def-real-exponential-function-and-e"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-finite-sum": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-natural-logarithm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-exponential-function-and-e": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Fix a finite alphabet $A=\\{1,\\ldots,q\\}$, $q\\ge1$, and real single-site energies $e_1,\\ldots,e_q$ in a fixed energy unit. At inverse parameter $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$Z_1(\\beta):=\\sum_{j=1}^q e^{-\\beta e_j},\\qquad p_j(\\beta):=e^{-\\beta e_j}/Z_1(\\beta),\\qquad E(\\beta):=\\sum_{j=1}^q p_j(\\beta)e_j.$$\nFor $N\\ge1$, a configuration $i=(i_1,\\ldots,i_N)\\in A^N$ has additive energy $H_N(i):=\\sum_{r=1}^Ne_{i_r}$. Given a width $\\delta_N\\ge0$, the **shell** is\n$$\\Gamma_N(\\beta,\\delta_N):=\\{i\\in A^N:|H_N(i)-NE(\\beta)|\\le\\delta_N\\}.$$\nWhen this finite set is nonempty, its microcanonical law means the uniform probability on $\\Gamma_N$, and its dimensionless entropy is $\\log|\\Gamma_N|$. A type is a vector $t=(n_1/N,\\ldots,n_q/N)$, where the nonnegative integer counts sum to $N$; its multiplicity is $N!/\\prod_j n_j!$.\n\nThe convention fixes both the shell center and the law within the shell. It makes no physical claim that an apparatus has resolution $\\delta_N$.",
          "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-finite-site-shell-and-canonical-law.md.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
