        {
          "id": "def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Ornstein–Uhlenbeck integral equation",
          "reference": "items/def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation.md#definition",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-brownian-motion",
            "def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-brownian-motion": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,(\\mathcal F_t)_{t\\ge0},P)$ carry a standard Brownian motion $B$ adapted to the filtration, with future increments independent of the present sigma-algebra. Fix constants $\\gamma>0$ and $\\sigma>0$, and an $\\mathcal F_0$-measurable real random variable $X_0$ with finite second moment. A **continuous adapted solution of the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck equation** on $[0,T]$ is a continuous adapted process $X$ satisfying, almost surely for every $t\\in[0,T]$,\n$$X_t=X_0-\\gamma\\int_0^tX_s\\,ds+\\sigma B_t,$$\nwhere the time integral is the ordinary Lebesgue integral along the continuous path. In differential notation this is $dX_t=-\\gamma X_t\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dB_t$, with the stochastic differential interpreted through the displayed integral equation.\n\nThe definition asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. The Itô integral in the explicit representation is the square-integrable predictable-process integral of [[def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes]].",
          "strategy": "Definition carrier is in items/def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation.md.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "G. A. Pavliotis, Stochastic Processes and Applications",
