            "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric",
            "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
            "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn",
            "thm-rank-nullity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy": "physical-assumption",
            "def-borel-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-probability-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-measure-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-measure-integral-agrees-with-root-lebesgue-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-integral-triangle-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-rank-nullity": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Mixtures of a compact set of declared phase-generator states with continuous entropy and a fixed barycenter; every feasible Borel probability mixture is included, while no attainment claim is made for noncompact domains or a different physical normalization.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty compact set of dimensionless generator coordinates. Each $x\\in K$ labels one model-specified phase-generator state; the model assigns it entropy per mole $s(x)\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $s:K\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous, using the numerical entropy value in that fixed unit. Define $\\operatorname{conv}K$ to be the set of all finite convex combinations of points of $K$, and fix $\\bar x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$. Use the Borel sigma algebra relative to $K$. A Borel probability mixture is a countably additive probability measure $P$ on that sigma algebra; its barycenter and entropy per mole are the coordinatewise integrals $\\int_Kx\\,dP$ and $\\int_Ks\\,dP$. For $x\\in K$, define the unit point mass $\\delta_x$ by $\\delta_x(A)=1$ if $x\\in A$ and $\\delta_x(A)=0$ otherwise, for Borel $A\\subseteq K$. Assume every Borel probability mixture with barycenter $\\bar x$ is feasible and has entropy per mole $\\int_Ks\\,dP$. Then there are $m\\le d+1$, points $x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in K$, and weights $\\theta_i\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i=1$ such that $P_*:=\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i\\delta_{x_i}$ is a feasible Borel probability mixture and $\\int_Ks\\,dP_*\\ge\\int_Ks\\,dP$ for every feasible mixture $P$.\n\nHere integrals use the measure convention of [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]. The entropy map is part of the model; this theorem derives neither its microscopic meaning nor compactness of a physical phase domain.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §5.1.1",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §4.1 First-order phase transitions",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/04%3A_Phase_Transitions/4.01%3A_First_order_phase_transitions"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Local regular dimension of a reacting Gibbs phase-coexistence manifold",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 10,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
            "pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity",
            "post-td-entropy-maximum",
            "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set",
            "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-td-intensive-coexistence-conditions": "physical-result",
            "post-td-bulk-additivity": "physical-assumption",
            "post-td-entropy-maximum": "physical-assumption",
            "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Interior compositions of finitely many C1 intensive phase branches with energy, volume, all listed species exchanges, and internal reactions allowed in every phase, at a solution where the combined coexistence-and-affinity Jacobian has a nonsingular minor; phase amounts and externally fixed variables are excluded, and the conclusion is not asserted where a C1 phase chart fails, composition lies on the simplex boundary, or the nonsingular-minor hypothesis fails.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the entropy of each phase is $C^2$ on its physical state chart and its derivative-defined temperature at the endpoint is finite and positive. The equation-set dimension below concerns this smooth interior state only.\n\nLet $C\\ge1$ be the number of named species, $P\\ge1$ the number of coexisting phase branches, and $R\\ge0$ the number of independent internal reaction directions. Consider an attained global entropy-maximizing endpoint on the exact feasible set of [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For each phase $\\alpha$, use an intensive phase branch from [[def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain]] and an interior composition $x^\\alpha\\in\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ}$. Let each branch chemical-potential map\n$$\n\\mu^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)=(\\mu_1^\\alpha,\\ldots,\\mu_C^\\alpha)\n$$\nbe $C^1$ on a common open domain\n$$\n\\mathcal U=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\times(\\Delta_{C-1}^{\\circ})^P \\subseteq\\mathbb R^{\\,n},\\qquad n=2+P(C-1).\n$$\nAssume energy, volume, and every named species can be exchanged between every phase pair in both directions, and every listed reaction direction is feasible in both extent directions in every phase while that phase's energy, volume, other reaction extents, and all other phase coordinates are held fixed at the coexistence point. Let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent, dimensionless stoichiometric vectors, with extents measured in moles. Phase equilibrium imposes $\\mu_i^\\alpha-\\mu_i^1=0$ for $1\\le i\\le C$, $2\\le\\alpha\\le P$; internal reaction equilibrium imposes $(\\nu^r)^{\\mathsf T}\\mu^\\alpha=0$ in every phase for $1\\le r\\le R$. On the phase-equality locus these are the same $R$ equations in each phase, so count them once using phase 1. Put $q:=C(P-1)$ and $m:=q+R$. If $m=0$ (equivalently $P=1,R=0$), no equation is imposed and the local zero set is the whole chart, of dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2-R$. If $m\\ge1$ and the combined equation map has an invertible $m\\times m$ derivative minor at the solution, with $m\\le n$, then the local zero set of these first-order coexistence-and-affinity equations is a graph of dimension\n$$\nn-q-R=C-P+2-R.\n$$\nThis is the dimension of the equation zero set in intensive coordinates only; phase amounts and their extensive conservation constraints are not counted, and the actual stable-coexistence set may be a proper subset after additional stability constraints. If the nonsingular-minor condition fails, no dimension formula is asserted.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §5.1.1",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §4.1 First-order phase transitions",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/04%3A_Phase_Transitions/4.01%3A_First_order_phase_transitions"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-td-nonsmooth-phase-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Scope at phase boundaries and in the thermodynamic limit",
          "reference": "items/rem-td-nonsmooth-phase-scope.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 11,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
            "def-td-thermodynamic-limit-contract",
            "pthm-td-clapeyron-local",
            "pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-thermodynamic-limit-contract": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-td-clapeyron-local": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture": "physical-result"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Interpretive limits for the phase-branch, coexistence, Clapeyron, and compact-mixture statements on this page; this remark establishes no universal critical or thermodynamic-limit result.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "The branch theorems on this page require the stated smooth local charts. At a phase boundary, a composition boundary, or a critical point, the branch derivatives may fail to exist or may cease to distinguish the phases. In particular, [[pthm-td-clapeyron-local]] requires a nonzero molar-volume jump; when that jump vanishes, its local graph argument and quotient formula do not apply.\n\nThe compact-mixture result [[pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture]] requires a compact phase-generator domain, a continuous entropy map, a fixed barycenter, and feasibility of all probability mixtures with that barycenter. It supplies no attainment theorem for noncompact domains. Likewise, [[def-td-thermodynamic-limit-contract]] specifies the sequence and normalization for a limiting claim but proves no limit exists and no derivatives commute with that limit.\n\nA finite-state partition sum is finite and positive at each finite system size. A singular phase behavior therefore needs a separately specified limit or another model with a separately established domain and regularity analysis. This page does not assert a universal thermodynamic-limit theorem, ensemble equivalence, critical exponent, or physical applicability of the van der Waals equation.\n\nThe local homogeneity used by [[pthm-td-clapeyron-local]] may be independently stipulated in a phase model or supplied on the separately designated common-law bulk branch under the local Euler theorem's inverse-chart and common-reference conditions. A constructed local Gibbs model need not be embedded in a global bulk cone to use the general conditional result. The added branch supplies no universal finite-size, long-range or gravitational extensivity and does not remove any smoothness, exchange or nonzero-volume-jump condition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §§5.1–5.4",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1–4.2",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/04%3A_Phase_Transitions/4.01%3A_First_order_phase_transitions"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md",
      "order": 281
    },
