        {
          "id": "ex-td-phase-rule-single-component",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "One component in two phases has one regular equation-set degree of freedom",
          "reference": "items/ex-td-phase-rule-single-component.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 11,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
            "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension",
            "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A constructed pair of smooth one-component Gibbs charts; the example verifies the regular equation-set rank and dimension only, and does not assert a stable or realized coexistence endpoint.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $C=1$, $P=2$, and $R=0$. Fix $T_0=300\\,\\mathrm K$ and $p_0=10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}$, and take a common open chart around $(T_0,p_0)$. On it define the branch Gibbs potentials per mole\n$$\ng_1(T,p)=g_0-s_0(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_1(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2,\\qquad g_2(T,p)=g_0-s_0(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_2(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2,\n$$\nwhere $g_0$ is in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $s_0,c>0$ are in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, $v_1,v_2>0$ are distinct molar volumes in $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, and $\\kappa>0$ is in $\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}Pa^{-1}}$. Set $G_i=n_i g_i$ with natural Gibbs assignments $S_i=-\\partial_TG_i$, $V_i=\\partial_pG_i$, and $\\mu_i=\\partial_{n_i}G_i$; define $U_i=G_i+TS_i-pV_i$. The derivative-defined chemical potentials are $\\mu_i=g_i$, and they agree at $(T_0,p_0)$. This is a candidate equation-map solution, not a claim of entropy-maximizing coexistence.\n\nThe constructed branch's local physical state map is $\\chi:(T,p,n)\\mapsto(U,V,n)$ (using the positive amount and interior composition coordinates displayed here). On the inverse patch verified below, define its numerical domain $O_c=\\Phi_c^{-1}(\\chi(\\mathcal O))$ and declare this patch to be the local model's $D_c$ patch, where $\\Phi_c(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n\\,\\mathrm{mol})$ is the unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Define the numerical entropy by $S_c=S^{\\rm SI}\\circ\\Phi_c$, with $S^{\\rm SI}$ supplied by the Gibbs assignments through this inverse. Thus the displayed derivatives are derivatives in physical SI coordinates on $\\Phi_c(O_c)$; the model patch is not an empirical claim about an independently specified material domain.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §5.1.1",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §4.1 First-order phase transitions",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/04%3A_Phase_Transitions/4.01%3A_First_order_phase_transitions"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-td-reactive-phase-rule-three-component",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A regular two-phase three-species reaction model",
          "reference": "items/ex-td-reactive-phase-rule-three-component.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 11,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
            "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A constructed smooth local two-phase Gibbs model with one formal internal reaction, used to verify the local equation-map nonsingular-minor hypothesis only; no stable or realized coexistence claim, empirical system, or chemical data are made.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $C=3$ species and $P=2$ phases. Write $x_3:=1-x_1-x_2$, and center the open composition charts at $x_1=x_2=x_3=1/3$. Fix $T_0>0$, $p_0>0$, $s_0,c,v_0,\\kappa,q>0$, and $\\eta=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. The parameters have units $s_0,c\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, $v_0\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\kappa\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}Pa^{-1}}$, and $q\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$. For $\\gamma\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$, set $a_\\alpha=2\\eta$, $a_\\beta=\\eta$, $q_\\alpha=0$, and $q_\\beta=q$, and define molar Gibbs potentials\n$$\ng^\\gamma(T,p,x)=-s_0(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_0(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2+\\frac{a_\\gamma}{2}(x_1^2+x_2^2)-\\frac{a_\\gamma}{3}(x_1+x_2)+\\frac{a_\\gamma}{9}+q_\\gamma(p-p_0)x_3.\n$$\nSet $G^\\gamma=m_\\gamma g^\\gamma$ on each fixed-tag branch and adopt the natural Gibbs assignments $S=-G_T$, $V=G_p$, and $\\mu_j=G_{n_j}$, with $U=G+TS-pV$. These smooth charts are scale-invariant in amount. Near the centered point the volumes are positive, and the $(U,V)$ chart Jacobian at fixed composition and amount is nonsingular: $U_T=m_\\gamma c$, $V_p=-m_\\gamma\\kappa$, and $V_T=0$ there, so its determinant is $-m_\\gamma^2c\\kappa\\ne0$. The reaction vector is $\\nu=(-1,-1/2,1)^{\\mathsf T}$, of rank $R=1$. At $(T_0,p_0,x_1,x_2)=(T_0,p_0,1/3,1/3)$, both phases have $\\mu^\\gamma=(0,0,0)$, so the phase-equality equations and reaction affinity vanish.\n\nThe constructed branch's local physical state map is $\\chi:(T,p,n)\\mapsto(U,V,n)$ (using the positive amount and interior composition coordinates displayed here). On the inverse patch verified below, define its numerical domain $O_c=\\Phi_c^{-1}(\\chi(\\mathcal O))$ and declare this patch to be the local model's $D_c$ patch, where $\\Phi_c(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n\\,\\mathrm{mol})$ is the unit map of [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. Define the numerical entropy by $S_c=S^{\\rm SI}\\circ\\Phi_c$, with $S^{\\rm SI}$ supplied by the Gibbs assignments through this inverse. Thus the displayed derivatives are derivatives in physical SI coordinates on $\\Phi_c(O_c)$; the model patch is not an empirical claim about an independently specified material domain.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §5.1.1",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5"
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §4.1 First-order phase transitions",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/04%3A_Phase_Transitions/4.01%3A_First_order_phase_transitions"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md",
      "order": 282
