      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Finite statistical microstate model",
          "reference": "items/def-td-finite-microstate-model.md#definition",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and prerequisite acceptance remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-function",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
            "def-td-galilean-rest-frame",
            "def-td-si-quantities"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-galilean-rest-frame": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. The physical energy of $x\\in\\Omega$ is $E(x)=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$, with its zero and any retained internal offsets fixed by the declared material model. For a physical internal-energy interpretation, the declared companion mechanical model supplies, for every admitted microstate preparation $x$, a positive total mass and a center-of-mass position map with a defined componentwise velocity at that preparation, obeying the Galilean transformation conditions of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. That velocity selects the statewise instantaneous rest chart in which $E(x)$ is recorded; a finite set and energy map alone do not construct these mechanical data or a common rest chart for a time interval. Bulk translational kinetic energy and separately tracked external-field energy are excluded from these internal-energy values. The energy zero and any internal offsets are fixed consistently across admitted states. If the statistical model also assigns a single equilibrium macrostate to a preparation, $U$ is its internal-energy coordinate of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]; the pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ alone does not assert that macroscopic identification. Equal energy values at distinct elements of $\\Omega$ count as distinct microstates.\n\nWhen two such models are declared independent, their joint microstate set is the Cartesian product $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$, and additivity of the microscopic energy is the explicit condition $\\epsilon_{AB}(x,y)=\\epsilon_A(x)+\\epsilon_B(y)$. Independence and this energy equation are model data; they do not follow merely from the two systems being separated. The definition itself assigns no weights, temperature, probability law, or dynamics.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §§1.2–1.3 (printed pp. 2–27)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.2–4.4 (printed pp. 192–208)",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Finite discrete statistical models whose energies are numerical values in joules in the statewise instantaneous center-of-mass rest chart selected by separately supplied compatible mechanical data; no probability law or equilibration claim is part of the definition.",
          "verification": {
            "precheck": "n/a"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-td-regular-hamiltonian-shell-preparation",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Regular Hamiltonian shell preparation data",
          "reference": "items/def-td-regular-hamiltonian-shell-preparation.md#definition",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and prerequisite acceptance remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell",
            "thm-tdmath-regular-energy-shell-measure"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-regular-energy-shell-measure": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Fix an integer $n\\ge1$ and an open phase-coordinate domain $O_{\\rm phys}\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2n}$ with physical canonical coordinates $q_i$ in metres and $p_i$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. The canonical symplectic form on this chart is $\\omega_{\\rm phys}=\\sum_{i=1}^n dq_i\\wedge dp_i$. Let $\\hat q_i=q_i/(1\\,\\mathrm m)$ and $\\hat p_i=p_i/(1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}})$, and let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{2n}$ be the open image of the chart in these dimensionless coordinates. The corresponding dimensionless canonical form is $\\hat\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n d\\hat q_i\\wedge d\\hat p_i$, so $\\omega_{\\rm phys}=(1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s})\\hat\\omega$.\n\nLet $\\hat H:O\\to\\mathbb R$ be a numerical energy function with continuous partial derivatives through order two, and let $H_{\\rm phys}:O_{\\rm phys}\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ be $H_{\\rm phys}(q,p)=(1\\,\\mathrm J)\\hat H(\\hat q,\\hat p)$. Where defined, its physical Hamiltonian vector field $X_{H_{\\rm phys}}$ is determined by $\\iota_{X_{H_{\\rm phys}}}\\omega_{\\rm phys}=dH_{\\rm phys}$. For each elapsed time $t$ in its domain, the physical flow map is a function $\\phi_t:D_t\\to O_{\\rm phys}$, where $D_t\\subseteq O_{\\rm phys}$ is the set of initial data whose trajectories exist through time $t$, and it satisfies $d\\phi_t/dt=X_{H_{\\rm phys}}\\circ\\phi_t$. Equivalently, in the dimensionless chart, the flow map $\\hat\\phi_{\\hat t}:\\hat D_{\\hat t}\\to O$ is generated by $X_{\\hat H}$ satisfying $\\iota_{X_{\\hat H}}\\hat\\omega=d\\hat H$, at dimensionless time $\\hat t=t/(1\\,\\mathrm s)$; here $\\hat D_{\\hat t}$ is the set of initial coordinates whose trajectories exist through $\\hat t$. These definitions specify the flow maps when they exist but impose no global-existence or completeness hypothesis on the shell preparation.\n\nA specified open interval $J_E\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a regular compact-shell band when $\\hat H^{-1}(K)$ is compact in $O$ for every compact $K\\subset J_E$ and $\\nabla\\hat H$ is nonzero at every point of $\\hat H^{-1}(J_E)$.\n\nFor $\\hat E\\in J_E$, the energy shell is $\\Sigma_{\\hat E}:=\\{x\\in O:\\hat H(x)=\\hat E\\}$. The graph-chart definition of the weighted shell integral is [[def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell]]: in a local graph chart $x_i=h(z,\\hat E)$, its coarea measure is $d\\mu_{\\hat E}=dA_{\\hat E}/|\\nabla\\hat H|$, with graph-area factor and gradient in the dimensionless Euclidean coordinates. Set $D(\\hat E):=\\int_{\\Sigma_{\\hat E}}d\\mu_{\\hat E}$ and admit the preparation only when $\\Sigma_{\\hat E}\\ne\\varnothing$ and $0<D(\\hat E)<\\infty$. Its normalized shell law is $\\nu_{\\hat E}(A):=D(\\hat E)^{-1}\\mu_{\\hat E}(A\\cap\\Sigma_{\\hat E})$. The current draft theorem [[thm-tdmath-regular-energy-shell-measure]] proves the coarea identity, chart independence and positive finite shell mass under its stated $C^2$, compact-regular-band hypotheses; its statement and proof are present, while its acceptance remains unresolved. Multiplying the unnormalized shell measure by a fixed positive phase-volume normalization does not change $\\nu_{\\hat E}$.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §1.2 (printed pp. 2–16)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.2–4.3 (printed pp. 192–204)",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
