          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-regular-hamiltonian-shell-preparation": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For the regular-shell branch below, assume AC as required by\n[[def-td-regular-hamiltonian-shell-preparation]] and its coarea-measure\nsupplier. No choice assumption is needed for the separate finite-window branch.\n\nFor a finite microstate model and a declared energy value $E_0$ in joules and resolution $\\delta\\ge0$ in joules, define the admitted window\n$$\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:|E(x)-E_0|\\le\\delta\\}.$$\nWhen this set is nonempty, adopt the uniform preparation $P(x)=|\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}|^{-1}$ on it and zero outside. Its dimensionless Boltzmann entropy is $\\log|\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}|$, and its physical entropy convention is $k_{\\mathrm B}\\log|\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}|$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. A zero-width window is allowed only when it contains at least one microstate.\n\nFor a continuous classical model, when the regular-band, nonempty-shell, and normalization hypotheses in [[def-td-regular-hamiltonian-shell-preparation]] hold, the microcanonical preparation is the normalized shell measure $\\nu_{\\hat E}$ defined there. The shell preparation does not require a globally defined flow. These finite-window and regular-shell rules are adopted model preparations. They do not assert Hamiltonian ergodicity, equality with long-time averages, canonical sampling, or equilibration.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §1.2 (printed pp. 2–16)",
