        {
          "id": "pthm-td-countable-canonical-identities",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Countable canonical identities under a local moment envelope",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-countable-canonical-identities.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and prerequisite acceptance remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling",
            "thm-tdmath-summable-canonical-differentiation"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-tdmath-summable-canonical-differentiation": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I$ be a specified nonempty countable microstate list with energies $E_i$ in joules, counted once, and let $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ be open. For $\\beta\\in J_\\beta$, define $Z(\\beta)=\\sum_i e^{-\\beta E_i}$ and, when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$, $p_i(\\beta)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z(\\beta)$. Suppose every compact interval $K\\subset J_\\beta$ admits a summable sequence $a_i\\ge0$ with $(1+|E_i|^2)e^{-\\beta E_i}\\le a_i$ for all $i$ and $\\beta\\in K$. Then $Z$ is $C^2$, $-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\bar E_\\beta$, $\\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$, and the dimensionless Gibbs entropy is finite with\n$$-\\sum_i p_i\\log p_i=\\log Z+\\beta\\bar E_\\beta.$$\nThus $F_{\\mathrm{stat}}=-k_{\\mathrm B}T\\log Z=\\bar E_\\beta-TS_\\beta$ for $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$. A lower bound on the energies alone does not supply the summable envelope or finite partition function.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §§1.3.1–1.3.4 (printed pp. 18–26)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.2–4.4 (printed pp. 192–208)",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Discrete countable energy lists with a specified enumeration and a uniform local summable second-moment envelope on an open positive inverse-temperature interval; no lower-energy-bound-only or arbitrary trace claim is made.",
          "verification": {
            "precheck": "pass"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-differentiable-pressure-concentration",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Energy-density concentration at differentiable lattice pressure",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-differentiable-pressure-concentration.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and prerequisite acceptance remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling",
            "pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure",
            "thm-tdmath-limiting-derivative-and-concentration"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure": "physical-result",
            "thm-tdmath-limiting-derivative-and-concentration": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For the lattice model and canonical law of [[pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure]], let $p_L(\\gamma)=L^{-d}\\log Z_L(\\gamma)$ converge pointwise on an open interval $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ to $p(\\gamma)$. If $p$ is differentiable at $\\beta\\in J_\\beta$, set $e=-p'(\\beta)$ in joules per site. Then the canonical mean energy density satisfies $\\mathbb E_{\\beta,L}[H_L]/L^d\\to e$, and for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there exist $c>0$ and $L_0$ such that\n$$\\mathbb P_{\\beta,L}\\!\\left(\\left|H_L/L^d-e\\right|\\ge\\varepsilon\\right)\\le2e^{-cL^d}\\qquad(L\\ge L_0).$$\nThe probability is the model's finite-volume canonical probability, not an observed frequency. No conclusion is asserted when $p$ is nondifferentiable.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §§1.3.2 and 5.3.1 (printed pp. 19–20 and 156–157)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.4.6 and 6.2.2 (printed pp. 207–208 and 330–333)",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional model probabilities for bounded finite-range lattice systems at a differentiability point of the limiting log-partition density; no concentration claim is made at a nondifferentiable point or for arbitrary observables.",
          "verification": {
            "precheck": "pass"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-finite-bath-canonical-error",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Finite-bath canonical error under a smooth shell-entropy model",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-finite-bath-canonical-error.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and prerequisite acceptance remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling",
            "post-td-microcanonical-sampling",
            "thm-tdmath-finite-bath-error-bound"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling": "physical-assumption",
            "post-td-microcanonical-sampling": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-tdmath-finite-bath-error-bound": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume AC for the regular-shell reading of [[post-td-microcanonical-sampling]]. Let the subsystem have a finite nonempty state set $\\Omega$ and internal energies $E_x$ in joules with $|E_x|\\le L$, where $L$ is in joules. The bath model supplies an open interval $U$ containing $[u_0-L,u_0+L]$, with bath energies $u,u_0\\in U$ in joules, and a dimensionless shell-log-weight function $s_B:U\\to\\mathbb R$; define the positive shell weight by $W_B(u):=e^{s_B(u)}$. Assume $s_B\\in C^2(U)$ and $|s_B''(u)|\\le M$ on the displayed interval, where $M$ is in $\\mathrm{J^{-2}}$. The model adopts $\\beta=s'_B(u_0)>0$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$, $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, and $T=(k_{\\mathrm B}\\beta)^{-1}$ in kelvins. Assume weak additive coupling and separately stipulate that microcanonical shell sampling induces subsystem weights $P_B(x)\\propto W_B(u_0-E_x)=e^{s_B(u_0-E_x)}$; this induced-marginal rule is an additional bath-model assumption, not a consequence of the microcanonical sampling postulate alone. Let $P_C(x)=e^{-\\beta E_x}/Z$ be the corresponding canonical law. With $\\delta=ML^2/2$ (dimensionless), the shell log-weight remainder obeys\n$$|s_B(u_0-E_x)-s_B(u_0)+\\beta E_x|\\le\\delta,$$\nand the normalized laws satisfy\n$$\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}|P_B(x)-P_C(x)|\\le e^{2\\delta}-1.$$\nThis is an $\\ell^1$ probability-vector bound; it does not assert a universal error or a thermodynamic limit.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §1.3 (printed pp. 17–20)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.2 and 4.4 (printed pp. 192–198 and 205–208)",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "A finite subsystem weakly coupled additively to a bath whose dimensionless shell entropy is twice continuously differentiable with bounded curvature on the full exchanged-energy interval; this is a conditional model bound, not a universal finite-size estimate.",
          "verification": {
            "precheck": "pass"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-finite-canonical-identities",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Finite canonical partition, energy, variance, entropy and free-energy identities",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-finite-canonical-identities.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and prerequisite acceptance remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-natural-logarithm",
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
            "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-natural-logarithm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ be a finite microstate model with energies $E_x=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$ from [[def-td-finite-microstate-model]]. Let $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$, and $P_\\beta(x)=e^{-\\beta E_x}/Z(\\beta)$ be the adopted preparation of [[post-td-canonical-sampling]]. With $\\bar E_\\beta=\\sum_xP_\\beta(x)E_x$, $\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)=\\sum_xP_\\beta(x)(E_x-\\bar E_\\beta)^2$, and $S_\\beta=-k_{\\mathrm B}\\sum_xP_\\beta(x)\\log P_\\beta(x)$, the finite partition sum is positive and\n$$-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\bar E_\\beta,\\qquad \\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E),\\qquad S_\\beta=k_{\\mathrm B}(\\log Z+\\beta\\bar E_\\beta).$$\nConsequently the statistical free-energy value $F_{\\mathrm{stat}}:=-k_{\\mathrm B}T\\log Z$ satisfies $F_{\\mathrm{stat}}=\\bar E_\\beta-TS_\\beta$. The derivatives hold the finite state set and its energy list fixed. No equivalence with a macroscopic entropy or Helmholtz potential is asserted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §§1.3.1–1.3.4 (printed pp. 18–26)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.4–4.6 (printed pp. 205–217)",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Exact finite-sum predictions for the adopted canonical law on a fixed finite microstate list with fixed internal energies; the statistical free-energy value is not automatically identified with a macroscopic Legendre potential or an empirical thermodynamic measurement.",
          "verification": {
            "precheck": "pass"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-finite-entropy-maximum",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Canonical weights uniquely maximize finite-state entropy at their mean energy",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-finite-entropy-maximum.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and prerequisite acceptance remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-natural-logarithm",
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling",
            "pthm-td-finite-canonical-identities",
            "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-natural-logarithm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-td-finite-microstate-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-td-finite-canonical-identities": "physical-result",
            "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ be a finite nonempty microstate model, let $T>0$, and let $q_x=e^{-\\beta E_x}/Z$ be its canonical probabilities at $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$. Set $\\bar E=\\sum_xq_xE_x$. Among all probability vectors $r=(r_x)_{x\\in\\Omega}$ with $\\sum_xr_x=1$ and $\\sum_xr_xE_x=\\bar E$, the Shannon entropy $S(r):=-k_{\\mathrm B}\\sum_{x:r_x>0}r_x\\log r_x$ is uniquely maximized by $r=q$. This is a finite-model variational theorem; it does not establish that a physical sample is prepared canonically.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §1.3.3 (printed pp. 22–25)",
