            "pthm-td-countable-canonical-identities": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-differentiable-pressure-concentration": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-finite-bath-canonical-error": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-finite-canonical-identities": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-finite-entropy-maximum": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-finite-mode-occupation-and-trace-formulas": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-finite-quantum-free-energy-minimum": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-general-quantum-canonical-identities": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-independent-factorization": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-invariant-regular-microcanonical-shell": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-measurable-canonical-identities": "physical-result",
            "pthm-td-qualified-discrete-quantum-dephasing": "physical-result",
            "post-td-canonical-sampling": "physical-assumption",
            "post-td-microcanonical-sampling": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "The finite canonical, maximum-entropy, independent-factorization and finite-bath claims concern the exact finite state spaces and shell-weight assumptions stated in their items. Countable and measurable derivative identities require their local summable or integrable moment envelopes; a lower energy bound alone does not normalize the partition function. The lattice bulk-pressure and concentration claims apply only to the bounded finite-range lattice model and, for concentration, a differentiability point of the limiting log-partition density. The page-level continuum-pressure prerequisite is a distinct interaction model; no stable-tempered continuum limit is inferred from the lattice theorem.\n\nThe classical shell result applies only to a nonempty compact regular level with the coarea normalization and a globally defined invertible flow on the stated band. It does not imply ergodicity or equality of long-time averages with the shell law. The quantum trace identities require a semibounded self-adjoint generator, a positive finite Gibbs trace and the stated moment envelope; discrete dephasing is a trace-norm Cesaro limit with degenerate eigenspaces retained, not pointwise relaxation or a Gibbs limit.\n\nBoltzmann microstate entropy, finite Gibbs entropy, von Neumann entropy and the macroscopic entropy function are distinct objects until a model supplies an explicit identification. The statistical value $-k_{\\mathrm B}T\\log Z$ is not silently identified with a macroscopic Helmholtz or grand potential. Canonical and microcanonical sampling are adopted preparation assumptions. None of the finite probability identities, continuum or lattice pressure limits, shell-invariance results or quantum trace calculations reports an experiment or establishes that nature follows the model.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, complete lecture notes, §§1.2–1.3 (printed pp. 2–27)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.2–4.6 (printed pp. 192–217)",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "A scope and hypothesis ledger for the preceding finite, countable, measurable, lattice, regular-shell and discrete-quantum branches; it supplies no additional ensemble-equivalence, ergodicity, thermalization, or empirical claim.",
          "verification": {
            "precheck": "n/a"
          }
        }
      ],
