        {
          "id": "ex-td-mean-vs-trial-outcome",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Positive ensemble mean with a negative realization",
          "reference": "items/ex-td-mean-vs-trial-outcome.md#example",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "deps": [
            "rem-td-fluctuation-model-hypotheses",
            "lem-expectation-by-distribution"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "rem-td-fluctuation-model-hypotheses": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-expectation-by-distribution": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega=\\{\\omega_+,\\omega_-\\}$, $\\mathcal F=\\mathcal P(\\Omega)$, and $w(\\omega_+)=3/4$, $w(\\omega_-)=1/4$. Define $P(A)=\\sum_{\\omega\\in A}w(\\omega)$ for each $A\\in\\mathcal F$; then $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ is a probability space. Let $Y:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be the random variable $Y(\\omega_+)=2a$ and $Y(\\omega_-)=-2a$, where $a>0$ and the values are numerical values in the unit $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. This finite model makes $Y$ measurable and integrable. The label $Y$ is only an illustrative entropy-valued variable; this example adopts no physical path law.\n\nThis arithmetic example illustrates the distinction in [[rem-td-fluctuation-model-hypotheses]].",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": []
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "A two-outcome probability model for an illustrative entropy-valued random variable; it is not a specified physical fluctuation law or reported observation.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-td-local-equilibrium-profile",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A nonconstant local-equilibrium temperature field",
          "reference": "items/ex-td-local-equilibrium-profile.md#example",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $\\mathcal O=(0,L)^3$ in metre coordinates and $I=(0,\\tau)$ in second coordinates, with $L>0\\,\\mathrm m$ and $\\tau>0\\,\\mathrm s$. Choose $\\Theta_0>0\\,\\mathrm K$ and dimensionless $0<\\alpha<1$. Instantiate the local-state parameter set as $\\mathcal Z=(0,\\infty)\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ and define its one-coordinate field $z:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to\\mathcal Z$ by\n\n$$z(\\mathbf x,t)=\\frac{\\Theta_0}{1\\,\\mathrm K}\\left(1+\\alpha\\frac{x_1}{L}\\right),\\qquad T(\\mathbf x,t)=(1\\,\\mathrm K)z(\\mathbf x,t)=\\Theta_0\\left(1+\\alpha\\frac{x_1}{L}\\right).$$\n\nStipulate the local-equilibrium approximation: each coarse-grained cell at $(\\mathbf x,t)$ is represented by the equilibrium constitutive state labelled by $z(\\mathbf x,t)$. The field is static and nonconstant; this stipulation is a model assumption, not an assertion that a physical sample realizes the profile.",
          "provenance": {
