      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-tdmath-vertical-separation-biconjugate",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A vertical separator and biconjugation outside the domain",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
            "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
            "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
            "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
            "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\{0,+\\infty\\}$ be the indicator of $[0,\\infty)$: $f(y)=0$ for $y\\ge0$ and $f(y)=+\\infty$ for $y<0$. At $z=(-1,0)$, the epigraph is separated by a vertical hyperplane (one whose coefficient of the epigraph-height coordinate is zero). The conjugate is $f^*(p)=0$ for $p\\le0$ and $+\\infty$ for $p>0$, so $f^{**}(-1)=+\\infty=f(-1)$. The affine minorants $\\ell_k(y)=-ky$, $k\\ge0$, display the unbounded dual values at $y=-1$.",
          "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct calculation.\n\n1.1 The domain is $[0,\\infty)$, so it is nonempty and $f$ is proper. Its epigraph is $E=\\{(y,s):y\\ge0,\\ s\\ge0\\}$, a nonempty closed convex set; [F3] therefore makes $f$ closed and convex. The point $(-1,0)$ lies outside $E$. With $u=(-1,0)$ and $c=0$, every $(y,s)\\in E$ satisfies $\\langle u,(y,s)\\rangle=-y\\le0<1=\\langle u,(-1,0)\\rangle$, so this is the strict vertical separator; its height coefficient is zero. [F1, F3, L1, given, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $p\\le0$ and $y\\ge0$, $py\\le0$, with equality at $y=0$, so $f^*(p)=0$. For $p>0$, given any real $M$ choose $y>\\max\\{0,M/p\\}$; then $py>M$, so the defining supremum is $+\\infty$. Therefore $\\operatorname{dom}f^*=(-\\infty,0]$. [F2, given, algebra, choose]\n\n2.1 At $x=-1$, the biconjugate is $\\sup_{p\\le0}(-p-f^*(p))=\\sup_{p\\le0}(-p)=+\\infty$: for every real bound $M$, the admissible choice $p=-\\max\\{M,0\\}-1$ gives $-p>M$. Thus $f^{**}(-1)=f(-1)$. [F2, step 1.2, algebra, choose]\n\n3.1 For each $k\\ge0$ and $y\\ge0$, $\\ell_k(y)=-ky\\le0=f(y)$, so $\\ell_k$ is an affine minorant. Its value at $-1$ is $k$, and the conjugate choice $p=-k$ has $f^*(-k)=0$, so the same family realizes arbitrarily large dual values at the point outside the domain. The conclusion is consistent with the full equality in [L2]. [F2, L2, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §12, Theorems 12.1–12.2",
                "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-tdmath-flat-face-nonunique-minimizer",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A flat exposed face with many Fenchel minimizers",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
            "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
            "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
            "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality",
            "thm-tdmath-fenchel-attainment"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tdmath-fenchel-attainment": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $f$ be the indicator of $[-1,1]$. The zero tilt $f(y)-0\\cdot y$ has every point of the interval as a minimizer, and $f^*(0)=0$ is attained at every such point. Equivalently, $0\\in\\partial f(x)$ for every $x\\in[-1,1]$. Thus the supporting face at slope zero is the whole interval, and coercive Fenchel attainment need not be unique.",
          "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct calculation.\n\n1.1 The epigraph is $[-1,1]\\times[0,\\infty)$, a nonempty closed convex rectangle, and the domain $[-1,1]$ is nonempty; hence the indicator is proper, closed, and convex by [F1, F3]. At slope $p=0$, every $y\\in[-1,1]$ contributes $0$ to the conjugate supremum, and no other $y$ is in the effective domain, so $f^*(0)=0$ and every point of the interval attains this supremum. [F1, F2, F3, given]\n\n2.1 Fix any $x\\in[-1,1]$. For every $y\\in\\mathbb R$, $f(y)\\ge0=f(x)+0(y-x)$: equality holds on the interval and the left side is $+\\infty$ outside it. Hence $0\\in\\partial f(x)$; [L1] also gives $f^*(0)=0=0\\cdot x-f(x)$ at each such $x$. [F2, L1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The zero tilt is $g=f$. It is coercive as an extended-real function: for any $M\\in\\mathbb R$, choosing $R=2$ ensures $|x|\\ge R$ implies $g(x)=+\\infty>M$. Its minimum is $0$, attained at every $x\\in[-1,1]$, so this example satisfies [L2] while exhibiting nonuniqueness and a whole flat supporting face. [L2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. T. Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §23",
                "url": "https://djvu.online/file/68u51YEBQaqiP"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-tdmath-singular-phase-rank",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A singular two-phase equation map with a four-dimensional solution set",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Take $C=2$ components and $P=2$ phases on the open coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times(0,1)^2\\subset\\mathbb R^4$. Set all four chemical-potential coordinate functions $\\mu_i^\\alpha$ identically equal to zero. The two coexistence equations then vanish identically, so the solution set is all of $U$ and has local dimension four. The regular formula would give $C-P+2=2$, but its nonsingular-minor hypothesis fails because the equation Jacobian is zero.",
          "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct calculation.\n\n1.1 For $C=P=2$, there are $n=2+2(2-1)=4$ coordinates and $q=2(2-1)=2$ equations. Since each $\\mu_i^\\alpha$ is identically zero, both differences $\\mu_i^2-\\mu_i^1$ equal zero at every point of $U$. Thus the zero set is exactly the open set $U$, which is locally parameterized by its four original coordinates. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 The derivative of each identically zero equation is the zero covector, so the equation Jacobian has rank zero and no $2\\times2$ minor is invertible. Therefore [L1] does not apply; the example does not contradict its regular dimension count, and it shows why equation count alone does not determine local dimension. [L1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Faye Jackson, MATH 395 notes, Implicit Function Theorem, pp. 99–106 and 127–132",
                "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~alephnil/notes/MATH-395-notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-tdmath-water-reaction-boundary-tangent",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Water formation at a boundary of the feasible extent interval",
          "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L337-L374",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For the reaction $2\\,\\mathrm{H_2}+\\mathrm{O_2}\\to2\\,\\mathrm{H_2O}$, let extent $\\xi$ and species amounts use moles. The dimensionless stoichiometric column is $\\nu=(-2,-1,2)^{\\mathsf T}$ in the species order $(\\mathrm{H_2},\\mathrm{O_2},\\mathrm{H_2O})$, and take $n^0=(2,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}$. Then the feasible extent interval is $[0,1]$, and at $\\xi=0$ the tangent cone permits only forward directions $h\\ge0$. The matrix of hydrogen and oxygen atom counts annihilates $\\nu$. For the explicitly mathematical objective $\\Phi(n)=n_{\\mathrm{H_2O}}$, the induced extent function is $f(\\xi)=2\\xi$ and has a boundary minimum at zero. This is a stoichiometric calculation, not a claim about chemical equilibrium or a measured system.",
          "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct calculation.\n\n1.1 The species-amount vector is $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi=(2-2\\xi,1-\\xi,2\\xi)^{\\mathsf T}$. Nonnegativity of its third component requires $\\xi\\ge0$, and nonnegativity of its first two components requires $\\xi\\le1$; hence the feasible set is exactly $[0,1]$. [given, algebra]\n\n1.2 The elemental count matrix is $D=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0&2\\\\0&2&1\\end{pmatrix}$, with first row counting hydrogen atoms and second row oxygen atoms per molecule. Direct multiplication gives $D\\nu=(-4+4,-2+2)^{\\mathsf T}=(0,0)^{\\mathsf T}$, so every change $n(\\xi)-n^0=\\nu\\xi$ preserves both atom totals. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 At $\\xi_*=0$, only the water component is zero. Its directional change is $(\\nu h)_3=2h$, so [L1] gives $T_{\\mathcal F}(0)=\\{h:2h\\ge0\\}=[0,\\infty)$. A negative direction is infeasible because it would make the water amount $2\\xi$ negative. [L1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For $\\Phi(n)=n_3$, the induced function is $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n(\\xi))=2\\xi$. Its derivative at $0$ is $Df(0)h=2h\\ge0$ for every feasible tangent $h\\ge0$, and $f(\\xi)\\ge f(0)$ for every $\\xi\\in[0,1]$. This verifies the boundary first-order condition and minimum directly for this chosen mathematical objective. [L1, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Bindel, Optimization Theory, §6.1.4, Numerical Methods for Data Science lecture notes",
                "url": "https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~bindel/nmds/00-Background/05-Optimization.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md",
      "order": 289
    },
