        {
          "id": "lem-td-rectangular-poincare",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Mean-zero Poincare inequality on a rectangle",
          "reference": "items/lem-td-rectangular-poincare.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $L_1,\\ldots,L_d>0$, and let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^d(0,L_i)$. If $w\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega,\\mathbb R)$ satisfies $\\int_\\Omega w(x)\\,dx=0$, write $\\nabla w=(\\partial_1w,\\ldots,\\partial_dw)$ and $|\\nabla w|^2=\\sum_{i=1}^d|\\partial_iw|^2$. Then\n$$\\int_\\Omega |w(x)|^2\\,dx\\le \\frac d2\\sum_{i=1}^d L_i^2\\int_\\Omega |\\partial_iw(x)|^2\\,dx\\le C_\\Omega\\int_\\Omega |\\nabla w(x)|^2\\,dx,$$\nwhere $C_\\Omega=\\frac d2\\max_iL_i^2$. All integrals are the multidimensional Riemann integrals of continuous functions on the indicated closed rectangles, with the usual extension to their interiors.",
          "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/lem-td-rectangular-poincare.md and its proof contract.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §4.4",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch4.pdf",
                "locator": "Official chapter PDF retrieved; §4.4, Theorem 4.9, Poincare inequalities."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-collision-h-theorem",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Conditional entropy production in the periodic Boltzmann model",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-collision-h-theorem.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model",
            "def-td-si-quantities",
            "post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model",
            "thm-tdmath-regular-collision-entropy"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-regular-elastic-collision-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-tdmath-regular-collision-entropy": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega_{\\rm per}=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ with opposite faces identified, and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval. Assume the current conditional mathematical result [[thm-tdmath-regular-collision-entropy]] at its exact draft interface [L1] below. Let $f:\\Omega_{\\rm per}\\times\\mathbb R^3\\times I\\to(0,\\infty)$ satisfy the periodic Boltzmann postulate [[post-td-boltzmann-molecular-chaos-model]] and the regularity, integrability, and domination hypotheses in [A2]–[A3]. Fix a positive constant reference density $f_{\\rm ref}$ with the same units as $f$, and define the kinetic entropy\n$$S_{\\rm kin}(t)=-k_B\\int_{\\Omega_{\\rm per}}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}f(x,v,t)\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{f(x,v,t)}{f_{\\rm ref}}\\right)\\,d^3v\\,d^3x.$$\nThen\n$$\\frac{dS_{\\rm kin}}{dt}=\\frac{k_B}{4}\\int_{\\Omega_{\\rm per}}\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(A-B)\\log\\!\\left(\\frac AB\\right)\\,d\\nu\\,d^3x\\ge0,$$\nwhere $A=f(x,v,t)f(x,w,t)$ and $B=f(x,v',t)f(x,w',t)$; the supplier's $\\log A-\\log B$ denotes the dimensionless difference $\\log(A/B)$ for these positive quantities of equal units. Equality holds exactly when $A=B$ for $\\nu$-almost every collision at almost every spatial point. This is a conditional deduction inside the adopted incoming-molecular-chaos kinetic model. The cited mathematical carrier is currently draft and has not received independent proof acceptance; no microscopic derivation, experimental claim, or Maxwellian characterization is included.",
          "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-collision-h-theorem.md and its proof contract.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §8.10",
                "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 607-page lecture notes read; printed pp. 529–534, collision term, incoming versus outgoing factorization, and H theorem."
              },
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, Chapter 6",
                "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 224-page current-edition PDF read; §§6.1–6.4, Boltzmann collision model and entropy inequality."
              },
              {
                "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 8.333 Statistical Mechanics I, Lecture 9",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/ce250b44390106351cb97aafb0060ca7_MIT8_333F13_Lec9.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 9-page lecture read; pp. 3–4, pre-collision factorization and reversal qualification."
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Plasma Physics, Boltzmann H-Theorem",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/plasma/lectures1/node40.html",
                "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML section read; equations (3.123)–(3.144), collision-production sign and equality discussion."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional kinetic-entropy production for a positive regular solution of the periodic, single-species elastic Boltzmann model with incoming molecular chaos; no many-particle derivation or Maxwellian equality characterization is asserted.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-fourier-entropy-production",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Entropy production in the local Fourier heat model",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-fourier-entropy-production.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields",
            "post-td-local-energy-balance",
            "post-td-fourier-conductivity",
            "post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-local-energy-balance": "physical-assumption",
            "post-td-fourier-conductivity": "physical-assumption",
            "post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval, $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ a fixed rectangle, and $(T,u,j_Q,K)$ a field record from [[def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields]] satisfying the adopted local-energy, Fourier, and local-entropy postulates [[post-td-local-energy-balance]], [[post-td-fourier-conductivity]], and [[post-td-local-equilibrium-entropy-density]]. Assume additionally that $j_Q$ and its first spatial derivatives are continuous on every compact subset of $\\overline\\Omega\\times I$. Then, throughout $\\Omega\\times I$,\n$$\\partial_t s(T)+\\operatorname{div}\\!\\left(\\frac{j_Q}{T}\\right)=\\frac{(\\nabla T)^{\\mathsf T}K_s(x,T)\\nabla T}{T^2}\\ge0,$$\nwhere $K_s=(K+K^{\\mathsf T})/2$. For every compact time interval $[t_0,t_1]\\subset I$, the model's total entropy $S(t)=\\int_\\Omega s(T(x,t))\\,dx$ is differentiable and nondecreasing, while $\\int_\\Omega u(T(x,t))\\,dx$ is constant. These are conditional deductions of the declared continuum model, not observations or a claim that every medium obeys Fourier's law.",
          "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-fourier-entropy-production.md and its proof contract.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §6.5, heat conduction",
                "url": "https://batch.libretexts.org/print/Letter/Finished/phys-32579/Full.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 6, §6.5, equations (114)–(119), Fourier flux and entropy production"
              },
              {
                "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare, 18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations, The 1-D Heat Equation",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/d11b374a85c3fde55ec971fe587f8a50_heateqni.pdf",
                "locator": "Full-text lecture note retrieved; §§1.1–1.2, Fourier flux, balance, and insulated boundary conventions."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional local entropy and energy balance for a sufficiently regular, fixed-composition local-equilibrium continuum field on a rectangular insulated body with a positive-semidefinite symmetric conductivity tensor.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Constant-conductivity heat evolution on an insulated rectangle",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields",
            "post-td-local-energy-balance",
            "post-td-fourier-conductivity",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "cor-l-one-approximate-identities-converge-uniformly-on-compacta-for-continuous-functions",
            "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
            "def-heat-kernel",
            "lem-heat-kernel-normalisation-scaling-and-derivatives",
            "lem-spatial-and-time-derivatives-pass-through-heat-convolution-for-positive-time",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant",
            "thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure",
            "thm-heat-cauchy-solution-for-bounded-continuous-data",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-td-rectangular-local-equilibrium-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-td-local-energy-balance": "physical-assumption",
            "post-td-fourier-conductivity": "physical-assumption",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-l-one-approximate-identities-converge-uniformly-on-compacta-for-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-heat-kernel": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-heat-kernel-normalisation-scaling-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-spatial-and-time-derivatives-pass-through-heat-convolution-for-positive-time": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heat-cauchy-solution-for-bounded-continuous-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^3(0,L_i)$ with $L_i>0$ metres, let $c>0$ be a constant volumetric heat capacity in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}\\,K^{-1}}$ with $u'(T)=c$ throughout the range of $g$, let $k>0$ be a constant scalar conductivity in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$ with $K\\equiv kI_3$ in [[post-td-fourier-conductivity]], and put $a=k/c$ in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\nFor $t>0$ define\n$$T(x,t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Gamma(x-y,at)g(y)\\,dy,\\qquad \\Gamma(z,\\theta)=(4\\pi\\theta)^{-3/2}e^{-|z|^2/(4\\theta)},$$\nand set $T(x,0)=g(x)$ on $\\overline\\Omega$. Here $\\nabla T=(\\partial_{x_1}T,\\partial_{x_2}T,\\partial_{x_3}T)$, $\\Delta T:=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\partial_{x_i}^2T$, and on a face with outward unit normal $n$, $\\partial_nT:=n\\cdot\\nabla T$. This field is the unique $C^1$-in-time, $C^2$-in-space solution on each finite time interval of $c\\partial_tT=k\\Delta T$ on $\\Omega$, with $\\partial_nT=0$ on every face and the given initial value. It satisfies $T_{\\min}\\le T(x,t)\\le T_{\\max}$ for all $t\\ge0$, has $C^2$ spatial derivatives continuous to $t=0$, and its right time derivative there is $a\\Delta g$. Under [[post-td-local-energy-balance]] and [[post-td-fourier-conductivity]], it is a global-in-time solution of that restricted physical continuum model.",
          "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence.md and its proof contract.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §5.1",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch5.pdf",
                "locator": "Official chapter PDF retrieved; §5.1, Theorem 5.5 and the heat-kernel representation."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Existence and uniqueness for the three-dimensional rigid Fourier heat model with constant positive volumetric heat capacity and scalar conductivity, homogeneous Neumann walls, and even-periodic uniformly regular positive initial data; no general rough-data or arbitrary-domain PDE claim is made.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Exponential relaxation of the constant-conductivity Neumann heat model",
          "reference": "items/pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-td-rectangular-poincare",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence": "physical-result",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-td-rectangular-poincare": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the hypotheses and solution $T$ of [[pthm-td-constant-conductivity-neumann-existence]], let $V=|\\Omega|=L_1L_2L_3$, define the spatial mean $\\overline T(t)=V^{-1}\\int_\\Omega T(x,t)\\,dx$, and put $E(t)=\\int_\\Omega|T(x,t)-\\overline T(t)|^2\\,dx$. With $a=k/c$ and $C_\\Omega=\\frac32\\max_iL_i^2$, the mean is constant and\n$$E(t)\\le e^{-2at/C_\\Omega}E(0)\\qquad(t\\ge0).$$\nThus this model solution converges exponentially to its initial spatial mean in $L^2(\\Omega)$; no pointwise-uniform convergence rate is asserted.",
          "strategy": "proof_strategy: direct; complete argument is in items/pthm-td-neumann-heat-relaxation.md and its proof contract.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §4.4 and §5.1",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch4.pdf",
                "locator": "Official chapter PDF retrieved; §4.4, Theorem 4.9, Poincare inequality discussion."
              },
              {
                "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations, §5.1",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/ch5.pdf",
                "locator": "Official chapter PDF retrieved; §5.1, Theorem 5.5, heat-kernel evolution."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional L2 relaxation for the compatible positive-temperature solution of the constant-capacity, constant-conductivity model on a three-dimensional insulated rectangle.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "rem-td-solution-existence-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Scope of the continuum heat solution results",
          "reference": "items/rem-td-solution-existence-scope.md#remarks",
          "design_state": "Complete draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "deps": [
