          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $a>0$ and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\lambda_3)$ be a real or complex valued Lebesgue integrable function on $\\mathbb R^3$ that vanishes $\\lambda_3$-almost everywhere outside the closed ball $B_a:=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\lVert y\\rVert_2\\le a\\}$ ([[def-l-one-of-a-measure]], [[def-metric-ball]]). Put\n\n$$Q:=\\int\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3,\\qquad p:=\\int y\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3\\in\\mathbb C^3,\\qquad \\lVert\\rho\\rVert_1:=\\int\\lvert\\rho\\rvert\\,d\\lambda_3,$$\n\nthe vector $p$ being defined by $p_k:=\\int y_k\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$ for the canonical coordinates $k<3$, namely $k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$, and $\\langle p,x\\rangle:=\\sum_{k<3}p_kx_k$ being the complex-bilinear coordinate contraction, with no complex conjugation, which agrees with the real Euclidean pairing when $p$ is real; if $\\rho$ is real-valued then $Q$ is real and $p\\in\\mathbb R^3$, while $R(x)$ below is real in that case and complex in general. Then for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $r:=\\lVert x\\rVert_2\\ge2a>0$,\n\n$$\\int\\frac{\\rho(y)}{\\lVert x-y\\rVert_2}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)=\\frac Qr+\\frac{\\langle p,x\\rangle}{r^3}+R(x),\\qquad R(x):=\\int_{B_a}\\rho(y)\\Bigl(\\frac1{\\lVert x-y\\rVert_2}-\\frac1r-\\frac{\\langle x,y\\rangle}{r^3}\\Bigr)d\\lambda_3(y),$$\n\nand the remainder satisfies the explicit bound\n\n$$\\lvert R(x)\\rvert\\le\\frac{16\\,a^2\\,\\lVert\\rho\\rVert_1}{r^3}.$$\n\nIn the all-space kernel integral define $K_x(y)=\\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{-1}$ for $y\\ne x$ and $K_x(x)=0$, so its integrand means $\\rho K_x$ and is an everywhere-defined measurable function. This convention cannot affect the integral: $x\\notin B_a$ and the density vanishes almost everywhere outside $B_a$. All integrals are Lebesgue integrals; the closed ball $B_a$ is Borel and $\\chi_{B_a}\\rho$ is integrable. The constant $16$ is permissible but not sharp, and $Q$, $p$ and $\\lVert\\rho\\rVert_1$ are finite. No derivative of the remainder in $x$ is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Set $h(s)=\\lvert x-sy\\rvert^{-1}$; $h$ is $C^2$ on $[0,1]$ and the identity $h(1)-h(0)-h'(0)=\\int_0^1(1-s)h''(s)\\,ds$ follows from the fundamental theorem applied to $\\frac{d}{ds}[(1-s)h'(s)+h(s)]=(1-s)h''(s)$. Because $\\lvert x-sy\\rvert\\ge r-a\\ge r/2$ for $r\\ge2a$, one has $\\lvert h''(s)\\rvert\\le4\\lvert y\\rvert^2/(r-a)^3\\le32\\lvert y\\rvert^2/r^3$, and integrating against $\\lvert\\rho\\rvert$ gives the bound with $C=16$, which is permissible but not sharp. Both leading terms follow directly by integrating h(0)=1/r and hprime(0)=(x dot y)/r³; no normalization-sensitive decay theorem is needed as a logical supplier. The parameter integral in $s\\in[0,1]$ is a continuous Riemann integral, so [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]] bounds it by $32|y|^2r^{-3}\\int_0^1(1-s)ds=16|y|^2r^{-3}$. The subsequent integral against the $L^1$ source density is Lebesgue: its majorant $16a^2r^{-3}|\\rho|$ is handled by [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]] and [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], not by the Darboux theorem. Both $Q$ and $p$ are finite by the same bounded-support estimate. No derivative bound on an unspecified remainder is inferred. Choose an a.e.-equal representative zero off the closed source ball. The integral equality and all moments are unchanged by [[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]; every bound is thus valid for the declared a.e. support hypothesis.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
