            "thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, $z:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ a $C^1$ trajectory, and $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ open containing its graph. Assume the graph map $\\gamma:I\\to\\Omega$, $\\gamma(t)=(z(t),t)$, is proper: the inverse image of every compact subset of $\\Omega$ is compact in $I$. For $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ define $\\langle\\rho,\\psi\\rangle=q\\int_I\\psi(z(t),t)\\,dt$ and $\\langle J,\\psi\\rangle=q\\int_I z'(t)\\psi(z(t),t)\\,dt$. These define distributions and $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$. Properness is automatic if $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3\\times I$, or if $I=\\mathbb R$. Endpoint variant: if $I=(a,b)$ has finite endpoints, $z$ extends continuously to $[a,b]$, the absolute improper integral $\\int_a^b|z'(t)|\\,dt$ is finite, and both endpoint events belong to $\\Omega$, the same integrals instead define distributions with $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=q\\delta_{(z(a),a)}-q\\delta_{(z(b),b)}$. A finite-history source is source-free only when the endpoint contribution vanishes on the chosen test domain; neither mere graph containment nor test compactness alone supplies this.",
          "strategy": "For a fixed compact test support $K\\subset\\Omega$, properness makes $\\gamma^{-1}(K)$ compact in $I$. It lies in one closed interior interval $[u,v]\\subset I$, on which $z'$ is bounded. Both pairings are bounded by constants depending on $K$ times $\\sup_K|\\psi|$, proving distribution continuity on each test-support space. The pullback of a test is $C^1$ and compactly supported in $I$. By the total chain rule, pairing the divergence gives $-q\\int_I[\\partial_t\\psi(z(t),t)+z'(t)\\cdot\\nabla\\psi(z(t),t)]dt=-q\\int_I(d/dt)\\psi(z(t),t)dt=0$ by interior FTC. For $I=\\mathbb R$, compact ambient support bounds the time coordinate and its graph preimage is a closed bounded subset of $\\mathbb R$; for $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3\\times I$, compact ambient support has time projection compact inside $I$. This proves both automatic properness claims. In the endpoint variant, finite interval length and the finite absolute improper integral of $z'$ bound the pairings on every compact test support. Apply the chain rule and FTC on $[a+\\epsilon,b-\\epsilon]$; the derivative pullback is absolutely integrable, and continuity at endpoints gives the limiting identity $q\\psi(z(a),a)-q\\psi(z(b),b)$. This proves the two endpoint deltas. In particular $I=(0,1),z=0,\\Omega=\\mathbb R^4$ gives $q\\delta_{(0,0)}-q\\delta_{(0,1)}$, refuting the old unconditional claim. The endpoint integration limit is explicitly this truncation argument, not an invented source reading. Maxwell existence and point self-force are not asserted. Here the two-ended improper integral means the sum of the limits of integrals on [a+epsilon,r] and [r,b−epsilon] for any fixed interior r. Monotonicity and completeness prove convergence of every nonnegative integrand bounded by a constant times 1+|z′|; signed limits then follow from the positive and negative parts. Thus the endpoint variant uses only the explicitly supplied improper convention and truncated ordinary FTC.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "S. Dyatlov, Lecture notes for 18.155 (MIT, 2026), Chapter 2 and Chapter 3",
                "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~dyatlov/18.155/155-notes.pdf"
