          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-surface-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distributional-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$). Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, let $S\\subset\\Omega$ be a two-sided compact embedded $C^2$ interface with unit normal $n$ from $\\Omega_-$ to $\\Omega_+$, and let $F$ be $C^1$ on $\\Omega_-\\cup\\Omega_+$ with one-sided $C^1$ traces on $S$. Then, away from the edge $\\partial S$ and in the distributional sense, $$\\operatorname{div}F=\\{\\operatorname{div}F\\}_{\\mathrm{piecewise}}+n\\cdot[F]\\,\\delta_S,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}F=\\{\\operatorname{curl}F\\}_{\\mathrm{piecewise}}+n\\times[F]\\,\\delta_S,$$ where the bracket terms denote the locally integrable functions equal to the classical divergence and curl on each open side and $[F]=F_+-F_-$.",
          "strategy": "Against a test supported away from the edge, split $-\\int F\\cdot\\nabla\\psi$ into the two sides and apply the divergence theorem to $\\psi F$ on each side; the outward normal is $n$ on the minus side and $-n$ on the plus side, so the boundary terms combine to $(F_+-F_-)\\cdot n$. Componentwise integration by parts gives $\\partial_iF_j=\\{\\partial_iF_j\\}+n_i[F_j]\\delta_S$, and antisymmetrizing the index pair gives the curl formula. A moving interface needs a separate account.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis, 2014), §1.10, §1.12, §2.7",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/pde_notes.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. Dyatlov, Lecture notes for 18.155 (MIT, 2026), Chapter 2 and Chapter 3",
                "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~dyatlov/18.155/155-notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Joint regularity of radial vector and scalar potentials on star-shaped domains",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "deps": [
            "thm-a-divergence-free-c1-field-on-a-star-shaped-open-set-has-a-vector-potential",
            "thm-poincare-lemma-for-star-shaped-domains",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn",
            "def-vector-potential-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn",
            "thm-heine-cantor-metric",
            "lem-uniform-integral-error-bound",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-a-divergence-free-c1-field-on-a-star-shaped-open-set-has-a-vector-potential": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-poincare-lemma-for-star-shaped-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-potential-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-cantor-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-uniform-integral-error-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "**Regularity convention for a possibly non-open parameter interval.** Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval and let $k\\in\\mathbb N$. A map on $U\\times I$ is **jointly $C^k$** when it is the restriction of a $C^k$ map on some open neighbourhood $N\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ of $U\\times I$, in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]; it is **jointly $C^\\infty$** when it is the restriction of one smooth map on such an open neighbourhood. For open $I$ these are exactly the usual open-domain classes; at included endpoints of a nondegenerate interval, time derivatives are the corresponding relative one-sided derivatives. For empty or singleton $I$ the convention still asserts extension existence and the spatial identities below, but it does not determine time jets uniquely from the restricted values alone. The convention does not require a fixed uniform enlargement of an unbounded $I$.\n\n**Vector witness.** Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open and star-shaped with centre $a\\in U$ ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]), let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval, let $k\\ge1$, and let $B:U\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be jointly $C^k$ with $\\operatorname{div}B(\\cdot,t)=0$ on $U$ for every $t\\in I$ ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]). Then\n\n$$A(x,t):=\\int_0^1s\\,B\\bigl(a+s(x-a),t\\bigr)\\times(x-a)\\,ds$$\n\nis jointly $C^k$ on $U\\times I$ and\n\n$$\\operatorname{curl}A(\\cdot,t)=B(\\cdot,t)\\qquad(t\\in I),$$\n\nthe curl being taken in the spatial variables and the cross product being that of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]].\n\n**Scalar witness.** If in addition $C:U\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is jointly $C^k$ with $\\operatorname{curl}C(\\cdot,t)=0$ on $U$ for every $t\\in I$, then\n\n$$\\phi(x,t):=-\\int_0^1\\bigl\\langle C\\bigl(a+s(x-a),t\\bigr),\\,x-a\\bigr\\rangle\\,ds$$\n\nis jointly $C^k$ on $U\\times I$ and\n\n$$\\nabla\\phi(\\cdot,t)=-C(\\cdot,t)\\qquad(t\\in I),$$\n\nthe gradient and inner product being those of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] and [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. For jointly $C^\\infty$ data both potentials are jointly $C^\\infty$. No global potential on a non-star-shaped open set is claimed, and the restrictions are continuous at included endpoints by joint regularity.",
          "strategy": "The interval regularity convention is supplied explicitly in the statement, rather than treating the imported open-domain definition as already covering non-open products. To prove regularity in that same extension sense, choose an open-neighbourhood Ck extension of the data (intersect the extensions' neighbourhoods if both B and C are used). Let P be the set of (x,t) for which every (a+s(x-a),t), 0<=s<=1, lies in that open extension domain. It contains UxI by star-shapedness. It is open: at any one point of P its compact radial segment has a finite cover by balls contained in the extension domain; equivalently its distance from the closed complement is positive, and continuity of the radial map uniformly in s keeps all nearby (x,t) segments inside those balls. The identical radial integral of the extension data is therefore defined on P and is Ck there by the compact parameter proof below; its restriction is the stated potential. For smooth input use its one smooth extension and the same argument through every finite order. Curl-freeness/divergence-freeness is only needed on the original slices to establish the field identities there, not on the enlarged neighbourhood. Set w=x-a and z_s=a+sw. The spatial star-shaped hypothesis puts z_s in U for 0<=s<=1. For any compact spatial box Q contained in U and compact parameter subinterval J contained in I, the radial image of [0,1]xQ is compact inside U. Each component of the vector and scalar radial integrands is a finite sum of products of the polynomial factors s,w and the Ck components of B(z_s,t) or C(z_s,t). Chain and product rules give every mixed spatial/time word derivative through total order k as a continuous function of (s,x,t) on the corresponding compact product. Apply thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle in each parameter coordinate, with others fixed, and induct on derivative order. Uniform continuity on the compact product and lem-uniform-integral-error-bound show that each resulting derivative integral is jointly continuous. Included endpoints of nondegenerate time intervals use the relative endpoint derivatives of the compact-rectangle supplier. For empty or singleton I, construct the potentials using a chosen ambient extension on its open radial-admissibility set and restrict; this proves the same extension existence and spatial identities, without claiming uniquely determined time jets on the degenerate restriction. Thus both explicit integrals are jointly Ck, without assuming k+1 derivatives, and applying this for every k gives the smooth assertion. No surface-measure or Lebesgue integration/Choice branch of lem-em-parameter-integrals is needed: these are ordinary one-dimensional integrals in s on [0,1].\n\nAt each fixed t, the positive vector integral is exactly the witness of thm-a-divergence-free-c1-field-on-a-star-shaped-open-set-has-a-vector-potential, so curl A=B. For the scalar branch put Psi_C(x,t)=integral_0^1 <C(z_s,t),w> ds, and take phi_C=-Psi_C. The published Poincare theorem gives grad Psi_C=C; its convention is positive gradient, so it cannot itself justify grad of the positive integral being -C. Explicitly, curl C=0 means partial_j C_i=partial_i C_j in three dimensions. Differentiation gives partial_j Psi_C=integral_0^1 [C_j(z_s,t)+s sum_i w_i partial_j C_i(z_s,t)] ds. Replace partial_j C_i by partial_i C_j; the integrand becomes d/ds[s C_j(z_s,t)]. thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative gives C_j(x,t)-0*C_j(a,t)=C_j(x,t), hence partial_j phi_C=-C_j. Therefore grad phi_C=-C with the stated finite-k and smooth joint regularity. In particular for C=e1, phi_C=-(x1-a1), so grad phi_C=-e1; the old positive integral gave +e1 and was a concrete contradiction. Neither a general non-star-shaped global existence claim nor a new physical assumption is introduced. On a ball the same construction is local even when the surrounding open set has holes; scalar circulation and vector-flux obstructions remain distinct.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Feldman, A. Rechnitzer, E. Yeager, CLP-4 Vector Calculus (UBC), §4.1 (Theorems 4.1.4-4.1.16)",
                "url": "https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~CLP/CLP4/clp_4_vc/sec_graadDivCurl.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis II, §9.1 (Leibniz integral rule), Theorem 9.1.1",
                "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/realanal2.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
