            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-classical-stokes-theorem-for-a-c2-surface-patch": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-positive-orientation-for-elementary-region-boundaries": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "Throughout this Example and Verification, the spatial labels are local one-based names: $x_i:=x(i-1)$ for $i=1,2,3$, $e_1=(1,0,0)$, $e_2=(0,1,0)$, $e_3=(0,0,1)$, and $\\partial_i$ denotes the canonical coordinate derivative $\\partial_{i-1}$ under this relabeling. Thus the locally named $e_i$ has canonical coordinate $1$ at $i-1$ and $0$ at the other coordinates; the tuples, inner product, cross product, divergence and curl retain the canonical meanings of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] and [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]. This local notation does not change the zero-based indices of [[lem-em-continuous-localization]].\n\nTake the unit square $D=[0,1]^2$ as parameter region, an open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a function $a\\in C^2(I)$ with $a>0$ everywhere, and\n\n$$r(u,v,t):=\\bigl(a(t)u,\\ a(t)v,\\ 0\\bigr),\\qquad B:=e_3,\\qquad v(x,t):=\\frac{a'(t)}{a(t)}\\,(x_1,x_2,0).$$\n\nThen $r$ is of class $C^2$ on the open set $\\mathbb R^2\\times I$, the tangent vectors are $r_u=(a,0,0)$ and $r_v=(0,a,0)$ with $r_u\\times r_v=(0,0,a^2)\\ne0$ on $D^\\circ\\times I^\\circ=D^\\circ\\times I$, the field $B$ is constant with $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, and $v$ is $C^1$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times I$ with $v(r(u,v,t),t)=(a'(t)u,a'(t)v,0)=r_t(u,v,t)$; so the data satisfy the standing hypotheses of [[def-em-moving-patch]]. For every $t\\in I$ the parametrized flux is $\\Phi(t)=a(t)^2$, hence $\\Phi'(t)=2a(t)a'(t)$; the fixed-time surface integral of $\\partial_tB$ is $0$; and $\\operatorname{curl}(v\\times B)=-2(a'(t)/a(t))\\,e_3$, so patch Stokes gives $\\oint_{\\partial S(t)}(v\\times B)\\cdot d\\mathbf r=-2a(t)a'(t)$, exactly the sign in the formula of [[lem-em-moving-flux]]. Dropping the term $-\\oint(v\\times B)\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ — that is, treating the moving patch as fixed — would leave $\\Phi'=0$, which the direct derivative contradicts whenever $a'(t)\\ne0$. All maps are of the stated regularity and no singular support is involved; the example verifies the sign convention and the necessity of the motion term, and asserts no physical law.",
          "strategy": "Verify each display by direct computation at the stated regularity. The example checks the sign convention of the moving-flux lemma and the necessity of the motion term; it does not assert any physical law.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "I. Galili, D. Kaplan, Y. Lehavi, Teaching Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction in an introductory physics course, Am. J. Phys. 74, 337 (2006), Appendix",
