      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-em-minkowski-affine-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The Minkowski affine four-space: the form $\\eta$, causal cones, frames and Lorentz maps",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08",
          "design_state": "complete-existing-local-argument; exact-hypotheses-retained",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-function-space",
            "def-vector-space",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-standard-bilinear-form-on-a-coordinate-space",
            "def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms",
            "def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form",
            "def-transpose-of-a-linear-map",
            "def-linear-basis",
            "def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map",
            "def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map",
            "thm-change-of-basis-for-a-bilinear-form-is-congruence",
            "def-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space",
            "def-determinant-of-a-linear-operator",
            "def-real-order",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix",
            "def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional",
            "thm-rank-nullity",
            "thm-dimension-of-a-linear-subspace"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-function-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-standard-bilinear-form-on-a-coordinate-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-transpose-of-a-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-basis": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-change-of-basis-for-a-bilinear-form-is-congruence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-determinant-of-a-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-order": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-rank-nullity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dimension-of-a-linear-subspace": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "With $\\mathbb R^4$ the space of functions $4\\to\\mathbb R$ ([[def-function-space]]) put $V:=\\mathbb R^4$, and let $A$ be the set $\\mathbb R^4$ together with the map $V\\times A\\to A$, $(v,p)\\mapsto v+p$, given by function addition; then $(v+v')+p=v+(v'+p)$, $0+p=p$, and for all $p,q\\in A$ there is exactly one $v\\in V$ with $v+p=q$ ([[def-vector-space]]), so $A$ is an affine space with translation space $V$. Fix the ordered basis $e_0,\\dots,e_3$ with $e_a(b):=1$ for $b=a$ and $0$ otherwise, and write $v=v^0e_0+\\dots+v^3e_3$ ([[def-linear-basis]], [[def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map]]). The **Minkowski form** is $\\eta(v,w):=-v^0w^0+v^1w^1+v^2w^2+v^3w^3$; it is the standard bilinear form of [[def-standard-bilinear-form-on-a-coordinate-space]] with the sign of the first coordinate reversed and the Euclidean inner product of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] on the last three coordinates, so it is a symmetric bilinear form ([[def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms]]) whose matrix in $(e_0,\\dots,e_3)$ is $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; that matrix is invertible, so $\\eta$ is nondegenerate ([[def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form]]). A vector $v\\ne0$ is **timelike** when $\\eta(v,v)<0$, **null** when $\\eta(v,v)=0$ and **spacelike** when $\\eta(v,v)>0$; it is **causal** when $\\eta(v,v)\\le0$, and then $v^0\\ne0$, so it is **future-directed** when $v^0>0$ and **past-directed** when $v^0<0$ ([[def-real-order]]). Put $C^+:=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)\\le0,\\ v^0>0\\}$ for the **future causal cone**. The **standard orientation** of $V$ is the orientation class of $(e_0,\\dots,e_3)$ ([[def-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]). A **Lorentz map** is an invertible linear $L:V\\to V$ ([[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]]) with $\\eta(Lv,Lw)=\\eta(v,w)$ for all $v,w\\in V$; in the basis above this is exactly $M^{\\mathsf T}\\eta M=\\eta$, where $M=[L]_{(e_0,\\dots,e_3)}$, $\\eta$ also denotes $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the matrix transpose is [[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]], by [[thm-change-of-basis-for-a-bilinear-form-is-congruence]] (a linear map preserving $\\eta$ is automatically invertible because $\\eta$ is nondegenerate). $L$ is **proper** when $\\det L=1$ ([[def-determinant-of-a-linear-operator]]) and **orthochronous** when $L(C^+)=C^+$; a **frame** of $A$ is a point $o\\in A$ with an ordered basis $(u_0,\\dots,u_3)$ of $V$ satisfying $\\eta(u_a,u_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$ and $u_0\\in C^+$, and the **coordinates** of $p\\in A$ in that frame are the unique $(x^0,\\dots,x^3)$ with $p=o+x^0u_0+\\dots+x^3u_3$. For nonnegative integers $p,q$, a **tensor of type\n$(p,q)$** on $V$ is a map $(V^*)^p\\times V^q\\to\\mathbb R$ that is linear in\neach argument separately, where $V^*$ is the algebraic dual of\n[[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]]. An empty product is a singleton,\nso a $(0,0)$ tensor is a scalar. Covectors are the $(0,1)$ case, a vector $v$\ndefines the $(1,0)$ tensor $\\theta\\mapsto\\theta(v)$, and a **two-form** is a\nskew-symmetric $(0,2)$ tensor. These objects are maps, with arrays recording\ntheir components in a chosen basis.\n\nFix a frame $(o,(u_a))$ and define its linear coordinate map\n$B:\\mathbb R^4\\to V$ by $B\\xi:=\\sum_a\\xi^au_a$. It is invertible because\n$(u_a)$ is a basis, and $\\eta(B\\xi,B\\zeta)=\\eta(\\xi,\\zeta)$ by its defining\npairings. For a Lorentz map $L:V\\to V$, put $N:=[L]_{(u_a)}$, identifying\nits matrix with the linear map $B^{-1}LB$ on $\\mathbb R^4$. With origin fixed\nand new basis $u'_a:=L^{-1}u_a$, the coordinates of the same point obey\n$x'=Nx$: indeed $p-o=Bx=L^{-1}Bx'$ is equivalent to this identity.\nThe basis is orthonormal since $L^{-1}$ preserves $\\eta$, and it is a frame\nwhen $L$ is orthochronous, since then $L^{-1}u_0\\in C^+$.\n\nFor a fixed vector $v$, covector $\\theta$ and two-form $F$, let\n$\\widehat v:=B^{-1}v$, $\\widehat\\theta_a:=\\theta(u_a)$ and\n$\\widehat F_{ab}:=F(u_a,u_b)$ be their old component columns and matrix;\nprimes denote the corresponding components in $(u'_a)$. Then\n\n$$\\widehat v'=N\\widehat v,\\qquad \\widehat\\theta'=(N^{-1})^{\\mathsf T}\\widehat\\theta,\\qquad \\widehat F'=(N^{-1})^{\\mathsf T}\\widehat F N^{-1}.$$\n\nThe vector law follows from $B'=L^{-1}B=BN^{-1}$. Substituting\n$u'_a=\\sum_bu_b(N^{-1})^b{}_a$ into $\\theta(u'_a)$ and $F(u'_a,u'_b)$ gives\nthe other two laws by linearity and bilinearity. Equivalently, the coordinate\nfunctional $\\theta\\circ B'$ is $(\\theta\\circ B)\\circ N^{-1}$, the transpose\noperation of [[def-transpose-of-a-linear-map]], and the coordinate bilinear\nform obeys $F(B'\\xi,B'\\zeta)=F(BN^{-1}\\xi,BN^{-1}\\zeta)$. The geometric\nobjects themselves are unchanged. In the standard frame $B=\\operatorname{id}$,\n$N$ is the standard coordinate matrix of $L$; in a general frame it is\n$B^{-1}LB$. Finally, a fixed positive real $c$ is separate extra structure: the coordinate $x^0$ has the dimension of length and the time coordinate is $t:=x^0/c$.",
          "strategy": "Direct definition with the elementary verifications recorded where they belong to the definition: bilinearity and symmetry of $\\eta$ from its coordinate formula, nondegeneracy from the invertible matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, the implication $\\eta(v,v)\\le0$ and $v\\ne0$ $\\Rightarrow$ $v^0\\ne0$ from $v^0^2\\ge v^1^2+v^2^2+v^3^2$, and the matrix form $\\eta(Lv,Lw)=\\eta(v,w)\\iff M^{\\mathsf T}\\eta M=\\eta$ from the congruence law for the matrix of a bilinear form under a change of basis. No physical claim is made; identifying $A$ with physical spacetime and $c$ with the speed of light in vacuum is a later physical premise.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, University of Cambridge Part IA Mathematical Tripos (Lent 2013), §7.1–§7.4.7, printed pp. 107–145",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1), Chapter 1, printed pp. 1–30",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-boost-preserves-metric",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "The explicit Lorentz boost preserves $\\eta$, has determinant $1$ and preserves the future cone",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08",
          "design_state": "complete-existing-local-argument; exact-hypotheses-retained",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-rational-power",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "lem-of-square-monotone",
            "def-real-order",
            "def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map",
            "def-determinant-of-a-linear-operator",
            "cor-operator-determinant-by-row-reduction",
            "def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-square-monotone": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-order": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-determinant-of-a-linear-operator": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-operator-determinant-by-row-reduction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Let $0\\le\\beta<1$ and put $\\gamma:=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}>0$ ([[def-rational-power]], [[lem-rational-power-laws]]). Define $\\Lambda_\\beta:V\\to V$ by $(\\Lambda_\\beta v)_0=\\gamma(v_0-\\beta v_1)$, $(\\Lambda_\\beta v)_1=\\gamma(v_1-\\beta v_0)$, $(\\Lambda_\\beta v)_2=v_2$, $(\\Lambda_\\beta v)_3=v_3$. Then $\\Lambda_\\beta$ is linear ([[def-linear-map]]), $\\Lambda_\\beta$ is a proper orthochronous Lorentz map, and more precisely: (a) $\\eta(\\Lambda_\\beta v,\\Lambda_\\beta w)=\\eta(v,w)$ for all $v,w\\in V$; (b) $\\det\\Lambda_\\beta=1$; (c) $\\Lambda_\\beta(C^+)=C^+$; (d) $\\Lambda_\\beta^{-1}=\\Lambda_{-\\beta}$. In particular a future causal vector has future causal image and $\\eta(\\Lambda_\\beta v,\\Lambda_\\beta v)=\\eta(v,v)$ for every $v$ ([[def-real-order]], [[lem-of-square-monotone]]). The hypothesis $0\\le\\beta<1$ is not removable: at $\\beta=1$ the factor $\\gamma$ is undefined.",
          "strategy": "Substitution in the coordinate formula of $\\eta$ gives $-\\gamma^2(v_0-\\beta v_1)^2+\\gamma^2(v_1-\\beta v_0)^2=\\gamma^2(1-\\beta^2)(-v_0^2+v_1^2)$ with the last two coordinates unchanged, and $\\gamma^2(1-\\beta^2)=1$; the $0$–$1$ block of the matrix has determinant $\\gamma^2(1-\\beta^2)=1$ and the remaining block is the identity, giving $\\det\\Lambda_\\beta=1$; for future causal $v$ one has $|v_1|\\le\\sqrt{v_1^2+v_2^2+v_3^2}\\le v_0$ from $\\eta(v,v)\\le0$, and $0\\le\\beta<1$ gives $(\\Lambda_\\beta v)_0=\\gamma(v_0-\\beta v_1)\\ge\\gamma(1-\\beta)v_0>0$, so $\\Lambda_\\beta(C^+)\\subseteq C^+$; applying the same argument to $\\Lambda_{-\\beta}$ and using $\\Lambda_\\beta\\Lambda_{-\\beta}=\\mathrm{id}$ (again $\\gamma^2(1-\\beta^2)=1$) gives equality. No empirical relativity principle is proved.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, University of Cambridge Part IA Mathematical Tripos (Lent 2013), §7.1–§7.4.7, printed pp. 107–145",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1), Chapter 1, printed pp. 1–30",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-proper-time-reparametrization",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Proper time along a $C^1$ future timelike curve: strictly increasing reparametrization with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08 (proper-time paragraph; local supplier item)",
          "design_state": "complete-existing-local-argument; exact-hypotheses-retained",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity",
            "thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity",
            "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions",
            "def-continuity-real",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "lem-of-square-monotone",
            "thm-continuous-inverse",
            "thm-composition-of-continuous-functions",
            "cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative",
            "def-derivative",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous",
            "thm-derivative-of-an-inverse",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-interval",
            "def-oriented-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-square-monotone": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-inverse": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-composition-of-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-an-inverse": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-oriented-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval ([[def-interval]]) and let $z:J\\to\\mathbb R^4$ be of class $C^1$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with $\\eta(z'(s),z'(s))<0$ and $z^0{}'(s)>0$ for every $s\\in J$; $z'$ is the derivative of the vector-valued curve ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]) and such a $z$ is called a $C^1$ future timelike curve. Put $g(s):=-\\eta(z'(s),z'(s))>0$, fix $s_0\\in J$, and define $\\tau(s):=c^{-1}\\int_{s_0}^{s}\\sqrt{g(u)}\\,du$ for $s\\in J$. Then: (1) $g$ is continuous on $J$ and so is $s\\mapsto\\sqrt{g(s)}$, where $\\sqrt{\\cdot}$ is the nonnegative square root ([[thm-of-square-roots]], [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], [[thm-composition-of-continuous-functions]]); (2) $\\tau$ is differentiable on $J$ with $\\tau'(s)=c^{-1}\\sqrt{g(s)}>0$, hence $\\tau$ is strictly increasing on $J$, injective, and continuous ([[cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function]], [[thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative]]); (3) the inverse $\\tau^{-1}:\\tau[J]\\to J$ is differentiable at every point $\\tau(s)$ of the interval $\\tau[J]$, with $(\\tau^{-1})'(\\tau(s))=c/\\sqrt{g(s)}$ ([[thm-continuous-inverse]], [[thm-derivative-of-an-inverse]]); (4) the reparametrized curve $z\\circ\\tau^{-1}:\\tau[J]\\to\\mathbb R^4$ is differentiable at every $\\tau(s)$, with tangent $U(\\tau(s)):=c\\,z'(s)/\\sqrt{g(s)}$ satisfying $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ ([[thm-chain-rule]], [[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]]); (5) if instead $\\eta(z'(s),z'(s))=0$ for all $s\\in J$, then $g=0$, $\\tau\\equiv0$ on $J$ and $\\tau$ is not injective, so a null curve carries no proper-time parametrization ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]); (6) if $z(s)=p+sv$ with $\\eta(v,v)<0$ and $v_0>0$, then $\\tau(s)=c^{-1}\\sqrt{-\\eta(v,v)}\\,(s-s_0)$ and $U=c\\,v/\\sqrt{-\\eta(v,v)}$ is constant ([[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]], [[def-derivative]]). The identification of such straight lines with inertial motion is a physical premise and is not asserted here.",
          "strategy": "$C^1$ gives continuity of the components $z^a{}'$ and [[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]] passes between the vector-valued and component statements; the four squares $(z^a{}')^2$ and their signed sum are continuous by [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], so $g$ is continuous; $\\sqrt g$ is continuous because squaring is a continuous injection of $[0,\\infty)$ onto itself with continuous inverse $\\sqrt{\\cdot}$ ([[lem-of-square-monotone]], [[thm-continuous-inverse]]), and composition preserves continuity. [[cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function]] makes $\\tau$ a primitive of the continuous function $c^{-1}\\sqrt g$ on $J$, giving (2) with [[thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative]]; [[thm-derivative-of-an-inverse]] (hypotheses: $\\tau$ continuous and injective on the interval $J$, $\\tau'(s)\\ne0$) gives (3); [[thm-chain-rule]] applied to the components of $z\\circ\\tau^{-1}$ and reassembled gives (4), where $\\eta(U,U)=(c^2/g)\\eta(z',z')=-c^2$ uses bilinearity and symmetry of $\\eta$; (5) is the zero integrand case; (6) is direct computation with constant $z'=v$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, University of Cambridge Part IA Mathematical Tripos (Lent 2013), §7.1–§7.4.7, printed pp. 107–145",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1), Chapter 1, printed pp. 1–30",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Antisymmetric contractions vanish and $mU'=q\\Phi(U)$ preserves $\\eta(U,U)$",
          "reference": "mathematical-prerequisites.md#M08",
          "design_state": "complete-existing-local-argument; exact-hypotheses-retained",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space",
            "def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms",
            "def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form",
            "thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-linear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be the four-dimensional real Minkowski vector space of [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]], with the fixed form of signature $(-1,1,1,1)$. Let $F:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb R$ be a skew-symmetric bilinear form, $F(u,v)=-F(v,u)$ for all $u,v\\in V$ ([[def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms]]). Since $\\eta$ is nondegenerate, for each $u\\in V$ there is exactly one $\\Phi(u)\\in V$ with $\\eta(\\Phi(u),w)=F(w,u)$ for all $w\\in V$ ([[thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual]], [[def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form]]), and $\\Phi:V\\to V$ is linear ([[def-linear-map]]); with $F_{\\alpha\\beta}:=F(e_\\alpha,e_\\beta)$ in a frame $\\Phi(u)^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}F_{\\alpha\\beta}u^\\beta$, the index-raised form of $F$. Then $\\eta(u,\\Phi(u))=0$ for every $u\\in V$; in components $u_\\mu F^\\mu{}_\\nu u^\\nu=0$. Consequently, if $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a nonempty open interval ([[def-interval]]), $U:J\\to V$ is differentiable ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]), and $m\\,U'(s)=q\\,\\Phi(U(s))$ on $J$ for some real $m\\ne0$ and $q$, then $s\\mapsto\\eta(U(s),U(s))$ is constant on $J$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]). The same norm-conservation conclusion holds for a family of skew forms $F_s$ and their pointwise operators $\\Phi_s$ defined by $\\eta(\\Phi_s(u),w)=F_s(w,u)$, when $mU'(s)=q\\Phi_s(U(s))$: no derivative of $F_s$ is required. In particular one may evaluate a varying field $F_x$ along a differentiable trajectory $x(s)$. This is a conditional statement about the given equation; it does not assert that this equation governs any particle.",
          "strategy": "For each $u$, $w\\mapsto F(w,u)$ is a linear functional. The nondegenerate finite-dimensional form $\\eta$ identifies $V$ with $V^*$, so it gives a unique $\\Phi(u)$; bilinearity and uniqueness make $\\Phi$ linear. In a frame, evaluating the defining pairing at $w=e_\\alpha$ gives $\\eta_{\\alpha\\mu}\\Phi(u)^\\mu=F_{\\alpha\\beta}u^\\beta$, hence $\\Phi(u)^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}F_{\\alpha\\beta}u^\\beta$. Taking $w=u$ gives $\\eta(\\Phi(u),u)=F(u,u)=0$ by skew-symmetry, and symmetry of $\\eta$ gives $\\eta(u,\\Phi(u))=0$. For the norm claim $f(s):=\\eta(U(s),U(s))=\\sum_a\\epsilon_a(U^a(s))^2$, with $\\epsilon=(-1,1,1,1)$, satisfies $f'=2\\eta(U,U')$ by the product rule. The equation gives $\\eta(U,U')=(q/m)\\eta(U,\\Phi(U))=0$, so the zero-derivative theorem on the open interval $J$ makes $f$ constant. The identical pointwise calculation with $\\Phi_s$ proves the varying-form clause: differentiate $\\eta(U,U)$ only, not $F_s$ or $\\Phi_s$. No constant-field assumption is used in that clause.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, University of Cambridge Part IA Mathematical Tripos (Lent 2013), §7.1–§7.4.7, printed pp. 107–145",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1), Chapter 1, printed pp. 1–30",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 307
    },
