            "lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "With $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$: (i) the vector $v$ with components\n$(1,0,0,0)$ is future timelike and unit, $\\eta(v,v)=-1$. (ii) For $0<\\beta<1$\nthe boost $\\Lambda_\\beta$ of\n[[lem-em-boost-preserves-metric]] sends $v$ to the vector with components\n$(\\gamma,-\\gamma\\beta,0,0)$,\nwhose quadratic value $\\eta(\\Lambda_\\beta v,\\Lambda_\\beta v)$ is\n$-\\gamma^2(1-\\beta^2)=-1$ and whose time component\n$\\gamma$ is positive; at $\\beta=1$ the factor $\\gamma$ is undefined, so the\nhypothesis $0\\le\\beta<1$ cannot be dropped. (iii) The null line\n$z(s)=(s,s,0,0)$, $s\\in\\mathbb R$, has $\\eta(z',z')=0$, so the proper-time\nconstruction of [[lem-em-proper-time-reparametrization]] gives $\\tau\\equiv0$,\nwhich is not an invertible parameter: the timelike hypothesis cannot be weakened\nto causal. (iv) For the symmetric nonzero form $F:=\\eta$ one has\n$\\Phi=\\mathrm{id}$ and $\\eta(v,\\Phi(v))=\\eta(v,v)=-1\\ne0$, so the antisymmetry\nhypothesis of [[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]] is genuine. (v) For\nthe skew form with $F_{01}=1$, $F_{10}=-1$ and other coefficients zero, the\noperator of [[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]] sends $e_0$ to $-e_1$:\n$\\eta(-e_1,w)=-w^1=F(w,e_0)$. Thus its invariant pairing and standard\nraised-index formula have the same sign, and $\\eta(e_0,\\Phi(e_0))=0$. No\nphysical clock premise or experimental claim is used.",
          "strategy": "Five direct computations in the fixed standard frame: evaluation of $\\eta$ on the given vectors, substitution of $v$ in the boost formula, the vanishing of the null tangent, and the identification $\\Phi=\\mathrm{id}$ when $F=\\eta$ (uniqueness in the defining property of $\\Phi$). For (v), the first column of $\\eta^{-1}F$ is $(0,-1,0,0)$; both pairings equal $-w^1$, and the time-space orthogonality is immediate. All are exact finite computations; no source or physical assumption is needed.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, University of Cambridge Part IA Mathematical Tripos (Lent 2013), §7.1–§7.4.7, printed pp. 107–145",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1), Chapter 1, printed pp. 1–30",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        }
      ],
