            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$). Let $n,m\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $g:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be continuous. If $\\int_U\\langle g,h\\rangle\\,d\\lambda=0$ for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R^m)$, then $g=0$ on $U$. Here $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ is the Euclidean inner product and $\\int_U\\langle g,h\\rangle\\,d\\lambda$ is the finite sum $\\sum_{i<m}\\int_U g_ih_i\\,d\\lambda$ of integrals of continuous compactly supported functions ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]). The scalar case is $m=1$: a continuous $g$ with $\\int_Ugh\\,d\\lambda=0$ for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$ vanishes on $U$. The vector case is deduced from the scalar case componentwise, each component tested separately. The conclusion is pointwise on $U$, and the hypothesis is the full family of compactly supported smooth test functions. The Axiom of Countable Choice is declared because the localization lemma [[lem-em-continuous-localization]] and the measurability fact [[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]] used in the recorded proof assume it; no other choice principle enters.",
          "strategy": "Scalar case. Suppose $g(p)\\ne0$ for some $p\\in U$; replacing $g$ by $-g$, which satisfies the same hypothesis, assume $g(p)>0$. Openness of $U$ and continuity of $g$ at $p$ give $r>0$ with $B(p,r)\\subseteq U$ and $g\\ge g(p)/2$ on $B(p,r)$. Apply the cutoff lemma to the compact set $\\{p\\}$ inside the open ball $B(p,r)$: it yields $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(B(p,r))$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$ and $\\chi=1$ on a neighbourhood of $p$, so $\\chi(p)=1$ and $\\chi$ extends by zero to a test function in $C_c^\\infty(U)$. Then $\\varphi:=g\\chi$ is continuous on $U$ as a product of continuous real functions, satisfies $\\varphi\\ge0$ because $g>0$ on the support of $\\chi$, and has $\\varphi(p)=g(p)>0$, so $\\varphi\\ne0$. Were $\\int_U\\varphi\\,d\\lambda=0$: the localization lemma applied to the continuous function $\\varphi$ (contrapositive form of [[lem-em-continuous-localization]]) produces a ball $V$ compactly contained in $U$ with $\\int_V\\varphi\\,d\\lambda\\ne0$; since $\\varphi\\ge0$ this integral is positive, and monotonicity of the nonnegative integral gives $\\int_U\\varphi\\,d\\lambda\\ge\\int_V\\varphi\\,d\\lambda>0$, contradicting $\\int_U\\varphi\\,d\\lambda=0$. But $\\chi$ is an admissible test function, so the hypothesis gives $\\int_U\\varphi\\,d\\lambda=\\int_U g\\chi\\,d\\lambda=0$; hence no point $p$ with $g(p)\\ne0$ exists. Vector case. For each $i<m$ and each $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$, apply the hypothesis to $h=\\psi e_i$ to get $\\int_U g_i\\psi\\,d\\lambda=0$; the scalar case gives $g_i=0$. Measurability of $\\varphi$ comes from continuity, and the positive-integral comparison uses the order rules for the nonnegative integral. Continuity is retained verbatim: a measurable $g$ that is $0$ almost everywhere but not everywhere passes every test pairing without being the zero function, so the pointwise conclusion genuinely uses continuity.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Figueroa-O'Farrill, Brief Notes on the Calculus of Variations (University of Edinburgh), §4 Theorem 1, printed pp. 4–5; §12 Theorem 4, printed p. 19; §14 compact-support reformulation, printed p. 23",
                "url": "https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~jmf/Teaching/Lectures/CoV.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-compact-field-variation",
