            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-borel-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-derivative-of-a-power": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$). Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be a bounded open set, let $N\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be an open neighbourhood of its closure, let $\\kappa>0$, and let $A:N\\to V=\\mathbb R^4$ and $J:N\\to V$ be smooth ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Use the Minkowski form $\\eta$ and the index conventions of [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]]: $A^\\mu$ denotes the components of $A$ in the standard basis, $A_\\nu:=\\eta_{\\nu\\mu}A^\\mu$, $F_{\\mu\\nu}:=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, $F^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}\\eta^{\\nu\\beta}F_{\\alpha\\beta}$, and $J^\\nu A_\\nu$ is the indicated contraction of the components of $J$ with the covector $A_\\nu$. Put $L:=-\\frac{1}{4\\kappa}F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}+J^\\nu A_\\nu$ and $S[A]:=\\int_U L\\,d\\lambda$, the $4$-dimensional Lebesgue integral of $L$ over $U$. Then $S[A]$ is a finite real number, and for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;V)$, with $h_\\nu:=\\eta_{\\nu\\mu}h^\\mu$ and $G_{\\mu\\nu}:=\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu h_\\mu$: (a) the map $s\\mapsto S[A+sh]$ is the quadratic polynomial $S[A]+s\\int_U\\ell_1\\,d\\lambda+s^2\\int_U\\ell_2\\,d\\lambda$, where $\\ell_1:=-\\kappa^{-1}F^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu+J^\\nu h_\\nu$ and $\\ell_2:=-\\frac{1}{4\\kappa}G_{\\mu\\nu}G^{\\mu\\nu}$, so that its derivative at $s=0$ is $\\int_U\\bigl(-\\kappa^{-1}F^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu+J^\\nu h_\\nu\\bigr)d\\lambda$; (b) that first variation also equals $\\int_U\\bigl(\\kappa^{-1}\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}+J^\\nu\\bigr)h_\\nu\\,d\\lambda$; (c) $A$ is stationary for $S$, meaning that the derivative in (a) vanishes for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;V)$, if and only if $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\kappa J^\\nu$ for every $\\nu$ on $U$, all derivatives being classical partial derivatives of the smooth field $A$. This is a pure mathematical statement about the functional $S$: existence of a stationary field $A$ is not asserted, and identifying $\\kappa$ with the vacuum permeability, $A$ with a physical potential or $S$ with a physical action is a later physical model choice.",
          "strategy": "Well-definedness. Write also $h$ for its extension by zero to $N$; that extension is smooth because the compact support of $h$ has positive distance from the complement of the open $U$, so $A+sh$ is smooth on $N$ and $S[A+sh]$ is defined for every real $s$. Expansion. For each $s$ one has $F_{\\mu\\nu}(s)=F_{\\mu\\nu}+sG_{\\mu\\nu}$ by linearity of the partial derivatives in the variation. Bilinearity of $\\eta$ gives $F_{\\mu\\nu}(s)F^{\\mu\\nu}(s)=F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}+2sF^{\\mu\\nu}G_{\\mu\\nu}+s^2G_{\\mu\\nu}G^{\\mu\\nu}$; the split $G_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu h_\\mu$ and the antisymmetry $F^{\\mu\\nu}=-F^{\\nu\\mu}$ give $F^{\\mu\\nu}G_{\\mu\\nu}=2F^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu$ (the two terms are equal after relabelling). Hence $L(A+sh)=L(A)+s\\ell_1+s^2\\ell_2$ pointwise on $N$ with the displayed $\\ell_1,\\ell_2$. The functions $L(A),\\ell_1,\\ell_2$ are continuous on the compact set $\\overline U$, hence bounded, and the bounded open $U$ lies in a box of finite Lebesgue measure, so all three are integrable on $U$; linearity of the integral in $s$ gives the polynomial identity in (a) and, by differentiating the polynomial, the displayed derivative, with no exchange of limit and integral. Conversion. Fix $\\nu$. The function $x\\mapsto F^{\\mu\\nu}(x)$ (sum over $\\mu$, with the repeated-index convention) is of class $C^1$ on the open set $U$, so by [[lem-classical-derivatives-are-weak-derivatives]] its classical partial derivative $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}$ is its weak derivative; the test identity of [[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]] with the test function $h_\\nu\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$ gives $\\int_U F^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu\\,d\\lambda=-\\int_U(\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu})h_\\nu\\,d\\lambda$. Substituting this into (a) yields (b). No boundary term remains precisely because $h$ is compactly supported in $U$; for a non-compactly-supported variation the identity fails, as the B example records. Coefficient equation. Put $C^\\nu:=\\kappa^{-1}\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}+J^\\nu$, a continuous coefficient array on $U$. By (b) the first variation is $\\int_U\\sum_\\nu C^\\nu h_\\nu\\,d\\lambda$, with $h_\\nu=\\eta_{\\nu\\mu}h^\\mu$. Set $k_\\nu:=h_\\nu$. Because the constant metric is invertible, every compactly supported smooth real four-tuple $k$ is obtained by $h^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}k_\\nu$, with the same support and smoothness. Thus stationarity is exactly vanishing of the Euclidean pairing of the numeric array $(C^0,C^1,C^2,C^3)$ with every numeric test tuple $(k_0,k_1,k_2,k_3)$, not an identification of the original contravariant $h$ with its lowered array. The vector clause of [[lem-em-fundamental-variation-lemma]] therefore gives $C^\\nu=0$ for all $\\nu$, and conversely these zero coefficients make (b) vanish for every admitted $h$. Existence of stationary fields and the physical reading of $\\kappa$, $A$ and $J$ are not claimed. The Axiom of Countable Choice is used exactly through [[lem-em-fundamental-variation-lemma]] and [[lem-classical-derivatives-are-weak-derivatives]]; the expansion step uses none.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism (University of Cambridge Part II lecture notes), Chapter 5, §5.5.3 'The Maxwell Action', equations (5.42)–(5.43), printed pp. 124–126",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro4.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Figueroa-O'Farrill, Brief Notes on the Calculus of Variations (University of Edinburgh), §14 'Classical fields', printed pp. 22–26",
                "url": "https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~jmf/Teaching/Lectures/CoV.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
