          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For $z\\in[-1,1]$ define the real polynomial $P_l(z)=\\pi^{-1}\\int_0^\\pi\\bigl[z+i\\sqrt{1-z^2}\\,\\cos\\theta\\bigr]^l\\,d\\theta$, $l\\in\\mathbb N_0$. Then $P_l$ is a real polynomial of degree at most $l$ with $|P_l(z)|\\le1$ on $[-1,1]$, $P_l(1)=1$, and for $|q|<1$ the series $\\sum_{l\\ge0}q^lP_l(z)$ converges absolutely and uniformly on $[-1,1]$ with\n\n$$(1-2qz+q^2)^{-1/2}=\\sum_{l=0}^{\\infty}q^lP_l(z),$$\n\nwhere the branch is the analytic square root equal to $1$ at $q=0$ ([[def-series-of-real-functions]], [[thm-weierstrass-m-test-for-function-series]]). Consequently, for $x\\ne0$, $r:=|x|>|y|$ and $n=x/|x|$ and $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$,\n\n$$\\bigl|\\,|x-y|^{-1}-\\sum_{l=0}^{N}|y|^l|x|^{-l-1}P_l(n\\cdot y/|y|)\\bigr|\\le|y|^{N+1}|x|^{-N-2}(1-|y|/|x|)^{-1},$$\n\nuniformly on $|y|\\le a<r$ (with $y=0$ interpreted by the zero higher terms), and after integrating against an $L^1$ source the corresponding multipole expansion converges with that error times its $L^1$ norm. For the integrated $L^1$-source and every-spatial-derivative assertions, assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) through the Lebesgue and harmonic-estimate interfaces. On an observation compact set disjoint from $\\overline{B_a(0)}$ every fixed spatial derivative series converges uniformly, uniformly also over $|y|\\le a$, by harmonicity of the coefficient terms and [[thm-interior-derivative-estimates-for-harmonic-functions]]. The initial finite-Riemann polynomial/generating assertions are choice-free. This lemma supplies the convergent zonal kernel; it does not by itself prove a spherical $L^2$ basis or boundary convergence.",
          "strategy": "Expand the finite power in the defining Riemann integral: odd cosine powers integrate to zero by the substitution theta to pi-theta, and even powers have factors $(1-z^2)^jz^{l-2j}$, so $P_l$ is a real polynomial, with $P_l(1)=1$ and $|P_l(z)|\\le1$. For each FIXED real $z\\in[-1,1]$, put $w(\\theta)=z+i\\sqrt{1-z^2}\\cos\\theta$; $|w|\\le1$, so the geometric series is uniformly convergent on every $|q|\\le q_0<1$ and finite-Riemann termwise integration gives $G_z(q)=\\sum_lq^lP_l(z)=\\pi^{-1}\\int_0^\\pi(1-qw(\\theta))^{-1}d\\theta$. The sum is holomorphic on the unit disk by [[thm-termwise-differentiation-of-complex-power-series]]. No holomorphy in z at the endpoints is asserted.\n\nFor real $a>|b|$ the tangent substitution gives $I(a,b)=\\int_0^\\pi(a+b\\cos\\theta)^{-1}d\\theta=\\pi/\\sqrt{a^2-b^2}$ by [[thm-substitution]] and [[thm-principal-inverse-tangent-calculus]]. Near $(a,b)=(1,0)$ the rational integrand and its first parameter derivatives are uniformly bounded on the compact theta interval, so uniform difference quotients through the finite Riemann integral give holomorphy in each parameter separately. The identity $I(a,b)^2(a^2-b^2)=\\pi^2$ first extends in complex a for every fixed small real b, then in complex b for every fixed nearby complex a, by the ONE-variable [[thm-identity-theorem-holomorphic-functions]]. Substitute $a=1-qz$, $b=-iq\\sqrt{1-z^2}$ near q0 to get $(1-2qz+q^2)G_z(q)^2=1$. This holomorphic identity extends over the connected unit disk by the same one-variable theorem. Since $G_z(0)=1$, it is exactly the specified analytic inverse-square-root branch. The zeros of the polynomial factor lie on the unit circle for every fixed real z. Substitution q=|y|/r now proves the exterior kernel series and its geometric remainder, including zero y.\n\nEach coefficient $c_l(x,y)=|y|^l|x|^{-l-1}P_l((x/|x|)\\cdot(y/|y|))$ is smooth and harmonic in x away from zero. Indeed it equals $1/l!$ times the lth t-derivative at t0 of $|x-ty|^{-1}$, by the proved scalar power series and [[cor-complex-power-series-coefficient-formula]]. On a neighbourhood of any fixed x nonzero and t0 this kernel is smooth and harmonic in x by [[lem-laplace-fundamental-solution-is-harmonic-off-its-pole]]; finite mixed derivative commutation [[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]] makes its t-coefficient harmonic. The corresponding degree-l solid polynomial in y is harmonic in y by the same argument, using $\\Delta_y|x-ty|^{-1}=0$ for all sufficiently small real t. This also supplies the solid-harmonic coefficient used by the later addition lemma without a cyclic dependence.\n\nThe empty observation compact has a vacuous uniform-convergence claim. Otherwise fix a nonempty compact observation set K with inf_K|x|>a. Choose a positive delta and a bounded closed neighbourhood Kplus of K with every closed delta-ball centred on K contained in Kplus and inf_Kplus|x|>a. The geometric kernel estimate gives a uniform tail bound E_N tending to zero on Kplus times the whole closed source ball |y|<=a. Harmonic partial-sum differences v satisfy the exact [[thm-interior-derivative-estimates-for-harmonic-functions]] bound $|D^\\alpha v(x)|\\le C_{3,\\alpha}\\delta^{-3-|\\alpha|}\\int_{B_\\delta(x)}|v|\\le C_{3,\\alpha}\\delta^{-3-|\\alpha|}|B_\\delta|\\sup_{Kplus}|v|$. Thus every spatial derivative is uniformly Cauchy on K, uniformly in y. Uniform derivative limits are identified inductively along short coordinate segments by [[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], so they are exactly the derivatives of the kernel. Against a compact $L^1$ density, the same bound times |rho| gives an integrable envelope and multiplies each tail by ||rho||1 using [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]. Countable Choice enters only the Lebesgue and harmonic-estimate branches; the initial finite polynomial and scalar generating argument uses no Choice. No multivariable complex Cauchy estimate is invoked. For an actual L1 source choose its representative zero outside the closed source ball. On each small open observation-coordinate interval inside Kplus, every kernel derivative is smooth on the fixed compact source ball and has a uniform finite bound C_alpha; the next derivative integrand is bounded by C_alpha times |rho|, an integrable majorant. Thus [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign]] applies with zero exceptional set after the off-ball integrand is defined as zero. This identifies all integral derivatives directly as well as by the uniform harmonic-series limit.",
          "provenance": {
