            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-intermediate-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ck-euclidean-maps-closed-under-algebra-and-composition": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-reverse-triangle-inequality-in-a-normed-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-higher-regularity-of-local-inverses": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $c>0$, $0\\le v_*<c$, and $z:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $C^1$ with $|z'(s)|\\le v_*$ for all $s$. For every $(x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ there is exactly one real root $s=s(x,t)$ of $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$, and $s\\le t$. Equality holds exactly when $x=z(t)$. Thus on the open off-instantaneous-worldline domain $D=\\{(x,t):x\\ne z(t)\\}$ one has $s<t$, $r=|x-z(s)|=c(t-s)>0$, and the root is $C^1$. With $n=(x-z(s))/r$, $\\beta=z'(s)/c$, and $\\kappa=1-n\\cdot\\beta$, its derivatives are $\\partial_t s=1/\\kappa$ and $\\nabla_xs=-n/(c\\kappa)$, where $1-v_*/c\\le\\kappa\\le1+v_*/c$. If $z$ is $C^k$, for an integer $k\\ge1$, the root is $C^k$ on $D$; in particular a $C^3$ trajectory gives a $C^3$ root. A smooth trajectory gives a smooth root. No higher regularity follows from a merely $C^1$ trajectory. These claims concern an all-time uniformly subluminal prescribed history; no finite-history or nonuniform-light-speed-limit assertion is made.",
          "strategy": "The ordinary vector FTC and integral triangle inequality give $|z(s_2)-z(s_1)|\\le v_*|s_2-s_1|$. For fixed $x$ put $g_x(s)=s+|x-z(s)|/c$. It is continuous and for $s_2>s_1$ satisfies $g_x(s_2)-g_x(s_1)\\ge(1-v_*/c)(s_2-s_1)>0$. Consequently $g_x(s)\\to\\pm\\infty$ as $s\\to\\pm\\infty$; IVT and strict monotonicity give a unique solution of $g_x(s)=t$. Since $g_x(s)\\ge s$, $s\\le t$, and equality is equivalent to $x=z(t)$. On $D$ the root has $r>0$. Continuity of $z$ makes $D$ open. Near any root on $D$, $F(x,t,s)=t-s-|x-z(s)|/c$ has precisely the regularity of $z$, because the Euclidean norm is smooth away from zero. Its $s$ derivative is $-\\kappa$, bounded away from zero. Apply the parametrized implicit-function theorem with its actual $C^1$ hypothesis first, or with $C^k$ when $z$ is $C^k$; global uniqueness glues these local maps. Differentiating $F=0$ gives the two stated formulas. Repeat for every finite $k$ in the smooth case. Do not infer $C^3$ from $C^1$, and do not exclude an observation point merely because the trajectory visits it at a different time. The exact non-improvement witness is a small uniformly subluminal $z(s)=(\\epsilon\\chi(s)|s|^{3/2},0,0)$ with smooth compact cutoff $\\chi=1$ near zero. At a distant positive axial point $L>\\sup|z|$, the retardation map is $g(s)=s+(L-\\epsilon\\chi(s)|s|^{3/2})/c$, $C^1$ but not $C^2$ at zero. A $C^2$ inverse near $t=L/c$, whose derivative is nonzero, would make $g$ $C^2$ by the inverse-function theorem, a contradiction."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-integrable-causal-dispersion",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Dispersion relations for an absolutely continuous integrable causal kernel",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#D2",
          "design_state": "complete-local-branch-argument",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-complex-domain",
            "thm-existence-of-complex-line-integrals-on-rectifiable-paths",
            "cor-cauchy-theorem-for-null-homotopic-loops",
            "cor-cauchy-theorem-star-shaped-domain",
