          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-complex-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-existence-of-complex-line-integrals-on-rectifiable-paths": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-theorem-for-null-homotopic-loops": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-theorem-star-shaped-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-ml-estimate-for-complex-line-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-absolutely-continuous-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-absolutely-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-integration-by-parts-for-absolutely-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-c1-lipschitz-ac-bv-hierarchy": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-dependent-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-cantor-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-uniform-limit-interchanges-complex-line-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-partials-and-cauchy-riemann-imply-holomorphic": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and Dependent Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]). Let $d\\ge1$ and let $K:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be a locally absolutely continuous matrix kernel with $K,K'\\in L^1(0,\\infty;\\mathbb R^{d\\times d})$ and $sK(s)\\in L^1(0,\\infty;\\mathbb R^{d\\times d})$ (entrywise integrability), and let $$\\chi(z)=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)e^{izs}\\,ds\\qquad(\\operatorname{Im}z>0)$$ be the matrix response function. Here locally absolutely continuous means that each real entry restricted to every compact interval $[a,b]\\subseteq[0,\\infty)$ satisfies the finite disjoint-interval condition of [[def-absolutely-continuous-function]]: for every $\\epsilon>0$ there is $\\delta>0$ such that $\\sum_\\ell(v_\\ell-u_\\ell)<\\delta$ implies $\\sum_\\ell|K_{jk}(v_\\ell)-K_{jk}(u_\\ell)|<\\epsilon$ for every finite family with disjoint interiors. $K\\prime$ denotes the almost-everywhere derivative, with any measurable zero extension on its null exceptional set. Then the entries of $\\chi$ are holomorphic functions on the upper half-plane ([[def-complex-domain]]), continuous and $C^1$ on its real boundary, bounded on the closed upper half-plane with $|\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le|K_{jk}(0)|+\\|K'_{jk}\\|_1$ for $|z|\\ge1$, and the entrywise principal-value dispersion formulas hold: for real $\\omega$, $$\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)=\\frac1\\pi\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}\\chi_{jk}(\\xi)}{\\xi-\\omega}\\,d\\xi,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)=-\\frac1\\pi\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{jk}(\\xi)}{\\xi-\\omega}\\,d\\xi .$$ The sharper bound $|\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le(|K_{jk}(0)|+\\|K\\prime_{jk}\\|_1)/|z|$ holds for every nonzero $z$ with $\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0$. Here $\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{\\mathbb R}F(\\xi)/(\\xi-\\omega)d\\xi$ means $\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\lim_{\\eta\\downarrow0}(\\int_{\\omega-R}^{\\omega-\\eta}+\\int_{\\omega+\\eta}^{\\omega+R})F(\\xi)/(\\xi-\\omega)d\\xi$; the tails converge absolutely, so the choice of centering at infinity does not change the limit. For an instantaneous real response matrix $\\chi_\\infty\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ define the total transfer function $\\chi_{\\mathrm{total}}:=\\chi_\\infty+\\chi$; the formulas then apply to $\\chi_{\\mathrm{total}}-\\chi_\\infty=\\chi$; the scalar case is $d=1$. Causality alone supplies neither growth assumptions beyond those stated nor the passivity quadratic-form condition, which is a separate model assertion.",
          "strategy": "Treat each real entry $k=K_{jk}$ separately. Its locally absolutely continuous representative is the one in the statement; the published [[thm-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-absolutely-continuous-functions]] applies on $[0,N]$ under the declared Countable Choice and Dependent Choice and yields $k(s)=k(0)+\\int_0^s k\\prime(u)du$. Since $k\\prime\\in L^1$, dominated convergence shows this has a finite limit $\\ell=k(0)+\\int_0^\\infty k\\prime$. If $\\ell\\ne0$, then $|k(s)|\\ge|\\ell|/2$ for all sufficiently large $s$, contradicting $k\\in L^1$ by monotonicity of the integral ([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). Hence $k(s)\\to0$.\nThe defining integral is bounded by $\\|k\\|_1$ on $\\overline H$, and dominated convergence gives joint continuity there ([[thm-dominated-convergence]]). Writing $z=x+iy$, the real parameter derivatives of $k(s)e^{izs}$ are $isk(s)e^{izs}$ and $-sk(s)e^{izs}$. On any rectangle in $H$ their absolute values are bounded by $s|k(s)|$, integrable by hypothesis. [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign]] applied separately in $x,y$ supplies the partials; dominated convergence makes them continuous, and $\\partial_y\\chi=i\\partial_x\\chi$ gives holomorphy by [[thm-continuous-partials-and-cauchy-riemann-imply-holomorphic]]. Along the real boundary the same theorem in the real parameter and the same majorant give the continuous derivative $\\chi\\prime(\\omega)=\\int_0^\\infty isk(s)e^{i\\omega s}ds$.\nFor fixed $z\\ne0$, the real and imaginary parts of $g(s):=e^{izs}$ are $C^1$ on each $[0,N]$, hence absolutely continuous by [[thm-c1-lipschitz-ac-bv-hierarchy]]. Apply [[thm-integration-by-parts-for-absolutely-continuous-functions]] separately to $(k,\\operatorname{Re}g)$ and $(k,\\operatorname{Im}g)$ and combine the real identities to get\n$$\\int_0^N k(s)e^{izs}ds=\\frac{k(N)e^{izN}-k(0)}{iz}-\\frac1{iz}\\int_0^N k\\prime(s)e^{izs}ds.$$\nThis is Lebesgue integration by parts for AC functions, not the ordinary everywhere-differentiable Riemann theorem. For $\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0$, $|e^{izs}|\\le1$; $k(N)\\to0$ kills the endpoint term and dominated convergence applies to both integrals. Thus\n$$\\chi_{jk}(z)=-\\frac{k(0)}{iz}-\\frac1{iz}\\int_0^\\infty k\\prime(s)e^{izs}ds,\\qquad |\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le\\frac{|k(0)|+\\|k\\prime\\|_1}{|z|}.$$\nFor completeness the boundary contour step is valid for any scalar $F$ holomorphic on $H:=\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>0\\}$, continuous on $\\overline H$, with $C^1$ real boundary restriction and $F(z)=O(1/|z|)$ uniformly on $\\overline H$ at infinity. Fix $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R$, $R>|\\omega|+2$ and $0<\\eta<1$. Use the real intervals from $\\omega-R$ to $\\omega-\\eta$ and $\\omega+\\eta$ to $\\omega+R$, a clockwise upper semicircle of radius $\\eta$ about $\\omega$, and the counterclockwise large upper semicircle of radius $R$ about $\\omega$. For $\\delta>0$ apply [[cor-cauchy-theorem-for-null-homotopic-loops]] to $F(z+i\\delta)/(z-\\omega)$ on $D_\\delta:=\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>-\\delta/2\\}\\setminus\\{\\omega-iv:v\\ge0\\}$. This open domain is star-shaped about any point $\\omega+iA$, $A>0$, so the contour is null-homotopic there and the integrand is holomorphic. All contour pieces lie in $D_\\delta$; no analytic extension of $F$ across the real boundary is assumed. On each fixed contour, continuity on a compact closed-half-plane neighborhood gives $F(z+i\\delta)\\to F(z)$ uniformly as $\\delta\\downarrow0$ by [[thm-heine-cantor-metric]], and [[thm-uniform-limit-interchanges-complex-line-integrals]] transfers the zero contour identity to $F(z)/(z-\\omega)$. The large arc has $|z-\\omega|=R$, $|z|\\ge R-|\\omega|$ and length $\\pi R$, hence its contribution is $O(1/R)$. Parametrizing the clockwise small arc by $z=\\omega+\\eta e^{i\\theta}$ with $\\theta$ decreasing from $\\pi$ to $0$ gives $-i\\pi F(\\omega)+o(1)$. Locally the divided difference $[F(\\xi)-F(\\omega)]/(\\xi-\\omega)$ is bounded because the boundary is $C^1$; the constant term cancels under the symmetric indentation. The tails are absolutely integrable by $F(\\xi)/(\\xi-\\omega)=O(|\\xi|^{-2})$. First $\\eta\\downarrow0$, then $R\\to\\infty$ therefore gives $\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{\\mathbb R}F(\\xi)/(\\xi-\\omega)d\\xi=i\\pi F(\\omega)$ and the two real/imaginary formulas. Apply the preceding step to each entry of $\\chi$. Adding the stipulated real instantaneous matrix means the total transfer function is $\\chi_\\infty+\\chi$; subtract $\\chi_\\infty$ before taking the principal values. All scalar claims are the $d=1$ case. Neither temporal causality nor these analytic identities impose the separate passivity quadratic-form condition."
        },
