            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice (used through the smooth-up-to-the-boundary density theorem below) and the Axiom of Countable Choice (declared by the flattening and Sobolev suppliers). The axiom interfaces are [[def-axiom-of-choice]] and [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded connected $C^1$ domain in the graph sense ([[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]], [[def-connected-space]]); its closure is compact, so the boundary has a finite cover by boundary charts ([[def-compact-space]]). There is a constant $C_\\Omega$, depending only on $\\Omega$, with $$\\|v-v_\\Omega\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\le C_\\Omega\\,\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H^1(\\Omega),\\qquad v_\\Omega:=\\frac1{|\\Omega|}\\int_\\Omega v\\,dx .$$ Here $H^1(\\Omega)=W^{1,2}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ with the weak coordinate derivatives, and $\\nabla v$ denotes the weak gradient. The estimate is the mean-zero (Poincare) inequality needed for weak Neumann solvability, where the mean-zero subspace carries the gradient norm.",
          "strategy": "On a rectangular box $R$ the one-dimensional fundamental theorem along coordinate segments with Cauchy-Schwarz ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]) gives $\\int_R\\int_R|v(x)-v(y)|^2dx\\,dy\\le C_R|R|\\int_R|\\nabla v|^2dx$; replacing $x$ by $y$ one coordinate at a time bounds the squared finite sum by three times the sum of the three one-coordinate differences, and Fubini-Tonelli ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]) integrates the remaining coordinates. For almost every fixed $x$, write $v(x)-v_R=|R|^{-1}\\int_R(v(x)-v(y))dy$. The $L^2$ Cauchy–Schwarz inequality ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]) gives $|v(x)-v_R|^2\\le |R|^{-1}\\int_R|v(x)-v(y)|^2dy$. Integrating in $x$ and using the preceding double-integral estimate gives $\\int_R|v-v_R|^2\\le C'_R\\int_R|\\nabla v|^2$. This is an integral Cauchy–Schwarz argument, not an application of finite Jensen to a continuous average. The same argument applies to a half-box. Each boundary graph chart is flattened to a half-box; the $C^k$ boundary flattening lemma ([[lem-c-k-boundary-flattening-preserves-wkp-locally]]) transports the estimate to the chart patch, initially for smooth functions and then for $H^1$ functions by the smooth-up-to-the-boundary density theorem ([[thm-smooth-up-to-the-boundary-density-on-smooth-domains]]), with bounded chart Jacobians and first derivatives. Interior balls are replaced by boxes contained in $\\Omega$. Compactness of the boundary and of the residual interior region gives a finite connected cover $\\Omega=\\bigcup_jU_j$ with every $|U_j|>0$ and a connected intersection graph: otherwise the finite disjoint unions of graph components would be nonempty relatively open sets partitioning the connected $\\Omega$ ([[def-connected-space]]). Writing $a_j$ for the mean of $v$ on $U_j$, the local estimates give $\\|v-a_j\\|_{L^2(U_j)}\\le C_j\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2(U_j)}$, and the mean differences $|a_j-a_k|$ over nonempty overlaps are bounded by the local gradient energy along a finite spanning tree of the intersection graph. Summing the local squared bounds and the squared mean differences over the finite cover, and using that the global mean minimises $a\\mapsto\\int_\\Omega|v-a|^2$, gives the stated bound."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-outgoing-spherical-hankel-dtn-bounds",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Outgoing spherical radial functions: exact bounds on the spherical Dirichlet-to-Neumann coefficient",
          "reference": "exterior-scattering-arguments.md#F1",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "thm-algebra-of-complex-derivatives",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "rem-complex-plane-euclidean-dictionary"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-complex-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "rem-complex-plane-euclidean-dictionary": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For $l\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and $z>0$ set $$h_l(z)=(-i)^{l+1}\\frac{e^{iz}}{z}\\sum_{j=0}^{l}\\frac{i^j\\,(l+j)!}{j!\\,(l-j)!\\,(2z)^j}.$$ Then $h_l$ is a complex-valued $C^2$ function of $z>0$ solving $$h_l''(z)+\\frac2z h_l'(z)+\\Bigl[1-\\frac{l(l+1)}{z^2}\\Bigr]h_l(z)=0,$$ it never vanishes on $(0,\\infty)$, and its Wronskian-type identity $\\operatorname{Im}\\bigl(\\overline{h_l(z)}\\,h_l'(z)\\bigr)=z^{-2}$ holds. For $k>0$ and $R>0$ the spherical Dirichlet-to-Neumann coefficient $$t_l=\\frac{k\\,h_l'(kR)}{h_l(kR)}$$ satisfies the exact bounds $$\\operatorname{Re}t_l\\le-\\frac1R,\\qquad |\\operatorname{Re}t_l|\\le\\frac{l+1}{R},\\qquad 0<\\operatorname{Im}t_l\\le k .$$ These are exact identities and inequalities for the displayed explicit functions, not asymptotic formulas; the argument uses only elementary differentiation and a constant Wronskian, so no large-$l$ asymptotic input is required.",
          "strategy": "Substituting $h=e^{iz}P(z)/z$ with $P(z)=\\sum_{j=0}^l b_jz^{-j}$ into the radial equation gives the coefficient recursion $b_{j+1}=i(l+j+1)(l-j)b_j/[2(j+1)]$ with $b_0=1$, terminating at $j=l$ because $l-j=0$; its solution is the displayed factorial formula, which proves the equation and shows the leading outgoing term $(-i)^{l+1}e^{iz}/z$ with a finite remainder. Writing $y=zh_l$ gives $y''+(1-l(l+1)z^{-2})y=0$ with real coefficient; differentiating $\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline y\\,y')$ and using the equation shows its derivative is zero, so by the zero-derivative-implies-constant corollary on the interval $(0,\\infty)$ ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]) it is constant, and its large-$z$ leading exponential gives the constant $1$: hence $\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline{h_l}h_l')=z^{-2}\\ne0$, so $h_l$ never vanishes. For the real bounds set $F=|y|^2=|P|^2$; since $b_j$ carries phase $i^j$ times a real number, odd cross terms cancel and $F$ is an even finite polynomial in $z^{-1}$, $F=\\sum_{j\\ge0}a_jz^{-2j}$ with $a_0=1$. Differentiating the real equation gives $F'''+4(1-l(l+1)z^{-2})F'+4l(l+1)Fz^{-3}=0$, whose coefficient matching yields $a_{j+1}=(2j+1)[l(l+1)-j(j+1)]a_j/[2(j+1)]$, all positive for $0\\le j\\le l$ and zero at $j=l+1$, with the closed form $a_j=(2j)!(l+j)!/[2^{2j}(j!)^2(l-j)!]$. Thus $|h_l|^2=z^{-2}\\sum a_jz^{-2j}\\ge z^{-2}$ and half the logarithmic derivative gives $\\operatorname{Re}[kh_l'/h_l]=-R^{-1}\\bigl[\\sum_ja_j(kR)^{-2j}+\\sum_j j a_j(kR)^{-2j}\\bigr]/\\sum_ja_j(kR)^{-2j}$, a weighted average of $j\\in\\{0,\\dots,l\\}$, so it lies in $[-\\,(l+1)/R,\\,-1/R]$. The Wronskian identity gives $\\operatorname{Im}[kh_l'(kR)/h_l(kR)]=k/(k^2R^2\\,|h_l(kR)|^2)=k/(k^2R^2|h_l|^2)\\in(0,k]$ using $|h_l|^2\\ge z^{-2}$. The derivative computations use the algebra of derivatives and the entireness of the complex exponential ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]])."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Rectangular PEC reflection class: Fourier completeness, weak solutions and energy conservation",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#G2",
          "design_state": "complete-local-branch-argument",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "thm-trigonometric-system-is-complete-in-l-two-of-the-torus",
            "thm-l-two-fourier-series-converges-in-mean-square",
            "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space",
            "def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
            "def-distributional-derivative",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "cor-globally-lipschitz-odes-have-global-solutions",
            "thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant",
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "thm-ftc-first-part",
            "thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-trigonometric-system-is-complete-in-l-two-of-the-torus": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-l-two-fourier-series-converges-in-mean-square": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distributional-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-globally-lipschitz-odes-have-global-solutions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-first-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $Q=\\prod_{j=1}^3(0,L_j)$ with $L_j>0$ and let $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$ be constants. Define the reflection boundary class by extending $E_1$ evenly across the $x_1$-walls and oddly across the $x_2,x_3$-walls, and cyclically for $E_2,E_3$; extend $B_1$ oddly across $x_1$ and evenly across $x_2,x_3$, cyclically for $B_2,B_3$. Work on the rectangular torus $\\prod_j\\mathbb R/(2L_j\\mathbb Z)$ in the real $L^2$ fields with these parities and distributional $\\operatorname{div}E=\\operatorname{div}B=0$. For smooth fields the parity class gives $n\\times E=0$ and $n\\cdot B=0$ on $\\partial Q$. Then: (i) the finite separated Fourier modes are dense in this class, and the class is complete for the $L^2$ pairing ([[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]], [[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]); (ii) for every initial datum in the class there is a unique $C(\\mathbb R;L^2)$ weak solution of $\\partial_tE=(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1}\\operatorname{curl}B$, $\\partial_tB=-\\operatorname{curl}E$ in the same class ([[def-distributional-derivative]]); (iii) the energy $\\tfrac12\\int_Q(\\varepsilon|E|^2+|B|^2/\\mu)\\,dx$ is constant along the solution. This is an explicit weak PEC model; it supplies neither trace regularity for arbitrary $H(\\operatorname{curl})$ fields nor completeness in lossy, open or non-rectangular guides.",
          "strategy": "The one-dimensional character system on the torus is complete for complex $L^2$ ([[thm-trigonometric-system-is-complete-in-l-two-of-the-torus]]) and its Fourier partial sums converge in mean square ([[thm-l-two-fourier-series-converges-in-mean-square]]). If an $L^2(Q)$ function is orthogonal to every product of characters, fix two indices and integrate against those two characters: Fubini and Cauchy-Schwarz ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]) make the remaining one-variable coefficient an $L^2$ function whose character coefficients all vanish, hence it is zero; repeating in the second and third coordinates on a single null set gives product completeness; orthogonality follows by iterated one-variable integration and Parseval by expanding the squared distance to a finite projection. Reflection parities force sine in odd coordinates and cosine in even coordinates; the trigonometric product modes are the paired $\\pm k_j$ projections. For each torus wavevector $k_j=\\pi n_j/L_j$ the Fourier coefficients satisfy the finite-dimensional constant-coefficient linear system $\\widehat E_t=i(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1}k\\times\\widehat B$, $\\widehat B_t=-ik\\times\\widehat E$ with $k\\cdot\\widehat E=k\\cdot\\widehat B=0$. Identify the complex coefficient space with its finite-dimensional real and imaginary parts. The constant linear right-hand side is globally state-Lipschitz on all time and state space, so [[cor-globally-lipschitz-odes-have-global-solutions]] gives a unique global solution through the initial coefficients. Differentiating $\\varepsilon|\\widehat E|^2+|\\widehat B|^2/\\mu$ gives zero by the scalar triple-product identity and conjugation, so its weighted norm is preserved; the constrained subspace and the parity patterns are preserved because $k\\cdot(k\\times v)=0$ and curl maps the stated parities into each other. Solve all coefficients, truncate to finitely many wavevectors, and pass to the limit with Parseval: the weighted tail norm equals its initial value, so the finite solutions converge uniformly in time in $L^2$, and the limit is $C_tL^2$ and satisfies the equations in the distributional sense ([[def-distributional-derivative]]) after testing against smooth periodic compactly supported-in-time tests: on each compact time support, uniform $L^2$ convergence and Cauchy-Schwarz pass the field pairings against the test time derivatives and spatial curls to the limit. No absolute summability of test Fourier coefficients is needed. Uniqueness: a $C_tL^2$ weak solution has continuous Fourier coefficients whose distributional derivatives are the displayed continuous right-hand sides; for each coefficient $z(t)$ and its continuous right-hand side $g(t)$, subtract the classical primitive $G(t)=\\int_0^t g(s)\\,ds$. On every compact real interval, each real and imaginary component of the continuous $g$ is Riemann integrable by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]. The oriented primitive $G$ is therefore $C^1$, with $G'=g$ by [[thm-ftc-first-part]], componentwise and for either sign of $t$. Under the already declared $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the $C^1$ regular-distribution compatibility clause of [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]] gives $\\partial_tu_G=u_g$. Subtracting this from the actual weak coefficient equation $\\partial_tu_z=u_g$ proves that $z-G$ has zero distributional derivative. [[thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant]] on $\\mathbb R$ makes this regular distribution constant; continuity makes the equality pointwise. Thus each coefficient is a classical solution of the displayed finite-dimensional ODE, and global ODE uniqueness with its given initial value identifies it. Product completeness then identifies the fields. Energy conservation passes to the limit in norm. Countable Choice covers the Fourier, Fubini, Hilbert-space and regular-distribution supplier contracts; the finite-dimensional modal equations require no extra choice premise."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Newtonian potential of smooth compact data is smooth and solves the Poisson equation pointwise",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#S1",
          "design_state": "complete-local-branch-argument",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-newtonian-potential",
            "thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign",
