            "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-positive-density-measures-assign-positive-volume-to-nonempty-open-sets-and-metric-balls": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-fundamental-lemma-of-the-calculus-of-variations": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $\\varepsilon_0>0$ be any scalar parameter and let $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ and let $$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{f(y)}{4\\pi|x-y|}\\,dy .$$ Then $Nf\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$, $-\\Delta Nf=f$ pointwise, and for every multi-index $\\alpha$ the spatial derivative satisfies $D^\\alpha Nf=N(D^\\alpha f)$. In particular, for smooth compactly supported $\\rho$ the field $E=-\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\nabla N\\rho$ is smooth. The statement is about the explicit integral transform of compactly supported smooth data and does not assume smoothness of the kernel at the pole; the distributional identity $-\\Delta Nf=f$ is the one supplied by the Newtonian-potential theorem ([[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]]), upgraded here from a distributional to a pointwise identity for smooth data.",
          "strategy": "Substitute $z=x-y$: $Nf(x)=\\int\\Phi(z)f(x-z)\\,dz$ with $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}$. On a compact set of $x$ the derivatives $D^\\alpha f(x-z)$ vanish outside a common bounded $z$-set and are bounded there, so every parameter derivative has an integrable bound $C|\\Phi|$ on that set; differentiation under the integral sign ([[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign]], applied coordinatewise) gives $D^\\alpha Nf(x)=\\int\\Phi(z)D^\\alpha f(x-z)\\,dz$ and continuity of the result, hence $C^\\infty$. The distributional identity $-\\Delta T_{Nf}=T_f$ is the cited Newtonian-potential theorem ([[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]]). Since both sides are smooth functions (the right side is $f$, the left side is a smooth representative by the first part), the distributional equality is a pointwise equality by localization against compactly supported nonnegative smooth tests. The derivative identity $D^\\alpha Nf=N(D^\\alpha f)$ is the parameter-differentiation formula just proved, and the final smoothness claim for $E$ follows by linearity. Countable Choice is inherited from the exact Statement of [[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]] and its regular-distribution interface; it is explicitly assumed here, not silently removed by the smooth upgrade."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-symmetric-coercive-variational-solver",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Unique solution of a symmetric coercive variational problem on a Hilbert space",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#B2",
          "design_state": "complete-local-branch-argument",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-complete-metric-space",
            "thm-parallelogram-law",
            "def-bounded-bilinear-map",
            "def-operator-norm",
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-infimum",
            "thm-infimum-property",
            "lem-inf-epsilon"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complete-metric-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-parallelogram-law": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-bilinear-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-operator-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-infimum": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-infimum-property": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-inf-epsilon": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice, or the Axiom of Choice as declared ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-complete-metric-space]]), let $a:H\\times H\\to\\mathbb R$ be a symmetric bounded bilinear form ([[def-bounded-bilinear-map]]) with $a(v,v)\\ge\\alpha\\|v\\|^2$ for all $v\\in H$ and some $\\alpha>0$, and let $\\ell:H\\to\\mathbb R$ be a bounded linear functional with dual norm $\\|\\ell\\|$ ([[def-operator-norm]]). Then there is exactly one $u\\in H$ with $$a(u,v)=\\ell(v)\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H,$$ and it satisfies $\\|u\\|\\le\\|\\ell\\|/\\alpha$. No Lax-Milgram theorem, Zorn-type argument or unproved minimiser existence is invoked: the proof minimises the explicit functional $J(v)=\\tfrac12a(v,v)-\\ell(v)$.",
          "strategy": "The functional $J(v)=\\tfrac12a(v,v)-\\ell(v)$ is bounded below by $\\tfrac\\alpha2\\|v\\|^2-\\|\\ell\\|\\|v\\|$ and tends to $+\\infty$ as $\\|v\\|\\to\\infty$. Choose a minimising sequence $v_n$ with $J(v_n)\\to m=\\inf J$; the choice of the sequence uses Countable Choice as declared ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). The parallelogram law ([[thm-parallelogram-law]]) gives $J((v_n+v_k)/2)=[J(v_n)+J(v_k)]/2-a(v_n-v_k,v_n-v_k)/8$, so $\\alpha\\|v_n-v_k\\|^2/8\\le[J(v_n)+J(v_k)]/2-m\\to0$: the sequence is Cauchy, and completeness of the Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-complete-metric-space]]) gives $v_n\\to u$ in norm, with $J(u)=m$ by continuity of the bounded form and functional ([[def-bounded-bilinear-map]]). For fixed $v$ the real quadratic $s\\mapsto J(u+sv)$ has a minimum at $s=0$, so its linear coefficient $a(u,v)-\\ell(v)$ vanishes; conversely any such $u$ satisfies $J(u+v)-J(u)=\\tfrac12a(v,v)\\ge0$. Uniqueness: for two solutions test the difference with itself to get $\\alpha\\|u_1-u_2\\|^2\\le0$. Testing the equation at $v=u$ gives $\\alpha\\|u\\|^2\\le\\|\\ell\\|\\|u\\|$, hence the estimate."
        },
