            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $\\gamma:\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a closed embedded $C^2$ curve parametrized by arc length and let $I\\in\\mathbb R$ be a constant and $\\mu_0>0$. Define the vector-valued distribution $$j(\\psi)=I\\int_0^L\\gamma'(s)\\cdot\\psi(\\gamma(s))\\,ds,\\qquad \\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3).$$ The test space is [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]. Then $\\operatorname{div}j=0$ in the distributional sense ([[def-distributional-derivative]]). For any family of nonnegative smooth radial kernels $\\eta_\\varepsilon$ of integral one supported in $B_\\varepsilon(0)$, $\\varepsilon>0$ (including the usual rescalings $\\eta_\\varepsilon(x)=\\varepsilon^{-3}\\eta(x/\\varepsilon)$ of a chosen unit-mass radial kernel supported in $B_1(0)$), the mollified fields $j_\\varepsilon=j*\\eta_\\varepsilon$ are smooth, compactly supported and divergence-free, and their Biot-Savart fields converge, with every derivative uniformly on any compact $K$ disjoint from $\\gamma$, to $$B(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0I}{4\\pi}\\int_0^L\\frac{\\gamma'(s)\\times(x-\\gamma(s))}{|x-\\gamma(s)|^3}\\,ds .$$ In addition, assigning arbitrary finite values on the curve gives a representative $B\\in L^1_{\\rm loc}(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$, smooth off the curve, with $\\operatorname{div}T_B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}T_B=\\mu_0j$ globally. The first identity is tested on scalar $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ and the second on vector $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$. If the curve lies in $B_a(0)$, then $|B(x)|\\le\\mu_0|I|L/[4\\pi(|x|-a)^2]$ for $|x|>a$, so $B$ decays at infinity. The distributional assertions are proved globally and do not follow merely from convergence away from the curve. For the circle $\\gamma(s)=a(\\cos(s/a),\\sin(s/a),0)$ and a point $z e_3$ on its axis the value is $B_z=\\mu_0Ia^2/[2(a^2+z^2)^{3/2}]$. Nothing is asserted about the divergent line self-energy or about fields on the curve itself.",
          "strategy": "First, for every scalar compact smooth test $\\varphi$, $\\langle\\operatorname{div}j,\\varphi\\rangle=-I\\int_0^L(d/ds)\\varphi(\\gamma(s))ds=0$ by [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] and periodicity. The order-zero bound $|j(\\psi)|\\le|I|L\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty$ makes $j$ a compactly supported vector distribution by [[thm-local-finite-order-characterization-of-distributions]]. Write the mollification explicitly as $j_\\varepsilon(x)=I\\int_0^L\\gamma'(s)\\eta_\\varepsilon(x-\\gamma(s))ds$. Compact parameter differentiation proves smoothness and support in the compact epsilon-neighbourhood of the curve; $\\operatorname{div}j_\\varepsilon=-I\\int_0^L(d/ds)\\eta_\\varepsilon(x-\\gamma(s))ds=0$. These statements include $I=0$.\n\nFor the global field, put $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)$ with finite pole value and $A(x)=\\mu_0I\\int_0^L\\gamma'(s)\\Phi(x-\\gamma(s))ds$. On a bounded observation set $Q$ and for $y$ on the compact curve, $Q-y$ lies in one fixed ball $B_R$. Polar integration gives $\\int_{B_R}|\\Phi|=R^2/2$ and $\\int_{B_R}|\\nabla\\Phi|=R$. Tonelli followed by the integral triangle inequality therefore bounds $\\int_Q|A|$ by $\\mu_0|I|LR^2/2$ and the absolute integral defining $B$ by $\\mu_0|I|LR$. The curve is Lebesgue-null: a length-L unit-speed curve is covered by at most $1+L/h$ balls of radius $2h$ from an equally spaced parameter partition, with total volume $O(Lh^2+h^3)\\to0$. The finite cover estimate uses [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]] and [[thm-finite-and-countable-subadditivity-of-measures]]; arbitrary finite assignments on the null curve remain measurable by [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]]. Reset exceptional curve values finitely. The explicit off-curve formula is smooth by compact differentiation; measurable kernels and Tonelli give the globally locally integrable representative.\n\nEstablish $\\partial_jT_\\Phi=T_{-z_j/(4\\pi|z|^3)}$ directly: integrate by parts on $\\{\\delta<|z|<R\\}$ containing a scalar test support; the outer term vanishes and the inner boundary term is bounded by $\\|\\varphi\\|_\\infty\\delta$, since $\\Phi=1/(4\\pi\\delta)$ and the sphere area is $4\\pi\\delta^2$. The integrals of $|\\Phi|$ and $|\\nabla\\Phi|$ on the omitted ball tend to zero by the displayed polar bounds, proving the first-derivative distribution identity. Translation and absolutely integrable Fubini now give $\\operatorname{curl}T_A=T_B$, with the stated cross-product sign.\n\nFor a scalar test $\\varphi$, let $H(y)=\\int\\Phi(z)\\varphi(z+y)dz$. Every derivative can be passed to the smooth test on a common bounded z-set, so $H$ is smooth near the compact curve. Then $\\langle\\operatorname{div}T_A,\\varphi\\rangle=-\\mu_0I\\int_0^L\\gamma'(s)\\cdot\\nabla H(\\gamma(s))ds=-\\mu_0I\\int_0^L(d/ds)H(\\gamma(s))ds=0$. The translated identity of [[thm-minus-laplacian-of-the-fundamental-solution-is-dirac]] similarly gives, componentwise, $\\langle-\\Delta T_A,\\psi\\rangle=\\mu_0I\\int_0^L\\gamma'(s)\\cdot\\psi(\\gamma(s))ds=\\mu_0j(\\psi)$. All interchanges are absolutely integrable by the uniform kernel bound times a finite test-derivative norm. Constant-coefficient distributional derivatives commute because mixed derivatives of smooth tests commute. Componentwise alternating-symbol cancellation thus yields $\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}T_A=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}T_A=\\nabla\\operatorname{div}T_A-\\Delta T_A=\\mu_0j$. This proves the global identities for $T_B$ without any unsupported limit through the wire. Since $B$ is smooth off the curve and $j$ vanishes there, the distributional identities imply the pointwise $\\operatorname{div}B=\\operatorname{curl}B=0$ there by regular-distribution injectivity and continuity.\n\nFor away-from-curve convergence, write the Biot-Savart field of $j_\\varepsilon$ by Fubini as the loop integral additionally averaged over $u$ against $\\eta_\\varepsilon(u)$. Absolute integrability follows from the same local kernel estimate and compact source support. For a compact $K$ disjoint from the curve and $d=\\operatorname{dist}(K,\\gamma)>0$, the kernel and each specified x-derivative are smooth and uniformly continuous on the enlarged compact difference set for $|u|\\le d/2$. Each derivative error is bounded by $\\mu_0|I|L/(4\\pi)$ times that derivative's uniform modulus at epsilon, since the averaging kernel is nonnegative with integral one. This tends to zero by [[thm-heine-cantor-metric]], and compact differentiation under the integral follows from [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign]]. For $|x|>a$, $|x-\\gamma(s)|\\ge|x|-a$ gives the stated far bound. For a circle at an axis point, the numerator integrates to $2\\pi a^2e_3$ and the denominator is $(a^2+z^2)^{3/2}$, giving the original formula. No value of the singular line integral on the curve, finite self-energy or force product is asserted. Countable Choice enters through the exact polar, surface, fundamental-solution and regular-distribution interfaces; no stronger axiom is used. The convergence proof uses only nonnegativity, unit mass and support in $B_\\varepsilon$; no fixed-shape dependence on epsilon is required. The usual rescaled unit-ball kernel is a special case, not a narrowing of the permitted family. In the first-derivative excision, present each spherical boundary by the same six signed-maximal-coordinate regular graph faces; their finite seam arcs have zero surface measure. This checks the precise finite-face hypothesis of the cited divergence theorem.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
