            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sobolev-spaces-are-banach-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-norm-and-normed-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-parallelogram-law": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice as declared by the Sobolev completeness theorem ([[thm-sobolev-spaces-are-banach-spaces]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $\\Omega\\subseteq(-L,L)^3$ be bounded and open, and write $H^1(\\Omega)=W^{1,2}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ with $H_0^1(\\Omega):=W_0^{1,2}(\\Omega)$ the closure of $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ in the $W^{1,2}$ norm ([[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], [[def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure]]). Then $H^1(\\Omega)$ and its closed subspace $H_0^1(\\Omega)$ are complete, the quadratic integral norm is a Hilbert norm (its coordinate $L^2$ inner products satisfy the parallelogram law), and $$\\|v\\|_{L^2}^2\\le(2L)^2\\,\\|\\partial_1v\\|_{L^2}^2\\le(2L)^2\\,\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2}^2\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega). $$ In particular the gradient seminorm is equivalent to the full $H^1$ norm on $H_0^1$, and the argument needs neither a smooth domain nor a general boundary-trace theorem.",
          "strategy": "For $v\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ extend $v$ by zero to the box $(-L,L)^3$ (legitimate because $\\Omega\\subseteq(-L,L)^3$). Along each coordinate line the Newton-Leibniz formula ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]]) gives $v(x_1,x_2,x_3)=\\int_{-L}^{x_1}\\partial_1v(s,x_2,x_3)\\,ds$; Cauchy-Schwarz for $L^2$ ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]) gives $|v|^2\\le2L\\int_{-L}^{L}|\\partial_1v|^2ds$, and integrating in the other coordinates and then in $x_1$ over a length-$2L$ interval gives $\\|v\\|_2^2\\le(2L)^2\\|\\partial_1v\\|_2^2$, hence the displayed squared inequality with $(2L)^2$ and the nonsquared bound $\\|v\\|_2\\le2L\\|\\nabla v\\|_2$. Approximation in the $W^{1,2}$ norm (the definition of $H_0^1$ as a closure, [[def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure]]) passes both the inequality and the equivalences to all of $H_0^1$ by continuity of the $L^2$ norm; completeness is the Sobolev completeness theorem ([[thm-sobolev-spaces-are-banach-spaces]]) restricted to the closed subspace, and the Hilbert-norm claim follows because the $W^{1,2}$ quadratic norm is induced by the coordinate $L^2$ inner products, which satisfy the parallelogram law."
        },
