          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let `Omega` be a bounded `C∞` domain in `R3`, in the graph sense of `def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts`; connectedness is unnecessary. Write `H^m(Omega)=W^{m,2}(Omega;R)` with the multi-index integral norm and `H0^1(Omega)` for the `H1` closure of `Cc∞(Omega)`. For every real `f in L2(Omega)` there is a unique `u in H0^1(Omega) ∩ H2(Omega)` satisfying\n\n$$\\int_\\Omega(\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v+uv)=\\int_\\Omega fv\\quad(v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega)).$$\n\nIt satisfies `(1−Delta)u=f` distributionally and almost everywhere as an `L2` identity, and\n\n$$\\|u\\|_{H^1(\\Omega)}\\le\\|f\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)},\\qquad \\|u\\|_{H^2(\\Omega)}\\le C_\\Omega\\|f\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}.$$\n\nMoreover `D={phi in C∞(closure Omega): phi|boundary Omega=0}` is dense in `H2(Omega) ∩ H0^1(Omega)` in the `H2` norm: every such `w` has `phi_j in D` with `phi_j→w` in `H2`. Here smoothness on the closure means restriction of a smooth function in a neighbourhood of the closure, equivalently the smooth chart representatives used below. In particular, `grad phi_j→grad w` in the graph norm of `H(curl) ∩ H(div)`, with zero curl and `div grad w=Delta w`; every `grad phi_j` is smooth and PEC since its tangential derivatives vanish at the boundary. For precision, let `X_Omega` be the graph-norm closure of smooth vector fields on a neighbourhood of the closure with `nu cross v=0` on the boundary, where `nu` is the outward Euclidean unit normal. The ambient graph space consists of real `L2` vector fields whose distributional `div v=sum_i partial_i v_i` and `curl v=(partial_2 v_3-partial_3 v_2, partial_3 v_1-partial_1 v_3, partial_1 v_2-partial_2 v_1)` lie in `L2`; its norm squared is `||v||2^2+||curl v||2^2+||div v||2^2`. Thus `grad w` belongs to this explicitly defined `X_Omega`, the same completion used by the later cavity item. No equality with every maximal trace realisation is asserted.\n\nNo smoothness of `f`, no classical boundary value for general `u`, and no general nonsmooth-domain, Neumann, interface, or variable-coefficient claim is included. Zero boundary condition means membership in `H0^1`; the published trace theorem identifies its trace as zero.",
          "strategy": "1. **Variational existence and its sharp H1 bound.** B1 supplies completeness and the Hilbert structure of `H0^1`. Equip it with the full quadratic `H1` norm and set `a(w,v)=integral(grad w·grad v+wv)`. Cauchy–Schwarz on the four-component `L2` tuple makes this a bounded symmetric form, and `a(v,v)=||v||H1²`, so the coercivity constant is exactly one. The functional `ell(v)=integral fv` has norm at most `||f||2` because `||v||2≤||v||H1`. Apply B2's explicit minimizing-sequence solver, with its declared Countable Choice covered by AC, to obtain the unique weak `H0^1` solution and `||u||H1≤||f||2`. Testing against compactly supported smooth functions gives the distributional equation. No Lax–Milgram result or unproved minimizer theorem is substituted. None of B1's numerical Poincaré constants is used.\n\n2. **Charts and admissible tangential tests.** Cover the compact closure by finitely many interior patches and smooth graph patches. Shrink each patch twice, with compact support away from its artificial sides, and choose cutoffs equal to one on the inner patches. A graph flattening followed, if needed, by reversal of the last coordinate sends the domain side to `t>0`; smooth charts and inverse bounds through order two give bounded local `H1` and `H2` changes of variables. With `y=Phi(x)`, `J=|det D(Phi^{-1})|`, `U=u∘Phi^{-1}`, the local weak equation is\n\n$$\\int A_{ij}\\partial_jU\\partial_iV+cUV=\\int FV,\\qquad A=J(D\\Phi)(D\\Phi)^{\\mathsf T},\\quad c=J,\\quad F=J(f\\circ\\Phi^{-1}).$$\n\nAll coefficients and first derivatives are bounded on the chosen patch, `A` is symmetric and uniformly positive, and `||F||2≤C||f||2` locally. The change-of-variables formula and weak chain rule justify these identities. A localized flattened `U` lies in `H0^1` of the half-space: take the sequence of `Cc∞(Omega)` functions defining the original `H0^1` membership, multiply by the patch cutoff, and pull back. These are smooth compactly supported functions on the open half-space and converge in `H1`; their supports need not have a uniform distance from its boundary. Hence tangential translations and smooth cutoffs preserve this closure. For `k=1,2`, `w_h=delta_h^k U` and `V=−delta_{−h}^k(eta² w_h)` are admissible tests, pulled back and extended by zero outside the chart. Use a larger preliminary localization equal to one on the quotient neighbourhood, so its cutoff never changes the original equation on the test support. Interior patches allow all three quotient directions. No normal quotient crosses the boundary.\n\n3. **The L2-data estimate, with no differentiated datum.** Put `S=F−cU`, only in `L2`. Discrete integration by parts and the quotient product rule give the principal term\n\n$$\\int\\eta^2 A_{ij}(y+he_k)\\partial_jw_h\\partial_iw_h\\ge\\alpha\\|\\eta\\nabla w_h\\|_2^2.$$\n\nThe exact remaining principal terms are `integral eta²(delta_h Aij)∂jU ∂iw_h` and `integral [Aij(y+h)∂jw_h+(delta_h Aij)∂jU](∂i eta²) w_h`. Thus their absolute sum is at most\n\n$$C\\bigl(\\|U\\|_{H^1}+\\|w_h\\|_2\\bigr)\\|\\eta\\nabla w_h\\|_2+C\\|U\\|_{H^1}\\|w_h\\|_2.$$\n\nHere `||delta_h A||infinity≤||∂k A||infinity`, and the published directional increment estimate yields `||w_h||2≤C||U||H1` on the required enlarged quotient neighbourhood. The undifferentiated right side obeys\n\n$$\\left|\\int S\\,\\delta_{-h}^k(\\eta^2w_h)\\right|\\le C\\|S\\|_2\\bigl(\\|\\eta\\nabla w_h\\|_2+\\|w_h\\|_2\\bigr),$$\n\nbecause the same increment bound applies to `eta²w_h` in that direction. Cauchy–Schwarz and Young's inequality absorb half the principal gradient square and give, uniformly for all sufficiently small nonzero `h`,\n\n$$\\|\\eta\\nabla\\delta_h^kU\\|_2\\le C(\\|F\\|_2+\\|U\\|_{H^1}).$$\n\nThis estimates the actual `L2` datum, not its difference quotient; no `delta_h F` or derivative of `f` is present. The published tangential-test lemma supplies the exact commutator identity, but this explicit estimate is still part of the new proof.\n\n4. **Weak mixed derivatives and corrected normal recovery.** On a smaller patch where `eta=1`, for every compactly supported smooth test `psi`, discrete integration by parts gives `integral delta_h^k(∂jU) psi = −integral ∂jU delta_{−h}^k psi`, whose limit is `−integral ∂jU ∂k psi`. The uniform bound in step 3 makes this distribution a bounded functional in the `L2` norm on tests. Extend it continuously to the closed `L2` subspace generated by those tests, which is Hilbert by `L2` completeness; Riesz gives an `L2` representing function with the same bound. This represents `∂k∂j U` distributionally. No weakly convergent subsequence, reflexivity theorem, or asserted density of tests in the whole `L2` space is required. Thus boundary patches have every second derivative with at least one tangential index; weak derivatives commute by testing smooth functions. The divergence-form equation gives the missing normal derivative with the signs\n\n$$A_{33}\\partial_{33}U=cU-F-\\sum_{(i,j)\\ne(3,3)}A_{ij}\\partial_{ij}U-\\sum_{i,j}(\\partial_iA_{ij})\\partial_jU.$$\n\nEvery term on the right is in `L2` and `A33≥alpha>0`, so division yields `∂33U in L2` and its bound. This follows distributionally before making the almost-everywhere identification. Interior patches already have all second derivatives. Return through the bounded `H2` chart maps, sum the finitely many inner-patch estimates, and obtain `||u||H2≤C(||f||2+||u||H1)≤C_Omega||f||2`. The equation now holds in `L2`. Uniqueness in `H0^1∩H2` is already uniqueness in the larger variational space.\n\n5. **Zero-trace H2 density: prerequisites and flat gluing.** This step applies to every `w in H2∩H0^1`, not just the resolvent. Use a finite smooth ambient partition with one interior part and boundary parts compactly contained in graph charts. Multiplication and flattening preserve `H2`; a boundary part gives a compactly supported `W in H2(H)` with flat trace zero by chart compatibility and bounded trace continuity (or by the `H0^1` approximating sequence from step 2). Its support is separated from the chart's artificial edges. To justify the reflection, first obtain unrestricted smooth `H2` approximants of `W` on the half-space from the published `k=2,p=2` half-space extension, multiplication by a fixed compact cutoff equal to one on its support, and whole-space mollification. They converge in `H2`, so traces of the functions and their first derivatives converge in `L2` by the published `H1` flat trace bound. For any `zeta in Cc∞(R2)` and tangential `k`, their classical boundary integration by parts gives `integral T(∂kW) zeta = −integral TW ∂k zeta = 0`. Hence `T(∂kW)=0` as a distribution and almost everywhere; uniqueness of regular distributions follows directly from mollification/test pairings. This proves the needed tangential trace identity, rather than assuming it.\n\nDefine the odd reflection `W_o(y,t)=W(y,t)` for `t>0`, `−W(y,−t)` for `t<0`. A piecewise `H1` function whose two traces agree is globally `H1`: prove this by testing each half against a compact smooth ambient test, using smooth half-space approximation, one-variable integration and Fubini; the sole interface contribution is the difference of its two traces and hence cancels. Apply this gluing calculation first to `W_o` (both traces zero), then to its first derivatives. The tangential derivatives are odd reflections of `∂kW`, with both traces zero by the preceding argument. The normal derivative is the even reflection of `∂tW`, with identical traces on both sides; its trace need not vanish. Thus `W_o in H2(R3)`, and each derivative has the indicated reflected `L2` class; its `H2` norm is `sqrt(2)||W||H2`. No delta layer occurs. Even reflection of `W`, or zero extension in `H2`, would generally fail and is not used.\n\n6. **Mollification, localization, and the actual cavity norm.** Choose a real compactly supported mass-one mollifier invariant under `t→−t`, for example a radial one, and convolve `W_o`. The mollifications converge in `H2(R3)` because weak derivatives commute with convolution and the `L2` approximate identity converges for each of the finitely many derivatives. They remain odd, so vanish exactly at `t=0`. Multiply by a fixed smooth cutoff in the flattened ambient chart equal to one near the support of `W_o`, with support separated from the artificial edges. This preserves boundary vanishing and convergence. For all sufficiently small radii the cutoff may also be chosen even in `t`, although only zero values at `t=0` are needed after multiplication. Pull back through the smooth chart and extend by zero outside its artificial support to get globally smooth functions on a neighbourhood of the closure with zero physical boundary value. Approximate the interior part by compactly supported smooth functions using legitimate compact-support zero extension and mollification, with radii smaller than its distance from the physical boundary. Add the finitely many parts, choosing radii tending to zero (AC covers sequence choices), to obtain `phi_j→w` in `H2` with `phi_j|boundary=0`. Each lies in `H0^1` by the published zero-trace kernel theorem. Finally `curl grad phi_j=0`, `div grad phi_j=Delta phi_j`, and `n×grad phi_j=0`, the last identity following by differentiating the identically zero smooth boundary restriction. Therefore\n\n$$\\|\\nabla\\phi_j-\\nabla w\\|_2+\\|\\operatorname{curl}(\\nabla\\phi_j-\\nabla w)\\|_2+\\|\\operatorname{div}(\\nabla\\phi_j-\\nabla w)\\|_2\\longrightarrow0.$$\n\nThe curls vanish distributionally by commutation of weak derivatives, which is a direct test-function calculation. This is exactly convergence in G3's graph norm, giving `grad w in X` without a maximal-trace-density assumption."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-finitely-subtracted-analytic-dispersion",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Dispersion relations for a finitely subtracted analytic response",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#D3",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-complex-domain",
            "thm-taylor-expansion-holomorphic-function",
            "cor-cauchy-theorem-star-shaped-domain",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn",
            "lem-em-integrable-causal-dispersion",
            "thm-heine-cantor-metric",
            "thm-uniform-limit-interchanges-complex-line-integrals",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-dependent-choice",
            "def-derivative"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-complex-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-taylor-expansion-holomorphic-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-theorem-star-shaped-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-integrable-causal-dispersion": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-cantor-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-uniform-limit-interchanges-complex-line-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-dependent-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$ be an integer, let $\\omega_0\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $\\chi$ be holomorphic on the upper half-plane and continuous on its closure, of class $C^1$ on its real boundary away from $\\omega_0$, holomorphic on a neighbourhood of $\\omega_0$ in $\\mathbb C$, and satisfying $|\\chi(z)|\\le C(1+|z|^{n-1})$ on $\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0$ outside a compact set ([[def-complex-domain]]). Let $P(z)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\chi^{(j)}(\\omega_0)(z-\\omega_0)^j/j!$ be the Taylor polynomial at $\\omega_0$ and $f(z)=[\\chi(z)-P(z)]/(z-\\omega_0)^n$, with the removable value at $\\omega_0$. Assume the boundary restriction of $f$ is class $C^1$ (the local holomorphy supplies this at $\\omega_0$). Then for real $\\omega$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{Re}\\chi(\\omega)=\\operatorname{Re}P(\\omega)+\\frac{(\\omega-\\omega_0)^n}{\\pi}\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}f(\\xi)}{\\xi-\\omega}\\,d\\xi,$$\n\n$$\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)=\\operatorname{Im}P(\\omega)-\\frac{(\\omega-\\omega_0)^n}{\\pi}\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\operatorname{Re}f(\\xi)}{\\xi-\\omega}\\,d\\xi.$$\n\nAt $\\omega=\\omega_0$ the formula is read as the stated Taylor value. The unsubtracted $n=0$ case is also valid with $P=0$, $f=\\chi$, growth $O(1/|z|)$, and the factor $(\\omega-\\omega_0)^0=1$ for all $\\omega$; at $\\omega_0$ use the same principal-value formula rather than a Taylor-value convention. For the separate constitutive polynomial branch assume Countable Choice and Dependent Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]), fix integers $d\\ge1$, $m\\ge0$, let $A_0,\\ldots,A_m\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ (real scalars when $d=1$), and let $K:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ satisfy all hypotheses of [[lem-em-integrable-causal-dispersion]]. On histories $E:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^d$ of class $C^m$ with $E^{(j)}$ bounded for $0\\le j\\le m$, the response $\\sum_{j=0}^mA_jE^{(j)}+K*E$ is causal, and its transfer function in the $e^{-i\\omega t}$ convention is $Q(z)+\\chi_K(z)$ with $Q(z):=\\sum_{j=0}^mA_j(-iz)^j$. Subtracting the declared polynomial $Q$ leaves the proved entrywise dispersion formulas for $\\chi_K$; this branch does not require holomorphic continuation through a real subtraction point. The Taylor-subtracted formulas above apply to the total response when its additional local-holomorphy, growth and boundary hypotheses are satisfied; $Q$ need not equal its Taylor polynomial $P$. General passivity and storage remain separate model conditions.",
          "strategy": "[[thm-taylor-expansion-holomorphic-function]] on a complex neighborhood of $\\omega_0$ shows that $\\chi-P$ has a zero of order at least $n$, so division gives the holomorphic removable continuation of $f$ there. Elsewhere in $H$ its denominator is nonzero. Continuity of $\\chi$ on $\\overline H$ and the removable continuation make $f$ continuous on $\\overline H$; its real restriction is $C^1$ by hypothesis. The growth bound on $\\chi$ and the degree of $P$ give $f(z)=O(1/|z|)$ uniformly in $\\overline H$ at infinity. For completeness the boundary contour step is valid for any scalar $f$ holomorphic on $H:=\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>0\\}$, continuous on $\\overline H$, with $C^1$ real boundary restriction and $f(z)=O(1/|z|)$ uniformly on $\\overline H$ at infinity. Fix $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R$, $R>|\\omega|+2$ and $0<\\eta<1$. Use the real intervals from $\\omega-R$ to $\\omega-\\eta$ and $\\omega+\\eta$ to $\\omega+R$, a clockwise upper semicircle of radius $\\eta$ about $\\omega$, and the counterclockwise large upper semicircle of radius $R$ about $\\omega$. For $\\delta>0$ apply [[cor-cauchy-theorem-for-null-homotopic-loops]] to $f(z+i\\delta)/(z-\\omega)$ on $D_\\delta:=\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>-\\delta/2\\}\\setminus\\{\\omega-iv:v\\ge0\\}$. This open domain is star-shaped about any point $\\omega+iA$, $A>0$, so the contour is null-homotopic there and the integrand is holomorphic. All contour pieces lie in $D_\\delta$; no analytic extension of $f$ across the real boundary is assumed. On each fixed contour, continuity on a compact closed-half-plane neighborhood gives $f(z+i\\delta)\\to f(z)$ uniformly as $\\delta\\downarrow0$ by [[thm-heine-cantor-metric]], and [[thm-uniform-limit-interchanges-complex-line-integrals]] transfers the zero contour identity to $f(z)/(z-\\omega)$. The large arc has $|z-\\omega|=R$, $|z|\\ge R-|\\omega|$ and length $\\pi R$, hence its contribution is $O(1/R)$. Parametrizing the clockwise small arc by $z=\\omega+\\eta e^{i\\theta}$ with $\\theta$ decreasing from $\\pi$ to $0$ gives $-i\\pi f(\\omega)+o(1)$. Locally the divided difference $[f(\\xi)-f(\\omega)]/(\\xi-\\omega)$ is bounded because the boundary is $C^1$; the constant term cancels under the symmetric indentation. The tails are absolutely integrable by $f(\\xi)/(\\xi-\\omega)=O(|\\xi|^{-2})$. First $\\eta\\downarrow0$, then $R\\to\\infty$ therefore gives $\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(\\xi)/(\\xi-\\omega)d\\xi=i\\pi f(\\omega)$ and the two real/imaginary formulas. Apply that boundary-contour argument to $f$, multiply both formulas by the real factor $(\\omega-\\omega_0)^n$, and add the complex polynomial $P$. At $\\omega_0$ the factor vanishes and the removable Taylor identity gives the asserted Taylor value. The imaginary numerator subtracts the full complex $P$; it cannot be omitted.\nFor the distinct constitutive branch, multiplication by $(-i\\omega)^j$ is the derivative multiplier on the stipulated monochromatic histories, so the transfer polynomial is exactly $Q$. Derivatives at a time are determined by a history agreeing on its whole past, including its endpoint, and the convolution is causal; hence their finite sum is causal on histories with the stated bounded continuous derivatives. The residual $\\chi-Q=\\chi_K$ satisfies [[lem-em-integrable-causal-dispersion]] entrywise. Without that lemma's AC and integrability conditions a merely integrable causal kernel still gives a bounded upper-half-plane holomorphic response, but does not automatically supply these principal values or Taylor continuation. The stated additional analytic assumptions, not causality alone, govern the Taylor branch."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-lift-specified-dirichlet-existence",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Weak Dirichlet existence for a prescribed lift and uniformly elliptic coefficient",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#B3",
          "design_state": "complete-local-branch-argument",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "lem-em-zero-boundary-coercivity",
            "lem-em-symmetric-coercive-variational-solver",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
            "def-distributional-derivative",
            "def-distribution",
            "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-zero-boundary-coercivity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-symmetric-coercive-variational-solver": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distributional-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq(-L,L)^3$ be bounded and open, let $\\varepsilon$ be a measurable symmetric real $3\\times3$ matrix field on $\\Omega$ with $\\alpha|\\xi|^2\\le\\xi\\cdot\\varepsilon(x)\\xi\\le\\beta|\\xi|^2$ for a.e. $x$ and all $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $0<\\alpha\\le\\beta<\\infty$, let $G\\in H^1(\\Omega)$ be a specified lift, and let $F\\in(H_0^1(\\Omega))^*$ be bounded. Then there is exactly one $u\\in G+H_0^1(\\Omega)$ with $$\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u=F(v)\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega),$$ where $H_0^1=W_0^{1,2}$ carries the gradient norm. For $F(v)=\\int_\\Omega\\rho v$ with $\\rho\\in L^2(\\Omega)$ this is the weak Poisson solution $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon\\nabla u)=\\rho$, with the homogeneous boundary datum represented by membership of $H_0^1$ rather than by an unproved classical trace. No boundary datum outside the specified lift class is assumed attainable; classical boundary regularity, general Neumann lifts and transmission regularity remain later obligations.",
          "strategy": "On $H_0^1$ the gradient norm is equivalent to the full $H^1$ norm and $H_0^1$ is complete ([[lem-em-zero-boundary-coercivity]]). Put $a(w,v)=\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla w$ and $\\ell(v)=F(v)-a(G,v)$ for $v,w\\in H_0^1$. The coefficient bounds and Cauchy-Schwarz ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]) make $a$ a bounded symmetric bilinear form with $a(v,v)\\ge\\alpha\\|\\nabla v\\|_2^2$, and $\\ell$ is bounded because $F$ is bounded and $a(G,\\cdot)$ is controlled by $\\beta\\|\\nabla G\\|_2\\|\\nabla\\cdot\\|_2$. The symmetric coercive variational solver ([[lem-em-symmetric-coercive-variational-solver]]) gives a unique $w$ with $a(w,v)=\\ell(v)$ for all $v$, and $u=G+w$ solves the stated problem; testing with compactly supported smooth $v$ gives the distributional equation by the weak first-derivative identity. For $F(v)=\\int\\rho v$ with $\\rho\\in L^2$, the bound $|F(v)|\\le\\|\\rho\\|_2\\|v\\|_2\\le2L\\|\\rho\\|_2\\|\\nabla v\\|_2$ ([[lem-em-zero-boundary-coercivity]], [[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]) makes $F$ bounded, so the existence conclusion applies. Across finitely many material interfaces the weak equation encodes flux continuity where one-sided classical traces exist, by integration by parts on each side."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-magnetic-dipole-remainder",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Magnetic dipole expansion with an explicit uniform remainder",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#S3",
          "design_state": "complete-local-branch-argument",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "lem-em-coulomb-dipole-remainder",
            "lem-integral-of-a-divergence-of-an-l-one-c-one-field-vanishes",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
