            "lem-the-divergence-and-curl-of-a-cross-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $\\mu_0>0$ be any scalar parameter and let $J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J=0$ and $\\operatorname{supp}J\\subset B_a(0)$, $a>0$, and put $m=\\tfrac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}y\\times J(y)\\,dy$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). Then for $r=|x|\\ge2a$ the vector potential and magnetic field $$A(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{J(y)}{|x-y|}\\,dy,\\qquad B=\\operatorname{curl}A,$$ satisfy, uniformly in $n=x/r$, $$A=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{m\\times x}{r^3}+O\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\mu_0a^2\\|J\\|_1}{r^3}\\Bigr),\\qquad B=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{3n(m\\cdot n)-m}{r^3}+O\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\mu_0a^2\\|J\\|_1}{r^4}\\Bigr).$$ The constants in the $O$ terms are absolute (the second follows by differentiating the first, not by differentiating an unspecified remainder), and $\\|J\\|_1=\\int|J|$.",
          "strategy": "Integrating $\\operatorname{div}(y_iJ)=J_i$ over $\\mathbb R^3$ and using the vanishing of the integral of a divergence of a compactly supported $C^1$ field ([[lem-integral-of-a-divergence-of-an-l-one-c-one-field-vanishes]]) gives $\\int J_i=0$; integrating $\\operatorname{div}(y_iy_jJ)=y_jJ_i+y_iJ_j$ gives antisymmetry of $M_{ij}=\\int y_jJ_i$, and with the definition of $m$ one has $M_{ij}=-\\varepsilon_{ijk}m_k$ and hence $Mx=m\\times x$. Writing $|x-y|^{-1}=r^{-1}+(x\\cdot y)/r^3+\\text{remainder}$ with the following explicit twice-FTC integral identity (not by interpreting a Peano remainder quantitatively): for $h(t)=|x-ty|^{-1}$, [[thm-ftc-second-part]] twice gives $h(1)=h(0)+h'(0)+\\int_0^1(1-t)h''(t)dt$, the integral remainder is $\\int_0^1(1-s)y_jy_k\\,\\partial_j\\partial_k|x-sy|^{-1}ds$, whose Hessian is $O(r^{-3})$ and whose spatial derivative is $O(r^{-4})$ with absolute constants because $|x-sy|\\ge r/2$ on the support; the modulus-of-integral bound ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) against $J$ gives the $A$ estimate and its differentiated estimate. Finally the curl computation $\\operatorname{curl}(m\\times x/r^3)=3x(m\\cdot x)/r^5-m/r^3$ follows by component differentiation of the cross product ([[lem-the-divergence-and-curl-of-a-cross-product]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]), the first term being the Coulomb-dipole expression of the cited remainder lemma ([[lem-em-coulomb-dipole-remainder]]). All current moment cancellations invoke [[lem-integral-of-a-divergence-of-an-l-one-c-one-field-vanishes]] with compact smooth fields $y_iJ$ and $y_iy_jJ$, whose fields and divergences are integrable; Countable Choice is inherited by that supplier and the Lebesgue measure interfaces. For the differentiated remainder, $D_x^\\alpha D^2|x-ty|^{-1}$ for $|\\alpha|\\le1$ is bounded by an absolute constant times $r^{-3-|\\alpha|}$ uniformly in $t\\in[0,1]$, $|y|\\le a$ and $r\\ge2a$ because $|x-ty|\\ge r/2$. On a small x-neighbourhood retain a positive lower bound and differentiate this explicit compact-t integral by uniformly bounded difference quotients; the compact y-integral then has integrable majorant $C|y|^2|J(y)|r^{-4}$. This proves the differentiated field remainder independently of any unspecified O term."
        },
