            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. For $k>0$ define $G_k(x)=e^{ik|x|}/(4\\pi|x|)$ off the origin, a locally integrable complex function. Then\n\n$$(-\\Delta-k^2)G_k=\\delta_0$$\n\ndistributionally: for every $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$, $T_{G_k}(\\psi)=\\int G_k\\psi$, $\\delta_0(\\psi)=\\psi(0)$, and $\\langle\\partial_iT,\\psi\\rangle=-\\langle T,\\partial_i\\psi\\rangle$ ([[def-locally-integrable-function-as-a-regular-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]); thus the displayed identity means $\\int G_k(-\\Delta-k^2)\\psi=\\psi(0)$. For $a>0$ and a finite complex measure $\\alpha$ supported in the closed ball $\\overline{B_a(0)}$, the field $u=G_k*\\alpha$ is smooth outside the support, solves $(\\Delta+k^2)u=0$ there and is outgoing: for $x=rn$, $r\\ge2a$,\n\n$$u(rn)=\\frac{e^{ikr}}{4\\pi r}\\int e^{-ikn\\cdot y}\\,d\\alpha(y)+O\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{\\|\\alpha\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}}{r}\\Bigl[\\frac{a}{r}+\\frac{ka^2}{r}\\Bigr]\\Bigr)$$\n\nuniformly in direction, and $r(\\partial_ru-iku)=O(r^{-1})$ uniformly. For a planar aperture $y=(y_1,y_2,0)$ with observation $x=(\\xi_1,\\xi_2,z)$, $z>0$, $|\\xi|\\le b$, $0\\le b$ and $(a+b)/z<1$, the Fresnel approximation replaces $R=|x-y|$ by $z+|\\xi-y|^2/(2z)$ and $1/R$ by $1/z$ with phase error $O(k(a+b)^4/z^3)$ and relative amplitude error $O((a+b)^2/z^2)$, hence absolute field error $O(\\|\\alpha\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}z^{-1}[(a+b)^2/z^2+k(a+b)^4/z^3])$. Aperture data are an explicitly specified scalar source, not asserted to be the unknown exact Maxwell boundary traces, and no uniqueness or existence for arbitrary obstacle boundary data is claimed.",
          "strategy": "For radial $f(r)$ one has $\\Delta f=f''+2f'/r$ by direct coordinate differentiation of $\\partial_ir=x_i/r$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]), and inserting $f=G_k$ gives $(\\Delta+k^2)f=0$ for $r>0$. For the distributional identity excise a ball $B_\\delta$ inside a compact test integration; the second Green identity ([[cor-second-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]) on the excised domain cancels the $k^2$ terms and leaves the inner flux, where $G_k=1/(4\\pi\\delta)+O(1)$ and $\\partial_rG_k=-1/(4\\pi\\delta^2)+O(1)$: the derivative-test boundary term is $O(\\delta)$ and the leading unit flux multiplies $\\psi(0)$, while the local $k^2G_k$ integral is $O(\\delta^2)$; the limit is $\\delta_0$ with the displayed sign. Off-support smoothness and all derivatives follow from the positive separation of compact observation and source sets, with differentiation under the source integral. For the Fraunhofer error write $R=|rn-y|$ and use the norm expansion of the cited dipole-remainder technique ([[lem-em-coulomb-dipole-remainder]]): $|R-r+n\\cdot y|\\le Ca^2/r$ and $|r/R-1|\\le Ca/r$; the exponential difference is bounded by $C r^{-1}(a/r+ka^2/r)$ via $|e^{i\\theta}-1|\\le|\\theta|$, and integrating against the total variation of $\\alpha$ with the integral triangle inequality ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]]) gives the displayed error; the outgoing relation follows from $\\partial_rR=1+O(a^2/r^2)$ and $R^{-2}=O(r^{-2})$. The Fresnel estimate is the Taylor expansion of the norm with explicit remainder on the compact small-$s$ region $s=|\\xi-y|^2/z^2$, using $|e^{i\\theta}-1|\\le|\\theta|$. Countable Choice is inherited by the actually used Green, surface/regular-distribution and/or harmonic-boundary supplier interfaces. The supplier updates do not alter the local source, trace or modal class used here. To make the cited norm-remainder TECHNIQUE an actual calculation, for h(s)=|x-sy| one has $h'(0)=-n\\cdot y$ and $h''(s)=|y|^2/|x-sy|-((x-sy)\\cdot y)^2/|x-sy|^3$, whose modulus is at most $2a^2/(r-a)$ when |y|<=a and r>=2a. The finite Riemann FTC remainder therefore gives |R-r+n dot y|<=Ca²/r; the same lower bound yields |r/R-1|<=Ca/r. Thus this is proved directly, not falsely deduced from a supplier statement about the inverse kernel integral. For integration against a finite COMPLEX measure alpha, the estimate |integral h d alpha|<=sup|h| times ||alpha||TV follows first for finite simple functions from the defining total variation, then for bounded continuous kernels by uniform finite-simple approximation of their range. Every off-support parameter derivative is continuous and uniformly bounded on compact observation/source products, so uniform difference quotients through this finite-measure bound prove all derivatives without an unprovided complex-measure dominated-convergence premise. Finally $\\partial_r R=1+O(a²/r²)$ in the explicit radial derivative formula gives the claimed outgoing O(r^-1) estimate after multiplication by r. For the Fresnel remainder, write s=|xi-y|²/z²; on any prescribed small-angle range (a+b)/z<=eta0<1, twice-FTC gives |sqrt(1+s)-1-s/2|<=s²/8 and |(1+s)^(-1/2)-1|<=s/2. Combined with |exp(i theta)-1|<=|theta| these yield exactly the original phase, relative amplitude and absolute field error bounds, uniformly on that declared range. The finite-total-variation branch and every original exterior/approximation claim are retained."
        },
