          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $P_l$ denote the real vector space of homogeneous polynomials of degree $l$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, $H_l=\\ker(\\Delta:P_l\\to P_{l-2})$ with $P_k=\\{0\\}$ for $k<0$, and let spherical harmonics of degree $l$ be the restrictions of $H_l$ to $S^2$, with unnormalised surface measure $d\\Omega$ of total mass $4\\pi$. Then $P_l=H_l\\oplus|x|^2P_{l-2}$, $\\dim H_l=2l+1$, harmonics of distinct degree are orthogonal in $L^2(S^2,d\\Omega)$, and the union over $l$ of the spaces of spherical harmonics admits a complete orthonormal basis of $L^2(S^2,d\\Omega)$ ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]). For any real orthonormal basis $Y_{lm}$, $m=1,\\dots,2l+1$, of degree-$l$ harmonics the addition theorem holds: $$\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}Y_{lm}(n)Y_{lm}(n')=\\frac{2l+1}{4\\pi}P_l(n\\cdot n'),$$ where $P_l$ is the Legendre polynomial of the convergent zonal kernel lemma ([[lem-em-convergent-legendre-kernel]]). The Laplacian is the Euclidean one ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]). This supplies the harmonic decomposition, orthogonality, completeness and exact addition kernel; it does not assert higher boundary regularity. The purely polynomial decomposition and finite dimension identities use no Choice; the surface/L2/completeness claims retain the stated Countable Choice.",
          "strategy": "For homogeneous $Q$ of degree $l-2j$, Euler's identity $x\\cdot\\nabla Q=(l-2j)Q$ gives $\\Delta(r^{2j}Q)=2j(2l-2j+1)r^{2j-2}Q+r^{2j}\\Delta Q$; the ansatz $H=\\sum_ja_jr^{2j}\\Delta^jP$ with $a_0=1$ and $a_j=-a_{j-1}/[2j(2l-2j+1)]$ cancels consecutive coefficients in $\\Delta H$, and the final $\\Delta^{\\lfloor l/2\\rfloor+1}P$ vanishes by degree, so $H$ is harmonic and $P-H$ is divisible by $r^2$; recursion gives the decomposition. For homogeneous harmonic $H_l,H_m$ the Green identity on the ball ([[cor-second-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]) gives $0=\\int_{S^2}(H_l\\partial_nH_m-H_m\\partial_nH_l)=(m-l)\\int_{S^2}H_lH_m$, whence orthogonality of distinct degrees; if a harmonic of degree $l$ equalled $r^2Q$, its orthogonality to all lower-degree harmonics would force $\\int_{S^2}H_l^2=0$, so $H_l=0$; the monomial count $\\dim P_l=(l+1)(l+2)/2$ gives $\\dim H_l=2l+1$, and restriction is injective by the same homogeneity argument. For density, continuous functions are dense in $L^2(S^2)$ by a finite smooth graph atlas with subordinate smooth cutoffs, pulling each localised function back, mollifying and using the $L^2$ translation and approximate-identity interfaces ([[lem-complex-translation-and-approximate-identity-interfaces]]); the restricted real polynomial algebra is unital and separates points, so Stone-Weierstrass ([[thm-real-stone-weierstrass-general]]) makes it uniformly dense in $C(S^2)$, and the homogeneous-degree decomposition puts every restriction in the finite span of spherical harmonics, giving $L^2$ density. Finite Gram-Schmidt ([[thm-gram-schmidt-orthonormalisation]]) applied to a fixed ordered monomial elimination basis of each $H_l$ produces the orthonormal bases without an undefined basis selection. For the addition theorem, $K_l(n,n')=\\sum_mY_{lm}(n)Y_{lm}(n')$ is independent of the orthonormal basis and rotation-invariant, and for fixed $n'$ it is the unique (up to scale) rotation-invariant homogeneous harmonic polynomial of degree $l$, which is the coefficient $|y|^lP_l(n'\\cdot y/|y|)$ of the convergent kernel of the zonal lemma ([[lem-em-convergent-legendre-kernel]]); evaluating the diagonal by integrating $K_l(n,n)$ gives $(2l+1)/(4\\pi)$, completing the addition theorem. Countable Choice is inherited from the exact Statements of the Green/surface and Euclidean L2 approximate-identity interfaces. The fixed ordered finite monomial elimination lists and finite Gram–Schmidt provide one explicit basis in each degree, with no arbitrary basis choice or Dependent Choice. The solid zonal coefficient is harmonic by the finite t-derivative argument in the corrected kernel supplier. No unproved Cauchy bound is used. For the finite zonal uniqueness step rotate the pole to e3. An invariant homogeneous polynomial has the form $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor l/2\\rfloor}b_j z^{l-2j}(x^2+y^2)^j$: writing planar coordinates as x+iy and x-iy, rotation multiplies their monomial with exponents a,b by exp(i(a-b)theta); invariance for every theta forces a=b, by finite Fourier orthogonality on [0,2pi]. Harmonicity gives the explicit recurrence $4(j+1)^2b_{j+1}=-(l-2j)(l-2j-1)b_j$. Thus all coefficients are determined by b0, and the zonal harmonic space is one-dimensional. The generating solid coefficient has b0=P_l(1)=1, so it supplies the nonzero normalized generator. Rotation invariance of sphere measure is exactly [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], under AComega. Hence the addition argument invokes no undeclared zonal uniqueness theorem.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Sheldon Axler, Paul Bourdon, Wade Ramey, Harmonic Function Theory, 2nd edition, author-hosted full text",
