          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-parameter-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-first-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]), carried by the cited sphere, parameter, polar and divergence suppliers. Let $c>0$, let $f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ (unbounded $f$ is allowed), and define the spherical mean\n\n$$M_rf(x)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}f(x+rn)\\,d\\Omega(n),\\qquad r\\ge0,$$\n\nwith the outward sphere measure of total area $4\\pi$ ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]). Then $r\\mapsto M_rf(x)$ is smooth for $r\\ge0$ and satisfies the Euler-Poisson-Darboux identity\n\n$$\\partial_r^2M_rf(x)+\\frac2r\\partial_rM_rf(x)=\\Delta M_rf(x)\\qquad(r>0),$$\n\nand the function $H_f(x,t)=t\\,M_{ct}f(x)$, $t\\ge0$, satisfies\n\n$$\\partial_t^2H_f=c^2\\Delta H_f,\\qquad H_f(x,0)=0,\\qquad\\partial_tH_f(x,0)=f(x).$$\n\nAll identities are local and need no decay of $f$, since every integration set is compact. This is the pure spherical-mean identity; it makes no claim about finite-domain boundary data or about general rough data.",
          "strategy": "Differentiating the compact sphere integral in the parameter $r$ is justified by the parameter-integral lemma ([[lem-em-parameter-integrals]], including its surface-measure form). One gets $\\partial_rM_rf(x)=(4\\pi)^{-1}\\int_{S^2}n\\cdot\\nabla f(x+rn)\\,d\\Omega$, and the divergence theorem on $B_r(x)$ ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], applied to the $C^1$ field $\\nabla f$ and using the Euclidean Laplacian, [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]) gives $4\\pi r^2\\partial_rM_rf(x)=\\int_{B_r(x)}\\Delta f(y)\\,dy$. Differentiating this last integral in $r$ (radial derivative of a ball integral; one-variable fundamental theorem, [[thm-ftc-first-part]]) gives $\\partial_r(r^2\\partial_rM_rf)=r^2M_r(\\Delta f)=r^2\\Delta M_rf$, where the second equality uses linearity and the parameter statement to move $\\Delta$ through the mean; dividing by $r^2$ gives the Euler-Poisson-Darboux identity. Twice differentiating $H_f(x,t)=tM_{ct}f(x)$ with the algebra of derivatives and the chain rule factor $c$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) gives $\\partial_t^2H_f=2c\\partial_rM+c^2t\\partial_r^2M=c^2t\\Delta M=c^2\\Delta H_f$ for $t>0$, using the identity just proved; at $t=0$ one has $M_0f=f$ and $\\partial_rM_0f=(4\\pi)^{-1}\\nabla f\\cdot\\int_{S^2}n\\,d\\Omega=0$ by reflection symmetry, so $H_f(x,0)=0$ and $\\partial_tH_f(x,0)=f(x)$; the defining compact integral is smooth in signed $r$ as well, so the equation extends to $t=0$."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-ball-poisson-series-boundary-convergence",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Ball Poisson series: interior convergence, boundary data and the Poisson kernel",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#H3",
