          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $f\\in L^2(S^2,d\\Omega)$ with harmonic coefficients $f_{lm}$ in an orthonormal basis of the spherical-harmonic lemma ([[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]]), and for $0\\le r<1$ set $u(rn)=\\sum_{l,m}r^lf_{lm}Y_{lm}(n)$ (the $l=0$ term being the constant value at $r=0$). Then the series and all its derivatives converge uniformly on every closed smaller ball, $u$ is harmonic there ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]), $u(r\\,\\cdot)\\to f$ in $L^2(S^2)$ as $r\\uparrow1$, and $u$ is the unique harmonic function on the unit ball with $\\sup_{r<1}\\|u(r\\,\\cdot)\\|_{L^2}<\\infty$ and $L^2$ boundary limit $f$. For continuous $f$ the same function is $$u(rn)=\\int_{S^2}\\frac{1-r^2}{4\\pi|rn-n'|^3}f(n')\\,d\\Omega(n'),$$ it extends continuously to the closed ball with boundary value $f$, and the kernel is positive of total mass one. This is the stated ball boundary branch; generic smooth-domain classical regularity remains a separate obligation.",
          "strategy": "Fix the canonical real spherical-harmonic basis of [[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]]. Its complete orthonormality gives norm convergence and Parseval directly: the finite orthogonal projection S_N has ||f-S_N||2 no larger than ||f-v||2 for every finite-span v in its subspace, by finite Pythagoras; density therefore makes this error vanish. No arbitrary sequence of approximants is selected. The addition identity gives sum_m|Y_lm(n)|²=(2l+1)/(4pi). Finite and then countable Cauchy-Schwarz gives the absolute series tail bound $\\|f\\|_2[\\sum_{l>N}r_0^{2l}(2l+1)/(4\\pi)]^{1/2}$ on r<=r0<1. The terms r^lY_lm are actual homogeneous harmonic polynomials, including their smooth values at0. On a smaller ball use harmonic partial-sum differences on fixed-radius balls inside an intermediate smaller ball; the exact [[thm-interior-derivative-estimates-for-harmonic-functions]] converts the uniform tail bound into a uniform bound for every derivative. Uniform derivative limits are identified by the finite-coordinate FTC [[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], yielding a smooth harmonic limit.\n\nOrthogonality gives $\\|u(r\\cdot)\\|_2^2=\\sum_{l,m}r^{2l}|f_{lm}|^2\\le\\|f\\|_2^2$ and $\\|u(r\\cdot)-f\\|_2^2=\\sum_{l,m}(1-r^l)^2|f_{lm}|^2$. The latter tends to0 by [[thm-dominated-convergence]] on the countable coefficient index set with counting measure and summable bound4sum|f_lm|².\n\nThe corrected generating kernel and addition identity sum the Poisson series as $P_r(n,n')=(1-r²)/(4\\pi|rn-n'|³)$: apply (1+2r partial_r) to the scalar generating function, justified on every r<=r0<1 by its derived power series. Positivity is explicit and integration in nprime leaves only the constant harmonic, so the total mass is1. For continuous f, integrating the uniformly convergent kernel series gives precisely u. The actual [[thm-dirichlet-problem-on-a-ball-by-the-poisson-integral]] (n3, Countable Choice) proves its continuous extension and the classical uniqueness for continuous data.\n\nFor general L2-boundary uniqueness, let v be any smooth harmonic function on the unit ball with the stated radial norm bound and L2 limit f. For0<r<s<1, apply the same published continuous Dirichlet theorem on radius-s ball to the continuous actual data v(s n). Scale x=s z and sphere surface measure by s²; the resulting unit angular Poisson kernel is P_(r/s). Expanding that kernel and using orthogonality gives $v_{lm}(r)=(r/s)^l v_{lm}(s)$. The L2 boundary limit implies $v_{lm}(s)\\to f_{lm}$ by Cauchy-Schwarz as s tends1. Thus $v_{lm}(r)=r^l f_{lm}$ for every degree and every fixed r>0. Completeness gives v(r dot)=u(r dot) in L2, and continuity on that sphere makes equality pointwise; equality at0 follows by continuity. This proves the full uniqueness branch without invoking an undeclared polar-Laplacian or radial ODE theorem. Countable Choice is inherited by the actual harmonic, finite-surface and L2 approximation suppliers, with no additional Dependent Choice or full Choice use.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
