            "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded C-infinity domain of [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]], let k>0 and x in Omega, and use the outgoing kernel $G_k$ of [[lem-em-outgoing-source-helmholtz-and-diffraction]]. A sufficient classical specialization is that u and f extend smoothly to an open neighbourhood of the closed domain and $(-\\Delta-k^2)u=f$ there. More generally, retain the justified weaker classical-trace branch: u is C2 inside Omega, u and all first derivatives extend continuously to its closure, f=(-Delta-k²)u belongs to L1(Omega), and the equation holds inside. The boundary values and normal derivative in the formula below are these actual continuous traces. Then\n$$u(x)=\\int_\\Omega G_k(x-y)f(y)\\,dy+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\\bigl[G_k(x-y)\\partial_nu(y)-u(y)\\partial_{n_y}G_k(x-y)\\bigr]dS_y,$$\nwith n the outward domain normal; both integrals are absolutely convergent. Merely being smooth on the OPEN domain without these trace/integrability data is insufficient.\n\nFor an exterior D=R3 minus closure of a bounded smooth obstacle, retain the exterior counterpart with actual inner boundary traces: suppose u is C2 on D with u and first derivatives continuous to its compact boundary, solves (-Delta-k²)u=f there, f is locally continuous and $\\int_D|G_k(x-y)f(y)|dy$ is finite at the observation x, and the prescribed outgoing condition gives, uniformly on spheres at infinity, $u(y)=O(|y|^{-1})$ and $\\partial_ru(y)-iku(y)=o(|y|^{-1})$. Then the same formula holds with volume D and its obstacle boundary, with n outward from D (pointing into the obstacle). Compactly supported L1 sources with the displayed local regularity are a sufficient source subclass. For the smooth-trace modal outgoing specialization beyond a source-containing sphere of radius $R_0>0$, retain the declared full Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) for this sufficient branch. Write the smooth angular trace as $g(n)=u(R_0n)$, use the real unit-sphere harmonics of [[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]], set $c_{lm}=\\int_{S^2}g(n)Y_{lm}(n)d\\Omega(n)$, and define the outgoing modal class outside that sphere by\n$$u(rn)=\\sum_{l=0}^\\infty\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}c_{lm}\\,\\frac{h_l(kr)}{h_l(kR_0)}Y_{lm}(n),\\qquad r\\ge R_0,$$\nwhere $h_l$ are exactly the earlier explicit functions of [[lem-em-outgoing-spherical-hankel-dtn-bounds]]. The proof below establishes smooth convergence, the homogeneous exterior equation, the actual trace g, the uniform bound $\\sup_{r\\ge R_0,n\\in S^2}r|u(rn)|<\\infty$ and the uniform Sommerfeld residual $\\sup_{n\\in S^2}r|\\partial_ru(rn)-iku(rn)|\\to0$. Thus this existing smooth modal branch satisfies the prescribed far conditions without a later same-page supplier. Its full-Choice premise is retained; the earlier prescribed-outgoing branch remains under Countable Choice.\n\nThe Green formula uses the consistent actual pair of traces. Assigning both aperture values independently does not establish an exact boundary problem; a declared scalar aperture-source model remains a separate approximation. No weak boundary trace or normal trace for arbitrary rough data is asserted by mere interior smoothness.",
          "strategy": "For the closure-smooth bounded specialization, apply [[cor-second-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] on Omega with a small ball about x removed. The k² terms cancel, the outgoing kernel's unit inner flux gives u(x), and its derivative-test term is O(delta); the source integral near x is finite since f is continuous there and G_k is locally integrable. This proves the stated signs.\n\nFor the weaker C2-interior/C1-closure trace branch, let S be the compact smooth boundary with its outward normal n. The exact [[thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem]] under Countable Choice gives a normal tube with positive smooth radius; compactness gives a positive minimum. For sufficiently small t>0, $S_t=\\{p-t n(p):p\\in S\\}$ lies inside Omega and bounds the inner domain Omega_t. In the normal graph charts the surface Jacobian and outward normals converge uniformly to those on S (the differential of p to p-tn(p) tends uniformly to the tangent identity). The one-sided graph convention makes this the actual inside collar; injectivity of the tube prevents overlap. For small t, x and a fixed ball about x lie in Omega_t. On each punctured Omega_t the ordinary Green theorem applies, since u is C2 on a neighbourhood of its compact closure. First remove the x-excision radius by the same local flux argument. Then take t down0: u and its gradient converge uniformly on S_t to their continuous actual traces, while G_k and its y-gradient are bounded and smooth on this collar away from x. The boundary integrals therefore converge to exactly the displayed trace integrals using the compact surface chart formula and the uniformly converging Jacobians. For the volume term, split off a small ball about x, where f is bounded and the kernel is integrable; on its complement G_k is bounded and f is L1. This gives an absolute integrable majorant, so [[thm-dominated-convergence]] on the increasing inner domains yields the full volume integral. This proves the weaker branch completely rather than assuming unspecified traces.\n\nFor the exterior branch, truncate by B_R and use the same inner-boundary trace limit and x-excision argument, with the outward obstacle normal pointing into the obstacle. For fixed observation x, on y=Rn the explicit kernel has $G_k(x-y)=O(R^{-1})$ and $\\partial_R G_k(x-y)-ikG_k(x-y)=O(R^{-2})$, uniformly in n; these follow by differentiating the norm and $\\partial_R|Rn-x|=1+O(R^{-2})$. In the outer Wronskian, write $G_k(\\partial_Ru-iku)-u(\\partial_RG_k-ikG_k)$: its supremum is o(R^-2)+O(R^-3), so multiplying by the sphere area O(R²) makes it tend to0. Absolute convergence of the stated volume integral then permits the limit R to infinity. The smooth-trace modal outgoing subclass has the explicit earlier-supplier proof below; its existing full-Choice sufficient premise is retained. Other prescribed uniform-outgoing data use only Countable Choice through Green, tubular and surface interfaces. No independent assignment of both boundary traces or unproved rough-trace Green theorem is used. The cited Green theorem has real inputs; apply it to the four real/imaginary component pairs and combine by complex bilinearity to obtain the displayed complex kernel identity, with no conjugation introduced.\n\nSmooth angular coefficient estimate from finite angular calculus. For smooth w on S2 let $L_jw(n)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}w(Q_j(t)n)\\right|_{t=0}$, where $Q_j(t)$ is the elementary coordinate rotation about e_j, and put $\\Lambda=\\sum_{j=1}^3L_j^2$. Its tangent velocity is $e_j\\times n$ with [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. Rotation invariance of surface measure is the exact earlier [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]. Differentiate the invariant integral of w(Q_j(t)n)v(Q_j(t)n) on the compact sphere: uniform difference quotients of its smooth integrand give $\\int(L_jw)v=-\\int w(L_jv)$, so Lambda is symmetric under the bilinear integral; for complex data apply the same finite identity to components. No polar-operator theorem is presumed. On an ambient smooth extension, direct coordinate differentiation gives\n$$\\sum_jL_j^2=|x|^2\\Delta-(x\\cdot\\nabla)^2-x\\cdot\\nabla.$$\nEach Y_lm is the restriction of a degree-l homogeneous harmonic polynomial by the earlier H2 supplier, so Euler's identity and this finite formula give $\\Lambda Y_{lm}=-l(l+1)Y_{lm}$. Therefore, for l>=1 and any integer p>=1, repeated symmetry and [[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]] yield\n$$[l(l+1)]^p c_{lm}=\\int_{S^2}(-\\Lambda)^pg\\,Y_{lm}\\,d\\Omega,\\qquad |c_{lm}|\\le\\frac{\\|\\Lambda^pg\\|_2}{[l(l+1)]^p}.$$\nThe smooth g and all these angular derivatives are bounded on the compact sphere and have finite L2 norms. Treat l0 separately as its single finite constant coefficient. The addition diagonal of H2 gives $\\|Y_{lm}\\|_\\infty\\le\\sqrt{(2l+1)/(4\\pi)}$. With p2, the total weighted absolute sum\n$$\\sum_{l,m}(l+1+2kR_0)|c_{lm}|\\,\\|Y_{lm}\\|_\\infty<\\infty$$\nfollows: for l>=1 its degree-l bound is a fixed constant times $(2l+1)(l+1+2kR_0)\\sqrt{(2l+1)/(4\\pi)}[l(l+1)]^{-2}=O(l^{-3/2})$. This is the actual summability argument for every smooth trace, not an unproved claim of coefficient decay.\n\nExact radial majorants. From the earlier Hankel proof, $F_l(z)=z^2|h_l(z)|^2=\\sum_{j=0}^la_jz^{-2j}$ has a0=1 and positive a_j, so it is nonincreasing in z>0. Consequently, for $\\phi_l(r)=h_l(kr)/h_l(kR_0)$,\n$$|\\phi_l(r)|\\le R_0/r\\quad(r\\ge R_0).$$\nThe same earlier supplier's exact t_l bounds, applied with radius r, give $|k h_l'(kr)/h_l(kr)|\\le(l+1)/r+k$, hence\n$$r|\\phi_l'(r)-ik\\phi_l(r)|\\le R_0[(l+1)/r+2k]\\le l+1+2kR_0.$$\nFor every FIXED l the explicit finite outgoing polynomial gives $r(\\phi_l'-ik\\phi_l)\\to0$ as r tends infinity. The weighted coefficient sum therefore bounds every residual tail uniformly for ALL r>=R0 and n on the sphere. Given epsilon, choose a finite degree cutoff making that tail smaller than epsilon/2; the finitely many remaining radial residuals tend to0 uniformly after multiplication by their fixed finite angular suprema. This finite-head/finite-tail argument proves the uniform Sommerfeld limit. Also $r|u(rn)|\\le R_0\\sum|c_{lm}|\\|Y_{lm}\\|_\\infty$ uniformly, which proves exactly the amplitude bound used in the outer Green Wronskian. The first radial derivative series is uniformly convergent on r>=R0 since $|\\phi_l'|\\le[(l+1)/R_0+k]|\\phi_l|$ and the same weighted sum dominates it; finite-coordinate FTC [[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]] identifies that derivative with partial_r u.\n\nSmooth modal realization and normalization. Rotational differentiation preserves the degree-l harmonic space. Skew symmetry gives $\\sum_j\\|L_jv\\|_2^2=l(l+1)\\|v\\|_2^2$ in that finite space, so each successive angular derivative has norm at most [l(l+1)]^(1/2) times the preceding norm. Its pointwise norm is bounded by sqrt((2l+1)/(4pi)) times its L2 norm by finite basis Cauchy-Schwarz and the addition identity. The radial ODE of the earlier Hankel supplier, differentiated finitely many times, gives for every fixed q a bound $|\\phi_l^{(q)}(r)|\\le C_{q,k,R_0}(l+1)^{2q}/r$ on r>=R0: q0 is the radial-ratio bound, q1 is the logarithmic-derivative bound, and the equation plus derivatives of r^-1,r^-2 gives the induction. Thus every radial/angular derivative series is dominated by a finite power of l times the coefficient estimate with a sufficiently large p. It converges uniformly on each closed bounded exterior annulus; FTC identifies all derivatives. Cartesian derivatives there are finite combinations of radial and angular derivatives with smooth coefficients (from $\\nabla=n\\partial_r-r^{-1}n\\times(L_1,L_2,L_3)$), so the series defines a smooth exterior field. The finite angular identity above gives the polar Laplacian formula on phi_l(r)Y_lm, and the radial ODE makes each term solve (Delta+k²)u0; termwise second derivatives prove the equation for the limit. At rR0 the absolute series has its L2 coefficients c_lm; completeness of H2 and continuity give its actual smooth trace g. If trace coefficients are recorded instead in the sphere-normalized basis $Y^{R_0}_{lm}(R_0n)=R_0^{-1}Y_{lm}(n)$, their coefficients are R0 c_lm, so the displayed expansion is identical after normalization. No weaker rough-trace claim or obstacle-boundary existence is inferred. All bounded Green, weaker C1-trace, exterior source and smooth-modal conclusions are preserved, with the same declared Choice strengths."
        },
