          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]) and fix a conversion constant $c>0$, let $[a,b]$ be a nondegenerate compact interval, let $X:[a,b]\\to A$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]), and let $\\mathcal A$ be a $C^2$ covector field on a neighbourhood of the image of $X$. An inertial frame is $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with future positively oriented $\\eta$-orthonormal basis, and its chart sends $p$ to the unique coordinates of $p-o$ in that basis; in these coordinates $\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{\\mu\\nu}$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). In any such chart write the covector components as $A_\\mu$ and define the antisymmetric covariant field $F$ by $F_{\\nu\\mu}=\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu-\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu$; this coordinate formula defines the notation $F=d\\mathcal A$ used below. Indices run from $0$ to $3$, repeated indices are summed, and $U_\\nu=\\eta_{\\nu\\mu}U^\\mu$. All integrals in this particle lemma are finite-interval Riemann integrals. On $[a,b]$, $C^2$ means continuous components and continuous first and second derivatives with their one-sided endpoint values; equations at endpoints use those same one-sided derivatives. For fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations $h:[a,b]\\to V$ with $h(a)=h(b)=0$ and for $m,c>0$ and real $q$ consider\n\n$$S[X]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-\\eta(\\dot X,\\dot X)}\\,ds+q\\int_a^bA_\\mu(X)\\dot X^\\mu\\,ds .$$\n\nThen for each such $h$ the curve $X+\\varepsilon h$ remains future timelike for all sufficiently small $|\\varepsilon|$, and stationarity of $S$ under all such variations is equivalent to\n\n$$m\\frac{dU_\\nu}{d\\tau}=qF_{\\nu\\mu}U^\\mu,\\qquad\\frac{d\\tau}{ds}=\\frac{\\sqrt{-\\eta(\\dot X,\\dot X)}}c,\\qquad F=d\\mathcal A,$$\n\nwhere $\\tau(s)=\\tau_0+c^{-1}\\int_a^s\\sqrt{-\\eta(\\dot X,\\dot X)}\\,dr$ with arbitrary $\\tau_0\\in\\mathbb R$, and $U(s)=\\dot X(s)/\\tau\\prime(s)$ are the proper-time function and four-velocity ([[def-sr-proper-time]], [[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]); $dU/d\\tau=U\\prime/\\tau\\prime$ and $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]). This is a calculation about the chosen functional; adopting it as a physical particle action is an additional model assumption, and it makes no existence claim for coupled singular particles and fields. The coefficient-localization proof below is one-dimensional and choice-free; it does not invoke an AC-qualified Lebesgue fundamental lemma.",
          "strategy": "Fix an inertial coordinate frame. The continuous functions $-\\eta(\\dot X,\\dot X)$ and $\\dot X^0$ have strictly positive minima on the compact nondegenerate interval; $h,\\dot h$ are bounded there ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]]). Continuity of the quadratic form therefore keeps both margins positive for sufficiently small signed $\\varepsilon$. The compact image of $X$ also has a finite neighbourhood cover inside the given potential domain; by shrinking $|\\varepsilon|$ and using bounded $h$, every $X+\\varepsilon h$ remains in that domain. Thus each admitted perturbation remains a future timelike curve with the same endpoints. The square-root derivative is the positive-base power derivative [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], with its agreement with the positive rational square root supplied by [[thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent]]. The two integrands and their $\\varepsilon$ derivatives are continuous on one compact parameter rectangle, with the square-root argument bounded away from zero. Apply exactly the finite-interval Riemann theorem [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]]; no Lebesgue conversion or AC-qualified parameter theorem is used.\n\nThe product and chain rules give\n$$\\delta S=\\int_a^b mU_\\nu\\dot h^\\nu ds+q\\int_a^b[(\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu)(X)h^\\nu\\dot X^\\mu+A_\\nu(X)\\dot h^\\nu]ds.$$\nHere $\\tau'=\\sqrt{-\\eta(\\dot X,\\dot X)}/c>0$ and $U=\\dot X/\\tau'$ by [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. All factors in the integrations by parts are $C^1$ on the closed interval, with continuous integrable derivatives. [[thm-integration-by-parts]], or the product rule followed by [[thm-ftc-second-part]], gives zero endpoint terms because $h(a)=h(b)=0$. Hence\n$$\\delta S=\\int_a^b C_\\nu(s)h^\\nu(s)ds,\\qquad C_\\nu=-m\\frac{dU_\\nu}{ds}+qF_{\\nu\\mu}(X)\\dot X^\\mu.$$\nEach coefficient is continuous on $[a,b]$.\n\nFor completeness prove the exact coefficient implication without the AC-qualified field lemma. If $C_\\nu(s_0)\\ne0$ at an interior point, choose its sign $\\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}$ and a small open interval $J\\Subset(a,b)$ on which $\\sigma C_\\nu\\ge d>0$. Choose a nondegenerate closed subinterval $K\\Subset J$. The ZF cutoff [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]], used in dimension one, supplies $\\zeta\\in C_c^\\infty(J)$, $0\\le\\zeta\\le1$, equal to one near $K$. Set the single nonzero variation component to $h^\\nu=\\sigma\\zeta$. This is an admitted fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variation, and Riemann monotonicity/additivity ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]], [[thm-additivity-over-subintervals]]) gives $\\delta S=\\int\\sigma C_\\nu\\zeta\\ge d\\,\\mathrm{length}(K)>0$, contradicting stationarity. Thus all coefficients vanish on $(a,b)$ and, by their continuity, at both endpoints. Conversely their vanishing makes the first variation zero for every fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variation, not only the compact tests.\n\nDivide by the positive $\\tau'$ to obtain $m\\,dU_\\nu/d\\tau=qF_{\\nu\\mu}U^\\mu$. Raising the index with the constant metric gives the same vector equation. The relative endpoint and parameter conventions above retain the closed-interval branch. Existence of trajectories is not asserted. Only finite choices, finite-interval Riemann calculus and the explicitly ZF cutoff are used; no AC or Countable Choice is spent."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Weak Dirichlet problem with prescribed fractional Sobolev boundary data",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#B4",
          "design_state": "complete-local-branch-argument",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "lem-em-lift-specified-dirichlet-existence",
            "thm-bounded-right-inverse-for-the-sobolev-trace",
