          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-lift-specified-dirichlet-existence": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bounded-right-inverse-for-the-sobolev-trace": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-kernel-of-the-trace-is-w-one-p-zero": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sharp-trace-theorem-for-w-one-p": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice as declared by the trace and Sobolev suppliers. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^1$ domain, let $\\varepsilon$ be a measurable symmetric uniformly elliptic coefficient as in the lift lemma ([[lem-em-lift-specified-dirichlet-existence]]), let $\\rho\\in L^2(\\Omega)$, and prescribe $g\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)=W^{1/2,2}(\\partial\\Omega)$, the fractional Sobolev space of the boundary definition, defined by a finite graph atlas and partition with norm given by the finite sums of localised $L^2$ norms and Gagliardo seminorms ([[def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary]], [[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]). Then there is exactly one $u\\in H^1(\\Omega)$ with trace $Tu=g$ solving $$\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u=\\int_\\Omega\\rho\\,v\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega),$$ and the solution depends continuously on $(\\rho,g)$: $\\|u\\|_{H^1}\\le C(\\|\\rho\\|_{L^2}+\\|g\\|_{H^{1/2}})$. The prescribed trace is exact and no classical $C^2$ boundary regularity is inferred.",
          "strategy": "The sharp trace theorem and its bounded right inverse ([[thm-sharp-trace-theorem-for-w-one-p]], [[thm-bounded-right-inverse-for-the-sobolev-trace]]) give a bounded linear $R:H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)\\to H^1(\\Omega)$ with $TRg=g$; the flat half-space estimates are the normal-line and Hardy estimates exposed in the research argument. The kernel of the trace is $H_0^1(\\Omega)$ ([[thm-kernel-of-the-trace-is-w-one-p-zero]], with the zero-boundary Sobolev space defined as a closure), so $u=Rg+w$ with $w\\in H_0^1$; applying the lift lemma with $G=Rg$ ([[lem-em-lift-specified-dirichlet-existence]]) gives the unique $w$ and hence existence, exact trace and uniqueness. Continuous dependence follows from the estimates of the lift and solver lemmas together with the boundedness of $R$: for two data sets, $\\|\\nabla(w_1-w_2)\\|_2\\le\\alpha^{-1}[2L\\|\\rho_1-\\rho_2\\|_2+\\beta\\|\\nabla R(g_1-g_2)\\|_2]$, and the $H^1$ estimate follows by adding $\\|Rg\\|_{H^1}\\le C\\|g\\|_{H^{1/2}}$."
        },
