          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-mean-zero-coercivity-on-a-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-symmetric-coercive-variational-solver": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice for the actually cited Sobolev trace, lift and mean-zero density interfaces, and its Countable Choice consequence for the measure and variational interfaces ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded connected $C^1$ domain, let $\\varepsilon$ be measurable symmetric uniformly elliptic, let $\\rho\\in L^2(\\Omega)$, and let $g\\in H^{-1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$, the bounded real dual of the boundary trace space ([[def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary]]). Define $\\ell(v)=\\int_\\Omega\\rho v+\\langle g,Tv\\rangle$ on $H^1(\\Omega)$ ([[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]). A weak Neumann solution is a $u\\in H^1$ with $$\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u=\\ell(v)\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H^1(\\Omega).$$ Such a solution exists if and only if $\\ell(1)=0$, and is then unique up to an additive constant; normalising $\\int_\\Omega u=0$ makes it unique. On finitely many connected components the compatibility and normalisation are imposed separately on each component. For finitely many disjoint smooth stationary interfaces with piecewise smooth uniformly elliptic $\\varepsilon$, bulk data $\\rho\\in L^2$ and prescribed sheet charge $\\sigma$ on the interfaces defining a bounded trace functional on $H^1$ (for instance $\\sigma\\in L^2$ of each smooth compact interface), the same argument gives weak Dirichlet or compatible Neumann existence for the transmission problem, with the globally $H^1$ potential having identical one-sided trace; where the solution is classically smooth on each side, integration by parts gives the jump $n\\cdot[\\varepsilon\\nabla u]=-\\sigma$, equivalently $n\\cdot[D]=\\sigma$ for the explicitly defined mathematical flux $D:=-\\varepsilon\\nabla u$ on each side. General piecewise classical regularity remains a separate requirement.",
          "strategy": "Full AC is inherited from [[lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence]], [[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] and [[lem-em-mean-zero-coercivity-on-a-c-one-domain]]; the symmetric minimising-sequence solver itself needs only Countable Choice. No stronger choice principle is used. The trace bound of the prescribed-data lemma and Holder's inequality make $|\\ell(v)|\\le C\\|v\\|_{H^1}$ ([[lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence]], [[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]], [[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]]). Testing the equation with $v=1$ makes $\\ell(1)=0$ necessary. The mean-zero subspace of $H^1$ is closed, and the mean-zero coercivity lemma makes its gradient norm equivalent to the complete $H^1$ norm ([[lem-em-mean-zero-coercivity-on-a-c-one-domain]]); the symmetric coercive variational solver ([[lem-em-symmetric-coercive-variational-solver]]) solves the coercive symmetric form there. Every $v\\in H^1$ is its mean-zero part plus a constant, and the constant term contributes zero on both sides by compatibility, so this solves the full problem; for the difference of two solutions tested against itself the gradient vanishes, and the mean-zero inequality makes the difference constant. Each finite disjoint component is treated separately. The transmission case replaces $\\ell$ by the sum of the bulk, boundary and interface trace functionals of the prescribed sheet charge; the same variational argument applies, and the one-sided trace identity holds for the globally $H^1$ potential by trace continuity in each chart, giving the stated jump relation where the solution is classically smooth on each side. In the classical jump equivalence define $D:=-\\varepsilon\\nabla u$ piecewise; then $[D]=-[\\varepsilon\\nabla u]$, so $n\\cdot[\\varepsilon\\nabla u]=-\\sigma$ is exactly $n\\cdot[D]=\\sigma$. Here $D$ is a mathematical flux vector, not an undeclared physical displacement field."
        },
