          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, as used by the complete variational, Sobolev-extension and compact-spectral suppliers. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain. Define $H(\\operatorname{curl})=\\{u\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3):\\operatorname{curl}u\\in L^2\\}$ and $H(\\operatorname{div})=\\{u\\in L^2:\\operatorname{div}u\\in L^2\\}$ with their graph norms, and let $X$ be the graph-norm closure in $H(\\operatorname{curl})\\cap H(\\operatorname{div})$ of the smooth fields on the closure with $n\\times u=0$; this is an explicit weak perfect-electric-conductor boundary class. Put $V=\\{u\\in X:\\operatorname{div}u=0\\}$ and $H=\\overline{V}^{\\,L^2}$. Then the curl form on $V$ has a complete orthonormal $L^2$ eigenbasis with nonnegative eigenvalues $\\lambda_j$ of finite multiplicity tending to infinity whenever $H$ is infinite dimensional, and the zero eigenspace is finite dimensional and must be retained when the topology allows it. Each eigenfield $u_j\\in V$ satisfies the full graph-class variational identity $\\int_\\Omega\\operatorname{curl}u_j\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}v=\\lambda_j\\int_\\Omega u_j\\cdot v$ for every $v\\in X$. The eigen equation is $$\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}u_j=\\lambda_ju_j$$ distributionally with the PEC trace, and for every explicitly chosen mathematical speed $v_{\\mathrm{wave}}>0$ and every initial displacement $q_0\\in V$ and velocity $q_1\\in H$, the associated second-order wave has coefficients $a_j''+v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\lambda_ja_j=0$ with those initial coefficients. Its displacement lies in $C(\\mathbb R;V)$ and its velocity in $C(\\mathbb R;H)$; finite modal sums converge respectively in $C(K;V)$ and $C(K;H)$ on every compact time interval $K$. The finite-dimensional $\\lambda=0$ sector is retained with $a_j(t)=a_j(0)+ta_j'(0)$. This speed is a positive abstract mathematical parameter, not a physical constant or prerequisite. No identification with every maximal tangential-trace realisation, and no lossy, open or non-smooth-domain spectral claim, is made.",
          "strategy": "First use [[thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem]] on the compact smooth boundary, obtain a uniform positive radius via [[thm-euclidean-heine-borel-pseudocompactness-and-extreme-values]], derive the inward/outward signed-distance normal collar, constant-along-normal extension and bounded positive Jacobian. The chart formula of [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]] and [[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]] transports the normal-line FTC estimate to the quantitative trace inequality. Compactness and $H^1$ equivalence. For smooth $u$ with $n\\times u=0$, expanding $|\\operatorname{curl}u|^2$ and integrating once gives $\\int_\\Omega(|\\operatorname{curl}u|^2+|\\operatorname{div}u|^2-|\\nabla u|^2)=\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}(\\operatorname{div}n)|u\\cdot n|^2dS$ ([[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] and the divergence theorem on the collar). The curvature is bounded on the compact smooth boundary, and the collar normal-line identity $|u(y,0)|^2=|u(y,t)|^2-\\int_0^t\\partial_s|u|^2ds$ with $2ab\\le\\delta a^2+\\delta^{-1}b^2$ gives $\\|u\\|_{L^2(\\partial\\Omega)}^2\\le\\delta\\|\\nabla u\\|_2^2+C_\\delta\\|u\\|_2^2$; absorbing the curvature term yields $\\|u\\|_{H^1}\\le C(\\|u\\|_2+\\|\\operatorname{curl}u\\|_2+\\|\\operatorname{div}u\\|_2)$, so the completion $X$ is a closed $H^1$ subspace with zero tangential trace by trace continuity. The $H^1$ inclusion in $L^2$ is compact by the elementary cube-averaging proof using the bounded smooth-domain extension ([[thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains]]): for a bounded family of extensions the rectangular mean estimate gives uniform $\\|u-P_hu\\|_2\\le Ch\\|\\nabla u\\|_2$ with $P_h$ of finite-dimensional range, so a finite $\\varepsilon$-net exists in total bounded and complete $L^2$. The shifted form $a(u,v)=\\int[\\operatorname{curl}u\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}v+\\operatorname{div}u\\operatorname{div}v+u\\cdot v]$ is coercive and complete, so the symmetric solver ([[lem-em-symmetric-coercive-variational-solver]]) defines $K_0$; if $f$ is distributionally divergence-free and $q=\\operatorname{div}K_0f$, testing with $v=\\nabla\\varphi$ gives $\\int q(\\Delta\\varphi-\\varphi)=0$ for all smooth zero-trace $\\varphi$, and the local Dirichlet resolvent and smooth-zero-boundary test-density lemma ([[lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density]]) gives the unique $\\varphi\\in H^2\\cap H_0^1$ solving $(1-\\Delta)\\varphi=q$. Its smooth zero-boundary approximants $\\varphi_j$ converge in $H^2$, so $\\nabla\\varphi_j$ are smooth PEC fields converging to $\\nabla\\varphi$ in the curl/div graph norm, proving this gradient belongs to the chosen completion $X$. The identity for smooth $\\varphi_j$ passes by $L^2$ continuity to $\\int q(\\Delta\\varphi-\\varphi)=-\\|q\\|_2^2=0$; hence $K=K_0|_H$ maps $H$ into $V$ and is bounded $H\\to V$ and compact $H\\to H$ ([[def-compact-linear-operator]]). Testing its defining equations with $Kg,Kf$ proves self-adjointness, and $(f,Kf)=\\|\\operatorname{curl}Kf\\|^2+\\|Kf\\|^2>0$ for $f\\ne0$ proves trivial kernel. The compact self-adjoint spectral theorem ([[thm-spectral-theorem-for-compact-self-adjoint-operators]]) now gives an orthonormal eigenbasis of $H$ with positive eigenvalues $\\kappa_j\\to0$ of finite multiplicity; substituting $Ku_j=\\kappa_ju_j$ into the full $X$ identity gives $\\int\\operatorname{curl}u_j\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}v=(\\kappa_j^{-1}-1)\\int u_j\\cdot v$ for every $v\\in X$, and testing with compactly supported $v$ gives $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}u_j=\\lambda_ju_j$ distributionally with $\\lambda_j=\\kappa_j^{-1}-1\\ge0$, the zero eigenspace being finite dimensional by the spectral theorem. The wave branch is proved for each fixed $v_{\\mathrm{wave}}>0$. Equip $V$ with $\\|u\\|_V^2=\\|u\\|_2^2+\\|\\operatorname{curl}u\\|_2^2$ (the divergence is zero). It is complete because $V$ is a closed subspace of the graph completion. The full-$X$ eigenidentity gives $(u,u_j)_V=(1+\\lambda_j)(u,u_j)_H$, so $u_j/\\sqrt{1+\\lambda_j}$ is an orthonormal family in $V$. Its orthogonal complement is zero: orthogonality in $V$ forces every $H$ coefficient of $u$ to vanish. [[thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace]] therefore gives completeness in $V$, and [[thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion]] yields $\\|u\\|_V^2=\\sum_j(1+\\lambda_j)|(u,u_j)_H|^2$. Write $\\alpha_j=(q_0,u_j)_H$ and $\\gamma_j=(q_1,u_j)_H$. For $\\lambda_j>0$, set $a_j(t)=\\alpha_j\\cos(v_{\\mathrm{wave}}\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}t)+\\gamma_j\\sin(v_{\\mathrm{wave}}\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}t)/(v_{\\mathrm{wave}}\\sqrt{\\lambda_j})$; for $\\lambda_j=0$, set $a_j(t)=\\alpha_j+t\\gamma_j$. The square roots are those of [[thm-of-square-roots]], and direct differentiation using [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] and [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] verifies each ODE and its initial values. The positive-mode energy $|a_j'(t)|^2+v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\lambda_j|a_j(t)|^2=|\\gamma_j|^2+v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\lambda_j|\\alpha_j|^2$ has zero derivative by the ODE and is constant by [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]. The positive eigenvalues are bounded below away from zero, since only finitely many eigenvalues lie in a bounded interval and the zero sector is finite dimensional. Thus this tail energy controls both the $V$ displacement tail and the $H$ velocity tail uniformly in time. The zero sector is finite and has the explicit affine solution. Initial $V\\times H$ summability makes all tails tend to zero; finite sums therefore converge in $C(K;V)$ for displacement and $C(K;H)$ for velocity on every compact $K$, and the series has the given initial data. The uniform limits of displacement and velocity give the actual $H$ derivative: pass the finite-sum FTC estimate ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]) $\\|q_N(t+h)-q_N(t)-hq_N'(t)\\|_H\\le |h|\\sup_{s\\text{ between }t,t+h}\\|q_N'(s)-q_N'(t)\\|_H$ to the limit and use continuity of the limiting velocity. The curl form is bounded on $V\\times V$; uniform convergence on compact times of displacement in $V$ and velocity in $H$ passes the finite equations against a compactly supported smooth time test and a vector in $V$, first for finite eigenfield sums and then by density. Thus the second-order equation holds in the variational, distribution-in-time sense; uniqueness follows from uniqueness of each scalar ODE and completeness of the $H$ eigenbasis. No positive-mode denominator is used for a zero eigenvalue. Positivity of $v_{\\mathrm{wave}}$ is essential for the stated arbitrary $V\\times H$ data interface: at speed zero an arbitrary $H$ velocity need not give a $V$ displacement. The spectral, full-$X$ variational, topology and boundary-class interfaces proved above are unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-pec-cavity-eigenfield-boundary-regularity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Smooth boundary regularity of PEC cavity eigenfields in the declared graph completion",
          "reference": "physics-em-sr-40-2026-10-08-pec-regularity-round1.evidence.json#mixed-vector-estimate",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure",
            "lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density",
            "thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain",
            "thm-wkp-extension-from-a-half-space",
            "lem-cutoff-difference-quotient-commutator-estimate",
            "thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains",
            "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding",
            "lem-em-parameter-preserving-smooth-extension-on-a-smooth-domain",
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
            "thm-dominated-convergence",
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-wkp-extension-from-a-half-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cutoff-difference-quotient-commutator-estimate": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-parameter-preserving-smooth-extension-on-a-smooth-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain, and let $X$ be exactly the curl/div graph-norm completion of smooth-on-the-closure fields with $\\nu\\times u=0$ defined in [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]]. If $u\\in X$, $\\operatorname{div}u=0$ weakly, and $\\lambda\\ge0$ satisfies $\\int_\\Omega\\operatorname{curl}u\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}v=\\lambda\\int_\\Omega u\\cdot v$ for every $v\\in X$, then $u\\in H^m(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ for every integer $m\\ge1$ and has a $C^\\infty$ representative on the closure (indeed a smooth ambient extension to a neighbourhood of the closure). For each $m$, $\\|u\\|_{H^m}\\le C_{m,\\Omega,\\lambda}\\|u\\|_{H^1}$. The equations $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}u=\\lambda u$, $\\operatorname{div}u=0$, $\\nu\\times u=0$ and $\\partial_\\nu u_n+(\\operatorname{div}\\nu)u_n=0$ hold classically, where $u_n=u\\cdot\\nu$ and the normal is extended constant along collar normal lines. This includes zero eigenvalues and disconnected domains; it asserts neither equality with every maximal tangential-trace realization nor smoothness of arbitrary finite-energy modal sums.",
          "strategy": "1. Bilinear boundary identity in the chosen completion. Write $\\nu$ for the outward unit normal, extend it smoothly as a unit field in a collar, put $\\kappa=\\operatorname{div}\\nu$ on the boundary, and write $u_n=u\\cdot\\nu$. Polarizing the identity proved in [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]] gives, for smooth PEC $u,v$,\n$$\\int_\\Omega(\\operatorname{curl}u\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}v+\\operatorname{div}u\\operatorname{div}v)=\\int_\\Omega\\nabla u:\\nabla v+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\\kappa u_n v_n.$$\nFor clarity the integration-by-parts boundary term before imposing the traces is $\\sum_{i,j}\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}(\\nu_j u_j\\partial_i v_i-\\nu_j u_i\\partial_i v_j)$; at the boundary $u=u_n\\nu$, $v=v_n\\nu$, the tangential derivatives of $v$ and $\\nu\\cdot\\partial_i\\nu=0$ reduce it to $\\kappa u_n v_n$. Thus the sign is positive for an outward convex sphere ($\\kappa=2/R$); no convexity is assumed. The $H^1$ norm equivalence of that supplier and the bounded trace [[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] extend this identity from smooth PEC approximants to every pair in the actual graph completion $X$. Since $\\operatorname{div}u=0$, the assumed full-$X$ eigenidentity therefore gives\n$$\\int_\\Omega\\nabla u:\\nabla v+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\\kappa u_n v_n=\\lambda\\int_\\Omega u\\cdot v\\qquad(v\\in X).$$\nThis is the weak mixed boundary identity; no classical normal derivative has yet been assumed.\n\n2. Flattening and actual admissibility. Shrink smooth boundary charts before taking a finite cover of the compact boundary, with nested half-boxes and cutoffs away from artificial sides. In each chart choose a smooth orthonormal frame $Q=(\\tau_1,\\tau_2,\\nu)$ on the boundary, by normalizing a graph tangent and putting $\\tau_2=\\nu\\times\\tau_1$, and extend it smoothly into the collar. In flattened coordinates $(y_1,y_2,s)$ with $s>0$ inside, write $u=QU$. The $H^1$ trace has $TU_1=TU_2=0$; $TU_3$ is free. Every localized $H^1$ triple $V$ with these two zero traces and compact support away from the artificial sides pulls back to an element of the original $X$: extend $V_1,V_2$ oddly and $V_3$ evenly across $s=0$. These are $H^1$ extensions. Indeed integrating against a smooth test on both half-spaces cancels the interface term for equal one-sided traces; the odd extensions have matching traces because their traces are zero, and the even extension has matching traces automatically. This is the first-order gluing calculation in [[lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density]] and the half-space trace integration argument of [[thm-wkp-extension-from-a-half-space]]. Mollify with a compact kernel even in $s$; convolution preserves these parities, converges in $H^1$, and makes the first two traces exactly zero. Multiply by an even smooth cutoff equal to one on the localized support and pull back through the smooth frame and chart. Extend by zero outside the chart. The resulting fields are smooth on the closure, have $\\nu\\times v_j=0$ exactly, and converge to the localized field in $H^1$, hence in the curl/div graph norm. This explicitly supplies the smooth PEC approximants required by the definition of $X$. Interior compactly supported tests are obtained by ordinary mollification. This local construction is used below; no equality with an unspecified maximal tangential-trace realization is asserted.\n\n3. The local coupled weak system. Expanding $\\nabla(QU)$ in the identity of step 1 and transporting the volume measure gives a real symmetric smooth principal matrix $a^{ij}$, uniformly positive on the smaller half-box, that is scalar in the three component indices. The frame derivative terms have smooth matrix coefficients and at most one derivative of $U$. Thus the local equation for all admissible triples $V$ is\n$$\\sum_m\\int a^{ij}\\partial_jU_m\\partial_iV_m+\\int_B\\beta U_3V_3=\\int f\\cdot V+\\sum_i\\int G_i\\cdot\\partial_iV,$$\nwhere $B=\\{s=0\\}$, $\\beta=\\kappa J_B$ is smooth (possibly signed), $f=\\lambda JU+\\sum_i C_i\\partial_iU+C_0U\\in L^2$, and $G_i=B_iU\\in H^1$ for smooth matrix coefficients $C_i,C_0,B_i$; $J$ and $J_B$ are the positive volume and surface densities. This follows directly by expanding the finite products: the only two differentiated factors produce the scalar $a^{ij}$ because $Q^TQ=I$; terms with a derivative of $Q$ on the test factor enter $G$, and those with a derivative of $Q$ on the solution factor enter $f$. Multipliers obey [[lem-cutoff-difference-quotient-commutator-estimate]]. More generally allow a boundary datum $\\int_B hV_3$ with $h\\in H^1(B)$ for the induction below. Localization $Z=\\eta U$ has the same principal and Robin terms and exactly $f_Z=\\eta f+\\sum_i G_i\\partial_i\\eta-\\sum_{i,j}a^{ij}\\partial_jU\\partial_i\\eta$, $G_{Z,i}=\\eta G_i+\\sum_j a^{ij}U\\partial_j\\eta$, and $h_Z=\\eta h$. In particular the divergence-type cutoff/frame terms are retained rather than silently assigning zero conormal data.\n\n4. Tangential mixed $H^2$ estimate. Let $k=1,2$, set $W=\\delta_h^k U=(U(y+he_k,s)-U(y,s))/h$, and test with $V=-\\delta_{-h}^k(\\eta^2W)$ on a nested patch. For each fixed small $h$, its first two traces vanish; step 2 makes its pullback an admissible element of $X$ by actual smooth PEC approximation. Discrete integration by parts and the shifted product rule give the principal term $\\sum_m\\int\\eta^2 a^{ij}(x+he_k)\\partial_jW_m\\partial_iW_m\\ge\\alpha\\|\\eta\\nabla W\\|_2^2$. The other principal terms involve $\\delta_h a$, $\\nabla\\eta$, $\\nabla U$ and $W$ and are bounded by $\\epsilon\\|\\eta\\nabla W\\|_2^2+C_\\epsilon\\|U\\|_{H^1}^2$ on the larger patch. The elementary line formula $\\delta_h U=\\int_0^1\\partial_kU(x+the_k)dt$, valid on a.e. ACL line, and Cauchy--Schwarz in $t$ followed by Fubini give $\\|W\\|_2\\le C\\|\\partial_kU\\|_2$ on the enlarged patch; it also bounds $\\|\\delta_{-h}(\\eta^2W)\\|_2$ by $C(\\|\\eta\\nabla W\\|_2+\\|W\\|_2)$. Hence the $f$ term is bounded without differentiating $f$. Transfer the quotient in the $G$ pairing to $G$; $\\|\\delta_hG\\|_2\\le C\\|G\\|_{H^1}$ gives the same bound. On $B$ the exact quotient of the Robin product gives $\\int_B\\beta(y+h)\\eta^2|W_3|^2+\\int_B(\\delta_h\\beta)U_3\\eta^2W_3$. The normal-line proof in G3 gives the localized trace estimate $\\|Tz\\|_2^2\\le\\epsilon\\|\\nabla z\\|_2^2+C_\\epsilon\\|z\\|_2^2$. It absorbs the first term even when $\\beta$ has either sign; bounded $\\delta_h\\beta$, $\\|TU_3\\|_2\\le C\\|U\\|_{H^1}$ and Young bound the second in the same way. The extra $h$ pairing transfers to $\\delta_h h$ and is bounded by $\\|h\\|_{H^1(B)}\\|T(\\eta^2W_3)\\|_2$, again absorbed by the trace estimate and Young. Choosing the finitely many epsilons small relative to $\\alpha$ yields\n$$\\|\\eta\\nabla\\delta_h^kU\\|_2\\le C(\\|U\\|_{H^1}+\\|f\\|_2+\\|G\\|_{H^1}+\\|h\\|_{H^1(B)}),$$\nuniformly for small $h$. These estimates apply to all three components and explicitly bound the coupled lower-order and Robin terms; a scalar Dirichlet theorem has not been substituted for this system.\n\n5. Recover normal derivatives. From the last bound each distributional tangential derivative of $\\partial_iU_m$ is $L^2$: for a smooth test $\\psi$ its pairing is the limit of $\\int\\delta_h^k(\\partial_iU_m)\\psi$, a bounded $L^2$ functional, represented by an $L^2$ class (Hilbert Riesz, available through the variational supplier in G3). Interior quotient tests give the same conclusion away from the boundary. The interior equation is $-\\partial_i(a^{ij}\\partial_jU_m)=f_m-\\partial_iG_{i,m}$. Therefore\n$$a^{33}\\partial_{33}U_m=\\partial_iG_{i,m}-f_m-\\sum_{(i,j)\\ne(3,3)}a^{ij}\\partial_{ij}U_m-\\sum_{i,j}(\\partial_i a^{ij})\\partial_jU_m.$$\nEvery term on the right is $L^2$, and $a^{33}\\ge\\alpha>0$, so all three normal second derivatives are $L^2$. This proves local $H^2$ with a bound on a smaller patch. It does not require a pre-existing classical Robin derivative.\n\n6. Full bootstrap with boundary commutators. Suppose $U\\in H^{r+1}$ on the larger patch, $r\\ge1$. Its actual lower-order data satisfy $f\\in H^r$ and $G\\in H^{r+1}$, since the equation's eigenvalue term is $\\lambda JU$ and all coefficients are smooth; no initially smooth right-hand side is presumed. For a tangential multi-index $\\alpha$ of order $r$, put $Z=D_y^\\alpha U\\in H^1$. Its first two traces are still zero. To justify trace commutation, extend any localized $H^{r+1}$ class by the integer-order half-space extension [[thm-wkp-extension-from-a-half-space]], mollify in that norm, and use the continuous $H^1$ trace for every derivative of order at most $r$; the smooth tangential trace identity passes to the limit. Apply tangential integration by parts to the weak system of step 3. Its principal term remains $a^{ij}\\partial_j Z_m\\partial_iV_m$, its boundary principal term remains $\\beta Z_3V_3$, its bulk datum is $D_y^\\alpha f\\in L^2$, and its divergence-type datum is\n$$\\widetilde G_i=D_y^\\alpha G_i-\\sum_{0<\\gamma\\le\\alpha}{\\alpha\\choose\\gamma}(D_y^\\gamma a^{ij})\\partial_jD_y^{\\alpha-\\gamma}U\\in H^1.$$\nThe boundary commutator is $\\widetilde h=-\\sum_{0<\\gamma\\le\\alpha}{\\alpha\\choose\\gamma}(D_y^\\gamma\\beta)T D_y^{\\alpha-\\gamma}U_3\\in H^1(B)$. Indeed $|\\alpha-\\gamma|\\le r-1$, so the indicated derivative of $U$ is $H^2$; its trace and each tangential derivative trace are $L^2$ by the $H^1$ trace and its just-proved commutation. This proves the claimed $H^1(B)$ bound without assuming a stronger trace theorem. Step 2 again makes localized tests for $Z$ admissible by smooth PEC approximation; differentiation of the weak identity is justified first by difference quotients and then distributional limits. Steps 4--5 give $Z\\in H^2$, controlling derivatives of total order $r+2$ with at most two normal derivatives. For the remaining derivatives, differentiate the normal-recovery equation of step 5 and induct on the number of normal derivatives at this fixed total order. The non-$(3,3)$ principal terms have strictly fewer normal derivatives; all differentiated coefficient terms use derivatives of $U$ of order at most $r+1$, and differentiated $f$ and $\\operatorname{div}G$ have that same available order. Dividing by positive $a^{33}$ recovers the next normal derivative. Thus $U\\in H^{r+2}$. Use nested patches for each finite order and the finite boundary/interior cover to obtain $u\\in H^m(\\Omega)$ for every integer $m$, with $\\|u\\|_{H^m}\\le C_{m,\\Omega,\\lambda}\\|u\\|_{H^1}$; no estimate uniform in $\\lambda$ is asserted.\n\n7. Classical conclusion. For each $j$ choose $m>j+3/2$ and apply [[thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains]] and [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]] componentwise. They yield continuous derivatives through order $j$ on the closure, compatible for different $j$ by weak derivative uniqueness and continuity on the interior. All jets therefore have continuous one-sided boundary limits. The fixed smooth extension [[lem-em-parameter-preserving-smooth-extension-on-a-smooth-domain]] applied to the constant-in-time field gives one actual smooth ambient representative near the entire closure, rather than unrelated finite-order extensions. The interior variational equation gives $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}u=\\lambda u$ pointwise, and the tangential trace is classically zero. Now integration by parts in step 1 with arbitrary smooth compactly supported normal boundary traces, admissible by step 2, gives $\\partial_\\nu u_n+\\kappa u_n=0$ pointwise. The unit-normal collar extension ensures $\\partial_\\nu u_n=(\\partial_\\nu u)\\cdot\\nu$ when the normal is extended constant along its normal lines. This agrees with the classical identity $\\operatorname{div}u=\\partial_\\nu u_n+\\kappa u_n$ for zero tangential trace. The argument covers $\\lambda=0$, disconnected smooth domains, and every topology allowed by G3; it makes no smoothness assertion for arbitrary finite-energy modal sums. Full AC is carried from G3, the trace, extension, Hilbert and Sobolev suppliers. Hunter's bounded scalar Dirichlet excerpt supports the difference-quotient method only; the vector coupling, actual graph-class tests and Robin bootstrap above are proved locally here.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis): scalar boundary difference-quotient method; the vector Robin extension is proved inline here",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/pde_notes.pdf",
                "locator": "Printed pp.114–117 (PDF pp.120–123), complete bounded excerpt of Theorems4.30–4.32; scalar Dirichlet only, not an applicable vector PEC theorem."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
