          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), declared by the surface-integral and parameter-integral conventions below. Let $t_0<T$ and let $h\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T])$ in the extension sense: $h$ is the restriction of a $C^\\infty$ function on $\\mathbb R^3\\times J$ for some open interval $J\\supset[t_0,T]$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Let $d\\Omega$ be the surface measure on the unit sphere $S^2\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ of total mass $\\int_{S^2}d\\Omega=4\\pi$ ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), and define the spherical-mean family $$M(x,s,v):=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}h(x+sn,v)\\,d\\Omega(n),\\qquad (x,s,v)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\times[t_0,T].$$ Then $M$ is of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\times[t_0,T]$ in the same extension sense, and for every multi-index $\\alpha$ in $x$ and all integers $m,l\\ge0$ $$D_x^\\alpha\\partial_s^m\\partial_v^lM(x,s,v)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}D_x^\\alpha\\partial_s^m\\partial_v^l\\bigl[h(x+sn,v)\\bigr]\\,d\\Omega(n),$$ where the right-hand integrand is the corresponding iterated partial derivative of the composed map $(x,s,v)\\mapsto h(x+sn,v)$; in particular $\\partial_s[h(x+sn,v)]=\\nabla_x h(x+sn,v)\\cdot n$, where $\\nabla_xh(y,v):=(\\partial_{y_0}h(y,v),\\partial_{y_1}h(y,v),\\partial_{y_2}h(y,v))\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is the spatial gradient at fixed $v$, so the $s$-derivatives are directional derivatives along $n$. Consequently, for every $c>0$ the map $\\tilde H(x,t,v):=(t-v)\\,M(x,c(t-v),v)$ is $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]\\times[t_0,T]$ in the extension sense, and for every fixed $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ the map $(t,v)\\mapsto\\tilde H(x,t,v)$ is $C^\\infty$ on an open neighbourhood of the closed triangle $\\{(t,v):t_0\\le v\\le t\\le T\\}$. Nothing here assumes decay, compact support or boundedness of $h$; the statement is local in $(x,s,v)$ and uses uniform bounds only on compact parameter sets.",
          "strategy": "Fix a point $(x_0,s_0,v_0)$ and a compact parameter box $K_x\\times S\\times V$ around it. The composed integrand $(x,s,v,n)\\mapsto h(x+sn,v)$ is $C^\\infty$ jointly on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\times[t_0,T]\\times S^2$ by the chain rule applied to the affine map $(x,s,v,n)\\mapsto x+sn$ and the projection $(x,v)\\mapsto(x,v)$, its partial derivatives being the corresponding iterated partial derivatives of $h$ evaluated at $(x+sn,v)$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Prove by induction on the total order $N=|\\alpha|+m+l$ that all partial derivatives of $M$ through order $N$ exist and are continuous on $K_x\\times S\\times V$ and that the displayed differentiated-integral formula holds at that order: the surface clause of the parameter-integral lemma ([[lem-em-parameter-integrals]], used with the fixed compact $C^1$ hypersurface $S^2$ and with each of the parameter directions $x_j$, $s$, $v$ in turn) states that the sphere integral is continuously differentiable in the chosen parameter with derivative equal to the sphere integral of the corresponding partial derivative of the composed integrand, because $h$ and all of its partial derivatives are continuous on the compact set $S^2\\times K_x\\times S\\times V$; since the differentiated integrand is again $C^\\infty$ jointly, the procedure can be iterated through all parameter directions in any order, which gives existence and continuity of every partial derivative through order $N$, together with the displayed formula at that order (the $s$-derivatives of $h(x+sn,v)$ are the corresponding directional derivatives along $n$, by the chain rule). As $N$ is arbitrary, $M$ is $C^\\infty$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); the dependence on the parameters is local, so no decay or support condition on $h$ is used. The final claims follow by composing with the smooth affine map $(t,v)\\mapsto(t-v,c(t-v),v)$ and multiplying by the smooth factor $t-v$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); the Axiom of Countable Choice is carried from the sphere-integral convention under which the integrals are read ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[def-countable-choice]]).",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
