            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), used through the cited published suppliers ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $c>0$, $t_0\\in\\mathbb R$, $T>t_0$, let $a,b\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ and let $h\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T])$ in the extension sense that $h$ is the restriction of a $C^\\infty$ function on $\\mathbb R^3\\times J$ for some open interval $J\\supset[t_0,T]$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Write $W:=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$, so that $c^2W=\\Box_c$ is the wave operator of speed $c$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed]]). For $\\varphi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ and $s\\ge0$ let $H_\\varphi(x,s):=s\\,M_{cs}\\varphi(x)$, where $M_r\\varphi(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}\\varphi(x+rn)\\,d\\Omega(n)$ is the spherical mean of [[lem-em-spherical-mean-wave-identity]]. Define, with $s=t-t_0$, $$u(x,t):=\\partial_sH_a(x,s)+H_b(x,s)+c^2\\int_{t_0}^{t}H_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)\\,dv,\\qquad (x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T],$$ where $h(\\cdot,v)$ denotes the map $x\\mapsto h(x,v)$ and $\\partial_sH_a$ is the partial derivative of $H_a$ in its second argument, evaluated at $s=t-t_0$. Then:\n\n(a) $u$ is of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$ in the extension sense, $Wu=h$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$ (indeed on the open neighbourhood of the closed time interval on which the extension of $h$ is defined), $u(\\cdot,t_0)=a$ and $\\partial_tu(\\cdot,t_0)=b$.\n\n(b) Uniqueness: every $v\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T])$ in the extension sense with $Wv=h$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$, $v(\\cdot,t_0)=a$ and $\\partial_tv(\\cdot,t_0)=b$ equals $u$.\n\n(c) Domain of dependence: let $\\tilde a,\\tilde b\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$, $\\tilde h\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T])$ and let $\\tilde u$ be the corresponding solution. If $\\tilde a=a$ and $\\tilde b=b$ on the closed ball $\\overline B_{c(t_*-t_0)}(x_*)$ and $\\tilde h=h$ on the closed backward cone $K(x_*,t_*):=\\{(y,v)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,t_*]:|y-x_*|\\le c(t_*-v)\\}$ ([[def-forward-and-backward-wave-cones-domain-of-dependence-and-influence]]), then $\\tilde u(x_*,t_*)=u(x_*,t_*)$ for every $x_*\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $t_*\\in[t_0,T]$.\n\n(d) Compact support: if $a$ vanishes off a compact $K_a\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, $b$ vanishes off a compact $K_b$, and $h(\\cdot,v)$ vanishes off a fixed compact $K_h$ for every $v\\in[t_0,T]$, then for every $t\\in[t_0,T]$ the function $u(\\cdot,t)$ has compact spatial support: if $K_a\\cup K_b\\cup K_h\\subseteq\\overline B_\\rho(0)$ for some $\\rho>0$ (which exists, [[thm-heine-borel-rn]]), then $u(x,t)=0$ whenever $|x|>\\rho+c(t-t_0)$.\n\nNo boundary condition on a finite spatial domain, no finite-total-energy hypothesis, and no decay of $a,b,h$ at infinity is assumed or asserted.",
          "strategy": "Time translation $t\\mapsto t-t_0$ (chain rule) reduces the proof to $t_0=0$; the operator and the formula are invariant under it. Decompose $u=u_h+u_s$ with $u_h:=\\partial_sH_a(x,s)+H_b(x,s)$ and $u_s:=c^2\\int_{t_0}^{t}H_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)\\,dv$, $s=t-t_0$.\n\n(1) Smoothness. By [[lem-em-spherical-mean-family-smoothness]] the map $(x,s,v)\\mapsto M(x,s,v)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}h(x+sn,v)\\,d\\Omega(n)$ is $C^\\infty$ jointly, hence so is $(x,t,v)\\mapsto H_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)=(t-v)M(x,c(t-v),v)$ on the region $t_0\\le v\\le t\\le T$, and for fixed $x$ it is $C^\\infty$ near the closed triangle $t_0\\le v\\le t\\le T$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). For the same reason $u_h\\in C^\\infty$, and for fixed $x$ the integrand $v\\mapsto H_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)$ is $C^\\infty$ on $[t_0,t]$; the fixed-interval clause of [[lem-em-parameter-integrals]] differentiates $u_s$ in $x$ under the integral sign, and [[lem-derivative-of-an-integral-with-moving-endpoints]] differentiates it in $t$ with the endpoint terms. Iterating these two steps (each differentiated integrand is again $C^\\infty$ jointly) gives $u_s\\in C^\\infty$ and $u\\in C^\\infty$ in the extension sense.\n\n(2) Equation and initial data. [[lem-em-spherical-mean-wave-identity]] gives, for every $\\varphi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$, $\\partial_t^2H_\\varphi=c^2\\Delta H_\\varphi$ for $t\\ge0$, $H_\\varphi(\\cdot,0)=0$ and $\\partial_tH_\\varphi(\\cdot,0)=\\varphi$. Hence $\\partial_t^2(\\partial_sH_a)=\\partial_s(\\partial_t^2H_a)=c^2\\Delta\\partial_sH_a$ and $\\partial_t^2H_b=c^2\\Delta H_b$, so $\\partial_t^2u_h=c^2\\Delta u_h$; here $\\partial_s$ commutes with $\\Delta$ on $C^\\infty$ functions ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]) and $\\Delta$ is linear ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]). Also $u_h(\\cdot,t_0)=a$, $\\partial_tu_h(\\cdot,t_0)=b$ because $\\partial_s^2H_a(\\cdot,0)=c^2\\Delta H_a(\\cdot,0)=0$. For $u_s$, two applications of [[lem-derivative-of-an-integral-with-moving-endpoints]] at fixed $x$ give $\\partial_tu_s=c^2\\int_{t_0}^t\\partial_tH_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)\\,dv$ (the endpoint term $H_{h(\\cdot,t)}(x,0)$ vanishes) and $\\partial_t^2u_s=c^2\\bigl[\\partial_tH_{h(\\cdot,t)}(x,0)+\\int_{t_0}^t\\partial_t^2H_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)\\,dv\\bigr]=c^2h(x,t)+c^2\\int_{t_0}^tc^2\\Delta_xH_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)\\,dv$; differentiating in $x$ under the integral sign with [[lem-em-parameter-integrals]] gives $\\Delta u_s=c^2\\int_{t_0}^t\\Delta_xH_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)\\,dv$, so $\\partial_t^2u_s=c^2h+c^2\\Delta u_s$, i.e. $Wu_s=h$. Finally $u_s(\\cdot,t_0)=0$ and $\\partial_tu_s(\\cdot,t_0)=0$ because $H_\\varphi(\\cdot,0)=0$. Adding the two parts gives (a); note the normalisation $c^2W=\\Box_c$ is what places the factor $c^2$ on the source integral.\n\n(3) Uniqueness and domain of dependence. If $v$ is a second $C^2$ solution with the same data, then $w:=u-v$ solves $\\Box_cw=0$ on a neighbourhood of $\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$ and has $w(\\cdot,t_0)=\\partial_tw(\\cdot,t_0)=0$. Fix $(x_*,t_*)$ with $t_*>t_0$ and shift time; on the closed backward cone of [[def-forward-and-backward-wave-cones-domain-of-dependence-and-influence]] the functions $w$ and the zero solution satisfy the hypotheses of [[thm-domain-of-dependence-and-local-uniqueness]] (zeros agree, and the sources $0$ agree), so $w\\equiv0$ on that cone and in particular $w(x_*,t_*)=0$; as $(x_*,t_*)$ is arbitrary, $w=0$. Claim (c) is the same argument applied to $u-\\tilde u$, whose data agree on the base ball $\\overline B_{c(t_*-t_0)}(x_*)$ and whose source vanishes on the cone.\n\n(4) Compact support. In the displayed formula for $u(x,t)$ each term integrates over spheres of radius $cs$ or $c(t-v)$, $0\\le t-v\\le t-t_0$, centred at $x$, the integrands being $a,\\nabla a,b$ or $h(\\cdot,v)$; if $\\overline B_{c(t-t_0)}(x)$ does not meet $K:=K_a\\cup K_b\\cup K_h$, all these integrals vanish and $u(x,t)=0$. If $K\\subseteq\\overline B_\\rho(0)$ and $|x|>\\rho+c(t-t_0)$, then every $y\\in\\overline B_{c(t-t_0)}(x)$ has $|y|\\ge|x|-c(t-t_0)>\\rho$, so $\\overline B_{c(t-t_0)}(x)\\cap K=\\emptyset$ and $u(x,t)=0$; the existence of $\\rho$ is the boundedness of the compact set $K$ ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]), which also gives that $\\overline B_{\\rho+c(t-t_0)}(0)$ is compact. This proves (d) and completes the proof.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Partial Differential Equations: From Classical to Modern (2025 archived author manuscript, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250324094647id_/https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-pde/pde.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.1, printed pp. 168-170, Theorem 7.2 and Corollary 7.3 (Kirchhoff formula and the Duhamel principle, proof of the source term read in full); §7.3, printed pp. 176-178, Theorem 7.12 and Corollary 7.13 (local energy, cone of dependence and uniqueness, proof read in full)"
              },
              {
                "title": "Victor Ivrii, Partial Differential Equations (University of Toronto, 2018, CC BY-SA)",
                "url": "https://www.math.toronto.edu/courses/apm346h1/20181/PDE-textbook/PDE-textbook.pdf",
