          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] and its suppliers ([[def-countable-choice]]). All three computations use the notation of that theorem: $c>0$, $H_\\varphi(x,s)=s\\,M_{cs}\\varphi(x)$ with the spherical mean $M$ of [[lem-em-spherical-mean-wave-identity]], $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ and $u$ the solution displayed there.\n\n(i) (The compact-support clause needs its hypothesis.) Take $t_0=0$, $a=b=0$ and the spatially constant source $h(x,t)=1$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times[0,T]$. Then the theorem's solution is $u(x,t)=c^2t^2/2$, which is independent of $x$; it satisfies $\\partial_t^2u=c^2=c^2h$ and $\\Delta u=0$, hence $Wu=h$, with $u(\\cdot,0)=\\partial_tu(\\cdot,0)=0$. For every $t>0$ the function $u(\\cdot,t)=c^2t^2/2$ is a nonzero constant on $\\mathbb R^3$, so it is not compactly supported: the hypothesis in clause (d) that $h(\\cdot,v)$ vanish off one fixed compact set cannot be dropped, and the corresponding hypothesis of [[thm-wave-duhamel-principle]] (source compactly supported in $x$ for each fixed $s$) is genuinely used by that item.\n\n(ii) (Support propagation made explicit.) Take $a=b=0$, $t_0=0$ and $h(x,t)=h_0(x)$ with $h_0\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ vanishing off $\\overline B_1(0)$. Then $u(x,t)=c^2\\int_0^t(t-v)M_{c(t-v)}h_0(x)\\,dv$, and $M_rh_0(x)=0$ for every $r>0$ with $S_r(x)\\cap\\overline B_1(0)=\\emptyset$; hence $u(x,t)=0$ whenever $|x|>1+ct$. This is clause (d) with $K_h=\\overline B_1(0)$ and $\\rho=1$, exhibited from the explicit formula.\n\n(iii) (The class contains non-decaying data with divergent total energy.) Take $a=0$, $b(x)=x_1$ and $h=0$. Since the first moment of the unit-sphere measure vanishes, $M_{cs}b(x)=x_1$ ([[lem-first-moment-of-the-unit-sphere-vanishes]], with the surface measure identified with the polar measure of [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]), so the theorem's solution is $u(x,t)=(t-t_0)x_1$: it satisfies $\\partial_t^2u=0=\\Delta u$, hence $Wu=0$, with $u(\\cdot,t_0)=0$ and $\\partial_tu(\\cdot,t_0)=x_1$. The solution is unbounded on every time slice $t>t_0$. Moreover its total energy at time $t$ in the sense of [[def-wave-energy-and-energy-flux]] is $+\\infty$ whenever $t\\ne t_0$: the energy density is $\\tfrac12(x_1^2+c^2(t-t_0)^2)$, and for every $R>0$ the box $[-R,R]^3$ gives $$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\tfrac12\\bigl(x_1^2+c^2(t-t_0)^2\\bigr)\\,d\\lambda\\;\\ge\\;\\frac12\\int_{[-R,R]^3}x_1^2\\,d\\lambda\\;=\\;\\frac12\\Bigl(\\frac{2R^3}{3}\\Bigr)(4R^2)\\;=\\;\\frac{4}{3}R^5\\longrightarrow+\\infty$$ ([[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]], [[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). Hence the finite-initial-energy hypothesis of [[cor-energy-uniqueness-for-the-wave-cauchy-problem]] fails for this smooth datum while the theorem's local uniqueness (b) still applies; the theorem asserts no decay at spatial infinity and no finite-energy property, and needs none.",
          "strategy": "All three clauses are direct substitutions into the displayed formula of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]], with the partial derivatives computed from the explicit expressions and the energy computed from its definition.\n\n(i) For the constant source $h=1$ the spherical mean is $M_{cs}h(\\cdot,v)(x)=1$ because $\\int_{S^2}d\\Omega=4\\pi$ ([[lem-em-spherical-mean-wave-identity]]); the source integral is $c^2\\int_0^t(t-v)\\,dv=c^2t^2/2$ and the two homogeneous terms vanish because $a=b=0$. The time derivatives are read off the polynomial $c^2t^2/2$ with the algebra of derivatives ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); $\\Delta u=0$ because $u$ is independent of $x$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]). A nonzero constant function on $\\mathbb R^3$ does not vanish off any compact set, so it is not compactly supported.\n\n(ii) With $h(x,t)=h_0(x)$ the mean $M_{c(t-v)}h_0(x)$ is an integral over the sphere $S_{c(t-v)}(x)$ of $h_0$; if $|x|>1+c(t-t_0)$ then for every $v\\in[t_0,t]$ every point of that sphere has norm at least $|x|-c(t-v)>1$, so the integrand vanishes identically on the sphere and the mean is $0$ ([[lem-em-spherical-mean-wave-identity]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Hence every term of the formula for $u(x,t)$ vanishes.\n\n(iii) The first-moment lemma states $\\int_{S^2}\\omega\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)=0$ for the polar surface measure ([[lem-first-moment-of-the-unit-sphere-vanishes]]), and [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]] identifies it with the surface measure used in $M$; therefore $M_{cs}b(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}(x_1+cs\\,n_1)\\,d\\Omega(n)=x_1$. Then $H_b(x,s)=sx_1$ and the other two terms vanish; differentiating $(t-t_0)x_1$ in $t$ twice and in $x$ twice gives $\\partial_t^2u=0$ and $\\Delta u=0$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]), so $Wu=0$, and the initial values are read off at $t=t_0$. For the energy, factor the box integral in the nonnegative Lebesgue integral ([[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]]) and evaluate the one-variable integral $\\int_{-R}^Rx_1^2\\,dx_1=2R^3/3$ ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]]); monotonicity of the nonnegative integral ([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]) bounds the energy over $\\mathbb R^3$ below by the box expression, which tends to $+\\infty$; [[def-wave-energy-and-energy-flux]] supplies the energy density and the convention that an infinite integral means total energy $+\\infty$. The solution's unboundedness on each slice $t>t_0$ is immediate from the expression $(t-t_0)x_1$.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
