            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), in the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $s\\ge1$ be an integer and put $\\tau=s+1$. Write $r(x)=|x|$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and let $\\Delta$ be the Euclidean Laplacian ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]). Then for every $u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$, $$\\|r^{-\\tau}u\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb R^3)}\\le\\frac{2}{s}\\,\\|r^{-\\tau+2}\\Delta u\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb R^3)}.$$ Moreover, if $u\\in H^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ is compactly supported in an annulus $\\{0<a\\le|x|\\le b\\}$, then the weak second derivatives of $u$ exist as locally square integrable functions, $\\Delta u$ denotes their sum in the weak sense ([[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]), and the same inequality holds for $u$. The weights $r^{-\\tau}$ and $r^{-\\tau+2}$ are bounded on every fixed compact annulus $\\{a_0\\le|x|\\le b_0\\}$ with $0<a_0\\le b_0$, and this boundedness on the support of the function is the only property of the weights used in the density extension below; the weight $r^{-\\tau}$ is unbounded at the origin for every $s\\ge1$, and the weight $r^{-\\tau+2}$ is unbounded at the origin for every $s\\ge2$ and equals the constant $1$ for $s=1$. That is why the extension is stated for functions whose support stays away from $r=0$. The constant $2/s$ is explicit and is not claimed to be sharp.",
          "strategy": "Change of variables. Write $x=rn$ with $r=e^{-t}$, $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $n\\in S^2$, and set $u(rn)=r^{-1/2}v(t,n)$, $w(t,n)=e^{st}v(t,n)$. The polar-coordinates formula ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]]) and the one-dimensional substitution ([[cor-one-dimensional-change-of-variables-with-absolute-derivative]]) give $dx=r^3\\,dt\\,d\\sigma(n)$, and with $u^2=r^{-1}v^2$ and $\\tau-1=s$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}r^{-2\\tau}u^2\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\int_{S^2}w^2\\,d\\sigma\\,dt.$$\nThe radial computation ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]) $\\Delta u=r^{-5/2}(\\partial_t^2v+\\Delta_{S^2}v-\\tfrac14v)$ and the substitution $v=e^{-st}w$ give $\\partial_t^2v=e^{-st}(\\partial_t-s)^2w$; the same factor $e^{2st}$ occurs in $r^{-2\\tau+4}(\\Delta u)^2r^3\\,dt\\,d\\sigma$, hence\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}r^{-2\\tau+4}(\\Delta u)^2\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\int_{S^2}\\bigl|\\bigl((\\partial_t-s)^2+\\Delta_{S^2}-\\tfrac14\\bigr)w\\bigr|^2\\,d\\sigma\\,dt.$$\nModal decomposition. Expand $w(t,\\cdot)$ for almost every $t$ in the complete orthonormal basis of spherical harmonics ([[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]]) and use the eigenvalue identity $\\Delta_{S^2}Y_{lm}=-l(l+1)Y_{lm}$, a direct computation from the homogeneous extension: $H_l$ is a homogeneous harmonic polynomial and $\\Delta=\\partial_r^2+2r^{-1}\\partial_r+r^{-2}\\Delta_{S^2}$ gives $0=r^{-2}(l^2+l+\\Delta_{S^2})H_l$. Parseval ([[thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family]]) and monotone convergence identify the two displayed integrals with the sums of the modal norms $\\sum_{l,m}\\|w_{lm}\\|^2$ and $\\sum_{l,m}\\|((\\partial_t-s)^2-(l+\\tfrac12)^2)w_{lm}\\|^2$. Fourier multiplier. In the $2\\pi$ convention of [[thm-fourier-differentiation-and-multiplication-identities-on-tempered-distributions]] and Plancherel ([[thm-plancherel]]) the mode operator has symbol $(2\\pi i\\xi-s)^2-(l+\\tfrac12)^2$; with $x=4\\pi^2\\xi^2\\ge0$, $A=s-l-\\tfrac12$ and $B=s+l+\\tfrac12$, its squared modulus is $(x+A^2)(x+B^2)$, which is increasing in $x\\ge0$ and therefore at least $A^2B^2=(s^2-(l+\\tfrac12)^2)^2$. For integers $l\\ge0$ and $s\\ge1$ the integer $l$ minimizing $|s^2-(l+\\tfrac12)^2|$ is $l=s-1$ or $l=s$, with value $s-\\tfrac14\\ge\\tfrac s2$, so the squared modulus is at least $s^2/4$. Hence $\\|w_{lm}\\|\\le\\tfrac2s\\|((\\partial_t-s)^2-(l+\\tfrac12)^2)w_{lm}\\|$ for every mode, and summing the nonnegative squared modal norms gives the inequality for smooth compactly supported $u$. Density extension. On a fixed annulus $a\\le r\\le b$ the weights $r^{\\pm\\tau}$, $r^{-\\tau+2}$ are bounded. Approximate $u\\in H^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ with support in that annulus in the $H^2$ norm by $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ functions ([[cor-compactly-supported-smooth-functions-are-dense-in-wkp-of-rn]]), multiply each approximant by a smooth cutoff equal to one on a neighbourhood of the support of $u$ and vanishing near the origin ([[lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls]], [[lem-weak-leibniz-rule-with-a-smooth-factor]]); the products lie in $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$, still converge to $u$ in $H^2$, and because the weights are bounded on the fixed annulus both sides of the inequality pass to the limit. Countable Choice is declared because it is assumed by [[thm-plancherel]] and by the density corollary; no other choice principle is used.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "D. Tataru, Carleman estimates, unique continuation and applications (Northwestern University, 1998 monograph draft), Chapter 3.6: Theorem 3.50 with Corollary 3.6.1 (singular-weight estimates for the Laplacian) and Theorem 3.52 (strong unique continuation)",
                "url": "https://math.berkeley.edu/~tataru/papers/book.pdf"
