            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-rational-points-and-boxes-in-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-product-topology-on-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-finite-and-countable-subadditivity-of-measures": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$). Let\n$\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be nonempty, open and connected, let\n$V\\in L^\\infty_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$, and let\n$\\psi\\in H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ satisfy $\\Delta\\psi=V\\psi$ in the\ndistributional sense: $\\int_\\Omega\\psi\\,\\Delta\\varphi\\,dx=\\int_\\Omega V\\psi\\,\\varphi\\,dx$\nfor every $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$, with $\\Delta\\varphi$ the classical\nLaplacian. Here $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ denotes the locally integrable\nclasses on $\\Omega$ whose weak derivatives of every order at most two exist\nand are locally square integrable\n([[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]],\n[[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]); $V\\in L^\\infty_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$\nmeans that $V$ is measurable and that $\\|V\\|_\\infty<\\infty$ on every compact\nsubset, with the essential supremum of\n[[def-essential-supremum-with-respect-to-a-measure]] and the space of\n[[def-l-infinity-on-a-measure-space]]. If $\\psi=0$ on a nonempty open subset\nof $\\Omega$, then $\\psi=0$ on $\\Omega$. No smoothness, sign, symmetry or decay\nhypothesis on $V$ beyond local essential boundedness is used, and no\nanalyticity is assumed.",
          "strategy": "Local propagation step. Let $z\\in\\Omega$ be a point on a neighbourhood of which $\\psi=0$, and let $R>0$ with $\\overline B_R(z)\\subseteq\\Omega$; translate the origin to $z$ so that the weight of the Carleman lemma is measured from $z$. Choose $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(B_R(0);[0,1])$ with $\\chi=1$ on $B_{R/2}(0)$ ([[lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls]]) and put $u=\\chi\\psi$. By the smooth-factor Leibniz rule with $|\\alpha|\\le2$ ([[lem-weak-leibniz-rule-with-a-smooth-factor]]) $u$ has weak derivatives up to order two in $L^2(\\mathbb R^3)$, is compactly supported in $B_R(0)$ and vanishes near $0$; since $\\Delta\\psi=V\\psi$ distributionally, $\\Delta u=Vu+e$ with $e=2\\nabla\\chi\\cdot\\nabla\\psi+(\\Delta\\chi)\\psi$, supported in the annulus $\\{R/2\\le|x|\\le R\\}$. Apply the radial Carleman lemma with parameter $s\\ge4MR^2$, where $M=\\|V\\|_\\infty$ on $B_R$ ([[def-essential-supremum-with-respect-to-a-measure]]): using $r\\le R$ on the ball and $|V|\\le M$, $\\|r^{-\\tau}Vu\\|\\le MR^2\\|r^{-\\tau}u\\|$, and the lemma plus absorption of the term $\\tfrac12\\|r^{-\\tau}u\\|$ gives\n$$\\|r^{-\\tau}u\\|_2\\le\\frac4s\\|r^{-\\tau+2}e\\|_2.$$\nFor $0<\\rho<R/2$ one has $u=\\psi$ on $B_\\rho(0)$ and $r^{-\\tau}\\ge\\rho^{-\\tau}$ there, while $e$ is supported where $r\\le R$, so\n$$\\|\\psi\\|_{L^2(B_\\rho(0))}\\le\\rho^{\\tau}\\|r^{-\\tau}u\\|_2\\le\\frac{4R^2}{s}\\Bigl(\\frac{2\\rho}{R}\\Bigr)^{\\tau}\\|e\\|_2\\longrightarrow0\\qquad(s\\to\\infty),$$\nas $e$ is fixed. Hence $\\psi=0$ on $B_{R/2}(z)$: vanishing near a point propagates to the ball of half the radius of any ball around that point that is relatively compact in $\\Omega$. Global propagation. Let $Z$ be the union of the open sets on which $\\psi$ vanishes; $Z$ is open and nonempty by hypothesis. Fix $x\\in\\Omega$ and a polygonal path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to\\Omega$ from a point of $Z$ to $x$, which exists because $\\Omega$ is a connected open subset of $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-connected-space]], [[cor-components-of-open-subsets-of-rn-are-polygonally-connected]], [[def-polygonal-path-and-polygonal-connectedness]]). The image $\\gamma([0,1])$ is compact and disjoint from the closed set $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\Omega$, so it has distance $\\delta>0$ from it ([[lem-relative-compact-closed-sets-have-a-positive-distance-gap]]). By uniform continuity of $\\gamma$ on $[0,1]$ choose $0=t_0<\\dots<t_N=1$ with $|\\gamma(t_{j+1})-\\gamma(t_j)|<\\delta/4$. Inductively, if $\\psi=0$ near $\\gamma(t_j)$, then $B_{\\delta/2}(\\gamma(t_j))$ has closure in $\\Omega$, so the propagation step with $R=\\delta/2$ gives $\\psi=0$ on $B_{\\delta/4}(\\gamma(t_j))$, a neighbourhood of $\\gamma(t_{j+1})$; hence $\\psi=0$ near $x=\\gamma(t_N)$. As $x\\in\\Omega$ was arbitrary, $Z=\\Omega$. Consequently $\\Omega\\setminus Z$, which is open in $\\Omega$, is empty: otherwise $Z,\\Omega\\setminus Z$ would be a separation of $\\Omega$ ([[def-connected-space]]). This proves $\\psi=0$ on $\\Omega$. Countable Choice is inherited through the Carleman lemma; no further choice principle is used.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "B. Davey, Quantitative unique continuation for the Laplacian plus a potential (arXiv:1903.04021)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1903.04021"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tataru, Carleman estimates, unique continuation and applications (Northwestern University monograph, 1998)",
                "url": "https://math.berkeley.edu/~tataru/papers/book.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-em-spherical-multipole-basis",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Exterior spherical multipole expansion and the ball boundary series",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#H1-H3",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-declared-branch-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "lem-em-coulomb-dipole-remainder",
            "lem-em-convergent-legendre-kernel",
