            "lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-ball-poisson-series-boundary-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-interior-derivative-estimates-for-harmonic-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $a>0$ and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ vanish outside $\\overline B_a(0)$. Use the complete orthonormal basis $\\{Y_{lm}\\}$ of real spherical harmonics on $S^2$ with unnormalised surface measure $d\\sigma$ of total mass $4\\pi$ and the addition theorem of [[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]], and the Legendre generating kernel and its compact remainder bound from [[lem-em-convergent-legendre-kernel]]. Write $x=rn$, $r=|x|$, $n\\in S^2$. Then:\n\n(a) **Exterior multipole expansion.** For every $x$ with $|x|>a$, $$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\rho(y)}{|x-y|}\\,dy=\\sum_{l=0}^\\infty\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}\\frac{4\\pi}{2l+1}\\frac{Y_{lm}(n)}{r^{l+1}}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|y|^lY_{lm}(y/|y|)\\rho(y)\\,dy,$$ the series converging absolutely and uniformly on $\\{|x|\\ge r_0\\}$ for every $r_0>a$, with tail after degree $N$ bounded by $\\|\\rho\\|_1\\,a^{N+1}/(r_0^{N+2}(1-a/r_0))$; for every fixed $r>a$, the degree-$l$ term is the $L^2(S^2,d\\sigma)$ angular projection of $n\\mapsto F_r(n):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|rn-y|^{-1}\\rho(y)\\,dy$ onto the degree-$l$ harmonics. This function is continuous and bounded by $\\|\\rho\\|_1/(r-a)$, so it is in $L^2(S^2)$; no sphere trace or $L^2$ property of the volume density $\\rho$ is asserted. The degree-zero and degree-one terms are the Coulomb and dipole terms with the explicit remainder bound of [[lem-em-coulomb-dipole-remainder]] ($Q=\\int\\rho$, $p=\\int y\\rho(y)\\,dy$, $|R(x)|\\le16a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1/|x|^3$ for $|x|\\ge2a$).\n\n(b) **Termwise differentiation.** On every compact $K$ with $\\inf_K|x|>a$ the series may be differentiated termwise to every order, the differentiated series converging uniformly on $K$; in particular the potential is $C^\\infty$ on $\\{|x|>a\\}$.\n\n(c) **Ball boundary branch.** For $f\\in L^2(S^2)$ with coefficients $f_{lm}$ the series $u(rn)=\\sum_{l,m}r^lf_{lm}Y_{lm}(n)$ converges in $L^2(S^2)$ as $r\\uparrow1$ and, with all derivatives, uniformly on each closed ball of radius $<1$, is harmonic on the open unit ball, and is the unique harmonic function there with a finite $\\sup_{r<1}\\|u(r\\cdot)\\|_2$ and $L^2$ boundary limit $f$; for continuous $f$ it equals the Poisson integral $(1-r^2)/(4\\pi|rn-n'|^3)$ of $f$ and extends continuously to the closed ball with boundary values $f$.\n\nThis is a statement about the Newtonian integral of a compactly supported $L^1$ density and about harmonic functions; no physical charge distribution or electrostatic interpretation is asserted. In every coefficient integral the factor $|y|^lY_{lm}(y/|y|)$ is defined at $y=0$ by its homogeneous polynomial extension: zero for $l\\ge1$ and the constant degree-zero harmonic for $l=0$. This is a finite assignment, and changing its value at that null singleton changes no integral.",
          "strategy": "Assembly of the recorded branches. Write $q=|y|/r$ and $z=n\\cdot y/|y|$ for $y\\ne0$; the convergent Legendre kernel [[lem-em-convergent-legendre-kernel]] gives $|x-y|^{-1}=\\sum_l(|y|^l/r^{l+1})P_l(z)$ for $|y|\\le a<r$, with the stated geometric tail, and integration against the finite measure $\\rho\\,dy$ yields the zonal multipole series with the displayed error times $\\|\\rho\\|_1$. Substituting the addition theorem $P_l(z)=\\frac{4\\pi}{2l+1}\\sum_mY_{lm}(n)Y_{lm}(y/|y|)$ from [[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]] into the $l$-th term and separating variables gives the displayed spherical-harmonic coefficients, the finite sums agreeing with the zonal series for every degree, hence in the uniform limit. The degree-zero and degree-one coefficients are $Q$ and the components of $p$, and comparison with the explicit Coulomb-dipole splitting of [[lem-em-coulomb-dipole-remainder]] (twice the fundamental theorem applied to $h(s)=|x-sy|^{-1}$) identifies the remainder and its bound. For the projection assertion fix r>a. Uniform convergence of the degree series on the observation sphere and the finite sphere measure allow its inner product with each Y_km to be taken termwise. Orthogonality gives $\\langle F_r,Y_{lm}\\rangle=(4\\pi/(2l+1))r^{-l-1}q_{lm}$, proving exactly the stated angular projection, not a nonexistent restriction of rho to a sphere. Absolute convergence of the full double series follows from $\\sum_m|Y_{lm}(n)q_{lm}|\\le\\int|y|^l|\\rho(y)|\\sum_m|Y_{lm}(n)Y_{lm}(y/|y|)|dy\\le((2l+1)/(4\\pi))a^l\\|\\rho\\|_1$ by finite Cauchy-Schwarz and the addition diagonal identity. The same geometric series yields the stated degree tail. For (b), the corrected kernel lemma supplies harmonicity of every coefficient term and uniform kernel/source tail on a bounded slightly larger exterior neighbourhood Kplus of K. Apply [[thm-interior-derivative-estimates-for-harmonic-functions]] to harmonic partial-sum differences on fixed-radius balls inside Kplus: their every-derivative suprema on K are bounded by fixed constants times the already vanishing uniform tail bound on Kplus. Finite-coordinate FTC [[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]] identifies the uniform derivative limits with the derivatives of the Newton integral. This proves the full every-order compact convergence using the exact real harmonic estimate, with no Cauchy estimate or unprovided complex-parameter domain. For (c), the addition theorem gives $\\sum_m|Y_{lm}(n)|^2=(2l+1)/4\\pi$, so Cauchy-Schwarz bounds the absolute tail at fixed $r<1$ by $\\|f\\|_2(\\sum_{l>N}r^{2l}(2l+1)/4\\pi)^{1/2}\\to0$; the derivative estimates and the interior harmonic estimates of [[lem-em-ball-poisson-series-boundary-convergence]] give uniform convergence of all derivatives on smaller balls, orthogonality gives $\\|u(r\\cdot)-f\\|_2\\to0$, and the corrected radius-s Poisson representation followed by harmonic orthogonality gives $v_{lm}(r)=(r/s)^l v_{lm}(s)$; the L2 boundary limit fixes the coefficient and proves uniqueness in the stated class; the Poisson kernel identity and uniform boundary convergence for continuous $f$ are the last part of that lemma. Completeness and orthonormality of the basis are those of [[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]. In the ball clause the L2 trace, finite radial L2 bound, uniqueness and continuous-data Poisson representation are the exact corrected H3 branches; their Countable Choice assumption is retained. No ball boundary assertion is inferred for rho at radius a.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis)",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/pde_notes.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "S. Axler, P. Bourdon, W. Ramey, Harmonic Function Theory, 2nd ed. (Springer GTM 137), author-hosted full text, Chapter 5 (harmonic polynomials, spherical harmonics and zonal harmonics) and Chapter 1 (Poisson kernel for the ball)",
                "url": "https://www.axler.net/HFT.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Well-posed weak solvability and classical regularity for declared smooth-domain elliptic boundary problems",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#B1-B7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-declared-branch-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain",
            "def-axiom-of-choice",
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm",
            "lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence",
            "lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity",
            "lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains",
            "lem-em-lift-specified-dirichlet-existence",
            "def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure",
            "thm-sharp-trace-theorem-for-w-one-p"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-lift-specified-dirichlet-existence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sharp-trace-theorem-for-w-one-p": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice as declared by the weak-solvability and regularity lemmas below ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain in the local graph sense of [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]], let $\\varepsilon\\in C^\\infty(\\overline\\Omega;\\operatorname{Sym}^3)$ be uniformly positive, that is $\\alpha|\\xi|^2\\le\\xi\\cdot\\varepsilon(x)\\xi\\le\\beta|\\xi|^2$ for all $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $x\\in\\Omega$ and some $0<\\alpha\\le\\beta<\\infty$, and let $\\rho$ be smooth on $\\overline\\Omega$. The Euclidean inner product is that of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]; at a boundary point with outward unit normal $\\nu$ the outward conormal vector is $\\varepsilon\\nu$ and the outward conormal derivative is $\\nu\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u$. Here $\\operatorname{Sym}^3$ is the space of real symmetric $3\\times3$ matrices, $H^1(\\Omega)=W^{1,2}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ is the Sobolev space of [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], $H_0^1(\\Omega)$ is the $H^1$ closure of $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ ([[def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure]]), and $T:H^1(\\Omega)\\to H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$ is the bounded onto trace of [[thm-sharp-trace-theorem-for-w-one-p]], with the fractional boundary space defined in [L1]. Then:\n\n(a) **Dirichlet.** For every $g\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega)$ there is exactly one weak solution $u\\in H^1(\\Omega)$ with trace $Tu=g$ of $\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u\\,dx=\\int_\\Omega\\rho v\\,dx$ for all $v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega)$; it depends continuously on the data, $\\|u\\|_{H^1}\\le C(\\|\\rho\\|_{L^2}+\\|g\\|_{H^{1/2}})$, has a $C^\\infty$ representative on $\\overline\\Omega$, and satisfies the classical equations $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon\\nabla u)=\\rho$ in $\\Omega$ and $u|_{\\partial\\Omega}=g$.\n\n(b) **Neumann.** Let $g\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega)$ be a smooth outward conormal datum satisfying $\\int_{\\Omega_j}\\rho\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}g\\,dS=0$ on every connected component $\\Omega_j$ of $\\Omega$. Then there is a weak solution of $\\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u=\\int_\\Omega\\rho v+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}g\\,Tv$ for all $v\\in H^1(\\Omega)$; it is unique up to a separately chosen additive constant on each component, and imposing $\\int_{\\Omega_j}u=0$ on every component gives a unique normalised solution which is $C^\\infty$ on each closed component and satisfies the classical equations $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon\\nabla u)=\\rho$ in $\\Omega$ and $\\nu\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u|_{\\partial\\Omega}=g$. In particular on a connected domain the compatibility is $\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}g=-\\int_\\Omega\\rho$, with a single additive constant.\n\n(c) **Transmission.** Let there be finitely many pairwise disjoint closed smooth interfaces strictly inside $\\Omega$, let $\\varepsilon$ be piecewise smooth on the closures of the resulting pieces and uniformly positive in the above sense, let $\\rho$ be piecewise smooth on the closed pieces and let $\\sigma$ be a smooth sheet charge on the union of the interfaces; the weak right-hand side is $\\int_\\Omega\\rho v+\\sum_\\Gamma\\int_\\Gamma\\sigma\\,Tv$, together with $\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}g\\,Tv$ in the Neumann case. Prescribe either the smooth outer Dirichlet datum of (a), or the smooth outer Neumann datum of (b) with the componentwise compatibility condition $\\int_{\\Omega_j}\\rho+\\sum_{\\Gamma\\subset\\Omega_j}\\int_\\Gamma\\sigma+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}g=0$ for every component $\\Omega_j$. With outer Dirichlet data the globally $H^1$ solution is unique; with outer Neumann data it exists exactly when that compatibility holds and is then unique modulo componentwise additive constants, the zero componentwise means making it unique; the equal one-sided traces and $C^\\infty$ regularity on each closed side hold in either case, and the flux jump is $n\\cdot[\\varepsilon\\nabla u]=-\\sigma$ in the sign convention of [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]], where $n$ is a chosen unit normal on the interface and $[\\varepsilon\\nabla u]=\\varepsilon^+\\nabla u^+-\\varepsilon^-\\nabla u^-$ is the difference from the $n$-positive side to the other.\n\nEdge and junction points, reentrant corners, coefficient loss of regularity and general piecewise classical regularity beyond finitely many disjoint smooth interfaces are excluded. The existence content of all three parts is that of the two weak solvability lemmas; this theorem states the combined existence-and-regularity conclusions on the declared domains and adds no existence assertion beyond them.",
          "strategy": "The claim collects three recorded branches and their compatibility. (a) Existence, uniqueness, exact prescribed trace and continuous dependence are [[lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence]]; the classical upgrade to $C^\\infty$ on the closure, with the classical equation and boundary values, is [[lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity]]. (b) Testing the displayed weak identity with the constant on each component gives $\\int_{\\Omega_j}\\rho+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}g=0$, including the minus sign when solved for the outer conormal integral. Necessity of componentwise compatibility, existence, uniqueness modulo componentwise constants and the normalised form are [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]]; the classical upgrade is again [[lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity]]. (c) Apply the transmission functional including the sheet-charge term, with outer Dirichlet tests in $H_0^1$ for full uniqueness, or outer Neumann tests in $H^1$ with that functional zero on every componentwise constant and the chosen mean normalisation. The weak existence and the one-sided trace identity are the transmission part of [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]], the two-sided classical smoothness and the jump relation are [[lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity]], and the passage from the weak identity to the interface flux uses the divergence theorem for the piecewise smooth configuration together with the first Green identity [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]. The smooth coefficients, conormal field and chart changes are smooth by the calculus conventions of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]; the inner products and the quadratic form $\\nabla v\\cdot\\varepsilon\\nabla u$ use the Euclidean inner product [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. No existence or regularity claim is made beyond the stated smooth-domain, finite-interface regime.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis)",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/pde_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-em-exterior-outgoing-scalar-uniqueness",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Uniqueness of the outgoing scalar Helmholtz solution with a smooth obstacle",
          "reference": "exterior-scattering-arguments.md#F3",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "lem-em-sphere-outgoing-dirichlet-to-neumann-map",
            "thm-bounded-potential-weak-unique-continuation-in-three-dimensions",
            "cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function",
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-sphere-outgoing-dirichlet-to-neumann-map": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bounded-potential-weak-unique-continuation-in-three-dimensions": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice inherited from the modal DtN/trace suppliers, covering the Countable Choice used by the local UCP supplier. Let $O\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded open set with $C^\\infty$ boundary ([[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]) whose exterior $D=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\overline O$ is connected, let $k>0$, and let $R>0$ with $\\overline O\\subset B_R(0)$. Suppose $u$ is $C^\\infty$ on $\\overline D$, solves $(\\Delta+k^2)u=0$ on $D$ with the Euclidean Laplacian $\\Delta$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]), vanishes on $\\partial O$, and is outgoing in the class of [[lem-em-sphere-outgoing-dirichlet-to-neumann-map]]: its trace $f=u|_{S_R}$ lies in $H^{1/2}(S_R)$ and its normal derivative on $S_R$ is $T_Rf$ in the weak trace sense of that lemma. Then $u=0$ on $D$. No decay rate, asymptotic expansion or pointwise Sommerfeld formula beyond the stated outgoing class is assumed.",
          "strategy": "Green's identity on the truncated exterior. Apply the first Green identity [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] to $u$ and its complex conjugate on the bounded $C^1$ domain $D\\cap B_R$, whose boundary consists of $\\partial O$ and $S_R$: the volume integral is the real number $\\int_{D\\cap B_R}(|\\nabla u|^2-k^2|u|^2)\\,dx$, and the inner boundary contributes zero because $u=0$ on $\\partial O$. The full boundary pairing equals that real volume integral, so its imaginary part is zero; its real part need not vanish. Thus $\\operatorname{Im}\\langle T_Rf,f\\rangle=0$. By [[lem-em-sphere-outgoing-dirichlet-to-neumann-map]] applied to $f$ the imaginary part is $\\operatorname{Im}\\langle T_Rf,f\\rangle=\\sum_{l,m}(\\operatorname{Im}t_l)|f_{lm}|^2$ with $\\operatorname{Im}t_l>0$ for every $l$; the already established zero imaginary pairing and the strictly positive modal coefficients force every $f_{lm}=0$. Consequently the outgoing exterior series of that lemma, which is the unique outgoing finite-local-energy solution with trace $f$ on $S_R$, vanishes on $\\{r>R\\}$; combined with $u=0$ on $\\partial O$ the function $u$ vanishes on the complement of $D\\cap\\overline B_R$. Propagation. On $D$ the real and imaginary parts of $u$ are $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ solutions of $\\Delta w=Vw$ with the bounded coefficient $V=-k^2$, so [[thm-bounded-potential-weak-unique-continuation-in-three-dimensions]] applies to each of them on the connected open $D$: each vanishes on the nonempty open set $D\\cap\\{r>R\\}$, hence on $D$. Therefore $u=0$ on $D$. No finite experimental observation or physical postulate is used.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "A. Moiola, Scattering of time-harmonic acoustic waves (University of Pavia lecture notes, 2022)",
                "url": "https://mate.unipv.it/moiola/T/MNAPDE2022/MNAPDE2022.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "TU Dortmund, Lecture notes: The Rellich Lemma on the Helmholtz equation",
                "url": "https://analysis.mathematik.tu-dortmund.de/storages/analysis-mathematik/r/dokumente/Kurzskripte/Rellich-Lemma-20260316.pdf"
              },
