          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, as used by the variational, Sobolev and compact-spectral suppliers and by the Fredholm alternative ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $O\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded open set with $C^\\infty$ boundary ([[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]) whose exterior $D=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\overline O$ is connected, let $k>0$, and let $R>0$ with $\\overline O\\subset B_R(0)$. Write $A:=B_R(0)\\setminus\\overline O$ and let $n$ be the outward unit normal field of $A$, so that on $\\partial O$ the field $n$ points into $O$; extend $n$ to a smooth normal collar by $n(p+sn(p)):=n(p)$, so $\\partial_n n=0$, using [[thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem]]; put $\\kappa:=\\operatorname{div}n|_{\\partial O}$ and, for a field $E$ on $\\partial O$, $E_n:=E\\cdot n|_{\\partial O}$.\n\nFor $e_T:\\partial O\\to\\mathbb C^3$ smooth in boundary charts and satisfying $e_T\\cdot n=0$, put\n$$\\operatorname{div}_{\\partial O}e_T:=\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde e_T-\\partial_n(\\widetilde e_T\\cdot n)\\quad\\text{on }\\partial O,$$\nwhere $\\widetilde e_T$ is any smooth extension of $e_T$ to a neighbourhood of $\\partial O$; the expression is independent of the extension and equals the surface divergence of $e_T$ with respect to the Euclidean surface measure of $\\partial O$. Write $\\Delta$ for the Euclidean Laplacian ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]), acting componentwise, and for a vector field $f=(f_1,f_2,f_3)$ on $S_R$ write $T_Rf:=(T_Rf_1,T_Rf_2,T_Rf_3)$ with $T_R$ the scalar Dirichlet-to-Neumann map of [[lem-em-sphere-outgoing-dirichlet-to-neumann-map]].\n\nThen there is exactly one field $E\\in C^\\infty(\\overline D;\\mathbb C^3)$ with\n$$(\\Delta+k^2)E=0\\ \\text{on }D,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}E=0\\ \\text{on }D,\\qquad E_{\\text{tangential}}=e_T\\ \\text{on }\\partial O,$$\nwhich is outgoing on the sphere $S_R$ in the modal sense of that lemma: its trace $f:=E|_{S_R}$ lies in $H^{1/2}(S_R;\\mathbb C^3)$ and the normal derivative of $E$ on $S_R$ equals $T_Rf$ componentwise in the weak trace sense of the sphere DtN lemma. With these data the normal boundary values of $E$ on $\\partial O$ satisfy the closure relation\n$$\\partial_nE_n+(\\operatorname{div}n)E_n=-\\operatorname{div}_{\\partial O}e_T$$\nas an identity of $C^\\infty$ functions on $\\partial O$. No scalar Dirichlet condition is imposed on the three Cartesian components of $E$, and no decay rate, asymptotic expansion or pointwise Sommerfeld formula beyond the stated modal outgoing class is assumed.",
          "strategy": "Reduction to a coercive shifted form. On the truncated annulus $A=B_R\\setminus\\overline O$ introduce $X=\\{v\\in H^1(A;\\mathbb C^3):v_{\\text{tangential}}=0\\text{ on }\\partial O\\}$, a closed subspace of $H^1$ by the trace continuity of [[lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence]]; the trace machinery is smooth for the classical solutions. With $\\kappa=\\operatorname{div}n$ on the inner boundary, the form\n$$a(E,v)=\\int_A\\bigl(\\nabla E:\\overline{\\nabla v}-k^2E\\cdot\\overline v\\bigr)+\\int_{\\partial O}\\kappa E_n\\overline{v_n}-\\langle T_RE,v\\rangle_{S_R}$$\nis bounded on $H^1$ by [[lem-em-sphere-outgoing-dirichlet-to-neumann-map]] and bounded $\\kappa$. Its real part is coercive after shifting by a real $\\lambda>k^2+C_\\delta+1$: the outer term is nonnegative by the sign of $\\operatorname{Re}T_R$, and the curvature term is absorbed by the collar inequality proved in [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]] with $2ab\\le\\delta a^2+\\delta^{-1}b^2$. The complex coercive form is inverted by the explicit contraction iteration (Hilbert Riesz representation [[thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space]] turns the shifted form into a bounded operator $B$ with $\\operatorname{Re}\\langle Bx,x\\rangle\\ge\\alpha\\|x\\|^2$; for small $t>0$, $x\\mapsto(I-tB)x+tf$ is a strict contraction in the complete space). The $L^2$ pairing $J:X\\to X^*$ is compact because the argument of [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]] gives $X\\hookrightarrow L^2$ compact on the smooth bounded domain $A$; the original form operator is a shifted inverse composed with $I-\\lambda K$, $K$ compact, so the Fredholm alternative [[thm-fredholm-alternative-for-identity-minus-compact]] (with its Axiom of Choice [[def-axiom-of-choice]]) gives bounded invertibility once the kernel is zero. Kernel. For a homogeneous solution the imaginary part of $a(E,E)$ is $-\\operatorname{Im}\\langle T_RE,E\\rangle=0$, and the strictly positive DtN imaginary coefficients force all three outer traces of $E$ to vanish. Extending the field by the outgoing series outside $B_R$ and integrating by parts with [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] gives a weak componentwise Helmholtz solution on $D$ with $H^1$ regularity on each bounded local patch, because the full traces agree across $S_R$, and it vanishes outside $B_R$. On each such patch apply [[thm-interior-h-two-regularity-for-divergence-form-equations]] with principal matrix $I$, $b=0$, $c=-k^2$, $f=0$ (equivalently $-\\Delta E=k^2E$), giving $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}$. The real and imaginary parts of each component then satisfy $\\Delta w=-k^2w$ on the connected $D$ and vanish on its nonempty exterior open subset, so the direct local supplier [[thm-bounded-potential-weak-unique-continuation-in-three-dimensions]] makes them zero. No scalar inner Dirichlet condition is inferred from tangential PEC data, and the scalar exterior-Dirichlet lemma is not invoked in this kernel step. Hence the kernel is zero and the shifted-then-unshifted problem is boundedly invertible. Inhomogeneous data. For prescribed smooth $e_T$, give each Cartesian scalar component the Dirichlet boundary datum $(e_T)_j$ on $\\partial O$ and zero on $S_R$. The weak prescribed-trace solver [[lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence]] with principal coefficient $I$ and zero bulk source, followed by the scalar smooth-data upgrade [[lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity]], supplies a smooth componentwise lift $G$ on $\\overline A$ with $G|_{\\partial O}=e_T$ and $G|_{S_R}=0$ (apply to real and imaginary parts). This is a genuine scalar use of B7, distinct from the inline vector mixed-boundary regularity proof. Now solve for $u\\in X$ with $a(u,v)=-a(G,v)-\\int_{\\partial O}(\\operatorname{div}_{\\partial O}e_T)\\overline{v_n}$; $E=G+u$ then has the prescribed tangential trace, and testing against compactly supported interior fields gives the componentwise Helmholtz equation and the stated normal relation. Regularity. At the artificial sphere the matched traces and normal derivatives make the extended field a weak Helmholtz solution across $S_R$, so [[cor-smooth-data-give-smooth-interior-solutions]] applied on bounded local patches to the constant-coefficient operator $-\\Delta-k^2$ makes the matched field smooth there; no boundary theorem is used at this artificial interior surface. At $\\partial O$ rotate into a smooth frame with two tangential components and one normal component; the principal part becomes a uniformly elliptic scalar principal matrix times the identity with smooth lower-order coupling, and the boundary system (two Dirichlet components and one Robin component) is complementing: in the frozen half-space a decaying principal mode $e^{i\\xi\\cdot y-|\\xi|t}a$ with $\\xi\\ne0$ has $a_1=a_2=0$ and $-|\\xi|a_3=0$, so all amplitudes vanish, while curvature and connection terms are lower order. Supply the vector boundary argument inline, rather than treating B7's scalar public statement as a vector theorem. After the smooth frame rotation and boundary flattening, tangential shifts preserve the two zero Dirichlet component traces, while the normal component has arbitrary admissible trace. Test with $-\\delta_{-h}(\\eta^2\\delta_h u)$ componentwise. The scalar principal matrix times the identity gives the sum of three positive gradient squares; smooth coefficient and frame commutators are lower-order $L^2$ terms bounded by Cauchy-Schwarz and Young. The differenced Robin boundary term consists of the bounded Robin coefficient times the normal quotient trace square and its smooth coefficient commutator; the collar estimate $\\|w\\|_{L^2(\\partial O)}^2\\le\\delta\\|\\nabla w\\|_2^2+C_\\delta\\|w\\|_2^2$ absorbs the first term, and trace continuity and Young bound the commutator by the same gradient bound and known $H^1$ data. This gives uniform tangential quotient-gradient bounds. Riesz identifies all mixed second derivatives in $L^2$, and the corrected component equation recovers each normal second derivative using the uniformly positive normal principal coefficient. Iterate the tangential quotient argument with differentiated smooth data and coefficient/Robin commutators, and recover remaining normal orders from the component equations; a finite boundary cover gives all orders. Local Sobolev extension/embedding then gives classical smooth representatives. This is the actual inline mixed-boundary product-space proof obligation, with two Dirichlet components and one Robin component; it is not replaced by a scalar Dirichlet-regularity citation. Divergence. The scalar $q=\\operatorname{div}E$ is a smooth Helmholtz solution on $D$; the divergence identity in a boundary frame turns the inner Robin relation into $q=0$ on $\\partial O$, and the outgoing component series differentiates to an outgoing $q$; [[lem-em-exterior-outgoing-scalar-uniqueness]] then gives $q=0$ on $D$. Thus $\\operatorname{div}E=0$ is deduced, not presumed.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "A. Moiola, Scattering of time-harmonic acoustic waves (University of Pavia lecture notes, 2022)",
                "url": "https://mate.unipv.it/moiola/T/MNAPDE2022/MNAPDE2022.pdf"
