          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "**Spatial coordinate convention.** Throughout this example, the local spatial labels $i=1,2,3$ name the canonical function coordinates $i-1\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$: $x_i:=x(i-1)$ and $n_i:=n(i-1)$. The local vector $e_i$ is defined by $e_i(k)=1$ when $k=i-1$ and $e_i(k)=0$ otherwise, for $k<3$. Likewise, $\\partial_i$ denotes differentiation in canonical coordinate $i-1$, and every spatial vector component with label $i$, including $g_i$, follows this same convention. A printed triple lists these components in the order $i=1,2,3$. This finite relabelling does not permute the coordinate order, change the Euclidean gradient, divergence or curl, or change the dimension subscripts in $\\mathbb R^3$, $\\lambda_3$ and $S^2$ ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]).\n\nAssume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Three exact boundary checks. (i) The sphere trace $f(n)=n_3$ is the trace of the homogeneous degree-one harmonic polynomial $u(x)=x_3$, so the ball boundary branch of [[thm-em-spherical-multipole-basis]] applies to it; the simultaneous conditions $\\Delta u=0$ in the unit ball $B_1$ and $\\partial_nu=1$ on $S^2$ are incompatible, because the divergence theorem gives $0=\\int_{B_1}\\Delta u\\,dx=\\int_{S^2}\\partial_nu\\,d\\sigma=\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$. (ii) For the coefficient $\\varepsilon=1$ on $\\{x_1<0\\}$ and $\\varepsilon=2$ on $\\{x_1>0\\}$, the function $u=x_1$ on the first side and $u=x_1/2$ on the second is continuous and has weak first derivatives represented by locally integrable functions, and $\\varepsilon\\partial_1u\\equiv1$, so $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon\\nabla u)=0$ weakly although $u$ is not $C^1$: its first derivative jumps from $1$ to $1/2$ across the interface. Hence the smooth-coefficient statements of [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]] cannot be extended to this piecewise-constant case, and the correct conclusion there is the piecewise regularity recorded by that item, not a global $C^1$ or $C^\\infty$-on-the-closure statement. (iii) The same coefficient configuration is a hypothesis boundary for the smooth-domain spectral completeness of [[thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness]]: the coefficient field is discontinuous across the interface and the explicit weak solution is not classical $C^1$ there, so the configuration lies outside the smooth classes of that theorem, which asserts no completeness or classical boundary regularity in non-smooth classes; the weak formulation nevertheless remains valid. All three parts are exact finite computations or classical divergence-theorem evaluations, and they assert no physical interpretation.",
          "strategy": "Direct computations. Part (i): $\\Delta x_3=0$ and $\\nabla x_3=e_3$, so on the unit sphere $\\partial_nx_3=e_3\\cdot n=n_3$; if a function were harmonic in $B_1$ with $\\partial_nu=1$ on $S^2$, the divergence theorem [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]] applied to $\\nabla u$ (a $C^1$ field) would give $0=\\int_{B_1}\\Delta u\\,d\\lambda=\\int_{S^2}\\partial_nu\\,d\\sigma$, while the polar-coordinates formula [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] and the defining normalisation give $\\sigma(S^2)=3\\lambda_3(B_1)=4\\pi$; the contradiction $0=4\\pi$ proves incompatibility, and the Laplacian is that of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]. Part (ii): on each open half-space the field $\\varepsilon\\nabla u$ is the constant $(1,0,0)$, so its distributional divergence is zero ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]): testing $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon\\nabla u)$ against a compactly supported smooth test function and integrating by parts reduces to $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\partial_1\\varphi\\cdot(-1)\\,dx=0$ for a constant field. The function $u$ is continuous because $x_1$ and $x_1/2$ both tend to $0$ at the interface, its distributional first derivatives are the locally integrable functions equal to $1,0,0$ respectively $1/2,0,0$ on the two sides ([[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]]), and these representatives jump at $x_1=0$, so $u$ is $H^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ but not $C^1$. Part (iii): this is the statement that the excluded case is excluded: the smooth coefficients and smooth interfaces required by [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]] and by the smooth-domain class of [[thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness]] fail, while the displayed weak solution exists and is not smooth on the closure. No physical postulate, measurement or empirical claim is used. Countable Choice is inherited by the actually used Green, surface/regular-distribution and/or harmonic-boundary supplier interfaces. The supplier updates do not alter the local source, trace or modal class used here.",
          "sources": {
