            "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
            "lem-em-integrable-causal-dispersion",
            "lem-em-period-average",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-arithmetic-and-lattice-operations-preserve-measurability",
            "thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
            "thm-continuous-partials-and-cauchy-riemann-imply-holomorphic",
            "thm-continuity-under-the-integral-sign",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign",
            "thm-dominated-convergence",
            "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
            "thm-integration-by-parts",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality"
          ],
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the smooth Riemann/Lebesgue comparison route below; in clause (c), also assume Dependent Choice ([[def-dependent-choice]]) inherited from the AC supplier. Let $d\\ge1$. All kernels below are real $d\\times d$ matrix kernels and all histories are $\\mathbb R^d$-valued; conditions on matrices are read entrywise, and $L^1(0,\\infty;\\mathbb R^{d\\times d})$ means entrywise Lebesgue integrability on $(0,\\infty)$.\n\n(a) (Causal convolution.) Let $K\\in L^1(0,\\infty;\\mathbb R^{d\\times d})$ and let $E:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be bounded and continuous. Then for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$ the integral $P(t):=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)E(t-s)\\,ds$ converges absolutely, $$|P_j(t)|\\le\\sum_{k=1}^d\\Bigl(\\sup_{s\\in\\mathbb R}|E_k(s)|\\Bigr)\\int_0^\\infty|K_{jk}(s)|\\,ds\\qquad(j=1,\\dots,d),$$ the response $P$ is bounded and continuous, the map $E\\mapsto P$ is linear, and it is causal: if $E_1,E_2$ are bounded continuous and coincide on $(-\\infty,t_0]$, then their responses coincide on $(-\\infty,t_0]$.\n\n(b) (Compact branch.) If $K\\in C_c^\\infty((0,\\infty);\\mathbb R^{d\\times d})$ (smooth, with compact support contained in the open half-line), then the transfer function $$\\chi(z):=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)e^{izs}\\,ds$$ is entire, satisfies $\\chi(-\\omega)=\\overline{\\chi(\\omega)}$ for every real $\\omega$, obeys $|\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le\\|K_{jk}''\\|_{L^1(0,\\infty)}|z|^{-2}$ for every $z\\ne0$ with $\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0$. For any $b>0$ with $\\operatorname{supp}K\\subseteq(0,b)$, the full entire-plane bound is $|\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le e^{b\\max(0,-\\operatorname{Im}z)}\\|K_{jk}\\prime\\prime\\|_1|z|^{-2}$ for every $z\\ne0$. Its entries satisfy the principal-value dispersion relations $$\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)=\\frac1\\pi\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}\\chi_{jk}(\\xi)}{\\xi-\\omega}\\,d\\xi,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)=-\\frac1\\pi\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{jk}(\\xi)}{\\xi-\\omega}\\,d\\xi$$ for every real $\\omega$.\n\n(c) (Integrable branch.) If instead $K:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ is locally absolutely continuous entrywise in the sense of [[def-absolutely-continuous-function]] on every compact subinterval of $[0,\\infty)$, with $K\\prime$ its almost-everywhere derivative with $K,K'\\in L^1(0,\\infty;\\mathbb R^{d\\times d})$ and $s\\mapsto sK(s)$ in $L^1(0,\\infty;\\mathbb R^{d\\times d})$, then $\\chi(z):=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)e^{izs}\\,ds$ is well defined and holomorphic on the open upper half-plane $\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>0\\}$, continuous on the closed upper half-plane with continuous first derivative on the real boundary, satisfies the same pair of dispersion relations for every real $\\omega$, and is bounded on $\\{\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0\\}$ by $|\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le|K_{jk}(0)|+\\|K_{jk}'\\|_{L^1(0,\\infty)}$ for $|z|\\ge1$; for an instantaneous real response matrix $\\chi_\\infty\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ define $\\chi_{\\mathrm{total}}:=\\chi_\\infty+\\chi$ and apply the relations to $\\chi_{\\mathrm{total}}-\\chi_\\infty=\\chi$.\n\n(d) (Monochromatic work and passivity.) Let $\\omega>0$, $e\\in\\mathbb C^d$, and let $\\varepsilon_0>0$ be a real parameter (a response normalization; the equivalence below is independent of its value). Put $E(t):=\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{-i\\omega t})$ and $P(t):=\\operatorname{Re}(\\varepsilon_0\\chi(\\omega)e\\,e^{-i\\omega t})$, real parts taken componentwise and $\\chi(\\omega)$ acting by matrix multiplication. Then, with $T:=2\\pi/\\omega$ and the Euclidean dot product on $\\mathbb R^d$, $$\\frac1T\\int_0^T E(t)\\cdot P'(t)\\,dt=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0\\omega}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\bigl(e^*\\chi(\\omega)e\\bigr),\\qquad e^*\\chi(\\omega)e:=\\sum_{j,k=1}^d\\overline{e_j}\\,\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)\\,e_k,$$ and this period-averaged work is nonnegative for every $e\\in\\mathbb C^d$ if and only if the Hermitian dissipative part $M(\\omega):=(\\chi(\\omega)-\\chi(\\omega)^*)/(2i)$ satisfies $e^*M(\\omega)e\\ge0$ for every $e\\in\\mathbb C^d$.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct, branch by branch; clausal tags below indicate the cited tools.\n\n(a) For each $j$ and $t$, absolute convergence and the displayed bound follow from $|K_{jk}(s)E_k(t-s)|\\le(\\sup|E_k|)|K_{jk}(s)|$, the triangle inequality for integrals and monotonicity of the nonnegative integral over the measurable set $(0,\\infty)$ ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]). Linearity in $E$ is linearity of the integral. Continuity in $t$: if $t_n\\to t$ then $K_{jk}(s)E_k(t_n-s)\\to K_{jk}(s)E_k(t-s)$ for every $s$ with the integrable dominating function $(\\sup|E_k|)|K_{jk}|$, so dominated convergence gives $P(t_n)\\to P(t)$ ([[thm-dominated-convergence]]). Causality: for $t\\le t_0$ and every $s\\ge0$ the argument $t-s$ lies in $(-\\infty,t_0]$, so replacing $E$ by a history agreeing with it there changes no integrand value and hence no integral.\n\n(b) Holomorphy is obtained from the real-parameter differentiation theorem rather than by differentiating in the complex variable directly. Writing $z=x+iy$, the integrand $K_{jk}(s)e^{izs}=K_{jk}(s)e^{ixs}e^{-ys}$ has continuous partials in $x$ and $y$; because $K_{jk}$ has compact support, on every compact set of parameters $(x,y)$ the partials are dominated by a fixed multiple of $|K_{jk}|(1+|s|)$, an integrable function, so [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign]] applied in the two real parameters gives the continuous partials $\\partial_x\\chi_{jk}=\\int_0^\\infty isK_{jk}(s)e^{izs}\\,ds$ and $\\partial_y\\chi_{jk}=\\int_0^\\infty(-s)K_{jk}(s)e^{izs}\\,ds$ ([[def-complex-exponential]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). These satisfy $\\partial_y\\chi_{jk}=-\\int sK_{jk}e^{izs}=i\\int isK_{jk}e^{izs}=i\\,\\partial_x\\chi_{jk}$, the Cauchy--Riemann equations for the pair $(\\partial_x\\chi_{jk},\\partial_y\\chi_{jk})$ with the identification $f=u+iv$; since the partials exist on a neighbourhood and are continuous, [[thm-continuous-partials-and-cauchy-riemann-imply-holomorphic]] makes $\\chi_{jk}$ holomorphic, and because the same argument is available at every $z\\in\\mathbb C$, each entry is entire ([[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]]). Iterating gives $\\chi_{jk}^{(k)}(z)=\\int_0^\\infty(is)^kK_{jk}(s)e^{izs}\\,ds$. For the reality identity at real $\\omega$: conjugation is an isometry and a real-field automorphism of $\\mathbb C$ ([[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]) and the series defining the complex exponential converges everywhere ([[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]]); conjugating the partial sums termwise and passing to the limit along the isometry gives $\\overline{e^{i\\omega s}}=e^{-i\\omega s}$, so conjugating the defining integral of $\\chi_{jk}$ and using that $K_{jk}$ is real gives $\\overline{\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)}=\\int_0^\\infty K_{jk}(s)e^{-i\\omega s}\\,ds=\\chi_{jk}(-\\omega)$ ([[def-complex-exponential]], [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]). On $[0,b]$, apply [[thm-integration-by-parts]] twice to the real and imaginary parts of the smooth exponential and real smooth kernel (first $K$, then $K\\prime$). All derivatives are continuous and Riemann integrable; [[thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral]] transfers each compact-interval identity to the Lebesgue integrals. With vanishing endpoint values because $K$ vanishes near $0$ and beyond $b$, these identities give $\\chi_{jk}(z)=-z^{-2}\\int_0^\\infty K_{jk}''(s)e^{izs}\\,ds$ for $z\\ne0$, hence the stated upper-half-plane $|z|^{-2}$ bound; for arbitrary complex $z$, $|e^{izs}|\\le e^{b\\max(0,-\\operatorname{Im}z)}$ on the support gives the full entire-plane bound. For the dispersion relations fix real $\\omega$ and form a contour consisting of the interval $[-R,R]$ indented above $\\omega$ on a clockwise semicircle of radius $\\delta$, closed by the large semicircle of radius $R$ in the upper half-plane; on the indented domain the integrand $\\chi_{jk}(z)/(z-\\omega)$ is holomorphic, so Cauchy's theorem for null-homotopic loops gives vanishing total integral ([[cor-cauchy-theorem-for-null-homotopic-loops]]). The large arc contributes at most $\\pi R\\sup_{|z|=R,\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0}|\\chi_{jk}|/(R-|\\omega|)\\to0$ by the $|z|^{-2}$ bound. The small arc contributes $-i\\pi\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)+o(1)$ as $\\delta\\to0$, by continuity of $\\chi_{jk}$ on the closed upper half-plane, which follows from dominated convergence as in (a) ([[thm-dominated-convergence]]). The remaining real integral converges to its principal value: near $\\omega$ subtract the constant $\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)$ so that the divided difference $[\\chi_{jk}(\\xi)-\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)]/(\\xi-\\omega)$ is bounded, and at infinity use the $|z|^{-2}$ decay. Letting $\\delta\\to0$, $R\\to\\infty$ gives $\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\chi_{jk}(\\xi)/(\\xi-\\omega)d\\xi=i\\pi\\chi_{jk}(\\omega)$; separating real and imaginary parts yields the displayed pair because $i(a+ib)=-b+ia$.\n\n(c) This is precisely the content of the cited integrable-causal-dispersion lemma: that item proves, under exactly these hypotheses, the holomorphy on the upper half-plane ([[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]]), the closed-half-plane bound, the boundary regularity and the two principal-value formulas, and records the instantaneous-response subtraction ([[lem-em-integrable-causal-dispersion]]). The present theorem assembles the response, its transfer function and the passivity relation around that lemma; its explicit AC definition and Lebesgue FTC/integration-by-parts route apply entrywise under the inherited Countable Choice and Dependent Choice; its boundary contour uses vertical shifts before passing to the real boundary.\n\n(d) The period-average of a product of real parts of complex exponentials is supplied by [[lem-em-period-average]], which states exactly $\\frac1T\\int_0^T\\operatorname{Re}(ae^{-i\\omega t})\\operatorname{Re}(de^{-i\\omega t})dt=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Re}(a\\cdot\\overline d)$; applying it with $a=e$ and $d=(-i\\omega)\\varepsilon_0\\chi(\\omega)e$ (the derivative of $t\\mapsto\\operatorname{Re}(\\varepsilon_0\\chi(\\omega)e\\,e^{-i\\omega t})$, computed with the chain rule) gives $$\\frac1T\\int_0^TE(t)\\cdot P'(t)\\,dt=\\frac12\\operatorname{Re}\\Bigl(e\\cdot\\overline{(-i\\omega)\\varepsilon_0\\chi(\\omega)e}\\Bigr)=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0\\omega}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\bigl(e^*\\chi(\\omega)e\\bigr),$$ using $e\\cdot\\overline{\\chi(\\omega)e}=\\overline{e^*\\chi(\\omega)e}$ and $\\operatorname{Re}(-iz)=\\operatorname{Im}z$ ([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]). Finally $e^*M(\\omega)e=(e^*\\chi(\\omega)e-e^*\\chi(\\omega)^*e)/(2i)=\\operatorname{Im}(e^*\\chi(\\omega)e)$ because $M(\\omega)$ is the Hermitian dissipative part of $\\chi(\\omega)$, so nonnegativity of the displayed average for every $e$ is exactly the stated quadratic-form condition. The logical independence of causality and this positivity (the design's 'no sign of dissipation follows merely from causality') is exhibited on the companion example page by a kernel satisfying (c) whose quadratic form is strictly negative.\n",
