          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as used by the declared polar/surface and change-of-variables suppliers. Let $c>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, $a>0$ and $t_0<T$ be constants, let $\\rho\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R)$ and $\\mathbf J\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R;\\mathbb R^3)$ satisfy (H1) $\\rho(y,v)=\\mathbf J(y,v)=0$ whenever $|y|>a$ or $v\\notin[t_0,T]$, and (H2) $\\partial_v\\rho+\\operatorname{div}_y\\mathbf J=0$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$. Here and below $B_a:=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^3:|y|<a\\}$ and $\\overline B_a:=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^3:|y|\\le a\\}$ are the open and closed Euclidean balls of radius $a$ about the origin, and $S^2=\\{n\\in\\mathbb R^3:|n|=1\\}$ is the unit sphere. On the open exterior $U:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:|x|>a\\}$ define the retarded scalar and vector potentials $$\\varphi(x,t):=\\frac{\\mu_0c^2}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\rho(y,t-|x-y|/c)}{|x-y|}\\,dy,\\qquad \\mathbf A(x,t):=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\mathbf J(y,t-|x-y|/c)}{|x-y|}\\,dy,$$ and the fields $\\mathbf E:=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_t\\mathbf A$, $\\mathbf B:=\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A$, where $\\nabla f=(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f,\\partial_3f)$, $\\operatorname{div}F=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_jF_j$ and $\\nabla\\times F=(\\partial_2F_3-\\partial_3F_2,\\partial_3F_1-\\partial_1F_3,\\partial_1F_2-\\partial_2F_1)$ are the spatial operators of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]] and [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]. The fields are defined on $U\\times\\mathbb R$ using these ordinary derivatives; the conclusions below use $r:=|x|\\ge2a$. Every displayed spatial integrand is evaluated on $\\overline B_a$ and extended by zero outside it, including at $y=x$, so no quotient $0/0$ is used. All vector-valued integrals are componentwise scalar Lebesgue integrals.\n\n(a) (Fields as retarded integrals.) On the open set $\\{|x|>2a\\}$ the potentials $\\varphi,\\mathbf A$ are of class $C^\\infty$ in $(x,t)$, and there $$\\mathbf E(x,t)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Bigl[\\frac{\\mathbf N}{R^2}c^2\\rho\\bigl(y,t-\\tfrac Rc\\bigr)+\\frac{c\\,\\mathbf N}{R}\\rho_t\\bigl(y,t-\\tfrac Rc\\bigr)-\\frac{\\mathbf J_t\\bigl(y,t-\\tfrac Rc\\bigr)}{R}\\Bigr]dy,\\qquad \\mathbf B(x,t)=-\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\mathbf N\\times\\Bigl[\\frac{\\mathbf J(y,t-\\frac Rc)}{R^2}+\\frac{\\mathbf J_t(y,t-\\frac Rc)}{cR}\\Bigr]dy,$$ where $R:=|x-y|$, $\\mathbf N:=(x-y)/R$, and $\\rho_t,\\mathbf J_t$ are the partial derivatives in the second variable. Moreover, on $\\{|x|\\ge2a\\}\\times[t_0,T]$, $\\varphi$ and each component of $\\mathbf A$ coincide with the corresponding solution constructed in the wave Cauchy problem of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] for zero Cauchy data at $t_0$ and scalar source $h=\\mu_0c^2\\rho$, respectively $h=\\mu_0 J_k$.\n\n(b) (Uniform far-zone expansion.) For $n\\in S^2$ and $u\\in[t_0,T]$ put $\\mathbf I(n,u):=\\int_{B_a}\\mathbf J(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)\\,dy$ and $\\mathbf V(n,u):=\\partial_u\\mathbf I(n,u)=\\int_{B_a}\\mathbf J_t(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)\\,dy$. Then $\\int_{B_a}\\rho_t(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)\\,dy=\\frac1c\\,n\\cdot\\mathbf V(n,u)$, and there are constants $\\Gamma_E,\\Gamma_B$, depending only on $a,c,\\mu_0$ and on the suprema of $|\\rho|,|\\rho_t|,|\\rho_{tt}|,|\\mathbf J|,|\\mathbf J_t|,|\\mathbf J_{tt}|$ on $\\overline B_a\\times[t_0,T]$, such that for every $n\\in S^2$, $u\\in[t_0,T]$ and $r\\ge2a$, with $\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}(n,u,r):=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}n\\times(n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u))$, $$\\Bigl|\\mathbf E(rn,u+\\tfrac rc)-\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}(n,u,r)\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{\\Gamma_E}{r^2},\\qquad \\Bigl|\\mathbf B(rn,u+\\tfrac rc)-\\frac{n\\times\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}(n,u,r)}{c}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{\\Gamma_B}{r^2}.$$\n\n(c) (Retarded-time flux limit.) Let $\\mathbf S:=\\mu_0^{-1}\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf B$ and let $d\\Omega$ be the surface measure on $S^2$ ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]). Then for every $u\\in[t_0,T]$, $$\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\int_{S^2}r^2\\,n\\cdot\\mathbf S\\bigl(rn,u+\\tfrac rc\\bigr)\\,d\\Omega(n)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{16\\pi^2c}\\int_{S^2}\\bigl|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)\\bigr|^2\\,d\\Omega(n).$$ This is a limit at fixed retarded time $u$, not at fixed observation time $t$; no decay or boundary condition at spatial infinity is assumed beyond the compact spatial support in (H1).",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct, with explicit uniform estimates; the polar-coordinate identity uses the wave Cauchy problem as the definition of the retarded solution.\n\n(a) On $\\{|x|>2a\\}$ one has $|x-y|\\ge a>0$ for $y\\in\\overline B_a$, so the integrands of $\\varphi$ and $\\mathbf A$ are of class $C^\\infty$ jointly in $(x,t,y)$ on a neighbourhood of $\\{|x|>2a\\}\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\overline B_a$; differentiating under the integral over the compact set $\\overline B_a$ in each parameter direction ([[lem-em-parameter-integrals]]) and using the chain rule for $(x,t)\\mapsto(y,t-|x-y|/c)$ gives $\\nabla_x[f(y,t-R/c)/R]=-\\mathbf N[f/R^2+f_t/(cR)]$ and $\\nabla_x\\times[\\mathbf J(y,t-R/c)/R]=-\\mathbf N\\times[\\mathbf J/R^2+\\mathbf J_t/(cR)]$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]); substituting into $\\mathbf E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_t\\mathbf A$ and $\\mathbf B=\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A$ gives the displayed field integrals and the smoothness claim. The identification with the wave Cauchy problem is the polar-coordinates identity: by [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], applied to $h=\\mu_0c^2\\rho$ and to each component $h=\\mu_0J_k$ (to the positive and negative parts, then subtracted), $$\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{h(y,t-|x-y|/c)}{|x-y|}\\,dy=\\int_0^\\infty r\\,M_rh(x,t-r/c)\\,dr,\\qquad M_rh(x,v)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}h(x+rn,v)\\,d\\sigma(n);$$ substituting $r=c(t-v)$ in the $r$-integral with the one-dimensional $L^1$ change-of-variables formula for the $C^1$ diffeomorphism $v\\mapsto c(t-v)$ ([[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]], applied on the compact interval where the continuous integrand is bounded) turns the right-hand side into $c^2\\int_{t_0}^t(t-v)M_{c(t-v)}h(\\cdot,v)(x)\\,dv$ for $t\\in[t_0,T]$, which is exactly the function displayed in clause (a) of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] for zero Cauchy data at $t_0$ and source $h$.\n\n(b) Elementary bounds in the Euclidean norm ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]): for $|y|\\le a$ and $r\\ge2a$ one has $R^2-(r-n\\cdot y)^2=|y|^2-(n\\cdot y)^2$ with $R+(r-n\\cdot y)\\ge r$, hence $|R-(r-n\\cdot y)|\\le a^2/r$, $|\\mathbf N-n|\\le(a+a^2/r)/(r-a)\\le5a/r$ and $|1/R-1/r|\\le(a+a^2/r)/(r(r-a))\\le2a/r^2$, uniformly in $n,y$. Consequently $t-R/c=u+n\\cdot y/c+O(a^2/(rc))$ uniformly, and comparing the field integrals of (a) with the candidate leading terms, each correction term (the $\\rho/R^2$-term, the $\\mathbf N-n$, $1/R-1/r$ and retarded-argument corrections) is bounded by an explicit constant times $r^{-2}$, the constants involving only $a,c,\\mu_0$ and the suprema of $|\\rho|,|\\rho_t|,|\\rho_{tt}|,|\\mathbf J|,|\\mathbf J_t|,|\\mathbf J_{tt}|$ on the compact set $\\overline B_a\\times[t_0,T]$ (mean value theorem in the time variable; [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]). The surviving terms of order $r^{-1}$ are $\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}\\bigl[c\\,n\\int_{B_a}\\rho_t(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)dy-\\int_{B_a}\\mathbf J_t(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)dy\\bigr]$, the second integral being $\\mathbf V(n,u)$ and the first computed from the continuity equation: applying the divergence theorem for a bounded $C^1$ Euclidean domain ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]], with $\\Omega$ a ball of radius $R>a$ containing the spatial support, whose boundary flux vanishes because $\\mathbf J$ vanishes off $B_a$) to $y\\mapsto\\mathbf J(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)$ gives $0=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\operatorname{div}_y[\\mathbf J(\\cdot,u+n\\cdot y/c)]=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}[\\operatorname{div}_y\\mathbf J+\\frac nc\\cdot\\mathbf J_t](y,u+n\\cdot y/c)dy$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]); by (H2) and continuity of $\\operatorname{div}_y\\mathbf J$, this is $\\int\\rho_t(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)dy=\\frac1c n\\cdot\\mathbf V(n,u)$. Substituting, $\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}[c\\,n\\,\\frac1c(n\\cdot\\mathbf V)-\\mathbf V]=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}[n(n\\cdot\\mathbf V)-\\mathbf V]=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}n\\times(n\\times\\mathbf V)$ by the vector triple-product identity, and the leading term of $\\mathbf B$ is $-\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi cr}n\\times\\mathbf V=\\frac{n\\times\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}}{c}$, so the stated uniform errors follow with the constants just described.\n\n(c) From the leading fields, $n\\cdot\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}=0$ and $\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}\\times\\mathbf B_{\\mathrm{lead}}=\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}\\times(n\\times\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}})/c=(|E_{\\mathrm{lead}}|^2n-(n\\cdot\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}})\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}})/c=|E_{\\mathrm{lead}}|^2n/c$, while $|\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}|=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}|n\\times(n\\times\\mathbf V)|=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}|n\\times\\mathbf V|$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). Hence $r^2n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,u+r/c)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{16\\pi^2c}|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)|^2+R(n,u,r)$ with $|R|\\le C/r$ uniformly in $n,u$ for an explicit $C$ obtained from $\\Gamma_E,\\Gamma_B$. The sphere is compact of finite surface measure, the map $n\\mapsto|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)|^2$ is continuous ([[lem-em-parameter-integrals]] gives smoothness of $\\mathbf I$, hence of $\\mathbf V$, in $n$), and uniform convergence of the integrands gives the displayed limit without any further limit/integration exchange ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]; the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention used by that definition is declared in the item contract, [[def-countable-choice]]).\n Scalar normalization check for (a): phi=mu0 c²/(4pi) integral rho(y,t-R/c)/R, so -grad phi=mu0/(4pi) integral [c²N rho/R²+c N rho_t/R]; subtracting A_t adds -mu0 J_t/(4pi R). The second electric term has one factor c, consistent with (b)'s c n integral rho_t and epsilon0^-1=mu0 c².",
