          "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct; part (a) differentiates the retarded integrals and identifies them with the wave Cauchy solution, part (b) expands the field integrals uniformly in the far zone and derives the continuity identity, part (c) passes to the surface integral.\n\n1.1 Smoothness of the potentials: for $x\\in U$ and $y\\in\\overline B_a$ one has $R=|x-y|\\ge|x|-a>0$, so the maps $(x,t,y)\\mapsto\\rho(y,t-R/c)/R$ and $(x,t,y)\\mapsto\\mathbf J(y,t-R/c)/R$ are $C^\\infty$ on a neighbourhood of $U\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\overline B_a$ by [F6] and [F7]. Apply [F5] to the fixed compact set $K=\\overline B_a$ once for each parameter direction with its one real parameter renamed to $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_3$ and $t$ (the remaining variables held fixed) and iterate on the resulting integrals: every differentiated integrand is again continuous on a neighbourhood of $K$, so each application is covered by the lemma and $\\varphi$ and each component of $\\mathbf A$ have continuous partial derivatives of every order on $U\\times\\mathbb R$, these differentiated parameter integrals are jointly continuous: on each compact parameter neighbourhood the continuous differentiated integrands are uniformly continuous on its product with $K$, so the integral of their difference is bounded by $\\lambda(K)$ times their uniformly vanishing difference [F2]. Hence the potentials are $C^\\infty$ on $U\\times\\mathbb R$ by [F7], so the derivatives defining the fields are ordinary derivatives even at $|x|=2a$. [F5, F6, F7, F3]\n\n1.2 Elementary estimates in the far zone: for $y\\in B_a$, $r\\ge2a$ and $n\\in S^2$, expanding $R^2=(r-\\sigma)^2+|y|^2-\\sigma^2$ with $\\sigma=n\\cdot y$ gives $|R-(r-\\sigma)|=\\bigl||y|^2-\\sigma^2\\bigr|/(R+r-\\sigma)\\le a^2/r$ because $R+r-\\sigma\\ge r-\\sigma+|r-\\sigma|\\ge r$; consequently $|r-R|\\le a+a^2/r$, $|\\mathbf N-n|=|(r-R)n-y|/R\\le(a+a^2/r+a)/(r-a)\\le5a/r$, $|1/R-1/r|\\le(a+a^2/r)/(r(r-a))\\le3a/r^2$, and $|\\delta_y|\\le a^2/(rc)$, all uniformly in $y,n$. [F12, F1]\n\n2.1 Field formulas: by [F6], $\\nabla_xR=\\mathbf N$, $\\nabla_x(t-R/c)=-\\mathbf N/c$ and $\\nabla_x\\times[\\mathbf J(y,t-R/c)]=-\\mathbf N\\times\\mathbf J_t(y,t-R/c)/c$, while $\\nabla_x(1/R)=-\\mathbf N/R^2$; hence $\\nabla_x[\\rho(y,t-R/c)/R]=-\\mathbf N[\\rho/R^2+\\rho_t/(cR)]$ and $\\nabla_x\\times[\\mathbf J(y,t-R/c)/R]=-\\mathbf N\\times[\\mathbf J/R^2+\\mathbf J_t/(cR)]$. Each of these first derivatives of the integrands is continuous on a neighbourhood of $\\overline B_a$, so by [F5] the derivatives may be taken under the integral: $-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_t\\mathbf A$ and $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A$ are given on $U$, in particular for $|x|\\ge2a$, by the two displayed zero-extended integrals of the statement, since $\\mathbf E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_t\\mathbf A$ and $\\mathbf B=\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A$. [step 1.1, F5, F6, F12]\n\n2.2 Polar-coordinates identity: fix $x\\in U$ and $t\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $h$ be either $\\rho$ or one component $J_k$; then $y\\mapsto h(y,t-|x-y|/c)/|x-y|$ vanishes off $\\overline B_a$ and $|x-y|\\ge|x|-a>0$ there, so the quotient on $\\overline B_a$, extended by zero elsewhere, defines a bounded compactly supported Borel function $f$ on all of $\\mathbb R^3$. The translation $T(z):=x+z$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism of $\\mathbb R^3$ with $\\det DT=1$, so [F14] applied to the positive and negative parts of $f$ gives $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}f(y)\\,dy=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}f(x+z)\\,dz$; applying [F9] to the positive and negative parts of $z\\mapsto f(x+z)$, which at $z=\\rho\\omega$ equals $h(x+\\rho\\omega,t-\\rho/c)/\\rho$ and therefore has inner angular integral $\\frac1\\rho\\int_{S^2}h(x+\\rho n,t-\\rho/c)\\,d\\Omega(n)=\\frac{4\\pi}{\\rho}M_\\rho h(x,t-\\rho/c)$, gives $\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{h(y,t-|x-y|/c)}{|x-y|}dy=\\int_0^\\infty r\\,M_rh(x,t-r/c)\\,dr$ with $M_rh(x,v)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}h(x+rn,v)\\,d\\Omega(n)$, because the inner angular integral is $4\\pi M_rh$ and the radial factor is $r^2/r=r$. [step 1.1, F9, F14, F1, F3]\n\n2.3 Continuity identity: fix $n,u$ and let $\\rho_0>a$; the map $F(y):=\\mathbf J(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)$ is $C^1$ on $\\overline B_{\\rho_0}$, and [F6] gives $\\operatorname{div}F(y)=\\operatorname{div}_y\\mathbf J(y,v_y)+\\frac nc\\cdot\\mathbf J_t(y,v_y)$ with $v_y=u+n\\cdot y/c$. By [F11] on $B_{\\rho_0}$, whose boundary flux vanishes because $\\mathbf J=0$ for $|y|>a$, $\\int_{B_{\\rho_0}}\\operatorname{div}F\\,dy=0$; substituting the chain-rule expression and using (H2), $0=\\int_{B_{\\rho_0}}\\bigl[-\\rho_t(y,v_y)+\\frac1cn\\cdot\\mathbf J_t(y,v_y)\\bigr]dy$. The integrands vanish for $|y|>a$ by (H1), so [F2] lets the integrals be taken over $B_a$; the second is $\\frac1cn\\cdot\\mathbf V(n,u)$ by the definition of $\\mathbf V$, whence $\\int_{B_a}\\rho_t(y,v_y)\\,dy=\\frac1cn\\cdot\\mathbf V(n,u)$. [step 1.2, F11, F6, F2]\n\n3.1 Identification with the Cauchy solution: fix $x\\in U$. For $t>t_0$ the radial integrand of step 2.2 vanishes when $r>c(t-t_0)$, because then its source time is less than $t_0$. Apply [F10] to the affine diffeomorphism $v\\mapsto c(t-v)$ from $(t_0,t)$ onto $(0,c(t-t_0))$, with absolute derivative $c$; the integrand is bounded on this finite interval, hence integrable. Endpoint values do not affect the Lebesgue integrals, and this gives $\\int_0^\\infty rM_rh(x,t-r/c)\\,dr=c^2\\int_{t_0}^t(t-v)M_{c(t-v)}h(x,v)\\,dv$. At $t=t_0$ both sides are zero by (H1), so the identity holds throughout $[t_0,T]$. Multiplying it by $\\mu_0c^2$ for $h=\\rho$, and by $\\mu_0$ for $h=J_k$, identifies $\\varphi$ and $A_k$ respectively with the zero-data Cauchy solutions of [F8] with sources $\\mu_0c^2\\rho$ and $\\mu_0J_k$. This holds on $U\\times[t_0,T]$ and in particular at $|x|=2a$. [step 2.2, F10, F8, F1]\n\n3.2 Uniform error estimates: fix $n,u,r$, let $\\mathrm{ret}$ denote the argument $v_y+\\delta_y$ and $0$ the argument $v_y$, and write $F_{\\mathrm{tot}}:=c^2\\mathbf N\\rho_{\\mathrm{ret}}/R^2+c\\mathbf N\\rho_{t,\\mathrm{ret}}/R-\\mathbf J_{t,\\mathrm{ret}}/R$ and $F_{\\mathrm{lead}}:=\\frac1r\\bigl(c\\,n\\,\\rho_{t,0}-\\mathbf J_{t,0}\\bigr)$, so that the field formula of step 2.1 and the definition of $\\mathbf V$ give $\\mathbf E(rn,u+r/c)-\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}(n,u,r)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{B_a}(F_{\\mathrm{tot}}-F_{\\mathrm{lead}})\\,dy$ by the linearity of the integral. Every source factor is bounded by its supremum on $\\overline B_a\\times[t_0,T]$, and (H1) makes $\\rho$ and $\\mathbf J$ vanish on the open set $\\mathbb R^3\\times((-\\infty,t_0)\\cup(T,\\infty))$, hence makes all their partial derivatives vanish there too, so these suprema also dominate the retarded arguments $v_y+\\delta_y$; the mean value theorem [F4] gives $|\\rho_{t,\\mathrm{ret}}-\\rho_{t,0}|\\le\\Gamma_{\\rho tt}|\\delta_y|$ and $|\\mathbf J_{t,\\mathrm{ret}}-\\mathbf J_{t,0}|\\le\\Gamma_{Jtt}|\\delta_y|$: when the difference is nonzero, apply [F4] to its scalar projection onto the unit direction of that difference, and use Cauchy-Schwarz from [F12], and using $R^{-1}\\le2/r$, $R^{-2}\\le4/r^2$ and $|\\mathbf N/R-n/r|\\le|\\mathbf N-n|/R+|1/R-1/r|\\le13a/r^2$ yields $|F_{\\mathrm{tot}}-F_{\\mathrm{lead}}|\\le\\Gamma_E'/r^2$, with $\\Gamma_E':=4c^2\\Gamma_\\rho+13ca\\Gamma_{\\rho t}+a^2\\Gamma_{\\rho tt}+3a\\Gamma_{Jt}+a^2\\Gamma_{Jtt}/c$ for all $r\\ge2a$; hence, by the triangle inequality for the integral and the finiteness of $\\lambda(B_a)$, $\\bigl|\\mathbf E(rn,u+r/c)-\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}(n,u,r)\\bigr|\\le\\Gamma_E/r^2$ with $\\Gamma_E:=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\Gamma_E'\\lambda(B_a)$. The same computation applied to $\\mathbf B(rn,u+r/c)=-\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{B_a}\\mathbf N\\times[\\mathbf J_{\\mathrm{ret}}/R^2+\\mathbf J_{t,\\mathrm{ret}}/(cR)]dy$ and to $n\\times\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}/c=-\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{B_a}\\frac1{cr}n\\times\\mathbf J_{t,0}\\,dy$ gives $\\bigl|\\mathbf B(rn,u+r/c)-n\\times\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}(n,u,r)/c\\bigr|\\le\\Gamma_B/r^2$, for $\\Gamma_B:=\\frac{\\mu_0\\lambda(B_a)}{4\\pi}(4\\Gamma_J+13a\\Gamma_{Jt}/c+a^2\\Gamma_{Jtt}/c^2)$. [step 1.2, step 2.3, F2, F3, F4, F12]\n\n4.1 Smoothness of the far-zone moments: the map $(n,u)\\mapsto\\mathbf I(n,u)=\\int_{B_a}\\mathbf J(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)\\,dy$ and its partial derivative $\\partial_u\\mathbf I=\\int_{B_a}\\mathbf J_t(y,u+n\\cdot y/c)\\,dy=\\mathbf V(n,u)$ are $C^\\infty$ in $(n,u)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$: apply [F5] repeatedly in the directions $n_1,n_2,n_3,u$ with the fixed compact set $\\overline B_a$, each differentiated integrand again being continuous on a neighbourhood of $\\overline B_a$; the integrals over $B_a$ and $\\overline B_a$ agree because the smooth sources and all their derivatives vanish on $|y|=a$ by continuity from $|y|>a$. In particular $n\\mapsto|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)|^2$ is continuous for each fixed $u$. [step 3.2, F5, F6, F7, F12]\n\n4.2 Pointwise flux asymptotics: by step 3.2 write $\\mathbf E=\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}+\\boldsymbol\\rho_E$ and $\\mathbf B=n\\times\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}/c+\\boldsymbol\\rho_B$ at $(rn,u+r/c)$ with $|\\boldsymbol\\rho_E|\\le\\Gamma_E/r^2$, $|\\boldsymbol\\rho_B|\\le\\Gamma_B/r^2$; then $n\\cdot\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}=0$ and $\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}\\times(n\\times\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}})=|\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}|^2n$ by [F12], while $|\\mathbf E_{\\mathrm{lead}}|=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi r}|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)|$ because $|n\\times(n\\times\\mathbf V)|=|n\\times\\mathbf V|$. Expanding the cross product bilinearly, the error terms are $O(r^{-3})$, so $r^2n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,u+r/c)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{16\\pi^2c}|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)|^2+R(n,u,r)$ with $|R(n,u,r)|\\le C/r$ for a constant $C$ depending only on $a,c,\\mu_0$ and the source suprema, uniformly in $n\\in S^2$ and $u\\in[t_0,T]$. [step 3.2, F12, F2]\n\n5.1 Flux limit: by [F13] the surface integral over the compact sphere of a bounded Borel function is finite and monotone, so for every $u\\in[t_0,T]$ and $r\\ge2a$ the difference $\\int_{S^2}r^2n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,u+r/c)\\,d\\Omega(n)-\\frac{\\mu_0}{16\\pi^2c}\\int_{S^2}|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)|^2\\,d\\Omega(n)=\\int_{S^2}R(n,u,r)\\,d\\Omega(n)$ has absolute value at most $\\frac{C}{r}\\int_{S^2}1\\,d\\Omega$, which tends to $0$; the function $n\\mapsto|n\\times\\mathbf V(n,u)|^2$ is continuous by step 4.1, so its surface integral is the displayed finite constant. Hence the stated limit at fixed retarded time $u$ follows; no step compares different values of $u$, so the limit is asserted only at fixed retarded time. [step 4.2, step 4.1, F13, F2] ∎"
        }
      ],
