          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the surface-integral conventions used by [[thm-em-radiation-asymptotic-flux]].\n\n(i) (Causality without passivity.) In this clause also assume Dependent Choice ([[def-dependent-choice]]), inherited from the exact AC supplier. Let $K(s):=-e^{-s}$ for $s\\ge0$. Then $K$ is of class $C^1$ on $[0,\\infty)$ with $K,K'\\in L^1(0,\\infty)$ and $sK(s)\\in L^1(0,\\infty)$ (indeed $K'=e^{-s}=-K$, $\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s}ds=1$ and $\\int_0^\\infty se^{-s}ds=1$), so the integrable branch of [[thm-em-causal-dispersive-response]] applies: $P(t)=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)E(t-s)ds$ is a bounded causal linear response on bounded continuous histories, its transfer function is $\\chi(z)=-1/(1-iz)$ on $\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0$, and its boundary value $\\chi(\\omega)=-(1+i\\omega)/(1+\\omega^2)$ satisfies $\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)=-\\omega/(1+\\omega^2)<0$ for every $\\omega>0$. Hence the Hermitian dissipative part $M(\\omega)=\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)$ is strictly negative, not positive semidefinite, and the monochromatic period-averaged work of clause (d) of that theorem is strictly negative for $d=1$, every $e\\ne0$ and every $\\omega>0$ (with any response normalization $\\varepsilon_0>0$), although the response is causal and its entry satisfies the dispersion relations of clause (c). Nonnegative period-averaged work (passivity) is therefore an additional condition on the model, not a consequence of temporal causality.\n\n(ii) (The flux limit needs fixed retarded time.) In the setting of [[thm-em-radiation-asymptotic-flux]], fix $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $n\\in S^2$, and let $r>\\max\\{2a,\\;a+c(t-t_0)\\}$. Then for every $y\\in B_a$ one has $R=|rn-y|\\ge r-a>c(t-t_0)$, so $t-R/c<t_0$; by hypothesis (H1) of that theorem the functions $\\rho,\\rho_t,\\mathbf J,\\mathbf J_t$ all vanish at $(y,t-R/c)$, so every integrand in the field formulas of clause (a) of that theorem vanishes and $\\mathbf E(rn,t)=\\mathbf B(rn,t)=0$, hence $n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,t)=0$ for every $n\\in S^2$. Therefore $\\int_{S^2}r^2\\,n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,t)\\,d\\Omega(n)=0$ for all such $r$: at fixed observation time the far-zone flux integral is identically zero beyond the light cone of the compact spacetime support, whereas clause (c) of that theorem is a limit at fixed retarded time. These limits may agree for a particular source, including the zero source. They cannot be interchanged as a general theorem: the conserved smooth sources constructed below have a strictly positive fixed-retarded-time flux limit.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct computation and direct substitution into the two cited theorems.\n\n(i) The real exponential is differentiable with $\\exp'=\\exp$ ([[thm-derivative-of-exponential]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]]) and $\\exp(-s)>0$ with $\\exp(-s)=1/\\exp(s)$ ([[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]]), so $s\\mapsto K(s)=-e^{-s}$ is of class $C^1$ on $[0,\\infty)$ with $K'=e^{-s}=-K$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). For every $N>0$ the Newton-Leibniz formula gives $\\int_0^Ne^{-s}ds=1-e^{-N}$ and integration by parts (with $u=s$, $v=-e^{-s}$) gives $\\int_0^Nse^{-s}ds=1-(1+N)e^{-N}$ ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], [[thm-integration-by-parts]]); the continuous real integrands have the same Lebesgue integrals by [[thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral]]; [[thm-c1-lipschitz-ac-bv-hierarchy]] proves the explicit $C^1$ kernel is absolutely continuous on each compact interval in the sense of [[def-absolutely-continuous-function]]; the displayed Newton-Leibniz integral representations agree with the Lebesgue FTC under this regularity. Thus it has the absolute-continuity property required by the integrable branch of [[thm-em-causal-dispersive-response]], and passing to the limit with monotone convergence for the nonnegative integrands gives $\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s}ds=\\int_0^\\infty se^{-s}ds=1$, so $K,K',sK(s)\\in L^1(0,\\infty)$ ([[thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral]], [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]). Clause (a) of the theorem therefore gives the bounded causal response. For the transfer function, fix real $\\omega$; the complex exponential is entire with derivative itself and the complex chain rule gives that $s\\mapsto F(s)=\\exp((-1+i\\omega)s)/(-1+i\\omega)$ is a primitive of $s\\mapsto\\exp((-1+i\\omega)s)$ on $\\mathbb C$ ([[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-complex-derivatives]], [[def-complex-exponential]]), so the fundamental theorem for complex line integrals along the interval path $[0,N]\\ni s\\mapsto s$ gives $\\int_{[0,N]}\\exp((-1+i\\omega)s)\\,ds=F(N)-F(0)$ ([[thm-fundamental-theorem-for-complex-line-integrals]], [[def-complex-primitive]], [[def-complex-line-integral-over-a-rectifiable-path]], [[thm-riemann-stieltjes-and-parametric-contour-integrals-agree]]). Hence $\\int_0^N(-e^{-s})e^{i\\omega s}ds=-(F(N)-F(0))$, and $\\left|e^{(-1+i\\omega)N}\\right|=e^{-N}\\to0$ ([[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]); letting $N\\to\\infty$ with dominated convergence for the integrands dominated by $e^{-s}$ gives $\\chi(\\omega)=-1/(1-i\\omega)$, and the same computation with $z$ in place of $\\omega$ gives $\\chi(z)=-1/(1-iz)$ for $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$ ([[thm-dominated-convergence]]). Taking real and imaginary parts ([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]) gives $\\chi(\\omega)=-(1+i\\omega)/(1+\\omega^2)$ and $\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)=-\\omega/(1+\\omega^2)<0$ because $\\omega>0$. With $d=1$, $e=1$ and $\\varepsilon_0>0$, clause (d) of the theorem gives period-averaged work $\\frac{\\varepsilon_0\\omega}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)<0$ and $M(\\omega)=\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)<0$, so the passivity condition fails while causality holds: this witnesses the logical independence recorded in the theorem's proof discussion.\n\n(ii) For $y\\in B_a$ the reverse triangle inequality gives $R=|rn-y|\\ge r-a>c(t-t_0)$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), hence $t-R/c<t_0$ and all four source functions vanish at the arguments appearing in the field integrals of clause (a) of [[thm-em-radiation-asymptotic-flux]]; each integrand is identically zero on $B_a$, so $\\mathbf E(rn,t)=\\mathbf B(rn,t)=0$ and therefore $n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,t)=\\mu_0^{-1}n\\cdot(\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf B)(rn,t)=0$ for every $n\\in S^2$. The surface integral of the zero function vanishes ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]), so the fixed-observation-time flux integral is $0$ for every $r>\\max\\{2a,a+c(t-t_0)\\}$, while the limit displayed in clause (c) of that theorem is taken with $u$ fixed and $r\\to\\infty$ along $t=u+r/c$; the two formulations therefore differ and the retarded-time one is essential.\n",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
