          "proof": "**Proof technique:** direct computation for clause (i); direct substitution into the cited theorem and the triangle inequality for clause (ii).\n\n1.1 The kernel $K(s)=-e^{-s}$ is of class $C^1$ on $[0,\\infty)$ with $K'=e^{-s}=-K$ by [F1] and [F6]; $K(s)<0$ for every $s$, and $|K(s)|=e^{-s}$, $|K'(s)|=e^{-s}$, $|sK(s)|=se^{-s}$. Newton–Leibniz gives $\\int_0^Ne^{-s}ds=1-e^{-N}$ and integration by parts gives $\\int_0^Nse^{-s}ds=1-(1+N)e^{-N}$ for every $N$, and [F2] converts these to Lebesgue integrals; for $N>0$ the nonnegative exponential series gives $e^N\\ge N^2/2$ by [F1], so $e^{-N}\\to0$ and $Ne^{-N}\\le2/N\\to0$; since the integrands are nonnegative, [F3] lets $N\\to\\infty$, giving $\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s}ds=1$ and $\\int_0^\\infty se^{-s}ds=1$. Hence $K,K',sK\\in L^1(0,\\infty)$ and, by [F5], $K$ is locally absolutely continuous on $[0,\\infty)$. [F1, F2, F3, F5, F6]\n\n1.2 A conserved smooth source with nonzero radiation. For the constants of clause (ii), put $u_*:=(t_0+T)/2$, $b:=(T-t_0)/8$ and $\\epsilon:=\\min(a/2,bc/2)>0$. By [F13] choose $\\psi:\\mathbb R^3\\to[0,1]$ smooth, equal to $1$ on $\\overline B_{\\epsilon/2}$ and supported in $B_\\epsilon$, and $\\theta:\\mathbb R\\to[0,1]$ smooth, equal to $1$ on $[-b,b]$ and supported in $(-2b,2b)$. Define $f(v):=(v-u_*)^2\\theta(v-u_*)$, $\\rho(y,v):=-\\partial_3\\psi(y)f(v)$ and $\\mathbf J(y,v):=(0,0,\\psi(y)f'(v))$. These functions are smooth by [F13], their spatial support is contained in $B_\\epsilon\\subset B_a$, and their temporal support is contained in $(u_*-2b,u_*+2b)\\subset(t_0,T)$, so (H1) holds. Direct coordinate differentiation gives $\\rho_t=-\\partial_3\\psi\\,f'$ and $\\operatorname{div}_y\\mathbf J=\\partial_3\\psi\\,f'$, proving (H2). By [F14] and monotonicity, $C_\\psi:=\\int_{B_a}\\psi(y)\\,dy$ satisfies $0<\\lambda(B_{\\epsilon/2})\\le C_\\psi\\le\\lambda(B_\\epsilon)<\\infty$. [F13, F14, F6, F4, construct]\n\n2.1 Therefore the integrable branch of clause (c) of [F9] applies to $K$: $P(t)=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)E(t-s)ds$ is a bounded causal linear response on bounded continuous histories by clause (a), $\\chi(z)=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)e^{izs}ds$ is well defined and holomorphic on $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, continuous on the closed upper half-plane, and its boundary values satisfy the dispersion relations of clause (c). [step 1.1, F9, F4]\n\n2.2 Positive fixed-retarded-time flux. If $y\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\psi$ and $n\\in S^2$, then $|n\\cdot y|/c\\le|y|/c<\\epsilon/c\\le b/2$, so $\\theta=1$ near $u_*+n\\cdot y/c-u_*$ and $f''(u_*+n\\cdot y/c)=2$. Thus $\\mathbf V(n,u_*)=2C_\\psi p$ for every $n$, where $p:=(0,0,1)$. Orthogonal invariance in [F14] makes the three integrals $\\int_{S^2}n_j^2\\,d\\Omega$ equal; their sum is $\\int_{S^2}|n|^2\\,d\\Omega=4\\pi$, so each is $4\\pi/3$. The cross-product coordinates give $|n\\times p|^2=n_1^2+n_2^2=1-n_3^2$, hence $\\int_{S^2}|n\\times p|^2\\,d\\Omega=8\\pi/3$. Clause (c) of [F10] now gives the fixed-retarded-time limit $\\frac{\\mu_0}{16\\pi^2c}\\,4C_\\psi^2\\,\\frac{8\\pi}{3}=\\frac{2\\mu_0C_\\psi^2}{3\\pi c}>0$. [step 1.2, F10, F11, F14, algebra]\n\n3.1 Computation of the transfer function: fix $z$ with $\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0$ and put $\\lambda:=iz-1$, so $\\lambda\\ne0$ and $|e^{\\lambda N}|=e^{-N}e^{-N\\operatorname{Im}z}\\le e^{-N}\\to0$ by [F8]. For every $N>0$ the function $s\\mapsto e^{\\lambda s}$ is continuous, so its Lebesgue integral over $(0,N)$ agrees with its Riemann and parametric line integral over $[0,N]$ by [F2] and [F7], and the first fundamental theorem of [F7] applied to the primitive $F(s)=e^{\\lambda s}/\\lambda$ gives $\\int_0^Ne^{\\lambda s}ds=(e^{\\lambda N}-1)/\\lambda$; the truncated integrands $s\\mapsto-e^{\\lambda s}\\mathbf 1_{(0,N)}(s)$ converge pointwise to $s\\mapsto-e^{\\lambda s}$ and are dominated by $e^{-s}\\in L^1(0,\\infty)$, so [F4] gives $\\chi(z)=\\int_0^\\infty(-e^{\\lambda s})ds=-\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}(e^{\\lambda N}-1)/\\lambda=1/\\lambda=-1/(1-iz)$, where for $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$ the first equality is the defining integral of clause (c) of [F9] and for real $z$ it is its continuous boundary value. [step 2.1, F7, F8, F4, F2]\n\n4.1 Boundary values and the dissipative part: for real $\\omega$, step 3.1 gives $\\chi(\\omega)=-1/(1-i\\omega)=-(1+i\\omega)/(1+\\omega^2)$ by multiplication with the conjugate denominator, so $\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)=-\\omega/(1+\\omega^2)<0$ for every $\\omega>0$; for $d=1$ and $e\\ne0$, $e^*\\chi(\\omega)e=|e|^2\\chi(\\omega)$ and $e^*M(\\omega)e=|e|^2\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)<0$, so the Hermitian dissipative part $M(\\omega)=\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)$ is strictly negative and in particular not positive semidefinite. By clause (d) of [F9] the monochromatic period-averaged work equals $(\\varepsilon_0\\omega/2)|e|^2\\operatorname{Im}\\chi(\\omega)$, which is strictly negative for every $e\\ne0$ and every $\\varepsilon_0>0$, while steps 2.1 and 3.1 exhibit the response as causal with dispersion relations. Passivity is therefore an additional condition and not a consequence of causality. [step 3.1, F9, F8, F4]\n\n5.1 Clause (ii): fix $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $n\\in S^2$ and $r>\\max\\{2a,a+c(t-t_0)\\}$, so $r>2a$ and $r-a>c(t-t_0)$. For every $y\\in B_a$ the triangle inequality of [F11] gives $R=|rn-y|\\ge r-|y|\\ge r-a>c(t-t_0)$, hence $t-R/c<t_0$; since (H1) makes $\\rho$ and $\\mathbf J$ vanish on the open set $\\mathbb R^3\\times((-\\infty,t_0)\\cup(T,\\infty))$, all their partial derivatives vanish there too, so $\\rho,\\rho_t,\\mathbf J,\\mathbf J_t$ all vanish at $(y,t-R/c)$ for every $y\\in B_a$, and the integrands of the field formulas of clause (a) of [F10] vanish pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$. Hence $\\mathbf E(rn,t)=\\mathbf B(rn,t)=0$, so $n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,t)=\\mu_0^{-1}n\\cdot(\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf B)(rn,t)=0$ for every $n\\in S^2$, and [F12] gives $\\int_{S^2}r^2n\\cdot\\mathbf S(rn,t)\\,d\\Omega(n)=0$ for all such $r$. [step 4.1, F10, F11, F12]\n\n6.1 For every admissible source, step 5.1 gives an eventually zero flux integral at fixed observation time. For the admissible source of step 1.2, step 2.2 gives a strictly positive limit along $t=u_*+r/c$. Consequently a fixed-observation-time limit cannot replace the fixed-retarded-time limit as a theorem about all these sources; zero or nonradiating sources may give zero in both limits. Together with step 4.1, this proves both claimed hypothesis boundaries. [step 4.1, step 5.1, step 1.2, step 2.2] ∎"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 316
    },
