            "def-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-limit-point-r": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-standard-basis-of-f-n": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-open-and-closed-in-r": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-continuity-real": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Identify $\\mathbb R$ with $\\mathbb R^1$ by $t\\mapsto(t)$, the function on $\\{0\\}$ with value $t$. This bijection preserves distances because $\\lVert(t)-(r)\\rVert_2=|t-r|$; the symbols $u=1$ and $\\nabla F=m$ below use this identification.\n\n(i) Specialise [[thm-em-uncertainty-analysis]] to $n=1$, $U=\\mathbb R$, $\\theta_0=0$, $u=1$, $I=\\mathbb R$ and $F(\\theta)=m\\theta$ with $m>0$. Then $F$ is $C^1$, $F(0)=0$, $\\nabla F(\\theta)=m$ and $\\langle\\nabla F(\\theta),1\\rangle=m\\ge m$, so the theorem's hypothesis holds with equality. Fix the recorded value $\\hat y=0$ (so $s=0$) and any $\\delta\\ge0$. A candidate $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$ is compatible with the calibration bound exactly when $|F(\\theta)-\\hat y|=|m\\theta|\\le\\delta$, that is, exactly when $|\\theta|\\le\\delta/m$; this is precisely the theorem's bound $(s+\\delta)/m=\\delta/m$, and the bound is attained (equality) at $\\theta=\\pm\\delta/m$. Hence the constant $(s+\\delta)/m$ cannot be improved for this instance, and the special case $\\delta=0$ leaves $\\theta=0$ as the only compatible candidate. (ii) The positive lower bound on the response is not removable. If it is dropped, take $m=0$ and $F\\equiv0$ on $\\mathbb R$: the hypothesis $\\langle\\nabla F(\\theta),u\\rangle\\ge m>0$ fails, and with the same data $\\hat y=0$, $s=0$, $\\delta\\ge0$ every $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$ is compatible, because $y=0$ and $|y-\\hat y|=0\\le\\delta$; no finite parameter bound then follows from the indicator and the two bounds alone. (iii) These are mathematical observations about a hypothetical bounded-error model: no experimental data, sampling law or statistical inference is used or asserted, and the displayed numbers are stipulated parameters, not measurements.",
          "strategy": "(i) The derivative of $F(\\theta)=m\\theta$ is $F'(\\theta)=m$: the identity $\\theta\\mapsto\\theta^1$ has derivative $1$ and scalar multiples inherit the scaled derivative ([[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). With $n=1$ the gradient is the derivative, $\\nabla F(\\theta)=(m)$ ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]), and the pairing with $u=1$ is $\\langle\\nabla F(\\theta),1\\rangle=m\\cdot1=m$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]); also $F(0)=0$ and $\\lVert u\\rVert_2=1$, so every hypothesis of the theorem is met. Multiplying the inequality $|\\theta|\\le\\delta/m$ by $m>0$ gives $|m\\theta|\\le\\delta$ and conversely, using $|m\\theta|=m|\\theta|$ and the defining clauses of the absolute value ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-abs-value]]); at $\\theta=\\pm\\delta/m$ one has $|m\\theta|=\\delta$, so equality holds in the bound while the candidate remains compatible; and for $\\delta=0$ the inequality $|m\\theta|\\le0$ forces $m\\theta=0$, hence $\\theta=0$. (ii) For $F\\equiv0$ the gradient vanishes ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]), so $\\langle\\nabla F(\\theta),u\\rangle=0$ is not $\\ge m$ for any $m>0$ and the theorem's hypothesis is not met; nevertheless $y=F(\\theta)=0=\\hat y$ for every $\\theta$, so $|y-\\hat y|=0\\le\\delta$ and $|\\hat y|=0\\le s$ hold for every $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$: the data cannot confine the parameter at all. (iii) The comparisons are order arithmetic in $\\mathbb R$ on stipulated quantities; no probabilistic or empirical claim is made ([[def-abs-value]], [[lem-of-abs-value]]).",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis I: Introduction to Real Analysis, Volume I (open textbook), section 4.1.1 (Example 4.1.3 and Proposition 4.1.7: affine derivatives and linearity) and section 4.2.4 (monotonicity from the sign of the derivative), printed pp. 154-168, version 6.3 (May 15, 2026); used only as the published companion of the cited derivative items, not as the source of the computed sharpness example",
                "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/realanal.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 318
