            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral",
            "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function",
            "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable",
            "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
            "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-compact-field-variation": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-fundamental-variation-lemma": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-derivative-of-a-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-borel-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Two exact boundary checks. (i) Stationarity is first order. On $\\mathbb R^2$ the polynomial $S(x,y)=x^2-y^2$ has differential $DS(0,0):\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ equal to the zero map at $(0,0)$ in the total-derivative sense of [[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], while $S(\\varepsilon,0)=\\varepsilon^2>0$ and $S(0,\\varepsilon)=-\\varepsilon^2<0$ for every $\\varepsilon\\ne0$; a point where the first variation vanishes need not be a minimum. Consequently the equivalence of [[lem-em-compact-field-variation]] between stationarity and the coefficient equation, and the conversion by [[lem-em-fundamental-variation-lemma]] of a vanishing first variation into pointwise equations, are first-order conditions: they carry no minimality and no existence content, and the example involves no adopted physical action. (ii) Compact support of the variation is essential. Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) for this Lebesgue-functional branch, exactly as in the compact-field supplier; the polynomial branch (i) remains choice-free. Take $U=(-1,1)^4$, $\\kappa=1$, $J=0$ and the smooth field $A$ with $A_0(x)=(x^1)^2$ and $A_1=A_2=A_3=0$, in the conventions of [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]]. Then $F_{01}=-2x^1$, $F_{10}=2x^1$, $F^{01}=\\eta^{00}\\eta^{11}F_{01}=2x^1$ and $F^{10}=-2x^1$, with every remaining component vanishing, so $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu0}=\\partial_1F^{10}=-2\\ne0$ and the coefficient equation $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\kappa J^\\nu$ fails at $\\nu=0$. Nevertheless the variation $h$ with $h_0\\equiv1$ and $h_1=h_2=h_3=0$ is smooth but not compactly supported in $U$; along it $A_0+sh_0=(x^1)^2+s$, so $F_{01}$ and the integrand $L=2(x^1)^2$ are independent of $s$ and $S[A+sh]=\\int_U2(x^1)^2\\,d\\lambda$ is constant in $s$, with first variation $0$ although the field equation fails. For the complementary stationarity-boundary check take instead $A_0=x^1$, the other covariant components zero, $J=0$, and $h_0=x^1$, $h_i=0$ (so $A^0=-x^1$, $h^0=-x^1$). Here $F_{01}=-1$ is constant, so the coefficient equations all hold, but this smooth noncompact direction gives $S[A+sh]=8(1+s)^2$ and derivative $16\\ne0$ at zero. Thus extending the compact stationarity condition to all smooth noncompact directions would invalidate the converse. These two witnesses show that the stated compact-support hypothesis cannot simply be removed: its coefficient conversion has no boundary term precisely in its declared compact class. Other variation classes require their own boundary hypotheses.",
          "strategy": "Direct computations. Part (i): apply the total-derivative remainder criterion to the zero linear map, using $|a^2-b^2|/\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\le\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\to0$, and evaluate $S$ at arbitrarily small axis points with opposite strict signs. Part (ii): substitute $A+sh$ into $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, raise both indices with $\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}\\eta^{\\nu\\beta}$, read off $\\partial_1F^{10}=-2$, then evaluate $L=-\\frac14F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}+J^\\nu A_\\nu=2(x^1)^2$ and observe that it does not depend on $s$. Both parts are exact finite computations; no physical postulate, physical action or empirical claim is used. The example checks the sign and index conventions of [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]] and the two lemmas' hypothesis boundaries. The assumed Countable Choice is the field lemma's measure convention in (ii), not an axiom of the finite polynomial branch. For the second witness the covariant potential is $A_0(s)=(1+s)x^1$, so $F_{01}(s)=-(1+s)$, $F_{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\mu\\nu}=-2(1+s)^2$ and $L(s)=(1+s)^2/2$. The box has volume16, hence $S(s)=8(1+s)^2$ and $S'(0)=16$. At the base field its constant $F$ has zero divergence and $J=0$. This explicitly demonstrates failure of stationarity against the enlarged noncompact class even when the coefficient equations hold; the first witness demonstrates the omitted boundary term in the IBP conversion. No empirical or physical action claim is added.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Figueroa-O'Farrill, Brief Notes on the Calculus of Variations (University of Edinburgh), §8 'Second variation' (printed pp. 10–11): an indefinite Hessian gives neither a maximum nor a minimum, the finite-dimensional model of part (i)",
                "url": "https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~jmf/Teaching/Lectures/CoV.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 319
    },
    {
      "id": "em-expanded-analysis-prerequisites-examples",
      "title": "Em Expanded Analysis Prerequisites — Examples",
      "kind": "B",
      "category": "classical-electromagnetism-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "em-expanded-analysis-prerequisites",
      "requires": [
        "em-expanded-analysis-prerequisites"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-em-outgoing-dtn-and-pec-zero-mode-boundaries",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Outgoing DtN coefficient at degree zero and the explicit PEC topology zero mode",
          "reference": "mathematical-example-verifications.md#outgoing-dtn",
          "design_state": "complete-explicit-mathematical-example",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "lem-em-outgoing-spherical-hankel-dtn-bounds",
            "lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes",
            "lem-laplace-fundamental-solution-is-harmonic-off-its-pole",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-outgoing-spherical-hankel-dtn-bounds": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-laplace-fundamental-solution-is-harmonic-off-its-pole": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), as required by the cavity supplier (and hence its Countable-Choice harmonic supplier). The following are explicit mathematical verifications of clauses of [[lem-em-outgoing-spherical-hankel-dtn-bounds]] and [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]]; no measurement, physical identification or empirical claim is made.\n\n(i) At degree $l=0$ the outgoing radial function of the spherical Hankel lemma is $h_0(z)=-ie^{iz}/z$, so for $k,R>0$ the Dirichlet-to-Neumann coefficient of [[lem-em-outgoing-spherical-hankel-dtn-bounds]] is\n\n$$t_0=\\frac{k\\,h_0'(kR)}{h_0(kR)}=ik-\\frac1R,$$\n\nwhose real part $-1/R$ is negative and whose imaginary part $k$ is positive, exactly as the exact bounds of that lemma require in degree zero.\n\n(ii) Fix $0<a<b$. In the spherical shell $a<|x|<b$, the field $u(x)=\\operatorname{grad}|x|^{-1}=-x/|x|^3$ is smooth on the closure, nonzero, normal to both boundary spheres, curl-free and divergence-free: $u=\\nabla\\Phi$ with $\\Phi(x)=|x|^{-1}$ harmonic off the origin, so $\\operatorname{curl}u=\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\Phi=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}u=\\Delta\\Phi=0$ there. Thus $u$ lies in the PEC class $X$ of the cavity spectral lemma with zero curl, and it has curl eigenvalue zero: eliminating all topology zero modes would incorrectly discard this explicit admissible vector field.",
          "strategy": "(i) Direct computation: with $h_0(z)=-ie^{iz}/z$, logarithmic differentiation using the algebra of derivatives and the derivative of the complex exponential ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]]) gives $h_0'(z)=h_0(z)\\,(i-1/z)$, hence $kh_0'(kR)/h_0(kR)=ik-1/R$ with real part $-1/R<0$ and imaginary part $k>0$; both agree with the exact bounds of the spherical Hankel lemma ([[lem-em-outgoing-spherical-hankel-dtn-bounds]]). (ii) The field $-x/|x|^3$ equals the gradient of $|x|^{-1}$ off the origin; the curl of a gradient vanishes ([[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]]) and its divergence is the Laplacian of the fundamental solution, which vanishes off the pole ([[lem-laplace-fundamental-solution-is-harmonic-off-its-pole]]); on each boundary sphere $|x|=c$ the field $-x/c^3$ is a nonzero multiple of the outward normal, so its tangential part is zero and it belongs to the weak PEC class of the cavity lemma ([[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]]); its curl is zero pointwise, so it is a nonzero vector in the kernel of the curl form, and such a vector is exactly a zero eigenvalue solution that any complete spectral description of the PEC class must retain."
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 320
    },
    {
      "id": "em-causal-wave-kernel-prerequisites",
      "title": "Causal kernels, gauge and point fields after wave existence",
      "category": "classical-electromagnetism-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "em-causal-wave-kernel-prerequisites-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "em-wave-equation-prerequisites",
        "density-separability-and-convolution-in-lp",
        "fubini-and-change-of-variables",
        "the-topology-of-euclidean-space"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
          "kind": "definition",
