            "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), used through the sphere and polar integrations below ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]]). Let $c>0$, write $Q:=\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, $\\mathcal D:=\\mathcal D(Q)$ for the test-function space of [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]], and $C:=\\{(y,t)\\in Q:t=|y|/c\\}$ for the retarded cone. Define the functional $K$ on test functions by $$\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle:=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\psi(y,|y|/c)}{|y|}\\,dy\\qquad(\\psi\\in\\mathcal D).$$ Then: (a) the integral converges absolutely for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$, the functional $K$ is a distribution on $Q$ ([[def-distribution]]), $(K,\\psi)=0$ for every $\\psi$ with $\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\cap C=\\varnothing$, and for every $(y_0,t_0)\\in C$ and every neighbourhood $U$ of $(y_0,t_0)$ there is $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$ with $\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\subseteq U$ and $\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle\\ne0$; consequently $\\operatorname{supp}K=C$. (b) For every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(Q)$ the function $$(K*h)(x,t):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{h(y,\\,t-|x-y|/c)}{4\\pi\\,|x-y|}\\,dy$$ is well defined (the integral converges absolutely), of class $C^\\infty$ on $Q$, and for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$ $$\\langle K*h,\\psi\\rangle=\\langle h,\\,K\\circledast\\psi\\rangle,\\qquad (K\\circledast\\psi)(y,s):=\\langle K,\\psi(y+\\cdot,s+\\cdot)\\rangle=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int\\frac{\\psi(y+z,s+|z|/c)}{|z|}\\,dz;$$ moreover $W(K*h)=h$ on $Q$ in the classical pointwise sense, where $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ is the wave expression of [[def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed]] and [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]. (c) Read $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ as the distributional operator built from [[def-distributional-derivative]]; then $WK=\\delta_{(0,0)}$ in the sense of [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]], that is, $(WK)(\\psi)=\\psi(0,0)$ for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$. This identifies $K$ as the retarded fundamental solution of $W$; no smoothness of $K$ itself on the cone is asserted, and the advanced fundamental solution is not treated.",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. (a) Well-definedness and continuity: fix $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$ with support in a compact set, choose $R>0$ with $\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\subseteq B_R(0)\\times[-R,R]$ ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]), and let $M:=\\sup|\\psi|<\\infty$ on the compact support ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]]). Then $|\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle|\\le\\frac{M}{4\\pi}\\int_{B_R}|y|^{-1}dy$ ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]), and by [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] applied to the nonnegative Borel function $y\\mapsto|y|^{-1}\\mathbf 1_{B_R}(y)$, with $d\\sigma$ the polar measure of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], $\\int_{B_R}|y|^{-1}dy=\\int_0^Rr^{-1}\\sigma(S^2)r^2\\,dr=\\tfrac12\\sigma(S^2)R^2<\\infty$. Hence the integral converges absolutely and the bound is a fixed-support seminorm of [[def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space]] on each $\\mathcal D_K$, i.e. $K$ is continuous for the LF topology of [[def-test-function-topology]]: $K\\in\\mathcal D'$. Support: if $\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\cap C=\\varnothing$ then $\\psi(y,|y|/c)=0$ for every $y\\ne0$, so the integrand vanishes off the null set $\\{0\\}$ and $\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle=0$. Conversely fix $(y_0,t_0)\\in C$ and a neighbourhood $U$. If $y_0\\ne0$, take a nonnegative bump $\\psi$ supported in a small ball inside $U$ about $(y_0,t_0)$ with $\\psi(y_0,t_0)>0$: by continuity $\\psi(\\cdot,|\\cdot|/c)$ is positive on a neighbourhood of $y_0$, a set of positive measure, and $|y|^{-1}$ is bounded there, so $\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle>0$ ([[thm-nonnegative-integral-zero-iff-zero-almost-everywhere]], [[lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure]]). If $y_0=0$, then $t_0=0$ and $\\psi(y,t)=\\rho(|y|)\\eta(t)$ with nonnegative bump factors supported in a small ball about the origin inside $U$ and positive at the origin gives $\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int\\rho(|y|)\\eta(|y|/c)|y|^{-1}dy>0$ by the same positivity argument. Hence no point of $C$ is outside the support and $\\operatorname{supp}K=C$. (b) For fixed $(x,t)$ the defining integral converges absolutely: if $\\operatorname{supp}h\\subseteq\\overline B_\\rho(0)\\times[-\\rho,\\rho]$ and $(y,t-|x-y|/c)\\in\\operatorname{supp}h$, then $y\\in\\overline B_{\\rho+|x|}(0)$; $|h|\\le\\|h\\|_\\infty$ and $1/(4\\pi|x-y|)$ is integrable over that compact ball by the same polar computation, so [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]] gives absolute convergence. Pairing identity: substitute $x=y+z$ in the $x$-integral and $t=s+|z|/c$ in the $t$-integral (two translations of Lebesgue measure, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant]]) and interchange the $y$- and $(z,t)$-integrations with [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] (absolute integrability follows by bounding h and psi and enclosing the contributing y,s,z in a bounded cylinder; the only pole is 1/|z|, whose integral over a bounded ball is finite by the preceding polar calculation; continuity at the pole is neither true nor required): $\\langle K*h,\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int h(y,s)\\Bigl[\\int\\frac{\\psi(y+z,s+|z|/c)}{|z|}dz\\Bigr]dyds=\\langle h,K\\circledast\\psi\\rangle$, the inner bracket being exactly the definition of $K$ applied to the test function $(z,u)\\mapsto\\psi(y+z,s+u)$ (its support is compact). Identification with the wave solution: let $h\\in C_c^\\infty(Q)$ and $t_0$ below the time support of $h$. Apply [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] with $a=b=0$ and source $h$ on the slab $\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$, $T$ above the time support: its solution is $u(x,t)=c^2\\int_{t_0}^tH_{h(\\cdot,v)}(x,t-v)\\,dv$ with $H_\\varphi(x,s)=sM_{cs}\\varphi(x)$, $M_{r}\\varphi(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}\\varphi(x+rn)\\,d\\Omega(n)$. Writing the sphere average as an integral over the sphere and using [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] on the compact rectangle $[t_0,t]\\times S^2$, the substitution $r=c(t-v)$ with the absolute derivative ([[cor-one-dimensional-change-of-variables-with-absolute-derivative]]) turns the integral into $u(x,t)=\\int_0^{c(t-t_0)}r\\,\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{S^2}h(x+rn,t-r/c)\\,d\\Omega(n)\\,dr$. The chart surface measure on $S^2$ agrees with the polar measure $\\sigma$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], $n=3$), so applying [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] to the nonnegative Borel function $y\\mapsto|h(x+y,t-|y|/c)|(4\\pi|y|)^{-1}\\mathbf 1_{|y|\\le c(t-t_0)}$ (and passing to positive and negative parts, [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]) gives $u(x,t)=\\int_{|y|\\le c(t-t_0)}\\frac{h(x+y,\\,t-|y|/c)}{4\\pi|y|}\\,dy$; the omitted range $|y|>c(t-t_0)$ has $t-|y|/c<t_0$, where $h=0$. After the reflection $y\\mapsto-y$ this is $(K*h)(x,t)$ on the whole slab. Since for every point $(x_*,t_*)\\in Q$ one can choose a slab $[t_0,T]$ containing it with $t_0$ below and $T$ above the time support of $h$, [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] gives $Wu=h$ at that point and $K*h=u$ there; hence $K*h$ (well defined on all of $Q$ and smooth on every slab) is $C^\\infty$ on $Q$ and $W(K*h)=h$ pointwise. (c) Let $\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(Q)$ be a nonnegative bump with $\\int\\eta=1$ and $\\operatorname{supp}\\eta\\subseteq\\overline B_1(0)$ and let $\\eta_\\varepsilon$ be its mollifier family ([[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]], [[def-l-one-approximate-identity-on-rn]]). By (b), $W(K*\\eta_\\varepsilon)=\\eta_\\varepsilon$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$. For bounded continuous $\\varphi$, $\\langle\\eta_\\varepsilon,\\varphi\\rangle\\to\\varphi(0)$: convolve with the reflected bump and apply [[cor-l-one-approximate-identities-converge-uniformly-on-compacta-for-continuous-functions]] at the origin. Hence for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$, $\\langle K*\\eta_\\varepsilon,\\psi\\rangle=\\langle\\eta_\\varepsilon,K\\circledast\\psi\\rangle\\to(K\\circledast\\psi)(0,0)=\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle$, because $K\\circledast\\psi$ is continuous (its defining integral is dominated by $2\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty/(4\\pi|z|)$ on a compact $z$-set, so [[thm-dominated-convergence]] applies as $(y,s)\\to(0,0)$), i.e. $K*\\eta_\\varepsilon\\to K$ in the weak topology of $\\mathcal D'$. The distributional operator $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ is a continuous linear map on $\\mathcal D'$ for the weak topology, being a finite linear combination of derivatives, each of which is continuous linear ([[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]); therefore $WK=W(\\lim_\\varepsilon K*\\eta_\\varepsilon)=\\lim_\\varepsilon W(K*\\eta_\\varepsilon)=\\lim_\\varepsilon\\eta_\\varepsilon=\\delta_{(0,0)}$ in $\\mathcal D'$ ([[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]). No smoothness of $K$ on the cone is claimed or used. For the approximate-identity application in (c), F(y,s)=(K circledast psi)(y,s) is bounded on ALL parameter space: if the spatial projection of supp psi lies in B_M with M>0, the contributing z lie in B_M-y. For |y|<=2M this is contained in B_3M and its |z|^-1 integral is finite; for |y|>2M one has |z|>=|y|-M>M and the integral is bounded by |B_M|/M. Multiply by ||psi||infinity/(4pi). Continuity on every bounded parameter neighborhood follows from the same fixed compact-z polar majorant and dominated convergence. Thus the stated bounded-continuous approximate-identity interface is actually applicable.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Partial Differential Equations: From Classical to Modern (2025 archived author manuscript, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250324094647id_/https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-pde/pde.pdf",
