            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), carried through [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] and its sphere integrations ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $c>0$, $t_0<T$, and let $(\\varphi,A)$ be a potential pair of class $C^\\infty$ on the slab $Q:=\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$ in the extension sense of [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]: $\\varphi$ and $A$ are restrictions of $C^\\infty$ functions on $\\mathbb R^3\\times J$ for some open interval $J\\supset[t_0,T]$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Put $G:=G(\\varphi,A)=\\operatorname{div}A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi$ and $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$. Then: (a) there is a gauge function $\\chi:\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^\\infty$ in the extension sense with $\\chi(\\cdot,t_0)=0$, $\\partial_t\\chi(\\cdot,t_0)=0$ and $W\\chi=G$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$; (b) the gauge-transformed pair $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)=(\\varphi-\\partial_t\\chi,\\,A+\\nabla\\chi)$ is again of class $C^\\infty$ in the extension sense and its fields are unchanged: $$E(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=E(\\varphi,A),\\qquad B(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=B(\\varphi,A)\\qquad\\text{on }Q;$$ (c) the transformed pair is in Lorenz gauge: $G(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=G-W\\chi=0$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$; (d) if $\\chi_1,\\chi_2$ are of class $C^2$ on $Q$ in the extension sense and $W\\chi_1=W\\chi_2=G$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$, then $W(\\chi_1-\\chi_2)=0$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$. Nothing is asserted about prescribed boundary values of $\\chi$ on a bounded spatial domain, about spatial decay of $\\chi$, or about Lorenz gauge fixing for a cavity problem with prescribed boundary data; the older global gauge promise on bounded domains is outside this result.",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. (a) $G=\\operatorname{div}A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi$ is $C^\\infty$ on $Q$ in the extension sense, being a sum of the divergence of a smooth field and a partial derivative of a smooth function ([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]], [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). Apply [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] with $a=b=0$ and source $h=G$; it supplies a function $\\chi$ of class $C^\\infty$ on $Q$ in the extension sense with $W\\chi=G$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$ and $\\chi(\\cdot,t_0)=0$, $\\partial_t\\chi(\\cdot,t_0)=0$. (b) $\\partial_t\\chi$ and $\\nabla\\chi$ are $C^\\infty$, so $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)$ is a $C^\\infty$ pair in the extension sense ([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]; sums and products of smooth functions, [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] componentwise). Magnetic field: $B(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=\\operatorname{curl}(A+\\nabla\\chi)=\\operatorname{curl}A+\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\chi=\\operatorname{curl}A$, using linearity of the curl ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]) and $\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\chi=0$ for the $C^2$ (indeed $C^\\infty$) function $\\chi$ ([[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]]). Electric field: $-\\nabla(\\varphi-\\partial_t\\chi)-\\partial_t(A+\\nabla\\chi)=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA+(\\nabla\\partial_t\\chi-\\partial_t\\nabla\\chi)$ componentwise, and the bracket vanishes by equality of mixed second partials of the $C^\\infty$ function $\\chi$ ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]); the componentwise linearity of $\\nabla$ and $\\partial_t$ is [[thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives]] and [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]. Hence $E(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=E(\\varphi,A)$ on $Q$. (c) Direct expansion using linearity and $\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\chi=\\Delta\\chi$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]): $$G(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=\\operatorname{div}A+\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\chi+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi-c^{-2}\\partial_t^2\\chi=G+\\Delta\\chi-c^{-2}\\partial_t^2\\chi=G-W\\chi=0$$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$. (d) $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ is linear on $C^2$ functions: each second partial of $\\chi_1-\\chi_2$ is the difference of the second partials (one-variable linearity applied along coordinate lines, [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]), so $W(\\chi_1-\\chi_2)=W\\chi_1-W\\chi_2=G-G=0$. No boundary or decay hypothesis is used; the construction is the free-space wave solution of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]], and it inherits that theorem's countable-choice premise.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing)",
