            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-forward-and-backward-wave-cones-domain-of-dependence-and-influence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) through [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $c,\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$ with $\\varepsilon\\mu c^2=1$, let $t_0<T$, and let $\\rho:\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]\\to\\mathbb R$ and $J:\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be of class $C^\\infty$ in the extension sense (restrictions of $C^\\infty$ functions on $\\mathbb R^3\\times I_{\\rm ext}$ for some open interval $I_{\\rm ext}\\supset[t_0,T]$, [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) satisfying the continuity equation $$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0\\qquad\\text{on }\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T).$$ Let $E_0,B_0\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ satisfy $\\operatorname{div}E_0=\\rho(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon$ and $\\operatorname{div}B_0=0$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Then: (a) the first-order system $$\\partial_tE=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B-\\frac{J}{\\varepsilon},\\qquad \\partial_tB=-\\operatorname{curl}E\\qquad\\text{on }\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T),$$ with $E(\\cdot,t_0)=E_0$, $B(\\cdot,t_0)=B_0$, has exactly one solution $(E,B)$ with $E,B$ of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$ in the extension sense; (b) this solution obeys both Gauss laws on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$: $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon$ and $\\operatorname{div}B=0$; (c) (domain of dependence) if $(\\tilde\\rho,\\tilde J,\\tilde E_0,\\tilde B_0)$ is second data of the same kind and, for some $x_*\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $t_*\\in(t_0,T]$, one has $\\tilde E_0=E_0$, $\\tilde B_0=B_0$ on the closed base ball $\\overline B_{c(t_*-t_0)}(x_*)$ and $\\tilde\\rho=\\rho$, $\\tilde J=J$ on the closed backward cone $K(x_*,t_*)=\\{(y,v):|y-x_*|\\le c(t_*-v),\\ t_0\\le v\\le t_*\\}$ ([[def-forward-and-backward-wave-cones-domain-of-dependence-and-influence]]), then $\\tilde E(x_*,t_*)=E(x_*,t_*)$ and $\\tilde B(x_*,t_*)=B(x_*,t_*)$. This is a statement about the stipulated smooth system with coefficients and sources given; no physical identification of $(E,B)$ with an electromagnetic field, no finite-domain boundary problem and no decay at spatial infinity is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Throughout, $\\operatorname{curl}$, $\\operatorname{div}$ and the wave expression $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ are those of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]] and [[def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed]], applied componentwise to vector fields; mixed second partials of $C^\\infty$ functions commute ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]), and divergence and curl are linear ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]); $\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}Z=0$ ([[thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes]]) and $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}Z=\\nabla\\operatorname{div}Z-\\Delta Z$ ([[cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field]]). Note $1/(\\varepsilon c^2)=\\mu$ and $c^2\\mu=1/\\varepsilon$ by $\\varepsilon\\mu c^2=1$. (1) Existence. Define $E$ componentwise as the solution of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] for the source $-\\nabla\\rho/\\varepsilon-\\mu\\partial_tJ$ with data $E(\\cdot,t_0)=E_0$, $\\partial_tE(\\cdot,t_0)=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0-J(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon$, and $B$ componentwise for the source $\\mu\\operatorname{curl}J$ with data $B(\\cdot,t_0)=B_0$, $\\partial_tB(\\cdot,t_0)=-\\operatorname{curl}E_0$. All sources and data are smooth in the extension sense, so W2 supplies $E,B\\in C^\\infty$ on the slab in the extension sense. (2) The first-order equations hold. Put $R:=E_t-c^2\\operatorname{curl}B+J/\\varepsilon$ and $Q:=B_t+\\operatorname{curl}E$. Since $\\operatorname{curl}$ commutes with $W$ on $C^\\infty$ fields, $W(E_t)=(WE)_t=-\\nabla\\rho_t/\\varepsilon-\\mu J_{tt}$ and $c^2W(\\operatorname{curl}B)=c^2\\mu\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}J=c^2\\mu(\\nabla\\operatorname{div}J-\\Delta J)$ and $W(J/\\varepsilon)=c^{-2}J_{tt}/\\varepsilon-\\Delta J/\\varepsilon=\\mu J_{tt}-c^2\\mu\\Delta J$; adding gives $WR=-\\nabla\\rho_t/\\varepsilon-c^2\\mu\\nabla\\operatorname{div}J=-\\nabla(\\rho_t+\\operatorname{div}J)/\\varepsilon=0$ by the continuity equation. Similarly $WQ=(WB)_t+\\operatorname{curl}(WE)=\\mu\\operatorname{curl}J_t+\\operatorname{curl}(-\\nabla\\rho/\\varepsilon-\\mu J_t)=-\\mu\\operatorname{curl}J_t+\\mu\\operatorname{curl}J_t=0$ using $\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\rho=0$ ([[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]]) and $\\operatorname{curl}(J_t)=(\\operatorname{curl}J)_t$. At $t_0$: $R(t_0)=E_t(t_0)-c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0+J(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon=0$ and $Q(t_0)=B_t(t_0)+\\operatorname{curl}E_0=0$ by the prescribed data. Moreover $R_t(t_0)=c^2\\nabla(\\operatorname{div}E_0-\\rho(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon)+(J_t(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon-c^2\\mu J_t(\\cdot,t_0))=0$ and $Q_t(t_0)=c^2\\nabla\\operatorname{div}B_0+(c^2\\mu-1/\\varepsilon)\\operatorname{curl}J(\\cdot,t_0)=0$, the computations using $E_{tt}(t_0)=c^2\\Delta E_0+c^2(WE)(t_0)$, $B_{tt}(t_0)=c^2\\Delta B_0+c^2\\mu\\operatorname{curl}J(\\cdot,t_0)$, $E_t(t_0)=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0-J(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon$, $B_t(t_0)=-\\operatorname{curl}E_0$ and the constraint and normalization identities. Hence $R,Q$ are smooth solutions of $W(\\cdot)=0$ with zero data, and the uniqueness clause of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] gives $R=Q=0$ on the slab, i.e. $E_t=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B-J/\\varepsilon$ and $B_t=-\\operatorname{curl}E$. (3) Constraints. $D:=\\operatorname{div}E-\\rho/\\varepsilon$ satisfies $WD=\\operatorname{div}(WE)-(c^{-2}\\rho_{tt}-\\Delta\\rho)/\\varepsilon=-\\Delta\\rho/\\varepsilon-\\mu\\operatorname{div}J_t-(c^{-2}\\rho_{tt}-\\Delta\\rho)/\\varepsilon=-\\mu(\\operatorname{div}J_t+\\rho_{tt})=0$, the last equality being the time derivative of the continuity equation; $D(t_0)=\\operatorname{div}E_0-\\rho(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon=0$ and $D_t(t_0)=\\operatorname{div}(c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0-J(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon)-\\rho_t(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon=-(\\operatorname{div}J+\\rho_t)(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon=0$ (using $\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}=0$). W2 uniqueness gives $D=0$, i.e. $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon$. Likewise $W(\\operatorname{div}B)=\\operatorname{div}(WB)=\\mu\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}J=0$, $\\operatorname{div}B(t_0)=0$ and $\\partial_t\\operatorname{div}B(t_0)=-\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}E_0=0$, so $\\operatorname{div}B=0$. (4) Uniqueness and cones. If $(\\tilde E,\\tilde B)$ is any second $C^\\infty$ solution of the system with the same data, then by (3) applied to it, $\\operatorname{div}\\tilde E=\\rho/\\varepsilon$ and $\\operatorname{div}\\tilde B=0$; differentiating $\\tilde B_t=-\\operatorname{curl}\\tilde E$ and using $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}=\\nabla\\operatorname{div}-\\Delta$ gives $c^{-2}\\tilde E_{tt}=-\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}\\tilde E-\\mu\\tilde J_t$, hence $W\\tilde E=-\\nabla\\operatorname{div}\\tilde E-\\mu J_t=-\\nabla\\rho/\\varepsilon-\\mu J_t$, and similarly $W\\tilde B=\\mu\\operatorname{curl}J$; the initial data and their time derivatives coincide with the data used to build $E,B$ (the derivatives are determined by the system and the data, e.g. $\\tilde E_t(\\cdot,t_0)=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0-J(\\cdot,t_0)/\\varepsilon$). By the uniqueness clause of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] componentwise, $\\tilde E=E$ and $\\tilde B=B$. For (c), the difference fields solve the same wave problems with data agreeing on the closed base ball and sources agreeing on the closed cone (the sources are the local differential expressions $-\\nabla\\rho/\\varepsilon-\\mu J_t$ and $\\mu\\operatorname{curl}J$, which agree on the open cone because $\\rho,J$ agree there, and the initial time-derivative data agree because $J(\\cdot,t_0)$ agrees on the base ball, which lies in $K$); the cone clause (c) of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] applied componentwise gives $\\tilde E(x_*,t_*)=E(x_*,t_*)$, $\\tilde B(x_*,t_*)=B(x_*,t_*)$.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Partial Differential Equations: From Classical to Modern (2025 archived author manuscript, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250324094647id_/https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-pde/pde.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.1, printed pp. 167-173, Corollary 7.3, (7.11)-(7.12) and Problem 7.9: the Duhamel solution and the reduction of the vacuum Maxwell system $B_t=-\\operatorname{curl}E$, $\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0E_t=\\operatorname{curl}B$, $\\operatorname{div}E=\\operatorname{div}B=0$ to component wave equations with $c=(\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0)^{-1/2}$ via the hint that the components of $E$ and $B$ satisfy the wave equation"
              },
              {
