          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $c>0$, $q\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\varepsilon_0>0$, put $\\mu_0:=(c^2\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}$ and $C:=q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$, and let $z:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be of class $C^3$ with $|z'(s)|\\le v_*<c$ for every $s\\in\\mathbb R$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]). Write $\\Omega:=\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ and $\\Gamma:=\\{(z(s),s):s\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\subseteq\\Omega$. For $(x,t)\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ let $s=s(x,t)$ be the unique retarded root of [[lem-em-global-retarded-root]] with $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$, and put $R:=x-z(s)$, $r:=|R|>0$, $n:=R/r$, $\\beta:=z'(s)/c$, $\\kappa:=1-n\\cdot\\beta$, $N:=n\\cdot\\beta$, $D:=\\kappa r$, $\\dot\\beta:=z''(s)/c$ and $d:=n\\cdot\\dot\\beta$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then: (a) the scalar distribution $\\rho\\in\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$ and the vector distribution $J\\in(\\mathcal D'(\\Omega))^3$ of [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]], $$\\langle\\rho,\\psi\\rangle=q\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\psi(z(s),s)\\,ds,\\qquad \\langle J,\\psi\\rangle=q\\int_{\\mathbb R}z'(s)\\,\\psi(z(s),s)\\,ds,$$ satisfy $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$. (b) Let $K$ be the retarded wave kernel of [[lem-em-retarded-wave-kernel]] and, for $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ and $\\tau\\in\\mathbb R$, $y\\in\\mathbb R^3$, put $(K\\circledast\\psi)(y,\\tau):=\\langle K,\\psi(y+\\cdot,\\tau+\\cdot)\\rangle$. Define the retarded-history pairings by the explicit integrals\n$$\\langle\\Phi,\\psi\\rangle:=\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(K\\circledast\\psi)(z(s),s)\\,ds,\\qquad\n\\langle\\Upsilon,\\psi\\rangle:=\\mu_0q\\int_{\\mathbb R}z'(s)(K\\circledast\\psi)(z(s),s)\\,ds.$$\nHere the second integral is componentwise. The notation $\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\langle\\rho,K\\circledast\\psi\\rangle$ and $\\mu_0\\langle J,K\\circledast\\psi\\rangle$, if used, means precisely these explicit history integrals, rather than ordinary distribution action on a compactly supported test. These pairings define distributions on $\\Omega$, are finite for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ and satisfy the exact retarded-convolution identities $$\\langle\\Phi,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma}\\frac{C\\,\\psi(x,t)}{\\kappa(x,t)\\,r(x,t)}\\,dx\\,dt,\\qquad \\langle\\Upsilon,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma}\\frac{\\beta(x,t)}{c}\\,\\frac{C\\,\\psi(x,t)}{\\kappa(x,t)\\,r(x,t)}\\,dx\\,dt$$ (for $\\Upsilon$, componentwise against $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$; both integrands are continuous on the open set $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ and integrable there against $\\psi$). Consequently the potentials are the $C^2$ functions $$\\varphi:=\\frac{C}{D}=\\frac{C}{\\kappa r}:\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R,\\qquad A:=\\frac{\\beta\\,\\varphi}{c}=\\frac{\\beta\\,C}{c\\,\\kappa r}:\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R^3$$ off the worldline, and the zero extensions $\\widetilde\\varphi,\\widetilde A$ on $\\Gamma$ belong to $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$; $\\Phi$ is the regular distribution of $\\widetilde\\varphi$, and $\\Upsilon$ that of $\\widetilde A$ ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function|regular distribution]]). (c) Let $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B:=\\operatorname{curl}A$ on $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ ([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]). Then, off the worldline, $$E=C\\left[\\frac{(1-|\\beta|^2)(n-\\beta)}{\\kappa^3r^2}+\\frac{n\\times\\bigl((n-\\beta)\\times\\dot\\beta\\bigr)}{c\\,\\kappa^3r}\\right],\\qquad B=\\frac{n\\times E}{c}.$$ The fields $E,B$ are $C^1$ off the worldline. More generally, if $z\\in C^k$, $k\\ge3$, the retarded root is $C^k$, the potentials are $C^{k-1}$ and the fields are $C^{k-2}$ there; if $z\\in C^\\infty$, all these objects are smooth off the worldline. The result is a mathematical model computation for the stipulated uniformly subluminal smooth worldline and the retarded choice; no self-force law, no radiation-reaction postulate and no physical Maxwell coupling is asserted or proved.",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, as stipulated in the statement. (a) Apply [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]] to the $C^1$ curve $z$ on $\\mathbb R$; $C^3$ is a special case, and its all-time graph is proper because a compact ambient set bounds the time coordinate.\n(b) Fix a compact observation set $Q\\subseteq\\{(x,t):|x|\\le M,\\ t_0\\le t\\le t_1\\}$, and tests supported in $Q$. If $g(s,y):=(z(s)+y,s+|y|/c)\\in Q$, then $s\\le t_1$. For $s\\le0$, using $|z(s)|\\le Z+v_*|s|$ with $Z:=|z(0)|$ gives\n$$t_0\\le s+|y|/c=s+|x-z(s)|/c\\le -(1-v_*/c)|s|+(M+Z)/c.$$\nThus $s\\ge-S$, where $S:=\\max\\{0,(M+Z-ct_0)/(c-v_*)\\}$. If $[-S,t_1]$ is empty no contributing point exists. Otherwise set $H:=\\max(S,|t_1|)$ and $L:=1+M+Z+v_*H$. Every contributing $y$ has $|y|\\le M+|z(s)|<L$. Consequently the pulled-back support lies in the bounded closed cylinder $[-S,t_1]\\times\\overline B_L$, possibly approaching $y=0$; it need not be compact inside $U:=\\mathbb R\\times(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$.\nOn that cylinder, $|\\psi(g(s,y))|/(4\\pi|y|)\\le\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty/(4\\pi|y|)$. The polar formula [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] gives $\\int_{B_L}|y|^{-1}dy=\\sigma(S^2)L^2/2<\\infty$. Borel product identification [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]] and the Euclidean/product-measure bridge [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]] allow [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] for the absolute value. Then [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] identifies the double integral with the explicit iterated history integral. In particular, with $T:=\\max(0,t_1+S)$ and $M_Q:=T\\sigma(S^2)L^2/(8\\pi)$,\n$$|\\langle\\Phi,\\psi\\rangle|\\le |q|M_Q\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad\n|\\langle\\Upsilon_j,\\psi\\rangle|\\le\\mu_0|q|v_*M_Q\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty.$$\nThe seminorm $\\psi\\mapsto|\\langle\\Phi,\\psi\\rangle|$ restricts continuously to each fixed-support test space by these bounds, and likewise for each component of $\\Upsilon$. Thus [[def-test-function-topology]] and [[def-distribution]] prove distribution continuity directly; no action of $\\rho$ or $J$ on a noncompact test is assumed.\nThe graph $\\Gamma$ is closed: convergence of $(z(s_j),s_j)$ forces $s_j\\to t$ and $z(s_j)\\to z(t)$. Thus $V:=\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ is open. On $U$, $g$ is $C^1$, and in coordinate order $(s,y_1,y_2,y_3)$ its determinant is $-\\kappa(s,y)$, with $\\kappa=1-(y/|y|)\\cdot z'(s)/c\\ge1-v_*/c>0$. If $g(s,y)=g(s',y')$, then $|s-s'|=||y'|-|y||/c\\le|z(s')-z(s)|/c\\le(v_*/c)|s-s'|$, so $s=s'$ and $y=y'$. No image point belongs to $\\Gamma$: otherwise $|y|=|z(t)-z(s)|\\le v_*(t-s)=v_*|y|/c$, contradicting $y\\ne0$. Conversely every point of $V$ has the unique retarded root supplied by [[lem-em-global-retarded-root]], giving inverse $(x,t)\\mapsto(s(x,t),x-z(s(x,t)))$. Its $C^1$ regularity follows from that supplier's $C^3$ branch on the open domain $x\\ne z(t)$, using this consumer's $C^3$ hypothesis. No exclusion of the entire spatial trajectory is imposed. Therefore $g:U\\to V$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism.\nFor any compact $Q\\subseteq\\Omega$, the nonnegative Borel substitution lemma [[lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness]] applied to $1_Q/(\\kappa r)$ on $V$ yields\n$$\\int_V\\frac{1_Q}{\\kappa r}\\,dx\\,dt=\\int_U\\frac{1_Q(g(s,y))}{|y|}\\,ds\\,dy<\\infty.$$\nThe same bound holds for $|\\beta|/(c\\kappa r)$ since $|\\beta|\\le v_*/c$. Extend the potentials by zero on the closed graph. These extensions are Borel and locally integrable on all of $\\Omega$. Applying the same Borel substitution first to the absolute value of $\\psi/(\\kappa r)$ establishes its $L^1$ integrability on $V$, and [[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]] then gives the scalar pairing formula in the statement. Apply it componentwise to $z'(s)$-weighted data for the vector formula. Its prefactor is\n$$\\frac{\\mu_0qz'(s)}{4\\pi\\kappa r}=\\frac{\\mu_0qc\\beta}{4\\pi\\kappa r}=\\frac{\\mu_0c\\varepsilon_0 C\\beta}{\\kappa r}=\\frac{C\\beta}{c\\kappa r},$$\nsince $\\mu_0c^2\\varepsilon_0=1$. The regular-distribution identities mean the zero extensions, as in [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]]; tests may be nonzero on $\\Gamma$. The retarded root is $C^3$ on $V$, and $r,n$ are $C^3$ while $\\beta,\\kappa,D$ are $C^2$ with $D>0$, so the potentials are $C^2$. The same composition argument gives the matching $C^k$ and $C^\\infty$ branches.\n(c) Derivatives of the retarded data: from $t-s=r/c$ and R1, $\\nabla s=-n/(c\\kappa)$, $s_t=1/\\kappa$, hence $\\nabla r=n/\\kappa$ and $r_t=-cN/\\kappa$ (chain rule, [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]); $R=x-z(s)$ gives $\\partial_iR=e_i+\\beta\\,n_i/\\kappa$ and $\\beta=z'(s)/c$ gives $\\partial_i\\beta=-\\dot\\beta\\,n_i/(c\\kappa)$, while $\\beta_t=\\dot\\beta/\\kappa$ (componentwise chain rule and [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]). Since $D=r-R\\cdot\\beta$, the product rules for the Euclidean pairing give $D_t=r_t-R_t\\cdot\\beta-R\\cdot\\beta_t=(c(|\\beta|^2-N)-rd)/\\kappa$ and $\\partial_iD=\\partial_ir-(\\partial_iR)\\cdot\\beta-R\\cdot\\partial_i\\beta$, hence $\\nabla D=((1-|\\beta|^2+rd/c)/\\kappa)\\,n-\\beta$; the required product rules are the coordinatewise one-variable product rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then $\\varphi=C/D$ gives $\\nabla\\varphi=-C\\nabla D/D^2$, $\\varphi_t=-CD_t/D^2$, and $A=\\beta\\varphi/c$ gives $A_t=(\\beta_t\\varphi+\\beta\\varphi_t)/c=\\dot\\beta C/(c\\kappa D)-\\beta CD_t/(cD^2)$, so $$E=-\\nabla\\varphi-A_t=\\frac{C}{D^2}\\Bigl(\\nabla D+\\frac{\\beta D_t}{c}\\Bigr)-\\frac{C\\dot\\beta}{c\\kappa D}.$$ The bracket equals $\\frac{(1-|\\beta|^2)(n-\\beta)+(rd/c)(n-\\beta)}{\\kappa}$ (substituting the two displayed identities and $\\kappa=1-N$), and writing the second term over the common denominator $\\kappa^3r^2$ replaces its numerator by $-r\\kappa\\dot\\beta/c$, giving $E=\\frac{C}{\\kappa^3r^2}\\bigl[(1-|\\beta|^2)(n-\\beta)+\\frac{r}{c}\\bigl(d(n-\\beta)-\\kappa\\dot\\beta\\bigr)\\bigr]$; the coordinate triple-product identity $n\\times((n-\\beta)\\times\\dot\\beta)=d(n-\\beta)-\\kappa\\dot\\beta$ (expand both sides with [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]) yields the displayed $E$. For the magnetic field, $\\operatorname{curl}(\\beta\\varphi/c)=\\nabla(\\varphi/c)\\times\\beta+(\\varphi/c)\\operatorname{curl}\\beta$ ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]), and $\\operatorname{curl}\\beta=\\nabla s\\times\\dot\\beta$ by the coordinate formula applied to $\\beta=z'(s(x,t))/c$ ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]); substituting $\\nabla\\varphi=-C\\nabla D/D^2$ and $\\nabla s=-n/(c\\kappa)$ gives $$B=-C\\left[\\frac{1-|\\beta|^2+rd/c}{c\\kappa D^2}\\,n\\times\\beta+\\frac{n\\times\\dot\\beta}{c^2\\kappa D}\\right].$$ Crossing the displayed $E$ with $n/c$ gives this same expression by $n\\times(n-\\beta)=-n\\times\\beta$ and $D=\\kappa r$, proving $B=n\\times E/c$. Both displayed fields are $C^1$ off the worldline, and no derivative of $z$ beyond the third order and no bound on $\\Gamma$ at infinity is used; the caveats in the statement are exactly those of the research branch argument.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing)",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/em/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§6.4.1, printed pp. 157-159, equations (6.30)-(6.36): the point charge density $\\rho=q\\delta^3(x-r(t))$, the current $J=qv\\delta^3(x-r(t))$, the delta-substitution $\\int dt'\\,R(t')^{-1}\\delta(t-f(t'))$ with $f(t')=t'+R(t')/c$, and the Liénard-Wiechert potentials $\\phi=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}\\frac{1}{\\kappa R}$, $A=\\frac{q\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{v}{\\kappa R}$ at the retarded root (6.36); §6.4.3, printed pp. 161-164, equations (6.39)-(6.45): the retarded derivatives with $\\kappa=1-\\hat R\\cdot v/c$, the field formulas (6.42) $E=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0}[\\frac{\\hat R-v/c}{\\gamma^2\\kappa^3R^2}+\\frac{\\hat R\\times((\\hat R-v/c)\\times a)}{\\kappa^3Rc^2}]$ and (6.44)-(6.45) $B=\\hat R\\times E/c$, read against the design's normalization $C=q/(4\\pi\\epsilon_0)$, $\\beta=z'/c$, $\\dot\\beta=z''/c$"
              },
              {
