            "thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-mean-value-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-nonnegative-integral-zero-iff-zero-almost-everywhere": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, Cambridge lecture notes (236-page author-hosted PDF)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections6.4.1–6.4.3 printed158–164 supply the potential/field background. The additional proper-history distributional wave/gauge and C1 cutoff proof is explicit here against exact library suppliers; no distributional point-source theorem is attributed to this text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let c>0, epsilon0>0, q in R, mu0=(epsilon0 c²)^-1, and let z:R->R³ be C³ with |z'(s)|<=v*<c for all s. These are mathematical parameters. On Q=R³ times R let rho,J and Phi,Upsilon be exactly the proper point-current and retarded-history distributions of [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]] and [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]], with the kernel K of [[lem-em-retarded-wave-kernel]] and no independent free addition. Put W=c^-2 partial_t²-Delta. Then (a)\n$$W\\Phi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad W\\Upsilon=\\mu_0J,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\Upsilon+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\Phi=0\\quad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(Q).$$\n(b) Define the vector distributions $\\mathcal E=-\\nabla\\Phi-\\partial_t\\Upsilon$ and $\\mathcal B=\\operatorname{curl}\\Upsilon$ using componentwise [[def-distributional-derivative]] and the usual finite-coordinate cross/curl convention [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. They satisfy\n$$\\operatorname{div}\\mathcal E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\quad\\operatorname{div}\\mathcal B=0,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\mathcal E=-\\partial_t\\mathcal B,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\mathcal B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\mathcal E.$$\n(c) On the complement of Gamma={(z(s),s)}, these distributions restrict to the exact C1 Lienard-Wiechert fields of the earlier point-potential lemma, with all its higher-regularity branches retained. The zero extensions of those E,B formulas on Gamma belong to L1loc(Q), and their regular distributions are precisely mathcal E,mathcal B ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]]). The off-worldline C1 fields also satisfy the source-free classical Maxwell equations there, because the point pair restricts to zero and each continuous regular residual is zero. Thus the equations in (b) are equations for locally integrable fields with the exact point source, not merely arbitrary field distributions. No field value on Gamma is physically assigned; zero extension is a representative convention on a null set. No physical adoption, nonlinear distribution product, finite self-energy, self-force, global acceleration bound, or incoming-field uniqueness theorem is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Use only the existing explicit histories, not an undefined convolution of two noncompact distributions. Fix psi in D(Q) with observation support |x|<=M,t0<=t<=t1. The earlier point-potential proof gives a common proper-history cylinder [-S,t1] times B_L for every contributing (s,y), where S=max(0,(M+|z(0)|-ct0)/(c-v*)) and L=1+M+|z(0)|+v*max(S,|t1|); derivatives of psi have the same observation support. All relevant absolute integrals are bounded by a derivative supremum times integral over that cylinder of 1/|y|, finite by [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], with the actual nonnegative order rules [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]. If the history interval is empty all pairings vanish.\nFor F(y,s)=(K circledast psi)(y,s), each parameter derivative is K circledast the corresponding test derivative. Indeed on a bounded parameter neighborhood, the spatial projection of psi's support confines the kernel variable to one bounded ball; every difference quotient is dominated by its test-derivative supremum times |w|^-1 there. Dominated convergence [[thm-dominated-convergence]] and the scalar FTC show F is smooth and give those identities for all derivatives. For each fixed (y,s), translation of WK=delta from [[lem-em-retarded-wave-kernel]] yields (K circledast Wpsi)(y,s)=psi(y,s). Apply this pointwise identity inside the bounded absolute history integrals: <Phi,Wpsi>=q/epsilon0 integral psi(z(s),s)ds and <Upsilon_j,Wpsi>=mu0 q integral z'_j(s)psi(z(s),s)ds, proving the two distributional wave equations, including q0.\nThe function s->F(z(s),s) is C1 by [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] and has compact support in the same finite history interval: cone causality gives the upper bound, uniform subluminality gives the lower bound. Its derivative is F_s+z' dot grad_y F. Therefore the distributional Lorenz residual tested on the ORIGINAL SMOOTH psi equals\n$$-\\mu_0q\\int_{\\mathbb R}[z'(s)\\cdot\\nabla_yF(z(s),s)+F_s(z(s),s)]ds=0,$$\nby [[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], using c^-2/epsilon0=mu0. No derivative or cutoff is moved onto a noncompact distribution test. By [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]], finite coordinate cancellations give div curl0,curl grad0 and curl curl Upsilon=grad div Upsilon-Delta Upsilon. With G=div Upsilon+c^-2 Phi_t, direct algebra gives div mathcal E=W Phi-G_t and curl mathcal B-c^-2 mathcal E_t=W Upsilon+grad G. The two wave identities and G0 prove all four equations.\nLocality and local integrability. A continuous regular distribution vanishing on an open set has its function identically zero: injectivity of [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]] gives zero almost everywhere, and a nonzero continuous value would give a nonzero neighborhood of positive measure by [[lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure]]. Thus classical derivative compatibility below also yields the four source-free classical equations off Gamma. The earlier lemma supplies regular Phi,Upsilon and C2 potentials on Q minus Gamma. Its C1 field formulas and the classical-compatibility clause of the distributional differentiation theorem give the stated restrictions. The graph is closed and null: each fixed-time spatial section is a singleton. The Borel graph is product-measurable by [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]], and [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]] identifies product and Euclidean measure under the declared Countable Choice; hence Tonelli [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] gives zero four-dimensional measure. On any compact observation cylinder Q0, retarded s lies in [-S,t1], so A_Q=max_{[-S,t1]}|z''| is finite. With b*=v*/c, kappa>=1-b*, the explicit fields obey |E|+|B|<=C_Q(r^-2+r^-1), with constants depending on |q|,c,epsilon0,b*,A_Q, not on the individual observation point. The earlier C1 diffeomorphism g(s,y)=(z(s)+y,s+|y|/c) has absolute Jacobian kappa<=1+b*. The earlier proof establishes its global diffeomorphism property; the exact [[lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness]], under the declared Countable Choice, gives\n$$\\int_{Q_0}r^{-p}dxdt\\le(1+b_*)(t_1+S)_+\\int_{B_L}|y|^{-p}dy<\\infty\\qquad(p=1,2).$$\nThe last integrals are 4pi L²/2 and 4pi L respectively. Thus zero-extended classical E,B are L1loc; this alone is not yet identification across Gamma.\nNo hidden curve delta in the first potential derivatives. Put d=|x-z(t)|. Since t-s=r/c and |z(t)-z(s)|<=b*r, one has (1-b*)r<=d<=(1+b*)r. Potentials are consequently bounded by C/d on each compact observation cylinder. Choose a smooth spatial cutoff chi on R³, zero on B1 and one outside B2, with bounded derivative ([[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]]); set chi_epsilon(x,t)=chi((x-z(t))/epsilon). This is C3, sufficient for CLASSICAL C1 integration by parts, but it need not be a C-infinity distribution test. Its spatial gradients are bounded by C/epsilon, its time derivative by Cv*/epsilon, and all are supported on epsilon<=d<=2epsilon. Ordinary compact C1 products with the original psi can be integrated by parts in each coordinate via [[thm-integration-by-parts]], followed by finite rectangle Fubini and [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] under the declared Countable Choice; equivalently extend chi_epsilon times each potential by zero, which is C1 across its vanished neighborhood. For any first spatial or time derivative D, the resulting ordinary integral identity is\n$$\\int \\chi_\\epsilon(Df)\\psi=-\\int \\chi_\\epsilon f D\\psi-\\int f(D\\chi_\\epsilon)\\psi,$$\nwhere f is any potential component off Gamma. On the cutoff shell |f|<=C/epsilon, |Dchi_epsilon|<=C'/epsilon. Spatial translations give shell volume O(epsilon³) at each t ([[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant]]), and the time interval of psi is fixed; the last integral is O(epsilon). Since f and its classical first derivatives are L1loc (the latter also follow directly from the earlier D-gradient and D_t formulas with bounded compact-history acceleration), dominated convergence lets epsilon tend0 in the first two integrals. It gives -integral f Dpsi=integral (Df)psi for each ORIGINAL smooth psi. Hence global distributional first derivatives have exactly the locally integrable classical representatives, and their finite combinations identify mathcal E,mathcal B with zero-extended E,B. No product of distributions, singular-field value, stronger worldline regularity, or noncompact test action enters."
        }
      ],
