            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-global-retarded-root": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. (i) Static specialization. Take $z\\equiv0$ (so $v_*=0$), $q\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\varepsilon_0>0$, in the setting of [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]]. Then for $x\\ne0$ the root is $s=t-|x|/c$, $r=|x|$, $n=x/|x|$, $\\beta=0$, $\\kappa=1$, $\\dot\\beta=0$, so the potentials and fields are $\\varphi=C/|x|$, $A=0$, $E=C\\,x/|x|^3$, $B=0$. Thus the retarded formulas reduce exactly to the Coulomb field with the convention $C=q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$, and the kernel's explicit retarded-history integral reproduces the time-independent Poisson kernel $1/(4\\pi|x-y|)$: for a static spatial source $h\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$, define the all-time retarded extension by\n$$u(x,t):=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{h(y)}{|x-y|}\\,dy.$$\nIndeed the retarded time is $t-|x-y|/c$ and evaluating the static source there leaves $h(y)$ unchanged. This finite integral is the extension of the compact spacetime-source formula by source-time cutoffs on each compact observation region; a time-independent nonzero source is not itself compactly supported in spacetime. (ii) Explicit Lorenz-gauge transformation. On $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ take $\\varphi\\equiv0$ and $A(x,t)=(x_1,0,0)$. Then $G(\\varphi,A)=\\operatorname{div}A=1$ and $E=-\\partial_tA=0$, $B=\\operatorname{curl}A=0$. The quadratic function $\\chi(x,t):=-|x|^2/6$ satisfies $W\\chi=G$: indeed $\\partial_t^2\\chi=0$ and $\\Delta\\chi=-1$, so $W\\chi=c^{-2}\\cdot0-(-1)=1=G$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]). Its gauge transform is $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)=(0,\\ ((2/3)x_1,-x_2/3,-x_3/3))$, which satisfies $G(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=\\frac23-\\frac13-\\frac13=0$ and has the same fields: $E(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=0=E(\\varphi,A)$ and $B(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=0=B(\\varphi,A)$. This checks clauses (b) and (c) of [[lem-em-gauge-wave-reachability]] in an instance where $G$ is a nonzero constant and where the gauge function does not have the zero Cauchy data used in the existence construction of that lemma; by its clause (d), the difference of this $\\chi$ and the zero-data solution of $W\\chi=G$ solves the homogeneous wave equation, which is why both may be used. (iii) Sharpness of the kernel support at the apex. For a nonnegative $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R)$ with $\\psi(0,0)>0$ one has $\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int\\psi(y,|y|/c)|y|^{-1}dy>0$, so $(0,0)\\in\\operatorname{supp}K$ and the apex is not an artefact of the definition; the same computation with a bump near $(y_0,|y_0|/c)$, $y_0\\ne0$, shows that every point of the cone is in the support. These are mathematical checks of the pair's conventions and hypotheses; no measurement, physical field identification, or empirical claim is made.",
          "strategy": "(i) With $z\\equiv0$ the equation $t-s=|x|/c$ has the unique solution $s=t-|x|/c$ for $x\\ne0$ ([[lem-em-global-retarded-root]] with $v_*=0$); then $R=x$, $r=|x|$, $n=x/|x|$, $\\beta=0$, $\\kappa=1$, $D=|x|$, so [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]] gives $\\varphi=C/|x|$, $A=0$, and its field formulas give $E=C(1-0)(n-0)/(1\\cdot|x|^2)=Cn/|x|^2=Cx/|x|^3$ and $B=n\\times E/c=C\\,n\\times n/(c|x|^2)=0$. For the static kernel computation, $h\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ is bounded with compact spatial support. On a compact observation region, $|x-y|$ is uniformly bounded for $y\\in\\operatorname{supp}h$; hence all contributing source times $s=t-|x-y|/c$ lie in a fixed bounded interval. A smooth time cutoff from [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]] equal to one there makes the source compact in spacetime; [[lem-em-retarded-wave-kernel]](b) applies to it and its evaluation gives exactly the displayed Poisson integral. This integral is finite by the same local $|x-y|^{-1}$ polar bound as in the point-source lemma. Thus the static formula is a justified extension of the compact-source statement. (ii) $\\operatorname{div}A=\\partial_1x_1=1$ and $\\operatorname{curl}A=0$ by the coordinate formulas of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]; $\\Delta(-|x|^2/6)=-\\frac16\\cdot6=-1$ because $\\Delta|x|^2=6$ (componentwise second derivatives, [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); hence $W\\chi=1=G$. The transformed pair is $(0-\\partial_t\\chi,\\ A+\\nabla\\chi)$ with $\\nabla\\chi=-x/3$ ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]), and $\\operatorname{div}A'=\\frac23-\\frac13-\\frac13=0$ ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]), while $\\operatorname{curl}A'=0$ and $-\\nabla0-\\partial_tA'=0$ directly from the coordinate formulas. The logical role of clause (d) is [[lem-em-gauge-wave-reachability]](d), which covers the difference of any two solutions with the same right-hand side. (iii) At the apex, continuity and $\\psi(0,0)>0$ give $a,\\delta>0$ such that $\\psi(y,|y|/c)\\ge a$ for $|y|<\\delta$. Its integral against $1/|y|$ is strictly positive by the polar formula and positive measure of Euclidean balls. At any nonapex cone point, [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]] gives in every neighborhood a nonnegative test positive at that point; continuity gives a small positive-radius ball of $y$-values with a positive lower bound on the pulled-back test and $|y|$ bounded away from zero. The same integral is strictly positive. These neighborhood tests establish every cone point belongs to the support, including the apex; no factorization of an arbitrary test into radial and time factors is assumed. No clause of [[lem-em-gauge-wave-reachability]] about boundary data is used or contradicted.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
