          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), used through the change-of-variables corollaries inside the proof of the retarded-convolution identity cited in clause (b) ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $c>0$, let $v_*<c$, let $z:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be of class $C^3$ with $|z'(s)|\\le v_*$ for every $s\\in\\mathbb R$ ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\varepsilon_0>0$, put $\\mu_0:=(c^2\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}$ and $C:=q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$, and write $\\Omega:=\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ and $\\Gamma:=\\{(z(s),s):s\\in\\mathbb R\\}$.\n\n(a) [Root and emission-time Jacobian] For every $(x,t)\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ (equivalently $x\\ne z(t)$) there is exactly one $s=s(x,t)<t$ with $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$. The map $s:\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R$ is of class $C^3$, and with $R:=x-z(s)$, $r:=|R|$, $n:=R/r$, $\\beta:=z'(s)/c$ and $\\kappa:=1-n\\cdot\\beta$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) one has $$\\partial_ts(x,t)=\\kappa^{-1},\\qquad \\nabla_xs(x,t)=-\\frac{n}{c\\,\\kappa},\\qquad \\kappa\\in[1-v_*/c,\\,1+v_*/c],$$ so $\\kappa\\ge 1-v_*/c>0$ uniformly on $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$. Moreover, for the fixed $x$ and near the root, the function $s'\\mapsto s'+|x-z(s')|/c$ is differentiable at $s'=s$ with derivative $\\kappa(x,t)$: this emission-time Jacobian $1-n\\cdot\\beta$ is the denominator produced by the delta substitution of clause (b).\n\n(b) [Delta substitution and Liénard-Wiechert potentials] Let $\\rho,J\\in\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$ be the point-source distributions of [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]] attached to $q$ and the worldline $z$, let $K$ be the retarded wave kernel of [[lem-em-retarded-wave-kernel]], and for $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ put $(K\\circledast\\psi)(y,\\tau):=\\langle K,\\psi(y+\\cdot,\\tau+\\cdot)\\rangle$ and define the retarded-history pairings by the explicit integrals $\\langle\\Phi,\\psi\\rangle:=(q/\\varepsilon_0)\\int_{\\mathbb R}(K\\circledast\\psi)(z(s),s)\\,ds$, $\\langle\\Upsilon,\\psi\\rangle:=\\mu_0q\\int_{\\mathbb R}z'(s)(K\\circledast\\psi)(z(s),s)\\,ds$, componentwise in the second case. These are the history-integral interpretation supplied by clause(b) of [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]], not ordinary distribution action on a noncompact test. Then these pairings are finite for every $\\psi$ and their exact retarded-convolution values are $$\\langle\\Phi,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma}\\frac{C\\,\\psi}{\\kappa\\,r}\\,dx\\,dt,\\qquad \\langle\\Upsilon,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma}\\frac{\\beta}{c}\\,\\frac{C\\,\\psi}{\\kappa\\,r}\\,dx\\,dt$$ (the second identity componentwise); equivalently, $\\Phi$ and $\\Upsilon$ are the regular distributions of the locally integrable zero extensions to $\\Gamma$ of the $C^2$ functions $\\varphi:=C/(\\kappa r)$ and $A:=\\beta\\varphi/c$ on $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$. The weight $(\\kappa r)^{-1}$ with $\\kappa=1-n\\cdot\\beta$ is the exact form of the substitution with denominator $1-n\\cdot\\beta$ (the rigorous statement is the displayed pairing identity; composition of $\\delta$ with a function is not part of the distribution calculus used here).\n\n(c) [Fields and the velocity/acceleration split] With $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B:=\\operatorname{curl}A$ on $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ ([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]), and writing $\\dot\\beta:=z''(s)/c$ at the root, $$E=E_{\\mathrm{vel}}+E_{\\mathrm{acc}},\\qquad E_{\\mathrm{vel}}=\\frac{C(1-|\\beta|^2)(n-\\beta)}{\\kappa^3r^2},\\qquad E_{\\mathrm{acc}}=\\frac{C\\,n\\times\\bigl((n-\\beta)\\times\\dot\\beta\\bigr)}{c\\,\\kappa^3r},\\qquad B=\\frac{n\\times E}{c}$$ at every event of $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]). Both $E$ and $B$ are $C^1$ on $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$, and the velocity field obeys the bound $$|E_{\\mathrm{vel}}(x,t)|\\le |C|\\,\\frac{1+v_*/c}{(1-v_*/c)^3}\\,r(x,t)^{-2}$$ at every event.\n\n(d) [Uniform far-zone remainder] For every $s_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and every $n\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $|n|=1$, put $x_r:=z(s_0)+rn$ and $t_r:=s_0+r/c$ for $r>0$, and set $\\beta_0:=z'(s_0)/c$, $\\dot\\beta_0:=z''(s_0)/c$, $\\kappa_0:=1-n\\cdot\\beta_0$. Then $(x_r,t_r)\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ and $s(x_r,t_r)=s_0$ for every $r>0$, and $$E(x_r,t_r)=\\frac{C\\,n\\times\\bigl((n-\\beta_0)\\times\\dot\\beta_0\\bigr)}{c\\,\\kappa_0^3\\,r}+\\rho_E(s_0,n,r),\\qquad B(x_r,t_r)=\\frac{n\\times E(x_r,t_r)}{c},$$ where the remainder is bounded uniformly in the direction, $$|\\rho_E(s_0,n,r)|\\le |C|\\,\\frac{1+v_*/c}{(1-v_*/c)^3}\\,r^{-2}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\in S^2\\text{ and every }r>0.$$ Equivalently, $rE(x_r,t_r)\\to C\\,n\\times((n-\\beta_0)\\times\\dot\\beta_0)/(c\\,\\kappa_0^3)$ as $r\\to\\infty$, uniformly over $n\\in S^2$ at the explicit rate $O(r^{-1})$ given by the same bound multiplied by $r$. The uniformity is in the direction $n$ at a fixed emission event $s_0$; no uniformity in $s_0$ is asserted ($z''$ is not assumed bounded), and no finite-energy, decay or radiation-flux conclusion is drawn.\n\nThis is a mathematical model computation for the stipulated $C^3$ uniformly subluminal worldline and the retarded choice; no self-force law, no radiation-reaction postulate and no physical Maxwell coupling is asserted or proved, and the uniform speed bound and the exclusion of $\\Gamma$ are essential hypotheses (see the companion example page).",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Fix $c>0$, $v_*<c$, $q\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\varepsilon_0>0$, $C=q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$ and a $C^3$ worldline $z$ with $|z'|\\le v_*$ everywhere. (a) Fix $(x,t)$ with $x\\ne z(t)$ and put $f(s):=t-s-|x-z(s)|/c$. Then $f$ is continuous, $f(t)=-|x-z(t)|/c<0$, and for $s_2>s_1$ the integral representation of $z(s_2)-z(s_1)$ with the integral triangle inequality ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]]) gives $|z(s_2)-z(s_1)|\\le v_*(s_2-s_1)$, hence $f(s_2)-f(s_1)\\le-(1-v_*/c)(s_2-s_1)<0$: $f$ is strictly decreasing. Since $|z(s)|\\le|z(0)|+v_*|s|$ makes $f(s)\\to+\\infty$ as $s\\to-\\infty$, the intermediate value theorem ([[thm-intermediate-value]]) on an interval $[s_1,t]$ with $f(s_1)>0$ produces a root $s<t$, and strict decrease makes it unique. This is the $C^3$ branch of [[lem-em-global-retarded-root]] applied in its corrected event form, which clauses (b)-(d) need because the root must be available at every event of $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$, including events whose spatial point $x$ lies on the worldline at another time. At the root $r=c(t-s)>0$, so $s'\\mapsto|x-z(s')|$ is near $s$ a composition of the $C^3$ map $s'\\mapsto x-z(s')$ with the smooth norm off the origin ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); therefore $F(x,t,s):=t-s-|x-z(s)|/c$ is $C^3$ near $(x,t,s)$ and $\\partial_sF=-\\kappa\\le-(1-v_*/c)<0$ there. The parametrized implicit function theorem with $C^3$ regularity ([[thm-parametrized-implicit-function-theorem-with-higher-regularity]]) supplies a local $C^3$ root map with $Ds=-(\\partial_sF)^{-1}D_{(x,t)}F$, and global uniqueness identifies the local maps, so $s$ is $C^3$ on $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$. Differentiating $F=0$ gives $\\partial_ts=\\kappa^{-1}$ and $\\nabla_xs=-n/(c\\kappa)$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). The emission-time derivative is the one-variable chain rule applied to $s'\\mapsto s'+|x-z(s')|/c$: its derivative is $1-n(s')\\cdot\\beta(s')$, which at $s'=s$ equals $\\kappa$; and $|n\\cdot\\beta|\\le|\\beta|\\le v_*/c<1$ gives $\\kappa\\in[1-v_*/c,1+v_*/c]$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). (b) is exactly clause (b) of [[lem-em-point-retarded-potential-and-fields]], which supplies the finiteness of the pairings, the pairing identities and the identification of $\\Phi,\\Upsilon$ as the regular distributions of the locally integrable zero extensions of the $C^2$ potentials $\\varphi=C/(\\kappa r)$ and $A=\\beta\\varphi/c$ (its proof bounds the explicit history integrals on a fixed-support observation cylinder, uses integrability of $1/|y|$ near zero, and applies the actual nonnegative-Borel and L1 $C^1$ change-of-variables suppliers; the exact consumed statement is the displayed pairing identity, since the library does not define $\\delta\\circ f$). (c) is clause (c) of the same item together with the elementary bounds: $|1-|\\beta|^2|\\le1$, $|n-\\beta|\\le1+v_*/c$ and $\\kappa^3\\ge(1-v_*/c)^3$ give $|E_{\\mathrm{vel}}|\\le |C|(1+v_*/c)(1-v_*/c)^{-3}r^{-2}$; the split and $B=n\\times E/c$ are read off that clause, and the cross-product identities used to recognise the acceleration numerator are in [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] and [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]. (d) For fixed $s_0$ and unit $n$, the same Lipschitz bound gives $|z(t_r)-z(s_0)|\\le v_*(t_r-s_0)=v_*r/c<r=|x_r-z(s_0)|$, so $x_r\\ne z(t_r)$ and $(x_r,t_r)\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$; since $t_r-s_0=r/c=|x_r-z(s_0)|/c$, the time $s_0$ is a root at that event, and uniqueness from (a) gives $s(x_r,t_r)=s_0$. Evaluating (c) at that event with $\\beta=\\beta_0$, $\\dot\\beta=\\dot\\beta_0$, $\\kappa=\\kappa_0$ and retarded distance $r$ gives the displayed expansion with remainder $E_{\\mathrm{vel}}$, whose bound is that of (c); multiplying the field error by $r$ gives the equivalent uniform limit. No uniformity in $s_0$ is claimed because $z''$ is not assumed bounded, and no clause asserts a self-force, radiation reaction, finite energy or physical Maxwell coupling. For the all-time history the point-current proper-graph hypothesis is automatic: compact ambient support bounds its time projection. The signed constant C remains in every field formula; absolute-value estimates use |C|. The witness q=−4pi epsilon0,z=0 gives |E_vel|=r^−2 while a signed-C upper bound would be negative.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§62-§63 printed pp. 158-163: the root equation (63.1) with the uniqueness footnote, the implicit derivatives (63.6)-(63.7), the potentials (63.5) and the field (63.8)-(63.9) with the 1/R^2 velocity part and the 1/R acceleration part; §66 printed pp. 170-172: the large-distance 1/R fields and the t-R'/c combination"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§10.3.1 printed pp. 429-434: the retarded-time equation (10.33), the single-retarded-point argument, the factor (1-n.v/c)^{-1} of (10.37)-(10.38) and the potentials (10.39)-(10.40); §10.3.2 printed pp. 435-441: (10.65)-(10.66) and the velocity/acceleration-field discussion on p. 438"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§6.4.1-§6.4.3 printed pp. 157-164: the delta substitution (6.33)-(6.34) with df/dt'=1-R.v/c, the retarded-time equation (6.36), the kappa bookkeeping (6.39)-(6.41) and the fields (6.42), (6.44)-(6.45)"
              }
