          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For clause (i) assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the Euclidean box-measure interface; the direct root counterexamples in (ii), (iii) are choice-free. Use the zero-based spatial coordinates $x=(x_0,x_1,x_2)$ and standard basis $e_0=(1,0,0)$, $e_1=(0,1,0)$, $e_2=(0,0,1)$ of [F8]. Here $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$ denotes the smooth real-valued functions $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ that vanish outside a compact subset of $\\mathbb R$; smoothness is defined in [F3].\n\n(i) [Plane-wave solutions with infinite spatial $L^2$ norm] Let $c>0$ and $W:=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ as in [[def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed]] and [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]. For every $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$ the function $u:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$, $u(x,t):=f(x_1-ct)$, is of class $C^\\infty$ and satisfies $Wu=0$ identically. If $f\\ne0$, then for every $t$ the level set $\\{x:u(x,t)\\ne0\\}=\\mathbb R\\times(ct+U)\\times\\mathbb R$, where $U:=\\{s\\in\\mathbb R:f(s)\\ne0\\}$ is nonempty and open, is unbounded, and $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|u(x,t)|^2\\,dx=+\\infty$. Consequently the smooth-data existence and smoothness clause (a) of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]] carries no global finite-energy or decay conclusion: compact support of the data is a genuine hypothesis of its clause (d), not a consequence of smoothness.\n\n(ii) [Failure of root existence without the uniform speed bound] Let $c>0$, $a>0$ and $z(s):=(\\sqrt{c^2s^2+a^2},0,0)$. Then $z$ is of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R$ with $|z'(s)|=\\dfrac{c^2|s|}{\\sqrt{c^2s^2+a^2}}<c$ for every $s\\in\\mathbb R$, while $\\sup_{s\\in\\mathbb R}|z'(s)|=c$; so there is no $v_*<c$ with $|z'(s)|\\le v_*$ for all $s$: the worldline is subluminal at every finite time but has no uniform subluminal bound. For the observation $(x,t)=(0,0)$ there is no root $s<t$ of $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$: the equation would read $-s=\\sqrt{c^2s^2+a^2}/c$ with $s<0$, and squaring gives $s^2=s^2+a^2/c^2$, i.e. $a=0$, a contradiction. Thus the existence clause of [[thm-em-retarded-root-and-fields]] genuinely needs its hypothesis $|z'|\\le v_*<c$; the substitution denominator degenerates along this curve in the same limit.\n\n(iii) [The event must lie off the worldline] Let $z$ be of class $C^1$ with $|z'|\\le v_*<c$, and let $x=z(t)$ for some $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Then $s=t$ solves $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$ trivially, and the strict decrease of $s\\mapsto t-s-|x-z(s)|/c$ shows that no root $s<t$ exists; hence the exclusion of the worldline $\\Gamma$ in clause (a) of [[thm-em-retarded-root-and-fields]] is a genuine hypothesis.\n\nThese are explicit mathematical checks of hypotheses and conventions of the wave and retarded-root results; no measurement, physical field identification or empirical claim is made.",
          "strategy": "(i) Smoothness: $u=f\\circ\\pi$ with $\\pi(x,t):=x_1-ct$ linear, hence $C^\\infty$, and $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$, so $u$ is $C^\\infty$ ([[thm-ck-euclidean-maps-closed-under-algebra-and-composition]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Derivatives: $\\partial_tu=-c\\,f'\\circ\\pi$, $\\partial_t^2u=c^2f''\\circ\\pi$, $\\nabla u=(0,f'\\circ\\pi,0)$ and $\\Delta u=f''\\circ\\pi$ ([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]), so $Wu=c^{-2}c^2f''-f''=0$. If $f\\ne0$ then $U=f^{-1}(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\})$ is nonempty and open by continuity, so $\\{x:u(x,t)\\ne0\\}=\\mathbb R\\times(ct+U)\\times\\mathbb R$ is unbounded and $u(\\cdot,t)$ is not compactly supported. For the divergence pick $s_0$ with $f(s_0)\\ne0$ and, by continuity, an interval $I\\ni s_0$ of length $|I|>0$ with $|f|^2\\ge\\eta>0$ on $I$; for every $R>0$ monotonicity ([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]) and Tonelli ([[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]) give $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|u(x,t)|^2dx\\ge\\int_{(-R,R)\\times(ct+I)\\times(-R,R)}|f(x_1-ct)|^2dx\\ge\\eta\\,|I|\\,(2R)^2$, which is unbounded as $R\\to\\infty$; hence the integral is $+\\infty$ (this box computation avoids relying on any convention for $0\\cdot\\infty$). (ii) Set $g(s):=\\sqrt{c^2s^2+a^2}>0$, so $g^2=c^2s^2+a^2$ and differentiation gives $2gg'=2c^2s$, i.e. $g'=c^2s/g$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]); hence $z$ is $C^\\infty$ with $z'(s)=(c^2s/g(s),0,0)$ and $|z'(s)|=c^2|s|/g(s)$. Then $|z'(s)|<c$ for every $s$ because $c^2s^2<c^2s^2+a^2$, while $|z'(s)|=c\\,c|s|/\\sqrt{c^2s^2+a^2}\\to c$ as $s\\to\\pm\\infty$; so no $v_*<c$ bounds $|z'|$ uniformly. At $(x,t)=(0,0)$ a root would satisfy $-s=\\sqrt{c^2s^2+a^2}/c$; the left side is positive only for $s<0$, and squaring gives $s^2=s^2+a^2/c^2$, i.e. $a=0$, so no root exists. This is the curve of the design's X8 counterexample, restated as a pure mathematical check; it also exhibits the collapse of the substitution denominator's uniform lower bound, since with $x=0$ one has $n(s)=-e_0$ and $\\beta(s)\\to-e_0$ as $s\\to-\\infty$, so $\\kappa=1-n\\cdot\\beta\\to0$. (iii) For $x=z(t)$ the value $s=t$ solves the equation, and for $s<t$ the C^1 branch of [[lem-em-global-retarded-root]], or its strict-decrease proof for $s\\mapsto t-s-|x-z(s)|/c$, gives $t-s-|x-z(s)|/c>0$, so there is no root below $t$; the domain $\\Omega\\setminus\\Gamma$ is therefore the correct one. No clause of (i)-(iii) invokes or contradicts the physical interpretation of the fields. In(i), the finite box volumes used in the lower bound are exactly [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]] under the stated Countable Choice. In(ii), repeated differentiation from the positive-domain power derivative in [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]] gives (d/dy)^m y^(1/2)=(product over 0<=j<m of (1/2-j)) y^(1/2-m), continuous for every m>=0 and y>0. Thus the square root is smooth before composition with the positive polynomial c^2s^2+a^2. The root counterexample and strict-decrease argument themselves require no measure assumption.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
