      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-em-lab-geometry",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Laboratory geometry: a restricted inertial chart, its spatial slices and fixed-time fields",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-lab-geometry",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-interval",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-vector-space",
            "lem-standard-basis-of-f-n",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-standard-basis-of-f-n": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space\n([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), let $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an inertial\nframe with inertial chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$\n([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), let $c>0$ be the framework's\nconversion constant, and let $\\delta$ be the Euclidean spatial form of that\nframe on $n^\\perp$, $n:=e_0$, with norm $|\\cdot|$ and positive spatial\norientation ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]). Fix a\nnonempty open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ ([[def-interval]]) and a nonempty\nopen set $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$, and put\n\n$$U:=\\{p\\in A:\\ \\text{there are }t\\in I,\\ y\\in\\Omega\\text{ with }x^0(p)=ct,\\ (x^1,x^2,x^3)(p)=y\\}.$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) [Chart restriction] $x(U)=\\{(ct,y):t\\in I,\\ y\\in\\Omega\\}$ and\n$\\Phi:U\\to I\\times\\Omega$, $\\Phi(p):=(x^0(p)/c,(x^1,x^2,x^3)(p))$, is a\nbijection, because the inertial chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$ is a bijection and\n$c>0$; its components are the **laboratory time coordinate**\n$t(p):=x^0(p)/c$ and the **laboratory position**\n$r(p):=(x^1(p),x^2(p),x^3(p))$. In the chart coordinates the pair\n$(t(p),r(p))$ is the reading of the laboratory decomposition of $p$. Writing\n$r(p)=(r^1(p),r^2(p),r^3(p))\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the standard basis of\n$\\mathbb R^3$ ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]) labels the three coordinate\ndirections, and the identification $\\mathbb R^3\\cong n^\\perp$ is the linear\nmap $\\sum_{i=1}^3 r^i e_i$ into the three-dimensional real vector space\n$n^\\perp$ ([[def-vector-space]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).\n\n(b) [Slices and spatial structure] For each $t\\in I$ the slice\n$U_t:=\\{p\\in U:t(p)=t\\}$ is carried bijectively onto $\\Omega$ by $r$; the\nconstant vector $n=e_0$ is future unit timelike, and $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is the\n$\\delta$-orthonormal positively oriented basis of $n^\\perp$ in which the\ncoordinates of $r$ are expressed.\n\n(c) [Laboratory fields] A **laboratory field of rank $m$** is a map\n$F:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^m$; it is identified with the map $U\\to\\mathbb R^m$,\n$p\\mapsto F(r(p),t(p))$, and with the family $(F(\\cdot,t))_{t\\in I}$ of maps\n$\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^m$, its values at fixed time. Such a field is of class\n$C^k$ when the map $(y,t)\\mapsto F(y,t)$ is $C^k$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]; then $\\partial_{y_i}F$ (at fixed\n$t$) and $\\partial_tF$ (at fixed $y$) are the partial derivatives of\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. No regularity of a field is\nasserted beyond the class stated for it.\n\n(d) [Conventions] The product decomposition is a coordinate identification\ninside the single inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$; it is not a Galilean\ntransformation, it does not compare two inertial frames, and it does not\nassert absolute simultaneity. The objects $I,\\Omega,t,r,\\delta$ and the\nidentification $\\mathbb R^3\\cong n^\\perp$ belong to the named frame; changes\nof inertial description are the proper orthochronous maps adopted in\n[[post-sr-inertial-covariance]]. The frame is an origin together with an\nordered basis; it is not a congruence of observers, and no moving observer\nfield is defined here.",
          "strategy": "A construction definition with elementary verifications. The inertial chart $x$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] is a bijection, so its restriction to the set of events whose chart coordinates lie in $cI\\times\\Omega$ has image $cI\\times\\Omega$; composing with the bijection $cI\\times\\Omega\\to I\\times\\Omega$, $(ct,y)\\mapsto(t,y)$ (bijective because $c>0$) gives the bijection $\\Phi$. Each slice $U_t$ is $\\Phi^{-1}(\\{t\\}\\times\\Omega)$, on which $r$ restricts to a bijection onto $\\Omega$. The claims about $n$, $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$, $\\delta$ and the orientation are exactly the content of [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]; the $C^k$ notion is that of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] and the two kinds of partial derivative are those of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] applied to the product coordinates. Clause (d) records conventions only: the frame dependence of $r,\\delta$ and slices is stated rather than hidden, and no Galilean or observer-congruence claim is made. Nothing here uses a theorem of the framework, and the laboratory region may be a proper subset of $A$; no global chart of $A$ other than the given inertial chart is asserted.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§1 printed pp. 1-3 (velocity of propagation of interaction; principle of relativity), §2 printed pp. 3-7 (intervals and the Minkowski coordinate system) and §4 printed pp. 9-12 (Lorentz transformation), read in the stamped 387-page full text; the laboratory notion of a restricted inertial chart is the library's own formulation of the cited coordinate structure"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.1-§5.1.4, printed pp. 95-101 (PDF pp. 103-109): inertial coordinates, Lorentz transformations, proper time and four-vector conventions. This supports the coordinate conventions; the restricted product-region construction is local."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§12.1 printed pp. 491-494 (the special theory of relativity: inertial frames, coordinates and the role of c), read in the stamped scanned full text as OCR cross-checked text"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The laboratory description of classical electromagnetism in one named inertial frame of flat Minkowski spacetime: a restricted inertial chart, its constant-time slices with the frame's Euclidean spatial structure, and fixed-time component fields. The region may be proper. No Galilean transformation, no observer congruence, no curvature and no quantum structure is defined here; all spatial structure belongs to the named frame."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-si-conventions",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Framework-local SI units, the vacuum constants and the exact/measured status of their values",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-si-conventions",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-lab-geometry"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt the SI system of units for this framework, with the following quantity\ntable and conventions; every symbol is a quantity of this framework and the\nlaboratory structure is that of [[def-em-lab-geometry]].\n\n(a) [Coordinates and constants] $x^0=ct$ and each spatial coordinate $x^i$\ncarries the metre (m); the time coordinate $t$ carries the second (s); the\nconversion constant $c>0$ carries m s$^{-1}$ and has the exact SI defining\nvalue $c=299\\,792\\,458\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$; the elementary charge $e$ has the\nexact SI defining value $e=1.602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{C}$, and this\nitem does not assert that physical charge is quantised; the magnetic constant\n$\\mu_0>0$ carries N A$^{-2}$ and is measured, so no exact numerical value of\n$\\mu_0$ is asserted. The pre-2019 SI fixed the historical nominal\n$\\mu_{0,\\mathrm{nom}}:=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}\n=4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ exactly; since the revised SI took\neffect on 20 May 2019, $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined. The SI brochure\nquotes the nominal $\\mu_{0,\\mathrm{nom}}$ as the estimate at the 2019 adoption\nwith relative standard uncertainty $2.3\\times10^{-10}$; this is historical\nestimate and uncertainty context, not an exact equality for the actual $\\mu_0$\nor a current measured value. The electric constant is defined within the\nframework by\n\n$$\\varepsilon_0:=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$$\n\nin F m$^{-1}$, consistent with the exact SI relation $\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1/c^2$,\nso that $c^2\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1$ and $\\varepsilon_0$ inherits the relative\nstandard uncertainty of $\\mu_0$.\n\n(b) [Electromagnetic quantities] Charge $q$ carries the coulomb (C); the\ncharge density $\\rho$ of the framework's source definition carries C m$^{-3}$\nand the current density $J$ carries A m$^{-2}$, so that the four-current\ncomponents $(c\\rho,J)$ carry A m$^{-2}$; the electric field $E$ carries\nV m$^{-1}$; the magnetic field $B$ carries the tesla (T), where\n$1\\ \\mathrm{T}=1\\ \\mathrm{N\\,A^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$; the scalar potential $\\varphi$\ncarries V and the vector potential $A$ carries T m, so that the four-potential\ncomponents $(\\varphi/c,A)$ carry T m; the components of the electromagnetic\ntwo-form of the framework's tensor definition carry T; a force carries the\nnewton (N), $1\\ \\mathrm{N}=1\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}}$.\n\n(c) [Mechanical quantities] A rest mass parameter $m$ carries the kilogram\n(kg) and a four-momentum $P=mU$ of the relativistic particle model carries\nkg m s$^{-1}$; field energy density carries J m$^{-3}$, energy flux\nW m$^{-2}$, so that $1\\ \\mathrm{W}=1\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s^{-1}}=1\\ \\mathrm{V\\,A}$.\n\n(d) [Bookkeeping only] The unit of a quantity records the adopted system of\nunits; it does not show that a quantity is primitive in this or any other\nframework, it contains no measurement, and it does not identify a quantity\nwith a similarly named quantity of another framework. The exact defining\nvalues of $c$ and $e$ fix numerical conventions of the SI; they do not by\nthemselves identify $c$ with the vacuum propagation constant of ideal light,\nwhich is the physical adoption of the framework's Minkowski postulate.",
          "strategy": "A convention definition: it fixes the coordinate normalisation $x^0=ct$, the unit of each quantity used in this framework, the exact SI defining values of $c$ and $e$ and the measured status of $\\mu_0$. The exact values $c=299\\,792\\,458$ m/s and $e=1.602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}$ C, and the statements that the new definition of the ampere fixes $e$ rather than $\\mu_0$, that $\\mu_0$ must now be determined experimentally, and that $\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1/c^2$ remains exact, are taken from the SI brochure's defining-constants table and its ampere section. The framework's definition of $\\varepsilon_0$ by $(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ is a definition consistent with that exact relation; the uncertainty carried by $\\varepsilon_0$ is recorded rather than hidden. The dimensional checks of the framework's equations and couplings are developed on the companion examples page of this pair. No exact or current measured numerical value of $\\mu_0$ is asserted and no experiment is claimed. The historical nominal and adoption uncertainty are explicitly source context, not a substitution for the current experimentally determined constant.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "BIPM, The International System of Units (SI), 9th edition, official current PDF version 4.01 (214 pages; PDF metadata dated 2 June 2026), retrieved 9 October 2026",
                "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.2, English printed pp. 123-124 (PDF pp. 125-126): exact defining constants c and e and their uncertainty-free status; §2.3.1, The ampere, English printed p. 128 (PDF p. 130): pre-2019 exact mu0, current experimental determination, exact eps0 mu0=1/c², same relative standard uncertainty, and historical 2019 adoption estimate with 2.3×10^-10 relative standard uncertainty; 26th CGPM (2018) Resolution 1, English printed p. 194 (PDF p. 196): revised SI effective 20 May 2019. These pages were read in the newly retrieved official PDF; no new current CODATA mu0 numerical estimate is claimed."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.1-§1.2, printed pp. 2-6: charge, sources and fields. §3.5, printed pp. 64-65 (PDF pp. 72-73): SI units and the post-2019 status of e and mu_0. The official BIPM reference controls the exact defining values and historical uncertainty qualification."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§5.3.2 printed pp. 224-225 (eq. (5.33)-(5.34): mu_0 = 4pi x 10^-7 N/A^2 in the pre-2019 edition and 1 T = 1 N/(A m); this printed value is not adopted as exact) and §4.3.2 printed pp. 180-181 (eps_0 = 8.85 x 10^-12 C^2/N m^2); read in the stamped scanned full text"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "SI unit and constant conventions for the classical electromagnetic framework, with the exact defining values of c and e, the measured status of mu_0, and the framework's definition of eps_0 = (mu_0 c^2)^-1. The item fixes units and dimensional bookkeeping only; it asserts no measurement, no charge quantisation and no cross-framework identification of quantities."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-sources",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Charge parameter, frame charge and current densities, volume charge and flux, and the four-current",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-sources",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "dependency_level": 12,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable",
            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric",
            "thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel",
            "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable",
            "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets",
            "prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory region and its chart be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. (a) [Charge parameter] The framework takes a real charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$, measured in coulombs, attached to a source; its sign is the sign of the source and it is a parameter of the model. Charge quantisation is not asserted. (b) [Smooth source fields] A smooth (volume) source field on $\\Omega\\times I$ is a pair $(\\rho,J)$ of maps $\\rho:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$, $J:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ that is continuous on $\\Omega\\times I$ and of class $C^1$ wherever a displayed equation differentiates it; $\\rho$ is the frame charge density (C m$^{-3}$) and $J$ the frame current density (A m$^{-2}$). For each fixed $t\\in I$ retain the compact Jordan branch: if $V\\subseteq\\Omega$ is compact and Jordan measurable, define $Q_R(V,t):=\\int_V\\rho(y,t)dy$ by [[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]], with integrability supplied by [[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]]. For the full measurable-volume branch assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the published Lebesgue-measure interface. If $V$ is any bounded Lebesgue-measurable subset with compact closure $\\overline V\\subseteq\\Omega$, define\n$$Q(V,t):=\\int_V\\rho(y,t)d\\lambda_3(y)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}F_t(y)\\mathbf1_V(y)d\\lambda_3(y),$$\nwhere $F_t=\\rho(\\cdot,t)$ on $\\Omega$ and $0$ elsewhere; the signed integral is defined through its positive and negative parts ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]). This integral is absolutely finite, since $\\rho(\\cdot,t)$ is bounded on $\\overline V$ and $V$ has finite Lebesgue measure. On the compact Jordan branch, under the same Countable Choice assumption, $Q=Q_R$ by [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]]. The empty region has charge zero in both branches. The current flux remains defined independently: for each oriented surface patch in $\\Omega$ with unit normal field $n_S$ the current flux at time $t$ is $\\int_S J(y,t)\\cdot n_S(y)\\,dS$ ([[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]]). Point sources and sheet sources are not source fields in this sense; they are different objects, defined by their own pairings when the framework uses them. (c) [Covariant identification] Write $j:=(c\\rho,J)$, so $j^0=c\\rho$ and $j^i=J^i$. The framework adopts as a model premise the identification of the charge-current of a source with a vector field of the affine Minkowski model, and hence the component transformation law of [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] for $j$ between inertial charts. This is a model premise, not a theorem about physical currents. (d) [Frame density, scalar multiplier and the normalized material branch] Suppose additionally that the identified $j$ is $C^1$ and $j=\\rho_0U$, with a $C^1$ future timelike vector field $U$ and a $C^1$ real scalar multiplier $\\rho_0$. Since $U^0>0$, define the normalized chart direction $u:=U/U^0$, so $u^0=1$, $u^i=U^i/U^0$. Then $j=(\\rho_0U^0)u$ and, because the electromagnetic convention is $j^0=c\\rho$,\n$$\\rho=\\rho_0U^0/c,\\qquad J^i=\\rho_0U^i=c\\rho\\,u^i.$$\nPointwise equality $\\rho=\\rho_0$ holds if and only if $\\rho_0(U^0-c)=0$, including the zero-multiplier case. Where $\\rho_0\\ne0$, equality is equivalent to $U^0=c$, which does not alone imply $U^i=0$ for an arbitrary future timelike $U$. Thus a scalar multiplier is not automatically a Lorentz rest charge density. The direction $u$ is chart normalized, with no unit or proper-four-velocity normalization imposed in this general branch; if its coordinate velocity interpretation is adopted, the spatial velocity is $v^i:=cu^i$.\nFor the separate convective source model, explicitly assume that $U$ is the material proper-time four-velocity, normalized by $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ as in [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. This is an additional model condition, not a consequence of the factorization $j=\\rho_0U$. In that branch, $U=\\gamma(c,v)$, $|v|<c$, $\\gamma=(1-|v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, and\n$$\\rho=\\gamma\\rho_0,\\qquad J=\\rho v.$$\nHere $\\rho_0$ is the rest charge density of this stipulated convective model: in a comoving inertial frame $U=(c,0,0,0)$ and $j=(c\\rho_0,0,0,0)$. The equality $\\rho=\\rho_0$ then holds exactly where $\\rho_0=0$ or $v=0$ (equivalently $\\gamma=1$). Both signs of charge and the zero-density case are retained. (e) [Scope] This definition fixes the source objects of the smooth branch and their transformation interface. It asserts no conservation law (that is proved for stated source classes) and no regularity of singular sources.",
          "strategy": "Keep clauses (a)-(c) as explicit source types and the adopted physical identification. The compact Jordan branch uses [[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]] and [[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]]; flux uses [[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]]. For the broader Lebesgue branch assume the explicitly stated Countable Choice, without Dependent Choice or a convergence/FTC assumption. Fix $t$ and an admitted nonempty $V$. The continuous function $\\rho(\\cdot,t)$ is bounded by $M_t<\\infty$ in absolute value on compact $\\overline V$ by [[thm-extreme-value-metric]]. Its zero extension $F_t$ is Borel: for every open $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, the inverse image inside the open set $\\Omega$ is relatively open and hence open in $\\mathbb R^3$; add the closed complement of $\\Omega$ precisely when $0\\in O$. This is also the open-set form of [[thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel]]. Under Countable Choice, Borel sets are Lebesgue measurable ([[thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable]]) and bounded measurable $V$ has finite measure ([[prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets]]). Positive/negative parts and their restrictions by $\\mathbf1_V$ are measurable by [[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]]. The bound $|F_t|\\mathbf1_V\\le M_t\\mathbf1_V$ and [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]] give\n$$\\int|F_t|\\mathbf1_Vd\\lambda_3\\le M_t\\lambda_3(V)<\\infty,$$\nwhere the indicator integral is its simple-function value by [[prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral]]. This proves exactly the absolute signed integrability required by [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]] and defines $Q$ using the nonnegative restrictions of [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]. For empty $V$ the product is zero. On compact Jordan $V$, the continuous density restricted to V has a bounded Borel zero extension to a bounding rectangle, so the exact compact-Jordan clause of [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]], also under Countable Choice, identifies the two finite integral values. The algebraic supplier [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]](vii) gives $j=\\rho_0U=\\rho_0U^0u$; its coordinate density is $j^0$, whereas the present SI source convention defines $j^0=c\\rho$. Division by $c>0$ gives $\\rho=\\rho_0U^0/c$ and $\\rho-\\rho_0=\\rho_0(U^0-c)/c$, proving exactly the stated equality cases. The spatial components give $J^i=c\\rho u^i$. Neither $U^0=c$ nor chart normalization $u^0=1$ is a proper-four-velocity condition.\nIn the additional normalized material model, write $v=c(U^1,U^2,U^3)/U^0$. The norm equation $-(U^0)^2+|U_{\\mathrm{sp}}|^2=-c^2$ gives $(U^0)^2(1-|v|^2/c^2)=c^2$, and future timelikeness gives $U^0>0$ and $|v|<c$, so $U^0=c\\gamma$. This is the exact four-velocity/relative-velocity content of [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]; substitution gives $\\rho=\\gamma\\rho_0$ and $J=\\rho v$. In its comoving frame $U^0=c$, hence the source convention identifies the scalar multiplier with the stipulated rest-frame density. Since $\\gamma=1$ if and only if $v=0$, the normalized equality cases follow, including $\\rho_0=0$. All density readings remain within the adopted current model; no conservation, measurement, general current factorization, force law or singular-source regularity is deduced.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.1 printed pp. 2-3 (charge q in R and the Coulomb; charge density rho with Q = integral of rho; current density J with I = surface integral of J; smooth densities and the point density q delta(x - r(t))); read in the stamped 236-page PDF"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§28 printed pp. 69-71 (the four-dimensional current vector j^i = (c rho, j) and the transformation of the charge-current) and §29 printed pp. 71-73 (the equation of continuity), read in the stamped 387-page full text in the Gaussian-unit conventions that the framework re-expresses in SI"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.3.3 printed pp. 326-327 (eq. (7.41): the continuity equation) and §8.3 printed pp. 356-358 (charge conservation and Maxwell's equations), read in the stamped scanned full text"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Charge and current sources of the classical electromagnetic framework in a named inertial laboratory frame: the charge parameter, smooth frame densities and their volume charge and flux integrals, and the adopted identification of the charge-current with a vector field of the Minkowski model. Point and sheet sources are excluded here and are defined by their own pairings; the item asserts no conservation law and no empirical measurement. Its optional convective branch identifies a scalar multiplier as rest charge density only when the material proper-time four-velocity normalization is explicitly adopted; the general future-timelike factorization is retained without that identification. The original compact Jordan/Riemann charge branch remains valid. The full bounded Lebesgue-measurable volume branch with compact closure inside the laboratory region is retained under the exact Countable Choice cost; no singular-source Maxwell postulate is supplied by that extension."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-fields",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The electric and magnetic fields as fixed-time spatial vector fields",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-fields",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "deps": [
            "def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-transpose-of-a-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$, its spatial Euclidean structure\nand its units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and\n[[def-em-si-conventions]], and let $k\\ge1$. An **electric field of class\n$C^k$** on the region is a map $E:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ that is of class\n$C^k$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]; a\n**magnetic field of class $C^k$** is a map $B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of\nthe same class. At fixed $t\\in I$ the maps $E(\\cdot,t),B(\\cdot,t):\\Omega\\to\n\\mathbb R^3$ are real spatial vector fields on the region $\\Omega$, read in the\n$\\delta$-orthonormal basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of the frame. The framework uses:\n$C^1$ fields in the classical Maxwell branch, $C^2$ fields where second or\nmixed time-space derivatives of the fields appear, and $C^\\infty$ fields in\nthe smooth existence branches; every statement carries its own regularity and\nno regularity beyond it is asserted.\n\nConventions:\n\n(a) The **polar-vector** and **axial-vector** conventions mean the following\ncomponent rules under a spatial orthogonal coordinate change $y'=Qy$, where\n$Q$ is a real $3\\times3$ matrix with $Q^{\\mathsf T}Q=I_3$. Transpose,\nmatrix product and identity are those of [[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]] and\n[[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]], and $\\det Q$ is the determinant\nof [[def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix]]. At corresponding\npoints $E'(y',t)=QE(y,t)$ and $B'(y',t)=(\\det Q)QB(y,t)$. Thus polar and axial\ncomponents agree in their transformation rule when $\\det Q=1$ and differ by\na sign when $\\det Q=-1$. These rules specify the orientation convention;\nthe adopted inertial descriptions have positive orientation, and no physical\norientation-reversing change of description is adopted here.\n\n(b) The fields are defined by their values on $\\Omega\\times I$, and no value at\na point of singular source support is asserted.\n\n(c) $E$ carries V m$^{-1}$ and $B$ carries T.\n\n(d) The identification of the pair $(E,B)$ with the components of an\nalternating two-form, including its transformation behaviour, is made in the\nframework's electromagnetic two-form definition on this page and is not\npresupposed here.",
          "strategy": "An explicit type/domain/convention definition: the fields are maps on the product $\\Omega\\times I$ of the laboratory region with a stated class of regularity, and their fixed-time readings are spatial vector fields in the frame basis. The regularity notion is that of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]; the polar/axial convention and the exclusion of pointwise values at singular source support are conventions recorded explicitly rather than used implicitly. No differential equation and no derived quantity is introduced by this definition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.2 printed pp. 4-6 (the electric and magnetic fields as vector fields and the SI units in which they are measured); read in the stamped 236-page PDF"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§1.1-§1.2 printed pp. 1-10 (the electric and magnetic fields as vector functions of position and time; the pseudovector character of B is stated at eq. (5.1) with footnote 1 on p. 204) and §5.1.2 printed p. 204 (B is a pseudovector), read in the stamped scanned full text"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§§24-25 printed pp. 62-66 (the field transformation and the invariants, in which E and H are the components of an antisymmetric tensor), read in the stamped 387-page full text"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The electric and magnetic fields of the classical electromagnetic framework as fixed-time spatial vector fields on a named laboratory region, with an explicit regularity class and the polar/axial convention. The definition asserts no field equation, no value at singular source support, and no physical covariance postulate for orientation-reversing descriptions."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-em-minkowski-physical-model",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted Minkowski physical model of the electromagnetic framework",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#post-em-minkowski-physical-model",
          "design_state": "explicit-adopted-postulate-contract; nonproof-source-scope-review",
          "dependency_level": 8,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-minkowski-affine-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt as physical assumptions of this framework:\n\n(i) The oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ used by\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]], together with the\nconversion constant $c>0$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]], is the spacetime of the\nframework: its points represent idealized physical events, displacements\nrepresent idealized spacetime separations, and the causal classification of the\nmodel is the framework's causal structure. In the concrete coordinate\nrealization [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]], $A=V=\\mathbb R^4$ and\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. That supplier uses the symbol $C^+$ for\nthe nonzero future causal cone, including null vectors; here, as in the\nspecial-relativity model, $C^+$ denotes its future timelike subset. The\nfuture null boundary vectors retain their separate causal classification.\n\n(ii) The inertial frames and charts of\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] are the inertial descriptions of the\nframework, and changes of description are the proper orthochronous isometries\nadopted in [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]].\n\n(iii) The ideal clock reading rule along future timelike curves and the\nidentification of ideal vacuum light rays with future null straight lines, both\nwith the same constant $c$, are those adopted in\n[[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]].\n\n(iv) The laboratory description of fields and sources is the restricted\ninertial chart of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with the spatial Euclidean structure\nand the SI conventions of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and\n[[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\nStatus and scope: the mathematical model defines the geometry and its theorems\nprove geometric facts only; that physical events are described by the model,\nthat ideal clocks read the stated proper time and that ideal vacuum light\nfollows null straight lines are adopted assumptions, not deductions from\nexperiment or from the mathematical axioms. The special-relativity postulates\nadopted here do not by themselves select the Maxwell equations, a force law or\nany matter model: the electromagnetic equations and couplings of this framework\nare further postulates of it. The framework is classical, flat and non-quantum;\ngravitational curvature, quantum fields and measurement postulates are outside\nits scope.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem: it records the adopted physical reading of the mathematical Minkowski model of [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]] and reuses the two special-relativity physical postulates [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]] and [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] with the shared constant $c$ as the vacuum propagation constant. The exposition must separate what the mathematical model fixes (the affine geometry, its causal cones, its frames) from what is adopted (the event interpretation, the clock and light interpretation, the inertial-description equivalence). It must also state that Maxwell's equations and the Lorentz coupling are additional postulates not implied by the SR assumptions, and it asserts no empirical measurement or verification.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§1 printed pp. 1-3 (the principle of relativity and the propagation of interactions with the velocity c), §2 printed pp. 3-7 (Minkowski spacetime and intervals), §3 printed pp. 7-9 (proper time) and §4 printed pp. 9-12 (Lorentz transformations), read in the stamped 387-page full text"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.1-§5.1.4, printed pp. 95-101 (PDF pp. 103-109): inertial descriptions, Lorentz transformations and proper time, with signature (+---) converted here to (-+++). The physical model is adopted, not deduced."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§12.1-§12.2 printed pp. 491-504 (the postulates of special relativity, their interpretation and the constancy of c), read in the stamped scanned full text"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The adopted physical interpretation of flat Minkowski spacetime for classical electromagnetism: events, inertial descriptions, ideal clocks and ideal vacuum light rays with the shared constant c. The postulate is an assumption, not an experimental result; it selects no field equation and no force law, and it excludes gravity, quantum effects and measurement postulates."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Worldlines, four-velocity, chart readings and the frame/observer distinction",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "dependency_level": 12,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration",
            "def-sr-proper-time",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline",
            "post-em-minkowski-physical-model",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-proper-time": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-minkowski-physical-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "The particle and light objects of the framework are the following curves and\nvector fields of the affine Minkowski model ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]],\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), with the physical interpretation of\n[[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]] and the units of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n(a) [Worldlines] A **worldline** is a regular causal curve $x:J\\to A$ in one of\nthe regularity classes of [[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]] ($J$ an\norder-convex subset of $\\mathbb R$ with at least two elements). A **massive\nworldline** is a $C^k$ future timelike worldline, $k\\ge1$, with everywhere\nnonzero future timelike tangent. A **future null curve** is a $C^1$ curve with\neverywhere nonzero future null tangent. An **ideal vacuum light ray** of the\nadopted physical model follows a null straight line: on its parameter interval\nit has the form $x(s)=p+\\lambda(s)v$, where $p\\in A$, $v\\in V$ is nonzero\nfuture null, and $\\lambda$ is $C^1$ with $\\lambda'(s)>0$. This is the\nstraight-ray adoption of [[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]]; arbitrary\nfuture null curves are retained as kinematic objects without asserting that\nthey describe vacuum light propagation. Along a null curve the integrand\n$-\\eta(x',x')$ of the proper-time construction\n([[def-sr-proper-time]]) vanishes identically, so that construction yields no\nproper-time parametrization of it, and a light ray is never treated as a\nmassive worldline. A worldline is a curve in spacetime; its reading $r\\circ x$\nin a named laboratory chart is a coordinate description of it, not an object of\nanother observer.\n\n(b) [Four-velocity and four-acceleration] For a massive worldline the\nfour-velocity $U$ and, when it is $C^2$, the four-acceleration $A$ are those of\n[[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]; wherever defined they satisfy\n$\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ and $\\eta(U,A)=0$, by\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]].\n\n(c) [Chart reading] If a massive $C^2$ worldline is described in a laboratory\nchart of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] by $t\\mapsto(ct,r(t))$ on an open interval of\nlaboratory times, then $|r'(t)|<c$ for every $t$, with four-velocity\n$U=\\gamma(c,r')$ and Lorentz factor\n$\\gamma=(1-|r'|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, by clause (e) of\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]].\n\n(d) [Frames and observers] An inertial frame in this framework is the\norigin-with-basis object of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]; a point\nobserver is a future unit timelike vector $n\\in V$ with rest space $n^\\perp$ and\nrest projection $h$, as in clauses (c)-(d) of\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. On an open spacetime region\n$W\\subseteq A$, an **observer congruence field** means a $C^1$ map $n:W\\to V$ with\n$n(p)$ future timelike and $\\eta(n(p),n(p))=-1$ at every $p$; its observer\nhistories, when given, are integral curves satisfying $dx/d\\tau=cn(x)$.\nA **tetrad field** is a tuple of $C^1$ maps $(f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3):W\\to V^4$\nwhose values form a positively oriented $\\eta$-orthonormal basis at every\nevent, with $f_0$ future timelike. Regularity here means componentwise $C^1$\nin inertial coordinates. These fields need not be constant and are distinct\nfrom an inertial origin-with-basis frame, its coordinate chart and a single\npoint observer; no global congruence or tetrad is asserted to exist.\n\n(e) [Scope] No force law, no self-field and no extended-body structure is\nsupplied here; an accelerating massive worldline requires a force model stated\nseparately, and an ideal light ray carries no dispersive, quantum or\npolarisation structure in this definition.",
          "strategy": "Definition-only formulation: regular causal curves with SR endpoint conventions, future timelike massive curves, straight regular future-null ideal rays, proper-time derivatives, and explicitly typed C1 observer and tetrad fields. No field existence or proof of a physical assumption is asserted.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§3 printed pp. 7-9 (proper time), §6-§7 printed pp. 14-22 (four-vectors and four-velocity) and §9 printed pp. 25-28 (energy and momentum), read in the stamped 387-page full text"
              },
