          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distributional-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt as a physical postulate of this framework the following four equations for the fields of [[def-em-fields]] and the sources of [[def-em-sources]] on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with $\\mu_0>0$, $c>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ and the SI units of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. (a) [Smooth branch] Let $E,B$ be jointly $C^1$ fields with the additional derivatives required by each equation existing and continuous, and let $\\rho,J$ be continuous. Then, with the divergence and curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and the partial derivative $\\partial_t$ at fixed position of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]],\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE.$$\n\n(b) [Weak branch] Let instead $E,B$ be locally integrable $\\mathbb R^3$-valued fields and $\\rho,J$ locally integrable scalar and vector fields on $I\\times\\Omega$, identified with their regular distributions ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]). Let $\\operatorname{div}$, $\\operatorname{curl}$ and $\\partial_t$ act as distributional derivatives, and let $\\psi$ range over $C_c^\\infty(I\\times\\Omega)$ ([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]). Then the same four identities hold in the sense of [[def-distribution]]: the pairings $\\langle\\operatorname{div}E-\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\psi\\rangle$, $\\langle\\operatorname{div}B,\\psi\\rangle$, $\\langle\\operatorname{curl}E+\\partial_tB,\\psi\\rangle$ and $\\langle\\operatorname{curl}B-\\mu_0J-c^{-2}\\partial_tE,\\psi\\rangle$ vanish for every such $\\psi$, all derivatives being distributional derivatives ([[def-distributional-derivative]]). (c) [Scope] The postulate contains no magnetic charge: $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and no magnetic-source term appears in $\\operatorname{curl}E$. Sources that are not locally integrable functions (point or sheet sources) are covered by neither branch and require their own definition. The equations are adopted classical vacuum physics in the named inertial chart; they are not deduced from charge conservation, from empirical agreement or from the special-relativity postulates, and this item by itself asserts no covariance under changes of inertial frame.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem. Obligations and conventions: the fields and sources are those of [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]], the operators are the published divergence and curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and the partial derivative of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and the weak reading uses exactly the regular-distribution embedding, the test space and the distributional derivative named in the statement. The exposition must state that the four equations are adopted, that the choice of the sign and constant conventions is the one fixed by [[def-em-si-conventions]], and that no magnetic charge is included; the sources not covered by either branch are excluded explicitly. Empirical support for Maxwell's equations is a nonlogical relation of the framework, not a proof, and no verification is claimed here.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.2.1 printed pp. 5-6, equations (1.4)-(1.7) in SI units (div E = rho/eps_0, div B = 0, curl E + d_t B = 0, curl B - mu_0 eps_0 d_t E = mu_0 J); read in the stamped 236-page PDF. The notes also print the pre-2019 exact value mu_0 = 4pi x 10^-7, which this framework does not adopt as exact"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.3.3 printed pp. 326-327, equations (7.39)-(7.40) in SI units, with the remark that they summarise the theoretical content of classical electrodynamics and eq. (7.41) the continuity equation; §7.3.4 printed p. 327: the magnetic-charge terms are set to zero, so div B = 0 and no magnetic source appears in curl E. Read in the stamped scanned full text"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§26 printed pp. 66-67 (the first pair: curl E = -(1/c) dH/dt and div H = 0) and §30 printed pp. 73-75 (the second pair: dF^{ik}/dx^k = -(4pi/c) j^i and its three-dimensional form), read in the stamped 387-page full text; the Gaussian-unit forms are re-expressed here in the SI conventions of [[def-em-si-conventions]]"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The adopted microscopic vacuum Maxwell equations of the classical framework, in both a smooth (pointwise) branch for jointly C^1 fields and a weak (distributional) branch for locally integrable fields and sources, in a named inertial chart with SI constants. No magnetic charge, no singular point or sheet source and no material constitutive law is included; the equations are adopted assumptions and are not claimed to describe nature exactly."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The electromagnetic two-form, its observer decomposition and the four-current",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
