            "thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distributional-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the vacuum Maxwell postulate [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the fields of [[def-em-fields]] and the sources of [[def-em-sources]], and fix the constants $\\mu_0>0$, $c>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Let $n\\ge1$, let $(E_1,B_1,\\rho_1,J_1),\\dots,(E_n,B_n,\\rho_n,J_n)$ be $n$ field-source pairs each satisfying one and the same branch of the postulate on the same region (all in the smooth branch, or all in the weak branch), and let $a_1,\\dots,a_n\\in\\mathbb R$. Put $E:=\\sum_{j=1}^na_jE_j$, $B:=\\sum_{j=1}^na_jB_j$, $\\rho:=\\sum_{j=1}^na_j\\rho_j$ and $J:=\\sum_{j=1}^na_jJ_j$. Then: (a) $(E,B,\\rho,J)$ satisfies the same branch of the postulate on the same region with the same constants: pointwise in the smooth branch and in the sense of the pairings of clause (b) of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] in the weak branch. (b) Nothing beyond this linearity is asserted: the result does not apply to a source whose density depends nonlinearly on the fields (a nonlinear constitutive law), and no convergence statement is made for infinite series or integrals of solutions or for infinite linear combinations. (c) The theorem is conditional on the adopted postulate and is not an empirical claim about nature.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct termwise computation, smooth branch and distributional branch separately. In the smooth branch, the divergence and curl are linear in their arguments because they are finite sums of first partial derivatives ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]), and $\\partial_t$ is linear likewise; hence $\\operatorname{div}E=\\sum_ja_j\\operatorname{div}E_j=\\sum_ja_j\\rho_j/\\varepsilon_0=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and, in the same way, $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$. In the weak branch the distributional derivatives are linear and the pairings are linear in the distributions ([[def-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]), so the four vanishing pairings of the postulate for the sum follow by summing the finitely many vanishing pairings of the summands with the same test function. The scope clauses (nonlinear sources, no infinite superposition) are recorded in the theorem itself; no physical premise beyond the postulate is used, and no empirical premise occurs, so no empirical qualifications are inherited beyond the postulate's own.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.2.1 printed pp. 5-6 (the Maxwell equations are linear in the fields and sources; the notes use linearity throughout, e.g. in the superposition of electrostatic fields in Chapter 2), read in the stamped 236-page PDF"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.3.3 printed pp. 326-327 (the Maxwell equations as linear equations in E, B and the sources; the superposition discussion in §2.1.4 printed pp. 68-70 for the electrostatic case), read in the stamped scanned full text"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§26 and §30 printed pp. 66-75 (the field equations are linear in the potentials, fields and current, so solutions add with their sources), read in the stamped 387-page full text"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Superposition of finitely many prescribed Maxwell field-source pairs on a common laboratory region, within the adopted vacuum Maxwell postulate, in either its smooth or its weak branch. The theorem excludes nonlinear constitutive laws and makes no convergence claim for infinite superpositions; it is a conditional deduction and not an empirical claim, and it inherits only the postulate's physical assumptions."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-em-lorentz-coupling",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Lorentz coupling: external force on a test charge and the continuum force and power densities",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#post-em-lorentz-coupling",
          "design_state": "explicit-adopted-postulate-contract; nonproof-source-scope-review",
          "dependency_level": 17,
          "deps": [
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames",
            "def-em-sources",
            "lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction",
            "post-em-minkowski-physical-model",
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
            "pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-minkowski-physical-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt as physical postulates of this framework, on the spacetime and particle\nmodel of [[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]] and\n[[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]] and on the special-relativity particle\nmodel [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]]:\n\n(i) [Test charge in a prescribed external field] Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ be a\nconstant charge parameter ([[def-em-sources]]), $m>0$ an externally supplied\nrest mass, $x:J\\to A$ a future timelike $C^2$ massive worldline with\nfour-velocity $U$, and let $F$ be a prescribed field of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] that is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of the graph\nof $x$ and is generated externally to the particle. Adopt the equation of\nmotion\n\n$$m\\,\\frac{dU}{d\\tau}=q\\,\\Phi(U),$$\n\nwhere, at each original parameter $s\\in J$, $\\Phi=\\Phi_{x(s)}$ is the\nraised-index operator of the field at $x(s)$, defined by\n$\\eta(\\Phi_{x(s)}(u),w)=F(x(s))(w,u)$\n([[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]]); equivalently\n$m\\,dU^\\mu/d\\tau=q\\,F^{\\mu\\nu}U_\\nu$. Here $dU/d\\tau$ is the derivative of $U$\nwith respect to its proper-time function $\\tau(s)$: explicitly\n$dU/d\\tau=U'(s)/\\tau'(s)$, with\n$\\tau'(s)=c^{-1}\\sqrt{-\\eta(x'(s),x'(s))}>0$. It is the four-acceleration of the worldline\n([[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]],\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]). The particle's own field\nis excluded from $F$: this is the external-field coupling, and it defines no\nself-force.\n\n(ii) [Continuum matter coupling] For a smooth source $(\\rho,J)$ of\n[[def-em-sources]] interacting with the field through an explicitly stated\nmatter model to which this coupling applies, adopt the force density\n$\\rho E+J\\times B$ (unit N m$^{-3}$) and the power density $J\\cdot E$ (unit\nW m$^{-3}$) as the electromagnetic source terms of that matter model, with the\nspatial cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; they describe the local\ninteraction of the field with matter and are not a property of a prescribed\ncurrent alone.\n\n(iii) [Scope] The rest mass and the charge are inputs; $m$ is not derived within\nelectromagnetism. The coupling is adopted for constant $q$ and $m>0$; no\nmagnetic-monopole coupling, no radiation reaction and no self-force are\nincluded. In particular the nonrelativistic coordinate statement\n$mr''=q(E+r'\\times B)$ is not adopted as exact: the adopted statement is the\ncovariant one of clause (i), and any low-speed statement requires its own\napproximation hypotheses and error bounds.",
          "strategy": "A postulate, not a theorem. The exposition must state the exact data (constant charge, externally supplied positive rest mass, future timelike C^2 worldline, prescribed external field C^1 near the graph), name the raised-index operator $\\Phi$ with its defining identity and index form from [[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]] so that the equation is unambiguous, and state the exclusion of the particle's own field. Clause (ii) is a formulation premise for matter models, with its units fixed by [[def-em-si-conventions]] and its cross product the spatial one of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; it is not a derivation from the field equations. Clause (iii) records the scope: no self-force, no radiation reaction, no magnetic monopole, and no exact Newtonian law. Empirical support for the coupling is a nonlogical relation of the framework; the item claims no verification and no measured value.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.2 printed pp. 4-6 (the Lorentz force law F = q(E + rdot x B) and its force-density form f = rho E + J x B, eqs. (1.2)-(1.3)); read in the stamped 236-page PDF"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§5.1.2 printed pp. 204-205 (eq. (5.2): F = Q[E + (v x B)], stated as a fundamental axiom of the theory) and §8.1.1 printed pp. 345-348 (the force density rho E + J x B and the work density J . E), read in the stamped scanned full text"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§17 printed pp. 46-49 (the equations of motion of a charge in a field) and §23 printed pp. 60-62 (eq. (23.4): mc du_i/ds = (e/c) F_ik u^k, the four-dimensional form of the coupling), read in the stamped 387-page full text; the Gaussian-unit form is re-expressed in the SI conventions of def-em-si-conventions with q and m as model parameters"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The adopted coupling of the classical framework between the electromagnetic field and matter: the covariant equation of motion of a test charge in a prescribed external field with constant charge and positive rest mass, and the continuum force and power densities for a matter model to which the coupling applies. The postulate excludes self-fields, radiation reaction, magnetic monopoles and any exact nonrelativistic law; it is a model assumption and not an experimental result."
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 325
    },
    {
      "id": "em-primitives-and-postulates-examples",
      "title": "Primitives and postulates — Examples",
