            "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the convention of\nthe surface-measure interface cited below ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, let $S\\subset\\Omega$ be a compact\nembedded $C^2$ hypersurface whose surface measure is that of the compact\nembedded $C^1$ class of\n[[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]] (the $C^2$ data are\nspecial cases of the $C^1$ data), and let $a:S\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous.\nSuppose that\n\n$$\\int_Sa\\psi\\,dS=0$$\n\nfor every $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ ([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]),\nthe pairing being the signed surface integral of\n[[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]] of the continuous\nfunction $a\\,(\\psi|_S)$. For complex tests the integral is defined componentwise in real and imaginary parts; the proof below uses real tests. Then $a=0$ on $S$. Equivalently, if two continuous\ndensities on $S$ pair equally against every test function, then they are equal\npointwise on $S$. The conclusion is pointwise and uses the continuity of $a$;\na merely locally integrable density would satisfy only an almost-everywhere\nconclusion, which this statement does not claim.",
          "strategy": "Assume the stated Countable Choice convention. If $a(x_0)\\ne0$, replace $a$ by $-a$ if needed and put $m=a(x_0)/2>0$. Continuity of $a$ on $S$ gives an ambient neighbourhood $N$ of $x_0$ on which $a\\ge m$ on $S\\cap N$. By the embedded $C^2$ hypothesis, the inverse/implicit-function suppliers give, after permuting coordinates, a graph chart $X(u)=(u,g(u))$ on an open $D\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ with $X(u_0)=x_0$ and $g\\in C^2(D)$. Choose an open ambient chart neighbourhood $O\\subset\\Omega\\cap N$ containing $x_0$, meeting $S$ only in this graph chart. Choose $0<r<R$ with $\\overline B_R(x_0)\\subset O$. The actual ambient bump supplied by [[lem-scaled-euclidean-bumps-with-compact-support-and-gradient-bound]] is a function $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(B_R(x_0))$, $0\\le\\psi\\le1$, equal to one on $\\overline B_r(x_0)$; extended by zero it is in $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ by [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]. There is no product-extension assertion for arbitrary planar tests. By continuity of $X$, choose a closed nondegenerate parameter square $C\\subset D$ around $u_0$ with $X(C)\\subset B_r(x_0)$. The graph density is $J_X=\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla g|^2}\\ge1$ by [[lem-surface-integral-is-independent-of-c-one-boundary-charts]]. The surface measure and restriction to Borel chart subsets are exactly those of [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]; chart independence lets the integral of a function supported in $S\\cap O$ be computed in this chart. Equivalently one may choose a compact Jordan region $C'\\Subset D$, obtained as a finite union of closed parameter squares lying in $D$ and covering the compact parameter preimage of its support and use [[thm-surface-integrals-over-c-one-graphs]] followed by [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] for the continuous graph integrand on $C'$. On the support of $\\psi|_S$ we have $a\\ge m$, and $\\psi=1$ on $X(C)$. Consequently monotonicity gives $\\int_S a\\psi\\,dS\\ge m\\int_{X(C)}1\\,dS=m\\int_C J_X\\,d\\lambda_2\\ge m\\lambda_2(C)>0$; [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]] gives $\\lambda_2(C)=\\ell^2>0$. This contradicts the given zero pairing. Every $x_0\\in S$ therefore has $a(x_0)=0$, and applying this to the difference proves the equal-density clause. The compact embedded $C^2$ interface here is within the published compact embedded $C^1$ surface-measure class, with no change to its measure convention; in particular the closed stationary interfaces used by the jump theorem are covered. No new Choice principle is used. The empty-surface case is immediate.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis), ch. 1",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/pde_notes.pdf",
                "locator": "$1.10.2 and $1.12, printed pp. 15-18: chart density, graph formula and surface integration; the localization step is the standard reduction to the fundamental lemma."
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Cristoferi, Calculus of Variations lecture notes (Carnegie Mellon University 2016)",
                "url": "https://www.math.cmu.edu/~rcristof/pdf/Teaching/Spring2016/Cristoferi-Calculus_of_Variations-Lecture%20notes.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 4, Section 4.1, Lemma 4.1 and its proof, printed pp. 47-48: the fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations used to conclude pointwise vanishing from vanishing against every test function."
              },
              {
                "title": "M. E. Taylor, Introduction to Analysis in Several Variables",
                "url": "https://mtaylor.web.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/16915/2018/04/analmv.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 3.2, formulas 3.2.12 and 3.2.21-3.2.23: regular patches, density and the graph formula used to transfer the surface pairing to the parameter region."
              }
            ]
