          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), the convention already carried by the imported finite piecewise $C^1$ boundary presentations and by the compact $C^1$ surface measure cited below ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ open and $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ a nonempty open interval. This definition fixes the time-independent spatial test geometries of the integral form of the Maxwell equations.\n\n(a) [Volume test geometry] A volume test geometry in $\\Omega$ is a pair $(V,\\nu_V)$ where $V\\subseteq\\Omega$ is a nonempty bounded open set with $\\overline V\\subseteq\\Omega$ and a specified finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation of its boundary ([[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]]) and $\\nu_V$ is the outward unit normal of that presentation off its edge set ([[def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal]]). The closed-surface flux of a field $F$ continuous on a neighbourhood of $\\overline V$ is the finite face sum\n$$\\oint_{\\partial V}F\\cdot\\nu_V\\,dS:=\\sum_j\\int_{S_j}F\\cdot\\nu_V\\,dS,$$\nwith the face surface integrals of [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]. For every closed axis-parallel box $K=\\prod_{i<3}[a_i,b_i]\\subseteq\\Omega$ with $a_i<b_i$, its open interior $V=\\prod_{i<3}(a_i,b_i)$ is a volume test geometry and $\\overline V=K$. Its natural six-face presentation is the boundary of $K$, with outward normals $\\pm e_i$. The volume variable is $V$, while $K$ denotes its compact closure. Each face is a degenerate three-dimensional box and hence has $\\lambda_3$-measure zero by [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]]; their finite union $K\\setminus V$ is null. Thus for continuous $h$ on a neighbourhood of $K$, $\\int_Vh\\,d\\lambda_3=\\int_Kh\\,d\\lambda_3$: the positive and negative integrals over the null difference vanish by [[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]] and [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]].\n\n(b) [Patch test geometry] A patch test geometry in $\\Omega$ is a regular $C^2$ surface patch $(D,r)$ over a finite elementary Green region in the sense of [[def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch]] ([[def-type-i-type-ii-and-elementary-green-regions]], [[def-admissible-regular-parametrized-surface-patch]]): $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ is a finite elementary Green region, $r$ is of class $C^2$ on an open neighbourhood of $D$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), $r_u\\times r_v\\ne0$ on the interior of $D$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]) and no interior parameter point shares its image with a distinct point of $D$. Its induced boundary chain $\\partial S=r(\\partial D)$ and its circulation $\\oint_{\\partial S}F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ for a field continuous on the image are those of [[def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch]] ([[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]); its oriented flux of a field $F$ continuous on the image is\n$$\\int_SF\\cdot n_S\\,dS:=\\int_D(F\\circ r)\\cdot(r_u\\times r_v)\\,dudv,$$\nthe scalar Riemann integral of the continuous integrand on the compact Jordan parameter region, in the orientation induced by $r$ ([[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]]). The pair $(D,r)$ is part of the data. Every affine graph patch $r(u,v)=(u,v,\\varphi(u,v))$ over an axis-parallel square $D=[c_1,c_1+\\ell]\\times[c_2,c_2+\\ell]$ with $\\ell>0$, whose graph lies in $\\Omega$ with $\\varphi$ affine (for instance constant), and more generally every affine regular patch over such a square, is a patch test geometry: the square is a finite elementary Green region, and $r$ is smooth and regular with $\\varphi_u,\\varphi_v$ constant.\n\n(c) [Regularity convention] When an integral identity of this framework is asserted for a volume test geometry $V$, respectively for a patch test geometry $S$, with respect to a field $F$ of stated class $C^k$, it concerns fields defined and of class $C^k$ on an open neighbourhood of $\\overline V$ in $\\Omega$, respectively of the image $r[D]$ in $\\Omega$; when the identity involves a time derivative of a flux, the field is defined with the stated regularity on a neighbourhood of that compact set times the compact time interval of the assertion. No regularity away from these neighbourhoods is asserted.\n\n(d) [Conventions and scope] Test geometries are fixed spatial subsets of $\\Omega$ and carry no motion. A patch's orientation is the one induced by its parametrization, and its induced boundary chain has the compatible orientation of [[def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch]], so that the classical Stokes theorem applies with a plus sign. The definition retains the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention of the imported boundary and surface-measure definitions; it asserts no differential equation, no physical law and no existence of fields.",
          "strategy": "An explicit type/domain/convention definition. Clause (a) composes the imported finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation with its outward normal and the compact-hypersurface face integral; the open box interior, with compact closure $K$, is verified as an instance by listing its six faces (each a compact subset of a regular $C^1$ hypersurface patch, namely a coordinate plane), its twelve edges and eight vertices as the edge set, and noting that the face-edge intersections have surface measure zero and that outside the edges the boundary is locally a coordinate $C^1$ graph with the box on one side. Clause (b) composes the $C^2$ patch over a finite elementary Green region with the induced boundary chain and the oriented patch flux; an affine graph over an axis-parallel square is verified as an instance because a rectangle is both a type $I$ and a type $II$ region with the natural elementary decomposition, so the square carries a finite elementary Green presentation. Clause (c) is a regularity convention recording that all integral identities of this page quantify over fields on neighbourhoods of the compact test sets, which is exactly the hypothesis class of the imported divergence and Stokes theorems. Clause (d) records the orientation and countability conventions and states explicitly that the definition introduces no physical law. Nothing here asserts that every bounded domain is admissible, that a disc is a legal parameter region, or that a physical test geometry is realised by an apparatus. The requirement $\\overline V\\subseteq\\Omega$ makes clause (c)'s existing neighbourhood requirement explicit and restores the canonical definition contract. The closed-box integral bridge follows from the six degenerate faces being volume-null and the signed-integral definition, independently of their nonzero surface areas.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 1.3.1 printed pp. 24-28 (line, surface and volume integrals), Section 1.3.4 printed pp. 31-34 (the fundamental theorem for divergences on volumes and the orientation of the outward normal) and Section 1.3.5 printed pp. 34-37 (the fundamental theorem for curls and the induced boundary orientation); read in the stamped scanned full text (OCR cross-checked), used only for the conventions of the test geometries, not for any electromagnetic law."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.1 printed p. 8 (flux through a closed surface) and Section 3.1 printed pp. 42-43 (Ampère's law in integral form with the right-handed boundary orientation); read in the stamped full text, used only for the orientation and flux conventions."
