            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "def-distribution",
            "def-distributional-derivative",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
            "def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function",
            "thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions",
            "thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distributional-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region of\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the constants and units of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]], let $E,B$ be the fields of [[def-em-fields]] and\n$\\rho,J$ the source fields of [[def-em-sources]], with\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$.\n\n(a) [Smooth branch] Let $E,B$ be jointly of class $C^2$ on $\\Omega\\times I$ and\n$\\rho,J$ of class $C^1$, and suppose the smooth branch of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] holds with these fields and sources, in particular\n$\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$. Then the continuity equation\n\n$$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$$\n\nholds pointwise on $\\Omega\\times I$, where $\\operatorname{div}$ is the\ndivergence of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and\n$\\partial_t$ the partial time derivative of\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\n(b) [Distributional branch] Let $E,B,\\rho,J$ be distributions on\n$\\Omega\\times I$ ([[def-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]); in\nparticular they may be locally integrable fields and sources identified with\ntheir regular distributions\n([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]],\n[[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]). Suppose the four\nequations\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$$\n\nhold in $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega\\times I)$ with distributional derivatives. This\nincludes the original locally integrable weak branch of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] and the separately adopted declared singular-source\nmodels of [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]], when their source and\nfield hypotheses hold. More generally the formal compatibility implication\napplies to every distributional source pair for which the four identities\nhold, without asserting physical adoption of arbitrary distributions. Then\n$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega\\times I)$.\n\nThe conclusion is a compatibility condition: it does not assert that every\nsource pair is compatible with the Maxwell equations, and by itself it proves\nno conservation of physical charge. The derivative compatibility argument is\nchoice-free. An application to the declared singular-source model retains the\nCountable Choice assumption of its defining source and surface-measure\nformulation ([[def-countable-choice]]).",
          "strategy": "(a) Smooth branch. By [[thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes]] applied to the $C^2$ field $B$, $\\operatorname{div}(\\operatorname{curl}B)=0$ pointwise. Taking the divergence of the displayed Amp\\`ere equation and using linearity of the coordinate derivatives in the sum defining the divergence ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) gives $0=\\mu_0\\operatorname{div}J+c^{-2}\\operatorname{div}(\\partial_tE)$, and [[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]] lets the two coordinate-derivative types be interchanged: $\\operatorname{div}(\\partial_tE)=\\partial_t(\\operatorname{div}E)$. The Gauss law of the smooth branch gives $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and hence $\\partial_t(\\operatorname{div}E)=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\partial_t\\rho$, using $\\rho$ of class $C^1$ ([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). Substituting, $0=\\mu_0\\operatorname{div}J+c^{-2}\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\partial_t\\rho=\\mu_0(\\operatorname{div}J+\\partial_t\\rho)$, because $c^2\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1$ by the definition $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and $\\mu_0>0$; division by $\\mu_0$ gives the claim.\n\n(b) Distributional branch. Only the two pairings with $\\operatorname{curl}B$ and with $\\operatorname{div}E$ are used. By [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]] the distributional derivatives commute, so for every test function $\\psi$\n$$\\langle\\operatorname{div}(\\operatorname{curl}B),\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j,k=1}^3\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\langle\\partial_i\\partial_jB_k,\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{i,j,k=1}^3\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\langle\\partial_j\\partial_iB_k,\\psi\\rangle=0$$\nafter reindexing $i\\leftrightarrow j$ and using the antisymmetry of $\\varepsilon_{ijk}$: the two expressions are negatives of each other. The weak Amp\\`ere equation therefore gives $\\mu_0\\operatorname{div}J+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\operatorname{div}E=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega\\times I)$, again by [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]] and linearity of the pairing ([[def-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]). The weak Gauss law gives $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and hence $\\partial_t\\operatorname{div}E=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\partial_t\\rho$; substituting and dividing as in (a) gives $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega\\times I)$. The locally integrable case is clause (a) of the embedding: the fields' regular distributions are well defined ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]), and the computation is unchanged. Every step is either an identity for distributions or the definition of the distributional derivative applied to a constant multiple, so no choice principle and no convergence theorem is used. The statement does not claim that every source pair admits fields satisfying the four equations: it claims only that whenever they do, continuity follows. Actual physical use with point/stationary-sheet source models invokes [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]], not an extension inferred from the old L1-only postulate. The formal distributional cancellation itself remains choice-free; the source-object formulation retains its explicit Countable Choice assumption.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 7.3.3 printed pp. 326-328 (compatibility of the Maxwell equations with charge continuity, including the divergence-of-curl cancellation); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
