                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 4.2.1 printed p. 81 (the divergence of the amended Ampere equation combined with Gauss law gives charge continuity), with Section 1.1.1 printed p. 4 for the local conservation formulation. The distributional extension is proved locally."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 29 printed pp. 71-72 (the continuity equation as a consequence of the field equations); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The conditional compatibility statement that any field and source tuple satisfying the framework's vacuum Maxwell equations, in the smooth C2 branch or in the distributional branch including the separately adopted singular-source models, satisfies div J + partial_t rho = 0 pointwise or in the sense of distributions. It is a compatibility condition on sources, not a conservation law of matter, not an existence statement and not empirical evidence; it inherits the regularity and source hypotheses of the branch used."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-integral-maxwell",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Smooth differential-integral equivalence for every admissible test geometry",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K1",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 14,
          "deps": [
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
            "def-em-fixed-test-geometry",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-sources",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains",
            "thm-the-classical-stokes-theorem-for-a-c2-surface-patch",
            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands",
            "lem-em-parameter-integrals",
            "lem-em-coordinate-cell-localization",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
            "cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "def-em-fixed-test-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-classical-stokes-theorem-for-a-c2-surface-patch": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-parameter-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-coordinate-cell-localization": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the convention of the\nimported piecewise $C^1$ boundary and surface-measure definitions\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region\nof [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; let\n$E,B$ be fields of [[def-em-fields]] and $\\rho,J$ source fields of\n[[def-em-sources]] on $\\Omega\\times I$; and let $(V,\\nu_V)$ and $(D,r)$ be the\nvolume and patch test geometries of [[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]]. Assume the\nfields are jointly $C^1$ on $\\Omega\\times I$ and that the additional\nderivatives appearing below exist and are continuous there; for instance $E,B$\njointly of class $C^2$ and $\\rho,J$ of class $C^1$ is sufficient.\n\n(a) [Differential implies integral] If the smooth branch of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] holds pointwise on $\\Omega\\times I$, then for every\nvolume test geometry $(V,\\nu_V)$ and every $t\\in I$,\n\n$$\\oint_{\\partial V}E(\\cdot,t)\\cdot\\nu_V\\,dS=\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\int_V\\rho(\\cdot,t)\\,d\\lambda_3,\\qquad \\oint_{\\partial V}B(\\cdot,t)\\cdot\\nu_V\\,dS=0,$$\n\nand for every patch test geometry $(D,r)$ the flux functions\n$t\\mapsto\\int_SB(\\cdot,t)\\cdot n_S\\,dS$ and\n$t\\mapsto\\int_SE(\\cdot,t)\\cdot n_S\\,dS$ are differentiable on $I$ and, for\nevery $t\\in I$,\n\n$$\\oint_{\\partial S}E(\\cdot,t)\\cdot d\\mathbf r=-\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_SB(\\cdot,t)\\cdot n_S\\,dS,\\qquad \\oint_{\\partial S}B(\\cdot,t)\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\mu_0\\int_SJ(\\cdot,t)\\cdot n_S\\,dS+c^{-2}\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_SE(\\cdot,t)\\cdot n_S\\,dS.$$\n\n(b) [Integral implies differential] Conversely, suppose the two volume\nidentities of (a) hold for every volume test geometry and every $t\\in I$, and\nthat for every patch test geometry the two flux functions are differentiable\non $I$ and the two patch identities of (a) hold for every $t\\in I$. Then the\nfour smooth-branch equations of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] hold pointwise on\n$\\Omega\\times I$:\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE.$$\n\nThe equivalence is asserted for the exact class of test geometries of\n[[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]], which contains the open interiors of all closed\naxis-parallel boxes compactly contained in $\\Omega$ and the affine square\npatches; it is not a recovery from one selected volume or one selected patch,\nand the divergence and curl of\n[[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and the time derivative of\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] are the operators used.",
          "strategy": "(a) Differential implies integral. Fix $t\\in I$ and a volume test geometry $V$; $E(\\cdot,t)$ is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of $\\overline V$, so [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]] applies (the theorem assumes the specified finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation and the stated $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) and gives $\\oint_{\\partial V}E\\cdot\\nu_V\\,dS=\\int_V\\operatorname{div}E\\,d\\lambda_3=(1/\\varepsilon_0)\\int_V\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3$ by the Gauss law of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]; the same computation with $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ gives the second identity. For a patch test geometry, [[thm-the-classical-stokes-theorem-for-a-c2-surface-patch]] applied to the $C^1$ fields gives $\\oint_{\\partial S}E\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\int_S\\operatorname{curl}E\\cdot n_S\\,dS$ with the induced orientation of [[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]], and $\\int_S\\operatorname{curl}E\\cdot n_S\\,dS=-\\int_S\\partial_tB\\cdot n_S\\,dS$ by Faraday's law. The flux function is $t\\mapsto\\int_D\\langle B(r(u,v),t),r_u\\times r_v\\rangle\\,dudv$, a Riemann integral over the compact Jordan parameter region of a function whose time derivative $\\langle\\partial_tB(r(u,v),t),r_u\\times r_v\\rangle$ is continuous on a neighbourhood of $D$ times a compact interval around $t$; [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] identifies this Riemann integral with the Lebesgue integral to which [[lem-em-parameter-integrals]] applies, giving differentiability on $I$ with $\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_SB\\cdot n_S\\,dS=\\int_S\\partial_tB\\cdot n_S\\,dS$. Combining gives the Faraday identity; the Amp\\`ere identity is the same with $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$.\n\n(b) Integral implies differential. Fix a point $p\\in\\Omega$, an index $i$, and the cyclic pair $(j,k)$ with $e_j\\times e_k=e_i$. For every closed box $K=\\prod_{i<3}[a_i,b_i]\\subset\\Omega$ with positive sides take its admitted open interior $V$. The volume identities and [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]] give $\\int_V(\\operatorname{div}E-\\rho/\\varepsilon_0)\\,d\\lambda_3=0$. The integrand $h=\\operatorname{div}E-\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ is continuous on $\\Omega$ and bounded on $K$. The six faces of $K\\setminus V$ are volume-null by [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]], so [[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]] applied to the positive and negative parts gives $\\int_Kh=\\int_Vh=0$. In particular this holds for every closed cube $K\\subset\\Omega$; [[lem-em-coordinate-cell-localization]] gives $h=0$ on $\\Omega$. The same argument applied to $h=\\operatorname{div}B$ gives $\\operatorname{div}B=0$. For the curl components, take the affine patch $r(u,v):=p+ue_j+ve_k$ over the square $D_\\ell=[-\\ell/2,\\ell/2]^2$ (a patch test geometry whose parameter region is a rectangle and whose normal is $r_u\\times r_v=e_i$); the Faraday identity of (b), Stokes' theorem and the differentiability of the flux function give $\\int_{D_\\ell}\\bigl(\\operatorname{curl}E+\\partial_tB\\bigr)(p+ue_j+ve_k)\\cdot e_i\\,dudv=0$ for every sufficiently small $\\ell>0$. Because the identities hold for every affine square patch contained in $\\Omega$, for each fixed coordinate plane every closed square in its open slice is covered (translate its centre to $p$); this is the hypothesis of the planar clause of [[lem-em-coordinate-cell-localization]] for the continuous function $u\\mapsto(\\operatorname{curl}E+\\partial_tB)(p+ue_j+ve_k)\\cdot e_i$ on the corresponding coordinate plane; hence $(\\operatorname{curl}E+\\partial_tB)\\cdot e_i=0$ at every point of $\\Omega$, for each coordinate $e_i$. The same argument with the Amp\\`ere identity gives $(\\operatorname{curl}B-\\mu_0J-c^{-2}\\partial_tE)\\cdot e_i=0$. Therefore the four equations hold pointwise. The argument shows why a single selected test geometry is insufficient: the recovery uses the full families of boxes and affine square patches of [[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]], and the marked $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is exactly the convention of the divergence theorem and the surface-measure interface.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 1.3.4-1.3.5 printed pp. 31-37 (the divergence and curl theorems and the localization argument for recovering the differential form) and Section 7.3.1-7.3.3 printed pp. 321-328 (the integral and differential forms of the Maxwell equations); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 2.1 and 3.1 printed pp. 8 and 42-43 (Gauss's law and Ampere's law in integral form) and Section 4.1 printed pp. 67-68 (Faraday's law in integral form); read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "Weng Cho Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory (Purdue University, Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.1 printed pp. 17-22 (Gauss's divergence theorem in integral form) and Section 2.2 printed pp. 22-26 (Stokes's theorem in integral form); read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 30 printed pp. 73-75 (the Maxwell equations in integral and differential form, read in Gaussian units and re-expressed in SI); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The equivalence, within the classical smooth branch of the framework's vacuum Maxwell postulate, between the four pointwise differential equations and the four integral identities tested against every volume test geometry and every patch test geometry of the fixed test-geometry class. The statement is conditional on the adopted smooth-branch postulate and on the stated joint regularity; it asserts no existence of fields, no empirical agreement and no integral identity for singular sources, and it does not extend the equivalence to test geometries outside the declared class."
        },
