          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the convention of\nthe interface and surface-measure definitions cited below\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory\nregion of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the constants of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]] and the fields of [[def-em-fields]] and source\nfields of [[def-em-sources]].\n\nLet $S\\subset\\Omega$ be a fixed compact embedded $C^2$ hypersurface with a\nchosen unit normal $n$ pointing from the side $\\Omega_-$ to the side\n$\\Omega_+$ ([[def-em-surface-distribution]]), and let\n$\\sigma:S\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ and $K:S\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be continuous\nwith $K(x,t)\\cdot n(x)=0$ for all $(x,t)$ (a tangential surface current\ndensity). Let $E,B$ be locally integrable on $\\Omega\\times I$ and $C^1$ on\n$(\\Omega\\setminus S)\\times I$ with the following explicit joint one-sided\nregularity: around every $(x_0,t_0)\\in S\\times I$, each side is the restriction\nof a $C^1$ function on an open spacetime neighbourhood of the corresponding\nclosed side of a smaller stationary graph cylinder. Thus the fields and their\nfirst space and time derivatives extend continuously to that side of\n$S\\times I$, locally uniformly on compact cylinders, and let $\\rho_v,J_v$ be\ncontinuous on $(\\Omega\\setminus S)\\times I$ and locally integrable on\n$\\Omega\\times I$. Write $[F]=F_+-F_-$ for the jump of the one-sided traces of\nsuch a field $F$.\n\nUnder the separate singular-source physical adoption\n[[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]], suppose that $E,B$ satisfy the weak\nMaxwell equations with the source pair\n\n$$\\rho=\\rho_v+\\rho_S,\\qquad J=J_v+J_S,$$\n\nwhere $(\\rho_S,J_S)$ is the stationary-sheet source of\n[[def-em-weak-sources]](c) with densities $\\sigma,K$ and the pairings there, in\nthe sense of [[def-em-weak-sources]](e), and where the volume parts\n$\\rho_v,J_v$ are identified with their regular distributions\n([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]],\n[[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]). Then, pointwise\non $S\\times I$,\n\n$$n\\cdot[E]=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad n\\times[B]=\\mu_0K,\\qquad n\\cdot[B]=0,\\qquad n\\times[E]=0,$$\n\nwith the ordinary Euclidean inner and cross products of the one-sided traces\n([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). Equivalently, the surface current density is\n$K=(\\mu_0)^{-1}\\,n\\times[B]$ and the surface charge density is\n$\\sigma=\\varepsilon_0\\,n\\cdot[E]$.\n\nThe theorem assumes a stationary interface: the hypersurface $S$ and its\nnormal do not depend on $t$, so the one-sided $C^1$ traces also make\n$\\partial_tE$ and $\\partial_tB$ locally bounded near $S$ and hence locally\nintegrable, contributing no delta terms. No magnetic surface charge or\nmagnetic surface current is present in the sources, and correspondingly the\nlast two identities assert the absence of magnetic sheet delta terms. A moving\ninterface, a normal sheet current, or a sheet whose support changes with time\nis outside this statement and would require additional terms.\n\n[General weak-trace branch] The weak Maxwell framework continues to allow the\nfull locally integrable field and source class, without asserting undefined\nclassical trace values. If instead of the joint extension condition the fields\nhave the original one-sided spatial $C^1$ extensions at each time, assume in\naddition that their piecewise first spatial and temporal derivatives are in\n$L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega\\times I)$ and that their one-sided field traces are\nmeasurable and locally integrable on $S\\times I$ with respect to $dS\\,dt$.\nThe one-sided traces are then locally integrable densities on the sheet, so\nthe four pairings\n$$\\int_I\\int_S\\bigl(n\\cdot[E]-\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0\\bigr)\\psi\\,dS\\,dt,\\quad\\int_I\\int_S(n\\cdot[B])\\psi\\,dS\\,dt,\\quad\\int_I\\int_S(n\\times[E])\\psi\\,dS\\,dt,\\quad\\int_I\\int_S\\bigl(n\\times[B]-\\mu_0K\\bigr)\\psi\\,dS\\,dt$$\nagainst every scalar $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U)$ (the vector integrals being componentwise) are well defined by the ordinary\nsurface-and-time integrals, with no product of distributions formed; these\nvanishing pairings are the precise weak form of the four jump identities, and\nthey do vanish. The identities then hold for $dS\\,dt$-almost every $(x,t)$;\nsince the traces are spatially continuous at each fixed time, they hold for\nevery $x\\in S$ for almost every $t\\in I$. They hold at every $(x,t)$ whenever\nthe jump densities are additionally jointly continuous. Merely having\none-sided spatial $C^1$ traces separately at every time does not justify this\nlast upgrade.",
          "strategy": "First justify the spacetime use of the spatial supplier. For a compactly supported spacetime test $\\psi$, let $K\\subset\\Omega$ be its compact spatial support projection and let $L\\Subset I$ be a compact interval containing its time support. Choose finitely many spatial stationary graph neighbourhoods and interior open sets covering $K$. [[lem-finite-ambient-partitions-for-euclidean-boundary-integration]] gives smooth nonnegative spatial functions $\\phi_j$ compactly supported in those sets with $\\sum_j\\phi_j=1$ near $K$. Explicitly, first choose finitely many nonnegative ambient bumps $\\chi_j$ subordinate to that cover whose sum $s$ is positive near $K$; a cutoff $\\theta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\{s>0\\})$ equal to one near $K$ exists by [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]], and $\\phi_j=\\theta\\chi_j/s$ on $\\{s>0\\}$, extended by zero elsewhere, is smooth since $\\operatorname{supp}\\theta$ stays compactly inside the positive set. Their sum is $\\theta=1$ near $K$, so $\\psi=\\sum_j\\phi_j\\psi$. This partition is independent of time, hence $\\partial_t(\\phi_j\\psi)=\\phi_j\\partial_t\\psi$ and temporal integration by parts acquires no partition derivative. On each spatial chart times $L$, the local joint one-sided extensions have a finite subcover of the relevant compact sheet-time subset, giving the compact bounds used below; interior compact supports are already covered by the ordinary joint $C^1$ assumption. In the joint branch the one-sided $C^1$ extensions and their derivatives are bounded on each compact closed half-cylinder, so the piecewise derivatives are locally integrable and the jumps are jointly continuous and bounded there. Apply [[lem-em-interface-derivatives]] to each component of the spatial slice at every fixed $t$ and integrate in $t$. The terms $F\\partial_i\\psi$, $(\\partial_i F)\\psi$ and $n_i[F]\\psi$ are absolutely integrable on the compact support (the last uses the finite compact surface measure of [[lem-surface-integral-is-independent-of-c-one-boundary-charts]]), so [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] justifies integrating the identity. This gives $\\partial_iF_j=\\{\\partial_iF_j\\}+n_i[F_j]\\delta_{S\\times I}$, and contraction/antisymmetrization gives the divergence/curl formulas. For the temporal derivative, on each fixed spatial side, [[thm-integration-by-parts]] on a compact time interval containing the test support, followed by [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]], gives $-\\int F_j\\partial_t\\psi=\\int (\\partial_tF_j)\\psi$, with no endpoint term since the test support is compact in $I$. Integrate in $x$ by the same Fubini theorem. The sides are stationary, so no derivative of a moving spatial indicator occurs and $\\partial_tF_j=\\{\\partial_tF_j\\}$ with no sheet term.\n\nThe spatial sheet $S$ has three-dimensional Lebesgue measure zero: in each finite graph chart every normal section is a singleton, so Fubini gives zero volume, and the coordinate permutation preserves Lebesgue measure. Consequently $S\\times I$ is locally four-dimensional null. Restrict each weak Maxwell identity to tests away from $S\\times I$. The resulting ordinary residuals are continuous on the open complement, so the existing sign-and-ambient-bump argument forces them to vanish pointwise there. The ordinary residuals are locally integrable and differ from zero only on the null sheet, hence represent zero regular distributions by [[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]]. Subtract them to obtain the four sheet identities with densities $n\\cdot[E]-\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$, $n\\cdot[B]$, $n\\times[E]$, $n\\times[B]-\\mu_0K$. For every spatial $\\varphi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$, each scalar component $H(t)=\\int_S a(x,t)\\varphi(x)\\,dS$ is continuous: on a compact time interval, joint continuity on the compact sheet gives uniform convergence as $t\\to t_0$, and the bound $|H(t)-H(t_0)|\\le S(S)\\|\\varphi\\|_\\infty\\sup_{x\\in S}|a(x,t)-a(x,t_0)|$ tends to zero. Testing $\\varphi(x)\\eta(t)$ and using the one-dimensional sign-and-bump argument shows $H(t)=0$ at every time. Apply [[lem-em-surface-density-localization]] at that time and componentwise to get the four pointwise jumps. Tangentiality of $n\\times[B]$ gives the stated current formula.\n\nFor the general weak trace branch, the additional local $L^1$ hypotheses on piecewise derivatives and traces justify the same Fubini arguments without compact uniform bounds. Temporal integration by parts is valid first on compact subintervals for each fixed off-sheet $x$, since the field is $C^1$ there; its integrable derivative and the test's compact support justify the integrated identity. The sheet residuals are therefore zero as distributions. For each fixed ambient spatial test $\\varphi$, Fubini gives the locally integrable time function $H_\\varphi(t)=\\int_S a(x,t)\\varphi(x)\\,dS$, and testing $\\varphi(x)\\eta(t)$ shows its regular distribution is zero. The one-dimensional case of [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]] gives $H_\\varphi(t)=0$ for almost every time. To obtain one exceptional time set, use the countable explicit ambient bumps with rational centres $q\\in\\mathbb Q^3$ and rational radii $0<r<R$ for which $\\overline B_R(q)\\subset\\Omega$, equal to one on $\\overline B_r(q)$ and supported in $B_R(q)$. They are defined by the fixed smooth-step formula in [[lem-scaled-euclidean-bumps-with-compact-support-and-gradient-bound]], so no arbitrary choice of a test for each triple is needed. Remove the countable union of the null time sets for these tests, which remains null by [[thm-finite-and-countable-subadditivity-of-measures]]. For a remaining time, a nonzero continuous spatial residual at any $x_0\\in S$ has one strict sign on a sufficiently small graph neighbourhood. Density of the rationals ([[lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r]]) gives one of those ambient balls wholly inside that neighbourhood whose inner ball contains $x_0$. A positive-area graph cell in its inner ball, as in [[lem-em-surface-density-localization]], makes its pairing nonzero, contradiction. Thus each residual vanishes at every spatial point for almost every time, which also proves the sheet-a.e. assertion. Joint continuity, if separately assumed, similarly upgrades to all times using positive-area cells times nondegenerate time intervals. The counterexample recorded in this proposal proves why this qualification cannot be omitted. All four electromagnetic conclusions and the unrestricted weak Maxwell framework are retained at their strongest justified regularity level; no Sobolev trace theorem or undeclared Choice principle is used.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
