          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region of\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]], with nonempty open time interval $I$\n([[def-interval]]), positive constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. Define the **Gauss defects**\n\n$$D_B:=\\operatorname{div}B,\\qquad D_E:=\\operatorname{div}E-\\rho/\\varepsilon_0.$$\n\n(a) [Smooth evolution branch] Let $E,B$ be jointly $C^2$ fields and $\\rho,J$\n$C^1$ source fields of [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]]. Assume only\nthe two evolution equations\n\n$$\\partial_tB=-\\operatorname{curl}E,\\qquad\\partial_tE=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B-c^2\\mu_0J,$$\n\nand, separately, source continuity $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$. No\nGauss constraint is assumed. Then $\\partial_tD_B=\\partial_tD_E=0$ pointwise.\nFor each fixed $x$, both defects are constant on $I$. Initial zero constraints\nat any $t_0\\in I$ persist at every time; a nonzero defect at a point persists\nwith the same value. Thus evolution and continuity cannot repair a nonzero\ninitial Gauss defect.\n\n(b) [Distributional evolution branch] The same derivative conclusion holds for\narbitrary scalar and vector distributions $E,B,\\rho,J$ on $U$ satisfying the\ntwo evolution identities and, independently, continuity in\n$\\mathcal D'(U)$ ([[def-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]).\nThis includes the original locally integrable field and source branch\nidentified with regular distributions and the declared singular-source class\nwhen its equations hold. Thus $\\partial_tD_B=\\partial_tD_E=0$\ndistributionally. If a defect has a continuous representative $h$ on $U$,\nassume Countable Choice for the indicated Lebesgue integral and localization\ninterface ([[def-countable-choice]]); then $h(x,t)$ is independent of $t$. In\nthis continuous-representative case its zero or nonzero value at a time\npersists. No restriction of an arbitrary distribution to a time slice is\nassumed.\n\n(c) [Full-Maxwell corollaries] Solutions with the regularity of (a) of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] satisfy the evolution and source-continuity\nhypotheses by [[pthm-em-charge-continuity]](a), and their Gauss defects are\nidentically zero by the two Gauss equations. Its original locally integrable\nweak branch has the same distributional conclusion. For admitted point and\nstationary-sheet models under [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]],\n[[pthm-em-charge-continuity]](b) supplies compatibility and the four\nidentities again give zero defects. Conversely, in (a), or in (b) when both\ndefects have continuous representatives, evolution plus continuity and zero\nGauss defects at one time give all four Maxwell identities on $U$, pointwise\nin (a) and distributionally in (b).\n\nThis theorem is conditional on the stated equations and regularity. It proves\nno existence, matter dynamics, observation or empirical fact. The derivative\nalgebra is choice-free; Countable Choice is confined to the\ncontinuous-representative localization and the inherited declared\nsingular-source formulation.",
          "strategy": "(a) Smooth algebra. For $C^2$ fields, [[thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes]] and [[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]] give\n$$\\partial_tD_B=\\operatorname{div}(\\partial_tB)=-\\operatorname{div}(\\operatorname{curl}E)=0,$$\nand, with the positive commutation sign,\n$$\\partial_tD_E=\\operatorname{div}(\\partial_tE)-\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\partial_t\\rho=c^2\\operatorname{div}(\\operatorname{curl}B)-c^2\\mu_0\\operatorname{div}J-\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\partial_t\\rho=-\\varepsilon_0^{-1}(\\operatorname{div}J+\\partial_t\\rho)=0.$$\nHere $c^2\\mu_0=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}$ and the final equality is the separately assumed continuity, not a consequence of the evolution equations alone. The coordinate operators and product/linear rules are [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] and [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]. For fixed $x$, [[cor-mean-value-theorem]] on compact subintervals of $I$ proves constancy, propagation and nonzero persistence.\n(b) The identical componentwise cancellation holds for distributions by [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]], whose derivative-commutation clause is choice-free: in $\\sum_{i,j,k}\\epsilon_{ijk}\\partial_i\\partial_jB_k$ the exchanged $i,j$ terms cancel. The two evolution identities and the independent continuity identity therefore give $\\partial_tD_B=\\partial_tD_E=0$ without multiplying distributions.\nFor the continuous-representative conclusion, assume the stated Countable Choice and let $\\partial_th=0$. Fix a spatial test $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ and put $F(t)=\\int_\\Omega h(x,t)\\varphi(x)d\\lambda_3(x)$. On any compact time subinterval the integrands have fixed compact spatial support and a uniform bound, by [[thm-extreme-value-metric]] and [[prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets]]. Hence [[thm-dominated-convergence]] makes $F$ continuous. All products with compact temporal tests are absolutely integrable; [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]], [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]] and [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] identify their multiple integrals with $\\int_I F\\eta$.\nIf $\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(I)$ has zero integral, extend it by zero and put $H(t):=\\int_{-\\infty}^t\\eta(v)dv$. Equivalently start at a point strictly left of its support. The compact-test integral agrees with its Riemann integral by [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]], and [[thm-ftc-first-part]] gives $H\\prime=\\eta$. Since the total integral is zero, $H$ vanishes both left and right of the support interval; it is smooth and compactly supported inside $I$. Therefore $\\Psi(x,t):=\\varphi(x)H(t)$ is a legitimate compact test with $\\partial_t\\Psi=\\varphi\\eta$. Apply the derivative equation to THIS primitive test:\n$$0=\\langle\\partial_th,\\Psi\\rangle=-\\langle h,\\partial_t\\Psi\\rangle=-\\int_I F(t)\\eta(t)dt.$$\nChoose a real nonnegative compact bump $\\beta$ in $I$ with integral one, using [[lem-scaled-euclidean-bumps-with-compact-support-and-gradient-bound]] and the positive finite measure of an inner interval ([[lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure]]). Decompose an arbitrary temporal test as $\\eta_0=\\eta-(\\int_I\\eta)\\beta$. The preceding equality gives $\\int_I(F-C)\\eta=0$ for every $\\eta$, where $C:=\\int_IF\\beta$. A continuous function with all such pairings zero vanishes pointwise: if a real or imaginary component is nonzero, continuity gives a subinterval where that component has one sign and is bounded away from zero; a nonnegative bump supported there, equal to one on a smaller interval, contradicts integral monotonicity ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure]]). Thus $F$ is constant. The same argument for the continuous spatial difference $h(\\cdot,t_1)-h(\\cdot,t_2)$, now using bumps in $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$, shows it is identically zero, since its pairing with every $\\varphi$ is zero. This establishes independence of time. Compact support of every test, the actual derivative-test slot, and the public Countable Choice cost are explicit; no arbitrary-basepoint primitive is used.\n(c) In the smooth full-Maxwell branch, [[pthm-em-charge-continuity]] supplies continuity, while Gauss gives $D_B=D_E=0$ directly; the original weak branch is the identical distributional argument. The separately adopted singular-source postulate uses the preceding solution predicate and the distributional continuity result. Conversely the propagated zero defects are exactly the missing two Gauss identities, so adding them to the evolution pair yields all four equations. No existence or empirical claim is inferred.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 7.3.1 printed pp. 321-323 (the divergence of Faraday's law vanishes by the divergence-of-curl identity) and Section 7.3.2-7.3.3 printed pp. 323-328 (continuity from the amended Ampere law, and the role of the two Gauss laws as constraints); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 1.2.1 printed p. 6 (the four Maxwell equations) and Section 4.2.1 printed p. 81 (charge continuity from the divergence of the amended Ampere law and Gauss law). The propagation deduction is supplied locally, rather than attributed to the static Ampere or Faraday discussions."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 30 printed pp. 73-75 (the field equations and their differential compatibility); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The propagation statement for the Gauss defects of a vacuum Maxwell evolution system in the named inertial laboratory model: from the two evolution equations and independently assumed source continuity, the defects div B and div E - rho/eps0 are constant in time (pointwise in the smooth branch, distributionally in general), so zero initial constraints persist and nonzero defects persist; full-Maxwell solutions have zero defects, and adding zero defects to the evolution pair recovers all four equations. The statement is conditional on the stated equations and regularity, asserts no existence and no empirical fact, and confines Countable Choice to the continuous-representative localization interface."
        }
      ],
