          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-charge-continuity": "physical-result",
            "def-em-weak-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-point-current-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-distributional-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-scaled-euclidean-bumps-with-compact-support-and-gradient-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $q\\ne0$ be a real charge parameter (coulombs) and let\n$U:=\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ with the laboratory conventions of\n[[def-em-weak-sources]]. Define a distribution $\\rho$ on $U$ by\n\n$$\\langle\\rho,\\psi\\rangle:=q\\int_0^\\infty\\psi(0,t)\\,dt,\\qquad \\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U)$$\n\n— an explicit switched-history distribution for $z(t)=0$ on $(0,\\infty)$, with\nno compensating current or source contribution at the appearance event; it does\nnot satisfy the proper-graph hypothesis of the zero-endpoint continuity branch\nof [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) — and let $J:=0$ be the zero distribution. Then:\n\n(a) $\\partial_t\\rho=q\\,\\delta_{(0,0)}$ and $\\operatorname{div}J=0$\n([[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]), hence\n$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=q\\,\\delta_{(0,0)}\\ne0$ in\n$\\mathcal D'(U)$: for every test function $\\psi$,\n$\\langle\\partial_t\\rho,\\psi\\rangle=-q\\int_0^\\infty\\partial_t\\psi(0,t)\\,dt=q\\,\\psi(0,0)$,\nbecause $t\\mapsto\\psi(0,t)$ is smooth with compact support. In particular the\ncontinuity identity fails at the appearance event $(0,0)$.\n\n(b) Consequently there are no locally integrable fields $E,B$ on $U$\nsatisfying the four weak residual identities of [[def-em-weak-sources]](e)\nwith the source pair $(\\rho,0)$, the $\\rho$-pairing being the explicit pairing\ndisplayed above and $J=0$: such fields would satisfy the distributional\ncontinuity identity of [[pthm-em-charge-continuity]](b), which applies to\nevery distributional source pair for which the four identities hold, and (a)\ncontradicts it. The source pair $(\\rho,0)$ is therefore incompatible with the\nformal four residual identities themselves; it is not one of the declared\npoint sources of [[def-em-weak-sources]](b), because the properness hypothesis\nfails here as recorded, and no fields turn it into an admitted singular-source\nmodel under [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]].\n\n(c) The failure is exactly the omitted endpoint term of the point-source\nconstruction: by the endpoint clause of [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]],\nthe same trajectory on a bounded interval $I_0=(a,b)$ has\n$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=q\\,\\delta_{\\gamma(a)}-q\\,\\delta_{\\gamma(b)}$\nwith $\\gamma(t)=(z(t),t)$. A switched point charge with $J=0$ and no endpoint\nor current term is thus inadmissible; a switched charge must be accompanied by\nan explicit current or endpoint source term, which the weak equations of\n[[def-em-weak-sources]] then require to satisfy continuity.\n\nThis is a mathematical incompatibility statement about a declared source\nmodel, not a report of a measurement: no apparatus, sampling model,\nuncertainty or empirical claim is involved.",
          "strategy": "Direct computation with the distributional definitions. (a) For $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U)$ the pullback $t\\mapsto\\psi(0,t)$ is smooth and compactly supported, so $\\langle\\partial_t\\rho,\\psi\\rangle=-\\langle\\rho,\\partial_t\\psi\\rangle=-q\\int_0^\\infty\\partial_t\\psi(0,t)\\,dt=q\\psi(0,0)$ by the fundamental theorem of calculus on the support interval ([[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]); the functional $\\psi\\mapsto q\\psi(0,0)$ is $q\\delta_{(0,0)}$ ([[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]), which is nonzero because $q\\ne0$ and some test function equals $1$ at $(0,0)$. Since $J$ is the zero distribution, $\\operatorname{div}J=0$. (b) If locally integrable fields $E,B$ satisfied the four weak equations in the sense of [[def-em-weak-sources]](e) with the distributional pair $(\\rho,0)$, then [[pthm-em-charge-continuity]](b) — stated for arbitrary distributional source pairs satisfying the four equations — would give $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(U)$, contradicting (a). (c) The endpoint clause of [[lem-em-point-current-continuity]] computes the endpoint deltas of a finite-history point source; setting $J=0$ while retaining the charge history $(0,\\infty)$ is precisely the omission of those endpoint terms. Nothing here is a physical claim about charge creation: it states that the declared source model fails its own continuity requirement, and it exhibits the general statement of the previous item on an explicit example. The item inherits the declared $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention of [[def-em-weak-sources]], on whose clause (b) it depends, and records it in its dependencies; the computation itself is choice-free and uses neither the surface-measure interface nor any selection. The adopted physical singular-source equations are now supplied by [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]]; the original weak postulate excludes singular densities. The nonexistence proof is stronger than any source-admission question, since the four formal residual identities themselves imply the continuity that the switched history violates.",
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 1.1.1 printed pp. 3-4 (local conservation of charge: charge cannot simply appear in or vanish from a region, it can only be transported by a current) and Section 4.2.1 printed pp. 79-81 (the continuity equation as the consistency condition of the Maxwell equations); read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 7.3.3 printed pp. 326-328 (charge continuity as a consistency condition and the appearance of a compensating current; the charged-particle current rho v); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 29 printed pp. 71-72 (continuity in integral and differential form and its role for point charges); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A mathematical incompatibility example for the declared distributional source model: the switched charge distribution defined by <rho,psi> = q int_0^infty psi(0,t) dt for every psi in C_c^infty(U), U = R^3 x R, with zero current has div J + partial_t rho = q delta_(0,0) nonzero, so it admits no locally integrable field pair satisfying the four formal weak Maxwell residual identities and cannot be an admitted source model under the singular-source adoption. This is a statement about the declared model, not a measurement; no apparatus, uncertainty or empirical claim is involved."
        },
        {
