          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), the convention of the boundary-chart and surface-measure interfaces used below ([[def-countable-choice]]).\nUse the explicit coordinate labels $x_k:=x(k-1)$ for $k=1,2,3$; these relabel the zero-based coordinates of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].\nOn $\\mathbb R^3$ with its Euclidean structure ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-metric-ball]]) let $0<\\varepsilon<R$ and put\n$$B_R:=\\{x:|x|<R\\},\\qquad \\overline B_R:=\\{x:|x|\\le R\\},\\qquad A_{\\varepsilon,R}:=\\{x:\\varepsilon<|x|<R\\},\\qquad S_r:=\\{x:|x|=r\\}.$$\nThen:\n\n(a) [Domains] $B_R$ and $A_{\\varepsilon,R}$ are bounded $C^1$ domains in the sense of [[def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal]]; their boundaries are $S_R$, respectively $S_\\varepsilon\\cup S_R$, and the corresponding outward unit normals are\n$$\\nu(x)=\\frac{x}{|x|}\\ \\ (x\\in S_R),\\qquad \\nu(x)=-\\frac{x}{|x|}\\ \\ (x\\in S_\\varepsilon,\\ \\text{boundary of }A_{\\varepsilon,R});$$\n\n(b) [Spheres] each $S_r$ is a compact embedded $C^1$ (indeed $C^\\infty$) hypersurface in the sense of [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]] with two-sided unit normals $\\pm x/|x|$, and the boundary components $S_\\varepsilon,S_R$, each subdivided into six compact signed-maximal-coordinate graph faces $F_{r,k,\\sigma}:=\\{x\\in S_r:\\sigma x_k\\ge |x_j|\\text{ for all }j\\}$ ($k=1,2,3$, $\\sigma=\\pm1$), with the compact edge set consisting of their seams $\\sigma x_k=|x_j|$ for $j\\ne k$, give a finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation of $A_{\\varepsilon,R}$ in the sense of [[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]];\n\n(c) [Consequences used downstream] the divergence theorem and Green's first identity of [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]] and [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] apply on $B_R$ and on $A_{\\varepsilon,R}$ with the outward normals of (a), and $S_r$ carries a finite Borel surface measure with the standard graph charts.",
          "strategy": "The continuous norm makes $B_R$ and $A_{\\varepsilon,R}$ nonempty bounded open sets, and radial perturbations show their boundaries are respectively $S_R$ and $S_\\varepsilon\\cup S_R$ ([[thm-continuity-preimage-characterisation]], [[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]], [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]]). Here is an explicit finite graph cover of a sphere $S_r$. For $k=1,2,3$ and $\\sigma\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ use the relatively open patch $\\sigma x_k>r/(2\\sqrt3)$; these six patches cover because some $|x_k|\\ge r/\\sqrt3$. In rigid coordinates $(y,z)$ with $z=\\sigma x_k$, each patch lies on the positive graph $z=h(y)=\\sqrt{r^2-|y|^2}$. Around each point choose a graph cylinder small enough that $z>0$, $|y|<r$ and any other annulus boundary is absent. The sphere is exactly this graph, and the ball is exactly $z<h(y)$ there. In particular reflection of a lower physical hemisphere yields the positive graph $+\\sqrt{r^2-|y|^2}$, not the negative graph. The chain and power rules make $h$ smooth on $|y|<r$ ([[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]]). For the inner boundary of the annulus the domain is locally the complement of $\\overline B_\\varepsilon$. Starting with $z=\\sigma x_k>0$, reflect once more to $w=-z$; the graph is $w=-h(y)$ and the exterior condition $z>h(y)$ becomes exactly the required subgraph $w<-h(y)$. The outer boundary uses the ball chart. The definition's graph normal $(-Dh,1)/\\sqrt{1+|Dh|^2}$, with $Dh=-y/h$, transports to $x/r$ for the ball/outer sphere. For the complement chart $g=-h$, its graph normal is $(-Dg,1)/\\sqrt{1+|Dg|^2}=(-y,h)/r$ in $(y,w)$; transport by $w=-\\sigma x_k$ gives $-x/r$, the annulus inner normal. Hence (a) and its signs follow from [[def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal]]. Each sphere is closed and bounded, hence compact by [[thm-heine-borel-rn]], and the six smooth graph patches make it a compact embedded smooth hypersurface with two-sided normals. For the finite piecewise presentation, subdivide each sphere component into the six compact Borel faces $F_{r,k,\\sigma}$ of (b). They cover $S_r$. On each face $\\sigma x_k\\ge r/\\sqrt3$, so the whole face is contained in the one regular positive graph patch $z=\\sigma x_k=\\sqrt{r^2-|y|^2}$ with $z>r/(2\\sqrt3)$. The parameter set of that face is compactly contained in $|y|<r$. Define $E$ to be the finite union, for $r=\\varepsilon,R$, of the seams where two or more coordinates have tied maximal absolute value. This is compact and contains every relative face boundary and every distinct-face overlap. In the chart of $F_{r,k,\\sigma}$ any seam is contained in $h(y)=|y_j|$ for one of the two other coordinates $j$, hence satisfies $y_j=\\pm\\sqrt{(r^2-y_\\ell^2)/2}$ with $\\ell\\ne j,k$. For each fixed $y_\\ell$ this has at most two $y_j$ values, so its Borel parameter set has planar Lebesgue measure zero by [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] and [[prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null]]. Its chart density $\\sqrt{1+|Dh|^2}$ is bounded on the compact face, so it also has zero chart surface measure by [[lem-surface-integral-is-independent-of-c-one-boundary-charts]] and [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]. Away from $E$ the already established smooth domain chart is a single graph and the normals are exactly the outward ones computed above. These twelve faces and their null seam set satisfy every requirement of [[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]]. The divergence theorem and first Green identity apply to these domains with precisely those normals ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]], [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]). The compact chart surface carries finite Borel measure by [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]. The only choice cost is the declared $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of that interface. The seam indicator is Borel, and the planar Euclidean/Borel product identification needed for that Fubini argument is [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]] with [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]].",
          "provenance": {
