          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the units, constants and symbols of [[def-em-si-conventions]], let $E:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be fields of [[def-em-fields]] and let $(\\rho,J)$ be source fields of [[def-em-sources]] on $\\Omega\\times I$, all of class $C^1$ (with every additional derivative appearing below existing and continuous); $\\partial_t$ is the partial time derivative at fixed position of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\n(a) [Static regime] The **static (time-independent) electromagnetic regime** on $\\Omega\\times I$ is the requirement\n$$\\partial_tE=0,\\qquad\\partial_tB=0,\\qquad\\partial_t\\rho=0,\\qquad\\partial_tJ=0\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I.$$\n\n(b) [Pure electrostatics] The **pure electrostatic regime** is the static regime together with $J=0$ and $B=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$.\n\n(c) [Reduced equations] If the smooth branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] holds in the static regime, then the four Maxwell equations reduce to\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}E=0,\\qquad\\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$$\npointwise on $\\Omega\\times I$, with $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] applied at each fixed time; in the pure electrostatic regime the reduction is exactly the pair $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$.\n\n(d) [Fixed-time reading] In the static regime $E,B,\\rho,J$ do not depend on $t$, so each is the pullback along $(y,t)\\mapsto y$ of a single map on $\\Omega$; this fixed-time reading (the spatial field or source) is the object analysed by the electrostatic statements of this page, and no other time dependence is introduced. The equations of (c) are equations between those spatial maps.\n\n(e) [Static-to-distributional reading] Let fields $E,B$ and volume densities $\\rho,J$ be locally integrable and time independent on $\\Omega\\times I$, and let $\\Omega$ be open. Then, under the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention of the cited measure and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]), the weak equations of the solution predicate of [[def-em-weak-sources]](e) for these time-independent objects hold if and only if the four spatial equations $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$ hold in $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$; the passage between them is the integration in $t$ of the pairings against $C_c^\\infty(I\\times\\Omega)$ tests, justified by [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] after identifying fields and densities with their regular distributions ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]). In particular a **stationary point charge** at $x_0\\in\\Omega$ with charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ is the point source of [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) with constant trajectory $z(t)\\equiv x_0$, whose pairing is $\\psi\\mapsto q\\int_I\\psi(x_0,t)\\,dt$ and whose fixed-time reading is the spatial point source $\\eta\\mapsto q\\eta(x_0)$ ([[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]).\n\n(f) [Conventions and scope] The regime is imposed in the one inertial frame of [[def-em-lab-geometry]]; no claim is made that time independence survives a change of inertial description, and where a frame change is used the transformed fields must be rechecked. The static regime is an adopted idealization, not a dynamical stability theorem: static charged matter requires external support or an adopted equilibrium model. No equation is asserted here for singular sources beyond the dictionary of (e), and no empirical claim is made.",
          "strategy": "For the locally integrable branch use Borel representatives of each spatial component, obtained under $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ from [[cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure]] and [[thm-completion-measurable-functions-have-base-measurable-representatives]] after zero extension outside $\\Omega$. These changes are on spatial null sets and their products with compact time intervals are null, so they do not change spatial or spacetime regular distributions. With [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]] and [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]], the pairings may therefore be evaluated using the product measure and Fubini below. A convention definition with the two elementary verifications (c) and (e). For (c): substituting $\\partial_tE=\\partial_tB=0$ into $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$ of the smooth branch ([[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]) gives $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$ at each fixed time, while $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ are the unchanged divergence equations; the identification of the operators at fixed time is that of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], applied to the fixed-time fields of [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]]. In the pure case $B=0$, $J=0$ this pair reads $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=0$, which are identities. For (d): time independence makes $(y,t)\\mapsto F(y,t)$ the composition of $(y,t)\\mapsto y$ with $y\\mapsto F(y,t_0)$ for any fixed $t_0$; that the fixed-time value is independent of $t_0$ uses only the vanishing of all the $\\partial_t$'s and the differentiability convention of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] (a $C^1$ function on the interval $I$ with zero derivative is constant, cf. the mean-value theorem for the single variable $t$). For (e): write the four pairings of [[def-em-weak-sources]](e) with $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(I\\times\\Omega)$; for each fixed $t$ the map $\\psi(\\cdot,t)$ has compact support in a fixed compact subset of $\\Omega$ and lies in $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$, so the spatial distributional equation evaluated at $\\psi(\\cdot,t)$ gives the identity for the inner integral; integrating over $t$ with [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] (for a compact test supported in $K\\times T\\Subset\\Omega\\times I$, each absolute field/density pairing is bounded by $C_\\psi\\mathbf1_T(t)|F(y)|\\mathbf1_K(y)$, whose integral is at most $C_\\psi|T|\\int_K|F|<\\infty$ since $F\\in L^1_{\\rm loc}$; continuity of $F$ is not required) produces the four-dimensional pairing. For each time-independent component $F$, the time derivative pairing is zero: $\\langle\\partial_tT_F,\\psi\\rangle=-\\int_K F(y)(\\int_I\\partial_t\\psi(y,t)dt)dy=0$, using the same absolute majorant, Fubini and the fundamental theorem with compact time support. Thus the Faraday and Ampere time terms vanish distributionally. Conversely, testing with $\\psi(y,t)=\\eta(y)\\chi(t)$ for $\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$, $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(I)$ with $\\int\\chi=1$ recovers the spatial equations, using that all objects are time independent. The point-charge dictionary is the specialisation of [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) to the constant trajectory $z\\equiv x_0$, which is $C^1$, proper into $U$ (its graph is $\\{x_0\\}\\times I$, whose preimages of compact sets are compact), and whose pairings reduce to $q\\int_I\\psi(x_0,t)\\,dt=\\langle q\\delta_{x_0},\\int_I\\psi(\\cdot,t)\\,dt\\rangle$; the spatial point source $q\\delta_{x_0}$ is that of [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]. Clause (f) records the frame dependence and the idealization; no boost statement and no dynamics are asserted, so nothing beyond the cited definitions and the two integrations is used. Finite-interval integrations of the continuous one-variable test/radial integrands above use [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] to identify their Riemann/FTC values with their Lebesgue values before Fubini, polar integration or passage to improper limits; real and imaginary test components are treated separately.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
