            "def-piecewise-c1-path-connected-conservative-and-path-independent": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty open set that is star-shaped with respect to some $a\\in\\Omega$ ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]), let $E:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be of class $C^1$ in the spatial vector-field sense of [[def-em-fields]] ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and let $\\rho:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous — the fixed-time reading, in the sense of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]], of a pure electrostatic regime with the units and constant $\\varepsilon_0>0$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]] ([[def-em-sources]]). Suppose the reduced smooth-branch equations hold pointwise on $\\Omega$:\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}E=0\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega,$$\nwith the divergence and curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]. Then:\n(a) [Closed field and potential] $-E$ is closed in the sense of [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]] — equivalently $\\operatorname{curl}(-E)=0$ ([[cor-curl-vanishes-exactly-when-a-field-is-closed]]) — and the function\n$$\\varphi(x):=-\\int_0^1E\\bigl(a+s(x-a)\\bigr)\\cdot(x-a)\\,ds$$\n(the Euclidean inner product of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) is a $C^2$ function on $\\Omega$ with\n$$\\nabla\\varphi=-E\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega$$\n([[thm-poincare-lemma-for-star-shaped-domains]] applied to the closed $C^1$ field $-E$; continuity of the integrand and its first parameter derivatives first gives $C^1$; the proved identity $\\nabla\\varphi=-E$ with $E\\in C^1$ then gives $C^2$).\n(b) [Poisson equation] with $\\Delta\\varphi=\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\varphi$ the Laplacian of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]],\n$$-\\Delta\\varphi=\\operatorname{div}E=\\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0}\\qquad\\text{pointwise on }\\Omega.$$\n(c) [Uniqueness up to constants] if $\\psi\\in C^2(\\Omega)$ satisfies $\\nabla\\psi=-E$ on $\\Omega$, then $\\psi-\\varphi$ is constant on $\\Omega$; in particular $\\varphi$ is uniquely determined by $\\nabla\\varphi=-E$ and one prescribed value. (Star-shapedness gives path-connectedness through the centre $a$, so the constant is the same on all of $\\Omega$; [[cor-potentials-differ-by-a-componentwise-constant]], [[def-piecewise-c1-path-connected-conservative-and-path-independent]].)\nNo normalisation of $\\varphi$ at infinity, and no boundary condition, is asserted on a general star-shaped $\\Omega$; the additive constant remains free.",
          "strategy": "Hypotheses and objects: $E$ is the fixed-time reading of a pure electrostatic regime ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]), so the reduced equations are exactly the pair displayed; $\\rho$ is continuous and $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}>0$ ([[def-em-si-conventions]]). For (a): $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$ is the same as closedness of $-E$ by [[cor-curl-vanishes-exactly-when-a-field-is-closed]], applied to the field $-E$, which is $C^1$ because $E$ is ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); the closedness convention is that of [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]]. The Poincare lemma [[thm-poincare-lemma-for-star-shaped-domains]] applies to the closed $C^1$ field $F:=-E$ on the star-shaped open set $\\Omega$ with centre $a$ and produces the $C^2$ potential\n$$\\varphi(x)=\\int_0^1\\bigl\\langle F(a+t(x-a)),x-a\\bigr\\rangle\\,dt=-\\int_0^1E(a+t(x-a))\\cdot(x-a)\\,dt$$\nwith $\\nabla\\varphi=F=-E$; the inner product and its continuity are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. For (b): $\\Delta\\varphi=\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\varphi$ by the definition of the Laplacian ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]), and $\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\varphi=\\operatorname{div}(-E)=-\\operatorname{div}E$ because the divergence of a $C^1$ field is linear in the field and the partial derivatives are those of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); substituting the reduced equation gives $-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$. For (c): if $\\nabla\\psi=-E=\\nabla\\varphi$ with both potentials $C^2$, then $\\nabla(\\psi-\\varphi)=0$, and [[cor-potentials-differ-by-a-componentwise-constant]] gives constancy on each piecewise-$C^1$ path component of $\\Omega$; every point of the star-shaped set is joined to the centre by the straight segment $s\\mapsto a+s(x-a)$, $s\\in[0,1]$, which lies in $\\Omega$ and is $C^1$, so $\\Omega$ has a single piecewise-$C^1$ path component ([[def-piecewise-c1-path-connected-conservative-and-path-independent]], [[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]) and $\\psi-\\varphi$ is constant on $\\Omega$. Nothing in the proof uses a boundary condition or decay; the additive constant remains undetermined, which is exactly the scope stated. The deduction is conditional on the pointwise reduced equations; no uniqueness of the field equations, no existence of a physical equilibrium and no empirical assertion is made.",
          "provenance": {
