          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue, polar, Newtonian and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]).\nWork in $\\mathbb R^3$ with its Euclidean structure ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-metric-ball]]) and the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]], $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}>0$.\nUse one-based labels $v_i:=v(i-1)$ for vectors $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $i=1,2,3$; $\\partial_i$ means the derivative in that coordinate (the supplier's $\\partial_{i-1}$), and $e_i$ below is the standard unit vector in that direction. Divergence, curl and gradient are the cited operators with these labels, so relabelling does not change them.\nLet $\\Phi$ be the $n=3$ fundamental solution of $-\\Delta$ of [[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]], normalised by $-\\Delta T_\\Phi=\\delta_0$; then $\\omega_2=4\\pi$ and\n$$\\Phi(z)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi|z|}\\qquad(z\\ne0)$$\n([[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]], [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]).\nLet $Nf$ be the Newtonian potential of [[def-newtonian-potential]].\n\n(a) [Smooth compact sources] Let $\\rho:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^1$ with compact support contained in $\\overline B_R$, $R>0$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-metric-ball]]), put $\\varphi:=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho$ and $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$.\nThen $\\varphi\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ ([[thm-newtonian-potential-for-holder-data-is-classical]]), and pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$\n$$-\\Delta\\varphi=\\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{div}E=\\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=0$$\nwith the operators of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]] and [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]].\nMoreover, with $Q:=\\int\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3$, for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ the absolutely convergent Coulomb integral representation holds, and its far-field bound holds for $|x|>2R$,\n$$E(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{x-y}{|x-y|^3}\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\quad(x\\in\\mathbb R^3),\\qquad |E(x)|\\le\\frac{\\| \\rho\\|_1}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^2}\\quad(|x|>2R),$$\nand for $|x|>2R$ the potential obeys the decay bound $|\\varphi(x)|\\le |Q|/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|)+R\\|\\rho\\|_1/(\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^2)$ ([[lem-em-newtonian-far-field-gradient]], [[thm-decay-of-the-newtonian-potential-of-compactly-supported-data]]).\nThe pair $(E,0,\\rho,0)$ therefore realises the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] with source $\\rho$, and $E$ is the field of the localised Newtonian potential.\n\n(b) [Uniqueness with vanishing at infinity] If $\\psi\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ satisfies $-\\Delta\\psi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\lim_{|x|\\to\\infty}\\psi(x)=0$, then $\\psi=\\varphi$ ([[thm-weak-maximum-principle-for-the-laplacian]]).\nIn particular, among $C^2$ solutions of the Poisson equation of a compactly supported $C^1$ source decaying at infinity, the localised Newtonian potential is the unique one.\n\n(c) [Compactly supported $L^1$ sources] If instead $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ is real, measurable and compactly supported, then $N\\rho$ is finite $\\lambda_3$-almost everywhere, belongs to $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$, is $C^\\infty$ and harmonic off the support of $\\rho$, and its regular distribution satisfies\n$$-\\Delta T_{\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho}=T_{\\rho/\\varepsilon_0}\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$$\n([[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]], scaled by $\\varepsilon_0^{-1}$; [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]).\n\n(d) [Point charge] Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and define, with arbitrary values at $x_0$,\n$$\\varphi_q(x):=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-x_0|},\\qquad E_q(x):=\\frac{q\\,(x-x_0)}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-x_0|^3}\\qquad(x\\ne x_0).$$\nThen $\\varphi_q\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^3)$, $E_q$ is locally integrable and $C^\\infty$ off $x_0$, $E_q=-\\nabla\\varphi_q$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{x_0\\}$, and, with $T_{\\varphi_q}$ the regular distribution of $\\varphi_q$ and $\\operatorname{div}E_q:=\\sum_i\\partial_iT_{E_{q,i}}$, $\\operatorname{curl}E_q:=(\\partial_2T_{E_{q,3}}-\\partial_3T_{E_{q,2}},\\ \\partial_3T_{E_{q,1}}-\\partial_1T_{E_{q,3}},\\ \\partial_1T_{E_{q,2}}-\\partial_2T_{E_{q,1}})$,\n$$-\\Delta T_{\\varphi_q}=\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}\\delta_{x_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{div}E_q=\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}\\delta_{x_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E_q=0\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$$\n([[thm-minus-laplacian-of-the-fundamental-solution-is-dirac]] for the first identity, [[lem-em-distributional-gradient-smooth-off-a-point]] for the passage from the potential to the field, [[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]).\nThe distribution $q\\delta_{x_0}$ is the fixed-time reading of the stationary point charge of [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) with charge parameter $q$ and constant trajectory $z\\equiv x_0$ ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](e)); so the pair $(E_q,0)$ satisfies the distributional pure electrostatic equations with that point source.\nThis is an idealised point-source model: no claim of uniqueness for the point source, no self-force and no statement about the field at $x_0$ itself is made.",
          "strategy": "All uses below inherit the stated $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$: Holder regularity, compact $L^1$ distributional Poisson and its Borel-representative construction, the fundamental Dirac identity, decay and the point-field regular-distribution reading. No stronger choice axiom is invoked. Objects and constants: $\\Phi$ and its normalisation are those of [[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]]; $\\omega_2=4\\pi$ follows from $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]) and $|B_1|=4\\pi/3$ ([[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]], $n=3$), giving $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)$.\n(a) $\\rho\\in C^1_c$ is bounded and Lipschitz: $[\\rho]_{0,1}=\\sup|\\nabla\\rho|<\\infty$ by compact support, continuity of $\\nabla\\rho$ and attainment of the supremum ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]], [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]); fix $\\alpha=1/2\\in(0,1)$; for $|x-y|\\le1$ the Holder quotient is at most $\\sup|\\nabla\\rho|$, while for $|x-y|\\ge1$ it is at most $2\\|\\rho\\|_\\infty$, so the global $[\\rho]_{0,1/2}$ is finite, so [[thm-newtonian-potential-for-holder-data-is-classical]] gives $N\\rho\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ with $-\\Delta(N\\rho)=\\rho$ pointwise. Scaling $\\varphi=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho$ and using linearity of the coordinate second derivatives ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) gives $-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$; then $\\operatorname{div}E=-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ because $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ and $\\Delta\\varphi=\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\varphi$, and $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\varphi=0$ by [[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]] (alternatively by [[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]] and the curl formula). The full first-derivative representation in [[thm-newtonian-potential-for-holder-data-is-classical]] proof steps 2.2–3.1 gives $\\partial_iN\\rho(x)=\\int\\partial_i\\Phi(x-y)\\rho(y)dy$ for every $x$, including inside the support: local integrability of $|z|^{-2}$ in dimension three and bounded compact $\\rho$ make the integral absolutely convergent, and the cutoff derivative integrals converge uniformly on compact sets to the continuous $G_i$ before the uniform derivative-limit theorem identifies $\\partial_iN\\rho$. Thus the displayed Coulomb integral is valid on all $\\mathbb R^3$. The far-field bound $|E(x)|\\le\\|\\rho\\|_1/(\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^2)$ are [[lem-em-newtonian-far-field-gradient]] (with $n=3$, $\\omega_2=4\\pi$, $\\nabla\\Phi(z)=-z/(4\\pi|z|^3)$); the potential decay is [[thm-decay-of-the-newtonian-potential-of-compactly-supported-data]] with the same constants, divided by $\\varepsilon_0$. The regime reading is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](c),(d): the displayed pair satisfies the reduced equations with $\\rho$ and the vanishing time derivatives, since the objects are time independent.\n(b) Let $\\psi$ be as stated and $h:=\\psi-\\varphi$; then $h\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ and $\\Delta h=0$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], linearity of the second derivatives), while $\\lim_{|x|\\to\\infty}h(x)=0$. Given $\\varepsilon>0$ choose $R_\\varepsilon$ with $|h|\\le\\varepsilon$ on $\\{|x|\\ge R_\\varepsilon\\}$. For $R>R_\\varepsilon$, [[thm-weak-maximum-principle-for-the-laplacian]] applied on the bounded open ball $B_R$ to $h$ (which is $C^2$ on $B_R$ and continuous on $\\overline B_R$) gives $\\max_{\\overline B_R}h=\\max_{S_R}h\\le\\varepsilon$, and applied to $-h$ gives $\\min_{\\overline B_R}h\\ge-\\varepsilon$; hence $|h|\\le\\varepsilon$ on $\\overline B_R$. Every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ lies in $B_R$ for $R$ large, so $|h(x)|\\le\\varepsilon$ for every $x$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$, whence $h=0$ ([[def-metric-ball]], [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]]).\n(c) $N\\rho$ finite a.e., locally integrable and class-independent is [[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]]; the identity $-\\Delta T_{N\\rho}=T_{\\rho}$, scaled by the constant $\\varepsilon_0^{-1}$ using linearity of distributional differentiation ([[def-distributional-derivative]], [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]]), gives $-\\Delta T_{\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho}=T_{\\rho/\\varepsilon_0}$; the smoothness and harmonicity off a closed compact set containing the (a.e.) support is the final clause of the same theorem. No differentiability at points of the support is claimed.\n(d) $\\varphi_q=(q/\\varepsilon_0)\\Phi(\\cdot-x_0)$ by the explicit kernel, hence belongs to $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ because $|z|^{-1}$ is locally integrable in $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]); $E_q$ equals $-\\nabla\\varphi_q$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{x_0\\}$ by the chain rule applied to $|x-x_0|^{-1}$ ([[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]), and $|\\nabla\\varphi_q|=|q|/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-x_0|^2)$ is locally integrable for the same reason. The first distributional identity is [[thm-minus-laplacian-of-the-fundamental-solution-is-dirac]] for the pole $x_0$, scaled by $q/\\varepsilon_0$ (linearity of distributional differentiation; [[def-distribution]]). For the field identities, apply [[lem-em-distributional-gradient-smooth-off-a-point]] to $u=\\varphi_q$: $u$ is continuous and $C^\\infty$ off the single point $x_0$ ([[lem-laplace-fundamental-solution-is-harmonic-off-its-pole]]; the value at the pole is arbitrary and a singleton is null, [[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]], [[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]]), and each classical partial $\\partial_i\\varphi_q$ is locally integrable, so $\\partial_iT_{\\varphi_q}=T_{\\partial_i\\varphi_q}$. Since $E_{q,i}=-\\partial_i\\varphi_q$ everywhere off $x_0$, the functions agree almost everywhere and hence have the same regular distribution ([[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]], [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]]), so $T_{E_{q,i}}=-\\partial_iT_{\\varphi_q}$. Taking one more distributional derivative ([[def-distributional-derivative]]) and summing over $i$ gives $\\operatorname{div}E_q=-\\sum_i\\partial_i\\partial_iT_{\\varphi_q}=-\\Delta T_{\\varphi_q}=(q/\\varepsilon_0)\\delta_{x_0}$, and pairing mixed derivatives with [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]] ($\\partial^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta=\\partial^{\\alpha+\\beta}$) cancels the curl components. The point-source reading is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](e) and [[def-em-weak-sources]](b); no product of distributions and no value at $x_0$ is used, and no uniqueness, self-force or empirical claim is made. For the point branch, translation and polar integration give $\\int_{|x-x_0|<b}|x-x_0|^{-1}dx=2\\pi b^2$ and $\\int_{|x-x_0|<b}|x-x_0|^{-2}dx=4\\pi b$, proving both local integrability requirements ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]]). Singletons are null by [[prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null]], so arbitrary finite pole assignments and $q=0$ do not change any distribution. For the decay theorem's open-ball support convention, $C^1_c$ data vanishing off $\\overline B_R$ also vanish on $S_R$ by continuity from the exterior, hence vanish off $B_R$. Finite-interval integrations of the continuous one-variable test/radial integrands above use [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] to identify their Riemann/FTC values with their Lebesgue values before Fubini, polar integration or passage to improper limits; real and imaginary test components are treated separately.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.1.2 printed pp. 59-60 (Coulomb's law and the electric field of a point charge) and Section 2.2.3 printed pp. 70-72 (Gauss's-law applications: Example 2.2, the field outside a uniformly charged solid sphere; Problem 2.12, the interior field). Read in the stamped scanned full text, OCR cross-checked."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.2 printed pp. 16-17 (E = -grad phi, the Poisson equation (2.12), uniqueness up to a constant and the phi -> 0 normalization at infinity) and Section 2.2.1 printed pp. 17-19 (the point charge, rho = Q delta_3(x) and its potential). Read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 3rd revised English edition (Pergamon 1971, 387 pp.; full text)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 36 printed pp. 88-89 (static Maxwell equations (36.1)-(36.2), E = -grad phi, the Poisson equation (36.4), the Coulomb law (36.6)-(36.7), superposition (36.8) and the point-source relation (36.9)); Gaussian units re-expressed in SI. Read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
