          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, star-shaped ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]) and the spatial region of a pure electrostatic regime as in [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]], with fields of [[def-em-fields]], charge parameters of [[def-em-sources]] and the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; thus $E:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is $C^1$, $B=0$ and $J=0$ ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]), and by [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]] there is $\\varphi\\in C^2(\\Omega)$ with $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$.\nLet $q\\in\\mathbb R$ (coulombs) be the charge of a test particle and let $\\gamma:[t_0,t_1]\\to\\Omega$ be a piecewise $C^1$ path ([[def-interval]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]).\nDefine the **work of the electric force** along the prescribed path by the vector line integral\n$$W(\\gamma):=\\int_\\gamma qE\\cdot d\\mathbf r$$\nof [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]; $qE$ is the laboratory three-force of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](i) when $B=0$, using the tensor components of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]](a) and the four-force/three-force relation of [[lem-sr-four-force-and-work-energy-identities]](c), as computed in [F5] below.\nThen:\n\n(a) [Value] $W(\\gamma)=q\\,(\\varphi(\\gamma(t_0))-\\varphi(\\gamma(t_1)))$;\n\n(b) [Path independence] if $\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2$ are piecewise $C^1$ paths in $\\Omega$ with the same initial and the same terminal point, then $W(\\gamma_1)=W(\\gamma_2)$; in particular $W(\\gamma)=0$ for every closed piecewise $C^1$ path;\n\n(c) [Potential energy] with the potential energy $U:=q\\varphi$ (joules) the identity reads $W(\\gamma)=-(U(\\gamma(t_1))-U(\\gamma(t_0)))$;\n\n(d) [Gauge invariance of the work] if $\\varphi' \\in C^2(\\Omega)$ is any other potential with $\\nabla\\varphi'=-E$, then $\\varphi'=\\varphi+c$ with a constant $c\\in\\mathbb R$ and the formula of (a) is unchanged ([[cor-potentials-differ-by-a-componentwise-constant]]).\n\nThe statement is a line-integral identity for a prescribed path in a prescribed static external field. It asserts no equation of motion, no radiation, no work done on the sources and no claim that the test particle actually follows $\\gamma$; the interpretation of $W$ as work done by the adopted external coupling is the physical reading supplied by [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]], not a dynamical theorem.",
          "strategy": "The potential exists by [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]] with $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ and $\\varphi\\in C^2$; in particular $\\varphi$ is $C^1$, and $\\gamma$ is piecewise $C^1$ with values in the open set $\\Omega$, so [[thm-gradient-theorem-for-line-integrals]] applies on every $C^1$ piece: the line integral of $\\nabla\\varphi$ equals the endpoint increment. Applying the vector line integral definitions of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]] to $qE=-q\\nabla\\varphi$, the constant $q$ factors out of the integral and the increments of the pieces telescope at the interior partition points ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]), leaving\n$$W(\\gamma)=-q\\int_\\gamma\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot d\\mathbf r=q\\,\\bigl(\\varphi(\\gamma(t_0))-\\varphi(\\gamma(t_1))\\bigr),$$\nwhich is (a). Claim (b) is immediate from (a) because the right-hand side depends only on the endpoints; a closed path has equal endpoints, so its work vanishes. Claim (c) is the definition $U=q\\varphi$ and rearrangement, with $q$ in coulombs and $\\varphi$ in volts ([[def-em-si-conventions]]). Claim (d): two $C^2$ potentials of the same field differ by a constant on each piecewise-$C^1$ path component ([[cor-potentials-differ-by-a-componentwise-constant]]); a star-shaped $\\Omega$ is path-connected through the segments to the centre, so the constant is global and the difference $\\varphi(\\gamma(t_0))-\\varphi(\\gamma(t_1))$ is unchanged ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). No dynamics is used: the path is prescribed, the force is the adopted external electric coupling of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](i), and in the pure electrostatic regime $B=0$ so no magnetic term appears in that coupling ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](b)).",
          "provenance": {
