          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) as carried by the Lebesgue, polar and surface interfaces cited below ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\rho\\in C^1_c(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ with support in $\\overline B_R$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-metric-ball]]), let $\\varphi=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho$ and $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ be the smooth-branch Coulomb solution of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](a) with the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]], and let $\\Phi$ be the $n=3$ kernel, $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)$ ([[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]], [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]], [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]). Then:\n(a) [Finiteness] the three quantities\n$$\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|E|^2\\,d\\lambda_3,\\qquad \\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3,\\qquad +\\frac{1}{2\\varepsilon_0}\\iint_{\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3}\\Phi(x-y)\\rho(x)\\rho(y)\\,d(\\lambda_3\\times\\lambda_3)(x,y)$$\nare finite real numbers;\n(b) [Identity] they are equal:\n$$\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|E|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac{1}{2\\varepsilon_0}\\iint_{\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3}\\Phi(x-y)\\rho(x)\\rho(y)\\,d(\\lambda_3\\times\\lambda_3)(x,y).$$\nThe last expression is the **external assembly work** of the ideal quasistatic bookkeeping family $\\rho_s=s\\rho$, $\\varphi_s=s\\varphi$ for $0\\le s\\le1$. An added test charge brought from infinity, where the potential is zero, has electric-force work $-dq\\,\\varphi_s$ by [[pthm-em-electrostatic-work]]; the balancing external work is $+dq\\,\\varphi_s$. Therefore $W_{\\rm ext}=\\int_0^1s\\,ds\\int\\rho\\varphi=\\tfrac12\\int\\rho\\varphi$. Both signs of $\\rho$ are allowed. The symmetric double kernel gives the equivalent ordered-pair interpretation of the factor $1/2$.\n(c) [Scope] the identity is a statement about the ideal quasi-static assembly of a compactly supported $C^1$ charge distribution in vacuum, all sources being held in place by external support ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]). It asserts no time-dependent work, no radiation, no material or stability model, and it does not identify the field energy with the rest mass of any particle; the point-source limit is treated separately and is divergent (see the point-charge field energy of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d)).",
          "strategy": "The compact continuous source is Borel and the kernel with finite pole assignment is Borel; the double-kernel integrands are therefore Borel/product measurable by [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]], and their Euclidean and product-measure integrals agree by [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]]. Finiteness: $\\varphi\\in C^2$ and $E\\in C^1$ ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](a)); on the bounded set $\\overline B_{2R}$ the continuous $|E|^2$ is bounded ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]]) and the ball has finite Lebesgue measure, while outside $\\{|x|>2R\\}$ one has $|E(x)|\\le\\|\\rho\\|_1/(\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^2)$ ([[lem-em-newtonian-far-field-gradient]], [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](a)), so $\\int_{|x|>2R}|E|^2\\le\\|\\rho\\|_1^2/(\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2)\\int_{|x|>2R}|x|^{-4}d\\lambda_3<\\infty$ by the polar formula ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], with $\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$ from $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$, [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], and $|B_1|=4\\pi/3$, [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]) and $\\int_{2R}^{\\infty}r^{-2}dr<\\infty$; the first integral is finite ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). The second integral is finite because $\\rho$ is bounded with compact support and $\\varphi$ is continuous ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]]). For the double integral, the integrand is measurable and $\\iint|\\Phi(x-y)||\\rho(x)||\\rho(y)|d\\lambda_3d\\lambda_3=\\int|\\rho(x)|\\bigl(\\int\\Phi(x-y)|\\rho(y)|dy\\bigr)dx$; put $M=\\|\\rho\\|_\\infty$. Uniformly in $x$, split the inner integral at $|x-y|=1$. On the near part the polar formula and translation invariance give $\\int_{|x-y|<1}\\Phi(x-y)|\\rho(y)|dy\\le M(4\\pi)^{-1}4\\pi\\int_0^1r\\,dr=M/2$; on the far part it is at most $\\|\\rho\\|_1/(4\\pi)$. Hence the absolute double integral is bounded by $\\|\\rho\\|_1(M/2+\\|\\rho\\|_1/(4\\pi))<\\infty$ via [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] and [[thm-integrals-are-invariant-under-measure-preserving-maps]], with translations preserving measure by [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant]]. No unproved continuity of $N|\\rho|$ is invoked.\n(b) For $R'>R$ consider the ball $B_{R'}$, a bounded $C^1$ domain with outward normal $x/|x|$ on its boundary sphere $S_{R'}$ ([[lem-em-euclidean-balls-and-annuli-are-bounded-c-one-domains]]); Green's first identity [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] with $u=v=\\varphi$ gives\n$$\\int_{B_{R'}}\\bigl(\\varphi\\Delta\\varphi+|\\nabla\\varphi|^2\\bigr)d\\lambda_3=\\int_{S_{R'}}\\varphi\\,\\partial_\\nu\\varphi\\,dS.$$\nSubstituting $-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](a)) and rearranging,\n$$\\int_{B_{R'}}|E|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\int_{B_{R'}}\\rho\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3+\\int_{S_{R'}}\\varphi\\,\\partial_\\nu\\varphi\\,dS.$$\nThe boundary term tends to $0$: $|\\int_{S_{R'}}\\varphi\\partial_\\nu\\varphi\\,dS|\\le\\sup_{S_{R'}}|\\varphi|\\sup_{S_{R'}}|\\nabla\\varphi|\\,|S_{R'}|$, and $|S_{R'}|=4\\pi R'^2$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]] with $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$ and [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]), while $|\\varphi(x)|\\le C/R'$ ([[thm-decay-of-the-newtonian-potential-of-compactly-supported-data]]) and $|\\nabla\\varphi(x)|\\le\\|\\rho\\|_1/(\\pi\\varepsilon_0R'^2)$ ([[lem-em-newtonian-far-field-gradient]]), giving a bound $O(1/R')$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]); the limit of the left-hand integrals is the full integral by monotone convergence ([[thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral]]), and the right-hand charge integral is eventually constant because $\\rho$ is supported in $\\overline B_R$ ([[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]], [[thm-dominated-convergence]] for the limit along any sequence $R'\\to\\infty$). This gives the first equality of (b); the eventual constancy also identifies the limit with the integral over all of $\\mathbb R^3$. For the second equality, insert $\\varphi(x)=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\int\\Phi(x-y)\\rho(y)d\\lambda_3(y)$ ([[def-newtonian-potential]], [[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]]): $\\int\\rho\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}\\iint\\rho(x)\\Phi(x-y)\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(x)d\\lambda_3(y)$ by Fubini ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]) applied to the integrable product (finiteness as in (a)); the kernel is symmetric, $\\Phi(x-y)=\\Phi(y-x)$, so the ordered-pair integral equals twice the unordered-pair sum, which is the displayed factor $\\tfrac12$. The assembly interpretation is external quasistatic bookkeeping with $\\rho_s=s\\rho$, $\\varphi_s=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho_s=s\\varphi$, and $E_s=sE$. For an infinitesimal charge $dq$ brought from a distant point to $x$, [[pthm-em-electrostatic-work]] gives electric work $dq(\\varphi_s({\\rm start})-\\varphi_s(x))$, whose infinity limit is $-dq\\varphi_s(x)$ by the proved decay. Balancing external work is its negative. With increment $d\\rho_s=\\rho\\,ds$, its integral is $dW_{\\rm ext}=ds\\int\\rho(x)\\varphi_s(x)dx=s\\,ds\\int\\rho\\varphi$. The absolute finiteness above justifies integration, including signed $\\rho$; $\\int_0^1s\\,ds=1/2$ proves the factor. External support holds the source profiles fixed. This family is an accounting prescription, not an asserted time-dependent Maxwell solution, material dynamics, or process without radiation; no particle mass inference follows. Finite-interval integrations of the continuous one-variable test/radial integrands above use [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] to identify their Riemann/FTC values with their Lebesgue values before Fubini, polar integration or passage to improper limits; real and imaginary test components are treated separately.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
