            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) as carried by the Lebesgue and polar interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$, $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and let $E_q$ be the ideal point-charge field of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d) ([[def-em-fields]], [[def-em-si-conventions]]). For $0<\\delta<R$ put\n$$U_q(\\delta,R):=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\{\\delta\\le|x-x_0|\\le R\\}}|E_q(x)|^2\\,d\\lambda_3(x)$$\n(the truncated field energy of the shell between the two spheres, [[def-metric-ball]]). Then:\n(a) [Exact truncated energy] for all $0<\\delta<R$,\n$$U_q(\\delta,R)=\\frac{q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\Bigl(\\frac1\\delta-\\frac1R\\Bigr);$$\n(b) [Divergence] $\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}U_q(\\delta,R)=q^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\delta)$ for every $\\delta>0$, and for $q\\ne0$\n$$\\lim_{\\delta\\downarrow0}U_q(\\delta,R)=+\\infty\\qquad\\text{for every }R>0;$$\nso the field energy of the ideal point charge is locally infinite at its own location, and any finite-energy treatment of a point-like charge must modify the source/field near the singularity or introduce an explicit cutoff;\n(c) [Limits of the mass inference] the identity is a divergence of the *field energy of the idealised point source* in the framework's SI conventions. It does not assert that a physical particle has infinite rest mass, does not identify field energy with rest mass, contains no measurement, and involves neither a self-force nor a contradiction claim for classical electrodynamics: the framework supplies no mass-energy identification for a charged particle, and the smooth compact-source field/assembly energy of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-field-energy]] remains finite. The compact $L^1$ branch of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] retains its locally integrable potential and distributional Poisson equation; it does not guarantee finite field energy for arbitrary $L^1$ densities. The point source itself is the idealisation of [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](e)); its energy divergence is a mathematical property of that idealisation, not an empirical result.",
          "strategy": "For (a), $|E_q(x)|=|q|/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-x_0|^2)$ for $x\\ne x_0$ ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d), [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]]), so $|E_q|^2=q^2/(16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2|x-x_0|^4)$ and by the polar formula ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] with the unit-sphere measure of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], $\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$ from [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]] and [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]; the integrand is nonnegative and Borel, [[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]])\n$$\\int_{\\delta\\le|x-x_0|\\le R}|E_q|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac{q^2}{16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2}\\cdot 4\\pi\\int_\\delta^R r^{-2}\\,dr=\\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0^2}\\Bigl(\\frac1\\delta-\\frac1R\\Bigr),$$\nwhere the one-dimensional integral is evaluated by the fundamental theorem and the derivative of $r\\mapsto-1/r$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]]); multiplying by $\\varepsilon_0/2$ gives (a) ([[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). For (b), let $R\\to\\infty$ in (a) for fixed $\\delta$ (the left-hand integral increases to the exterior integral by monotone convergence, [[thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral]], giving the first limit) and let $\\delta\\downarrow0$ for fixed $R$: $1/\\delta\\to\\infty$ for $q\\ne0$ (directly from the explicit formula and the order properties of the reals), so the truncated energies tend to $+\\infty$. Any neighbourhood of $x_0$ therefore contains field energy exceeding any prescribed bound. For (c): the computation uses only the idealised point-source field of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d); it makes no use of, and does not assert, a mass-energy relation for a charged particle, a self-force, a regularisation or any measurement ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]); the contrast with the finite energies of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-field-energy]] is the contrast between an extended compactly supported source and the point idealisation. No claims about the field at $x_0$ itself are made: the truncated integrals avoid the singularity ([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]], [[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]]). The choice cost is the declared $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of the Lebesgue/polar interface. Finite-interval integrations of the continuous one-variable test/radial integrands above use [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] to identify their Riemann/FTC values with their Lebesgue values before Fubini, polar integration or passage to improper limits; real and imaginary test components are treated separately.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.3 printed pp. 25-28 (the assembly energy (2.23)-(2.26), the field-energy identity (2.27), and the self-energy of a point particle with its divergent truncated field integral on pp. 27-28). Read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.4.1-2.4.4 printed pp. 90-96: the work done to move a charge and the assembly of point charges (pp. 90-93), the continuous-distribution identities W = (1/2) integral rho V and W = (epsilon_0/2) integral E^2 (Eqs. (2.43) and (2.45), pp. 93-95), and the comment that the point-charge field energy is infinite while the assembly expression omits self-terms (pp. 95-96). Read in the stamped scanned full text, OCR cross-checked."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 3rd revised English edition (Pergamon 1971, 387 pp.; full text)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 37 printed pp. 89-90 (the field energy U = (1/8pi) integral E^2 dV, its transformation to U = (1/2) integral rho phi dV, and the point-charge self-energy divergence with the classical-radius discussion); Gaussian units re-expressed in SI. Read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "Weng Cho Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory (Purdue University, Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 1.3.1-1.3.2 printed pp. 8-9 (Coulomb's law and the electric field) and Sections 3.3.2-3.3.3 printed pp. 35-36 (Poisson's equation (3.3.9), the point-charge solution (3.3.14), the static Green's function (3.3.16) and the convolution solution for general sources). Read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The truncated electric field energy of the idealised point charge in vacuum: the shell energy is $q^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0)(1/\\delta-1/R)$, finite for $0<\\delta<R$ but divergent as the inner cutoff $\\delta\\downarrow0$ for $q\\ne0$. The item records this divergence as a property of the point idealisation and explicitly excludes inferences about particle rest mass, self-force, empirical measurement or inconsistency of the framework; finite-energy statements remain those of the compact-source branches."
        }
      ],
