          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue, polar, Newtonian and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]).\nUse the one-based coordinate and derivative labels of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]]: $x_k:=x(k-1)$, $\\partial_k$ is differentiation in that coordinate, and $e_k$ is its unit vector, for $k=1,2,3$.\nLet $\\Omega:=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{x:x_1=x_2=0\\}$ (the complement of the $z$-axis), let $\\rho(x):=\\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2}$ and $\\hat e_r(x):=(x_1,x_2,0)/\\rho(x)$ for $x\\in\\Omega$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-metric-ball]]), let $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ be a line charge density in $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-1}}$, let $r_0>0$, and define, with the constants and units of [[def-em-si-conventions]] ([[def-em-fields]], [[def-em-sources]]),\n$$E(x):=\\frac{\\lambda\\,\\hat e_r(x)}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\,\\rho(x)},\\qquad \\varphi(x):=-\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\log\\frac{\\rho(x)}{r_0}\\qquad(x\\in\\Omega).$$\nThen:\n\n(a) [Gradient] $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ on $\\Omega$ ([[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]);\n\n(b) [Classical equations] $\\operatorname{div}E=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$ pointwise on $\\Omega$; hence the fixed-time pair $(E,0)$ satisfies the reduced smooth-branch electrostatic equations with zero volume source on $\\Omega$ ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (c), [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]);\n\n(c) [Flux per length] for every $r>0$ and $L>0$ the flux of $E$ through the lateral cylinder surface $\\{x:\\rho(x)=r,\\ |x_3|\\le L/2\\}$ in the outward orientation is $\\Phi_r=\\lambda L/\\varepsilon_0$; the parametrization $(\\theta,z)\\mapsto(r\\operatorname{Re}e^{i\\theta},r\\operatorname{Im}e^{i\\theta},z)$ over the rectangle $[0,2\\pi]\\times[-L/2,L/2]$ is an admissible regular surface patch ([[def-admissible-regular-parametrized-surface-patch]], [[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]], [[def-complex-exponential]], [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]], [[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]], [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]], [[thm-surface-area-density-is-cross-product-norm]]);\n\n(d) [Distributional line source] extend $E$ by $0$ on the axis and let $\\rho_\\lambda(\\psi):=\\lambda\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\psi(0,0,z)\\,dz$ be the line-charge distribution on $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-distribution]], [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]). Then, with distributional derivatives and the regular distribution of $E$ ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]),\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\rho_\\lambda,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}E=0\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3),$$\nby superposition of the point-charge identities of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d) along the axis ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]); the line density fixed by the field in this weak sense is exactly $\\lambda$;\n\n(e) [Normalization] for fixed $r_0$, $\\varphi(\\rho)\\to-\\infty$ as $\\rho\\to\\infty$ if $\\lambda>0$ and $\\to+\\infty$ if $\\lambda<0$, while changing $r_0$ to $r_0'$ shifts $\\varphi$ by $\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\log(r_0'/r_0)$ and leaves $E$ unchanged. For $\\lambda\\ne0$ the infinity normalization of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (b) is therefore unavailable; for $\\lambda=0$ the field, potential and line source are zero and all cutoff energies and fluxes vanish; and $\\Omega$ is not star-shaped (for every $a\\in\\Omega$ the segment from $a$ to $-a$ passes through the axis, which is not in $\\Omega$), so [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]] does not apply to $\\Omega$, although the displayed $\\varphi$ still satisfies $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]);\n\n(f) [Energy per length] for $0<\\delta<r<R$ and $L>0$,\n$$\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\{\\delta<\\rho<R,\\ |x_3|<L/2\\}}|E|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac{\\lambda^2L}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\log\\frac{R}{\\delta},$$\nwhich for $\\lambda\\ne0$ diverges both as $\\delta\\downarrow0$ and as $R\\to\\infty$; there is no finite energy per unit length ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]);\n\n(g) [Scope and idealization] the line charge $\\rho_\\lambda$ is *not* a source type of [[def-em-weak-sources]]: only point sources and stationary sheets are declared there, and no line current, moving sheet or other distribution is included. The item therefore verifies classical, flux and distributional identities for an explicitly specified distribution $\\rho_\\lambda$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ with a noncompact singularity; it is not a framework postulate covering infinite line charges, asserts no physical realisation, equilibrium or empirical content, and is not a compact-source Coulomb corollary. Real line-like distributions have finite length and transverse size, and their far fields and energies require their own hypotheses.",
          "strategy": "For (a): on the open set $\\Omega$ ([[def-metric-topology]]; $\\rho>0$ is the preimage of the open half-line under the continuous $\\rho$, [[thm-continuity-preimage-characterisation]], [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]]) the function $\\rho$ is smooth — it is the composition of $x\\mapsto x_1^2+x_2^2$ with the square root, positive on $\\Omega$ ([[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) — and the chain rule together with the logarithm derivative gives $\\nabla\\varphi=-\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{1}{\\rho}\\hat e_r=-E$ ([[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]).\nFor (b): with $E_i=\\lambda x_i/(2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\rho^2)$, direct differentiation on $\\Omega$ gives $\\partial_1E_1+\\partial_2E_2=\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{(\\rho^2-2x_1^2)+(\\rho^2-2x_2^2)}{\\rho^4}=0$ and $\\partial_1E_2-\\partial_2E_1=\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{-2x_1x_2+2x_1x_2}{\\rho^4}=0$; the third components vanish since $E_3=0$ and nothing depends on $x_3$ ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). The electrostatic reading is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (c).\nFor (c): the displayed map is $C^1$ on an open neighbourhood of the compact Jordan region $D:=[0,2\\pi]\\times[-L/2,L/2]$; writing $u(\\theta)=\\operatorname{Re}e^{i\\theta}$, $v(\\theta)=\\operatorname{Im}e^{i\\theta}$, the derivative $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}e^{i\\theta}=ie^{i\\theta}$ ([[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]], [[def-complex-exponential]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]) gives $\\Phi_\\theta=(ru',rv',0)=r(-v,u,0)$ and $\\Phi_z=(0,0,1)$, so $\\Phi_\\theta\\times\\Phi_z=r(u,v,0)\\ne0$ on $D^\\circ$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]); the only seam is $\\theta=0\\leftrightarrow2\\pi$ on the boundary, so $(D,\\Phi)$ is an admissible regular surface patch ([[def-admissible-regular-parametrized-surface-patch]], [[thm-surface-area-density-is-cross-product-norm]]). Since $E(\\Phi(\\theta,z))=\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}(u,v,0)$ and $u^2+v^2=|e^{i\\theta}|^2=1$ ([[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]), the flux integrand of [[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]] is the constant $\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$, and the patch integral equals $\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\cdot\\lambda_2(D)=\\frac{\\lambda L}{\\varepsilon_0}$ ([[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]], [[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]], [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]).\nFor (d): the superposition identity $E(x)=\\lambda\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{x-z e_3}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-z e_3|^3}\\,dz$ is verified componentwise: $\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{dt}{(\\rho^2+t^2)^{3/2}}=\\frac{2}{\\rho^2}$ and the third component is odd in $t$ with absolutely convergent integral zero; the whole $z$-integral is absolutely convergent because $\\int_{\\mathbb R}|x-z e_3|^{-2}dz=\\pi/\\rho$ and $x\\mapsto|D\\psi(x)|/\\rho(x)$ is integrable for a compactly supported scalar or vector test: a bounding cylinder $\\rho\\le A$, $|x_3|\\le H$ gives $\\int 1/\\rho\\le(2H)2\\pi A<\\infty$ by transverse polar integration. The same estimate without $D\\psi$ proves $E\\in L^1_{\\rm loc}(\\mathbb R^3)$, and $\\int\\int|E_{1,z}(x)||D\\psi(x)|dzdx\\le(4\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}\\int|D\\psi|/\\rho<\\infty$ proves absolute Fubini integrability. Here $E_{1,z}$ is the translated unit-charge field with the $\\varepsilon_0$ normalization of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d); its value on its pole is immaterial ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], [[thm-extreme-value-metric]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). The pairing of the $z$-translated unit point-charge field with $\\nabla\\psi$ is $-\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\psi(0,0,z)$ by [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d); substituting into $\\langle\\operatorname{div}E,\\psi\\rangle=-\\int E\\cdot\\nabla\\psi$ and interchanging by [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] gives $\\langle\\operatorname{div}E,\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{\\lambda}{\\varepsilon_0}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\psi(0,0,z)\\,dz$ for every test function; the curl pairing with every vector test $\\Psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^3)$ is $\\int E\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\Psi=\\lambda\\int dz\\int E_{1,z}\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\Psi=0$ by that same absolute majorant, since each translated unit-charge field has vanishing distributional curl ([[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]).\nFor (e): the stated limits of $\\varphi$ in $\\rho$ and the effect of changing $r_0$ are the elementary properties of the logarithm ([[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]]); for the non-star-shapedness witness, given $a\\in\\Omega$ the point $-a$ lies in $\\Omega$ and the segment $s\\mapsto a+s(-a)=a(1-2s)$ passes through $0$ at $s=1/2$, which is on the excluded axis ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]).\nFor (f): $|E|^2=\\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2\\rho^2}$, and the polar formula in the two transverse coordinates ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], $\\sigma(S^1)=2\\pi$ from $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$ with $|B_1|=\\pi$ in the plane, [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]) gives $\\int_{\\delta<\\rho<R}\\rho^{-2}\\,d\\lambda_2=2\\pi\\log(R/\\delta)$; multiplying by the height $L$ and by $\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\cdot\\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2}$ yields the displayed identity, and $\\log(R/\\delta)\\to\\infty$ in either limit ([[thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral]] for the monotone convergence of the truncated integrals).\nClause (g) records the scope: [[def-em-weak-sources]] declares only point and stationary-sheet sources, so $\\rho_\\lambda$ is outside the framework's source types and the item makes no physical, equilibrium or empirical claim. For $\\lambda=0$, both $E$ and $\\varphi$ and $\\rho_\\lambda$ are zero, all fluxes and cutoff energies vanish, and the infinity normalization is available; the failure of that normalization and the two energy divergences concern only $\\lambda\\ne0$. The axis is null (a transverse singleton times bounded longitudinal intervals), so the zero extension does not alter regular distributions. The line-source functional is a distribution because on each compact test support its absolute pairing is at most $|\\lambda|(2H)\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty$. The axis is contained in the coordinate hyperplane $x_1=0$, which is null by [[prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null]]. The translated unit-point field, with zero value at its pole, is jointly Borel in $(x,z)$; the scalar/vector-test products are Borel, and [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]] with [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]] supplies the product-measure identification for these Fubini calculations. The longitudinal integrals in (d) are evaluated first on finite intervals by [[thm-ftc-second-part]] and [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]]: $t/(\\rho^2\\sqrt{\\rho^2+t^2})$ is a primitive of $(\\rho^2+t^2)^{-3/2}$, with limiting values $\\pm1/\\rho^2$; the third component is odd. Absolute convergence then passes to the full line. For the exact absolute-kernel majorant, evenness and the monotone substitution $t=\\rho\\sqrt{u/(1-u)}$ on $0<u<1$ give $\\int_{\\mathbb R}(\\rho^2+t^2)^{-1}dt=\\rho^{-1}B(1/2,1/2)=\\pi/\\rho$. The substitution has positive continuous derivative on compact truncations; [[thm-substitution-for-improper-integrals]] applies, with Riemann/Lebesgue comparison on those truncations and monotone convergence at the endpoints. The value follows from [[def-real-beta-integral]], [[thm-real-beta-gamma-identity]], [[cor-real-gamma-one-half-is-root-pi]] and $\\Gamma(1)=1$ in [[thm-real-gamma-functional-equation]].",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
