          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue, polar, Newtonian and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $a>0$, $Q\\in\\mathbb R$, let $\\overline B_a=\\{x:|x|\\le a\\}$ ([[def-metric-ball]]), and define, with the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]] ([[def-em-fields]], [[def-em-sources]]),\n$$\\rho(x):=\\frac{3Q}{4\\pi a^3}\\,\\mathbf 1_{\\{|x|<a\\}}(x),\\qquad\nE(x):=\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\begin{cases}x/a^3,&|x|<a,\\\\ x/|x|^3,&|x|\\ge a,\\end{cases}\\qquad\n\\varphi(x):=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{Q(3a^2-|x|^2)}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3},&|x|\\le a,\\\\[6pt]\\dfrac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|},&|x|\\ge a.\\end{cases}$$\n(The value of $\\rho$ on the sphere $\\{|x|=a\\}$ is immaterial: it is a Lebesgue-null set by the polar calculation $\\lambda_3(S_a)=4\\pi\\int_0^\\infty\\mathbf1_{\\{a\\}}(r)r^2dr=0$, since the one-dimensional singleton has measure zero ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], [[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]], [[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]]).) Then:\n(a) [Regularity and gradient] $E$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R^3$, of class $C^\\infty$ on each of the open regions $\\{|x|<a\\}$, $\\{|x|>a\\}$; $\\varphi\\in C^1(\\mathbb R^3)$, its displayed branch derivatives agree on $\\{|x|=a\\}$, and $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]]);\n(b) [Classical equations] for every $x$ with $|x|\\ne a$,\n$$\\operatorname{div}E(x)=\\rho(x)/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E(x)=0$$\n([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]);\n(c) [Weak equations] the pair $(E,(\\rho,0))$ satisfies the spatial distributional equations $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$: $E$ is $C^1$ on each side of the sphere $S_a=\\{|x|=a\\}$ with one-sided continuous traces that agree, $[E]=0$, so [[lem-em-interface-derivatives]] gives\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\{\\operatorname{div}E\\}_{\\mathrm{piecewise}}+n\\cdot[E]\\,\\delta_{S_a}=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=\\{\\operatorname{curl}E\\}_{\\mathrm{piecewise}}+n\\times[E]\\,\\delta_{S_a}=0,$$\nwith $\\rho$ the displayed locally integrable density and no surface-charge term ([[def-em-weak-sources]], [[lem-em-euclidean-balls-and-annuli-are-bounded-c-one-domains]] for the sphere as a compact embedded smooth hypersurface); by the fixed-time reading of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e) these are the weak equations of the static model for this source, with zero singular part;\n(d) [Energy] the field energy is finite and equals the assembly integral,\n$$\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|E|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac{3Q^2}{20\\pi\\varepsilon_0a},$$\ncomputed with the polar formula ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]); the value agrees with the smooth-source identity of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-field-energy]] although the density here is only piecewise continuous;\n(e) [Consistency and contrast] for $|x|>a$ the field is exactly the point-charge Coulomb field of total charge $Q$ centred at the origin ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d) with $x_0=0$) although the source is the extended ball, and for $|x|<a$ it is the affine field $Qx/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3)$; $\\varphi$ is continuous at $|x|=a$ and decays at infinity; the field is bounded on $\\mathbb R^3$, so no divergence of field energy occurs for the extended source;\n(f) [Scope] the example verifies the displayed candidate and its energy; it does not claim uniqueness of the solution (this would require boundary data and an infinity condition, and the density is not continuous), it asserts no empirical content, and the density $\\rho$ is treated as a bounded $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ density with the sphere carrying no surface charge ($[E]=0$).",
          "strategy": "For (a): on $\\{|x|<a\\}$, $E$ is the affine map $Qx/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3)$ and on $\\{|x|>a\\}$ it is $Qx/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^3)$; both are $C^\\infty$ on their open domains ([[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). At $|x|=a$ the two formulas coincide ($x/a^3=x/a^3$), so $E$ is continuous on all of $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]], [[def-metric-ball]]). For $\\varphi$, the interior branch has gradient $-Qx/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3)$ and the exterior branch has gradient $-Qx/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^3)$ by the chain rule, and the two gradients agree on $\\{|x|=a\\}$; the matching derivatives also exist on the interface, as follows. Let $F(r)$ be the displayed radial potential profile on $r\\ge0$. Its two branches agree at $a$, and their one-sided derivatives both equal $-Q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2)$, so the difference quotient $(F(a+h)-F(a))/h$ has that common limit from either sign. Thus $F$ is differentiable at $a$, and its derivative is continuous there by the branch formulas. Since $x\\mapsto|x|$ is differentiable near each point of $S_a$, the chain rule proves actual total differentiability of $\\varphi=F(|x|)$ there and gives the matching gradient. At the origin the interior polynomial already gives smoothness. The resulting partial derivatives exist and are continuous everywhere, whence $\\varphi$ is $C^1$ with $\\nabla\\varphi=-E$ ([[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]).\nFor (b): inside, $\\operatorname{div}(Qx/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3))=3Q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3)=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and the curl of an affine field vanishes ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]], [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]); outside, $\\operatorname{div}(Qx/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^3))=\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\sum_i\\partial_i(x_i|x|^{-3})=\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}(3|x|^{-3}-3|x|^{-3})=0$ and the curl components $\\partial_j(x_k|x|^{-3})-\\partial_k(x_j|x|^{-3})=0$ vanish by the same direct differentiation ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]), matching $\\rho=0$ outside.\nFor (c): the set $S_a$ is a compact embedded smooth hypersurface with unit normal $n=x/|x|$, and the two sides $\\Omega_-=\\{|x|<a\\}$, $\\Omega_+=\\{|x|>a\\}$ are open; $E$ is $C^1$ on each side and its one-sided traces agree on $S_a$ (from (a)), so $[E]=F_+-F_-=0$ in the notation of [[def-em-surface-distribution]] and [[lem-em-interface-derivatives]] applies: the distributional divergence is the piecewise classical divergence plus $n\\cdot[E]\\delta_{S_a}=0$, and the distributional curl is the piecewise classical curl plus $n\\times[E]\\delta_{S_a}=0$. By (b) the piecewise divergence is $\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ as a locally integrable function and the piecewise curl is $0$; the sphere is null by applying [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] to its Borel indicator: $\\lambda_3(S_a)=4\\pi\\int_0^\\infty\\mathbf1_{\\{a\\}}(r)r^2dr=0$. A singleton is contained in intervals of arbitrarily small length, so its one-dimensional measure is zero; [[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]] then applies, and the pairing conventions are those of [[def-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]] and [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]. The static reading is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e), and the source objects are those of [[def-em-weak-sources]] with zero singular part.\nFor (d): by the polar formula and $\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]) one has $\\int_{B_a}|x|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=4\\pi\\int_0^ar^4\\,dr=\\frac{4\\pi a^5}{5}$ and $\\int_{B_a}d\\lambda_3=\\frac{4\\pi a^3}{3}$; hence\n$$\\int_{B_a}|E|^2=\\Bigl(\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3}\\Bigr)^2\\frac{4\\pi a^5}{5}=\\frac{Q^2}{20\\pi\\varepsilon_0^2a},\\qquad \\int_{|x|>a}|E|^2=\\frac{Q^2}{16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2}\\cdot 4\\pi\\int_a^\\infty r^{-2}dr=\\frac{Q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0^2a},$$\nusing $\\int_a^\\infty r^{-2}dr=1/a$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]] and monotone convergence, [[thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]); the sum times $\\varepsilon_0/2$ is $\\frac{Q^2}{40\\pi\\varepsilon_0a}+\\frac{Q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0a}=\\frac{3Q^2}{20\\pi\\varepsilon_0a}$. The assembly integral is computed from the same two radial integrals, $\\frac12\\int\\rho\\varphi=\\frac12\\frac{3Q}{4\\pi a^3}\\frac{Q}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^3}\\bigl(3a^2\\frac{4\\pi a^3}{3}-\\frac{4\\pi a^5}{5}\\bigr)=\\frac{3Q^2}{20\\pi\\varepsilon_0a}$, so the identity of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-field-energy]] extends to this piecewise-continuous bounded density by direct verification; the density is bounded with compact support and all integrands are continuous off a null sphere ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]]).\nFor (e): the exterior branch equals the formula of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d) with $q=Q$, $x_0=0$; $\\varphi$ is continuous at $|x|=a$ since both branches give $Q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a)$, and decays like $1/|x|$; $|E|\\le |Q|/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2)$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, so the energy of (d) is finite and no point singularity arises. For (f): no uniqueness or empirical statement is used anywhere; the sphere's zero jump is exactly the checked equality of the two branches on $\\{|x|=a\\}$. The one-dimensional singleton used in the polar null-sphere calculation is null by [[prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null]]. Finite-interval integrations of the continuous one-variable test/radial integrands above use [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] to identify their Riemann/FTC values with their Lebesgue values before Fubini, polar integration or passage to improper limits; real and imaginary test components are treated separately.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.1.2 printed pp. 11-12 (the uniform sphere: interior field linear in $r$, exterior inverse-square, continuity at the surface) and Section 2.3 printed pp. 25-28 for the energy identities."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.2.3 printed pp. 70-72 (Example 2.2, exterior field of a uniformly charged solid sphere, and Problem 2.12, the interior field) and Section 2.4.3 printed pp. 93-95 (energy integrals over charge and field). Read in the stamped scanned full text, OCR cross-checked."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 3rd revised English edition (Pergamon 1971, 387 pp.; full text)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 36-37 printed pp. 88-90 (Coulomb law, superposition and the electrostatic energy identities; Gaussian units re-expressed in SI). Read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "Weng Cho Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory (Purdue University, Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 1.3.1-1.3.2 printed pp. 8-9 (Coulomb's law and the electric field) and Sections 3.3.2-3.3.3 printed pp. 35-36 (Poisson's equation (3.3.9), the point-charge solution (3.3.14), the static Green's function (3.3.16) and the convolution solution for general sources). Read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The uniformly charged ball of radius $a$ and total charge $Q$ in vacuum: the candidate piecewise field and continuous potential, the pointwise and distributional static equations with the piecewise-constant density and zero surface charge, the exterior Coulomb field of total charge $Q$, the vanishing jump of the normal field, and the finite energy $3Q^2/(20\\pi\\varepsilon_0a)$ equal to the assembly integral. The density is only piecewise continuous; no uniqueness, empirical or stability claim is made."
        }
      ],
