            "def-em-weak-sources": "operational-prerequisite",
            "post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Under the Countable Choice convention of the stated boundary and sheet-distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]), Adopt as a physical model of ideal electrostatic conductors, for the classical laboratory framework of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the units and constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]: a **conductor configuration** consists of the following data, and every model of this postulate is required to satisfy the clauses below. No experiment is reported and no empirical value is inferred; this is an explicitly adopted equilibrium idealization together with its geometric and source conventions.\n(a) [Geometry] a laboratory region $\\Omega\\times I$ with $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ an arbitrary open ambient region, a finite family $D_1,\\dots,D_m$ ($m\\ge1$) of bounded $C^\\infty$ domains with pairwise disjoint closures in the sense of [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]] with closures strictly inside $\\Omega$ (the **conductor bodies**), the **conductor** $D:=D_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup D_m$, its **surface** $\\partial D$, and the **vacuum region** $\\Omega_v:=\\Omega\\setminus\\overline D$. By [[lem-em-boundary-of-a-bounded-smooth-domain-is-a-compact-embedded-hypersurface]], $\\partial D=\\partial D_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup\\partial D_m$ is a disjoint union of compact embedded $C^\\infty$ hypersurfaces. The vacuum $\\Omega_v$ is open, and near each conductor surface it is locally the smooth exterior side of that conductor. No boundedness or regularity of the ambient boundary is inferred from openness. In the additional **bounded smooth ambient branch**, assume that $\\Omega$ is a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain; only then [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]] gives that $\\Omega_v$ is a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain with the specified outer/conductor boundary charts; we additionally require $\\Omega_v$ to be connected (configurations whose vacuum region is separated by the conductors, for instance by an enclosing closed shell, are outside this model as formulated here).\n(b) [Equilibrium and sources] the configuration is in the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] on the vacuum region: $B=0$, $J=0$, $E$ is the fixed-time electric field of [[def-em-fields]], the volume source density $\\rho_v$ is a continuous real map on $\\overline{\\Omega_v}$ (C m$^{-3}$, [[def-em-sources]]) extended by zero to $D$; the **conductor charge is a stationary surface charge**, i.e. a continuous density $\\sigma:\\partial D\\to\\mathbb R$ (C m$^{-2}$) whose pairing with test functions is the stationary-sheet source of [[def-em-weak-sources]](c) with tangential current $K=0$ and $S=\\partial D$; and the field pair $(E,0)$ together with the source pair $(\\rho_v+\\sigma\\,\\delta_{\\partial D},\\ 0)$ satisfies the adopted distributional vacuum Maxwell equations [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]]. Inside the conductor the field vanishes: $E\\equiv0$ on $D$ (the **equilibrium condition**); equivalently the conductor interior is field-free and carries no volume charge.\n(c) [Potential and equipotential convention] there is a function $\\varphi\\in C^0(\\Omega)\\cap C^2(\\Omega_v)$ with $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ on $\\Omega_v$ and with $\\varphi$ constant on each conductor body, $\\varphi|_{D_j}\\equiv V_j$ for real constants $V_j$ (volts, [[def-em-si-conventions]]); by continuity of $\\varphi$ the trace of $\\varphi$ on each $\\partial D_j$ from the vacuum side equals $V_j$ as well. This is the single-function convention of an ideal conductor with no double layer: the model reads the same potential value on both sides of $\\partial D$, while the normal component of $E$ may jump.\n(d) [Data] a model is completed either by the component potentials $V_1,\\dots,V_m$ together with the volume source $\\rho_v$ and the data on $\\partial\\Omega$ where the laboratory region is bounded, or by the component total charges $Q_j:=\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\sigma\\,dS$ (C) together with those same ambient data. These are two declared data types; the postulate asserts neither that both data types are simultaneously specifiable nor that either is solvable for arbitrary data.\n(e) [Scope] This is a static, frame-relative equilibrium idealization: it excludes transient finite-conductivity dynamics, relaxation to equilibrium, moving surface charge, electrode polarization and contact effects, and it is not derived from a microscopic matter model. It asserts no existence or uniqueness theorem (those are separate conditional results), no force, no energy, no radiation, no measurement and no apparatus realization; the value $V_j$ is a model parameter and the surface density $\\sigma$ is an idealization variable determined by the equilibrium problem when it is solvable. The general open ambient branch retains compact conductor bodies in exterior configurations. Its additional bounded smooth ambient branch is distinct from the separately declared grounded-half-space and infinite parallel-plane idealizations, whose conductor bodies are themselves noncompact. Those examples retain the equilibrium/equipotential interpretation but their noncompact bodies and plane distributions are not instances of this compact-body configuration or its compact-sheet predicate. No arbitrary exterior solvability is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Nonproof formulation and scope review: adopt the equilibrium objects already defined in the framework and record their exact interfaces instead of proving a physical law. Textbook conductor treatments support each clause as a model choice: the vanishing interior field with all charge on the surface and the constant potential of an equipotential conductor ([[def-em-weak-sources]]'s stationary-sheet source is the framework's own surface-distribution object), the grounded/fixed-potential and fixed-charge data distinction, and the idealization's limits are stated rather than hidden. The mathematical identities used to name the objects are exact: $\\partial D$ is a finite disjoint union of compact embedded smooth hypersurfaces by [[lem-em-boundary-of-a-bounded-smooth-domain-is-a-compact-embedded-hypersurface]]. For general open $\\Omega$, removing the finite compact separated closures gives an open vacuum, with smooth local exterior conductor charts because each compact body is strictly inside $\\Omega$ and separated from the others. The vacuum need not be bounded or have a smooth outer boundary. Only under the additionally imposed bounded $C^\\infty$ ambient hypothesis does [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]] give a bounded smooth vacuum; the surface pairing $\\sigma\\delta_{\\partial D}$ is the one of [[def-em-weak-sources]](c) under the surface-distribution conventions of [[def-em-surface-distribution]] and carries its declared $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]); the field, source and regime objects are those of [[def-em-fields]], [[def-em-sources]] and [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]; and the adopted solution predicate is [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]], not a consequence of the smooth postulate. The clause (d) data distinction is a definitional choice (Dirichlet data $V_j$ versus charge data $Q_j$), and clause (e) states the exclusions; in particular no claim is made that a configuration exists for arbitrary data, and no empirical or metrological content is attached to the idealization.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 2.5.1-2.5.3 printed pp. 96-103 (conductors: E = 0 in the equilibrium interior, charge on the surface, surface-charge relation (2.48) and normal-derivative form (2.49)); Section 2.5.4 printed pp. 103-106 (capacitance Q = CV (2.53)); Sections 3.1.5-3.1.6 printed pp. 116-121 (first and second uniqueness theorems, grounded-conductor data)."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (Lent Term 2015, 236 PDF pages)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.4 printed pp. 30-31 (conductors: E = 0 and constant phi inside, no interior charge, surface charge with outside field sigma/epsilon_0 n-hat, Eq. (2.31)); Section 2.4.2 printed pp. 33-35 (fixed-potential versus fixed-charge conductors, grounded conductors, Dirichlet/Neumann data and the uniqueness theorem)."
              },
              {
