          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) inherited from the surface and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $(\\Omega;D_1,\\dots,D_m;\\Omega_v;E;\\rho_v;\\sigma;V_j)$ be a model of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]] with surface $S:=\\partial D$ and vacuum region $\\Omega_v$, with the fields of [[def-em-fields]], the constants and units of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] carried by the model, and suppose additionally that the field $E$ satisfies the joint one-sided $C^1$ regularity hypothesis of [[pthm-em-fixed-vacuum-jumps]] across $S$: around every $x_0\\in S$ each side of $S$ is the restriction of a $C^1$ field on an open neighbourhood of the corresponding closed side of a smaller graph chart. Let $n$ denote the unit normal on $S$ pointing from the conductor side into the vacuum side (equivalently: $n$ is the inward normal of $\\Omega_v$ and the outward normal of $D$ on $S$, by the local exterior conductor chart; in the bounded smooth ambient branch this is the sign convention of [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]](c)), and let $E_+$ denote the vacuum-side trace of $E$ (the field is zero on the conductor side, so $[E]=E_+$). Then, pointwise on $S$:\n(a) [Normal sheet charge] $n\\cdot E_+=\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$ and $n\\times E_+=0$; equivalently $\\sigma=\\varepsilon_0\\,n\\cdot E_+$ and the tangential part of the vacuum-side field vanishes: $E_+$ has no component tangent to $S$. In particular a conductor surface with $\\sigma=0$ at a point has $E_+=0$ there (both traces vanish), and the sign of $\\sigma$ is the sign of the outward normal field component.\n(b) [Normal derivative of the potential] with $\\varphi$ the model's potential and $\\partial\\varphi/\\partial n:=\\nabla\\varphi_+\\cdot n$ the one-sided normal derivative in the direction $n$ from the conductor into the vacuum ([[def-classical-normal-derivative]]), one has\n$$\\frac{\\partial\\varphi}{\\partial n}=-\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad \\sigma=-\\varepsilon_0\\,\\frac{\\partial\\varphi}{\\partial n}.$$\n(c) [Equipotential boundary traces] the trace $\\varphi_+$ of $\\varphi$ on $S$ is constant on each connected component of $S$, with value $V_j$ on every connected component contained in $\\partial D_j$; consequently the boundary value problem in the vacuum region is a Dirichlet problem whose data are the constants $V_j$ on the conductor surfaces.\n(d) [Total charges] the total charge of conductor $j$ is\n$$Q_j=\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\sigma\\,dS=\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial D_j}n\\cdot E_+\\,dS=\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\frac{\\partial\\varphi}{\\partial\\nu}\\,dS,$$\nwhere $\\nu$ is the local outward normal of the vacuum along the smooth conductor interface $S$, without asserting regularity of a general ambient outer boundary (so $\\nu=-n$ on $\\partial D$ and the last equality uses $\\partial\\varphi/\\partial\\nu=-\\partial\\varphi/\\partial n$); the surface integrals exist as integrals of continuous functions over the compact embedded hypersurfaces $\\partial D_j$ ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[lem-em-boundary-of-a-bounded-smooth-domain-is-a-compact-embedded-hypersurface]]).\n(e) [Data reading] the two data types of the model are related through (d): if the component potentials and ambient data are fixed and the equilibrium solution is given, the total charges are determined by (d); prescribing the total charges instead determines the potentials only through the (separate) equilibrium problem, and this theorem asserts no existence or uniqueness for charge data.\n(f) [Scope] The statement is pointwise and stationary: it assumes the one-sided joint regularity above, a $C^\\infty$ conductor surface, and the pure electrostatic regime; it excludes moving surface charge, normal sheet currents, magnetic sheet sources, nonsmooth or cornered conductors, fields without one-sided traces, forces, energy and any measurement; no self-force or material response is asserted.",
          "strategy": "The model supplies $E\\equiv0$ on the conductor side and the source pair $(\\rho_v+\\sigma\\delta_S,0)$ with $K=0$ as the stationary-sheet pairing of [[def-em-weak-sources]](c), and [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]] supplies the adopted weak Maxwell equations; the joint one-sided regularity is the hypothesis of [[pthm-em-fixed-vacuum-jumps]]. Applying that theorem with $\\Omega_-$ the conductor side, $\\Omega_+$ the vacuum side and the chosen normal $n$ (its sign convention is exactly the one of [[def-em-surface-distribution]] fixed in the model) gives, pointwise on $S$, $n\\cdot[E]=\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$ and $n\\times[E]=0$ together with the two magnetic identities. Since $E\\equiv0$ on the conductor side, $[E]=E_+-0=E_+$, proving (a) and the formula $\\sigma=\\varepsilon_0n\\cdot E_+$. For (b): on the vacuum side $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ by the model, and $\\varphi$ is $C^2$ in the vacuum interior, is continuous on $S$, and its gradient has the continuous one-sided trace $-E_+$ by the admitted field regularity; the parallel-graph derivative argument below identifies the actual tangential derivatives of the trace; hence $n\\cdot E_+=-n\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi_+=-\\partial\\varphi/\\partial n$ with the classical normal derivative of [[def-classical-normal-derivative]], and substituting into (a) gives the stated sign. For (c): $n\\times E_+=0$ says precisely that all tangential first derivatives of $\\varphi_+$ vanish at every point of $S$; the trace $t\\mapsto\\varphi_+(t)$ is therefore locally constant on the embedded hypersurface $S$, and a locally constant continuous function on a connected component is constant there ([[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]]; in each graph chart the trace has zero gradient, so [[thm-zero-derivative-on-connected-open-euclidean-set-iff-constant]] makes it constant on the chart, and local constancy on a connected set gives constancy on the whole component). On a component contained in $\\partial D_j$ the model's continuity convention gives the value $V_j$ ([[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]](c)). For (d): the total charge is the surface integral of $\\sigma$, which exists because $\\sigma$ is continuous on the compact $S$ and the surface measure is finite ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[lem-em-boundary-of-a-bounded-smooth-domain-is-a-compact-embedded-hypersurface]]); substituting (a) gives the middle expression, and the last equality is $\\nu=-n$ and $\\partial\\varphi/\\partial\\nu=\\nabla\\varphi_+\\cdot\\nu=-\\nabla\\varphi_+\\cdot n=-\\partial\\varphi/\\partial n$, with the local exterior-side vacuum normal, agreeing with [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]](c) whenever its additional bounded ambient hypothesis holds. No product of distributions is formed, no equation at a corner is asserted, and the result is exactly the pointwise form of the conductor boundary condition under the stated regularity. The potential's gradient trace needed in (b)-(c) is justified by the field trace: $\\nabla\\varphi=-E$ in the vacuum, so its first derivatives extend continuously to the boundary by $-E_+$. In a graph chart, restrict $\\varphi$ to parallel graphs inside the vacuum. On a compact coordinate segment their derivatives are $-E\\cdot\\partial_yX_s$ and converge uniformly to $-E_+\\cdot\\partial_yX_0$ as $s\\downarrow0$; the functions converge to the continuous boundary trace. [[thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval]] proves that this is the actual derivative of the trace, so constancy of the trace and tangential-field cancellation are legitimate without assuming an unprovided ambient extension of the potential.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
