          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice as declared by the cited weak-solvability and trace suppliers ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\Omega_v\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain with finitely many connected components (the vacuum region of the additional bounded $C^\\infty$ ambient branch of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]] by [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]], or any bounded smooth domain), let $\\rho$ be continuous on $\\overline{\\Omega_v}$ (the fixed-time volume source density, C m$^{-3}$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]]), and let $\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2\\in C^2(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ be real potentials of the pure electrostatic regime [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] with fields $E_i:=-\\nabla\\varphi_i$ of [[def-em-fields]] and\n$$-\\Delta\\varphi_i=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0\\qquad\\text{pointwise on }\\Omega_v,\\qquad \\Delta=\\operatorname{div}\\nabla.$$ The Laplacian convention is [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]].\nLet $\\nu$ denote the outward unit normal of $\\Omega_v$ ([[def-classical-normal-derivative]]). Then:\n(a) [Classical Dirichlet uniqueness] if $\\varphi_1=\\varphi_2$ on $\\partial\\Omega_v$, then $\\varphi_1=\\varphi_2$ on $\\overline{\\Omega_v}$, and hence $E_1=E_2$. The conclusion holds component by component; each connected component of a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain is again a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain, to which the connected-domain uniqueness corollary applies.\n(b) [Classical Neumann uniqueness and compatibility] if $\\partial_\\nu\\varphi_1=\\partial_\\nu\\varphi_2$ on $\\partial\\Omega_v$, then $\\varphi_1-\\varphi_2$ is constant on each connected component of $\\Omega_v$, and conversely adding one real constant per component to a solution preserves both the equation and the Neumann data; hence the field $E$ is unique while the potential is determined only up to componentwise constants. Moreover the Neumann data are necessarily compatible:\n$$\\int_{\\Omega_v^{(j)}}\\rho\\,dx+\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_v^{(j)}}\\partial_\\nu\\varphi\\,dS=0\\qquad\\text{for every connected component }\\Omega_v^{(j)}.$$\nThis compatibility is necessary, not sufficient, and is exactly the flux identity of the divergence theorem for the Poisson equation.\n(c) [Weak variants] (i) if $g\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega_v)$ ([[def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary]]) is prescribed as a trace and $\\varphi_1,\\varphi_2\\in H^1(\\Omega_v)$ are weak solutions of the Dirichlet problem with the same trace $g$, then $\\varphi_1=\\varphi_2$; (ii) if the conormal data are prescribed compatibly, weak $H^1$ solutions are unique modulo constants on each connected component, and for connected $\\Omega_v$ this is the normalised solution of [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]]; (iii) [sheet/transmission variant] for the finite disjoint smooth interfaces and outer Dirichlet data of [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]](c), the globally $H^1$ solution is unique, and for compatible outer Neumann data it is unique modulo componentwise constants until the componentwise means are fixed.\n(d) [Physics reading and conductor case] on a star-shaped vacuum domain the curl-free $C^1$ field has an interior $C^2$ potential by [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]]. This alone does not supply closure regularity, $H^1$ membership, a trace, or prescribed boundary data; each application of (a)-(c) additionally requires its stated regularity and identical boundary/source data. In the bounded conductor model, prescribed absolute conductor potentials $V_j$ and absolute outer Dirichlet data determine the potential and field uniquely in the admitted classical or weak class. Grounding fixes the additive constant exactly. If only relative potential differences are prescribed and the outer/reference data are also allowed to shift by the same constant, the common gauge constant is free; merely omitting outer boundary data does not establish field uniqueness.\n(e) [Scope] uniqueness is conditional on the stated boundary or decay data: on an unbounded exterior region without a decay condition uniqueness fails: on $\\Omega_R=\\{x:|x|>R\\}$ the functions $0$ and $u(x)=1-R/|x|$ are two distinct bounded harmonic functions with the same zero boundary trace and different limits at infinity (the witness is verified in the strategy), so the boundary, decay and regularity hypotheses above cannot be dropped. No existence is inferred from uniqueness; the classical branch requires $C^2$ up to the closure, the weak branches carry their own domain, coefficient and trace hypotheses, and no dielectric material coefficient or empirical claim is included. For the admitted conductor class with connected vacuum, fixed absolute conductor potentials and identical outer Neumann data also give exact uniqueness: this is a mixed boundary problem with a nonempty Dirichlet part, so there is no surviving additive constant. Existence for those mixed data is not asserted here. In that weak mixed branch, write $\\mathcal V_D=\\{v\\in H^1(\\Omega_v):Tv=0\\text{ on }\\partial D\\}$. The compared potentials have the same prescribed conductor trace and satisfy $\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\Omega_v}\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi=\\int_{\\Omega_v}\\rho v+\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}g\\,Tv$ for every $v\\in\\mathcal V_D$, with the same smooth outer normal datum $g$.",
          "strategy": "All four statements are read off their suppliers, with the physics conventions made explicit. (a) On each connected component $U$ of $\\Omega_v$, the restricted potentials are $C^2(\\overline U)$ with equal Laplacians $\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and equal boundary traces, so the classical Dirichlet corollary ([[cor-classical-dirichlet-and-poisson-problems-are-unique]] claim 1) gives equality on $\\overline U$; components are bounded by [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]] and inherit the smooth-domain property as in [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]](d). The fields then agree because $E_i=-\\nabla\\varphi_i$ and the gradient is determined by the potential. (b) The same corollary's second claim ([[cor-neumann-solutions-are-unique-modulo-componentwise-constants]]) gives constancy of $\\varphi_1-\\varphi_2$ on each component from equal normal derivatives. The compatibility identity is [[lem-neumann-compatibility-from-the-divergence-theorem]] applied to $u=\\varphi$, $f=-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $g=\\partial_\\nu\\varphi$; its conclusion $\\int f=-\\int g$ is the displayed equation, and the source notes of that lemma record that it is necessary rather than sufficient. (c) (i) is the uniqueness clause of [[lem-em-prescribed-fractional-dirichlet-existence]] (the weak solution with prescribed trace is exactly one); (ii) is the uniqueness-modulo-constants and normalisation clause of [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]] with the trace spaces of [[def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary]]; (iii) is [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]](c) verbatim in its uniqueness clause: outer Dirichlet data give the globally $H^1$ solution uniquely, outer compatible Neumann data give uniqueness modulo componentwise constants until the means are fixed. (d) The radial Poincare construction provides $\\varphi\\in C^2(\\Omega_v)$ in the interior only. Before invoking (a), require the asserted continuous derivatives through order two on the closure and matching classical traces; before invoking (c), require $H^1$ membership, the specified trace/conormal problem, and matching weak right-hand sides. These are independent data/regularity hypotheses. Under the conductor Dirichlet data, the differences of admitted solutions have zero trace on every conductor and the prescribed outer boundary, so (a) or (c)(i) gives zero difference, not an unfixed constant. A common gauge shift preserves a relative-potential problem only when every reference datum is shifted as well. (e) For the failure of uniqueness on unbounded regions, put $\\Omega_R:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:|x|>R\\}$ and $u(x):=1-R/|x|$; then $u\\in C^\\infty(\\Omega_R)$, $u=0$ on $\\{|x|=R\\}$, $0<u<1$ on $\\Omega_R$ and $u(x)\\to1$ as $|x|\\to\\infty$, while $\\Delta u=0$ because $R/|x|$ is the constant $4\\pi R$ times the $n=3$ fundamental solution, harmonic off the origin ([[lem-laplace-fundamental-solution-is-harmonic-off-its-pole]]) with the constant multiple and the constant function having zero Laplacian by linearity ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); hence $0$ and $u$ are two distinct bounded harmonic functions with the same zero boundary trace and different limits at infinity, so the boundary/decay hypotheses in (a)-(c) cannot be dropped. No existence statement is made anywhere: the existence clauses are the separate declared existence result of this page and the cited weak-solvability lemmas, and the compatibility identity is used only in its necessary direction. For the mixed conductor data mentioned in (d), let $w$ be the difference of two admitted solutions. It is harmonic, $w=0$ on the conductor boundary and $\\partial_\\nu w=0$ on the outer boundary. Green's first identity gives $\\int_{\\Omega_v}|\\nabla w|^2=\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_v}w\\partial_\\nu w=0$ because each boundary piece has one zero factor. Thus $w$ is constant on the connected vacuum and its zero trace on the nonempty conductor boundary makes that constant zero. For admitted weak mixed solutions, their difference has zero conductor trace, is an admissible test of the homogeneous mixed difference equation, and the same zero-energy argument applies. This conditional uniqueness requires the displayed boundary/trace class and does not supply a mixed existence theorem. In the weak mixed case the mean-zero inequality [[lem-em-mean-zero-coercivity-on-a-c-one-domain]] turns $\\nabla w=0$ into $w=w_{\\Omega_v}$ as an $H^1$ class. Its constant trace on the nonempty conductor boundary is zero, so the constant is zero; no classical differentiability of a general $H^1$ representative is assumed.",
          "provenance": {
