          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice as declared by the cited solvability theorem ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\Omega_v\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^\\infty$ domain in the sense of [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]] with finitely many connected components (the vacuum region of the additional bounded $C^\\infty$ ambient branch of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]] by [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]], or any bounded smooth domain), let $\\rho$ be smooth on $\\overline{\\Omega_v}$ (fixed-time volume source density, C m$^{-3}$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]]) and let $\\varepsilon_0>0$ be the framework constant. Then the electrostatic boundary value problems on $\\Omega_v$ are solvable in the following exact senses, and the solutions are the fields of the pure electrostatic regime [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] with volume source $\\rho$:\n(a) [Dirichlet branch] for every $g\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega_v)$ there is exactly one $\\varphi\\in C^\\infty(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ with\n$$-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0\\quad\\text{on }\\Omega_v,\\qquad \\varphi|_{\\partial\\Omega_v}=g,$$\nwith $\\Delta$ the Laplacian of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]];\nand it is the unique weak $H^1$ solution with trace $g$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] for the class); the field $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ ([[def-em-fields]]) is $C^\\infty$ on $\\overline{\\Omega_v}$ and $(E,0)$ realises the pure electrostatic regime with source $\\rho$ on the vacuum region.\n(b) [Neumann branch] for every $g\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega_v)$ with\n$$\\int_{\\Omega_v^{(j)}}\\rho\\,dx+\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_v^{(j)}}g\\,dS=0\\qquad\\text{for every connected component }\\Omega_v^{(j)}\\text{ of }\\Omega_v,$$\nthere is $\\varphi\\in C^\\infty(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ with $-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and $\\partial_\\nu\\varphi=g$ (outward normal $\\nu$, [[def-classical-normal-derivative]]), unique up to one additive constant per connected component; the normalisation $\\int_{\\Omega_v^{(j)}}\\varphi\\,dx=0$ for every $j$ selects exactly one. The compatibility is also necessary in the sense of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](b).\n(c) [Conductor reading and surface-charge realization] for the separated compact-body conductor geometry and zero-interior-volume convention of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]], additionally assuming $\\Omega$ is a bounded $C^\\infty$ ambient domain, with outer boundary data $g_0\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega)$ on $\\partial\\Omega$ and constants $V_j$ on the conductor surfaces $\\partial D_j$ (constant functions are smooth on the compact disjoint boundary pieces), the combined datum is a special case of (a); the field $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ on $\\Omega_v$ extended by $E:=0$ on the conductor satisfies the adopted distributional vacuum Maxwell equations [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]] with the source pair $(\\rho_v+\\sigma\\,\\delta_{\\partial D},\\,0)$, where $\\sigma:=\\varepsilon_0\\,n\\cdot E_+\\in C^\\infty(\\partial D)$ is the constructed continuous surface-charge density, agreeing afterwards with [[pthm-em-conductor-surface-charge]] ([[def-em-weak-sources]](c), [[def-em-surface-distribution]], [[lem-em-interface-derivatives]]); the total charges $Q_j=\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial D_j}n\\cdot E_+\\,dS$ ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]) are thereby determined by the data, and $\\varphi$ is constant on each $\\partial D_j$ with value $V_j$.\n(d) [Stationary sheet charge in the vacuum region] for finitely many disjoint closed smooth interfaces $\\Gamma$ strictly inside $\\Omega_v$, a smooth sheet density $\\sigma_\\Gamma$ on them, and outer Dirichlet data $g\\in C^\\infty(\\partial\\Omega_v)$, there is a weak solution $\\varphi\\in H^1(\\Omega_v)$ which is $C^\\infty$ on the closure of each side of every $\\Gamma$, unique by [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](c)(iii), solving the weak equation with right-hand side $\\int_{\\Omega_v}\\rho v+\\sum_\\Gamma\\int_\\Gamma\\sigma_\\Gamma\\,Tv$; its flux jump is $n\\cdot[\\varepsilon_0\\nabla\\varphi]=-\\sigma_\\Gamma$, equivalently $n\\cdot[E]=\\sigma_\\Gamma/\\varepsilon_0$ in the sign convention of [[pthm-em-fixed-vacuum-jumps]]. This is the vacuum ($\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_0$) transmission branch; no dielectric coefficient is introduced.\n(e) [Scope] all existence statements are the ones proved in [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]] for its stated smooth bounded domains, smooth uniformly positive coefficients (here $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_0 I$), smooth data and finitely many disjoint smooth interfaces; the coefficient is the smooth positive constant $\\varepsilon_0I$ on every side in the transmission clause. Excluded: nonsmooth boundaries, corners, edges, coefficient loss of regularity, prescribed total charges for conductor bodies (the free equipotential problem), unbounded exterior regions without a stated decay class, dielectric material response (belonging to the material-response page) and any measurement, apparatus or empirical claim. The theorem asserts existence of the potential, not of a physical equilibrium; the ideal conductor is the adopted model of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]].",
          "strategy": "The conductor application in (c) explicitly uses the additional bounded $C^\\infty$ ambient branch, not the general open ambient model. The proof is a careful application of [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]] to the vacuum geometry, combined with the framework's distributional reading.\n(a) Take $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_0 I$, which is smooth on $\\overline{\\Omega_v}$ and uniformly positive; the theorem's clause (a) gives exactly one weak solution with trace $g$, $C^\\infty$ on the closure, with $-\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon_0\\nabla\\varphi)=-\\varepsilon_0\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho$ pointwise and the prescribed boundary values. Dividing by $\\varepsilon_0>0$ gives the displayed equation; the field $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ is $C^\\infty$ with $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$, so the static reduction of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](c) holds with $\\partial_t=0$ and the pair $(E,0)$ is a pure electrostatic model. (b) The theorem's clause (b) with conormal datum $\\varepsilon_0g$, since $\\nu\\cdot\\varepsilon_0\\nabla\\varphi=\\varepsilon_0\\partial_\\nu\\varphi=\\varepsilon_0g$ gives a weak solution exactly under the displayed compatibility, unique modulo componentwise constants, normalised by the mean conditions; the classical upgrade to $C^\\infty$ on the closure is the same clause. On each connected component, the weak identity tested with the constant $1$ reproduces the compatibility, matching [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](b). (c) The datum that is $g_0$ on $\\partial\\Omega$ and $V_j$ on $\\partial D_j$ is a function on the disjoint union $\\partial\\Omega_v=\\partial\\Omega\\sqcup\\partial D$ restricting to a smooth function on each piece, hence smooth on the boundary manifold; clause (a) applies with this $g$ and gives $\\varphi\\in C^\\infty(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ with $-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho_v/\\varepsilon_0$ (the volume source is $\\rho_v$). Define $\\sigma:=\\varepsilon_0 n\\cdot E_+\\in C^\\infty(\\partial D)$; the solution has constant trace $V_j$ on $\\partial D_j$ because it solves the Dirichlet problem. Extend the potential by the constant $V_j$ inside $D_j$; the resulting function is continuous across each boundary. The tangential derivatives of the vacuum trace are zero: pull back the smooth up-to-boundary solution to a boundary graph and use the chain rule (or the uniform derivative-limit argument for parallel interior graphs). Therefore $E_+\\cdot\\tau=-\\partial_\\tau\\varphi=0$ for every tangent vector $\\tau$, and $n\\times E_+=0$ is derived directly from the boundary datum. The density is defined by the displayed normal trace, before using any theorem that assumes a completed conductor model. Extend the field by zero on $D$ and let $\\widetilde E$ denote the extension; on the open sets $\\Omega_v$ and $D$ it is respectively $C^\\infty$ and zero, so it has $C^\\infty$ one-sided traces across the compact embedded interface $\\partial D$ with normal $n$, and [[lem-em-interface-derivatives]] gives, distributionally, $\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde E=\\{\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde E\\}_{\\text{piecewise}}+n\\cdot[\\widetilde E]\\,\\delta_{S}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E=\\{\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E\\}_{\\text{piecewise}}+n\\times[\\widetilde E]\\,\\delta_S$ with $S=\\partial D$. The piecewise divergence is $\\rho_v/\\varepsilon_0$ on $\\Omega_v$ and $0$ on $D$; the piecewise curl is zero on both sides; $[\\widetilde E]=E_+$ and $n\\cdot E_+=\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$, $n\\times E_+=0$ by the constant-trace argument just proved. Hence $\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde E=(\\rho_v+\\sigma\\delta_S)/\\varepsilon_0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$, with the sheet pairing of [[def-em-weak-sources]](c) and the surface conventions of [[def-em-surface-distribution]]; together with $B=0$ and $J=0$ these are the four adopted equations [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]] on the (time-independent) laboratory region, by the static dictionary of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]. The total charges follow by integrating the continuous density over the compact hypersurfaces ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]). (d) This is [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]](c) with the constant coefficient $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_0 I$, piecewise smooth bulk data and smooth sheet charge; the equal one-sided traces, one-sided smoothness and the jump sign are as stated there, and the identification with the framework's jump convention is [[pthm-em-fixed-vacuum-jumps]] ($n$ from the minus to the plus side). (e) records the exact hypotheses and the exclusions; no existence claim is made beyond the cited theorem, and in particular the free charge-data conductor problem and the exterior problem are left open. The extended potential, the zero interior field, the constructed sheet density and the verified distributional equations now satisfy the bounded conductor model; only after this construction may [[pthm-em-conductor-surface-charge]] be invoked as a consequence. For (d) the coefficient form is explicitly $\\varepsilon_0\\int\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi=\\int\\rho v+\\sum_\\Gamma\\int_\\Gamma\\sigma_\\Gamma Tv$; hence bulk and sheet data are not multiplied by $\\varepsilon_0$, whereas a prescribed outward normal datum $g$ corresponds to conormal datum $\\varepsilon_0g$. Both the sheet jump and all source factors follow from this same form.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
