          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) inherited from the Newtonian, polar and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$$H:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:x_3>0\\},\\qquad \\Pi:=\\{x_3=0\\},\\qquad p:=a e_3\\ (a>0),$$\nlet $q\\in\\mathbb R$ be a charge parameter (coulombs) and $\\varepsilon_0>0$ the framework constant ([[def-em-si-conventions]]). Define, with arbitrary values on the exceptional sets,\n$$\\varphi_I(x):=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac1{|x-p|}-\\frac1{|x+ae_3|}\\right)\\quad(x\\in H\\setminus\\{p\\}),\\qquad E_I:=-\\nabla\\varphi_I.$$ The map $E_I$ is a spatial field of [[def-em-fields]] in the fixed-time reading, with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]].\nThen:\n(a) [Candidate field] $\\varphi_I\\in C^\\infty(H\\setminus\\{p\\})$ and $\\Delta\\varphi_I=0$ pointwise on $H\\setminus\\{p\\}$: the first term is the Coulomb potential of $p$, whose $n=3$ fundamental solution is harmonic off its pole ([[lem-laplace-fundamental-solution-is-harmonic-off-its-pole]], [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d)), and the second term is $C^\\infty$ and harmonic on the whole of $H$ because its pole $-ae_3$ lies outside $H$; $E_I$ is $C^\\infty$ on $H\\setminus\\{p\\}$.\n(b) [Distributional point source] $\\varphi_I\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(H)$ and\n$$-\\Delta T_{\\varphi_I}=\\frac{q}{\\varepsilon_0}\\,\\delta_p\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(H),$$\nwhere $T_{\\varphi_I}$ is the regular distribution of $\\varphi_I$ ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]) and $\\delta_p$ the Dirac distribution at $p$ ([[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]); moreover the field satisfies $\\operatorname{div}E_I=(q/\\varepsilon_0)\\delta_p$ and $\\operatorname{curl}E_I=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(H)$, where the divergence and curl of $E_I$ are the distributional derivatives of its components ([[def-distributional-derivative]]). Thus $(E_I,0)$ is a pure electrostatic model on $H$ with the fixed-time point source $q\\delta_p$, the static reading of the stationary point charge of [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](e)).\n(c) [Boundary trace and decay] $\\varphi_I$ extends continuously to $\\overline H\\setminus\\{p\\}$ with $\\varphi_I=0$ on $\\Pi$ (the two denominators are equal there), and for $|x|\\ge2a$ the cancellation bound holds:\n$$|\\varphi_I(x)|\\le\\frac{2|q|\\,a}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x|^2}.$$\nThe bound follows by applying the mean value theorem along the segment from $-ae_3$ to $ae_3$ to the function $y\\mapsto|x-y|^{-1}$, whose gradient is bounded by $(|x|-a)^{-2}$ there ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). In particular $\\varphi_I(x)\\to0$ as $|x|\\to\\infty$ in $H$.\n(d) [Uniqueness in the declared class] let $\\psi:H\\setminus\\{p\\}\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^2$ and harmonic on $H\\setminus\\{p\\}$, continuous on $\\overline H\\setminus\\{p\\}$, with $\\psi=0$ on $\\Pi$, $|x-p|(\\psi(x)-\\varphi_I(x))\\to0$ as $x\\to p$ and $\\psi(x)\\to0$ as $|x|\\to\\infty$ in $H$. Then $\\psi=\\varphi_I$ on $H\\setminus\\{p\\}$. Equivalently, the Dirichlet problem $\\Delta\\psi=0$ on $H\\setminus\\{p\\}$, $\\psi=0$ on $\\Pi$, with the leading singularity of the point charge at $p$ and decay at infinity, has exactly one solution in the stated class.\n(e) [Physics reading: ideal grounded plane in the fixed-time reading of [[def-em-lab-geometry]]] the ideal grounded conducting half-space $\\{x_3<0\\}$ with the field $E_I$ on $H$, $E\\equiv0$ below $\\Pi$ and the induced density $\\sigma:=\\varepsilon_0\\,n\\cdot E_+|_\\Pi=-\\varepsilon_0\\,\\partial_3\\varphi_I|_\\Pi$ (using the normal $n=e_3$ from the conductor into the vacuum; $\\sigma\\in C^\\infty(\\Pi)$) is a model of the same equilibrium structure as [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]]: it is a pure electrostatic field on $H$ with the point source at $p$ and the equipotential boundary condition $\\varphi_I=0$ on $\\Pi$; the pair $(E_I,0)$ satisfies the static electrostatic equations with source $q\\delta_p$ on $H$, and the induced density is explicitly $\\sigma(x_1,x_2)=-qa/[2\\pi(x_1^2+x_2^2+a^2)^{3/2}]$, is absolutely integrable over the plane, and has total charge $\\int_\\Pi\\sigma\\,dS=-q$. However, the half-space conductor is not a compact conductor body: the plane is not a compact embedded hypersurface, $\\Pi$ and $\\{x_3<0\\}$ are not among the geometric objects of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]], the density $\\sigma$ is not a declared stationary-sheet source of [[def-em-weak-sources]](c), and the noncompact plane pairing is not an adopted compact-sheet source of that definition. As a separately specified mathematical idealization, extending $E_I$ by zero below the plane gives $\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde E=(q\\delta_p+\\sigma\\delta_\\Pi)/\\varepsilon_0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E=0$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, where $(\\sigma\\delta_\\Pi)(\\eta)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\sigma(x')\\eta(x',0)dx'$. This verified identity does not extend the framework postulate to arbitrary noncompact sheets. The clause is the explicitly declared idealization whose exact mathematical content is (a)-(d).\n(f) [Scope] the statement concerns the ideal point charge above an ideal grounded plane in the declared decay class; it excludes finite-plane edge effects, conductor thickness, transients, moving charges, radiation, self-force and any measurement; no uniqueness is asserted outside the class of (d), and the point-source singularity is never evaluated.",
          "strategy": "(a) $|x-p|^{-1}$ is the $n=3$ fundamental solution kernel, harmonic off its pole, and $|x+ae_3|^{-1}$ is $C^\\infty$ on $H$ because $|x+ae_3|\\ge a>0$ there; sums of harmonic functions are harmonic by linearity of second derivatives ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and $\\nabla\\varphi_I$ is computed off the pole by the chain and power rules ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). (b) Local integrability: $|x-p|^{-1}$ is locally integrable in dimension three and $|x+ae_3|^{-1}$ is continuous, so both have locally integrable components and the sum does (measurability is [[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]]) ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]); the identification with regular distributions is [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]] and [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]. The distributional Laplacian of the first term is $(q/\\varepsilon_0)\\delta_p$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ and hence on the open subset $H$ ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d), [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]), while the second term $u(x):=-(q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0))|x+ae_3|^{-1}$ is $C^\\infty$ on $H$ with $\\Delta u=0$ classically; for every compactly supported test $\\eta\\in\\mathcal D(H)$ and every coordinate interval containing its support, [[thm-integration-by-parts]] gives $\\langle\\partial_iT_u,\\eta\\rangle=-\\int u\\,\\partial_i\\eta=\\int(\\partial_iu)\\eta$, so $\\partial_iT_u=T_{\\partial_iu}$; applying the same identity to the $C^\\infty$ function $\\partial_iu$ gives $\\partial_i\\partial_iT_u=T_{\\partial_i\\partial_iu}$, and summing gives $\\Delta T_u=T_{\\Delta u}=0$ as the regular distribution of the zero function ([[def-distribution]], [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]). The field is exactly the difference of the two point fields $E_q^p-E_q^{-p}$ restricted to $H$. Each component of the first field is locally integrable and represents $-\\partial_iT_{\\varphi_q^p}$ by [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d), not merely by potential integrability; the second point field is smooth on $H$ and satisfies the same identity there. Thus every field component is locally integrable, $T_{E_{I,i}}=-\\partial_iT_{\\varphi_I}$, and the divergence and curl identities follow from the point-field supplier and [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]]. The first and second pole kernels and their gradients have radial absolute integrals $2\\pi b^2$ and $4\\pi b$ on radius-$b$ balls, respectively, by [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], confirming local integrability. The reading of $q\\delta_p$ as the fixed-time point source is the dictionary of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]](e) and [[def-em-weak-sources]](b) on the half-space; the pair $(E_I,0)$ is then a pure electrostatic model there. (c) On $\\Pi$ the two distances $|x-p|$ and $|x+ae_3|$ are equal, so the bracket vanishes; continuity on $\\overline H\\setminus\\{p\\}$ is continuity of the two kernels away from their poles. For the bound, write $f(y):=|x-y|^{-1}$ and apply the mean value theorem to $f$ on the segment from $-ae_3$ to $ae_3$: $|f(ae_3)-f(-ae_3)|\\le 2a\\sup_{|y|\\le a}|\\nabla f(y)|$ with $|\\nabla f(y)|=|x-y|^{-2}\\le(|x|-a)^{-2}$ ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]]); for $r=|x|\\ge2a$ the gradient is at most $4/r^2$, so the endpoint difference is at most $(2a)(4/r^2)=8a/r^2$; multiplication by $|q|/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$ gives exactly the displayed bound $2|q|a/(\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^2)$. (d) Apply [[lem-em-half-space-harmonic-uniqueness-with-decay]] to $w=\\psi-\\varphi_I$: both terms are $C^2$ harmonic on $H\\setminus\\{p\\}$ and continuous on $\\overline H\\setminus\\{p\\}$, the trace on $\\Pi$ vanishes because both do, and the difference has sub-Coulomb growth at $p$ and tends to zero at infinity by the bound of (c) and the hypothesis on $\\psi$. The lemma yields $w=0$. (e)-(f) record the reading and scope: the geometric and source-theoretic exclusions are exactly those of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]] and [[def-em-weak-sources]](c) (noncompact plane), The additional full-space plane identity is proved below as a separate mathematical idealization, without changing those physical source-type exclusions. The choice cost is the declared $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of the cited integral and distribution interfaces. Differentiate the two smooth kernels at $x_3=0$: $\\partial_3\\varphi_I=q a/[2\\pi\\varepsilon_0(r^2+a^2)^{3/2}]$, $r^2=x_1^2+x_2^2$, and tangential derivatives vanish since the boundary potential is identically zero. Hence $\\sigma=-qa/[2\\pi(r^2+a^2)^{3/2}]$, smooth even at $r=0$. Plane polar integration gives $\\int_\\Pi|\\sigma|=|q|a\\int_0^\\infty r(r^2+a^2)^{-3/2}dr=|q|$ and $\\int_\\Pi\\sigma=-q$, using the primitive $-(r^2+a^2)^{-1/2}$ on finite intervals, [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] and monotone convergence. Thus the sheet pairing is an order-zero distribution bounded by $|q|\\|\\eta\\|_\\infty$. For the full-space identity set $R=\\widetilde E-E_q^p$. In $H$ it equals $-E_q^{-p}$ and below the plane it equals $-E_q^p$; both pieces are smooth through their closed half-spaces, with zero classical divergence and curl. For a compact test, coordinate integration by parts and Fubini give $\\partial_iT_{R_j}=T_{\\{\\partial_iR_j\\}}+\\delta_{i3}[R_j]\\delta_\\Pi$: all products are absolutely integrable because the fields and derivatives are bounded on the compact half-space pieces and the test has bounded compact support. Tangential boundary terms vanish and the two vertical endpoint terms give the displayed jump. Here $[R]=E_q^p-E_q^{-p}=E_I|_\\Pi$, whose normal component is $\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$ and tangential component is zero. Adding the full-space point-field identities yields precisely the asserted divergence and zero curl of $\\widetilde E$. The product integrands are Borel; the exact product-measure identification and finite continuous integral comparison are [[thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel]], [[thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets]] and [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]]. This noncompact-plane calculation is a local test identity, not a use of the compact-sheet physical postulate. For $q=0$ every field, potential and induced charge vanishes. The plane has three-dimensional Lebesgue measure zero by [[prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null]], so values assigned to the field on it do not change regular distributions. On $H$, the point-source physical reading uses the separate adoption [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]] for the stationary point source. Its four pairings hold by the computed spatial identities and the static dictionary. This adoption on the declared point-source class is distinct from the merely mathematical noncompact-plane pairing on all of $\\mathbb R^3$.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 3.2.1-3.2.2 printed pp. 121-123 (the classic image problem, potential (3.9), induced surface charge (3.10) and total induced charge -q (3.11))."
