          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice for the cited unit-Dirichlet solvability and trace interfaces, and its Countable Choice consequence for surface and integral interfaces ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $(\\Omega;D_1,\\dots,D_m;\\Omega_v)$ be the conductor geometry of [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]], additionally with $\\Omega$ a bounded $C^\\infty$ ambient domain and $\\Omega_v$ connected and with the **outer boundary $\\partial\\Omega$ grounded**: the data on $\\partial\\Omega$ are the constant Dirichlet datum $0$; let $\\rho_v\\equiv0$ on $\\Omega_v$ (the pure capacitor configuration: no volume source), and let the framework constants be those of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. For each $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ let $\\varphi_j\\in C^\\infty(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ be the unit-potential solution of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-existence]](c) with the smooth data\n$$\\varphi_j|_{\\partial D_k}=\\delta_{jk},\\qquad \\varphi_j|_{\\partial\\Omega}=0,$$\nso that $-\\Delta\\varphi_j=0$ on $\\Omega_v$ with the Laplacian of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], and let $\\nu$ be the outward normal of $\\Omega_v$ (so on $\\partial D_j$ the normal $\\nu$ points into the conductor). Define the **capacitance matrix** by\n$$C_{jk}:=\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\frac{\\partial\\varphi_k}{\\partial\\nu}\\,dS,\\qquad j,k=1,\\dots,m,$$\nthe flux integrals of continuous functions over the compact embedded hypersurfaces $\\partial D_j$ (normal derivative of [[def-classical-normal-derivative]]) ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]]). Then:\n(a) [Linearity and matrix form] for every $V=(V_1,\\dots,V_m)\\in\\mathbb R^m$ the unique equilibrium potential with conductor potentials $V$ and grounded outer boundary is $\\varphi^V=\\sum_{j=1}^mV_j\\varphi_j$, and the conductor charges of [[pthm-em-conductor-surface-charge]](d) are\n$$Q_j(V)=\\varepsilon_0\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\frac{\\partial\\varphi^V}{\\partial\\nu}\\,dS=\\sum_{k=1}^mC_{jk}V_k,\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad Q=CV.$$\n(b) [Reciprocity] $C$ is symmetric: $C_{jk}=C_{kj}$ for all $j,k$; the symmetry is Green's symmetric pairing of the harmonic potentials $\\varphi_j,\\varphi_k$.\n(c) [Energy identity] the field energy of the configuration is\n$$U(V)=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\Omega_v}|\\nabla\\varphi^V|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac12V^{T}CV=\\frac12\\sum_{j=1}^mV_jQ_j(V),$$\nand $U(V)>0$ for every $V\\ne0$: $C$ is symmetric positive definite. In particular $U(V)\\ge0$, with equality exactly for $V=0$, so every nonzero conductor-potential data set stores positive field energy.\n(d) [Two-conductor reading] with only conductor $1$ driven and every other conductor grounded, $V=te_1$: $Q_1=C_{11}t$, $Q_j=C_{j1}t$ for $j\\ne1$, and $U=\\tfrac12C_{11}t^2=\\tfrac12Q_1V_1=Q_1^2/(2C_{11})$; the **capacitance of the arrangement** at conductor $1$ with respect to the grounded reference is the positive geometric number $C_{11}>0$, with $C_{11}=Q_1/V_1$ when $t=V_1\\ne0$, and the energy identities $U=\\tfrac12QV=Q^2/(2C)=\\tfrac12CV^2$ hold with $C=C_{11}$, $Q=Q_1$, $V=V_1$. By (c) the positivity holds for every diagonal entry, $C_{jj}>0$.\n(e) [Assembly reading] use the family $V_j(s)=sV_j$, $\\varphi_s=s\\varphi^V$, $Q_j(s)=sQ_j(V)$ for $0\\le s\\le1$, with fixed geometry and grounded reference. Prescribe the quasistatic external charge-transfer work by $dW_{\\rm ext}=\\sum_jV_j(s)dQ_j(s)$. Then $W_{\\rm ext}=\\int_0^1s\\,ds\\sum_jV_jQ_j(V)=U$. The potentials vary during assembly; only the final potentials and geometry are fixed. This derives the sheet-charge accounting directly and does not apply the volume-source theorem [[pthm-em-electrostatic-field-energy]] to singular sheets. The family is an accounting prescription, not an asserted time-dependent Maxwell/material solution.\n(f) [Scope] the theorem assumes: the compact separated conductor geometry, the additional bounded smooth ambient domain, and connected vacuum region of the model, the grounded outer boundary $\\partial\\Omega$, zero volume source, and the existence and uniqueness of the unit-potential Dirichlet solutions ([[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-existence]](c), [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](a)). It asserts no solvability for arbitrary boundary data (the existence theorem is used only for the constant data above), no prescribed-total-charge problem, no dielectric or material response, no finite-conductivity or transient charging dynamics, no radiation, and no measurement; the matrix $C$ is a geometric object of the ideal electrostatic model, and the ideal parallel-plate and similar formulas are statements about this model only.",
          "strategy": "Use the additional bounded smooth ambient hypothesis with the separated compact body closures to obtain a bounded smooth vacuum by [[lem-em-vacuum-region-of-conductors-is-a-bounded-smooth-domain]]. The general open ambient branch alone supplies no bounded Dirichlet solver. Existence of each $\\varphi_j$: the pure electrostatic regime is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] with fields [[def-em-fields]]; the datum that is $\\delta_{jk}$ on $\\partial D_k$ and $0$ on $\\partial\\Omega$ is constant, hence smooth, on each compact boundary piece of the disjoint union $\\partial\\Omega_v$, so [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-existence]](c) gives $\\varphi_j\\in C^\\infty(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with $-\\Delta\\varphi_j=0$ and those boundary values. (a) Linearity: $\\sum_jV_j\\varphi_j$ is a $C^\\infty(\\overline{\\Omega_v})$ function with $-\\Delta\\bigl(\\sum_jV_j\\varphi_j\\bigr)=0$ and boundary values $V_k$ on $\\partial D_k$, $0$ on $\\partial\\Omega$; by the Dirichlet uniqueness of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](a) it is the unique equilibrium potential with those data, and the charges are obtained by substituting into the flux formula of [[pthm-em-conductor-surface-charge]](d): the integral is linear in $\\varphi$, giving $Q_j(V)=\\sum_kC_{jk}V_k$. (b) Green's first identity on the bounded $C^1$ domain $\\Omega_v$ ([[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]], applied twice and subtracted) gives, for the harmonic $\\varphi_j,\\varphi_k$,\n$$0=\\int_{\\Omega_v}\\bigl(\\varphi_j\\Delta\\varphi_k-\\varphi_k\\Delta\\varphi_j\\bigr)d\\lambda_3=\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_v}\\bigl(\\varphi_j\\partial_\\nu\\varphi_k-\\varphi_k\\partial_\\nu\\varphi_j\\bigr)dS;$$\nthe outer boundary contributes zero because both potentials vanish there, and on the conductor surfaces $\\varphi_j=\\delta_{j\\ell}$, so the sum over components $D_\\ell$ reduces to $\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\partial_\\nu\\varphi_k-\\int_{\\partial D_k}\\partial_\\nu\\varphi_j$, i.e. $C_{jk}=C_{kj}$ after multiplying by $\\varepsilon_0$. (c) Apply Green's first identity once more with $u=v=\\varphi^V$:\n$$\\int_{\\Omega_v}\\bigl(\\varphi^V\\Delta\\varphi^V+|\\nabla\\varphi^V|^2\\bigr)d\\lambda_3=\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_v}\\varphi^V\\partial_\\nu\\varphi^V\\,dS.$$\nThe Laplacian term vanishes, the outer boundary contributes $0$, and on $\\partial D_j$ the trace of $\\varphi^V$ is the constant $V_j$, so the boundary integral equals $\\sum_jV_j\\int_{\\partial D_j}\\partial_\\nu\\varphi^VdS=\\sum_jV_jQ_j(V)/\\varepsilon_0$ by [[pthm-em-conductor-surface-charge]](d); multiplying by $\\varepsilon_0/2$ gives $U(V)=\\tfrac12V^TCV$. Positivity: $\\nabla\\varphi^V$ is continuous on the compact $\\overline{\\Omega_v}$, hence Borel and bounded, and $|\\nabla\\varphi^V|^2$ is integrable with finite integral ([[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]); $U(V)\\ge0$ because the integrand is nonnegative. If $U(V)=0$ then $\\int|\\nabla\\varphi^V|^2=0$, hence $\\nabla\\varphi^V=0$ $\\lambda_3$-almost everywhere by [[thm-nonnegative-integral-zero-iff-zero-almost-everywhere]], hence $\\nabla\\varphi^V=0$ everywhere by continuity, hence $\\varphi^V$ is constant on the connected open set $\\Omega_v$ ([[thm-zero-derivative-on-connected-open-euclidean-set-iff-constant]]); the constant is $0$ because $\\varphi^V=0$ on the nonempty boundary $\\partial\\Omega$ and $\\varphi^V$ is continuous on the closure; finally $V_j=\\varphi^V|_{\\partial D_j}=0$ for every $j$. Thus $C$ is positive definite and in particular symmetric with positive diagonal. (d) is the case $V=te_1$ of (a) and (c): $Q_1=C_{11}t$, $U=\\tfrac12C_{11}t^2=\\tfrac12Q_1V_1$, and eliminating $t=Q_1/C_{11}$ gives $U=Q_1^2/(2C_{11})$; $C_{11}>0$ is (c) with $V=e_1$. (e) Linearity gives $\\varphi_s=s\\varphi^V$, $V(s)=sV$, $Q(s)=sQ(V)$ and hence the surface density scales by $s$. Moving an infinitesimal charge $dQ_j$ from the grounded reference at zero potential to conductor $j$ has electric-force work $-V_j(s)dQ_j$, so the balancing quasistatic external contribution is $+V_j(s)dQ_j$. Prescribing this charge-transfer accounting and summing gives $\\sum_jV_j(s)dQ_j(s)=s\\,ds\\sum_jV_jQ_j(V)$. Integrate $s$ from zero to one to obtain $W_{\\rm ext}=\\tfrac12\\sum_jV_jQ_j(V)=U$. All signs of charges and voltages are admitted. The reference is grounded throughout but the driven potentials vary with $s$; external support is assumed. This is derived for the surface charges and not imported from the smooth volume-source hypothesis of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-field-energy]]. (f) lists the hypotheses: only constant boundary data are solved, so no claim of arbitrary-boundary solvability or of charge-data solvability is made, and no material, radiative or empirical content is introduced. For (d), handle $t=0$ first: $\\varphi^V=0$, all charges vanish and $U=0$, while $C_{11}$ is still defined by its unit-potential flux and is strictly positive. Only for $t\\ne0$ is the ratio $Q_1/V_1$ formed. Full AC is inherited solely through the unit Dirichlet/trace solvability suppliers; all subsequent finite linearity and energy calculations add no stronger assumption. The continuous nonnegative energy density can vanish almost everywhere only by vanishing everywhere in the open vacuum: a nonzero value would stay bounded below on a small Euclidean ball of positive measure ([[lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure]]). The endpoint work calculation uses the known global potential directly: [[thm-gradient-theorem-for-line-integrals]] gives $\\int_\\gamma E_s\\cdot dr=\\varphi_s({\\rm start})-\\varphi_s({\\rm end})$ on each interior piecewise $C^1$ path, and boundary endpoints are limits using the continuous closure traces. It does not apply the star-shaped existence hypotheses of the earlier electrostatic-work theorem to a conductor vacuum with holes. The charge-transfer family is prescribed bookkeeping, and no actual particle/source dynamics is inferred.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.5.4 printed pp. 103-106: two conductors with charges +Q and -Q, the definition C = Q/V (2.53), the geometric nature and positivity of C, Example 2.10 parallel plates C = epsilon_0 A/d and Example 2.11 concentric spheres, and the charging energy Q^2/2C."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (Lent Term 2015, 236 PDF pages)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.4.1 printed pp. 32-33: parallel-plate capacitor with E = sigma/epsilon_0 z-hat, V = Qd/(A epsilon_0), C = A epsilon_0/d and stored energy U = Q^2/2C; and the concentric-sphere capacitor. Section 2.4.2 printed pp. 33-34 (fixed-potential conductors and the boundary value problem solved for them)."
              },
              {
                "title": "Weng Cho Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory (Purdue University, Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 24.4 printed p. 263: the capacitance as the proportionality constant Q = CV; Section 3.4 printed pp. 37-38 (the parallel-plate boundary value problem with prescribed boundary potentials)."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Capacitance and stored electrostatic energy for a specified linear ideal-conductor geometry in a named inertial frame: with the additional bounded smooth ambient domain, a connected vacuum region, a grounded outer boundary and no volume source, the charge-potential map of the conductor bodies is linear with a symmetric positive definite capacitance matrix, the field energy equals one half of the pairing of potentials and charges, and for a single driven conductor the usual two-terminal relations and positivity of the capacitance hold. The item is conditional on the smooth solvability theorem and asserts no arbitrary-boundary or charge-data solvability, no material response, no transient charging dynamics, no radiation and no empirical claim.",
          "dependency_level": 20
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 331
    },
    {
      "id": "em-electrostatic-boundaries-and-conductors-examples",
