            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-metric-ball": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Countable Choice convention of [[pthm-em-image-method-certificate]] and [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] ([[def-countable-choice]]). In the ideal grounded-plane configuration of [[pthm-em-image-method-certificate]]: vacuum half-space $H=\\{x_3>0\\}$, point charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ at $p=ae_3$ ($a>0$), field $E_I$ given by the image potential, and $E_I$ has the Coulomb singularity $(q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0))(x-p)/|x-p|^3$ of the source charge as $x\\to p$ ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]](d), [[def-em-fields]]). Split the field near $p$ as\n$$E_I(x)=E_q(x)+E_{\\rm ext}(x),\\qquad E_q(x):=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{x-p}{|x-p|^3}\\quad(x\\ne p),$$\nthe **self-field** $E_q$ of the source charge and the **external (mirror) field** $E_{\\rm ext}:=E_I-E_q$, which is $C^\\infty$ on a neighbourhood of $p$ (it is minus the gradient of the smooth mirror term $-q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)|x+ae_3|^{-1}$). Then:\n(a) [External field at the charge] the external field at the location of the charge is\n$$E_{\\rm ext}(p)=-\\frac{q}{16\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2}\\,e_3 .$$\nIt is the gradient of the mirror term evaluated at $p$ ([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).\n(b) [Force on the held charge] under the adopted external Lorentz coupling [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](i) at zero velocity, a test particle of charge $q$ held at $p$ experiences the electric force\n$$F=q\\,E_{\\rm ext}(p)=-\\frac{q^2}{16\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2}\\,e_3,$$\nstrictly attractive toward the plane when $q\\ne0$ and zero when $q=0$, of magnitude $q^2/(16\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2)$; the particle's own field is excluded from the coupling by the postulate, so the result is a two-body-style external force of the mirror field and is not a self-force.\n(c) [Reading and scope] for either nonzero sign of $q$ the force is attractive (the magnitude depends on $q^2$), while $q=0$ gives zero force; the induced total plane charge is $-q$ and its distribution is concentrated under the charge; the example is the exact ideal-model result for a held point charge, an ideal grounded plane and the adopted static external force law. It asserts no measurement, no dynamics of the charge (no motion or radiation), no finite-plane or finite-conductor correction, no self-force and no claim that the pair of idealisations is realised by an apparatus; the source is the idealised stationary point charge of [[pthm-em-image-method-certificate]](e), not a declared compact-source model. Here the displayed force is the electric contribution at rest. Holding the charge at $p$ requires a balancing support force $F_{\\rm support}=-F$; the held trajectory is not asserted to solve the equation with the electric Lorentz force as its sole force.",
          "strategy": "Both $E_I$ and $E_q$ are gradients off $p$; subtracting, $E_{\\rm ext}=-\\nabla u$ with $u(x):=-\\dfrac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\dfrac{1}{|x+ae_3|}$, which is $C^\\infty$ on the ball $|x-p|<a$ because $|x+ae_3|\\ge a>0$ there ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). Differentiating $u$ by the chain and power rules gives $\\nabla u(x)=+\\dfrac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\dfrac{x+ae_3}{|x+ae_3|^3}$ for $x$ in that ball; at $x=p=ae_3$ this is $\\dfrac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\dfrac{2ae_3}{(2a)^3}=\\dfrac{q\\,e_3}{16\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2}$, and $E_{\\rm ext}(p)=-\\nabla u(p)=-\\dfrac{q}{16\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2}e_3$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]], [[def-metric-ball]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). This proves (a). For (b): [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](i) adopts the covariant equation of motion with the prescribed external field and explicitly excludes the particle's own field; at zero coordinate velocity the magnetic term vanishes and the coordinate force is $q$ times the external electric field, so the displayed force follows by substituting (a) ([[def-em-si-conventions]] fixes the units, N). The magnitude and attraction statements are immediate from the formula and $q^2\\ge0$ with equality only for $q=0$, in which case there is no charge and no force. Clause (c) records the idealization: the total induced charge $-q$ is the induced-charge normalisation of the ideal plane recorded with the certificate, and nothing dynamical, radiative, measured or finite-geometry is claimed. In the adopted tensor convention [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], an instantaneous rest state has $U=(c,0,0,0)$ and $U_0=-c$, so the spatial electric four-force components are $qF^{i0}U_0=q(-E_i/c)(-c)=qE_i$; $dt/d\\tau=1$ at rest identifies these with coordinate-force components. This is the electric contribution. The holding support supplies the opposite force, rather than requiring a constant trajectory to satisfy the sole-force equation. The certificate's explicit plane density integrates to $-q$ and supplies the induced-charge assertion; for $q\\ne0$ its absolute density is largest under the charge and decreases with transverse radius.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 3.2.3 printed p. 123, Eq. (3.12) (force on the charge above a grounded conducting plane)."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (Lent Term 2015, 236 PDF pages)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.4.3 printed p. 36 (force F = -q^2/(16 pi epsilon_0 d^2) x-hat on the charge)."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The external electrostatic force on an ideal point charge held at height a above an ideal grounded conducting plane, within the declared image model: the mirror field at the charge is -(q/(16 pi epsilon_0 a^2)) e_3 and the adopted external Lorentz coupling gives the attractive force -(q^2/(16 pi epsilon_0 a^2)) e_3 with self-field excluded. The item asserts no measurement, no motion, no radiation, no finite-geometry fringe corrections and no self-force. The attraction is strict for $q\\ne0$ and the force vanishes for $q=0$; holding the charge requires the opposite support force. This is the electric contribution at rest, not a sole-force trajectory solution.",
          "dependency_level": 18
        },
