            "thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) as carried by the integral and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). **Declare the ideal parallel-plate model** of two infinite parallel charged planes in vacuo, read as equipotential ideal conductor surfaces of the same equilibrium structure as [[post-em-electrostatic-conductor-model]] (the pure electrostatic regime [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] with fields [[def-em-fields]]): for a separation parameter $d>0$, half-separation $h=d/2$, a surface-density parameter $\\sigma>0$ (C m$^{-2}$) and the framework constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]], the model consists of the field\n$$E(x):=\\begin{cases}-\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}e_3,&|x_3|<h,\\\\[2pt]0,&|x_3|>h,\\end{cases}\\qquad x=(x_1,x_2,x_3),$$\ntogether with the plane densities $+\\sigma$ on $\\Pi_+:=\\{x_3=h\\}$ and $-\\sigma$ on $\\Pi_-:=\\{x_3=-h\\}$, and with the piecewise potential $\\varphi(x):=+\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}x_3$ for $|x_3|\\le h$, $\\varphi:=+\\dfrac{\\sigma h}{\\varepsilon_0}$ for $x_3\\ge h$ and $\\varphi:=-\\dfrac{\\sigma h}{\\varepsilon_0}$ for $x_3\\le-h$. Then:\n(a) [Direct distributional verification] $E$ and $\\varphi$ are locally integrable and $C^\\infty$ off $\\Pi_+\\cup\\Pi_-$ with $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ there, $\\varphi$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R^3$ and constant on each plane with the values $\\varphi|_{\\Pi_+}=+\\sigma h/\\varepsilon_0$, $\\varphi|_{\\Pi_-}=-\\sigma h/\\varepsilon_0$; and in $\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}\\bigl(\\delta_{\\Pi_+}-\\delta_{\\Pi_-}\\bigr),\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=0,$$\nwhere $\\delta_{\\Pi_\\pm}(\\psi):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\psi(x_1,x_2,\\pm h)\\,dx_1\\,dx_2$ for compactly supported smooth $\\psi$ are the plane distributions defined by the iterated Lebesgue integrals. Equivalently, the model realises the static electrostatic equations with the declared pair of plane densities and no other source, and the potential differences read off $\\varphi$ are $\\varphi(h)-\\varphi(-h)=2\\sigma h/\\varepsilon_0=\\sigma d/\\varepsilon_0$.\n(b) [Uniform field and voltage] the field between the planes has constant magnitude $|E|=\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$ and is normal to the planes, and the potential difference between the plates is $V:=\\varphi|_{\\Pi_+}-\\varphi|_{\\Pi_-}=\\sigma d/\\varepsilon_0$.\n(c) [Charge, capacitance and energy of the ideal model] for any Lebesgue-measurable region $P\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ with finite positive area $A=\\lambda_2(P)>0$ the charge on the portion of area $A$ of the positive plate is $Q:=\\sigma A$, so $Q=(\\varepsilon_0A/d)V$ with the geometric ratio\n$$C:=\\frac QV=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0A}{d}>0,$$\nindependent of $\\sigma$ and $V$; and the field energy contained in the slab of area $A$ between the plates is\n$$U=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\int_{\\text{slab of area }A}|E|^2\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac{\\sigma^2Ad}{2\\varepsilon_0}=\\frac12QV=\\frac{Q^2}{2C}=\\frac12CV^2 .$$\n(d) [Relation to the bounded-conductor theorem and scope] the linearity $Q=CV$, the positivity $C>0$ and the energy identities $U=\\frac12QV=\\frac{Q^2}{2C}=\\frac12CV^2$ are exactly the conclusions that [[pthm-em-capacitance-energy]] proves for bounded conductor configurations with prescribed potentials and a grounded reference; this example verifies them directly for the ideal model because the infinite planes and the unbounded declared field are outside that theorem's hypotheses (compact conductors in a bounded vacuum region) and outside the declared stationary-sheet source type of [[def-em-weak-sources]], exactly as the ideal line source lies outside the compact-source types of the electrostatic page. No approximation theorem from finite plates to the infinite model is asserted; for a finite real plate pair the uniform-field model neglects the fringing field near the edges, which is not computed here, and the idealisation also omits the finite-thickness, material, transient and measurement content of any real capacitor.",
          "strategy": "All identities are computed directly from the explicit piecewise formulas; no compact-sheet jump theorem is invoked, because the planes are noncompact and the model is a declared idealization. (a) Local integrability is immediate from the boundedness of the piecewise field and the finiteness of the slab's intersections with compact sets ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]]); the identification with regular distributions is [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]] and [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]. For a compactly supported smooth test $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^3)$ ([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]), $\\langle\\operatorname{div}E,\\psi\\rangle=-\\int E\\cdot\\nabla\\psi=+\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}\\int_{|x_3|<h}\\partial_3\\psi\\,d\\lambda_3$ (inner product [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and norm of [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]]); Fubini ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]) writes the slab integral as $\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\bigl(\\int_{-h}^{h}\\partial_3\\psi(x_1,x_2,s)\\,ds\\bigr)dx_1dx_2$, and the inner integral is $\\psi(x_1,x_2,h)-\\psi(x_1,x_2,-h)$ by [[thm-integration-by-parts]] (the one-dimensional Newton-Leibniz identity, applicable because $\\psi$ is smooth and compactly supported and the interval is bounded); hence $\\langle\\operatorname{div}E,\\psi\\rangle=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}(\\delta_{\\Pi_+}-\\delta_{\\Pi_-})(\\psi)$, proving the divergence identity with the sign convention of [[def-distributional-derivative]] and [[def-distribution]]. The curl identity: $E_1=E_2=0$ and $E_3=-\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_0}\\mathbf 1_{\\{|x_3|<h\\}}$ is a function of $x_3$ alone, so its distributional $x_1$- and $x_2$-derivatives vanish identically ([[def-distributional-derivative]]); hence $\\operatorname{curl}E=(\\partial_2E_3-\\partial_3E_2,\\partial_3E_1-\\partial_1E_3,\\partial_1E_2-\\partial_2E_1)=0$. The potential formulas give $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ on the three open regions ([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), and continuity across the planes is the equality of the two one-sided constants. (b) is the explicit evaluation, and (c) is arithmetic with the slab integral computed by Fubini: $\\int_{\\text{slab}}|E|^2=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{\\varepsilon_0^2}Ad$. (d) states the exact relation to [[pthm-em-capacitance-energy]]: that theorem's structure (linearity in the voltage, positive capacitance, energy equal to half the pairing $QV$) is what the computation reproduces, but its hypotheses (bounded conductor bodies, bounded vacuum region, Dirichlet solvability) are not met by the infinite-plane idealization, so the identities are verified here directly and the theorem is not applied; the fringing qualification is the ideal-model warning recorded by the design's X2 verification, recorded as scope rather than computed. No empirical content, no measurement and no claim about real capacitors beyond the stated approximation are made. The slab in (c) is precisely $P\\times(-h,h)$ for any such measurable $P$, not necessarily bounded or Jordan; the constant nonnegative integrand and finite area give the same energy by Tonelli, $\\lambda_3(P\\times(-h,h))=Ad$. The plane-charge portion is $\\sigma\\int_P1=\\sigma A$. The sheet functionals are continuous on compact test supports because the projected support has finite area and the density is bounded. All test/field products and test derivatives are absolutely integrable on a common compact box. Fubini with the exact Borel product identification and finite-interval Riemann/Lebesgue comparison justifies the vertical FTC calculation and the vanishing horizontal derivatives. This example compares with the algebraic conclusions of the capacitance theorem but does not invoke its full-AC Dirichlet existence branch: its direct declared plane-model verification retains only $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For merely Lebesgue-measurable $P$, choose a Borel set equal to $P$ off a Borel planar null set by [[cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure]]. Its product with the finite-height interval differs only inside the product of that null set with the interval, which has zero volume by the Borel product formula and Tonelli. Completeness then gives the same slab measure and integral. Thus the arbitrary measurable-area branch does not assume that every Lebesgue product set is Borel.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
