            "def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-gamma-functional-equation": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-real-gamma-one-half-is-root-pi": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-electrostatic-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-existence": "physical-result",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice for the cited Sobolev trace/weak-solvability interfaces and Countable Choice for the surface-measure interfaces ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $B:=B_1(0)=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:|x|<1\\}$ be the open unit ball, a bounded $C^\\infty$ (in particular $C^1$) domain with $\\partial B=S^2$ and outward normal $\\nu(x)=x$ ([[lem-em-euclidean-balls-and-annuli-are-bounded-c-one-domains]], [[def-metric-ball]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), and consider the pure electrostatic Neumann problem on $B$ (regime [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]], fields [[def-em-fields]], constants [[def-em-si-conventions]]) with **zero volume source and unit normal field**:\n$$-\\Delta\\varphi=0\\ \\text{ in }B,\\qquad \\partial_\\nu\\varphi=1\\ \\text{ on }\\partial B .$$\nThen:\n(a) [No classical solution] there is no real $\\varphi\\in C^2(\\overline B)$ satisfying these equations: if such a $\\varphi$ existed, [[lem-neumann-compatibility-from-the-divergence-theorem]] with $f=-\\Delta\\varphi=0$ and $g=\\partial_\\nu\\varphi\\equiv1$ would give\n$$0=\\int_B f\\,d\\lambda_3=-\\int_{\\partial B}g\\,dS=-|S^2|=-4\\pi,$$\nbecause the surface area of the unit sphere is $|S^2|=4\\pi$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]); the contradiction is the failure of the necessary flux compatibility of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](b).\n(b) [No weak solution] more generally there is no $u\\in H^1(B)$ with $\\int_B\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla u\\,d\\lambda_3=\\langle g,Tv\\rangle$ for all $v\\in H^1(B)$ with $g\\equiv1$ read as an element of the dual trace space ([[def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary]]): testing $v\\equiv1$ would force $\\ell(1)=\\int_{\\partial B}1\\,dS=4\\pi=0$, and the weak Neumann existence lemma states existence exactly when $\\ell(1)=0$ ([[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]]); equivalently the unit ball's boundary flux budget fails already in the weak formulation.\n(c) [Physics reading and scope] in the pure electrostatic regime the datum $\\partial_\\nu\\varphi\\equiv1$ on the whole boundary of the unit ball is inadmissible: no electrostatic potential with zero volume source satisfies it, and the obstruction is the strictly positive total boundary flux $4\\pi$, not a smoothness or compactness artefact. The admissible Neumann data are exactly those satisfying the necessary compatibility $\\int_{\\partial B}g\\,dS=0$ for the zero-source problem, which is a flux budget and not an existence theorem (sufficiency at the smooth level is the separate content of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-existence]](b) under its hypotheses). The item is a mathematical impossibility statement about the ideal boundary value problem: it reports no measurement, no apparatus and no material property, and the companion failure with unit source and zero boundary field is the same flux obstruction, obtained by evaluating that lemma with $f\\equiv1$ and $g\\equiv0$.",
          "strategy": "Assume the Countable Choice carried by the cited integral-surface interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). The unit ball is a bounded $C^1$ (indeed $C^\\infty$) domain by [[lem-em-euclidean-balls-and-annuli-are-bounded-c-one-domains]], with outward normal $\\nu(x)=x$ on $S^2$. (a) Let $\\varphi\\in C^2(\\overline B)$ satisfy the displayed equations, so that $f:=-\\Delta\\varphi=0$ and $g:=\\partial_\\nu\\varphi\\equiv1$ on $\\partial B$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[def-classical-normal-derivative]]); [[lem-neumann-compatibility-from-the-divergence-theorem]] gives $\\int_Bf=-\\int_{\\partial B}g$, i.e. $0=-|S^2|$. The unit-sphere area is computed from the polar formula: $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]) and $|B_1|=\\pi^{3/2}/\\Gamma(5/2)=4\\pi/3$ ([[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]), so $|S^2|=4\\pi\\ne0$; this is a contradiction, so no such $\\varphi$ exists. (b) For the weak formulation, the density $g\\equiv1$ defines the bounded functional $h\\mapsto\\int_{\\partial B}h\\,dS$ on the actual trace space: Cauchy–Schwarz gives $|\\int h|\\le(4\\pi)^{1/2}\\|h\\|_{L^2(\\partial B)}\\le C\\|h\\|_{H^{1/2}(\\partial B)}$. The final inclusion follows directly from the finite chart norm in [[def-fractional-sobolev-space-on-a-compact-c-one-boundary]]: its localized norms include $L^2$ norms and the smooth graph densities are bounded on the compact supports of the partition. Thus this density lies in the bounded dual $H^{-1/2}$; $H^{1/2}$ itself is not that dual, hence an element of the bounded dual used by [[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]]; the defect functional $\\ell(v)=\\int_B0\\cdot v+\\langle g,Tv\\rangle$ has $\\ell(1)=\\int_{\\partial B}1\\,dS=4\\pi$ by the same area computation and the trace of the constant function $1$ being the constant $1$; since the lemma's weak Neumann problem on the connected bounded $C^1$ domain $B$ is solvable if and only if $\\ell(1)=0$, the datum is incompatible in the weak class too, with no $H^1$ solution. (c) is the reading: the necessary compatibility of [[pthm-em-electrostatic-bvp-uniqueness]](b) is exactly the vanishing total flux, and the companion failure with unit source and zero boundary field follows from the same lemma evaluated at $f\\equiv1$, $g\\equiv0$, where $\\int_Bf=|B|=4\\pi/3$ and $\\int_{\\partial B}g=0$ violate $\\int f=-\\int g$. No step uses the ball's symmetry beyond the surface area, so the area evaluation via the polar and Gamma items is the only geometric input; no choice beyond the declared interfaces of the cited items is used. The trace $T1=1$ follows from the agreement with continuous Sobolev functions in [[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]. Hence testing the purported equation with $v=1$ alone already gives $0=4\\pi$, independently of any existence assertion. Its cited bounded trace operator requires full AC, as now stated. The same Cauchy–Schwarz estimate is [[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]].",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
