          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For the density-integral branch assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) through the Lebesgue interface ([[def-countable-choice]]); the finite-point branch of all identities below is choice-free. Let $a>0$ and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real and vanish $\\lambda_3$-a.e. outside $\\overline B_a(0)$ ([[def-metric-ball]]), or let $(x_k,q_k)_{k=1}^N$ be a finite system of stationary point charges, with the source moments of [[def-em-electric-moments]] branches (a) and (c) respectively. For $b\\in\\mathbb R^3$ define the moments **about the reference point $b$** by replacing, in each integrand of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (a) and in each summand of (c), the vector $y$ by $y-b$ (respectively $x_k$ by $x_k-b$):\n\n$$Q^{(b)}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3,\\qquad p^{(b)}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}(y-b)\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),$$\n\n$$M^{(b)}_{i_1\\dots i_l}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\prod_{r=1}^l(y-b)_{i_r}\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\quad(l\\ge1),\\qquad M^{(b,0)}:=Q^{(b)},$$\n\n$$Q^{(b)}_{ij}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\bigl(3(y-b)_i(y-b)_j-|y-b|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),$$\n\nand analogously with the finite sums $M^{(b)}_{i_1\\dots i_l}=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_k\\prod_{r=1}^l(x_k-b)_{i_r}$ $(l\\ge1)$ and $Q^{(b)}_{ij}=\\sum_{k=1}^Nq_k\\bigl(3(x_k-b)_i(x_k-b)_j-|x_k-b|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)$ in the point-charge branch; for $N=0$ the sums are the empty sum $0$. Every displayed integral is absolutely convergent: since $\\rho=0$ $\\lambda_3$-a.e. off $\\overline B_a(0)$, the estimates $|(y-b)_{i_1}\\cdots(y-b)_{i_l}|\\le(|y|+|b|)^l\\le(a+|b|)^l$ and $|3(y-b)_i(y-b)_j-|y-b|^2\\delta_{ij}|\\le4(|y|+|b|)^2\\le4(a+|b|)^2$ hold $\\lambda_3$-a.e. on the support, so each integrand is dominated $\\lambda_3$-a.e. by a finite constant times $|\\rho|\\in L^1$ ([[def-em-electric-moments]] (a), [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then:\n\n(a) $Q^{(b)}=Q$ for every $b$;\n\n(b) $p^{(b)}=p-Qb$, so the dipole moment is independent of the reference point exactly when $Q=0$;\n\n(c) for every $l\\ge0$,\n\n$$M^{(b)}_{i_1\\dots i_l}=\\sum_{S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,l\\}}(-1)^{|S|}\\Bigl(\\prod_{r\\in S}b_{i_r}\\Bigr)M_{S^{c}},$$\n\nwhere for a subset $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,l\\}$ we write $S^{c}:=\\{1,\\dots,l\\}\\setminus S$ and $M_{S^{c}}$ for the Cartesian moment whose index list is $(i_r)_{r\\in S^{c}}$ in increasing order of $r$, with the empty index list read as $M_{\\emptyset}:=Q$; in particular\n\n$$M^{(b)}_{ij}=M_{ij}-b_ip_j-b_jp_i+b_ib_jQ;$$\n\n(d) $Q^{(b)}_{ij}=Q_{ij}-3\\bigl(b_ip_j+b_jp_i\\bigr)+2(b\\cdot p)\\delta_{ij}+\\bigl(3b_ib_j-|b|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)Q$; consequently $Q^{(b)}_{ij}=Q_{ij}$ for every $b$ and all $i,j$ if and only if $Q=0$ and $p=0$;\n\n(e) [Scope] this is an exact identity between the declared source moments of [[def-em-electric-moments]]; it asserts nothing about fields, forces, dynamics or nature, and the reference point is a choice of coordinate origin, not an operation performed on the source.",
          "strategy": "The theorem is the expansion of the polynomial $\\prod_{r=1}^l(y_{i_r}-b_{i_r})$ followed by termwise integration (or summation). For (a) and (b) the degrees $0$ and $1$ are immediate; constant multiples of $\\rho$ and of $y_i\\rho$ pass through the integral by linearity of the Lebesgue integral on $L^1$ ([[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]), and the finite-sum branch by linearity of finite sums ([[def-finite-sum]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]]). For (c), expand by finite distributivity,\n$$\\prod_{r=1}^l(y_{i_r}-b_{i_r})=\\sum_{S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,l\\}}(-1)^{|S|}\\Bigl(\\prod_{r\\in S}b_{i_r}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\prod_{r\\notin S}y_{i_r}\\Bigr),$$\nan identity of real polynomials proved by induction on $l$ (finitely many distribution steps, [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] for the polynomial calculus), and integrate term by term: each complement monomial in the term indexed by S is dominated $\\lambda_3$-a.e. by $a^{l-|S|}|\\rho|$, and its fixed coefficient has modulus $\\prod_{r\\in S}|b_{i_r}|$. A common envelope for all finitely many expanded terms is $(1+a+|b|)^l|\\rho|$ on the support, hence absolutely integrable by [[def-em-electric-moments]] (a), so the finitely many applications of linearity are legitimate and the constants $\\prod_{r\\in S}b_{i_r}$ factor out ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). For (d), multiply the case $l=2$ by $3$ and subtract $\\delta_{ij}\\sum_kM^{(b)}_{kk}$, using $Q^{(b)}_{ij}=3M^{(b)}_{ij}-\\delta_{ij}\\sum_kM^{(b)}_{kk}$, $Q_{ij}=3M_{ij}-\\delta_{ij}\\sum_kM_{kk}$ and $\\sum_kM^{(b)}_{kk}=\\sum_kM_{kk}-2b\\cdot p+|b|^2Q$; this gives the displayed identity, and its four $b$-dependent terms vanish identically when $Q=0$ and $p=0$. Replacing $\\rho$ by an equal $\\lambda_3$-a.e. density changes no integral ([[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]). The finite-sum branch repeats the same algebra with sums. No Choice beyond the declared $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used; (e) records the scope. For the quadrupole converse, evaluate the 11 component at b=e1 and b=-e1 to get Q=0, then evaluate the ii component at b=ei to get pi=0 for every i. These finite computations also hold in the point branch.",
          "provenance": {
