            "lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the Lebesgue interface ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $a>0$ and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real and vanish $\\lambda_3$-a.e. outside $\\overline B_a(0)$; let $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a prescribed external vector-field datum, with electric-field units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], that is continuous on $\\overline B_a(0)$ and Lipschitz there with constant $L\\ge0$, i.e. $|E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(x)-E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(x')|\\le L|x-x'|$ for all $x,x'\\in\\overline B_a(0)$. Consider the matter model in which the prescribed static distribution $\\rho$ is carried by a body at rest in the laboratory frame and adopt additionally the prescribed external force model $f_{\\mathrm{ext}}=\\rho E_{\\mathrm{ext}}$. This is a declared extension to these locally integrable charge data and Lipschitz fields, motivated by the smooth continuum coupling of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] (ii); that postulate covers smooth sources and does not itself establish this extension. The only force counted by the present model is $f_{\\mathrm{ext}}$; the self-field of $\\rho$, all other fields, mechanical support and back-reaction are excluded by this model. Define the **total external force** and the **total external torque about the origin** by the componentwise Lebesgue integrals\n\n$$F:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho(y)\\,E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad \\tau:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho(y)\\,\\bigl(y\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)\\bigr)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\in\\mathbb R^3,$$\n\nwith units N and N m respectively; $L$ has units V m$^{-2}$. The integrands are interpreted through their a.e.-equal measurable zero extensions from the closed ball: continuity of $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ there gives Borel factors, and null modifications remain Lebesgue measurable by [[cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure]]. They are absolutely convergent because a.e. on the support $|E_{\\mathrm{ext}}|\\le|E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)|+La$ and $|y\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}|\\le a(|E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)|+La)$, so each component is dominated by a constant times $|\\rho|\\in L^1$ ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant]], [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). Then:\n\n(a) [Uniform force and torque expansions]\n\n$$F=Q\\,E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)+R_F,\\qquad |R_F|\\le L\\,a\\,\\|\\rho\\|_1,\\qquad \\tau=p\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)+R_\\tau,\\qquad |R_\\tau|\\le L\\,a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1,$$\n\nwhere $Q$ and $p$ are the total charge and dipole moment of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (a) and $\\|\\rho\\|_1=\\int|\\rho|\\,d\\lambda_3$;\n\n(b) [Neutral distribution] if $Q=0$ then the leading force term vanishes, $F=R_F$ with $|R_F|\\le La\\|\\rho\\|_1$, and the torque is the ideal-dipole torque $p\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)$ up to the bound $|R_\\tau|\\le La^2\\|\\rho\\|_1$; in this sense a neutral distribution of size $a$ in a field with Lipschitz constant $L$ carries the dipole torque $p\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)$ with an explicit remainder;\n\n(c) [Reference-point shift] for any reference point $o\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the torque about $o$, $\\tau_o:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho(y)\\,(y-o)\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$, equals $\\tau-o\\times F$ exactly;\n\n(d) [Ideal pair of test charges at rest] let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $d\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, put $a=|d|/2$, and consider the two stationary point charges $+q$ at $d/2$ and $-q$ at $-d/2$ ([[def-em-electric-moments]] (c)), each treated as a test charge at rest in the laboratory chart in the same prescribed external field $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}$, with the mutual interaction and the self-field of each charge excluded by the model. Adopt for this finite prescribed-charge branch the external force assignment $F_k=q_kE_{\\rm ext}(x_k)$ and torque $x_k\\times F_k$. It is defined for merely continuous external field values at the two points, independently of a worldline equation. For the same Lipschitz bound as above, finite summation gives the expansions of (a),(b), with $Q=0$, $p=qd$, and total variation $2|q|$: $|R_F|\\le2|q|La$, $|R_\\tau|\\le2|q|La^2$. More generally the exact finite force/torque sum requires only the specified field values; no Lipschitz estimate is asserted without its hypothesis.\n\n(d1) [Covariant sufficient specialization] If, in addition, an actual prescribed electromagnetic tensor $F$ of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of the graph of each massive future-timelike $C^2$ test worldline with constant charge $q'$ and supplied rest mass $m>0$, and the external-field coupling [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] (i) applies, then at an instant with zero coordinate velocity $U=(c,0,0,0)$ its coordinate three-force $\\mathbf f=d\\mathbf p/dt$ is $q'E_{\\rm ext}$: the four-force is $\\gamma(\\mathbf f\\cdot v/c,\\mathbf f)$ with $\\gamma=1$ at rest ([[pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force]] (b)), and $U_0=-c$ and $F^{i0}=-E_i/c$. This reproduces the prescribed point-force assignment at that instant. Similarly the smooth-source $J=0$ matter-coupling branch of postulate (ii) reproduces the continuum force assignment when its own source/field/matter hypotheses hold. No $C^1$ tensor or worldline is inferred from merely continuous $E$ data, and no two-charge trajectory existence or permanent rest is claimed.\n\n(e) [Scope] the statement is conditional on the explicitly stated matter model: only the prescribed external field acts on the distribution, the distribution is prescribed and at rest, and no self-force, back-reaction, mechanical equilibrium, stability or dynamics of the body is asserted; the external field is a datum, not solved for. Clause (d) is an instantaneous idealisation of two ideal point test charges with mutual interaction excluded; it is not a two-body dynamics, a bound-state or a stability statement. No radiation, medium response or empirical claim is made. The prescribed finite force assignment and finite moment algebra in (d) are choice-free; the volume integrals in (a)-(c) carry their explicit measure premise, while (d1) is a separate conditional instantiation of the exact physical coupling.",
          "strategy": "Write $E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)=E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)+\\bigl(E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)-E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)\\bigr)$ $\\lambda_3$-a.e. on the support and integrate term by term. For (a), the constant term of the force gives $\\bigl(\\int\\rho\\,d\\lambda_3\\bigr)E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)=QE_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)$, and the constant term of the torque gives $\\bigl(\\int\\rho(y)\\,y\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\bigr)\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)=p\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)$ by linearity of the integral on $L^1$ and the bilinearity of the cross product ([[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]], [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). For the force remainder, if $R_F\\ne0$ test it against the unit vector $u:=R_F/|R_F|$: $|R_F|=u\\cdot R_F=\\int\\rho(y)\\,u\\cdot\\bigl(E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)-E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)\\bigr)d\\lambda_3(y)\\le\\int|\\rho(y)|\\,|E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)-E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)|\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\le L\\int|y|\\,|\\rho(y)|\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\le La\\|\\rho\\|_1$, using $u\\cdot v\\le|v|$ ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]), the triangle and monotonicity rules for the integral ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]) and $|y|\\le a$ $\\lambda_3$-a.e. on the support; the case $R_F=0$ is trivial. For the torque remainder, $\\bigl|\\int\\rho(y)\\,y\\times\\bigl(E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)-E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)\\bigr)d\\lambda_3(y)\\bigr|\\le\\int|\\rho(y)|\\,|y|\\,|E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)-E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(0)|\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\le L\\int|y|^2|\\rho|\\,d\\lambda_3\\le La^2\\|\\rho\\|_1$, using $|y\\times v|\\le|y|\\,|v|$ ([[thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Since the two integrals are absolutely convergent, all interchanges are finitely many applications of linearity and the triangle rule. For (b) insert $Q=0$. For (c), subtract and add $o$: $\\int\\rho(y)(y-o)\\times E_{\\mathrm{ext}}(y)d\\lambda_3=\\tau-o\\times F$ by linearity and bilinearity of the cross product. For (d), under the explicitly adopted point-force assignment sum the two forces q E(d/2) and -q E(-d/2) and their position cross products. Finite bilinearity gives p=qd and the same estimates as(a), replacing the density integral by the finite sum and its total variation2|q|. This algebra uses only the actual field values and the Lipschitz assumption for the numerical error bounds, not an unstated C1 regularity premise. For(d1), when the additional C1 prescribed tensor and massive C2-worldline hypotheses explicitly hold, the exact Lorentz postulate gives $m dU^i/d\\tau=q'F^{i0}U_0=q'(-E_i/c)(-c)=q'E_i$ at rest, using the cited tensor/worldline conventions. This is an instantaneous sufficient instantiation only. The smooth continuum branch likewise reproduces rho E only under its explicit smooth-source matter-coupling hypotheses; the broader L1/Lipschitz model is separately adopted. Clause (e) records the model assumptions. Countable Choice is used only through the Lebesgue integrals and their suppliers; the Lipschitz constant and the field values enter only through the displayed estimates, and the norm continuity of [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]] is used to read the pointwise bounds. Changing $\\rho$ on a $\\lambda_3$-null set changes no integral ([[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]). The zero extensions of the field factors from the closed source ball are Borel; completed Lebesgue measure permits arbitrary finite-valued null modifications. Bound vector integrals by pairing with the unit vector in the direction of a nonzero remainder, with the zero case immediate. In (d1), [[pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force]] gives the spatial four-force gamma times the coordinate three-force; gamma=1 at rest. Force, torque and L have units N, N m and V m^-2.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
