          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue and Newtonian interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $a>0$, let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real and vanish $\\lambda_3$-a.e. outside the closed ball $\\overline B_a(0)$ ([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]]), let $Q$ and $p$ be the total charge and dipole moment of $\\rho$ about the chart origin ([[def-em-electric-moments]] (a)), let $\\|\\rho\\|_1:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|\\rho|\\,d\\lambda_3$, and let $\\varphi:=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho$, where $N\\rho$ is the Newtonian potential of [[def-newtonian-potential]] and $\\varepsilon_0$ is the constant of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Then:\n\n(a) [Uniform monopole-dipole expansion] for every $x$ with $r:=|x|\\ge2a$ the integral $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho(y)|x-y|^{-1}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$ is absolutely convergent and equals $4\\pi N\\rho(x)$, and\n\n$$\\varphi(x)=\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r}+\\frac{p\\cdot x}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}+R(x),\\qquad |R(x)|\\le\\frac{16\\,a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3},$$\n\nthe bound being uniform over the whole exterior region $\\{x:|x|\\ge2a\\}$;\n\n(b) [Leading-term form] consequently, for $r\\ge2a$,\n\n$$\\Bigl|\\varphi(x)-\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{|p|}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^2}+\\frac{16\\,a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3},$$\n\nand if $Q=0$ and $p\\ne0$, then for every unit direction $n\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $|n|=1$, with $p\\cdot n\\ne0$ and every $r\\ge\\max\\bigl(2a,\\ 32a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1/|p\\cdot n|\\bigr)$ the dipole term is the leading term in the sense that $\\varphi(rn)=\\frac{p\\cdot n}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^2}(1+\\delta)$ with $|\\delta|\\le\\frac12$, so $|\\varphi(rn)|\\ge\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^2}$ and the remainder bound decays one power of $r$ faster than the dipole term;\n\n(c) [Physical reading] by [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (c) the function $\\varphi$ is finite $\\lambda_3$-a.e., locally integrable, of class $C^\\infty$ and harmonic off $\\overline B_a(0)$, and satisfies $-\\Delta T_\\varphi=T_{\\rho/\\varepsilon_0}$ in $\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$; the displayed expansion is therefore the exterior expansion of the potential of the compactly supported source $\\rho$ in the fixed-time reading of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e), and in the smooth branch ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (a)) the field $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ of [[def-em-fields]] realises the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (c) with source $\\rho$;\n\n(d) [Scope and exclusions] the hypotheses are a real compactly supported $L^1$ density, the region $r\\ge2a$, and the framework's constants; $\\rho$ need not be continuous or bounded; the statement concerns $\\varphi$ itself, and no derivative estimate for $R$, no expansion of the field $E$, and no assertion on $|x|<2a$ or on the support of $\\rho$ is claimed.",
          "strategy": "First, for $|x|\\ge2a$ and $\\lambda_3$-a.e. $y$ one has $\\rho(y)=0$ off $\\overline B_a(0)$, while for $y\\in\\overline B_a(0)$ one has $|x-y|\\ge r-a\\ge r/2>0$ ([[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]], [[def-metric-ball]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]); hence $|x-y|^{-1}\\le2/r$ on the support and the integral against $\\rho$ converges absolutely ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]). Since the kernel is bounded on the support and the support is $\\lambda_3$-a.e. contained in $\\overline B_a(0)$, the unnormalised integral equals $4\\pi N\\rho(x)$ by the defining formula of [[def-newtonian-potential]] ([[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]). Because $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)$ for $n=3$ ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (c)), one has $N\\rho(x)=(4\\pi)^{-1}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho(y)|x-y|^{-1}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$ and hence $\\varphi(x)=(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho(y)|x-y|^{-1}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$ for $r\\ge2a$. The monopole-dipole splitting of [[lem-em-coulomb-dipole-remainder]] applied with the same $a$ gives $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\rho(y)|x-y|^{-1}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)=Q/r+p\\cdot x/r^3+R_0(x)$ with $|R_0(x)|\\le16a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1/r^3$ for all $r\\ge2a$; multiplying the identity by the constant $(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}$ (linearity of the integral on $L^1$, [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]) gives (a) with $R:=(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}R_0$ and the displayed bound $|R(x)|\\le16a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3)$. Nothing at $r<2a$ is asserted, because the kernel singularity may meet the support there; the proof uses no differentiation of the remainder. For (b), $|p\\cdot x|\\le|p|\\,|x|$ by Cauchy-Schwarz for the Euclidean inner product ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]), so $|p\\cdot x|/r^3\\le|p|/r^2$, and the triangle inequality for the modulus completes the estimate. For (c), the regularity, the harmonicity off $\\overline B_a(0)$ and the identity $-\\Delta T_\\varphi=T_{\\rho/\\varepsilon_0}$ are exactly [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (c), whose potential is already $\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho$; no second scaling by $\\varepsilon_0^{-1}$ is applied; the fixed-time dictionary between the spatial equations and the weak four-dimensional reading is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e); in the smooth branch the pointwise equations and the field $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ are those of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (a) ([[def-em-fields]] for the field object).",
          "provenance": {
