          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue, spherical-harmonic and Newtonian interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $a>0$ and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real and vanish $\\lambda_3$-a.e. outside $\\overline B_a(0)$ ([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]]); let $Q$, $p$ and $Q_{ij}$ be the total charge, dipole moment and traceless quadrupole of $\\rho$ about the chart origin ([[def-em-electric-moments]] (a)); let $\\varphi:=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\\rho$ be the potential of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (c) with the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]] ([[def-newtonian-potential]]); and let $\\{Y_{lm}:l\\ge0,\\ m=1,\\dots,2l+1\\}$ be a real orthonormal basis of spherical harmonics of $L^2(S^2,d\\sigma)$ of degree $l$, with $d\\sigma$ the surface measure of total mass $4\\pi$ and the addition theorem of [[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]] ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). For every $l,m$ define the **spherical multipole moment of degree $l$**\n\n$$q_{lm}:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|y|^l\\,Y_{lm}(y/|y|)\\,\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),$$\n\nabsolutely convergent because $|Y_{lm}|\\le((2l+1)/(4\\pi))^{1/2}$ on $S^2$ by the addition theorem and $|y|\\le a$ $\\lambda_3$-a.e. on the support, so the integrand is dominated by $a^l((2l+1)/(4\\pi))^{1/2}|\\rho|$ ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). In every coefficient integral the factor $|y|^lY_{lm}(y/|y|)$ is defined at $y=0$ by its homogeneous polynomial extension: zero for $l\\ge1$ and the constant degree-zero harmonic for $l=0$; this is a finite assignment at a null singleton and changes no integral. Write $x=rn$ with $r=|x|$ and $n\\in S^2$. Then:\n\n(a) [Exterior spherical multipole expansion] for every $x$ with $r>a$,\n\n$$\\varphi(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\sum_{l=0}^{\\infty}\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}\\frac{4\\pi}{2l+1}\\,\\frac{Y_{lm}(n)}{r^{l+1}}\\,q_{lm},$$\n\nthe series converging absolutely and uniformly on $\\{|x|\\ge r_0\\}$ for every $r_0>a$, with the tail after degree $N$ bounded by\n\n$$\\Bigl\\|\\,\\varphi-\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\sum_{l=0}^{N}\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}\\frac{4\\pi}{2l+1}\\frac{Y_{lm}(n)}{r^{l+1}}q_{lm}\\Bigr\\|_{\\infty,\\{|x|\\ge r_0\\}}\\le\\frac{\\|\\rho\\|_1\\,a^{N+1}}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\,r_0^{N+2}\\,(1-a/r_0)};$$\n\n(b) [Monopole, dipole and quadrupole terms] the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ terms of (a) are the monopole and dipole terms $Q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r)$ and $p\\cdot x/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3)$, and for $r\\ge2a$ the difference between $\\varphi$ and the truncation after degree $1$ obeys the remainder bound of [[pthm-em-electric-dipole-expansion]]; the degree-$2$ term equals the traceless-quadrupole term\n\n$$\\frac{1}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}\\,n_iQ_{ij}n_j;$$\n\n(c) [Regularity and termwise differentiation] $\\varphi$ is of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\{|x|>a\\}$, the series in (a) may be differentiated termwise to every order, the differentiated series converging uniformly on every compact subset of $\\{|x|>a\\}$; in particular the field $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ of [[def-em-fields]] equals $-\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\sum_{l,m}\\frac{4\\pi}{2l+1}\\nabla_x\\bigl(Y_{lm}(n)r^{-l-1}\\bigr)q_{lm}$ on $\\{|x|>a\\}$, with the same uniform convergence on compact subsets;\n\n(d) [Scope of exterior clauses (a)-(c)] those clauses concern a real compactly supported $L^1$ density and the exterior region $r>a$; they make no claim on or inside $\\overline B_a(0)$, no convergence at $r=a$ is asserted, the exterior-density expansion is not asserted for point or singular sources, and no empirical content is involved. The expansion is the exterior expansion of the potential of the static source $\\rho$ read in the fixed-time dictionary of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e);\n\n(e) [Separate source-free ball boundary branch] let $R>0$ be a radius and let real $f\\in L^2(S^2,d\\sigma)$ be prescribed scalar-potential boundary data (unit V) on the sphere $x=Rn$, with $f_{lm}=\\int_{S^2}fY_{lm}\\,d\\sigma$. Independently of the exterior density $\\rho$, define on $|x|<R$\n\n$$\\varphi_f(rn)=\\sum_{l=0}^\\infty\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}(r/R)^lf_{lm}Y_{lm}(n),$$\n\nwith the constant degree-zero value at $r=0$. Then this is smooth and harmonic in the ball; its series and every derivative converge uniformly on every smaller closed ball, $\\sup_{0\\le t<1}\\|\\varphi_f(Rt\\,\\cdot)\\|_2\\le\\|f\\|_2$, and $\\varphi_f(Rt\\,\\cdot)\\to f$ in $L^2$ as $t\\uparrow1$; it is the unique harmonic scalar potential in the class with bounded radial $L^2$ norm and that $L^2$ boundary limit. With $E_f=-\\nabla\\varphi_f$, $B=0$, $\\rho=J=0$ and time-independent fields, this gives a full source-free electrostatic Maxwell model inside the ball under [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]. For continuous $f$ the same potential is\n\n$$\\varphi_f(x)=\\int_{S^2}\\frac{1-|x|^2/R^2}{4\\pi|x/R-n'|^3}f(n')\\,d\\sigma(n'),$$\n\nand it extends continuously to the closed ball with value $f(n)$ at $Rn$; the angular Poisson kernel is positive of mass one. No boundary value or boundary electric-field trace for the exterior arbitrary-$L^1$ density is inferred, and no derivative boundary regularity for arbitrary $f$ is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Scale the spherical multipole expansion of [[thm-em-spherical-multipole-basis]] (a)-(b) by the constant $(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}$, exactly as in [[pthm-em-electric-dipole-expansion]]: since $N\\rho(x)=(4\\pi)^{-1}\\int\\rho(y)|x-y|^{-1}d\\lambda_3(y)$ for the observation points considered, $\\varphi=(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}\\int\\rho(y)|x-y|^{-1}d\\lambda_3(y)$, and part (a) of the supplier gives the displayed series, its absolute and uniform convergence on exterior regions, and the stated tail bound, all multiplied by the constant $(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}$ ([[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]] for scaling the integral, [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]). The coefficients $q_{lm}$ are absolutely convergent by the addition theorem's bound $\\sum_{m=1}^{2l+1}Y_{lm}(n)^2=(2l+1)/(4\\pi)$ of [[lem-em-spherical-harmonic-basis-and-addition]], which gives $|Y_{lm}|\\le((2l+1)/(4\\pi))^{1/2}$, and by $|y|\\le a$ on the support ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]). For (b): the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ identifications and the remainder bound are parts of the supplier's clause (a) (with $Q=\\int\\rho$, $p=\\int y\\rho(y)d\\lambda_3(y)$ and the bound $16a^2\\|\\rho\\|_1/|x|^3$ for $|x|\\ge2a$), scaled by $(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)^{-1}$, and they reproduce [[pthm-em-electric-dipole-expansion]] (a). For the degree-$2$ term, use the addition theorem at $l=2$: $\\sum_mY_{2m}(n)Y_{2m}(y/|y|)=\\frac{5}{4\\pi}P_2(n\\cdot y/|y|)$, so that\n$$\\sum_{m=1}^{5}Y_{2m}(n)q_{2m}=\\frac{5}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|y|^2P_2\\Bigl(n\\cdot\\frac{y}{|y|}\\Bigr)\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y),$$\nand the second Legendre polynomial is fixed by the defining identity $(1-2qz+q^2)^{-1/2}=\\sum_{l\\ge0}q^lP_l(z)$ of [[lem-em-convergent-legendre-kernel]]: the coefficient of $q^2$ in the Taylor expansion of the left side at $q=0$ is $\\frac12\\frac{d^2}{dq^2}(1-2qz+q^2)^{-1/2}\\big|_{q=0}=\\frac{3z^2-1}{2}$ (the first and second derivatives computed by the chain rule, [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]]), while that coefficient on the right side is $P_2(z)$ (uniqueness of Taylor coefficients, [[def-taylor-polynomial-and-remainder]], [[lem-taylor-polynomial-derivatives]]); hence $P_2(z)=(3z^2-1)/2$. Therefore $|y|^2P_2(n\\cdot y/|y|)=\\bigl(3(n\\cdot y)^2-|y|^2\\bigr)/2$, and\n$$\\sum_{m=1}^{5}Y_{2m}(n)q_{2m}=\\frac{5}{8\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\bigl(3(n\\cdot y)^2-|y|^2\\bigr)\\rho(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)=\\frac{5}{8\\pi}\\,n_iQ_{ij}n_j$$\nby the definition of $Q_{ij}$ in [[def-em-electric-moments]] (a) and linearity of the integral; the degree-$2$ term of (a) is then $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\cdot\\frac{4\\pi}{5}\\cdot\\frac{1}{r^3}\\cdot\\frac{5}{8\\pi}n_iQ_{ij}n_j=\\frac{n_iQ_{ij}n_j}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}$. For (c): part (b) of [[thm-em-spherical-multipole-basis]] gives that $\\varphi$ is $C^\\infty$ on $\\{|x|>a\\}$ and that the series may be differentiated termwise to every order with uniform convergence of each differentiated series on compact subsets; the displayed field series is the termwise gradient, with [[def-em-fields]] for the field object. The fixed-time dictionary is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e). All estimates use the triangle and monotonicity rules for the Lebesgue integral ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]), the $\\lambda_3$-a.e. support convention ([[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]], [[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]) and the declared Countable Choice of the measure, harmonic and Newtonian interfaces; no convergence at $r=a$ and no point-source assertion is made. For(e), apply [[thm-em-spherical-multipole-basis]](c), equivalently [[lem-em-ball-poisson-series-boundary-convergence]], to f in the unit variable z=x/R. The scaling gives exactly (r/R)^l, preserves the stated radial L2 norm/limit, and multiplies a derivative of order alpha by R^(-|alpha|), so every smaller-ball derivative convergence and uniqueness follows. For continuous f the same substitution yields the displayed angular Poisson kernel with total mass one; no accidental factor R² is introduced since integration is on the unit sphere. Harmonicity gives div E_f=-Delta phi_f0 and smooth mixed-derivative cancellation gives curl E_f0 via [[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]]. B and all time/source terms vanish, proving all four adopted equations inside the ball. The branch has independent boundary datum f and no density rho in that ball; it restores the approved H3 content without a false trace conclusion at the exterior support radius a.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 3.4.1 printed pp. 148-149 (the general multipole expansion V = (1/4 pi epsilon_0) sum over n of (1/r^{n+1}) integral (r')^n P_n(cos theta') rho d tau', Eqs. (3.95)-(3.96): monopole, dipole, quadrupole, octopole hierarchy). Read in the stamped scanned full text, OCR cross-checked."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.2.3 printed pp. 20-22 (the multipole expansion in spherical harmonics; the traceless quadrupole Q_ij and its potential term Delta phi = r_i r_j Q_ij / (2 . 4 pi epsilon_0 r^5)). Read in the stamped full text."
              },
