      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-em-quadrupole-symmetry",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Four stationary point charges with vanishing total charge and dipole moment and traceless quadrupole diag(6qa^2,-6qa^2,0)",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X2",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-electric-moments",
            "pthm-em-moment-origin-change",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-finite-sum",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-electric-moments": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-moment-origin-change": "physical-result",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-finite-sum": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "statement": "Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $a>0$, let $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be the $\\delta$-orthonormal positively oriented basis of the laboratory frame ([[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]), and consider the four stationary point charges of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (c): $+q$ at $ae_1$, $+q$ at $-ae_1$, $-q$ at $ae_2$, $-q$ at $-ae_2$. Then:\n\n(a) $Q=0$ and $p=0$; more generally every odd Cartesian moment vanishes, $M_{i_1\\dots i_{2k+1}}=0$ for every $k\\ge0$ and all indices, because the configuration is invariant under the central reflection $x\\mapsto-x$ with the charges preserved;\n\n(b) the degree-$2$ Cartesian moment is $M_{ij}=2qa^2\\bigl(\\delta_{i1}\\delta_{j1}-\\delta_{i2}\\delta_{j2}\\bigr)$, i.e. $\\operatorname{diag}(2qa^2,-2qa^2,0)$ in the frame basis, and its trace vanishes;\n\n(c) the traceless quadrupole is $Q_{ij}=6qa^2\\bigl(\\delta_{i1}\\delta_{j1}-\\delta_{i2}\\delta_{j2}\\bigr)=\\operatorname{diag}(6qa^2,-6qa^2,0)$ and $\\sum_iQ_{ii}=0$; since $Q=p=0$, it is independent of the reference point by the origin-shift theorem ([[pthm-em-moment-origin-change]] (d));\n\n(d) [Scope] this is a finite source-moment computation for prescribed stationary point charges. No field, force, energy (in particular no claim that the self-energy of the point charges is finite), dynamics, stability or empirical content is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Insert the four points into the finite sums of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (c) and use $\\delta$-orthonormality of the frame basis: $Q=q+q-q-q=0$ and $p=q(ae_1)+q(-ae_1)+(-q)(ae_2)+(-q)(-ae_2)=0$. For (a), the configuration is invariant under $x\\mapsto-x$ with the charge of each point preserved ($+q$ at $\\pm ae_1$ and $-q$ at $\\pm ae_2$), so pairing each point with its image and reindexing the finite sum ([[def-finite-sum]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]]) gives $M_{i_1\\dots i_{2k+1}}=\\sum_k q_k x_k^{\\otimes(2k+1)}=0$: the terms at $x$ and $-x$ contribute $q\\bigl(1+(-1)^{2k+1}\\bigr)x^{\\otimes(2k+1)}=0$. For (b), direct substitution gives $M_{ij}=q(ae_1)_i(ae_1)_j+q(-ae_1)_i(-ae_1)_j-q(ae_2)_i(ae_2)_j-q(-ae_2)_i(-ae_2)_j=2qa^2\\bigl(e_{1i}e_{1j}-e_{2i}e_{2j}\\bigr)=2qa^2\\bigl(\\delta_{i1}\\delta_{j1}-\\delta_{i2}\\delta_{j2}\\bigr)$, whose trace $2qa^2(1-1)$ vanishes; the components are read in the $\\delta$-orthonormal basis ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]). For (c), use $Q_{ij}=\\sum_kq_k\\bigl(3x_{k,i}x_{k,j}-|x_k|^2\\delta_{ij}\\bigr)=3M_{ij}-\\delta_{ij}\\sum_kq_k|x_k|^2$ from [[def-em-electric-moments]] (c); each $|x_k|=a$, so $\\sum_kq_k|x_k|^2=a^2(q+q-q-q)=0$ and $Q_{ij}=3M_{ij}=6qa^2\\bigl(\\delta_{i1}\\delta_{j1}-\\delta_{i2}\\delta_{j2}\\bigr)$, with trace $6qa^2(1-1)=0$; the reference-point independence for $Q=p=0$ is clause (d) of [[pthm-em-moment-origin-change]]. The equality $|x_k|=a$ for $x_k=\\pm ae_1,\\pm ae_2$ uses $\\delta(e_r,e_r)=1$ ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]) and the Euclidean norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] (with [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]] for reading norms of coordinate vectors). Clause (d) records that no field, energy or dynamics is computed. No Choice axiom is used: all sums are finite ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]]).",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 3.4.2 printed pp. 150-151 (the four charges at the corners of a square, Fig. 3.30: combining the charges in pairs makes the net dipole moment vanish and the configuration is a quadrupole; Eq. (3.100), the dipole moment of a collection of point charges). Read in the stamped scanned full text, OCR cross-checked."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 3rd revised English edition (Pergamon 1971, 387 pp.; full text)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 41 printed p. 98 (the quadrupole moment D_ab = sum e (3 x_a x_b - r^2 delta_ab) by inserting the charges, and its tracelessness D_aa = 0, Eqs. (41.3)-(41.4); Gaussian units re-expressed in SI). Read in the stamped full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 2.2.3 printed pp. 21-22 (the quadrupole moment Q_ij = integral rho (3 r'_i r'_j - delta_ij r'^2) as the next multipole after the dipole). Read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A finite source-moment computation for four stationary point charges in a named inertial frame: vanishing total charge and dipole moment, vanishing odd Cartesian moments, and the exact traceless quadrupole $\\operatorname{diag}(6qa^2,-6qa^2,0)$. No field, force, self-energy, dynamics, stability or empirical claim is made."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-two-charge-dipole",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Exact two-charge dipole potential, its moments and the explicit far-field remainder",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X2",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-electric-dipole-expansion",
            "def-em-electric-moments",
