          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), inherited from the cited distribution and Lebesgue interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and $d\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, put $a:=|d|/2$, and let the source be the two stationary point charges $+q$ at $d/2$ and $-q$ at $-d/2$ (charge parameters in coulombs and the constant $\\varepsilon_0$ of [[def-em-si-conventions]]), with fixed-time reading $q\\delta_{d/2}-q\\delta_{-d/2}$ ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e), [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]]). Define, with arbitrary values at the two points,\n\n$$\\varphi(x):=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x-d/2|}-\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0|x+d/2|}\\qquad\\bigl(x\\notin\\{d/2,-d/2\\}\\bigr).$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) [Exact potential and equations] $\\varphi$ is locally integrable on $\\mathbb R^3$ and of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{d/2,-d/2\\}$; with $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ on that set ([[def-em-fields]]), the pair $(E,0)$ satisfies the distributional pure electrostatic equations\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\frac{q\\,\\delta_{d/2}-q\\,\\delta_{-d/2}}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=0\\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$$\n\nof the fixed-time dictionary of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e) ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d) for each point charge, added by linearity of distributional differentiation: [[def-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]], [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]], [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]]);\n\n(b) [Moments] in the finite-sum branch of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (c) about the chart origin of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], $Q=0$, $p=qd$, every even Cartesian moment vanishes, $M_{i_1\\dots i_{2k+1}}=2q\\,(d/2)_{i_1}\\cdots(d/2)_{i_{2k+1}}$ for every $k\\ge0$, and the traceless quadrupole vanishes, $Q_{ij}=0$;\n\n(c) [Exact far-field comparison] for every $x$ with $r:=|x|\\ge2a=|d|$, the exact difference obeys the expansion form of [[pthm-em-electric-dipole-expansion]] with the point-pair total variation in place of $\\|\\rho\\|_1$:\n\n$$\\varphi(x)=\\frac{p\\cdot x}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}+R(x),\\qquad |R(x)|\\le\\frac{16\\,a^2\\,(|q|+|q|)}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}=\\frac{8|q|a^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3};$$\n\n(d) [Comparison with the compact-density theorem] the hypothesis of [[pthm-em-electric-dipole-expansion]] is an $L^1$ density; for $q\\ne0$ the point pair has no such density, while for $q=0$ its source is the zero density. In either case the proof below computes the two kernels directly; the identity and bound of (c) are proved directly for the two kernels by the twice-fundamental-theorem computation used in that theorem's remainder, and the leading term, the $a^2$ decay and the appearance of the total variation agree with the compact-density expansion;\n\n(e) [Scope] the potential and the distributional equations are stated for the idealised singular source; no self-energy, self-force, dynamics, bound-state, stability or empirical claim is made for the pair. The quadrupole in (b) is computed about the specified symmetric origin; at a general reference point $b$ its value is $-3(b_ip_j+b_jp_i)+2(b\\cdot p)\\delta_{ij}$, since $Q=0$ and $Q_{ij}=0$.",
          "strategy": "The individual point-charge objects and their distributional identities are those of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d): for a charge $q'$ at $x_0$, $\\varphi_{q'}$ is locally integrable, $C^\\infty$ off $x_0$, $E_{q'}=-\\nabla\\varphi_{q'}$ off $x_0$, and $\\operatorname{div}E_{q'}=(q'/\\varepsilon_0)\\delta_{x_0}$, $\\operatorname{curl}E_{q'}=0$ in $\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^3)$. Summing the two locally integrable potentials gives the displayed $\\varphi$ (values at the two points are immaterial, [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]], [[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]), and the distributional derivative of the sum is the sum of the distributional derivatives ([[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]], [[def-distribution]], [[def-distributional-derivative]]); adding the two point-charge identities componentwise and using [[def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives]] gives (a), and the fixed-time dictionary is [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e). For (b), substitute into the finite sums of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (c): $Q=q+(-q)=0$, $p=q(d/2)+(-q)(-d/2)=qd$, and $M_{i_1\\dots i_l}=q(d/2)^{\\otimes l}+(-q)(-d/2)^{\\otimes l}=q\\bigl(1-(-1)^l\\bigr)(d/2)^{\\otimes l}$, which vanishes for even $l$ and equals $2q(d/2)^{\\otimes l}$ for odd $l$; since $M^{(2)}=0$ and $\\sum_kq_k|x_k|^2=qa^2+(-q)a^2=0$, the traceless quadrupole $Q_{ij}=3M_{ij}-\\delta_{ij}\\sum_kq_k|x_k|^2$ vanishes, at the chosen chart origin. Here $Q=0$ but $p=qd$ is generally nonzero. By [[pthm-em-moment-origin-change]] (d), the quadrupole at reference point b is $-3(b_i p_j+b_jp_i)+2(b\\cdot p)\\delta_{ij}$; hence it is not generally origin invariant. If $q=0$, all moments and the potential vanish and the invariance special case holds ([[def-finite-sum]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). For (c), apply the twice-fundamental-theorem remainder computation to each of the two kernels directly, since the density theorem does not apply to the pair. Write $h_y(s):=|x-sy|^{-1}$ for $y\\in\\{d/2,-d/2\\}$; for $r\\ge2a$ and $s\\in[0,1]$ one has $|x-sy|\\ge r-a\\ge r/2$, the chain rule gives $h_y'(s)=\\frac{(x-sy)\\cdot y}{|x-sy|^3}$ and $h_y''(s)=-\\frac{|y|^2}{|x-sy|^3}+3\\frac{((x-sy)\\cdot y)^2}{|x-sy|^5}$ with $|h_y''(s)|\\le4|y|^2/|x-sy|^3\\le32|y|^2/r^3=32a^2/r^3$, and the identity $h_y(1)-h_y(0)-h_y'(0)=\\int_0^1(1-t)h_y''(t)\\,dt$ is the fundamental theorem applied to $\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl[(1-t)h_y'(t)+h_y(t)\\bigr]=(1-t)h_y''(t)$ ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]], [[def-metric-ball]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), so $h_y(1)=1/r+x\\cdot y/r^3+R_y(x)$ with $|R_y(x)|\\le16|y|^2/r^3$ ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). Substituting the two kernels with their charges and using $|y|=a$, with the two linear terms adding to $q(x\\cdot d)/r^3=p\\cdot x/r^3$,\n$$\\varphi(x)-\\frac{p\\cdot x}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\bigl(R_{d/2}(x)-R_{-d/2}(x)\\bigr),\\qquad \\Bigl|\\varphi(x)-\\frac{p\\cdot x}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{|q|(16a^2+16a^2)}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}=\\frac{32|q|a^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3}=\\frac{8|q|a^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^3},$$\nwhich is (c); reading it against [[pthm-em-electric-dipole-expansion]] gives (d). The two vector terms use the Euclidean inner product and norm ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]); no differentiation of $\\varphi$ beyond the off-pole gradient is used and no value at the poles is asserted. For (e), no field energy is computed and the self-energy of a point charge is not claimed finite here. Countable Choice is used only through the cited distribution and Lebesgue interfaces; the potential and field of each charge are the idealised singular objects of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d) and [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e). The h_y parameter remainder is a continuous finite Riemann integral: its bound may use [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]] on [0,1]. All volume/source Lebesgue estimates instead use the nonnegative-integral rules. This distinguishes the actual Darboux use from the Lebesgue uses. For q!=0, shrinking smooth bumps from [[lem-scaled-euclidean-bumps-with-compact-support-and-gradient-bound]] at d/2 exclude -d/2, pair with the source as q and tend to zero a.e.; any proposed locally integrable density pairs to zero in the limit by [[thm-dominated-convergence]] with |g| on one fixed compact ball, a contradiction. For q=0 the source is the zero density and the potential is zero off the poles. At a shifted origin, contract the zero quadrupole with p and then p again: -3|p|^2b-(b.p)p=0 implies b.p=0 and b=0 for p!=0; q=0 instead gives zero at every origin.",
