          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-distribution",
            "def-distributional-derivative",
            "def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function",
            "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "thm-lebesgue-product-measure-agrees-with-euclidean-lebesgue-on-borel-sets",
            "prop-countable-subsets-of-rn-are-lebesgue-null",
            "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains",
            "thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $a>0$, write $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ and $\\rho=\\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2}$, let\n$$S:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\rho=a\\}$$\nbe the circular cylinder of radius $a$ about the third coordinate axis, with unit normal $e_\\rho=\\rho^{-1}(x_1,x_2,0)$ pointing from the interior region $\\{\\rho<a\\}$ to the exterior region $\\{\\rho>a\\}$, and let $e_\\varphi=\\rho^{-1}(-x_2,x_1,0)$ and $e_3=(0,0,1)$. Let $g:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be the piecewise constant field\n$$g(x):=\\begin{cases}e_3,&\\rho<a,\\\\ 0,&\\rho>a,\\end{cases}$$\nextended arbitrarily on the three-dimensional Lebesgue-null set $S$, and let $T_g$ be its regular distribution ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]). For an $\\mathbb R^3$-valued distribution $T=(T_1,T_2,T_3)$ whose components are scalar distributions of [[def-distribution]], a scalar test $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ and a vector test $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$, the pairings used below are the componentwise readings of [[def-distributional-derivative]]:\n$$\\langle\\operatorname{div}T,\\varphi\\rangle:=-\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\langle T_i,\\partial_i\\varphi\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle\\operatorname{curl}T,\\psi\\rangle:=\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\langle T_i,(\\operatorname{curl}\\psi)_i\\rangle .$$\nThen for every vector test $\\psi$ and every scalar test $\\varphi$,\n$$\\langle\\operatorname{curl}T_g,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_S e_\\varphi\\cdot\\psi\\,dS,\\qquad \\langle\\operatorname{div}T_g,\\varphi\\rangle=0,$$\nthat is, $\\operatorname{curl}T_g=e_\\varphi\\,\\delta_S$ and $\\operatorname{div}T_g=0$ in the distributional sense of [[def-distributional-derivative]]. The surface integral is that of [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]; since $\\psi$ has compact support it equals the integral over any compact cylinder segment containing $\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\cap S$. Equivalently, with $g_\\pm$ the one-sided values and the normal $e_\\rho$ from the minus to the plus side, $g_+-g_-=-e_3$, $e_\\rho\\times(g_+-g_-)=e_\\varphi$ and $e_\\rho\\cdot(g_+-g_-)=0$. No product of distributions is formed.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct; the nullity of the cylinder surface, the divergence theorem on the cylinder, and two coordinate computations.\n\n1.1 The cylinder $S=\\{x:x_1^2+x_2^2=a^2\\}$ is closed, hence Borel. For each $(x_2,x_3)\\in\\mathbb R^2$, its section $S^{(x_2,x_3)}:=\\{x_1\\in\\mathbb R:(x_1,x_2,x_3)\\in S\\}$ consists of at most the two values $\\pm\\sqrt{a^2-x_2^2}$ when $|x_2|\\le a$, and is empty otherwise. Each section is $\\lambda_1$-null by [F4]. Tonelli and product-measure agreement [F4] give $\\lambda_3(S)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\lambda_1(S^{(x_2,x_3)})\\,d\\lambda_2=0$. [given, F4]\n\n2.1 The bounded Borel function $g_0:=e_3\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\rho<a\\}}$ agrees with $g$ off the null set $S$ of step 1.1, so completeness [F5] makes $g$ Lebesgue measurable with the same almost-everywhere class as $g_0$; by [F5] this class is locally integrable. Hence $T_g:=T_{g_0}$ is a well-defined distribution by [F2], and for the tests of [F1] the componentwise pairings of [F3] read $\\langle\\operatorname{curl}T_g,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}g_0\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\psi\\,d\\lambda_3$ and $\\langle\\operatorname{div}T_g,\\varphi\\rangle=-\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}g_0\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3$, where the componentwise sums against the vector tests are the ordinary finite sums of the stated integrals. [given, F1, F2, F3, F5, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 Choose $R>a$ with $\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\cup\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi\\subset W:=\\{\\rho<R,\\ |x_3|<R\\}$ and put $V:=\\{\\rho<a,\\ |x_3|<R\\}$. Since $\\operatorname{curl}\\psi$ and $\\nabla\\varphi$ are supported in $W$, and $g_0$ equals $e_3$ on $V$, equals $0$ on the open set $W\\setminus\\overline V$, and the remaining part $W\\cap\\partial V$ lies in the null set $S$ of step 1.1, the two integrals of step 2.1 reduce to integrals over $V$: $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}g_0\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\psi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\int_V e_3\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\psi\\,d\\lambda_3$ and $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}g_0\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\int_V e_3\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3$. [given, step 1.1, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 The field $F_0:=\\varphi e_3$ is $C^1$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, hence on a neighborhood of $\\overline V$, and by [F9] its divergence is $\\operatorname{div}F_0=\\partial_3\\varphi=e_3\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi$. The divergence theorem [F7] on the presentation [F6] gives $\\int_V e_3\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\int_{\\partial V}\\varphi\\,e_3\\cdot\\nu\\,dS$. On the lateral face $\\nu=e_\\rho$ and $e_3\\cdot e_\\rho=0$ by [F8]; on the two disc caps $x_3=\\pm R$ the test $\\varphi$ vanishes because $\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi\\subset\\{|x_3|<R\\}$; hence $\\int_V e_3\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi\\,d\\lambda_3=0$. With steps 2.1 and 3.1 this proves $\\langle\\operatorname{div}T_g,\\varphi\\rangle=0$ for every scalar test. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F6, F7, F8, F9]\n\n4.2 The field $G_0:=\\psi\\times e_3$ is $C^1$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, and by [F8] its coordinates are $G_0=(\\psi_2,-\\psi_1,0)$, so by [F9] $\\operatorname{div}G_0=\\partial_1\\psi_2-\\partial_2\\psi_1=(\\operatorname{curl}\\psi)_3=e_3\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\psi$. The divergence theorem [F7] on the presentation [F6] gives $\\int_V e_3\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\psi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\int_{\\partial V}G_0\\cdot\\nu\\,dS$. The disc caps carry $\\psi=0$ by the support condition, while on the lateral face $\\nu=e_\\rho$ and $G_0\\cdot e_\\rho=(\\psi\\times e_3)\\cdot e_\\rho=\\psi\\cdot e_\\varphi$ by [F8]; therefore $\\int_V e_3\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}\\psi\\,d\\lambda_3=\\int_{\\rho=a,\\ |x_3|<R}e_\\varphi\\cdot\\psi\\,dS$, and by [F10] the last integral equals $\\int_S e_\\varphi\\cdot\\psi\\,dS$ because $e_\\varphi\\cdot\\psi$ is compactly supported and vanishes off the compact band $\\{\\rho=a,\\ |x_3|\\le R\\}$. With steps 2.1 and 3.1 this is $\\langle\\operatorname{curl}T_g,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_S e_\\varphi\\cdot\\psi\\,dS$ for every vector test, which is the claimed identification $\\operatorname{curl}T_g=e_\\varphi\\,\\delta_S$. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10]\n\n5.1 The one-sided values of $g$ across $S$ are $g_+=0$ and $g_-=e_3$, so $g_+-g_-=-e_3$ and, by [F8], $e_\\rho\\times(g_+-g_-)=e_\\rho\\times(-e_3)=e_\\varphi$ and $e_\\rho\\cdot(g_+-g_-)=e_\\rho\\cdot(-e_3)=0$, which is the stated equivalence. The two distributional identities of steps 4.1 and 4.2 involve only integrals of locally integrable functions against smooth tests, so no product of two distributions is formed anywhere; the only multiplication on $S$ is that of the compactly supported function $e_\\varphi\\cdot\\psi$ with the surface measure of [F10]. Countable Choice is used in step 1.1 through the product-measure and finite-null-set suppliers [F4] and is inherited only through the cited measure, surface and distribution interfaces [F11]; no stronger choice principle occurs. [step 4.1, step 4.2, F2, F4, F8, F10, F11] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
