        {
          "id": "lem-em-newtonian-kernel-curl-identity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Divergence and curl of the Newtonian potential of a smooth compact vector field",
          "reference": null,
          "design_state": "new-local-mathematical-prerequisite; complete-excision-and-limit-argument-supplied",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
            "def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign",
            "def-newtonian-potential",
            "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface",
            "lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade",
            "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains",
            "thm-dominated-convergence",
            "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
            "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ and define componentwise\n$$A_i(x):=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{f_i(y)}{|x-y|}\\,dy,\\qquad A:=(A_1,A_2,A_3),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^3,$$\nso that $A=N(f)$ componentwise with the Newtonian-potential transform $N$ of [[lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade]] and [[def-newtonian-potential]]. Then $A\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$, both integrals below are absolutely convergent for every $x$, and for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$\n$$\\operatorname{div}A(x)=N(\\operatorname{div}f)(x)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{(\\operatorname{div}f)(y)}{|x-y|}\\,dy,$$\n$$\\operatorname{curl}A(x)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{f(y)\\times(x-y)}{|x-y|^3}\\,dy .$$\nThe curl identity needs no divergence-free hypothesis and holds pointwise at every point of $\\mathbb R^3$, including points of the support of $f$; the kernel is differentiated, its smoothness at the pole is not assumed. Countable Choice is inherited from the smooth Newtonian supplier and the polar-coordinate and surface-measure interfaces of the estimates.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** the componentwise derivative identity of the smooth Newtonian upgrade, then a finite excision (spherical shell) integration by parts and one dominated limit.\n\n1.1 Since each $f_i$ is smooth and compactly supported, [F2] gives $A_i=N(f_i)\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ with $D^\\alpha A_i=N(D^\\alpha f_i)$ for every multi-index $\\alpha$; in particular $A\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ by [F3], and $\\partial_iA_i=N(\\partial_if_i)$ for each $i$. Summing over $i$ and using finite additivity of the integral [F8], $\\operatorname{div}A=\\sum_i\\partial_iA_i=N(\\sum_i\\partial_if_i)=N(\\operatorname{div}f)$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$, which is the first displayed identity; its integral is absolutely convergent because $|\\operatorname{div}f|\\le\\|\\operatorname{div}f\\|_\\infty$ is bounded and compactly supported and $\\int_{B_R(x)}|x-y|^{-1}d\\lambda_3<+\\infty$ by [F7]. [given, F1, F2, F3, F7, F8]\n\n1.2 Fix $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $j,k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)$, and choose $R>0$ with $\\operatorname{supp}f\\subset B_R(x)$. For $0<\\varepsilon<R$ let $\\Omega_\\varepsilon:=B_R(x)\\setminus\\overline{B_\\varepsilon(x)}$, presented as in [F5]. On a neighborhood of the compact closure the scalar product $y\\mapsto f_k(y)\\Phi(x-y)$ is $C^1$, and by [F4] its partial derivative is $\\partial_{y_j}(f_k\\Phi(x-y))=(\\partial_jf_k)(y)\\Phi(x-y)+f_k(y)\\partial_{y_j}\\Phi(x-y)$. Applying the divergence theorem [F5] to the field $f_k\\Phi(x-\\cdot)e_j$ on $\\Omega_\\varepsilon$ gives $\\int_{\\Omega_\\varepsilon}(\\partial_jf_k)\\Phi(x-y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)=\\int_{\\partial B_R(x)}f_k\\Phi(x-y)(\\nu_R)_j\\,dS+\\int_{\\partial B_\\varepsilon(x)}f_k\\Phi(x-y)(\\nu_\\varepsilon)_j\\,dS-\\int_{\\Omega_\\varepsilon}f_k(y)\\partial_{y_j}\\Phi(x-y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$. The outer sphere contributes $0$ because $f=0$ there; the inner sphere term is bounded in absolute value, by [F8] and the bound $|\\Phi|=1/(4\\pi|y-x|)=1/(4\\pi\\varepsilon)$ there, by $\\|f_k\\|_\\infty\\frac1{4\\pi\\varepsilon}\\,|\\partial B_\\varepsilon(x)|=\\|f_k\\|_\\infty\\frac{|S^2|}{4\\pi}\\varepsilon\\to0$ as $\\varepsilon\\to0$ by [F6]. [given, F4, F5, F6, F8]\n\n2.1 Let $\\varepsilon\\to0$ in the identity of step 1.2. The shell integrands $(\\partial_jf_k)\\Phi(x-y)\\mathbf 1_{\\Omega_\\varepsilon}$ are dominated on $B_R(x)$ by $\\|\\partial_jf_k\\|_\\infty|\\Phi|$, and the shell integrands $f_k\\partial_{y_j}\\Phi(x-y)\\mathbf 1_{\\Omega_\\varepsilon}$ by $\\|f_k\\|_\\infty|\\partial_{y_j}\\Phi|\\le\\|f_k\\|_\\infty\\frac{1}{4\\pi}|x-y|^{-2}$; both majorants are integrable on $B_R(x)$ by [F7], and both families converge pointwise off the null singleton $\\{x\\}$ to the corresponding unrestricted integrands. Dominated convergence [F9] therefore gives $\\int_{B_R(x)}(\\partial_jf_k)\\Phi(x-y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)=-\\int_{B_R(x)}f_k(y)\\partial_{y_j}\\Phi(x-y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$; the left side is $N(\\partial_jf_k)(x)=\\partial_jA_k(x)$ by step 1.1, and since $\\partial_{y_j}\\Phi(x-y)=\\frac{x_j-y_j}{4\\pi|x-y|^3}$ for $y\\ne x$, this reads $\\partial_jA_k(x)=-\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{f_k(y)(x_j-y_j)}{|x-y|^3}\\,dy$, the integral being absolutely convergent because $|f_k(y)(x_j-y_j)|\\le\\|f_k\\|_\\infty|x-y|$ on $B_R(x)$ and $\\int_{B_R(x)}|x-y|^{-2}d\\lambda_3<+\\infty$ by [F7]. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F4, F7, F9]\n\n3.1 Summing the identity of step 2.1 with the alternating symbol, $(\\operatorname{curl}A)_i=\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\partial_jA_k=-\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\varepsilon_{ijk}f_k(y)(x_j-y_j)}{|x-y|^3}\\,dy=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{(f(y)\\times(x-y))_i}{|x-y|^3}\\,dy$ by the component identity of [F10] and finite linearity of the integral [F8]; as this holds for each $i$, $\\operatorname{curl}A(x)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{f(y)\\times(x-y)}{|x-y|^3}\\,dy$, and the integral is absolutely convergent because $|f(y)\\times(x-y)|\\le\\|f\\|_\\infty|x-y|$ and $\\int_{B_R(x)}|x-y|^{-2}<+\\infty$ by [F7]. With step 1.1 this proves both displayed identities at the arbitrary point $x$; the point was allowed to lie in $\\operatorname{supp}f$, since the excision argument only removed a ball around it and used the bound on the differentiated kernel, so no smoothness of the kernel at the pole is assumed. Countable Choice is used only through the polar and surface-measure interfaces [F11] and the stated choice assumption of the smooth-newtonian supplier, and no stronger principle occurs. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F7, F8, F10, F11] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.3.2 printed pp. 222-225 (the product-rule computation of $\\nabla\\cdot B$ and $\\nabla\\times B$ from the volume Biot-Savart integral, with the switching $\\nabla\\to-\\nabla'$ and the integration by parts (5.50)-(5.53) that this lemma makes rigorous at the singular kernel); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
