        {
          "id": "post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Physical adoption of the declared ideal magnetostatic loop, line and cylindrical-sheet models",
          "reference": null,
          "design_state": "explicit-source-backed-model-adoption",
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. In the named inertial laboratory model of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with the positive constants and SI conventions of [[def-em-si-conventions]], adopt the following additional classical physical idealization: a loop, infinite straight line or infinite cylindrical sheet source defined by [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c1)-(c3), with zero charge density, is modeled by a time-independent $B$ satisfying exactly the admissibility predicate (c4), together with $E=0$ and $\\rho=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$ hold distributionally on $\\mathbb R^3$, with the stated off-source regularity, symmetry and decay; the electrostatic and temporal Maxwell identities are zero. This physical adoption extends to these explicitly declared source types only. It is separate from the earlier $L^1$-source and point/compact-sheet postulates, which do not include these line and noncompact sheet sources. No existence, uniqueness, finite self-energy, force product, self-force, apparatus accuracy, matter dynamics or empirical certainty is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Nonproof formulation review. Tong chapter3 printed pp.41-46 and51 adopts steady neutral currents, infinite wire and solenoid idealizations and the thin-wire Biot-Savart model. Here those physical assumptions are adopted with exact source functionals and compact-test equations from the preceding definition. Mathematical existence and compatibility are separately verified by the line/sheet lemmas and the strengthened thin-current supplier; textbook model adoption is not a proof of them.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge lecture notes",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 3 printed pp. 41-46 and 51: neutral steady-current reduction, infinite wire, infinite cylindrical solenoid and thin-wire model. These sections were read in the available full 236-page PDF text; no whole-book reading is claimed."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Additional classical physical adoption in the named inertial laboratory for the exact ideal closed loop, infinite straight line and infinite cylindrical sheet models, with $E=0$ and $\\rho=0$. No experiment, matter dynamics or self-force.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-magnetic-moment",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The magnetic dipole moment of a compact conserved current and of the declared ideal thin loop",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-magnetic-moment",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable",
            "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties"
          ],
          "statement": "Let the compact smooth current class and the declared ideal models be those of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]], with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n**(a) [Compact smooth currents].** Let $J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J=0$, extended by zero to $\\mathbb R^3$. The **magnetic dipole moment** of $J$ is the vector\n$$m:=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}y\\times J(y)\\,dy\\in\\mathbb R^3,$$\nof unit A m$^2$. The integral is computed componentwise over any nondegenerate closed bounding rectangle $Q$ containing $\\operatorname{supp}J$, using the continuous functions $y\\mapsto(y\\times J(y))_i$ on $Q$ and their zero extension outside $Q$. Its value is independent of the chosen larger bounding rectangle by finite rectangular subdivision and vanishing of the integrand off its support; the support itself need not be Jordan measurable. Thus this is a Riemann integral of continuous compactly supported functions ([[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]], [[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]); it is absolutely finite because $J$ is bounded with compact support.\n\n**(b) [Declared ideal thin loop].** For a closed embedded $C^2$ curve $\\gamma:\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with $|\\gamma'|\\equiv1$ and $I\\in\\mathbb R$, the **magnetic moment** of the ideal loop current of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c1) is\n$$m:=\\frac I2\\int_0^L\\gamma(s)\\times\\gamma'(s)\\,ds,$$\nagain a componentwise Riemann integral over the compact interval $[0,L]$ of continuous functions; for the circle $\\gamma(s)=a(\\cos(s/a),\\sin(s/a),0)$ traversed in the positive orientation this is $m=I\\pi a^2e_3$.\n\n**(c) [Status and scope].** In both clauses $m$ is a source moment of the declared current model: it is not a rest mass, not an intrinsic spin and not an observed quantity. The definition asserts no far-field property of $m$ (that is the content of [[pthm-em-magnetic-dipole-field]] under its hypotheses), no agreement between the two clauses in a common limit, and no moment for currents outside the two classes; no higher multipole is defined here. The finite Riemann moment definitions and circular-loop evaluation themselves are choice-free; no distribution-embedding or surface-measure theorem is used to compute these moments.",
          "strategy": "Two type definitions with their well-definedness checks. (a) The map $y\\mapsto y\\times J(y)$ is continuous on every closed bounding rectangle $Q$ containing its support and vanishes off that support, so each component is Riemann integrable on $Q$ ([[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]]); boundedness on $Q$ makes the integral absolutely finite. For two bounding rectangles take a common larger rectangle and subdivide it at their face coordinates; all extra cells have zero integrand off the support, so finite additivity identifies both values without integrating over the support itself, and the units follow from $y$ in metres and $J$ in A m$^{-2}$ ([[def-em-si-conventions]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). (b) $\\gamma$ and $\\gamma'$ are continuous on the compact circle and their cross product is periodic, so the componentwise integral over $[0,L]$ exists; for the circle $\\gamma\\times\\gamma'=ae_3$ and $\\int_0^{2\\pi a}ae_3\\,ds=2\\pi a^2e_3$, giving $m=I\\pi a^2e_3$. (c) records the status: the moment is a model quantity of an idealised source, and its far-field interpretation belongs to [[pthm-em-magnetic-dipole-field]]; no measurement, uncertainty or empirical claim is made. The displayed finite Riemann computations use no Choice axiom. The physical/distributional interpretations of source models retain their separately stated premises.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.4.3 printed pp. 242-246 (multipole expansion of the vector potential; the line-current and volume-current magnetic moments, including the volume-current formula (5.91)) and Problem 5.34 printed p. 246 (circular-loop moment); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 3.3.1-3.3.2 printed pp. 54-57 (the current loop and the general distribution, with $m=\\frac12\\int d^3r'\\,r'\\times J(r')$, eq. (3.24)); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 44 printed pp. 103-105 (definition of the magnetic moment $m=\\frac1{2c}\\sum e\\,r\\times v$ of a system of charges and its use for the average field); read in the stamped 387-page full text, re-expressed in SI."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The magnetic moment defined here is a source moment of two declared current models (the compact smooth conserved current and the ideal closed thin loop). It is not a rest mass, not an intrinsic spin and not an empirically measured quantity; the definition asserts no far-field property, no equivalence between the two clauses and no moment for any other source. No measurement, apparatus or uncertainty is involved.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties": "mathematical-premise"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Coulomb-gauge Newtonian vector potential for a compact smooth conserved current",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K4",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
            "lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function",
            "thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes",
            "cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J=0$ be a compact smooth current of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b), extended by zero to $\\mathbb R^3$, and let $\\mu_0>0$, $c>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ be the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Define, for $i\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$,\n$$A_i(x):=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{J_i(y)}{|x-y|}\\,dy=\\mu_0\\,N(J_i)(x),\\qquad A:=(A_1,A_2,A_3),$$\nthe componentwise Newtonian potentials of [[lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade]]. Then:\n\n(a) $A\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ and $-\\Delta A=\\mu_0J$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$, componentwise ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]);\n\n(b) $\\operatorname{div}A=0$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$: the Coulomb-gauge condition holds;\n\n(c) the field $B:=\\operatorname{curl}A$ is of class $C^\\infty$ and satisfies the magnetostatic pair of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](a) pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$,\n$$\\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J ;$$\nconsequently $E=0$, $\\rho=0$, with $B,J$ independent of time, gives an explicit full smooth-Maxwell completion on $\\mathbb R^3\\times I$ for every open time interval $I$ under [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]. More generally any compatible steady electrostatic pair satisfying $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ can be combined with $B,J$; no electrostatic existence theorem is implied;\n\n(d) [Scope] $A$ is a vector potential for $B$ in the Coulomb gauge; the gauge condition is a construction choice and no uniqueness of $A$ or of $B$ is asserted. The construction is carried out for the compact smooth class only: nothing is claimed for the declared ideal models of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c), for noncompact currents or for currents of weaker regularity.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** componentwise reduction to the Newtonian-potential lemma and the standard second-derivative vector identities. 1.1 For each $i$ the datum $J_i$ is smooth and compactly supported, so [[lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade]] gives $A_i=\\mu_0N(J_i)\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$, $D^\\alpha A_i=\\mu_0N(D^\\alpha J_i)$ for every multi-index $\\alpha$ and $-\\Delta A_i=\\mu_0J_i$ pointwise ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]); hence (a). 2.1 Take $\\alpha=e_i$ in the same derivative identity. Since $\\operatorname{div}J=\\sum_i\\partial_iJ_i=0$ and $N$ is componentwise linear, $$\\operatorname{div}A=\\sum_i\\partial_iA_i=\\mu_0\\sum_iN(\\partial_iJ_i)=\\mu_0N(\\operatorname{div}J)=0$$ pointwise ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]); this is (b), and $\\operatorname{div}J=0$ is used nowhere else. 3.1 $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ is $C^\\infty$ because $A$ is. Then $\\operatorname{div}B=\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}A=0$ by [[thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes]], and $$\\operatorname{curl}B=\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}A=\\nabla(\\operatorname{div}A)-\\Delta A=\\mu_0J$$ by [[cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field]] combined with (b) and (a). This is the pointwise magnetostatic pair of (c). 3.2 Reading (c) inside the framework: [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](a) identifies the magnetostatic pair as the reduction of the adopted equations of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] when $\\partial_tE=\\partial_tB=0$, with the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]: choose $E=\\rho=0$ and extend $B,J$ independently of time; the electrostatic identities and both time derivatives then vanish. Alternatively a given compatible electrostatic pair supplies them. No unspecified electric field or charge density is being asserted to exist (the identity $c^2\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0=1$ is not used in this item). 3.3 (d) is scope bookkeeping: the gauge freedom and homogeneous solutions are not analysed here, and no claim is extended beyond the compact smooth class. Countable Choice is inherited through the cited measure, surface, regular-distribution and/or Newtonian supplier interfaces; no stronger choice axiom is used in this argument.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.4.1 printed pp. 234-240 (the vector potential, the Coulomb condition $\\nabla\\cdot A=0$ (5.61), the Poisson equation $\\nabla^2A=-\\mu_0J$ and $A=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int\\frac{J}{\\mathcal r}\\,d\\tau'$ (5.63)); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 3.2-3.2.3 printed pp. 46-52 (the vector potential, Coulomb gauge $\\nabla\\cdot A=0$ and the three Poisson equations $\\nabla^2A_i=-\\mu_0J_i$, eqs. (3.11)-(3.14)); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 43 printed pp. 101-103 (constant field: $\\operatorname{grad}\\operatorname{div}A-\\Delta A=-4\\pi j/c$ in Gaussian units, the auxiliary condition $\\operatorname{div}A=0$ and the Poisson solution (43.4)-(43.5)); read in the stamped 387-page full text, re-expressed in SI."
              },
              {
                "title": "W. C. Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory, Purdue University (Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Lecture 4 Section 4.1 printed pp. 39-41 (the vector Poisson equation (4.1.10) and its free-space solution (4.1.11)); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A conditional construction theorem for the compact smooth current class of the framework's steady-current regime (adopted Maxwell equations, one inertial frame, SI units). The Coulomb gauge is a construction choice; no uniqueness, no noncompact-current or singular-source statement and no empirical claim about any apparatus or observed field is made.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
