        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-biot-savart",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Biot-Savart representation for a compact smooth conserved current and its declared thin-loop limit",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K4",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 19,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential",
            "lem-em-newtonian-kernel-curl-identity",
            "lem-em-thin-current-limit",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J=0$ be a compact smooth current of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b), extended by zero to $\\mathbb R^3$, let $A$ be the vector potential of [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]] and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ its magnetostatic field, with the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Then for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ the absolutely convergent Biot-Savart integral represents the field,\n$$B(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{J(y)\\times(x-y)}{|x-y|^3}\\,dy .$$\nMoreover the same formula holds in the declared thin-loop limit, constructed in the all-space laboratory $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$: if $\\gamma:\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is a closed embedded $C^2$ curve parametrized by arc length ($|\\gamma'|\\equiv1$), $I\\in\\mathbb R$, and $j_\\varepsilon=j*\\eta_\\varepsilon$ are the mollified divergence-free compact currents of [[lem-em-thin-current-limit]] associated with the tangent line current of $\\gamma$, then their fields (given by the displayed formula with $J:=j_\\varepsilon$) converge, with every derivative uniformly on every compact $K$ disjoint from $\\gamma$, to\n$$B_\\gamma(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi}\\int_0^L\\frac{\\gamma'(s)\\times(x-\\gamma(s))}{|x-\\gamma(s)|^3}\\,ds,$$\na locally integrable global distributional field with $\\operatorname{div}B_\\gamma=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}B_\\gamma=\\mu_0J_\\gamma$, smoothness off the curve and decay at spatial infinity, hence the admitted field of the declared ideal thin loop under [[post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models]]. Its almost-everywhere representative on $\\mathbb R^3$ is supplied by [[lem-em-thin-current-limit]], independently of the away-from-curve convergence. Nothing is asserted on the wire, about line self-energy, about the infinite-wire or sheet models, or for currents outside the compact smooth class and its declared thin-loop limit.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** the differentiated-kernel identity of [[lem-em-newtonian-kernel-curl-identity]], then the thin-current limit of [[lem-em-thin-current-limit]]. 1.1 By [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]], $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ with $A_i=\\mu_0N(J_i)$. Applying [[lem-em-newtonian-kernel-curl-identity]] to $f:=J$ and multiplying by $\\mu_0$ gives, at every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $$B(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{J(y)\\times(x-y)}{|x-y|^3}\\,dy .$$ The integral is absolutely convergent: $|J(y)\\times(x-y)|\\le\\|J\\|_\\infty|x-y|$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]) and $\\int_{B_R(x)}|x-y|^{-2}\\,d\\lambda_3<+\\infty$, the estimate supplied by [[lem-em-newtonian-kernel-curl-identity]]; this proves the first assertion at every point, including points of the support. 2.1 Thin-loop limit: [[lem-em-thin-current-limit]] states that the tangent distribution $j$ of the closed curve has $\\operatorname{div}j=0$, that the mollified currents $j_\\varepsilon=j*\\eta_\\varepsilon$ are smooth, compactly supported and divergence-free, and that their Biot-Savart fields converge with every derivative uniformly on every compact $K$ disjoint from $\\gamma$ to the displayed loop integral. By step 1.1 with $J:=j_\\varepsilon$ (each $j_\\varepsilon$ is a compact smooth conserved current) the field of $j_\\varepsilon$ is exactly its displayed Biot-Savart integral; hence the limit is the field of the declared ideal thin loop of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c1). The strengthened supplier separately proves global local integrability, the two distributional identities, off-curve smoothness and decay; these establish all clauses of admissibility in [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c4). The physical reading is the explicit adoption [[post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models]]. The every-derivative compact convergence remains an additional away-from-curve conclusion and is not used to deduce a global distributional identity. 2.2 Scope: the statement is confined to the compact smooth class and its declared thin-loop limit; no formula is asserted for noncompact currents (the infinite-wire model is verified separately on the companion examples page), and no near-wire, self-energy or radiation claim is made. Countable Choice is inherited through the cited measure, surface, regular-distribution and/or Newtonian supplier interfaces; no stronger choice axiom is used in this argument. The thin-loop construction uses the all-space laboratory Omega=R3, so every compact mollified loop current satisfies the smooth-current domain condition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.2.2 printed pp. 215-219 (the Biot-Savart law (5.34) and (5.39) for line, surface and volume currents, with the straight-wire and circular-loop evaluations (5.36), (5.38)) and Section 5.3.2 printed pp. 222-225 (derivation of $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J$ from the volume integral); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 3.2.3 printed pp. 49-52 (the Biot-Savart law (3.15) and its thin-wire form (3.16)); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 43-44 printed pp. 101-104 (the average field of a system of moving charges and the law of Biot and Savart (43.7)); read in the stamped 387-page full text, re-expressed in SI."
              },
              {
                "title": "W. C. Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory, Purdue University (Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Lecture 5 Section 5.1 printed pp. 49-52 (derivation of the Biot-Savart law from the vector potential, eqs. (5.1.1)-(5.1.9)); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Biot-Savart representation of the magnetostatic field of a compact smooth conserved current, together with the declared thin-loop limit of that representation. Both legs are conditional on the framework's adopted equations and on the declared ideal models; the smooth-current formula holds even on its support, whereas no pointwise value on the ideal loop is asserted; nothing is claimed about self-energy, radiation, forces or noncompact currents, and no measurement, apparatus or uncertainty is involved.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
