            "post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models": "physical-assumption"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-magnetic-dipole-field",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Far-field dipole expansion of a compact conserved current with a uniform explicit remainder",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K4",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 19,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-magnetic-moment",
            "pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential",
            "lem-em-magnetic-dipole-remainder",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $J\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J=0$ and $\\operatorname{supp}J\\subset B_a(0)$ for some $a>0$, let $A$ be the vector potential of [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]] and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ its magnetostatic field, and let $m=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}y\\times J(y)\\,dy$ be the magnetic moment of [[def-em-magnetic-moment]](a). Then for $r=|x|\\ge2a$, uniformly in the unit vector $n=x/r$,\n$$A(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{m\\times x}{r^3}+O\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mu_0a^2\\|J\\|_1}{r^3}\\right),\\qquad B(x)=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{3n(m\\cdot n)-m}{r^3}+O\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mu_0a^2\\|J\\|_1}{r^4}\\right),$$\nwhere $\\|J\\|_1=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|J|\\,d\\lambda_3$ and the constants implicit in the $O$ terms are absolute: one constant works for all $r\\ge2a$ and all unit $n$. This is a controlled two-term far-field estimate: nothing is asserted for $r<2a$ or at the support, no higher multipole is defined here, and the leading dipole term is not claimed to be exact.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** transport of the explicit uniform-remainder lemma to the framework's objects. 1.1 The hypotheses of [[lem-em-magnetic-dipole-remainder]] are exactly those stated here for $J$ (smooth, compactly supported, divergence-free, contained in $B_a(0)$), and the objects of that lemma, $A=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int J(y)|x-y|^{-1}dy$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$, are verbatim the $A$ and $B$ of [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]]; the moment $m=\\frac12\\int y\\times J(y)\\,dy$ of the lemma is the moment of [[def-em-magnetic-moment]](a) ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). 2.1 Substituting these identifications into the lemma's conclusion gives the displayed estimates for the framework's $A$ and $B$, with the lemma's uniformity in $n$ and its absolute constants; no additional analysis is required. 2.2 Scope: the estimate is a remainder-controlled far-field expansion; nothing is asserted at smaller radii or at the source, and no empirical content is added. The far-field identification of $m$ as the source moment is exactly the content of this theorem under its hypotheses. Countable Choice is inherited through the cited measure, surface, regular-distribution and/or Newtonian supplier interfaces; no stronger choice axiom is used in this argument. The magnetic-moment definition uses a finite Riemann integral on a closed bounding rectangle, whereas the remainder supplier writes the Lebesgue integral. Each smooth compact component of $y\\times J(y)$ is bounded Borel and continuous on that rectangle, so [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]], under the stated Countable Choice, equates them; zero extension gives the same all-space Lebesgue moment.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.4.3 printed pp. 242-247 (multipole expansion of the vector potential: dipole potential (5.83), dipole field (5.85)-(5.87), and the coordinate-free form $\\mathbf B_{\\rm dip}=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{3(\\mathbf m\\cdot\\hat r)\\hat r-\\mathbf m}{r^3}$ with its delta term (5.90)); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 3.3.1-3.3.2 printed pp. 54-57 (far field of a circular current loop and the general localised distribution, with the moment (3.24)); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 44 printed pp. 103-105 (the average vector potential to first order in the source radius, $H=\\frac{3n(m\\cdot n)-m}{R^3}$ (44.4)); read in the stamped 387-page full text, re-expressed in SI."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A far-field dipole estimate for the magnetostatic field of a compact smooth conserved current, uniform for $r\\ge2a$, with an explicit remainder and absolute constants. The leading term is not an exact field; nothing is asserted inside $r<2a$ or at the support, and no experiment, apparatus or uncertainty is involved.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-magnetic-moment": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential": "physical-result",
            "lem-em-magnetic-dipole-remainder": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands": "mathematical-premise"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-magnetic-loop-force-torque",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Force and torque on a declared ideal thin-wire loop in a prescribed external field",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K4",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 20,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-magnetic-moment",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
            "pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential",
            "pthm-em-biot-savart",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable",
            "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties",
            "def-distributional-derivative",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "def-dirac-delta-and-its-derivatives",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $\\gamma:[0,L]\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a $C^2$ curve parametrized by arc length ($|\\gamma'|\\equiv1$) that is either closed ($\\gamma(0)=\\gamma(L)$ with matching one-sided derivatives) or a wire segment with free endpoints, let $I\\in\\mathbb R$, let $m=\\frac I2\\int_0^L\\gamma(s)\\times\\gamma'(s)\\,ds$ be the finite Riemann source moment defined by this displayed integral in the closed case. For an embedded closed loop this agrees with [[def-em-magnetic-moment]](b); embeddedness is not required for the present parametrized-curve calculation. Let $B$ be a prescribed external magnetic field, of class $C^0$ on a neighbourhood of the trace of $\\gamma$ in part (a) and of class $C^2$ on an open set containing the closed ball $\\overline{B_a(0)}$ in parts (b)-(d). Adopt the thin-wire coupling hypothesis (W): for a general closed parametrized curve use the stated direct neutral-wire Riemann coupling (with multiplicity of traversal retained); in the embedded closed-loop specialization its source interpretation is exactly [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c1); for an open curve use a compact segment of a fed or closed neutral circuit, including a segment of the declared infinite wire of (c2). Adopt separately for that parametrized wire or local segment the direct external magnetic coupling $dF=I\\,d\\gamma\\times B$ and $dN=\\gamma\\times dF$, as an ideal extension motivated by the smooth continuum Lorentz law [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](ii); that smooth-source postulate does not itself include singular currents. An isolated static neutral open segment is not asserted: its standalone current satisfies $\\operatorname{div}J_{\\rm seg}=I(\\delta_{\\gamma(0)}-\\delta_{\\gamma(L)})$, so its endpoint feed/return cannot be suppressed in a global model. Force and torque here concern only the specified local segment under this adopted coupling. Then:\n\n**(a) [Force and torque under (W)]**\n$$F:=I\\int_0^L\\gamma'(s)\\times B(\\gamma(s))\\,ds,\\qquad N:=I\\int_0^L\\gamma(s)\\times\\bigl(\\gamma'(s)\\times B(\\gamma(s))\\bigr)\\,ds,$$\nthe componentwise Riemann integrals of continuous functions on the compact interval $[0,L]$ ([[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]], [[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]]).\n\n**(b) [Constant field]** If $\\gamma$ is closed and $B$ is constant on a neighbourhood of the loop, then $F=0$ and $N=m\\times B$ exactly.\n\n**(c) [Slowly varying field]** Let $\\gamma$ be closed with $|\\gamma(s)|\\le a$ for all $s$, let $B$ be $C^2$ on an open set containing $\\overline{B_a(0)}$ with constants $M_1,M_2$ such that $|B(x)-B(y)|\\le M_1|x-y|$ and $|B(x)-B(0)-DB(0)x|\\le\\frac12M_2|x|^2$ for $|x|,|y|\\le a$, and suppose $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ on $B_a(0)$ (as holds for the external part of any smooth-branch vacuum field by [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]], [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]). Then\n$$F=\\nabla(m\\cdot B)(0)+R_F,\\qquad N=m\\times B(0)+R_N,$$\nwith $|R_F|\\le\\frac12|I|\\,L\\,a^2M_2$ and $|R_N|\\le|I|\\,L\\,a^2M_1$, where $\\nabla(m\\cdot B)(0)$ is the gradient at $0$ of $x\\mapsto m\\cdot B(x)$ for the fixed vector $m$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).\n\n**(d) [Sufficient condition]** If the external field is the magnetostatic field of a compact smooth steady current $J_{\\rm ext}\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}J_{\\rm ext}=0$ ([[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]], [[pthm-em-biot-savart]]), then it is $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, satisfies $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, and meets the hypotheses of (c) on every ball $\\overline{B_a(0)}$ with finite constants $M_1,M_2$.\n\n**(e) [Scope]** Only the external field acts on the loop: the loop's own field, self-force, radiation reaction, mechanical support and material response are outside the statement, and no claim is made about the loop's dynamics, deformation or failure. The coupling (W) is an explicit idealisation of a neutral thin wire at rest in the laboratory frame; it is adopted, not derived, and the theorem is conditional on it. No measurement or uncertainty is involved. Parts (a)-(c), conditional on the explicit coupling (W) and the supplied curve and external field, are finite Riemann-integral calculations and use no Choice axiom. Part (d), transporting the Newtonian-potential construction, assumes Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]).",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct evaluation of the pairings under the coupling, the antisymmetric first-moment identities for a closed curve, one Taylor expansion with an explicit remainder, and the compact-current sufficient condition. 1.1 (a) Under the separately adopted coupling (W), integrate the continuous external force along the compact curve directly in the Riemann sense: $F=I\\int_0^L\\gamma'(s)\\times B(\\gamma(s))\\,ds$, and taking moments gives $N=I\\int_0^L\\gamma(s)\\times(\\gamma'(s)\\times B(\\gamma(s)))\\,ds$; both integrands are continuous on $[0,L]$ because $\\gamma,\\gamma'$ and $B\\circ\\gamma$ are ([[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). 1.2 (b) For constant $B$, $F=I(\\int_0^L\\gamma'\\,ds)\\times B=0$ because $\\gamma(L)=\\gamma(0)$. For the torque, $\\gamma\\times(\\gamma'\\times B)=\\gamma'(\\gamma\\cdot B)-B(\\gamma\\cdot\\gamma')$, so with $M_{jl}:=\\int_0^L\\gamma_j\\gamma'_l\\,ds$, $$N=I\\int_0^L(\\gamma\\cdot B)\\gamma'\\,ds-I B\\int_0^L\\gamma\\cdot\\gamma'\\,ds=I B_jM_{jl}e_l .$$ The second integral vanishes because $\\gamma\\cdot\\gamma'=\\frac12\\frac{d}{ds}|\\gamma|^2$ integrates to $0$ over a closed curve. For the first, integrating $\\frac{d}{ds}(\\gamma_j\\gamma_l)=0$ over the closed curve gives $M_{jl}+M_{lj}=0$; for such an antisymmetric matrix the moment relation $m_p=\\frac I2\\varepsilon_{pab}M_{ab}$ is equivalent to $I M_{jl}=\\varepsilon_{jlp}m_p$, and substituting this into $I B_jM_{jl}e_l$ and using $B_j\\varepsilon_{jlp}m_p e_l=m\\times B$ gives $N=m\\times B$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]).\n\n2.1 (c) Write $B(x)=B(0)+DB(0)x+R(x)$ with $|R(x)|\\le\\frac12M_2|x|^2$ for $|x|\\le a$; this is the quantitative second-order Taylor hypothesis, the case $k=1$ of [[thm-multivariable-taylor-formula-with-lagrange-remainder]] applied componentwise, with the bound on the Lagrange term supplied by the stated $M_2$. Splitting the force of (a), the constant term vanishes as in (b); the linear term is $I\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\partial_lB_k(0)M_{lj}e_i$ and, with $I M_{lj}=\\varepsilon_{ljp}m_p$, the contraction $\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\varepsilon_{ljp}=\\delta_{kp}\\delta_{il}-\\delta_{kl}\\delta_{ip}$ yields $$I\\varepsilon_{ijk}\\partial_lB_k(0)M_{lj}e_i=\\bigl(\\partial_i(m\\cdot B)(0)-m_i\\operatorname{div}B(0)\\bigr)e_i=\\nabla(m\\cdot B)(0),$$ using $\\operatorname{div}B(0)=0$; the remainder obeys $|I\\int_0^L\\gamma'\\times R\\,ds|\\le|I|L\\sup_{|x|\\le a}|R(x)|\\le\\frac12|I|La^2M_2$ ([[thm-multidimensional-integral-properties]]). For the torque, step 1.2 gives $N=m\\times B(0)+I\\int_0^L\\gamma\\times(\\gamma'\\times(B(\\gamma)-B(0)))\\,ds$, and the remainder is bounded by $|I|\\int_0^L|\\gamma||\\gamma'||B(\\gamma)-B(0)|\\,ds\\le|I|\\,a\\cdot1\\cdot M_1a\\cdot L$. This proves (c).\n\n2.2 (d) By [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]] and [[pthm-em-biot-savart]] the external field of a compact smooth conserved current is $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ with $A$ of class $C^\\infty$, hence $B\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}B=0$; on a bounded ball the suprema of $|DB|$ and $|D^2B|$ are finite, and the mean-value and second-order Taylor estimates provide the constants $M_1,M_2$ of (c). 2.3 (e) records that (W) is a declared idealisation: only the external field acts, and no self-field, matter model or dynamics enters. Countable Choice is inherited in part (d) through the Newtonian suppliers; parts (a)-(c) use the stated finite Riemann coupling directly and are choice-free. The moment identity follows by contracting two alternating symbols and uses no division by $I$; for $I=0$ the moment, force, torque and both remainders are zero. No scalar distribution action $\\langle J,B\\rangle$ is equated with a vector force, and continuity rather than smoothness of the external field suffices for the adopted compact-curve Riemann coupling. For an open segment the scalar divergence test gives $-I\\int_0^L\\frac{d}{ds}\\varphi(\\gamma(s))ds=I(\\varphi(\\gamma(0))-\\varphi(\\gamma(L)))$, the stated endpoint identity. The force/moment algebra applies to all the stated closed parametrized $C^2$ curves, including self-intersecting or multiply traversed curves: closure, unit-speed parametrization and the explicit finite moment integral suffice. Only the embedded closed specialization invokes the ideal-loop source object of the preceding definition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.1.3 printed pp. 208-214 (the force on a current-carrying wire $\\mathbf F=\\int I\\,d\\mathbf l\\times\\mathbf B$ (5.17)) and Section 6.1.2 printed pp. 255-260 (torque $\\mathbf N=\\mathbf m\\times\\mathbf B$ on a loop in a uniform field (6.1), zero net force in a uniform field, and $\\mathbf F=\\nabla(\\mathbf m\\cdot\\mathbf B)$ (6.3) for a small loop in a nonuniform field, with Problems 6.2 and 6.4); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 3.4.1-3.4.2 printed pp. 57-62 (force between currents, the Taylor expansion of the field over a small localised current, and the moment manipulations that reduce the first-order force term); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 45 printed pp. 105-106 (the average moment of the forces on a system in a constant uniform magnetic field, $K=m\\times H$ (45.1)); read in the stamped 387-page full text, re-expressed in SI."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Force and torque on a declared ideal thin-wire loop in a prescribed external magnetic field, under an explicitly adopted neutral-wire Lorentz coupling. Only the external field acts: self-fields, radiation reaction, mechanical support, deformation and material failure are outside the statement; no dynamics, radiation or empirical measurement is claimed, and the result is conditional on the adopted coupling (W).",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
