      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-em-circular-loop-axis-field",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Axis field of the declared ideal circular thin loop",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X3",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 20,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-magnetic-moment",
            "pthm-em-biot-savart",
            "lem-em-thin-current-limit",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "thm-substitution"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $a>0$, $I\\in\\mathbb R$, let $\\gamma(s)=a(\\cos(s/a),\\sin(s/a),0)$, $s\\in[0,2\\pi a]$, be the circle of radius $a$ in the plane $x_3=0$ parametrized by arc length, let the declared ideal thin-loop current of amplitude $I$ run along $\\gamma$ in the sense of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c1), with moment $m=I\\pi a^2e_3$ ([[def-em-magnetic-moment]](b)), and let $\\mu_0$, $c$, $\\varepsilon_0$ be the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Then for every point $x=ze_3$ on the axis ($z\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\gamma)=\\sqrt{a^2+z^2}>0$) the field of the ideal loop is the absolutely convergent line integral\n$$B(ze_3)=\\frac{\\mu_0I}{4\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi a}\\frac{\\gamma'(s)\\times\\left(ze_3-\\gamma(s)\\right)}{|ze_3-\\gamma(s)|^3}\\,ds=\\frac{\\mu_0Ia^2}{2(a^2+z^2)^{3/2}}\\,e_3,$$\ni.e. the Biot-Savart field of [[pthm-em-biot-savart]] and the declared thin-current limit of [[lem-em-thin-current-limit]] agree on the axis, with the sign fixed by the positive (counterclockwise) orientation of $\\gamma$ and by the sign of $I$; in particular $B(0)=\\frac{\\mu_0I}{2a}e_3$. This is a property of the declared idealisation; it is not the field of any physical finite-radius coil, nothing is claimed at points off the axis or on the wire, and no measurement is involved. The explicit finite Riemann axis evaluation and circle moment calculation alone are choice-free. Countable Choice is inherited by the asserted global distributional model and thin-current supplier.",
          "strategy": "**Verification.** The circle is a closed embedded $C^2$ curve parametrized by arc length, and every axis point lies at distance $\\sqrt{a^2+z^2}\\ge a>0$ from it, so the field of the declared ideal loop is the Biot-Savart line integral on the axis: the mollified fields of [[lem-em-thin-current-limit]] converge uniformly on a compact neighbourhood of the axis point to that integral, and [[pthm-em-biot-savart]] identifies the same integral as the field of the compact smooth currents and their declared thin limit. For the evaluation, write $t=s/a$; then $\\gamma'(s)=(-\\sin t,\\cos t,0)$, $ze_3-\\gamma(s)=(-a\\cos t,-a\\sin t,z)$ and $|ze_3-\\gamma(s)|=(a^2+z^2)^{1/2}$ is constant, so $$\\gamma'(s)\\times(ze_3-\\gamma(s))=(z\\cos t,\\ z\\sin t,\\ a),$$ and $ds=a\\,dt$; hence $$\\int_0^{2\\pi a}\\frac{\\gamma'(s)\\times(ze_3-\\gamma(s))}{|ze_3-\\gamma(s)|^3}\\,ds=\\frac{a}{(a^2+z^2)^{3/2}}\\int_0^{2\\pi}(z\\cos t,z\\sin t,a)\\,dt=\\frac{2\\pi a^2}{(a^2+z^2)^{3/2}}\\,e_3,$$ the first two components vanishing by periodicity of $\\cos$ and $\\sin$ over a full period. Multiplying by $\\mu_0I/(4\\pi)$ gives the displayed field. Each component is the Riemann integral of a continuous function on the compact interval $[0,2\\pi a]$ ([[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]], [[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]), and the moment $m=I\\pi a^2e_3$ is the computation $\\gamma\\times\\gamma'=ae_3$ of [[def-em-magnetic-moment]](b). The example is a conditional ideal-model computation: no apparatus, sampling model, uncertainty or empirical inference is involved. Countable Choice is inherited through the cited measure, surface, regular-distribution and/or Newtonian supplier interfaces; no stronger choice axiom is used in this argument. The global distributional admissibility is supplied separately by the strengthened [[lem-em-thin-current-limit]] and the explicit physical adoption [[post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models]]; the axis integral itself is away from the wire.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.2.2 printed pp. 217-218 (Example 5.6: the field a distance $z$ above the centre of a circular loop, eq. (5.38), $B=\\mu_0IR^2/[2(R^2+z^2)^{3/2}]$); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 3.2.3 printed pp. 49-52 and Section 3.3.1 printed pp. 54-56 (the thin-wire Biot-Savart integral and the far field of a circular loop); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "W. C. Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory, Purdue University (Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Lecture 5 Section 5.1 printed pp. 49-52 (the current-element form $d H=I\\,d l'\\times\\hat R/(4\\pi R^2)$ of the Biot-Savart law); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "An explicit ideal-model computation: the axis field of a declared ideal circular thin loop. It involves no apparatus, no sampling, no uncertainty and no empirical claim, and it is explicitly not the field of any physical finite-radius coil.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-substitution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-magnetic-moment": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-biot-savart": "physical-result",
            "lem-em-thin-current-limit": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
