            "post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models": "physical-assumption"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-infinite-wire-and-solenoid",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Declared ideal infinite wire and cylindrical current sheet: explicit fields and their distributional equations",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X3",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 19,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "lem-em-ideal-line-current-field",
            "lem-em-ideal-cylindrical-sheet-field",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set",
            "def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths",
            "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface",
            "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]].\n\n**(a) [Ideal infinite straight wire].** Let $\\gamma(s)=(0,0,s)$, $s\\in\\mathbb R$, let $J_{\\rm w}$ be the declared ideal line current of amplitude $I$ along $\\gamma$ ([[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c2)), and define, on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\gamma$,\n$$B(x):=\\frac{\\mu_0I}{2\\pi\\rho}\\,e_\\varphi,\\qquad \\rho=\\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2},\\qquad e_\\varphi=\\rho^{-1}(-x_2,x_1,0).$$\nThen $B$ is locally integrable on $\\mathbb R^3$ and of class $C^\\infty$ off $\\gamma$; $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=0$ pointwise off $\\gamma$; $B$ is invariant under translations along $\\gamma$ and rotations about it, and its transverse far-zone supremum tends to $0$ as $\\rho\\to\\infty$; for every $r>0$ and $h\\in\\mathbb R$, the circulation of $B$ along $\\gamma_{r,h}(t)=(r\\cos t,r\\sin t,h)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$, traversed once with increasing $t$, is $\\mu_0I$ ([[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]); and $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J_{\\rm w}$ hold in the distributional sense of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c4). Hence $B$ is an admitted field of the declared wire model; the transverse decay excludes an added homogeneous longitudinal field, and no further selection, uniqueness or energy claim is made.\n\n**(b) [Ideal infinite cylindrical sheet].** Let $a>0$, $S=\\{\\rho=a\\}$, $K\\in\\mathbb R$, let $J_S$ be the declared ideal sheet current of amplitude $K$ along $S$ ([[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c3)), and define $B:=\\mu_0Ke_3$ for $\\rho<a$ and $B:=0$ for $\\rho>a$. Then $B$ is locally integrable on $\\mathbb R^3$ and of class $C^\\infty$ off $S$; $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=0$ pointwise off $S$; $B$ has the declared symmetry (translations along and rotations about the axis) and its transverse far-zone supremum is $0$; the one-sided jump across $S$, with $n=e_\\rho$ from the interior to the exterior and $[B]=B_+-B_-$, is $n\\times[B]=\\mu_0Ke_\\varphi$ and $n\\cdot[B]=0$, the stationary tangential-sheet jump for this noncompact cylinder, established by the test-localized [[lem-em-ideal-cylindrical-sheet-field]] for the current density $Ke_\\varphi$ (computed directly as $e_\\rho\\times(0-\\mu_0Ke_3)=\\mu_0Ke_\\varphi$); and $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J_S$ hold in the distributional sense of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c4). Hence $B$ is an admitted field of the declared solenoid model.\n\n**(c) [Scope].** Both models are declared noncompact idealisations: they are not finite-energy localized-current theorems; nothing is asserted about self-energy, inductance, self-force, the fields on the sources, or the approximation of any physical apparatus; and the fields are selected within the declared symmetry and decay class, not shown to be the only solutions of the magnetostatic equations off the sources without that class. The external force between two such wires is treated in [[ex-em-wire-force-coupling]] under the adopted thin-wire coupling.",
          "strategy": "Scalar multiples of the two mathematical ideal-field lemmas, followed by symmetry, distributional and decay checks against c4. Circulation is computed only along the positively oriented once-traversed gamma_r,h defined in Example(a). All source values, energy and uniqueness extrapolations remain excluded.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.2.2 printed pp. 215-217 (Example 5.5: the field of a long straight wire (5.36)) and Section 5.3.3 printed pp. 226-228 (Example 5.7 the straight wire and Example 5.9 the infinite solenoid, with the field $\\mu_0nI$ inside and zero outside); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 3.1.1-3.1.2 printed pp. 42-46 (the wire field $B=\\mu_0I/(2\\pi r)$, the surface-current discontinuity (3.5)-(3.6) and the solenoid $B=\\mu_0IN\\hat z$ (3.7)); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "W. C. Chew, Lectures on Electromagnetic Field Theory, Purdue University (Fall 2020, 497 pp.)",
                "url": "https://engineering.purdue.edu/wcchew/ece604f20/EMFTAll.pdf",
                "locator": "Lecture 4 Section 4.3.3 printed pp. 46-48 (the Ampere-law jump condition at a current sheet) and Section 4.3.4 printed p. 48 (the normal-$B$ continuity); read in the stamped full text."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Explicit verification of the two declared ideal noncompact source models, the infinite straight wire and the infinite cylindrical current sheet (ideal solenoid). The models carry declared symmetry and decay data; they are not finite-energy localised-current theorems, nothing is claimed about self-energy, inductance or self-forces, and no measurement, apparatus or statistical inference is involved.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
