        {
          "id": "ex-em-wire-force-coupling",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Force per unit length between two declared ideal parallel wires",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X3",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 21,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "pthm-em-magnetic-loop-force-torque",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "post-em-ideal-magnetostatic-models",
            "ex-em-infinite-wire-and-solenoid"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $d>0$ and $I_1,I_2\\in\\mathbb R$; let wire 1 be the declared ideal line current of amplitude $I_1$ along the third coordinate axis and wire 2 the translate of that ideal line current by $d\\,e_1$, of amplitude $I_2$ ([[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c2)), and adopt for the force on wire 2 the thin-wire coupling (W) of [[pthm-em-magnetic-loop-force-torque]], restricted to the external field of wire 1. Then the force per unit length on wire 2 is\n$$\\mathbf f=\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}\\,e_3\\times e_2=-\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}\\,e_1,$$\ni.e. attraction towards wire 1 (direction $-e_1$) for currents in the same direction ($I_1I_2>0$), repulsion for opposite directions, and magnitude $\\mu_0|I_1I_2|/(2\\pi d)$ per unit length. The comparison uses only the external field of wire 1 at the position of wire 2; the self-fields and the mechanical support forces of the two infinite wires are excluded, no claim is made about the total force on either infinite wire, and no measurement is involved.",
          "strategy": "**Verification.** Wire 1 has the admitted field $B_1=\\mu_0I_1/(2\\pi\\rho)\\,e_\\varphi$ of [[ex-em-infinite-wire-and-solenoid]](a), which at the points $(d,0,x_3)$ of wire 2 takes the constant value $B_1=\\frac{\\mu_0I_1}{2\\pi d}\\,e_2$ (there $e_\\varphi=e_2$). By the coupling (W) of [[pthm-em-magnetic-loop-force-torque]](a) applied to the compact straight segments $\\{(d,0,x_3):|x_3|\\le S\\}$ of wire 2 (with arc-length parameter $x_3$ and $|\\gamma'|=1$), the force on the segment is $$I_2\\int_{-S}^{S}e_3\\times B_1\\,dx_3=2S\\,\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}\\,(e_3\\times e_2),$$ the Riemann integral of a constant continuous integrand over a compact interval ([[thm-multidimensional-integral-properties]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]). Dividing by the length $2S$ gives the force per unit length of the displayed form, independent of $S$; the sign of $I_1I_2$ gives attraction or repulsion as stated. Only the external field of wire 1 is used, and the self-field of wire 2 and the support forces of both infinite wires are excluded; the computation is a local external-force comparison, not a solution of the two-wire dynamics, and involves no measurement. The preceding same-B-page example is a permitted intra-page supplier and its corrected proof uses the A-level ideal-line lemma and explicit model adoption. The compact segment uses the separately adopted local coupling, with the return/feed outside the force segment; no isolated neutral open segment is claimed to be globally conserved. Countable Choice is inherited by the asserted ideal-line model, while the final constant-force Riemann calculation is finite.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Section 5.2.2 printed pp. 217-218 (the force of attraction between two long parallel wires a distance $d$ apart, using the field $\\mu_0I_1/(2\\pi d)$ and the line-current force law); read in the stamped scanned full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 3.4.1 printed pp. 57-59 (force between parallel wires, $f=-(\\mu_0I_1I_2/2\\pi d)\\hat x$ (3.25), with attraction for equal directions and repulsion for opposite ones); read in the stamped 236-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 42 printed pp. 100-101 (interaction of two systems through their fields and the force on a system in an external field); read in the stamped 387-page full text, re-expressed in SI."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A local external-force comparison between two declared ideal parallel straight wires, restricted to the force per unit length that wire 1's external field exerts on wire 2 under the adopted neutral-thin-wire coupling. Self-fields and support forces of infinitely long wires are excluded; no dynamics, circuit model, material response or measurement is claimed.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-magnetic-loop-force-torque": "physical-result",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
