      "items": [
        {
          "id": "post-em-coarse-graining",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Declared averaging kernel, macroscopic scale assumptions and averaged fields",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#post-em-coarse-graining",
          "design_state": "explicit-adopted-postulate-contract; nonproof-source-scope-review",
          "deps": [
            "def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-em-sources",
            "def-locally-integrable-function-on-r-n",
            "lem-complex-translation-and-approximate-identity-interfaces",
            "def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump",
            "thm-convolution-with-a-mollifier-is-smooth-and-differentiates-under-the-integral-sign",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
            "thm-countable-union-of-null-is-null",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn",
            "thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality",
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-locally-integrable-function-on-r-n": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-complex-translation-and-approximate-identity-interfaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-convolution-with-a-mollifier-is-smooth-and-differentiates-under-the-integral-sign": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-countable-union-of-null-is-null": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt as a modelling postulate of this framework the following coarse-graining convention for matter. Let the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$, its frame basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$, its units, its fields and its sources be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-si-conventions]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]], and assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), the hypothesis of the smoothing theorem cited in clause (b).\n\n**(a) [Averaging length, kernel and footprint]** Fix $\\ell>0$ and nonnegative $\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\int\\eta=1$, in the Euclidean test-function convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]. Put $\\eta_\\ell(x)=\\ell^{-3}\\eta(x/\\ell)$, $K=\\operatorname{supp}\\eta$ and choose a declared finite bound $R_\\eta>0$ with $K\\subseteq\\overline B_{R_\\eta}(0)$. The kernel and length are fixed data; the imported mass-one mollifier definition [[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]] does not require $K\\subseteq\\overline B_1(0)$. At $x$ the exact averaging footprint is $x-\\ell K$. Define the open interior averaging domain\n\n$$\\Omega_{\\ell,\\eta}:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:x-\\ell K\\subseteq\\Omega\\}.$$\n\nThe sufficient condition $\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\Omega)>\\ell R_\\eta$ implies membership. If the additional unit-support condition $K\\subseteq\\overline B_1(0)$ is declared, the original sufficient region $\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\Omega)>\\ell$ and the ball $B_\\ell(x)$ are valid special cases; unit support is not inferred from mass one.\n\n**(b) [Averaged fields and input regularity]** For an open spatial domain $V$, locally integrable means measurable and absolutely integrable on every compact subset of $V$, componentwise for maps to $\\mathbb R^m$, $m=1$ or $3$ ([[def-locally-integrable-function-on-r-n]]). First suppose $F:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^m$ has a locally integrable spatial slice $F(\\cdot,t)$ for every $t$ under consideration. No integrability across the boundary of $\\Omega$ is required for the interior branch. Define, for $x\\in\\Omega_{\\ell,\\eta}$,\n\n$$F_\\ell(x,t)=\\int_\\Omega\\eta_\\ell(x-y)F(y,t)\\,dy.$$\n\nThe compact footprint lies in $\\Omega$, so this integral exists absolutely, and for each such $t$ it is smooth in $x$ on $\\Omega_{\\ell,\\eta}$ with\n\n$$\\partial_x^\\alpha F_\\ell(x,t)=\\int_\\Omega(\\partial^\\alpha\\eta_\\ell)(x-y)F(y,t)\\,dy$$\n\nfor every multi-index; these are applications of the pinned mollifier smoothing supplier [[thm-convolution-with-a-mollifier-is-smooth-and-differentiates-under-the-integral-sign]] after localization: near a fixed $x_0\\in\\Omega_{\\ell,\\eta}$, compactness of $x_0-\\ell K\\subset\\Omega$ gives a small closed ball $Q$ of averaging centres whose footprints have compact union inside $\\Omega$. Choose $\\zeta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ equal to one near that union ([[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]]); the zero extension of $\\zeta F(\\cdot,t)$ is globally locally integrable and its mollification agrees with the displayed average on the interior of $Q$. This also proves that $\\Omega_{\\ell,\\eta}$ is open, without assuming that the zero extension of $F$ itself is locally integrable. For each admissible fixed $t$ the spatial convolution is measurable in $x$ and independent of a.e.-equal spatial representatives ([[thm-the-lebesgue-integral-respects-almost-everywhere-equality]]); no joint $(x,t)$ measurability follows from the every-slice hypothesis alone. Joint $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega\\times I)$ input alone instead gives these slice statements for almost every $t$, by local Fubini on compact spatial/time exhaustions ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-countable-union-of-null-is-null]], [[thm-heine-borel-rn]]); it gives no every-time slice claim. A claim for every $t$ must explicitly supply locally integrable slices for every $t$ (for example jointly continuous fields supply that condition). A spacetime a.e. class alone determines the resulting averages only for almost every $t$; changing an exceptional time slice may change its pointwise-time assignment. Spatial smoothing asserts no time differentiability or joint smoothness without separately stated time hypotheses.\n\n**[Compatible extension branch]** If a declared extension $\\widehat F(\\cdot,t)$ agrees with $F$ a.e. on $\\Omega$ and is locally integrable on an open larger spatial domain $V$, use the same formula there for all $x$ whose compact footprint lies in $V$. If $V=\\mathbb R^3$ and every declared slice is globally locally integrable, the average is defined and spatially smooth on all $\\mathbb R^3\\times I$. If only joint local integrability of the extension is supplied, the global spatial conclusion is for almost every $t$ unless the every-slice hypothesis is added. The zero extension is an allowed choice only when it meets this local-integrability condition: equivalently, at each declared $t$, $\\int_{Q\\cap\\Omega}|F(y,t)|\\,dy<\\infty$ for every compact $Q\\subset\\mathbb R^3$. Mere local integrability inside $\\Omega$ does not imply that boundary condition. All these extension and per-time conditions are part of the declared configuration, with the original interior branch retaining every locally integrable spatial input.\n\n**(c) [Macroscopic reading and scale separation]** The framework reads its macroscopic fields and source densities as such averages only in situations where a separation of microscopic and observation scales is stated for the problem at hand; the averaging length and the actual kernel footprint (or its radius bound $\\ell R_\\eta$) are part of that statement, and no canonical value of $\\ell$ is asserted. This is a formulation choice for the macroscopic description, not a consequence of the vacuum equations.\n\n**(d) [Boundary handling]** For requested averages at $x\\in\\Omega$, boundary handling concerns the locations where $x-\\ell K$ is not contained in the original spatial domain. The region $\\{x\\in\\Omega:\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Omega)\\le\\ell R_\\eta\\}$ is a sufficient enclosing boundary layer, with $\\ell$ in place of $\\ell R_\\eta$ only in the declared unit-support special case. Every requested average at an exterior location $x\\notin\\Omega$ must separately satisfy the extension/domain conditions of (b); the interior boundary-layer statement makes no claim to enclose exterior locations. In the interior layer a zero-extension average is used only under the compatible-extension hypothesis of (b). Otherwise one must declare another locally integrable extension, a boundary-layer model with its own well-definedness hypotheses, or restrict the averaging domain to the exact interior footprint domain. No boundary model is supplied by local integrability alone. An extension does not automatically extend a microscopic field equation across the original boundary; any averaging of such equations must either declare their validity on all used extended footprints or retain the resulting boundary distribution terms. These boundary qualifications do not impose a constitutive law.\n\n**(e) [No constitutive content]** Coarse graining reorganises the description; it does not by itself produce a constitutive relation between averaged fields and averaged sources, and no linear, local or instantaneous response is asserted by this postulate. Such a response is the separate assumption `post-em-material-closure`, and the derivation of macroscopic field equations from the vacuum equations is the separate result `pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell`.",
          "strategy": "An adopted nonproof formulation: the macroscopic identification and scale-separation/boundary conventions are adopted, not deduced from vacuum Maxwell or asserted empirically. Its mathematical referent is checked against the exact published mollifier contract, which permits any smooth compactly supported mass-one kernel and requires locally integrable input on the region used by its compact translates; it imposes no unit support.\n\nInterior applicability: for x0 in the exact footprint domain, x0-ell K is compact inside Omega. A sufficiently small closed ball of x-values about x0 has all its translated kernel supports in one compact Q contained in Omega. This follows from compactness and openness, and proves the footprint domain open. At each admissible t let f_Q be F(.,t) on Q and zero elsewhere. Since Q is compact inside Omega, f_Q belongs to global L1 and hence L1loc. The published mollifier smoothing theorem applied to f_Q gives a smooth convolution and every derivative by differentiating the kernel. In that small x-neighbourhood it equals the declared interior integral, and every derivative has support inside Q as well. This proves absolute existence, spatial smoothness and the derivative formula for arbitrary interior L1loc data. Spatial a.e. representatives give equal integrals for each x because translated kernel weights are bounded and have compact support. Compactness of K gives its finite support-radius bound; no equality to 1 is assumed.\n\nSlice qualification: exhaust Omega by compact sets K_j={x:|x|<=j,dist(x,R3 minus Omega)>=1/j} (use the usual convention if Omega=R3), and the open laboratory interval by compact subintervals. On each compact product joint local integrability makes F absolutely integrable. Apply the L1 Fubini theorem to these compact restrictions extended by zero to the Euclidean product. Outside a null exceptional time set the spatial integrals on each K_j are finite; a countable union of these exceptional sets is null under the already declared countable-choice convention. Thus a.e. time slices are locally integrable, because every compact subset of Omega lies in some K_j. It does not prove every-time slices or uniquely determine assignments at exceptional times. No time derivative is inferred. The compatible-extension branch uses the same precise supplier with its hypotheses checked on the declared larger domain; the all-space branch applies only when the extension is globally L1loc per slice. Zero extension has that property exactly when its absolute integral on Q equals the finite integral over Q intersect Omega for every global compact Q. A field such as 1/x1 on x1>0 fails this condition, so no unconditional boundary extension is asserted.\n\nThe enclosing boundary-layer claim is restricted to x inside Omega; requested exterior averages are governed separately by the footprint/extension conditions. Measurability in the every-slice branch refers to x at each admissible fixed t, with no unassumed joint measurability. Clauses (c)-(e) retain the adopted macroscopic reading, require the actual kernel footprint in scale separation, and distinguish smoothing from constitutive closure and field equations. No deductive proof of this modelling postulate is supplied or claimed. These local applicability arguments and inherited mathematical supplier pins are not independent source/audit acceptance.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.1 printed pp. 172-175 (PDF pp. 180-183): matter is described through macroscopic averaged fields (polarisation as a dipole-moment density), and the notes state explicitly that the coarse-graining step is informal; the precise kernel, averaging length and scale-separation convention of this postulate are the framework's own formulation. Read in the retained 236-page full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Electromagnetism (University of Texas at Austin course), node 69: Polarization",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node69.html",
                "locator": "node 69, opening paragraph: a neutral molecular medium is described macro- \\u00adscopically by the dipole moment per unit volume, with the microscopic atomic detail absorbed into $\\mathbf P$; read in the retained offline full text of the course."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§4.4.1 printed pp. 179-180: the constitutive discussion states that the polarisation of a dielectric 'ordinarily results from an electric field' and that the material response is a model choice; read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The declared smoothing convention for matter: a fixed smooth unit-mass kernel and averaging length, the definition and interior spatial regularity of the spatial averages of locally integrable fields on the laboratory region, the every-time versus almost-every-time input qualification, the compatible-extension branch, the scale-separation and boundary-layer conventions, and the explicit statement that averaging supplies neither a constitutive law nor a field equation. A modelling postulate of the framework, not derived from the vacuum equations and not an empirical claim.",
          "dependency_level": 13
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-polarization-magnetization",
