          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$, its frame basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$, its units, its fields $E,B$ and its source densities $\\rho,J$ be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-si-conventions]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]], and let the averaging convention be that of [[post-em-coarse-graining]], in whose sense the objects below are macroscopic. Any convolution used to instantiate that reading is taken on its declared exact interior-footprint domain, or under a declared locally integrable compatible extension covering every used footprint. Its every-time use requires every-time locally integrable spatial slices; joint spacetime local integrability alone supplies only the almost-every-time branch. The joint $C^2$ regularity specified below is a separate model hypothesis, not a consequence of spatial smoothing.\n\n**(a) [Polarization and magnetization]** The material model specifies two maps $P,M:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of class $C^2$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] on the open set $\\Omega\\times I$: $P$ is the electric dipole moment per unit volume, in $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$, and $M$ the magnetic dipole moment per unit volume, in $\\mathrm A\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, both read in the frame basis. They are data of the chosen material model: this definition does not compute them from $E$ and $B$, and no relation between $(P,M)$ and the fields is asserted here.\n\n**(b) [Bound sources]** Write $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ for the divergence and curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] taken at fixed $t$, and $\\partial_t$ for the partial derivative of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] at fixed position, and define the bound charge density and bound current density by\n\n$$\\rho_b:=-\\operatorname{div}P,\\qquad J_b:=\\partial_tP+\\operatorname{curl}M.$$\n\nSince $P,M$ are jointly $C^2$, $\\rho_b$ and $J_b$ are continuous, indeed $C^1$, by the componentwise sum and product rules of [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]; $\\rho_b$ carries $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$ and $J_b$ carries $\\mathrm A\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$.\n\n**(c) [Free sources]** Define $\\rho_f:=\\rho-\\rho_b$ and $J_f:=J-J_b$, in the same units as $\\rho$ and $J$; these are the free charge and free current densities of the model. The decomposition $\\rho=\\rho_f+\\rho_b$, $J=J_f+J_b$ is part of the chosen material model: the same total pair $(\\rho,J)$ with a different pair $(P,M)$ is a different model.\n\n**(d) [Displacement and magnetic intensity]** Define the electric displacement and the magnetic intensity by\n\n$$D:=\\varepsilon_0E+P,\\qquad H:=\\mu_0^{-1}B-M,$$\n\nwhere $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ and $\\mu_0,c>0$ are the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; $D$ carries $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$ and $H$ carries $\\mathrm A\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\n**(e) [Status]** This item fixes objects and the bound/free convention only. No field equation (the item `pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell`), no constitutive relation (the item `post-em-material-closure`), no conservation of the bound pair (the item `pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell`, clause (a)) and no identification of $P,M$ with microscopic dipole sums is asserted here.",
          "strategy": "An explicit type/domain/convention definition. The regions, fields and sources are those of the named suppliers; the derivatives in (b) are the published divergence, curl and partial derivative; the constants and units are those of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; the $C^2$ class is that of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]; the macroscopic reading is the convention of [[post-em-coarse-graining]], with its actual footprint/compatible-extension and slice hypotheses carried whenever convolution is used. Joint C2 regularity of the model maps is assumed separately; the spatial averaging operation proves neither time differentiability nor the existence of microscopic dipole sums. The definition records explicitly that $(P,M)$ are model data read in the laboratory frame basis, that the bound/free split is a modelling choice, and that no constitutive relation between $(P,M)$ and $(E,B)$ is included. Well-definedness of $\\rho_b,J_b$ as continuous fields is the componentwise sum/product differentiability calculus of [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]. No field equation, no derivative of the physical fields and no microscopic interpretation is used.",
