          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the smooth branch of the vacuum Maxwell postulate [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]]: fields $E,B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ jointly of class $C^1$ with the additional derivatives required by each equation existing and continuous, and continuous source densities $\\rho,J$ of [[def-em-sources]]. Let $P,M$ and the derived objects $\\rho_b,J_b,\\rho_f,J_f,D,H$ be those of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]], with $P,M$ of class $C^2$, and use the operators of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. Then:\n\n**(a) [Bound-source conservation]** $\\partial_t\\rho_b+\\operatorname{div}J_b=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$. Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the regular-distribution and classical-compatibility interfaces below. Identify continuous sources with their regular distributions using [[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]] and [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]. Then\n\n$$\\partial_t\\rho_f+\\operatorname{div}J_f=\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J$$\n\nas distributions on $U$, with derivatives as in [[def-distributional-derivative]]: the free pair is distributionally conserved exactly when the total pair is. Under the adopted four vacuum equations both sides are zero distributions, by [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]]. These statements do not require classical derivatives of $\\rho,J$. Where the partial derivatives $\\partial_t\\rho$ and $\\partial_{x_i}J_i$ exist, the same identity holds pointwise by differentiating the free/bound decomposition; in particular, if these derivatives are continuous on $U$, both pairs satisfy the classical pointwise continuity equation. The bound-source conservation equation remains classical and uses only $P,M\\in C^2$.\n\n**(b) [Macroscopic equations]** On $\\Omega\\times I$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}D=\\rho_f,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}H=J_f+\\partial_tD.$$\n\n**(c) [Interpretation and scope]** Equations (b) are an exact rewriting of the adopted vacuum equations under the decomposition of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]]: the two equations written in $E$ and $B$ are unchanged, and the $D$ and $H$ equations hold for every $C^2$ pair $(P,M)$, independently of any constitutive law. The classical bound identity of (a) uses the exchange of $\\partial_t$ with $\\operatorname{div}$ for $C^2$ data; the free/total identity is distributional in the stated continuous-source class, with its classical interpretation only under the additional derivatives stated in (a). The distributional conservation conclusion embeds the adopted smooth solution only; no macroscopic field-equation claim is made for the weak branch of the Maxwell postulate, for singular or sheet sources or for discontinuous $(P,M)$; no constitutive relation and no empirical statement is made.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct substitution, componentwise. (a): from [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]](b), $\\operatorname{div}J_b=\\operatorname{div}\\partial_tP+\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}M$. The second term vanishes: in components $\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}M=\\sum_{i,j,k=1}^3\\epsilon_{ijk}\\partial_i\\partial_jM_k$, and pairwise cancellation of the mixed partials $\\partial_i\\partial_jM_k=\\partial_j\\partial_iM_k$ is [[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]] applied to the $C^2$ components of $M$; the first term equals $\\partial_t\\operatorname{div}P$ by the same theorem applied to the $C^2$ components of $P$; adding $-\\partial_t\\operatorname{div}P$ gives zero. Linearity of $\\partial_t$, $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ is [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] read componentwise. (b): Gauss's law $\\operatorname{div}E=(\\rho_f+\\rho_b)/\\varepsilon_0$ together with $\\rho_b=-\\operatorname{div}P$ gives $\\operatorname{div}(\\varepsilon_0E+P)=\\rho_f$, i.e. $\\operatorname{div}D=\\rho_f$. The equations $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$ are unchanged. Ampere's law $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$ with $J=J_f+\\partial_tP+\\operatorname{curl}M$ gives, after dividing by $\\mu_0$ and moving $\\operatorname{curl}M$ to the left, $\\operatorname{curl}(\\mu_0^{-1}B-M)=J_f+\\partial_tP+\\varepsilon_0\\partial_tE$, where $(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}=\\varepsilon_0$; the right-hand side is $J_f+\\partial_t(\\varepsilon_0E+P)=J_f+\\partial_tD$, so $\\operatorname{curl}H=J_f+\\partial_tD$. (c) records the scope: the identities are conditional on the adopted postulates and on $P,M\\in C^2$; no constitutive content is used. For the free/total identity, subtract the regular distributions of the classical bound pair from those of the continuous total pair and differentiate linearly. Under $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]] identifies the $C^1$ bound derivatives with their classical regular distributions, so their sum is zero; this proves the distributional identity without differentiating the merely continuous total sources classically. Embed the four adopted $C^1$ Maxwell equations and apply spatial divergence to Ampere: commuting distributional derivatives cancels $\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}B$ and Gauss gives $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}J=0$. If the displayed source derivatives exist, linearity gives the pointwise free/total identity; if they are continuous, classical compatibility and injectivity of the regular-distribution embedding give pointwise zero. Only the regular-distribution/classical-compatibility branch invokes $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$; the classical bound calculation and the macroscopic substitution in (b) remain choice-free.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
