            "def-l-one-of-a-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt as a modelling postulate of this framework the following constitutive branches for the material described by the macroscopic fields of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]].\n\n**(a) [Material rest frame]** The relations below are stated in a material rest frame: an inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of the Minkowski model of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]) in whose coordinates each modelled material element has fixed spatial position $x$, hence history $t\\mapsto(ct,x)$ on the declared time interval. When this is the laboratory frame, the components below are laboratory components. Boosting the material changes both the fields and the rest observer; this postulate does not assert that the relations below hold with the same constants in every frame.\n\n**(b) [Elementary inertial-rest branch]** In the rest frame, with constant symmetric positive-definite matrices $\\varepsilon,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ (the isotropic scalar cases $\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon\\mathbf 1$, $\\mu=\\mu\\mathbf 1$ with $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$ included), the model takes\n\n$$D=\\varepsilon E,\\qquad B=\\mu H,\\qquad J_c=\\sigma E,$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ is symmetric positive semidefinite and $J_c$ is the conduction part of the free current, $J_f=J_c+J_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ for a declared external free current $J_{\\mathrm{ext}}:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ in $\\mathrm A\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$. For a real matrix $T$, symmetric means $T^{\\mathsf T}=T$, positive definite means $v^{\\mathsf T}Tv>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and positive semidefinite means $v^{\\mathsf T}Tv\\ge0$ for every $v$. Entries of $\\varepsilon,\\mu,\\sigma$ carry respectively $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, $\\mathrm H\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $\\mathrm S\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, where $\\mathrm F=\\mathrm C/\\mathrm V$, $\\mathrm H=\\mathrm T\\,\\mathrm m^2/\\mathrm A$ and $\\mathrm S=\\mathrm A/\\mathrm V$; these make the three displayed relations dimensionally consistent. Local, linear and nondispersive (instantaneous) response is part of this restricted assumption. In the isotropic case an exact broadband interpretation of the constant-coefficient law additionally requires $\\varepsilon\\mu\\ge c^{-2}$, so that the characteristic speed $1/\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\mu}$ does not exceed the vacuum speed $c$; without that condition, and in the anisotropic branch, the constant-coefficient law is a declared finite-band effective phase-response model and carries no universal front-speed claim.\n\n**(c) [Causal memory branch]** Alternatively, at each fixed material position $x$, declare a bounded continuous full history $E_x:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ in $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and its response $P_x:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ in $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$. Write $E(t)=E_x(t)$ and $P(t)=P_x(t)$ in the following formula; any field on a smaller time interval uses a declared full-history extension, not unspecified past values. The polarisation response is the causal convolution\n\n$$P(t)=\\varepsilon_0\\int_0^\\infty K(s)\\,E(t-s)\\,ds$$\n\nfor a declared real matrix kernel $K:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ with entries in $L^1(0,\\infty)$ ([[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]) and units $\\mathrm s^{-1}$; the integral then converges absolutely because $|K_{jk}(s)E_k(t-s)|\\le(\\sup|E_k|)|K_{jk}(s)|$. The convolution uses only the past history; its continuity and causality are proved in `pthm-em-causal-dispersive-response`, while passivity is an additional model condition. Spatial or temporal differentiability of the resulting fields requires separate hypotheses when used. A finitely-subtracted analytic response may instead be declared by a dimensionless scalar transfer function $\\chi$ that is holomorphic on the open upper half-plane, continuous on its closure, of class $C^1$ on the real boundary away from a real subtraction point $\\omega_0$, holomorphic on a neighbourhood of $\\omega_0$ in $\\mathbb C$, and satisfies $|\\chi(z)|\\le C(1+|z|^{n-1})$ on $\\{\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0\\}$ outside a compact set for some integer $n\\ge1$ and constant $C>0$ ([[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]]). Here $z,\\omega_0$ are numerical angular frequencies in $\\mathrm s^{-1}$ and the growth bound uses those SI numerical coordinates. Require also $\\chi(-\\omega)=\\overline{\\chi(\\omega)}$ for every real $\\omega$, hence $\\chi(0)\\in\\mathbb R$, so that the response is defined on real harmonic histories independently of their representation. For $\\omega\\ne0$ the amplitude at that frequency is unique and reversing frequency conjugates it; the symmetry gives the same real output. At $\\omega=0$ only $\\operatorname{Re}e$ determines the input, and real $\\chi(0)$ makes the output depend only on that input. For $e\\in\\mathbb C^3$, real $\\omega$, and componentwise real part, declaring this transfer means adopting the harmonic relation $E(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(e e^{-i\\omega t})\\mapsto P(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(\\varepsilon_0\\chi(\\omega)e e^{-i\\omega t})$; upper-half-plane holomorphy alone does not construct a time-domain response for arbitrary histories.\n\n**(d) [Status]** These are adopted model assumptions, one branch at a time; the framework does not derive them from the vacuum equations or from coarse graining, and no microscopic mechanism, material data or empirical validity is claimed. Ferroelectric and ferromagnetic response, hysteresis, nonlinear response and general nonlocal response lie outside the branches declared here.",
          "strategy": "An adopted nonproof formulation. The objects are those of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]]; the macroscopic field equations whose formulation the closure completes are those of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]]; the rest-frame notion is the inertial frame of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] used by [[def-em-lab-geometry]], and the memory-branch kernel class is the $L^1$ notion of [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]], whose absolute convergence for bounded continuous histories is the elementary domination displayed in the statement. The finitely-subtracted branch uses the holomorphy notion of [[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]] and states its growth and boundary hypotheses explicitly rather than hiding them. Clause (d) records that the branches are assumptions with no microscopic or empirical content. No proof is supplied and none is claimed, in accordance with the nonproof status of a postulate; the source and scope review covers the formulation, the rest-frame and scale qualifications and the non-derivation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.3 printed p. 184 (PDF p. 192): 'in the simplest, linear materials' $\\mathbf D=\\epsilon\\mathbf E$ and $\\mathbf B=\\mu\\mathbf H$, with the warning that the permittivity is not as constant as it is pretended there; §7.5.4 printed pp. 200-204 (PDF pp. 208-212): response functions, the causal convolution and its analyticity; §7.6.1 printed pp. 205-206 (PDF pp. 213-214): Ohm's law $\\mathbf J=\\sigma\\mathbf E$ and its DC limit. Read in the retained 236-page full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Electromagnetism (University of Texas at Austin course), node 78: Magnetic energy",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node78.html",
