        {
          "id": "pthm-em-causal-dispersive-response",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Specified causal material response: transfer function, dispersion relations and the passivity condition",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#D1",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-dependent-choice",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "lem-em-finitely-subtracted-analytic-dispersion",
            "post-em-material-closure",
            "thm-em-causal-dispersive-response",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "lem-em-period-average",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-dependent-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-finitely-subtracted-analytic-dispersion": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-material-closure": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-em-causal-dispersive-response": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-period-average": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the memory branch of the material-closure postulate [[post-em-material-closure]] in a material rest frame, and assume the hypotheses of the mathematical suppliers [[thm-em-causal-dispersive-response]] and [[lem-em-finitely-subtracted-analytic-dispersion]]: Countable Choice for the smooth comparison route and, in the integrable branches, Dependent Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]). Let $E:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be the electric-field history in the material rest frame, read componentwise in its spatial basis ([[def-em-fields]]), and let $P$ be the polarisation response declared by the model.\n\n**(a) [Causal response]** If the kernel of [[post-em-material-closure]](c) satisfies $K\\in L^1(0,\\infty;\\mathbb R^{3\\times3})$, then for every bounded continuous history $E$ the response $P(t)=\\varepsilon_0\\int_0^\\infty K(s)E(t-s)\\,ds$ is well defined for every $t$, bounded and continuous, linear in $E$, and causal: histories equal on $(-\\infty,t_0]$ produce responses equal on $(-\\infty,t_0]$.\n\n**(b) [Transfer function and dispersion relations]** If $K$ is smooth with compact support in $(0,\\infty)$, the transfer function $\\chi(z)=\\int_0^\\infty K(s)e^{izs}\\,ds$ is entire, satisfies $\\chi(-\\omega)=\\overline{\\chi(\\omega)}$ for real $\\omega$, obeys $|\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le\\|K_{jk}''\\|_{L^1}|z|^{-2}$ for $z\\ne0$ with $\\operatorname{Im}z\\ge0$ (with the whole-plane bound carrying the exponential factor $e^{b\\max(0,-\\operatorname{Im}z)}$ when $\\operatorname{supp}K\\subseteq(0,b)$), and its entries satisfy the principal-value dispersion relations of [[thm-em-causal-dispersive-response]](b). If $K$ is locally absolutely continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ with $K,K'\\in L^1$ and $sK\\in L^1$, then $\\chi$ is holomorphic on the open upper half-plane, continuous with continuous first derivative on the real boundary, obeys the stated growth bound, and satisfies the same pair of relations; an instantaneous real part $\\chi_\\infty$ may be added to the model, and the relations apply to $\\chi_{\\mathrm{total}}-\\chi_\\infty$ for $\\chi_{\\mathrm{total}}=\\chi_\\infty+\\chi$.\n\n**(c) [Finitely-subtracted and polynomial branches]** For each specified integer $n\\ge1$ for which the full hypotheses of [[lem-em-finitely-subtracted-analytic-dispersion]] hold for the declared response transfer function, its $n$-subtracted relations apply entrywise. Any transfer-only physical branch additionally requires $\\chi(-\\omega)=\\overline{\\chi(\\omega)}$ entrywise for every real $\\omega$, including real $\\chi(0)$, as in [[post-em-material-closure]](c); scalar transfers act as scalar matrices. These hypotheses include upper-half-plane holomorphy, continuity on the closed upper half-plane, the stated boundary $C^1$ regularity, holomorphy near the real subtraction point $\\omega_0$, and the order-specific growth bound $|\\chi_{jk}(z)|\\le C_{jk}(1+|z|^{n-1})$ outside a compact set. The Taylor polynomial and residual are\n\n$$P_n(z)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{\\chi^{(j)}(\\omega_0)}{j!}(z-\\omega_0)^j,\\qquad f_n(z)=\\frac{\\chi(z)-P_n(z)}{(z-\\omega_0)^n},$$\n\nwith removable values and the boundary $C^1$ hypothesis read entrywise as in the supplier. A growth bound for one order $n_0$ permits every order $n\\ge n_0$ under the same local analytic and boundary hypotheses; it does not justify lower orders for which the required growth fails. The unsubtracted $n=0$ case is included only under its own decay and boundary hypotheses: $P_0=0$, $f_0=\\chi$, growth $O(1/|z|)$, and factor $(\\omega-\\omega_0)^0=1$ with the supplier's principal-value convention. The separate constitutive-polynomial branch declares the normalized polarisation $P/\\varepsilon_0=\\sum_{j=0}^mA_jE^{(j)}+K*E$, with real scalar coefficients (acting as scalar matrices) or real $3\\times3$ matrices $A_j$ whose entries carry $\\mathrm s^j$, and $K$ satisfying the supplier's integrable causal hypotheses, is causal on histories with bounded continuous derivatives through order $m$. Its transfer is $Q(z)+\\chi_K(z)$ with $Q(z)=\\sum_{j=0}^mA_j(-iz)^j$. Subtracting the declared $Q$ leaves the proved relations for $\\chi_K$, without assuming that $Q$ is the Taylor polynomial $P_n$.\n\n**(d) [Monochromatic work, instantaneous work and passivity]** Let $\\omega>0$, $e\\in\\mathbb C^3$ and $E(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(e e^{-i\\omega t})$. Write $\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\omega)$ for the full transfer matrix used in the response: it is $\\chi(\\omega)$ without an instantaneous term and $\\chi_\\infty+\\chi(\\omega)$ when the arbitrary real instantaneous matrix $\\chi_\\infty\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ is present. For a transfer-function-only branch adopt $P(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(\\varepsilon_0\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\omega)e e^{-i\\omega t})$ as the harmonic response convention; in a kernel or normalized polynomial branch this follows by substitution. Here $e$ has units $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, $\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ is dimensionless, $e^*$ denotes conjugate transpose, $(C^*)_{jk}=\\overline{C_{kj}}$ for a complex matrix $C$, a matrix $H$ is Hermitian when $H^*=H$ and is positive semidefinite when $v^*Hv\\ge0$ for every $v\\in\\mathbb C^3$, in the convention of [[thm-em-causal-dispersive-response]](d), and $\\langle g\\rangle:=T^{-1}\\int_0^Tg(t)\\,dt$ with $T=2\\pi/\\omega$. The period-averaged work per unit volume, in $\\mathrm W\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$, is\n\n$$\\langle E\\cdot\\partial_tP\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0\\omega}{2}\\operatorname{Im}(e^*\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\omega)e).$$\n\nIt is nonnegative for every $e$ if and only if the full Hermitian dissipative part\n\n$$M_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\omega)=\\frac{\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\omega)-\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\omega)^*}{2i}$$\n\nis positive semidefinite. This frequencywise monochromatic passivity is an additional model condition, not a consequence of causality or dispersion.\n\nFor the instantaneous part set $S=(\\chi_\\infty+\\chi_\\infty^{\\mathsf T})/2$ and $A=(\\chi_\\infty-\\chi_\\infty^{\\mathsf T})/2$, so $S^{\\mathsf T}=S$ and $A^{\\mathsf T}=-A$. For any real $C^1$ history its exact instantaneous work split is\n\n$$E\\cdot\\partial_t(\\varepsilon_0\\chi_\\infty E)=\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}E\\cdot S E\\right)+\\varepsilon_0 E\\cdot A\\partial_tE.$$\n\nThe symmetric part therefore supplies the quadratic storage term; no positivity of that term is asserted unless $S$ is additionally positive semidefinite. The antisymmetric part has $E\\cdot A E=0$ but can supply nonzero work over a cycle. Its monochromatic contribution is $\\varepsilon_0\\omega\\operatorname{Im}(e^*A e)/2$, and\n\n$$M_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\omega)=M_K(\\omega)+A/i,\\qquad M_K(\\omega)=\\frac{\\chi(\\omega)-\\chi(\\omega)^*}{2i}.$$\n\nThus only a symmetric real instantaneous matrix contributes purely this storage term for all histories; for an arbitrary real instantaneous matrix the passivity test must include its antisymmetric contribution. For example, $\\chi_\\infty=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\\\-1&0&0\\\\0&0&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $K=0$ and $e=(1,i,0)^{\\mathsf T}$ give $E=(\\cos\\omega t,\\sin\\omega t,0)$, zero quadratic storage and averaged work $\\varepsilon_0\\omega>0$. The opposite circular amplitude $(1,-i,0)^{\\mathsf T}$ gives averaged work $-\\varepsilon_0\\omega$, so this instantaneous response is causal but fails the all-amplitude passivity condition.\n\n**(e) [Scope]** The response is declared in the material rest frame of [[post-em-material-closure]](a); boosting the material changes the fields and the rest observer, and no frame-independent instantaneous response law is asserted. The framework does not claim that any particular material realises these branches, no microscopic mechanism is derived, and no empirical statement is made.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** supplier identification for the analytic branches, followed by finite-dimensional work calculations.\n\n(a)–(b) Apply the fully stated mathematical response supplier entrywise to the declared rest-frame kernel and multiply its convolution by the framework's positive normalization $\\varepsilon_0$. This retains existence, continuity, boundedness, linearity, causality, the stated entire or upper-half-plane analytic transfer, reality identity, decay and dispersion conditions. Adding any real constant matrix changes only the total transfer, and the dispersion formulas remain those for $\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}-\\chi_\\infty$; this analytic subtraction asserts no work or storage property.\n\n(c) Fix an order $n$ satisfying the exact finite-subtraction supplier hypotheses. Taylor expansion near $\\omega_0$ makes the zero in $\\chi-P_n$ removable after division by $(z-\\omega_0)^n$, and the order-specific growth yields $f_n(z)=O(1/|z|)$ in the closed upper half-plane. The supplier's boundary contour proof then gives its two principal-value relations. Do not apply it to a lower order without that order's growth bound. If the bound holds at $n_0$, then $1+|z|^{n_0-1}\\le2(1+|z|^{n-1})$ for $|z|\\ge1$ and $n\\ge n_0$, so the growth bound also holds at every larger order. Local holomorphy near $\\omega_0$ supplies all the corresponding Taylor derivatives and removable boundary regularity; away from that point division by a nonzero polynomial preserves the stated $C^1$ boundary property. The distinct $n=0$ branch requires its own decay. For the constitutive polynomial, the derivative multiplier on $e^{-i\\omega t}$ is $(-i\\omega)^j$, so the declared transfer polynomial is exactly $Q$, and the remainder $\\chi_K$ uses the integrable supplier rather than an assumed Taylor identification.\n\n(d) Apply clause (d) of [[thm-em-causal-dispersive-response]] to the whole monochromatic transfer matrix. Its proof uses the finite period-average identity and makes no analytic, decay, symmetry or memory-kernel assumption about that matrix, so it applies equally to $\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}$. The identity $e^*M_{\\mathrm{tot}}e=\\operatorname{Im}(e^*\\chi_{\\mathrm{tot}}e)$ proves the exact positive-semidefinite criterion. For arbitrary real $\\chi_\\infty$, decompose it as $S+A$. The coordinate product rule and symmetry give $\\frac{d}{dt}(E^{\\mathsf T}SE)=2E^{\\mathsf T}S\\partial_tE$, whereas $E^{\\mathsf T}AE=0$ by transposition. Hence the displayed exact work split. On a periodic history the storage derivative has zero average. Because a real symmetric $S$ is Hermitian, $e^*Se$ is real; the remaining average is the stated antisymmetric quadratic form. The real matrix identity $\\chi_\\infty^*=\\chi_\\infty^{\\mathsf T}$ gives $(\\chi_\\infty-\\chi_\\infty^*)/(2i)=A/i$ and the total dissipative-part formula. In the explicit skew example, $\\chi_\\infty e=(i,-1,0)^{\\mathsf T}$ and $e^*\\chi_\\infty e=2i$; directly $\\partial_tE=\\omega(-\\sin\\omega t,\\cos\\omega t,0)$ and $\\chi_\\infty\\partial_tE=\\omega E$, giving work $\\varepsilon_0\\omega$ at every time. Replacing $i$ by $-i$ reverses the work. More generally, if a real antisymmetric matrix has $A_{jk}=a\\ne0$, choosing $e=e_j+i e_k$ yields $e^*Ae=2ia$, hence nonzero cycle work. Therefore pure storage for all histories requires $A=0$, while $A=0$ suffices. No sign condition for an arbitrary matrix or positivity of storage is inferred from reality alone.\n\n(e) Retain the material-rest-frame scope, every stated Choice hypothesis and the absence of microscopic or empirical conclusions. These finite-dimensional corrections add no Choice assumption and no material-realization claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.5.4 printed pp. 200-204 (PDF pp. 208-212): the response function and causal convolution (7.39)-(7.40), the requirement $\\tilde\\alpha(t)=0$ for $t<0$, the analyticity of $\\alpha(\\omega)$ in $\\operatorname{Im}\\omega>0$, and the Kramers-Kronig relations with the explicit caveat that $\\alpha$ must fall off faster than $1/|\\omega|$ at infinity. Read in the retained 236-page full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§4.4.1 printed pp. 179-181, equations (4.30)-(4.33): the linear-dielectric polarisation $\\mathbf P=\\epsilon_0\\chi_e\\mathbf E$, the permittivity $\\epsilon=\\epsilon_0(1+\\chi_e)$ and the statement that the response is a model of many (but not all) substances; read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The causal polarisation response of the memory branch of the material-closure postulate, with the transfer function, its regularity and growth properties, the principal-value dispersion relations (unsubtracted, instantaneous-subtracted, finitely Taylor-subtracted and constitutive-polynomial branches) and the monochromatic work formula together with the exact positive-semidefiniteness criterion for passivity. Conditional on the declared kernel or transfer-function hypotheses and on AC_omega (and Dependent Choice in the integrable branches); passivity is not inferred from causality; no material, microscopic or empirical claim. For arbitrary real instantaneous matrices, storage is assigned only to the symmetric quadratic part and the antisymmetric part is retained in work and in the total frequencywise passivity criterion. Each finite subtraction order requires its own growth and regularity hypotheses; higher allowed orders are retained.",
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-restricted-material-energy",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Energy balance and Ohmic dissipation for the constant nondispersive linear model",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K5",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
