          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-polarization-magnetization": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-parameter-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-the-divergence-and-curl-of-a-cross-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-material-closure": "physical-assumption",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell": "physical-result",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-the-image-of-a-lower-dimensional-c1-manifold-is-null": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-null-sets-in-rn-closed-under-subsets-and-countable-unions": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the elementary inertial-rest branch of [[post-em-material-closure]]: constant symmetric positive-definite matrices $\\varepsilon,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ (isotropic scalar case included) with $D=\\varepsilon E$ and $B=\\mu H$, and the conduction relation $J_f=\\sigma E+J_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ with $\\sigma$ symmetric positive semidefinite and a declared external free current $J_{\\mathrm{ext}}$. Assume the smooth branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] with the regularity hypotheses of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]], so that its macroscopic equations hold on $\\Omega\\times I$. Define the stored-energy density and the energy-flux vector\n\n$$u:=\\tfrac12\\bigl(E\\cdot D+B\\cdot H\\bigr),\\qquad S:=E\\times H,$$\n\nwith the Euclidean inner product of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and the cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; $u$ carries $\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}=\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}\\mathrm s^{-2}$ and $S$ carries $\\mathrm W\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$. Then:\n\n**(a) [Local balance and positivity]** On $\\Omega\\times I$,\n\n$$\\partial_tu+\\operatorname{div}S=-J_f\\cdot E.$$\n\nMoreover $u=\\tfrac12(E\\cdot\\varepsilon E+H\\cdot\\mu H)\\ge0$, with $u(x,t)>0$ whenever $(E(x,t),H(x,t))\\ne(0,0)$.\n\n**(b) [Dissipation reading]** With $J_f=\\sigma E+J_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ and $\\sigma$ symmetric positive semidefinite, $E\\cdot\\sigma E\\ge0$ and the balance reads\n\n$$\\partial_tu+\\operatorname{div}S+E\\cdot\\sigma E=-J_{\\mathrm{ext}}\\cdot E;$$\n\nthe nonnegative term $E\\cdot\\sigma E$ is the Ohmic dissipation density of the model.\n\n**(c) [Fixed-volume balance]** Let $\\Omega'\\subseteq\\Omega$ be open with $\\overline{\\Omega'}\\subseteq\\Omega$ carrying the finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation of [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], let $t_0<t_1$ with $(t_0,t_1)\\subseteq I$, assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) as in that divergence theorem, and let $n$ be the outward unit normal of $\\partial\\Omega'$. Then for every $t\\in(t_0,t_1)$,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{\\Omega'}u(x,t)\\,dx=-\\int_{\\Omega'}J_f\\cdot E\\,dx-\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega'}S\\cdot n\\,dS.$$\n\n**(d) [Scope]** The density $u=\\tfrac12(E\\cdot D+B\\cdot H)$ is a stored-energy density only for this constant symmetric nondispersive model; for general dispersive, nonlocal, nonlinear or hysteretic response no instantaneous stored-energy density is defined or claimed by this item, and the general dispersive work balance is not supplied. The identities hold only where the fields and sources have the stated regularity, the fixed-volume form keeps its boundary term, and conservation of $\\int_{\\Omega\\prime}u$ requires the sum of outward flux and volume work on the right of (c) to vanish, not merely zero boundary flux. No empirical claim is made.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** dot the macroscopic curl equations with the complementary fields and use the cross-product divergence identity. (a): by [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]], $\\operatorname{curl}H=J_f+\\partial_tD$ and $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$. Taking the Euclidean inner product ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]) of the first with $E$ and of the second with $H$ gives $E\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}H=E\\cdot J_f+E\\cdot\\partial_tD$ and $H\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}E=-H\\cdot\\partial_tB$. By [[lem-the-divergence-and-curl-of-a-cross-product]], $\\operatorname{div}(E\\times H)=H\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}E-E\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}H$; subtracting the two scalar equations from this identity gives $\\operatorname{div}S+\\partial_t\\bigl(\\tfrac12E\\cdot D+\\tfrac12B\\cdot H\\bigr)+J_f\\cdot E=0$, because the symmetry of $\\varepsilon$ gives $E\\cdot\\partial_tD=\\tfrac12\\partial_t(E\\cdot\\varepsilon E)$ and the symmetry of $\\mu$ gives $H\\cdot\\partial_tB=\\tfrac12\\partial_t(H\\cdot\\mu H)$, using $D=\\varepsilon E$, $B=\\mu H$ and the product rule of [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]. Positivity is positive definiteness of $\\varepsilon,\\mu$: $u$ is a sum of two nonnegative quadratic forms, vanishing only when $E=H=0$. (b): $J_f\\cdot E=E\\cdot\\sigma E+J_{\\mathrm{ext}}\\cdot E$ and $E\\cdot\\sigma E\\ge0$ for symmetric positive semidefinite $\\sigma$. (c): [[lem-em-parameter-integrals]] differentiates $t\\mapsto\\int_{\\Omega'}u$ under the integral sign and [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]] converts $\\int_{\\Omega'}\\operatorname{div}S$ into the boundary flux; $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is exactly the hypothesis of that divergence theorem and is used only in this clause. (d) records the restrictions. No constitutive assumption beyond the declared closure branch, and no empirical premise, is used.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Electromagnetism (University of Texas at Austin course), node 78: Magnetic energy",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node78.html",
                "locator": "node 78: Ohm's law (886), the ohmic heating rate $j_t^2/\\sigma$ (887), the vector identity $\\nabla\\cdot(\\mathbf E\\times\\mathbf H)=\\mathbf H\\cdot(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf E)-\\mathbf E\\cdot(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf H)$ (889), and $W=\\frac12\\int\\mathbf H\\cdot\\mathbf B$ (893)-(894) for linear media, with the hysteresis-loop failure discussed; read in the retained offline full text of the course."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§4.4.3 printed pp. 191-193, equations (4.57)-(4.58): $\\Delta W=\\int(\\Delta\\mathbf D)\\cdot\\mathbf E\\,d\\tau$ valid for any material, and $W=\\frac12\\int\\mathbf D\\cdot\\mathbf E\\,d\\tau$ for a linear dielectric; read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.6.1 printed pp. 205-206 (PDF pp. 213-214): Ohm's law $\\mathbf J=\\sigma\\mathbf E$ and the dissipative reading of the real part of the conductivity; §7.6.2 printed p. 207 (PDF p. 215): the macroscopic equations with free charge and current (7.49). Read in the retained 236-page full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin; complete course book, 364 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.physics.ntua.gr/ph03/doc/Fitzpatrick.R.@.Classical.Electromagnetism.pdf",
                "locator": "\\u00a76.5 'Energy density within a dielectric medium' printed p. 203, equations (6.34)-(6.38), and \\u00a76.11 'Magnetic energy' printed p. 213: the ohmic heating rate, the magnetic energy \\u00bd\\u222bH\\u00b7B for linear media and the hysteresis-loop failure; read in the fetched complete 364-page course book."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The local and fixed-volume energy balance d_t u + div(E x H) = -J_f.E with u=(E.D+B.H)/2 and the Ohmic dissipation reading E.sigma E>=0 for the constant symmetric nondispersive linear model with symmetric positive-semidefinite sigma, under AC_omega for the fixed-volume form. Restricted to this model: no stored-energy claim for dispersive, nonlocal, nonlinear or hysteretic media; boundary term retained; no conservation assertion and no empirical claim.",
          "dependency_level": 17
