      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-em-constitutive-unit-counterexample",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Matching units does not determine the permittivity or the response law",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X4",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-polarization-magnetization",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "post-em-material-closure",
            "pthm-em-causal-dispersive-response",
            "thm-ftc-second-part"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-polarization-magnetization": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-material-closure": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-em-causal-dispersive-response": "physical-result",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "**(a) [Dimensional exclusion of $E=\\varepsilon_0D$]** In the SI conventions of [[def-em-si-conventions]]: the electric constant $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ carries $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}=\\mathrm C^2\\mathrm s^2\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\\mathrm m^{-3}$, the displacement $D$ of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]] carries $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$, and the electric field $E$ of [[def-em-fields]] carries $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}=\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}\\mathrm C^{-1}$. The product $\\varepsilon_0D$ therefore carries $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}=\\mathrm C^3\\mathrm s^2\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\\mathrm m^{-5}$, which is not the dimension of $E$: the proposed identification $E=\\varepsilon_0D$ fails dimensionally in SI and cannot be a field relation of the framework.\n\n**(b) [Units do not determine the law]** Solving the definition $D=\\varepsilon_0E+P$ of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]] gives $E=(D-P)/\\varepsilon_0$, which in vacuum ($P=0$) reads $E=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}D$. Any proposed law $E=\\eta D$ with a constant $\\eta>0$ of dimension $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm C^{-1}$ is dimensionally admissible; dimensional consistency therefore does not identify $\\eta$, and it does not determine whether the response is local, linear or instantaneous. In the model of [[post-em-material-closure]] the scalar relation is $D=\\varepsilon E$, i.e. $\\eta=\\varepsilon^{-1}$, and $\\varepsilon$ (or $\\chi_e=\\varepsilon/\\varepsilon_0-1$) is fixed by the declared material model, not by units.\n\n**(c) [Frequency-domain symbols are not instantaneous coefficients]** In the memory branch of [[post-em-material-closure]](c) the response is a causal convolution in time; a scalar $\\varepsilon(\\omega)$ introduced in the harmonic description is defined for a single harmonic history $E(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(e e^{-i\\omega t})$ by the response $D(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(\\varepsilon(\\omega)e e^{-i\\omega t})$; for a scalar kernel this means $\\varepsilon(\\omega)=\\varepsilon_0(1+\\int_0^\\infty K(s)e^{i\\omega s}ds)$. This is the harmonic convention of [[pthm-em-causal-dispersive-response]], not an assumption that an arbitrary bounded continuous history has a Fourier transform. The framework therefore does not read $\\varepsilon(\\omega)$ as an instantaneous multiplicative coefficient in the time domain: doing so silently replaces the convolution by an instantaneous law and is a model-identification error of the same kind as (a).\n\n**(d) [Scope]** A dimensional and model-identification statement about the framework's own conventions; it asserts no measurement, no material property and no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct dimensional computation and immediate consequences of the definitions. (a): the units of $\\varepsilon_0$, $D$ and $E$ are those of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; the derived-unit identities $\\mathrm F=\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm V^{-1}$ and $\\mathrm V=\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m^2\\mathrm s^{-2}\\mathrm C^{-1}$ give $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}=\\mathrm C^2\\mathrm s^2\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\\mathrm m^{-3}$ and $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}=\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}\\mathrm C^{-1}$; multiplying the first by $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$ gives $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$, a different combination of the base units $\\mathrm{kg},\\mathrm m,\\mathrm s,\\mathrm C$. (b): $D=\\varepsilon_0E+P$ is a definition of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]], and solving it is elementary algebra; the vacuum constant $\\eta=\\varepsilon_0^{-1}$ has the dimension of $E/D$ while being a model constant. (c): the memory branch is a convolution in time by [[post-em-material-closure]](c); a harmonic symbol acts on Fourier components and does not define an instantaneous coefficient. No physical measurement and no particular material is asserted.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§4.4.1 printed pp. 179-181, equations (4.30)-(4.34): the polarisation $\\mathbf P=\\epsilon_0\\chi_e\\mathbf E$, the permittivity $\\epsilon=\\epsilon_0(1+\\chi_e)$ and the warning that the permittivity does not convey information beyond the material's susceptibility. Read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              },
              {
                "title": "BIPM, Le Systeme international d'unites / The International System of Units (SI), 9th edition (Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, 2019, 214 pp., bilingual French-English)",
                "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.3.3-§2.3.4 (English printed pp. 129-140): the coulomb, volt, farad and tesla as derived units with their defining relations, from which $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}=\\mathrm C^2\\mathrm s^2\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\\mathrm m^{-3}$ and $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}=\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-2}\\mathrm C^{-1}$. Read in the stamped 214-page bilingual PDF."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked dimensional and model-identification example for the framework's SI conventions: the proposed law E=eps0 D is excluded by dimensional analysis, dimensionally admissible laws do not determine the permittivity or the response law, and a frequency-domain symbol epsilon(omega) is not an instantaneous time-domain coefficient. No measurement, no material data and no empirical claim.",
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-dielectric-interface-example",
