          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory frame, coordinates $(x,y,z)$ and units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], and let $S=\\{z=0\\}\\cap\\Omega$ be a stationary planar interface with unit normal $n=e_z$ pointing from the side $\\Omega_-=\\Omega\\cap\\{z<0\\}$ to the side $\\Omega_+=\\Omega\\cap\\{z>0\\}$. Suppose each half-space carries a homogeneous linear dielectric at rest in the laboratory frame in the elementary branch of [[post-em-material-closure]]: constants $\\varepsilon_1,\\varepsilon_2\\in(0,\\infty)$ with $D=\\varepsilon_1E$ on $\\Omega_-$ and $D=\\varepsilon_2E$ on $\\Omega_+$. Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) and let $E,B,D,H$ be locally integrable, $C^1$ off the plane and jointly one-sided $C^1$ up to it on every compact test cylinder, as in the joint branch of [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]. Assume locally the four distributional macroscopic equations of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]], with volume sources $\\rho_v,J_v$ continuous off the plane and locally integrable across it, free charge $\\rho_f=\\rho_v+\\sigma_f\\delta_S$ for a constant $\\sigma_f\\in\\mathbb R$ in $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$, and free current $J_f=J_v$ (no free sheet current). Here the plane distribution is defined locally by $\\langle\\delta_S,\\psi\\rangle=\\int\\psi(x,y,0,t)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dt$ for compactly supported tests on the laboratory region; no global compactness of this plane is asserted. These are the local planar counterparts of the compact-interface hypotheses in [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]. Write $E_{j\\,t}$, $E_{j\\,n}$ for the tangential and normal parts of the one-sided trace $E_j$ and $D_j$ ($j=1$ on $\\Omega_-$, $j=2$ on $\\Omega_+$), defined by $E_{jn}:=n\\cdot E_j$ and $E_{jt}:=E_j-E_{jn}n$, with the analogous definitions for $D_j$ and $B_j$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Then:\n\n**(a) [Tangential field]** $n\\times[E]=0$, i.e. $E_{2t}=E_{1t}$: the tangential electric field is continuous across the interface.\n\n**(b) [Normal displacement]** $n\\cdot[D]=\\sigma_f$, i.e. $\\varepsilon_2E_{2n}-\\varepsilon_1E_{1n}=\\sigma_f$, hence $E_{2n}=\\frac{\\varepsilon_1}{\\varepsilon_2}E_{1n}+\\frac{\\sigma_f}{\\varepsilon_2}$.\n\n**(c) [No free sheet charge]** If $\\sigma_f=0$, then $\\varepsilon_2E_{2n}=\\varepsilon_1E_{1n}$ and $E_{2n}=\\frac{\\varepsilon_1}{\\varepsilon_2}E_{1n}$: the normal component is scaled by the positive ratio $\\varepsilon_1/\\varepsilon_2$, so $E_{2n}$ and $E_{1n}$ have the same sign (and vanish together). Precisely, $E_{2n}=E_{1n}$ if and only if $(\\varepsilon_1-\\varepsilon_2)E_{1n}=0$: equality holds when the normal field is zero even for unequal permittivities; if $E_{1n}\\ne0$, it holds exactly when $\\varepsilon_1=\\varepsilon_2$. A free surface charge $\\sigma_f\\ne0$ modifies the normal condition as displayed and is not silently set to zero.\n\n**(d) [Magnetic companions]** $n\\cdot[B]=0$ and, with $K_f=0$, $n\\times[H]=0$; for nonmagnetic media with $H=\\mu_0^{-1}B$ on both sides this gives $B_{2n}=B_{1n}$ and $B_{2t}=B_{1t}$.\n\n**(e) [Scope]** A local interface computation under the stated model hypotheses: it solves the electric jump relations against the linear constitutive relations on the two sides and asserts no global field configuration, no existence of the fields, no constitutive law beyond the declared branch and no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** local compact-test interface integration by parts, followed by substitution of the constitutive relations. The generally noncompact plane is not substituted into a globally compact-interface theorem. On each compact test cylinder, split the $z$ integral at zero; [[thm-integration-by-parts]] applied to the one-sided continuously differentiable factors on each side gives $\\partial_z F_j=\\{\\partial_zF_j\\}+[F_j]\\delta_S$, while tangential derivatives have no sheet term. The compact-support integrands and their piecewise derivatives are bounded on the two closed half-cylinders and integrable; [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] therefore integrates these identities over $x,y,t$ and gives $\\operatorname{div}F=\\{\\operatorname{div}F\\}+e_z\\cdot[F]\\delta_S$ and $\\operatorname{curl}F=\\{\\operatorname{curl}F\\}+e_z\\times[F]\\delta_S$. Joint one-sided $C^1$ regularity bounds all regular terms locally, and time differentiation at this fixed plane contributes no sheet term. Insert these formulas into the assumed four distributional equations. To isolate a continuous sheet coefficient, test with a fixed tangential/time test times a rescaled normal cutoff supplied by [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]], equal to one at zero and supported in $|z|<h$; the regular difference terms are locally integrable (field derivatives are locally bounded, and volume sources are locally integrable), so their integrals tend to zero as $h\\to0$ by [[thm-dominated-convergence]], dominated by their absolute values on a fixed compact cylinder; the plane is a volume-null set, whereas the sheet pairing is unchanged. Thus the four continuous coefficients agree pointwise. This proves the local identities $n\\cdot[D]=\\sigma_f$, $n\\cdot[B]=0$, $n\\times[E]=0$, $n\\times[H]=0$, with precisely the signs of the compact-interface theorem. (a)-(b): in particular $n\\times[E]=0$ and $n\\cdot[D]=\\sigma_f$ pointwise on $S$; decomposing $E_j=E_{j\\,t}+E_{j\\,n}\\,n$ and using $D_j=\\varepsilon_jE_j$ from [[post-em-material-closure]] (with the constant scalars $\\varepsilon_1,\\varepsilon_2>0$) yields the displayed scalar relations, and solving the second for $E_{2n}$ is division by the positive constant $\\varepsilon_2$. (c): set $\\sigma_f=0$ in (b); subtract $E_{1n}$ to obtain $E_{2n}-E_{1n}=((\\varepsilon_1-\\varepsilon_2)/\\varepsilon_2)E_{1n}$. Since $\\varepsilon_2>0$, its vanishing is exactly the displayed product condition; division by $E_{1n}$ is permitted only in the nonzero branch. (d): the remaining two local identities just proved; in the nonmagnetic case $H=\\mu_0^{-1}B$ on both sides, so $n\\times[H]=0$ reads $n\\times[B]=0$. The tangential/normal decomposition uses [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. (e) records the scope. Only the jump theorem, the declared constitutive branch and elementary algebra are used.",
