          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory frame and units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], with spatial coordinates $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$, $z=x_3$, $r=|x|$ and the applied field along $e_3$. The potentials below are functions on the full ball and full exterior in $\\mathbb R^3$; their physical fields are restricted to the laboratory region, while (c)-(d) concern the full exterior. Fix a radius $a>0$ in metres with $\\overline B_a\\subseteq\\Omega$, so that the interface $S=\\{r=a\\}$ below lies in the laboratory region, a field magnitude $E_0>0$ in $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and constants $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}},\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\in(0,\\infty)$ (the interior and exterior permittivities of the elementary branch of [[post-em-material-closure]], the material being at rest in the laboratory frame), and put\n\n$$C:=\\frac{3\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}}{\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}},\\qquad d:=\\frac{E_0a^3(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}})}{\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}}.$$\n\nConsider the time-independent potentials\n\n$$\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}(x):=-C\\,E_0\\,z\\quad(r<a),\\qquad \\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}(x):=-E_0\\,z+\\frac{d\\,z}{r^3}\\quad(r>a),$$\n\nwith fields $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$ and $D:=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}E$ inside, $D:=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}E$ outside ($E$ being of the class of [[def-em-fields]] restricted to this time-independent configuration). The coefficient $C$ is dimensionless and $d$ has units $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^2$, so both potentials carry volts. Write $S=\\{r=a\\}$, $n=e_r:=x/r$ for the outward unit normal, and $\\partial_r\\varphi:=n\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi$. For a trace $F$, its normal and tangential parts are $n\\cdot F$ and $F-(n\\cdot F)n$. Then:\n\n**(a) [Harmonicity]** $\\Delta\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=0$ on $r<a$ and $\\Delta\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}=0$ on $r>a$ in the classical sense of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], and the potentials and their first derivatives extend continuously to the closed sides of $S$.\n\n**(b) [Interface conditions]** With one-sided traces at $r=a$, $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$ and $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}\\,\\partial_r\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\,\\partial_r\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$ on $S$. Potential-trace equality implies tangential-electric continuity, and the weighted radial-derivative equality is equivalent to normal-$D$ continuity. Tangential-electric continuity alone determines the potential-trace difference only up to an additive constant; the displayed potentials have that constant fixed to zero. These electric matching identities hold independently of any magnetic completion. In addition, declare the static completion $B=H=0$, $\\rho_f=J_f=0$, with any constant symmetric positive-definite permeability and the zero-conductivity branch $\\sigma=0$, $J_{\\mathrm{ext}}=0$ of [[post-em-material-closure]]. The four fields then satisfy all four thin-interface macroscopic equations distributionally with $\\sigma_f=K_f=0$, and the joint one-sided regularity at the compact sphere verifies the hypotheses of [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]. Assume its inherited $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention ([[def-countable-choice]]) for this distribution/surface reading.\n\n**(c) [Asymptotics]** $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}(x)+E_0z=dz/r^3$ and $\\nabla(\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}+E_0z)=O(r^{-3})$ as $r\\to\\infty$: the difference from the applied uniform potential decays like $r^{-2}$ and its gradient like $r^{-3}$.\n\n**(d) [Uniqueness in the declared class]** Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as required by the Green and harmonic derivative-estimate interfaces used in this branch. Let $(\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}},\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}})$ be $C^2$ on the closed ball $\\overline{B_a}$ respectively on every closed exterior region $\\{a\\le r\\le R\\}$, harmonic on $r<a$ and $r>a$, satisfying the same interface conditions (b), and satisfying $|\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}+E_0z|\\le M/r$ for some $M$ and all sufficiently large $r$. Then $\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and $\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}$. Hence the displayed pair is the unique solution of the dielectric-sphere transmission problem in that class, existence being exhibited explicitly; the uniqueness proof uses only the Green identity, the matching conditions and the decay, with no maximum principle.\n\n**(e) [Fields, polarisation and induced dipole]** On $r<a$ the field is uniform, $E=C E_0e_3$ with $D=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}CE_0e_3$; outside, the field is the applied uniform field plus $E_p=-\\nabla\\Phi_p$, where the dipole potential in a uniform background of permittivity $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}$ is here defined as $\\Phi_p(x):=(p\\cdot x)/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}|x|^3)$ for $x\\ne0$. Its effective moment is $p=4\\pi\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}d e_3$ in $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m$; this field identification on $r>a$ asserts no point source inside the actual sphere; the interior polarisation is $P_{\\mathrm{in}}=(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\varepsilon_0)CE_0e_3$ and the bound surface charge density on $S$ is $\\sigma_b=n\\cdot(P_{\\mathrm{in}}-P_{\\mathrm{out}})$, the singular coefficient of $\\rho_b=-\\operatorname{div}P$ at $S$ (with the exterior polarisation $P_{\\mathrm{out}}=(\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}-\\varepsilon_0)E_{\\mathrm{out}}$).\n\n**(f) [Regime and scope]** The computation is a quasistatic result of the declared linear branch at $\\sigma_f=0$; using $p$ as a dynamic wave polarisability of the sphere requires a separate quasistatic/dipole approximation and is not asserted here. The example asserts no material data and no empirical claim.\n\nCompactness of $S$ follows from its closedness and boundedness ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]). Its smooth surface charts are obtained by solving $x_j=\\pm\\sqrt{a^2-\\sum_{i\\ne j}x_i^2}$ near any point where $x_j\\ne0$; such a coordinate exists because $a>0$.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** explicit substitution plus a Green-identity uniqueness argument. (a): $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}$ is linear and $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}=-E_0z+d z/r^3$; the second partials are computed with [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] and [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], giving $\\Delta z=0$, $\\Delta(r^{-3})=6r^{-5}$ and hence $\\Delta(d z/r^3)=d(z\\cdot6r^{-5}+2(-3z r^{-5}))=0$ by the product rule; both sides extend smoothly to $r=a$ from their own side. (b): evaluate $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}(a)=-CE_0a\\cos\\theta$ and $\\varphi_{\\mathrm{out}}(a)=(-E_0a+d/a^2)\\cos\\theta$, whose equality is $C=1-d/(E_0a^3)$; the radial derivatives are $-CE_0\\cos\\theta$ and $(-E_0-2d/a^3)\\cos\\theta$, whose equality after weighting by $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}},\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}$ is $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}C=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}(1+2d/(E_0a^3))$. The linear system has determinant $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}>0$ and the displayed solution, so the interface equations hold; the equivalence with the jump conditions uses $E=-\\nabla\\varphi$, $D=\\varepsilon E$ and [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]] with $\\sigma_f=0$. For the stated static completion, harmonicity gives $\\operatorname{div}D=0$ on each side and the matched normal displacement removes its sheet coefficient; the piecewise potential is continuous, so its tangential-gradient jump vanishes and $\\operatorname{curl}E=0$ distributionally. The mathematical fixed-interface identities of [[lem-em-interface-derivatives]] give these regular-plus-sheet formulas under the declared joint one-sided smoothness and $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Also $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}H=J_f+\\partial_tD=0$ because all fields are static and $B=H=J_f=0$. Thus this is an explicit local-integrable distributional macroscopic solution, not merely an assumption that potentials supplied missing fields; no microscopic-realisation or empirical claim is added. (c) is immediate from $|z|\\le r$. (d): the difference $(w_{\\mathrm{in}},w_{\\mathrm{out}})$ is harmonic on each side with matching traces $f=\\tilde\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}$ on $S$ and flux continuity $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}\\partial_rw_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\partial_rw_{\\mathrm{out}}$, and $|w_{\\mathrm{out}}|\\le(M+|d|)/r\\to0$ uniformly. Apply the first Green identity [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] to $u=v=w_{\\mathrm{in}}$ on the ball and to $u=v=w_{\\mathrm{out}}$ on the annulus $\\{a<r<R\\}$ (both are bounded C^1 domains and the functions are $C^2$ up to the boundary): since $\\Delta w=0$, this gives $\\int_{B_a}|\\nabla w_{\\mathrm{in}}|^2=\\oint_S w_{\\mathrm{in}}\\partial_rw_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and $\\int_{a<r<R}|\\nabla w_{\\mathrm{out}}|^2=\\oint_{\\{r=R\\}}w_{\\mathrm{out}}\\partial_rw_{\\mathrm{out}}-\\oint_S w_{\\mathrm{out}}\\partial_rw_{\\mathrm{out}}$, all with the outward normal of the respective domain. Multiply the first by $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and the second by $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}$ and add: the two surface terms on $S$ cancel by $w_{\\mathrm{in}}=w_{\\mathrm{out}}=f$ and $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}}\\partial_rw_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}\\partial_rw_{\\mathrm{out}}$, while the term on $\\{r=R\\}$ tends to $0$ as $R\\to\\infty$ by $|w_{\\mathrm{out}}|=O(R^{-1})$ and $|\\nabla w_{\\mathrm{out}}|=O(R^{-2})$ ([[cor-harmonic-cauchy-estimates-in-supremum-norm]] on balls of radius $R/2$). Since $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{in}},\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}>0$, both gradients vanish, so $w_{\\mathrm{in}}$ (on the ball) and $w_{\\mathrm{out}}$ (on $\\{r>a\\}$) are constant; $w_{\\mathrm{out}}\\to0$ at infinity forces $w_{\\mathrm{out}}=0$, hence the common trace $f=0$, and then $w_{\\mathrm{in}}=0$ by uniqueness for the classical Dirichlet problem [[cor-classical-dirichlet-and-poisson-problems-are-unique]](1) applied on the ball to $w_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and the zero function. $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is carried from the Green-identity and Dirichlet-uniqueness suppliers. (e): $E_{\\mathrm{in}}=-\\nabla\\varphi_{\\mathrm{in}}=CE_0e_3$; the exterior potential is the uniform potential plus the standard dipole term $p\\cdot x/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}r^3)$ with $p=4\\pi\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{out}}d\\,e_3$, and the bound surface charge is the singular coefficient $-n\\cdot[P]$ of $\\rho_b=-\\operatorname{div}P$, with the one-sided polarisations from $D=\\varepsilon E$. (f) records the regime. No empirical premise and no material data are used.",
          "provenance": {
