            "cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-lebesgue-measure-is-the-completion-of-the-product-of-lebesgue-measures": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-borel-representatives-make-the-convolution-integrand-borel-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-composition-with-borel-functions-preserves-measurability": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $N$ be the Newtonian potential transform of [[def-newtonian-potential]] in dimension $n=3$: for a measurable finite-valued function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ whose support $\\operatorname{supp}f=\\overline{\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:f(x)\\ne0\\}}$ is compact ([[def-support-and-compactly-supported-riemann-integral-in-rn]]),\n$$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(y)$$\nat each $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ where this integral is absolutely finite, where $\\Phi$ is the normalized kernel of [[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]] with its value at the pole assigned $0$, so that $\\Phi(z)=1/(4\\pi|z|)$ for $z\\ne0$; write also $g(0):=0$ and $g(z):=|z|^{-1}$ for $z\\ne0$, so that $\\Phi=g/(4\\pi)$ pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$. Use the coordinate indices $i\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], with $u_i(x):=u(x)(i)$ and $\\langle u(x),v(y)\\rangle=\\sum_{i=0}^2u_i(x)v_i(y)$. Let $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle$ be that Euclidean inner product, and let $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ be the set of maps each of whose three components is smooth ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) with compact support in the above sense; for a vector map $u$, set $\\operatorname{supp}u:=\\overline{\\{x:u(x)\\ne0\\}}$, equivalently the finite union of its component supports. For $u,v\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ write $Nu:=(Nu_0,Nu_1,Nu_2)$ componentwise, so that the linkage pairing is the map\n$$B(u,v):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle u(x),Nv(x)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3(x).$$\nThen all three integrals\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle u(x),Nv(x)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3(x),\\qquad\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle Nu(x),v(x)\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3(x),\\qquad\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\langle u(x),v(y)\\rangle}{|x-y|}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(x)$$\nconverge absolutely ([[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]], [[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]]) and have the same value; therefore $B$ is well defined, real-valued, symmetric, $B(u,v)=B(v,u)$, and bilinear over $\\mathbb R$ in each argument. In particular the common value of the three integrals is unchanged by exchanging the two density fields $u$ and $v$.",
          "strategy": "Absolute convergence first, then Fubini. Choose $R>0$ such that both supports are contained in the open ball $B_R(0)$. Assign $g(0)=0$ and $g(z)=|z|^{-1}$ otherwise. This is Borel, and the kernel-local-integrability supplier [[lem-laplace-fundamental-kernel-is-locally-integrable]] gives $K_R:=\\int_{B_{2R}}g(z)d\\lambda_3(z)<\\infty$. For each $x\\in B_R$, the translation $z=x-y$ carries $B_R$ into $B_{2R}$ and has absolute Jacobian one. The actual $L^1$ change-of-variables formula [[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]], applied to the restriction $g\\mathbf1_{B_{2R}}$, therefore gives $\\int_{B_R}g(x-y)dy\\le K_R$. Hence\n$$\\int_{B_R}\\int_{B_R}g(x-y)dy\\,dx\\le\\lambda_3(B_R)K_R<\\infty.$$\nThe ball has finite measure by [[lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure]], and the bound uses [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]. The product integrand is Borel: subtraction is continuous, the assigned kernel is Borel and the smooth density factors are Borel. Under [[thm-euclidean-lebesgue-measure-is-the-completion-of-the-product-of-lebesgue-measures]], its Euclidean and product-measure interpretations agree; in particular it is measurable for the product sigma-algebra, so [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] applies before finiteness has been established. Multiplying the displayed bound by $\\|u\\|_\\infty\\|v\\|_\\infty$ proves absolute finiteness of $\\iint\\langle u(x),v(y)\\rangle/(4\\pi|x-y|)$ (with assigned diagonal value), and [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]] permits either order. For any fixed $x$, compact bounded $v$ has an everywhere-finite Newtonian integral: choose a ball containing $x-\\operatorname{supp}v$ and bound the translated kernel integral there as above. The same estimate applies to $u$. Thus the pointwise $Nu,Nv$ of [[def-newtonian-potential]] are legitimate, and $|u\\cdot Nv|\\le\\int |u(x)|\\,|v(y)|g(x-y)/(4\\pi)dy$ makes both outer pairings integrable by the already-proved bound ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]] componentwise). Fubini identifies them with the common double integral. Exchanging the variables gives symmetry because $g(x-y)=g(y-x)$ and the Euclidean pairing is symmetric; finite linearity of the two integrals gives bilinearity ([[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]). The arbitrary diagonal assignment changes each section only at one null point and does not change these integrals, as in the Newtonian/kernel interfaces. The choice cost is exactly the declared AComega of the translated Lebesgue, ball, kernel and product-measure interfaces.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis, 2014)",
