          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-invertible-matrix-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-material-closure": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "statement": "Adopt the following reduced circuit model in the laboratory frame and SI units of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]]. The continuum material reference is [[post-em-material-closure]]; the terminal laws below are additional assumptions, rather than consequences of its pointwise constitutive relations or of Maxwell's equations.\n\n(a) **Branch data.** Fix a nonempty interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a finite set of nodes $\\mathcal N$, and a finite set of oriented branches $\\mathcal E$, each with specified tail and head nodes. Designate $n$ inductive/resistive branches, $m$ capacitive branches and any source branches. On the first family specify currents $I:J\\to\\mathbb R^n$ and separate voltage-drop vectors $V_L,V_R:J\\to\\mathbb R^n$; on the second specify charges $Q:J\\to\\mathbb R^m$, currents $I_C:J\\to\\mathbb R^m$ and voltage drops $V_C:J\\to\\mathbb R^m$. Currents $I$ and charges $Q$ admit $C^1$ extensions to an open interval containing $J$; included endpoint derivatives are their one-sided derivatives, and at a singleton the chosen extensions are part of the data supplying the jets. Voltages and source currents are continuous. The matrices $L,R\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ and $C\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times m}$ are constant, with $L^{\\mathsf T}=L$, $C^{\\mathsf T}=C$, $z^{\\mathsf T}Cz>0$ for every $z\\ne0$, $R^{\\mathsf T}=R$, and $y^{\\mathsf T}Ry\\ge0$ for every $y$. These inequalities define positive definiteness and positive semidefiniteness here. Symmetry alone is imposed on a declared $L$; a smooth compact-current realization can supply its additional positivity. Geometry, orientations and source data are fixed.\n\n(b) **Element and network relations.** Adopt\n$$V_L=L\\dot I,\\qquad V_R=RI,\\qquad I_C=\\dot Q,\\qquad V_C=C^{-1}Q.$$\nPositive definiteness makes $C^{-1}$ well defined: $Cz=0$ implies $z^{\\mathsf T}Cz=0$, hence $z=0$; an injective square finite matrix is invertible by [[thm-invertible-matrix-theorem]]. The voltage drop on an inductive/resistive branch is $(V_L+V_R)_\\alpha$, with either element omitted by setting its corresponding matrix to zero; a capacitive branch has drop $(V_C)_\\beta$. Thus the three voltage symbols refer to distinct element drops. In a purely inductive use, abbreviate $V_L$ by $V$. The linkage is $LI$ and the induced emf convention is $e_L:=-V_L$. The capacitor scalar law is $V_C=Q/C$ when $m=1$.\n\nFor a branch $b$ let $i_b$ be its oriented current and $v_b$ its voltage drop from tail to head (a source may have a negative drop). Define the incidence matrix by $A_{ab}:=\\mathbf1_{\\{a=\\operatorname{tail}(b)\\}}-\\mathbf1_{\\{a=\\operatorname{head}(b)\\}}$. **Kirchhoff's current law** is $Ai(t)=0$, expressing no node-charge accumulation. A **closed walk** is a finite sequence of traversals of branches with matching successive nodes and final node equal to initial node. If its signed traversal count is $z_b$ (forward minus backward), **Kirchhoff's voltage law** is $\\sum_bz_bv_b(t)=0$. Adopt it for every closed walk. Equivalently it suffices to impose it on a chosen linearly independent spanning set (basis) of the real span of their traversal-count vectors; this is the meaning of independent loops here. These are imposed ideal network constraints, not statements about arbitrary field circulations.\n\nThe bookkeeping energies are $\\frac12I^{\\mathsf T}LI$ and $\\frac12Q^{\\mathsf T}C^{-1}Q$. If a smooth compact conserved-profile realization of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b) is specified, identification of its linkage matrix with the declared $L$ is an additional model premise, not automatic.\n\n(c) **Quasistatic regime.** Specify a geometric size $\\ell>0$, a time scale $T>0$ and a chosen dimensionless tolerance $0<\\epsilon_{\\rm qs}<1$. The declaration $\\ell/(cT)\\ll1$ means $\\ell/(cT)\\le\\epsilon_{\\rm qs}$ with a tolerance regarded as small in the chosen application. Negligible radiation and leakage, ideal terminals and the fixed conductor geometry are separately adopted idealizations. Neither this inequality nor these idealizations give a quantitative error bound for an arbitrary full Maxwell solution; no universal accuracy is asserted.\n\n(d) **Units and scope.** $V_L,V_R,V_C,v_b$ carry volts, $I,I_C,i_b$ amperes, $Q$ coulombs, $t$ seconds, $L$ henries ($\\mathrm H=\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm s\\,\\mathrm A^{-1}$), $C$ farads ($\\mathrm F=\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm V^{-1}$), and $R$ ohms ($\\Omega=\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm A^{-1}$). The model excludes radiation, distributed-element, nonlinear, hysteretic, leakage and proximity effects; resistor loss is retained through $R$. Subluminal material speeds are required if a relativistic moving-matter interpretation is added. The model is adopted and contains no measurement or derivation from the vacuum Maxwell equations.",
          "strategy": "Explicitly adopt finite graph laws and separate inductive, resistive and capacitive drops, with specified current/charge extension jets, matrix assumptions and a tolerance-based quasistatic declaration without an error bound.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (Lent Term 2015, 236 pp.)",
