            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-nonnegative-integral-zero-iff-zero-almost-everywhere": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $\\mu_0>0$, $c>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ be the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and let $j_1,\\dots,j_n\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\operatorname{div}j_\\alpha=0$ be fixed compact smooth conserved current profiles of the class of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b), extended by zero to $\\mathbb R^3$. Fix amplitudes $I\\in\\mathbb R^n$, label them by $I_\\alpha:=I(\\alpha-1)$ for $1\\le\\alpha\\le n$ in the zero-based convention of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and put $J_I:=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^nI_\\alpha j_\\alpha$, let $A_{J_I}$ be the Coulomb-gauge Newtonian vector potential of [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]] applied to the conserved current $J_I$, and let $B_{J_I}=\\operatorname{curl}A_{J_I}$. Define the linkage coefficients\n$$L_{\\alpha\\beta}:=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\langle j_\\alpha(x),j_\\beta(y)\\rangle}{|x-y|}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(x),\\qquad\\alpha,\\beta=1,\\dots,n .$$\nThe amplitudes $I_\\alpha$ carry amperes and the profiles $j_\\alpha$ are shapes per unit current, with units $\\mathrm m^{-2}$; their numerical SI maps belong to the stated smooth class. Consequently $J_I$ has unit $\\mathrm A\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$, $A_{J_I}$ has $\\mathrm T\\,\\mathrm m$, $L_{\\alpha\\beta}$ has H, the linkage has Wb and $U$ has joules. $A_{j_\\alpha}$ denotes the linear potential response per ampere. Then:\n\n(a) [Finiteness and self-energy] every $L_{\\alpha\\beta}$ is a finite real number: the defining double integral converges absolutely, and in particular every self-coefficient $L_{\\alpha\\alpha}$ is finite, so the compact smooth profiles carry a finite self-energy. This is a statement about the smooth profiles only; no finite value is assigned to a zero-radius thin-wire self-inductance.\n\n(b) [Reciprocity and flux linkage] $L_{\\alpha\\beta}=L_{\\beta\\alpha}$ for all $\\alpha,\\beta$; $A_{J_I}=\\sum_{\\alpha}I_\\alpha A_{j_\\alpha}$ componentwise; and for every $\\beta$ the linkage integral is\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle j_\\beta,A_{J_I}\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^nL_{\\beta\\alpha}I_\\alpha .$$\nThus the numbers $L_{\\alpha\\beta}$ are the linkage coefficients of the profiles: the linkage of profile $\\beta$ with the field of the superposition is the linear combination $\\sum_\\alpha L_{\\beta\\alpha}I_\\alpha$.\n\n(c) [Magnetic energy and positivity] with $U(I):=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle J_I,A_{J_I}\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3$,\n$$U(I)=\\frac12\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^nL_{\\alpha\\beta}I_\\alpha I_\\beta=\\frac1{2\\mu_0}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|B_{J_I}|^2\\,d\\lambda_3\\ \\ge0,$$\nall quantities finite; moreover $U(I)=0$ holds precisely when $J_I=0$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Identify these profile-labelled coefficients with the standard matrix by $L(i,j):=L_{i+1,j+1}$ for $0\\le i,j<n$. Consequently the symmetric matrix $L$ is positive semidefinite, and it is positive definite exactly when the profiles are pointwise linearly independent, i.e. when $\\sum_\\alpha I_\\alpha j_\\alpha\\equiv0$ forces $I=0$.\n\n(d) [Scope] the theorem concerns fixed compact smooth conserved current profiles and their Coulomb-gauge fields. It asserts no thin-wire, sheet or singular self-inductance; no time-dependent, moving, radiating or material configuration; and no uniqueness of the vector potential. The identification of these coefficients with the inductance matrix of the adopted lumped circuit model, and of the linkage derivatives with terminal voltages, is adopted in [[post-em-circuit-approximation]]; the strict material-loop theorem applies separately only to an actual moving sheet and compatible smooth Maxwell fields satisfying its hypotheses. No terminal-voltage law for arbitrary varying frozen profiles is inferred from the present magnetostatic computation.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** the Fubini/symmetry lemma [[lem-em-newtonian-linkage-symmetry]], the curl-field energy identity [[lem-em-newtonian-curl-field-energy]], and the Fubini representation of the linkage integrals. 1.1 Components: $j_\\alpha$ is smooth, compactly supported and divergence-free, so [[pthm-em-magnetostatic-vector-potential]] applied to $J_I$ (equivalently to each $j_\\alpha$, whose sum is $J_I$) gives $A_{J_I}\\in C^\\infty$, $\\operatorname{div}A_{J_I}=0$, $-\\Delta A_{J_I}=\\mu_0J_I$ and $B_{J_I}=\\operatorname{curl}A_{J_I}$ with $\\operatorname{div}B_{J_I}=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}B_{J_I}=\\mu_0J_I$ pointwise; by linearity of the Lebesgue integral ([[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]], [[def-newtonian-potential]]) $A_{J_I}=\\sum_\\alpha I_\\alpha A_{j_\\alpha}$ with $A_{j_\\alpha}$ the potentials of the individual profiles. 1.2 [[lem-em-newtonian-linkage-symmetry]] applied to $(u,v)=(j_\\alpha,j_\\beta)$ shows that each $L_{\\alpha\\beta}$ is a finite real number given by the absolutely convergent double integral and that $L_{\\alpha\\beta}=L_{\\beta\\alpha}$; applied to $(j_\\beta,J_I)$ it gives $\\int\\langle j_\\beta,A_{J_I}\\rangle=\\mu_0\\sum_\\alpha I_\\alpha\\,(4\\pi)^{-1}\\iint\\langle j_\\beta(x),j_\\alpha(y)\\rangle/|x-y|=\\sum_\\alpha L_{\\beta\\alpha}I_\\alpha$, which is clause (b). 1.3 By the same symmetry, $\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta}L_{\\alpha\\beta}I_\\alpha I_\\beta=\\mu_0\\int\\langle J_I,NJ_I\\rangle$, hence ([[lem-em-newtonian-curl-field-energy]] applied to the divergence-free current $J_I$ with parameter $\\mu_0$) $U(I)=\\frac12\\int\\langle J_I,A_{J_I}\\rangle=\\frac1{2\\mu_0}\\int|\\operatorname{curl}A_{J_I}|^2\\ge0$, the integrals being finite by the same lemma; this is the energy identity of (c). 1.4 Vanishing: if $U(I)=0$ then $\\int|B_{J_I}|^2=0$, and since $|B_{J_I}|^2$ is continuous and nonnegative, [[thm-nonnegative-integral-zero-iff-zero-almost-everywhere]] gives $|B_{J_I}|=0$ almost everywhere ([[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]]); continuity then forces $B_{J_I}=0$ everywhere (otherwise $|B_{J_I}|$ would exceed a positive constant on a ball of positive measure by [[lem-euclidean-balls-have-positive-finite-lebesgue-measure]] and [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]). Then $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}A_{J_I}=\\nabla\\operatorname{div}A_{J_I}-\\Delta A_{J_I}=\\mu_0J_I$ ([[cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]) vanishes, so $J_I=0$; conversely $J_I=0$ gives $A_{J_I}=0$ and $U(I)=0$. Symmetry of $L$ and the vanishing criterion give the stated positive-definiteness characterisation. Countable Choice is inherited from the magnetostatic vector-potential theorem and the Newtonian and Lebesgue supplier interfaces.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
