            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-em-circuit-approximation": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity": "physical-result",
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-oriented-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume the lumped circuit model of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]] with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]) for clause (b), where it is inherited from the linkage suppliers.\n\nFor $y\\in\\mathbb R^n$ write $y_\\alpha:=y(\\alpha-1)$, and for $L\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ write $L_{\\alpha\\beta}:=L(\\alpha-1,\\beta-1)$, where $1\\le\\alpha,\\beta\\le n$, consistently with the zero-based coordinates of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and the profile labels of [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]]. All sums over $\\alpha,\\beta$ below range from $1$ to $n$.\n\n(a) [Terminal-energy bookkeeping] Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval ([[def-interval]]), let $t_0,t_1\\in J$, let $L\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be constant and symmetric, let $I:J\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be of class $C^1$ in the following sense: $I$ extends to a $C^1$ map on an open interval containing $J$, so that the derivatives used below are those of the extension (one-sided at included endpoints of $J$, and not intrinsic to a value restriction when $J$ is a singleton), and let $V(t):=L\\dot I(t)$ be the terminal-voltage vector of the inductive branches as adopted in [[post-em-circuit-approximation]] (the Faraday branch: the terminal voltage of a branch is the rate of change of its flux linkage $\\Phi=LI$). Then\n$$\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\langle V(t),I(t)\\rangle\\,dt=\\frac12\\langle I(t_1),LI(t_1)\\rangle-\\frac12\\langle I(t_0),LI(t_0)\\rangle ,$$\nand for $n=1$ with $L>0$ this reads $\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}V I\\,dt=\\frac12L\\bigl(I(t_1)^2-I(t_0)^2\\bigr)$: the accumulated terminal energy equals the change of the inductor bookkeeping energy $\\frac12LI^2$. The identity is exact for every such $C^1$ current and fixed geometry; it uses only symmetry of $L$, the product rule and the fundamental theorem of calculus. For a changing geometry the mechanical work done on the circuit is an additional term and is not covered here.\n\n(b) [Identification with the frozen field energy] Suppose in addition that the currents are realized by fixed compact smooth conserved profiles $j_1,\\dots,j_n$ of the class of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b), with instantaneous source $J_I(t):=\\sum_\\alpha I_\\alpha(t)j_\\alpha$, and that the inductance matrix is the linkage matrix of those profiles in the sense of [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]]: $L_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\iint\\frac{\\langle j_\\alpha(x),j_\\beta(y)\\rangle}{|x-y|}\\,d\\lambda_3(y)\\,d\\lambda_3(x)$. Then for every $t\\in J$\n$$\\frac12\\langle I(t),LI(t)\\rangle=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\langle J_I(t),A_{J_I(t)}\\rangle\\,d\\lambda_3=\\frac1{2\\mu_0}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|B_{J_I(t)}|^2\\,d\\lambda_3\\ \\ge0,$$\nwhere $A_{J_I(t)}$ is the Coulomb-gauge Newtonian vector potential and $B_{J_I(t)}=\\operatorname{curl}A_{J_I(t)}$ of that theorem: the right-hand side is the magnetic field energy of the frozen instantaneous configuration, and the accumulated terminal energy of (a) between $t_0$ and $t_1$ is the change of this field energy. The relation $V=L\\dot I$ is the explicitly adopted terminal law of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]]; the identification of the model's inductance matrix with the smooth-profile linkage matrix is part of the adopted model of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]].\n\n(c) [Scope] no radiation, distributed-element, material-loss, moving-geometry or thin-wire self-inductance claim is made; the frozen-configuration energy is not asserted to be the energy of a time-dependent full Maxwell solution.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** product rule plus the fundamental theorem of calculus for (a); the reciprocity theorem for (b). 1.1 The quadratic function $W(I)=\\frac12\\langle I,LI\\rangle$ is differentiable with $D W(I)\\dot I=\\langle L\\dot I,I\\rangle$ for symmetric $L$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]); equivalently $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}W(I(t))=\\langle\\dot I(t),LI(t)\\rangle=\\langle L\\dot I(t),I(t)\\rangle=\\langle V(t),I(t)\\rangle$. 1.2 Since $t\\mapsto\\langle V(t),I(t)\\rangle$ is continuous and equals the derivative of the $C^1$ function $W\\circ I$ on the interval $J$, the fundamental theorem of calculus in its second form ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]) gives the displayed integral identity of (a). For $n=1$ the quadratic form is $L I^2/2$ by inspection. 1.3 Clause (b) is [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]](c) applied to the profiles $j_\\alpha$ and amplitudes $I(t)$ at each fixed $t$, combined with (a); the frozen instantaneous source is a legitimate input to the magnetostatic theorem at each time, and no time derivative of the fields is taken. 1.4 The mechanical-work caveat and the scope restrictions are those of the adopted model; the terminal-voltage/flux-derivative reading is the circuit postulate, and the material-loop theorem would require a separately compatible smooth Maxwell and moving-sheet realization, and no claim on a time-dependent Maxwell solution is made. Countable Choice is required only in clause (b), inherited through the linkage theorem. For $t_0=t_1$ the oriented integral and energy difference are both zero. For $t_1<t_0$, apply the FTC on $[t_1,t_0]$ and reverse the sign using [[def-oriented-integral]]; for $t_0<t_1$ apply it on $[t_0,t_1]$. Thus all endpoint orders and degenerate cases of the promised identity are retained.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin; complete course book, 364 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.physics.ntua.gr/ph03/doc/Fitzpatrick.R.@.Classical.Electromagnetism.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.5 printed pp. 225-231 (the RL circuit equation with the battery work for two coupled coils splitting into Ohmic terms plus the inductive energy $\\frac12L_1I_1^2+\\frac12L_2I_2^2+MI_1I_2$, and the statement that the energy stored in an inductor is stored in the surrounding magnetic field); read in the fetched complete 364-page course book."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (Lent Term 2015, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§4.1.2 printed pp. 71-73 (the work done against the induced emf when building a current, integrated to $W=\\frac12LI^2$, and rewritten as the field energy $\\frac12\\int J\\cdot A\\,d^3x=(1/2\\mu_0)\\int B\\cdot B\\,d^3x$ under a decay assumption); read in the retained complete text of the 236-page printing."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.2.4 printed pp. 317-320 (the energy stored in a magnetic field, $\\frac12LI^2$ for a circuit and $U=\\frac1{2\\mu_0}\\int B^2\\,d\\tau$ for the field); read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The restricted inductive-energy bookkeeping of the adopted lumped model: for fixed geometry and a constant symmetric inductance matrix the accumulated terminal energy is the change of $\\frac12I^{\\mathsf T}LI$, and for a smooth compact conserved-current realization this equals the change of the frozen magnetic field energy $\\frac1{2\\mu_0}\\int|B|^2$. It excludes changing geometry, radiation, distributed and material-loss effects, thin-wire self-inductance, and any empirical claim.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 20
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-rl-lc-dynamics",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Restricted RL decay and LC oscillation under the adopted circuit model",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K6",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "post-em-circuit-approximation",
            "pthm-em-inductor-energy",
            "pthm-em-capacitance-energy",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-interval",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution",
            "thm-first-order-linear-ode-integrating-factor",
            "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "def-complex-exponential",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
