            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-eulers-formula",
            "thm-complex-trigonometric-hyperbolic-dictionary",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-em-circuit-approximation": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-em-inductor-energy": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-capacitance-energy": "physical-result",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-first-order-linear-ode-integrating-factor": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-differentiability-real-linearity-wirtinger-and-cauchy-riemann": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-complex-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-complex-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-eulers-formula": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-trigonometric-hyperbolic-dictionary": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume the lumped circuit model and its units as in [[post-em-circuit-approximation]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval ([[def-interval]]) and let $t_0\\in J$. For a nondegenerate $J$, derivatives are domain-relative, with one-sided values at included endpoints, in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]; the functions constructed below also admit the stated $C^k$ open-neighbourhood extensions. If $J=\\{t_0\\}$, the formulas specify the initial values and the jets of the explicitly constructed extensions below, rather than an intrinsic derivative of a singleton restriction. The RL solution, the LC candidate and the bookkeeping-energy algebra use no choice principle. Only the optional geometric capacitor identification in (b) assumes full Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) and its $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ consequence ([[def-countable-choice]]), exactly as [[pthm-em-capacitance-energy]] requires. The inductive energy is that of [[pthm-em-inductor-energy]].\n\n(a) [RL circuit] Let $L>0$ and $R\\ge0$ be constants and let $V:J\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. Then the initial value problem\n$$L\\dot I(t)+R\\,I(t)=V(t),\\qquad I(t_0)=I_0\\in\\mathbb R,$$\nhas exactly one $C^1$ solution on every nondegenerate $J$, namely\n$$I(t)=\\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{R(t-t_0)}{L}\\Bigr)\\Bigl[I_0+\\frac1L\\int_{t_0}^{t}\\exp\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{R(s-t_0)}{L}\\Bigr)V(s)\\,ds\\Bigr],$$\nand along it the inductive bookkeeping energy satisfies the power identity\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\Bigl(\\frac12LI(t)^2\\Bigr)=L\\dot I(t)I(t)=V(t)I(t)-R\\,I(t)^2 ,$$\ni.e. the terminal power splits into the rate of change of the inductive energy and the Ohmic term $RI^2$. (For $R=0$ the same formula reduces to $I=I_0+L^{-1}\\int_{t_0}^tV$.) For $J=\\{t_0\\}$ its unique restriction is $I(t_0)=I_0$; the canonical extension obtained using the constant source $V(t_0)$ has jet $\\dot I(t_0)=(V(t_0)-RI_0)/L$ and satisfies the same power algebra. No uniqueness of arbitrary extension germs is asserted.\n\n(b) [LC oscillation] Let $L>0$, $C>0$ and set $\\Omega:=(LC)^{-1/2}>0$, $z_0:=Q_0-\\mathrm i\\,I_0/\\Omega$, $w(t):=z_0\\exp(\\mathrm i\\Omega(t-t_0))$ with the complex exponential of [[def-complex-exponential]], and $Q(t):=\\operatorname{Re}w(t)$. Then $Q$ is of class $C^2$ on $J$, $Q(t_0)=Q_0$, $Q'(t_0)=I_0$, and\n$$L\\,Q''(t)+\\frac{Q(t)}{C}=0\\qquad\\text{on }J ;$$\nequivalently, with the real trigonometric functions of [[thm-eulers-formula]],\n$$Q(t)=Q_0\\cos\\bigl(\\Omega(t-t_0)\\bigr)+\\frac{I_0}{\\Omega}\\sin\\bigl(\\Omega(t-t_0)\\bigr).$$\nMoreover the total bookkeeping energy\n$$E(t):=\\frac12L\\,Q'(t)^2+\\frac{Q(t)^2}{2C}$$\nsatisfies $E'(t)=Q'(t)\\bigl(LQ''(t)+Q(t)/C\\bigr)=0$ on $J$, hence $E$ is constant with value $\\frac12LI_0^2+\\frac{Q_0^2}{2C}$: the two terms are the adopted capacitive and inductive bookkeeping energies, the latter as in [[pthm-em-inductor-energy]](a), so in the lossless loop their sum is conserved. For the additional geometric identification, explicitly choose the fixed compact separated conductor arrangement of [[pthm-em-capacitance-energy]] with bounded smooth ambient domain, connected vacuum, grounded outer boundary, zero volume source and its unit-Dirichlet solutions; drive only conductor 1 and ground the others, set the circuit parameter $C$ equal to that arrangement's $C_{11}>0$, and at each time set $V_1=Q/C_{11}$. Under full AC that supplier gives the frozen electrostatic field energy $Q^2/(2C_{11})$. An arbitrary declared $C>0$ is model data and is not automatically the capacitance of a particular arrangement. Any magnetic field-energy identification likewise requires the extra smooth-profile linkage realization of [[pthm-em-inductor-energy]](b), under its $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ hypothesis. Neither frozen realization is an asserted time-dependent full Maxwell solution. At a singleton time the entire LC candidate supplies the jets $\\dot Q=I_0$, $\\ddot Q=-Q_0/(LC)$ and the same constant energy.\n\n(c) [Scope] both statements are consequences of the expressly adopted circuit reduction, with the sign conventions and relations of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]]; they assert no general network completeness, no derivation from the full time-dependent Maxwell system, no radiation or distributed-element effect and no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** the published integrating-factor theorem for (a); an explicit complex-exponential candidate verified by differentiation and an energy-conservation comparison for (b).\n\n1.1 (a) For nondegenerate $J$, with $p:=R/L$ constant and $q:=V/L$ continuous on $J$ ([[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]]), the integrating factor theorem [[thm-first-order-linear-ode-integrating-factor]] gives existence, uniqueness of the $C^1$ solution of the IVP in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]] and exactly the displayed formula. The power identity is the product rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) applied to $\\frac12LI^2$ together with the circuit equation.\n\n1.2 (b) The map $w$ is the restriction to the real interval $J$ of the entire function $z\\mapsto z_0\\exp(\\mathrm i\\Omega(z-t_0))$; the complex exponential is entire with derivative itself ([[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]]), the affine map is complex-linear, so the algebra and chain rules for complex derivatives ([[thm-algebra-of-complex-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-complex-derivatives]]) give $w'=\\mathrm i\\Omega w$ and $w''=(\\mathrm i\\Omega)^2w=-\\Omega^2w$ on $J$; complex differentiability implies real differentiability with the complex-linear derivative ([[thm-complex-differentiability-real-linearity-wirtinger-and-cauchy-riemann]]) and the real chain rule ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]) gives the corresponding real derivatives of the components. Taking real parts, $Q=\\operatorname{Re}w$ is $C^2$ with $Q'=\\operatorname{Re}w'$ and $Q''=\\operatorname{Re}w''=-\\Omega^2Q$, which is $LQ''+Q/C=0$ because $\\Omega^2=1/(LC)$; moreover $w(t_0)=Q_0-\\mathrm iI_0/\\Omega$ gives $Q(t_0)=Q_0$ and $Q'(t_0)=\\operatorname{Re}(\\mathrm i\\Omega z_0)=I_0$. Euler's formula ([[thm-eulers-formula]]) expands $\\exp(\\mathrm i\\Omega(t-t_0))=\\cos+\\mathrm i\\sin$ with the real power-series trigonometric functions, and the dictionary theorem [[thm-complex-trigonometric-hyperbolic-dictionary]] records that the complex trigonometric functions restrict to those real series, yielding the displayed cosine-sine form.\n\n1.3 (b) The energy identity is the product rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) applied to $E$: $E'=LQ'Q''+QQ'/C=Q'(LQ''+Q/C)=0$ by the equation of the previous step. Since $E$ is continuous on the interval $J$ with vanishing derivative at every interior point, [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]] gives $E\\equiv E(t_0)=\\frac12LI_0^2+\\frac{Q_0^2}{2C}$. The capacitive term is initially only the declared circuit bookkeeping quantity. Under the additional specified conductor geometry, full AC, and parameter identification $C=C_{11}$ in the Statement, apply [[pthm-em-capacitance-energy]](d) to $V(t)=(Q(t)/C_{11})e_1$, which gives exactly the frozen field-energy equality. The inductive term is the bookkeeping energy of [[pthm-em-inductor-energy]](a); the signs and branch relations are those adopted in [[post-em-circuit-approximation]], so the verified equations are exactly the model equations. Full AC is inherited exactly by the optional [[pthm-em-capacitance-energy]] geometric identification; AComega suffices for the separately declared magnetic smooth-profile identification, and the remaining algebra is choice-free; the complex-exponential and ODE suppliers are published mathematics. For the endpoint and extension contracts: if a finite endpoint belongs to $J$, extend the continuous $V$ by its endpoint value just beyond that endpoint; leave any excluded endpoint outside the extension interval. This yields a continuous source on an open interval containing nondegenerate $J$. Apply the same integrating-factor formula there, giving a $C^1$ extension with the same restriction and the prescribed one-sided derivatives; uniqueness of the restriction is the supplier’s nondegenerate interval uniqueness. If $J=\\{t_0\\}$, use $V\\equiv V(t_0)$ on an open interval to construct the claimed RL candidate jet explicitly; other extensions with the same first jet are not unique. The LC formula is already entire on the real line and therefore supplies its indicated jets even for singleton data; its constant energy then follows by the same global calculation. The assumption $t_0\\in J$ excludes an empty interval without an additional restriction.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin; complete course book, 364 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.physics.ntua.gr/ph03/doc/Fitzpatrick.R.@.Classical.Electromagnetism.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.5-7.6 printed pp. 225-234 (the RL rise $V=L\\,\\mathrm dI/\\mathrm dt+RI$, the LCR circuit equation $V=IR+L\\,\\mathrm dI/\\mathrm dt+\\int I\\,\\mathrm dt/C$ with the ideal inductor and capacitor relations and resonance at $\\omega=1/\\sqrt{LC}$); read in the fetched complete 364-page course book."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.2.3 printed pp. 310-317 (the battery-plus-RL circuit solution $I(t)=\\frac{\\mathcal E_0}{R}+k\\mathrm e^{-Rt/L}$ with time constant $\\tau=L/R$); read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (Lent Term 2015, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§4.1.3 printed pp. 74-77 (the resistance relation $\\mathcal E=IR$ as an additional material input, and the sliding-bar example solved with Ohm's law); read in the retained complete text of the 236-page printing."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional scalar RL/LC dynamics of the adopted lumped model, with arbitrary positive model parameters, endpoint intervals and explicit singleton candidate jets retained. Algebraic bookkeeping is choice-free; the optional identification with a specified geometric capacitor $C=C_{11}$ carries full AC and the bounded smooth conductor hypotheses of its supplier, and the frozen magnetic realization separately carries the compact-profile and $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ premises of the linkage theorem. Neither frozen realization is a time-dependent full Maxwell solution; no radiation or distributed effect is included.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 21
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 339
    },
    {
      "id": "em-induction-and-circuit-limits-examples",
