      "items": [
        {
          "id": "texp-em-sliding-bar-emf",
          "kind": "thought-experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Sliding bar on fixed rails: motional emf and mechanical-electrical power balance",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K6",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-moving-circuit-geometry",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
            "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-moving-circuit-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling": "physical-assumption",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-over-a-measurable-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integral-of-a-nonnegative-simple-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-the-nonnegative-integral-agrees-with-the-simple-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the explicitly used Lebesgue bar-volume interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Work in the laboratory frame and units of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], choosing $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$ and an open laboratory time interval containing $J$. Fix $l>0$, $B_0\\ge0$, a nonempty interval $J$ with a specified $C^1$ neighbourhood extension for motion and its velocity jets when $J$ is not open (in particular singleton data are read through that supplied extension), and a prescribed $C^1$ motion $x:J\\to(a_0,a_1)$ with $a_0<a_1$ and $|x'(t)|<c$. The fixed rails have centerlines $(s,\\pm l/2,0)$, $a_0\\le s\\le a_1$, and the left closing side is $(a_0,w,0)$, $|w|\\le l/2$. At time $t$ the ideal oriented contour $C(t)$ comprises only the rail portions $a_0\\le s\\le x(t)$, that left side, and the sliding bar centerline $(x(t),w,0)$. Give it normal $e_3$, hence traverse the bar in the $+e_2$ direction. The rails and closing side have material velocity zero; the bar velocity is $v_b(t)=x'(t)e_1$. These are separate segment velocities with sliding contacts; endpoint values at the contacts are taken independently on each segment. Define the contact-model emf as the sum of its four segment integrals $\\sum_\\gamma\\int_\\gamma(E+v_\\gamma\\times B)\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ in [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]. This is not a single-valued smooth moving material sheet of [[def-em-moving-circuit-geometry]]. Prescribe an arbitrary finite-valued signed current $I:J\\to\\mathbb R$, in amperes; no time regularity of $I$ is needed for these instantaneous identities. The external test fields are $E(y,t)=0$, $B(y,t)=B_0e_3$ on the laboratory region; all self-fields are neglected.\n\nFor a measure space $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$, a measurable function $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ and $K\\in\\mathcal A$, write $\\int_K f\\,d\\mu:=\\int(f\\chi_K)\\,d\\mu$ using the signed Lebesgue integral of [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]: $(f\\chi_K)^+=\\max\\{f\\chi_K,0\\}$ and $(f\\chi_K)^-=\\max\\{-f\\chi_K,0\\}$, and the integral is their integral difference when both are finite. For a measurable vector map $G:X\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with all three components integrable on $K$, define $\\int_K G\\,d\\mu:=(\\int_K G_0\\,d\\mu,\\int_K G_1\\,d\\mu,\\int_K G_2\\,d\\mu)$ in the ordered spatial coordinates of the laboratory frame; the stored coordinate indices are $0,1,2$ and the corresponding physical basis labels are $e_1,e_2,e_3$.\n\nFor force bookkeeping choose an actual finite-volume homogeneous bar, separate from the ideal centerline: fix $d,h>0$ with cross-section area $A=dh$ and set $K_t=[x(t)-d/2,x(t)+d/2]\\times[-l/2,l/2]\\times[-h/2,h/2]$, so $\\lambda_3(K_t)=Al$. For each frozen time prescribe the uniform current density $j_t=(I(t)/A)e_2$ on its interior, with the ideal local conductor-matter coupling of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](ii) applied there. Its extension to the boundary is used only for the volume integral; contacts/end faces have zero volume and no additional contact force is included. The same signed lumped current runs around the contour. The local force-law adoption and prescribed current do not assert a total Maxwell or matter-dynamical solution.\n\n(a) The rectangular flux is $\\Phi(t)=B_0l(x(t)-a_0)$ and the segmentwise emf is $\\mathrm{emf}(t)=-B_0lx'(t)=-\\Phi'(t)$. Its magnitude is $B_0l|x'|$, including either motion direction and $B_0=0$.\n\n(b) The finite-volume force is\n$$F(t)=\\int_{K_t}j_t\\times B\\,d\\lambda_3=I(t)B_0l e_1.$$\n\n(c) The signed instantaneous balance is\n$$F(t)\\cdot v_b(t)+\\mathrm{emf}(t)I(t)=I(t)B_0lx'(t)-B_0lx'(t)I(t)=0.$$\nIt is ideal mechanical/electrical transfer bookkeeping with prescribed motion and current; no dissipative, radiation or field-storage term is included. A support enforcing the motion may supply the opposite mechanical power.\n\n(d) This is a hypothetical conditional sliding-contact model with no measurement. Self-fields, radiation, friction, contact resistance, deformation and constitutive details are neglected; no bar equation of motion, physical realization, or total Maxwell solution is asserted. The strict $C^2$ material-sheet theorem remains a separate result under its own hypotheses.",
          "strategy": "At each frozen time the four segments are ordinary affine paths. Their integrands are continuous on the separate closed segments, so the individual line integrals exist; no global velocity at a contact is required. The three resting segments contribute zero because $E=0$ and their material velocity is zero. On the bar, $v_b\\times B=x'e_1\\times B_0e_3=-B_0x'e_2$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]); integrating $w$ from $-l/2$ to $l/2$ gives $-B_0lx'$. Direct integration of the constant field over the frozen rectangle of area $l(x-a_0)$ gives $\\Phi=B_0l(x-a_0)$, whose derivative exists for the prescribed $C^1$ motion and is $B_0lx'$. This proves the flux relation directly, without applying material transport: the often-used affine filling $r(u,w,t)=(a_0+u(x(t)-a_0),(w-1/2)l,0)$ has $r_t=ux'e_1$ on the rails and is not the fixed-material rail motion; with merely $C^1$ $x$ it is not a $C^2$ spacetime sheet either. For (b), [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]] supplies the actual volume $d l h=Al$ of $K_t$ and nullity of its faces. The constant local force density is $(I/A)B_0e_1$, so componentwise Lebesgue linearity ([[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]) yields $F=(I/A)B_0e_1 Al=IB_0l e_1$; the integral is absolutely finite. This is the prescribed local continuum matter coupling, not a force inferred for an arbitrary source without such a matter model. (c) uses the signed velocity vector $v_b=x'e_1$ in the Euclidean dot product, giving exactly $IB_0lx'-B_0lx'I=0$, even when $I$ or $x'$ is negative or zero. The centerline is a contour for emf and has zero three-dimensional volume; the force integral is over $K_t$. Nothing here supplies the neglected self-field or a smooth global current through the sliding contacts. The original material-sheet definition remains only a scope comparison, and its theorem is not used in this verification; AComega is inherited from the explicitly used bar-volume measure interfaces. For the force integral use the signed scalar integral of [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]] on each of the three spatial components; |q_t chi_K| has integral |q_t|Al, so the force components are integrable for every finite signed current.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.1.3 printed pp. 294-296 (the primitive generator: a loop pulled at speed v through a uniform field, emf = vBh, the flux rule for motional emf, and the work/power discussion); read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (Lent Term 2015, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§4.1.1 and §4.1.3 printed pp. 69-77 (the sliding bar: emf = vBd, the Lorentz force F = IBd on the bar, and the coupled motion with the resistor); read in the retained complete text of the 236-page printing."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A hypothetical conditional sliding-contact model in a uniform static external test field: four separate segment velocities at sliding contacts, the direct segmentwise emf and flux computation, an adopted finite-volume bar force integral and the signed instantaneous mechanical/electrical power balance. Self-fields, radiation, contact resistance and dynamics are neglected; no measurement, no bar equation of motion and no total Maxwell or matter-dynamical solution is asserted.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 19
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-transformer-idealization",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Two-circuit mutual inductance, the perfect-linkage bound and ideal-transformer limitations",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X5",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "post-em-circuit-approximation",
            "pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity",
            "pthm-em-inductor-energy",
            "def-em-steady-current-regime",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-em-circuit-approximation": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-inductor-energy": "physical-result",
            "def-em-steady-current-regime": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume the lumped circuit model of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]] with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and let $L=\\begin{pmatrix}L_1&M\\\\M&L_2\\end{pmatrix}$ be a constant symmetric block of its inductance matrix with $L_1,L_2>0$. Let $I_1,I_2:J\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^1$ currents on a nonempty interval $J$ (each extending to a $C^1$ map on an open interval containing $J$, as in the model's conventions) with terminal voltages $V_\\alpha=\\sum_\\beta L_{\\alpha\\beta}\\dot I_\\beta$. Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]) for the realization and bounded-linking clauses (b), inherited from the linkage and energy suppliers.\n\n(a) [Energy and instantaneous power] the stored inductive bookkeeping energy is\n$$W(t)=\\tfrac12L_1I_1(t)^2+\\tfrac12L_2I_2(t)^2+MI_1(t)I_2(t),$$\nand $V_1I_1+V_2I_2=\\dot W$ by part (a) of [[pthm-em-inductor-energy]]; the mutual term increases the energy for currents of the same sign and decreases it for opposite signs (as the sign of the product $I_1I_2$ indicates when $M>0$).\n\n(b) [Perfect linkage and the bound] if the two currents are realised by fixed compact smooth conserved profiles of the class of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b) with linkage matrix $L$ as in [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]], then $W(t)\\ge0$ for all currents, and evaluating the quadratic form on both $(\\sqrt{L_2},-\\sqrt{L_1})$ and $(\\sqrt{L_2},+\\sqrt{L_1})$ gives the bound\n$$|M|\\le\\sqrt{L_1L_2};$$\nmoreover if $M=s\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$ with $s\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ (perfect linkage), the null direction $I=(\\sqrt{L_2},-s\\sqrt{L_1})$ has $J_I=0$ pointwise by the vanishing clause of [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]](c).\n\n(c) [Ideal common-flux model] if $M=\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$, choose any $\\Lambda>0$ in henries and set $N_1:=\\sqrt{L_1/\\Lambda}$, $N_2:=\\sqrt{L_2/\\Lambda}$; then $L_1=\\Lambda N_1^2$, $L_2=\\Lambda N_2^2$ and $M=\\Lambda N_1N_2$. Writing $\\Phi_c:=\\Lambda(N_1I_1+N_2I_2)$ for the common flux linkage, the terminal voltages are\n$$V_1=N_1\\dot\\Phi_c,\\qquad V_2=N_2\\dot\\Phi_c,\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad \\frac{V_1}{N_1}=\\frac{V_2}{N_2}=\\dot\\Phi_c,$$\nand the stored energy is $W=\\Phi_c^2/(2\\Lambda)$; equivalently the induced emfs $e_\\alpha:=-V_\\alpha$ satisfy $e_1/N_1=e_2/N_2=-\\dot\\Phi_c$. Here $N_1,N_2>0$ are dimensionless real effective winding factors, rather than asserted integer turn counts; $\\Phi_c$ has unit Wb. This is the explicitly adopted common-linkage ('ideal transformer') model with effective ratio $N_1:N_2$.\n\n(d) [Limitations] for an imperfect realized linkage, $|M|<\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$, the perfect common-flux voltage ratio is not an identity for all current histories; a particular history can nevertheless satisfy that ratio accidentally (including both voltages zero). Reversing the second winding retains $s=-1$: with $M=s\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$ put $\\Phi_c=\\Lambda(N_1I_1+sN_2I_2)$ and obtain $V_1/N_1=V_2/(sN_2)=\\dot\\Phi_c$, with the same energy $\\Phi_c^2/(2\\Lambda)$. The positive-orientation model (c) remains as stated. Winding resistance, hysteresis and core loss, stray capacitance, magnetising current and finite permeability are outside the declared model and are not inferred from it. No measurement is involved; the statements are conditional computations in the ideal model. Regularity on a non-open interval uses the stated $C^1$ neighbourhood extension; if the interval is a singleton its terminal-voltage jets are those of a specified extension, not derivatives inferred from its value restriction.",
          "strategy": "**Verification.** (a) is the two-dimensional instance of the quadratic form and power identity of [[pthm-em-inductor-energy]](a), together with the product rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). (b) is [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]](c) applied to the two profiles: $W(t)=\\frac12\\sum_{\\alpha\\beta}L_{\\alpha\\beta}I_\\alpha I_\\beta\\ge0$, and substituting $I=(\\sqrt{L_2},-\\sqrt{L_1})$ gives $W=\\frac12L_1L_2+\\frac12L_2L_1-M\\sqrt{L_1L_2}=L_1L_2-M\\sqrt{L_1L_2}\\ge0$, i.e. $M\\le\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$; substituting the opposite sign gives the lower bound, hence $|M|\\le\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$. If $M=s\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$, the substitution $(\\sqrt{L_2},-s\\sqrt{L_1})$ gives $W=L_1L_2-sM\\sqrt{L_1L_2}=0$, so the associated current $J_I$ vanishes pointwise by the vanishing clause of the same theorem. (c) With $N_1,N_2,\\Lambda$ as defined, $L_1=\\Lambda N_1^2$ and $L_2=\\Lambda N_2^2$ by construction and $M=\\Lambda N_1N_2$ because $\\sqrt{\\Lambda N_1^2\\cdot\\Lambda N_2^2}=\\Lambda N_1N_2$; hence $V_1=L_1\\dot I_1+M\\dot I_2=\\Lambda N_1(N_1\\dot I_1+N_2\\dot I_2)=N_1\\dot\\Phi_c$ and likewise $V_2=N_2\\dot\\Phi_c$, while $W=\\frac12\\Lambda(N_1I_1+N_2I_2)^2=\\Phi_c^2/(2\\Lambda)$ by direct expansion. The sign convention is the terminal-voltage convention of [[post-em-circuit-approximation]]; the physically induced emfs are the negatives, by the adopted circuit sign convention, which is why the stated ratios carry the common minus sign. (d) records the model boundaries; the example asserts nothing about a physical transformer and involves no measurement. Countable Choice is used only through the realization clauses (b) and is inherited from [[pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity]] and [[pthm-em-inductor-energy]]. For $s=-1$ expand $W=\\frac12\\Lambda(N_1I_1-N_2I_2)^2$, and differentiate to obtain the signed winding ratios in (d). For strict realized linkage the matrix is positive definite and has rank two. A universal proportionality $V_2=kV_1$ for all histories would force the second row of $L$ to equal $k$ times the first, since at any time the derivative vector $\\dot I$ can be arbitrary (use affine histories); this would force rank one, a contradiction. It does not forbid histories whose derivative vector lies in the kernel of the single row difference $\\operatorname{row}_2L-k\\operatorname{row}_1L$. The perfect-linkage and strict-linkage claims of (b),(d) concern the additional realized PSD matrix, not every declared symmetric model matrix. On a non-open current interval use the stated C1 neighbourhood convention to evaluate derivatives; singleton values alone have no intrinsic derivative, so a specified extension supplies the terminal jets if such data are considered.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin; complete course book, 364 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.physics.ntua.gr/ph03/doc/Fitzpatrick.R.@.Classical.Electromagnetism.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.4 printed pp. 222-225 (the two-solenoid mutual inductance $M=\\mu_0N_1N_2\\pi r^2l$, $M=\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$ under perfect linkage and the warning that flux leakage reduces $M$); read in the fetched complete 364-page course book."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.2.3 printed pp. 310-317 (reciprocity $M_{21}=M_{12}$ and the Neumann formula; Problem 7.20, where the equality of the two mutual inductances is confirmed); read in the stamped 596-page scan."
