        {
          "id": "ex-em-rl-time-response",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Exponential RL response under a constant terminal voltage",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X5",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-rl-lc-dynamics",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-interval",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-exponential-limits-and-range",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-rl-lc-dynamics": "physical-result",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-exponential-limits-and-range": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "In the RL branch of [[pthm-em-rl-lc-dynamics]] with the declared units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], let the source voltage be the constant $V_0\\in\\mathbb R$, let $R>0$ and $L>0$, let $t_0\\in J$ and $I_0\\in\\mathbb R$, with $J$ an interval and derivatives read in the model's domain-relative convention, through the stated $C^1$ open-neighbourhood extension for a non-open $J$. For nondegenerate $J$ the unique $C^1$ solution of $L\\dot I+RI=V_0$, $I(t_0)=I_0$, is\n$$I(t)=\\frac{V_0}{R}+\\Bigl(I_0-\\frac{V_0}{R}\\Bigr)\\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{R(t-t_0)}{L}\\Bigr),\\qquad t\\in J .$$\nIn particular $I(t)-V_0/R=(I_0-V_0/R)\\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ and hence $|I(t)-V_0/R|=|I_0-V_0/R|\\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ for every $t\\in J$, with time constant $\\tau=L/R$; $I\\equiv V_0/R$ is the unique stationary solution (an extension with zero derivative, also in the singleton convention); if $J$ is unbounded above, then $I(t)\\to V_0/R$ as $t\\to+\\infty$ along $J$; and the transient part is the free decay $\\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ of the initial deviation $I_0-V_0/R$. For $J=\\{t_0\\}$ the restriction is $I(t_0)=I_0$ and the jets are those of the same formula read through the stated extension, giving $\\dot I(t_0)=(V_0-RI_0)/L$. The computation is conditional on the adopted lumped model, constant elements and fixed geometry; no measurement is involved.",
          "strategy": "**Verification.** The function $t\\mapsto\\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ is differentiable with derivative $-(R/L)\\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ by the chain rule for the affine substitution in the real exponential ([[thm-derivative-of-exponential]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). Substituting the displayed $I$ gives $\\dot I=-(R/L)(I_0-V_0/R)\\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$, hence $L\\dot I+RI=V_0$ identically, and $I(t_0)=V_0/R+(I_0-V_0/R)=I_0$. Uniqueness of this solution within the RL theorem [[pthm-em-rl-lc-dynamics]](a) is inherited from the published integrating-factor theorem; the limit statement follows from [[thm-exponential-limits-and-range]] since $-R(t-t_0)/L\\to-\\infty$ on a time interval unbounded above and the special case $I_0=V_0/R$ is constant. No claim beyond the ideal model is made.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§7.2.3 printed pp. 310-317 (the battery-plus-RL circuit: $I(t)=\\frac{\\mathcal E_0}{R}+k\\mathrm e^{-Rt/L}$, the initial condition $I(0)=0$ giving $k=-\\mathcal E_0/R$, and the time constant $\\tau=L/R$); read in the stamped 596-page scan."
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin; complete course book, 364 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.physics.ntua.gr/ph03/doc/Fitzpatrick.R.@.Classical.Electromagnetism.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.5 printed pp. 225-231 (the RL circuit equation $V=L\\,\\mathrm dI/\\mathrm dt+RI$ and its exponential approach to the steady current); read in the fetched complete 364-page course book."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The exponential transient of the scalar RL circuit of the adopted lumped model under a constant source voltage and constant positive $L$ and $R$, with time constant $L/R$. It carries the model idealisations (fixed geometry, ideal elements, no radiation or distributed effects) and contains no measurement or empirical claim.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 22
        }
      ],
