      "items": [
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-spacetime-potentials",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Smooth scalar and vector potentials on a star-shaped space-time region",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K7 and mathematical-prerequisites.md#M05; interfaces aligned to lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials and def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument; local second application of the published joint-regularity supplier; not an independent acceptance",
          "deps": [
            "lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn",
            "def-interval",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials",
            "lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $c>0$, let $k\\ge2$ be an integer, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be an open set that is star-shaped with respect to a point $a\\in\\Omega$ ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]), and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two elements ([[def-interval]]). Joint regularity on $\\Omega\\times I$ is read in the restriction convention of [[lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials]]: a map on $\\Omega\\times I$ is **jointly $C^m$** when it is the restriction of a $C^m$ map on some open neighbourhood of $\\Omega\\times I$ in $\\mathbb R^4$. For open $I$ this is the ordinary open-domain class of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] and agrees with the field class of [[def-em-fields]]; for non-open $I$ it is the convention that makes the statements below meaningful, and no time jet is asserted beyond the restricted values.\n\nLet $E,B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be jointly $C^k$ and satisfy the two source-free Maxwell equations of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] pointwise on $\\Omega\\times I$:\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,$$\n\nwith the divergence and curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and the partial derivative $\\partial_t$ at fixed position of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] (at included endpoints of a non-open $I$, as relative derivatives in the restriction convention). Then there are a scalar potential $\\varphi:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ that is jointly $C^{k-1}$ and a vector potential $A:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ that is jointly $C^k$, both in the restriction convention, with\n\n$$B=\\operatorname{curl}A,\\qquad E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I,$$\n\nso that $(\\varphi,A)$ is a potential pair whose field pair is $(E,B)$, in the vocabulary of [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]. If $E$ and $B$ are jointly $C^\\infty$, then $\\varphi$ and $A$ may be chosen jointly $C^\\infty$. Conversely, if $\\varphi$ and $A$ are jointly $C^\\infty$ on $\\Omega\\times I$ in the restriction convention and $E:=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$, $B:=\\operatorname{curl}A$ are their field pair, then $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$ on $\\Omega\\times I$.\n\nThe theorem asserts existence on the stated star-shaped domain only: it claims no global potential on a general open set, no boundary, decay or growth condition on the potentials, and no uniqueness. Potentials are auxiliary objects of the framework and are not claimed to be observable.",
          "strategy": "Radial-witness construction of [[lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials]] applied twice, following the research route core-conditional-proofs.md#K7. (i) Vector step: $B$ is jointly $C^k$ with $\\operatorname{div}B(\\cdot,t)=0$ for every $t$, so the lemma supplies $A(x,t)=\\int_0^1s\\,B(a+s(x-a),t)\\times(x-a)\\,ds$, jointly $C^k$ on $\\Omega\\times I$, with $\\operatorname{curl}A(\\cdot,t)=B(\\cdot,t)$ for every $t$. (ii) Define $C:=E+\\partial_tA$. Since $E$ is jointly $C^k$ and $A$ jointly $C^k$ with $k\\ge2$, $C$ is jointly $C^{k-1}$; the commutativity of $\\partial_t$ with the coordinate partial derivatives on the $C^2$ field $A$ ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]], componentwise) and the linearity of curl ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]) give $\\operatorname{curl}C(\\cdot,t)=\\operatorname{curl}E(\\cdot,t)+\\partial_t\\operatorname{curl}A(\\cdot,t)=-\\partial_tB(\\cdot,t)+\\partial_tB(\\cdot,t)=0$ for every $t$. (iii) Apply the lemma's scalar clause to $C$ (legitimate because $k-1\\ge1$): the corrected negative radial witness $\\varphi(x,t)=-\\int_0^1\\langle C(a+s(x-a),t),x-a\\rangle\\,ds$ is jointly $C^{k-1}$ and $\\nabla\\varphi(\\cdot,t)=-C(\\cdot,t)$ for every $t$, that is $E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$; the substitution uses the componentwise linearity of $\\nabla$ and $\\partial_t$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). (iv) The smooth clause is the lemma's $C^\\infty$ clause. (v) Conversely a jointly $C^\\infty$ pair gives $\\operatorname{div}B=\\operatorname{div}\\operatorname{curl}A=0$ ([[thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes]]) and $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_t\\operatorname{curl}A=-\\partial_tB$, using $\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\varphi=0$ ([[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]]) and the equality of the spatial/time mixed partials of the vector field $A$ ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]). No choice principle, boundary condition, decay or non-star-shaped generality is used. The same interval convention is carried through both applications: choose a Ck extension of E and B, construct the radial A extension on its open radial-admissibility neighbourhood, and apply the scalar construction to the C(k-1) extension of E+A_t. Their restrictions are the asserted potentials on the original product, with relative derivatives at included endpoints. No finite-k case is discarded.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§16 printed pp. 44-45 (the four-potential A_k and its relation to the scalar and vector potentials) and §17 printed pp. 46-48, equations (17.3)-(17.4): E = -grad phi - (1/c) dA/dt and H = curl A"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.3.1 printed pp. 105-106: the potential representation E = -grad phi - dA/dt, B = curl A as the general solution of the two source-free Maxwell equations"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§10.1.1 printed pp. 416-418: the potential formulation and the two inhomogeneous equations for V and A on which the potential representation rests"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The fields and potentials of the classical vacuum electromagnetic framework of [[def-em-fields]], [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] and [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]] on a single inertial laboratory region over a star-shaped spatial region. The only physical hypothesis used is the pair of source-free Maxwell equations (absence of magnetic charge and Faraday's law) as adopted in the postulate; no source equation, no regularity beyond the stated joint class, and no assertion that the potentials are observable are included. The star-shaped hypothesis is a genuine restriction on the spatial region, and the companion examples exhibit a divergence-free field on a non-star-shaped region with no global vector potential.",
          "dependency_level": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-gauge-transformation",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Potential representations, gauge transformations and gauge equivalence of the framework's fields",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-gauge-transformation; vocabulary supplied by def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; class bookkeeping and the smooth group action are stated exactly; reachability is excluded by contract",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "pthm-em-spacetime-potentials",
