          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-spacetime-potentials": "physical-result",
            "lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $c>0$, let $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ be the laboratory region of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with its restricted inertial chart, let fields and units be those of [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], and let the scalar-vector potential vocabulary on that region be that of [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]: a **potential pair** $(\\varphi,A)$ of class $C^k$ on $\\Omega\\times I$ consists of $\\varphi:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ and $A:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of class $C^k$ jointly in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], and for a non-open time interval $I$ the joint classes in this item are read in the open-neighbourhood restriction convention of [[lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials]] (for open $I$ this is the ordinary class of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); for $k\\ge1$ the **field pair** is\n\n$$E(\\varphi,A):=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA,\\qquad B(\\varphi,A):=\\operatorname{curl}A,$$\n\nwhere $\\nabla$ is the gradient, $\\operatorname{div}$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ are those of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], and $\\partial_t$ is the partial derivative at fixed position of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]. For $k\\ge1$ the **Lorenz divergence** of the pair is $G(\\varphi,A):=\\operatorname{div}A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi$. The definitions in this item are stated on the laboratory region; the framework's fields are those of [[def-em-fields]], and no field equation is assumed anywhere below.\n\n(a) [Representations] Let $k\\ge2$ be an integer and let $(E,B)$ be a field pair of class $C^{k-1}$ on $\\Omega\\times I$, meaning $E,B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of class $C^{k-1}$ jointly as in [[def-em-fields]]. A **potential representation of class $C^k$** of $(E,B)$ is a potential pair $(\\varphi,A)$ of class $C^k$ on $\\Omega\\times I$ whose field pair is $(E,B)$, that is, $E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ on $\\Omega\\times I$. More generally, a potential pair of class $C^1$ represents a specified field pair whenever these two identities hold, even if the fields have greater regularity than the defining derivatives alone guarantee. This definition asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. On a star-shaped spatial region [[pthm-em-spacetime-potentials]] supplies $\\varphi\\in C^{r-1}$ and $A\\in C^r$ for jointly $C^r$ field data, $r\\ge2$, and smooth potentials for smooth data; it does not assert a $C^r$ scalar potential for merely $C^r$ data. Equality of field pairs does not by itself assert gauge relatedness on an arbitrary region; a converse needs its own domain and regularity hypotheses.\n\n(b) [Gauge functions and gauge equivalence] A **gauge function** of class $C^m$, $m\\ge2$, on $\\Omega\\times I$ is a function $\\chi:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^m$ jointly; its **gauge transformation** sends a potential pair $(\\varphi,A)$ of class $C^m$ to\n\n$$T_\\chi(\\varphi,A):=(\\varphi-\\partial_t\\chi,\\ A+\\nabla\\chi),$$\n\na potential pair of class $C^{m-1}$. Two potential pairs are **gauge related** when one is the gauge transform of the other by some gauge function. On pairs and generators of class $C^\\infty$, $T_0$ is the identity and $T_{\\chi_1}\\bigl(T_{\\chi_2}(\\varphi,A)\\bigr)=T_{\\chi_1+\\chi_2}(\\varphi,A)$ for all $C^\\infty$ generators $\\chi_1,\\chi_2$; hence gauge relatedness is an equivalence relation on the $C^\\infty$ potential pairs, the inverse of $T_\\chi$ being $T_{-\\chi}$. For a fixed finite class the transform drops one order, and relatedness is used only through the transform it records.\n\n(c) [Conditions] A potential pair is **in Lorenz gauge** on $\\Omega\\times I$ when $G(\\varphi,A)=\\operatorname{div}A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi=0$ throughout $\\Omega\\times I$, and **in Coulomb gauge** in the chosen spatial chart when $\\operatorname{div}A=0$ throughout $\\Omega\\times I$. These are properties of a pair in the stated conventions. The existence of a gauge function achieving either condition, with or without prescribed initial or boundary data, is a separate PDE conclusion with its own hypotheses and is not asserted by this definition.\n\n(d) [Physical status] The physical content of a representation is its field pair $(E,B)$ of [[def-em-fields]]; the values of $\\varphi$, of $A$ and of any gauge function are convention dependent and are auxiliary, and gauge related representations have the same field pair. The scalar potential $\\varphi$ carries the volt, the vector potential $A$ carries the tesla metre, and a gauge function carries V s $=$ T m$^2$, so its time derivative and gradient have the units of $\\varphi$ and $A$, respectively, consistently with [[def-em-si-conventions]]. No value of $\\varphi$ or $A$ is by itself an observable or evidence for a field.",
          "strategy": "A vocabulary and bookkeeping definition over the exact interfaces of [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]; it makes no existence or solvability claim. Obligations checked: (i) the field-pair formulas and the transform are those of the cited definition, so the regularity statements (a $C^m$ pair gives $C^{m-1}$ fields; a $C^m$ generator gives a $C^{m-1}$ transform) are inherited exactly; (ii) the composition identity is the componentwise linearity of the coordinate partial derivatives, $\\partial_t(\\chi_1+\\chi_2)=\\partial_t\\chi_1+\\partial_t\\chi_2$ and $\\nabla(\\chi_1+\\chi_2)=\\nabla\\chi_1+\\nabla\\chi_2$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]), together with $T_0=\\mathrm{id}$; the inverse of $T_\\chi$ is $T_{-\\chi}$, so the relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive on $C^\\infty$ pairs; (iii) the definition deliberately separates the two gauge conditions from their reachability: no conclusion that a pair can be put in either gauge, with or without prescribed data, is drawn here (compare [[pthm-em-spacetime-potentials]] for existence of representations and the gauge-reachability theorem later on this page for the two declared branches); (iv) no choice principle, no domain hypothesis beyond the laboratory region, and no Maxwell equation is used.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
