            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $c>0$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with at least two elements ([[def-interval]]), and let $(\\varphi,A)$ be a potential pair of class $C^2$ jointly on $\\Omega\\times I$ in the restriction convention of [[lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials]] ([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]): for open $I$ this is the ordinary open-domain class of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], and for non-open $I$ the pair is the restriction of a $C^2$ pair on an open neighbourhood of $\\Omega\\times I$, with relative derivatives at included endpoints and no time jet beyond the restricted values. Write $(E,B)$ for the field pair of $(\\varphi,A)$ ([[def-em-fields]]) and\n\n$$G:=\\operatorname{div}A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi,\\qquad W:=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$$\n\nfor the Lorenz divergence and the wave expression of [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]], with $\\Delta$ the Laplacian of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]. Let $\\rho:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ and $J:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be continuous source fields of the framework ([[def-em-sources]]).\n\n(a) [Exact identities] Independently of any field equation,\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=W\\varphi-\\partial_tG,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B-c^{-2}\\partial_tE=WA+\\nabla G\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I.$$\n\nConsequently the sourced Maxwell equations of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] hold for $(E,B,\\rho,J)$ if and only if\n\n$$W\\varphi-\\partial_tG=\\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0},\\qquad WA+\\nabla G=\\mu_0J\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I,$$\n\nwith $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ the constant of that postulate.\n\n(b) [Gauge shift] If $\\chi:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ is a gauge function of class $C^2$ jointly on $\\Omega\\times I$ ([[def-em-gauge-transformation]]), then $G(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=G-W\\chi$ on $\\Omega\\times I$ and the transformed pair has the same field pair $(E,B)$ ([[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]]); hence $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)$ is in Lorenz gauge if and only if $W\\chi=G$ on $\\Omega\\times I$.\n\n(c) [Decoupling in Lorenz gauge] Suppose in addition that the given pair is in Lorenz gauge, $G=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$. Then its field pair solves the sourced Maxwell system of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] if and only if the pair satisfies the decoupled sourced wave equations\n\n$$W\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad WA=\\mu_0J\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I.$$\n\nThe two source-free equations $\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$ hold automatically for every potential pair of class $C^2$. For an arbitrary given potential pair, the conjunction of Lorenz gauge with these two wave equations implies the sourced Maxwell system, but Maxwell's equations alone do not impose $G=0$ on that representative: their exact equivalent conditions remain the residual equations of (a), whose right-hand sides retain $\\partial_tG$ and $\\nabla G$.\n\n(d) [Residual generators] If the $C^2$ gauge function of (b) satisfies $W\\chi=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$, then the transform of a pair in Lorenz gauge is again in Lorenz gauge and has the same field pair. When $\\Omega$ is nonempty the Lorenz condition does not determine the pair: the generator $\\chi(x,t)=x_1$, read in numerical SI components with gauge units as in [[def-em-gauge-transformation]], has $W\\chi=0$ and changes $A$ by the nonzero constant vector $(1,0,0)$.\n\n(e) [Scope] The identities and equivalences are properties of the given $C^2$ representation. No gauge function with $W\\chi=G$, and no generator meeting prescribed initial or boundary values, is asserted to exist; that is a separate PDE conclusion with its own hypotheses, treated by the gauge-reachability theorem later on this page. No boundary, decay or initial condition on the pair is imposed, and no empirical claim or observability of $\\varphi$ or $A$ is included. A pair of class $C^1$ defines $E$, $B$ and $G$ but need not possess the second derivatives appearing in $W\\varphi$, $WA$, $\\Delta$ or $\\partial_tG$, so $C^2$ is the exact standing regularity here.",
          "strategy": "(a) Componentwise expansion using the sum rules for divergence and curl ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]), the vector identity $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}A=\\nabla\\operatorname{div}A-\\Delta A$ for the $C^2$ field $A$ ([[cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field]]), the definition $\\Delta\\varphi=\\operatorname{div}\\nabla\\varphi$ ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]) and the equality of mixed partials of the $C^2$ functions $\\varphi,A$ ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]): $\\operatorname{div}E=-\\Delta\\varphi-\\partial_t\\operatorname{div}A=W\\varphi-\\partial_tG$, and $\\operatorname{curl}B-c^{-2}\\partial_tE=\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\nabla\\varphi+c^{-2}\\partial_t^2A=\\nabla\\operatorname{div}A-\\Delta A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\nabla\\varphi+c^{-2}\\partial_t^2A=WA+\\nabla G$. The two source-free equations hold automatically for a potential pair by [[thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes]] and [[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]] with the linearity of curl ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]]); hence the equivalence with the sourced system. (b) Direct expansion of $T_\\chi$: $G(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=\\operatorname{div}A+\\Delta\\chi+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi-c^{-2}\\partial_t^2\\chi=G-W\\chi$, using linearity and the $C^2$ mixed-partials identity for $\\chi$ ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]); the field part is [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]]. (c) Under the explicitly imposed hypothesis $G=0$, the continuously defined derivatives $\\partial_tG$ and $\\nabla G$ vanish. Substitution in (a) turns the two sourced residual equations exactly into $W\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ and $WA=\\mu_0J$. Conversely those two wave equations with $G=0$ make the residual equations hold, and the two source-free equations were already proved for every potential pair. There is no inference from Maxwell alone to a gauge condition; (d) is (b) with $G=0$ and $W\\chi=0$. All statements are pointwise on $\\Omega\\times I$; the constants $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ are those of the framework postulates ([[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]). No choice principle, no hypothesis on the shape of $\\Omega$ and no solvability of $W\\chi=G$ is used. The decoupling inference uses a time interval with at least two elements, so relative endpoint derivatives are determined; singleton use requires ambient hypotheses before restriction. The explicit residual nonuniqueness witness requires a nonempty spatial domain.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§6.1 printed pp. 134-135, eqs. (6.1)-(6.4): the potential formulation, the reduction of the Maxwell equations to the sourced wave equation in Lorentz gauge, and the gauge-transformation identity for the divergence"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§18 printed pp. 49-50: the gauge transformation and its effect on the potentials, with the fields unchanged"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Partial Differential Equations: From Classical to Modern (2025 archived author manuscript, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250324094647id_/https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-pde/pde.pdf",
