            "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ in branch (a), and the Axiom of Choice together with $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ as declared by its weak-solvability lemmas in branch (b) ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $c>0$ and let $(\\varphi,A)$ be a potential pair of the framework ([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]], [[def-em-fields]]) with field pair $(E,B)$ and Lorenz divergence $G=\\operatorname{div}A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\varphi$.\n\n(a) [All-space slab; Lorenz gauge] Let $t_0<T$ and let $(\\varphi,A)$ be of class $C^\\infty$ in the extension sense on $\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$ (the restriction of a $C^\\infty$ pair on $\\mathbb R^3\\times J$ for some open interval $J\\supset[t_0,T]$; [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Then there is a gauge function $\\chi$ of class $C^\\infty$ in the extension sense on $\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$ with $\\chi(\\cdot,t_0)=0$, $\\partial_t\\chi(\\cdot,t_0)=0$ and $W\\chi=G$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$; the transformed pair $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)$ is in Lorenz gauge on $\\mathbb R^3\\times(t_0,T)$, has the same field pair $(E,B)$, and any two such gauge functions differ by a solution of $W\\chi=0$. This is the exact content of [[lem-em-gauge-wave-reachability]] read for potential pairs of the framework; its physical content is that every smooth potential representation of a field pair on a slab admits a Lorenz-gauge representative with zero gauge Cauchy data at $t_0$, without changing the fields.\n\n(b) [Bounded smooth domain; Coulomb gauge with admissible boundary families] Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^\\infty$ graph domain ([[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]), let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an arbitrary interval, possibly unbounded, and let $(\\varphi,A)$ be $C^\\infty$ on an actual neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega\\times\\overline I$. Prescribe either (i) a Dirichlet family $g_D(p,t)$ smooth in boundary charts on a neighbourhood of $\\partial\\Omega\\times\\overline I$, or (ii) an outward conormal family $\\kappa(p,t)$ with the same smoothness and the compatibility $\\int_{\\Omega_j}\\varepsilon_0\\operatorname{div}A(\\cdot,t)\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}\\kappa(\\cdot,t)\\,dS=0$ for every connected component $\\Omega_j$ of $\\Omega$ and every $t\\in I$. Then there is a gauge generator $\\psi$, the restriction of one $C^\\infty$ function on an actual open neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega\\times\\overline I$, such that $\\operatorname{div}(A+\\nabla\\psi)=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$. In case (i), $\\psi(p,t)=g_D(p,t)$ on $\\partial\\Omega\\times I$, and its restriction to $\\Omega\\times I$ is unique with those Dirichlet values. In case (ii), $\\varepsilon_0\\partial_\\nu\\psi(p,t)=\\kappa(p,t)$ there, and imposing $\\int_{\\Omega_j}\\psi(\\cdot,t)\\,dx=0$ for every component and every $t$ gives a unique generator on $\\Omega\\times I$; without that normalization it is unique modulo smooth time-dependent spatial constants on each component. The transformed pair $T_\\psi(\\varphi,A)$ is in Coulomb gauge and has the same physical field pair ([[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]]). Any two Coulomb-gauge generators differ by a function spatially harmonic at every $t$; equal Dirichlet data eliminate that residual freedom, whereas equal Neumann data leave only the componentwise constants just described. Zero Dirichlet data are the corresponding special case. These uniqueness statements concern generator values on $\\Omega\\times I$. For $I$ with at least two elements, they also determine the transformed potential pair in the Dirichlet or normalized Neumann case, since spatial and relative time derivatives of equal smooth restrictions agree. For singleton $I$, the ambient time derivative of the generator is not determined by its restricted values, so uniqueness of the transformed pair is not asserted. Auxiliary ambient continuations away from the physical region need not be unique.\n\n(c) [Residual freedom on star-shaped domains] Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open and star-shaped ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]) and let $(\\varphi,A)$, $(\\tilde\\varphi,\\tilde A)$ be two potential pairs of class $C^2$ jointly on $\\Omega\\times I$ ([[def-interval]]) with the same field pair, both in Lorenz gauge. If $I$ is non-open, these $C^2$ classes and the resulting generator use the open-neighbourhood restriction convention of [[lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials]]; for the physical open laboratory interval they have their ordinary $C^2$ meaning. If a degenerate $I$ is used in this branch, choose the ambient $C^2$ extensions as part of the data: the same-field and Lorenz conditions refer to these extensions on an ambient time neighbourhood before restriction, and their time jets are not determined uniquely by the restricted values alone. Then there is a gauge function $\\chi$ of class $C^2$ jointly with $(\\tilde\\varphi,\\tilde A)=T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)$ and $W\\chi=0$ on $\\Omega\\times I$.\n\n(d) [Scope] Nothing is asserted about gauge functions meeting arbitrary prescribed boundary or initial restrictions: in (a) the gauge function has zero Cauchy data at $t_0$ and no spatial boundary condition or decay, and $\\Omega$ is all of $\\mathbb R^3$; in (b) the datum is either a smooth time-dependent Dirichlet family or a smooth outward conormal family satisfying the stated componentwise compatibility at every physical time, with zero-mean normalization for Neumann uniqueness; the older promise of a global Lorenz gauge on a bounded domain with prescribed boundary data is explicitly outside this result. The Coulomb-gauge statement concerns the spatial divergence in the chosen inertial chart, and the instantaneous appearance of the Coulomb potential is a property of the representation, not a signal: only the field pair $(E,B)$ is physical content ([[def-em-fields]]).",
          "strategy": "(a) is [[lem-em-gauge-wave-reachability]] with its hypotheses and countable choice carried verbatim; the pair of the framework enters only through the vocabulary, so nothing is reproved. (b) The fixed-time smooth Dirichlet and compatible Neumann interfaces are those of [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]], and their parameter-compatible ambient smoothness, exact boundary lifts, complete natural-Neumann estimates and normalized uniqueness are supplied by the earlier pure-mathematical [[lem-em-parameter-dependent-smooth-poisson-boundary-solvers]]. Use the original open neighbourhood of the potentials and of the boundary family to choose an open time interval $J$ containing $\\overline I$ by compactness of each fixed spatial/boundary fibre; only $\\overline\\Omega\\times K$, with compact $K\\subset J$, is used for derivative bounds, never compactness of all $\\overline I$. Put $F_t=\\varepsilon_0\\operatorname{div}A(\\cdot,t)$. In the Dirichlet case the supplier constructs a fixed finite-chart spatial lift $H_t=L_Dg_D(\\cdot,t)$ and the zero-trace operator $R$ for $-\\varepsilon_0\\Delta$ from its exact weak solver, including every $L^2$ source, with its higher-order bounds. Set $\\psi_t=H_t+R(F_t+\\varepsilon_0\\Delta H_t)$. Differentiation includes the lift at every order: $\\partial_t^j\\psi_t=L_D\\partial_t^jg_D+R(\\partial_t^jF_t+\\varepsilon_0\\Delta L_D\\partial_t^jg_D)$. Thus the spatial equation is $-\\varepsilon_0\\Delta\\psi_t=F_t$ and the Dirichlet family is exact. In the Neumann case use the same supplier's compatible normalized weak solver and natural-boundary estimates. Compatibility is imposed only for $t\\in I$: on an open $J$, define $c_j(t)=\\int_{\\Omega_j}F_t+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega_j}\\kappa_t$ and the auxiliary bulk extension $\\widehat F_t=F_t-c_j(t)/|\\Omega_j|$ on each of the finitely many components. It is smooth, compatible for all $t\\in J$ and agrees with $F_t$ on $I$; no equation for the gauge outside the physical interval is promised. Apply the parameter solver to $(\\widehat F,\\kappa)$ with zero mean on each component. In either case locally uniform compact-time difference-quotient estimates give all mixed derivatives up to the boundary, and the supplier's fixed parameter-preserving Seeley construction supplies one actual ambient $C^\\infty$ continuation, not merely separate finite-order Sobolev representatives. The resulting generator obeys $\\Delta\\psi=-\\operatorname{div}A$ on every physical time, with the requested boundary data, so it is Coulomb gauge and preserves the fields by [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]]. Elliptic uniqueness gives the stated normalized uniqueness on $\\Omega\\times I$; subtracting any two Coulomb generators gives $\\Delta\\chi=0$ and the listed Dirichlet/Neumann residual freedom. This argument retains the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice hypotheses of the variational, chart and Sobolev suppliers, and imposes no global time bounds or extra regularity restriction. (c) Put $\\delta\\varphi=\\tilde\\varphi-\\varphi$ and $\\delta A=\\tilde A-A$. Equality of the field pairs gives $\\operatorname{curl}\\delta A=0$ and $\\nabla\\delta\\varphi+\\partial_t\\delta A=0$. Apply the corrected scalar lemma to $C=\\delta A$ and denote its negative-gradient witness by $\\phi_{\\delta A}(x,t)=-\\int_0^1\\langle\\delta A(a+s(x-a),t),x-a\\rangle ds$. Then $\\nabla\\phi_{\\delta A}=-\\delta A$ and $\\phi_{\\delta A}$ is jointly $C^2$. Take $$\\chi_0=-\\phi_{\\delta A}=\\int_0^1\\langle\\delta A(a+s(x-a),t),x-a\\rangle ds,$$ so $\\nabla\\chi_0=\\delta A$. This additional negation is essential; using the lemma's negative-gradient witness itself as the gauge generator would reverse the vector-potential difference. Mixed partials of the $C^2$ function $\\chi_0$ now give $\\nabla(\\delta\\varphi+\\partial_t\\chi_0)=\\nabla\\delta\\varphi+\\partial_t\\delta A=0$. For fixed t, restrict this $C^1$ scalar to each radial segment $a+s(x-a)$; the chain rule gives zero derivative, and cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant gives its common value $h(t)=\\delta\\varphi(a,t)+\\partial_t\\chi_0(a,t)$. Since $\\chi_0(a,t)=0$ for every t, the time derivative there is zero, so $h(t)=\\delta\\varphi(a,t)$ (in particular h is C1; indeed C2 under the stated hypotheses). For nondegenerate I, fix $t_*\\in I$ and set $$\\eta(t)=-\\int_{t_*}^{t}h(u)du.$$ cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function gives $\\eta'=-h$ on I, with relative endpoint derivatives; since h is C1, eta is C2. Put $\\chi=\\chi_0+\\eta$. Then $\\nabla\\chi=\\delta A$ and $\\partial_t\\chi=\\partial_t\\chi_0-h=-\\delta\\varphi$, so $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)=(\\tilde\\varphi,\\tilde A)$. Finally the unchanged exact gauge-shift identity in pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form(b) gives $0=G(\\tilde\\varphi,\\tilde A)=G(\\varphi,A)-W\\chi=-W\\chi$, hence $W\\chi=0$. The corrected radial construction gives chi0 as a C2 restriction from an open radial-admissibility neighbourhood. At the centre, the data extension gives h(t)=delta phi(a,t) on an open time neighbourhood; take the interval component containing I and construct the time primitive there. Adding this primitive to the chi0 extension yields a C2 extension on the intersection neighbourhood of Omega x I. Thus the asserted generator retains the same regularity convention at included endpoints of nondegenerate time intervals. Degenerate interval restrictions do not supply unique time jets: any use of this time-derivative branch there carries chosen ambient extensions and the same-field/Lorenz hypotheses on an ambient time neighbourhood, to which the nondegenerate construction applies before restriction. On an empty restriction there are no pointwise equations to prove. The physical laboratory branch already uses a nonempty open time interval.  This residual construction uses no choice principle; the preexisting Choice assumptions in branches (a)-(b) remain exactly as stated. The linearity of $\\nabla$ and $\\partial_t$ used throughout is [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]; $H^m$ notation is [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], and the elliptic problems use the domains of [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]. The declared Choice assumptions enter only through the elliptic existence theorem and the wave supplier and are carried in the statement. No claim beyond (a)-(c) is made. Dirichlet and normalized Neumann uniqueness concern generator values for arbitrary I; they imply transformed-pair uniqueness only when I has at least two elements, as Proof 3.1 now establishes.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§6.1 printed pp. 134-135 (the claim and proof that a gauge function with square-box chi = f can always be found, reducing the Maxwell equations to the sourced wave equation) and §3.2.2 printed pp. 48-49 (the Coulomb-gauge construction by solving the Poisson equation for chi)"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Partial Differential Equations: From Classical to Modern (2025 archived author manuscript, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250324094647id_/https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-pde/pde.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.1 printed pp. 167-170, Theorem 7.2 and Corollary 7.3 with (7.11)-(7.12): existence, uniqueness and regularity for the wave Cauchy problem with source and zero data on a slab"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§10.1.3 printed pp. 421-423: the Coulomb gauge div A = 0 with the Poisson equation for the scalar potential, the Lorentz gauge, and the explicit remark that the instantaneous appearance of the Coulomb potential is not a measurable signal because only E and B are observable"
              },
              {
                "title": "John K. Hunter, Notes on Partial Differential Equations (UC Davis, revised 18 June 2014, complete 242-page two-quarter graduate notes)",
                "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/pdes/pde_notes.pdf",
                "locator": "§4.12 printed pp. 116-117, Theorem 4.31 and Corollary 4.32: the higher-order H^{k+2} Dirichlet estimate and the C^infty regularity for smooth data used in the parameter-smoothness argument of branch (b)"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Gauge reachability for potential representations of the classical vacuum electromagnetic framework, in exactly two declared branches: the all-space smooth slab branch, where a Lorenz-gauge representative with zero gauge Cauchy data exists (carrying countable choice through the wave supplier), and the bounded smooth-domain branch, where a Coulomb-gauge generator with prescribed smooth parameter-dependent Dirichlet data exists with unique values on the physical region, or with componentwise-compatible smooth Neumann flux data exists with unique values after zero-mean normalization; these generator-value uniqueness statements determine a unique transformed potential pair when the physical time interval has at least two elements (carrying the choice assumptions declared by the elliptic supplier). The item also classifies residual freedom on star-shaped domains. It makes no empirical claim, asserts nothing about arbitrary prescribed boundary or initial restrictions, and does not claim that gauge conditions are physically observable.",
          "dependency_level": 18
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 341
    },
