            "lem-radial-normalisation-is-continuous": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-simply-connected": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-path-connected": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-euclidean-spheres-are-path-connected": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy-are-equivalence-relations": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-linear-maps-with-finite-dimensional-domain-are-bounded": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bounded-linear-operator-equivalences": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-algebra-of-continuous-real-maps-on-a-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-path-connected-implies-connected": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Write points of $\\mathbb R^3$ as $x=(x,y,z)$ with the Euclidean inner product and norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and let $U_1:=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{(0,0,z):z\\in\\mathbb R\\}$ and $U_2:=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ be the complements of the $z$-axis and of the origin, two open sets.\n\n(a) [Scalar obstruction] On $U_1$ the field\n\n$$F(x,y,z):=\\frac{(-y,\\,x,\\,0)}{x^2+y^2}$$\n\nis of class $C^\\infty$, satisfies $\\operatorname{curl}F=0$ on $U_1$ with the curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], and is not the gradient of any $C^2$ function on $U_1$, in the sense of exactness of [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]]. Its circulation around the positively oriented unit circle $\\gamma(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t,0)$, $t\\in[0,2\\pi]$, in the sense of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]], equals $2\\pi$; the gradient theorem would force circulation zero for a gradient of a $C^2$ function. Hence a curl-free smooth field on a connected open set need not have a global scalar potential, and the star-shaped hypothesis of [[pthm-em-spacetime-potentials]] cannot be dropped.\n\n(b) [Vector obstruction] On $U_2$ the field\n\n$$B(x):=\\frac{x}{\\lVert x\\rVert_2^{\\,3}}$$\n\nis of class $C^\\infty$ and satisfies $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, but there is no vector potential $A$ of class $C^1$ on $U_2$ with $\\operatorname{curl}A=B$ in the sense of [[def-vector-potential-of-a-c1-vector-field]]. Indeed the flux of $B$ through the unit sphere in the orientation of the spherical parametrization $\\Phi(\\phi,\\theta):=(\\sin\\phi\\cos\\theta,\\ \\sin\\phi\\sin\\theta,\\ \\cos\\phi)$ is $4\\pi$, while the classical Stokes theorem applied to the two closed hemispherical $C^2$ patches with induced boundary chains forces that flux to equal a sum of boundary circulations which cancels to zero.\n\n(c) [The punctured space is simply connected] $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ is simply connected in the sense of [[def-simply-connected]]: it is path-connected and every based loop in it is null-homotopic. Consequently simple connectivity of the domain is by itself not sufficient for the existence of a vector potential.\n\n(d) [Scope] The two obstructions have different characters: (a) is detected by the circulation of a closed loop, (b) by the flux through a closed surface. The fields displayed are smooth mathematical witnesses on the punctured open sets; the framework's postulate [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] excludes magnetic charge, and no physical source, physical singularity or empirical claim is attached to them.",
          "strategy": "(a) Differentiate the components of $F$ by the quotient rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) to get $\\operatorname{curl}F=0$; parametrize the unit circle, use the pullback identity for vector line integrals and the sine/cosine derivative and Pythagorean identities to get $F(\\gamma(t))\\cdot\\gamma'(t)=1$, and evaluate $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$ by the fundamental theorem ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]). The gradient theorem for a $C^2$ potential gives zero circulation around the closed curve $\\gamma$, so $F$ is not a gradient ([[thm-gradient-theorem-for-line-integrals]], [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]]). (b) Compute $\\operatorname{div}B=\\sum_i\\partial_i(x_i\\|x\\|_2^{-3})=3\\|x\\|_2^{-3}-3\\|x\\|_2^{-5}\\sum_ix_i^2=0$ by the chain and product rules ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]]). For the flux, differentiate $\\Phi$ to get $\\Phi_\\phi\\times\\Phi_\\theta=\\sin\\phi\\,\\Phi$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]]); since $B(\\Phi)=\\Phi$ on the unit sphere, the integrand is $\\sin\\phi$, and Fubini with the fundamental theorem evaluate the double integral as $4\\pi$ ([[thm-jordan-fubini-by-sections]], [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]]). Suppose $A$ is $C^1$ on the open set $U_2$ with $\\operatorname{curl}A=B$. Each hemisphere rectangle is a finite elementary Green region with its positive boundary chain and the spherical parametrization restricted to it is a $C^2$ regular patch whose image lies in $U_2$, so the classical Stokes theorem applies to each ([[def-type-i-type-ii-and-elementary-green-regions]], [[def-positive-orientation-for-elementary-region-boundaries]], [[def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch]], [[thm-the-classical-stokes-theorem-for-a-c2-surface-patch]]). In each hemisphere the induced boundary chain has four entries: one equator arc, two meridian arcs with the same image and opposite orientations, and one degenerate polar arc; the two meridian integrals cancel by reversal ([[thm-line-integrals-under-reversal-and-concatenation]]) and the degenerate one is zero. Hence the north circulation is the equator integral and the south circulation is its reverse, so their sum is zero; summing the two Stokes identities gives zero for the total flux of $B$ over the sphere, contradicting $4\\pi\\ne0$, and no such $A$ exists. (c) For the basepoint reduction, $L$ and $L^{-1}$ are linear maps of $\\mathbb R^3$, hence bounded and continuous on the finite-dimensional space ([[cor-linear-maps-with-finite-dimensional-domain-are-bounded]], [[thm-bounded-linear-operator-equivalences]]), so a null-homotopy of $L\\circ\\gamma$ composed with $L^{-1}$ is one of $\\gamma$; for a basepoint on the unit sphere, $H$ is the continuous radial homotopy of [[thm-radial-straight-line-map-on-punctured-rn]] fixing the unit sphere, so it deforms the loop to a loop in $S^2$ rel basepoint ([[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]], [[def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group]]), which is null-homotopic rel basepoint by simple connectivity of $S^2$ ([[thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected]]); composing with the inclusion and concatenating gives the required null-homotopy in $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Path connectedness of $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ is seen by routing through the unit sphere ([[cor-euclidean-spheres-are-path-connected]], [[def-path-connected]]). (d) is the comparison of the two mechanisms: circulation versus flux. All suppliers are published items used at their stated hypotheses; no choice principle is used.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§3.2.1 printed p. 47 (magnetic monopoles and the inverse-square field) and §3.2.4 printed pp. 52-53 (the linking-number discussion of the curl-free field near a line), §3.2.2 printed pp. 48-49 (the vector potential and gauge freedom)"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§16 printed pp. 44-45 and §18 printed p. 49: the potential representation of the fields and the non-uniqueness of the potentials"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A mathematical example delimiting the hypotheses of the framework's potential-existence theorem: curl-free and divergence-free smooth fields on two explicit punctured spatial regions that admit no global scalar, respectively vector, potential, showing that the star-shaped hypothesis is substantive and that simple connectivity is not sufficient for a vector potential. The fields are mathematical witnesses on the punctured regions; the framework's postulate excludes magnetic charge, and no physical source, no physical singularity and no empirical claim is asserted.",
          "dependency_level": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-pure-gauge-fields",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Nonzero pure-gauge potentials representing the zero field pair",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X5",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument; explicit constant instance with the linear gauge function",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-gauge-invariance",
            "def-em-gauge-transformation",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
            "def-em-fields",
            "lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-gauge-invariance": "physical-result",
            "def-em-gauge-transformation": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval ([[def-interval]]); for a possibly non-open $I$, the joint classes below are read in the restriction convention of [[lem-em-joint-smooth-potentials]] (for open $I$ this is the ordinary class of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Let $\\chi:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ be of class $C^2$ jointly. If $I$ is a singleton, choose one ambient $C^2$ extension as part of the data and define the potential pair and all its derivatives by the same ambient formulas before restriction; singleton values of $\\chi$ alone do not determine these time derivatives. For an empty product all identities below are vacuous.\n\nThen the pair\n\n$$(\\varphi,A):=T_\\chi(0,0)=(-\\partial_t\\chi,\\ \\nabla\\chi)$$\n\nis a potential pair of class $C^1$ jointly on $\\Omega\\times I$ whose field pair is the zero field pair:\n\n$$E(\\varphi,A)=E(0,0)=0,\\qquad B(\\varphi,A)=\\operatorname{curl}\\nabla\\chi=0\\qquad\\text{on }\\Omega\\times I$$\n\n([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]), the electric identity by the gauge invariance of [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]] applied to the zero pair, whose field pair is $(0,0)$, and the magnetic identity also directly by [[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]] for the $C^2$ function $\\chi$.\n\nAll displayed quantities may be read as numerical SI components: $x$ in m, $t$ in s, $\\chi$ in V s, $a$ in T m and $\\beta$ in V, as in [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]; in particular $e_1$ below specifies a vector-potential component of one T m.\n\nExplicit instance: for $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\chi(x,t)=a\\cdot x-\\beta t$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, the pair is the constant pair $(\\varphi,A)=(\\beta,a)$, with $E=-\\nabla\\beta-\\partial_t a=0$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}a=0$ by [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and the vanishing of all first derivatives of a constant ([[thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). This pair is nonzero if and only if $(\\beta,a)\\ne(0,0)$; for $\\beta=0$ and $a=0$ it is exactly the zero pair. To exhibit noninjectivity explicitly, take $\\beta=0$ and $a=e_1=(1,0,0)$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$. Then $(\\varphi,A)=(0,e_1)$ and $(0,0)$ are distinct potential pairs with the same zero field pair. Thus the map from potential pairs to field pairs of [[def-em-fields]] is not injective: nonzero potentials may represent the zero field pair (pure-gauge fields), and no nonzero value of $\\varphi$ or $A$ alone is evidence for a nonzero field, consistently with [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]] and [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]. The example is a statement about the representation of the zero field configuration; it asserts no Maxwell equation, no source configuration and no empirical situation.",
          "strategy": "Apply the gauge transformation $T_\\chi$ to the zero pair $(0,0)$, using the linearity of the componentwise partial derivatives and of the gradient ([[thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). The field pair of the zero pair is $(0,0)$; by [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]] the transformed pair has the same field pair, which is $(0,0)$. The explicit formulas are then recomputed directly: with $\\chi(x,t)=a\\cdot x-\\beta t$ one has $\\partial_t\\chi=-\\beta$ and $\\nabla\\chi=a$, so $(\\varphi,A)=(\\beta,a)$; the fields of a constant pair vanish because every partial derivative of a constant is zero ([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). Both computations use only the definitions; no choice principle and no hypothesis on $\\Omega$ or $I$ beyond openness and being an interval are used. The formula $(\\varphi,A)=(\\beta,a)$ includes all zero and nonzero parameter choices. Equality of this constant pair to zero is equivalent, component by component, to $\\beta=0$ and $a=0$. Noninjectivity is established by the particular nonzero witness $a=e_1$, $\\beta=0$, compared with the zero pair; it is not inferred by claiming that every parameter choice is nonzero. On singleton time domains choose the ambient extension as data and apply invariance on open boxes before restriction; empty products are vacuous.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§18 printed p. 50: the addition of constants to the vector and scalar potentials does not affect the field intensities, since E and H contain only derivatives of A and phi"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.3.1 printed pp. 105-106: the gauge transformation phi -> phi - dchi/dt, A -> A + grad chi with the fields unchanged"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "An example of gauge representations of the zero field pair: the full linear-generator family includes the zero potential pair at zero parameters and nonzero constant pairs at nonzero parameter tuples. An explicit nonzero witness demonstrates noninjectivity of the potential-to-field map. It asserts no source model, Maxwell equation, empirical situation or observable potential value.",
          "dependency_level": 17
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-residual-lorenz-example",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A homogeneous-wave gauge generator changes Lorenz potentials without changing their fields",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X5",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument; explicit real cosinusoidal generator with |k| = omega/c",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form",
            "pthm-em-gauge-invariance",
            "def-em-gauge-transformation",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
            "def-em-complex-plane-wave",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function",
            "def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "thm-eulers-formula"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-gauge-invariance": "physical-result",
            "def-em-gauge-transformation": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-complex-plane-wave": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-eulers-formula": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $c>0$, let $k\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, put $\\omega:=c\\lVert k\\rVert_2>0$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), and let\n\n$$\\chi(x,t):=\\cos(k\\cdot x-\\omega t),$$\n\nthe real part, by $\\exp(i\\theta)=\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta$ of [[thm-eulers-formula]], of the complex plane wave $e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}$ of [[def-em-complex-plane-wave]] with wave vector $k$ and frequency $\\omega=c\\lVert k\\rVert_2$. For the SI interpretation, use numerical components $x$ in m, $t$ in s, $k$ in m$^{-1}$ and $\\omega$ in s$^{-1}$, so the phase is dimensionless, and give this gauge generator amplitude one V s as in [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]; the displayed $\\chi$ is its numerical value in V s. Then $\\chi$ is of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ and $W\\chi=0$, where $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$ is the wave expression of [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]] (the normalization $W=c^{-2}\\Box_c$ of the wave operator of [[def-wave-equation-cauchy-data-and-wave-speed]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]); indeed $\\partial_t^2\\chi=-\\omega^2\\chi$, $\\Delta\\chi=-\\lVert k\\rVert_2^2\\chi$ and $\\omega^2=c^2\\lVert k\\rVert_2^2$, so $W\\chi=c^{-2}(-c^2\\lVert k\\rVert_2^2\\chi)+\\lVert k\\rVert_2^2\\chi=0$ ([[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]).\n\nConsequently, for every potential pair $(\\varphi,A)$ of class $C^2$ jointly on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ ([[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]]), the gauge-transformed pair satisfies\n\n$$G(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=G(\\varphi,A)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad E(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=E(\\varphi,A),\\quad B(T_\\chi(\\varphi,A))=B(\\varphi,A)$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ ([[pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form]], [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]], [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]); in particular, if $(\\varphi,A)$ is in Lorenz gauge then $T_\\chi(\\varphi,A)$ is again in Lorenz gauge with the same field pair. Explicitly $\\partial_t\\chi=\\omega\\sin(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)$ and $\\nabla\\chi=-k\\sin(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)$, so $\\varphi'=\\varphi-\\omega\\sin(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)$ and $A'=A-k\\sin(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)$. The zero pair is in Lorenz gauge, and its transform $(-\\omega\\sin(k\\cdot x-\\omega t),\\ -k\\sin(k\\cdot x-\\omega t))$ is a nonzero pair that remains in Lorenz gauge and still represents the zero field pair: it is nonzero because otherwise $\\partial_t\\chi$ would vanish identically, forcing $\\partial_t^2\\chi=0$, whereas $\\partial_t^2\\chi(0,0)=-\\omega^2\\ne0$. Thus the Lorenz condition does not determine the potential pair. The example is the residual generator $W\\chi=0$ of [[pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form]] on the free-space branch; on a bounded domain with prescribed gauge boundary data the admissible generators are restricted, and no such data-dependent claim is made here.",
          "strategy": "Compute with the chain and product rules: $\\partial_t\\chi=\\omega\\sin\\theta$, $\\partial_t^2\\chi=-\\omega^2\\cos\\theta$, $\\nabla\\chi=-k\\sin\\theta$ and $\\Delta\\chi=-|k|^2\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta=k\\cdot x-\\omega t$ ([[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]). Substitute $\\omega^2=c^2|k|^2$ to get $W\\chi=0$. Then use the gauge-shift identity and field invariance of [[pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form]](b) and [[pthm-em-gauge-invariance]]; the class bookkeeping is that of [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]. No choice principle is used and no boundary condition is imposed, matching the free-space branch of the gauge-reachability statement on this page.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§6.1 printed pp. 134-135: the gauge transformation of the potential and the wave equation square-box chi = f whose homogeneous case leaves the gauge condition and the fields unchanged"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Partial Differential Equations: From Classical to Modern (2025 archived author manuscript, AMS Graduate Studies in Mathematics)",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250324094647id_/https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-pde/pde.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.1 printed pp. 167-170, eqs. (7.1)-(7.4): the plane-wave solutions of the homogeneous wave equation and the dispersion relation underlying |k| = omega/c"
              }
            ]
