          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the smooth branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] and clause (ii) of\n[[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$\nof [[def-em-lab-geometry]]: the fields $E,B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ are\njointly $C^1$ of [[def-em-fields]], the source fields $\\rho,J$ are continuous\nof [[def-em-sources]], the constants are those of [[def-em-si-conventions]],\nthe force density is\n$f:=\\rho E+J\\times B$ of that clause (ii), in\n$\\mathrm N\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$, and $g,\\sigma,\\Pi=-\\sigma$ are the momentum\ndensity, Maxwell stress and momentum flux of\n[[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]]. Then:\n\n(a) [Local identity] On $\\Omega\\times I$, componentwise,\n\n$$\\partial_tg-\\operatorname{div}\\sigma=-f,\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad\\partial_tg+\\operatorname{div}\\Pi=-f,$$\n\nthat is, $\\partial_tg_i-\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\partial_j\\sigma_{ij}=-(\\rho E_i+(J\\times B)_i)$ for every $i$, with $\\partial_t$ the partial derivative at fixed position and $\\operatorname{div}$ taken at fixed time.\n\n(b) [Fixed-volume identity] Let $\\Omega'\\subseteq\\Omega$ be open with\n$\\overline{\\Omega'}\\subseteq\\Omega$ carrying the finite piecewise $C^1$\npresentation of\n[[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], let\n$t_0<t_1$ with $(t_0,t_1)\\subseteq I$, assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]), and let $n$ be the outward unit normal. Then for\nevery $t\\in(t_0,t_1)$ and every $i$,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{\\Omega'}g_i\\,dx=\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega'}\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\sigma_{ij}n_j\\Bigr)dS-\\int_{\\Omega'}f_i\\,dx=-\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega'}\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\Pi_{ij}n_j\\Bigr)dS-\\int_{\\Omega'}f_i\\,dx,$$\n\nthe closed-surface integrals being the finite face sums of that divergence\ntheorem.\n\n(c) [Scope] The identities are conditional on the two adopted postulates. The\nforce density $f$ is defined only for the matter model of clause (ii), so no\nclaim is made about a prescribed current without such a matter model. No\nconservation of field-plus-matter momentum follows without explicit matter\nbalances and their boundary fluxes. The field-momentum rate in (b) is the\nboundary traction minus the integrated matter force; these terms can cancel,\nand neither may be discarded without justification. The weak branch, singular sources and\nnon-integrable products are excluded as in\n[[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]; no self-force, radiation reaction or\nmagnetic-monopole coupling is included, in accordance with the exclusions of\n[[post-em-lorentz-coupling]]. This is not an empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "direct; apply the quadratic stress identity with coefficients $\\varepsilon_0$ and $\\mu_0^{-1}$, substitute the four smooth Maxwell equations, identify the time derivative of the momentum density by the product rule, and integrate the resulting pointwise identity over an admissible fixed volume with the divergence theorem.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (cover dated Lent Term 2015, 236-page PDF)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.6.1 printed pp. 128-129 (PDF pp. 136-137), eqs. (5.46)-(5.48): momentum density and local force balance; Tong's stress equals our Pi=-sigma. Relevant passages independently read during Step 5a reader-32, 10 October 2026."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§8.2.1-8.2.2 printed pp. 349-353, eqs. (8.19)-(8.21): the stress tensor components, the momentum density eps_0mu_0S and the balance dP/dt = surface traction - force integral; read in the stamped scanned full text"
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§32 printed pp. 79-80 (PDF pp. 91-92), eqs. (32.11)-(32.14): generic flux balance; §33 printed pp. 81-83 (PDF pp. 93-95), eqs. (33.3), (33.7): electromagnetic spatial stress and field force exchange, in Gaussian units re-expressed in SI. Relevant passages independently read during Step 5a reader-32, 10 October 2026."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The conditional momentum balance of the classical vacuum electromagnetic field: the local identity linking the time derivative of the momentum density and the divergence of the momentum flux tensor to the adopted continuum force density, and its fixed-volume traction form with the boundary stress retained, deduced from the smooth branch of the adopted vacuum Maxwell equations and the adopted continuum coupling. The statement is conditional on those adopted postulates, excludes the weak branch, singular source support and non-integrable products, and includes no self-force, radiation reaction or monopole term. No empirical claim is made."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-angular-balance",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Orbital field angular momentum and torque balance",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 19,
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum",
