            "def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-parameter-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-quadratic-stress-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-em-momentum-balance": "physical-result",
            "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-graphs-of-continuous-functions-have-content-zero": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling": "physical-assumption",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the hypotheses of [[pthm-em-momentum-balance]]: the smooth branch of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]], clause (ii) of [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]], the\njointly $C^1$ fields of [[def-em-fields]], continuous sources, and the force\ndensity $f=\\rho E+J\\times B$. Let $g$, $\\sigma$, $\\Pi=-\\sigma$, the orbital\nangular momentum density $\\ell=x\\times g$ and its flux $x\\times\\Pi$ be those\nof [[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]] relative to the origin fixed there. Then:\n\n(a) [Local identity] On $\\Omega\\times I$,\n\n$$\\partial_t\\ell+\\operatorname{div}(x\\times\\Pi)=-x\\times f,$$\n\ncomponentwise\n$\\partial_t\\ell_i+\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\partial_j(x\\times\\Pi)_{ij}=-(x\\times f)_i$,\nwhere $(\\operatorname{div}(x\\times\\Pi))_i:=\\sum_{j=1}^{3}\\partial_j(x\\times\\Pi)_{ij}$\nand $(x\\times f)_i=\\sum_{j,k=1}^{3}\\epsilon_{ijk}x_jf_k$ with the Levi-Civita\nsymbol of [[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]], and $\\partial_t$ is the partial\ntime derivative at fixed position\n([[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]).\n\n(b) [Fixed-volume identity] Under the domain, time-interval and\n$\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ hypotheses of [[pthm-em-momentum-balance]](b), and with\n$n$ the outward unit normal, for every $t\\in(t_0,t_1)$ and each $i$,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{\\Omega'}\\ell_i\\,dx=-\\oint_{\\partial\\Omega'}\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{3}(x\\times\\Pi)_{ij}n_j\\Bigr)dS-\\int_{\\Omega'}(x\\times f)_i\\,dx,$$\n\nthe closed-surface integral being the finite face sum of the divergence\ntheorem used in that clause.\n\n(c) [Scope] The identities depend on the origin fixed in\n[[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]](e) through the explicit factor $x$; no\nfield-only conservation follows by dropping the boundary flux: (b) retains\nboth that flux and the integrated torque, which can cancel to give a zero\nfield-angular-momentum rate. Conservation of field-plus-matter angular\nmomentum requires explicit matter balances and their boundary fluxes. The hypotheses and exclusions of\n[[pthm-em-momentum-balance]](c) are inherited. The density $\\ell$ is orbital,\n$\\ell=x\\times g$; no spin or intrinsic angular momentum of matter is defined\nor claimed by this item. This is not an empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "direct; apply the angular identity of the quadratic stress lemma to the momentum balance, and integrate the result over an admissible fixed volume with compact-parameter differentiation and the same divergence theorem.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596 pp.; full scanned text)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§8.2.4 printed pp. 358-360 (PDF pp. 374-376), eq. (8.34): orbital angular density, followed by the field/matter angular exchange example. This section does not give the flux tensor; that tensor and its balance are derived locally. Relevant passages independently read during Step 5a reader-32, 10 October 2026."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (cover dated Lent Term 2015, 236-page PDF)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.6.3 printed pp. 132-133 (PDF pp. 140-141), eq. (5.51) and its preceding calculation: angular density x cross P and cancellation of the symmetric stress contraction. Tong's stress equals our Pi; the sourced torque term is deduced locally from the matter-coupled momentum identity. Relevant passages independently read during Step 5a reader-32, 10 October 2026."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, Third Revised English Edition (Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 2, Pergamon Press 1971, 387 pp.)",
                "url": "http://www.elegio.it/mc2/LandauLifshitz_TheClassicalTheoryOfFields_text.pdf",
                "locator": "§32 printed p. 79 (PDF p. 91), eqs. (32.8)-(32.9): angular momentum tensor and the symmetric-stress cancellation. The local sourced and fixed-volume orbital balances are proved here. Relevant passages independently read during Step 5a reader-32, 10 October 2026."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "The conditional orbital angular momentum balance of the classical vacuum electromagnetic field about the fixed origin of the laboratory chart: the local identity linking the time derivative of the orbital angular momentum density and the divergence of its flux tensor to the torque density, and its fixed-volume form with the boundary angular flux retained, deduced from the momentum balance of the same framework. The density is orbital only; no spin or intrinsic angular momentum of matter is defined or claimed, and no empirical claim is made."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-coupled-total-conservation",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Closed-system energy, momentum and angular balance conditional on a matter model",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 20,
          "deps": [
