            "pthm-em-momentum-balance": "physical-result",
            "lem-em-period-average": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-multidimensional-darboux-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $k\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $\\omega>0$ with $\\omega=c|k|$, let\n$e\\in\\mathbb C^3$ with $k\\cdot e=0$ and $b=k\\times e/\\omega$, and let $(E,B)$ be\nthe corresponding plane-wave solution of [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]]\n([[def-em-plane-wave-definition]]) on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$. Let $u$ and\n$S$ be the energy density and Poynting vector of\n[[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]], and $g=S/c^2$, $\\sigma$, $\\Pi=-\\sigma$ the\nmomentum density, Maxwell stress and momentum flux of\n[[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]]. Write $T=2\\pi/\\omega$,\n$|e|^2=e\\cdot\\overline e$, $\\hat k=k/|k|$, and let\n$\\langle f\\rangle:=\\frac1T\\int_0^Tf(x,s)\\,ds$ be the period average at fixed\n$x$. Then, for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$:\n\n(a) [Averaged energy density] $\\langle u\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}|e|^2$,\nindependent of $x$ and of the starting time.\n\n(b) [Averaged Poynting vector]\n$\\langle S\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0c}{2}|e|^2\\hat k$; hence\n$\\langle S\\rangle=\\langle u\\rangle c\\hat k$, and the period-averaged intensity\nis $I:=|\\langle S\\rangle|=c\\langle u\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0c}{2}|e|^2$.\n\n(c) [Averaged momentum density, stress and momentum flux]\n$\\langle g\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2c}|e|^2\\hat k$, and componentwise\n$\\langle\\sigma_{ij}\\rangle=-\\langle u\\rangle\\hat k_i\\hat k_j$, equivalently\n$\\langle\\Pi_{ij}\\rangle=\\langle u\\rangle\\hat k_i\\hat k_j$; the period-averaged\nmomentum flux across a plane with unit normal $n$ (with the physical balance\ninterpretation conditional as in (d)) is therefore\n$\\langle u\\rangle(\\hat k\\cdot n)\\hat k$, a pure flux along $\\hat k$ with signed\ncoefficient $(I/c)(\\hat k\\cdot n)$ and magnitude $(I/c)|\\hat k\\cdot n|$. The\ntensor identity holds for every complex transverse amplitude $e$, not only for\nreal or circular ones.\n\n(d) [Balance reading with continuum coupling] For this clause additionally\nadopt [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](ii) for the empty-matter model on\n$\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$: the matter energy density\n$w:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$, energy flux\n$Q:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$, momentum density\n$p:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and momentum flux\n$P:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ are all identically\nzero, as are the source fields $\\rho,J$. The adopted electromagnetic force\nand power densities are then $f:=\\rho E+J\\times B=0$ and $J\\cdot E=0$,\nrespectively; the zero matter balances are satisfied. Under this additional\ninterpretation premise the local identities of [[pthm-em-poynting-balance]]\nand [[pthm-em-momentum-balance]] hold for this field; since $u,g,S,\\sigma$ are $T$-periodic in $t$, the period averages of\ntheir time derivatives vanish ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]), so averaging the\nidentities gives $\\operatorname{div}\\langle S\\rangle=0$ and\n$\\operatorname{div}\\langle\\sigma\\rangle=0$ consistently (both averaged fields\nare in fact constant), and the reading of $u,S,g,\\Pi$ as energy and momentum\ndensities and fluxes is that of those two theorems.\n\n(e) [Scope] These are exact identities for the ideal plane wave; $I$ is the\nintensity used by the radiation-pressure thought experiment of the companion\npage and in radiation-flux arguments. No detection statistics, no finite-beam\nor finite-time realisation and no empirical claim is made, and the zero\namplitude gives the zero wave and zero total energy, whereas every nonzero\namplitude gives infinite total energy over $\\mathbb R^3$.",
          "strategy": "Clause (a)-(c) are the compact-period calculation of [[lem-em-period-average]] applied componentwise: with $E=\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i\\theta})$, $B=\\operatorname{Re}(b\\,e^{i\\theta})$, $\\theta=k\\cdot x-\\omega t$, the lemma gives $\\langle E_iE_j\\rangle=\\frac12\\operatorname{Re}(e_i\\overline{e_j})$, $\\langle B_iB_j\\rangle=\\frac12\\operatorname{Re}(b_i\\overline{b_j})$ and $\\langle(E\\times B)_i\\rangle=\\frac12\\operatorname{Re}((e\\times\\overline b)_i)$; hence $\\langle|E|^2\\rangle=|e|^2/2$ and, using $b=k\\times e/\\omega$ and $|k|=\\omega/c$, $\\langle|B|^2\\rangle=|b|^2/2=|e|^2/(2c^2)$. Substituting into $u$ and $S$ of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]] and using $1/(\\mu_0c^2)=\\varepsilon_0$ gives (a) and (b), since $e\\times\\overline b=e\\times(k\\times\\overline e)/\\omega=k|e|^2/\\omega$ by the cross-product identity of [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]] and $e\\cdot k=0$. For (c), expand $\\langle\\sigma_{ij}\\rangle=(\\varepsilon_0/2)\\operatorname{Re}(e_i\\overline{e_j})-(\\varepsilon_0/4)\\delta_{ij}|e|^2+(1/(2\\mu_0))\\operatorname{Re}(b_i\\overline{b_j})-(1/(4\\mu_0))\\delta_{ij}|b|^2$ and substitute $b_i\\overline{b_j}=(k\\times e)_i\\overline{(k\\times e)_j}/\\omega^2$ together with the coordinate identity $(k\\times e)_i\\overline{(k\\times e)_j}=|k|^2|e|^2\\delta_{ij}-k_ik_j|e|^2-|k|^2\\overline{e_i}e_j$ (a bilinear coordinate expansion of the cross product, again from the cited lemma); with $|k|^2/\\omega^2=1/c^2$ and $1/(\\mu_0c^2)=\\varepsilon_0$ $\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{e_i}e_j)=\\operatorname{Re}(e_i\\overline{e_j})$, so all terms combine to $-\\langle u\\rangle\\hat k_i\\hat k_j$. Clause (d) additionally adopts [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](ii) in the explicitly defined empty-matter model with zero source fields and zero matter energy/momentum densities and fluxes; it applies both balance theorems with zero force and power density and averages their identities: for the $T$-periodic smooth quantities the mean of the time derivative is zero by the fundamental theorem ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]), and the remaining flux terms are the averaged quantities just computed, which are constant and hence divergence-free. The average formulas in (a)-(c) are independent of this extra interpretation premise. For the last scope assertion, $e=0$ implies $b=0$ and $u=0$. If $e\\ne0$, write $e=a+id$, so $u=\\varepsilon_0|a\\cos\\theta-d\\sin\\theta|^2$. Choose one coordinate $j$ with $k_j\\ne0$ and put $P=2\\pi/|k_j|$. At fixed other coordinates and time, integration in $x_j$ over any interval of length $P$ gives $\\varepsilon_0P(|a|^2+|d|^2)/2=\\varepsilon_0P|e|^2/2$: expand the square and integrate $\\cos^2$, $\\sin^2$ and $\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ over a full period, using the fundamental theorem and $\\partial_{x_j}\\theta=k_j$. Thus for $R=NP$ with a positive integer $N$, every such section of $[-R,R]^3$ has integral $N\\varepsilon_0P|e|^2$. The continuous density and all its sections are Riemann integrable by [[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]. [[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]] (applied successively, with coordinate reordering justified by the identical finite product-grid Darboux sums) yields $\\int_{[-R,R]^3}u=(2R)^2N\\varepsilon_0P|e|^2\\to\\infty$. Nonnegativity and monotonicity of the box integral ([[def-multidimensional-darboux-integral]]) give divergence along all $R\\to\\infty$, not only this sequence. No interchange of time and space averages or choice principle is used.",
          "physical_scope": "Exact period-average identities for an ideal whole-space plane-wave solution of the smooth source-free vacuum Maxwell branch, in a named inertial chart with SI constants: averaged energy density, Poynting flux, momentum density and Maxwell-stress/momentum-flux tensor, with the physical balance reading additionally conditional on Lorentz continuum coupling (ii) in the explicitly defined empty-matter model. Excluded: finite beams and pulses, sources, boundaries, material media, any ensemble or detection statistics, any nonzero finite-total-energy whole-space plane wave, and any empirical claim. The admitted zero amplitude has zero total energy.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§4.4 pp. 92-94 (plane-wave Poynting vector $S=(E_0^2/(c\\mu_0))\\hat k\\sin^2(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)$, the period averages $\\bar S=E_0^2/(2c\\mu_0)\\hat k$, $\\bar u=\\epsilon_0E_0^2/2$ and $\\bar S=c\\bar u\\hat k$)"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§9.2.3 printed pp. 381-382 (cycle averages $\\langle u\\rangle=\\frac12\\epsilon_0E_0^2$, $\\langle S\\rangle=\\frac12c\\epsilon_0E_0^2\\hat k$, $\\langle P\\rangle=\\frac12(\\epsilon_0E_0^2/c)\\hat k$; intensity $I$; Problem 9.11 averaging device; Problem 9.12 stress tensor)"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-classical-interference",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Superposition intensity and the coherent interference cross term",
          "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 20,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-linear-superposition",
            "pthm-em-wave-energy-average",
            "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell",
            "def-em-plane-wave-definition",
            "lem-em-period-average",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
            "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-linear-superposition": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-wave-energy-average": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell": "physical-result",
            "def-em-plane-wave-definition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-period-average": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\omega>0$ and let $k_1,k_2\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ satisfy\n$|k_1|=|k_2|=\\omega/c$. For $j=1,2$ let $e_j\\in\\mathbb C^3$ with\n$k_j\\cdot e_j=0$, put $b_j=k_j\\times e_j/\\omega$ and let $(E_j,B_j)$ be the\nplane-wave solutions of [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]] given by\n[[def-em-plane-wave-definition]]. Let $a_1,a_2\\in\\mathbb R$ and put\n$(E,B):=a_1(E_1,B_1)+a_2(E_2,B_2)$. Write\n$\\hat k_j=k_j/|k_j|$, $T=2\\pi/\\omega$,\n$|z|^2:=z\\cdot\\overline z=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3|z_\\ell|^2$ for $z\\in\\mathbb C^3$,\nand $\\langle f\\rangle(x):=T^{-1}\\int_0^Tf(x,s)\\,ds$ componentwise.\nThen:\n\n(a) [Superposition] $(E,B)$ is a smooth source-free solution of the vacuum\nMaxwell equations ([[pthm-em-linear-superposition]]), and at each fixed $x$ it\ncan be written with a single phase,\n$$E(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha(x)\\,e^{i(k_1\\cdot x-\\omega t)}),\\qquad B(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}(\\beta(x)\\,e^{i(k_1\\cdot x-\\omega t)}),$$\nwhere $\\alpha(x):=a_1e_1+a_2e_2e^{i\\delta(x)}$,\n$\\beta(x):=a_1b_1+a_2b_2e^{i\\delta(x)}$ and\n$\\delta(x):=(k_2-k_1)\\cdot x$; constant relative phases are absorbed in $e_2$.\n\n(b) [Averaged energy density with the coherent cross term] For every $x$,\n$$\\langle u\\rangle(x)=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{4}|\\alpha(x)|^2+\\frac{1}{4\\mu_0}|\\beta(x)|^2=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}\\bigl(a_1^2|e_1|^2+a_2^2|e_2|^2\\bigr)+\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}a_1a_2\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(W(x)\\bigr),$$\nwhere\n$W(x):=\\bigl(e_1\\cdot\\overline e_2+(\\hat k_1\\times e_1)\\cdot(\\hat k_2\\times\\overline e_2)\\bigr)e^{-i\\delta(x)}$.\nThe first two terms are the sum of the averaged energy densities of the two\nwaves ([[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]]); the third is the interference cross\nterm, which may remain nonzero after time averaging at fixed relative phase.\n\n(c) [Averaged flux and momentum; equal wavevectors] (with $u$ the energy\ndensity and $S$ the Poynting vector of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]])\n$\\langle S\\rangle(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\mu_0}\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(\\alpha(x)\\times\\overline{\\beta(x)}\\bigr)$.\nIn particular, if $k_1=k_2=:k$ then the superposition is the single plane wave\nwith amplitude $a_1e_1+a_2e_2$, and\n$\\langle u\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}|a_1e_1+a_2e_2|^2$,\n$\\langle S\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0c}{2}|a_1e_1+a_2e_2|^2\\hat k$\n([[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]]).\n\n(d) [No incoherent or statistical claim] The formulas are deterministic\nidentities for the specified coherent superposition; they are not an ensemble\naverage over phases, and no detection statistics, finite-sample visibility\nestimate or empirical conclusion is drawn. The computation is a conditional\nconsequence of the adopted Maxwell postulate and does not by itself distinguish\nclassical from quantum descriptions of light.",
          "strategy": "Clause (a): $E_j,B_j$ are source-free smooth solutions of the vacuum Maxwell postulate by [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]](a); the superposition is again such a solution by [[pthm-em-linear-superposition]] (real coefficients). The single-phase rewriting uses $e^{i\\theta_2}=e^{i\\delta(x)}e^{i\\theta_1}$ with $\\theta_j=k_j\\cdot x-\\omega t$ and $\\delta=(k_2-k_1)\\cdot x$. Clauses (b),(c): at each fixed $x$ write $E=\\operatorname{Re}(A e^{-i\\omega t})$, $B=\\operatorname{Re}(D e^{-i\\omega t})$, with $A=\\alpha(x)e^{ik_1\\cdot x}$ and $D=\\beta(x)e^{ik_1\\cdot x}$; the common spatial phase cancels in averages and apply the componentwise averages of [[lem-em-period-average]], $\\langle\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha e^{-i\\omega t})\\operatorname{Re}(\\beta e^{-i\\omega t})\\rangle=\\frac12\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha\\overline{\\beta})$ and the cross-product version; substituting into $u$ and $S$ of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]] and using $|k_j|=\\omega/c$, $b_j=k_j\\times e_j/\\omega$ together with the identity $(k\\times u)\\cdot(k\\times v)=|k|^2u\\cdot v$ for $u,v\\perp k$ (from [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]) gives the displayed cross term with $W$: expanding $|\\alpha|^2$ gives $a_1^2|e_1|^2+a_2^2|e_2|^2+2a_1a_2\\operatorname{Re}(e_1\\cdot\\overline e_2e^{-i\\delta})$, and similarly for $\\beta$. For $k_1=k_2$ the bracket reduces to $2e_1\\cdot\\overline e_2$ and the formulas reduce to the single-wave averages of [[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]] for the amplitude $a_1e_1+a_2e_2$. No ensemble or statistical averaging is performed, which is exactly the content of the scope clause (d).",
          "physical_scope": "Deterministic superposition identities for two same-frequency ideal plane-wave solutions of the smooth source-free vacuum Maxwell branch in a named inertial chart with SI constants: the averaged energy density of the coherent sum with its interference cross term, the averaged Poynting vector, and the equal-wavevector specialization. Excluded: ensembles or random phases, partially coherent and finite sources, detection or sampling statistics, any empirical conclusion, and any claim discriminating classical from quantum descriptions.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§4.3.1 p. 86 (the principle of superposition for linear Maxwell theory: any sum of solutions of the form (4.15)-(4.16) is again a solution, and waves travelling in different directions pass through one another)"
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§9.2.2 printed pp. 376-379 (linearity of the wave equation and of Maxwell's equations in vacuum; the polarization vector and monochromatic amplitudes on which the superposition is written)"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
