            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a nonempty open set, let $\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_m\\in\\mathbb C^n$ ($m\\ge1$) be pairwise distinct, and let $c_1,\\dots,c_m\\in\\mathbb C^q$ for some $q\\ge1$ (the scalar case $q=1$ included; all statements below are componentwise). Write $\\lambda_j\\cdot z$ for the componentwise bilinear extension of the Euclidean inner product of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]; the variable $z$ remains real and $e^{iw}$ for the complex exponential of [[def-complex-exponential]] at $iw$, and consider the $\\mathbb C^q$-valued map $F(z):=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\,e^{i\\lambda_j\\cdot z}$ on $U$. Then: (a) [Complex independence] if $F(z)=0$ for every $z\\in U$, then $c_1=\\dots=c_m=0$. (b) [Real-part grouping rule] if $\\operatorname{Re}F(z)=0$ for every $z\\in U$, then for every $\\nu\\in\\mathbb C^n$ the grouped coefficient $$C_\\nu:=\\sum_{\\lambda_j=\\nu} c_j+\\sum_{\\lambda_j=-\\overline\\nu}\\overline{c_j}$$ vanishes; in particular $c_j=0$ whenever $\\lambda_j\\ne-\\overline{\\lambda_j}$ and $\\lambda_j\\notin\\{\\lambda_k,-\\overline{\\lambda_k}:k\\ne j\\}$. At a self-paired frequency $\\nu=-\\overline\\nu$ the coefficient is $2\\operatorname{Re}\\sum_{\\lambda_j=\\nu}c_j$; in particular at $\\nu=0$ only the real part is constrained, and a purely imaginary coefficient is allowed. (c) [Scope] no hypothesis beyond pairwise distinctness of the $\\lambda_j$ and nonemptiness and openness of $U$ is used; $\\lambda_j=0$ is allowed and the $\\lambda_j$ need not be nonzero, ordered or normalized. The statement concerns the open set $U$ and asserts nothing on sets with empty interior, where distinct characters can agree.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct; reduce to the scalar case, conjugate the exponential by its series, prove (a) by induction on the dimension using the one-dimensional annihilating operator $\\prod_{j\\ne j_0}(d/dt-i\\lambda_j)$, then obtain (b) by applying (a) to $F+\\overline F$ grouped by frequency. 1.1 Reduction to the scalar case. Both assertions are componentwise in $\\mathbb C^q$: $F(z)=0$ holds if and only if every component $F_k(z)=\\sum_j(c_j)_k e^{i\\lambda_j\\cdot z}$ vanishes, and $\\operatorname{Re}F(z)=0$ if and only if $\\operatorname{Re}F_k(z)=0$ for every $k$, while the vector $C_\\nu$ of clause (b) vanishes if and only if each of its components $\\sum_{\\lambda_j=\\nu}(c_j)_k+\\sum_{\\lambda_j=-\\overline\\nu}\\overline{(c_j)_k}$ vanishes. Applying the scalar assertions $q=1$ to each component function therefore yields the general assertions, so we may and do assume $c_1,\\dots,c_m\\in\\mathbb C$ in the rest of the proof. [given, F3, algebra] 1.2 Conjugation of the exponential. For every $w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\overline{\\exp w}=\\exp\\overline w$. By [F1] the complex numbers $\\exp w$ and $\\exp\\overline w$ are the sums of the everywhere convergent series $\\sum_nw^n/n!$ and $\\sum_n\\overline w^n/n!$, with partial sums $S_N(w)=\\sum_{n\\le N}w^n/n!$ and $S_N(\\overline w)=\\sum_{n\\le N}\\overline w^n/n!$; by [F2] conjugation fixes each real number $n!$ and satisfies $\\overline{zw}=\\overline z\\,\\overline w$ and $\\overline{z+w}=\\overline z+\\overline w$, so $\\overline{S_N(w)}=S_N(\\overline w)$ for every $N$. Also by [F2], $\\overline u-\\overline v=\\overline{u-v}$ and, by the coordinate formula, $|\\overline u|=\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{Re}u)^2+(-\\operatorname{Im}u)^2}=|u|$, whence $|\\overline u-\\overline v|=|u-v|$. Writing $d=\\overline{\\exp w}-\\exp\\overline w$, for every $N$ we have $d=(\\overline{\\exp w}-S_N(\\overline w))+(S_N(\\overline w)-\\exp\\overline w)=(\\overline{\\exp w}-\\overline{S_N(w)})+(S_N(\\overline w)-\\exp\\overline w)$, so $|d|\\le|\\exp w-S_N(w)|+|S_N(\\overline w)-\\exp\\overline w|$; given $\\varepsilon>0$, the definition of convergence of the two series supplies $N$ with both summands below $\\varepsilon/2$, so $|d|<\\varepsilon$, and since $\\varepsilon>0$ was arbitrary $|d|=0$, which forces $d=0$ by the definiteness of the modulus in [F2]. [F1, F2, algebra] 1.3 Characters and the annihilating operator. For $\\mu\\in\\mathbb C$ let $\\varphi_\\mu(t):=\\exp(i\\mu t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$, the one-variable restriction of the complex exponential along the real-linear map $t\\mapsto i\\mu t$. By [F5], the chain rule applied to that map and the entire exponential of [F1] gives $\\varphi_\\mu'(t)=i\\mu\\exp(i\\mu t)=i\\mu\\,\\varphi_\\mu(t)$, and the sum, scalar-multiple and product rules of [F5] give, for any finite combination $\\sum_\\mu a_\\mu\\varphi_\\mu$ with complex coefficients, derivative $\\sum_\\mu a_\\mu i\\mu\\,\\varphi_\\mu$, hence $(d/dt-i\\mu)\\varphi_\\mu=0$ and $(d/dt-i\\mu)\\varphi_\\nu=i(\\nu-\\mu)\\varphi_\\nu$ for all $\\nu\\in\\mathbb C$. Consequently, for pairwise distinct $\\mu_1,\\dots,\\mu_m$ and a fixed $j_0$, the composed operator $L_{j_0}:=\\prod_{j\\ne j_0}(d/dt-i\\mu_j)$ is complex-linear on finite combinations and acts on $\\varphi_{\\mu_k}$ as multiplication by $0$ if $k\\ne j_0$ and by the constant $\\prod_{j\\ne j_0}i(\\mu_{j_0}-\\mu_j)$ if $k=j_0$; that constant is nonzero because $i\\ne0$ and $\\mu_{j_0}-\\mu_j\\ne0$ for $j\\ne j_0$ by pairwise distinctness, and a product of nonzero field elements is nonzero by [F3]. [F1, F3, F5, algebra] 1.4 Induction step: reduction to a product box. Let $n\\ge2$ and assume assertion (a) established in dimension $n-1$ for every number of frequencies and every nonempty open subset of $\\mathbb R^{n-1}$. Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty and open, fix $z^*\\in U$ and, by openness, $\\varepsilon>0$ with the open product box $B:=\\prod_{k=1}^n(z^*_k-\\varepsilon,z^*_k+\\varepsilon)\\subseteq U$; put $B':=\\prod_{k=2}^n(z^*_k-\\varepsilon,z^*_k+\\varepsilon)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n-1}$. Write $z=(z_1,z')$ with $z'\\in\\mathbb R^{n-1}$ and $\\lambda_j=(\\lambda_{j1},\\lambda_j')$. For each value $\\mu$ in the finite set $\\{\\lambda_{11},\\dots,\\lambda_{m1}\\}$ put $I_\\mu:=\\{j:\\lambda_{j1}=\\mu\\}$ and $P_\\mu(z'):=\\sum_{j\\in I_\\mu}c_je^{i\\lambda_j'\\cdot z'}$; then, by the splitting identity of [F4], the exponential addition law of [F1] and the distributivity of the finite sum, $F(z_1,z')=\\sum_\\mu e^{i\\mu z_1}P_\\mu(z')$ for every $(z_1,z')\\in B$, the outer sum running over the pairwise distinct values $\\mu$. [given, F1, F4, algebra] 2.1 Base case, dimension one. Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be nonempty and open, so that it contains a nonempty open interval $J$; suppose $F=\\sum_{j=1}^mc_j\\varphi_{\\lambda_j}\\equiv0$ on $U$. Fix $j_0$; then $L_{j_0}F\\equiv0$ on $J$, and by step 1.3 and the linearity of $L_{j_0}$ we have $L_{j_0}F=c_{j_0}\\bigl(\\prod_{j\\ne j_0}i(\\lambda_{j_0}-\\lambda_j)\\bigr)\\varphi_{\\lambda_{j_0}}$ on $J$. Choose $t_0\\in J$. Evaluating there gives $c_{j_0}\\bigl(\\prod_{j\\ne j_0}i(\\lambda_{j_0}-\\lambda_j)\\bigr)\\exp(i\\lambda_{j_0}t_0)=0$; cancelling the nonzero exponential by [F1] gives $c_{j_0}\\prod_{j\\ne j_0}i(\\lambda_{j_0}-\\lambda_j)=0$; the second factor is a product of nonzero complex numbers, hence nonzero by [F3], so $c_{j_0}=0$ by the field property in [F3]. As $j_0$ was arbitrary, $c_1=\\dots=c_m=0$; this proves (a) for $n=1$. [given, step 1.3, F1, F3] 2.2 The conjugate sum. Suppose now that $\\operatorname{Re}F\\equiv0$ on $U$, and define $\\widetilde F(z):=\\sum_{j=1}^m\\overline{c_j}\\,e^{-i\\overline{\\lambda_j}\\cdot z}$ on $U$. For real $z$ and every $j$, step 1.2 applied to $w=i\\lambda_j\\cdot z$ gives $\\overline{e^{i\\lambda_j\\cdot z}}=\\exp\\bigl(\\overline{i\\lambda_j\\cdot z}\\bigr)$, and by [F2] and the reality of $z$ we have $\\overline{i\\lambda_j\\cdot z}=\\overline i\\,\\overline{\\lambda_j\\cdot z}=(-i)\\bigl(\\overline{\\lambda_j}\\cdot z\\bigr)=-i\\overline{\\lambda_j}\\cdot z$; therefore $\\overline{F(z)}=\\sum_j\\overline{c_j}\\,e^{-i\\overline{\\lambda_j}\\cdot z}=\\widetilde F(z)$ on $U$ by [F2]. Since $w+\\overline w=2\\operatorname{Re}w$ by [F2], the hypothesis $2\\operatorname{Re}F\\equiv0$ reads $F+\\widetilde F\\equiv0$ on $U$. [given, step 1.2, F2] 3.1 One-dimensional slices. Suppose $F\\equiv0$ on $U$, fix $z'\\in B'$ and define $G(t):=\\sum_\\mu e^{i\\mu t}P_\\mu(z')$ for $t$ in the interval $J:=(z^*_1-\\varepsilon,z^*_1+\\varepsilon)$. By step 1.4, $G(t)=F(t,z')=0$ for every $t\\in J$, and $G$ is a finite combination of the pairwise distinct characters $\\varphi_\\mu$ with complex coefficients $P_\\mu(z')$; the base case of step 2.1 applied on the nonempty open interval $J$ therefore gives $P_\\mu(z')=0$ for every $\\mu$. As $z'\\in B'$ was arbitrary, each $P_\\mu$ vanishes identically on the nonempty open set $B'$. [step 2.1, step 1.4] 4.1 Induction hypothesis on each group. Fix a value $\\mu$. The vectors $\\lambda_j'$, $j\\in I_\\mu$, are pairwise distinct: if $\\lambda_j'=\\lambda_k'$ and $\\lambda_{j1}=\\lambda_{k1}=\\mu$, then $\\lambda_j=\\lambda_k$, hence $j=k$ by pairwise distinctness of the $\\lambda_j$. The function $P_\\mu=\\sum_{j\\in I_\\mu}c_je^{i\\lambda_j'\\cdot z'}$ vanishes identically on the nonempty open set $B'\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n-1}$, so assertion (a) in dimension $n-1$ gives $c_j=0$ for every $j\\in I_\\mu$. Every index lies in $I_{\\lambda_{j1}}$, so $c_1=\\dots=c_m=0$; this completes the induction and proves (a) in every dimension $n\\ge1$. [step 3.1, step 1.4, induction] 5.1 Frequency grouping and application of part (a). Let $\\Phi\\subseteq\\mathbb C^n$ be the finite set of distinct values among $\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_m,-\\overline{\\lambda_1},\\dots,-\\overline{\\lambda_m}$, and for $\\nu\\in\\Phi$ put $C_\\nu:=\\sum_{j:\\lambda_j=\\nu}c_j+\\sum_{j:-\\overline{\\lambda_j}=\\nu}\\overline{c_j}=\\sum_{\\lambda_j=\\nu}c_j+\\sum_{\\lambda_j=-\\overline\\nu}\\overline{c_j}$. Regrouping the finite sum of step 2.2 over the distinct frequencies and using the bilinearity of the extended inner product in [F4] gives $F+\\widetilde F=\\sum_{\\nu\\in\\Phi}C_\\nu e^{i\\nu\\cdot z}$ on $U$; the frequencies $\\nu\\in\\Phi$ are pairwise distinct by construction, so assertion (a) as proved in steps 2.1 and 4.1 applied to this finite combination gives $C_\\nu=0$ for every $\\nu\\in\\Phi$. For $\\nu\\notin\\Phi$ both defining sums are empty, so $C_\\nu=0$ trivially; hence $C_\\nu=0$ for every $\\nu\\in\\mathbb C^n$. [given, step 2.2, step 4.1, F4, algebra] 6.1 Consequences and scope. (i) If $\\nu=-\\overline\\nu$ is self-paired, the condition $-\\overline{\\lambda_j}=\\nu$ is equivalent to $\\lambda_j=\\nu$, so both sums of $C_\\nu$ run over the same index set and $C_\\nu=\\sum_{\\lambda_j=\\nu}(c_j+\\overline{c_j})=2\\operatorname{Re}\\sum_{\\lambda_j=\\nu}c_j$ by [F2]; at $\\nu=0$ this constrains only the real part, and the constraint is genuinely not stronger: for $m=1$, $\\lambda_1=0$, $q=1$ and $c_1=i$ one has $F\\equiv i$, so $\\operatorname{Re}F\\equiv0$ and $C_0=c_1+\\overline{c_1}=2\\operatorname{Re}i=0$, a nonzero purely imaginary coefficient at frequency $0$. (ii) If $\\lambda_j\\ne-\\overline{\\lambda_j}$ and $\\lambda_j\\notin\\{\\lambda_k,-\\overline{\\lambda_k}:k\\ne j\\}$, then $\\{k:\\lambda_k=\\lambda_j\\}=\\{j\\}$ and $\\{k:\\lambda_k=-\\overline{\\lambda_j}\\}=\\emptyset$ (a member $k=j$ would give $\\lambda_j=-\\overline{\\lambda_j}$, and $k\\ne j$ is excluded by hypothesis), so $C_{\\lambda_j}=c_j+0=c_j=0$ by step 5.1, which is the stated consequence. (iii) The scope (c) holds: steps 2.1, 1.4 and 4.1 used only pairwise distinctness of the $\\lambda_j$ and the nonemptiness and openness of $U$, through the interval $J$ and the product box $B$; the frequencies were never assumed nonzero, ordered or normalized; the proof is a finite construction using complex arithmetic, the series for the exponential and its derivative, and no choice principle. No assertion is made for sets of empty interior: the openness of $U$ is used when a nonempty open interval and a product box are extracted from it, and the statement (c) records that distinct characters can agree on such smaller sets. [step 5.1, step 2.2, F1, F2, F3, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Physicspages, 'Linearly independent exponentials' (post of 26 December 2024, 2 pp.)",
