                "locator": "§7.5.2 printed pp. 194-198 (frequency-dependent response, speed and index in a medium; the constant-coefficient scalar case) and §7.6.2 printed pp. 207-209: $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\varepsilon+i\\sigma/\\omega$, the dispersion relation $k\\cdot k=\\mu\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\omega^{2}$, the root selection and the skin depth $\\delta=\\sqrt{2/(\\mu\\omega\\sigma)}$ in the low-frequency regime; the sign convention is $e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}$."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§9.3.1 printed pp. 382-383: $v=1/\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\mu}$, the index $n=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\mu/(\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0)}$ and the transcription of the vacuum plane-wave results to a linear medium; §9.4.1 printed pp. 394-395: the complex wave number $k^{2}=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^{2}+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega$ and the skin depth $d=1/\\kappa$ defined as the $1/e$ amplitude-decay distance. The scan is a retained 596-page third-edition copy with OCR noise; the displayed formulas were cross-checked against Tong and against the local computations recorded in the batch notes."
              }
            ]
