            "def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the elementary inertial-rest branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] in its isotropic scalar case at rest in the laboratory frame, with constants $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, the constitutive relations $D=\\varepsilon E$, $B=\\mu H$ of [[def-em-polarization-magnetization]], the macroscopic equations of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]], the fields of [[def-em-fields]] on the laboratory region of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and the parameters and phase convention of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]]. Consider a declared region with vanishing free charge density and no external free current, so that $\\rho_f=0$ and the free current is the conduction current $J_f=\\sigma E$. Let $k\\in\\mathbb C^{3}$, $\\omega>0$ and $e,h\\in\\mathbb C^{3}$, and for $(x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R$ define $E(x,t):=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e\\,e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$ and $H(x,t):=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(h\\,e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$ componentwise, i.e. with the phase convention $e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}$ and the real-part encoding of [[def-em-complex-plane-wave]]; put $D:=\\varepsilon E$, $B:=\\mu H$ and $J_f:=\\sigma E$. Extend the Euclidean inner and cross products to $\\mathbb C^{3}$ by the same componentwise polynomial formulas ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]). For a complex vector $z\\in\\mathbb C^3$, use the Hermitian norm $|z|:=\\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^3|z_j|^2}$, so $|z|^2=z\\cdot\\overline z=|\\operatorname{Re}z|^2+|\\operatorname{Im}z|^2$; the dot product itself remains complex bilinear. Then: (a) [Amplitude vanishing] if $\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(w\\,e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}\\bigr)=0$ for some $w\\in\\mathbb C^{3}$ and all $(x,t)$ in a nonempty open subset of $\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R$, then $w=0$. (b) [Amplitude conditions] the quadruple $(E,H,D,B)$ satisfies the four source-free macroscopic equations $\\operatorname{div}D=0$, $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$, $\\operatorname{curl}H=J_f+\\partial_tD$ on $\\mathbb R^{3}\\times\\mathbb R$ if and only if $$k\\cdot e=0,\\qquad k\\cdot h=0,\\qquad k\\times e=\\omega\\mu\\,h,\\qquad k\\times h=-(\\omega\\varepsilon+i\\sigma)\\,e .$$ (c) [Structure, dispersion and solution family] if $e\\ne0$ and (b) holds, then $h=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)$, $k\\cdot h=0$, $e\\cdot h=0$ and $k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^{2}+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega$; conversely, if $k\\in\\mathbb C^{3}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and $\\omega>0$ satisfy $k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^{2}+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega$, then for every $e\\in\\mathbb C^{3}$ with $k\\cdot e=0$ the pair $(e,\\,k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu))$ satisfies (b), with $(e,h)=(0,0)$ if and only if $e=0$; on the dispersion relation the solution set of (b) for fixed $(k,\\omega)$ is exactly $\\{(e,\\,k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)):k\\cdot e=0\\}$, a complex two-dimensional space when $k\\ne0$; off the dispersion relation the only solution is $e=h=0$. (d) [Lossless propagating waves: instantaneous identities and period averages] if (b) holds and $\\sigma=0$ and $k\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ with $e\\ne0$, then $|k|^{2}=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^{2}$, $|k|=k(\\omega)$ in the notation of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]], and for every $(x,t)$ $k\\cdot E=k\\cdot B=0$, $E\\cdot B=0$, $B=\\hat k\\times E/v$ and $|B|=|E|/v$ with $\\hat k=k/|k|$; the period-$T$ averages with $T=2\\pi/\\omega$ ([[lem-em-period-average]]) satisfy, for $u=\\tfrac12(E\\cdot D+B\\cdot H)$ and $S=E\\times H$ of [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]], $$\\langle u\\rangle=\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}|e|^{2},\\qquad \\langle S\\rangle=\\frac{|e|^{2}}{2\\mu\\omega}\\,k=\\frac{|e|^{2}}{2Z}\\,\\hat k=v\\,\\langle u\\rangle\\hat k .$$ (e) [Evanescent waves] if $\\sigma=0$, $n'\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ is a unit vector, $k=\\kappa_0+i\\beta n'$ with $\\kappa_0\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$, $\\kappa_0\\cdot n'=0$, $\\beta>0$ and $k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^{2}$ (so $|\\kappa_0|^{2}=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^{2}+\\beta^{2}$ and $\\kappa_0\\ne0$; define $\\hat\\kappa_0:=\\kappa_0/|\\kappa_0|$), and if $e\\ne0$ satisfies $k\\cdot e=0$ with $h:=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)$, then the field factorizes exactly as $E(x,t)=e^{-\\beta\\,n'\\cdot x}\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e\\,e^{i(\\kappa_0\\cdot x-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$, and the time-averaged flux satisfies $\\langle S\\rangle=e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}\\,\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Re}(e\\times\\overline h)$ with normal component $\\langle S\\cdot n'\\rangle=-\\dfrac{e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}}{2\\mu\\omega}\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl[(e\\cdot n')(\\kappa_0\\cdot\\overline e)\\bigr]$; this normal component is in fact zero for every such transverse amplitude. For the transverse-electric polarisation $e=E_0(n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0)$, $E_0\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and for the transverse-magnetic data $h=H_0\\hat y'$ with $\\hat y':=n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0$, $H_0\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and electric amplitude $e=-(k\\times\\hat y')H_0/(\\omega\\varepsilon)$, the normal component vanishes identically, $\\langle S\\cdot n'\\rangle\\equiv0$, while the tangential averaged flux is positive: $\\langle S\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0\\rangle=\\dfrac{|\\kappa_0||e|^{2}}{2\\mu\\omega}e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}>0$ in the transverse-electric case and $\\langle S\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0\\rangle=\\dfrac{|\\kappa_0||h|^{2}}{2\\omega\\varepsilon}e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}>0$ in the transverse-magnetic case. (f) [Scope] the four equations are verified only for the explicit ideal whole-space plane-wave fields; for $k$ with $\\operatorname{Im}k\\ne0$ the factor $e^{-\\operatorname{Im}k\\cdot x}$ grows in some directions, so clauses (b)-(e) are read on regions where the model declares the field bounded (in (e), on the half-space $n'\\cdot x>0$). No completeness, energy-finiteness, boundary, interface, material-validity or empirical claim is made, and the finite-band caveat of [[post-em-material-closure]](b) applies whenever $\\varepsilon\\mu<c^{-2}$.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct; exhibit the partial derivatives of the encoded fields, reduce each equation to a complex coefficient identity by the amplitude-vanishing fact, analyse the resulting algebraic system, and compute the lossless and evanescent structures with the period-average supplier. 1.1 [Derivatives of the encoded fields] For $w\\in\\mathbb C^3$ and $\\theta(x,t)=k\\cdot x-\\omega t$, each component $x\\mapsto\\operatorname{Re}(w_j\\exp(i\\theta(x,t)))$ has partial derivatives $\\partial_{x_l}\\operatorname{Re}(w_j\\exp(i\\theta))=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(i\\,k_l\\,w_j\\exp(i\\theta)\\bigr)$ and $\\partial_t\\operatorname{Re}(w_j\\exp(i\\theta))=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(-i\\omega\\,w_j\\exp(i\\theta)\\bigr)$: indeed $\\theta$ is the real-linear map $(x,t)\\mapsto\\sum_lk_lx_l-\\omega t$ with partial derivatives $k_l$ in $x_l$ and $-\\omega$ in $t$ as derivatives of line maps by [F4], and the chain rule [F2] applied to the entire exponential gives $\\partial_{x_l}\\exp(i\\theta)=ik_l\\exp(i\\theta)$ and $\\partial_t\\exp(i\\theta)=-i\\omega\\exp(i\\theta)$, after which the real-linear coordinate projection $\\operatorname{Re}$ commutes with taking the derivative by [F1]. The complex multipliers stay inside $\\operatorname{Re}$. Consequently $\\operatorname{div}D=\\varepsilon\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\cdot e\\,e^{i\\theta})$, $\\operatorname{div}B=\\mu\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\cdot h\\,e^{i\\theta})$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\times e\\,e^{i\\theta})$, $\\partial_tB=\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega\\mu\\,h\\,e^{i\\theta})$, $\\operatorname{curl}H=\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\times h\\,e^{i\\theta})$ and $J_f+\\partial_tD=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((\\sigma-i\\omega\\varepsilon)e\\,e^{i\\theta}\\bigr)$ with the componentwise complex inner and cross products of [F5] and the uniform notation $e^{i\\theta}:=\\exp(i\\theta)$. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, algebra] 1.2 [Nonvanishing of the exponential] For every $w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp w\\ne0$: by [F2], $\\exp w\\exp(-w)=\\exp 0=1$, and a product equal to $1\\ne0$ has no zero factor in the field $\\mathbb C$ by [F3]. In particular $e^{i\\theta}\\ne0$ for all $(x,t)$, and $e^{ik\\cdot x_0}\\ne0$ for every fixed $x_0$. [F2, F3] 2.1 [Amplitude vanishing, part (a)] Let $Z\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ be nonempty and open with $\\operatorname{Re}(w\\,e^{i\\theta})=0$ on $Z$ for a fixed $w\\in\\mathbb C^3$. Fix $(x_0,t_0)\\in Z$; by openness of $Z$ there is $\\delta>0$ with $\\{x_0\\}\\times(t_0-\\delta,t_0+\\delta)\\subseteq Z$. The components of the map $t\\mapsto\\operatorname{Re}(W\\exp(-i\\omega t))$ with $W:=w\\,e^{ik\\cdot x_0}$ vanish on that interval, and by step 1.1 its $t$-derivative is $\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega W\\exp(-i\\omega t))$, so at $t_0$ both $\\operatorname{Re}(W\\exp(-i\\omega t_0))=0$ and $\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega W\\exp(-i\\omega t_0))=0$. Writing $W\\exp(-i\\omega t_0)=u+iv$ with real coordinate vectors $u,v$ by [F3], the first identity is $u=0$ and the second is $\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega(u+iv))=\\omega v=0$; since $\\omega>0$, $v=0$; hence $W\\exp(-i\\omega t_0)=0$, and since $\\exp(-i\\omega t_0)\\ne0$ by step 1.2 the field property in [F3] gives $W=0$, i.e. $w\\,e^{ik\\cdot x_0}=0$, and $\\exp(ik\\cdot x_0)\\ne0$ by step 1.2 gives $w=0$. This proves (a). [given, step 1.1, step 1.2, F2, F3] 3.1 [Equations as coefficient identities] By step 1.1 the four equations of (b) are, on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, the identities $\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\cdot e\\,e^{i\\theta})=0$, $\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\cdot h\\,e^{i\\theta})=0$, $\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\times e\\,e^{i\\theta})+\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega\\mu\\,h\\,e^{i\\theta})=0$ and $\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\times h\\,e^{i\\theta})-\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((\\sigma-i\\omega\\varepsilon)e\\,e^{i\\theta}\\bigr)=0$; all fields are smooth on the open set $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, so the operators of [F4] apply. By the additivity of $\\operatorname{Re}$ in [F1] and [F3], each of these is an identity $\\operatorname{Re}(W_j\\,e^{i\\theta})=0$ for a constant scalar or vector $W_j$ (embedding a scalar as $(W_j,0,0)$ to apply (a)); by (a) of step 2.1, with the open set $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, they hold if and only if the constants vanish. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F1, F3, F4] 4.1 [Proof of (b)] By step 3.1, the first and second equations hold if and only if $k\\cdot e=0$ and $k\\cdot h=0$, since multiplication by the nonzero field element $i$ is injective by [F3]. The third holds if and only if $i\\,k\\times e-i\\omega\\mu\\,h=0$, that is $k\\times e=\\omega\\mu\\,h$ after dividing by the nonzero factor $i$ and using $\\omega\\mu\\ne0$. The fourth holds if and only if $i\\,k\\times h-(\\sigma-i\\omega\\varepsilon)e=0$, because $\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega\\mu h e^{i\\theta})=-\\operatorname{Re}(i\\omega\\mu h e^{i\\theta})$ by [F3]; dividing by $i$ and using $(\\sigma-i\\omega\\varepsilon)/i=-(\\omega\\varepsilon+i\\sigma)$ — indeed $(\\sigma-i\\omega\\varepsilon)=i\\cdot\\bigl(-(\\omega\\varepsilon+i\\sigma)\\bigr)$ by the field arithmetic of [F3] — gives $k\\times h=-(\\omega\\varepsilon+i\\sigma)e$. Hence the quadruple satisfies the four equations if and only if the four displayed amplitude conditions hold, which is (b). [step 3.1, F3, algebra] 5.1 [Structure and dispersion, forward in (c)] Assume $e\\ne0$ and the four conditions of (b). Since $\\omega\\mu\\ne0$, the third condition gives $h=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)$; then $k\\cdot h=k\\cdot(k\\times e)/(\\omega\\mu)=0$ and $e\\cdot h=e\\cdot(k\\times e)/(\\omega\\mu)=0$ by the orthogonality in [F5]. Crossing the third condition with $k$ and substituting the fourth, $k\\times(k\\times e)=\\omega\\mu\\,k\\times h=-\\omega\\mu(\\omega\\varepsilon+i\\sigma)e$; the vector triple-product identity $k\\times(k\\times e)=k(k\\cdot e)-(k\\cdot k)e$, a componentwise polynomial identity for the bilinear extension by [F5], together with $k\\cdot e=0$, gives $-(k\\cdot k)e=-\\omega\\mu(\\omega\\varepsilon+i\\sigma)e=-(\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega)e$, and choosing a nonzero coordinate of $e$ and cancelling that complex scalar gives $k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega$. [step 4.1, F3, F5, algebra] 6.1 [Converse and solution family in (c)] Let $k\\ne0$, $\\omega>0$ satisfy $k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega$, and let $e\\in\\mathbb C^3$ with $k\\cdot e=0$; put $h:=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)$. Then $k\\cdot e=0$ and $k\\cdot h=0$ by orthogonality in [F5], $k\\times e=\\omega\\mu h$ by definition, and $k\\times h=k\\times(k\\times e)/(\\omega\\mu)=\\bigl[k(k\\cdot e)-(k\\cdot k)e\\bigr]/(\\omega\\mu)=-\\bigl(\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega\\bigr)e/(\\omega\\mu)=-(\\omega\\varepsilon+i\\sigma)e$, so the pair $(e,h)$ satisfies (b) by step 4.1. If $e=0$ then $h=0$; conversely if $h=0$ then $k\\times e=0$, so the two-vector family $(k,e)$ is linearly dependent over the field $\\mathbb C$: writing the vanishing minors of $(k\\ e)$ — if the coordinate $k_l\\ne0$ then $e_j=(e_l/k_l)k_j$ for the two other coordinates $j$ and also for $l$ — gives $e=\\alpha k$ with $\\alpha=e_l/k_l\\in\\mathbb C$ by [F3]; then $0=k\\cdot e=\\alpha(k\\cdot k)$ and $k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega\\ne0$ (its real part is $\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2>0$ by [F3]) force $\\alpha=0$, so $e=0$. Thus $(e,h)=(0,0)$ if and only if $e=0$. For the solution set with $(k,\\omega)$ on the dispersion relation: every solution $(e,h)$ of (b) has $k\\cdot e=0$ and $h=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)$ by step 5.1, so it lies in the displayed set; conversely every element of that set is a solution by the first part of this step, and the correspondence $e\\mapsto(e,k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu))$ is injective, so the solution set is exactly $\\{(e,k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)):k\\cdot e=0\\}$. If $k_l\\ne0$ the map $e\\mapsto(e_j)_{j\\ne l}\\in\\mathbb C^2$ is a bijection from $\\{e:k\\cdot e=0\\}$ onto $\\mathbb C^2$ with inverse $(e_j)_{j\\ne l}\\mapsto e$ where $e_l=-\\bigl(\\sum_{j\\ne l}k_je_j\\bigr)/k_l$, both maps being linear over $\\mathbb C$; hence that solution space is a complex two-dimensional space. Off the dispersion relation, if (b) holds then either $e=0$, which gives $h=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)=0$, or $e\\ne0$, which by step 5.1 forces the dispersion relation; so the only solution is $e=h=0$. [step 4.1, step 5.1, F3, F5, algebra] 6.2 [Lossless pointwise identities in (d)] Assume $\\sigma=0$ and $k\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $e\\ne0$, so the four conditions of (b) hold and (c) applies by steps 4.1 and 5.1. From $k\\cdot k=|k|^2=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2$ and $|k|\\ge0$ we get $|k|=\\omega(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{1/2}$ by [F10], which is $k(\\omega)=\\omega/v$ by [F7]. For every $(x,t)$: $k\\cdot E=k\\cdot\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i\\theta})=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((k\\cdot e)e^{i\\theta}\\bigr)=0$ and $k\\cdot B=\\mu\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((k\\cdot h)e^{i\\theta}\\bigr)=0$ by [F1] and the reality of $k$; further $B=\\mu H=\\mu\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((k\\times e)/(\\omega\\mu)e^{i\\theta}\\bigr)=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((k\\times e)e^{i\\theta}\\bigr)/\\omega=k\\times\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i\\theta})/\\omega=k\\times E/\\omega=(\\hat k\\times E)/v$ because $k$ is real and $|k|/\\omega=1/v$ by [F7]; and $E\\cdot B=E\\cdot(\\hat k\\times E)/v=\\langle E,\\hat k\\times E\\rangle/v=\\det[E,\\hat k,E]/v=0$ by [F5] because a determinant with two equal columns vanishes. [step 4.1, step 5.1, F1, F5, F7, F10, algebra] 7.1 [Evanescent factorisation in (e)] Assume the hypotheses of (e). Since $k=\\kappa_0+i\\beta n'$ with $\\kappa_0,n'\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\kappa_0\\cdot n'=0$, $|n'|=1$, the bilinear extension of [F5] gives $k\\cdot x=\\kappa_0\\cdot x+i\\beta\\,n'\\cdot x$ for real $x$ and $k\\cdot k=|\\kappa_0|^2-\\beta^2$ (the cross terms $2i\\beta\\,\\kappa_0\\cdot n'$ vanish), so the dispersion relation gives $|\\kappa_0|^2=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2+\\beta^2>0$ and hence $\\kappa_0\\ne0$. Writing $\\varphi:=\\kappa_0\\cdot x-\\omega t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $b:=\\beta\\,n'\\cdot x\\in\\mathbb R$, the identity $i\\theta=i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)=-b+i\\varphi$ combines with the addition law and real extension of [F2] to give $e^{i\\theta}=\\exp(-b+i\\varphi)=e^{-b}\\exp(i\\varphi)$; the Cartesian form of [F2] gives $\\exp(i\\varphi)=\\cos\\varphi+i\\sin\\varphi$, and the coordinate formula for the real part of a product in [F1] and [F3] then gives, componentwise, $\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e\\,e^{i\\theta}\\bigr)=e^{-\\beta n'\\cdot x}\\bigl[(\\operatorname{Re}e)\\cos\\varphi-(\\operatorname{Im}e)\\sin\\varphi\\bigr]=e^{-\\beta n'\\cdot x}\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e\\,e^{i\\varphi}\\bigr)$, the last equality being the same coordinate formula read backwards. This is the stated factorisation, and $h=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)$ by the hypothesis of (e); step 6.1 verifies the amplitude conditions. [given, step 6.1, F2, F3, F5, algebra] 7.2 [The cross-product norm identity] For real vectors $u,v\\in\\mathbb R^3$ the component expansion gives $|u\\times v|^2=|u|^2|v|^2-\\langle u,v\\rangle^2$: both sides equal $\\sum_{j\\ne l}u_j^2v_l^2-2\\sum_{j<l}u_ju_lv_jv_l$ by the coordinate formulas of [F5]. Applying this with $u=\\hat k$ and $v=E$, where $|\\hat k|=1$ since $|k|=\\omega(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{1/2}\\ne0$ and $\\langle\\hat k,E\\rangle=(k\\cdot E)/|k|=0$ by step 6.2, gives $|\\hat k\\times E|^2=|E|^2$, hence $|B|=|\\hat k\\times E|/v=|E|/v$ by step 6.2 and the positivity of $v$ in [F7]. Write $e=a+ib$ with real $a,b$. Since $k$ is real and $k\\cdot e=0$, $k\\cdot a=k\\cdot b=0$. Applying the real norm identity separately to $(k,a)$ and $(k,b)$ gives $|k\\times e|^2=|k\\times a|^2+|k\\times b|^2=|k|^2(|a|^2+|b|^2)=|k|^2|e|^2$. Thus $|h|=|k\\times e|/(\\omega\\mu)=|k||e|/(\\omega\\mu)$, so $|h|=\\omega(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{1/2}|e|/(\\omega\\mu)=|e|(\\varepsilon/\\mu)^{1/2}=|e|/Z$ by [F7]. [step 6.2, F5, F7, algebra] 8.1 [Evanescent averaged flux in (e)] By step 7.1 the fields are $E=e^{-b}\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i\\varphi})$ and $H=e^{-b}\\operatorname{Re}(h\\,e^{i\\varphi})$ with $\\varphi=\\kappa_0\\cdot x-\\omega t$, so $E\\times H=e^{-2b}\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{-i\\omega t'})\\times\\operatorname{Re}(h\\,e^{-i\\omega t'})$ evaluated at $t'=t-\\kappa_0\\cdot x/\\omega$, and the period average in $t$ equals that in $t'$; the cross-product form of [F9] applied with $a=e$, $d=h$ gives $\\langle S\\rangle=e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Re}(e\\times\\overline h)$. Since $\\overline h=\\overline{k\\times e}/(\\omega\\mu)$ and $\\overline{k\\times e}=\\overline{k}\\times\\overline e=(\\kappa_0-i\\beta n')\\times\\overline e$ by [F3], the triple-product expansion of [F5] gives $e\\times\\overline h=\\bigl[\\overline k(e\\cdot\\overline e)-\\overline e\\,(e\\cdot\\overline k)\\bigr]/(\\omega\\mu)$, whose inner product with $n'$ is $\\bigl[(\\overline k\\cdot n')|e|^2-(\\overline e\\cdot n')(e\\cdot\\overline k)\\bigr]/(\\omega\\mu)$; here $\\overline k\\cdot n'=-i\\beta$ and $e\\cdot\\overline k=\\kappa_0\\cdot e-i\\beta\\,(e\\cdot n')$ by the bilinear expansion of [F5]. Hence $\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl[-\\overline{(e\\cdot n')}(e\\cdot\\overline k)\\bigr]=-\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl[\\overline{(e\\cdot n')}(\\kappa_0\\cdot e)\\bigr]-\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl[i\\beta|e\\cdot n'|^{2}\\bigr]=-\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl[\\overline{(e\\cdot n')}\\cdot\\overline{(\\kappa_0\\cdot\\overline e)}\\bigr]=-\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl[(e\\cdot n')(\\kappa_0\\cdot\\overline e)\\bigr]$, where the middle term vanishes as a purely imaginary number and the last identity uses $\\overline{zw}=\\overline z\\,\\overline w$ and $\\operatorname{Re}\\overline\\zeta=\\operatorname{Re}\\zeta$ from [F3]; substituting into the previous display gives the stated normal component. [step 7.1, F3, F5, F9, algebra] 8.2 [Lossless averages in (d)] With $T=2\\pi/\\omega$, step 6.2 gives $E\\cdot E=\\operatorname{Re}(e_je^{i\\theta})\\operatorname{Re}(e_je^{i\\theta})$ summed over $j$, and its period average is $\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Re}(e\\cdot\\overline e)=\\tfrac12|e|^2$ by the vector form of [F9] applied with $a=d=e$ (the phase $e^{ik\\cdot x}$ is constant in $t$ and cancels in the product $e^{ik\\cdot x}e^{-ik\\cdot x}=1$ by [F2]); similarly $\\langle H\\cdot H\\rangle=\\tfrac12|h|^2=\\tfrac12|e|^2/Z^2$ by step 7.2. Hence $\\langle u\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\varepsilon\\langle E\\cdot E\\rangle+\\mu\\langle H\\cdot H\\rangle\\bigr)=\\tfrac14\\bigl(\\varepsilon|e|^2+\\mu|e|^2/Z^2\\bigr)=\\tfrac14\\bigl(\\varepsilon|e|^2+\\varepsilon|e|^2\\bigr)=\\tfrac{\\varepsilon}{2}|e|^2$ because $u=\\tfrac12(E\\cdot D+B\\cdot H)$ is the stored-energy quantity of [F8] and $\\mu/Z^2=\\varepsilon$ by [F7] and [F3]. Similarly $\\langle S\\rangle=\\langle E\\times H\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Re}(e\\times\\overline h)$ by the flux definition $S=E\\times H$ of [F8] and the cross-product form of [F9] with $a=e$, $d=h$; substituting $\\overline h=\\overline{k\\times e}/(\\omega\\mu)=(k\\times\\overline e)/(\\omega\\mu)$ for real $k$ by [F3] and using the triple-product expansion $e\\times(k\\times\\overline e)=k(e\\cdot\\overline e)-\\overline e(e\\cdot k)=k|e|^2$ by [F5] and $k\\cdot e=0$ gives $\\langle S\\rangle=|e|^2k/(2\\mu\\omega)=|e|^2\\hat k/(2Z)$ because $k/(\\mu\\omega)=\\hat k|k|/(\\mu\\omega)=\\hat k/(\\mu v)=\\hat k/Z$ by [F7]; and $v\\langle u\\rangle\\hat k=v\\varepsilon|e|^2\\hat k/2=|e|^2\\hat k/(2Z)$ because $v\\varepsilon=1/Z$ by [F7], so the three displayed expressions agree. [step 6.2, step 7.2, F2, F3, F5, F7, F8, F9, algebra] 9.1 [Vanishing on transverse amplitudes] Under the hypotheses of (e) with $k\\cdot e=0$, the bilinear expansion of [F5] gives $0=k\\cdot e=\\kappa_0\\cdot e+i\\beta\\,(n'\\cdot e)$, so $\\kappa_0\\cdot\\overline e=\\overline{\\kappa_0\\cdot e}=\\overline{-i\\beta\\,(n'\\cdot e)}=i\\beta\\,\\overline{(n'\\cdot e)}=i\\beta\\,(n'\\cdot\\overline e)$ by [F3] and the reality of $\\kappa_0$, and hence $(e\\cdot n')(\\kappa_0\\cdot\\overline e)=i\\beta\\,(e\\cdot n')\\overline{(e\\cdot n')}=i\\beta\\,|e\\cdot n'|^2$, a purely imaginary number; therefore its real part vanishes and step 8.1 gives $\\langle S\\cdot n'\\rangle\\equiv0$ for every transverse amplitude. [step 8.1, F3, F5, algebra] 10.1 [The polarised evanescent cases] In the transverse-electric case $e=E_0(n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0)$ with $\\hat\\kappa_0=\\kappa_0/|\\kappa_0|$ and $E_0\\ne0$: the triple-product expansion of [F5] gives $k\\times(n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0)=(k\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0)n'-(k\\cdot n')\\hat\\kappa_0=|\\kappa_0|n'-i\\beta\\hat\\kappa_0$ because $k\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0=\\kappa_0\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0=|\\kappa_0|$ and $k\\cdot n'=i\\beta$, and $k\\cdot(n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0)=\\langle n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0,k\\rangle=\\det[n',\\hat\\kappa_0,k]=\\det[n',\\hat\\kappa_0,\\kappa_0]+i\\beta\\det[n',\\hat\\kappa_0,n']=|\\kappa_0|\\det[n',\\hat\\kappa_0,\\hat\\kappa_0]+i\\beta\\cdot0=0$ because a determinant with two equal columns vanishes by [F5]; so this is a transverse amplitude with $h=E_0(|\\kappa_0|n'-i\\beta\\hat\\kappa_0)/(\\omega\\mu)$ and $\\overline h=\\overline E_0(|\\kappa_0|n'+i\\beta\\hat\\kappa_0)/(\\omega\\mu)$. With the positively oriented orthonormal triad $(\\hat\\kappa_0,\\hat y',n')$ where $\\hat y'=n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0$, the cross-product relations $\\hat y'\\times n'=\\hat\\kappa_0$ and $\\hat y'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0=-n'$ hold by [F5], so $e\\times\\overline h=|E_0|^2\\bigl[|\\kappa_0|\\hat\\kappa_0-i\\beta n'\\bigr]/(\\omega\\mu)$ and $\\langle S\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0\\rangle=e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}|E_0|^2|\\kappa_0|/(2\\mu\\omega)=e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}|e|^2|\\kappa_0|/(2\\mu\\omega)>0$ because $|e|^2=|E_0|^2\\ne0$, $|\\kappa_0|>0$ and $\\mu,\\omega,v>0$. In the transverse-magnetic case $h=H_0\\hat y'$ with $H_0\\ne0$ and $e=-(k\\times\\hat y')H_0/(\\omega\\varepsilon)$: the same expansion gives $k\\times\\hat y'=|\\kappa_0|n'-i\\beta\\hat\\kappa_0$, so $e=H_0(i\\beta\\hat\\kappa_0-|\\kappa_0|n')/(\\omega\\varepsilon)$; then $k\\cdot e=H_0(i\\beta\\,k\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0-|\\kappa_0|\\,k\\cdot n')/(\\omega\\varepsilon)=H_0(i\\beta|\\kappa_0|-|\\kappa_0|i\\beta)/(\\omega\\varepsilon)=0$, and $k\\times e=H_0(i\\beta\\,k\\times\\hat\\kappa_0-|\\kappa_0|\\,k\\times n')/(\\omega\\varepsilon)$. Here $k\\times n'=(\\kappa_0+i\\beta n')\\times n'=\\kappa_0\\times n'=-|\\kappa_0|\\hat y'$ and $k\\times\\hat\\kappa_0=(\\kappa_0+i\\beta n')\\times\\hat\\kappa_0=i\\beta(n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0)=i\\beta\\hat y'$ by [F5] and $\\kappa_0\\times\\hat\\kappa_0=0$, whence $k\\times e=H_0\\bigl((i\\beta)^2+|\\kappa_0|^2\\bigr)\\hat y'/(\\omega\\varepsilon)=H_0\\bigl(|\\kappa_0|^2-\\beta^2\\bigr)\\hat y'/(\\omega\\varepsilon)=\\omega\\mu H_0\\hat y'=\\omega\\mu h$, because $|\\kappa_0|^2-\\beta^2=k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2$ by step 7.1 and [F7]; so this data satisfies the amplitude conditions by step 4.1, and $\\langle S\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0\\rangle=e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}|H_0|^2|\\kappa_0|/(2\\omega\\varepsilon)=e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}|h|^2|\\kappa_0|/(2\\omega\\varepsilon)>0$ with $|h|^2=|H_0|^2\\ne0$. In both polarisations the normal component vanishes identically by step 9.1. [step 7.1, step 8.1, step 9.1, step 4.1, F3, F5, F7, algebra] 11.1 [Scope (f)] Clause (f) holds: all computations were carried out for the explicit fields defined by $(k,\\omega,e,h)$ on the whole space, so no existence or completeness of any plane-wave family, no energy finiteness, no boundary or interface conditions, no material validity and no empirical statement is claimed; for $\\operatorname{Im}k\\ne0$ the factor $e^{-\\operatorname{Im}k\\cdot x}$ is unbounded in some directions, so the statements are read where the model declares the field bounded, and in (e) the decay factor $e^{-\\beta n'\\cdot x}$ is the bounded branch on $n'\\cdot x>0$; the finite-band caveat of [F6] applies throughout; and no choice principle is used, every construction being finite-dimensional algebra for exhibited data. [given, step 9.1, step 10.1, F6, F7] ∎",
          "physical_scope": "Exact conditional statement about ideal plane-wave fields in a homogeneous isotropic Ohmic medium at rest, on the laboratory chart: the four macroscopic equations hold if and only if the four complex amplitude conditions hold, with the dispersion relation, transverse structure, lossless period-averaged energy density and flux, and the evanescent decay and normal-flux identities. Excluded: general bounded-field or finite-energy existence theory, interfaces, anisotropic or dispersive media, nonlinear response, ideal conductors, any completeness of the plane-wave family, and all empirical statements.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
