          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the elementary inertial-rest branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] in the isotropic scalar case at rest in the laboratory frame with constants $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$ and $\\sigma>0$, the fields of [[def-em-fields]] on the laboratory region of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and the plane-wave structure of [[pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields]]: let $\\omega>0$, $e\\in\\mathbb C^{3}\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $n\\in\\mathbb R^3$ a unit vector and $k=\\lambda n$, $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C$, and let $E(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e\\,e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$ be a plane-wave field satisfying the amplitude conditions of [[pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields]](b) with $k\\cdot e=0$. Put $a:=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^{2}>0$, $b:=\\mu\\sigma\\omega>0$, $r:=\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}$ and let $p,q>0$ be the quantities of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]]. Then: (a) [Exact propagation constant and decay] $\\lambda^{2}=k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^{2}+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega=a+ib$, so $\\lambda=\\pm(p+iq)$; if $x_3':=n\\cdot x$, then for $\\lambda=p+iq$ $$E(x,t)=e^{-q x_3'}\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e\\,e^{i(p x_3'-\\omega t)}\\bigr),$$ so the field oscillates with wavenumber $p$ and its amplitude decays in the direction of increasing $x_3'$ at the exact rate $q$; with $\\lambda=-(p+iq)$ the exact formula is $E(x,t)=e^{q x_3'}\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i(-p x_3'-\\omega t)})$, which grows in that direction, so the decaying branch is the one with $\\lambda=p+iq$, in agreement with the selection rule of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]](b). (b) [Exact skin depth] $q^{2}=\\bigl(\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}-a\\bigr)/2$, $p^{2}=\\bigl(\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}+a\\bigr)/2$, $pq=b/2=\\mu\\sigma\\omega/2$, and the skin depth of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]] equals the exact closed expression $$\\delta(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{q}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\,(r+a)}}{b}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\bigl(\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}+a\\bigr)}}{\\mu\\sigma\\omega}.$$ (c) [Good-conductor regime] put $t:=a/b=\\varepsilon\\omega/\\sigma>0$. For every $t\\le1/4$ the exact value satisfies the explicit bounds $$\\bigl|q-\\sqrt{b/2}\\bigr|\\le2t\\sqrt{b/2}=\\frac{2\\varepsilon\\omega}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu\\sigma\\omega}{2}},\\qquad \\bigl|\\delta-\\sqrt{2/b}\\bigr|\\le4t\\sqrt{2/b}=\\frac{4\\varepsilon\\omega}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\mu\\sigma\\omega}} ;$$ equivalently $\\delta=\\sqrt{2/(\\mu\\sigma\\omega)}\\,\\bigl(1+O(\\varepsilon\\omega/\\sigma)\\bigr)$ in the declared regime $\\varepsilon\\omega/\\sigma\\le1/4$, with the displayed explicit remainder bounds. (d) [Poor-conductor regime] put $t':=b/a=\\sigma/(\\varepsilon\\omega)>0$. For every $t'\\le1/2$ the exact value satisfies $$\\Bigl|q-\\frac{\\sigma}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\varepsilon}}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{t'^{2}}{4}\\cdot\\frac{\\sigma}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\varepsilon}},\\qquad\\Bigl|\\delta-\\frac{2}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\varepsilon}{\\mu}}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{t'^{2}}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\varepsilon}{\\mu}},$$ so in this regime $\\delta$ is frequency-independent up to the displayed relative error $O\\bigl((\\sigma/(\\varepsilon\\omega))^{2}\\bigr)$. (e) [Phase relation for a real amplitude] if $e\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ and $\\lambda=p+iq$, then $$E(x,t)=e^{-q x_3'}e\\cos(px_3'-\\omega t),\\qquad H(x,t)=\\frac{e^{-q x_3'}}{\\mu\\omega}\\bigl(p\\cos(px_3'-\\omega t)-q\\sin(px_3'-\\omega t)\\bigr)\\,(n\\times e),$$ and the ratio of the quadrature to the in-phase coefficient is $q/p=\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t$ with $1-2t\\le q/p\\le1$ for every $t\\le1/2$; in particular $q/p\\to1$ as $t\\downarrow0$, so the magnetic scalar factor is $\\sqrt{p^2+q^2}\\cos(px_3'-\\omega t+\\eta)$ with $\\eta:=\\arctan(q/p)\\to\\pi/4$ (45 degrees), not a 90-degree quadrature phase shift. At fixed position with the $e^{-i\\omega t}$ convention this corresponds to the stated signed $+\\eta$ phase in the decreasing phase variable. (f) [Limits and scope] for $\\sigma=0$ one has $b=0$, $q=0$, no attenuation and $\\delta=+\\infty$, and the approximations of (c) and (d) are not applicable; the ideal-conductor limit $\\sigma\\to\\infty$ is not covered by this theorem, which works inside the finite-Ohmic branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] (a separate boundary model is needed for an ideal conductor); the frequency dependence of $\\sigma$ (Drude or other) is not supplied, and $\\sigma$ is the declared constant of the branch; the conclusions are exact identities for the ideal plane-wave field and carry no empirical content, no measured conductivity and no claim about any laboratory field.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct; substitute the concrete data into the medium plane-wave theorem, extract the decaying root via Euler's Cartesian form, and bound the exact radicals with elementary square-root inequalities. 1.1 [Propagation constant and decay, part (a)] By [F1], $k\\cdot k=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^{2}+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega=a+ib$, and since $k=\\lambda n$ with $n\\cdot n=1$ the bilinear expansion gives $\\lambda^{2}=k\\cdot k=a+ib$; by [F1] and [F5] with [F3], the solutions of $z^{2}=a+ib$ are exactly $z=\\pm(p+iq)$. Writing $x_3'=n\\cdot x$, so that $k\\cdot x=\\lambda x_3'$, the exponent is $i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)=i\\lambda x_3'-i\\omega t$; for $\\lambda=p+iq$ this is $-qx_3'+i(px_3'-\\omega t)$, and the Cartesian form [F2] gives $e^{i(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)}=e^{-qx_3'}(\\cos(px_3'-\\omega t)+i\\sin(px_3'-\\omega t))$, whence $E=e^{-qx_3'}\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(e\\,e^{i(px_3'-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$ componentwise; the amplitude factor $e^{-qx_3'}$ decreases as $x_3'$ increases because $q>0$ by [F1]. For $\\lambda=-(p+iq)$ the same computation gives $i\\lambda x_3'-i\\omega t=qx_3'+i(-px_3'-\\omega t)$ and $E=e^{qx_3'}\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i(-px_3'-\\omega t)})$, which grows in the direction of increasing $x_3'$; the decaying branch is therefore $\\lambda=p+iq$, matching the root selection of [F1]. [given, F1, F2, F3, F5, algebra] 2.1 [Exact skin depth, part (b)] By [F1], $q^{2}=(r-a)/2$ and $p^{2}=(r+a)/2$ with $r=\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}$; multiplying, $(pq)^{2}=\\bigl(r^{2}-a^{2}\\bigr)/4=b^{2}/4$, and since $p,q\\ge0$ and $b>0$ the uniqueness of the nonnegative square root in [F4] gives $pq=b/2=\\mu\\sigma\\omega/2$. For the skin depth $\\delta=1/q$ of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]](c): $\\delta^{2}=1/q^{2}=2/(r-a)=2(r+a)/\\bigl((r-a)(r+a)\\bigr)=2(r+a)/b^{2}$, where $(r-a)(r+a)=r^{2}-a^{2}=b^{2}$ by [F4]; since $\\delta>0$ and $b>0$, taking the nonnegative square root gives $\\delta=\\sqrt{2(r+a)}/b$, and substituting $a=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^{2}$ and $b=\\mu\\sigma\\omega$ gives the displayed closed form. [step 1.1, F1, F4, algebra] 3.1 [Ratio identities] With $t=a/b>0$ one has $r=b\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}$ because $r^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}=b^{2}(t^{2}+1)$ and both sides are nonnegative, by [F4]; hence $q^{2}=(r-a)/2=\\tfrac{b}{2}\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t\\bigr)$ and $\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t=\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}+t\\bigr)^{-1}$, the second identity because $\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t\\bigr)\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}+t\\bigr)=1+t^{2}-t^{2}=1$ with all factors positive by [F4] and [F1]. Moreover $q/p=\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t$: indeed $q^{2}/p^{2}=(r-a)/(r+a)$, and the same rationalisation gives $(r-a)/(r+a)=\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t\\bigr)^{2}$, so the positive quantities $q/p$ and $\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t$ have equal squares and are equal by [F4]. With $s=t'=b/a>0$ one has analogously $r=a\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}$ and $q^{2}=\\tfrac{a}{2}\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}-1\\bigr)$. Writing $q_{t}:=\\frac{\\sigma}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\varepsilon}}$, the identity $q_{t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{a}\\,s}{2}$ holds: expanding $\\sqrt a=\\omega(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{1/2}$ and $s=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon\\omega}$ by [F1] gives $\\frac{\\sqrt a\\,s}{2}=\\frac{\\omega(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{1/2}\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon\\omega}=\\frac{\\sigma}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\varepsilon}}$; hence $q^{2}/q_{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}-1)}{s^{2}}=\\frac{2}{1+\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}}$ by the same rationalisation. [step 2.1, F1, F4, algebra] 4.1 [Good-conductor bounds, part (c)] For $t\\ge0$ one has $1\\le\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}\\le1+t$: the left inequality because $1\\le1+t^{2}$ and both sides are nonnegative, the right because $1+t^{2}\\le(1+t)^{2}=1+2t+t^{2}$, both by [F4]. Hence $\\frac{1}{1+2t}\\le\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t\\le\\frac{1}{1+t}\\le1$ by step 3.1 and [F4], and $\\frac{1}{1+2t}\\ge1-2t$ because $0\\le1-2t\\le1$ and $(1-2t)(1+2t)=1-4t^{2}\\le1$ for $t\\le1/2$; therefore $\\sqrt{1-2t}\\le\\frac{q}{\\sqrt{b/2}}\\le1$, that is $\\sqrt{b/2}\\sqrt{1-2t}\\le q\\le\\sqrt{b/2}$. For $t\\le1/4$ one has $1-2t\\ge0$ and $(1-2t)^{2}\\le1-2t$, so $\\sqrt{1-2t}\\ge1-2t$ by [F4], giving $\\bigl|q-\\sqrt{b/2}\\bigr|\\le2t\\sqrt{b/2}$ with the stated equivalent form since $\\sqrt{b/2}=\\sqrt{\\mu\\sigma\\omega/2}$. For $\\delta=1/q$ one has $\\sqrt{2/b}\\le\\delta\\le\\frac{\\sqrt{2/b}}{1-2t}$ from the bound on $q$ and [F4]; and $\\frac{1}{1-2t}\\le1+4t$ for $t\\le1/4$ because $1\\le(1+4t)(1-2t)=1+2t-8t^{2}$, i.e. $0\\le2t(1-4t)$, by [F4]; hence $\\bigl|\\delta-\\sqrt{2/b}\\bigr|\\le4t\\sqrt{2/b}$ with the stated equivalent form. [step 3.1, F1, F4, algebra] 4.2 [Poor-conductor bounds, part (d)] By step 3.1, $q^{2}=\\tfrac{a}{2}\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}-1\\bigr)$ and $q^{2}/q_{t}^{2}=\\frac{2}{1+\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}}$ with $q_{t}=\\frac{\\sigma}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\varepsilon}}$, $s=t'=b/a>0$. Since $\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}\\ge1$, the ratio is at most $1$, so $q\\le q_{t}$; and $\\sqrt{1+s^{2}}\\le1+\\frac{s^{2}}{2}$ because $1+s^{2}\\le\\bigl(1+\\frac{s^{2}}{2}\\bigr)^{2}=1+s^{2}+\\frac{s^{4}}{4}$, so $q^{2}/q_{t}^{2}\\ge\\frac{2}{2+\\frac{s^{2}}{2}}=\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{s^{2}}{4}}$; for $s^{2}\\le1$ (which holds when $s\\le1/2$) one has $\\frac{1}{1+s^{2}/4}\\ge1-\\frac{s^{2}}{4}$ since $\\bigl(1-\\frac{s^{2}}{4}\\bigr)\\bigl(1+\\frac{s^{2}}{4}\\bigr)=1-\\frac{s^{4}}{16}\\le1$ with $1-\\frac{s^{2}}{4}\\ge0$; therefore $q/q_{t}\\ge1-\\frac{s^{2}}{4}$, i.e. $\\bigl|q-q_{t}\\bigr|\\le\\frac{s^{2}}{4}q_{t}$. For $\\delta=1/q$ and $\\delta_{t}=1/q_{t}$ we get $\\delta/\\delta_{t}=q_{t}/q\\le\\frac{1}{1-s^{2}/4}\\le1+\\frac{s^{2}}{2}$ whenever $s^{2}\\le1$ (since $\\frac{1}{1-s^{2}/4}\\le1+\\frac{s^{2}}{2}$ is equivalent to $1\\le1+\\frac{s^{2}}{4}-\\frac{s^{4}}{8}$, true for $s^{2}\\le2$), whence $\\bigl|\\delta-\\delta_{t}\\bigr|\\le\\frac{s^{2}}{2}\\delta_{t}$; substituting $\\delta_{t}=\\frac{2}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\varepsilon}{\\mu}}$ gives both displayed bounds, by [F4] throughout. [step 3.1, F4, algebra] 5.1 [Phase relation, part (e)] Assume $e\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$ and $\\lambda=p+iq$; by [F1], $h=k\\times e/(\\omega\\mu)=\\lambda\\,(n\\times e)/(\\omega\\mu)$ because $k=\\lambda n$ and $n\\times e$ is real, and the exponent is computed as in step 1.1. Writing $\\theta:=px_3'-\\omega t$ and using $\\lambda/(\\mu\\omega)=(p+iq)/(\\mu\\omega)$ with real $p,q$, the Cartesian form [F2] gives $\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((p+iq)(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)\\bigr)=p\\cos\\theta-q\\sin\\theta$, so $E=e^{-qx_3'}e\\cos\\theta$ and $H=e^{-qx_3'}(p\\cos\\theta-q\\sin\\theta)(n\\times e)/(\\mu\\omega)$, the two displayed identities. By step 3.1, $q/p=\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}-t$, and the bounds $1-2t\\le q/p\\le1$ for $t\\le1/2$ are the ones established in step 4.1 for the same quantity; as $t\\downarrow0$ the inequality $0\\le\\sqrt{1+t^2}-1=t^2/(\\sqrt{1+t^2}+1)\\le t^2/2$ gives $|\\sqrt{1+t^2}-t-1|\\le t+t^2/2\\to0$, so $\\eta:=\\arctan(q/p)$ tends to $\\arctan 1=\\pi/4$ by the continuity of $\\arctan$ in [F6]. Put $r:=q/p>0$ and $\\eta=\\arctan r\\in(0,\\pi/2)$; by [F6], $\\sin\\eta=r\\cos\\eta$, and the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}\\eta+\\cos^{2}\\eta=1$ with $\\cos\\eta>0$ gives $\\cos\\eta=1/\\sqrt{1+r^{2}}$ and $\\sin\\eta=r/\\sqrt{1+r^{2}}$; equivalently $\\cos\\eta=p/\\sqrt{p^{2}+q^{2}}$ and $\\sin\\eta=q/\\sqrt{p^{2}+q^{2}}$, since $\\sqrt{1+r^{2}}=\\sqrt{p^{2}+q^{2}}/p$ with $p>0$ by [F4]. The addition formula of [F6] then gives $\\sqrt{p^{2}+q^{2}}\\cos(\\theta+\\eta)=p\\cos\\theta-q\\sin\\theta$, which is the stated form of the magnetic scalar factor. [given, step 1.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, F1, F2, F4, F6, F7, algebra] 6.1 [Limits and scope, part (f)] Clause (f): for $\\sigma=0$ the definition of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]] gives $b=0$, $q=0$, no attenuation and $\\delta=+\\infty$, and the divisions and bounds of steps 3.1-4.2 use $b>0$, $q>0$ and are not evaluated at zero; the limit $\\sigma\\to\\infty$ is outside the finite-Ohmic branch and the frequency dependence of $\\sigma$ is not supplied; all identities are exact for the plane-wave field and involve no measurement, material data or empirical statement, and no choice principle is used since every quantity is defined by explicit formulas in the given scalars. [given, step 4.2, F1, F8] ∎",
          "physical_scope": "Exact conditional statement about a monochromatic plane-wave field in a homogeneous isotropic Ohmic medium at rest with constant scalar eps,mu>0 and sigma>0: the exact complex propagation constant and decay, the closed-form skin depth, explicit bounded remainder inequalities in the good-conductor regime eps*omega/sigma<=1/4 and the poor-conductor regime sigma/(eps*omega)<=1/2, and the phase relation. Excluded: sigma=0 (no attenuation, infinite skin depth), the ideal-conductor limit, frequency-dependent or tensor conductivity, anisotropy, dispersion, nonlinearity, any laboratory material and all empirical claims.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.6.2 printed pp. 207-209: the conductor ansatz with $J=\\sigma(\\omega)E$, the effective permittivity $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\varepsilon+i\\sigma/\\omega$, the dispersion relation (7.54), the low-frequency Drude regime $\\sigma(\\omega)\\approx\\sigma_{\\mathrm{DC}}$ with $k=k_1+ik_2=\\sqrt{\\mu\\omega\\sigma_{\\mathrm{DC}}/2}\\,(1+i)$, the skin depth $\\delta=1/k_2=\\sqrt{2/(\\mu\\omega\\sigma_{\\mathrm{DC}})}$ and the $\\pi/4$ phase statement; the high-frequency plasma-frequency discussion is disposed separately in the coverage file and is not used here."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§9.4.1 printed pp. 393-396: the modified wave equations (9.122), the attenuated plane waves (9.123), the complex wave number (9.124) $k^{2}=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^{2}+i\\mu\\sigma\\omega$, the decomposition $k=k+i\\kappa$ (9.125) and the skin depth $d=1/\\kappa$ (9.128); Problems 9.19(a),(b),(c) printed pp. 395-396 state the poor-conductor frequency-independent skin depth, the good-conductor depth $\\lambda/2\\pi$ and the $\\pi/4$ phase lag. Retained scan with OCR noise; the exact formulas were cross-checked against Tong and against the local computations in the batch notes."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
