          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the following plane-interface material setting, with the same explicitly adopted stationary interface identities as [[pthm-em-phase-matching]] (including its absent-wave qualification), at normal incidence: medium 1 in $\\{x_3<0\\}$ with $\\varepsilon_1,\\mu_1>0$, $\\sigma_1=0$, medium 2 in $\\{x_3>0\\}$ with $\\varepsilon_2,\\mu_2>0$ and either $\\sigma_2=0$ (lossless) or $\\sigma_2>0$ (Ohmic), both homogeneous and at rest in the laboratory chart, with the plane interface $S=\\{x_3=0\\}$ and normal $n=e_3$. Let $\\omega_i,\\omega_r,\\omega_t>0$ and let three plane waves of [[pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields]] be given with wavevectors $k_i=k_1n$ ($k_1>0$), $k_r=-k_1n$ and $k_t=k\\,n$, where for $\\sigma_2=0$ one takes $k=\\omega_t/v_2>0$ and for $\\sigma_2>0$ one takes $k=\\kappa_2(\\omega_t)=p+iq$ with the complex propagation constant of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]]; amplitudes $e_i\\in\\mathbb C^{3}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and $e_r,e_t\\in\\mathbb C^{3}$ and $h_j=k_j\\times e_j/(\\mu_j\\omega_j)$ satisfying the conditions of [[pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields]](b)-(c) in their media, and the four stationary interface identities of [[pthm-em-phase-matching]] holding on $\\mathbb R^{2}\\times I$ for a nonempty open interval $I$. Then: (a) [Common frequency] The transmitted amplitude is nonzero and $\\omega_i=\\omega_t=:\\omega$ for some $\\omega>0$. If $e_r\\ne0$, then $\\omega_r=\\omega$ as well; if $e_r=0$, assign $\\omega_r=\\omega$ conventionally, since an absent field cannot determine a frequency. Matching is proved directly from the interface characters, so the lossless-only physical scope of [[pthm-em-phase-matching]] is not used to infer the Ohmic branch. (b) [Amplitude system and solution] all three electric amplitudes are tangential to $S$, and the two tangential interface identities reduce to $$e_i+e_r=e_t,\\qquad Y_1\\,(e_i-e_r)=Y_2\\,e_t,\\qquad Y_1:=\\frac{k_1}{\\mu_1\\omega}>0,\\qquad Y_2:=\\frac{k}{\\mu_2\\omega};$$ hence $e_r=r\\,e_i$ and $e_t=t\\,e_i$ with the complex scalars $$r=\\frac{Y_1-Y_2}{Y_1+Y_2},\\qquad t=\\frac{2Y_1}{Y_1+Y_2};$$ in particular the reflected and transmitted electric phasors are parallel to the incident one (normal-incidence polarisation preservation), and $t\\ne0$. The reflection coefficient is zero exactly when $Y_1=Y_2$, in particular at a lossless impedance-matched interface; the zero reflected field remains part of the theorem. (c) [Lossless transmitting medium] if $\\sigma_2=0$ then $Y_2=1/Z_2>0$ with $Z_2=\\sqrt{\\mu_2/\\varepsilon_2}$ of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]], so $$r=\\frac{Z_2-Z_1}{Z_1+Z_2}\\in\\mathbb R,\\qquad t=\\frac{2Z_2}{Z_1+Z_2}\\in\\mathbb R,$$ in agreement with [[lem-em-interface-wave-linear-system]]; the period-$T$ averaged normal fluxes of the stored-energy model, $T=2\\pi/\\omega$ ([[pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields]](d), [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]]), are $\\langle S_i\\cdot n\\rangle=\\frac{|e_i|^{2}}{2Z_1}$, $\\langle S_r\\cdot n\\rangle=-\\frac{|e_r|^{2}}{2Z_1}$ and $\\langle S_t\\cdot n\\rangle=\\frac{|e_t|^{2}}{2Z_2}$, and with $R:=r^{2}$, $T:=\\frac{Z_1}{Z_2}t^{2}=\\frac{4Z_1Z_2}{(Z_1+Z_2)^{2}}$ one has $R+T=1$; $R$ is the reflected and $T$ the transmitted fraction of the incident normal averaged flux. (d) [Ohmic transmitting medium] if $\\sigma_2>0$ then $Y_2=\\kappa_2(\\omega)/(\\mu_2\\omega)$ has $\\operatorname{Re}Y_2=p/(\\mu_2\\omega)>0$, and $$r=\\frac{Y_1-Y_2}{Y_1+Y_2},\\qquad t=\\frac{2Y_1}{Y_1+Y_2},\\qquad |r|^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{Re}Y_2}{Y_1}\\,|t|^{2}=1 .$$ The second summand is the fraction of the incident period-averaged normal flux that enters the conductor at the interface; the transmitted field has the exact decay factor $e^{-qx_3}$ (the explicit factorization $e^{i(p+iq)x_3}=e^{-qx_3}e^{ipx_3}$) and, by the Ohmic dissipation clause of [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]], the entering flux is absorbed in the conductor: the reflected fraction $|r|^{2}$ and the absorbed fraction $(\\operatorname{Re}Y_2/Y_1)|t|^{2}$ add to $1$. (e) [Scope] no existence or uniqueness of interface-consistent data, no oblique incidence, no anisotropic or dispersive media, no ideal conductor ($\\sigma_2=\\infty$), no nonlinear response and no empirical claim is made. The interface identities are the stationary plane-interface conditions of the model, supplied for compact interfaces by [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]; the flux identities are the model's stored-energy and Ohmic-dissipation reading, not an observational claim.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct; evaluate the normal-incidence traces as characters, apply the character lemma to the combined six-component data to force the common frequency, solve the resulting two-equation admittance system, and compute the lossless and Ohmic flux budgets. 1.1 [Transversality and the trace identity] Since $k_j\\cdot e_j=0$ and $k_j\\cdot n\\ne0$ for each actual wave ($k_i\\cdot n=k_1>0$, $k_r\\cdot n=-k_1<0$, and $k_t\\cdot n=k$ with $k>0$ in the lossless case or $k=p+iq$ with $p>0$ in the Ohmic case), each electric amplitude is tangential: writing $e_j=(e_j\\cdot n)n+e_j^\\tau$ by [F11] gives $0=k_j\\cdot e_j=(k_j\\cdot n)(e_j\\cdot n)$. Likewise $h_j=k_j\\times e_j/(\\mu_j\\omega_j)$ is tangential because $k_j$ is normal. Evaluating at $x=(y,0)$ gives $k_j\\cdot x=0$, so $E_j=\\operatorname{Re}(e_j e^{-i\\omega_jt})$ and $H_j=\\operatorname{Re}(h_j e^{-i\\omega_jt})$ there; the two tangential interface identities of [A1] are identities of tangential fields, and for tangential $w$ the vanishing $n\\times w=0$ forces $w=0$ by [F11], so with the sign convention $A_j:=(e_j,h_j)$ and $\\lambda_j:=(0,0,-\\omega_j)\\in\\mathbb C^3$ they read $\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(A_i e^{i\\lambda_i\\cdot z}+A_r e^{i\\lambda_r\\cdot z}-A_t e^{i\\lambda_t\\cdot z}\\bigr)=0$ for all $z=(y,t)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times I$, by the additivity of the real part in [F10]. [given, A1, F1, F10, F11, algebra] 2.1 [The transmitted amplitude is nonzero] Suppose $e_t=0$; then $h_t=k\\,(n\\times e_t)/(\\mu_2\\omega_t)=0$ and $A_t=0$, so the identity of step 1.1 has the two terms $A_i,A_r$ with nonzero frequency parts $-\\omega_i,-\\omega_r$. Since $-\\overline{\\lambda_j}=(0,0,+\\omega_j)$ has positive last coordinate while every actual $\\lambda_l$ has negative last coordinate, [F5] applied to the electric and magnetic traces separately gives: for each actual frequency $\\nu$, the sum of the coefficients of the waves with that frequency vanishes. If $e_r=0$ then $A_r=0$ and the electric trace is a single character with coefficient $e_i\\ne0$, contradicting [F5]. If $e_r\\ne0$ and $\\lambda_r\\ne\\lambda_i$, both groups are singletons and the electric trace gives $e_i=0$ and $e_r=0$, again a contradiction; hence $\\lambda_r=\\lambda_i$, that is $\\omega_r=\\omega_i$, and the electric and magnetic trace coefficients at that frequency vanish: $e_i+e_r=0$ and $h_i+h_r=0$. By step 1.1, $h_i=(k_1/(\\mu_1\\omega_i))(n\\times e_i)$ and $h_r=(-k_1/(\\mu_1\\omega_i))(n\\times e_r)$, so $h_i+h_r=(k_1/(\\mu_1\\omega_i))\\,n\\times(e_i-e_r)=(k_1/(\\mu_1\\omega_i))\\,n\\times(2e_i)=0$, hence $n\\times e_i=0$; together with $e_i\\cdot n=0$ from step 1.1 this gives $e_i=0$ by [F11], contradicting $e_i\\ne0$. Therefore $e_t\\ne0$. [step 1.1, F1, F5, F10, F11, algebra] 3.1 [Common frequency] By step 2.1, $A_t=(e_t,h_t)$ is nonzero; $A_i$ is nonzero because $e_i\\ne0$; if $e_r\\ne0$ then $A_r\\ne0$. Applying [F5] to the combined six-component identity of step 1.1 and grouping as in step 2.1: for each actual frequency $\\nu$ the signed sum of the $A_j$ over the waves with that frequency vanishes. If the frequencies of the nonzero-$A_j$ waves were not all equal, some group would contain exactly one of them and would give $\\pm A_j=0$, a contradiction; and negative-conjugate partners cannot occur because of the sign of the last coordinate. Hence: if $e_r\\ne0$, then $\\lambda_i=\\lambda_r=\\lambda_t$, so $\\omega_i=\\omega_r=\\omega_t$; if $e_r=0$, then $\\lambda_i=\\lambda_t$, so $\\omega_i=\\omega_t$ and $\\omega_r$ is unconstrained by the absent field, matching the Statement. Setting $\\omega:=\\omega_i=\\omega_t$, the two identities of step 1.1 with the common frequency $-\\omega$ in the exponent give, after applying [F5] to the single-frequency electric and magnetic traces, $e_i+e_r=e_t$ and $h_i+h_r=h_t$. This also proves the lossless nonzero-reflected case via the normal-incidence instance [F2] of the phase-matching theorem, which is consistent with the direct argument just given. [step 2.1, step 1.1, F2, F5, F10, algebra] 4.1 [The amplitude system, part (b)] With the common frequency of step 3.1, $Y_1:=k_1/(\\mu_1\\omega)>0$ is real and positive, and $h_i=Y_1\\,n\\times e_i$, $h_r=-Y_1\\,n\\times e_r$, $h_t=(k/(\\mu_2\\omega))\\,n\\times e_t=:Y_2\\,n\\times e_t$ by [F1] and [F11]; note $\\operatorname{Re}Y_2=p/(\\mu_2\\omega)>0$ in the Ohmic case and $Y_2>0$ real in the lossless case, so $Y_1+Y_2\\ne0$ in both. The magnetic identity of step 3.1 reads $n\\times\\bigl(Y_2e_t-Y_1(e_i-e_r)\\bigr)=0$, and its argument is tangential, so $Y_1(e_i-e_r)=Y_2e_t$ by [F11]; together with $e_i+e_r=e_t$ this is exactly the system of [F3], and since $Y_1+Y_2\\ne0$ the solution formulas $r=(Y_1-Y_2)/(Y_1+Y_2)$, $t=2Y_1/(Y_1+Y_2)$ hold with $e_r=r\\,e_i$, $e_t=t\\,e_i$; here $r,t$ are scalars in $\\mathbb C$ multiplying the fixed nonzero vector $e_i$, so the reflected and transmitted electric phasors are parallel to the incident one. Moreover $t\\ne0$ because $|t|^2=4Y_1^2/|Y_1+Y_2|^2>0$ by [F3] and [F10]; and $r=0$ if and only if $Y_1=Y_2$. [step 3.1, F1, F3, F10, F11, algebra] 5.1 [Lossless transmitting medium, part (c)] If $\\sigma_2=0$ then $k=\\omega/v_2>0$ by the Statement, so $Y_2=k/(\\mu_2\\omega)=\\omega/(v_2\\mu_2\\omega)=1/(v_2\\mu_2)=1/Z_2$ by [F9] ($v_2\\mu_2=\\sqrt{\\mu_2/\\varepsilon_2}=Z_2$), and $Y_1=1/Z_1$ similarly; hence substituting $Y_j=1/Z_j$ into the formulas of step 4.1 gives $r=(1/Z_1-1/Z_2)/(1/Z_1+1/Z_2)=(Z_2-Z_1)/(Z_1+Z_2)$ and $t=2Z_2/(Z_1+Z_2)$, both real, the same formulas as in the scalar parametrisation of [F4]. The period-averaged normal fluxes at the interface: for a nonzero lossless wave with real wavevector the flux of [F1] is $\\langle S_j\\rangle=|e_j|^2k_j/(2\\mu_j\\omega)$ with $\\omega$ the common frequency, so $\\langle S_i\\cdot n\\rangle=|e_i|^2k_1/(2\\mu_1\\omega)=|e_i|^2/(2Z_1)$ because $k_1/(\\mu_1\\omega)=Y_1=1/Z_1$; similarly $\\langle S_r\\cdot n\\rangle=|e_r|^2(-k_1)/(2\\mu_1\\omega)=-|e_r|^2/(2Z_1)$ and $\\langle S_t\\cdot n\\rangle=|e_t|^2k/(2\\mu_2\\omega)=|e_t|^2/(2Z_2)$. If $e_r=0$, then $h_r=0$ and $S_r=0$, so the reflected formula holds directly as well. With $R=|r|^2$ and $T=(Z_1/Z_2)|t|^2$, the identity $R+T=1$ is [F4]; since $|e_r|^2=R|e_i|^2$ and $|e_t|^2=(Z_2/Z_1)T|e_i|^2=|e_i|^2 T Z_2/Z_1$, the three fluxes are $|e_i|^2/(2Z_1)$ times $1$, $-R$ and $T$ respectively, which is the stated fraction reading. [step 4.1, F1, F4, F9, F10, algebra] 5.2 [Ohmic transmitting medium: system and budget, part (d) first half] If $\\sigma_2>0$ then $k=\\kappa_2(\\omega)=p+iq$ with $p,q>0$ by [F9], so $Y_2=(p+iq)/(\\mu_2\\omega)$ has $\\operatorname{Re}Y_2=p/(\\mu_2\\omega)>0$ and $\\operatorname{Im}Y_2=q/(\\mu_2\\omega)>0$; the solution formulas of step 4.1 give $r,t$, and [F3] gives $|r|^2+(\\operatorname{Re}Y_2/Y_1)|t|^2=1$ and $|t|^2=4Y_1^2/|Y_1+Y_2|^2>0$. Since $\\operatorname{Im}Y_2\\ne0$, the numerator $Y_1-Y_2$ of $r$ is nonzero, so $r\\ne0$ in this branch. [step 4.1, F3, F9, F10, algebra] 6.1 [Attenuated transmitted field and entering flux] With $k=p+iq$ and the common frequency, the transmitted field factorizes exactly: for $x_3\\in\\mathbb R$, $i(kx_3-\\omega t)=-qx_3+i(px_3-\\omega t)$, and [F6] gives $e^{i(kx_3-\\omega t)}=e^{-qx_3}(\\cos(px_3-\\omega t)+i\\sin(px_3-\\omega t))$, so $E_t(x,t)=e^{-qx_3}\\operatorname{Re}(e_t e^{i(px_3-\\omega t)})$ componentwise (the exact decay factor $e^{-qx_3}$), and $H_t=e^{-qx_3}\\operatorname{Re}(h_t e^{i(px_3-\\omega t)})$ for the same reason since $h_t$ is a constant phasor. The period-averaged normal flux of the transmitted field is, by the cross-product form of [F7] applied to the factorized fields, $\\langle S_t\\cdot n\\rangle=e^{-2qx_3}\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl((e_t\\times\\overline h_t)\\cdot n\\bigr)$; from $h_t=Y_2\\,n\\times e_t$ with $Y_2=(p+iq)/(\\mu_2\\omega)$ by step 4.1, $\\overline h_t=\\overline{Y_2}\\,n\\times\\overline e_t=((p-iq)/(\\mu_2\\omega))\\,n\\times\\overline e_t$ by [F10], and the triple-product expansion of [F11] gives $e_t\\times(n\\times\\overline e_t)=n\\,|e_t|^2-\\overline e_t\\,(e_t\\cdot n)=n\\,|e_t|^2$ because $e_t$ is tangential; hence $(e_t\\times\\overline h_t)\\cdot n=(p-iq)|e_t|^2/(\\mu_2\\omega)$ and $\\langle S_t\\cdot n\\rangle=e^{-2qx_3}p|e_t|^2/(2\\mu_2\\omega)=:F(x_3)$. At the interface $x_3=0$ this equals $(p/(\\mu_2\\omega))|e_t|^2/2$, and dividing by the incident flux $|e_i|^2/(2Z_1)=Y_1|e_i|^2/2$ of step 5.1 (which applies to medium 1, lossless) gives $\\frac{p}{\\mu_2\\omega Y_1}|t|^2=\\frac{\\operatorname{Re}Y_2}{Y_1}|t|^2$, the stated entering fraction of the incident period-averaged normal flux. [step 4.1, step 5.1, F6, F7, F10, F11, algebra] 7.1 [Ohmic absorption] In the conductor the free current is the conduction current $J_f=\\sigma_2E_t$ with the declared positive scalar $\\sigma_2$ (the model of [F1] has no external free current), and the Ohmic dissipation density of [F8] averaged over a period is $d(x_3):=\\sigma_2\\langle|E_t|^2\\rangle=\\sigma_2|e_t|^2e^{-2qx_3}/2$ by [F7] applied to the factorized field of step 6.1; the flux of step 6.1 is $F(x_3)=p|e_t|^2e^{-2qx_3}/(2\\mu_2\\omega)$. Since $2pq=\\mu_2\\sigma_2\\omega$ by [F9], $F'(x_3)=-2q\\,p|e_t|^2e^{-2qx_3}/(2\\mu_2\\omega)=-pq|e_t|^2e^{-2qx_3}/(\\mu_2\\omega)=-\\sigma_2|e_t|^2e^{-2qx_3}/2=-d(x_3)$: the flux lost per unit length is exactly the Ohmic dissipation. For every $L>0$ the integrand $d$ is continuous on $[0,L]$, hence Riemann integrable by [F13], and the exponential antiderivative is differentiable through the endpoints; the fundamental theorem [F13] gives $\\int_0^Ld(x_3)\\,dx_3=\\sigma_2|e_t|^2(1-e^{-2qL})/(4q)=F(0)-F(L)$, where the last identity uses $p/(2\\mu_2\\omega)=\\sigma_2/(4q)$, which is $2pq=\\mu_2\\sigma_2\\omega$ rearranged. Since $q>0$, $e^{-2qL}\\to0$ as $L\\to\\infty$ by [F13], and defining the improper integral as the limit of the proper integrals per [F13] gives $\\int_0^\\infty d(x_3)\\,dx_3=\\sigma_2|e_t|^2/(4q)=F(0)$: the whole entering flux is dissipated in the conductor, and with the budget of step 5.2 the reflected fraction $|r|^2$ and the absorbed fraction $(\\operatorname{Re}Y_2/Y_1)|t|^2$ add to $1$. [step 5.2, step 6.1, F8, F9, F13, algebra] 8.1 [Scope, part (e)] All conclusions are conditional on the given wave data and the interface identities [A1]; the argument used only the two tangential identities, the amplitude conditions and dispersion of [F1], the character lemma [F5], the admittance algebra [F3], the Euler form [F6] and the period averages [F7]; the normal interface identities, the optional normal-root selection rule and any outgoing condition were not used, and the oblique-incidence theorem [F2] was cited only as the consistent lossless cross-check. Nothing about existence or uniqueness of interface-consistent data, oblique incidence, anisotropy, dispersion, ideal conductors ($\\sigma_2=\\infty$), nonlinear response or empirical data is claimed; the entering-flux absorption reading is the model's stored-energy and Ohmic-dissipation statement, not an observational claim. The interface identities are stated hypotheses on the noncompact plane; for compact interfaces they follow from [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]. No choice principle is used: every quantity is finite-dimensional algebra in the given data, and the only limiting passage is the explicit exponential improper integral of step 7.1. [given, step 7.1, F2, F12] ∎",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional normal-incidence reflection and transmission theorem at a plane interface between two homogeneous isotropic scalar media at rest, the second either lossless or Ohmic: the amplitude system, the reflection and transmission amplitudes, the flux budget R+T=1 in the lossless case and |r|^2+(ReY_2/Y_1)|t|^2=1 with the absorbed fraction in the Ohmic case. Excluded: oblique incidence, anisotropic, dispersive or nonlinear media, ideal conductors, mobile media, existence or uniqueness of interface-consistent data, and all empirical claims.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.4.1 printed pp. 189-190: the normal-polarisation interface system $E_I+E_R=E_T$ and $(E_I-E_R)/v_1\\cos\\theta_I=(E_T/v_2)\\cos\\theta_T$, reducing at $\\theta=0$ to the two-equation system solved here, with the solution (7.26); §7.6.2 printed pp. 207-208 for the complex propagation constant of the Ohmic medium entering the transmitted admittance."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§9.3.2 printed pp. 384-386, Eqs. (9.74)-(9.88): the normal-incidence boundary conditions (9.74), the amplitude system (9.78)-(9.81), the solutions (9.82), the intensity coefficients (9.86)-(9.87) with $R+T=1$ (9.88), Problem 9.13 (exact coefficients without $\\mu_1=\\mu_2=\\mu_0$) and Problem 9.14 (the reflected and transmitted polarisations must lie along the incident one); §9.4.2 printed pp. 396-398, Eqs. (9.139)-(9.147): the analogous normal-incidence system at a conducting surface with the complex $\\beta=\\mu_1v_1\\widetilde{k}_2/(\\mu_2\\omega)$ of Eq. (9.146), where $\\widetilde{k}_2$ is the complex transmitted wave number, and the total-reflection limit (9.148). Retained scan with OCR noise; the formulas were cross-checked against Tong and the local algebra in the batch notes."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
