      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-em-skin-depth-approximation-example",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Numerical check of the good-conductor skin-depth bounds and the $\\sigma=0$ exclusion",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 20,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-conducting-skin-depth",
            "def-em-medium-wave-parameters",
            "post-em-material-closure"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-conducting-skin-depth": "physical-result",
            "def-em-medium-wave-parameters": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-material-closure": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt the isotropic scalar Ohmic branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] at rest and the exact quantities of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]] and [[pthm-em-conducting-skin-depth]]. Let the declared parameters be $$\\varepsilon=4\\ \\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^{-1},\\qquad \\mu=1\\ \\mathrm H\\,\\mathrm m^{-1},\\qquad \\sigma=100\\ \\mathrm S\\,\\mathrm m^{-1},\\qquad \\omega=3\\ \\mathrm s^{-1};$$ these are declared model parameters of the illustration, not measured values of any material. The numerical evaluations below use these SI units: $a,b,r$ have unit $\\mathrm m^{-2}$, $p,q$ have unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and $\\delta$ has unit m; $t=a/b$ is dimensionless. Then: (i) $a=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^{2}=36$, $b=\\mu\\sigma\\omega=300$, $r=\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}=\\sqrt{91296}$ and $t=a/b=3/25=0.12\\le1/4$, so the good-conductor regime of [[pthm-em-conducting-skin-depth]](c) applies; (ii) the exact propagation and attenuation constants are $p=\\sqrt{(r+36)/2}$ and $q=\\sqrt{(r-36)/2}$, with $pq=b/2=150$ exactly; the decimal evaluations $r\\approx302.15228$, $p\\approx13.00293$, $q\\approx11.53586$ satisfy $q<\\sqrt{b/2}=\\sqrt{150}\\approx12.24745$ with $$|q-\\sqrt{150}|\\approx0.71159\\le 2t\\sqrt{150}=0.24\\sqrt{150}\\approx2.93939,$$ and for the skin depth $\\delta=1/q\\approx0.08669$ against $\\sqrt{2/b}=1/\\sqrt{150}\\approx0.08165$ one has $$|\\delta-\\sqrt{2/b}|\\approx0.00504\\le 4t\\sqrt{2/b}=0.48\\sqrt{2/b}\\approx0.03919 .$$ Thus the exact skin depth satisfies the stated good-conductor bound with the margin predicted by the theorem. (iii) for $\\sigma=0$ (same $\\varepsilon,\\mu,\\omega$) one has $b=0$, $q=0$, no attenuation and $\\delta=+\\infty$ by the convention of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]](c); the inequalities of (ii) do not apply, consistently with the exclusion recorded in [[pthm-em-conducting-skin-depth]](f). (iv) [Scope] the computation is an exact check of the stated inequalities for the declared model parameters (with dimensionless ratio $t$); it reports no measurement, no material identification and no empirical claim, and the decimal evaluations are displayed only as approximate illustrations of the exact expressions.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct substitution in the exact identities and bounds of the skin-depth theorem, with the decimal displays read as roundings of the exact radicals. Given: The declared parameters $\\varepsilon=4$, $\\mu=1$, $\\sigma=100$, $\\omega=3$ as the stated dimensionless representatives of the SI quantities of the Example, the branch assumption of [[post-em-material-closure]], and the exact quantities $a,b,r,p,q,\\delta,t$ of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]] and [[pthm-em-conducting-skin-depth]]. [A1] The declared Ohmic branch with $\\sigma>0$: the plane-wave attenuation theorem [[pthm-em-conducting-skin-depth]] holds, with its clauses (a)-(f) as stated, in particular the exact identities of (b) and the good-conductor bounds of (c) for $t\\le1/4$. [F1] The medium parameters ([[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]]): $a=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^2$, $b=\\mu\\sigma\\omega$, $r=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$, $p=\\sqrt{(r+a)/2}$, $q=\\sqrt{(r-a)/2}$, $\\delta=1/q$ for $\\sigma>0$ and $\\delta=+\\infty$ for $\\sigma=0$; $t:=a/b$. 1.1 [Substitution, clause (i)] By [F1], $a=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^2=1\\cdot4\\cdot9=36$ and $b=\\mu\\sigma\\omega=1\\cdot100\\cdot3=300$; hence $r=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}=\\sqrt{36^2+300^2}=\\sqrt{1296+90000}=\\sqrt{91296}$ and $t=a/b=36/300=3/25$. The regime condition $3/25\\le1/4$ is $12\\le25$ after multiplying by $100$, so the good-conductor bounds of [A1](c) apply. [given, A1, F1, algebra] 1.2 [Exact identities, clause (ii)] By [F1] and [A1](b), $p=\\sqrt{(r+a)/2}=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{91296}+36)/2}$, $q=\\sqrt{(r-a)/2}=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{91296}-36)/2}$ and $pq=b/2=150$ exactly. Since $91296<336^2=112896$, positivity and the square-root order give $r=\\sqrt{91296}<336$, hence $q^2=(r-36)/2<300/2=b/2$, so $q<\\sqrt{150}=\\sqrt{b/2}$; and the exact bounds of [A1](c) read $|q-\\sqrt{150}|\\le(6/25)\\sqrt{150}=\\frac{2\\varepsilon\\omega}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu\\sigma\\omega}{2}}$ and $|\\delta-\\sqrt{2/b}|\\le(12/25)\\sqrt{2/b}=\\frac{4\\varepsilon\\omega}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\mu\\sigma\\omega}}$. [A1, F1, algebra] 1.3 [The $\\sigma=0$ exclusion, clause (iii)] For the same $\\varepsilon,\\mu,\\omega$ and $\\sigma=0$, [F1] gives $b=0$, $r=a$, $q=\\sqrt{(r-a)/2}=0$ and $\\delta=+\\infty$ by the convention of [F1]; the identities and bounds of [A1](b),(c) carry the hypothesis $b>0$, equivalently $\\sigma>0$, and are not evaluated at $b=0$, which is the exclusion recorded in [A1](f). [given, A1, F1, algebra] 2.1 [Decimal displays and the bound comparisons] The decimal numbers displayed in clause (ii) of the Example are the stated roundings of the exact quantities $r=\\sqrt{91296}$, $p=\\sqrt{(r+36)/2}$, $q=\\sqrt{(r-36)/2}$, $\\delta=1/q$, $\\sqrt{150}$ and $\\sqrt{2/300}$; reading them as roundings, the comparisons $|q-\\sqrt{150}|\\le(6/25)\\sqrt{150}$ and $|\\delta-\\sqrt{2/b}|\\le(12/25)\\sqrt{2/b}$ displayed there are instances of the exact inequalities of step 1.2, and the numerical magnitudes quoted in the Example are not independent assertions of exact decimal arithmetic. In particular the inequalities hold with the margin predicted by [A1](c) for the declared dimensionless ratio $t=3/25$. [step 1.2, A1, algebra] 3.1 [Scope, clause (iv)] The computation used only the declared parameters, the branch assumption [A1] and the exact definitions [F1]; it reports no measurement, no material identification and no empirical or statistical claim, and the decimal displays of the Example are approximate illustrations of the exact expressions, as stated. No choice principle is used. [given, step 2.1, step 1.3, A1, F1] ∎",
          "physical_scope": "Numerical illustration for declared model parameters in the isotropic scalar Ohmic branch: the good-conductor bounds |q-sqrt(b/2)|<=2t*sqrt(b/2) and |delta-sqrt(2/b)|<=4t*sqrt(2/b) at eps*omega/sigma=3/25 and the exclusion of sigma=0 from the finite-depth approximation. Excluded: any measured conductivity, any laboratory material, and any experimental or statistical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.6.2 printed pp. 207-209: the skin depth $\\delta=\\sqrt{2/(\\mu\\omega\\sigma_{\\mathrm{DC}})}$ and its regime of validity in the low-frequency Ohmic conductor; the numerical values here are declared dimensionless illustrations of that closed form, not data from the source."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§9.4.1 printed pp. 394-395 and Problem 9.19(a),(b) printed p. 396: the good-conductor skin depth $\\delta=\\lambda/(2\\pi)$ and the poor-conductor frequency-independent depth; the monotone comparison of the exact $q$ with $\\sqrt{\\mu\\sigma\\omega/2}$ used here is the quantitative form of the source's 'good conductor' discussion. Retained scan with OCR noise; the bounds were verified by the local computation in the batch notes."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-brewster-angle-example",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The Brewster angle at an equal-permeability interface and the surviving $s$-reflection",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 21,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-oblique-fresnel",
            "def-em-medium-wave-parameters",
            "post-em-material-closure",
            "def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine",
            "def-principal-inverse-tangent",
            "def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant",
            "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
            "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-oblique-fresnel": "physical-result",
            "def-em-medium-wave-parameters": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-material-closure": "physical-assumption",
            "def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-principal-inverse-tangent": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt the setting of [[pthm-em-oblique-fresnel]] with two lossless media at rest, equal permeabilities $\\mu_1=\\mu_2=:\\mu>0$ and declared refractive indices $n_1=1$, $n_2=3/2$ (declared model parameters, not optical measurements), so that $\\varepsilon_j=n_j^{2}/(c^{2}\\mu)$ and $K_j=n_j\\omega/c$ in the notation of [[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]]. Then: (i) [Brewster angle] by [[pthm-em-oblique-fresnel]](c) the TM amplitude vanishes exactly at the unique angle $\\theta_B\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ with $\\tan\\theta_B=n_2/n_1=3/2$, i.e. $\\sin\\theta_B=3/\\sqrt{13}$, $\\cos\\theta_B=2/\\sqrt{13}$; the transmitted angle satisfies $n_1\\sin\\theta_B=n_2\\sin\\theta_t$, hence $\\sin\\theta_t=2/\\sqrt{13}$, and $\\theta_B+\\theta_t=\\pi/2$; (ii) [Amplitudes] at $\\theta_i=\\theta_B$ one has $Y^{p}_1=Y^{p}_2=\\sqrt{13}/(2c\\mu)$, hence $r_p=0$ and $t_p=1$; the remaining $s$-amplitudes are $Y^{s}_1=2/(\\sqrt{13}c\\mu)$, $Y^{s}_2=9/(2\\sqrt{13}c\\mu)$ and $$r_s=\\frac{2/\\sqrt{13}-9/(2\\sqrt{13})}{2/\\sqrt{13}+9/(2\\sqrt{13})}=-\\frac{5}{13},\\qquad t_s=\\frac{8}{13};$$ (iii) [Flux budget] with $R_s=|r_s|^{2}=25/169$ and $T_s=(Y^{s}_2/Y^{s}_1)|t_s|^{2}=(9/4)(64/169)=144/169$ one has $R_s+T_s=169/169=1$ exactly, while the TM channel has $R_p=0$, $T_p=1$; (iv) [Scope] the numbers are declared model parameters; the example is a deterministic computation of the classical interface model, reports no measurement, and a pure TM incident wave has zero reflected field at the Brewster angle. For incident data with a nonzero TE component, the surviving reflected field is TE-polarised because $r_p=0$ and $r_s\\ne0$; no unpolarised-beam statistics are modelled here.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct substitution in the oblique-incidence theorem and the medium parameters, with all numerical values kept exact. Given: The lossless two-media setting of [[pthm-em-oblique-fresnel]] with $\\mu_1=\\mu_2=\\mu>0$, $n_1=1$, $n_2=3/2$, $\\varepsilon_j=n_j^2/(c^2\\mu)$, and the Brewster angle $\\theta_B$ of [A1](c). [A1] The oblique-incidence theorem [[pthm-em-oblique-fresnel]]: its clause (a) gives the two admittance systems with $Y^s_1=k_{13}/(\\mu_1\\omega)$, $Y^p_1=\\varepsilon_1\\omega/k_{13}$, $Y^s_2=k_{t3}/(\\mu_2\\omega)$, $Y^p_2=\\varepsilon_2\\omega/k_{t3}$ and the amplitude formulas; clause (b) gives the angle law $n_1\\sin\\theta_i=n_2\\sin\\theta_t$, the normal fluxes $Y^\\chi_1|E^\\chi_i|^2/2$, $-Y^\\chi_1|E^\\chi_r|^2/2$, $Y^\\chi_2|E^\\chi_t|^2/2$ and $R_\\chi+T_\\chi=1$ with $R_\\chi=|r_\\chi|^2$, $T_\\chi=(Y^\\chi_2/Y^\\chi_1)|t_\\chi|^2$; clause (c) gives the unique Brewster angle with $\\tan\\theta_B=n_2/n_1$ for $n_1\\ne n_2$ and the nonzero $s$-reflection there. [F1] Medium parameters ([[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]]): $K_j=\\omega(\\varepsilon_j\\mu_j)^{1/2}=\\omega n_j/c$ for $\\mu_j=\\mu$, $k_{13}=\\sqrt{K_1^2-|\\kappa|^2}=K_1\\cos\\theta_i$ and $k_{t3}=K_2\\cos\\theta_t$ for $|\\kappa|<K_2$, and $\\varepsilon_j=n_j^2/(c^2\\mu)$. [F2] Trigonometry: $\\sin(\\arcsin y)=y$ with sine strictly increasing on $[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$ ([[def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine]]); $\\tan$ the strictly increasing principal inverse on $(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ with $\\tan(\\arctan y)=y$ ([[def-principal-inverse-tangent]]); $\\tan x=\\sin x/\\cos x$ ([[def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant]]); $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$ and $\\cos(-x)=\\cos x$ ([[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]]); sine strictly increasing on $[0,\\pi/2]$ with $\\sin 0=0$ ([[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]]); and $\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x$ ([[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]]). 1.1 [The Brewster angle and its sine and cosine, clause (i)] Since $n_1=1\\ne3/2=n_2$ and $\\mu_1=\\mu_2$, [A1](c) gives a unique Brewster angle $\\theta_B\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ with $\\tan\\theta_B=n_2/n_1=3/2$. By [F2], $\\sin^2\\theta_B=\\tan^2\\theta_B/(1+\\tan^2\\theta_B)=(9/4)/(13/4)=9/13$; since $\\theta_B\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ and sine is strictly increasing on $[0,\\pi/2]$ with $\\sin 0=0$, $\\sin\\theta_B>0$, hence $\\sin\\theta_B=3/\\sqrt{13}$; and $\\cos\\theta_B=\\sin\\theta_B/\\tan\\theta_B=(3/\\sqrt{13})/(3/2)=2/\\sqrt{13}$. [A1, F2, algebra] 2.1 [Transmitted angle and the right-angle relation, clause (i)] Snell's law $n_1\\sin\\theta_B=n_2\\sin\\theta_t$ of [A1](b) gives $\\sin\\theta_t=(2/3)(3/\\sqrt{13})=2/\\sqrt{13}$. Since also $\\cos\\theta_B=2/\\sqrt{13}=\\sin\\theta_t$ and $\\sin(\\pi/2-\\theta_B)=\\cos\\theta_B$ by [F2], the equality $\\sin\\theta_t=\\sin(\\pi/2-\\theta_B)$ holds with $\\theta_t,\\pi/2-\\theta_B\\in(0,\\pi/2)$; sine is strictly increasing on that interval, hence injective, so $\\theta_t=\\pi/2-\\theta_B$, i.e. $\\theta_B+\\theta_t=\\pi/2$. [step 1.1, A1, F2, algebra] 3.1 [The admittances, clause (ii)] By [F1], $K_1=\\omega/c$, $K_2=3\\omega/(2c)$, $k_{13}=K_1\\cos\\theta_B=(\\omega/c)(2/\\sqrt{13})$ and $k_{t3}=K_2\\cos\\theta_t=K_2\\sin\\theta_B=(3\\omega/(2c))(3/\\sqrt{13})=9\\omega/(2\\sqrt{13}c)$. With $\\varepsilon_1=1/(c^2\\mu)$ and $\\varepsilon_2=9/(4c^2\\mu)$, the admittances of [A1](a) are $Y^s_1=k_{13}/(\\mu\\omega)=2/(\\sqrt{13}c\\mu)$, $Y^s_2=k_{t3}/(\\mu\\omega)=9/(2\\sqrt{13}c\\mu)$, $Y^p_1=\\varepsilon_1\\omega/k_{13}=\\sqrt{13}/(2c\\mu)$ and $Y^p_2=\\varepsilon_2\\omega/k_{t3}=(9/(4c^2\\mu))\\omega\\cdot2\\sqrt{13}c/(9\\omega)=\\sqrt{13}/(2c\\mu)$, so $Y^p_1=Y^p_2$ exactly. [step 1.1, step 2.1, A1, F1, algebra] 4.1 [The amplitudes, clause (ii)] By [A1](a) and step 3.1, $r_p=(Y^p_1-Y^p_2)/(Y^p_1+Y^p_2)=0$ and $t_p=2Y^p_1/(Y^p_1+Y^p_2)=1$. For the $s$-channel, factoring the common positive scale $1/(\\sqrt{13}c\\mu)$ from numerator and denominator, $r_s=(2-9/2)/(2+9/2)=(-5/2)/(13/2)=-5/13$ and $t_s=2\\cdot2/(2+9/2)=4/(13/2)=8/13$. [step 3.1, A1, algebra] 5.1 [Flux budget, clause (iii)] By [A1](b), $R_s=|r_s|^2=25/169$ and $T_s=(Y^s_2/Y^s_1)|t_s|^2=(9/4)(64/169)=144/169$, so $R_s+T_s=(25+144)/169=169/169=1$ exactly; and in the TM channel $R_p=|r_p|^2=0$, $T_p=(Y^p_2/Y^p_1)|t_p|^2=1$, so $R_p+T_p=1$ as well. [step 4.1, A1, algebra] 6.1 [Scope, clause (iv)] All quantities are declared model parameters and the computation is exact rational or quadratic-irrational algebra of the interface model; no measured refractive index, no absorption, no unequal-permeability interface, no unpolarised-beam statistics and no empirical claim is used. Since $r_p=0$ and $r_s\\ne0$ at $\\theta_B$, an incident wave with a nonzero TE component has a surviving reflected TE component; no statement about random or unpolarised beams is made. No choice principle is used. [given, step 5.1, A1] ∎",
          "physical_scope": "Deterministic example of the oblique-incidence interface model for two declared lossless media with equal permeabilities and indices n_1=1, n_2=3/2: the unique Brewster angle tan(theta_B)=n_2/n_1, r_p=0, t_p=1, the surviving s-reflection r_s=-5/13 with R_s+T_s=1 exactly. Excluded: measured refractive indices, absorption, interfaces between unequal permeabilities, unpolarised-beam statistics and every empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.4.1 printed pp. 190-191: Brewster's angle from $n_1\\cos\\theta_T=n_2\\cos\\theta_I$ and $\\theta_I+\\theta_T=\\pi/2$, giving $\\tan\\theta_B=n_2/n_1$ and the statement that in the 'normal' (s) polarisation no such quenching occurs; the numerical instance $n_1=1$, $n_2=3/2$ is evaluated here from the formulas, not copied from the source."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "§9.3.3 printed pp. 390-391, Eqs. (9.109)-(9.112): Fresnel's equations for the parallel polarisation and Brewster's angle $\\tan\\theta_B=n_2/n_1$ for $\\mu_1=\\mu_2$; Problem 9.16 printed p. 392: the perpendicular polarisation has no Brewster angle and its reflected wave is never zero unless the media are optically indistinguishable. Retained scan with OCR noise; the exact rational values were verified by the local computation in the batch notes."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-evanescent-flux-example",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Total internal reflection: decaying transmitted field, vanishing normal flux and the reflection phase",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 21,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-oblique-fresnel",
            "pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields",
            "def-em-medium-wave-parameters",
            "post-em-material-closure",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
            "def-principal-inverse-tangent",
            "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
            "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions",
            "def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-oblique-fresnel": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields": "physical-result",
            "def-em-medium-wave-parameters": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-material-closure": "physical-assumption",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-principal-inverse-tangent": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt the setting of [[pthm-em-oblique-fresnel]] with equal permeabilities $\\mu_1=\\mu_2=:\\mu>0$ and declared indices $n_1=3/2$, $n_2=1$ (declared model parameters, not measurements), choose the tangential wavevector $\\kappa=(6\\omega/(5c))e_1$, so $\\hat\\kappa=e_1$ and $\\hat y=n\\times\\hat\\kappa=e_2$, and take the transverse-electric polarisation $e_j=E_j\\hat y$. Let the incidence angle be given by $\\sin\\theta_i=4/5$, which exceeds the critical angle $\\theta_c$ with $\\sin\\theta_c=n_2/n_1=2/3$, so $|\\kappa|=K_1\\sin\\theta_i=(6/5)(\\omega/c)>(\\omega/c)=K_2$. Then with $\\beta=\\sqrt{|\\kappa|^{2}-K_2^{2}}=(\\sqrt{11}/5)(\\omega/c)$ and $k_{13}=(\\omega/c)(3/2)(3/5)=(9/10)(\\omega/c)$: (i) [Reflection] $Y^{s}_1=9/(10\\mu c)$, $Y^{s}_2=i\\sqrt{11}/(5\\mu c)$ and $$r_s=\\frac{9/10-i\\sqrt{11}/5}{9/10+i\\sqrt{11}/5},\\qquad |r_s|=1,\\qquad t_s=\\frac{9/5}{9/10+i\\sqrt{11}/5},\\qquad |t_s|^{2}=\\frac{324}{125};$$ the reflection factor is the phase $r_s=e^{-2i\\varphi}$ with $\\varphi=\\arctan\\!\\bigl(2\\sqrt{11}/9\\bigr)\\approx0.63514703$ (about $36.39124^\\circ$), i.e. the reflected wave acquires the phase $-2\\varphi\\approx-1.27029406$ (about $-72.78249^\\circ$); (ii) [Transmitted field] the transmitted field is $E_t(x,t)=e^{-\\beta x_3}\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(E_t\\hat y\\,e^{i(|\\kappa|x_1-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$ with $\\beta=(\\sqrt{11}/5)(\\omega/c)$: it decays by the factor $e^{-\\sqrt{11}/5}\\approx0.51514$ when $x_3$ increases by $c/\\omega$, its period-averaged normal flux vanishes identically, $\\langle S_t\\cdot n\\rangle\\equiv0$, while its tangential averaged flux is positive, $\\langle S_t\\cdot\\hat\\kappa\\rangle=e^{-2\\beta x_3}\\,\\frac{(6/5)(\\omega/c)|E_t|^{2}}{2\\mu\\omega}=e^{-2\\beta x_3}\\frac{3|E_t|^{2}}{5\\mu c}$; (iii) [Flux budget] $|r_s|^{2}+(\\operatorname{Re}Y^{s}_2/Y^{s}_1)|t_s|^{2}=1+0=1$, so the whole incident normal averaged flux is reflected, while the evanescent field carries a tangential averaged flux only; (iv) [Scope] the example is an exact deterministic computation of the lossless interface model for declared parameters; it reports no measurement, and the presence of a tangential evanescent flux is not a claim about information speed or signal propagation.",
          "strategy": "Proof technique: direct substitution in the oblique-incidence and medium plane-wave theorems with exact rational and quadratic-irrational arithmetic, and an explicit phase computation via Euler's form. Given: The lossless two-media setting of [[pthm-em-oblique-fresnel]] with $\\mu_1=\\mu_2=\\mu>0$, $n_1=3/2$, $n_2=1$, the tangential wavevector $\\kappa=(6\\omega/(5c))e_1$, the transverse-electric data $e_j=E_j\\hat y$ with $\\hat y=e_2$, and the incidence data $\\sin\\theta_i=4/5$. [A1] The oblique-incidence theorem [[pthm-em-oblique-fresnel]]: clause (a) and (d) give $Y^s_1=k_{13}/(\\mu_1\\omega)$, $Y^s_2=k_{t3}/(\\mu_2\\omega)$, the amplitude formulas, and in the evanescent branch $|r_s|=1$, $|t_s|^2=4(Y^s_1)^2/((Y^s_1)^2+|Y^s_2|^2)>0$ together with the flux budget; clause (d) also records that the transmitted field decays and its normal averaged flux vanishes. [A2] The medium plane-wave structure [[pthm-em-medium-plane-wave-fields]]: the transmitted evanescent field of clause (e) with the substitution $n'=n$, $\\kappa_0=\\kappa$: the exact factorization, the vanishing normal component $\\langle S\\cdot n'\\rangle\\equiv0$ and the positive tangential averaged flux $\\langle S\\cdot\\hat\\kappa_0\\rangle=|\\kappa_0||e|^2e^{-2\\beta n'\\cdot x}/(2\\mu\\omega)$ for the transverse-electric data $e=E_0(n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0)$. [F1] Medium parameters ([[def-em-medium-wave-parameters]]): $K_j=\\omega(\\varepsilon_j\\mu_j)^{1/2}=\\omega n_j/c$, $k_{13}^2=K_1^2-|\\kappa|^2$, $\\beta^2=|\\kappa|^2-K_2^2$, $k_{t3}=i\\beta$ and $\\varepsilon_j=n_j^2/(c^2\\mu)$. [F2] Complex arithmetic and modulus ([[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]): $\\overline{z+w}=\\overline z+\\overline w$, $\\overline{zw}=\\overline z\\,\\overline w$, $|zw|=|z||w|$, $|z|^2=z\\overline z$ and $|z|\\ge0$ with $|z|=0$ only for $z=0$; in particular a complex number of modulus one is nonzero and the sum of a complex number and its conjugate is twice its real part. [F3] Trigonometry and phases: $\\arctan$ is the principal inverse tangent with $\\tan(\\arctan y)=y$ ([[def-principal-inverse-tangent]]); $\\tan x=\\sin x/\\cos x$ ([[def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant]]); $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$ and $1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$ ([[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]]); cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$ with $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$ ([[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]], [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]]); and for real $y$, $\\exp(iy)=\\cos y+i\\sin y$ with $|\\exp(iy)|=1$ ([[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]). 1.1 [The geometry and the evanescent data] With $\\kappa=(6\\omega/(5c))e_1$ one has $\\hat\\kappa=e_1$, $|\\kappa|=(6/5)(\\omega/c)$ and $\\hat y=n\\times\\hat\\kappa=e_3\\times e_1=e_2$; $K_1=(3/2)(\\omega/c)$, $K_2=\\omega/c$ by [F1], so $|\\kappa|=K_1\\sin\\theta_i$ with $\\sin\\theta_i=4/5$, and $|\\kappa|>K_2$ because $6/5>1$. Hence the evanescent branch applies: $\\beta=\\sqrt{|\\kappa|^2-K_2^2}=\\sqrt{(36/25-1)}(\\omega/c)=\\sqrt{11/25}\\,(\\omega/c)=(\\sqrt{11}/5)(\\omega/c)$ and $k_{13}=\\sqrt{K_1^2-|\\kappa|^2}=\\sqrt{(9/4-36/25)}(\\omega/c)=\\sqrt{81/100}\\,(\\omega/c)=(9/10)(\\omega/c)$; the critical angle satisfies $\\sin\\theta_c=n_2/n_1=2/3<4/5=\\sin\\theta_i$, both angles being in $(0,\\pi/2)$. [given, A1, F1, algebra] 2.1 [Admittances and reflection data, clause (i)] By [A1](a) and [F1], $Y^s_1=k_{13}/(\\mu_1\\omega)=9/(10\\mu c)$, $Y^s_2=k_{t3}/(\\mu_2\\omega)=i\\beta/(\\mu\\omega)=i\\sqrt{11}/(5\\mu c)$ and $t_s=2Y^s_1/(Y^s_1+Y^s_2)=(9/(5\\mu c))/((9/10+i\\sqrt{11}/5)/(\\mu c))=(9/5)/(9/10+i\\sqrt{11}/5)$; the numerator $9/10-i\\sqrt{11}/5$ of $r_s=(Y^s_1-Y^s_2)/(Y^s_1+Y^s_2)$ is the complex conjugate of its denominator, so $|r_s|=1$ by [F2] (and [A1](d)). For the transmitted modulus, $|t_s|^2=4(Y^s_1)^2/((Y^s_1)^2+|Y^s_2|^2)$ by [A1](d); computing $(Y^s_1)^2=81/(100\\mu^2c^2)$ and $|Y^s_2|^2=11/(25\\mu^2c^2)=44/(100\\mu^2c^2)$ gives $|t_s|^2=(324/100)/(125/100)=324/125$. [step 1.1, A1, F2, algebra] 2.2 [The transmitted field and its fluxes, clause (ii)] By $|t_s|^2$-normalised data we take the transmitted electric amplitude $E_t\\hat y$ with $E_t=t_sE_i$; the evanescent structure of [A2] applied with $n'=n=e_3$, $\\kappa_0=\\kappa$, $\\beta$ as in step 1.1 and $e=E_t\\hat y=E_t(n'\\times\\hat\\kappa_0)$ gives exactly $E_t(x,t)=e^{-\\beta x_3}\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(E_t\\hat y\\,e^{i(|\\kappa|x_1-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$, the identity $\\langle S_t\\cdot n\\rangle\\equiv0$, and the tangential averaged flux $\\langle S_t\\cdot\\hat\\kappa\\rangle=e^{-2\\beta x_3}|\\kappa||E_t|^2/(2\\mu\\omega)$; substituting $|\\kappa|=(6/5)(\\omega/c)$ gives $\\langle S_t\\cdot\\hat\\kappa\\rangle=e^{-2\\beta x_3}(6/5)(\\omega/c)|E_t|^2/(2\\mu\\omega)=e^{-2\\beta x_3}3|E_t|^2/(5\\mu c)$. The decay over a distance $c/\\omega$ is the factor $e^{-\\beta c/\\omega}=e^{-\\sqrt{11}/5}$, whose decimal display in clause (ii) is the stated rounding. [step 1.1, A1, A2, algebra] 3.1 [The reflection phase, clause (i)] Put $Y_1:=9/10$ and $\\beta':=\\sqrt{11}/5$ in the units $1/(\\mu c)$ and $\\alpha:=\\beta'/Y_1=2\\sqrt{11}/9$, so that $r_s=(1-i\\alpha)/(1+i\\alpha)$; let $\\varphi:=\\arctan\\alpha\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ and $w:=1+i\\alpha$, $|w|=\\sqrt{1+\\alpha^2}$. By [F3], $\\tan\\varphi=\\alpha$ and $1+\\tan^2\\varphi=\\sec^2\\varphi$, so $\\cos^2\\varphi=1/(1+\\alpha^2)=1/|w|^2$; since $\\cos\\varphi>0$ (cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$ with $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$ and $\\varphi\\in(0,\\pi/2)$), $\\cos\\varphi=1/|w|$ and $\\sin\\varphi=\\alpha\\cos\\varphi=\\alpha/|w|$. Euler's form of [F3] then gives $w=|w|(\\cos\\varphi+i\\sin\\varphi)=|w|e^{i\\varphi}$ and $\\overline w=|w|e^{-i\\varphi}$ by [F2], so $r_s=\\overline w/w=e^{-2i\\varphi}$. The decimal values displayed in clause (i) are the stated roundings of $\\varphi=\\arctan(2\\sqrt{11}/9)$ and of the reflected phase $-2\\varphi$. [step 2.1, F2, F3, algebra] 4.1 [Flux budget and scope, clauses (iii)-(iv)] By [A1](d), $|r_s|^2+(\\operatorname{Re}Y^s_2/Y^s_1)|t_s|^2=1$; here $\\operatorname{Re}Y^s_2=0$ by step 2.1, so the identity reads $1+0=1$: the whole incident normal averaged flux is reflected, while the tangential averaged flux of step 2.2 is positive and transports no normal flux. All quantities are declared model parameters; the computation is deterministic classical interface algebra, reports no measurement, and neither the tangential evanescent flux nor the reflection phase is a claim about information speed, signal propagation or empirical data. No choice principle is used. [given, step 2.1, step 2.2, A1, F2] ∎",
          "physical_scope": "Deterministic example of total internal reflection in the lossless interface model for declared indices n_1=3/2, n_2=1 at sin(theta_i)=4/5: the unit-modulus reflection factor with its phase, the evanescent transmitted field with decay factor e^{-beta x_3}, the vanishing normal and positive tangential averaged fluxes. Excluded: measured indices, absorption, information-speed or signal-propagation claims, and every empirical statement.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§7.4.2 printed pp. 191-193: total internal reflection beyond $\\sin\\theta_C=n_2/n_1$, the imaginary normal component of the transmitted wavevector and the decaying evanescent solution $E_{\\mathrm{trans}}=E_T e^{(ik_T\\cdot\\hat z-\\omega t)}e^{\\mp\\omega\\alpha x/v_2}$; the explicit numbers here are evaluated from those formulas and the interface amplitudes, not copied from the source."
              },
              {
                "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
                "locator": "Problem 9.37 printed pp. 413-415 (part (f) on p. 415): the evanescent transmitted field $E_T=E_{0T}e^{-Kz}e^{i(kx-\\omega t)}$ with $k=(\\omega/c)n_1\\sin\\theta_I$, $K=(\\omega/c)\\sqrt{n_1^{2}\\sin^{2}\\theta_I-n_2^{2}}$, the reflection coefficient of magnitude one for both polarisations and the statement that no energy is transmitted into medium 2 on average (part (f)); §9.3.3 printed pp. 388-390 for the amplitude formulas used. Retained scan with OCR noise; the displayed numbers were verified by the local computation in the batch notes."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
