          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $L_1,L_2>0$ and $R:=(0,L_1)\\times(0,L_2)$. For integers $m,n\\ge0$ put $h_{mn}(x_1,x_2):=\\cos\\bigl(\\frac{m\\pi x_1}{L_1}\\bigr)\\cos\\bigl(\\frac{n\\pi x_2}{L_2}\\bigr)$ and $k_{mn}^2:=\\bigl(\\frac{m\\pi}{L_1}\\bigr)^2+\\bigl(\\frac{n\\pi}{L_2}\\bigr)^2$; for integers $m,n\\ge1$ put $e_{mn}(x_1,x_2):=\\sin\\bigl(\\frac{m\\pi x_1}{L_1}\\bigr)\\sin\\bigl(\\frac{n\\pi x_2}{L_2}\\bigr)$. Then, with $\\Delta=\\partial_1^2+\\partial_2^2$, $\\nabla=(\\partial_1,\\partial_2)$ and the Riemann integrals over the rectangle of [[def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume]]:\n(a) $\\Delta h_{mn}=-k_{mn}^2h_{mn}$ on $R$ and $\\partial_1h_{mn}=0$ on both faces $x_1\\in\\{0,L_1\\}$, $\\partial_2h_{mn}=0$ on both faces $x_2\\in\\{0,L_2\\}$; hence $\\partial_nh_{mn}=0$ on every open face of $\\partial R$;\n(b) $\\Delta e_{mn}=-k_{mn}^2e_{mn}$ on $R$ and $e_{mn}=0$ on $\\partial R$ for $m,n\\ge1$;\n(c) [orthogonality and norms] $\\int_Rh_{mn}h_{m'n'}=0$ unless $(m,n)=(m',n')$, and $\\int_Rh_{mn}^2=\\frac{L_1L_2}{4}(1+\\delta_{m0})(1+\\delta_{n0})$; $\\int_Re_{mn}e_{m'n'}=0$ unless $(m,n)=(m',n')$, and $\\int_Re_{mn}^2=\\frac{L_1L_2}{4}$ for $m,n\\ge1$; the mixed integrals are instead $$\\int_Rh_{mn}e_{pq}=J_{mp}(L_1)J_{nq}(L_2),\\qquad J_{mp}(L):=\\int_0^L\\cos(m\\pi x/L)\\sin(p\\pi x/L)\\,dx,$$ for $m,n\\ge0$, $p,q\\ge1$. Here $J_{mp}(L)=0$ when $m,p$ have matching parity (including $p=m$), and $J_{mp}(L)=2pL/[\\pi(p^2-m^2)]$ when their parities differ. In particular $\\int_Rh_{00}e_{11}=4L_1L_2/\\pi^2$, so the sine and cosine systems are not mutually orthogonal;\n(d) [Dirichlet-type identities] $\\int_R|\\nabla h_{mn}|^2=k_{mn}^2\\int_Rh_{mn}^2$ for all $m,n\\ge0$ and $\\int_R|\\nabla e_{mn}|^2=k_{mn}^2\\int_Re_{mn}^2$ for $m,n\\ge1$.",
          "strategy": "Differentiate the products with [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]] and the product rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); the boundary values follow from $\\sin(0)=\\sin(m\\pi)=0$ and $\\cos'(t)=-\\sin t$. The orthogonality and norm values are iterated one-dimensional integrals computed with the product-to-sum identities of [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]] and the second fundamental theorem ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]) applied to the continuous integrands; Fubini on the rectangle ([[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]], [[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]) justifies iterated integration. For the mixed term use $\\cos(m\\pi x/L)\\sin(p\\pi x/L)=\\tfrac12[\\sin((p+m)\\pi x/L)+\\sin((p-m)\\pi x/L)]$. If $p=m$, the second term is identically zero and the first integrates to zero. Otherwise FTC gives $L[(1-(-1)^{p+m})/(p+m)+(1-(-1)^{p-m})/(p-m)]/(2\\pi)$, which is the stated parity formula (and includes $m=0$). The integer endpoint values are furnished by [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]] and [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]]. Rectangle Fubini then gives the product $J_{mp}J_{nq}$. For (d) integrate $\\partial_1(h\\partial_1h)=(\\partial_1h)^2+h\\partial_1^2h$ over the interval in $x_1$ for fixed $x_2$ with the FTC; the boundary term vanishes by (a), and the same in $x_2$; the $e_{mn}$ case is identical with the boundary term vanishing by (b). All steps are choice-free finite computations over the exhibited profiles.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
