            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $L>0$ and $R=(0,L_1)\\times(0,L_2)$ with $L_1,L_2>0$.\n(a) [One interval] The families $\\{\\cos(m\\pi x/L)\\}_{m\\ge0}$ and $\\{\\sin(m\\pi x/L)\\}_{m\\ge1}$ are orthogonal complete systems in the real Hilbert space $L^2(0,L)$ ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]], [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]], [[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]): each is orthogonal, and its closed linear span is all of $L^2(0,L)$.\n(b) [Rectangle] With $m,n$ ranging over $\\mathbb N_0$ in cosine slots and over $\\mathbb N_{\\ge1}$ in sine slots, each of the four product families $\\{\\cos\\frac{m\\pi x_1}{L_1}\\cos\\frac{n\\pi x_2}{L_2}\\}$, $\\{\\cos\\frac{m\\pi x_1}{L_1}\\sin\\frac{n\\pi x_2}{L_2}\\}$, $\\{\\sin\\frac{m\\pi x_1}{L_1}\\cos\\frac{n\\pi x_2}{L_2}\\}$ and $\\{\\sin\\frac{m\\pi x_1}{L_1}\\sin\\frac{n\\pi x_2}{L_2}\\}$ is an orthogonal complete system in $L^2(R)$; in particular the Neumann family $\\{h_{mn}\\}_{m,n\\ge0}$ and the Dirichlet family $\\{e_{mn}\\}_{m,n\\ge1}$ of [[lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities]] are orthogonal complete systems in $L^2(R)$.",
          "strategy": "(a) Rescale $x=L u/\\pi$ to period $2\\pi$ ([[thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure]]). For $f\\in L^2(0,L)$ orthogonal to every $\\cos(m\\pi x/L)$, extend $f$ evenly to $(-L,L)$ and $2L$-periodically; the extended $F$ lies in $L^2$ of the torus and every character coefficient $\\widehat F(k)$ is a fixed multiple of $\\int_0^Lf(x)\\cos(k\\pi x/L)\\,dx$, computed by splitting the integral at $0$ and substituting $x\\mapsto-x$ on $(-L,0)$ ([[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]]); hence all coefficients vanish and [[thm-trigonometric-system-is-complete-in-l-two-of-the-torus]] gives $F=0$, so $f=0$. The sine case uses the odd extension, whose character coefficients are the sine coefficients (the $k=0$ coefficient vanishes identically). (b) Orthogonality is product integration. If $f\\in L^2(R)$ is orthogonal to every $c_m(x_1)d_n(x_2)$, then for each $m$ the function $g_m(x_2):=\\int_0^{L_1}f(x_1,x_2)c_m(x_1)\\,dx_1$ is in $L^2(0,L_2)$ by Cauchy-Schwarz and Fubini ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]], [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]), and $\\int_0^{L_2}g_md_n=0$ for all $n$ by Fubini, so $g_m=0$ a.e. by (a). Taking the countable union of the exceptional null sets, for a.e. $x_2$ one has $\\int_0^{L_1}f(x_1,x_2)c_m(x_1)\\,dx_1=0$ for all $m$, so $f(\\cdot,x_2)=0$ a.e. in $x_1$ by (a), and Fubini gives $f=0$ a.e. Completeness as closed span is therefore established; the orthogonal expansions converge in $L^2$ by [[thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion]]. The argument is symmetric in the four parity choices.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zurich, 2024 FS (German), Chapter 13 'Wellenleiter' and 'Kavitaeten', PDF pp. 153-162 (printed pp. 13.1-13.10); complete 176-page author-hosted PDF and official TeX read, with the conjugate-error in (13.26), the 'Wendepunkt' mislabel and the general group-velocity restriction recorded as source defects not copied",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf"
              },
