                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-guide-cavity-geometry",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Rectangular PEC guide and cavity geometry, the zero-normal-B reflection class, and axis-relative TE/TM/TEM",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-guide-cavity-geometry",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 17,
          "deps": [
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$, its frame basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$, its SI units and its fields $E,B$ be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-si-conventions]] and [[def-em-fields]]. Fix $a,b>0$, write $x_t=(x_1,x_2)$, $e_z:=e_3$, and define $\\nabla_t f=(\\partial_1f,\\partial_2f,0)$, $\\Delta_t f=\\partial_1^2f+\\partial_2^2f$ and $\\operatorname{div}_tu_t=\\partial_1u_1+\\partial_2u_2$ for the stated regularity, using [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]].\n(a) [Straight guide] A straight guide region is $\\Omega_A:=A\\times\\mathbb R$ where $A$ is either a bounded connected $C^1$ plane domain ([[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]) or the explicit rectangular cross-section; the rectangle has corners and is treated by its open faces, not asserted to be a $C^1$ boundary domain, with longitudinal axis $e_z$; for a rectangular cross-section $A=R_{ab}:=(0,a)\\times(0,b)$ the region is called the rectangular guide and its walls are the four open faces $\\{x_1=0\\},\\{x_1=a\\},\\{x_2=0\\},\\{x_2=b\\}$. A rectangular cavity region is $\\Omega_c:=(0,a)\\times(0,b)\\times(0,d)$ for fixed $d>0$, with the six open faces as its walls. The separate smooth-cavity spectral branch uses an explicitly declared bounded $C^\\infty$ domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ with its smooth PEC boundary; it is not the rectangular reflection class. Spatial derivatives are taken at fixed $t$; the fields are defined on $\\Omega\\times I$ with the regularity declared by each theorem.\n(b) [PEC boundary model] On every open wall (a wall minus its edges) the ideal perfectly conducting boundary model declares the two conditions $n\\times E=0$ and $n\\cdot B=0$, where $n$ is the outward unit normal of that wall ($-e_1,+e_1,-e_2,+e_2$ on the four rectangular walls, and $\\pm e_j$ on the six cavity walls). Its physical reading is the ideal-conductor limit of the stationary interface conditions of [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]]: with $E=B=0$ inside the ideal conductor the tangential electric field and the normal magnetic field vanish, while the surface-charge and surface-current readings $\\sigma_f=-n\\cdot D$ and $K_f=-n\\times H$ for the filling-side traces (the wall normal points from the filling into the conductor, so the jump is $0$ minus the filling trace) are determined by the traces and are not prescribed here. The supplier's hypotheses (a compact embedded $C^2$ hypersurface with one-sided $C^1$ fields) cover a smooth stationary interface, not the $\\sigma\\to\\infty$ idealization nor the box edges; the guide/cavity conditions on the open walls are therefore declared as an idealization of this definition, and no pointwise statement on the edges is made.\n(c) [Zero-normal-B reflection class] For the rectangular box $Q=\\prod_{j=1}^3(0,L_j)$ with $L=(a,b,d)$, the reflection class is the class of locally integrable fields $E,B$ on $Q$ whose extensions to the rectangular torus $\\prod_j\\mathbb R/(2L_j\\mathbb Z)$, defined by extending $E_1$ evenly across the $x_1$-walls and oddly across the $x_2$-, $x_3$-walls and cyclically for $E_2,E_3$, and extending $B_1$ oddly across $x_1$ and evenly across $x_2,x_3$ and cyclically for $B_2,B_3$, are $L^2$ fields, together with the distributional constraints that $\\operatorname{div}E$ and $\\operatorname{div}B$ vanish on the torus for the extensions. This is the declared weak PEC model used for the cavity initial-value problem; when the reflected extensions are continuous across the walls, their odd components vanish there, giving $n\\times E=0$ and $n\\cdot B=0$ on the open walls. Smoothness only inside $Q$ does not imply this extension continuity. Initial data not in this class (for example data with trapped normal $B$) are excluded, as are the general $H(\\operatorname{curl})$ boundary classes, which are defined by the mathematical suppliers and are not identified here with the classical conditions.\n(d) [TE, TM, TEM] For a guide or cavity field $(E,B)$, the longitudinal components are $E_z:=E\\cdot e_z$ and $B_z:=B\\cdot e_z$; the field is called TE (transverse electric) if $E_z\\equiv0$, TM (transverse magnetic) if $B_z\\equiv0$, and TEM if both vanish, always relative to the named axis $e_z$.\n(e) [Separated phasor convention] For $\\omega>0$ and $\\beta\\in\\mathbb C$, a $\\beta$-$\\omega$ separated phasor field on the guide is a real field of the form $F(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(\\tilde F(x_1,x_2)e^{i(\\beta x_3-\\omega t)}\\bigr)$ with a complex amplitude $\\tilde F$, componentwise; $\\omega/(2\\pi)$ is the frequency and $\\beta$ the longitudinal propagation constant; when $\\beta$ is real, $\\beta>0$ describes propagation in the $+e_z$ direction under this phase convention, while a complex $\\beta$ gives the spatial factor $e^{-\\operatorname{Im}\\beta x_3}$ and may grow in the opposite direction; each consuming branch states its selection.\n(f) [Scope] The definition fixes the geometry, the PEC boundary model, the weak reflection class and the mode terminology; it asserts no field equation, no constitutive law and no existence or completeness of modes, Rectangular TE/TM candidates and the rectangular reflection class, declared smooth bounded PEC cavity spectral classes, and smooth straight-guide TEM potential branches (including the coaxial annulus) are within this geometry. No general non-rectangular TE/TM guide theory, circular-guide spectral classification, open-end or junction scattering, or arbitrary lossy model is supplied. The same stationary PEC geometry also hosts the explicitly declared constant scalar Ohmic cavity class and the full longitudinal finite-energy reflection class of the straight rectangular guide; constitutive and evolution claims belong to their consuming theorems, not this definition.",
          "strategy": "An explicit type/domain/convention definition: geometry and transversality are fixed by coordinates and normals; the PEC model is declared with its interface reading and its excluded idealization/edges; the reflection class is defined by the reflection rules and distributional divergence constraints; the phasor convention is stated as an encoding of real fields. No differential equation is introduced.",
          "physical_scope": "The ideal PEC boundary model of the straight rectangular guide and cavity (and of straight guides with a bounded connected C1 cross-section) in the declared laboratory chart, with lossless and explicitly declared constant scalar Ohmic fillings distinguished by their consuming theorems, together with the zero-normal-B reflection class and the TE/TM/TEM classification. The boundary model is an idealization of an interface with a perfectly conducting medium on the open walls; the definition asserts no field equations, constitutive laws or mode completeness.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
