            "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the constant isotropic inertial-rest material branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] with $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$, $\\sigma\\ge0$, $D=\\varepsilon E$, $H=B/\\mu$, $J_{\\rm ext}=0$. The exact broadband physical reading additionally requires $\\varepsilon\\mu\\ge c^{-2}$ as in that closure; otherwise the positive coefficients define its finite-band effective phase model without a universal front-speed claim. Put $v^2=(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1}$ and $\\gamma=\\sigma/\\varepsilon$. The smooth equations of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]] with initially zero volume charge give $E_t=v^2\\operatorname{curl}B-\\gamma E$, $B_t=-\\operatorname{curl}E$ and zero divergences; in the finite-energy classes below these first-order equations with the declared PEC weak/variational boundary realization are adopted explicitly, rather than deduced from a smooth-only postulate. The quadratic stored energy is $\\mathcal U(t)=\\tfrac12(\\varepsilon\\|E(t)\\|_2^2+\\mu^{-1}\\|B(t)\\|_2^2)$, consistent with [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]].\n(a) [Rectangular cavity] In the exact zero-normal-$B$ reflection-parity and divergence class of [[lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness]] and [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]](c), every initial pair has a unique $C(\\mathbb R;L^2)$ distributional solution of this damped system in the same class. Finite Fourier mode sums converge in energy norm uniformly on compact time intervals and are dense at every time. The Fourier coefficient equations at $k_j=\\pi n_j/L_j$ are $\\widehat E_t=iv^2 k\\times\\widehat B-\\gamma\\widehat E$, $\\widehat B_t=-ik\\times\\widehat E$, with both transverse constraints and the original parities retained.\n(b) [Smooth closed PEC cavity] Assume the full Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) inherited from the smooth spectral and elliptic suppliers. On a bounded smooth cavity $\\Omega$, let $X,V,H$ and its smooth orthonormal eigenfields $u_j$, $\\lambda_j\\ge0$, be exactly [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]] and [[thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness]]. Define the full weak normal-zero divergence-free magnetic space $$M:=\\{B\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3):\\int_\\Omega B\\cdot\\nabla\\psi=0\\text{ for every smooth scalar }\\psi\\text{ on }\\overline\\Omega\\}.$$ This is the distributional divergence constraint together with weak zero normal flux; for smooth fields it is $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $n\\cdot B=0$. For every positive eigenvalue put $w_j=\\operatorname{curl}u_j/\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}$ and put $K_M=\\{B\\in M:\\operatorname{curl}B=0\\text{ distributionally}\\}$. Then $$M=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}\\{w_j:\\lambda_j>0\\}}^{L^2}\\mathbin{\\oplus}K_M.$$ The declared first-order PEC modal energy class is $H\\times M$, with weak modal equations $a_j'=v^2\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}b_j-\\gamma a_j$, $b_j'=-\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}a_j$ for positive modes, $a_j'=-\\gamma a_j$ for electric zero modes, and $(B,K_M)'=0$ for every kernel test. Define $D_M$ as the graph-norm completion of finite $w_j$ sums plus $K_M$, with norm squared $\\|w\\|_2^2+\\|\\operatorname{curl}w\\|_2^2$. The equivalent weak-in-time variational equations are $\\langle E_t,\\phi\\rangle=v^2\\langle B,\\operatorname{curl}\\phi\\rangle-\\gamma\\langle E,\\phi\\rangle$ for $\\phi\\in V$, and $\\langle B_t,\\psi\\rangle=-\\langle E,\\operatorname{curl}\\psi\\rangle$ for $\\psi\\in D_M$. They use the actual electric tangential-zero domain $V$ and magnetic normal-zero space $M$, with no claim of maximal-trace equality. For every initial pair in $H\\times M$ there is a unique $C(\\mathbb R;H\\times M)$ solution of that declared realization, which also satisfies the displayed evolution equations distributionally in the interior. Finite electric and paired-magnetic modal sums, with the exact static magnetic kernel component retained, converge uniformly on compact time intervals in energy norm. No equality with every maximal rough tangential-trace realization is asserted.\n(c) [Exact energy balance, decay and zero sectors] In both declared classes, for every $t_0<t_1$, $$\\mathcal U(t_1)+\\sigma\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\|E(t)\\|_2^2\\,dt=\\mathcal U(t_0),\\qquad \\mathcal U'(t)=-\\sigma\\|E(t)\\|_2^2\\le0.$$ For positive smooth-cavity modes $a_j''+\\gamma a_j'+v^2\\lambda_ja_j=0$. If $\\Delta_j=\\gamma^2/4-v^2\\lambda_j$ is positive, the two roots are $-\\gamma/2\\pm\\sqrt{\\Delta_j}$; if negative they are $-\\gamma/2\\pm i\\sqrt{-\\Delta_j}$; at zero include $(A+Bt)e^{-\\gamma t/2}$. This finite scalar characteristic classification applies to every parameter $\\lambda\\ge0$ and uses no choice principle; its general zero-eigenvalue associated-wave solutions and the narrower first-order compatibility are stated separately in (d). The full Choice premise belongs to the smooth-cavity spectral realization, not to these finite root formulas. For $\\sigma>0$ every positive mode decays, electric zero modes obey $a_j(t)=a_j(0)e^{-\\gamma t}$, and magnetic kernel data stay constant. In the smooth class the solution therefore tends in energy norm as $t\\to+\\infty$ to $(0,P_{K_M}B(0))$. For $\\sigma=0$ energy is constant, electric zero modes are static and the original lossless branch is recovered. The zero sectors are retained, not divided by their eigenvalues; no universal decay to zero in the presence of magnetic kernel data is claimed.\n(d) [Associated second-order branch] Independently of first-order compatibility, the same spectral basis gives the damped associated electric wave for every $q_0\\in V$, $q_1\\in H$, with $q\\in C(\\mathbb R;V)$, $q'\\in C(\\mathbb R;H)$ and modal convergence uniformly on compact times. Its coefficients satisfy $a_j''+\\gamma a_j'+v^2\\lambda_ja_j=0$ with arbitrary prescribed initial coefficients. At $\\lambda_j=0$ they are $\\alpha_j+\\beta_j(1-e^{-\\gamma t})/\\gamma$ when $\\gamma>0$, and $\\alpha_j+t\\beta_j$ when $\\gamma=0$. Such arbitrary second-order data need not arise from the first-order Maxwell system: its zero modes additionally satisfy $a_j'=-\\gamma a_j$. This broader wave is retained alongside the physical first-order branch. All square roots and real derivatives use [[thm-of-square-roots]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and the real-component exponential formula [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]].\n(e) [Scope] The conductor occupies the declared cavity interior while its stationary walls obey the independent ideal PEC boundary model. No arbitrary loss tensor, memory law, changing interface, open-end, rough-wall or empirical conclusion is made. Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) suffices for the rectangular/Fourier branch; the smooth branch retains its actual full Choice premise. Weak energy balance is proved by modal limits and does not assert an unprovided nonlinear weak local Poynting theorem.",
          "strategy": "For (a), use the exact G2 product Fourier representation and parity constraints. Add the scalar term $-\\gamma\\widehat E$ to its finite real/imaginary matrix system. It is globally Lipschitz, so [[cor-globally-lipschitz-odes-have-global-solutions]] supplies a unique solution on all real time. Direct conjugate triple-product cancellation gives $(\\varepsilon|\\widehat E|^2+\\mu^{-1}|\\widehat B|^2)'=-2\\sigma|\\widehat E|^2$. Divergence constraints are preserved ($k\\cdot\\widehat E$ obeys $d'=-\\gamma d$, $k\\cdot\\widehat B$ is constant), and damping preserves each original parity. Modal energy is nonincreasing forward and at most $e^{2\\gamma T}$ times its initial value on $[-T,T]$, because its derivative is bounded below by $-2\\gamma$ times itself; multiply by $e^{2\\gamma t}$ and differentiate to prove that bound. Parseval therefore controls every omitted tail uniformly on compact times. Finite solutions converge in $C_tL^2$ and pass the linear distributional equations against compact-time smooth periodic tests by Cauchy-Schwarz, exactly as in G2. The current G2 continuous-coefficient-minus-FTC-primitive argument, using [[thm-ftc-first-part]], [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]], [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]] and [[thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant]], converts weak coefficient equations to classical ODEs and proves uniqueness.\nFor (b), G3's full-$X$ identity gives $(\\operatorname{curl}u_j,\\operatorname{curl}u_l)=\\lambda_j\\delta_{jl}$, so the $w_j$ are orthonormal. Smooth tangential-zero $u_j$ imply $w_j\\in M$: ordinary curl integration by parts against $\\nabla\\psi$ has zero boundary term and zero interior curl. Indeed [[lem-the-divergence-and-curl-of-a-cross-product]] applied to $u_j\\times\\nabla\\psi$ and [[thm-the-curl-of-a-gradient-vanishes]] give its divergence $(\\nabla\\psi)\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}u_j$; [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]] integrates this to the boundary term $(n\\times u_j)\\cdot\\nabla\\psi=0$. All inputs are smooth on the bounded smooth closure, and the inherited choice premise meets the actual divergence supplier. G3's weighted $V$ completeness proves that the closed span of these $w_j$ is the closure of $\\operatorname{curl}V$. Since $M$ is a closed subspace, orthogonal projection [[thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace]] decomposes any $B\\in M$ into that closure and its orthogonal complement. To identify the latter with $K_M$, take a compactly supported smooth vector test $\\phi$. The actual smooth Dirichlet solver [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]] with coefficient identity, zero trace and source $-\\operatorname{div}\\phi$ supplies $f\\in C^\\infty(\\overline\\Omega)$ with $\\Delta f=\\operatorname{div}\\phi$. Then $\\phi-\\nabla f\\in V$ (zero divergence and tangential trace), and it has the same curl as $\\phi$. Orthogonality to $\\operatorname{curl}V$ therefore gives $\\langle B,\\operatorname{curl}\\phi\\rangle=0$, which is exactly distributional $\\operatorname{curl}B=0$. Conversely if $B\\in M$ is distributionally curl-free, pairing with each $u_j$ is legitimate by the actual tangential-zero collar cutoff: $\\eta_\\delta u_j\\to u_j$ and their curls converge in $L^2$, since $n\\times u_j=O(s)$ on a layer $s\\le2\\delta$. The distributional compact-test pairing then gives $(B,w_j)=0$. The collar exists by [[thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem]] and uses [[def-the-standard-smooth-step-function]], with their stated choice premises. This proves the full decomposition, including arbitrary magnetic kernel data, without assuming a missing Hodge theorem or imposing electric tangential-zero data on $B$.\nEach paired modal system and every electric zero equation has unique global solutions; keep the given $K_M$ component exactly. Orthogonality and the same scalar weighted-energy identity prove compact-time uniform energy-tail convergence for arbitrary $H\\times M$ data by [[thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion]]. Finite paired fields satisfy the interior equations because $\\operatorname{curl}w_j=\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}u_j$ distributionally by G3 and $\\operatorname{curl}u_j=\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}w_j$, while kernel fields have zero curl. Linear compact-test pairings pass to the limit in $L^2$. The finite spectral tests are dense in $V$ with its G3 graph norm and in $D_M$ with its defined curl graph norm, so Cauchy-Schwarz passes the modal time-test equations to precisely the stated variational equations. Conversely testing those equations at $u_j$, $w_j$ and every $K_M$ vector gives the modal equations. The weak modal boundary realization therefore explicitly tests these spectral equations, so the scalar ODE uniqueness and complete decompositions give uniqueness in the stated class; merely satisfying interior equations without this boundary realization is not asserted sufficient. For strong first-order solutions with $E\\in C^1_tH\\cap C_tV$ and $B\\in C^1_tL^2\\cap C_tH(\\operatorname{curl})$, the same collar pairing gives $a_j'=v^2(B,\\operatorname{curl}u_j)-\\gamma a_j$. Differentiating using $B'=-\\operatorname{curl}E$ and the full-$X$ identity gives $a_j''+\\gamma a_j'+v^2\\lambda_ja_j=0$, with $a_j'=-\\gamma a_j$ at zero eigenvalues.\nFor (c), sum the finite identities and integrate them on $[t_0,t_1]$. Uniform energy-norm convergence on that compact interval passes both energy and the continuous $\\|E(t)\\|_2^2$ integral to the limit, proving the exact balance. FTC then yields its derivative for every time. This argument proves rough finite-energy dissipation without using a smooth-only local balance; the smooth reading agrees with the restricted material-energy supplier. The characteristic roots follow by completing the square; their explicit real exponential or sine/cosine solutions are verified by the stated derivative suppliers. Repeated roots retain $te^{-\\gamma t/2}$. For $\\gamma>0$, $\\lambda>0$ the real roots are negative or the complex roots have negative real part, so each positive paired mode tends to zero by [[thm-exponential-limits-and-range]], with the repeated-root polynomial factor covered by [[thm-exponential-beats-every-polynomial]]; electric zero modes decay and the magnetic kernel is static. Forward contractivity plus a finite-head/finite-tail argument passes this to the whole energy norm. No magnetic kernel component is discarded.\nFor (d), repeat G3's weighted $V$ Parseval argument for arbitrary initial displacement/velocity. For every positive mode $Q_j=|a_j'|^2+v^2\\lambda_j|a_j|^2$ satisfies $Q_j'=-2\\gamma|a_j'|^2$; the positive eigenvalues have a positive lower bound, so compact-time exponential bounds on $Q_j$ control the $V$ displacement and $H$ velocity tails. The finitely many zero modes have the separately displayed solutions. Uniform limits and the same finite-sum FTC estimate used by G3 identify the actual $H$ derivative and pass the variational second-order equation against compact-time tests. This mathematical wave-energy estimate is distinct from the physical first-order stored energy; no compatibility of arbitrary $V\\times H$ data is inferred. The first-order zero relation follows directly from its modal equation. No original spectral/variational supplier Statement is changed.",
          "physical_scope": "The explicitly declared constant scalar Ohmic PEC energy classes of K10, including the lossless sigma=0 branch, with exact modal/Fourier completeness and energy balance. No arbitrary dispersive, nonlinear, hysteretic, nonsmooth-wall, open-end or junction-scattering claim; no empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
