          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and the constant isotropic material branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] with $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$, $\\sigma\\ge0$, $J_{\\rm ext}=0$ and initially zero volume charge. For an exact broadband physical interpretation retain the closure condition $\\varepsilon\\mu\\ge c^{-2}$; without it this is the declared effective model with no universal front-speed claim. Put $v^2=(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1}$, $\\gamma=\\sigma/\\varepsilon$. On the infinite straight rectangular guide $Q=(0,a)\\times(0,b)\\times\\mathbb R$ of [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]], declare the finite-energy reflection class as follows. Extend in the two transverse variables to $T_{ab}^2\\times\\mathbb R$, $T_{ab}^2=\\mathbb R/(2a\\mathbb Z)\\times\\mathbb R/(2b\\mathbb Z)$. The electric components have transverse parities even/odd, odd/even, odd/odd respectively; magnetic components have odd/even, even/odd, even/even respectively. Require the extended real fields to be $L^2$ and have both divergences zero distributionally. When the reflected extensions are continuous across the walls, their odd components give the four-wall conditions $n\\times E=0$, $n\\cdot B=0$; it is a precise weak PEC realization, not an assertion about every rough trace class. There is no end cap and no junction or open-end scattering datum.\n(a) [Complete mixed transform] Let $\\phi_{mn}(x_t)=(4ab)^{-1/2}e^{i\\pi(mx_1/a+nx_2/b)}$, $(m,n)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$, be the orthonormal transverse characters. For each component first form $F_{mn}(z)=\\int_{T_{ab}^2}F(x_t,z)\\overline{\\phi_{mn}(x_t)}\\,dx_t$, then apply the exact negative-sign $2\\pi$ Fourier transform $\\mathcal F_2$ of [[thm-plancherel]] in $z$. Write $\\widehat F_{mn}(\\xi)=\\mathcal F_2F_{mn}(\\xi)$ and $\\beta=2\\pi\\xi$. The transform is a unitary representation of $L^2(T_{ab}^2\\times\\mathbb R)$ by $\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^2;L^2(\\mathbb R))$, with inverse in $L^2$ from [[thm-l-two-fourier-inversion]], and $$\\|F\\|_{L^2(Q)}^2=\\tfrac14\\sum_{m,n}\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\widehat F_{mn}(\\xi)|^2\\,d\\xi$$ for these reflection fields. The parity and reality relations are retained, including $\\widehat F_{-m,-n}(-\\xi)=\\overline{\\widehat F_{mn}(\\xi)}$. With $K=(\\pi m/a,\\pi n/b,2\\pi\\xi)$, the divergence constraints become $K\\cdot\\widehat E=K\\cdot\\widehat B=0$ for almost every $\\xi$. This is a complete transverse sin/cos and longitudinal $L^2$ Fourier representation, not pointwise Fourier convergence or a finite-energy monochromatic plane wave.\n(b) [Unique energy evolution] Adopt in that weak reflection class the constant-Ohmic first-order material evolution $E_t=v^2\\operatorname{curl}B-\\gamma E$, $B_t=-\\operatorname{curl}E$, distributionally, consistent with the smooth [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]] and [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]] branches. For every initial pair in the class there is a unique $C(\\mathbb R;L^2(Q))$ solution in the same class. Its mixed coefficients solve, for every transverse index and almost every $\\xi$, $$\\partial_t\\widehat E=iv^2K\\times\\widehat B-\\gamma\\widehat E,\\qquad\\partial_t\\widehat B=-iK\\times\\widehat E.$$ Finite transverse sums and bounded longitudinal frequency truncations reconstruct the solution in energy norm uniformly on compact time intervals; such reconstructed fields are dense in the class at every time. They retain the divergence constraints and the original parities, including zero coefficient cases without dividing by $K$ or $|K|$.\n(c) [Energy and dispersion] The total stored energy of this whole infinite guide is finite and equals $$\\mathcal U(t)=\\tfrac18\\sum_{m,n}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(\\varepsilon|\\widehat E|^2+\\mu^{-1}|\\widehat B|^2)\\,d\\xi.$$ For every $t_0<t_1$, $\\mathcal U(t_1)+\\sigma\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\|E(t)\\|_{L^2(Q)}^2dt=\\mathcal U(t_0)$, and $\\mathcal U'(t)=-\\sigma\\|E(t)\\|_2^2$. At $\\sigma=0$ energy is conserved and each constrained electric coefficient satisfies $\\widehat E''+v^2|K|^2\\widehat E=0$. A nonzero harmonic coefficient $\\widehat E(t)=e\\,e^{-i\\omega t}$ with $e\\in\\mathbb C^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and $\\omega>0$ therefore requires $\\omega^2=v^2[(\\pi m/a)^2+(\\pi n/b)^2+\\beta^2]$; the zero coefficient imposes no frequency condition. These are the exact finite transverse-mode matrices of the approved K10 branch; individual separated TE/TM candidate fields still have their own cutoff and zero-profile restrictions, and no TE$_{00}$ mode is created by a scalar constant coefficient. For $\\sigma>0$ the electric equation is $\\widehat E''+\\gamma\\widehat E'+v^2|K|^2\\widehat E=0$ with the distinct/repeated-root alternatives of the choice-free finite-parameter clause of [[pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay]](c), here at $\\lambda=|K|^2\\ge0$. Its full-Choice smooth-cavity realization is not used. At $K=0$ keep the direct first-order equations $\\widehat E\\prime=-\\gamma\\widehat E$, $\\widehat B\\prime=0$, without dividing by $K$; the original parity and $L^2$ relations still apply. No signal-speed or empirical inference is made.\n(d) [Scope] This theorem supplies the previously missing complete longitudinal $L^2$ expansion of the declared straight rectangular guide, including its lossless and constant-Ohmic energy evolutions. It makes no completeness claim for arbitrary non-rectangular, dispersive, nonlocal, nonlinear, rough-wall or open-end/junction problems. The weak energy result is proved by linear mixed-transform evolution and modal limits, not by applying a nonlinear smooth Poynting identity to rough data.",
          "strategy": "For (a), the two transverse character factors are the complete systems used by the G2 proof [[lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness]]; equivalently use the exact sine/cosine product completeness [[lem-em-rectangular-transverse-mode-completeness]]. The normalized characters have norm1 on the $4ab$-area torus fundamental rectangle by elementary exponential integration. Product completeness and Hilbert Parseval [[thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion]], followed by Fubini/Tonelli ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]), give for almost every $z$ the transverse expansion and the integrated sum of squared coefficient norms. Apply the actual AComega unitary [[thm-plancherel]] separately to each of the countably many scalar coefficient functions; surjectivity and [[thm-l-two-fourier-inversion]] give the inverse coefficient functions. Their transverse series converges in product $L^2$, proving both directions of the isometry without an unprovided vector-valued Fourier theorem. Reflection multiplies the physical $Q$ norm squared by4, giving the exact $1/4$ factor. Parity relations follow by the coordinate changes on the finite torus; conjugation/reflection reality follows first for real integrable compact longitudinal data by the defining integral, then by $L^2$ approximation and transform continuity. No pointwise reconstruction is asserted.\nThe derivative correspondence is justified on smooth periodic transverse tests times longitudinal Schwartz tests by the exact identity $\\mathcal F(\\partial_z\\psi)=2\\pi i\\xi\\widehat\\psi$ of [[thm-fourier-transform-maps-schwartz-space-continuously-to-itself]], finite transverse integration by parts and complex bilinear/componentwise pairing. These Schwartz spatial tests are legitimate for the originally compact-test equations: multiply them by $\\chi_R(z)$ from [[lem-schwartz-cutoffs-from-the-standard-smooth-step]], with $\\chi_R=1$ on $|z|\\le R$ and $\\|\\chi_R\\prime\\|_\\infty\\le C/R$, and pass to the limit, since the tests and their first derivatives converge in $L^2$ and all field pairings are bounded by Cauchy-Schwarz. The $L^2$ pairing then extends this test identity to distributional divergence constraints. Conversely if $K\\cdot\\widehat F=0$ almost everywhere, testing with any finite transverse/Schwartz product makes the weak divergence pairing zero. General compact smooth tests are reached by the transverse $L^2$ expansions of their derivatives and the Schwartz transform isomorphism; all required pairings are bounded by Cauchy-Schwarz. Thus the exact original constrained class corresponds to the stated a.e. coefficient constraints, not just a scalar basis without Gauss equations.\nFor (b), fix $(m,n,\\xi)$; the real and imaginary parts give a finite globally Lipschitz matrix system on all real time, so [[cor-globally-lipschitz-odes-have-global-solutions]] gives its unique solution. The entries depend continuously on $\\xi$, and [[thm-continuous-dependence-of-odes-on-initial-data-and-parameters]] gives a continuous fundamental solution matrix on every compact parameter/time set: apply its local common-cylinder conclusion and finite gluing along a compact time interval, using the uniform energy bound below to keep nearby solutions in a bounded state cylinder. Multiplying that continuous matrix by the measurable initial coefficient vector therefore gives measurable coefficient functions; no uncountable choice of solutions is made. Direct conjugate cross-product cancellation yields $$q'= -2\\sigma|\\widehat E|^2,\\qquad q=\\varepsilon|\\widehat E|^2+\\mu^{-1}|\\widehat B|^2,$$ hence $q(t)\\le e^{2\\gamma T}q(0)$ for $|t|\\le T$ and forward contractivity. The constraints and parity/reality relations persist by the same algebra and matrix uniqueness. Spectral cutoffs $|m|,|n|\\le N$, $|\\xi|\\le N$ preserve every relation. Their initial energy tails tend to0 by Tonelli and [[thm-dominated-convergence]], and the uniform bound makes the time-dependent tails tend to0 uniformly on compact times. Reconstruct by the proved mixed inverse: each truncated solution is strongly differentiable in $L^2$ because $K$ is bounded on that cutoff. Its linear equations follow from the test derivative identity. Compact-time $L^2$ limits pass the equations against compact smooth tests and are continuous in time with the prescribed initial data.\nFor uniqueness, take mixed coefficients of any weak $C_tL^2$ reflection solution and project to the same bounded spectral cutoff. The test derivative identity turns its evolution into $f'=A_Nf$ distributionally in time in a Hilbert space, where the multiplication matrix $A_N$ is bounded. Finite transverse tests and smooth frequency tests supported in $(-N,N)$ are dense in the cutoff $L^2$ space (the cutoff endpoints have measure zero); their inverse transforms are Schwartz. Thus the compact/Schwartz test identity extends to every cutoff $L^2$ vector using the bounded multiplier and Cauchy-Schwarz. Subtract the continuous Hilbert-valued primitive $\\int_0^tA_Nf(s)ds$; pairing with every fixed $L^2$ vector and applying the scalar zero-distribution-derivative theorem [[thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant]] shows that the difference is its constant initial value. The Hilbert primitive is constructed by finite Riemann sums: on a fixed compact time interval, norm continuity is uniform, and the difference of any two sufficiently fine tagged sums is bounded by the interval length times the uniform oscillation bound, after passing to a common refinement. Completeness of $L^2$ supplies the limit, independent of tags, and the same bound gives uniform limits for all integration endpoints; its derivative is identified by the usual average-increment norm estimate, the vector version of the [[thm-ftc-first-part]] argument. The difference of two such solutions with zero data satisfies $d(t)=\\int_0^t A_Nd(s)ds$; on an interval of length $h$ with $h\\|A_N\\|<1$ the supremum norm forces $d=0$. Finitely iterate these intervals on any compact time interval, in either direction. Let the cutoffs exhaust the representation to obtain uniqueness of the whole field. This proves the continuum-frequency weak uniqueness rather than assuming that an arbitrary weak solution has differentiable pointwise frequency trajectories.\nFor (c), finite/cutoff modal energy identities integrate over $\\xi$ and sum the finite transverse indices; dominated convergence with the compact-time tail bound passes their integrated balance to the limit. The result is the physical $Q$ energy since the squared norm of the reflection extension is4 times that on $Q$. Its right-hand dissipation norm is continuous in time, so ordinary FTC proves the stated derivative. On the constrained coefficient subspace, differentiating the finite ODE once and using $K\\times(K\\times e)=K(K\\cdot e)-|K|^2e$ gives the displayed scalar electric ODE, including zero $K$. The derivative and characteristic-root calculations are the same real component algebra as the declared constant-Ohmic modal theorem. The normalized Fourier frequency is $\\xi$, so the actual physical longitudinal wavenumber is $\\beta=2\\pi\\xi$, never silently $\\xi$. The transverse parities and vector constraints remain in force; a scalar $m=n=0$ character is not by itself a nonzero TE reconstruction. The smooth material interpretation and weak evolution adoption remain separate, with no constitutive conclusion beyond the declared branch.",
          "physical_scope": "The explicitly declared constant scalar Ohmic PEC energy classes of K10, including the lossless sigma=0 branch, with exact modal/Fourier completeness and energy balance. No arbitrary dispersive, nonlinear, hysteretic, nonsmooth-wall, open-end or junction-scattering claim; no empirical claim.",
