            "pthm-em-open-rectangular-guide-completeness": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-restricted-material-energy": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay": "physical-result",
            "thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice as declared by the cited mathematical suppliers ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]]), the lossless isotropic scalar constant branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] with $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$ and $\\sigma=0$, the source-free smooth macroscopic equations of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]] in the smooth branches, with $\\rho_f=0$, $J_f=0$, and the explicitly declared weak first-order material equations in the weak cavity branches below, and the guide, cavity, PEC and reflection-class conventions of [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]].\n(a) [Transverse profile completeness] For the cross-section $(0,a)\\times(0,b)$, the families $\\{h_{mn}\\}_{m,n\\ge0}$ and $\\{e_{mn}\\}_{m,n\\ge1}$ of [[lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities]] are orthogonal complete systems in $L^2$ of the rectangle ([[lem-em-rectangular-transverse-mode-completeness]]); The full scalar Neumann and Dirichlet families have these $L^2$ expansions. The physical TE candidate family excludes $h_{00}=1$ by the full Maxwell equations: the remaining $h_{mn}$ are complete in the zero-mean subspace $\\{f\\in L^2:\\int_Rf=0\\}$, not in all scalar $L^2$. The Dirichlet TM profiles retain their full scalar completeness. The constant Neumann scalar profile is retained in the mathematical basis but is not asserted to be a nonzero positive-frequency TE mode.\n(b) [Rectangular cavity reflection class] For the box $Q=(0,a)\\times(0,b)\\times(0,d)$ with the reflection class and distributional constraints $\\operatorname{div}E=\\operatorname{div}B=0$ of [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]](c): the finite separated Fourier modes are dense in the class at every time; every initial datum in the class has a unique $C(\\mathbb R;L^2)$ weak solution of the lossless first-order system $\\partial_tE=(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1}\\operatorname{curl}B$, $\\partial_tB=-\\operatorname{curl}E$ in the same class, with derivatives as in [[def-distributional-derivative]]; this lossless first-order distributional system is the explicitly adopted weak material evolution of the reflection model, not inferred from the smooth-only hypotheses of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]]; along the solution the total stored energy $\\int_Q u\\,dx$ equals $\\frac12\\int_Q(\\varepsilon|E|^2+|B|^2/\\mu)\\,dx$ and is constant, where the weak stored-energy density is defined almost everywhere by this nonnegative quadratic expression, consistent with the smooth local definition of [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]]. Its integrability and constancy are furnished by the mathematical reflection-class energy result; no nonlinear weak Poynting identity is asserted. For $C^1$-in-time solutions the four macroscopic equations with $D=\\varepsilon E$, $B=\\mu H$ hold distributionally.\n(c) [Smooth PEC cavity] For a bounded $C^\\infty$ cavity domain $\\Omega$ and the PEC class $X,V,H$ of [[thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness]](a): the curl form on $V$ has a complete orthonormal $L^2$ eigenbasis $\\{u_j\\}$ with nonnegative eigenvalues $\\lambda_j$ of finite multiplicity, $\\lambda_j\\to\\infty$ whenever $H$ is infinite dimensional, and a finite-dimensional zero eigenspace that is retained; the eigen-equation $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}u_j=\\lambda_ju_j$ holds distributionally with the PEC trace, and each $u_j$ has a $C^\\infty$ representative on the closure. Put $v_{\\mathrm{wave}}=(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1/2}>0$. The associated second-order electric wave has the full supplied interface: for arbitrary initial displacement in $V$ and velocity in $H$, its displacement is in $C(\\mathbb R;V)$, its velocity in $C(\\mathbb R;H)$, and finite modal sums converge in these respective norms uniformly on compact time intervals. Its zero eigenmodes may be affine in time. Separately, let $J$ be an open time interval and let a strong first-order solution obey $E\\in C^1(J;H)\\cap C(J;V)$ and $B\\in C^1(J;L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3))\\cap C(J;H(\\operatorname{curl}))$, with the first-order equations as $L^2$ identities and both distributional divergence constraints. Electric tangential-zero boundary data are encoded by $E(t)\\in V$. The magnetic normal-zero condition is declared weakly by $\\int_\\Omega B(t)\\cdot\\nabla\\psi=0$ for every smooth scalar $\\psi$ on the closure; when $B\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega)$ this is $n\\cdot B=0$, using $\\operatorname{div}B=0$. No magnetic membership in the electric tangential-zero class $X$ is assumed. Then $a_j(t):=\\langle E(t),u_j\\rangle$ is $C^2$ and satisfies $a_j''+v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\lambda_ja_j=0$. For $\\lambda_j=0$, the admissible first-order coefficients satisfy $a_j'=0$ and are constant, with the zero eigenfields retained. This extra compatibility distinguishes first-order Maxwell data from arbitrary second-order $V\\times H$ data; it does not remove the broader supplied second-order wave or its affine zero modes.\n(d) [Scope] The theorem asserts completeness only for the transverse $L^2$ profiles and for the two declared cavity classes with their exact hypotheses; this lossless theorem does not itself supply the longitudinal expansion or dissipative evolution. Those approved additional classes are supplied separately by [[pthm-em-open-rectangular-guide-completeness]] and [[pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay]], respectively. None of these statements supplies arbitrary lossy, dispersive, open-end scattering, non-smooth or exterior problems, or arbitrary non-rectangular guide problems beyond the explicitly declared smooth-cavity spectral class, no trace regularity for arbitrary $H(\\operatorname{curl})$ fields, and no identification of the weak classes with every maximal tangential-trace realization. The zero eigenvalues and zero modes are retained throughout.",
          "strategy": "Clause (a) is [[lem-em-rectangular-transverse-mode-completeness]](b) instantiated at the cross-section, whose profiles are those of the candidate theorem. Clause (b) is the rectangular reflection-class lemma [[lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness]] read physically, as packaged in [[thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness]](b): the class, the first-order system, density of finite separated modes, unique $C_tL^2$ weak solutions and constant energy all come from that supplier; the identification of the energy with $\\int_Qu$ uses $D=\\varepsilon E$, $B=\\mu H$ and [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]](a), and the macroscopic equations follow from the two evolution equations together with the distributional divergence constraints of the class. For the scalar basis distinction in (a), every nonconstant Neumann product is orthogonal to $h_{00}=1$, and the complete orthogonal expansion of [[lem-em-rectangular-transverse-mode-completeness]] shows that deleting that one basis element gives precisely its orthogonal complement, the zero-mean subspace. The constant scalar element remains in the full basis and is excluded only as a monochromatic TE reconstruction. In (b), the explicit weak first-order model is solved by [[lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness]] as packaged in the wrapper; its Fourier energy proof gives the quadratic energy conservation without applying a smooth-only local balance to rough fields. For (c), [[thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness]] supplies the spectral and smooth-eigenfield assertions and the associated wave at the positive parameter $v_{\\mathrm{wave}}=(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1/2}$, with its exact arbitrary $V\\times H$ initial-data interface, displacement $C_tV$ and velocity $C_tH$. To derive the first-order coefficient equations, prove the actual curl pairing against each smooth PEC eigenfield $u_j$, not an unproved equality of electric and magnetic boundary classes. A smooth normal collar of the compact boundary exists by [[thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem]]. Write $s$ for the inward normal coordinate (its gradient is the negative extended unit normal) and use the standard smooth step [[def-the-standard-smooth-step-function]] to set a cutoff $\\eta_\\delta=0$ for $s\\le\\delta$ and $1$ for $s\\ge2\\delta$. The normal tube has bounded Jacobians on the compact collar. Since $n\\times u_j=0$ on the boundary and $u_j$ is smooth, $|n\\times u_j|\\le Cs$ in the collar. Thus $\\eta_\\delta u_j\\to u_j$ and $\\operatorname{curl}(\\eta_\\delta u_j)\\to\\operatorname{curl}u_j$ in $L^2$: the derivative-cutoff term is bounded on a layer of volume $O(\\delta)$ because $|\\nabla\\eta_\\delta|\\le C/\\delta$ and its cross product with $u_j$ is $O(s/\\delta)$. For every $F\\in H(\\operatorname{curl})$, the distributional identity on the compactly supported smooth test $\\eta_\\delta u_j$ is $\\langle\\operatorname{curl}F,\\eta_\\delta u_j\\rangle=\\langle F,\\operatorname{curl}(\\eta_\\delta u_j)\\rangle$. Cauchy-Schwarz and the two $L^2$ limits give $\\langle\\operatorname{curl}F,u_j\\rangle=\\langle F,\\operatorname{curl}u_j\\rangle$. Apply this only to $F=B(t)$, which has the stated actual graph regularity. Then $a_j'=v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\langle B,\\operatorname{curl}u_j\\rangle$. The assumed $B\\in C^1_tL^2$ permits one further scalar derivative, giving $a_j''=v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\langle B',\\operatorname{curl}u_j\\rangle=-v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\langle\\operatorname{curl}E,\\operatorname{curl}u_j\\rangle=-v_{\\mathrm{wave}}^2\\lambda_j\\langle E,u_j\\rangle$ by the full-$X$ eigenvariational identity of [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]], since $E(t)\\in V\\subset X$. No second time derivative of the whole field is assumed. If $\\lambda_j=0$, that identity tested at $u_j$ gives $\\operatorname{curl}u_j=0$, so the preceding formula for $a_j'$ is zero. Such electric zero modes are constant under first-order Maxwell evolution, while the associated unrestricted second-order equation retains affine zero modes. Divergence constraints and the declared magnetic normal condition remain part of the physical model; the pairing does not impose a magnetic electric-type trace. The inherited AC and AComega assumptions cover the spectral/Fourier and collar suppliers. No lossy or open-domain step is made.",
          "physical_scope": "The completeness statements available for the declared classes of the lossless isotropic scalar constant branch: transverse L^2 profile completeness of the TE/TM families on the rectangular cross-section, the rectangular cavity reflection class with its unique finite-energy weak solutions and constant stored energy, and the smooth bounded PEC cavity curl eigenbasis with modal expansions retaining zero modes. The separate declared open-longitudinal rectangular guide and constant-Ohmic classes have their own completeness/energy theorems. No arbitrary lossy, non-smooth or exterior completeness claim, nor a general non-rectangular guide claim beyond the stated smooth-cavity spectral class. Displacement and velocity topologies and first-order zero-mode compatibility are explicit.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
