      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-em-te-ten-example",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The TE10 candidate of a lossless rectangular guide: explicit fields, cutoff and propagating branch",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 19,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-guide-cavity-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates",
            "lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-guide-cavity-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates": "physical-result",
            "lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$, $a,b>0$, $\\omega>0$, $\\beta>0$ with $\\beta^2=\\omega^2\\varepsilon\\mu-(\\pi/a)^2>0$, and put $\\omega_c:=\\pi/(a\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\mu})$, $\\theta:=\\beta x_3-\\omega t$. In the rectangular guide of [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]] with cross-section $(0,a)\\times(0,b)$, take the TE candidate of [[pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates]](a) with $(m,n)=(1,0)$, whose amplitudes are $$\\tilde E=\\bigl(0,\\,i\\omega\\mu\\tfrac{a}{\\pi}\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi x_1}{a},\\,0\\bigr),\\qquad \\tilde H=\\bigl(-i\\beta\\tfrac{a}{\\pi}\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi x_1}{a},\\,0,\\,\\cos\\tfrac{\\pi x_1}{a}\\bigr).$$ Then the real fields $E=\\operatorname{Re}(\\tilde Ee^{i\\theta})$, $B=\\mu\\operatorname{Re}(\\tilde He^{i\\theta})$ are smooth, have $E_z=0$ (TE) with $E$ linearly polarized along $e_2$, satisfy the source-free lossless macroscopic equations and the PEC wall conditions, and carry a half-sine standing profile $\\sin(\\pi x_1/a)$ in $x_1$ with a traveling wave in $x_3$. The cutoff of this mode is $\\omega_c=\\pi/(a\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\mu})$: real nonzero $\\beta$ exists exactly for $\\omega>\\omega_c$, at $\\omega=\\omega_c$ the mode is at cutoff ($\\beta=0$), and for $\\omega<\\omega_c$ it is evanescent. If $a\\ge b$, then $\\omega_c=\\pi/(a\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\mu})$ is the lowest cutoff among all TE and TM candidates of the cited theorem (attained also by $(m,n)=(0,1)$ when $a=b$); the case $(m,n)=(0,0)$ is not a mode by the index restriction of the theorem. This is an explicit conditional candidate of the adopted model; it asserts no measurement and no completeness.",
          "strategy": "Instantiate [[pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates]](a) at $(m,n)=(1,0)$, so that $h_{10}=\\cos(\\pi x_1/a)$, $k_c=\\pi/a$, $\\nabla_th_{10}=-(\\pi/a)\\sin(\\pi x_1/a)e_1$ and $e_z\\times\\nabla_th_{10}=-(\\pi/a)\\sin(\\pi x_1/a)e_2$; substitution gives the displayed amplitudes and the theorem's clauses supply smoothness, the equations, the TE property and the wall conditions. The cutoff and the propagation alternative are the theorem's clause (d) with $k_c=\\pi/a$. For $a\\ge b$ for every $(m,n)\\ne(0,0)$, either $m\\ge1$ and $k_{mn}^2\\ge\\pi^2/a^2$, or $m=0,n\\ge1$ and $k_{mn}^2\\ge\\pi^2/b^2\\ge\\pi^2/a^2$. Thus $k_{mn}^2\\ge\\min\\{\\pi^2/a^2,\\pi^2/b^2\\}=\\pi^2/a^2$, achieved by $(1,0)$; when $a=b$, $(0,1)$ also achieves it. Every TM profile has both indices positive and hence strictly larger cutoff than this minimum; the exclusion of $(0,0)$ is the theorem's index restriction. All quantities are declared model parameters; no empirical value is asserted.",
          "physical_scope": "The explicit TE10 candidate mode of the lossless rectangular guide in the lossless isotropic scalar branch, with its polarization, standing profile, cutoff and propagating/evanescent branches. Conditional on the adopted model; no measurement or empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zurich, 2024 FS (German), Chapter 13 'Wellenleiter' and 'Kavitaeten', PDF pp. 153-162 (printed pp. 13.1-13.10); complete 176-page author-hosted PDF and official TeX read, with the conjugate-error in (13.26), the 'Wendepunkt' mislabel and the general group-velocity restriction recorded as source defects not copied",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin, 2006-02-02), node 105 'Wave-guides', complete author-hosted HTML node read in the run's pinned full-notes snapshot",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node105.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596-page full scan), sections 9.5.1-9.5.3 printed pp. 405-412 (wave guides, TE waves in a rectangular wave guide, coaxial transmission line), read in the stamped full text; OCR noise cross-checked against the other treatments",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf"
              },
              {
