              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-rectangular-cavity-example",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A rectangular-cavity standing mode at resonance: explicit fields and the PEC end-cap check",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X7",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "dependency_level": 19,
          "deps": [
            "def-em-guide-cavity-geometry",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities",
            "lem-em-transverse-vector-identities",
            "pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-guide-cavity-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-transverse-vector-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates": "physical-result"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $a,b,d>0$ and $\\varepsilon,\\mu>0$ be constants, put $E_{\\mathrm{sp}}(x):=e_1\\sin\\frac{\\pi x_2}{b}\\sin\\frac{\\pi x_3}{d}$, and for $\\omega>0$ define $E(x,t):=E_{\\mathrm{sp}}(x)\\cos(\\omega t)$ and $B(x,t):=-\\frac1\\omega(\\operatorname{curl}E_{\\mathrm{sp}})(x)\\sin(\\omega t)$, where the curl is the fixed-time spatial curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]. Then $E,B$ are smooth, $\\operatorname{div}E_{\\mathrm{sp}}=0$, $\\Delta E_{\\mathrm{sp}}=-[(\\pi/b)^2+(\\pi/d)^2]E_{\\mathrm{sp}}$, and $E,B$ satisfy the four source-free macroscopic equations on the box $\\Omega_c=(0,a)\\times(0,b)\\times(0,d)$ in the lossless isotropic scalar branch exactly when $$\\omega^2=\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon\\mu}\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{b}\\Bigr)^2+\\Bigl(\\frac{\\pi}{d}\\Bigr)^2\\Bigr];$$ with that resonance, $n\\times E=0$ and $n\\cdot B=0$ hold on all six walls, so $(E,B)$ is a PEC cavity mode. It is the cutoff instance $\\beta=0$ of the TM family of [[pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates]](b) taken with axis $e_1$, cross-section $(0,b)\\times(0,d)$ and indices $(m,n)=(1,1)$: the field is independent of $x_1$, which is why it also satisfies the $x_1=0$ and $x_1=a$ end-cap conditions for every box length $a>0$. The example asserts a conditional model solution, not an observed resonance.",
          "strategy": "By [[lem-em-transverse-vector-identities]](b), $\\operatorname{curl}(e_1e_{11})=-e_1\\times\\nabla e_{11}$ is $x_1$-independent and divergence-free, and $E=E_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\cos\\omega t$, $B=-\\frac1\\omega\\operatorname{curl}E_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\sin\\omega t$ arise from the TM amplitudes with $\\beta=0$ and $e_{11}=\\sin\\frac{\\pi x_2}{b}\\sin\\frac{\\pi x_3}{d}$ (at $\\beta=0$ the transverse electric amplitude vanishes and $\\tilde H=\\frac{i\\omega\\varepsilon}{k_c^2}e_1\\times\\nabla e_{11}$ is imaginary, giving the $\\sin\\omega t$ field). The resonance condition is exactly $k_{11}^2=\\omega^2\\varepsilon\\mu$ with $k_{11}^2=(\\pi/b)^2+(\\pi/d)^2$ from [[lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities]](a). By the candidate theorem the fields satisfy the four equations and the guide wall conditions on the four faces with normals $\\pm e_2,\\pm e_3$; the amplitudes are independent of $x_1$, so $n\\times E=e_1\\times E$ and $n\\cdot B=B_1$ vanish on $x_1=0,a$ as well, giving the cavity conditions. The magnetic partner is fixed by $\\partial_tB=-\\operatorname{curl}E$ with the displayed sign. No empirical premise is used.",
          "physical_scope": "An explicit conditional standing-wave cavity mode of the lossless isotropic scalar branch in the rectangular perfectly conducting cavity, with its exact resonance condition and end-cap check. No measured resonance or empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zurich, 2024 FS (German), Chapter 13 'Wellenleiter' and 'Kavitaeten', PDF pp. 153-162 (printed pp. 13.1-13.10); complete 176-page author-hosted PDF and official TeX read, with the conjugate-error in (13.26), the 'Wendepunkt' mislabel and the general group-velocity restriction recorded as source defects not copied",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin, 2006-02-02), node 105 'Wave-guides', complete author-hosted HTML node read in the run's pinned full-notes snapshot",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node105.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596-page full scan), sections 9.5.1-9.5.3 printed pp. 405-412 (wave guides, TE waves in a rectangular wave guide, coaxial transmission line), read in the stamped full text; OCR noise cross-checked against the other treatments",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 printing, 236 pp.), section 4.3.3 'An Application: Reflection off a Conductor' printed pp. 89-90 (ideal-conductor boundary reading: E=0 inside, tangential E continuity and n.B=0, surface current), read in the stamped full PDF",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
