          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the lossless isotropic scalar constant branch of [[post-em-material-closure]], the macroscopic equations of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]] with $\\rho_f=0$, $J_f=0$, and the straight-guide, TEM and separated-phasor conventions of [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]]; put $Z:=\\sqrt{\\mu/\\varepsilon}$ and $v:=(\\varepsilon\\mu)^{-1/2}$.\n(a) [Potential form] Let $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ be a bounded connected $C^1$ domain and let $\\varphi\\in C^2(\\overline A;\\mathbb C)$ (with real-valued potentials included) satisfy $\\Delta\\varphi=0$ on $A$ (the plane Laplacian of [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]; a $C^2$ function with $\\Delta\\varphi=0$ is called harmonic). For either sign $\\zeta\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$ define $\\tilde E:=-\\nabla\\varphi$, $\\beta:=\\zeta\\omega/v$ and $\\tilde H:=\\zeta e_z\\times\\tilde E/Z$; then the separated real fields $E=\\operatorname{Re}(\\tilde Ee^{i(\\beta x_3-\\omega t)})$, $B=\\mu\\operatorname{Re}(\\tilde He^{i(\\beta x_3-\\omega t)})$ satisfy the source-free lossless macroscopic equations with $E_z=B_z=0$ (TEM), and the PEC condition on a smooth wall with unit normal $n$ is equivalent to $\\partial_\\tau\\varphi=0$ along that boundary curve, so that $\\varphi$ is constant on each connected component of $\\partial A$. Moreover, if $A$ is star-shaped ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]), every separated TEM phasor with $C^1$ transverse amplitudes has on the interior a complex harmonic potential $\\varphi\\in C^2(A;\\mathbb C)$, obtained by applying [[cor-closed-exact-and-conservative-equivalence-on-star-shaped-domains]] to the real and imaginary electric amplitudes separately. If the field is nonzero, the equations force $\\beta^2=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2$, hence $\\beta=\\pm\\omega/v$, and $\\tilde H=\\beta e_z\\times\\tilde E/(\\omega\\mu)$, giving the two displayed signs. If the field is zero, $\\tilde E=\\tilde H=0$ solves the equations for every longitudinal label $\\beta$, with no dispersion conclusion. The boundary potential description requires the stated continuous differentiable boundary trace (in particular the initial $C^2(\\overline A)$ potential has it); the interior exactness supplier alone is not asserted to produce a real potential or automatic boundary extension.\n(b) [Single-conductor obstruction] If in the potential-form setting $\\partial A$ is connected, then $\\varphi$ is constant on $\\partial A$, hence $\\varphi$ is constant on $\\overline A$ by the two-dimensional classical Dirichlet uniqueness [[cor-uniqueness-for-the-classical-dirichlet-problem]], applied to its real and imaginary parts with their respective constants as second solutions, and therefore $\\tilde E=\\tilde H=0$: a single-conductor cross-section with connected boundary admits no nonzero TEM field in the potential form. The same potential-form obstruction applies to a rectangular cross-section with a potential in $C^2(A)\\cap C(\\overline A)$ having constant boundary trace, because this uniqueness supplier does not require a $C^1$ boundary. For rectangular separated TEM amplitudes that are $C^1$ on the interior and continuous on the closure with the classical PEC traces on open edges, the star-centre radial primitive extends continuously to the rectangle closure. The edgewise tangential derivative is the electric trace, so PEC makes the potential constant on each open edge and continuity joins those constants at the corners. Thus this stated rectangular class has no nonzero TEM field.\n(c) [Coaxial example] For the following Lebesgue polar-coordinate energy calculation assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $0<a<b$, $A:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:a<|x|<b\\}$ (two boundary components), fix $V\\in\\mathbb R$, choose the forward label $k:=\\omega/v$, and define $\\varphi(x):=V\\ln(b/|x|)/\\ln(b/a)$, so that $\\tilde E=V e_r/(|x|\\ln(b/a))$ with $e_r:=x/|x|$ and $\\tilde H=(V/(Z|x|\\ln(b/a)))e_\\varphi$ with $e_\\varphi:=e_z\\times e_r$. Then $\\Delta\\varphi=0$ on $A$: indeed $\\partial_i\\ln|x|=x_i/|x|^2$ by [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]] and the chain rule ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]), and the second partial derivatives sum to zero; hence $\\varphi$ is constant on each boundary circle (values $V$ at $|x|=a$ and $0$ at $|x|=b$), the fields satisfy the source-free lossless macroscopic equations and the PEC conditions, and the relation $k=\\omega/v$ is nondispersive with no cutoff. The amplitude integrals computed in polar coordinates with [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] (the Jacobian factor $r$ is read from that formula) are $$W_e:=\\tfrac12\\varepsilon\\int_A|\\tilde E|^2\\,dA=\\tfrac12CV^2,\\qquad W_m:=\\tfrac12\\mu\\int_A|\\tilde H|^2\\,dA=\\tfrac12CV^2,\\qquad C:=\\frac{2\\pi\\varepsilon}{\\ln(b/a)};$$ the instantaneous per-length energies are $\\tfrac12CV^2\\cos^2\\theta$ and $\\tfrac12LI^2\\cos^2\\theta$ with $\\theta=kx_3-\\omega t$; equivalently, with the per-length inductance $L:=\\mu\\ln(b/a)/(2\\pi)$ in $\\mathrm H\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, per-length capacitance $C$ in $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and inner-conductor current amplitude $I:=2\\pi V/(Z\\ln(b/a))$ in $\\mathrm A$ one has $W_m=\\tfrac12LI^2$ and $LC=\\varepsilon\\mu$. For $V\\ne0$ the two boundary components carry distinct constants, allowing a nonzero field; $V=0$ gives equal constants and the zero field.\n(d) [Scope] The obstruction clause (b) is proved for the continuous-boundary harmonic-potential form. The star-shaped interior classification in (a) covers both signs and the zero field, with boundary conclusions under the stated potential-trace hypotheses; the general reduction of an arbitrary TEM field to the potential form on a merely simply connected non-star-shaped cross-section is not asserted here. The coaxial clause is an explicit conditional computation in the declared model with no empirical content; no lossy, radiation or signal-speed claim is made.",
          "strategy": "For (a), each real and imaginary plane harmonic potential component is smooth in the interior by [[thm-plane-harmonic-functions-are-smooth-and-real-analytic]]. Hence the constructed fields and their constant-coefficient polarization/magnetization representatives have the interior regularity required by the smooth macroscopic supplier; the stated boundary potential trace is used separately for PEC. Work componentwise with the complex potential: $\\tilde E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ is closed and divergence-free because $\\Delta\\varphi=0$. With $\\tilde H=\\beta e_z\\times\\tilde E/(\\omega\\mu)$ and $\\beta^2=\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2$, [[lem-em-separated-phasor-calculus]] and [[lem-em-transverse-vector-identities]] (applied separately to real and imaginary parts) verify the two curl equations and both divergences. Conversely a $C^1$ TEM amplitude has $E_z=H_z=0$. The longitudinal components of the curl equations give $\\operatorname{curl}_2\\tilde E=\\operatorname{curl}_2\\tilde H=0$, and Gauss gives both transverse divergences zero. The transverse Faraday equation is $i\\beta e_z\\times\\tilde E=i\\omega\\mu\\tilde H$, so $\\tilde H=\\beta e_z\\times\\tilde E/(\\omega\\mu)$ without division by $\\beta$. Substituting into Ampere yields $(\\beta^2-\\varepsilon\\mu\\omega^2)\\tilde E=0$. If $\\tilde E\\ne0$, this gives both roots $\\beta=\\pm\\omega/v$; if $\\tilde E=0$, Faraday forces $\\tilde H=0$ and the label $\\beta$ is unrestricted. On a star-shaped domain the closed-field supplier gives real potentials for the real and imaginary parts, hence a complex $C^2$ interior potential with $\\tilde E=-\\nabla\\varphi$ after a sign change; Gauss makes it harmonic. No real-only or boundary-extension conclusion is inferred from interior exactness. If the potential has the stated differentiable trace, $\\tau=e_z\\times n$ gives $n\\cdot(e_z\\times\\tilde E)=\\partial_\\tau\\varphi$ and $n\\times\\tilde E=-e_z\\partial_\\tau\\varphi$. Thus PEC is equivalent to the zero tangential derivative. The chain rule and FTC [[thm-ftc-second-part]] on boundary charts make the trace locally constant, and connectedness makes it constant on each boundary component. For (b), apply [[cor-uniqueness-for-the-classical-dirichlet-problem]] to the real and imaginary potential components and their constant boundary values; it requires only bounded nonempty open $A$, not a smooth boundary, and hence also covers the rectangle under the stated trace. For the rectangular class with continuous-closure amplitudes, the explicit radial primitive from an interior star centre is an integral of the continuous amplitude along segments of the closed rectangle, so it extends continuously to the closure. On an open edge, pass the interior line-integral FTC identity to the edge using uniform continuity of the amplitude; its trace has tangential derivative minus the tangential electric amplitude. PEC makes this zero, and the connected edges have equal constants by continuity at corners. The uniqueness supplier then applies to each harmonic component without a false smooth-boundary assumption. For (c), keep the forward real-potential example exactly as displayed with $k=\\omega/v$: direct logarithmic differentiation and the chain rule give $\\Delta\\varphi=0$, and the radial/tangential amplitudes satisfy both curl equations and PEC on the two circles. Under $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the polar Lebesgue formula [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] applies to the continuous nonnegative squared amplitudes on the annulus. Its angular normalization in dimension2 is $2\\pi$: equivalently use the $C^1$ polar diffeomorphism $(r,\\theta)\\mapsto(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)$ on $(a,b)\\times(0,2\\pi)$, with Jacobian $r$, using [[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]]. The omitted radial segment is Lebesgue null by [[prop-degenerate-boxes-and-coordinate-hyperplanes-are-lebesgue-null]]. The finite radial integral is $\\int_a^b dr/r=\\log(b/a)$ by [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]], so $\\int_A|\\tilde E|^2=\\int_A|Z\\tilde H|^2=2\\pi V^2/\\log(b/a)$. Substitution gives all displayed capacitance, inductance and energy formulas and $LC=\\varepsilon\\mu$; the zero value $V=0$ gives zero fields and energies. Only the local smooth energy-reading clause is used. Construction, the two-sign/zero classification and the potential-form obstruction are choice-free; Countable Choice is confined to the explicitly stated polar-measure energy branch, with no empirical premise.",
          "physical_scope": "The explicit coaxial TEM candidate of the lossless isotropic scalar branch with its per-length capacitance and inductance, and the obstruction showing that a single-conductor cross-section with connected boundary admits no nonzero TEM field in the harmonic-potential form. Includes complex potentials, both propagation signs, the zero-field label case and the star-shaped interior reduction with explicit boundary traces; no general simply connected reduction, lossy or empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zurich, 2024 FS (German), Chapter 13 'Wellenleiter' and 'Kavitaeten', PDF pp. 153-162 (printed pp. 13.1-13.10); complete 176-page author-hosted PDF and official TeX read, with the conjugate-error in (13.26), the 'Wendepunkt' mislabel and the general group-velocity restriction recorded as source defects not copied",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin, 2006-02-02), node 105 'Wave-guides', complete author-hosted HTML node read in the run's pinned full-notes snapshot",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node105.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "D. J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall 1999, 596-page full scan), sections 9.5.1-9.5.3 printed pp. 405-412 (wave guides, TE waves in a rectangular wave guide, coaxial transmission line), read in the stamped full text; OCR noise cross-checked against the other treatments",
                "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf"
