      "items": [
        {
          "id": "post-em-radiation-selection",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Adopted retarded solution selection: the physical branch carries no incoming free field",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#post-em-radiation-selection",
          "design_state": "explicit-adopted-postulate-contract; nonproof-source-scope-review",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell",
            "def-em-weak-sources",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-weak-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Adopt as a physical assumption of this framework the following solution-selection rule for the classical vacuum model on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with the constants and SI units of [[def-em-si-conventions]] (so $c>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$). (a) [Formulation] In the potential formulation of [[pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form]] with the conventions of [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]], for a $C^2$ potential pair in Lorenz gauge the sourced Maxwell system of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] is equivalent to the decoupled sourced wave equations $W\\varphi=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$, $WA=\\mu_0J$, where $W=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2-\\Delta$. The wave expression $W$ is invariant under $t\\mapsto-t$; where explicit retarded or advanced constructions for a prescribed history exist, they give distinguished particular solutions built from the corresponding forward or backward fundamental kernels. Every other solution differs from a chosen particular solution by a source-free addition; retarded and advanced particular solutions also differ by such an addition. This describes the affine free-solution freedom, not a claim that arbitrary histories have exactly two solutions or that either construction always exists. (b) [Adopted rule] The framework adopts the retarded branch with no free field added: the physical potential pair of the model is the retarded one, that is, the one whose support and propagation lie in the forward light cones of the source and which carries no field arriving from the past independently of the source. For the zero-incoming model the selected sourced solution has no independently prescribed homogeneous addition. If nonzero Cauchy data are instead prescribed, their compatible homogeneous addition is a separate initial-data model; it is not equivalent to setting every free addition to zero. The advanced construction and independent incoming free fields are excluded by the present retarded selection. (c) [Status and scope] This is an adopted extra temporal condition carrying the model's arrow-of-time content. It is not a theorem, not a consequence of the time-symmetric wave or Maxwell equations (which admit advanced and free solutions as mathematical objects), and not a deduction from experiment; it asserts no existence, uniqueness, regularity, support or decay for any source class, all of which must be supplied separately by theorems of this page. It selects a solution only of the models to which it is applied; it introduces no product of distributions, no source model, no self-force, no radiation reaction, no measured signal and no statistical claim. The retarded compact-source branch to which this selection applies is developed on this page as a conditional theorem, and the point-source branch is developed later on this page; neither existence statement is part of this postulate. For the separately declared point-source branch assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), as in its exact source/field interfaces, and adopt the same no-free-addition retarded selection in the weak equations of [[post-em-distributional-vacuum-maxwell]], for the locally integrable field and proper point-current types of [[def-em-weak-sources]]. This extends the selection's model scope explicitly; neither existence nor an arbitrary convolution of noncompact distributions is postulated.",
          "strategy": "A postulate: the item records an adopted solution-selection rule, and receives a source/scope review rather than a proof. The review checks that (i) the formulation correctly displays the wave expression $W$, its time-reversal symmetry, the retarded and advanced fundamental-solution branches and the free-field freedom, all with the exact interfaces of [[pthm-em-lorenz-gauge-wave-form]], [[def-em-potential-gauge-and-field-conventions]] and [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]; (ii) the adopted rule is stated as a modelling choice with its arrow-of-time content, and is not presented as a consequence of the equations; (iii) the formulation does not pretend to prove existence or uniqueness for any source class and expressly defers those to the consuming theorems; (iv) no empirical, statistical, measurement or apparatus claim is made, and no quantitative precision is invented; and (v) the scope exclusions (advanced branch, free fields, arbitrary distributions, self-force) are explicit. The classical textbook treatments listed in the sources present the retarded/advanced distinction and the extra choice of the retarded Green function (Tong's 'extra choice' at the displayed page); they support the formulation and the honest status, and are not evidence for the physical correctness of the choice. The weak point-source extension is an explicit physical selection on the already-adopted singular-source equations, not a deduction from the old smooth-only potential theorem. A consuming mathematical theorem must construct its actual history integrals and prove weak admissibility.",
          "physical_scope": "Adopted selection rule for the classical vacuum Maxwell/wave model in the framework's inertial laboratory chart and SI conventions: the physical potential pair is the retarded one with zero incoming free field, both for the smooth compact-source branch and for the separately declared weak point-source equations. Excluded: existence, uniqueness, regularity or decay claims for any source class (consuming theorems must supply them); empirical, statistical or measurement claims; the advanced branch and free fields as physical solutions; products of distributions, self-force, radiation reaction and any apparatus model.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (author-hosted 236-page PDF, title page dated Lent Term 2015)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "$\\S6.1$ printed pp. 134-143, especially the retarded/advanced choice in $\\S6.1.1$ printed pp. 136-137 and the wave kernels in $\\S6.1.2$ printed pp. 138-142: the Helmholtz and wave Green functions, the two possible time-asymmetric Green functions and the explicit extra (retarded) choice at the displayed 'Retarded Potentials' page; read in the retained full text of the 236-page revision."
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course, University of Texas at Austin (complete author-hosted HTML lecture notes, document dated 2006-02-02)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node50.html",
                "locator": "Nodes 49-51 ('Green's functions', 'Retarded potentials', 'Advanced potentials?') of chapter 4 'Time-dependent Maxwell's equations': the retarded and advanced potentials are both displayed and the extra temporal choice is identified as additional structure."
              },
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, ETH Zurich 2024 FS (author-hosted 176-page script)",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf",
                "locator": "$\\S11.2$ printed pp. 11.4-11.7 (PDF pp. 124-127): retarded and advanced Green kernels, pole prescriptions and the causal potential construction, with the retarded selection stated as additional to the time-reversible equations."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 18
        },
        {
          "id": "def-em-retarded-point-geometry",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Uniformly subluminal prescribed worldline, retarded root and retarded geometric data off the worldline",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-retarded-point-geometry",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames",
            "post-em-radiation-selection",
            "lem-em-global-retarded-root",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-em-sources"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-radiation-selection": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-global-retarded-root": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory region, inertial chart, spatial Euclidean structure and SI units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], in particular $c>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. A **retarded point-geometry datum** consists of a real charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ in coulombs ([[def-em-sources]](a)) and a prescribed map $z:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of class $C^3$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) together with a constant $0\\le v_*<c$ such that $|z'(s)|\\le v_*$ for every $s\\in\\mathbb R$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]); the spacetime curve $s\\mapsto(z(s),s)$ is the laboratory reading of a prescribed massive future timelike worldline of the physical model ([[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]]), and $v_*$ is a declared bound on its coordinate speed, not a measured quantity. Write $\\Gamma:=\\{(z(s),s):s\\in\\mathbb R\\}$ and $D:=\\{(x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R:x\\ne z(t)\\}$ for the open off-worldline observation domain. Then: (a) [Retarded root] For every observation event $(x,t)\\in D$ there is exactly one $s=s(x,t)<t$ with $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$; the map $s:D\\to\\mathbb R$ is of class $C^3$, and with the data of (b) below one has $\\partial_ts=1/\\kappa$ and $\\nabla_xs=-n/(c\\kappa)$ ([[lem-em-global-retarded-root]], whose $C^3$ branch applies because $z$ is $C^3$ and uniformly subluminal). (b) [Retarded data] For $(x,t)\\in D$ define $R:=x-z(s(x,t))$, $r:=|R|>0$, $n:=R/r$, $\\beta:=z'(s(x,t))/c$, $\\kappa:=1-n\\cdot\\beta$, $\\dot\\beta:=z''(s(x,t))/c$; then $r=c(t-s)$ and $\\kappa\\in[1-v_*/c,\\,1+v_*/c]\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$. On $D$ the objects $r$ and $n$ are of class $C^3$, $\\beta$ and $\\kappa$ are of class $C^2$, and $\\dot\\beta$ is of class $C^1$ (each is a composition of the $C^3$ root with $z$ or its derivatives). (c) [Emission-event data and emitted spheres] At an emission event $s_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and a direction $n\\in S^2$ put $x_r:=z(s_0)+rn$, $t_r:=s_0+r/c$ for $r>0$, and $\\beta_0:=z'(s_0)/c$, $\\kappa_0:=1-n\\cdot\\beta_0$. Then $(x_r,t_r)\\in D$ and $s(x_r,t_r)=s_0$ for every $r>0$: the sphere $\\{x_r\\}$ at observation time $t_r$ is emitted at the single event $s_0$. (d) [Branches and scope] Only the all-time uniformly subluminal branch (a)-(c) is defined by this datum. For a curve prescribed on a finite interval $J$ the same formulas define retarded data only at observation events for which the root equation has a solution $s\\in J$; each consumer must assume that cone intersection explicitly. Pointwise subluminality $|z'(s)|<c$ for every finite $s$ does not imply (a): the companion example on the examples page exhibits a smooth curve with $|z'|<c$ pointwise for which the retarded equation has no root at some event, so the uniform bound $v_*<c$, or an explicit cone-intersection hypothesis, is part of the datum. This definition is kinematic: it fixes the root, the retarded data and the notation, and asserts no field, no dynamics, no force law, no self-force and no empirical content. The identification of these data with the emission data of the physical field is made by the selection postulate [[post-em-radiation-selection]] together with the theorems of this page.",
          "strategy": "An explicit definition with its well-definedness discharged by a cited mathematical supplier. Existence, uniqueness and $C^3$ regularity of $s$ on $D$, together with $\\kappa\\in[1-v_*/c,1+v_*/c]$ and the derivative formulas, are exactly the all-time uniformly subluminal $C^3$ branch of [[lem-em-global-retarded-root]] (its statement covers every event of $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, hence every off-worldline event, and identifies equality $s=t$ with $x=z(t)$); no new analysis is needed, and the phrase 'prescribed worldline' is licensed by [[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]](a),(c), which relates chart readings $t\\mapsto r(t)$ of massive worldlines to the condition $|r'(t)|<c$. Clause (c) is the elementary root check $t_r-s_0=|x_r-z(s_0)|/c=r/c$ combined with the uniqueness in (a); no boundedness of $z''$ is used. Clause (d) records the branch boundaries honestly: the finite-history and nonuniform cases are left to consumers, and the failure of existence under mere pointwise subluminality is not asserted here but exhibited on the examples page. The definition introduces no physical field, so no Maxwell, self-force or empirical assertion is made; the cross-reference to the selection postulate records that the retarded root is the kinematic object that the adopted selection turns into an emission time. No choice principle beyond that carried by the cited root lemma is used.",
          "physical_scope": "Kinematic definition of the retarded emission data of a prescribed uniformly subluminal $C^3$ worldline in the framework's laboratory chart: retarded root, distance, direction, speed ratio, emission-time Jacobian and emitted-sphere family off the worldline. Excluded: any field, Maxwell equation, dynamics, force law, self-force, radiation reaction, existence under a merely pointwise subluminal or finite-history curve, and every empirical or measurement claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (author-hosted 236-page PDF, title page dated Lent Term 2015)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
