          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), as carried by the surface-measure interface below ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\sigma$ be the polar surface measure on the unit sphere $S^2\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]), let $e_3:=(0,0,1)$ and write $n_3=n\\cdot e_3$ for $n\\in S^2$. Then: (a) [Zonal identity] for every continuous $f:[-1,1]\\to\\mathbb R$, $$\\int_{S^2}f(n_3)\\,d\\sigma(n)=2\\pi\\int_{-1}^{1}f(u)\\,du.$$ (b) [Linear weighted moment] for every continuous $f$ and every $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $$\\int_{S^2}(n\\cdot a)\\,f(n_3)\\,d\\sigma(n)=2\\pi\\,a_3\\int_{-1}^{1}u\\,f(u)\\,du.$$ (c) [Quadratic weighted moment] for every continuous $f$ and every $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $$\\int_{S^2}(n\\cdot a)^2f(n_3)\\,d\\sigma(n)=2\\pi\\,a_3^{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}u^{2}f(u)\\,du+\\pi\\,(a_1^{2}+a_2^{2})\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-u^{2})f(u)\\,du.$$ (d) [Moments] $\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$, $\\int_{S^2}n\\,d\\sigma=0$, $\\int_{S^2}n_in_j\\,d\\sigma=(4\\pi/3)\\delta_{ij}$, and for every $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$ $$\\int_{S^2}(n\\cdot a)^2d\\sigma=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}|a|^2,\\qquad \\int_{S^2}|n\\times a|^2d\\sigma=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}|a|^2,$$ where $\\delta_{ij}$ is the Kronecker symbol and $\\times$ the cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. (e) [Scope] (a)-(d) are identities for the polar surface measure of the unit sphere with only the stated continuity of $f$; they assert no physical field, flux or radiation statement. The measure is finite, so all displayed integrals are absolutely convergent; $f$ need not be nonnegative. For the uses on this page $f$ is a rational function with denominator bounded below by $1-b>0$, hence continuous on $[-1,1]$.",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. (a) Parametrize $S^2$ by $X(\\theta,\\varphi):=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\varphi,\\ \\sin\\theta\\sin\\varphi,\\ \\cos\\theta)$ on $[0,\\pi]\\times[0,2\\pi]$. Computing the coordinate cross product with the sin/cos derivatives and the Pythagorean identity ([[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]) gives $X_\\theta\\times X_\\varphi=\\sin\\theta\\,X(\\theta,\\varphi)$, whose norm is $\\sin\\theta\\ge0$; by [[thm-surface-area-density-is-cross-product-norm]] and [[def-surface-area-and-scalar-surface-integral-of-a-patch]] the chart surface integral of $F(n)=f(n_3)$ is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{\\pi}f(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\,d\\varphi$, with the seam and the poles lying on the parameter boundary exactly as in the sphere-area computation, so this is the integral over $S^2$ ([[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]], [[lem-parameter-boundary-exceptions-do-not-affect-surface-integrals|the boundary-exception convention]]). The chart measure equals the polar measure $\\sigma$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]). By [[thm-jordan-fubini-by-sections]] the $\\varphi$-integral is the constant $2\\pi$, and with $P(u):=\\int_0^u f$ ([[thm-ftc-first-part]]) the chain rule and [[thm-ftc-second-part]] give $\\int_0^{\\pi}f(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta=[-P(\\cos\\theta)]_0^{\\pi}=P(1)-P(-1)=\\int_{-1}^{1}f(u)\\,du$, using $\\cos0=1$, $\\cos\\pi=-1$ and the derivative $\\cos'=-\\sin$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]]). This proves (a), including the endpoint values by continuity of $P\\circ\\cos$ on $[0,\\pi]$ and the two-sided fundamental theorem. (b) Write $n\\cdot a=a_1n_1+a_2n_2+a_3n_3$. The maps $\\eta_1(n_1,n_2,n_3):=(-n_1,n_2,n_3)$ and $\\eta_2(n_1,n_2,n_3):=(n_1,-n_2,n_3)$ are orthogonal and preserve $\\sigma$ ([[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]); since $n_1f(n_3)$ and $n_2f(n_3)$ change sign under $\\eta_1$ and $\\eta_2$ respectively, the substitution formula for the measure-preserving involution $\\eta_i$ (indicators by definition, simple functions by linearity, bounded Borel functions by the usual approximation, exactly as in the vanishing-first-moment computation of [[lem-first-moment-of-the-unit-sphere-vanishes]]) gives $\\int n_if(n_3)d\\sigma=-\\int n_if(n_3)d\\sigma=0$ for $i=1,2$; the remaining term is $a_3\\int n_3f(n_3)d\\sigma=2\\pi a_3\\int_{-1}^1uf(u)du$ by (a) applied to $F(u)=uf(u)$. (c) Expanding the square, the mixed terms $n_in_jf(n_3)$ with $i\\ne j$ vanish by the same reflection argument; the terms $n_1^2$ and $n_2^2$ are equal by the swap $(n_1,n_2,n_3)\\mapsto(n_2,n_1,n_3)$, which is orthogonal and preserves $\\sigma$. Hence $\\int n_1^2f(n_3)d\\sigma=\\int n_2^2f(n_3)d\\sigma=\\tfrac12\\int(1-n_3^2)f(n_3)d\\sigma=\\pi\\int_{-1}^1(1-u^2)f(u)du$ by (a) applied to $F(u)=(1-u^2)f(u)$, while $\\int n_3^2f(n_3)d\\sigma=2\\pi\\int_{-1}^1u^2f(u)du$ by (a) applied to $F(u)=u^2f(u)$; linearity of the $\\sigma$-integral on the (finite-measure) integrable functions is [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]. This proves (c). (d) Taking $f=1$ in (a) gives $\\sigma(S^2)=2\\pi\\cdot2=4\\pi$, which also evaluates the closed form of [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]] at $n=3$ together with the Gamma values $\\Gamma(5/2)=(3/2)(1/2)\\Gamma(1/2)=3\\sqrt\\pi/4$ ([[thm-real-gamma-functional-equation]], [[cor-real-gamma-one-half-is-root-pi]]), so the two routes agree. Taking $f=1$ in (b),(c) gives $\\int n_if(n_3)=0$ for $i=1,2$ and the matrix identity $\\int n_in_jd\\sigma=(4\\pi/3)\\delta_{ij}$ (equivalently $\\int(n\\cdot a)^2d\\sigma=(4\\pi/3)|a|^2$); the first moment statement is the three-dimensional case of [[lem-first-moment-of-the-unit-sphere-vanishes]]. Finally $|n\\times a|^2=|a|^2-(n\\cdot a)^2$ by the cross-product norm identity ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), so $\\int|n\\times a|^2d\\sigma=4\\pi|a|^2-(4\\pi/3)|a|^2=(8\\pi/3)|a|^2$. Clause (e) records that no physical interpretation is attached. No choice principle beyond $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ (carried by the surface-measure interface) is used.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
