          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ as carried by the cited point-source suppliers. Let the laboratory region, chart and units be those of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]], let $q\\in\\mathbb R$, let $z,v_*$ be a retarded point-geometry datum of [[def-em-retarded-point-geometry]] (prescribed $C^3$ uniformly subluminal worldline, $C:=q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$), let $s_0$ be an emission event with $z'(s_0)=0$ (the charge is momentarily at rest in the laboratory chart), and put $a:=z''(s_0)$ and, for $n\\in S^2$, $x_r:=z(s_0)+rn$, $t_r:=s_0+r/c$. Let $E,B$ be the retarded fields of the charge given by [[pthm-em-lienard-wiechert]] and $S:=E\\times B/\\mu_0$ ([[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]], [[pthm-em-poynting-balance]]). Then: (a) [Field on the emitted sphere] For every $r>0$ and $n\\in S^2$, $$E(x_r,t_r)=\\frac{C\\,n\\times(n\\times a)}{c^2r}+\\rho_E(s_0,n,r),\\qquad|\\rho_E(s_0,n,r)|\\le|C|\\frac{1+v_*/c}{(1-v_*/c)^3}r^{-2},\\qquad B(x_r,t_r)=\\frac{n\\times E(x_r,t_r)}{c}.$$ (b) [Angular flux density] The limits $$\\frac{dP}{d\\sigma}(n):=\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}r^2\\,n\\cdot S(x_r,t_r)=\\frac{C^{2}\\,|n\\times(n\\times a)|^{2}}{\\mu_0c^{5}},\\qquad P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0):=\\int_{S^2}\\frac{dP}{d\\sigma}(n)\\,d\\sigma(n),$$ exist with the convergence uniform in $n\\in S^2$; $d\\sigma$ is the polar surface measure of the unit sphere (equal to the chart surface measure by [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]). (c) [Total power] $$P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0)=\\frac{C^{2}}{\\mu_0c^{5}}\\cdot\\frac{8\\pi}{3}|a|^{2}=\\frac{q^2|a|^2}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}},$$ the Larmor power of the momentarily resting prescribed charge. (d) [Angular distribution] For $a\\ne0$, define $\\theta\\in[0,\\pi]$ by $\\cos\\theta=(n\\cdot a)/|a|$; this angle exists uniquely because the quotient is in $[-1,1]$ and cosine is continuous and strictly decreasing from $1$ to $-1$ on $[0,\\pi]$. When $a=0$ the angle is undefined and $dP/d\\sigma\\equiv0$. Then $$\\frac{dP}{d\\sigma}(n)=\\frac{q^2|a|^2}{16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0c^{3}}\\sin^2\\theta:$$ when $q\\ne0$ and $a\\ne0$ the radiation pattern vanishes exactly along the instantaneous acceleration axis and is maximal in the perpendicular plane; when $q=0$ it vanishes in every direction; $\\int_{S^2}dP/d\\sigma\\,d\\sigma=P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0)$. If $a=0$ all displayed radiation quantities vanish. (e) [Scope and conventions] The result is exact at an emission event at which the charge is instantaneously at rest in the laboratory chart: then $\\kappa_0(n)=1$ for every $n$, so no retarded-time conversion factor appears and the displayed limits are simultaneously the fixed-sphere flux and the emitted power per unit emission time. The item uses only the prescribed worldline and the retarded model fields -- it is not a mechanical evolution law, does not assert a self-force or radiation reaction, derives no mass, and makes no empirical or measurement claim. For a general emission event the exact formula and its low-speed comparison are supplied by clause (f); 'slow' motion alone is never substituted for an error bound. The prescribed curve, the uniform subluminal bound and $z'(s_0)=0$ are hypotheses; for a varying-velocity worldline the instantaneous rest event is a single parameter value, not an interval. (f) [General low-speed branch] At any emission event, without imposing $z'(s_0)=0$ for this clause, define emitted power by the weighted far-zone flux integral $\\kappa_0(n)\\,r^2\\,n\\cdot S$ on the emitted spheres, with $\\kappa_0(n)=1-n\\cdot\\beta_0$, $\\beta_0=z'(s_0)/c$, $|\\beta_0|\\le v_*/c$ and $\\gamma_0:=(1-|\\beta_0|^2)^{-1/2}$. The same field formula and angular computation give $$P_{\\mathrm{em}}=\\gamma_0^{6}\\bigl(|a|^{2}-|\\beta_0\\times a|^{2}\\bigr)\\frac{q^{2}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}}.$$ Put $P_{\\mathrm L}=q^{2}|a|^{2}/(6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3})$. For $|\\beta_0|\\le\\delta<1$, $$P_{\\mathrm L}\\le P_{\\mathrm{em}}\\le(1-\\delta^{2})^{-3}P_{\\mathrm L},\\qquad |P_{\\mathrm{em}}-P_{\\mathrm L}|\\le3\\delta^{2}(1-\\delta^{2})^{-3}P_{\\mathrm L}.$$ This includes $q=0$ or $a=0$ without division by $P_{\\mathrm L}$. Uniform angular comparison is also explicit: if $N_\\beta=n\\times((n-\\beta_0)\\times a)$, $N_0=n\\times(n\\times a)$ and $H_\\beta=q^{2}|N_\\beta|^{2}/(16\\pi^{2}\\varepsilon_0c^{3}\\kappa_0^{5})$, $H_0=q^{2}|N_0|^{2}/(16\\pi^{2}\\varepsilon_0c^{3})$, then $$|H_\\beta-H_0|\\le\\frac{q^2|a|^2}{16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0c^3}\\,\\delta\\left[\\frac{2+\\delta}{(1-\\delta)^5}+\\frac{5}{(1-\\delta)^6}\\right]$$ for all $n$. The exact rest-event clauses (a)-(e) retain their hypotheses; this additional branch supplies the general low-speed comparison, not a mechanical loss law.",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. (a) At the emission event $s_0$ one has $\\beta_0=z'(s_0)/c=0$, $\\kappa_0=1$ and $\\dot\\beta_0=a/c$; clause (c) of [[pthm-em-lienard-wiechert]] (equivalently clause (d) of [[thm-em-retarded-root-and-fields]], which also supplies the uniform remainder bound and the root identification $s(x_r,t_r)=s_0$) gives $E_{\\mathrm{vel}}=C(1-|\\beta|^2)(n-\\beta)/(\\kappa^3r^2)=C\\,n/r^2$ and $E_{\\mathrm{acc}}=C\\,n\\times((n-\\beta)\\times\\dot\\beta)/(c\\kappa^3r)=C\\,n\\times(n\\times a)/(c^2r)$; the displayed remainder is their sum restricted to the leading term, bounded by $|E_{\\mathrm{vel}}|\\le|C|(1+v_*/c)(1-v_*/c)^{-3}r^{-2}$; $B=n\\times E/c$. (b) For fields with $B=n\\times E/c$ one has $n\\cdot S=n\\cdot(E\\times B)/\\mu_0=(|E|^2-(n\\cdot E)^2)/(\\mu_0c)=|n\\times E|^2/(\\mu_0c)$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]). Hence $r^2n\\cdot S=r^2|n\\times E|^2/(\\mu_0c)$; replacing $E=E_{\\mathrm{lead}}+\\rho_E$ and using the bound on $\\rho_E$ together with $|n\\times w|\\le|w|$ gives $r^2n\\cdot S=C^{2}|n\\times(n\\times a)|^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{5})+O(r^{-1})$ uniformly on $S^2$ ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]); integrating over the compact sphere and passing to the limit ([[thm-dominated-convergence]]) proves the limits and their uniformity. (c) Expand $|n\\times(n\\times a)|^{2}=|a|^{2}-(n\\cdot a)^{2}$ and apply [[lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments]](d): $\\int_{S^2}(n\\cdot a)^2d\\sigma=(4\\pi/3)|a|^2$, so the integral is $4\\pi|a|^2-(4\\pi/3)|a|^2=(8\\pi/3)|a|^2$; with $C^2=q^2/(16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0^2)$ and $\\mu_0c^2\\varepsilon_0=1$ the coefficient is $C^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{5})\\cdot(8\\pi/3)=q^2/(6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3})$. (d) The vanishing of the pattern along $\\pm a$ and the value $\\sin^2\\theta$ follow from $n\\times(n\\times a)=n(n\\cdot a)-a$ and $|\\cdot|^2=|a|^2\\cos^2\\theta-2|a|^2\\cos^2\\theta+|a|^2=|a|^2\\sin^2\\theta$; the displayed coefficient is $C^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{5})=q^{2}/(16\\pi^2\\varepsilon_0c^{3})$ by the same unit relation. The integral statement is (c). (e) At $\\beta_0=0$ the emission-time Jacobian $\\kappa_0(n)=1-n\\cdot\\beta_0$ equals $1$ for all $n$, so the retarded-time conversion factor is trivial and the fixed-sphere flux limit is the emitted power per unit emission time ([[def-em-retarded-point-geometry]](a) for the Jacobian); the scope exclusions are those of the point-field supplier, and no mechanical, self-force or empirical conclusion is drawn. For (f), integrate the weighted angular density directly, without invoking a later item. Rotate beta=b e3 and resolve a=a_parallel e3+a_perp e1. Expanding |N_beta|² gives -(1-b²)(n dot a)²+2(1-bu)b a_parallel(n dot a)+(1-bu)²|a|², u=n3. The earlier zonal lemma supplies the azimuthal averages; the parallel coefficient reduces to1-u², perpendicular coefficient to (1-bu)²-(1-b²)(1-u²)/2. For 0<b<1, y=1-bu yields integral_-1^1 (1-u²)/(1-bu)^5 du=4/[3(1-b²)^3] and integral_-1^1 (1-bu)^-3 du=2/(1-b²)^2. An antiderivative in y for the former transformed integrand is b^-3[(1-b²)y^-4/4-2y^-3/3+y^-2/2]; the second is elementary. FTC and the chain/power rules give these identities; at b0 integrate directly, avoiding division by b. Thus integral |N_beta|²/kappa^5=(8pi/3)[gamma^6 a_parallel²+gamma^4 a_perp²], proving the general power formula. Eventwise gamma^4 P_L<=P_em<=gamma^6 P_L implies the uniform bounds since gamma>=1 and b<=delta. The difference bound follows from (1-delta²)^-3-1<=(3delta²)/(1-delta²)^3. For angular comparison |N_beta-N0|<=delta|a|, |N_beta|<= (1+delta)|a|, and the scalar mean-value bound |(1-n dot beta)^-5-1|<=5delta(1-delta)^-6 give the displayed estimate. These direct finite calculations use only the earlier point-field and zonal suppliers and the declared power/FTC rules.",
          "physical_scope": "Exact rest-event radiation of the prescribed retarded point-source model (angular flux density, Larmor power and sin^2 pattern with the uniform velocity-field remainder) and a separately quantified general uniform low-speed power and angular comparison; no particle dynamics, self-force, radiation reaction, mass derivation or empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
