          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ as carried by the cited point-source suppliers. Let the setting be that of [[pthm-em-lienard-wiechert]]: a charge $q\\in\\mathbb R$ ($C:=q/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)$) on a prescribed $C^3$ uniformly subluminal worldline $z$ in the laboratory chart of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], with retarded fields $E,B$ off the worldline and the uniform far-zone remainder of that item; let $S:=E\\times B/\\mu_0$ ([[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]). Let $s_0$ be an emission event, $\\beta_0:=z'(s_0)/c$, $\\gamma_0:=(1-|\\beta_0|^2)^{-1/2}$, $a:=z''(s_0)$, and for $n\\in S^2$ put $x_r:=z(s_0)+rn$, $t_r:=s_0+r/c$, $\\kappa_0(n):=1-n\\cdot\\beta_0$. Define the **emitted power at the emission event $s_0$** by the $\\kappa_0$-weighted far-zone flux $$P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0):=\\lim_{r\\to\\infty}\\int_{S^2}\\kappa_0(n)\\,r^{2}\\,n\\cdot S(x_r,t_r)\\,d\\sigma(n),$$ $d\\sigma$ the polar surface measure (equal to the chart surface measure). The weight $\\kappa_0$ is the emission-time/observation-time conversion factor of the retarded root, $\\partial_ts=1/\\kappa$, equivalently the moving-boundary correction $S\\cdot n-(z'\\cdot n)u$ at leading radiation order, where $u=|S|/c$ for the transverse far field. Then: (a) [Weighted flux limit] The limit exists and $$P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0)=\\frac{C^{2}}{\\mu_0c^{5}}\\int_{S^2}\\frac{\\bigl|n\\times\\bigl((n-\\beta_0)\\times a\\bigr)\\bigr|^{2}}{\\kappa_0(n)^{5}}\\,d\\sigma(n).$$ (b) [Closed form and four-acceleration form] If $b:=|\\beta_0|<1$ and $a=a_\\parallel e_3+a_\\perp$ is decomposed along $\\beta_0=|\\beta_0|e_3$ and perpendicular to it, then $$P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0)=\\frac{q^{2}\\gamma_0^{6}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}}\\bigl(|a|^{2}-|\\beta_0\\times a|^{2}\\bigr)=\\frac{q^{2}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}}\\,\\eta(A,A),$$ where $A$ is the four-acceleration of the worldline at $s_0$ and $\\eta(A,A)=\\gamma_0^{4}|a|^{2}+\\gamma_0^{6}(\\beta_0\\cdot a)^{2}=\\gamma_0^{6}(|a|^{2}-|\\beta_0\\times a|^{2})$ ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]](e)). At $\\beta_0=0$ the weight is $1$ and (b) reduces to the instantaneous-rest Larmor power of [[pthm-em-larmor-nonrelativistic]]. (c) [Low-speed comparison] For every $\\delta\\in(0,1)$ and every emission event with $|\\beta_0|\\le\\delta$, $$\\frac{q^{2}|a|^{2}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}}\\le P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0)\\le\\frac{(1-\\delta^{2})^{-3}q^{2}|a|^{2}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}},\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad \\Bigl|P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0)-\\frac{q^{2}|a|^{2}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{3\\delta^{2}}{(1-\\delta^{2})^{3}}\\frac{q^{2}|a|^{2}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}},$$ a comparison uniform over the events with $|\\beta_0|\\le\\delta$. In particular the instantaneous-rest formula is the exact $\\delta=0$ case, and no term of the comparison is asserted to be small without the explicit hypothesis $|\\beta_0|\\le\\delta$. (d) [Scope] $P_{\\mathrm{em}}(s_0)$ is the power emitted at the single retarded event $s_0$ per unit emission time, defined by the weighted far-zone flux; it is not a coordinate-time loss rate of a dynamical particle, and no dynamics (self-force, radiation reaction, mass), no uniformity in $s_0$, and no empirical or measurement claim is made. The near fields are $O(r^{-2})$ with their uniform remainder as in [[pthm-em-lienard-wiechert]](c), so their contribution to the weighted $r^2$ flux vanishes; the $\\kappa_0$-weighting is part of the definition of emitted power and is not an additional physical assumption.",
          "strategy": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. (a) On the emitted sphere the retarded data are those of the emission event ($s(x_r,t_r)=s_0$, [[thm-em-retarded-root-and-fields]](d)), and the uniform remainder gives $E= E_{\\mathrm{acc,lead}}+\\rho_E$ with $E_{\\mathrm{acc,lead}}=C\\,n\\times((n-\\beta_0)\\times\\dot\\beta_0)/(c\\kappa_0^{3}r)$, $|\\rho_E|\\le|C|(1+v_*/c)(1-v_*/c)^{-3}r^{-2}$ and $B=n\\times E/c$. For such fields $n\\cdot S=|n\\times E|^{2}/(\\mu_0c)$ (expand $E\\times(n\\times E/c)$ and use the triple-product orthogonality, [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]). Therefore $\\kappa_0r^{2}n\\cdot S=\\kappa_0r^{2}|n\\times E|^{2}/(\\mu_0c)=\\kappa_0|C|^{2}|n\\times((n-\\beta_0)\\times\\dot\\beta_0)|^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{3}\\kappa_0^{6})+O(r^{-1})$ with a remainder controlled by the uniform bound ([[thm-integral-triangle-inequality]], [[prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral]]); note $C^{2}=|C|^{2}$ as squares. Integrating over the compact sphere and passing to the limit ([[thm-dominated-convergence]]) gives (a) with $\\dot\\beta_0=a/c$ absorbed: $|n\\times((n-\\beta_0)\\times a)|^{2}$ replaces $c^{2}|n\\times((n-\\beta_0)\\times\\dot\\beta_0)|^{2}$, giving $(C^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{5}))\\int|n\\times((n-\\beta_0)\\times a)|^{2}/\\kappa_0^{5}d\\sigma$ because $|C|^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{3}c^{2})=C^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{5})$ and one power of $\\kappa_0$ cancels. (b) Rotate the integration variable so that $\\beta_0=be_3$ with $b=|\\beta_0|$ and $a=a_\\parallel e_3+a_\\perp e_1$ (rotation invariance of $\\sigma$, [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]); put $u=n_3$ and $\\kappa_0=1-bu$. Expanding the numerator with $N=n\\cdot\\beta_0=bu$, $d=n\\cdot\\dot\\beta_0$ and $|n-\\beta_0|^{2}=1-2bu+b^{2}$ gives, exactly as in the recorded research computation, $$\\bigl|n\\times\\bigl((n-\\beta_0)\\times a\\bigr)\\bigr|^{2}=-(1-b^{2})(n\\cdot a)^{2}+2\\kappa_0 b\\,a_\\parallel(n\\cdot a)+\\kappa_0^{2}|a|^{2}.$$ Apply [[lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments]](b),(c) with $f(u)=(1-bu)^{-5}$ and $g(u)=(1-bu)^{-4}$: the azimuthal average turns $(n\\cdot a)^2$ into $u^{2}a_\\parallel^{2}+\\tfrac12(1-u^{2})a_\\perp^{2}$ and $n\\cdot a$ into $ua_\\parallel$, and $(1-bu)^{-3}$ weights the $|a|^{2}$ term. The resulting one-dimensional integrals are elementary: $\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-u^{2})(1-bu)^{-5}du=\\tfrac{4}{3}(1-b^{2})^{-3}$ and $\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-bu)^{-3}du=2(1-b^{2})^{-2}$, both verified by the displayed antiderivatives in $y=1-bu$ using the power derivative, chain rule and fundamental theorem ([[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). Collecting terms gives $\\int|\\cdot|^{2}/\\kappa_0^{5}d\\sigma=(8\\pi/3)[\\gamma_0^{6}a_\\parallel^{2}+\\gamma_0^{4}a_\\perp^{2}]= (8\\pi/3)\\gamma_0^{6}(|a|^{2}-|\\beta_0\\times a|^{2})$; multiplying by $C^{2}/(\\mu_0c^{5})=q^{2}/(16\\pi^{2}\\varepsilon_0c^{3})$ produces (b). The four-acceleration identity is clause (e) of [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]], where $\\eta(A,A)=\\gamma_w^{4}|a|^{2}+\\gamma_w^{6}(w\\cdot a)^{2}/c^{2}$ with $w=z'(s_0)=c\\beta_0$ reads $\\gamma_0^{4}|a|^{2}+\\gamma_0^{6}(\\beta_0\\cdot a)^{2}=\\gamma_0^{6}(|a|^{2}-|\\beta_0\\times a|^{2})$; the $\\beta_0=0$ case is the statement of [[pthm-em-larmor-nonrelativistic]]. (c) The strongest immediate eventwise bounds are gamma0^4 P_L<=P_em<=gamma0^6 P_L, since |a|²-|beta0 cross a|² lies between (1-b²)|a|² and |a|². With b<=delta, gamma0>=1 and gamma0^6<=(1-delta²)^-3, they imply P_L<=P_em<=(1-delta²)^-3 P_L. A larger inverse factor in the UNIFORM lower bound is not justified by an upper bound on b (the rest event b0 disproves it). The rational identity (1-delta²)^-3-1=(3delta²-3delta^4+delta^6)/(1-delta²)^3<=3delta²/(1-delta²)^3 proves the unchanged difference estimate, including P_L0. (d) records that the definition is per unit emission time, that the near-field contribution vanishes after weighting by the uniform remainder and that no dynamics or empirical content is added. To make the finite angular integration fully explicit: the parallel averaged numerator is1-u²; the perpendicular one is(1-bu)²-(1-b²)(1-u²)/2. For b>0 the first transformed antiderivative is b^-3[(1-b²)y^-4/4-2y^-3/3+y^-2/2], y=1-bu, evaluated from1-b to1+b. At b0 direct angular moments give8pi|a|²/3; never evaluate a b^-3 antiderivative at b0. The angle-axis rotation preserves the existing sphere measure.",
          "physical_scope": "Emitted power per unit emission time of a prescribed point charge at a general emission event on a uniformly subluminal $C^3$ worldline: the $\\kappa$-weighted far-zone flux, the closed form $q^2\\gamma^6(|a|^2-|\\beta\\times a|^2)/(6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^3)=q^2\\eta(A,A)/(6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^3)$ and the uniform $\\delta$-comparison with the instantaneous-rest Larmor value. Excluded: dynamical evolution, self-force, radiation reaction, mass derivation, coordinate-time loss rates, uniformity in the emission event and every empirical or measurement claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
