          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $c>0$ and $a>0$, and define $z:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $z(s):=(\\sqrt{c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}},0,0)$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[thm-of-square-roots]]). Then: (a) $z$ is of class $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R$ and $|z'(s)|<c$ for every $s\\in\\mathbb R$; nevertheless there is no constant $v_*<c$ with $|z'(s)|\\le v_*$ for all $s$, because $|z'(s)|\\to c$ as $s\\to\\pm\\infty$; (b) at the event $(x,t)=(0,0)$ the retarded equation $t-s=|x-z(s)|/c$ of [[def-em-retarded-point-geometry]] has no solution in $\\mathbb R$: the equation reads $-s=\\sqrt{c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}}/c$, whose right-hand side is at least $a/c>0$, and squaring both sides would give $c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}=c^{2}s^{2}$, i.e. $a^{2}=0$, contrary to $a>0$. Hence pointwise subluminality of a smooth curve does not by itself produce the retarded root, and the uniform bound $v_*<c$ of [[def-em-retarded-point-geometry]], or an explicit cone-intersection hypothesis, cannot be dropped; the supplier [[lem-em-global-retarded-root]] indeed assumes such a uniform bound. (c) [Scope] This is a mathematical example about the prescribed curve and the root equation. The curve is future timelike pointwise ($|z'(s)|<c$ for every $s$) but not uniformly subluminal; it is not asserted to be the worldline of any physical charge, and no measurement is reported. The example contradicts no theorem: it exhibits a curve lying outside the hypotheses of the root lemma.",
          "strategy": "Direct verification. (a) $s\\mapsto c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}$ is smooth, positive and bounded below by $a^{2}>0$, and $\\sqrt\\cdot$ is smooth on $(0,\\infty)$; the chain rule and the derivative $\\tfrac{d}{dt}\\sqrt t=1/(2\\sqrt t)$ give $z'(s)=(c^{2}s/\\sqrt{c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}},0,0)$, whence $|z'(s)|=c^{2}|s|/\\sqrt{c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}}<c$ for every $s$ (at $s=0$ the speed is0; for $s\\ne0$ it also equals $c/\\sqrt{1+a^{2}/(c^{2}s^{2})}$) ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[thm-of-square-roots]], [[def-abs-value]], [[lem-of-square-monotone]], algebra), while $|z'(s)|\\to c$ as $s\\to\\infty$; so no $v_*<c$ bounds $|z'|$ on all of $\\mathbb R$. (b) For $x=0$, $t=0$ the root equation of [[def-em-retarded-point-geometry]] is $-s=|z(s)|/c=\\sqrt{c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}}/c$. Since the right-hand side is positive, $s<0$, so both sides of $-s=\\sqrt{c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}}/c$ are positive and squaring is an equivalence: it gives $c^{2}s^{2}=c^{2}s^{2}+a^{2}$, i.e. $a^{2}=0$, contradicting $a>0$; alternatively, the root lemma's existence proof requires the uniform Lipschitz bound $|z(s_2)-z(s_1)|\\le v_*|s_2-s_1|$ with $v_*<c$, which fails here. (c) states the scope; no physical or empirical interpretation is attached.",
          "physical_scope": "Mathematical example delimiting the kinematic hypothesis of the retarded point geometry: a smooth curve with $|z'|<c$ pointwise but no uniform bound, for which the retarded root equation fails at one event. Excluded: any claim about physical charges, any contradiction with theorems (the curve lies outside their hypotheses) and every empirical statement.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
