          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the surface and finite-source branches. Adopt the electric-dipole radiation-law idealization whose leading far fields and angular flux are the dipole expressions of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]], evaluated for the prescribed smooth waveform $p(u)=p_0\\cos(\\omega u)e_3$ with $\\omega>0$ and $p_0\\ne0$. This is an explicit ideal radiation-law model, not an eternal compact-time source claimed to satisfy that theorem with $D=0$. Here $r>0$, $u\\in\\mathbb R$, $p_0\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$ carries C m, $\\omega$ carries s$^{-1}$, and $e_1,e_2,e_3$ are the standard orthonormal spatial basis. The waveform is an explicitly prescribed time-dependent dipole moment as defined in [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]], not measured data; [[def-em-electric-moments]] supplies only the static moment convention. All exact quantities below refer to that ideal leading radiation law. Then: (a) [Fields] With $n=\\sin\\theta\\cos\\varphi\\,e_1+\\sin\\theta\\sin\\varphi\\,e_2+\\cos\\theta\\,e_3$, $\\theta\\in[0,\\pi]$, $\\varphi\\in[0,2\\pi]$, the ideal leading far fields are $$E_{\\mathrm{lead}}(rn,u+r/c)=-\\frac{\\mu_0\\omega^{2}p_0\\cos(\\omega u)}{4\\pi r}\\,n\\times(n\\times e_3),\\qquad B_{\\mathrm{lead}}=\\frac{n\\times E_{\\mathrm{lead}}}{c},$$ with $|n\\times(n\\times e_3)|=|\\sin\\theta|$, so the amplitude pattern is $\\propto\\sin\\theta$ and vanishes on the dipole axis $\\theta\\in\\{0,\\pi\\}$. (b) [Angular power density] The retarded-time angular power density of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]] is $$\\frac{dP}{d\\sigma}(n,u)=\\frac{\\mu_0|n\\times p''(u)|^{2}}{16\\pi^{2}c}=\\frac{\\mu_0\\omega^{4}p_0^{2}\\cos^{2}(\\omega u)}{16\\pi^{2}c}\\,\\sin^{2}\\theta,$$ the $\\sin^{2}\\theta$ pattern with zeros along $\\pm e_3$ and maximum in the equatorial plane when $\\cos(\\omega u)\\ne0$; at times with $\\cos(\\omega u)=0$ the density vanishes in every direction; its integral over the sphere is $$\\int_{S^2}\\frac{dP}{d\\sigma}d\\sigma=\\frac{\\mu_0\\omega^{4}p_0^{2}\\cos^{2}(\\omega u)}{6\\pi c},$$ using $\\int_{S^2}\\sin^{2}\\theta\\,d\\sigma=8\\pi/3$ from [[lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments]](a). (c) [Period average] The mean of the total power over one retarded-time period $u\\in[0,2\\pi/\\omega]$ is $$\\frac{\\omega}{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi/\\omega}\\frac{\\mu_0\\omega^{4}p_0^{2}\\cos^{2}(\\omega u)}{6\\pi c}\\,du=\\frac{\\mu_0\\omega^{4}p_0^{2}}{12\\pi c}.$$ (d) [Scope] The computation is the exact dipole law of the model; a finite prescribed source realizes it only within the absolute error bound of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]] with its $D(u)$, and a physical antenna's feed, current distribution and material realization are additional source models not supplied here. The average in (c) is taken in the retarded-time variable, not in observation time; no measurement, radiation resistance, matching condition or effective area is reported, and no empirical claim is made. (e) [Actual compact-window realization] Choose a compact smooth time cutoff $\\eta\\equiv1$ near one selected period, a nonnegative $C^\\infty$ bump $\\sigma$ supported in $B_a$ with integral $1$, and $p_\\eta(u)=\\eta(u)p_0\\cos(\\omega u)e_3$. Define $\\rho(y,u)=-p_\\eta(u)\\cdot\\nabla\\sigma(y)$, $J(y,u)=p_\\eta'(u)\\sigma(y)$. These are smooth compact spacetime sources, satisfy continuity, carry zero total charge and have moment $p_\\eta$. On the chosen period the moment equals the harmonic waveform, while the actual retarded field has the full $D(u)$, $\\Gamma_E/r^{2}$ and $(\\Gamma_B+\\Gamma_E/c)/r^{2}$ errors of the dipole theorem. Its asymptotic total-power deviation from the ideal law is bounded by that theorem's explicit $R(u)$; its period-averaged deviation is bounded by the average of $|R(u)|$. The condition $V=p''$ is a sufficient ideal transverse-moment condition; it is not equivalent to $D=0$. No eternal nonzero harmonic moment is asserted for a compact-time source.",
          "strategy": "Direct substitution in [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]]. (a) $p(u)=p_0\\cos(\\omega u)e_3$ gives $p''(u)=-\\omega^{2}p_0\\cos(\\omega u)e_3$ ([[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives|the scalar multiple rule]]), and clause (c) of the theorem gives $E_{\\mathrm{lead}}=\\mu_0\\,n\\times(n\\times p'')/(4\\pi r)$ with $B_{\\mathrm{lead}}=n\\times E_{\\mathrm{lead}}/c$ up to the stated errors; these are the ideal leading amplitudes; a finite-radius compact-source field still has its Gamma/r² remainder, even if a dipole-replacement error happens to vanish. The identity $n\\times(n\\times e_3)=n(n\\cdot e_3)-e_3$ with $n\\cdot e_3=\\cos\\theta$ and the Pythagorean identity give $|n\\times(n\\times e_3)|=|\\sin\\theta|$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]]). (b) Clause (d) of the theorem gives the limit $\\mu_0/(16\\pi^{2}c)\\int|n\\times V|^{2}d\\sigma$ with $V=p''$; substituting $p''$ and using $|n\\times p''|^{2}=|p''|^{2}\\sin^{2}\\theta$ yields the displayed density. Its integral uses $\\int_{S^2}\\sin^{2}\\theta\\,d\\sigma=2\\pi\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-u^{2})du=2\\pi\\cdot(4/3)=8\\pi/3$ by [[lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments]](a) with $f(u)=1-u^{2}$; multiplying $\\mu_0\\omega^{4}p_0^{2}\\cos^{2}(\\omega u)/(16\\pi^{2}c)$ by $8\\pi/3$ gives $\\mu_0\\omega^{4}p_0^{2}\\cos^{2}(\\omega u)/(6\\pi c)$. (c) The average uses $\\int_0^{2\\pi/\\omega}\\cos^{2}(\\omega u)du=\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$: substitute $v=\\omega u$ or exhibit the antiderivative $\\tfrac{u}{2}+\\tfrac{\\sin(2\\omega u)}{4\\omega}$ and evaluate at the endpoints by the fundamental theorem ([[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]]); multiplying the mean value of $\\cos^{2}$ by the coefficient gives the displayed result. (d) states the scope; no measured or fitted quantity appears, and the period average is explicitly in retarded time. For (e), choose a nonnegative smooth compact cutoff positive on a ball, normalize by its strictly positive finite Lebesgue integral, and scale that unit-mass bump to have support inside B_a ([[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]]) and eta by [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]]. Continuity is rho_t=-p_eta' dot grad sigma and div J=p_eta' dot grad sigma. Integration by parts with zero boundary gives integral rho0 and integral y_i rho=p_eta_i integral sigma=p_eta_i. Therefore [[pthm-em-retarded-potentials]] constructs the actual fields; [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]] gives their moment identities and complete finite-radius and flux errors, not an exact D0 premise for a time-varying harmonic current. Integrating the absolute error over one selected period gives the claimed average-error bound.",
          "physical_scope": "Deterministic model computation in the exact electric-dipole law for declared parameters: the $\\sin^2\\theta$ angular pattern and period-averaged power $\\mu_0\\omega^{4}p_0^{2}/(12\\pi c)$ of a prescribed harmonic dipole moment, together with an actual compact-window source realization carrying the dipole theorem's explicit finite-radius and flux errors. Excluded: antenna feed, material and matching models, radiation resistance, measurements and every empirical statement.",
          "provenance": {
