          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-larmor-nonrelativistic": "physical-result",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling": "physical-assumption",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-derivative-of-a-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-interval": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "When invoking the cited point-source emitted-power theorem as an established interpretation, retain its Countable Choice premise ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]). The reduced loss-rate formula in (H) below is independently adopted as a modelling hypothesis; the conditional finite-calculus orbit computation from (H) uses no choice. Adopt the following idealized, hypothetical setup of a prescribed slowly orbiting charge; it is a thought experiment with explicit premises, not a reported measurement. (H) Let $m>0$ be an externally supplied rest mass and $q\\ne0$ a charge parameter ([[def-em-si-conventions]], [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]]), let $\\kappa>0$ carry J m, and let $J_t\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, $t_0\\in J_t$, and $r:J_t\\to(0,\\infty)$ be $C^1$, with $r_0:=r(t_0)>0$. The reduced model assigns to each radius the circular-orbit values about a fixed laboratory centre ([[def-em-lab-geometry]]) for the attractive potential $V(r)=-\\kappa/r$: the circular-orbit energy is $E_{\\mathrm{orb}}(r)=-\\kappa/(2r)$ and the circular orbit with speed $v$ satisfies $mv^{2}=\\kappa/r$, with centripetal acceleration $a=\\kappa/(mr^{2})$. These are instantaneous circular reference values, not the exact kinematics of a curve with varying radius. To specify the intended validity window, choose dimensionless bounds $0<\\delta_v,\\delta_a<1$ and require $v(t)/c\\le\\delta_v$ and $T_{\\rm orb}(t)|r'(t)|/r(t)\\le\\delta_a$, where $v(t):=\\sqrt{\\kappa/(mr(t))}$ and $T_{\\rm orb}(t):=2\\pi r(t)/v(t)$; the second ratio measures fractional radius change in one reference period. Independently adopt that the electromagnetic energy emitted per unit time is instantaneously taken to be the Larmor value $q^{2}a(t)^{2}/(6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3})$ of [[pthm-em-larmor-nonrelativistic]] evaluated at the instantaneous centripetal acceleration, so that the adopted energy balance of the reduced model reads $$\\frac{d}{dt}E_{\\mathrm{orb}}(r(t))=-\\frac{q^{2}a(t)^{2}}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3}}.$$ Then, with $A:=q^{2}\\kappa/(3\\pi\\varepsilon_0m^{2}c^{3})>0$: (a) the reduced model implies $r'(t)=-A/r(t)^{2}$ for all $t$ where the model applies, and the circular orbit radius in the reduced model obeys $$r(t)^{3}=r_{0}^{3}-3A(t-t_{0})$$ on any interval containing $t_0$ on which $r>0$; (b) $r$ is strictly decreasing on $J_t$. The scalar balance ODE, considered without the validity-window inequalities, has the unique maximal positive continuation $\\widehat r(t):=(r_0^3-3A(t-t_0))^{1/3}$ on $(-\\infty,t_*)$, where $t_*:=t_0+r_0^3/(3A)$. It restricts to $r$ on $J_t$ and satisfies $\\widehat r(t)\\downarrow0$ as $t\\uparrow t_*$; assigning zero at $t_*$ is only a continuous endpoint extension, not a positive-radius solution; (c) [Scope and qualifications] the deduction is conditional on (H). The low-speed premise $v^{2}=\\kappa/(mr)\\ll c^{2}$ necessarily fails as $r\\to0$, so the model's own hypotheses break down near the collapse time and the computation is not a proof that a physical orbit reaches $r=0$; it is not a derivation of atomic lifetimes, not a coupled point-particle self-force calculation (that dynamics is not available in this framework), and it supplies no empirical evidence. The reduced-model finite-time collapse is retained as a mathematical property of (H), while all conclusions about nature remain conditional on (H) and on its regime of validity. No measurement, dataset, statistical model or uncertainty is introduced.",
          "strategy": "A complete conditional deduction from (H). (a) Differentiate $E_{\\mathrm{orb}}(r)=-\\kappa/(2r)$ along $r(t)$: the power rule gives $\\tfrac{d}{dt}(-\\kappa/(2r))=\\kappa r'/(2r^{2})$ ([[lem-derivative-of-a-power]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); the balance (H) reads $\\kappa r'/(2r^{2})=-q^{2}a^{2}/(6\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^{3})$; substituting $a=\\kappa/(mr^{2})$ and solving for $r'$ gives $r'=-q^{2}\\kappa/(3\\pi\\varepsilon_0m^{2}c^{3}r^{2})=-A/r^{2}$. Multiplying by $3r^{2}$ gives $\\tfrac{d}{dt}(r^{3})=-3A$, and the fundamental theorem applied on any interval where $r>0$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[def-interval]]) yields the displayed cubic; equivalently, differentiating the cubic reproduces the ODE, so the two are equivalent in the reduced model. (b) $r'=-A/r^{2}<0$ since $A>0$, so $r$ is strictly decreasing wherever the model applies; from the cubic, $r(t)^{3}\\downarrow0$ as $t\\uparrow t_{*}=t_{0}+r_{0}^{3}/(3A)$, and $r(t)^{3}<0$ beyond $t_{*}$ records the breakdown rather than a negative radius. (c) The scope clause records the failure of $v^{2}=\\kappa/(mr)\\ll c^{2}$ as $r\\to0$, the absence of any coupled self-force dynamics in the framework (the point self-field is singular and its product with the current is undefined, as recorded by the scope remark on this page), and the absence of empirical content: this is a mapped, reduced model with a finite-time formal collapse, exactly as stated, and the conclusion is conditional on (H) throughout. The Larmor energy-loss expression is explicitly ADOPTED in(H) for the reduced slowly orbiting mechanical model, not obtained by pretending each laboratory orbit event is a rest event. The preceding Larmor theorem supplies the rest law and quantified general low-speed comparison; converting a prescribed emitted power into this mechanical balance remains the separate assumption(H). The ODE deduction itself uses only the stated scalar balance and finite calculus, and proves no self-force law.",
          "physical_scope": "Reduced quasistatic circular-orbit model with an adopted Larmor energy-balance premise (H): the radius ODE $r'=-A/r^{2}$, the cubic solution $r^{3}=r_{0}^{3}-3A(t-t_{0})$ and the formal finite-time collapse limit as a property of (H). Excluded: any claim that a physical orbit reaches the centre or an atom decays in finite time; coupled self-force dynamics; derivation of lifetimes; measurements, statistical models and every empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
