          "id": "pthm-em-tensor-maxwell-equivalence",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Tensor Maxwell equations equal the four laboratory Maxwell equations",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#C2",
          "design_state": "complete-explicit-branch-argument; not-independent-acceptance",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-sources",
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ with its inertial chart $x$, the electric and magnetic fields $E,B$ of [[def-em-fields]], the source fields $(\\rho,J)$ of [[def-em-sources]] and the SI conventions of [[def-em-si-conventions]] with $\\varepsilon_0:=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ be those of the framework, and let $F$ be the electromagnetic two-form of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] with the components fixed there, $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, $F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ and $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and let $j=(c\\rho,J)$ carry the tensor-component identification adopted in [[def-em-sources]]. Assume the smooth branch of the vacuum Maxwell postulate [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]: $E,B$ are jointly $C^1$ on $\\Omega\\times I$ with the additional derivatives required by each displayed equation existing and continuous, and $\\rho,J$ are continuous. Write $\\partial_0=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ and $\\partial_i$ for the coordinate partial derivatives of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and $\\operatorname{div},\\operatorname{curl}$ for those of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]. Then: (a) [Equivalence] On $I\\times\\Omega$ the four smooth-branch equations $$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$$ hold if and only if the two systems $$(\\mathrm{TM1})\\quad \\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\mu_0\\,j^\\nu\\ (\\nu=0,1,2,3),\\qquad (\\mathrm{TM2})\\quad \\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}=0\\ (\\lambda,\\mu,\\nu\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\})$$ hold on $I\\times\\Omega$. Every instance of $(\\mathrm{TM2})$ with a repeated index vanishes identically because $F$ is alternating, so $(\\mathrm{TM1})$ and $(\\mathrm{TM2})$ carry exactly eight independent scalar equations: $(\\mathrm{TM1})$ with $\\nu=0$ is $\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$ (using $\\mu_0c^2\\varepsilon_0=1$), $(\\mathrm{TM1})$ with $\\nu=i$ is the $i$-th component of $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE$, $(\\mathrm{TM2})$ with $(\\lambda,\\mu,\\nu)=(1,2,3)$ is $\\operatorname{div}B=0$, and the three cyclic triples $(0,i,j)$ are the three components of $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$; conversely these eight scalar equations reproduce the two systems. (b) [Form invariance under a change of inertial description] Let $x'$ be another inertial chart with transition $x'=\\Lambda x+b$, $\\Lambda$ a proper orthochronous Lorentz matrix (the changes of inertial description adopted in [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]]), and let the primed components of the same objects be the tensor components of [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iii): $F'^{\\,ab}=\\Lambda^a{}_c\\Lambda^b{}_dF^{cd}$, $F'_{ab}=(\\Lambda^{-1})^c{}_a(\\Lambda^{-1})^d{}_bF_{cd}$, $j'^a=\\Lambda^a{}_bj^b$. Then $(\\mathrm{TM1})$ holds in $x$ if and only if $(\\mathrm{TM1})$ holds in $x'$ with $j'$, and $(\\mathrm{TM2})$ holds in $x$ if and only if it holds in $x'$; by (a) applied in both charts the four smooth-branch equations are likewise form-invariant, the primed fields satisfying the primed equations with the primed sources. (c) [Hypotheses actually used] Only first derivatives of $E,B$ occur; the equivalence uses exactly the component dictionary, the alternating character of $F$, the constants $\\mu_0,c,\\varepsilon_0$ and the identification $j=(c\\rho,J)$, and the covariance clause uses the constant-coefficient component law and the invertibility of $\\Lambda$. No potential, no second derivative, no boundary, decay or smoothness hypothesis beyond the stated class, and no converse from the wave equations to the Maxwell equations is used or asserted. (d) [Scope] Only the smooth branch is covered; the weak branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] and field or source points of singular support are excluded. The conventions $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ fix the signs of $(\\mathrm{TM1})$, and the alternative sign convention, which reverses the form of the sourced equation, is not mixed with them. The statement is conditional on the adopted postulates, uses no choice principle and makes no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** component computation, then the transformation law. (a) Substitute the displayed components: $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu0}=\\partial_iF^{i0}=-\\operatorname{div}E/c$ versus $-\\mu_0j^0=-\\mu_0c\\rho$ gives $\\operatorname{div}E=\\mu_0c^2\\rho=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0$; $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu i}=\\partial_0F^{0i}+\\partial_jF^{ji}=c^{-2}\\partial_tE_i-(\\operatorname{curl}B)_i$ versus $-\\mu_0j^i$ gives the $i$-th Ampère component; $\\partial_1F_{23}+\\partial_2F_{31}+\\partial_3F_{12}=\\operatorname{div}B$; and the cyclic sums $\\partial_0F_{ij}+\\partial_iF_{j0}+\\partial_jF_{0i}=c^{-1}\\left(\\epsilon_{ijk}\\partial_tB_k+\\partial_iE_j-\\partial_jE_i\\right)$ for the three cyclic pairs $(i,j)$ give the three components of $\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB$. Each display is an identity after substituting the components, so both implications follow; no derivative is commuted and no epsilon-identity beyond the coordinate definition of the components is used. (b) For $(\\mathrm{TM1})$: $\\partial'_aF'^{ab}=(\\Lambda^{-1})^c{}_a\\Lambda^a{}_e\\Lambda^b{}_f\\partial_cF^{ef}=\\Lambda^b{}_f\\partial_cF^{cf}$ by [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iv) and orthogonality $\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}\\eta\\Lambda=\\eta$, so $\\partial_cF^{cf}=-\\mu_0j^f$ is equivalent to $\\partial'_aF'^{ab}=-\\mu_0j'^b$. For $(\\mathrm{TM2})$ the three derivative factors contribute the same trilinear factor $(\\Lambda^{-1})^{\\otimes3}$ to the cyclic sum, and $\\Lambda^{-1}$ is invertible; hence zero is preserved in both directions. No physical equivalence of descriptions is proved: clause (b) is conditional on the adoption of [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]].",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional statement about the smooth branch of the adopted vacuum Maxwell postulate on the laboratory region: the four SI Maxwell equations are equivalent to the two tensor equations for the components of the electromagnetic two-form and four-current fixed by [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] and [[def-em-sources]], with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $x^0=ct$, $\\mu_0c^2\\varepsilon_0=1$, and form-invariance under proper orthochronous changes of inertial chart. Excluded: the weak branch, singular source support, magnetic charge, potentials, and any dynamics beyond the adopted equations; no empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.1-§5.1.4 printed pp. 95-102 (four-vectors, proper time, raising and lowering indices, tensor transformation law (5.3)-(5.10)) and §5.3-§5.3.2 printed pp. 105-108 (the electromagnetic tensor (5.15) and the transformation law (5.16)), read in the full extracted text of the 236-page author-hosted revision; the source's $(+---)$ and $A_\\mu=(\\varphi/c,-A)$ conventions are converted to the framework's $(-+++)$, $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ dictionary"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course, University of Texas at Austin (complete author-hosted lecture notes, document dated 2006-02-02)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node106.html",
                "locator": "node 121 'The electromagnetic field tensor' (eqs. (1467)-(1475): the antisymmetric field tensor with $F_{i4}=-F_{4i}=-E_i$, $F_{ij}=-cB_{ij}$) and node 123 'Transformation of fields', read in the complete author-hosted HTML lecture notes; conventions converted to $x^0=ct$ and the framework's field dictionary"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zürich 2024 FS (author-hosted 176-page lecture script)",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf",
                "locator": "§9.2 'Tensoren' pp. 9.5-9.8 and §9.3 'Kovariante Elektrodynamik' pp. 9.8-9.12 (field-strength tensor (9.42), four-current (9.43), $\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}\\partial^\\mu F^{\\nu\\rho}=\\mu_0J^\\rho$ (9.44), conservation (9.45), and the homogeneous equations (9.46)-(9.48)), read in the author-hosted 176-page script; the source's index placement and overall sign are converted to the framework's dictionary"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Covariance, boosts and the two field invariants",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#C3",
          "design_state": "complete-explicit-branch-argument; not-independent-acceptance",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-tensor-maxwell-equivalence",
