            "def-em-plane-wave-definition": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform": "physical-result",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light": "physical-assumption",
            "post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration": "physical-result",
            "thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-boost": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-orthochronous-lorentz-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-sr-inertial-covariance": "physical-assumption",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $E,B$ be jointly $C^k$ fields of [[def-em-fields]] on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of an inertial chart of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] in SI units ([[def-em-si-conventions]]), $k\\ge1$, and let $F$ be the electromagnetic two-form of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] with its fixed components $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. Let $n\\in V$ be a future unit observer vector, $\\eta(n,n)=-1$, with rest space $n^\\perp$ and positive rest metric ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (c)), and suppose its coordinate velocity in the laboratory frame is $w$, $|w|<c$, so that $n=\\gamma_w(1,w/c)$ with $\\gamma_w:=(1-|w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$ ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (d)-(e)). Then: (a) [Observer split and measured vectors] with $e_a:=F_{ab}n^b$, $b_a:=(*F)_{ab}n^b$ as in [[thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition]], one has $e(n)=b(n)=0$, $e^\\sharp,b^\\sharp\\in n^\\perp$, the reconstruction $F=n^\\flat\\wedge e-*(n^\\flat\\wedge b)$ (so the six rest components $(e,b)$ determine $F$ at the event), and in the laboratory frame $$e_i=\\frac{\\gamma_w}{c}\\bigl(E_i+(w\\times B)_i\\bigr),\\qquad b_i=-\\gamma_w\\Bigl(B_i-\\frac{(w\\times E)_i}{c^2}\\Bigr),\\qquad e_0=-\\frac{\\gamma_w}{c^2}E\\cdot w,\\qquad b_0=\\frac{\\gamma_w}{c}B\\cdot w.$$ Define the measured electric and magnetic vectors $E_n:=c\\,e^\\sharp$ and $B_n:=-b^\\sharp$, the covectors first restricted to $n^\\perp$ and raised with its positive rest metric (equivalently their spacetime metric-dual vectors, since both annihilate $n$); under the model assumption (A1) that an ideal field detector comoving with the observer at the reception event reads the components of $F$ in the observer's rest frame — equivalently that $E_n$ and $B_n$ are the electric and magnetic fields read there — the observed components are, in the observer's inertial rest frame, the arbitrary-direction boosted components of [[pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform]] (a′) for the vector parameter $\\beta=w/c$: the components along $w$ equal $E_\\parallel$ and $B_\\parallel$, and the components perpendicular to $w$ equal $\\gamma_w(E+w\\times B)_\\perp$ and $\\gamma_w(B-w\\times E/c^2)_\\perp$. At $w=0$ the observer is the laboratory rest observer and reads $E,B$ themselves; there is no distinguished direction of motion. Orthogonality means $e(n)=b(n)=0$, not vanishing temporal covector components in an unrelated laboratory frame. (b) [Plane wave and exact frequency reading] let $K>0$, $\\omega=cK$, a unit vector $s$, and complex amplitudes $e_{\\mathrm a},b_{\\mathrm a}\\in\\mathbb C^3$ be the data of a plane wave of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] whose amplitude equations hold as in [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]], so that the pair is a source-free smooth Maxwell field and $s$ is the propagation direction. Then the phase covector with components $k_a=(-\\omega/c,(\\omega/c)s_1,(\\omega/c)s_2,(\\omega/c)s_3)$ in the laboratory frame is a nonzero future null covector, and under the ideal-plane-wave reading rule of [[post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model]] (ii) the observer reads the exact angular frequency $$\\omega_n:=-c\\,k(n)=\\gamma_w\\Bigl(1-\\frac{w\\cdot s}{c}\\Bigr)\\omega>0,$$ with the direction transformation (aberration), longitudinal formulas and two transverse protocols of [[pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration]] (i)-(v) apply with their nonzero-motion qualifications; at $w=0$ frequency and direction are unchanged. Successive inertial changes act on the same phase covector by [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (ii); each stage updates the direction as well as the frequency, and positive successive frequency ratios multiply. This composition statement is a direct covector/reading calculation, not a nonexistent composition clause of the Doppler supplier. (c) [Status and scope] the observer split, the nullity of $k$ and the frequency formula are conditional deductions from the stated items, while the identification of $(E_n,B_n)$ and of $\\omega_n$ with apparatus readings is the explicitly adopted ideal-detector model assumption (A1) together with the ideal-plane-wave model [[post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model]] (with [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] for the clock and light readings); no calibration, apparatus, statistical or empirical claim is made, no photon model and no identification of frequency with energy by a factor $\\hbar$ is used, and the statement is pointwise at a reception event: the decomposition and the reconstruction of clause (a) hold pointwise for any future unit observer $n$, while the frequency and aberration readings of clause (b) are stated for inertial observers — an accelerating observer requires its own analysis, since differentiating the decomposition adds derivatives of $n$ — and media, dispersion, modulation, boundaries and finite line widths are outside the item.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** evaluate the observer decomposition of [[thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition]] at the given $n$, then apply the exact SR Doppler theorem to the wave data. (a) Directly evaluate the adopted dictionary at $n=\\gamma_w(1,w/c)$: $e_0=-\\gamma_w E\\cdot w/c^2$, $e_i=\\gamma_w(E_i+(w\\times B)_i)/c$. The dual dictionary is $(*F)_{0i}=B_i$, $(*F)_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}E_k/c$, so $b_0=\\gamma_w B\\cdot w/c$ and $b_i=-\\gamma_w(B_i-(w\\times E)_i/c^2)$. In particular $n^ae_a=n^ab_a=0$, using $w\\cdot(w\\times B)=w\\cdot(w\\times E)=0$; antisymmetry gives the same annihilation intrinsically. Raising with the spacetime metric maps these annihilating covectors into $n^\\perp$, and its restriction agrees with the positive rest-space musical isomorphism. The reconstruction is exactly [[thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition]] (a). The vector boost [[def-lorentz-boost]] with parameter $w/c$ sends $n$ to $(1,0,0,0)$: its temporal component is $\\gamma_w^2(1-|w|^2/c^2)=1$ and the spatial block cancels. Thus its primed oriented axes are the observer's canonical inertial rest axes. Apply the repaired arbitrary-direction law [[pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform]] (a′); in those axes $e'_0=b'_0=0$, $E'_i=c e'_i$ and $B'_i=-b'_i$, giving exactly the measured-vector components under assumption (A1). For $w=0$ the boost is the identity. No rest-space metric lowering can erase a nonzero laboratory component; the two component descriptions are distinct. (b) The amplitude equations give $|k|=\\omega/c$ and $s=k/|k|$ with $|s|=1$ ([[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]] (b)); raising with $\\eta$ gives $k^0=\\omega/c>0$, $k^i=(\\omega/c)s^i$ and $\\eta(k^\\sharp,k^\\sharp)=-(\\omega/c)^2+(\\omega/c)^2|s|^2=0$, so $k$ is future null and nonzero. The data $(\\omega,s)$ therefore satisfy the hypotheses of [[pthm-sr-null-wave-doppler-and-aberration]], whose clauses (i)-(v) give $\\omega_n=\\gamma_w(1-w\\cdot s/c)\\omega>0$, the aberrated direction and the longitudinal/transverse cases; the reading rule is clause (ii) of [[post-sr-ideal-plane-wave-model]]. For successive changes $x_1=\\Lambda_1x_0+b_1$, $x_2=\\Lambda_2x_1+b_2$, the covector laws give $k_2=(\\Lambda_2^{-1})^{\\mathsf T}(\\Lambda_1^{-1})^{\\mathsf T}k_0=((\\Lambda_2\\Lambda_1)^{-1})^{\\mathsf T}k_0$. The affine phase constant transforms with the translations as in [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (ii). Apply the positive frequency reading at each stage, obtaining $\\omega_2/\\omega_0=(\\omega_2/\\omega_1)(\\omega_1/\\omega_0)$ with the correctly updated intermediate direction. (c) records the adopted interpretation premise (A1) and the scope; no choice principle is used and no empirical premise enters.",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional statement about the smooth framework fields at one event: the decomposition of the field into the rest components of a future unit observer, the reconstruction of $F$, the measured electric and magnetic vectors of an inertial observer with coordinate velocity $w$, and the exact angular-frequency reading and aberration of an ideal plane wave, with the ideal-detector and ideal-plane-wave model assumptions stated. Excluded: apparatus calibration, real detectors, accelerated observers, media and dispersion, modulation, finite line widths, intensity and polarization measurement, quantum photon models, and any statistical or empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§4.3.1 printed pp. 84-87 (the monochromatic plane wave with positive angular frequency, the dispersion relation $\\omega^2=c^2k^2$ and the right-moving profile (4.15)-(4.16)) and §5.1.2-§5.1.3 printed pp. 97-99 (proper time and index raising), read in the full extracted text"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course, University of Texas at Austin (complete author-hosted lecture notes, document dated 2006-02-02)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node106.html",
                "locator": "node 122 'The dual electromagnetic field tensor' (the dual components used by the observer split) and node 123 'Transformation of fields' (the boosted field components read by a moving observer), read in the complete author-hosted lecture notes"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zürich 2024 FS (author-hosted 176-page lecture script)",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf",
                "locator": "§9.3 'Kovariante Elektrodynamik' pp. 9.8-9.9 (the field-strength tensor and its dual (9.42), (9.48)), read in the author-hosted script"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 16
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "The four-force equation reproduces the coordinate Lorentz force and power",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#C4",
          "design_state": "complete-explicit-branch-argument; not-independent-acceptance",
          "deps": [
