            "thm-intermediate-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the framework's spacetime, field and worldline objects of [[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]], [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]], with the SI units of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and clause (i) of the adopted Lorentz coupling [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]]: a constant charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ of [[def-em-sources]] (a), an externally supplied rest mass $m>0$, a future timelike $C^2$ massive worldline $x:J\\to A$ with four-velocity $U$, and a prescribed field $F$ of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] that is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of the graph of $x$ and is generated externally to the particle; the adopted equation of motion is $$m\\,\\frac{dU}{d\\tau}=q\\,\\Phi(U),\\qquad \\eta(\\Phi(u),w)=F(w,u),$$ $U$ the four-velocity of [[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]] (b) with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $t\\mapsto r(t)$ be the laboratory chart reading of the worldline, $v:=r'$ its coordinate velocity and $\\gamma:=(1-|v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, so that $U=\\gamma(c,v)$ ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (e)). Then, wherever the worldline is $C^2$: (a) [Coordinate force and power] with the coordinate three-momentum $p:=m\\gamma v$ of [[pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force]] (a), $$\\frac{dp}{dt}=q\\,(E+v\\times B),\\qquad \\frac{d(\\gamma mc^2)}{dt}=q\\,E\\cdot v,$$ the derivatives taken with respect to the chart time $t$ and $E,B$ the components of $F$ in the laboratory frame. (b) [Norm preservation] $s\\mapsto\\eta(U(s),U(s))$ is constant on $J$ with value $-c^2$; in particular a solution with future timelike initial data remains future timelike, and $|v(t)|<c$ wherever the reading is differentiable ([[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]]). (c) [Controlled low-speed expansion] for every $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, on any interval where $|v|/c\\le\\delta$, $$|\\gamma-1|\\le\\frac{|v|^2/c^2}{2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad \\Bigl|\\gamma-1-\\frac{|v|^2}{2c^2}\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\delta^2)^{-5/2}\\Bigl(\\frac{|v|^2}{c^2}\\Bigr)^{2},$$ and consequently $$|p-mv|\\le m|v|\\,\\frac{|v|^2}{2c^2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2}},\\qquad \\Bigl|\\gamma mc^2-mc^2-\\tfrac12m|v|^2\\Bigr|\\le\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\delta^2)^{-5/2}\\frac{m|v|^4}{c^2};$$ the constants depend only on $\\delta$, so the bounds are uniform in $|v|/c\\le\\delta$. These are pointwise algebraic expansions; they assert no approximation of the trajectory over a time interval, which would require a separate stability argument. (d) [Scope] The charge is constant, $m>0$ is an externally supplied parameter and $F$ is a prescribed exterior field: the particle's own field, radiation reaction and any self-force are excluded, as are magnetic monopoles and zero or variable mass; the nonrelativistic coordinate law is not adopted as exact, and (c) is a controlled kinematic expansion, not a trajectory approximation. The statement is conditional on the adopted postulates and makes no empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** contract the field with the four-velocity in the fixed component dictionary, then apply the mean value theorem to $(1-z)^{-1/2}$. (a) With $\\Phi(u)^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}F_{\\alpha\\beta}u^\\beta$ ([[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]]) and $U=\\gamma(c,v)$, $U_\\nu=\\gamma(-c,v)$: $q\\,\\Phi(U)^i=q\\gamma\\bigl(cF^i{}_0+F^i{}_jv^j\\bigr)=q\\gamma(E_i+(v\\times B)_i)$ since $F^i{}_0=\\eta_{00}F^{i0}=E_i/c$ and $F^i{}_j=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$; and $q\\,\\Phi(U)^0=q\\gamma E\\cdot v/c$. Since $m\\,dU^i/d\\tau=q\\Phi(U)^i$ and $d/dt=\\gamma^{-1}d/d\\tau$ ([[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]] (e)), $dp_i/dt=\\gamma^{-1}\\,q\\gamma(E+v\\times B)_i=q(E+v\\times B)_i$ with $p=m\\gamma v$ from [[pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force]] (a); the temporal component gives $d(\\gamma mc^2)/dt=qE\\cdot v$ after multiplying by $c$. (b) $\\eta(U,U)$ has derivative $2\\eta(U,U')=2(q/m)\\eta(U,\\Phi(U))=0$ by the orthogonality clause of [[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]] (which needs no derivative of the field); the initial value $-c^2$ is that of the four-velocity. For the stated solution-preservation conclusion, differentiability makes $U$ continuous and the norm identity gives $(U^0)^2=c^2+|U_{\\mathrm{sp}}|^2\\ge c^2$. Thus $U^0$ cannot vanish; on a connected interval its sign cannot change by [[thm-intermediate-value]]. Positive initial $U^0$ therefore stays positive and the solution stays in the future timelike component. This conditional argument asserts no existence or continuation of solutions. (c) $z:=|v|^2/c^2$ and $f(z):=(1-z)^{-1/2}$; by [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]] and [[thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent]], $f'(z)=\\tfrac12(1-z)^{-3/2}$ and $f''(z)=\\tfrac34(1-z)^{-5/2}$ with $a^{-r}=(a^r)^{-1}$ from [[lem-rational-power-laws]]. At $z=0$ all stated differences and bounds are zero directly; henceforth assume $0<z\\le\\delta^2$. First-order bound by the mean value theorem ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]): $|f(z)-1|=|f'(\\xi)|z\\le z\\,/\\,(2(1-\\delta^2)^{3/2})$ for some $\\xi\\in(0,z)$, using monotonicity of the positive power ([[lem-rational-power-monotone]], [[lem-of-inverse-positive]]). Second-order remainder by Cauchy's mean value theorem ([[thm-cauchy-mean-value]]) applied on $[0,z]$ to $q(s):=f(s)-1-\\tfrac s2$ and $g(s):=s^2$: $q(z)/z^2=q'(\\xi)/g'(\\xi)=(f'(\\xi)-\\tfrac12)/(2\\xi)$ for some $\\xi\\in(0,z)$, and the mean value theorem on $[0,\\xi]$ applied to $f'$ gives $f'(\\xi)-\\tfrac12=f'(\\xi)-f'(0)=\\xi f''(\\eta)$ for some $\\eta\\in(0,\\xi)$, so $|f(z)-1-\\tfrac z2|=\\tfrac12|f''(\\eta)|z^2\\le\\tfrac38(1-\\delta^2)^{-5/2}z^2$. The two displays for $p$ and $\\gamma mc^2$ are the multiplication of these bounds by $m|v|$ and $mc^2$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-abs-value]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] for the product $p=m\\gamma v$). (d) The scope clause records the postulates and exclusions; no choice principle and no empirical premise are used.",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional statement about one charged massive particle of constant charge and positive external rest mass moving on a future timelike $C^2$ worldline in a prescribed exterior electromagnetic field that is $C^1$ near the graph, under the adopted covariant coupling: the coordinate Lorentz force and power, preservation of the four-velocity norm, and uniform low-speed bounds with constants depending only on the speed interval. Excluded: the particle's own field, radiation reaction, self-force, magnetic monopoles, variable or zero mass, trajectory approximation over time without a stability argument, and any measurement or empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
