            "thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-em-poynting-balance": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-momentum-balance": "physical-result",
            "def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the smooth branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]]: $E,B$ jointly $C^1$ fields of [[def-em-fields]] with the additional derivatives required by each Maxwell equation existing and continuous, continuous sources $(\\rho,J)$ of [[def-em-sources]] and the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; let $F$ be the electromagnetic two-form of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] with its fixed components, $j=(c\\rho,J)$ the four-current, $u$ and $S$ the energy density and Poynting vector of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]], and $g$, $\\sigma$, $\\Pi=-\\sigma$ the momentum density, Maxwell stress and momentum flux of [[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]]. Define the stress-energy tensor of the field by $$T^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\Bigl(F^{\\mu\\alpha}F^\\nu{}_\\alpha-\\frac14\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}F^{\\alpha\\beta}F_{\\alpha\\beta}\\Bigr).$$ Then: (a) [Object] $T$ is a symmetric type-$(2,0)$ tensor field of [[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]] with $\\partial_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu}$ continuous on $U$, it is traceless, $\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}T^{\\mu\\nu}=0$, and under a proper orthochronous change of inertial chart with transition $x'=\\Lambda x+b$ its components transform as $T'^{\\mu\\nu}=\\Lambda^\\mu{}_\\alpha\\Lambda^\\nu{}_\\beta T^{\\alpha\\beta}$ ([[pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform]], [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iii)). (b) [Components] in the laboratory frame $$T^{00}=u=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0|E|^2}{2}+\\frac{|B|^2}{2\\mu_0},\\qquad T^{0i}=T^{i0}=\\frac{S_i}{c}=c\\,g_i=\\frac{(E\\times B)_i}{\\mu_0c},\\qquad T^{ij}=-\\sigma_{ij},$$ where $\\sigma_{ij}=\\varepsilon_0\\bigl(E_iE_j-\\tfrac12\\delta_{ij}|E|^2\\bigr)+\\mu_0^{-1}\\bigl(B_iB_j-\\tfrac12\\delta_{ij}|B|^2\\bigr)$; in particular the spatial block is minus the Maxwell stress of [[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]]. (c) [Balance] under the smooth branch, $$\\partial_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu}=-f^\\nu,\\qquad f^\\nu:=F^{\\nu\\lambda}j_\\lambda,$$ where algebraically $f=(J\\cdot E/c,\\rho E+J\\times B)$. Its interpretation as the electromagnetic four-force density delivered to matter uses the separately adopted continuum coupling [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] (ii) for matter models and sources satisfying that postulate's hypotheses. The component identities are $\\partial_tu+\\operatorname{div}S=-J\\cdot E$ and $\\partial_tg-\\operatorname{div}\\sigma=-(\\rho E+J\\times B)$ of [[pthm-em-poynting-balance]] (a) and [[pthm-em-momentum-balance]] (a). (d) [Matter coupling and finite-flux conservation] let $T_{\\mathrm m}$ be a $C^1$ symmetric type-$(2,0)$ field on $U$ satisfying $\\partial_\\mu T_{\\mathrm m}{}^{\\mu\\nu}=f^\\nu$ (an adopted matter model, not derived here); then $\\partial_\\mu(T+T_{\\mathrm m})^{\\mu\\nu}=0$, on every finite axis-parallel box with an open neighbourhood contained in $U$, the box and slice balance identities of [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]] hold for the total tensor with zero divergence, retaining all side-flux terms. For each component $b$ and $c_0\\le s_1<s_2\\le d_0$, set $\\mathcal F^b(s_1,s_2):=\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\left(\\int_{[s_1,s_2]\\times K_i^+}T_{\\mathrm{total}}{}^{ib}\\,d^3x-\\int_{[s_1,s_2]\\times K_i^-}T_{\\mathrm{total}}{}^{ib}\\,d^3x\\right)$, with $K:=\\prod_{i=1}^{3}[c_i,d_i]$, its faces $K_i^\\pm$, and $Q^b(s):=\\int_K T_{\\mathrm{total}}{}^{0b}(s,x)\\,d^3x$ as in that theorem. Then $Q^b(s_2)-Q^b(s_1)=-\\mathcal F^b(s_1,s_2)$, so $Q^b(s_2)=Q^b(s_1)$ if and only if $\\mathcal F^b(s_1,s_2)=0$. In particular, $Q^b$ is constant throughout $[c_0,d_0]$ if and only if this net signed side flux vanishes on every subinterval $[s_1,s_2]$. The theorem's facewise-zero side-flux hypothesis is sufficient, but is not necessary, for constancy; zero divergence alone does not guarantee it. The angular current $M^{\\lambda\\mu\\nu}:=x^\\mu T_{\\mathrm{total}}{}^{\\lambda\\nu}-x^\\nu T_{\\mathrm{total}}{}^{\\lambda\\mu}$ satisfies $\\partial_\\lambda M^{\\lambda\\mu\\nu}=0$ by symmetry. (e) [Scope] the divergence is a product identity of $C^1$ objects and is not asserted for the weak branch, at singular source support or for any product of distributions; symmetry, zero trace and the component display are algebraic; no matter tensor is derived from vacuum Maxwell, no radiation reaction or self-force is included, and no empirical claim is made.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct algebra from the components for (a)-(b); the product rule and the cyclic identity for (c); summation and the finite-box theorem for (d). (a) Symmetry: $\\eta_{\\alpha\\beta}F^{\\mu\\alpha}F^{\\nu\\beta}=\\eta_{\\alpha\\beta}F^{\\mu\\beta}F^{\\nu\\alpha}$ by relabelling, and $\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}$ is symmetric; trace: $\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}T^{\\mu\\nu}=\\mu_0^{-1}(F^{\\alpha\\beta}F_{\\alpha\\beta}-\\tfrac14\\cdot4\\cdot F^{\\alpha\\beta}F_{\\alpha\\beta})=0$; the transformation law is contraction of two copies of the component law of [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iii) with $\\eta$ invariant, and the regularity is the component criterion of [[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]] (every term is a polynomial in the $C^1$ components). (b) Substituting $F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ and $F^{00}=0$: $T^{00}=\\mu_0^{-1}(|E|^2/c^2+\\tfrac14\\cdot2(|B|^2-|E|^2/c^2))=\\varepsilon_0|E|^2/2+|B|^2/(2\\mu_0)$ using $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$; $T^{0i}=\\mu_0^{-1}F^{0j}F^i{}_j=(E\\times B)_i/(\\mu_0c)$ with [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; $T^{ij}=-\\sigma_{ij}$ by the component computation $\\sum_k\\epsilon_{ikm}\\epsilon_{jkn}B_mB_n=\\delta_{ij}|B|^2-B_iB_j$ and the negative temporal contraction of $F^{i0}F^{j}{}_0$. (c) Product rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) gives $\\mu_0\\partial_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu}=(\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\alpha})F^\\nu{}_\\alpha+F^{\\mu\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu F^\\nu{}_\\alpha-\\tfrac12F^{\\alpha\\beta}\\partial^\\nu F_{\\alpha\\beta}$; the first factor is $-\\mu_0j^\\alpha$ by [[pthm-em-tensor-maxwell-equivalence]] ((TM1)), giving $-f^\\nu$; for the cancellation set $I=F^{\\alpha\\beta}F_{\\alpha\\beta}$ and fix a free index $\\kappa$. Contract the cyclic identity $\\partial_\\mu F_{\\kappa\\alpha}+\\partial_\\kappa F_{\\alpha\\mu}+\\partial_\\alpha F_{\\mu\\kappa}=0$ with $F^{\\mu\\alpha}$. Its first and third terms both equal $C_\\kappa:=F^{\\mu\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu F_{\\kappa\\alpha}$, the third by interchanging the dummy indices and using alternation twice; the middle term is $-F^{\\mu\\alpha}\\partial_\\kappa F_{\\mu\\alpha}=-\\tfrac12\\partial_\\kappa I$. Hence $C_\\kappa=\\tfrac14\\partial_\\kappa I$. Raise only this free index: $F^{\\mu\\alpha}\\partial_\\mu F^\\nu{}_\\alpha=\\tfrac14\\partial^\\nu I=\\tfrac12F^{\\alpha\\beta}\\partial^\\nu F_{\\alpha\\beta}$, so the two product-rule terms cancel. The first term is $-\\mu_0 j^\\alpha F^\\nu{}_\\alpha=-\\mu_0 F^{\\nu\\lambda}j_\\lambda$, giving precisely $-f^\\nu$ after dividing by $\\mu_0$. All repeated indices here are dummy indices and the free index is never reused in $I$. The component identities are (b) read in $(c)$; they coincide with the independently proved local identities of [[pthm-em-poynting-balance]] (a) and [[pthm-em-momentum-balance]] (a). (d) Add the matter equation; the finite box and slice balance identities, with side flux retained, are [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]] applied to the $C^1$ symmetric total tensor with vanishing divergence; the angular identity uses $\\partial_\\lambda x^\\mu=\\delta^\\mu_\\lambda$ and the symmetry $T_{\\mathrm{total}}{}^{\\mu\\nu}=T_{\\mathrm{total}}{}^{\\nu\\mu}$. The algebra gives $f^0=J\\cdot E/c$ and $f^i=\\rho E_i+(J\\times B)_i$; identifying those source terms with matter work and force uses [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] (ii) only in that interpretation. (e) records the regularity scope. No choice principle and no empirical premise are used.",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional statement on the smooth branch of the framework's Maxwell fields: the symmetric traceless stress-energy tensor quadratic in the field, its component identification with the energy density, Poynting flux, momentum density and Maxwell stress, the local balance $\\partial_\\mu T^{\\mu\\nu}=-f^\\nu$ with the Lorentz four-force density, and conditional total conservation with an adopted $C^1$ symmetric matter tensor together with finite-box flux identities. Excluded: the weak branch, singular support, products of distributions, any derivation of a matter tensor from vacuum Maxwell, radiation reaction, self-force and any empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.6.1 printed pp. 127-130 (local energy and momentum conservation and their combination) and §5.6.2-§5.6.3 printed pp. 130-133 (the energy-momentum tensor (5.49), its transformation law (5.50), $\\\\partial_\\\\mu T^{\\\\mu\\\\nu}=-F^{\\\\nu\\\\rho}J_\\\\rho$ and tracelessness, and the angular-momentum continuity requiring a symmetric stress tensor (5.51)), read in the full extracted text"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course, University of Texas at Austin (complete author-hosted lecture notes, document dated 2006-02-02)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node106.html",
                "locator": "node 128 'The electromagnetic energy tensor' (the tensor components $T^{44}$, $T^{i4}$, $T^{ij}$, the balance $f^\\\\nu=-\\\\partial_\\\\mu T^{\\\\mu\\\\nu}$, and the vector identities $\\\\partial U/\\\\partial t+\\\\nabla\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf u=-\\\\mathbf E\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf j$, $\\\\partial\\\\mathbf g/\\\\partial t+\\\\nabla\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf G=-\\\\rho\\\\mathbf E-\\\\mathbf j\\\\times\\\\mathbf B$), read in the complete author-hosted lecture notes; conventions converted to $x^0=ct$ and the framework's dictionary"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zürich 2024 FS (author-hosted 176-page lecture script)",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf",
                "locator": "§9.3 'Kovariante Elektrodynamik' pp. 9.11-9.12 (the conserved stress-energy tensor of the electromagnetic field and its divergence against the four-force density), read in the author-hosted script"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 19
        }
      ],
