          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform": "physical-result",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $E_0>0$ carry the unit $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and consider the constant fields of [[def-em-fields]] $$E:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3,\\quad E\\equiv E_0e_2,\\qquad B\\equiv0,$$ which form a source-free smooth solution of the vacuum Maxwell equations [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] (all first derivatives vanish and $\\operatorname{div}E=\\operatorname{div}B=0$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=\\operatorname{curl}B=0$). Let $0\\le\\beta<1$, $\\gamma:=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$ and $v:=\\beta c\\,e_1$, and let $x'=\\Lambda_\\beta x+b$ be the boosted inertial chart of [[pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform]]. Then the components of the same field in $x'$ are $$E'=\\gamma E_0\\,e_2,\\qquad B'=-\\frac{\\gamma\\beta E_0}{c}\\,e_3=-\\frac{\\gamma |v|E_0}{c^2}\\,e_3,$$ so the moving observer reads an electric field of magnitude $\\gamma E_0$ along $e_2$ and, for $\\beta>0$, a nonzero magnetic field along $-e_3$: a purely electric uniform field acquires a magnetic component in the boosted description. The two invariants check directly: with the spatial Euclidean structure of the chart ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]) $$|B'|^2-\\frac{|E'|^2}{c^2}=\\gamma^2\\Bigl(\\frac{\\beta^2E_0^2}{c^2}-\\frac{E_0^2}{c^2}\\Bigr)=-\\frac{E_0^2}{c^2}=|B|^2-\\frac{|E|^2}{c^2},\\qquad E'\\cdot B'=0=E\\cdot B,$$ using $\\gamma^2(1-\\beta^2)=1$ ([[def-rational-power]], [[lem-rational-power-laws]]). At $\\beta=0$ the formulas reduce to $E'=E$, $B'=0$. The computation is exact for the constant field; the example reports no measurement, identifies no source producing the field, and asserts nothing about finite-energy or bounded configurations.",
          "strategy": "**Verification:** substitute the data into clause (a) of [[pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform]] with $\\beta$ as given. Here $E_\\parallel=0$, $B_\\parallel=0$ since $E\\perp e_1$ and $B=0$, and $v\\times B=0$, while $v\\times E=(\\beta c e_1)\\times(E_0e_2)=\\beta cE_0e_3$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]); the boost formulas give $E'=\\gamma E_0e_2$ and $B'=-\\gamma |v|E_0e_3/c^2=-\\gamma\\beta E_0e_3/c$. The invariant values are computed with [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and the identity $\\gamma^2(1-\\beta^2)=1$ from [[lem-rational-power-laws]]; the constant field is a solution of the source-free smooth branch because every displayed first derivative vanishes. No measurement, empirical premise or choice principle is used.",
          "physical_scope": "An exact algebraic example for one uniform static electric field ($E\\equiv E_0e_2$, $B\\equiv0$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, $E_0>0$ in $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$) and one passive boost along $e_1$ with $0\\le\\beta<1$: the boosted components, the resulting mixed magnetic field and the two field invariants. Excluded: real sources of the uniform field, boundary or finite-support configurations, measurements and any empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.3.2 printed pp. 107-108 (the boosted-field formulas (5.17)-(5.18) and the remark that a magnetic field in one frame is an electric field in another), read in the full extracted text"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course, University of Texas at Austin (complete author-hosted lecture notes, document dated 2006-02-02)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node106.html",
