        {
          "id": "ex-em-constant-field-trajectory",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Exact trajectory in a uniform electric field and the failure of the unscoped Newtonian extrapolation",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X8",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-rational-power",
            "lem-rational-power-laws",
            "lem-of-square-monotone",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
            "thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
            "thm-of-square-roots",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
            "lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force": "physical-result",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rational-power": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rational-power-laws": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-of-square-monotone": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $q\\in\\mathbb R$ be a constant charge parameter, $m>0$ an externally supplied rest mass and $E_0>0$ a field strength in $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and consider the prescribed exterior fields $E,B:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of [[def-em-fields]] with $$E\\equiv E_0e_1,\\qquad B\\equiv0,$$ a source-free constant solution of the smooth vacuum Maxwell branch. Put $h:=qE_0/(mc)$, carrying the unit $\\mathrm s^{-1}$, and for $r_0\\in\\mathbb R^3$ define $r:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and its chart reading $t\\mapsto(ct,r(t))$ by $$r(t):=r_0+\\frac{c}{h}\\bigl(\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2}-1\\bigr)e_1\\quad(h\\ne0),\\qquad r(t):=r_0\\quad(h=0),$$ $$v(t):=r'(t)=\\frac{c\\,ht}{\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2}}\\,e_1\\ (h\\ne0),\\qquad \\gamma(t):=\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2},\\qquad p(t):=m\\gamma(t)v(t).$$ Then: (i) [Exact solution] for $h\\ne0$ the curve is a future timelike $C^\\infty$ curve with coordinate velocity $v(t)$, $|v(t)|<c$ for every finite $t$ and $|v(t)|\\to c$ as $t\\to\\pm\\infty$, and $p(t)=qE_0t\\,e_1$, so that $$\\frac{dp}{dt}=qE_0e_1=q(E+v\\times B),\\qquad \\frac{d(\\gamma mc^2)}{dt}=qE\\cdot v$$ hold identically, as required by [[pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force]] (a); at $h=0$ (equivalently $q=0$) the curve is the constant $r\\equiv r_0$ with $v\\equiv0$, $p\\equiv0$ and the same equations hold trivially. (ii) [Energy] $\\gamma(t)mc^2=mc^2\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2}$ and $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\gamma mc^2)=qE_0\\,v_1(t)$: the field delivers power $qE\\cdot v$ to the particle. (iii) [Unscoped Newtonian extrapolation] For $h\\ne0$, the Newtonian constant-acceleration prediction $v_N(t):=(qE_0/m)t\\,e_1$ satisfies $v(t)=v_N(t)/\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2}$, so the two agree only to first order in $|ht|$ and $|v_N(t)|=\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2}\\,|v(t)|$ exceeds $c$ for $|t|>1/|h|$, whereas the exact solution of the adopted coupling remains subluminal at every finite time; for this explicit solution the approximation is quantified directly. Put $r_N(t)=r_0+\\tfrac12hc t^2e_1$. For $h\\ne0$, $$|v-v_N|\\le\\frac c2|ht|^3,\\qquad |r-r_N|\\le\\frac{c}{8|h|}|ht|^4.$$ Thus on $|ht|\\le\\delta$ the velocity error is at most $c\\delta^3/2$ and the displacement error at most $c\\delta^4/(8|h|)$; these are special-model comparisons, not a general trajectory-stability consequence of [[pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force]] (c). When $h=0$ both trajectories and velocities coincide, and the Newtonian superluminal-time condition containing $1/|h|$ is not used. (iv) [Scope] the field is prescribed and external, the particle's own field and any radiation reaction or self-force are excluded, and no measurement or empirical claim is made; the solution is exact for the stated coupling with $E_0>0$ and arbitrary constant $q$.",
          "strategy": "**Verification:** direct differentiation and substitution. For $h\\ne0$ set $g(t):=\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2}$; by [[thm-of-square-roots]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]] and [[thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent]] the map $t\\mapsto(1+h^2t^2)^{1/2}$ is differentiable with $g'(t)=h^2t/g(t)$ ([[thm-chain-rule]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[lem-rational-power-laws]]), hence $r'(t)=(c/h)g'(t)e_1=chte_1/g(t)=v(t)$ and $|v(t)|/c=|ht|/g(t)<1$ because $h^2t^2<1+h^2t^2$ ([[lem-of-square-monotone]]), with $|v(t)|\\to c$ as $|t|\\to\\infty$. Then $p(t)=m\\gamma(t)v(t)=mc\\,ht\\,e_1=qE_0t\\,e_1$, so $dp/dt=qE_0e_1$, and $q(E+v\\times B)=qE_0e_1$ since $B=0$ and $v\\times0=0$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]]); the power identity is $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\gamma mc^2)=mc^2h^2t/g(t)=qE_0\\,v_1(t)$. The curve is future timelike: its tangent $(c,v(t))$ has $\\eta$-square $-c^2+|v(t)|^2<0$ with positive time component, in the sense of [[def-em-relativistic-worldlines-frames]] (a) and [[def-em-si-conventions]]; the vector-valued derivative is [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]. Part (iii) is the displayed identity $v=v_N/\\sqrt{1+h^2t^2}$ with $v_N=(qE_0/m)te_1=hct\\,e_1$, whose norm exceeds $c$ exactly when $|ht|>1$. For the direct error bounds let $s=ht$ and $g=\\sqrt{1+s^2}\\ge1$. Rationalization gives $g-1=s^2/(g+1)\\le s^2/2$ and $1-g^{-1}=(g-1)/g\\le s^2/2$. Since $v=v_N/g$ and $|v_N|=c|s|$, the velocity bound follows. Also $1+s^2/2-g=(g-1)^2/2\\le s^4/8$, so multiplying its absolute value by $c/|h|$ gives the displacement bound. This directly proves the special-model approximation on the declared interval without importing a general stability claim. For $h=0$, $q=0$, $r=r_N=r_0$ and $v=v_N=0$, and every force/power identity is zero; no formula divides by $h$ in that branch. No measurement, empirical premise or choice principle is used.",
          "physical_scope": "An exact conditional solution example for one prescribed uniform external electric field ($E\\equiv E_0e_1$, $B\\equiv0$, $E_0>0$ in $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$) acting on a particle of constant charge $q$ and external rest mass $m>0$ under the framework's adopted covariant coupling: the trajectory, its subluminality at all finite times, the work-energy identity, and the comparison with the unscoped Newtonian constant-acceleration extrapolation. Excluded: the particle's own field, radiation reaction and self-force, the production of the field, measurement, and any empirical claim.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "§5.4.2 printed pp. 116-118 (motion in constant fields: the relativistic trajectory in a uniform electric field and its limiting speed), read in the full extracted text"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course, University of Texas at Austin (complete author-hosted lecture notes, document dated 2006-02-02)",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node106.html",
                "locator": "node 126 'Relativistic particle dynamics' and node 127 'The force on a moving charge', read in the complete author-hosted lecture notes"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, Vorlesungsskript, ETH Zürich 2024 FS (author-hosted 176-page lecture script)",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf",
                "locator": "§9.4 'Geladene Teilchen' pp. 9.12-9.13 (the coordinate equations (9.70)-(9.71) used to verify the displayed solution), read in the author-hosted script"
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 19
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-classical-electromagnetism-track.md",
      "order": 354
    },
    {
      "id": "em-empirical-tests-and-scope",
      "title": "Empirical tests and limitations",
      "category": "classical-electromagnetism",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "em-empirical-tests-and-scope-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "em-experiment-analysis-prerequisites",
        "em-material-response",
        "em-retarded-fields-and-radiation",
        "em-vacuum-waves-and-polarization"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-em-experiment-record-contract",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Report-backed operational evidence with uncertainty",
          "reference": "definition-contracts.md#def-em-experiment-record-contract",
          "design_state": "explicit-definition-contract; supplier-validation-recorded-separately",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-lab-geometry",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-em-fields",
            "def-em-sources",
            "def-em-fixed-test-geometry",
            "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-fixed-test-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 13,
          "statement": "Adopt the laboratory region with its inertial chart and fixed-time reading of\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]], the electric and magnetic fields of [[def-em-fields]],\nthe charge parameter, frame charge and current densities and charge integrals\nof [[def-em-sources]], and the SI units and constants of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]]. This definition fixes the framework's report-record\nvocabulary; it selects no experiment, records no value, evaluates no indicator\nand asserts no measurement.\n\n(a) [Primary source] A *primary source* is a declared bibliographic\nidentification of a report (author, title, date, publication, and the\nidentification of the passages actually inspected) together with the declared\nlimits of the reading: which parts of the report were read, which were not, and\nany carrier defects (for instance corrupted optical-character-recognition text\nof formulae or tables).\n\n(b) [Comparison quantity and measurand] Let $d\\ge1$ be an integer. A\n*comparison quantity* of dimension $d$ is a declared map from a declared\ncondition set to $\\mathbb R^d$, with declared units, whose components are\nfinite expressions in specified framework quantities. These may include the\nvalues of $E,B,\\rho,J$ at declared events, the charge parameter $q$, and the\ndeclared charge or current integrals of [[def-em-sources]]; declared field\ncirculations and surface fluxes on the admitted fixed geometries of\n[[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]]; and declared finite-time or period averages of\nspecified derived field quantities, including $u$ and $S$ of\n[[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]. An integral or average is admitted only with its\ndomain, orientation where needed, integration convention and actual\nwell-definedness hypotheses declared. For example, a component $Q(t)$\ncontinuous on a declared compact interval $[a,b]$ inside the laboratory time\ndomain, with $a<b$, has the finite Riemann average\n$(b-a)^{-1}\\int_a^b Q(t)\\,dt$ by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]; a\nperiod average additionally declares the period and interval. Field fluxes\ninherit the regularity, compactness, boundary presentation and measure/choice\nconventions of their fixed-test supplier. A declared detector-response\nintegral may be used only when its declared model and integral suppliers\nestablish a finite real value. An extended nonnegative integral with value\n$+\\infty$ is not a component of this real-valued comparison map or a recorded\nfinite rate. No existence or finiteness of an arbitrary field integral,\ndistributional product or detector response is asserted. A *measurand* is a\ncomparison quantity together with a declared condition description under which\nit is to be read. All quantities carry the SI conventions of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n(c) [Apparatus and preparation description] An *apparatus-and-preparation\ndescription* is a declared finite record of the apparatus, the preparation,\nthe controls and the environment in the report's own terms, with the\nidentification of the passage of the primary source of (a) from which each\nitem of the description is taken. The definition asserts only that the\ndescription is declared and finite; it asserts nothing about its correctness\nor completeness.\n\n(d) [Recorded data and reported observations] For an integer $d\\ge1$, a\n*recorded datum* is a vector $z_k\\in\\mathbb R^{d}$ with declared units\ntogether with a *condition annotation* $a_k$, a declared finite text\nidentifying the declared run condition of the datum and, when the datum is\ntaken from a report, its locator in the primary source. A *reported\nobservation* is a declared finite text recording a qualitative observation of\nthe report. A *record* is a finite tuple of recorded data and reported\nobservations together with the declared condition annotations. The record\ncontains no probability space, random variable, distribution, sampling law,\nindependence assumption, likelihood or coverage statement.\n\n(e) [Calibration model] A *calibration model* is a tuple $(Z,c,\\delta)$ in\nwhich $Z$ is a declared nonempty set of *apparatus-output values*,\n$c:Z\\to\\mathbb R^d$ is a declared *calibration map* and\n$\\delta\\in[0,\\infty)$ is a declared *calibration-error bound*: it declares\nthat for every output $o\\in Z$ obtained under the declared conditions, the\nvalue of the comparison quantity of (b) lies in the closed box\n$\\prod_{j=1}^d[c_j(o)-\\delta,\\,c_j(o)+\\delta]$ in the declared units. A\ncalibration model is a declaration; the definition asserts no accuracy beyond\nthe bound $\\delta$ and no statistical property of $c$ or $\\delta$. A declared\n*missing* calibration is recorded as missing, and no value or bound is then\navailable from this clause.\n\n(f) [Uncertainty record] An *uncertainty record* is a finite tuple of declared\nbounds, each carrying a declared *source label* from the finite list\n$\\{calibration,\\ resolution,\\ systematic,\\ sampling-variability,\\ rounding,\\ other\\}$\nand a declared value in the declared units; each entry is a declared\nconstraint of the form of (e), and the record may state that a bound is\nunavailable. No entry is a standard-deviation convention, a coverage\nprobability, a confidence level, a $p$-value, a posterior or a probability\nlaw.\n\n(g) [Analysis assumptions] An *analysis-assumption list* is a finite tuple of\ndeclared assumptions used to obtain the recorded data or to compare them with\na model (binning, background model, selection rules, corrections, fitting or\nestimation procedure, independence or dependence of entries, and the\ntreatment of the sources of uncertainty); declared omissions are recorded as\nomissions and are not filled in by the definition.\n\n(h) [Interpretation] An *interpretation* is a declared statement that\nidentifies a specified model prediction and declares the result of comparing\nthe record with it (support, constraint, failure to distinguish, or no\nconclusion), conditional on (a)-(g). The interpretation is part of the report\naccount and not of the data; it asserts no probability, no significance level\nand no truth of any framework, and it records a comparison of a specified\nmodel, not of a whole theory.\n\n(i) [Well-definedness and scope] All components of (a)-(h) are finite tuples,\nreal numbers, declared maps on declared sets and finite text labels; forming\nthis finite vocabulary uses no choice principle. Evaluating a comparison\nquantity inherits every hypothesis and axiom of its actual suppliers,\nincluding countable choice when the selected fixed-test surface or\nLebesgue-measure interface requires it; no measurability, integrability,\ncompleteness or differentiability is inferred merely from the record. A\ncomparison quantity is evaluated only where the cited items supply its\ningredients and a finite value. Missing source detail stays missing: no\nuniversal sampling law, precision, independence, coverage or calibration\ncorrectness is part of the definition, and none may be inferred from it. The\ndefinition is the framework-local vocabulary for the reports of this page; it\ndoes not assert that any laboratory realises a measurand or that any reported\nobservation is correct.",
          "strategy": "An explicit finite record definition, not a field or apparatus theorem. Clauses (a)-(h) declare finite tuples, real vectors, maps, labels, conditions and interpretations; calibration boxes require only the declared $\\delta\\ge0$. The enlarged clause (b) names the observables actually used by consumers: fixed-time electric flux on the admitted geometries of [[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]], and componentwise finite-time or period averages of the derived quantities of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]. Their evaluation is conditional on the cited contracts. In particular the latter definition makes $u,S$ continuous in the smooth branch, so restricting to a compact time interval gives real continuous components and [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]] supplies finite Riemann integrals. It does not make weak-field products integrable automatically. The fixed-test geometry's compactness, orientation, regularity and countable-choice measure conventions remain explicit prerequisites of a selected flux evaluation. A further detector model must separately supply measurable integrands and finite values before its response can be a real comparison quantity; an extended infinite value is not a finite datum. The syntax of this vocabulary is choice-free, while selected evaluations carry their suppliers' axioms. Missing calibration, sampling or source information stays missing. No measurement, probability law, accuracy or correctness of an apparatus is deduced.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "JCGM 100:2008, Evaluation of measurement data - Guide to the expression of uncertainty in measurement (GUM 1995 with minor corrections), Joint Committee for Guides in Metrology, first edition September 2008, 134 PDF pages",
                "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_100_2008_E.pdf",
                "locator": "Clause 2 (2.1-2.3.6, printed pp. 2-4), Clause 3 (3.1-3.4, printed pp. 4-8) and Clause 4.1 (printed pp. 8-9), read in the complete 134-page PDF: metrological vocabulary, the definition of uncertainty of measurement and its notes (2.2.3), standard, Type A/Type B, combined and expanded uncertainty and coverage factor (2.3.1-2.3.6), the specification of the measurand and the incompleteness caveat (3.1.1-3.1.3), errors, effects and corrections (3.2), the enumerated sources of uncertainty (3.3.2) and the measurement model $Y=f(X_1,\\dots,X_N)$ (4.1.1); used for the record vocabulary only, with no numerical evaluation"
              },
              {
                "title": "Michael Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I, First Series (read 24 November 1831), 1849 collected second edition, Project Gutenberg transcription",
                "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm",
                "locator": "First Series paragraphs 1-33 (induction experiments); used as the report whose record fields (apparatus, preparation, reported observations, missing calibration and analysis detail) the definition formalises"
              },
              {
                "title": "Heinrich Hertz, Electric Waves, authorized Jones English translation (Macmillan 1893), Chapter VII (Berlin Academy 2 February 1888; Wiedemann's Annalen 34 p. 661), Internet Archive OCR",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/electricwavesbe00hertgoog/electricwavesbe00hertgoog_djvu.txt",
                "locator": "Chapter VII printed pp. 107-123; used as the report whose record fields the definition formalises"
              },
              {
                "title": "Henry Cavendish, The Electrical Researches (ed. J. Clerk Maxwell, Cambridge 1879), Internet Archive OCR",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/electricalresea00cavegoog/electricalresea00cavegoog_djvu.txt",
                "locator": "Arts. 217-235 (printed pp. 104-113) and arts. 244-249 (printed pp. 119-121); used as the report whose record fields the definition formalises"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Framework-local vocabulary for report-backed operational evidence in classical electromagnetism: comparison quantities built from the fields and sources of the framework at declared events or regions in the SI units of the laboratory frame; apparatus-and-preparation descriptions; finite recorded data or reported qualitative observations with condition annotations; calibration models with a declared error bound; uncertainty records with declared source labels; analysis-assumption lists; and qualified interpretations. The definition asserts no measurement, no apparatus behaviour, no sampling law, no precision, no independence, no coverage, no calibration correctness, no empirical support and no value of any physical constant; it selects no experiment and missing report detail stays missing."
        },
        {
          "id": "exp-em-induction-report",
          "kind": "experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Faraday's 1831 induction experiments: reported transients and the iron-ring apparatus",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#E1",
          "design_state": "source-backed-qualified-primary-qualitative-account; no-invented-quantitative-inference",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 14,
          "statement": "**Primary source and reading limits.** Michael Faraday, *Experimental Researches in\nElectricity*, Volume I, First Series (read 24 November 1831), 1849 collected\nsecond edition, read in the Project Gutenberg transcription derived from\nBnF/Gallica images; the First Series paragraphs 1-33 were read completely, and\nno claim is made to have read the whole volume. This is an original report\nreprint transmitted by a later transcription, not a newly repeated experiment.\nThe account below is the report's apparatus descriptions and reported\nobservations, structured by the record contract of\n[[def-em-experiment-record-contract]]; it is not a deduction, and 'no perceived\ndeflection' is everywhere the report's finite-resolution verbal observation.\n\n## Setup\n\nInsulated copper helices wound on a wooden cylinder, each containing about\ntwenty-seven feet of wire and kept from touching by interposed twine, twelve\nhelices superposed in the same direction with the first, third, fifth, seventh,\nninth and eleventh joined into one helix and the remainder into a second\n(paragraph 6). A pair of two-hundred-and-three-foot coils interleaved as a\nspiral on a wooden block with metallic contact prevented by twine (paragraph\n10). A small hollow helix wound on a glass tube into which a steel needle could\nbe inserted (paragraph 13). Insulated zigzag wire boards moved relatively to\none another (paragraph 18). A welded soft-iron ring, seven-eighths of an inch\nthick and six inches in external diameter, with three insulated inducing\nhelices A of about twenty-four feet each and a separate helix B made from\nabout sixty feet of wire in two pieces. The A helices were joined end to end\nand connected to a battery of ten pairs of plates; B was connected to a\ngalvanometer three feet from the ring (paragraphs 27-28). Paragraph 31 reports\nthe ring trial with one hundred pairs; paragraph 11 reports a separate\ncoil trial with one hundred and twenty pairs.\n\n## Procedure\n\nMaking and breaking the battery circuit of the inducing helix while the\ndetector loop is kept complete (paragraphs 10-11); removing the inserted needle\nbefore breaking the battery contact, and inserting it after make but before\nbreak (paragraphs 13-14);\ncompleting the detector circuit before the battery contact is made or broken\n(paragraph 17); moving the zigzag boards together and apart with a steady\nbattery current (paragraphs 18-19); reversing the direction of the battery\nconnection (paragraph 30); repeating with larger batteries and with charcoal\ncontacts (paragraphs 31-32).\n\n## Observations\n\nA slight deflection of the galvanometer needle at the moment of completing the\nconnection, always in one direction, with the opposite deflection at breaking,\nand no sustained perceived deflection under the continued steady battery\nconnection (paragraphs 11 and 28-31); magnetization of an inserted steel needle,\nwith opposite poles at make and at break and near-cancellation when both\ntransients act on the same inserted needle (paragraphs 13-17); opposite\ntransient deflections as the boards approach and recede, with return to the\nordinary position when the movement ceases (paragraphs 18-20); a minute spark\nat the secondary helix with charcoal contacts (paragraph 32); and the control\nthat, after the first effect, the galvanometer needle resumes its position\nunder the continued battery current (paragraphs 28 and 33). These are reported\nfinite-resolution qualitative observations.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nThe read passages identify deflection direction, needle-pole comparison,\nbattery reversal and background and timing controls, but supply no calibrated\nconversion from deflection to current, no detector response or transfer\nfunction, no trial-level dataset, no declared sampling law, no error bars and\nno formal significance test; dimensions, wire lengths and battery plate counts\nare historical approximate apparatus descriptions, not modern precision\nestimates. The sign interpretation of the induced current depends on\ngalvanometer and needle response conventions. Missing information is retained\nas missing; no error bars, precision or independence are invented.\n\n## Interpretation\n\nThe reported observations support a transient induction model for the described\napparatus under the stated apparatus assumptions and are qualitatively\nconsistent with conditional framework predictions of transient induction where\nthose are instantiated for the described circuits; they do not establish a\nuniversal exact induction law, do not isolate all auxiliary assumptions\n(magnetic inertia, switching transients, leakage, current history, geometry)\nand supply no quantitative model comparison. The empirical support is a\nnonlogical relation to the induction predictions, not a proof of any\npostulate, and the source limitations of the reading travel into any downstream\nconclusion.",
          "strategy": "Source-backed experiment exposition, not a deductive proof. Author the five public sections and the recorded observations from the actually read paragraphs 1-33 of the stamped Project Gutenberg transcription (https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm, read extent and source limitations preserved in research/first-principles-2026-10-03/classical-electromagnetism/scaffold/faraday-primary-reading.json): apparatus from paragraphs 6, 10, 13, 18 and 27-28; procedure from paragraphs 10-11, 13, 17, 18-19 and 30; reported observations from paragraphs 11, 13-17, 18-20 and 28-33; uncertainty and interpretation from the absence of calibration, sampling and significance detail in those paragraphs. Keep the record structure of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]] (source, apparatus/preparation, recorded data or reported observations, calibration model marked missing, uncertainty record, analysis assumptions, interpretation) and set `empirical_result` with the qualitative observation summary, the 'no calibration, dataset or sampling law reported' uncertainty statement, the stated apparatus conditions and the source URL. Do not transcribe a deflection-to-current model, a dataset or an uncertainty interval; the qualitative account remains qualitative. Leave provenance.proof not-applicable, with no Proof section and no proof-audit claim; independent source reviewers check faithfulness, calibration/sampling caveats and the interpretation against the stamped text.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Michael Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I, First Series (read 24 November 1831), 1849 collected second edition, Project Gutenberg transcription (URL stamped and hashed in the batch coverage file; sha256 61a33e232f34d99c2a526ccd5d1cac42c32190e90083b10b7dba51838be6f7f0)",
                "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm",
                "locator": "First Series, paragraphs 1-33 completely (apparatus paragraphs 6, 10, 13, 18, 27-28; procedure and observations paragraphs 10-11, 13, 17, 18-20, 28-33); no claim to have read the whole volume"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (author-hosted, 236 pp.); modern account of Faraday's law and the moving-wire emf used only as the context of the conditional induction predictions named in the interpretation",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 4.1 'Faraday's Law of Induction' printed pp. 67-69 (differential law (4.1), Stokes reduction and the circuit emf) and Section 4.1.1 'Faraday's Law for Moving Wires' printed pp. 69-71, read in the stamped complete 236-page author-hosted text"
              }
            ]
          },
          "empirical_result": {
            "observation": "Reported make/break transient galvanometer deflections in opposite directions with no sustained perceived deflection under continued battery current; needle magnetization with opposite poles at make and break; opposite approach/recede deflections returning to the ordinary position when motion ceases; stronger iron-ring transients with battery-direction reversal (paragraphs 11, 13-17, 18-20, 28-33); qualitative finite-resolution observations only.",
            "uncertainty": "No calibrated deflection-to-current conversion, detector response function, trial-level dataset, declared sampling law, error bars or significance test in the read paragraphs; apparatus dimensions are historical approximations; 'none perceived' is not an exact zero. No precision is inferred.",
            "conditions": "Insulated copper helices and interleaved coil pairs (including the one-hundred-and-twenty-pair battery trial of paragraph 11), hollow helix with steel needle, zigzag wire boards, and a welded soft-iron ring with three inducing A helices and a separate B helix connected to the galvanometer, driven with ten and one hundred pairs of plates (paragraphs 27-31), with make/break, battery-reversal, needle-insertion-order and detector-circuit-timing controls, as described in the First Series.",
            "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Primary-report account of Faraday's 1831 induction experiments for the described apparatus: transient deflections at make and break of the battery circuit, needle magnetization and reversal controls, relative-motion transients, and the soft-iron-ring apparatus. Qualitative observations with finite resolution; no calibrated current, no sampling law, no precision, no single quantitative model comparison, and no proof or refutation of any induction law or postulate is claimed."
        },
        {
          "id": "exp-em-wave-propagation-report",
          "kind": "experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Hertz's 1888 finite-velocity tests: sparks, wire nodes and qualitative interference",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#E2",
          "design_state": "source-backed-qualified-primary-qualitative-account; no-invented-quantitative-inference",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 14,
          "statement": "**Primary source and reading limits.** Heinrich Hertz, 'On the finite velocity\nof propagation of electromagnetic actions' (Berlin Academy, 2 February 1888;\n*Wiedemann's Annalen* 34 p. 661), in the authorized English *Electric Waves*\ntranslation (D. E. Jones, Macmillan 1893), Chapter VII, printed pp. 107-123; the\ncomplete chapter narrative was read in the Internet Archive OCR of\n`electricwavesbe00hertgoog`, whose tables are visibly scrambled and have not\nbeen reconstructed or recomputed, and the rest of the volume was not read. This\nis a primary-report translation with an OCR limitation, not a modern\nreplication or a measured-$c$ precision source. The account below is the\nreport's apparatus descriptions, procedures and reported observation\nindicators, structured by the record contract of\n[[def-em-experiment-record-contract]].\n\n## Setup\n\nA spark-excited primary conductor with brass end plates and a central spark gap\ndrove rapid oscillations; circular or square secondary conductors with\nmicrometer-adjusted spark gaps served as qualitative detectors; the comparison\nused waves on a long wire versus direct action through air, with tuned\nsecondary orientations and adjustable additional wire length; the room was\ndarkened, and reflections and nearby fixtures were apparatus concerns (chapter\nVII narrative, printed pp. 107-123).\n\n## Procedure\n\nLocating nodal points of the wire standing wave by moving the tuned secondary\nalong the wire and marking their positions with paper riders; comparing spark\nstrength and suppression at different positions along a defined baseline;\nlengthening the added wire from 100 cm to 600 cm in 50 cm increments and\nrepeating the comparison (printed pp. 117-118); changing the distance between\nthe primary and secondary circuits;\nand comparing the direct air-path action with the wire-wave action (chapter VII\nnarrative).\n\n## Observations\n\nOrientation-dependent spark responses of the secondary detector; approximately\nrecurring and roughly equally spaced nodal points along the wire; and changing\nsigns of the interference indicator as the path length or the distance is\nvaried. These are the reported observed indicators; the finite phase delay and\nthe propagation speed of the order of light speed are the author's\nmodel-dependent inferences, requiring his assumed oscillator, detector,\nwire-wave and air-path phase interpretation.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nThe report expressly treats its inferred absolute period from circuit theory as\ndoubtful beyond order of magnitude; it supplies no modern calibrated\nspark-intensity transfer function, no independent absolute period calibration,\nno trial-level likelihood or sampling model, no confidence interval and no\nsynchronized-clock travel-time measurement; the numerical tabular signs cannot\nbe faithfully recovered from the OCR used, and no quantitative fit, exact speed\nor uncertainty interval is claimed here. The author's rough order-of-magnitude\nremarks are not confidence intervals.\n\n## Interpretation\n\nThe described apparatus observations qualitatively support finite-propagation\nand wave-interference models under the stated auxiliary assumptions; they do\nnot deductively prove the Maxwell equations, do not exactly establish the value\nof $c$, and do not eliminate every alternative, since the comparison involves\nthe assumed oscillator, detector and wire-wave models. The historical\ndecomposition into separately propagating electrostatic and electromagnetic\ncomponents is not promoted to a modern Lorentz-covariant theorem. The source\nlimitations travel into any downstream historical or empirical conclusion, and\nthe support is a nonlogical relation to the wave predictions.",
          "strategy": "Source-backed experiment exposition, not a deductive proof. Author the five public sections from the actually read chapter VII narrative in the stamped Internet Archive OCR (https://archive.org/download/electricwavesbe00hertgoog/electricwavesbe00hertgoog_djvu.txt; read extent, OCR limitation and source limitations preserved in research/first-principles-2026-10-03/classical-electromagnetism/scaffold/hertz-primary-reading.json): apparatus and oscillator/detector description, the node-marking and wire-length comparison procedure, the orientation- and distance-dependent indicator observations, and the explicit separation of reported indicators from the author's model-dependent inferences. Keep the record structure of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]] and set `empirical_result` with the qualitative indicator summary, the 'no calibrated transfer function, absolute period calibration, sampling law or confidence interval' uncertainty statement, the stated wire/air comparison conditions and the source URL. Do not reconstruct the scrambled numerical tables, do not invent counts or precision, and do not present the order-of-magnitude speed estimate as a measurement of $c$. Leave provenance.proof not-applicable with no Proof section; independent source reviewers check the reported-observation extent against the stamped text.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Heinrich Hertz, Electric Waves, authorized Jones English translation (Macmillan 1893), Chapter VII, Internet Archive OCR of electricwavesbe00hertgoog (URL stamped and hashed in the batch coverage file; sha256 e64ca355422b781ab29a54939bd1ca7bbd7e70062ed540fef250cbae9098be31)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/electricwavesbe00hertgoog/electricwavesbe00hertgoog_djvu.txt",
                "locator": "Chapter VII 'On the finite velocity of propagation of electromagnetic actions', printed pp. 107-123, complete narrative; tables scrambled in the OCR and not reconstructed; rest of the volume not read"
              }
            ]
          },
          "empirical_result": {
            "observation": "Reported orientation-dependent spark responses of a tuned secondary detector, approximately recurring and roughly equally spaced nodal points along a long wire marked with paper riders, and changing signs of the interference indicator as wire length or distance is varied (chapter VII narrative, printed pp. 107-123).",
            "uncertainty": "No calibrated spark-intensity transfer function, no independent absolute-period calibration, no trial-level likelihood or sampling model and no confidence interval; the author treats the circuit-theory period as doubtful beyond order of magnitude; the numerical tables are scrambled in the OCR used and were not reconstructed. The roughly light-speed value is a model-dependent inference, not a synchronized-clock measurement.",
            "conditions": "Spark-excited primary conductor with brass end plates and central spark gap; circular/square secondary conductors with micrometer-adjusted spark gaps; comparison of direct air-path action with waves on a long wire, with adjustable wire length and tuned secondary orientation; darkened room with reflections present.",
            "source_url": "https://archive.org/download/electricwavesbe00hertgoog/electricwavesbe00hertgoog_djvu.txt"
