        {
          "id": "exp-em-inverse-square-report",
          "kind": "experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Cavendish's enclosed-globe inverse-square nulls with a sensitivity control",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#E3",
          "design_state": "source-backed-qualified-primary-qualitative-account; no-invented-quantitative-inference",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract": "operational-prerequisite"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 14,
          "statement": "**Primary source and reading limits.** Henry Cavendish's original manuscripts as\nedited by J. Clerk Maxwell, *The Electrical Researches of the Honourable Henry\nCavendish* (Cambridge 1879), 'Experimental determination of the law of electric\nforce', arts. 217-235, printed pp. 104-113, with the detector and electrometer\ndescriptions arts. 244-249, printed pp. 119-121; read in the Internet Archive\nOCR of `electricalresea00cavegoog`. The original apparatus, procedure, reported\nobservations and sensitivity check are identifiable, but the fractional\ncalibration ratios and the exponent computation are corrupted by the OCR and no\nnumerical exponent limit is transcribed or recomputed here. The account below\nis the report's descriptions and observations structured by the record contract\nof [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]]; it is a reported finite-resolution\nnull, not a proof that a true charge is exactly zero.\n\n## Setup\n\nA conducting inner globe 12.1 inches in diameter, supported by a glass stick\ncoated with sealing wax, enclosed between two tinfoil-coated pasteboard\nhemispheres 13.3 inches in diameter with a small clearance, the two hemispheres\napplied to each other to form a complete sphere; a removable wire connected the\ninner globe and the hemispheres, and could be drawn out by a silk string;\nlinked strings operated the apparatus so that the charging wire, the inner\nconnecting wire and the hemispheres were successively separated (paragraphs\n219-222). A coated glass jar and an electrometer monitored and repeated the\ncharging level through a separate electrometer fastened to the prime conductor\n(paragraphs 224-225). The inner-globe detector was a pair of pith balls\nabout one-fifth of an inch in diameter on fine linen threads (paragraphs\n220-222 and 244). The paper-cylinder electrometer of paragraph 248 monitored\ncharging; the later straw electrometer with cork balls, suspended from the\nprime conductor, served that monitoring role in the subsequent charge-comparison\nexperiments (paragraph 249), rather than replacing the inner-globe detector in\nthe reported null tests. A second experiment enclosed\na wooden piece between two wooden drawers forming a hollow box (paragraph 235).\n\n## Procedure\n\nThe hemispheres were charged from a Leyden vial through a wire, which was then\nwithdrawn; immediately afterwards the wire connecting the inner globe and the\nhemispheres was drawn out by the silk string, the hemispheres were separated\nwithout touching the inner globe, and the outer shells and charging wire were\ndischarged; a pith-ball detector was then brought to the inner globe\n(paragraphs 220-222 and 226). For greater sensitivity the pith balls were\nprecharged positively before the charge, the comparison being made against the\nnegative-precharge case at monitored equal charging levels (paragraphs\n228-229). For the sensitivity control the hemispheres and their frames were\nremoved, leaving the globe and pith balls. A smaller coated glass element was\ncharged to the monitored level and its charge transferred to the initially\nuncharged main jar; that jar then charged the exposed globe. After withdrawal\nof the charging wire the precharged pith balls were compared (paragraph 230).\nThe report's fractional charge comparisons are unusable in this OCR; no\ncalibrated charge value is assigned here.\n\n## Observations\n\nSeveral repetitions without perceived pith-ball separation or signs of residual\ninner charge (paragraph 227); in the precharged-ball comparison, equal small\nresidual separations for positive and negative precharge rather than a\ndetectable charge-sign asymmetry (paragraphs 228-229); in the sensitivity\ncontrol, the small deliberately imposed charge was visibly distinguished by\nthe same detector (paragraph 230); and a corresponding qualitative null with\nthe enclosed-box geometry (paragraph 235). The reported 'must be inversely as\nthe square' inference (paragraph 232) is recorded as the author's conclusion\nfrom the null under his theory, not as a modern theorem.\n\n## Uncertainty\n\nThe read passages supply charge-level monitoring, the precharge comparison, a\ndeliberate sensitivity check and the pith-ball and electrometer construction,\nbut no calibrated charge transfer function, no trial-level sampling model, no\nconfidence interval and no modern SI traceability. Insulation leakage, finite\nseparation time, imperfect spherical geometry, shell holes, accidental contact\nand outside charges are apparatus-model and systematic conditions. The OCR\ncannot faithfully recover all fractional calibration ratios or the exponent\ncalculation, so no numerical exponent bound, precision, likelihood or certainty\nis invented.\n\n## Interpretation\n\nAn ideal inverse-square enclosed-conductor equilibrium model predicts zero\ncharge in the connected enclosed interior under its idealization, and the\nreported observations support that model within the apparatus sensitivity and\nauxiliary assumptions; they constrain specified alternative force laws only\nafter an explicit response and sensitivity model, which the read passages do\nnot supply in numerically usable form. A finite null cannot prove an exact\nexponent, and the empirical support is a nonlogical relation to the static\nprediction.",
          "strategy": "Source-backed experiment exposition, not a deductive proof. Author the five public sections and `empirical_result` from the actually read arts. 217-235 and 244-249 of the stamped Internet Archive OCR (https://archive.org/download/electricalresea00cavegoog/electricalresea00cavegoog_djvu.txt; read extent and OCR limitation preserved in research/first-principles-2026-10-03/classical-electromagnetism/scaffold/cavendish-primary-reading.json): apparatus from arts. 219-222 and 244-249; procedure from arts. 220-226, 228-230; observations from arts. 227-235; uncertainty from the absent calibration ratios and sampling model, retaining the OCR corruption explicitly; interpretation restricted to the qualitative null and the sensitivity control. Keep the record structure of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]] and set `empirical_result` with the qualitative null/sensitivity summary, the 'no calibrated transfer function, trial sampling law or numerical exponent bound' uncertainty statement, the stated apparatus conditions and the source URL. Do not transcribe the corrupted fractional ratios, do not invent an exponent limit, and do not promote art. 232's 'must' to an unconditional theorem. Leave provenance.proof not-applicable with no Proof section; independent source reviewers check the null and sensitivity-control reading against the stamped text.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Henry Cavendish, The Electrical Researches of the Honourable Henry Cavendish (ed. J. Clerk Maxwell, Cambridge 1879), Internet Archive OCR of electricalresea00cavegoog (URL stamped and hashed in the batch coverage file; sha256 8c54fe399606a71186f8c0717e3bdd00ec614ccd2bb040ebbb3b2cf787135133)",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/electricalresea00cavegoog/electricalresea00cavegoog_djvu.txt",
                "locator": "'Experimental determination of the law of electric force', arts. 217-235, printed pp. 104-113 (apparatus arts. 219-222, procedure arts. 220-230, observations arts. 227-235), and detector/electrometer descriptions arts. 244-249, printed pp. 119-121; fractional computations corrupted by the OCR and not recomputed"
              }
            ]
          },
          "empirical_result": {
            "observation": "Several repetitions without perceived pith-ball separation or signs of residual inner charge on the enclosed globe (art. 227); equal small residual separations under positive and negative detector precharge rather than a sign asymmetry (arts. 228-229); a deliberately imposed small charge visibly distinguished by the same detector (art. 230); and a qualitative null with an enclosed-box geometry (art. 235).",
            "uncertainty": "No calibrated charge transfer function, trial-level sampling model, confidence interval or numerical exponent bound in the read passages; the fractional calibration ratios and exponent computation are corrupted in the OCR and were not transcribed or recomputed; insulation leakage, finite separation time, imperfect geometry, shell holes, accidental contact and outside charges are apparatus-model conditions.",
            "conditions": "Conducting inner globe enclosed between two tinfoil-coated pasteboard hemispheres with a removable inner-outer connecting wire and string-operated separation; charging from a Leyden vial with a separate prime-conductor electrometer monitoring the charging level; inner-globe pith-ball detector (about 1/5 inch balls); a second enclosed-box geometry. The later straw electrometer of art. 249 is a prime-conductor charging monitor in the subsequent charge-comparison experiments.",
            "source_url": "https://archive.org/download/electricalresea00cavegoog/electricalresea00cavegoog_djvu.txt"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Primary-report account of Cavendish's enclosed-globe null tests of the inverse-square law: reported absence of detectable charge in the connected enclosed interior, the precharge sign comparison, a deliberate small-charge sensitivity control and the box-geometry null. Qualitative finite-resolution nulls under the described apparatus; no numerical exponent limit, no calibrated sensitivity function, no sampling law and no proof of an exact exponent or of any force law is claimed."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-em-specified-model-comparison",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Conditional predictions and qualified experimental comparison",
          "reference": "completed-expansion-arguments.md#U2",
          "design_state": "complete-bounded-error-conditional-model-comparison; not-numerically-applied",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-integral-maxwell",
            "pthm-em-wave-energy-average",
            "def-em-fixed-test-geometry",
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract",
            "thm-em-uncertainty-analysis",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-em-integral-maxwell": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-wave-energy-average": "physical-result",
            "def-em-fixed-test-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-em-uncertainty-analysis": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 20,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$,\n[[def-countable-choice]]) exactly as inherited from clause (a) of\n[[pthm-em-integral-maxwell]], which is used in clause (a)(i) below with its\npiecewise $C^1$ boundary and surface-measure conventions; no other choice\nprinciple is used. Let the laboratory region, its inertial chart, the fields\n$E,B$ and the source fields $\\rho,J$ be those named in\n[[pthm-em-integral-maxwell]], with its constants and units, and let $R$ be a\nrecord of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]] of dimension $d=1$ (primary\nsource, apparatus-and-preparation description, recorded data or reported\nobservations, calibration model, uncertainty record, analysis-assumption list,\ninterpretation). Write $I_t$ for the laboratory time interval. Let\n$I_\\theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open parameter interval,\n$\\theta_0\\in I_\\theta$, and $f:I_\\theta\\to\\mathbb R$ a declared comparison map\nof class $C^1$ such that $f(\\theta_0)=0$ and $f'(\\theta)\\ge m>0$ for every\n$\\theta\\in I_\\theta$. Suppose the record's declared apparatus-and-calibration\ndictionary identifies the comparison quantity with the deviation $y=f(\\theta)$\nof a declared model family from the exact framework reference prediction $y_0$\nof clause (a), and declares the recorded deviation $\\hat y$ and the bounds\n$|\\hat y|\\le s$ and $|y-\\hat y|\\le\\delta$ with $s,\\delta\\ge0$.\n\n(a) [Exact framework reference predictions] (i) [Static branch] Assume the\nsmooth branch of the adopted vacuum Maxwell equations with the regularity\nhypotheses of [[pthm-em-integral-maxwell]] (fields jointly $C^1$ on\n$\\Omega\\times I_t$ with the additional derivatives appearing in the displayed\nequations existing and continuous there; for instance $E,B$ jointly of class\n$C^2$ and $\\rho,J$ of class $C^1$). Let $(V,\\nu_V)$ be a volume test geometry\nof [[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]] and let $t\\in I_t$ be such that\n$\\rho(\\cdot,t)$ vanishes identically on $V$. Then clause (a) of\n[[pthm-em-integral-maxwell]] gives\n$$\\oint_{\\partial V}E(\\cdot,t)\\cdot\\nu_V\\,dS=\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\int_V\\rho(\\cdot,t)\\,d\\lambda_3=0.$$\nOn the smooth branch every admitted boundary $\\partial V$ whose enclosed volume\n$V$ is charge-free has vanishing electric flux; charge-freeness of the surface\nalone is insufficient, and the exact framework prediction for the\nenclosed-flux observable is the value $y_0=0$.\n\n(ii) [Wave branch] Let $(E,B)$ be the plane-wave solution of\n[[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]] with data $(k,\\omega,e)$ there, and let\n$\\langle S\\rangle$ be the period average of its Poynting vector. Then that item\ngives the signed identity\n$$\\langle S\\rangle\\cdot n=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0c}{2}|e|^2\\,(\\hat k\\cdot n)$$ for\nevery unit vector $n$; if the declared dictionary fixes the comparison\nquantity as the magnitude $|\\langle S\\rangle\\cdot n|$ of the normal\nperiod-averaged flux (a declared intensity convention), then the exact\nframework prediction is\n$$y_0=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0c}{2}|e|^2\\,|\\hat k\\cdot n|.$$ The absolute value is\npart of that declared magnitude convention and is not a consequence of the\nsigned identity.\n\n(b) [Conditional confinement] Under the stated identifications, every\nparameter value $\\theta\\in I_\\theta$ compatible with the declared model\nfamily, the apparatus-and-calibration dictionary and the two declared bounds\nsatisfies\n$$|\\theta-\\theta_0|\\le\\frac{s+\\delta}{m}.$$ Equivalently, a proposed $\\theta$\nviolating this bound is incompatible with the conjunction (specified family,\ndictionary, calibration bound, recorded bound); the conclusion is a\ndeterministic statement about the declared bounds.\n\n(c) [Status and qualifications] The conclusion is conditional on the declared\nfamily, dictionary, calibration and recorded bounds and on the declared\nmonotonicity margin $m$; it is not a sampling law, a likelihood, a confidence\nlevel, a coverage probability, a $p$-value or a probability that any model or\nframework is true, and it asserts no rejection error rate. Agreement of a\nrecord with a specified family, like disagreement with it, concerns the\nconjunction of that family with the apparatus, calibration and analysis\nassumptions; a discrepancy may lie in the family, the dictionary, the\ncalibration or the analysis, and neither agreement nor disagreement proves or\ndeductively falsifies the adopted framework. The three primary reports\nrecorded on this page supply, in their recorded read extents, no calibrated\nresponse transfer function, no trial-level dataset and no declared sampling\nlaw; accordingly this item asserts no numerical bound and performs no\nnumerical application for any of them, and their own uncertainty records carry\ntheir source limitations. Empirical support and its constraints are nonlogical\nrelations, not dependencies, and any consumer of this item inherits $s$,\n$\\delta$, $m$, the declared family, the record's conditions and the source\nlimitations.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** instantiate the cited framework predictions, then apply the cited bounded-error analysis to the declared record dictionary. (a)(i): fix $(V,\\nu_V)$ and $t$ as in the statement; the smooth branch of [[pthm-em-integral-maxwell]] applies under its stated regularity hypotheses, and its clause (a) gives the flux identity; the integral of the identically vanishing function $\\rho(\\cdot,t)$ on $V$ is zero, so the flux is zero. Only the first Maxwell equation and the test-geometry vocabulary of [[def-em-fixed-test-geometry]] are used; no boundary, decay or symmetry hypothesis is added. (a)(ii): read the exact averaged-flux identity from clause (b) of [[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]] at the unit normal $n$; the absolute value records only that an intensity reading is nonnegative. (b): set $J_\\theta:=I_\\theta-\\theta_0=\\{r-\\theta_0:r\\in I_\\theta\\}$, an open displacement interval containing $0$. Apply [[thm-em-uncertainty-analysis]] with $n=1$, $U=I_\\theta$, base point $\\theta_0$, unit direction $u=1$, $F=f$ and the supplier's scalar displacement interval $J_\\theta$. Its path is $h\\mapsto\\theta_0+h\\in U$ for every $h\\in J_\\theta$, and $D_1F(\\theta_0+h)=f'(\\theta_0+h)\\ge m$. For a candidate $\\theta\\in I_\\theta$ take $h=\\theta-\\theta_0\\in J_\\theta$; the declared deviation is exactly $y=F(\\theta_0+h)=f(\\theta)$, so the supplier gives $|h|\\le(s+\\delta)/m$, the stated bound. Laboratory time $I_t$ is independent of this parameter and displacement interval. The record's declared bounds $|\\hat y|\\le s$ and calibration bound $|y-\\hat y|\\le\\delta$ are the mathematical hypotheses of that theorem; the identification of the record's quantities with the theorem's $y,\\hat y,s,\\delta$ is part of the declared dictionary and is not deducible from the record structure alone. (c): scope statement recording the conditional status and the absence of any numerically adequate premises in the three reports. No existence, sampling, likelihood or probability claim is made; $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used only in clause (a)(i) through [[pthm-em-integral-maxwell]].",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "JCGM 100:2008, Evaluation of measurement data - Guide to the expression of uncertainty in measurement (GUM 1995 with minor corrections), Joint Committee for Guides in Metrology, first edition September 2008, 134 PDF pages",
                "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_100_2008_E.pdf",
                "locator": "Clause 2.3.1-2.3.6 (standard, Type A/Type B, combined and expanded uncertainty, coverage factor, printed pp. 3-4), clause 3.3.3-3.3.7 (evaluation categories, standard and expanded uncertainty, printed pp. 6-7) and clause 4.1.1 (the measurement model $Y=f(X_1,\\dots,X_N)$, printed pp. 8-9), read in the complete PDF; used as the source of the measurement-model and uncertainty vocabulary that clause (c) keeps separate from the declared bounds"
