            "post-em-material-closure",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling",
            "pthm-em-classical-interference",
            "def-em-fields",
            "pthm-em-point-self-energy-divergence"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "rem-em-radiation-reaction-limits": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-em-material-closure": "physical-assumption",
            "post-em-lorentz-coupling": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-em-classical-interference": "physical-result",
            "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-point-self-energy-divergence": "physical-result"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 24,
          "statement": "Every cited conditional result retains its stated hypotheses. In particular, the point-source and causal-wave comparisons below assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]) through their source suppliers; this qualification supplies no additional field or self-interaction model.\n\nRecord the established scope limits of the classical electromagnetic results\nassembled in this framework, with no speculative content. Each clause names\nwhat is and is not supplied; the remark asserts no theorem and adds no result.\n\n(a) [Continuum and regularity regime] The fields of the framework are the maps\nof [[def-em-fields]] on the laboratory region, with the regularity class\ncarried by each statement; the solved branches are the smooth and stated weak\nbranches of the adopted equations, and every conclusion holds only under its\nown stated regularity, domain and boundary hypotheses. No discrete, atomic or\nfinite-resolution structure of matter or charge is assumed, and none is\nderived.\n\n(b) [Material-model regime] The material results rest on the declared\nconstitutive branches of [[post-em-material-closure]] (the elementary\ninertial-rest linear nondispersive Ohmic branch and the causal memory branch\nwith its declared kernel or transfer-function hypotheses); arbitrary\nnonlinear, nonlocal, hysteretic, or beyond-branch anisotropic and strongly\ndispersive response is not established by this framework, and each declared\nbranch carries its own regime of validity, including the finite-band\nqualification of the constant-coefficient branch.\n\n(c) [Singular sources and self-interaction]\nFor a nonzero point charge $q\\ne0$,\n[[pthm-em-point-self-energy-divergence]](a)-(b) gives divergence of the\nself-field energy as the inner cutoff is removed; for $q=0$ its displayed\ntruncated energy is identically zero and this divergence does not occur.\n[[rem-em-radiation-reaction-limits]] records that products of a singular\nself-field with the charge's own distributional current are not defined by\nthe adopted source model, and that the emitted-power results concern\nprescribed sources and determine no back-reaction. No self-force dynamics, runaway\nanalysis, finite-size regularization or electromagnetic derivation of rest\nmass is supplied by this framework.\n\n(d) [External-force and mechanical coupling] Particle motion enters only\nthrough the adopted Lorentz coupling [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] with a\nprescribed external field; the framework determines the motion of a specified\ntest charge in a specified field and supplies no material law, no self-field\ndynamics and no derivation of the particle parameters. Any coupling to matter\nbeyond the declared branches is an additional model assumption.\n\n(e) [Quantum and ontological exclusions] The framework adopts no quantisation\nof charge, no spin or magnetic-moment degree of freedom, no measurement\npostulates and no photon model. Its interference results, such as the coherent\ncross term of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]], are deterministic statements\nabout classical wave fields; classical electromagnetic waves interfere, so an\ninterference pattern alone neither establishes a quantum description nor\nfalsifies classical electromagnetism, and the framework's field predictions\ndo not single out an ontology of the underlying field or of a particle.\n\n(f) [Empirical status] The empirical content of this page is report-backed and\nqualitative: the records of the three primary reports, in their stated read\nextents, carry no calibrated response functions, trial-level datasets or\ndeclared sampling laws, so they support and constrain the specified models\nonly within their declared apparatus, calibration and analysis assumptions;\nagreement does not prove the framework and disagreement does not deductively\nfalsify it. No measurement, precision, probability or confidence level is\ninvented or inferred by this remark.\n\n(g) [Status] This is a scope remark, not an established theorem; it records the\nlimits of the results assembled here and asserts no new result, no measurement\nand no proof.",
          "strategy": "A scope remark recording limits, not a proof. Each clause cites the item that carries the result whose scope is delimited: (a) the field definition and the regularity hypotheses of the statements; (b) the material-closure postulate and its declared branches; (c) the radiation-reaction limits remark; (d) the Lorentz-coupling postulate; (e) the deterministic interference theorem together with the framework's absence of quantum postulates; (f) the report accounts of this page. The remark asserts nothing beyond the cited statements: no self-force, no material law beyond the declared branches, no quantum claim, no empirical certainty. The clause (c) content is the recorded scope of [[rem-em-radiation-reaction-limits]] (the supplier's role in this manifest is formulation-prerequisite rather than the design's physical-result label, since a remark is not one of the established-result kinds; the dependency is on its recorded scope content). No choice principle is used. The self-energy use is the stationary Coulomb branch with q!=0 for divergence and zero truncated energy for q=0; producer radiation scope clause (b) remains escalated pending its owner correction.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Michael Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I, First Series (read 24 November 1831), 1849 collected second edition, Project Gutenberg transcription",
                "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm",
                "locator": "First Series paragraphs 1-33; the qualitative-reported account cited in clause (f) for the empirical-limitations record"
              },
              {
                "title": "Heinrich Hertz, Electric Waves, authorized Jones English translation (Macmillan 1893), Chapter VII, Internet Archive OCR",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/electricwavesbe00hertgoog/electricwavesbe00hertgoog_djvu.txt",
                "locator": "Chapter VII printed pp. 107-123; the qualitative-reported account cited in clause (f), including the author's order-of-magnitude qualifications"
              },
              {
                "title": "Henry Cavendish, The Electrical Researches (ed. J. Clerk Maxwell, Cambridge 1879), Internet Archive OCR",
                "url": "https://archive.org/download/electricalresea00cavegoog/electricalresea00cavegoog_djvu.txt",
                "locator": "Arts. 217-235 (printed pp. 104-113); the qualitative null account cited in clause (f), including the corrupted calibration fractions"
              },
              {
                "title": "JCGM 100:2008, Evaluation of measurement data - Guide to the expression of uncertainty in measurement (GUM 1995 with minor corrections), 134 PDF pages",
                "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_100_2008_E.pdf",
                "locator": "Clauses 2.2-2.3 and 3.3 (printed pp. 2-7), read in the complete PDF; the source of the distinction between declared uncertainty components and coverage or confidence statements used in clause (f)"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Scope statement for classical electromagnetism as assembled in this framework: continuum and regularity regime; the declared material-closure branches and their excluded nonlinear, nonlocal, hysteretic and beyond-branch responses; the point-source self-interaction exclusions; the external-force Lorentz coupling; the absence of quantum charge, spin, measurement and photon postulates, with the classical-wave interference statement; and the qualitative, report-backed status of the empirical content. The remark asserts no theorem, no measurement, no probability and no proof, and it carries the qualifications of every item it cites."
        }
      ],
