      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-em-apparatus-model-ambiguity",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A discrepancy can test auxiliaries as well as field assumptions",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X8",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract",
            "thm-reals-ordered-field"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-em-experiment-record-contract": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-reals-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 14,
          "statement": "Let the record vocabulary be that of [[def-em-experiment-record-contract]], and\nadopt a declared affine response model in which the model prediction for a\nrecorded comparison quantity is $$y(s,b):=G\\,s+b,$$ where $G>0$ is a declared\nresponse coefficient (with declared units [recorded]/[source]), $s\\in\\mathbb R$\na declared source-strength parameter and $b\\in\\mathbb R$ a declared background\nparameter in the units of the recorded quantity; the model is part of the\nrecord's analysis assumptions. Then:\n\n(i) [Exact ambiguity] For every $\\delta\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$y(s+\\delta,\\,b-G\\delta)=G(s+\\delta)+(b-G\\delta)=Gs+b=y(s,b),$$ by the field\narithmetic of $\\mathbb R$ ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]] makes $\\mathbb R$ a\ntotally ordered field). Hence the two parameter pairs $(s,b)$ and\n$(s+\\delta,b-G\\delta)$ produce the same predicted value at every recorded\ncoordinate, and every recorded value or declared closed bound interval\ncompatible with the prediction of one pair is compatible with the prediction\nof the other.\n\n(ii) [Nonidentifiability] When $G\\ne0$ and $\\delta\\ne0$ the two pairs are\ndistinct, yet their model predictions coincide; consequently comparison through\nthe affine model alone constrains only the combination $Gs+b$.\nFor an exact recorded equality $y(s,b)=z$ it fixes that combination to $z$;\nfor a declared bound $|y(s,b)-z|\\le r$, $r\\ge0$, it only constrains it to\n$[z-r,z+r]$. It cannot distinguish the pairs without additional independent\ninformation that distinguishes them. Independently fixing $b$ or $s$ is\nsufficient, though other independently declared constraints can also\ndistinguish the pairs.\n\n(iii) [Explicit instance] With $G=2$, $s=3$, $b=1$ and $\\delta=1$ one has\n$y(3,1)=2\\cdot3+1=7$ and $y(4,-1)=2\\cdot4-1=7$, so the pairs $(s,b)=(3,1)$ and\n$(4,-1)$ are indistinguishable by the recorded value $7$ alone; the same holds\nfor every declared bound interval, since the two predictions coincide exactly.\n\n(iv) [Status] This is a deterministic algebraic nonidentifiability statement\nabout the declared affine response model. It asserts no property of any\napparatus, no historical value, no dataset and no statistical or probabilistic\nclaim; it records that a discrepancy (or an agreement) can test the auxiliary\nbackground and calibration parameters as well as the field or source\nassumptions of a model, so that attributing a recorded outcome to one premise\nrequires independent information resolving the ambiguity.",
          "strategy": "Direct substitution and declared assumptions (the recorded research argument X8 of `worked-example-verifications.md`). In (i), expand and rearrange in the field $\\mathbb R$ ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]] supplies the ordered-field, hence field, axioms for $\\mathbb R$: distributivity gives $G(s+\\delta)=Gs+G\\delta$, associativity and commutativity of addition regroup $(Gs+G\\delta)+(b-G\\delta)$ as $Gs+(G\\delta-G\\delta)+b$, and $G\\delta-G\\delta=0$ with $Gs+0+b=Gs+b$). The same identity shows that any condition, bound interval or comparison expression depending only on the predicted value is unchanged, which proves (i) and the distinctness/nonidentifiability claim (ii) for $G\\ne0$, $\\delta\\ne0$. Clause (iii) is the explicit arithmetic instance $2\\cdot3+1=7=2\\cdot4-1$. Clause (iv) records the scope: no apparatus, historical, dataset or probabilistic claim is made, and the example uses only the record vocabulary and the real field arithmetic. An independent background or source-strength declaration, when available, is an additional hypothesis of any consumer, not a consequence of this example.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "JCGM 100:2008, Evaluation of measurement data - Guide to the expression of uncertainty in measurement (GUM 1995 with minor corrections), Joint Committee for Guides in Metrology, first edition September 2008, 134 PDF pages",
                "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/2071204/JCGM_100_2008_E.pdf",
                "locator": "JCGM 100:2008 clause 3.2 (printed p. 5) and clause 4.1.1 (printed pp. 8-9), read in the complete PDF: corrections, influence quantities and the functional model $Y=f(X_1,\\dots,X_N)$ of which the affine model of this example is the two-parameter case"
              },
              {
                "title": "Michael Faraday, Experimental Researches in Electricity, First Series (read 24 November 1831), Project Gutenberg transcription",
                "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14986/14986-h/14986-h.htm",
                "locator": "First Series paragraphs 1-33; an instance of a report with background, timing and detector-response auxiliaries that are not calibrated, motivating the declared affine model's ambiguity"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Elementary conditional statement about a declared affine response model inside the framework's record vocabulary: the parameter pairs $(s,b)$ and $(s+\\delta,b-G\\delta)$ produce identical predictions, so comparison through this model alone constrains only $Gs+b$ and cannot separate the pairs without additional independent information that distinguishes them. Independently fixing background or source strength is sufficient. It asserts nothing about any apparatus, no historical claim, no dataset, no sampling law and no probability; it is an exact algebraic nonidentifiability of the declared model."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-em-ideal-and-measured-fringes",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "An ideal electric-field zero and a declared detector-bin response",
          "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X8",
          "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-em-classical-interference",
