            "lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 18,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice, inherited from the cited theorems. Work in the field-action setting of [[def-em-field-action-definition]] with $U=(-1,1)^4$, constants $c,\\mu_0>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ ([[def-em-si-conventions]]), and the component dictionary of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]: $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{10}=-E_1/c$.\n\n(a) [Source-free zero field] For $j\\equiv0$ and $A=0$ the first-variation coefficient (whose integral is multiplied by $c^{-1}$) $-\\mu_0^{-1}F^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu h_\\nu+j^\\nu h_\\nu$ of clause (a) of [[lem-em-compact-field-variation]] vanishes identically for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R^4)$, because $F(A)=0$ and $j=0$; hence every compact first variation of $S$ at $A=0$ is zero and $A=0$ is stationary.\n\n(b) [A non-trivial compact test variation] Let $A$ be the smooth potential with $A_0(x)=x^1$ and $A_1=A_2=A_3=0$, and let $j\\equiv0$. Then $F_{01}=-\\partial_1A_0=-1$, $F^{01}=+1$, $F^{10}=-1$ and all other components vanish, so the represented field is $E=(c,0,0)$, $B=0$ (clause (b) of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]) and $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=0$ on $U$. By the equivalence of [[pthm-em-action-maxwell-variation]], $A$ is stationary; directly, for every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R^4)$ the first variation of $S$ at $A$ is\n$$\\frac{d}{ds}\\Big|_{s=0}S[A+sh]=-\\frac{1}{c\\mu_0}\\int_U\\bigl(\\partial_0h_1-\\partial_1h_0\\bigr)d\\lambda=\\frac{1}{c\\mu_0}\\int_U\\bigl(\\partial_1h_0-\\partial_0h_1\\bigr)d\\lambda=0,$$\nwhere each of $\\int_U\\partial_0h_1\\,d\\lambda$ and $\\int_U\\partial_1h_0\\,d\\lambda$ vanishes by the fundamental theorem in one variable combined with Fubini, and no boundary term appears because $h$ is compactly supported in $U$ ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]).\n\n(c) [Gauge-source sign check with actual overlap] Independently choose the smooth prescribed source $j^0=c\\rho(x^0)$, $j^i=0$, with nonzero nonnegative $\\rho\\in C_c^\\infty((-1/4,1/4))$ extended by zero. Choose $0\\le\\zeta\\in C_c^\\infty((-1,1))$ with $\\zeta=1$ near $[-1/2,1/2]$, and nonzero nonnegative $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty((-1,1)^3)$, supplied by [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]]. Put $\\varphi_\\pm(u)=(2\\pm u)\\zeta(u)$ and $\\chi_\\pm(x)=\\varphi_\\pm(x^0)\\psi(x^1,x^2,x^3)$. Both $\\varphi_\\pm$ are nonnegative compact bumps, but they are not globally monotone: on the entire support of $\\rho$ their derivatives equal $+1$ and $-1$, respectively. Use the contravariant gauge variations $(\\partial^\\sharp\\chi_\\pm)^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\nu\\chi_\\pm$ from [[pthm-em-action-gauge-source-compatibility]]. Then\n$$S[A+\\partial^\\sharp\\chi_\\pm]-S[A]=\\left(\\int_{-1}^1\\rho\\varphi_\\pm'\\,dx^0\\right)\\left(\\int_{(-1,1)^3}\\psi\\,d^3x\\right)=\\pm\\left(\\int\\rho\\right)\\left(\\int\\psi\\right),$$\nwhich is strictly positive for $+$ and strictly negative for $-$, since both factors are positive. Integration by parts gives exactly the same value as $-c^{-1}\\int_U(\\partial_\\mu j^\\mu)\\chi_\\pm d\\lambda$ with $\\partial_\\mu j^\\mu=c\\rho'$. This verifies the relative sign of the $+j^\\mu A_\\mu$ coupling, the raised-vector/covariant-lowered gauge shift and the conservation condition, using explicitly overlapping supports. If instead $\\rho$ is constant on the whole neighbourhood, the source is conserved and every compact gauge difference is zero. No compact nonzero globally increasing or decreasing function is used.\n\n(d) [Scope] The example is an exact computation in the declared smooth finite-region model; it asserts no existence of sources or fields, no numerical value of $\\mu_0$, and no global gauge statement.",
          "strategy": "Direct substitution in the declared functionals. (a) is the first-variation formula of [[lem-em-compact-field-variation]] with $F=0$ and $j=0$; the pointwise vanishing of the integrand gives a vanishing integral for every $h$. (b) The components follow from clause (a) of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]: $F_{01}=\\partial_0A_1-\\partial_1A_0=-1$, $F^{01}=\\eta^{00}\\eta^{11}F_{01}=+1$, $F^{10}=-1$. The coefficient equation $\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\mu_0j^\\nu$ holds because $F$ is constant and $j=0$, so [[pthm-em-action-maxwell-variation]] gives stationarity. The direct computation uses the first-variation expression of the cited lemma and the one-variable fundamental theorem for the compactly supported functions $h_0,h_1$, with Fubini reducing the four-dimensional integral; the absence of boundary terms is exactly the compact-support hypothesis. (c) Apply the corrected raised-gradient gauge formula. The cutoff $\\zeta$ is one on a neighbourhood of the support of $\\rho$, so $\\zeta'=0$ there and $\\varphi_+'=1$, $\\varphi_-'=-1$ there. Although both compact functions must turn back to zero outside that plateau, those derivative changes do not meet $\\rho$. Thus Fubini gives the two signed products of positive integrals exactly. The signs agree with one-dimensional [[thm-integration-by-parts]], whose endpoint term is zero because the factors are compactly supported. Constant $\\rho$ instead gives $\\int\\rho\\varphi_\\pm'=0$ by FTC, consistently with source conservation. The prescribed source and potential here need not satisfy Maxwell together; this is the deliberately nonconserved-source functional witness, not a claim of a dynamically admissible source-field configuration. All relevant functions are smooth; the existing field-sector Countable Choice convention is retained.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Worked computation in the smooth finite-region field action on a box: the explicit first variation of a constant-field potential vanishes with no boundary term, and the exact gauge difference of the action pins the relative sign between the source coupling, the gauge shift and source conservation, including a non-conserved source exhibiting both signs. Excluded: existence statements, numerical values of constants, and any global or empirical claim.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos), Chapter 5, §5.5.3",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro4.pdf",
                "locator": "printed pp. 125-126: the variation display discarding the total derivative, and the gauge-shift paragraph $S\\to S-\\tfrac1c\\int\\chi\\,\\partial_\\mu J^\\mu$"
              },
              {
                "title": "James Binney, Classical Fields (University of Oxford lecture course 2006-2007, complete 95-page notes)",
