          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, $c>0$ and $\\omega>0$, and let $\\widetilde E,\\widetilde B:D\\to\\mathbb C^3$. Define $E,B:D\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $E(x,t):=\\operatorname{Re}(\\widetilde E(x)e^{-i\\omega t})$ and $B(x,t):=\\operatorname{Re}(\\widetilde B(x)e^{-i\\omega t})$ componentwise ([[def-complex-exponential]], [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]; [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]] for the classes). Then: (a) [Calculus] if $\\widetilde E,\\widetilde B$ are $C^1$, then $E,B$ are $C^1$ and at every $(x,t)\\in D\\times\\mathbb R$: $\\partial_tE(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega\\widetilde E(x)e^{-i\\omega t})$, and likewise for $B$; for each $j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, $\\partial_jE(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}(\\partial_j\\widetilde E(x)e^{-i\\omega t})$, and likewise for $B$; and with the divergence and curl of [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] acting on the spatial variable, $\\operatorname{div}E(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}((\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde E)(x)e^{-i\\omega t})$, $\\operatorname{curl}E(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}((\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E)(x)e^{-i\\omega t})$, and likewise for $B$. (b) [Injectivity] if $w\\in\\mathbb C^3$ and $\\operatorname{Re}(we^{-i\\omega t})=0$ for all $t\\in\\mathbb R$, then $w=0$; more generally, if $w:D\\to\\mathbb C^3$ is continuous and $\\operatorname{Re}(w(x)e^{-i\\omega t})=0$ for all $(x,t)\\in D\\times\\mathbb R$, then $w\\equiv0$ on $D$. (c) [Equivalence] if $\\widetilde E,\\widetilde B$ are $C^1$, the phasor system $$\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde E=0,\\quad\\operatorname{div}\\widetilde B=0,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E=i\\omega\\widetilde B,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde B=-i\\omega c^{-2}\\widetilde E$$ holds on $D$ if and only if the real system $$\\operatorname{div}E=0,\\quad\\operatorname{div}B=0,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\quad\\operatorname{curl}B=c^{-2}\\partial_tE$$ holds on $D\\times\\mathbb R$.",
          "strategy": "Write the encoding in real components: by the Cartesian form and Euler identity of [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]], $\\operatorname{Re}(w(x)e^{-i\\omega t})=(\\operatorname{Re}w(x))\\cos(\\omega t)+(\\operatorname{Im}w(x))\\sin(\\omega t)$ componentwise, with the real and imaginary parts of [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]] and the complex arithmetic of [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. (a) For fixed $x$ each component is a constant-coefficient combination of $\\cos(\\omega t)$ and $\\sin(\\omega t)$; the affine map $t\\mapsto\\omega t$ has derivative $\\omega$ by [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], and [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] applied to the differentiable real sine and cosine maps of [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]] gives their composite derivatives. The sum, scalar-multiple and product rules of [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] then give $\\partial_tE=\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega\\widetilde Ee^{-i\\omega t})$, and the spatial derivatives pass through the constant coefficients, giving the spatial formulas for each component; the divergence and curl formulas follow by applying the componentwise formulas and the linearity of [[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]] (for complex fields, componentwise in real and imaginary parts). (b) Evaluating at $t=0$ gives $\\operatorname{Re}w=0$; evaluating at $t=\\pi/(2\\omega)$ gives $\\operatorname{Re}(-iw)=\\operatorname{Im}w=0$ by the quarter-turn values of [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]] and the Cartesian form of [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]], so $w=0$. The continuous case applies the pointwise result at each $x\\in D$; no choice principle is used. (c) Direction (i)$\\Rightarrow$(ii): substitute the phasor equations into the calculus formulas of (a); e.g. $\\operatorname{curl}E=\\operatorname{Re}((\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E)e^{-i\\omega t})=\\operatorname{Re}(i\\omega\\widetilde Be^{-i\\omega t})=-\\partial_tB$ and $\\operatorname{curl}B=\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega c^{-2}\\widetilde Ee^{-i\\omega t})=c^{-2}\\partial_tE$. Direction (ii)$\\Rightarrow$(i): subtract the complex expressions and apply (b), e.g. $\\operatorname{Re}((\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E-i\\omega\\widetilde B)e^{-i\\omega t})=0$ for all $(x,t)$ forces $\\operatorname{curl}\\widetilde E=i\\omega\\widetilde B$; the other three equations are analogous. All statements are pointwise algebraic identities; no PDE existence, no boundary condition and no choice principle is used.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
