            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-em-harmonic-forcing-periodic-response": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\omega>0$, $k:=\\omega/c$ with $c$ the vacuum speed of [[def-em-si-conventions]], $\\mu_0>0$ the vacuum permeability of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and $p_0\\in\\mathbb C^3$ (the complex dipole-moment amplitude, in $\\mathrm C\\,\\mathrm m$). Define, on the punctured space $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and all times $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $$E_{\\rm d}(x,t):=\\operatorname{Re}\\Bigl(-\\frac{\\mu_0\\omega^2}{4\\pi r}\\,n\\times(n\\times p_0)\\,e^{i(kr-\\omega t)}\\Bigr),\\qquad B_{\\rm d}(x,t):=\\frac1c\\,n\\times E_{\\rm d}(x,t),$$ where $r:=|x|$ and $n:=x/r$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]), with the real part and complex exponential of [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]] and [[def-complex-exponential]] and the componentwise real-part encoding convention of [[def-em-complex-plane-wave]] (the spatial amplitude here varies with x; no plane-wave instance is asserted). Call $(E_{\\rm d},B_{\\rm d})$ the **ideal harmonic electric-dipole radiation model of frequency $\\omega$ and amplitude $p_0$**, and call $S_{\\rm d}:=E_{\\rm d}\\times B_{\\rm d}/\\mu_0$ its model flux ([[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]); the model fields are read as an electric and a magnetic field of [[def-em-fields]]. [Adopted status] This is an explicitly adopted ideal radiation-law model, fixed by the following declarations: (i) it is the leading transverse far-zone field of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]](c) evaluated at the harmonic transverse moment $V(u)=\\operatorname{Re}(-\\omega^2p_0e^{-i\\omega u})$, i.e. at $p''$ for the waveform $p(u)=\\operatorname{Re}(p_0e^{-i\\omega u})$; (ii) the model contains no independent magnetic-dipole or higher-multipole radiation, no near-zone or static field and no other radiating component; (iii) it is not asserted to solve the vacuum Maxwell equations at finite $r$, to be the field of any actual source, to satisfy any boundary condition or to have finite total energy on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$; (iv) for compact smooth conserved sources, comparison with these prescribed model fields uses the actual source's moment p_act(u), the harmonic waveform p_h(u)=Re(p0 exp(-i omega u)), their second-derivative mismatch M(u)=|p_act''(u)-p_h''(u)|, and the explicit D,Gamma bounds of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]]. A bound depending only on that source's D and Gamma is valid when M0 on the observation window; the source's moment is not automatically the independently prescribed p0. These actual-source comparison uses assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) carried by the cited theorem. The explicit ideal fields themselves and their pointwise flux definition remain choice-free. No measured, empirical or material-response content is adopted: $p_0$ is prescribed model data, not a derived polarizability.",
          "strategy": "This is a definition contract; well-definedness is discharged by the cited suppliers. (i) The formula is the leading transverse term $E_{\\rm lead}=\\mu_0n\\times(n\\times V)/(4\\pi r)$ of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]](c) at $V(u)=\\operatorname{Re}(-\\omega^2p_0e^{-i\\omega u})$: since $p''(u)=\\operatorname{Re}(-\\omega^2p_0e^{-i\\omega u})$ for $p(u)=\\operatorname{Re}(p_0e^{-i\\omega u})$, the derivative supplied by [[lem-em-harmonic-forcing-periodic-response]](a) with w=-omega²p0, using its explicit C-infinity real harmonic waveform, the model field is exactly $E_{\\rm lead}$ rewritten with $u=t-r/c$ and $e^{i(kr-\\omega t)}=e^{-i\\omega(t-r/c)}$; the cross product, inner product and norm are those of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] and [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. (ii) $r>0$ on the declared domain, so $E_{\\rm d}$ and $B_{\\rm d}$ are $C^\\infty$ there and the same componentwise real-part convention as [[def-em-complex-plane-wave]] is used; the varying radial amplitude is not a plane-wave amplitude; $E_{\\rm d}\\cdot n=0$ because $n\\times(n\\times p_0)$ is orthogonal to $n$, and $B_{\\rm d}=n\\times E_{\\rm d}/c$ by definition. (iii) The model flux $S_{\\rm d}$ is the quantity of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]] evaluated at the model fields; that definition applies to any pair of fields of the declared regularity and assigns no field equation. (iv) The status clause lists the model assumptions (i)-(iv) and excludes exact Maxwell, source-realization, boundary, finite-energy and empirical claims; the model's relation to actual sources is only through the explicit bounds of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]], which are quantified in the item below. The explicit formula/flux construction uses no choice. Actual compact-source comparisons retain the stated Countable Choice of [[pthm-em-dipole-radiation]], including a moment-mismatch term; no comparison to an arbitrary unrelated harmonic amplitude follows from D alone. Smoothness off r0 follows by coordinate sum/product/chain rules and the positive square-root/reciprocal powers of [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]] for r=|x|; The harmonic lemma supplies all derivatives of the real waveform; compose its second derivative with u=t-r/c using the declared total chain rule. No complex-to-real derivative theorem is silently substituted for that real-waveform interface. Orthogonality is the finite cross-product identity of [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]] applied separately to real and imaginary components. The ideal model is meaningful also at p0=0, giving zero fields; no actual source realization or finite-radius Maxwell equation is deduced.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos, author-hosted complete 236-page notes)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
                "locator": "section 6.2 'Dipole Radiation', printed pp. 143-153, and section 6.3 'Scattering', printed pp. 154-157"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. Fitzpatrick, Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course (University of Texas at Austin), nodes 94-95 ('The Hertzian dipole', 'Electric dipole radiation')",
                "url": "https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node94.html",
                "locator": "nodes 94-95 of chapter 9, including the far-zone mean flux and differential power of an oscillating dipole"
              },
              {
