            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) as carried by [[pthm-em-dipole-scattering-cross-section]]; the linearization below and [[lem-em-harmonic-forcing-periodic-response]] use no choice. Assume the setting and postulates of [[pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force]] for a test particle with charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ ([[def-em-sources]](a)) and externally supplied rest mass $m>0$, in the incident plane-wave field of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] with wavevector $\\kappa$, complex amplitude $e\\ne0$, $\\kappa\\cdot e=0$, $|\\kappa|=\\omega/c$ and $b=\\kappa\\times e/\\omega$ as in [[pthm-em-dipole-scattering-cross-section]]; write $E_{\\rm inc}(x,t)=\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i(\\kappa\\cdot x-\\omega t)})$ and $B_{\\rm inc}=\\kappa\\times E_{\\rm inc}/\\omega$ ([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]], [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-si-conventions]]). Assume that for some $\\eta_0>0$ and a common open interval $I\\ni0$, for every $\\eta\\in(-\\eta_0,\\eta_0)$ there is a $C^2$ future timelike massive solution $r_\\eta:I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ of the adopted four-force law in the external field $\\eta(E_{\\rm inc},B_{\\rm inc})$ (no self-field, recoil or material force), and that the family admits a uniform first-order expansion: there are $r_1\\in C^2(I;\\mathbb R^3)$ and, for every compact $K\\subset I$, a constant $C_K$ with $$|r_\\eta(t)-x_0-\\eta r_1(t)|+|\\dot r_\\eta(t)-\\eta\\dot r_1(t)|\\le C_K\\eta^2\\qquad(t\\in K,\\ |\\eta|<\\eta_0)$$ about a fixed position $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^3$, The family time interval I is not required to contain a full period, and no solution r_eta outside I is assumed. For the periodic-response conclusions(b),(c), impose the following explicit preparation(P): the unique global harmonic-ODE extension of r1 supplied after(a) by [[lem-em-harmonic-forcing-periodic-response]](d), with its actual value and derivative at0, is T-periodic, T=2pi/omega. Equivalently its affine drift is zero, or $r_1'(0)=\\operatorname{Re}(iw/\\omega)$ with $w=(q/m)e e^{i\\kappa\\cdot x_0}$. The linearized equation and affine local/global classification in(a) hold for every stated family even without(P); overlap-only periodicity on a short I is not substituted for(P). Then: (a) [Linearized equation] $\\ddot r_1(t)=\\frac qmE_{\\rm inc}(x_0,t)=\\frac qm\\operatorname{Re}(e\\,e^{i(\\kappa\\cdot x_0-\\omega t)})$ on $I$. (b) [Induced dipole under preparation(P)] $x_0$ is the zeroth-order rest/reference position. Let a be the mean of the global periodic first-order response, so on I one has r1=z_p+a. The arbitrary constant a is retained: no exact mean or periodicity of the full nonlinear family is asserted. The oscillating part of the first-order dipole coefficient $q(r_1-a)$ is $\\operatorname{Re}(p_0e^{-i\\omega t})$ with complex amplitude $$p_0=-\\frac{q^2}{m\\omega^2}\\,e\\,e^{i\\kappa\\cdot x_0};$$ equivalently $p_0=\\alpha e'$ with $\\alpha=-\\frac{q^2}{m\\omega^2}$ (units $\\mathrm F\\,\\mathrm m^2$) and the unimodular factor $e'=e\\,e^{i\\kappa\\cdot x_0}$, $|e'|=|e|$. (c) [Leading Thomson-model cross section] substituting this $p_0$ into [[pthm-em-dipole-scattering-cross-section]](b) gives $$\\sigma_{\\rm T}=\\frac{q^4}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0^2m^2c^4}=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}\\Bigl(\\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0mc^2}\\Bigr)^2,$$ independent of $\\omega$ and of the phase factor. (d) [Scope] this is a controlled conditional first variation of the adopted relativistic external-force law under the stated solution-family hypotheses. It is not the exact Newtonian force postulate, it does not assert existence, persistence, stability or self-consistency of the family, it excludes self-force, radiation reaction and material media, and it makes no empirical, measurement, atomic-physics or frequency-response claim. In(a), every local r1 has the unique global harmonic extension z_p+a+bt, with z_p=Re(-w omega^-2 exp(-i omega t)), b=r1'(0)-z_p'(0), a=r1(0)-z_p(0). Thus the general affine-drift response is also retained; preparation(P) sets b0 for(b),(c) and leaves a arbitrary. For a nonzero eta, the associated LEADING ideal harmonic model has incident amplitude eta e and induced dipole amplitude eta p0. Their squared flux factors eta² cancel in its cross-section ratio; sigma_T is this leading-model coefficient, not an assertion of the exact nonlinear particle's emitted field or cross section. At q0 the induced amplitude and sigma_T are zero without any division by q. All eta estimates apply only to the open parameter domain on which the family was declared.",
          "strategy": "Use the exact integrated momentum ODE; never differentiate a uniform-in-time Oeta² remainder. Fix a compact K in I and choose a closed interval L contained in I that contains K and0. Put v_eta=r_eta', gamma_eta=(1-|v_eta|²/c²)^-1/2, and P_eta=m gamma_eta v_eta. The stated family expansion implies r_eta->x0 and v_eta/eta->r1' uniformly on L as eta->0 with eta!=0, and |v_eta|=O(|eta|). For small eta the fixed speed bound |v_eta|/c<=1/2 holds on L; the actual algebraic low-speed clause of [[pthm-em-relativistic-lorentz-force]] gives gamma_eta=1+O(eta²) uniformly. Hence P_eta/eta->m r1' uniformly, using only multiplication of uniformly bounded functions.\nThe EXACT coordinate ODE of that supplier is\n$$P_\\eta'(t)=q\\eta\\,[E_{\\rm inc}(r_\\eta(t),t)+v_\\eta(t)\\times B_{\\rm inc}(r_\\eta(t),t)].$$\nThe plane-wave fields are smooth with bounded spatial first derivatives on a common compact tube around x0 times L, so their values at r_eta converge uniformly to their values at x0; the magnetic term is uniformly Oeta since B is bounded and v_eta=Oeta. Thus for eta!=0, P_eta'/eta converges uniformly to q E_inc(x0,t). This derivative convergence comes from the adopted ODE, not from differentiating the family expansion. Apply componentwise scalar FTC [[thm-ftc-second-part]] to the actual C1 momentum on [0,t], with oriented limits for t<0:\n$$\\frac{P_\\eta(t)-P_\\eta(0)}\\eta=q\\int_0^t[E_{\\rm inc}(r_\\eta(s),s)+v_\\eta(s)\\times B_{\\rm inc}(r_\\eta(s),s)]ds.$$\nUniform convergence passes through these finite ordinary integrals: each component's error is at most the interval length times its uniform supremum, by [[lem-integral-elementary-bounds]]. Taking the limit gives\n$$m[r_1'(t)-r_1'(0)]=q\\int_0^tE_{\\rm inc}(x_0,s)ds.$$\nThe continuous integrand and [[thm-ftc-first-part]] now imply m r1''=q E_inc(x0,t) on I. This proves(a) and the available first-order equation while retaining the original position/velocity assumptions and arbitrary local I; no differentiability in eta beyond the given uniform expansion and no acceleration-error differentiation is assumed. The parameter endpoints eta=+-eta0 are never evaluated. A continuous differentiable family was not replaced by a stronger C2-in-eta family.\nBy the LOCAL interval/global extension clause(d) of [[lem-em-harmonic-forcing-periodic-response]] with w=(q/m)e exp(i kappa dot x0), r1=z_p+a+bt on I and its explicit extension on R is unique. Preparation(P) says this extension is periodic, hence b0; this is nonvacuous even when I is shorter than a period. Its mean a comes from the harmonic lemma's finite-period mean calculation. Therefore q(r1-a)=Re[-q² e exp(i kappa dot x0) exp(-i omega t)/(m omega²)], proving(b), with the arbitrary constant mean retained and x0 only the zeroth-order reference position. The phase factor has modulus1 by [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]], so |p0|=q²|e|/(m omega²) and p0=alpha e', alpha=-q²/(m omega²).\nSubstituting into [[pthm-em-dipole-scattering-cross-section]](b) cancels omega^4 and gives sigma_T=q^4/(6pi epsilon0²m²c^4)=(8pi/3)[q²/(4pi epsilon0 m c²)]². The formula is for the leading ideal harmonic model: for nonzero eta scale BOTH incident amplitude and harmonic dipole amplitude by eta, so their ratio is unchanged. At q0 the same algebra gives zero response amplitude and zero cross section. The adopted external-force law excludes self-field and material/recoil forces; neither the local solution-family assumption nor the global extension of its LINEAR response proves global persistence/periodicity of the nonlinear family or a self-consistent scattered field. Finite-interval FTC, uniform limits and harmonic affine classification are choice-free; only the stated cross-section source/sphere premise carries Countable Choice.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism (University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos, author-hosted complete 236-page notes)",
