                "locator": "eqs. (1120)-(1130) of node 96, including the differential and total Thomson cross section"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. Beisert, Elektrodynamik, ETH Zurich 2024 FS, section 12.3 'Streuung an Materie'",
                "url": "https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/ED-24FS-Skript.pdf",
                "locator": "printed pp. 12.8-12.10, eqs. (12.36)-(12.45): cross section, dipole scattering, dielectric sphere"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional weak-field (small-amplitude) first variation of the adopted relativistic external-force law for a test charge in an ideal plane wave: the linearized periodic displacement, the induced harmonic dipole amplitude $p_0=-\\frac{q^2}{m\\omega^2}e e^{i\\kappa\\cdot x_0}$, and the resulting leading Thomson-model cross section $\\frac{q^4}{6\\pi\\varepsilon_0^2m^2c^4}$ independent of frequency. Excluded: exact Newtonian force laws, existence, persistence, stability or self-consistency of the weak-field family, self-force and radiation reaction, material media, quantum and atomic-resonance content, and every measured, statistical or empirical claim. The nonlinear family remains on any common open I containing0; only its harmonic first-order ODE response is globally extended and periodically prepared. x0 is a zeroth-order rest/reference position, with arbitrary first-order response mean a; no exact nonlinear-family mean/periodicity is claimed. All zero-charge and complex-polarization cases remain.",
          "dependency_level": 23
        }
      ],
