          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) as carried by [[pthm-em-scalar-aperture-diffraction]] and by the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison and Fubini suppliers used in the integral reduction below ([[def-countable-choice]]). Work in the scalar aperture model of [[post-em-scalar-aperture-model]] and [[pthm-em-scalar-aperture-diffraction]], with frequency $\\omega>0$, $k=\\omega/c$, aperture plane $y_3=0$, model field $u=G_k*\\alpha$ and $I(x)=\\frac12|u(x)|^2$. Fix $a>0$, $b>0$ and $A_0\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$; identify the rectangle $R:=[-a,a]\\times[-b,b]\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ with the planar aperture $\\{(y_1,y_2,0):(y_1,y_2)\\in R\\}$ and take the **uniform rectangular aperture distribution** let $\\iota:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $\\iota(y_1,y_2)=(y_1,y_2,0)$, and define its plane-embedding pushforward $$\\alpha(E):=A_0\\,\\lambda_2(R\\cap\\iota^{-1}(E))\\qquad(E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3\\text{ Borel}).$$ This is a finite complex measure on $\\mathbb R^3$, supported on the embedded compact rectangle $S=\\iota(R)$ with $|y|\\le a':=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$ and total variation $\\|\\alpha\\|_{\\rm TV}=4ab|A_0|$ ([[def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume]], [[def-measure-with-density]], [[def-integration-against-a-signed-or-complex-measure]]). Then: (a) [Fraunhofer amplitude] with $\\operatorname{sinc}\\xi:=\\sin\\xi/\\xi$ for $\\xi\\ne0$ and $\\operatorname{sinc}0:=1$, the Fraunhofer amplitude factor of [[pthm-em-scalar-aperture-diffraction]](a) is exactly $$\\int e^{-ikn\\cdot y}\\,d\\alpha(y)=4abA_0\\operatorname{sinc}(ka\\,n_1)\\operatorname{sinc}(kb\\,n_2)\\qquad(n=(n_1,n_2,n_3)\\in S^2),$$ an identity for every direction $n$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-complex-exponential]], [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]], [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]], [[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]], [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]]). (b) [Fraunhofer intensity] in the Fraunhofer regime of [[pthm-em-scalar-aperture-diffraction]](a) with aperture radius $a':=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$, i.e. for $r\\ge2a'$, the model intensity is $$I_{\\rm F}(rn)=\\frac{a^2b^2|A_0|^2}{2\\pi^2r^2}\\operatorname{sinc}^2(ka\\,n_1)\\operatorname{sinc}^2(kb\\,n_2)$$ up to the Fraunhofer absolute error of that theorem, transferred to $I$ by its clause (c). The pattern vanishes exactly where $ka\\,n_1\\in\\pi\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$ or $kb\\,n_2\\in\\pi\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and, as a mathematical pattern on all $S^2$, has its global maximum exactly at the two poles $n=(0,0,\\pm1)$. The model observes only $x_3>0$; its allowed directions have $n_3>0$, where the central maximum is only $n=(0,0,1)$. (c) [Scope] this is an exact computation inside the adopted ideal scalar model, not a measured diffraction record: no absolute radiometric calibration, polarization content, screen interaction, apparatus model or sampling law is asserted, and the Fraunhofer/Fresnel error bounds of [[pthm-em-scalar-aperture-diffraction]] are inherited rather than re-estimated.",
          "strategy": "(a) The continuous embedding $\\iota$ has Borel inverse images by [[thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel]]. Disjoint Borel sets have disjoint inverse images; countable additivity of $\\lambda_2$ therefore proves directly that the displayed $\\alpha$ is a complex measure as in [[def-complex-measure]]. It is supported on $S=\\iota(R)$. For every countable partition, the sum of the moduli of its values is $|A_0|\\lambda_2(R)=4ab|A_0|$ by [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]], so [[def-total-variation-of-a-signed-or-complex-measure]] gives the stated finite total variation. For a simple Borel $f$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ the defining sum gives $\\int f\\,d\\alpha=A_0\\int_Rf(\\iota(y))\\,d\\lambda_2(y)$; uniform finite-simple approximations of any bounded Borel $f$ and the total-variation bound pass this identity to the integrals of [[def-integration-against-a-signed-or-complex-measure]]. Thus the plane measure is correctly typed for the three-dimensional model. For $f(y)=e^{-ik(n_1y_1+n_2y_2)}$ (the $y_3$-coordinate is zero on the aperture, and $n\\cdot y=n_1y_1+n_2y_2$ by [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]), $f$ is continuous on the compact rectangle $R$ ([[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]), so [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]] identifies the Lebesgue integral with the Riemann double integral; [[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]] factors it into the product of the two one-dimensional integrals. For $n_1\\ne0$, the one-dimensional integral is $\\int_{-a}^a e^{-ikn_1y_1}\\,dy_1=2\\sin(ka\\,n_1)/(kn_1)=2a\\operatorname{sinc}(ka\\,n_1)$ by [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]] and [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] for the affine phase $y_1\\mapsto-kn_1y_1$, applied to the real and imaginary parts via [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]; for $n_1=0$ it equals $2a=2a\\operatorname{sinc}0$ by the separately defined value, so the formula holds for every direction. The second factor is analogous, giving the displayed product $4abA_0\\operatorname{sinc}(ka\\,n_1)\\operatorname{sinc}(kb\\,n_2)$. (b) Insert the product amplitude into the Fraunhofer expression $u(rn)=\\frac{e^{ikr}}{4\\pi r}\\int e^{-ikn\\cdot y}d\\alpha(y)+$error of [[pthm-em-scalar-aperture-diffraction]](a), square the modulus and use $I=\\frac12|u|^2$; the modulus of the amplitude is $4ab|A_0||\\operatorname{sinc}(ka\\,n_1)||\\operatorname{sinc}(kb\\,n_2)|$, so $|u_{\\rm F}|^2=\\frac{a^2b^2|A_0|^2}{\\pi^2r^2}\\operatorname{sinc}^2(ka\\,n_1)\\operatorname{sinc}^2(kb\\,n_2)$ and the factor $\\frac12$ gives the displayed $I_{\\rm F}$. The zero assertion follows from [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]] and the separate value at0. To prove the exact maxima, $|\\sin x|<|x|$ for every $x\\ne0$: for $x>0$ set $\\delta=\\min\\{x/3,\\pi/6\\}>0$. On $[\\delta,2\\delta]$, the cosine signs and strict monotonicity of [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]] give $|\\cos t|\\le\\cos\\delta<1$, while globally $|\\cos t|\\le1$. FTC and the Riemann integral triangle/monotonicity estimate (applied to $-|\\cos|\\le\\cos\\le|\\cos|$) give $|\\sin x|\\le\\int_0^x|\\cos t|\\,dt\\le x-\\delta(1-\\cos\\delta)<x$; continuous integrability and subinterval additivity are supplied by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]], [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]] and [[thm-additivity-over-subintervals]]. Negative $x$ follows from sine oddness in [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]]. Thus $|\\operatorname{sinc}x|=1$ exactly at0, and since $k,a,b>0$ the product attains its maximum exactly when $n_1=n_2=0$, the two spherical poles. Only the positive pole belongs to the model upper half-space. The embedded corners have norm exactly $a'=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$; the support lies in the closed radius set $|y|\\le a'$, so the parent theorem's directly proved closed-support estimate applies at the unchanged endpoint $r\\ge2a'$; clause (c) of the theorem transfers the field error to $I$. (c) is the scope of the model; no additional approximation is introduced. The computation uses only the standard Riemann/Lebesgue and trigonometric suppliers listed in the dependencies; the Countable Choice assumption is exactly the one carried by the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison and Fubini interfaces, with no additional choice.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
