        {
          "id": "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Massive spinless test-particle action",
          "statement": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $a<b$, and let $x:[a,b]\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve on the prescribed background of [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]]. Let $H:(-\\epsilon_0,\\epsilon_0)\\times[a,b]\\to M$ be a $C^2$ variation, for some $\\epsilon_0>0$, such that each $H(\\epsilon,\\cdot)$ is future timelike, $H(0,s)=x(s)$, and $H(\\epsilon,a)=x(a)$ and $H(\\epsilon,b)=x(b)$. For a spinless, structureless massive test body of constant primitive mass $m>0$, measured in kilograms, and the SI conversion constant $c>0$ metres per second, adopt the action on this finite segment\n\n$$S_m[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds=-mc^2\\Delta\\tau,$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta\\tau:=c^{-1}\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds$ is the ideal-clock increment specified by [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]] on this smooth segment. The model's free trajectories are precisely the curves stationary under the fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations just specified; this is the local variation class in [[lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation]]. When $s$ is measured in seconds, $T$ has units metres per second, and the action has units joule-seconds. This is an adopted model premise, not a consequence of the metric or clock postulate. It excludes self-gravity, radiation reaction, spin, finite-size structure, particle creation, and quantum effects; it does not assert empirical adequacy for any particular body.",
          "deps": [
            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
            "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization",
            "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted lecture-note chapter excerpt, §1.1.1 particle action",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr1.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, Ch. 3 §3.3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Classical spinless structureless point test body of constant primitive mass m>0 on a prescribed smooth background; no self-field, radiation reaction, finite-size, quantum, or global-completeness claim.",
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Positive-einbein variation and massive/null sector equations",
          "statement": "Let $a<b$, let $x:[a,b]\\to M$ be a $C^2$ curve with parameter $s$ and $T=dx/ds$, fix $c>0$, let $e(s)>0$ be smooth, and let $m\\ge0$ be constant. Take independent $C^2$ fixed-endpoint variations of $x$ and smooth interior variations of $e$. For the action\n\n$$S_e[x,e]=\\frac12\\int_a^b\\!\\left(e^{-1}g(T,T)-em^2c^2\\right)ds,$$\n\nindependent fixed-endpoint variations in $x$ and interior variations in $e$ give\n\n$$g(T,T)+e^2m^2c^2=0,\\qquad D_s(T/e)=0.$$\n\nOn the future timelike massive branch $m>0$, this constraint uniquely gives $e=\\ell/(mc)$, $T/e=mU$, and the action reduces to $-mc\\int\\ell ds$; in proper-time gauge $s=\\tau$, $e=1/m$. On the nonzero future null branch $m=0$, $P:=T/e$ is nonzero future null and $D_sP=0$; if $d\\lambda=e\\,ds$, then $dx/d\\lambda=P$ and $D_\\lambda P=0$. There is no null proper-time gauge, and the scale of $P$ remains initial data. For finite intervals $[a',b']$ and $[a,b]$, an orientation-preserving $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism $s=\\phi(r):[a',b']\\to[a,b]$ with $\\phi'(r)>0$ gives $T_r(r)=\\phi'(r)T(\\phi(r))$ and $e_r(r)=\\phi'(r)e(\\phi(r))$; the einbein action is invariant, and this transformation preserves the stated $C^2$ curve and smooth-einbein configuration class.",
          "deps": [
            "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
            "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization",
            "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation",
            "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
            "lem-gr-connection-conditions-in-chart-components",
            "def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle",
