        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-free-massive-and-null-geodesics",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Free massive and null particle geodesics",
          "statement": "On a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background, stationarity for the massive action of [[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]] is defined on a finite segment $[a,b]$ with the stated fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations. A stationary massive path on that segment satisfies $D_\\tau U=0$ in proper-time parameter, with $P=mU$ and $m>0$ constant. In the separately adopted null sector of [[post-rpm-null-test-particle-action]], stationarity for the einbein action is likewise defined on a finite segment, using independent fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations of the curve and smooth interior variations of the einbein. Its nonzero future-null branch has momentum $P$ satisfying $D_\\lambda P=0$ in an affine parameter $\\lambda$ with $dx/d\\lambda=P$. These equations hold locally on the interiors of the finite segments; their ODE solutions extend uniquely to maximal solutions on open intervals, with no action integrated over an infinite interval. For any $\\tau_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and $p_0\\in M$, an initial vector $U_0\\in T_{p_0}M$ that is future timelike and satisfies $g_{p_0}(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$ determines a unique local massive solution with $x(\\tau_0)=p_0$, $U(\\tau_0)=U_0$, and a unique maximal solution on an open interval containing $\\tau_0$. For any $\\lambda_0\\in\\mathbb R$ and $p_0\\in M$, a nonzero future-null vector $P_0\\in T_{p_0}M$ determines a unique local null solution with $x(\\lambda_0)=p_0$, $P(\\lambda_0)=P_0$, and a unique maximal solution on an open interval containing $\\lambda_0$. Neither maximal interval is asserted to be all of $\\mathbb R$.\n\nMore explicitly, there are open intervals $J\\ni\\tau_0$ and $J'\\ni\\lambda_0$ such that the massive local solution is a $C^2$ curve $x:J\\to M$ with $U=dx/d\\tau$, and the null local solution is a $C^2$ curve $x:J'\\to M$ with $P=dx/d\\lambda$; their stated initial values are imposed at the indicated parameter values.",
          "deps": [
            "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action",
            "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
            "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation",
            "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence",
            "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization",
            "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action": "physical-assumption",
            "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action": "physical-assumption",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted lecture-note chapter excerpt, §§1.1.1–1.1.2",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr1.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, Ch. 3 §3.3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, String Theory, complete lecture notes, §1.1.2",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/string/string1.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional model deduction for free spinless test bodies on a prescribed smooth background; local initial-value solutions only, with no geodesic completeness or empirical universality claim.",
          "library": "physics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Prescribed constant-mass force law",
          "statement": "For a named massive test body with constant primitive mass $m>0$ measured in kilograms, SI conversion constant $c>0$ in metres per second, and momentum $P=mU$ as defined in [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]], define the future unit timelike shell by\n\n$$\\Sigma_c^+:=\\{(p,V)\\in TM:V\\text{ future timelike},\\ g_p(V,V)=-c^2\\}.$$\n\nLet $\\mathcal V\\subset TM$ be an open neighborhood of $\\Sigma_c^+$ contained in the future timelike velocity cone. For a massive body trajectory $x:I\\to M$ that is $C^2$ and parameterized by proper time $\\tau$, with $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ a nonempty open interval, put $U=dx/d\\tau$ and require $(x(\\tau),U(\\tau))\\in\\Sigma_c^+\\subseteq\\mathcal V$ for every $\\tau\\in I$. Specify a smooth map $f:\\mathcal V\\to TM$ over the bundle projection $\\pi:TM\\to M$, meaning $\\pi(f(p,V))=p$ (equivalently, $f(p,V)\\in T_pM$), and require $g_p(V,f(p,V))=0$ for every $(p,V)\\in\\mathcal V$. One may adopt this force map and the equation\n\n$$D_\\tau P=f(x,U).$$\n\nThe orthogonality condition is part of this model's compatibility assumptions, imposed throughout $\\mathcal V$; it preserves $g(U,U)=-c^2$ for constant $m$. Since $P=mU$, this equation is equivalent to $D_\\tau U=f(x,U)/m$ on the stated domain. It is not a law selecting a force for every particle or interaction. A local IVP follows only for data in $\\mathcal V$, as in [[lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp]]. The force has SI units newtons when $\\tau$ is in seconds and $P$ has units kg m s$^{-1}$. No empirical force measurements, universal coupling, or global existence are asserted.",
          "deps": [
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
            "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, Ch. 1 four-force",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted lecture-note chapter excerpt, §1.1.1 particle motion",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr1.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A selected smooth external force law for a named massive test body of constant mass on a prescribed background; orthogonality is a compatibility condition preserving the mass shell, not a universal force principle.",
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-killing-particle-charge",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Killing particle charge and force balance",
          "statement": "Let $K$ be a smooth Killing field on a region of the prescribed background. For a massive test body obeying the force postulate, with constant $m>0$, $P=mU$ and $D_\\tau P=f$, the scalar $g(K,P)$ obeys\n\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}g(K,P)=g(K,f).$$\n\nThus it is constant when the body is free ($f=0$). For a free null geodesic with affine tangent momentum $P$, $g(K,P)$ is constant along its affine parameter as well. If $K$ is a future unit timelike observer field along the curve, then $-c\\,g(K,P)$ has energy units and is the energy defined by that observer; for a general Killing field it is only a conserved scalar, not automatically an energy.",
          "deps": [
            "def-gr-killing-field",
            "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
            "lem-gr-connection-conditions-in-chart-components",
            "lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
            "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law",
            "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action",
            "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-gr-killing-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-gr-connection-conditions-in-chart-components": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law": "physical-assumption",
            "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action": "physical-assumption",
            "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, Ch. 5 Killing vectors and conserved quantities",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted lecture-note chapter excerpt, §5.3 Killing vectors",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr4.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional scalar balance for a test particle and a specified Killing field; energy interpretation only for a future unit time-translation field, with no global symmetry assumption.",
          "library": "physics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-mass-shell-and-force-work",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Mass shell, observer energy and inertial work",
          "statement": "For a massive body with constant $m>0$, $P=mU$, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, and the force law of [[post-rpm-prescribed-force-law]],\n\n$$g(P,P)=-m^2c^2.$$\n\nFor every observer $n$ at the event, the energy and spatial momentum defined in [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]] satisfy\n\n$$E_n(P)^2=m^2c^4+c^2g(q_n(P),q_n(P)),\\qquad E_n(P)>0.$$\n\nFor a nonzero future-null $P$, $E_n(P)=c\\sqrt{g(q_n(P),q_n(P))}$. In a flat inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with $n=e_0$ and inertial chart $x^0=ct$ from [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]], let $E=E_n(P)$, let $\\mathbf p$ be the spatial coordinates of $q_n(P)$ in $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$, set $\\mathbf v=d\\mathbf x/dt$, and define $\\mathbf F=d\\mathbf p/dt$. Then $dE/dt=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v$ under the constant-mass force hypotheses. If $m(\\tau)>0$ is $C^1$, $U$ is $C^1$ with $g(U,U)=-c^2$, and $P=m(\\tau)U$, then the kinematic identity $g(U,D_\\tau P)=-c^2\\dot m$ holds; it specifies no variable-mass law.",
          "deps": [
            "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law",
            "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
            "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
            "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition",
            "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors",
            "lem-sr-four-force-and-work-energy-identities",
            "lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
