    {
      "id": "rpm-momentum-collisions",
      "title": "Mass shell inversion, observer work and variable mass",
      "category": "relativistic-particle-mechanics",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "rpm-momentum-collisions-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "rpm-geometry-actions",
        "sr-causality-and-signals"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-inverse-momentum-and-work",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Mass shell inversion, observer work and variable mass",
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, and let $x:I\\to A$ be a $C^2$ future-timelike worldline in the flat Minkowski model of [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. Use one inertial frame and parameterize the curve by that chart's time coordinate $t$, so its coordinates are $(ct,\\mathbf x(t))$ ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]). Identify the observer rest space with $\\mathbb R^3$ by the frame's spatial orthonormal basis and use its Euclidean dot product and norm ([[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]). Write $\\mathbf v=d\\mathbf x/dt$, $\\gamma=(1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$, proper time $\\tau$, and $U=dx/d\\tau$. In the constant-mass sector of [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]], let $m>0$ be constant and $P=mU$. In this chart write $P=(E/c,\\mathbf p)$, where $E$ and $\\mathbf p$ are the observer energy and spatial momentum of [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]]. Then $|\\mathbf v|<c$, $E=\\gamma mc^2>0$, $\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v$, and\n\n$$E^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2,\\qquad \\mathbf v=\\frac{c^2\\mathbf p}{E}.$$\n\nDefine the three-force by $\\mathbf F:=d\\mathbf p/dt$ and the four-force by $K:=dP/d\\tau$ in this flat inertial chart. In SI units $m$ is in kilograms, $c$ and $\\mathbf v$ in metres per second, $E$ in joules, $\\mathbf p$ in kilogram metres per second, and $\\mathbf F,K$ in newtons. Then\n\n$$\\frac{dE}{dt}=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v,\\qquad K=\\gamma\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v}{c},\\mathbf F\\right).$$\n\nSeparately, let $I_\\tau\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open proper-time interval, let $m:I_\\tau\\to(0,\\infty)$ be $C^1$, and let $U:I_\\tau\\to V$ be a $C^1$ future timelike vector field with $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$. Measure $m$ in kilograms and $dm/d\\tau$ in kilograms per second, and set $P(\\tau):=m(\\tau)U(\\tau)$. With $K:=dP/d\\tau$, the kinematic ansatz satisfies\n\n$$\\eta(P,K)=-m\\,\\frac{dm}{d\\tau}\\,c^2.$$\n\nThe last identity is only a differentiated mass-shell relation; it prescribes no evolution for $m$. None of these conclusions assumes a force law.",
          "deps": [
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, Chapter 7 Special Relativity lecture notes",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel7.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Taylor and Wheeler, Spacetime Physics, 2nd ed., Chapter 7, Momenergy",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Relativity/Spacetime_Physics_%28Taylor_and_Wheeler%29/07%3A_Momenergy/7.03%3A_Momenergy_Components_and_Magnitude"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional kinematic identities for a massive point particle of constant positive mass in one inertial chart of flat Minkowski spacetime, plus a separate variable-mass ansatz; no force law or variable-mass dynamics is adopted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "library": "physics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-rpm-finite-future-momentum-sum",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite causal sums and center-of-momentum rest observer",
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski model with $c>0$, and fix an inertial frame. For a finite nonempty list $P_1,\\ldots,P_N$ of nonzero future-causal vectors in $V$, let $Q:=\\sum_{i=1}^N P_i$, let $m_i:=\\sqrt{-\\eta(P_i,P_i)}/c$ (so $m_i=0$ exactly for a null leg), and let $M(Q)$ be the invariant mass of the total when $Q$ is timelike, as defined in [[def-sr-total-four-momentum-and-invariant-mass]].\n\nThen $Q$ is nonzero and future causal. If at least one $P_i$ is timelike, then $Q$ is future timelike. If all $P_i$ are null, then $Q$ is null exactly when all $P_i$ lie on the same future null ray; otherwise $Q$ is future timelike. Whenever $Q$ is timelike, the center-of-momentum frame exists, its time-axis observer $n_Q=Q/(M(Q)c)$ has zero total spatial momentum and total observer energy $M(Q)c^2$, and\n\n$$M(Q)\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^N m_i.$$\n\nEquality holds exactly when every $P_i$ is massive and there is one future unit timelike vector $n$ such that $P_i=m_i c\\,n$ for every $i$. A nonzero future-null total has no center-of-momentum frame.",
          "deps": [
            "def-sr-total-four-momentum-and-invariant-mass",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion",
            "lem-sr-center-of-momentum-frame",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-sr-total-four-momentum-and-invariant-mass": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-future-causal-sums-and-inertial-coordinate-criterion": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-center-of-momentum-frame": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-sr-energy-momentum-observer-identities": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Dynamics and Relativity, Chapter 7 Special Relativity lecture notes",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/dynamics-and-relativity/dynrel7.pdf"
