        },
        {
          "id": "lem-rpm-charge-gauge-boundary",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Gauge boundary term and kinetic/canonical momentum",
          "statement": "Let $O$ be a potential domain with a $C^2$ metric $g$, a $C^2$ one-form $A$, and $F=dA$. Let $\\chi\\in C^3(O,\\mathbb R)$ and set $A^\\chi:=A+d\\chi$. For $a<b$, any $C^2$ future-timelike curve segment $z:[a,b]\\to O$ parameterized by proper time $\\tau$ so $g(z',z')=-c^2$ for $c>0$, define its four-velocity by $U:=dz/d\\tau=z'$. For constants $m>0$ and $q\\in\\mathbb R$ and the functional\n\n$$S_A[z]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(z',z') }\\,d\\tau+q\\int_a^b A(z')\\,d\\tau,$$\n\nthe field strength is unchanged, $dA^\\chi=dA$, and\n\n$$S_{A^\\chi}[z]-S_A[z]=q\\bigl(\\chi(z(b))-\\chi(z(a))\\bigr).$$\n\nThus the difference is constant over every fixed-endpoint variation, so stationarity and the kinetic covector $p:=mU^\\flat$ are gauge invariant. If the local covector $\\pi_A:=p+qA$ is defined, then $\\pi_{A^\\chi}=\\pi_A+q\\,d\\chi$. These are local statements on $O$; they assert no global exactness of $F$.",
          "deps": [
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials",
            "thm-chain-rule",
            "thm-ftc-second-part"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted Chapter 1 lecture-note excerpt",
                "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr1.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 55-page Chapter 1 excerpt; §1.1 fixed-endpoint Euler–Lagrange variation and §1.2.3, especially equation (1.21), printed pp. 8–28. The curved local variation is proved from inspected mathematical suppliers."
              },
              {
                "title": "Eric Poisson, Adam Pound and Ian Vega, The Motion of Point Particles in Curved Spacetime, author manuscript arXiv:1102.0529v3 (2011)",
