      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Cotangent phase data, observables and action dimensions",
          "statement": "Assume countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$: every sequence of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary, of dimension $n$. Its cotangent bundle is\n\n$$T^*Q=\\coprod_{q\\in Q}T_q^*Q,\\qquad \\pi:T^*Q\\to Q,\\quad \\pi(q,p)=q,$$\n\nwith the canonical smooth structure and induced cotangent charts supplied by the manifold construction in [[def-tautological-one-form-on-a-cotangent-bundle]]. The **tautological one-form** is $\\theta_{(q,p)}(\\xi)=p(d\\pi_{(q,p)}\\xi)$, and the **canonical cotangent two-form** is $\\omega=-d\\theta$. The exact theorem [[thm-the-canonical-cotangent-two-form-is-symplectic]] makes $(T^*Q,\\omega)$ a symplectic manifold in the sense of [[def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold]]. We call this pair the **cotangent phase space** of $Q$.\n\nIn an induced chart $(q^1,\\ldots,q^n,p_1,\\ldots,p_n)$, $\\theta=p_i\\,dq^i$ and $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$. A mathematical **observable** on this phase space means a smooth function $F:T^*Q\\to\\mathbb R$; this definition does not assert that a measurement has been performed or that every such function is physically measurable.\n\nFor an interpreted particle model, assign coordinates $q^i$ their declared units and assign the conjugate covector components $p_i$ units of action divided by the units of $q^i$. Thus $\\theta$ and $\\omega$ have action units, conventionally joule-seconds, and a bracket of quantities with units $[F]$ and $[G]$ has units $[F][G]/(\\text{action})$. These are dimensional interpretations of the model, not additional mathematical premises. The use of $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$ is exactly the use in the smooth cotangent-bundle suppliers; no full Axiom of Choice is used.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-tautological-one-form-on-a-cotangent-bundle",
            "thm-the-canonical-cotangent-two-form-is-symplectic",
            "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tautological-one-form-on-a-cotangent-bundle": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-the-canonical-cotangent-two-form-is-symplectic": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Eckhard Meinrenken, Symplectic Geometry, complete lecture notes",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250806200149if_/https://www.math.utoronto.ca/mein/teaching/LectureNotes/symplectic.pdf",
                "locator": "§3.4, Definition 3.10, printed pp. 34–35: tautological cotangent one-form."
              },
              {
                "title": "Ana Cannas da Silva, Lectures on Symplectic Geometry, complete lecture notes",
                "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20120413034139if_/http://www.math.ist.utl.pt:80/%7Eacannas/Books/symplectic.pdf",
                "locator": "Lecture 1, printed pp. 11–12: cotangent bundle canonical symplectic form."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Mathematical cotangent phase-space conventions; action units are interpretations assigned by a particle model, with no dynamics or measurement asserted."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Temporal chart with lapse, shift and positive slice metric",
          "statement": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, with the geometric meaning fixed by [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the future-cone field called $N$ in that definition. A **temporal product chart** is a smooth coordinate map from an open product $I\\times\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3$ onto an open subset of $M$, with coordinates $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$. We require $x^0$ to have length units and each $x^i$ to have length units in the physical interpretation.\n\nThe chart has **lapse-shift data** $(N,\\beta,h)$ when $N:I\\times\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$, $\\beta^i:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$, and $h_{ij}:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ are smooth, $h_{ij}=h_{ji}$, and the matrix $(h_{ij})$ is positive definite at each point, with\n\n$$g=-N^2(dx^0)^2+h_{ij}(dx^i+\\beta^i dx^0)(dx^j+\\beta^j dx^0).$$\n\nWrite $h^{ij}$ for the inverse matrix. The normal field is\n\n$$n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i).$$\n\nDirect contraction gives $g(n,n)=-1$; require that $n$ is future-directed in the supplied time orientation. This field of observers is distinct from the coordinate vector $\\partial_0$, which may be spacelike. The coefficients $N,\\beta^i,h_{ij}$ are dimensionless when all four coordinates are in metres. This definition is local to the specified product chart; it asserts no global temporal foliation.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Eric Gourgoulhon, 3+1 Formalism and Bases of Numerical Relativity, complete lecture notes (arXiv:gr-qc/0703035)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/0703035",
                "locator": "Complete 220-page notes; §§3.3.1–3.3.3, printed pp. 41–44, and §§4.2.2–4.2.3, pp. 56–58; full relevant sections inspected."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Local lapse-shift chart and observer split for particle models; no global foliation or empirical claim is made."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-rpm-h-almost-regular-legendre",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Regular, hyperregular and almost-regular Legendre domains",
          "statement": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $\\mathcal D$ be an open subset of the time-dependent tangent bundle $\\mathbb R\\times TQ$ of a finite-dimensional smooth manifold $Q$, and let $L:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth. For fixed $t$, the **fibre derivative** is the map\n\n$$\\mathbb F L(t,q,v)=(t,q,p),\\qquad p(w)=\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}\\right|_{s=0}L(t,q,v+sw),$$\n\nas in [[def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian]]. In induced coordinates $p_i=\\partial L/\\partial v^i$.\n\nThe Lagrangian is **regular** at $(t,q,v)$ when the velocity Hessian $(\\partial^2L/\\partial v^i\\partial v^j)$ is invertible there; regularity gives only a local inverse branch. It is **hyperregular on a declared domain** when $\\mathbb F L$ is a diffeomorphism from that entire domain onto an open subset of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$.\n\nIt is **almost regular on a declared open domain $\\mathcal D_0\\subseteq\\mathcal D$**, with the inherited open-submanifold structure, when the following are all assumed: $\\mathbb F L|_{\\mathcal D_0}$ has constant rank; its image $N$ is an embedded submanifold of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$; its corestriction $\\mathcal D_0\\to N$ is a surjective submersion (its differential is surjective at every point); and every fibre of that corestriction is connected. These hypotheses are not consequences of singularity alone.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
            "def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Andrew Hanson, Tullio Regge and Claudio Teitelboim, Constrained Hamiltonian Systems, Part 1, Chapter I",
                "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstreams/0e1d11ee-2741-4da4-83ed-b7cabc0cd7d6/download",
                "locator": "Printed pp. 7–9: singular Lagrangians, primary constraints and the failure of unique velocity inversion."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Mathematical regularity package for Lagrangian models; it does not assert that a particular physical Lagrangian satisfies the package."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Lorentz background, particle quantities and parameter units",
          "statement": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nFix a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,T)$ with signature $(-+++)$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the supplied smooth timelike field selecting the future cone, called $N$ in that geometric definition. Use the distinction among a spacetime, a worldline, a pointwise observer, an observer congruence, a tetrad and a chart given in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. This definition does not make $g$ dynamical.\n\nIn this relativistic-particle model, $m>0$ is a fixed **primitive mass parameter** measured in kilograms, and $c>0$ is a fixed **primitive conversion parameter** measured in metres per second. They are parameters of the adopted physical formulation, not quantities derived by this definition. Coordinates $x^a$ in an SI chart have length units; a cotangent vector $p=p_a\\,dx^a$ has units of momentum, $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, when all four coordinates are in metres. The canonical one-form $p_a\\,dx^a$ therefore has action units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Along a future-directed timelike curve with tangent $V=dx/d\\lambda$, set $s:=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$; then $d\\tau=s\\,d\\lambda/c$, independently of the increasing regular parameter. The length action $-mc\\int s\\,d\\lambda$ has action units. For a future unit observer $n$ and future causal covector $p$, define the model normal-observer energy and spatial momentum covector by\n\n$$E_n(p):=-c\\,p(n),\\qquad q_n(p):=p+p(n)n^\\flat,$$\n\nwhere $n^\\flat=g(n,\\cdot)$. The restriction of $g^{-1}$ to covectors annihilating $n$ is positive definite, so it supplies the norm of $q_n(p)$. These are quantity definitions and unit conventions; they do not identify canonical momentum with mechanical momentum, assert that an apparatus measured these values, or establish a dynamical law.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
            "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019",
                "locator": "Complete 238-page notes; Chapter 1, printed pp. 25–27, and Chapter 3, printed pp. 68–72; full relevant sections inspected."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "physical_scope": "Classical spinless test-particle quantities on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold. Mass and c are primitive parameters of this model; no particle action, force law, backreaction, empirical result, or universal identification with another framework is included.",
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Regular primary constraints and total generator",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$ and let $(M^{2n},\\omega)$ be a smooth symplectic manifold. A finite family of smooth functions $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_r$ defined on an open neighborhood $U\\subseteq M$ is a **regular primary constraint family** on\n\n$$C:=\\{z\\in U:\\chi_1(z)=\\cdots=\\chi_r(z)=0\\}$$\n\nwhen $d\\chi_1(z),\\ldots,d\\chi_r(z)$ are linearly independent at every $z\\in C$. The regular-level theorem [[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] then gives $C$ its embedded-submanifold structure and $T_zC=\\bigcap_a\\ker d\\chi_a(z)$. For functions $F,G$ on $U$, write $F\\approx G$ when $F|_C=G|_C$; thus $F\\approx0$ means that $F$ vanishes on $C$, without requiring it to vanish off $C$.\n\nGiven a smooth canonical Hamiltonian $H_c$ on $U$ and multiplier functions $u^a(t)$, the **total generator** is\n\n$$H_T(t,z):=H_c(t,z)+\\sum_{a=1}^r u^a(t)\\chi_a(z).$$\n\nThe functions $u^a$ are independent parameter functions; the definition does not assert that their equations determine them. If $H_c$ is an energy and $\\chi_a$ has units $[\\chi_a]$, then $u^a$ has units energy divided by $[\\chi_a]$, so every term in $H_T$ has the same units. A later constraint, if generated by consistency, is not declared primary by this definition.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
            "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold",
            "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Andrew Hanson, Tullio Regge and Claudio Teitelboim, Constrained Hamiltonian Systems, Part 1, Chapter I",
                "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstreams/0e1d11ee-2741-4da4-83ed-b7cabc0cd7d6/download",
                "locator": "Printed pp. 7–10: weak equality, primary and secondary constraints, multipliers and consistency."
              },
              {
                "title": "Ghanashyam Date, Lectures on Constrained Systems, §§3–4",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.2062",
                "locator": "Printed pp. 12–22: regular constraint surfaces, multiplier consistency and first/second-class classification."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "library": "physics",
          "physical_scope": "Mathematical local constraint-surface and total-generator conventions; no dynamics or physical constraint model is adopted."
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Canonical Poisson brackets and local Hamiltonian flow",
          "statement": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
            "thm-smooth-functions-form-a-poisson-algebra",
            "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-smooth-functions-form-a-poisson-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Andrew Hanson, Tullio Regge and Claudio Teitelboim, Constrained Hamiltonian Systems, Part 1",
                "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstreams/0e1d11ee-2741-4da4-83ed-b7cabc0cd7d6/download",
                "locator": "Chapter I, printed pp. 7–11: canonical Hamiltonian conventions in constrained mechanics."
              },
              {
                "title": "Ghanashyam Date, Lectures on Constrained Systems, arXiv:1010.2062",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1010.2062",
                "locator": "§§3–4, printed pp. 12–22: phase-space Poisson bracket convention."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Mathematical cotangent phase space; physical dimensions are interpreted only when a model assigns units to coordinates and observables.",
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "post-rpm-h-free-test-particle-action",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Free massive test-particle action",
          "statement": "For a $C^2$ future-directed timelike worldline $x:I\\to M$ on a prescribed smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,T)$ of signature $(-+++)$, with $m>0$ a fixed primitive mass in kg and $c>0$ in m/s, and any compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$, adopt the stationary-action model on that segment\n\n$$S_{m,[a,b]}[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x',x') }\\,d\\lambda.$$\n\nHere $x$ is $C^2$ on a neighborhood of $[a,b]$ (so its tangent is $C^1$ there), and $\\lambda$ is an increasing regular parameter. An admissible variation is a one-parameter family $x_\\epsilon(\\lambda)$ that is $C^2$ jointly in $(\\epsilon,\\lambda)$, has fixed endpoint events $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$, and remains future timelike for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. The integrand is the proper-time length density, so $S_{m,[a,b]}=-mc^2\\int_a^b d\\tau$; its dimension is action (J s). The adopted premise concerns this ideal free test body only. It is neither a theorem about all matter nor an empirical report, and supplies no backreaction or completeness claim. The flat ideal-clock specialization agrees with [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; adopting this action on a curved background remains a separate premise.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice ACω only; full choice is not assumed.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Andrew Hanson, Tullio Regge and Claudio Teitelboim, Constrained Hamiltonian Systems, Part 1, complete monograph",
                "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstreams/0e1d11ee-2741-4da4-83ed-b7cabc0cd7d6/download",
                "locator": "Complete 35-page Part 1; Chapter II, printed pp. 27–30, relativistic spinless point particle."
              },
              {
                "title": "B. F. Chagas-Filho, Free massless particles and extended space-time algebra, comparison for the distinct massive square-root action",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0401091",
                "locator": "§1, pp. 1–2."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "physical_scope": "Classical spinless structureless point test body with fixed primitive mass m>0 on a prescribed background; excludes self-field, radiation reaction, finite size, spin and quantum effects.",
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "post-rpm-h-massless-shell-action",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Separate massless shell action",
          "statement": "On a prescribed background $(M,g,T)$, choose an open parameter interval $I$ and a compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$. In a spacetime chart containing the curve and sufficiently small variations, let $x:I\\to M$ be smooth near $[a,b]$, let $p(\\lambda)\\in T^*_{x(\\lambda)}M$ be a smooth covector field along $x$, and let $e$ be smooth and positive near $[a,b]$. Adopt the distinct massless classical particle action on this segment\n\n$$S_{0,[a,b]}[x,p,e]=\\int_a^b\\left[p_a x'^a-eC_0(x,p)\\right]d\\lambda,\\qquad C_0=\\tfrac12g^{ab}p_ap_b.$$\n\nAdmissible variations are smooth one-parameter families of chart fields $(x_\\epsilon,p_\\epsilon,e_\\epsilon)$ on a common neighborhood of $[a,b]$, with $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$; the $p_\\epsilon$ and $e_\\epsilon$ variations are independent and have no endpoint constraints, while $e_\\epsilon>0$ for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. Impose the physical branch only on stationary solutions after these variations: $p\\ne0$ and $p^\\sharp$ is future null. With all spacetime coordinates measured in metres and dimensionless $\\lambda$, $p_a$ has units kg m/s, $C_0$ has units kg²m²/s² and $e$ has units s/kg, making the action J s. Other parameter units scale $e$ accordingly. This is a separate adopted model: null curves have no proper-time parameter, the model does not arise by setting $m=0$ in $P=mU$, and its positive momentum scale is additional initial data. On the nonzero branch, the vertical differential is $d_pC_0=p^\\sharp\\ne0$: nondegeneracy of $g^{-1}$ and $p\\ne0$ give $p^\\sharp\\ne0$. Hence the shell is a regular primary constraint in the sense of [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]]. This postulate is not an empirical observation or a deduction from the massive model.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice ACω only; full choice is not assumed.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
            "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "W. F. Chagas-Filho, Free massless particles and extended space-time algebra, complete paper",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0401091",
                "locator": "§§1–2, pp. 1–3, einbein action and massless constraint."
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean M. Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes (arXiv:gr-qc/9712019v1)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019",
                "locator": "Complete 238-page notes; Chapter 1, printed pp. 25–27, and Chapter 3, printed pp. 68–72; full relevant sections inspected."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "physical_scope": "Separate classical massless test-particle formulation with nonzero future-null momentum; no proper-time parameter, no mU identification and no empirical realization claim.",
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-rpm-h-constraint-consistency",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Constraint consistency for a regular first-order model",
          "statement": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open interval and $(P,\\omega)$ a symplectic manifold. Let $\\chi_a:I\\times P\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth and suppose the covectors $d_z\\chi_a(\\lambda,z)$ are linearly independent at every $z\\in N_\\lambda:=\\{z:\\chi_1(\\lambda,z)=\\cdots=\\chi_r(\\lambda,z)=0\\}$; this is the regular constraint condition of [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]] on each slice. Let $H_c:I\\times P\\to\\mathbb R$ be a smooth ambient extension of a specified descended Hamiltonian. Work in a Darboux chart with coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$ and local potential $\\theta=p_i\\,dq^i$, so $d\\theta=-\\omega$ under the convention $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$; paths stay in this chart.\nFor any compact interval $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$, take paths $z:[a,b]\\to P$ that extend smoothly to a neighborhood of $[a,b]$ and stay in the chart, and multipliers $u^a$ that extend smoothly to a neighborhood of $[a,b]$. Their action is\n\n$$I[z,u]=\\int_a^b\\left[\\theta(\\dot z)-H_c(\\lambda,z)-u^a(\\lambda)\\chi_a(\\lambda,z)\\right]d\\lambda.$$\n\nUse smooth one-parameter families of such $z$ with fixed endpoints, whose paths remain in the chart, and unrestricted smooth one-parameter variations of $u$. These classes satisfy the $C^2$ path and fixed-endpoint hypotheses of [[thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange]]. They imply $z(\\lambda)\\in N_\\lambda$ and $\\dot z=X_{H_c(\\lambda,\\cdot)}+u^a(\\lambda)X_{\\chi_a(\\lambda,\\cdot)}$. A trajectory remains in the moving level set only when\n\n$$\\partial_\\lambda\\chi_a+\\{\\chi_a,H_c(\\lambda,\\cdot)\\}+u^b(\\lambda)\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}=0\\quad\\text{on }N_\\lambda.$$\n\nFor constraints independent of $\\lambda$, the first term is zero.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
            "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system",
            "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
