        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-h-free-relativistic-complete-integral",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Explicit massive and nonzero-null free complete integral",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the cotangent-shell supplier. Work with spatial position $q\\in\\mathbb R^3$ in metres and momentum parameter $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R^3$ in kg m/s, with the Euclidean dot product and norm $|\\alpha|=\\sqrt{\\alpha\\cdot\\alpha}$; at fixed time the spatial phase space has canonical form $\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^3dq^i\\wedge dp_i$. Here $t\\in\\mathbb R$ is measured in seconds, $m\\ge0$ is in kg, and $c>0$ is in m/s. The parameter domain is all $\\mathbb R^3_\\alpha$ when $m>0$, and $\\mathbb R^3_\\alpha\\setminus\\{0\\}$ when $m=0$. For a free massive particle with $m>0$, or a nonzero massless particle with $m=0$ and $\\alpha\\ne0$, define\n\n$$S(t,q,\\alpha)=q\\cdot\\alpha-tE(\\alpha),\\qquad E(\\alpha)=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\alpha|^2}.$$\n\nThen $S_t+E(S_q)=0$, $S_{q\\alpha}=I$, and its integration coordinates satisfy $\\beta=S_\\alpha=q-tc^2\\alpha/E$, equivalently $q=\\beta+t c^2\\alpha/E$. The mixed Hessian is everywhere nonsingular on the stated domain, so this is a complete integral there. $[S]=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $[\\alpha]=\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}$, and $[\\beta]=\\mathrm m$.",
          "deps": [
            "thm-rpm-h-complete-integral-coordinates",
            "thm-rpm-h-inertial-time-reduced-hamiltonian",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-rpm-h-complete-integral-coordinates": "mathematical-premise",
