            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice for the canonical tangent-bundle smooth structure supplied by the GR items below. Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold with its metric-compatible connection $\\nabla$, let $c>0$, and let $z:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve parametrized by proper time $\\tau$, with $U=D_\\tau z$ and $g(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $F_\\tau$ be a continuous alternating two-form along $z$. Define $F^\\sharp_\\tau$ by\n$$g(F^\\sharp_\\tau(v),w)=F_\\tau(w,v).$$\nThen $F^\\sharp_\\tau$ is skew-adjoint with respect to $g$.\n\nFor constants $\\alpha,a,b\\in\\mathbb R$, consider the restricted linear transport equations\n$$D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U),\\qquad D_\\tau s=\\alpha\\left(aF^\\sharp(s)+\\frac{b}{c^2}U\\,\\kappa\\right),\\qquad \\kappa:=g(s,F^\\sharp(U)).$$\nFor every initial $s(\\tau_0)$ there is a unique solution on each compact subinterval of $I$. Along any such solution,\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}g(U,s)=\\alpha(1-a-b)\\kappa.$$\nConsequently, the orthogonality constraint $g(U,s)=0$ is preserved when $a+b=1$, and on that constraint $g(s,s)$ is constant. For the coefficient comparison, fix a parameter value $\\tau$ and put $n:=U(\\tau)/c$. Then $n$ is an observer at $z(\\tau)$ and its rest space is the three-dimensional positive-definite subspace $E_\\tau:=n^\\perp=U(\\tau)^\\perp$ (the observer and projection conventions are those of [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]] and [[lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition]]). Choose an orientation of $E_\\tau$ and a positively oriented orthonormal basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$; set $e_0:=n$. For $v\\in E_\\tau$, write $\\mathbf v=(g(v,e_1),g(v,e_2),g(v,e_3))$, use $(\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v)_i=\\epsilon_{ijk}u_jv_k$, and define $\\mathbf B$ by $F_{ij}:=F(e_i,e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. Let $P_U(v):=v+g(U,v)U/c^2$, the projection onto $E_\\tau$. The rest-space matching condition means the pointwise equality\n$$\\bigl(P_U(D_\\tau s)\\bigr)^i=\\alpha G\\,(\\mathbf s\\times\\mathbf B)_i\\qquad(i=1,2,3)$$\nfor every $\\mathbf s,\\mathbf B$, when $s(\\tau)\\in E_\\tau$. Thus it compares spatial components of the covariant derivative at the event, not the derivative of components in an unspecified moving tetrad. For $\\alpha\\ne0$, if the coefficients both preserve $g(U,s)=0$ for every initially orthogonal datum and every allowed alternating field and satisfy this matching condition, then $a=G$ and $b=1-G$. Conversely, these coefficients satisfy both requirements. This uniqueness is only within the displayed two-term ansatz; no uniqueness among all covariant spin-transport laws is asserted. If $\\alpha=0$, the rest-space matching condition does not identify $a$ or $b$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
