            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Fix $c>0$ and a future unit observer $n$ in an affine Minkowski spacetime as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. Identify the positive-definite rest space $n^\\perp$ isometrically with $\\mathbb R^3$, with its Euclidean norm $|\\cdot|$ and Lebesgue measure $d^3x$. Define the closed Euclidean ball by\n$$\\overline B_\\ell(0):=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:|x|\\le\\ell\\}.$$\nLet $e:\\mathbb R^3\\to[0,\\infty)$ be measurable with $e\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$, and let $j:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be measurable with each component $j_i\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ for $i=1,2,3$. Thus $j$ is vector-valued integrable and every vector integral below is the componentwise Lebesgue integral. Assume $e$ and $j$ are both zero almost everywhere outside $\\overline B_\\ell(0)$ for some $\\ell>0$, and that $|j(x)|\\le e(x)/c$ almost everywhere. Define\n$$E_0:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e(x)\\,d^3x>0,\\qquad M:=E_0/c^2,\\qquad \\mathbf s_*:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}x\\times j(x)\\,d^3x.$$\nThen $\\mathbf s_*$ is absolutely integrable and\n$$|\\mathbf s_*|\\le\\ell Mc,\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad \\ell\\ge\\frac{|\\mathbf s_*|}{Mc}.$$\nThe hypotheses are the full mathematical content of this estimate; it is not a bound for a quantum point particle or for a source whose energy density or momentum density fails them.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Section 2, dominant-energy-condition bound on the Møller radius; this item proves the stated integral inequality under its explicit pointwise hypotheses and restores SI units."
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-rpm-charge-multipole-remainder",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite-size charge multipole remainder",
          "reference": "items/lem-rpm-charge-multipole-remainder.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
            "def-l-one-of-a-measure",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn",
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-l-one-of-a-measure": "mathematical-premise",
            "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-integral-triangle-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Let $\\ell>0$, $z\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and define the closed Euclidean ball\n$$\\overline B_\\ell(z):=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^3:|y-z|\\le\\ell\\},$$\nusing the norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real and zero almost everywhere outside $\\overline B_\\ell(z)$. Write $\\|\\rho\\|_1:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|\\rho(y)|\\,d^3y$. Let $\\mathcal U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open with $\\overline B_\\ell(z)\\subset\\mathcal U$, and let $b:\\mathcal U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be $C^2$ for a finite integer $m\\ge1$. For each component $b_k$, let\n$$M_k:=\\sup_{x\\in\\overline B_\\ell(z)}\\|D^2b_k(x)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}<\\infty,$$\nwhere, for a real bilinear map $B:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$, define explicitly $\\|B\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}:=\\sup\\{|B[u,v]|:|u|\\le1,\\ |v|\\le1\\}$ using the Euclidean vector norm. This norm is finite, and the continuous Hessian coefficients on the compact ball make each $M_k$ finite. Bilinearity gives $|B[u,v]|\\le\\|B\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}|u||v|$ for all $u,v$. Define $Q:=\\int\\rho\\,d^3y$, $d:=\\int(y-z)\\rho(y)\\,d^3y$, and $\\mathcal B:=\\int\\rho(y)b(y)\\,d^3y$, with vector integrals taken componentwise. Then\n$$\\mathcal B_k=Q\\,b_k(z)+Db_k(z)d+R_k,\\qquad |R_k|\\le\\frac12M_k\\ell^2\\|\\rho\\|_1\\quad(k=1,\\ldots,m).$$\nThus every fixed component in a smooth finite-component trivialization has the stated charge multipole remainder. A tensor-valued component may be treated only after a fixed smooth trivialization over the ball; derivatives of that trivialization enter $M_k$, and no covariant tensor remainder without this data is asserted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Carnegie Mellon 21-269, Riemann integration notes",
                "url": "https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html",
                "locator": "Fundamental-theorem-of-calculus method used for the integral Taylor remainder; the item derives the bound directly."
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Exact flat centroid translation and disk",
          "reference": "items/lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space, with signature $(-+++)$, and fix $c>0$. Let $p$ be any future timelike vector and define $M:=\\sqrt{-\\eta(p,p)}/c>0$. Choose an inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] with $e_0=p/(Mc)$, so its ordered orthonormal basis is future and positively oriented and $p$ has components $(Mc,\\mathbf 0)$ in this frame. Its positive-definite rest space is $e_0^\\perp=\\operatorname{span}(e_1,e_2,e_3)$; use $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ for spatial components and the induced right-handed symbol $\\epsilon_{ijk}$. Let $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$ satisfy the momentum supplementary condition $S^{ab}p_b=0$, and set $s_*^k:=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{kij}S^{ij}$ in this oriented orthonormal rest-space basis. Every future unit observer has the components\n$$n=\\gamma\\left(e_0+\\sum_{i=1}^3\\frac{v^i}{c}e_i\\right),\\qquad \\gamma=(1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2},\\qquad |\\mathbf v|<c,$$\nby [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. For every such observer, define\n$$\\delta^a:=\\frac{S^{ab}n_b}{p\\cdot n},\\qquad S'^{ab}:=S^{ab}-(\\delta^ap^b-\\delta^bp^a).$$\nThen $p\\cdot n\\ne0$, $\\delta\\cdot p=\\delta\\cdot n=0$, and $S'^{ab}n_b=0$. If the representative point changes by $z\\mapsto z+\\delta$, the total angular momentum $J=z\\wedge p+S$ is unchanged by this stipulated spin-shift rule. In the rest space,\n$$\\boldsymbol\\delta=\\frac{\\mathbf s_*\\times\\mathbf v}{Mc^2},$$\nIf $\\mathbf s_*\\ne0$, the shifts of all future timelike observers fill the open disk in $\\mathbf s_*^\\perp$ of radius $|\\mathbf s_*|/(Mc)$; the boundary is a supremum and is not attained. If $\\mathbf s_*=0$, only the zero shift occurs. This is an exact affine-flat algebraic result.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Section 2, observer-dependent centroid shift, the centroid disk and the Møller radius; the result below restores SI units and treats timelike observers only."
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Differentiated SSC momentum–velocity identity",
          "reference": "items/lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
