            "thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Use the charged/spinning body-model conventions of the now-authored batch-24 carrier [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]]: a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, coordinates $x^0=ct$ in metres, $c>0$, and a $C^2$ future timelike representative curve $z:I\\to M$ parametrized by proper time $\\tau$ in seconds, with $U=dz/d\\tau$ and $g(U,U)=-c^2$. The actual supplier defines these quantities and the SI electromagnetic-tensor convention used here. Assume Countable Choice for the GR smooth-structure, convex-normal and parallel-transport suppliers used below.\n\n**(a) Flat isolated-body moments.** Choose an inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ and its chart $x:A\\to\\mathbb R^4$ as in [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]], with the SI coordinate convention $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]]. Thus $e_0$ is future unit timelike and $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is the positively oriented orthonormal spatial basis. Let $T$ be a $C^\\infty$ symmetric type-$(2,0)$ tensor field on the affine Minkowski spacetime $A$, whose chart components $T^{ab}$ are the model stress-energy densities with units $\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$ and have spatially compact support on every finite time slab. At a fixed time $t$, the spatial slice has coordinates $\\mathbf x=(x^1,x^2,x^3)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and Lebesgue measure $d^3x$; define\n$$p^a(t):=\\frac1c\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}T^{0a}(ct,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3x,$$\nand, for a representative point $z(t)$ on that time slice, define\n$$S_z^{ab}(t):=\\frac1c\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\left((x-z(t))^aT^{0b}(ct,\\mathbf x)-(x-z(t))^bT^{0a}(ct,\\mathbf x)\\right)d^3x.$$\nHere $(x-z(t))^0=0$ on the chosen slice, $p$ has units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, and $S_z$ has units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$. These are definitions of moments; no conservation is asserted by clause (a).\n\n**(b) Local curved-body moments.** Let $z\\in M$ and let $U_0$ be a convex normal neighborhood containing $z$. Let $\\Sigma\\subset U_0$ be a $C^1$ embedded spacelike hypersurface through $z$: each tangent space $T_x\\Sigma$ is three-dimensional and the restriction of $g_x$ to it is positive definite. Let $n_x$ be its continuous future unit normal, so $g_x(n_x,n_x)=-1$ and $g_x(n_x,w)=0$ for every $w\\in T_x\\Sigma$, and let $d\\Sigma$ be the positive volume measure induced by $g|_{T\\Sigma}$. Let $T$ be a smooth symmetric type-$(2,0)$ tensor field on $U_0$; only its restriction $T|_\\Sigma$ is used. With $(n_x)_b:=g_{bc}(x)n_x^c$, define\n$$\\operatorname{supp}_\\Sigma(T|_\\Sigma):=\\overline{\\{x\\in\\Sigma:T_x\\ne0\\}}^{\\,\\Sigma}.$$\nAssume this restricted support is a compact subset of $U_0$ and that all displayed integrands are absolutely integrable with respect to $d\\Sigma$. For every $x\\in\\Sigma$, let $v(x):=\\exp_z^{-1}(x)\\in T_zM$ be the unique joining position vector supplied by [[thm-gr-convex-normal-neighborhoods]] and [[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]]. Let $H_{x\\to z}:T_xM\\to T_zM$ be parallel transport along that unique geodesic, with existence, uniqueness and metric preservation as in [[lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed]]. Define $T^\\sharp(n_x)^a:=T^{ab}(x)(n_x)_b$ and\n$$p_z:=\\frac1c\\int_\\Sigma H_{x\\to z}\\!\\left(-T^\\sharp(n_x)\\right)d\\Sigma,\\qquad S_z:=\\frac1c\\int_\\Sigma v(x)\\wedge H_{x\\to z}\\!\\left(-T^\\sharp(n_x)\\right)d\\Sigma,$$\nwhere $(v\\wedge w)^{ab}:=v^aw^b-v^bw^a$ and the finite-dimensional vector and tensor integrals are taken componentwise in any basis of $T_zM$. The resulting $p_z$ and $S_z$ are a vector and an antisymmetric tensor at $z$; their units are $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$. “Support on $\\Sigma$” here means only compact support of the restriction $T|_\\Sigma$; it does not assert that the ambient smooth field $T$ has spacetime support contained in $\\Sigma$.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Section 2 defines momentum and angular momentum with respect to an observer-dependent centroid and explains the local normal-coordinate convention; the definition below fixes its own SI transport interface."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Definition of flat and local curved stress-energy moments for a smooth compact test body. It supplies moment and transport conventions only; it does not derive MPD equations, a multipole truncation, a matter constitutive law, self-gravity, or an empirical body model."
        },
        {
          "id": "post-rpm-mpd-truncation",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Pole-dipole and closed multipole test-body models",
          "reference": "items/post-rpm-mpd-truncation.md#Postulate",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors",
            "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time",
            "def-rpm-body-moments"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-body-moments": "formulation-prerequisite"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice as inherited from [[def-rpm-body-moments]]. For a neutral test body, let $z:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve parametrized by proper time, let $U=dz/d\\tau$, and let $p^a$ and $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$ be the momentum and spin moments of [[def-rpm-body-moments]]. Adopt the pole-dipole evolution equations\n$$D_\\tau p^a=-\\frac12 R^a{}_{bcd}U^bS^{cd},\\qquad D_\\tau S^{ab}=p^aU^b-p^bU^a.$$\nA spin-supplementary condition (SSC) is an additional equation $S^{ab}n_b=0$ for a specified future unit timelike field $n$ along $z$; this postulate does not select one SSC, and $p$ is an independently evolved moment rather than a primitive assumption $p=mU$.\n\nFor a retained quadrupole or higher-multipole model, adopt\n$$D_\\tau p^a=-\\frac12 R^a{}_{bcd}U^bS^{cd}+f_{\\rm extra}^a,\\qquad D_\\tau S^{ab}=p^aU^b-p^bU^a+N_{\\rm extra}^{ab},$$\nwhere $f_{\\rm extra}$ is a specified smooth force map along the state and $N_{\\rm extra}^{ab}=-N_{\\rm extra}^{ba}$ is a specified smooth torque map. A quadrupole convention that may be adopted is\n$$f_{\\rm quad}^a=-\\frac16J^{bcde}\\nabla^aR_{bcde},\\qquad N_{\\rm quad}^{ab}=\\frac43R^{[a}{}_{cde}J^{b]cde},$$\nwith $J^{bcde}$ satisfying the algebraic symmetries of the curvature tensor, carrying units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^4\\,s^{-2}}$, and brackets including the factor $1/2$. Any evolved $J$ or higher moment needs its own transport or constitutive law; without it the multipole equations are not a closed initial-value model.\n\nThe pole-dipole and multipole equations are adopted finite-dimensional truncations, not derivations from the moment integrals above. Their use as approximations for a material body is restricted to regimes in which its size is small compared with the relevant curvature and background-variation lengths and omitted moment effects are negligible on the stated finite interval. These conditions are qualitative applicability assumptions here: this postulate supplies no uniform error bound, no convergence of truncations, and no global existence claim. No experimental result or measurement is asserted.",
          "strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Section 3, equations (14)-(16), for the pole-dipole force/spin system and the need for an SSC; the quadrupole extension below is an explicit adopted closure convention."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adopted neutral pole-dipole and explicitly closed multipole test-body truncations on a prescribed smooth Lorentzian background. They exclude self-gravity, self-force, arbitrary stress-integral derivation, and any unqualified error or convergence theorem."
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-bmt-constraints",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "BMT orthogonality and conserved spin length",
          "reference": "items/pthm-rpm-bmt-constraints.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
            "lem-rpm-bmt-transport-algebra",
            "pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence",
            "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-rpm-bmt-transport-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence": "physical-result",
            "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]] for the canonical tangent-bundle and connection suppliers used by the BMT transport and charged-action equivalence. No full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nAdopt the charged-body quantities and SI tensor convention of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]] and [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], the polarization transport postulate [[post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model]], and its translational equation obtained from [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]]. On a local potential domain, assume the finite curve segments are stationary for the adopted action. Let $z$ be a $C^2$ future timelike proper-time curve, $U=dz/d\\tau$, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, with prescribed continuous alternating field $F$ along it. Let $m>0$, $q\\in\\mathbb R$, $g_s\\in\\mathbb R$, and an initial $C^1$ spin vector satisfy $g(s,U)=0$. Put $\\alpha=q/m$, $G=g_s/2$, and $\\kappa=g(s,F^\\sharp(U))$. The adopted equations and action result give\n\n$$D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U),\\qquad D_\\tau s=\\alpha\\left[G F^\\sharp(s)+(1-G)\\frac{U}{c^2}\\kappa\\right].$$\n\nThen $g(s,U)=0$ and $g(s,s)$ are constant along every solution. In the restricted ansatz $D_\\tau s=\\alpha[aF^\\sharp(s)+bU\\kappa/c^2]$, require preservation of orthogonality for every initially orthogonal datum and every allowed alternating field. For $\\alpha\\ne0$ this gives $a+b=1$. If, in addition, the pointwise projected rest-space matching condition of [[lem-rpm-bmt-transport-algebra]] holds with target coefficient $G$, that lemma gives $a=G$, and hence $b=1-G$. This coefficient conclusion is only within that ansatz and the stated joint conditions. If $\\alpha=0$, the rest-space matching condition does not identify $a,b$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "University of Virginia, Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation, Electromagnetic Theory lecture",
                "url": "https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/Elec_Mag/2022_Lectures/EM_71_g-2.html",
                "locator": "Section “Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation”, covariant four-spin constraint and coefficient determination; the conditional model proof is given locally."
              },
              {
                "title": "V. Bargmann, L. Michel and V. L. Telegdi, Precession of the Polarization of Particles Moving in a Homogeneous Electromagnetic Field, Physical Review Letters 2 (1959), 435–437",
                "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~vergne/LouisMichel/publications/PhysRevLett.2.435.pdf",
                "locator": "Both pages of the complete author-hosted paper; the flat homogeneous-field polarization law is source context, while the curved model remains an adopted postulate."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional constraint preservation and restricted-ansatz coefficient matching for the adopted classical BMT polarization model on a prescribed external field. No measured value of the gyromagnetic ratio, quantum-spin derivation, or empirical validation is asserted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Spherical charge exterior-energy size bound",
          "reference": "items/pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux",
            "def-em-si-conventions",
            "def-rpm-body-moments",
            "pthm-em-coulomb-solution",
            "pthm-em-point-self-energy-divergence",
            "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rpm-body-moments": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-em-coulomb-solution": "physical-result",
            "pthm-em-point-self-energy-divergence": "physical-result",
            "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Let an extended static spherical charged body have radius $\\ell>0$ metres, total charge $q\\in\\mathbb R$ coulombs, and finite total rest energy $Mc^2$ with $M>0$ kilograms. Use the flat-body moment convention of [[def-rpm-body-moments]] in a momentum-rest inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]], with $p=Mc\\,e_0$ and $o$ the stipulated center of the spherical body. Its rest spatial chart has coordinates $\\mathbf x=(x^1,x^2,x^3)$; define $r:=|\\mathbf x|:=\\sqrt{(x^1)^2+(x^2)^2+(x^3)^2}$, $\\widehat{\\mathbf r}:=\\mathbf x/r$ for $r>0$, and $\\overline B_\\ell(0):=\\{\\mathbf x:r\\le\\ell\\}$. The body support lies in $\\overline B_\\ell(0)$, and $\\ell$ is the radius of the specified exterior region. Use the SI constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]], including $c$ in metres per second and $\\varepsilon_0>0$. On the open exterior spatial region $\\Omega_\\ell:=\\{\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^3:r>\\ell\\}$, the static field is assumed to obey\n$$\\mathbf E(\\mathbf x)=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0r^2}\\widehat{\\mathbf r},\\qquad \\mathbf B=0.$$\nLet $u(\\mathbf x)$ be the time-independent vacuum energy density of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]] on this exterior region and define $E_{\\rm ext}:=\\int_{\\Omega_\\ell}u(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3x$. Assume the total rest energy is the sum of this exterior field energy and a remaining material-plus-interior contribution $E_{\\rm rem}\\ge0$. Then\n$$Mc^2\\ge\\frac{q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\ell},\\qquad \\ell\\ge\\frac{q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0Mc^2}.$$\nThe exact Coulomb exterior and nonnegative energy accounting are hypotheses. The theorem does not infer an exterior field from spherical symmetry alone. No identification of field energy with an ideal point particle's rest mass is made.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Electrodynamics, §1.3 Scalar Potential and Electric Field Energy",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Electricity_and_Magnetism/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Classical_Electrodynamics_%28Likharev%29/01%3A_Electric_Charge_Interaction/1.03%3A_Scalar_Potential_and_Electric_Field_Energy",
                "locator": "Full HTML section §1.3, Eqs. (1.34)–(1.35) Coulomb field/potential and (1.65)–(1.68) field energy with a uniform sphere's interior/exterior calculation; the point-charge interpretation is context only."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A conditional static spherical-body energy inequality when its vacuum exterior is exactly Coulomb and its total rest energy includes that exterior field energy plus nonnegative remaining energy. It is not a universal radius theorem for arbitrary charge distributions, and it makes no mass inference for an ideal point charge.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-hidden-momentum",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "SSC choices and hidden momentum",
          "reference": "items/pthm-rpm-hidden-momentum.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "def-rpm-body-moments",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
            "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors",
            "lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity",
            "post-rpm-mpd-truncation"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-rpm-body-moments": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-rpm-mpd-truncation": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "statement": "Assume the neutral pole-dipole equations adopted in [[post-rpm-mpd-truncation]] for the local moments of [[def-rpm-body-moments]]: along a $C^2$ future timelike proper-time curve $z$, with $U=dz/d\\tau$, $p$ future timelike and $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$,\n$$D_\\tau p^a=-\\frac12R^a{}_{bcd}U^bS^{cd},\\qquad D_\\tau S^{ab}=p^aU^b-p^bU^a.$$\nFor any $C^1$ future unit observer field $n$ along the curve satisfying the SSC $S^{ab}n_b=0$, put $\\gamma=-g(n,U)/c>0$ and $E_n=-cg(p,n)$. Then\n$$c\\gamma p^a=\\frac{E_n}{c}U^a+S^{ab}D_\\tau n_b.$$\nIn the Pirani branch $n=U/c$, define $m=-g(p,U)/c^2>0$ and $a=D_\\tau U$; then\n$$p^a=mU^a+\\frac{1}{c^2}S^{ab}a_b.$$\nIn the parallel-observer (OKS) branch $D_\\tau n=0$, $p$ is a positive scalar multiple of $U$. In the TD branch $n=p/\\mu$, $\\mu=\\sqrt{-g(p,p)}$, the derivative term generally remains and the identity gives $mc^2p=\\mu^2U+S D_\\tau p$; it does not by itself imply $p\\parallel U$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Sections 2 and 3, especially equation (16), the general momentum–velocity identity and Pirani, OKS and TD supplementary-condition branches; the SI and sign conventions are stated explicitly here."
              },
              {
                "title": "University of Virginia, Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation, Electromagnetic Theory lecture",
                "url": "https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/Elec_Mag/2022_Lectures/EM_71_g-2.html",
                "locator": "Full relevant BMT lecture section read; it does not establish the extended-body SSC identities, which are proved conditionally from the MPD postulate and local lemma."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional momentum–velocity identities for the adopted neutral pole-dipole test-body equations under Pirani, parallel-observer (OKS), and Tulczyjew–Dixon supplementary conditions. The identity distinguishes the momentum, curve tangent, and observer; no universal identification of momentum with mass times velocity is asserted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-rest-spin-and-size-bound",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Rest-frame spin transport and finite-body size bounds",
          "reference": "items/pthm-rpm-rest-spin-and-size-bound.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-rpm-body-moments",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "lem-rpm-bmt-transport-algebra",
            "lem-rpm-body-spin-size-bound",
            "lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift",
            "pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence",
            "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
            "thm-sr-proper-orthochronous-lorentz-algebra"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-body-moments": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rpm-bmt-transport-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rpm-body-spin-size-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence": "physical-result",
            "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model": "physical-assumption",
            "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sr-proper-orthochronous-lorentz-algebra": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]] for the canonical tangent-bundle and connection suppliers used by the BMT transport and charged-action equivalence. No full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nAdopt the charged-body quantities of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]], the SI field convention of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], and the classical BMT law [[post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model]]. For a trajectory stationary under the local action postulate, the translational force equation is supplied by [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]]. In flat Minkowski spacetime let $m>0$, $q,g_s\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\alpha=q/m$, $G=g_s/2$, and let $U,s$ satisfy\n$$D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U),\\qquad D_\\tau s=\\alpha\\left[GF^\\sharp(s)-(G-1)\\frac{U}{c^2}g(s,F^\\sharp(U))\\right],\\qquad g(U,U)=-c^2,\\quad g(U,s)=0.$$\nThe tensor equation is covariant under proper orthochronous Lorentz transformations of the complete field and worldline data. If $F=0$, then $D_\\tau U=0$ and $D_\\tau s=0$. For any acceleration $a=D_\\tau U$ within the Lorentz-force model, the $G=0$ branch is Fermi–Walker transport on the orthogonal spin subspace:\n$$D_\\tau s=\\frac{1}{c^2}\\bigl(Ug(a,s)-a g(U,s)\\bigr)=\\frac{U}{c^2}g(a,s).$$\nFor the uniform-field branch, choose an inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] with $e_0=U/c$ for the stationary trajectory and with the positively oriented orthonormal spatial basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$. Let $\\mathbf E=0$ and let the constant spatial field be $\\mathbf B\\ne0$; its norm and cross product are those of the frame's positive-definite spatial structure. Then $U=(c,\\mathbf0)$ is stationary, $\\widehat{\\mathbf B}:=\\mathbf B/|\\mathbf B|$, and for $\\mathbf s_0:=\\mathbf s(\\tau_0)$ define $\\mathbf s_{\\parallel,0}:=(\\mathbf s_0\\cdot\\widehat{\\mathbf B})\\widehat{\\mathbf B}$ and $\\mathbf s_{\\perp,0}:=\\mathbf s_0-\\mathbf s_{\\parallel,0}$. The $G$ branch has the exact solution\n$$\\mathbf s(\\tau)=\\mathbf s_{\\parallel,0}+\\cos(\\omega\\Delta\\tau)\\mathbf s_{\\perp,0}-\\sin(\\omega\\Delta\\tau)\\widehat{\\mathbf B}\\times\\mathbf s_{\\perp,0},\\qquad \\omega=\\frac{qG}{m}|\\mathbf B|,\\quad \\Delta\\tau=\\tau-\\tau_0.$$\nThe $F=0$, $G=0$, and uniform-field claims are deductions within the adopted model, not experimental statements. For the independent finite-body branch, use the moment and unit convention of [[def-rpm-body-moments]]; the density-to-spin identity below is stipulated data.\n\nSeparately, let a flat body have rest momentum $p$, and define $M:=\\sqrt{-g(p,p)}/c>0$. Choose its momentum-rest inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] with $e_0=p/(Mc)$ and spatial origin at the stipulated body center. Define $\\overline B_\\ell(0):=\\{\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^3:|\\mathbf x|\\le\\ell\\}$ using the Euclidean norm of its positive-definite spatial rest space. In this frame $p$ has components $(Mc,\\mathbf0)$; let $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$ be its angular-momentum tensor, with $S^{0i}=0$ (hence $S^{ab}p_b=0$), and let $e:\\mathbb R^3\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $j:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be measurable rest densities, with each component of $j$ in $L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$; their units are joules per cubic metre and kilogram per square metre per second, respectively. Assume $e$ and $j$ vanish almost everywhere outside $\\overline B_\\ell(0)$, $|j|$ is the Euclidean norm and satisfies $|j|\\le e/c$ almost everywhere, $\\int e\\,d^3x=Mc^2$, and the stipulated componentwise spin-density identity\n$$\\mathbf s_*:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\mathbf x\\times j(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3x=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{kij}S^{ij}\\,\\mathbf e_k,$$\nwhere the vector integral is componentwise in the positively oriented spatial basis of this inertial frame.\nThen\n$$\\ell\\ge\\frac{|\\mathbf s_*|}{Mc},\\qquad |\\boldsymbol\\delta|<\\frac{|\\mathbf s_*|}{Mc}$$\nfor every observer-induced centroid shift $\\boldsymbol\\delta$ of the flat SSC translation law; the disk radius is a supremum, not an attained boundary for a timelike observer. These rest-body quantities are Lorentz invariant under transforming the complete body data and its momentum-rest hypersurface. No equality between $\\mathbf s$ and $\\mathbf s_*$ is assumed, so the size inequalities do not bound the BMT polarization unless such an identification is separately adopted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "University of Virginia, Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation, Electromagnetic Theory lecture",
                "url": "https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/Elec_Mag/2022_Lectures/EM_71_g-2.html",
                "locator": "Full section “Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation,” including its rest-frame torque, covariant equation and coefficient matching; the exact conditional branches are derived locally."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Section 2, observer-dependent centroid shifts, the centroid disk and the Møller radius; the positive-energy size estimate is proved here under its exact integral hypotheses."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional consequences of the adopted classical BMT polarization law in free and uniform-rest-magnetic-field branches, together with separate flat-body centroid and positive-energy size bounds. The BMT polarization vector and body angular-momentum vector are distinct unless an additional identification is explicitly adopted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-td-invariants",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "TD spin and momentum norms",
          "reference": "items/pthm-rpm-td-invariants.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
