      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-bmt-fw-and-g-two",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "BMT G=0 Fermi–Walker and G=1 shared-generator cases",
          "reference": "items/ex-rpm-bmt-fw-and-g-two.md#Example",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
            "pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence",
            "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model",
            "pthm-rpm-bmt-constraints"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence": "physical-result",
            "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model": "physical-assumption",
            "pthm-rpm-bmt-constraints": "physical-result"
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]] for the canonical tangent-bundle and connection suppliers used by the BMT transport and charged-action equivalence. No full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nUse the charged-body conventions and adopted BMT transport equation of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]] and [[post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model]]. On a local potential domain, take the trajectory stationary under the action postulate so that [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]] supplies its translation equation. Let $U$ be future timelike, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, and $g(U,s)=0$. Write $\\alpha=q/m$, $G=g_s/2$, $\\kappa=g(s,F^\\sharp(U))$, and $a=D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U)$. The BMT law is\n$$D_\\tau s=\\alpha\\left[GF^\\sharp(s)-(G-1)\\frac{U\\kappa}{c^2}\\right].$$\n\n(a) If $g_s=0$ ($G=0$), then $D_\\tau s=Ug(a,s)/c^2$, the Fermi–Walker transport law on $U^\\perp$.\n\n(b) If $g_s=2$ ($G=1$), then $D_\\tau s=\\alpha F^\\sharp(s)$ while $D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U)$, so the same linear field operator transports both vectors. Both parameter cases preserve orthogonality and spin norm by [[pthm-rpm-bmt-constraints]].",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "University of Virginia, Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation, Electromagnetic Theory lecture",
                "url": "https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/Elec_Mag/2022_Lectures/EM_71_g-2.html",
                "locator": "Full BMT lecture section, including the four-vector transport equation and rest-frame precession interpretation; the two parameter substitutions are verified here."
              },
              {
                "title": "V. Bargmann, L. Michel and V. L. Telegdi, Precession of the Polarization of Particles Moving in a Homogeneous Electromagnetic Field, Physical Review Letters 2 (1959), 435–437",
                "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~vergne/LouisMichel/publications/PhysRevLett.2.435.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete two-page author-hosted article; historical flat homogeneous-field context only."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Exact parameter specializations of the adopted classical BMT transport model: $g_s=0$ gives Fermi–Walker transport on the orthogonal spin space, and $g_s=2$ makes the same field generator act on $U$ and $s$. These are model deductions, not measured cases.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-fixed-versus-scaled-charge",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Fixed-charge and scaled small-body limits",
          "reference": "items/ex-rpm-fixed-versus-scaled-charge.md#Example",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound": "physical-result"
          },
          "statement": "Assume the static spherical-body hypotheses of [[pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound]], so every member obeys $\\ell\\ge q^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0Mc^2)$.\n\n(a) For fixed $q\\ne0$ and fixed finite $M>0$, the lower bound is the positive constant $q^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0Mc^2)$. Thus no family satisfying these hypotheses can have $\\ell\\to0$ while keeping both $q$ and $M$ fixed.\n\n(b) For $q(\\lambda)=\\lambda q_0$, $M(\\lambda)=\\lambda M_0$, and $\\ell(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\ell_0$ with $\\lambda>0$, $q_0\\ne0$ coulombs, $M_0>0$ kilograms, and $\\ell_0>0$ metres, the same necessary condition reduces to $\\ell_0\\ge q_0^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0M_0c^2)$, independent of $\\lambda$. The inequality therefore does not rule out this scaling, although it does not construct a body family or prove that its interior energy remains admissible.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Electrodynamics, §1.3 Scalar Potential and Electric Field Energy",
                "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Electricity_and_Magnetism/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Classical_Electrodynamics_%28Likharev%29/01%3A_Electric_Charge_Interaction/1.03%3A_Scalar_Potential_and_Electric_Field_Energy",
                "locator": "Full HTML section §1.3, field-energy density, uniform-sphere energy and point-charge short-distance divergence; the fixed-versus-scaled algebra is derived here under the local exterior bound."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Section 2, discussion of small-body scales and the Møller radius; it is context only and supplies no existence theorem for the scaled family."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Algebraic consequences of the conditional static spherical exterior-energy bound for a fixed-charge point limit and a scaled family with charge and mass tending to zero. No existence of a material family, experimental process, or universal point-particle limit is asserted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-rest-spin-and-size-bound",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Rest magnetic spin rotation and independent centroid disk",
          "reference": "items/ex-rpm-rest-spin-and-size-bound.md#Example",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
            "lem-rpm-body-spin-size-bound",
            "lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift",
            "pthm-rpm-rest-spin-and-size-bound"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-rpm-body-spin-size-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-rpm-rest-spin-and-size-bound": "physical-result"
          },
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of [[def-countable-choice]] for the canonical tangent-bundle and connection suppliers used by the BMT transport and charged-action equivalence. No full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nIn a charged-body inertial frame of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] with $e_0=U/c$, positively oriented spatial basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$, and constant coordinates for the stationary trajectory, let $U=(c,\\mathbf0)$, $\\mathbf E=0$, and $\\mathbf B=B\\mathbf e_3$ with constant $B>0$ tesla. Let the adopted polarization law have $q$ in coulombs, $m>0$ kilograms, $\\alpha=q/m$, $G=g_s/2$, and choose initial polarization $\\mathbf s(\\tau_0)=s_0\\mathbf e_1$ in kilogram metres squared per second. Independently, choose a separate momentum-rest inertial frame $(o';f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] with $f_0=p/(Mc)$, spatial origin $o'$ at the stipulated body center, and positive spatial basis $(f_1,f_2,f_3)$. Let a flat material body have rest momentum components $p=(Mc,\\mathbf0)$ in this frame, $M>0$ kilograms, body angular momentum $\\mathbf s_*=S_*f_3$ with $S_*>0$ in kilogram metres squared per second, and measurable densities $e:\\mathbb R^3\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $j:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with each component of $j$ in $L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$, expressed in its spatial coordinates $\\mathbf x$. Define $\\overline B_\\ell(0):=\\{\\mathbf x:|\\mathbf x|\\le\\ell\\}$ using this rest-space Euclidean norm. Assume $e$ and $j$ vanish almost everywhere outside this ball for $\\ell>0$ metres, $|j|$ is that norm and satisfies $|j|\\le e/c$ almost everywhere, $\\int e\\,d^3x=Mc^2$, and the componentwise vector integral satisfies $\\mathbf s_*=\\int\\mathbf x\\times j\\,d^3x$. Assume also that its spin tensor is the spatial dual of $\\mathbf s_*$ and obeys the momentum SSC. Then the polarization and body-centroid results are\n\n$$\\mathbf s(\\tau)=s_0\\bigl(\\cos(\\omega\\Delta\\tau)\\mathbf e_1-\\sin(\\omega\\Delta\\tau)\\mathbf e_2\\bigr),\\qquad \\omega=\\frac{qGB}{m},\\quad \\Delta\\tau=\\tau-\\tau_0,$$\n\n$$\\ell\\ge\\frac{S_*}{Mc},\\qquad \\boldsymbol\\delta=(\\delta_1,\\delta_2,0)\\ \\text{in the body-frame spatial components},\\quad \\delta_1^2+\\delta_2^2<\\left(\\frac{S_*}{Mc}\\right)^2.$$\n\nEvery interior shift vector is realized by some future timelike observer. There is no identification between $s_0$ and $S_*$ in this example.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "University of Virginia, Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation, Electromagnetic Theory lecture",
                "url": "https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/Elec_Mag/2022_Lectures/EM_71_g-2.html",
                "locator": "Full BMT section, rest-frame precession equation and constant-field specialization; the sign-specific solution is differentiated below."
              },
              {
                "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
                "locator": "Section 2, observer-dependent centroid displacement disk and Møller radius; the separate body-size inequality is taken from its explicit local proof supplier."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A worked exact rest-frame BMT rotation in a uniform magnetic field alongside the separate flat-body SSC centroid disk and positive-energy size bound. The polarization and body angular momentum are kept distinct; no experiment or material family is reported.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-spinless-td",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Spinless TD geodesic limit",
          "reference": "items/ex-rpm-spinless-td.md#Example",
          "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-rpm-body-moments",
            "post-rpm-mpd-truncation",
