          "strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Conditional model prediction for ideal static clocks, observer energy/frequency ratios in geometric optics, and the proper acceleration required to remain static in the Schwarzschild exterior. The weak-field statement is a uniform mathematical remainder bound. No clock, spectral, acceleration, or gravitational-field measurement is asserted.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-phase-system",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Repulsive relativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian phase system",
          "statement": "Let $m,c,\\kappa>0$, let $x,p\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and set\n\n$$D:=(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})\\times\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad r:=|x|,\\qquad \\mathcal E(p):=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|p|^2}.$$\n\nThe **repulsive relativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian** is\n\n$$H(x,p):=\\mathcal E(p)+\\frac{\\kappa}{r},$$\n\nand its **phase system** is the autonomous ODE on the open set $D$\n\n$$\\dot x=\\nabla_pH=\\frac{c^2p}{\\mathcal E(p)},\\qquad \\dot p=-\\nabla_xH=\\frac{\\kappa x}{r^3}.$$\n\nHere the system parameter $t$ is time in seconds and dot denotes $d/dt$; $m$ is a positive mass parameter (kilograms in the SI interpretation), $c$ a positive speed parameter (metres per second), and $\\kappa$ a positive energy-times-length parameter (joule-metres). Define the radial momentum $p_r:=x\\cdot p/r$ and angular-momentum vector $J:=x\\times p$. A trajectory is a $C^1$ solution of this ODE on an interval; maximality is in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]].\n\nThis is only a mathematical phase system. Its physical interpretation as a charged particle in an externally prescribed Coulomb field requires separate model assumptions.",
          "deps": [
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, The Classical Theory of Fields, 4th English edition, §39 and Problem 1",
                "url": "https://pierre.ag.gerard.web.ulb.be/textbooks/books/Landau_Lifshitz_T2.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Timothy H. Boyer, “Unfamiliar trajectories for a relativistic particle in a Kepler or Coulomb potential,” Am. J. Phys. 72 (2004), arXiv:physics/0405090v1, complete paper",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/physics/0405090v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "Pure mathematical definition of a smooth relativistic phase-space ODE with repulsive inverse-radius potential. It does not identify parameters with measured quantities or assert an electromagnetic model."
        },
        {
