    {
      "id": "rpm-curved-orbits-scattering-examples",
      "title": "Circular timelike orbit and reduced radial stability — Examples",
      "kind": "B",
      "category": "relativistic-particle-mechanics",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "rpm-curved-orbits-scattering",
      "requires": [
        "rpm-curved-orbits-scattering"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-schwarzschild-radial-stability-values",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Fixed-angular-momentum stability at 2s, 3s and 4s",
          "statement": "Use $s>0$ and the reduced potential $W_\\ell(r)=(1-s/r)(c^2+\\ell^2/r^2)$ of [[pthm-rpm-schwarzschild-circular-stability]]. At a circular orbit, $\\ell^2=c^2sr_0^2/(2r_0-3s)$.\n\n1. **Stable radial equilibrium at $r_0=4s$.** The circular value is $\\ell^2=16c^2s^2/5$, and\n\n   $$W_\\ell''(4s)=\\frac{2c^2s(4s-3s)}{(4s)^3(8s-3s)}=\\frac{c^2}{160s^2}>0.$$\n\n   The fixed-$\\ell$ radial equilibrium is Lyapunov stable.\n2. **Unstable radial equilibrium at $r_0=2s$.** Here $\\ell^2=4c^2s^2$ and\n\n   $$W_\\ell''(2s)=\\frac{2c^2s(2s-3s)}{(2s)^3(4s-3s)}=-\\frac{c^2}{4s^2}<0.$$\n\n   The fixed-$\\ell$ equilibrium is unstable.\n3. **Degenerate endpoint at $r_0=3s$.** Here $\\ell^2=3c^2s^2$ and $W_\\ell'(r)=c^2s(r-3s)^2/r^4$. It has a stationary inflection rather than a strict extremum, and the radial equilibrium is not Lyapunov stable by the one-sided perturbation argument in the theorem.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-rpm-schwarzschild-circular-stability",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-circular-orbits-and-photon-sphere"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-rpm-schwarzschild-circular-stability": "physical-result",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-circular-orbits-and-photon-sphere": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted Chapter 1 HTML of the complete lecture notes, §§1.2.5 and 1.3.1–1.3.5 (printed pp. 24–46)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S1"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, §7, pp. 174–177",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Worked parameter values for the conditional Schwarzschild fixed-angular-momentum radial model; not full orbital stability or an observation.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-schwarzschild-null-critical-impact",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "An exact turning ray and the critical Schwarzschild impact parameter",
          "statement": "Let $s>0$. The critical impact parameter is $b_c=3\\sqrt3s/2$. Choose $b=2\\sqrt2s>b_c$. The radial factor is $1-b^2(1-s/r)/r^2$.\n\n1. At $r=2s$, this factor is $1-(8s^2)(1/2)/(4s^2)=0$. Because $2s>3s/2$ and $H(r)=(1-s/r)/r^2$ is strictly decreasing for $r>3s/2$, $r_*=2s$ is the unique outer turning point. The ideal incoming ray turns there and returns outward.\n2. At the critical value, direct factorization gives\n\n   $$1-b_c^2H(r)=\\frac{(2r-3s)^2(r+3s)}{4r^3}.$$\n\n   Thus the critical inward ray from infinity approaches $r=3s/2$ asymptotically, and the azimuthal integral diverges logarithmically; it does not complete a finite number of windings before reaching the circular orbit in affine parameter.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-rpm-schwarzschild-null-scattering",
            "lem-rpm-schwarzschild-null-geodesic-reduction"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-rpm-schwarzschild-null-scattering": "physical-result",
            "lem-rpm-schwarzschild-null-geodesic-reduction": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted Chapter 1 HTML of the complete lecture notes, §§1.2.5 and 1.3.1–1.3.5 (printed pp. 24–46)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S1"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, §7, pp. 177–179",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Exact Schwarzschild exterior geodesic example for the ideal-light model. It is not a finite-resolution image or measured lensing result.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-schwarzschild-static-redshift-acceleration",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Static Schwarzschild clocks at 2s and 4s, and the near-horizon acceleration limit",
          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be the adopted Schwarzschild exterior datum with $s>0$ and $f(r)=1-s/r$. Compare ideal static clocks at radii $r_E=2s$ and $r_R=4s$ over a common Schwarzschild coordinate-time interval of duration $\\Delta t>0$. For the frequency ratio, choose a specified smooth, affinely parametrized future-directed null geodesic $\\eta:J\\to M$ in the exterior with endpoint parameters $\\lambda_E<\\lambda_R$ on those static worldlines, and use the ideal geometric-optics frequencies assigned by the endpoint static observers. Here $f(2s)=1/2$ and $f(4s)=3/4$.\n\n1. The exact model ratios are\n\n   $$\\frac{\\omega_R}{\\omega_E}=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(2s)}{f(4s)}}=\\sqrt{\\frac23},\\qquad \\frac{\\Delta\\tau_R}{\\Delta\\tau_E}=\\sqrt{\\frac{f(4s)}{f(2s)}}=\\sqrt{\\frac32}.$$\n\n   The static proper acceleration at $2s$ is $a(2s)=c^2/(4\\sqrt2s)$.\n2. Write $r=s(1+\\epsilon)$ for $\\epsilon>0$. The exact static proper acceleration is\n\n   $$a(r)=\\frac{c^2}{2s\\sqrt\\epsilon(1+\\epsilon)^{3/2}},$$\n\n   which tends to $+\\infty$ as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$. In contrast, the curvature scalar has limit $K\\to12/s^4$ by [[lem-gr-schwarzschild-curvature-and-kretschmann-scalar]], and the advanced extension chart is smooth through $r=s$ by [[lem-gr-schwarzschild-extension-chart]]. The acceleration divergence is therefore a property of maintaining a static worldline near the boundary, not evidence of a curvature blow-up there.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-rpm-static-clock-redshift",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-curvature-and-kretschmann-scalar",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-extension-chart"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-rpm-static-clock-redshift": "physical-result",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-static-congruence-and-clock-rates": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-curvature-and-kretschmann-scalar": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-gr-schwarzschild-extension-chart": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted Chapter 1 HTML of the complete lecture notes, §§1.2.5 and 1.3.1–1.3.5 (printed pp. 24–46)",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S1"
              },
              {
                "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, §7, pp. 180–181",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
              }
            ]
          },
          "strategy": "direct",
          "physical_scope": "Worked model calculation for ideal static observers and an ideal light ray. The near-horizon acceleration is a model requirement for static motion, not an observation; curvature and extension statements are geometric properties of the specified Schwarzschild model.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-newtonian-limit",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Formal nonrelativistic limit of the repulsive Coulomb scattering angle",
          "statement": "Fix $m,\\kappa,K,b>0$ and let $c\\to\\infty$ with $W_c=mc^2+K$ and $J_c=b\\sqrt{A_c}$, where $A_c=(W_c^2-m^2c^4)/c^2$. For sufficiently large $c$, $J_c>\\kappa/c$ because $J_c^2-\\kappa^2/c^2\\to2mKb^2>0$.\n\n1. The relativistic scattering angle tends to the nonrelativistic repulsive Coulomb angle:\n\n   $$\\theta_c\\longrightarrow2\\arctan\\left(\\frac{\\kappa}{2Kb}\\right).$$\n\n   This agrees with [[thm-cm-coulomb-scattering]] at kinetic energy $K$ and impact parameter $b$.\n2. The limit compares ideal mathematical model formulas while holding the indicated parameters fixed; it supplies no experimental evidence for either model.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-scattering",
            "prop-rpm-relativistic-coulomb-scattering-branch",
            "thm-cm-coulomb-scattering",
            "lem-rpm-arctangent-reciprocal-angle",
