        {
          "id": "pthm-rpm-prescribed-force-local-motion",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Local massive motion in smooth orthogonal prescribed forces",
          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold and let $m,c>0$. Let $\\mathcal V\\subset TM$ be an open set of future timelike vectors, and let $f$ be a smooth vector field along the bundle projection $\\mathcal V\\to M$, with $f(p,V)\\in T_pM$. For the named massive test body, adopt the constant-mass force model of [[post-rpm-prescribed-force-law]]: $P=mU$, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, $D_\\tau P=f(x,U)$, and $g(U,f(x,U))=0$. If $\\tau$ is measured in seconds, then $P$ has SI units kilogram metres per second and $f$ has units newtons.\n\nFix an initial proper-time value $\\tau_0\\in\\mathbb R$. For each initial datum $(p_0,U_0)\\in\\mathcal V$ with $g_{p_0}(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$, there is a unique local $C^2$ future-timelike worldline $x:J\\to M$ on an open interval $J\\ni\\tau_0$, with $C^1$ tangent $U=dx/d\\tau$, $x(\\tau_0)=p_0$, and $U(\\tau_0)=U_0$, satisfying the adopted equation. It remains on the future unit timelike shell and has a unique maximal solution on an open interval containing $\\tau_0$. In any chart-state domain where the coordinate force vector field is smooth, if a finite endpoint is approached while the state remains in a compact subset of that domain, the solution extends past the endpoint. No global lifetime or spacetime completeness is asserted.",
          "deps": [
            "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
            "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
            "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law",
            "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp",
            "lem-rpm-shell-force-ivp"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
