            "def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart",
            "def-smooth-differential-k-form",
            "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition",
            "thm-gr-curvature-tensoriality-and-coordinate-formula",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-smooth-differential-k-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-gr-curvature-tensoriality-and-coordinate-formula": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth, Hausdorff, second-countable four-manifold without boundary, with smooth Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and smooth time-orientation field $N$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Supply an independent orientation $o$ of $M$ in the sense of [[def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart]]. The metric and time orientation do not select $o$. Write $\\mathrm{vol}_g$ for the oriented metric volume form of [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]]; in any positively oriented chart it has components $\\sqrt{|\\det(g_{ab})|}\\,dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$.\n\nA smooth real $k$-form is a smooth section of $\\bigwedge^kT^*M$, and its space is denoted $\\Omega^k(M)$, as in [[def-smooth-differential-k-form]]. In this page, $F\\in\\Omega^2(M)$ means an arbitrary smooth real two-form. No equation such as $dF=0$, no global potential, and no physical field law is included in this definition.\n\nAn **observer at $p\\in M$** is a vector $n\\in T_pM$ with $g_p(n,n)=-1$ and $g_p(n,N_p)<0$; its **rest space** is $n^\\perp=\\{v:g_p(v,n)=0\\}$, as in [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]. A smooth **observer congruence** on an open set $U\\subseteq M$ is a smooth vector field whose value at every point is an observer. Its observer curves are the maximal integral curves, and a chart is adapted to it when its three spatial coordinate functions are constant along those curves, as in [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]. Such a congruence need not be hypersurface-orthogonal.\n\nA **local frame field** on $U$ is an ordered smooth basis field of $TM|_U$. A **coordinate frame** is the basis of coordinate vector fields supplied by a chart. A **tetrad field** is a local frame $(E_0,E_1,E_2,E_3)$ with $g(E_A,E_B)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{AB}$; it is future-directed when $E_0$ is future-directed, as in [[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. These are distinct objects: an observer congruence is a vector field, an adapted chart is a coordinate chart, and a tetrad is a basis field. A tetrad field is not presumed to be a coordinate frame. For every observer congruence $n$, each point has a neighborhood carrying an $n$-adapted tetrad; this is a local assertion only.\n\nA **worldline** on this Lorentzian manifold means a $C^1$ or piecewise-$C^1$ regular curve with future-timelike tangent and specified interval domain, as defined precisely in [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]. Its parameter is arbitrary; the proper-time and four-velocity constructions of that definition apply under their stated hypotheses. A worldline is a mathematical curve here and does not assert the existence or motion of a physical body.\n\nFor coordinate bookkeeping only, when a chart is chosen with all four coordinate values assigned the metre, write $x^0=ct$, where $t$ is assigned seconds and $c>0$ is the corresponding scale in $\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$. Then $g_{ab}$ is recorded as dimensionless. This is a unit convention on a chart, not a claim that the chart is inertial, orthonormal, global, or physically realized; $c$ in this mathematical convention has no asserted measured value.\n\nUse the unique torsion-free metric-compatible connection $\\nabla^g$ and the curvature convention of [[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]] and [[thm-gr-curvature-tensoriality-and-coordinate-formula]]. In a length-coordinate chart, the displayed Christoffel formula differentiates dimensionless metric components once, so $\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}$ is recorded in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. The curvature coordinate formula contains one derivative of $\\Gamma$ or a product of two $\\Gamma$ components, so $R^a{}_{bcd}$ and its Ricci and scalar contractions are recorded in $\\mathrm m^{-2}$. These component assignments apply only in length-coordinate charts; other coordinate units transform by their Jacobians.",
          "strategy": "Normalize the supplied future timelike field; construct an adapted tetrad by projected-frame Gram–Schmidt on a neighbourhood; derive connection and curvature length units from the stated coordinate formulas.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, section 2.4.2, The Electromagnetic Field, equations (2.85)-(2.87)",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Section 2.4.2, equations (2.85)-(2.87): differential forms and the electric/magnetic components of a two-form in Minkowski coordinates."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, Cambridge Part III lecture notes, sections 3.1.3 and 3.2.2-3.2.3, the Hodge dual and metric-selected connection",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gr/grhtml/S3.html",
                "locator": "Section 3.1.3, equations (3.97)-(3.98): oriented volume, Hodge dual and the Lorentzian star-square sign; sections 3.2.2-3.2.3: metric-compatible connection and curvature conventions."
              },
              {
                "title": "H. S. Reall, Part III General Relativity, Cambridge, October 12 2022 revision, sections 2.2, 3.2, 4.2, and 8.1-8.5",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/hsr1000/part3_gr_lectures.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 2.2, 3.2 and 4.2: Lorentzian orthonormal bases, Levi-Civita connection and curvature; sections 8.1 and 8.5: forms, volume form and Hodge dual."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
