      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-qft-positive-tempered-moments",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Positive tempered moments on the tensor test algebra",
          "statement": "Use the Schwartz spaces and their seminorm topologies of\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]] and\n[[def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence]]. Put\n$S:=\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$ and\n$S_n:=\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^{4n};\\mathbb C)$ for $n\\ge1$, and set\n$S_0:=\\mathbb C$. A tempered distribution in $S_n'$ means a continuous\ncomplex-linear functional on $S_n$\nas in [[def-tempered-distribution]]; its pairing with a test is bilinear.\n\nFor complex vector spaces $S$, let $S^{\\otimes_{\\rm alg} n}$ be the algebraic\ntensor product, characterized by multilinear maps out of $S^n$, with\n$S^{\\otimes_{\\rm alg}0}:=\\mathbb C1$. Define the algebraic tensor test algebra\n$$T(S):=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}^{\\rm alg}S^{\\otimes_{\\rm alg}n},$$\nwhere the direct sum consists of finite-support sequences. Its multiplication\nis concatenation of tensor factors, extended bilinearly, and its unit is $1$\nin degree zero. Its conjugate-linear involution is specified on elementary\ntensors by\n$$(f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n)^*:=\\overline{f_n}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\overline{f_1},\\qquad (c1)^*=\\overline c1.$$\n\nFor $f_j\\in S$, the elementary tensor is evaluated against a distribution by\nthe test function\n$$J_n(f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n)(x_1,\\ldots,x_n):=\\prod_{j=1}^n f_j(x_j).$$\nThis is in $S_n$: for multi-indices split into four-coordinate blocks\n$(\\alpha_j,\\beta_j)$, its Schwartz seminorm is\n$$p_{(\\alpha_j)_j,(\\beta_j)_j}(J_n(f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n))=\\prod_{j=1}^n p_{\\alpha_j\\beta_j}(f_j).$$\nThus $J_n$ is multilinear and continuous, and the displayed formula extends\nlinearly to the algebraic tensor product.\nIn degree zero set $J_0(c1):=c$.\n\nBy these definitions concatenation is associative with the degree-zero unit,\nand reversal together with complex conjugation is an involution satisfying\n$(ab)^*=b^*a^*$; thus $T(S)$ is a unital complex star algebra.\n\nA **positive tempered moment family** is a sequence consisting of $W_0=1$\nand $W_n\\in S_n'$ for each $n\\ge1$ such that the multilinear maps\n$$f_1,\\ldots,f_n\\longmapsto\\langle W_n,J_n(f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n)\\rangle$$\nextend, degree by degree and then over finite sums, to a complex-linear\nfunctional $\\omega:T(S)\\to\\mathbb C$ satisfying\n$$\\omega(1)=1,\\qquad \\omega(a^*a)\\in[0,\\infty)\\quad(a\\in T(S)).$$\nEquivalently, the family supplies an actually positive normalized functional\non this algebra. Positivity is a hypothesis on the distributions; formal\ncoefficient families do not qualify merely because they obey algebraic\nidentities. No topology on $T(S)$, norm completion, C*-algebra, or existence\nclaim for an interacting field is part of this definition.",
          "strategy": "Definition of the algebraic tensor test data, positive functional, and exact topology conventions; no existence claim.",
          "deps": [
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence",
            "def-tempered-distribution"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tempered-distribution": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jens Mund, General Aspects of Relativistic Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, DF-UFJF (2012), §2.1, Theorem 2.6 and proof",
                "url": "https://www.ufjf.br/fisica/files/2022/03/AQFT.pdf",
                "locator": "PDF pp. 15–17: the tensor test algebra, positive Wightman functional, and its GNS construction. The notes identify themselves as incomplete course notes; this item uses only the explicitly stated algebraic definitions."
              },
              {
                "title": "Konrad Schmüdgen, An Invitation to Unbounded Representations of *-Algebras on Hilbert Space, Graduate Texts in Mathematics 285, Springer Nature (2020)",
                "url": "https://dokumen.pub/an-invitation-to-unbounded-representations-of-algebras-on-hilbert-space-1nbsped-3030463656-9783030463656.html",
                "locator": "§2.4, printed pp. 20–21, Definition 2.32: positive functionals on unital *-algebras."
              },
              {
                "title": "Christopher J. Fewster and Kasia Rejzner, Algebraic Quantum Field Theory—an introduction, arXiv:1904.04051v2 (2019)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04051",
                "locator": "§2.3, printed pp. 7–9: algebraic states and the GNS construction."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "library": "mathematics"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-qft-weak-operator-valued-tempered-distribution",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Weak operator-valued tempered distribution on a common domain",
          "statement": "Let $S=\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$ with its Schwartz topology, let $H$\nbe a complex Hilbert space, and let $D\\subseteq H$ be a dense complex linear\nsubspace. Use the inner product linear in its first argument and conjugate-linear\nin its second, as in [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. A **weak\noperator-valued tempered distribution on the common domain $D$** is a\ncomplex-linear map\n$$\\Phi:S\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Lin}(D),$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Lin}(D)$ is the vector space of linear maps $D\\to D$,\nsuch that for every $u,v\\in D$ the scalar map\n$$f\\longmapsto (\\Phi(f)u,v)_H$$\nis a continuous complex-linear functional on $S$, hence an element of\n$S'=\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$ by [[def-tempered-distribution]]. In particular,\nevery smeared map preserves the same domain $D$. This definition is weak in\nthe matrix-coefficient sense; it does not define point values $\\Phi(x)$,\noperators on all of $H$, or self-adjoint closures.",
          "strategy": "Definition by a common dense domain and continuous linear matrix coefficients.",
          "deps": [
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
            "def-tempered-distribution"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tempered-distribution": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jens Mund, General Aspects of Relativistic Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, DF-UFJF (2012), §2.1, proof of Theorem 2.6",
                "url": "https://www.ufjf.br/fisica/files/2022/03/AQFT.pdf",
                "locator": "PDF pp. 15–17: fields act on the dense GNS domain and their matrix elements are tempered distributions."
              },
              {
                "title": "Christopher J. Fewster and Kasia Rejzner, Algebraic Quantum Field Theory—an introduction, arXiv:1904.04051v2 (2019)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04051",
                "locator": "§2.3, printed pp. 7–9: common-domain GNS representations and the first-variable-linear pairing convention used locally."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "library": "mathematics"
