        {
          "id": "cex-qft-formal-factorial-series",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Every formal coefficient exists while convergence radius vanishes",
          "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/proposed-inventory.json#cex-qft-formal-factorial-series; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/complete-local-arguments.md#C5",
          "design_state": "reserved-QFT-MC03 B-leaf counterexample; complete local argument",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_roles": {},
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Define $\\mathbb C\\llbracket g\\rrbracket$ to be the set of coefficient sequences $(a_n)_{n\\ge0}$, written $\\sum_{n\\ge0}a_ng^n$, with coefficientwise equality and addition and Cauchy multiplication: the coefficient of $g^n$ in a product is $\\sum_{k=0}^na_kb_{n-k}$, a finite sum. Formal membership makes no numerical convergence assertion. For numerical evaluation use $g^0:=1$ for every $g$, including $g=0$; numerical convergence means convergence of the partial sums in $\\mathbb C$. In the extended nonnegative reals $[0,+\\infty]$, the numerical radius of a coefficient sequence is\n\n$$R:=\\sup\\{r\\in[0,+\\infty): \\sum_{n\\ge0}a_ng^n\\text{ converges for every }g\\in\\mathbb C\\text{ with }|g|<r\\},$$\n\nwhere the supremum is taken in the extended order, so $R=+\\infty$ when the set is unbounded. The claim to refute is that every element of $\\mathbb C\\llbracket g\\rrbracket$ has $R>0$.\n\nFor $n\\in\\mathbb N_0$, define $n!:=\\prod_{k=1}^{n}k$, with the empty product $0!:=1$; thus $(n+1)!=(n+1)n!$. View these integer products as coefficients in $\\mathbb C$ through the canonical embedding $\\mathbb Z\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb C$.",
          "strategy": "Use the coefficient sequence n! to define a formal series, then show its numerical terms fail the necessary term test for every nonzero complex coupling.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "counterexample"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "OpenStax, Calculus Volume 2, §6.1 Power Series and Functions",
                "url": "https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/6-1-power-series-and-functions"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. W. Howell, Complex Analysis (free online textbook; formerly Complex Analysis for Mathematics and Engineering), §4.4",
                "url": "https://complexanalysis.org/web/sec_power-series.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 512
    },
    {
      "id": "qft-mf01",
      "title": "Schwartz/distribution spaces and Hilbert Fock conventions",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "qft-mf01-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "fourier-transform-convolution-and-approximate-identities",
        "qm-math-oscillator",
        "schwartz-space-and-the-plancherel-theorem",
        "field-extensions-and-the-complex-numbers",
        "tempered-distributions-and-the-fourier-transform",
        "density-separability-and-convolution-in-lp"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Schwartz/distribution spaces and Hilbert Fock conventions",
          "deps": [
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
            "def-test-function-topology",
            "def-tempered-distribution",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tempered-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "statement": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$ and its tensor extension is the bounded map of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jan Dereziński, Mathematical Introduction to Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, §§5.1–5.5 and 5.7–5.9 (Hilbert and Fock spaces)",
                "url": "https://www.fuw.edu.pl/~derezins/qft-lectures.pdf",
