            "def-measure-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-hermite-graph-core-for-position": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-completeness": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ be its bosonic Fock space and finite-particle domain.\nFor $h\\in H$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the Segal field\n$$\\phi_0(h):=2^{-1/2}\\bigl(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h)\\bigr).$$\nFor $h\\ne0$, this symmetric operator is essentially self-adjoint; write\n$\\Phi(h)=\\overline{\\phi_0(h)}$. If $r=\\|h\\|$, $e=h/r$, $K=e^\\perp$,\n$G=\\mathcal F_s(K)$, choose a countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)$ of $G$\nwhose vectors are finite-particle, and use the one-mode splitting of\n[[lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting]] followed by the Hermite unitary\n$|n\\rangle\\mapsto g_n$ into $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C)$ with pairing\n$\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline f g$ to write a vector as\n$(f_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\ell^2(J;L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C))$. Then the exact domain and\naction are\n$$D(\\Phi(h))=\\left\\{(f_j):\\sum_{j\\in J}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+r^2x^2)|f_j(x)|^2\\,dx<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad (\\Phi(h)f)_j(x)=rx f_j(x).$$\nFor $h=0$, $\\phi_0(0)$ closes to the zero operator on all of $\\mathcal F_s(H)$.\n\nDefine $\\epsilon(z)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}z^{\\otimes n}/\\sqrt{n!}$ for $z\\in H$.\nThere are unitaries $W(h)$ determined on these total exponential vectors by\n$$W(h)\\epsilon(z)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|h\\|^2}{4} +\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}\\langle h,z\\rangle_2\\right) \\epsilon\\!\\left(z+\\frac{i h}{\\sqrt2}\\right).$$\nThey obey\n$$W(h)W(k)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right)W(h+k),\\qquad \\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4},$$\nand $h\\mapsto W(h)$ is strongly continuous. For every $h$, the one-parameter\ngroup $t\\mapsto W(th)$ has norm derivative at zero exactly on\n$D(\\Phi(h))$, with derivative $i\\Phi(h)$ (for $h=0$ this is the zero\ngenerator). Thus the displayed bounded Weyl operators have precisely the\nclosed Segal fields as their generators; no point-field or measurement claim\nis included.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jan Dereziński, Mathematical Introduction to Quantum Field Theory, §§5.4–5.9 and 7.4, 7.7",
                "url": "https://www.fuw.edu.pl/~derezins/qft-lectures.pdf",
                "locator": "Full 136-page author-hosted notes; Fock fields and exponential law, PDF pp. 35–40; Weyl relations and Fock representation, pp. 57–59."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §§2.2–2.4",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qft.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 155-page author-hosted notes; printed pp. 23–25 and 29–30; mode formula and Fock-space discussion, with formal-domain caveat."
              },
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, §8.3",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 317-page book; printed pp. 178–179, oscillator Hermite basis and ladder recurrence."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 513
    },
    {
      "id": "qft-mf01-examples",
      "title": "Schwartz/distribution spaces and Hilbert Fock conventions — Examples",
      "kind": "B",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "qft-mf01",
      "requires": [
        "qft-mf01"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-qftmath-mode-domain",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "One-mode CCR/CAR and a vector outside the number-operator domain",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
            "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Let $H=\\mathbb Ce$ with $\\|e\\|=1$, and write $|n\\rangle=e^{\\otimes n}$\nfor its normalized bosonic $n$-particle vector, including $|0\\rangle=\\Omega$.\nOn the one-mode fermionic Fock space write $|1\\rangle=c^\\dagger(e)\\Omega$.\nThe one-mode bosonic and fermionic operators satisfy the CCR and CAR on their\nstated domains. The bosonic number operator from\n[[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]] is\n$N|n\\rangle=n|n\\rangle$ with\n$$D(N)=\\left\\{(c_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\sum_{n\\ge0}n^2|c_n|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThe Fock vector\n$$\\Psi=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(n+1)^{-3/2}|n\\rangle$$\nbelongs to $D(a(e))\\cap D(a^\\dagger(e))$ but not to $D(N)$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jan Dereziński, Mathematical Introduction to Quantum Field Theory, §§5.4–5.5",
                "url": "https://www.fuw.edu.pl/~derezins/qft-lectures.pdf",
                "locator": "Full 136-page author-hosted notes; creation, annihilation and Fock sectors, PDF pp. 35–37."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §2.4",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qft.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 155-page author-hosted notes; number operator and multiparticle Fock states, printed pp. 29–30."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 514
    },
    {
      "id": "qft-mf02",
      "title": "Scalar future mass-shell measure and test restriction",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "qft-mf02-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "qft-mf01",
        "sr-spacetime-primitives",
        "smooth-partitions-of-unity-and-exhaustions"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Scalar future mass shell, invariant measure and test restriction",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad p_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))= \\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x =\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} \\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)} \\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.\n\nThis is a mathematical free scalar one-particle construction. It adopts the\nchosen coordinates, measure and Fourier convention; it asserts no experimental\nobservation, physical mass identification or measurement outcome.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, section 2.4.1",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2, section 2.4.1 equations 2.133–2.141, printed pages 23–25; the complete lecture-note set is also listed as a 155-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author draft, chapters 3–4",
                "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; chapter 3 equations 3.15–3.19 and chapter 4 equations 4.11–4.15."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-mass-shell-measure-invariant",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Local finiteness and Lorentz invariance of the scalar shell measure",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the measure $d\\nu_\\mu$ of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] is locally finite and sigma-finite on $H_\\mu^+$.\nFor every proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation $\\Lambda$ and every\nnonnegative Borel function $g$ on $H_\\mu^+$,\n$$\\int_{H_\\mu^+}g(\\Lambda^{-1}p)\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p) =\\int_{H_\\mu^+}g(p)\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p).$$\nThe claim applies off the omitted apex when $\\mu=0$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author draft, chapter 3",
                "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete author-hosted draft; equations 3.15–3.19 derive the positive-energy mass-shell measure."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, section 2.4.1",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2, section 2.4.1 equations 2.133–2.141 discusses Lorentz-invariant one-particle normalization; the complete lecture-note set is also listed as a 155-page PDF."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Density of Schwartz test restrictions in the scalar shell space",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "thm-rational-box-step-functions-form-a-countable-dense-subset-of-l-p-of-rn",
            "lem-smooth-compactly-supported-functions-are-dense-in-ltwo-of-an-open-set",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
            "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
            "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-rational-box-step-functions-form-a-countable-dense-subset-of-l-p-of-rn": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-smooth-compactly-supported-functions-are-dense-in-ltwo-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the\nspace $H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu)$ of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] is separable, and\n$$\\{K_\\mu f:f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)\\}$$\nis dense in $H_\\mu$. If $\\mu>0$, the range contains every function in\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$; if $\\mu=0$, it contains every function in\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Richard B. Melrose, MIT OpenCourseWare 18.155 Differential Analysis, Fall 2004, lecture notes, section 9",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-155-differential-analysis-fall-2004/fa60b20e070ee676b70cdf8a373e6197_section9.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete five-page official MIT OCW PDF, printed pages 58–62; Theorem 9.1 proves the Fourier transform isomorphism on Schwartz space and gives its inverse."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Continuity and Klein–Gordon annihilation of scalar shell restriction",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
            "thm-fourier-transform-maps-schwartz-space-continuously-to-itself"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fourier-transform-maps-schwartz-space-continuously-to-itself": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the\npointwise restriction\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x$$\nof [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] maps $\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ continuously\nand linearly into $H_\\mu$. It also annihilates the Klein–Gordon test operator:\n$$K_\\mu\\bigl((\\partial_0^2-\\Delta+\\mu^2)f\\bigr)=0 \\qquad(f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$\nThe coordinates are the fixed length coordinates of that definition and\n$\\mu$ has inverse-length units.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Sections 2.2 and 2.4, free scalar Fourier modes and relativistic shell normalization."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author draft, chapters 3–4",
                "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; chapter 3 equations 3.15–3.19 and chapter 4 equations 4.11–4.15."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-fock-unitary-lift",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Unitary lift to bosonic Fock space",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ and $K$ be complex\nHilbert spaces and let $U:H\\to K$ be unitary. The sector maps\n$U^{\\otimes n}$ restrict to unitaries $U_s^{(n)}:H_s^{(n)}\\to K_s^{(n)}$;\ntheir direct sum\n$$\\Gamma_s(U)=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^\\infty U_s^{(n)}$$\nis unitary, maps vacuum to vacuum, and maps the finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(H)$ onto $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}(K)$. It intertwines the\ncreation and annihilation maps:\n$$\\Gamma_s(U)a_H^\\dagger(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K^\\dagger(Uh),\\qquad \\Gamma_s(U)a_H(h)\\Gamma_s(U)^{-1}=a_K(Uh)$$\non the finite-particle domain. If $g\\mapsto U_g$ is a strongly continuous\nfamily of unitaries, then $g\\mapsto\\Gamma_s(U_g)$ is strongly continuous.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, section 2.4",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2, section 2.4, multiparticle Fock states; the complete lecture-note set is also listed as a 155-page PDF. The arbitrary-unitary lift and continuity claim are proved from the exact tensor-sector definitions on this page."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Strongly continuous scalar one-particle Poincare action",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-measure-invariant",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
            "thm-dominated-convergence"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-measure-invariant": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Use the proper\northochronous Poincare group\n$G=\\mathbb R^4\\rtimes SO^+(1,3)$ in the coordinates of\n[[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For $g=(a,\\Lambda)\\in G$, define\n$$f_g(x)=f(\\Lambda^{-1}(x-a)),\\qquad [U_\\mu(g)h](p)=e^{ip\\circ a}h(\\Lambda^{-1}p).$$\nThen $f_g\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $U_\\mu(g)$ is a well-defined unitary\non $H_\\mu$, $g\\mapsto U_\\mu(g)$ is a strongly continuous representation, and\n$$K_\\mu f_g=U_\\mu(g)K_\\mu f \\qquad(f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$\nThe group product convention is\n$(a,\\Lambda)(b,\\Gamma)=(a+\\Lambda b,\\Lambda\\Gamma)$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Sections 2.4.1 and 2.6, relativistic one-particle normalization and the Poincare-covariant Heisenberg field."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author draft, chapters 3–4",
                "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; chapter 3 relativistic shell normalization and chapter 4 free scalar modes."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Smeared free scalar field and its Poincare representation",
          "deps": [
            "lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
            "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
            "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-measure-invariant",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense",
            "lem-qft-fock-unitary-lift",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity",
            "lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-measure-invariant": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-range-dense": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-unitary-lift": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2, sections 2.4.1, 2.6.1 and 2.7; relevant equations 2.133–2.167 and two-point function. The complete lecture-note set is also listed as a 155-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author draft, chapters 3–4",
                "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; equations 3.15–3.19 and 4.11–4.15."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Causal support of the scalar commutator distribution",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
            "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
            "def-tempered-distribution",
            "thm-fourier-inversion-on-schwartz-space",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
            "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tempered-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fourier-inversion-on-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$\nwith the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$\nbehavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then\n$$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$\nthe closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported\nsmooth $f,g$, put\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$\nOn $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy\n$$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$\nConsequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, section 2.6.1",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2, section 2.6.1, equations 2.160–2.167; the complete lecture-note set is also listed as a 155-page PDF."
              },
              {
                "title": "Fewster and Rejzner, Algebraic Quantum Field Theory—an introduction",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04051v2",
                "locator": "Complete 47-page article, version 2; section 4.2, PDF pages 14–18, causal propagator and free scalar Weyl algebra."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-scalar-local-weyl-algebra",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Isotony and spacelike commutativity of scalar local Weyl algebras",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
            "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
            "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
            "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]].\nLet $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be open. For real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O)$ define\n$$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere the Segal normalization is exactly\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, as in [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. This scale makes the generator\nof $t\\mapsto W_\\mu(tf)$ equal to the real scalar field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the unital norm-closed operator\nalgebra\n$$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O)= \\overline{\\operatorname{alg}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|} \\{I,W_\\mu(f),W_\\mu(f)^*:f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)\\}.$$\nThen $O_1\\subseteq O_2$ implies\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)\\subseteq\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. If every difference\n$x-y$, with $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, is spacelike, then every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)$ commutes with every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. These are algebraic properties of the constructed\nrepresentation; they are not empirical signaling or measurement claims.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Fewster and Rejzner, Algebraic Quantum Field Theory—an introduction",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04051v2",
                "locator": "Complete 47-page article, version 2; section 4.2 and PDF pages 14–18, free scalar Weyl algebra and local nets."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, section 2.6.1",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Author-hosted HTML text of chapter 2, section 2.6.1, equations 2.160–2.167; the complete lecture-note set is also listed as a 155-page PDF."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 515
    },
    {
      "id": "qft-mf02-examples",
      "title": "Scalar future mass-shell measure and test restriction — Examples",
      "kind": "B",
