      "items": [
        {
          "id": "texp-qft-two-level-measurement",
          "kind": "thought-experiment",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Two-level measurement and formal-coefficient boundary",
          "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/quantities-and-postulates.md#framework-examples",
          "design_state": "planned B leaf; complete finite two-level conditional argument and formal versus numerical convergence proof authored",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement",
            "def-qft-finite-bounded-kraus-instrument",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-qft-finite-instrument-normalization",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
            "thm-complex-plane-is-complete",
            "thm-reals-ordered-field",
            "def-formal-power-series-and-coefficient-extraction",
            "lem-cauchy-reals-archimedean"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement": "physical-assumption",
            "def-qft-finite-bounded-kraus-instrument": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-finite-instrument-normalization": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-complex-plane-is-complete": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-reals-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-formal-power-series-and-coefficient-extraction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cauchy-reals-archimedean": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "physical_scope": "Conditional ideal two-level instrument; no observed outcome and no formal coefficient identified as an exact probability.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. At one fixed measurement time, take the\nconstructed two-dimensional Hilbert space $\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C^2$ with\ninner product\n$\\langle x,y\\rangle=x_0\\overline{y_0}+x_1\\overline{y_1}$, linear in the\nfirst variable. Let $e_0=(1,0)$, $e_1=(0,1)$ and\n$v=\\alpha e_0+\\beta e_1$ with\n$|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1$. Prepare the rank-one density operator\n$\\rho=T_{v,v}$, where\n$T_{v,v}x=\\langle x,v\\rangle v$ as in\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]. Define the two measurement\noperators\n\n$$P_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad P_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad M_{0,1}=P_0,\\quad M_{1,1}=P_1.$$\n\nThis is a finite normalized instrument. The postulate\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]] assigns model probabilities\n$p_0=|\\alpha|^2$ and $p_1=|\\beta|^2$, which sum to one. When $p_i>0$ its\nconditional state is $P_i$; when $p_i=0$ the normalized update is undefined.\nThis is a conditional model calculation, not a report of an observed result.\n\nFor the formal comparison, let $g$ be an indeterminate; the formal algebra\nassigns it no SI unit or numerical value. The formal series\n\n$$F(g):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}n!g^n\\in\\mathbb R\\llbracket g\\rrbracket$$\n\nhas a coefficient for every $n$, while its numerical power series has radius\nzero after the real coefficients are embedded in $\\mathbb C$ for evaluation.\nThis example separates coefficient data from numerical convergence.\nThe Born postulate does not assign an exact probability to a coefficient in\nisolation: an additional map from formal data to a constructed state and\nmeasurement, together with any approximation and error assumptions, would be\nneeded. No such map or observed outcome is claimed.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "J. Watrous, The Theory of Quantum Information (Cambridge University Press, 2018; author-hosted prepublication manuscript), §2.3.1 pp. 100–102 and §2.3.2 pp. 111–113",
                "url": "https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~watrous/TQI/TQI.2.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. B. Griffiths, Notes on Quantum Information Theory, version 30 Jan. 2004, complete §7, printed pp. 1–3, equations (7.2)–(7.9)",
                "url": "https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/course/33-658/qit7.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "OpenStax, Calculus Volume 2, §6.1 ‘Power Series and Functions,’ Example 6.1(2)",
                "url": "https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/6-1-power-series-and-functions"
              },
              {
                "title": "R. W. Howell, Complex Analysis, §4.4, Theorem 4.4.1 and Exercise 3(b)",
                "url": "https://complexanalysis.org/web/sec_power-series.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 520
