    {
      "id": "qft-mx01",
      "title": "Example conventions, energy units and constructed/formal data",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "qft-mx01-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "countability-and-uncountability",
        "qm-math-controlled-dynamics",
        "schwartz-space-and-the-plancherel-theorem",
        "qm-math-oscillator"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-qft-example-data",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "QFT example units, metric, gauge, and pairing conventions",
          "statement": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nUse the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. In the common natural-unit field convention, $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, a scalar field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and a Dirac field has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation set $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$, with $A_a$ in tesla-metres and gauge function $\\chi$ in webers. With charge $q$ in coulombs define\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_roles": {},
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
