    {
      "id": "qft-mx04",
      "title": "Ideal linear Penning mode-frequency invariant",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "qft-mx04-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "qft-mx01"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-example-penning-frequency-invariant",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Ideal linear Penning mode-frequency invariant",
          "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/examples/proof-modules.md#qx10",
          "design_state": "Complete conditional trace proof authored. The actual draft QFT-MX01 definition carrier was inspected and its M=mc^2 convention matches; batch-41 manifest syntax currently prevents run-wide metadata certification.",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-example-data"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $q\\ne0$ be a signed charge in coulombs, $M>0$ a rest-mass energy in joules, $c>0$ the speed of light in metres per second, and $\\mathbf B\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ a constant magnetic-field vector in teslas. Set $m:=M/c^2>0$. Let $K\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ be a real symmetric matrix with $\\operatorname{tr}K=0$, with units volts per square metre, and define the quadratic scalar potential $\\Phi(\\mathbf r):=\\tfrac12\\mathbf r^{T}K\\mathbf r$ for $\\mathbf r\\in\\mathbb R^3$ in metres. Thus $\\partial_i\\partial_j\\Phi=K_{ij}$ and $\\sum_{i=1}^3\\partial_i^2\\Phi=\\operatorname{tr}K=0$. Put $\\alpha:=qc^2/M=q/m$, $D:=-\\alpha K$, and define $C:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $C\\mathbf w:=\\alpha\\,\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf B$. The ideal linear model is the real linear map $A:\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R^3$, $A(\\mathbf r,\\mathbf v):=(\\mathbf v,D\\mathbf r+C\\mathbf v)$, equivalently $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I_3\\\\D&C\\end{pmatrix}$. Equivalently, its equations are $\\dot{\\mathbf r}=\\mathbf v$ and $\\dot{\\mathbf v}=-(qc^2/M)K\\mathbf r+(qc^2/M)\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B$. The entries of $\\mathbf r,\\mathbf v$ are their real numerical coordinates in the fixed SI units; a state solution $y=(\\mathbf r,\\mathbf v)$ is $C^1$ on an open time interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$. Here $\\mathbf v$ has units metres per second, $D$ has units inverse seconds squared, and $C$ has units inverse seconds.\n\nDefine a linear mode to be a complex eigenvector of the complexified state matrix $A$; its eigenvalue $+i\\omega$ or $-i\\omega$ gives the time factor $e^{\\pm i\\omega t}$, with angular frequency $\\omega>0$ in radians per second. Assume the complexification of $A$ is diagonalizable with eigenvalues $+i\\omega_1,-i\\omega_1,+i\\omega_2,-i\\omega_2,+i\\omega_3,-i\\omega_3$, where each $\\omega_j$ is counted with its eigenvalue multiplicity. Define the free cyclotron angular frequency $\\omega_c:=|q|c^2|\\mathbf B|/M$. Then\n\n$$\\omega_c^2=\\omega_1^2+\\omega_2^2+\\omega_3^2.$$\n\nConsequently, if $\\nu_c:=\\omega_c/(2\\pi)$ and $\\nu_j:=\\omega_j/(2\\pi)$ are the corresponding ordinary frequencies in hertz, then $\\nu_c^2=\\nu_1^2+\\nu_2^2+\\nu_3^2$. The claim is conditional on the stated uniform-field, harmonic-quadrupole, and complete stable-mode hypotheses; it is an identity for this linear model.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 By [L1], the inertial-mass coefficient in the SI equation is $q/m=qc^2/M=\\alpha$. Block multiplication gives $A^2=\\begin{pmatrix}D&C\\\\CD&D+C^2\\end{pmatrix}$, so $\\operatorname{tr}(A^2)=2\\operatorname{tr}D+\\operatorname{tr}(C^2)$. [L1, A1, algebra]\n\n1.2 By [A2], write the complexification as $A=P\\Lambda P^{-1}$, where $\\Lambda$ is diagonal with the six stated eigenvalues. Then $A^2=P\\Lambda^2P^{-1}$; the finite-sum identity $\\operatorname{tr}(XY)=\\sum_{k,\\ell}X_{k\\ell}Y_{\\ell k}=\\operatorname{tr}(YX)$ gives $\\operatorname{tr}(A^2)=\\operatorname{tr}(\\Lambda^2)=\\sum_{j=1}^3((i\\omega_j)^2+(-i\\omega_j)^2)=-2\\sum_{j=1}^3\\omega_j^2$. [A2, algebra]\n\n2.1 Since $D=-\\alpha K$ and $\\operatorname{tr}K=0$, $\\operatorname{tr}D=0$. In the fixed right-handed Cartesian coordinates, $C$ has matrix $\\alpha\\begin{pmatrix}0&B_3&-B_2\\\\-B_3&0&B_1\\\\B_2&-B_1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, so the diagonal entries of $C^2$ are $-\\alpha^2(B_2^2+B_3^2)$, $-\\alpha^2(B_1^2+B_3^2)$, and $-\\alpha^2(B_1^2+B_2^2)$. Therefore $\\operatorname{tr}(C^2)=-2\\alpha^2|\\mathbf B|^2=-2\\omega_c^2$ and $\\operatorname{tr}D=0$ gives $\\operatorname{tr}(A^2)=-2\\omega_c^2$ by step 1.1. [A1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Equating the trace calculations in steps 2.1 and 1.2 and dividing by $-2$ gives $\\omega_c^2=\\omega_1^2+\\omega_2^2+\\omega_3^2$. [step 2.1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 Dividing the angular-frequency identity in step 3.1 by $(2\\pi)^2$ gives the same sum-of-squares identity for $\\nu_c,\\nu_1,\\nu_2,\\nu_3$. [step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. S. Brown and G. Gabrielse, Geonium theory: Physics of a single electron or ion in a Penning trap, Reviews of Modern Physics 58 (1986), §II.A and §II.D, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.17), (2.70)–(2.79)",
                "url": "https://cfp.physics.northwestern.edu/documents/GeoniumTheoryReview.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Manuel Vogel, Particle Confinement in Penning Traps: An Introduction, Springer Series on Atomic, Optical, and Plasma Physics 100 (2018), Chapter 5, §§5.1–5.3.2, Eqs. (5.1)–(5.27)",
                "url": "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/72726/1/243.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Cambridge Part III complete lecture notes, §0.1 Units and Scales",
                "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/quantum-field-theory/qfthtml/S0"
              },
              {
                "title": "X. Fan et al., Measurement of the Electron Magnetic Moment, Physical Review Letters 130, 071801 (2023), arXiv:2209.13084v2 (2022)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2209.13084"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-example-deterministic-ratio-error",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Deterministic ratio error and Taylor remainder",
          "reference": "research/scaffold-audit-2026-10-12/qft/complete-local-arguments.md#MX04-D",
          "design_state": "Complete local algebraic proof; no in-run supplier.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_roles": {},
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Define $R:\\mathbb R\\times(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ by $R(a,c):=1+a/c$. Here $c$ is only the positive denominator variable in this lemma, not the speed of light. For real $\\delta a,\\delta c$ and nonnegative bounds $\\epsilon_a,\\epsilon_c$ satisfying $|\\delta a|\\le\\epsilon_a$ and $|\\delta c|\\le\\epsilon_c<c$, both denominators are positive and\n\n$$\\left|R(a+\\delta a,c+\\delta c)-R(a,c)\\right|\\le\\frac{c\\epsilon_a+|a|\\epsilon_c}{c(c-\\epsilon_c)}.$$\n\nFor the first-order statement, write $x=(a,c)$, $h=(u,v)$, and $\\|h\\|_2:=\\sqrt{u^2+v^2}$. Suppose the segment $\\{x+th:0\\le t\\le1\\}$ lies in the closed rectangle $Q=[A_-,A_+]\\times[c_-,c_+]$, where $A_-\\le A_+$ and $0<c_-\\le c_+$. Define $A_*:=\\max(|A_-|,|A_+|)$, $K_Q:=(A_*+c_+/2)/c_-^3$, and the real-linear map $L_x(u,v):=u/c-av/c^2$. The total derivative of $R$ at $x$ means a real-linear map $L$ such that $\\lim_{h\\to0}|R(x+h)-R(x)-L(h)|/\\|h\\|_2=0$. Then $L_x$ is the total derivative, and\n\n$$\\left|R(x+h)-R(x)-L_x(h)\\right|\\le K_Q\\|h\\|_2^2.$$",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 From [A1], $c+\\delta c\\ge c-\\epsilon_c>0$. Thus both ratio values are defined, and subtracting their fractions gives\n$R(a+\\delta a,c+\\delta c)-R(a,c)=\\frac{c\\delta a-a\\delta c}{c(c+\\delta c)}$. [A1, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $\\eta\\to0$ with $c+\\eta>0$, the coordinate difference quotients are\n$\\frac{R(a+\\eta,c)-R(a,c)}{\\eta}=\\frac1c$ and\n$\\frac{R(a,c+\\eta)-R(a,c)}{\\eta}=-\\frac{a}{c(c+\\eta)}$.\nTheir limits give the candidate linear map $L_x(u,v)=u/c-av/c^2$; the total-derivative conclusion follows from the full remainder estimate below. [A2, algebra]\n\n1.3 Direct subtraction gives\n$R(x+h)-R(x)-L_x(h)=\\frac{av^2-cuv}{c^2(c+v)}$. [A2, algebra]\n\n2.1 The triangle inequality gives $|c\\delta a-a\\delta c|\\le c\\epsilon_a+|a|\\epsilon_c$, while [A1] gives $c(c+\\delta c)\\ge c(c-\\epsilon_c)>0$. Dividing these positive-denominator bounds proves the displayed deterministic ratio-error inequality. [A1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 Since the segment lies in $Q$, $c,c+v\\ge c_-$, $c\\le c_+$, and $|a|\\le A_*$. Also $2|uv|\\le u^2+v^2$, because $(|u|-|v|)^2\\ge0$. Therefore\n$|av^2-cuv|\\le A_*v^2+c_+|uv|\\le(A_*+c_+/2)(u^2+v^2)$, and $c^2(c+v)\\ge c_-^3$. Combining these estimates with the exact remainder in step 1.3 gives\n$|R(x+h)-R(x)-L_x(h)|\\le K_Q\\|h\\|_2^2$. [A2, step 1.3, algebra]\n\n3.1 For fixed $x=(a,c)$ with $c>0$, choose $Q=[a-1,a+1]\\times[c/2,3c/2]$, so $x$ is interior and its lower second-coordinate bound is positive. For every sufficiently small $h$, both endpoints lie in $Q$, and convexity places the segment there. Step 2.2 then gives\n$\\frac{|R(x+h)-R(x)-L_x(h)|}{\\|h\\|_2}\\le K_Q\\|h\\|_2\\to0$.\nBy the stated definition, $DR(x)=L_x$. [A2, step 2.2, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "L. S. Brown and G. Gabrielse, Geonium theory: Physics of a single electron or ion in a Penning trap, Reviews of Modern Physics 58 (1986), §II.A and §II.D",
                "url": "https://cfp.physics.northwestern.edu/documents/GeoniumTheoryReview.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Manuel Vogel, Particle Confinement in Penning Traps: An Introduction, Springer Series on Atomic, Optical, and Plasma Physics 100 (2018), Chapter 5",
                "url": "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/72726/1/243.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 535
    },
