    {
      "id": "qft-mg02",
      "title": "Exact compact U1 rotor plus finite CAR data",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "qft-mg02-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "orthonormal-bases-parseval-and-fourier-series",
        "qft-mf01",
        "unbounded-self-adjoint-operators-and-stones-theorem"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite fermion-mode occupation basis",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\mathcal K$ be a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space with a finite ordered orthonormal basis $(e_j)_{j\\in J}$, and use the antisymmetric Fock construction and second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Assume its fermionic operators satisfy the CAR of [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $S=\\{j_1<\\cdots<j_k\\}\\subseteq J$, set\n$$\\xi_S=c^\\dagger(e_{j_1})\\cdots c^\\dagger(e_{j_k})\\Omega,\\qquad \\xi_\\varnothing=\\Omega.$$\nThen $(\\xi_S)_{S\\subseteq J}$ is an orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$. For each $j\\in J$, the bounded number operator $N_j=c^\\dagger(e_j)c(e_j)$ is an orthogonal projection and\n$$N_j\\xi_S=\\mathbf1_{j\\in S}\\xi_S.$$\nConsequently, for any partition $J=\\bigsqcup_{x\\in V}J_x$ into finite site-mode sets, $n_x=\\sum_{j\\in J_x}N_j$ is bounded self-adjoint and acts on $\\xi_S$ by the occupation count $N_x(S)=|S\\cap J_x|\\in\\{0,\\ldots,|J_x|\\}$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979)",
                "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 55-page review; relevant Hamiltonian and Gauss-law treatment in §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696. The Rome1 PDF is byte-identical to the previously retrieved full text."
              },
              {
                "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294",
                "locator": "Full-text arXiv HTML rendition; §§2.1–2.4, printed pp. 3–8. The corresponding complete 32-page PDF was also retrieved and read."
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": null,
          "reference": "Local A-page prerequisite: prove the finite-mode occupation ONB and site-number spectrum used by the exact Gauss multipliers.",
          "design_state": "Added after dependency audit; complete local conditional proof from the current Fock and CAR interfaces.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Exact compact U(1) rotor plus finite CAR data",
          "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q5; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data.md",
          "design_state": "Current batch-37 QFT-MF01 Fock/CAR interfaces inspected and matched; model remains conditional on draft suppliers; finite occupation prerequisite supplied locally.",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Gamma=(V,E,s,t,\\mathcal P)$ be a finite oriented graph with nonempty finite vertex set $V$, nonempty finite link set $E$, endpoint maps $s,t:E\\to V$, and a finite list $\\mathcal P$ of oriented simple cycles. Parallel links and self-loops are allowed. An oriented simple cycle $p\\in\\mathcal P$ is a finite closed path using each link at most once; write $\\sigma_{p,e}\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ for its signed use of link $e$, so its signed incidence at every vertex is zero. Fix enumerations $E=(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_N)$ and $V=(v_1,\\ldots,v_M)$, where $N=|E|$ and $M=|V|$. Write $\\mathbb T_1=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ and $\\mathbb T_{2\\pi}=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$, and set $\\mathbb T=\\mathbb T_{2\\pi}$ with normalized Haar measure $d\\theta/(2\\pi)$ and product measure $m_E$ on $\\mathbb T^E$.\n\nThe coordinatewise map\n$$\\Phi_E:\\mathbb T_{2\\pi}^E\\to\\mathbb T_1^E,\\qquad ([\\theta_e])_{e\\in E}\\mapsto([\\theta_e/(2\\pi)])_{e\\in E},$$\nis a well-defined measure-preserving group isomorphism for the normalized product Haar measures. The fixed enumeration of $E$ identifies $\\mathbb T_1^E$ with $\\mathbb T_1^N$; pulling back the Fourier characters $x\\mapsto\\exp(2\\pi i\\sum_{e\\in E}n_ex_e)$ along $\\Phi_E$ gives $e_{\\mathbf n}(\\theta)=\\exp(i\\sum_{e\\in E}n_e\\theta_e)$. Thus the Fourier basis theorem [[thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus]], whose torus is $\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ and whose characters are $\\exp(2\\pi i kx)$, gives an orthonormal basis $\\{e_{\\mathbf n}:\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb Z^E\\}$ of $L^2(\\mathbb T^E)$. The second-variable-linear $L^2$ pairing used below is the conjugate of the first-variable-linear pairing in that theorem, so orthonormality and completeness are unchanged.\n\nFix an integer $s\\ge1$ and let $\\mathcal K=\\bigoplus_{x\\in V}\\mathbb C^s$ with orthonormal modes $e_{x\\alpha}$, $1\\le\\alpha\\le s$. This is finite-dimensional and hence separable. Fix the lexicographic order on $J=V\\times\\{1,\\ldots,s\\}$ induced by the chosen order on $V$ and the usual order on $\\{1,\\ldots,s\\}$. Use the antisymmetric Fock space and second-variable-linear inner product convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The current draft supplier [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]] states that, assuming Countable Choice, $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$ is a Hilbert space and its creation and annihilation operators are bounded and satisfy the CAR; that supplier is still a draft carrier, not an accepted result in this run. For $\\mathbf m=(m_{x\\alpha})\\in\\{0,1\\}^J$, put $S(\\mathbf m)=\\{(x,\\alpha):m_{x\\alpha}=1\\}$ and write $\\xi_{\\mathbf m}=\\xi_{S(\\mathbf m)}$ for the ordered wedge basis vector of [[lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis]]. This is a complete orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$; changing the chosen order changes individual wedge vectors only by signs. Write $c_{x\\alpha}=c(e_{x\\alpha})$, $c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}=c^\\dagger(e_{x\\alpha})$, and $n_x=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}c_{x\\alpha}$. On $\\xi_{\\mathbf m}$, $n_x$ acts by $N_x(\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}$; it is bounded self-adjoint with spectrum contained in $\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$.\n\nUse the second-variable-linear $L^2$ pairing $\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb T^E}\\overline{f(\\theta)}g(\\theta)\\,dm_E(\\theta)$. The kinematical Hilbert space is\n$$\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}=L^2(\\mathbb T^E)\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K),$$\nwhere $\\widehat\\otimes$ has the same completion norm as [[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]] and the second-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating its first-variable-linear tensor pairing, as in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Concretely, the tensor supplier is applied to the conjugate first-variable-linear pairings $(f,g)_1=\\langle g,f\\rangle_2$ and $(\\Phi,\\Psi)_1=\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi\\rangle_2$, and the resulting tensor pairing is conjugated; this preserves the norm and the product-basis assertion. Consequently\n$$\\{e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m}:\\mathbf n\\in\\mathbb Z^E,\\ \\mathbf m\\in\\{0,1\\}^J\\}$$\nis a complete orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$ by the product-basis assertion of [[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]]. This is the joint basis used for all diagonal multipliers below. Write\n$\\psi=\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m}$.\nAll matter operators previously defined on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$ act on\n$\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$ as identity on the rotor factor tensor that operator.\nOn the full kinematical basis define\n$$E_e(e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m})=n_e(e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m}),\\qquad D(E_e)=\\left\\{\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}n_e^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThus $E_e$ is the maximal real diagonal multiplier, self-adjoint with integer\nspectrum, and on its domain its coefficients are $n_e\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}$.\nDefine the bounded unitary $U_e$ as multiplication by $e^{i\\theta_e}$ on the\nrotor factor and identity on the matter factor. Equivalently,\n$$U_e(e_{\\mathbf n}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m})=e_{\\mathbf n+\\delta_e}\\otimes\\xi_{\\mathbf m},\\qquad (U_e\\psi)_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}=\\psi_{\\mathbf n-\\delta_e,\\mathbf m}.$$\nEach $U_f$ preserves $D(E_e)$, since\n$(n_e+\\delta_{ef})^2\\le2n_e^2+2$ and the coefficient sequence is square\nsummable; its inverse preserves that domain by the same estimate. Computing\non coefficients gives $[E_e,U_f]=\\delta_{ef}U_f$ on $D(E_e)$.\n\n\nFix background integers $b_x\\in\\{0,\\ldots,s\\}$. In the joint basis define the real integer multiplier\n$$\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{e:s(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{e:t(e)=x}n_e-\\left(\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}-b_x\\right),$$\nand let $G_x$ be its maximal diagonal operator, with domain $D(G_x)=\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)|^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\}$. On the common link-electric domain this is the finite sum $\\sum_{s(e)=x}E_e-\\sum_{t(e)=x}E_e-(n_x-b_xI)$. These are the Gauss operators; the operator constraint defines $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}:=\\bigcap_{x\\in V}\\ker G_x$. For $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb T^V$ define the diagonal multiplier $U(\\alpha)=\\exp(i\\sum_x\\alpha_xG_x)$; the next lemma proves that these operators form a strongly continuous representation and that their normalized Haar average is the orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$.\n\nFor each $x$, choose a Hermitian matrix $M_x\\in M_s(\\mathbb C)$, and for each link $e$ choose a matrix $T_e\\in M_s(\\mathbb C)$. Choose real coefficients $\\lambda_E>0$ and $\\lambda_p\\in\\mathbb R$ for $p\\in\\mathcal P$. Write\n$$W_p=\\prod_{e\\in E}U_e^{\\sigma_{p,e}},\\qquad V_B=-\\frac12\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\lambda_p(W_p+W_p^\\dagger),$$\nand\n$$V_F=\\sum_{x\\in V}\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^s c^\\dagger_{x\\alpha}(M_x)_{\\alpha\\beta}c_{x\\beta}+\\sum_{e\\in E}\\left(A_e+A_e^\\dagger\\right),\\qquad A_e=\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^s c^\\dagger_{s(e),\\alpha}(T_e)_{\\alpha\\beta}U_e c_{t(e),\\beta}.$$\nThe operator $H_0=\\lambda_E\\sum_{e\\in E}E_e^2$ is the diagonal multiplier with domain\n$$D(H_0)=\\left\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}\\left(\\lambda_E\\sum_{e\\in E}n_e^2\\right)^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nwhere $\\mathbf m=(m_{x\\alpha})$ ranges over occupation configurations with each $m_{x\\alpha}\\in\\{0,1\\}$, and $N_x(\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}$ is the eigenvalue of $n_x$ on that basis vector. The coefficients $\\lambda_E,\\lambda_p$ and entries of $M_x,T_e$ are ordinary real or complex scalars here; no physical units are assigned. Set $H=H_0+V_B+V_F$ on $D(H_0)$. This is a mathematical finite-graph regulator definition. It asserts no physical adoption, continuum limit, Lorentz invariance, or identification with a particular continuum QED model.\n\nFor sign comparison only, if a smooth path $\\gamma_e:[0,1]\\to X$ in a smooth manifold $X$, a real $C^1$ connection one-form $a$, and a real $C^1$ function $\\lambda$ are supplied, take $U_e^{\\rm cont}=\\exp(-ig\\int_0^1a_{\\gamma_e(r)}(\\dot\\gamma_e(r))\\,dr)$ for real $g$. Under $a\\mapsto a+d\\lambda$, the fundamental theorem along the supplied path gives the endpoint factor\n$e^{ig(\\lambda(\\gamma_e(0))-\\lambda(\\gamma_e(1)))}$.\nThis is an edgewise sign comparison: it has the same source-minus-target\nangle pattern as the finite vertex-phase convention. Neither $s(e)$ nor\n$t(e)$ is evaluated by $\\lambda$; they are vertices in $V$, whereas\n$\\gamma_e(0),\\gamma_e(1)$ are points of $X$. No map $V\\to X$ or compatible\npaths for the whole graph are supplied, and no continuum limit is asserted.",
          "strategy": "Define the finite graph, link torus, CAR Fock factor, maximal diagonal Gauss operators, bounded plaquette and hopping terms, and exact electric Hamiltonian domain. Preserve the conditional QFT-MF01 CAR supplier and the fixed-regulator scope.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979), §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696",
                "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 55-page review retrieved; §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696, read for the compact angular link, integer electric spectrum, Hamiltonian, gauge generator and Gauss constraint."
              },
              {
                "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020), §§2.1–2.4",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294",
                "locator": "Full-text HTML version cited; the corresponding 32-page PDF was retrieved and read at §§2.1–2.4 for Hilbert-space factors, fermionic matter, U(1) link variables, Gauss generators and invariant Hamiltonian terms. Its quantum-link model is not identified with the rotor model defined here."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics"
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Gauss operators, gauge group and nonempty physical projection",
          "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q5; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection.md",
          "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the conditional compact-rotor/CAR data of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]]. In the joint Fourier/occupation basis write\n$$\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{e:s(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{e:t(e)=x}n_e-\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^s m_{x\\alpha}+b_x\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor every vertex $x$, let $G_x$ be multiplication by $\\gamma_x$ on its maximal diagonal domain\n$$D(G_x)=\\left\\{\\psi:\\sum_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)|^2|\\psi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThen each $G_x$ is self-adjoint, the $G_x$ strongly commute, and $U(\\alpha)=\\exp(i\\sum_x\\alpha_xG_x)$ is a strongly continuous unitary representation of $\\mathbb T^V$. Its action obeys\n$$U(\\alpha)c_{x\\beta}U(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i\\alpha_x}c_{x\\beta},\\qquad U(\\alpha)U_eU(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i(\\alpha_{s(e)}-\\alpha_{t(e)})}U_e.$$\nThe normalized Haar average\n$$P=\\frac1{(2\\pi)^{|V|}}\\int_{[0,2\\pi]^{|V|}}U(\\alpha)\\,d\\alpha$$\nis the orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}=\\bigcap_x\\ker G_x$. This subspace is nonzero for every admissible fixed background $b$: it contains the normalized state with link Fourier labels $n_e=0$ and site occupations $m_{x\\alpha}$ chosen so $\\sum_\\alpha m_{x\\alpha}=b_x$.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979)",
                "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 55-page review; relevant Hamiltonian and Gauss-law treatment in §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696. The Rome1 PDF is byte-identical to the previously retrieved full text."
              },
