            "lem-qft-lattice-hamiltonian-selfadjoint",
            "thm-stone-one-parameter-unitary-groups",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-lattice-hamiltonian-selfadjoint": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-stone-one-parameter-unitary-groups": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the model data of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]] and the Axiom of Choice. Let $H$ be the self-adjoint Hamiltonian on the exact domain supplied by [[lem-qft-lattice-hamiltonian-selfadjoint]], and let $P$ be the physical projection of [[lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection]]. For any scalar $\\hbar>0$, the family\n$$\\mathcal U(t)=e^{-itH/\\hbar},\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R,$$\nis a strongly continuous unitary group on $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$, preserves $D(H)$, and satisfies\n$$\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\mathcal U(h)\\psi-\\psi}{h}=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}H\\psi\\quad(\\psi\\in D(H)).$$\nIt commutes with every gauge transformation $U(\\alpha)$ and with $P$. Hence $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}=\\operatorname{ran}P$ is a reducing subspace and $\\mathcal U(t)|_{\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}}$ is a strongly continuous unitary group with skew-adjoint infinitesimal generator $-iH|_{D(H)\\cap\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}}/\\hbar$; its self-adjoint Stone operator is $-H|_{D(H)\\cap\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}}/\\hbar$. Equivalently, the restricted physical Hamiltonian $H|_{D(H)\\cap\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}}$ is self-adjoint. This is an exact fixed-graph result; no continuum dynamics are asserted.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979)",
