      "items": [
        {
          "id": "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Formal diagram coefficient-to-observable adoption",
          "statement": "For a specifically named finite tree calculation, adopt its selected formal\ndiagram coefficient as a **leading perturbative coefficient** with a stated\nphenomenological reading. For QED, use the vertex and free-line conventions\nof the current draft supplier\n[[post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients]]. A particle of natural charge $q_n$ has\nvertex $iq_n\\gamma^a$; the electron has $q_n=-e_n$ and vertex\n$-ie_n\\gamma^a$. The rational fermion line is\n$$ i\\frac{\\not p+\\mu}{h(p,p)-\\mu^2}, $$\nfor $\\mu>0$ and off-shell $p$ with $h(p,p)-\\mu^2\\ne0$. In Feynman gauge\nthe auxiliary photon line is\n$$ -i\\frac{h_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0}=i\\frac{\\eta_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0}. $$\nFor a finite matrix kernel the coefficientwise inverse in\n[[post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients]] is part of the formal convention and\nasserts no convergence. If a time-ordered distribution is used, use the\nactual free carrier specified there; that identification is conditional on\n[[def-countable-choice]]. The displayed pole notation does not define loop\nproducts. In the pair-production channel below the selected muon also has\nmodel charge $-e$, and only the photon line is internal.\n\nFor declared incoming spin probabilities, sum over declared final spin\nlabels, and a specified formal amplitude $\\mathcal A$, adopt the\ncross-section coefficient map of\n[[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. Its scattering inputs are\npositive-shell energy vectors $p_i=(E_i,\\mathbf p_i)$ with\n$E_i=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p_i|^2+\\mathsf M_{i,\\rm in}^2}>0$,\n$\\mathsf M_{i,\\rm in}\\ge0$, and common future-timelike total\n$P=p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2$. The outgoing $k_j$ are on their specified positive\nshells. Require $s=h(P,P)>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$ for the outgoing\nrest energies and positive incoming flux $F>0$, precisely the supplier's\nadmissible scattering domain. In the center-of-momentum frame it\nuses the invariant incoming flux $F$, the two-body measure $d\\Phi_2$, and the\nfinal-state factor $S_f=2$ for identical particles or $S_f=1$ for\ndistinguishable particles, integrated over the full labeled sphere. When\nthis coefficient is read phenomenologically, the initial preparation,\nchannel, threshold, and approximation are part of the model statement. No\nuniversal error estimate for omitted terms is implied.\n\nFor the scalar examples, separately adopt the constant formal amplitudes\n$\\mathcal A=-\\lambda$ for the quartic contact channel and $\\mathcal G=G_E$\nfor the cubic parent decay, with the units and external normalization stated\nat each use. The scalar scattering inputs lie on their stated positive\nshells with a common total and the same strict threshold and positive-flux\nconditions. For decay, the parent is $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$,\n$\\mathsf M_0>\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2$, and its two positive-shell daughters\nsum to $P_0$. Their coefficient map is the one in\n[[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. If a rate $\\Gamma$ is reported as\nan energy width, the convention is explicitly $\\Gamma_E:=\\hbar\\Gamma$; it\ndoes not supply a survival law.\n\nThe readings above do not identify a formal coefficient with an exact\ncontinuum cross section, probability, decay rate, or measured estimate. In\nparticular they do not construct an interacting continuum $S$-matrix or LSZ\nlimit, establish convergence of a perturbation series, bound loop or\nsoft-radiation effects, specify beam or detector cuts, resolve threshold\nresummation, or reproduce the statistical analysis of an experiment. A\ncomparison with observations would require the actual apparatus, sampling,\ncalibration, uncertainty, and radiative-inclusiveness conditions. The\nreported electron magnetic-moment measurements, including the Fan et al.\nreport listed in the source references, remain source-reported\nempirical results and are not premises of this adoption.",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-example-observable-types",
            "post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients",
            "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-example-observable-types": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 3 §§3.5.3 and 3.6.2–3.6.3, and Chapter 6 §6.6",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6 §§6.4.1, 6.5 and 6.6",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted prepublication draft, §§11, 46–48, 59 and 64",
                "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "X. Fan et al., Measurement of the Electron Magnetic Moment, Physical Review Letters 130, 071801 (2023), arXiv:2209.13084v2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2209.13084"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A model-specific phenomenological reading of named formal tree coefficients through the stated two-body flux and phase-space maps. It adopts no exact continuum scattering operator, LSZ limit, convergence estimate, loop or soft-radiation correction, detector model, or empirical result.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Formal tree scattering and scalar-decay coefficients",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ [[def-countable-choice]]. Use the fixed Cartesian\nMinkowski chart $x=(ct,\\mathbf x)$, the geometric metric\n$\\eta=(-,+,+,+)$, and the distinct algebraic contraction\n$h=-\\eta=(+,-,-,-)$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Let $m,M\\geq0$ be the\nelectron and muon rest energies in joules. In this chart, a future energy\nfour-vector is\n$q=(E,\\mathbf q)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ with $E>0$; it has rest energy $\\mathsf M\\geq0$\nwhen $h(q,q)=\\mathsf M^2$. All four-vector components are in joules, and\n$\\mathbf q$ is $c$ times physical spatial momentum. Let $p_1,p_2$ be future electron\nand positron energy four-vectors with rest energy $m$, and $k_1,k_2$ future\nmuon and antimuon energy four-vectors with rest energy $M$, satisfying\n$p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$ and\n$$ s=h(P,P)>4\\max(m^2,M^2). $$\nBy [[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]], choose a proper orthochronous\n$h$-Lorentz matrix $\\Lambda\\in GL(4,\\mathbb R)$, meaning\n$\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and preservation of the future\ncausal cone $C_+:=\\{(E,\\mathbf q):E>0,\\ h((E,\\mathbf q),(E,\\mathbf q))\\geq0\\}$,\nwith $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$.\nIts boost construction supplies such a map because $P$ is future timelike.\nThe **center-of-momentum inertial chart** has coordinates\n$x^{\\rm CM}=\\Lambda x$ on spacetime and corresponding four-vector components\n$q^{\\rm CM}=\\Lambda q$; from here on, four-vector components are in that\nchart, so $P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$. The strict threshold makes\n$\\mathbf p_1$ and $\\mathbf k_1$ nonzero. Define the\nscattering angle $\\theta\\in[0,\\pi]$ by\n$$\\theta:=\\arccos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf p_1\\cdot\\mathbf k_1}{|\\mathbf p_1|\\,|\\mathbf k_1|}\\right),$$\nusing the Euclidean inner product on $\\mathbb R^3$.\nWrite $g=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0$, as in\n[[def-qft-example-data]], and\n$$ \\beta_e=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{4m^2}{s}},\\qquad \\beta_\\mu=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{4M^2}{s}}. $$\nFor the selected formal one-photon tree coefficient, take equal incoherent\nweight $1/4$ for the four initial spin-label pairs and sum over final spin\nlabels. Take the selected muon model charge to be $-e$ as well. Up to an\nirrelevant common phase, its amplitude is\n$$ \\mathcal M_{\\rm tree}=\\frac{g^2}{s} [\\bar v(p_2)\\gamma^a u(p_1)] [\\bar u(k_1)\\gamma_a v(k_2)], $$\nwhere the brackets denote the product of the two currents. This selected\nformal amplitude adopts the current draft QFT-G02 line/vertex convention;\nit is not an exact amplitude of a constructed continuum theory.\n\nConditional on this amplitude adoption and\n[[post-qft-example-formal-observable-map]], the spin-averaged squared\ncoefficient is\n$$ \\overline{|\\mathcal M_{\\rm tree}|^2} =g^4[3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2 +\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta], $$\nThe distinguishable-particle differential and total\ncross-section coefficients are\n$$ \\frac{d\\sigma_{\\rm coeff}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2\\beta_\\mu}{4s\\beta_e} [3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta], $$\n$$ \\sigma_{\\rm coeff} =\\frac{\\pi\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2}{s}\\frac{\\beta_\\mu}{\\beta_e} [3-\\beta_e^2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\tfrac13\\beta_e^2\\beta_\\mu^2]. $$\nFor $\\delta=4m^2/s\\leq1/2$, the ratio of this differential coefficient to\nthe coefficient at $m=0$ with the same $M,s,\\theta$ belongs to\n$[1,1+4\\delta]$. This bound is only the incoming-electron-mass effect\nwithin the selected tree coefficient.\n\nThe on-shell equations also give\n$$ P_a\\bar v(p_2)\\gamma^a u(p_1)=0,\\qquad P_a\\bar u(k_1)\\gamma^a v(k_2)=0. $$\nThese are the two current contractions for this finite external-spinor\ncalculation; they do not assert an all-orders or loop Ward theorem.\n\nFor the separate scalar conventions of\n[[post-qft-example-formal-observable-map]], let $m_s\\geq0$ be a scalar rest\nenergy, let the incoming $r_1,r_2$ and outgoing $l_1,l_2$ lie on the\npositive $m_s$-shell, meaning each $(E,\\mathbf q)$ has\n$E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf q|^2+m_s^2}>0$, and require\n$r_1+r_2=l_1+l_2=P_s$ with $s=h(P_s,P_s)>4m_s^2$.\nEvaluate in a proper orthochronous center-of-momentum chart taking $P_s$\nto $(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ as in [F4]. Let $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ be the dimensionless\nconstant quartic amplitude parameter. The identical-final full-sphere and\ndistinguishable total coefficients are respectively\n$$ \\sigma_{\\rm coeff}^{\\rm identical} =\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{32\\pi s},\\qquad \\sigma_{\\rm coeff}^{\\rm distinguishable} =\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{16\\pi s}. $$\nFor a parent at rest of energy $\\mathsf M_0>2m_s$ and a constant identical\ndaughter amplitude $G_E$ in joules, take the daughters on the positive\n$m_s$-shell with total $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$. The rate coefficient is\n$$ \\Gamma_{\\rm coeff} =\\frac{|G_E|^2}{32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0} \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4m_s^2}{\\mathsf M_0^2}}\\quad[\\mathrm{s}^{-1}]. $$\nIf an energy width is named, its convention is $\\Gamma_E:=\\hbar\\Gamma_{\\rm\ncoeff}$ in joules. None of these coefficient statements implies an exact\nsurvival law. The QFT-G02 postulate is a current draft carrier; its use here\nretains draft status and does not imply independent acceptance.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-qft-example-data",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types",
            "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness",
            "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
            "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients",
            "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map",
            "post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map": "physical-assumption",
            "post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "provenance": {
