        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-dirac-magnetic-benchmark",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Minimal Dirac magnetic benchmark from the adopted action",
          "statement": "Assume the adopted local Maxwell–Dirac action of\n[[post-qft-qed-local-coupling]] with electron charge $q=-e$, $e>0$, and\nrest mass $m>0$, with $q,e$ in coulombs and $m$ in kilograms. Use the\noriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime\nand inertial chart $x=(ct,\\mathbf x)$ with\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ of [[def-qft-framework-units]]. On\n$\\mathbb R^4$, the static lower potential is\n$A_{{\\rm SI},a}(x^0,\\mathbf x)=(0,\\mathbf A(\\mathbf x))$: it is independent\nof $x^0$, with $\\phi=0$ and\n$\\mathbf A\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ in SI units\n$\\mathrm{T\\,m}$. Define $\\mathbf B=\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A$ in tesla,\n$$ \\pi_i=-i\\hbar\\partial_i-qA_i,\\qquad H_D=c\\sum_{i=1}^3\\alpha_i\\pi_i+\\beta mc^2, $$\nas spatial differential expressions on $C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$.\nLet $\\mathcal D=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$ be their common test\ndomain; smooth coefficients make $\\pi_i$ and $H_D$ map $\\mathcal D$ into\nitself. Use the Dirac matrices and adjoint of\n[[def-qft-dirac-projections]] and\n$$ \\Sigma_i=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_i),\\qquad S_i=\\frac{\\hbar}{2}\\Sigma_i. $$\nThe matrices $\\alpha_i,\\beta,\\Sigma_i$ are dimensionless,\n$S_i$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $\\pi_i$ has units\n$\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, and $H_D$ has units joules. For each smooth\nspacetime spinor field $\\psi:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$, the\ncompactly supported spinor-variation Euler–Lagrange equation of the adopted\naction, after conversion to SI units, holds pointwise as a differential\nequation, with $H_D$ acting on each spatial slice. In the natural action\nnormalization of [[def-qft-framework-units]], $\\psi$ has engineering length\ndegree $-3/2$. Thus the differential equation is\n$$ i\\hbar\\partial_t\\psi=H_D\\psi $$\nfor this pure magnetic specialization. On $\\mathcal D$ its square is\n$$ H_D^2=m^2c^4I_4+c^2(\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B). $$\nWriting the formal first-order square-root coefficient as\n$mc^2+K/(2m)$, where\n$K=\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B$, the spin\nterm is $-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$. Under the explicitly chosen\nmagnetic-energy convention\n$$ H_{\\rm mag}=-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\rm mag}\\cdot\\mathbf B,\\qquad \\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\rm mag}=g_s\\frac{q}{2m}\\mathbf S, $$\nwhere $H_{\\rm mag}$ is an energy-valued matrix coefficient in joules,\n$\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\rm mag}$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix-valued magnetic moment\ncoefficient with units $\\mathrm{A\\,m^2}$, and $g_s$ is dimensionless.\nComparing the coefficient of\n$\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$ as a linear-form coefficient gives $g_s=2$ for\nthis minimal tree coefficient.\nThe equation is an Euler–Lagrange conclusion, not a claim that every field\nis a stationary solution. The square and coefficient are formal on the\nstated test domain; no magnetic self-adjoint closure, full spectrum, radiative\ncorrection, or empirical measurement is asserted.",
          "deps": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-qft-dirac-projections",
            "def-qft-example-data",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types",
            "def-qft-framework-units",
            "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
            "def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities",
            "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square",
            "post-qft-qed-local-coupling"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-qft-framework-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-qft-qed-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-qft-qed-local-coupling": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 4 §§4.3 and 4.7, and Chapter 6 §6.3.1",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S4.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted prepublication draft, §§58 and 64",
                "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "J. Weigand, Quantum Field Theory I and II, complete lecture notes, Chapter 5.1",
                "url": "https://physics.umd.edu/grt/taj/624b/WeigandQFT.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A conditional compact-variation Dirac equation and formal minimal magnetic coefficient for a smooth static pure magnetic background. The result is the minimal/tree Pauli coefficient only: it proves no self-adjoint magnetic closure, full spectrum, Landau-level construction, radiative correction, or empirical equality.",
