    {
      "id": "qft-x02-examples",
      "title": "Formal diagram coefficient-to-observable adoption — Examples",
      "kind": "B",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory",
      "library": "physics",
      "companion": "qft-x02",
      "requires": [
        "qft-x02"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-qft-x04",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Massless formal annihilation angular contrast",
          "statement": "Use the massless $m=M=0$ case of the formal tree theorem\n[[pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay]] with $s>0$. Explicitly take\nincoming $p_1,p_2$ and outgoing $k_1,k_2$ on the positive massless shell,\nso each $(E,\\mathbf q)$ has $E=|\\mathbf q|>0$, with\n$p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$ and $h(P,P)=s>0$. Use the theorem's proper\northochronous center-of-momentum chart $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$;\n$\\theta$ is the Euclidean angle between the nonzero spatial parts of\n$\\Lambda p_1$ and $\\Lambda k_1$. The differential\ncoefficient at $\\theta=0$ is twice its value at $\\theta=\\pi/2$; integrating\nover the full sphere gives\n$$ \\sigma_{\\rm coeff}=\\frac{4\\pi\\alpha^2(\\hbar c)^2}{3s}. $$\nAll three quantities are formal coefficients in the adopted map, not event\ncounts or exact measured cross sections.",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-example-data",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types",
            "pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay",
            "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay": "physical-result",
            "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6 §6.6",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted prepublication draft, §§11 and 46–48",
                "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A massless specialization of the adopted formal one-photon tree coefficient. It compares two angular coefficients and integrates their formal coefficient over the sphere; it is not a measured event ratio or exact continuum QED cross section.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-qft-x05",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Scalar symmetry factor and conditional lifetime model",
          "statement": "Let $s>0$, dimensionless $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, parent energy\n$\\mathsf M_0>0$, and\n$G_E\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$ in joules. For scattering, take the incoming $p_1,p_2$ and outgoing $k_1,k_2$\non the positive massless scalar shell, each $(E,\\mathbf q)$ having\n$E=|\\mathbf q|>0$, with common total\n$p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$, $h(P,P)=s>0$, and evaluate the coefficient in a\nproper orthochronous center-of-momentum chart\n$\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ as in the phase-space supplier.\nFor decay, take massless positive-shell daughters summing to the rest parent\n$P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$. With these data the identical\nfinal-state quartic total coefficient is\n$$ \\sigma_{\\rm coeff}^{\\rm identical} =\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{32\\pi s}, $$\nand the distinguishable-final coefficient is twice this value. The\nidentical-daughter decay-rate coefficient is\n$$ \\Gamma_{\\rm coeff}=\\frac{|G_E|^2}{32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0} \\quad[\\mathrm{s}^{-1}]. $$\nSet $\\Gamma:=\\Gamma_{\\rm coeff}>0$. For the lifetime calculation only, let\n$(\\Xi,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space and let\n$T:\\Xi\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a measurable parent lifetime measured in seconds.\nDefine its law on Borel sets $B\\subseteq[0,\\infty)$ by\n$\\mu_T(B):=\\mathbb P(T\\in B)$, and separately assume that $\\mu_T$ has\ndensity $f_T(t)=\\Gamma e^{-\\Gamma t}$ relative to Lebesgue measure. This is\nthe additional exponential lifetime model; it gives\n$P_{\\rm surv}(t):=\\mathbb P(T>t)=e^{-\\Gamma t}$ for $t\\geq0$. Define the\nmean lifetime as $\\mathbb E[T]:=\\int_{[0,\\infty)}t\\,d\\mu_T(t)$, and define\nthe half-life $t_{1/2}$ by $P_{\\rm surv}(t_{1/2})=1/2$. Then the mean\nlifetime is $1/\\Gamma$ and the half-life is $\\ln 2/\\Gamma$. The exponential\nlaw is not a consequence of the formal coefficient calculation.",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-example-data",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types",
            "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
            "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients",
            "pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay",
            "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients": "mathematical-premise",
            "pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay": "physical-result",
            "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 3 §§3.5.3 and 3.6",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted prepublication draft, §11",
                "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "physical_scope": "A massless-daughter scalar formal coefficient example. It explicitly adds an exponential survival model before deriving a mean and half-life; the formal rate coefficient by itself implies neither time law.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "dependency_level": 9,
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-qft-x09",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Minimal Dirac spin and orbital coefficient comparison",
          "statement": "Take $q=-e$ with $e>0$, $m>0$, and a constant field\n$\\mathbf B=B_0\\hat z$ with $B_0>0$. It is realized by the smooth SI\npotential\n$$ \\mathbf A(x,y,z)=\\frac{B_0}{2}(-y,x,0),\\qquad \\phi=0, $$\nfor which $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A=B_0\\hat z$. By\n[[pthm-qft-example-dirac-magnetic-benchmark]], the formal spin term is\n$-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B=(e/m)S_zB_0$. Since\n$\\Sigma_z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,1,-1)$, its two spin eigenvalue\ncoefficients are\n$$ \\Delta E_{\\uparrow,\\downarrow}=\\pm\\frac{e\\hbar B_0}{2m},\\qquad \\Delta E_{\\rm gap}=\\frac{e\\hbar B_0}{m}. $$\nDefine the comparison orbital coefficient by\n$\\omega_{c,\\rm orb}:=eB_0/m$ and\n$\\nu_{c,\\rm orb}:=\\omega_{c,\\rm orb}/(2\\pi)$. The cyclic coefficient\nfrequency of the spin gap is\n$$ \\nu_{s,\\rm coeff}:=\\frac{\\Delta E_{\\rm gap}}{2\\pi\\hbar} =\\frac{eB_0}{2\\pi m}=\\nu_{c,\\rm orb}. $$\nThis is a comparison of formal minimal coefficients. It is not the full\nLandau spectrum, a radiative correction, a prediction of zero measured\nanomaly, or a laboratory measurement.",
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-example-data",
            "def-qft-example-observable-types",
            "pthm-qft-example-dirac-magnetic-benchmark",
            "post-qft-qed-local-coupling"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
