          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-framework-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-qft-free-field-model": "physical-assumption",
            "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement": "physical-assumption",
            "def-qft-free-field-observables": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "lem-qft-free-domain-observables": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-scalar-local-weyl-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The selected flat four-dimensional real scalar bosonic and Dirac CAR Fock models, with their chosen vacua and bounded smeared observable algebras. Operator-domain and point-smearing conclusions are conditional on the named mathematical carriers. No interacting theory, pointwise field operator, coincident product, thermal trace, apparatus, or empirical outcome is asserted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice, $\\mu\\ge0$, and the selected free-field model of\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]]. Use the four-dimensional affine Minkowski chart,\nsignature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinate $x^0=ct$,\nand inverse-length parameter $\\mu$ of [[def-qft-framework-units]]. For an open\nregion $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, use the scalar and Dirac test functions and\nbounded local observable algebras defined in\n[[def-qft-free-field-observables]]: scalar tests are real members of\n$C_c^\\infty(O)$, Dirac tests are members of\n$C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb C^4)$, and the Dirac observable algebra is generated by\neven products of bounded smeared CAR fields and their adjoints.\n\nFor a real Schwartz scalar test $f$, the closure of $\\Phi_\\mu(f)$ is\nself-adjoint on its exact Segal-field multiplier domain. The scalar number,\nHamiltonian and momentum operators and the Dirac Hamiltonian and momentum\noperators have the exact maximal sector-sum domains stated in\n[[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]]. In particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nIf $A$ is one of these self-adjoint operators and $\\Psi$ is normalized with\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$, then its model mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and variance\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2$$\nare finite; this needs $\\Psi\\in D(A)$, not $\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. The product\n$AB\\Psi$ is defined only when $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$.\n\nIf $O_1,O_2$ are open regions and\n$\\eta_{ab}(x-y)^a(x-y)^b>0$ for every $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, then the\nscalar Weyl algebras of the two regions commute, and the even local Dirac\nalgebras of the two regions commute. These are algebraic properties of the\nspecified free representations.\n\nThe chosen construction supplies only test-smeared fields and does not define\n$\\Phi_\\mu(x)$, $\\Psi(x)$, or coincident diagonal products. Fix a point\n$x_0\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and a nonnegative normalized Schwartz Gaussian\n$\\rho\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$, and put\n$\\rho_{\\epsilon,x_0}(x)=\\epsilon^{-4}\\rho((x-x_0)/\\epsilon)$. These tests\napproximate $\\delta_{x_0}$ on Schwartz functions, but\n$\\|\\Phi_\\mu(\\rho_{\\epsilon,x_0})\\Omega\\|\\to\\infty$ as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$.\nFor an equal-time Dirac field, fix a unit spinor $z$ and a nonnegative\nnormalized Schwartz Gaussian $\\sigma$ on $\\mathbb R^3$, and put\n$u_{\\epsilon,x_0}(\\mathbf x)=\\epsilon^{-3}\n\\sigma((\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0)/\\epsilon)z$. These tests approximate a\nspatial point mass, while $\\|\\Psi_t(u_{\\epsilon,x_0})\\|\\to\\infty$. Neither\nfamily has a finite limit in its respective Fock or operator-norm topology.\nThis restriction does not rule out other distributional or renormalized\nconstructions. The Born interpretation of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]] applies only after a density\nstate and normalized finite bounded instrument have actually been supplied;\nthis item reports no apparatus or outcome.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted complete lecture notes, Chapter 2, §§2.2, 2.3 and 2.6.1",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
                "locator": "Complete HTML Chapter 2; free scalar modes, vacuum and point-field smearing in §2.2, vacuum in §2.3, and spacelike commutativity in §2.6.1."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, Part I: Spin Zero, author prepublication textbook draft, arXiv:hep-th/0409035v1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/hep-th/0409035",
                "locator": "Complete full-text Part I; §3, relativistic scalar-field modes, canonical commutators and formal vacuum terms."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, Part II: Spin One Half, author prepublication textbook draft, arXiv:hep-th/0409036v1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/hep-th/0409036",
                "locator": "Complete full-text Part II; §§37 and 39, Dirac plane-wave modes, canonical anticommutators and particle/antiparticle operators."
              },
              {
                "title": "Christopher J. Fewster and Kasia Rejzner, Algebraic Quantum Field Theory—an introduction, complete author manuscript, arXiv:1904.04051v2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1904.04051v2",
                "locator": "Complete full-text lecture article; §§2.2 and 4.2 on test-function-labelled fields, the free scalar Weyl algebra, local algebras and even Dirac observables."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics"
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-classical-quantum-free-correspondence",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "Classical equations and adopted exact free quantum representation",
          "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/proposed-inventory.json#QFT-F04",
          "design_state": "Checks the classical compact-variation scalar and Dirac equations against the tested equations of the separately adopted free representations. Quantization and statistics assignments are model assumptions, not deductions from the classical actions.",
          "dependency_level": 7,
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-framework-units",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "post-qft-free-field-model",
            "lem-qft-free-action-equations",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity",
            "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
            "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-framework-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-qft-free-field-model": "physical-assumption",
            "lem-qft-free-action-equations": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "The selected flat four-dimensional free real scalar and Dirac models, with the stated (-+++) signature, inverse-length mass parameter and chosen Fock representations. The result matches compact-variation classical equations with weak test equations of the adopted representations. It does not derive quantization, a general spin-statistics rule, interacting dynamics, empirical validity, or a measurement outcome.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the selected free-field model of\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]], using the metric, length coordinates, inverse-\nlength parameter $\\mu\\ge0$, field dimensions and Clifford convention fixed by\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. The classical scalar field\n$\\phi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R$ is smooth and real; the classical\nDirac field $\\psi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$ is smooth with\ncommuting components. Their adopted free actions are the scalar and Dirac\nfunctionals stated in [[post-qft-free-field-model]]. Action symbols are used\nonly through compactly supported smooth variations, so no global\nintegrability of the classical background fields is assumed.\n\nThe compact-variation stationarity equations are\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=0,$$\nwith $\\Box=-\\partial_0^2+\\Delta_{\\mathbf x}$. In the separately adopted\nscalar and Dirac Fock representations, the corresponding weak test equations\nare, for $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ and\n$F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$,\n$$\\Phi_\\mu((\\Box-\\mu^2)f)=0,\\qquad \\Psi\\!\\left((i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F\\right)=0.$$\nHere “weak test equation” means precisely that smearing the constructed field\nwith the displayed differential operator gives the zero operator on its\nstated common domain. With $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf k+\\beta\\mu$,\n$\\gamma^0=\\beta$, and $\\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha^j$ as adopted in\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]], the Dirac test operator is equivalent to\n$i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu$.\n\nThus the classical action equations and the tested equations of the selected\nfree representations have the same differential expressions at these\nnormalizations. The Fock representation, species/statistics assignments and\nvacua are additional physical model assumptions; they are not deductions from\nthe classical actions. No empirical outcome or measurement is asserted.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted complete lecture notes, Chapter 1, §1.1.1",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S1.html",
                "locator": "Complete HTML Chapter 1; §1.1.1, the real scalar action and Klein–Gordon Euler–Lagrange equation."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted complete lecture notes, Chapter 4, §§4.2–4.3",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S4.html",
                "locator": "Complete HTML Chapter 4; Dirac action, independent variation of ψ and ψ-bar, and the Dirac equation. Tong uses the opposite metric signature to the fixed local convention."
              },
              {
                "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted complete lecture notes, Chapter 5, §5.2",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S5.html",
                "locator": "Complete HTML Chapter 5; §5.2, the adopted fermionic quantization convention and free Dirac mode construction."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, Part I: Spin Zero, author prepublication textbook draft, arXiv:hep-th/0409035v1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/hep-th/0409035",
                "locator": "Complete full-text Part I; §3, relativistic scalar-field action, on-shell modes and canonical quantization."
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, Part II: Spin One Half, author prepublication textbook draft, arXiv:hep-th/0409036v1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/hep-th/0409036",
                "locator": "Complete full-text Part II; §§36–37 and 39, spinor-field actions, Dirac equation, canonical anticommutators and particle/antiparticle modes."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "physics"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 555
    },
