      "requires": [
        "qft-ir01"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "rem-qft-cutoff-vs-continuum-interaction",
          "kind": "remark",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Fixed finite-mode results do not establish a continuum limit",
          "reference": "items/rem-qft-cutoff-vs-continuum-interaction.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian",
            "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "In [[def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian]], the regulator is one fixed\nfinite mode count $N$ and one fixed polynomial on $\\mathbb R^N$. The self-adjoint\nand compact-resolvent conclusions of\n[[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]] concern that fixed Hilbert space\nand form realization. They assert no continuum or infinite-volume limit. Here\n“continuum limit” means a limit of a specified family of finite-regulator\nobjects as a cutoff parameter changes, together with an identified comparison\nspace and convergence notion. No such family or convergence structure is part\nof the two cited items. This scope note does not assert that a continuum limit\ndoes not exist.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Given:** [F1] and [F2].\n\n1.1 The hypotheses in [F1] specify a single integer $N$, a single coordinate space $\\mathbb R^N$, and a single potential $V$; they contain no indexed family with varying cutoff. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 A convergence assertion for varying cutoffs requires at least a family of objects, a common comparison space or maps between the spaces, and a convergence relation. Neither those data nor a limiting object occur in [F1] or [F2]. Therefore the theorems establish only the stated fixed-$N$ operator properties. The absence of those hypotheses is not a proof of nonexistence of a limit in a separately specified model. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §2.3, pp.76–83",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.3, printed pp.76–83: quadratic-form construction for a fixed operator domain and Hilbert space."
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 8, pp.48–50",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
                "locator": "Chapter 8, printed pp.48–50: compactness and spectral conclusions for a fixed confining operator."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "proof_strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-qft-quartic-ground-energy-bound",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "One-mode quartic ground-energy increment bound",
          "reference": "items/ex-qft-quartic-ground-energy-bound.md#Statement",
          "design_state": "Authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "deps": [
            "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian",
            "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction",
            "lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier",
            "lem-qft-regulated-ground-variational-bound",
            "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
            "thm-dominated-convergence",
            "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "cor-mean-value-theorem"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-regulated-ground-variational-bound": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
            "cor-mean-value-theorem": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume $a>0$, $m>0$, and $\\lambda\\ge0$, all real mathematical parameters.\nFor $N=1$ take the polynomial\n$$V_\\lambda(q)=\\frac{m^2}{2}q^2+\\lambda q^4$$\nand the differential expression $h_\\lambda=-a\\,d^2/dq^2+V_\\lambda(q)$ with\nthe form realization of [[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]]. Write\n$E_g(\\lambda)$ for its least eigenvalue. Then\n$$0\\le E_g(\\lambda)-E_g(0)\\le\\frac{3a\\lambda}{2m^2}.$$\nThe parameters are mathematical and dimensionless here; no physical mass,\nunit, measurement, or continuum claim is made.",
          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Given:** $a,m>0$, $\\lambda\\ge0$, and the forms $q_0,q_\\lambda$.\n\n1.1 Write $a_\\lambda=q_\\lambda+2\\|\\cdot\\|_2^2$ for the shifted form inner product of the $\\lambda$ completion. On $C_c^\\infty$, $V_\\lambda\\ge V_0$ gives $a_\\lambda(u,u)\\ge a_0(u,u)$, so a $Q_\\lambda$-Cauchy sequence is $Q_0$-Cauchy and the identity extends continuously to $Q_\\lambda\\to Q_0$ with the same $L^2$ representative. The unshifted forms $q_\\lambda=a_\\lambda-2\\|\\cdot\\|_2^2$ and $q_0=a_0-2\\|\\cdot\\|_2^2$ are continuous on their respective form spaces. For any $u\\in Q_\\lambda$, approximate it in $Q_\\lambda$ by $u_r\\in C_c^\\infty$; then $q_\\lambda(u_r)\\ge q_0(u_r)$ for every $r$, and continuity of both forms gives $q_\\lambda(u)\\ge q_0(u)$ in the limit. For normalized $u\\in Q_\\lambda$, [F3] then gives $q_\\lambda(u)\\ge q_0(u)\\ge E_g(0)$. Taking the infimum yields $E_g(\\lambda)\\ge E_g(0)$. [A1, F1, F3, F4, algebra]\n\n1.2 The Gaussian $\\phi_b$ from [F2] and its derivative are polynomial times a Gaussian. For cutoffs $\\chi_R$ as in the cutoff construction of\n[[lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier]], $\\chi_R\\phi_b\\in C_c^\\infty$\nconverges to $\\phi_b$ in the $\\lambda$ form norm: the derivative error is\n$(1-\\chi_R)\\phi_b'-(\\chi_R')\\phi_b$, whose first term has vanishing Gaussian\ntail and whose second has norm at most $C\\|\\phi_b\\|_2/R$; the weighted error\nhas squared magnitude bounded by $(m^2q^2/2+\\lambda q^4+2)|\\phi_b|^2$, an\nintegrable polynomial-Gaussian tail. Thus $\\phi_b\\in Q_\\lambda$. [A1, F2, F4, algebra]\n\n2.1 Let $\\mathbb E_b[g]:=\\int g(q)\\sqrt{b/\\pi}e^{-bq^2}\\,dq$. The Hermite\nground function in [F2] is normalized, so $\\mathbb E_b[1]=1$. On any compact\ninterval of positive $b$ values, the derivatives of the integrands through\norder two are bounded by a constant times $(1+|q|^4)e^{-b_*q^2}$ for some\n$b_*>0$, which is integrable; [F5] therefore permits differentiation under the\nintegral. Differentiating $\\mathbb E_b[1]=1$ gives\n$$0=\\mathbb E_b\\!\\left[\\frac1{2b}-q^2\\right],\\qquad \\mathbb E_b[q^2]=\\frac1{2b}.$$\nDifferentiating the second identity and using\n$\\partial_b(\\sqrt{b/\\pi}e^{-bq^2})=(\\frac1{2b}-q^2)\\sqrt{b/\\pi}e^{-bq^2}$ gives\n$$-\\frac1{2b^2}=\\mathbb E_b\\!\\left[q^2\\left(\\frac1{2b}-q^2\\right)\\right] =\\frac1{4b^2}-\\mathbb E_b[q^4],$$\nso $\\mathbb E_b[q^4]=3/(4b^2)$. Since $\\phi_b'=-bq\\phi_b$, this gives\n$\\|\\phi_b'\\|_2^2=b/2$. Hence\n$$\\|\\phi_b\\|_2^2=1,\\quad \\|\\phi_b'\\|_2^2=b/2,\\quad \\int q^2|\\phi_b|^2=1/(2b),\\quad\\int q^4|\\phi_b|^2=3/(4b^2).$$\nThe unperturbed form value\nis $ab/2+m^2/(4b)=m\\sqrt{a/2}=E_g(0)$, using $b=m/\\sqrt{2a}$, and the quartic\ncontribution is $3\\lambda/(4b^2)=3a\\lambda/(2m^2)$. The variational bound\n[F3] applied to this normalized vector yields\n$$E_g(\\lambda)\\le E_g(0)+\\frac{3a\\lambda}{2m^2}.$$\nCombining with step 1.1 proves both inequalities, including $\\lambda=0$.\n[A1, F2, F3, F5, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §8.3, pp.212–214",
                "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
                "locator": "§8.3, printed pp.212–214: the Hermite ground state and oscillator eigenvalue."
              },
              {
                "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chapter 3, pp.21–24",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
                "locator": "Chapter 3, printed pp.21–24: the normalized Gaussian ground state of the one-dimensional oscillator."
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "library": "mathematics"
        }
      ],
      "scaffold": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md",
      "order": 558
    },
    {
