    {
      "id": "qft-ir03",
      "title": "Regular polynomial field functional algebra",
      "category": "quantum-field-theory-mathematics",
      "library": "mathematics",
      "companion": "qft-ir03-examples",
      "kind": "A",
      "requires": [
        "distributions-test-functions-and-differentiation",
        "qft-mf04",
        "the-riemann-integral-in-rn-and-jordan-content",
        "fejer-and-poisson-summability-of-fourier-series"
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "physical_scope": null,
          "title": "Regular polynomial field functional algebra",
          "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#regular-polynomial-field-functional-algebra",
          "design_state": "Finite symmetric compact coefficient families define the regular algebra and its smooth functional derivative kernels; local diagonal distributions are excluded.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
            "def-test-function-topology",
            "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
            "thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space",
            "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-test-function-topology": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and $n\\ge1$. Write\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ for the complex test-function\nspace of smooth compactly supported functions\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]), with its LF topology\n([[def-test-function-topology]]). The convergence criterion used below is\nthat convergent test sequences have one common compact support and converge\nuniformly with every derivative\n([[thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space]]). Here $C^\\infty$\nmeans every coordinate derivative exists and is continuous\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]). All integrals are\ncompact Riemann integrals: each integrand is continuous with compact support,\nso restrict it to a compact rectangle and apply\n[[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ be the subspace of kernels invariant under\nevery permutation of their $n$ $X$-variables. Fields are real smooth\nconfigurations $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(X;\\mathbb R)$. For an integer $N\\ge0$\nand a finite family\n$f_0\\in\\mathbb C$, $f_n\\in\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ for\n$1\\le n\\le N$, define\n$$ F_f(\\phi)=f_0+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\int_{X^n}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_n. $$\nThe pairing is complex-bilinear, with no conjugation on the kernel or field.\nAn element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$ is this finite symmetric\ncoefficient family, evaluated on fields by $F_f$. Pointwise sums, scalar\nmultiples, and products again have finite symmetric compactly supported\ncoefficient families: for products, expand the two finite sums and\nsymmetrize each tensor product $f_m\\otimes g_n$. Thus these elements form\nan algebra; the product-integral identity follows from Riemann Fubini on a\nrectangle containing the compact supports\n([[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]).\n\nFor $k=0$, set $F_f^{(0)}(\\phi)=F_f(\\phi)$. For $1\\le k\\le N$,\nthe $k$-th functional derivative kernel assigned to this coefficient\nfamily is\n$$ F_f^{(k)}(\\phi)(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)=\\sum_{n=k}^{N}\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\int_{X^{n-k}}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=k+1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_{k+1}\\cdots dx_n. $$\nFor $k>N$ it is zero. Each displayed kernel is smooth, symmetric and compactly supported in its $k$ remaining variables: compactness follows by projection of the finite union of coefficient-kernel supports, and derivatives pass through the finite-compact integrals by [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]].\nIn the term $n=k$, the empty integral and empty product mean simply\n$f_k(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)$.\n\nThe scope is exactly smooth compact coefficient kernels. A local functional\nwhose higher derivative kernels are distributions on a thin diagonal is not\nincluded merely because its field expression is polynomial.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
