            "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "statement": "Let $d,n\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and give\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ its LF test-function topology.\nThe finite linear span of elementary product tests\n$$ (x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\longmapsto\\prod_{j=1}^{n}g_j(x_j),\\qquad g_j\\in\\mathcal D(X), $$\nis dense. More precisely, for each $\\Phi\\in\\mathcal D(X^n)$, there is a\nsequence of such finite sums whose supports lie in one fixed compact product\nand whose derivatives of every multi-index converge uniformly to those of\n$\\Phi$. Symmetrized finite sums are dense in\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$. Consequently the symmetric-kernel\nrepresentation in [[def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra]] is\nunique: if a finite symmetric-kernel polynomial vanishes on every real smooth\nfield, every coefficient kernel vanishes.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
