          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "R. Brunetti and K. Fredenhagen, Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds, Theorems 5.2–5.3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math-ph/9903028"
              },
              {
                "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, “Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory,” in Mathematical Aspects of Quantum Field Theories, ed. Damien Calaque and Thomas Strobl, Springer (2015), Section 7",
                "url": "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73875/1/95.pdf.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "K. J. Keller, Dimensional Regularization in Position Space and a Forest Formula for Regularized Epstein-Glaser Renormalization, dissertation, Theorem I.6",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1006.2148"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, let $t\\in\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\{0\\})$ have finite real scaling degree $\\delta$, and let $\\mathcal E_\\delta(t)$ be the set of its extensions in $\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^n)$ having scaling degree exactly $\\delta$. This set is nonempty by [[thm-qft-finite-scaling-degree-extension]]. If $\\delta<n$, then $\\mathcal E_\\delta(t)$ has exactly one member. If $\\delta\\ge n$, let $k$ be the unique nonnegative integer with $k\\le\\delta-n<k+1$ (the floor of $\\delta-n$). Then, for any $T_0\\in\\mathcal E_\\delta(t)$,\n\n$$\\mathcal E_\\delta(t)=T_0+\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb C}\\{\\partial^\\alpha\\delta_0:|\\alpha|\\le k\\}.$$\n\nHere a **point-local counterterm** means a distribution whose support, as defined in [[def-support-of-a-distribution]], is contained in $\\{0\\}$. Thus the same-degree freedom is finite dimensional, with precisely the displayed delta derivatives as its basis; at the threshold $\\delta=n$, only a multiple of $\\delta_0$ is allowed."
        },
